              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n`PdfParser`'s `get_value()` can recurse without any limit and crash with an uncaught `RecursionError`. It is reachable through `Image.save(im, fp=\u003cpdf\u003e, format=\"PDF\", append=True)` when the existing PDF file is attacker-controlled. This is the same class of bug as https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-r73j-pqj5-w3x7 (the /Prev trailer chain loop, fixed earlier this year), in the same file, but in two different functions that fix did not touch.\n\n### Details\n**`get_value()` in `src/PIL/PdfParser.py`**\n\nThis is a classmethod that takes `max_nesting: int = -1` and has a guard:\n\n```python\nif max_nesting == 0:\n    return None, None\n```\n\nEvery recursive call (nested arrays, dictionaries, indirect object definitions) passes `max_nesting=max_nesting - 1`. Since the default and every call site start at -1, this only ever counts down further negative: -1, -2, -3, and so on. It never equals exactly 0, so the guard never runs. A PDF object with a value nested a few thousand levels deep is enough to hit Python's recursion limit. It does not even need a valid `/Pages` tree, since `read_pdf_info()` parses the `/Root` object's own dictionary first, before checking anything else.\n\nThis advisory reports a single vulnerability: an ineffective recursion-depth guard in `PdfParser.get_value()` (the sentinel  `-1`  default never reaches the `== 0` check). The mention of GHSA-r73j-pqj5-w3x7 is comparative context for an already-fixed, already-CVE'd bug in a different function — it is not a second vulnerability being reported. The three trigger paths (nested arrays/dicts/indirect objects) are not independently fixable; they share one root cause and one fix location in  get_value() .\n\n### Steps to reproduce\nBoth PDFs below are plain text, built directly in Python, no PDF library needed.\n\n```python\nfrom PIL.PdfParser import PdfParser\n\ndef build_pdf(root):\n    out = bytearray(b\"%PDF-1.4\\n\")\n    offset = len(out)\n    out += f\"1 0 obj\\n\".encode()\n    out += root\n    out += b\"\\nendobj\\n\"\n    out += b\"xref\\n\"\n    out += f\"0 2\\n\".encode()\n    out += b\"0000000000 65535 f \\n\"\n    out += f\"{offset:010d} 00000 \".encode() + b\"n \\n\"\n    xref_offset = len(out)\n    out += b\"xref\\n\"\n    out += f\"0 2\\n\".encode()\n    out += b\"0000000000 65535 f \\n\"\n    out += f\"{offset:010d} 00000 \".encode() + b\"n \\n\"\n    out += b\"trailer\\n\"\n    out += f\"\u003c\u003c /Size 2 /Root 1 0 R \u003e\u003e\\n\".encode()\n    out += b\"startxref\\n\"\n    out += f\"{xref_offset}\\n\".encode()\n    out += b\"%%EOF\"\n    return bytes(out)\n\nbomb = b\"[\" * 3000 + b\"]\" * 3000\nroot = b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Catalog /Pages 2 0 R /Bomb \" + bomb + b\" \u003e\u003e\"\nwith open(\"nesting_bomb.pdf\", \"wb\") as f:\n    f.write(build_pdf(root))\n\nPdfParser(\"nesting_bomb.pdf\")     # raises RecursionError\n```\n\nExpected: a clean `PdfFormatError`.\n\nActual: raises a raw `RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded`.\n\n### Impact\n- Availability: an attacker who controls the content of a PDF file that an application later appends an image page to (`Image.save(..., format=\"PDF\", append=True)`) can crash that request with a RecursionError, using a file of a few hundred bytes.\n- Confidentiality: none.\n- Integrity: none.\n- Pillow cannot open PDFs for reading through `Image.open()`. `PdfParser` is only reached through the append-mode save path, so the app has to be doing that specific thing (for example, stamping or watermarking an existing PDF) for this to matter.",
                "text": "### Summary\n`PdfParser`'s `get_value()` can recurse without any limit and crash with an uncaught `RecursionError`. It is reachable through `Image.save(im, fp=\u003cpdf\u003e, format=\"PDF\", append=True)` when the existing PDF file is attacker-controlled. This is the same class of bug as https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-r73j-pqj5-w3x7 (the /Prev trailer chain loop, fixed earlier this year), in the same file, but in two different functions that fix did not touch.\n\n### Details\n**`get_value()` in `src/PIL/PdfParser.py`**\n\nThis is a classmethod that takes `max_nesting: int = -1` and has a guard:\n\n```python\nif max_nesting == 0:\n    return None, None\n```\n\nEvery recursive call (nested arrays, dictionaries, indirect object definitions) passes `max_nesting=max_nesting - 1`. Since the default and every call site start at -1, this only ever counts down further negative: -1, -2, -3, and so on. It never equals exactly 0, so the guard never runs. A PDF object with a value nested a few thousand levels deep is enough to hit Python's recursion limit. It does not even need a valid `/Pages` tree, since `read_pdf_info()` parses the `/Root` object's own dictionary first, before checking anything else.\n\nThis advisory reports a single vulnerability: an ineffective recursion-depth guard in `PdfParser.get_value()` (the sentinel  `-1`  default never reaches the `== 0` check). The mention of GHSA-r73j-pqj5-w3x7 is comparative context for an already-fixed, already-CVE'd bug in a different function — it is not a second vulnerability being reported. The three trigger paths (nested arrays/dicts/indirect objects) are not independently fixable; they share one root cause and one fix location in  get_value() .\n\n### Steps to reproduce\nBoth PDFs below are plain text, built directly in Python, no PDF library needed.\n\n```python\nfrom PIL.PdfParser import PdfParser\n\ndef build_pdf(root):\n    out = bytearray(b\"%PDF-1.4\\n\")\n    offset = len(out)\n    out += f\"1 0 obj\\n\".encode()\n    out += root\n    out += b\"\\nendobj\\n\"\n    out += b\"xref\\n\"\n    out += f\"0 2\\n\".encode()\n    out += b\"0000000000 65535 f \\n\"\n    out += f\"{offset:010d} 00000 \".encode() + b\"n \\n\"\n    xref_offset = len(out)\n    out += b\"xref\\n\"\n    out += f\"0 2\\n\".encode()\n    out += b\"0000000000 65535 f \\n\"\n    out += f\"{offset:010d} 00000 \".encode() + b\"n \\n\"\n    out += b\"trailer\\n\"\n    out += f\"\u003c\u003c /Size 2 /Root 1 0 R \u003e\u003e\\n\".encode()\n    out += b\"startxref\\n\"\n    out += f\"{xref_offset}\\n\".encode()\n    out += b\"%%EOF\"\n    return bytes(out)\n\nbomb = b\"[\" * 3000 + b\"]\" * 3000\nroot = b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Catalog /Pages 2 0 R /Bomb \" + bomb + b\" \u003e\u003e\"\nwith open(\"nesting_bomb.pdf\", \"wb\") as f:\n    f.write(build_pdf(root))\n\nPdfParser(\"nesting_bomb.pdf\")     # raises RecursionError\n```\n\nExpected: a clean `PdfFormatError`.\n\nActual: raises a raw `RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded`.\n\n### Impact\n- Availability: an attacker who controls the content of a PDF file that an application later appends an image page to (`Image.save(..., format=\"PDF\", append=True)`) can crash that request with a RecursionError, using a file of a few hundred bytes.\n- Confidentiality: none.\n- Integrity: none.\n- Pillow cannot open PDFs for reading through `Image.open()`. `PdfParser` is only reached through the append-mode save path, so the app has to be doing that specific thing (for example, stamping or watermarking an existing PDF) for this to matter."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112391](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112391)**.\n\n## [GHSA-254f-3936-288h](https://osv.dev/GHSA-254f-3936-288h)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e `PdfParser`'s `get_value()` can recurse without any limit and crash with an uncaught `RecursionError`. It is reachable through `Image.save(im, fp=\u003cpdf\u003e, format=\"PDF\", append=True)` when the existing PDF file is attacker-controlled. This is the same class of bug as https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-r73j-pqj5-w3x7 (the /Prev trailer chain loop, fixed earlier this year), in the same file, but in two different functions that fix did not touch.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e **`get_value()` in `src/PIL/PdfParser.py`**\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a classmethod that takes `max_nesting: int = -1` and has a guard:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e if max_nesting == 0:\n\u003e     return None, None\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Every recursive call (nested arrays, dictionaries, indirect object definitions) passes `max_nesting=max_nesting - 1`. Since the default and every call site start at -1, this only ever counts down further negative: -1, -2, -3, and so on. It never equals exactly 0, so the guard never runs. A PDF object with a value nested a few thousand levels deep is enough to hit Python's recursion limit. It does not even need a valid `/Pages` tree, since `read_pdf_info()` parses the `/Root` object's own dictionary first, before checking anything else.\n\u003e \n\u003e This advisory reports a single vulnerability: an ineffective recursion-depth guard in `PdfParser.get_value()` (the sentinel  `-1`  default never reaches the `== 0` check). The mention of GHSA-r73j-pqj5-w3x7 is comparative context for an already-fixed, already-CVE'd bug in a different function — it is not a second vulnerability being reported. The three trigger paths (nested arrays/dicts/indirect objects) are not independently fixable; they share one root cause and one fix location in  get_value() .\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Steps to reproduce\n\u003e Both PDFs below are plain text, built directly in Python, no PDF library needed.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from PIL.PdfParser import PdfParser\n\u003e \n\u003e def build_pdf(root):\n\u003e     out = bytearray(b\"%PDF-1.4\\n\")\n\u003e     offset = len(out)\n\u003e     out += f\"1 0 obj\\n\".encode()\n\u003e     out += root\n\u003e     out += b\"\\nendobj\\n\"\n\u003e     out += b\"xref\\n\"\n\u003e     out += f\"0 2\\n\".encode()\n\u003e     out += b\"0000000000 65535 f \\n\"\n\u003e     out += f\"{offset:010d} 00000 \".encode() + b\"n \\n\"\n\u003e     xref_offset = len(out)\n\u003e     out += b\"xref\\n\"\n\u003e     out += f\"0 2\\n\".encode()\n\u003e     out += b\"0000000000 65535 f \\n\"\n\u003e     out += f\"{offset:010d} 00000 \".encode() + b\"n \\n\"\n\u003e     out += b\"trailer\\n\"\n\u003e     out += f\"\u003c\u003c /Size 2 /Root 1 0 R \u003e\u003e\\n\".encode()\n\u003e     out += b\"startxref\\n\"\n\u003e     out += f\"{xref_offset}\\n\".encode()\n\u003e     out += b\"%%EOF\"\n\u003e     return bytes(out)\n\u003e \n\u003e bomb = b\"[\" * 3000 + b\"]\" * 3000\n\u003e root = b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Catalog /Pages 2 0 R /Bomb \" + bomb + b\" \u003e\u003e\"\n\u003e with open(\"nesting_bomb.pdf\", \"wb\") as f:\n\u003e     f.write(build_pdf(root))\n\u003e \n\u003e PdfParser(\"nesting_bomb.pdf\")     # raises RecursionError\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Expected: a clean `PdfFormatError`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Actual: raises a raw `RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e - Availability: an attacker who controls the content of a PDF file that an application later appends an image page to (`Image.save(..., format=\"PDF\", append=True)`) can crash that request with a RecursionError, using a file of a few hundred bytes.\n\u003e - Confidentiality: none.\n\u003e - Integrity: none.\n\u003e - Pillow cannot open PDFs for reading through `Image.open()`. `PdfParser` is only reached through the append-mode save path, so the app has to be doing that specific thing (for example, stamping or watermarking an existing PDF) for this to matter.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-254f-3936-288h | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112391\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112391](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112391)**.\n\n## [GHSA-254f-3936-288h](https://osv.dev/GHSA-254f-3936-288h)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e `PdfParser`'s `get_value()` can recurse without any limit and crash with an uncaught `RecursionError`. It is reachable through `Image.save(im, fp=\u003cpdf\u003e, format=\"PDF\", append=True)` when the existing PDF file is attacker-controlled. This is the same class of bug as https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-r73j-pqj5-w3x7 (the /Prev trailer chain loop, fixed earlier this year), in the same file, but in two different functions that fix did not touch.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e **`get_value()` in `src/PIL/PdfParser.py`**\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a classmethod that takes `max_nesting: int = -1` and has a guard:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e if max_nesting == 0:\n\u003e     return None, None\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Every recursive call (nested arrays, dictionaries, indirect object definitions) passes `max_nesting=max_nesting - 1`. Since the default and every call site start at -1, this only ever counts down further negative: -1, -2, -3, and so on. It never equals exactly 0, so the guard never runs. A PDF object with a value nested a few thousand levels deep is enough to hit Python's recursion limit. It does not even need a valid `/Pages` tree, since `read_pdf_info()` parses the `/Root` object's own dictionary first, before checking anything else.\n\u003e \n\u003e This advisory reports a single vulnerability: an ineffective recursion-depth guard in `PdfParser.get_value()` (the sentinel  `-1`  default never reaches the `== 0` check). The mention of GHSA-r73j-pqj5-w3x7 is comparative context for an already-fixed, already-CVE'd bug in a different function — it is not a second vulnerability being reported. The three trigger paths (nested arrays/dicts/indirect objects) are not independently fixable; they share one root cause and one fix location in  get_value() .\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Steps to reproduce\n\u003e Both PDFs below are plain text, built directly in Python, no PDF library needed.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from PIL.PdfParser import PdfParser\n\u003e \n\u003e def build_pdf(root):\n\u003e     out = bytearray(b\"%PDF-1.4\\n\")\n\u003e     offset = len(out)\n\u003e     out += f\"1 0 obj\\n\".encode()\n\u003e     out += root\n\u003e     out += b\"\\nendobj\\n\"\n\u003e     out += b\"xref\\n\"\n\u003e     out += f\"0 2\\n\".encode()\n\u003e     out += b\"0000000000 65535 f \\n\"\n\u003e     out += f\"{offset:010d} 00000 \".encode() + b\"n \\n\"\n\u003e     xref_offset = len(out)\n\u003e     out += b\"xref\\n\"\n\u003e     out += f\"0 2\\n\".encode()\n\u003e     out += b\"0000000000 65535 f \\n\"\n\u003e     out += f\"{offset:010d} 00000 \".encode() + b\"n \\n\"\n\u003e     out += b\"trailer\\n\"\n\u003e     out += f\"\u003c\u003c /Size 2 /Root 1 0 R \u003e\u003e\\n\".encode()\n\u003e     out += b\"startxref\\n\"\n\u003e     out += f\"{xref_offset}\\n\".encode()\n\u003e     out += b\"%%EOF\"\n\u003e     return bytes(out)\n\u003e \n\u003e bomb = b\"[\" * 3000 + b\"]\" * 3000\n\u003e root = b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Catalog /Pages 2 0 R /Bomb \" + bomb + b\" \u003e\u003e\"\n\u003e with open(\"nesting_bomb.pdf\", \"wb\") as f:\n\u003e     f.write(build_pdf(root))\n\u003e \n\u003e PdfParser(\"nesting_bomb.pdf\")     # raises RecursionError\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Expected: a clean `PdfFormatError`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Actual: raises a raw `RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e - Availability: an attacker who controls the content of a PDF file that an application later appends an image page to (`Image.save(..., format=\"PDF\", append=True)`) can crash that request with a RecursionError, using a file of a few hundred bytes.\n\u003e - Confidentiality: none.\n\u003e - Integrity: none.\n\u003e - Pillow cannot open PDFs for reading through `Image.open()`. `PdfParser` is only reached through the append-mode save path, so the app has to be doing that specific thing (for example, stamping or watermarking an existing PDF) for this to matter.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-254f-3936-288h | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112391\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-112391",
              "name": "CVE-2026-112391",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.3"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-112391: Pillow: PdfParser can recurse without limit in get_value() and linearize_page_tree()",
                "text": "CVE-2026-112391: Pillow: PdfParser can recurse without limit in get_value() and linearize_page_tree()"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-112392",
                "BIT-pillow-2026-112392",
                "PYSEC-2026-4326",
                "GHSA-27h8-gf7j-v5hc"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "```\n============================================================================\nNULL dereference in ImagingExpand via rank filters on zero-size images\nLocation: src/libImaging/Filter.c:79  in ImagingExpand()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nApplying Pillow's median, minimum, maximum or rank filter to an image with zero width or zero height crashes the whole Python process instead of raising an error. Empty images like this are easy to produce with ordinary calls, for example a crop box whose left and right edges (or top and bottom edges) are equal, or the second page of a crafted multi-page image file. An attacker who can supply crop coordinates or an uploaded file to an application that runs one of these filters can kill its worker process. The effect is a crash only, with no memory disclosure or corruption.\n\nThe fault is in `ImagingExpand()` in `src/libImaging/Filter.c`. This is the edge-padding step that `RankFilter.filter()` always runs through the core `expand()` method, so `MedianFilter`, `MinFilter` and `MaxFilter` go through it too. It fills the margin by copying the input's edge pixels, reading `imIn-\u003eimage[y][0]` and rows `0` / `ysize-1`, and never checks that the input has any pixels. For an empty image, `ImagingAllocateArray` leaves the line pointers NULL, so the first read dereferences NULL and the process dies with SIGSEGV, in every mode.\n\nZero-size images reach this point through public API alone:\n\n- `Image.crop()`, `ImageOps.crop()` and `Image.new()`;\n- a multi-page TIFF opened by path whose second IFD declares `ImageLength = 0`, on which `seek(1)` and `load()` succeed.\n\nEvery other `ImageFilter` returns an empty image for the same inputs. The zero-height case has crashed since the original PIL import, and the zero-width case since Pillow 4.3.0. This is separate from the `margin == 0` fix in #9755: the path is the same, but here the degenerate value is the image size rather than the kernel size. It has the same shape as the zero-dimension `ImagingOffset` crash fixed under GHSA-f87v-9578-p47w.\n\nThe attached reproducer shows a one-pixel-wide control crop filtering normally, while four empty crops passed through the rank filters are each killed by SIGSEGV. A patch that returns early in `ImagingExpand` for empty input is included.\n\n## Details\n\n`ImagingExpand()` (`src/libImaging/Filter.c`), the edge-padding helper that `ImageFilter.RankFilter` runs before the actual rank filter, reads the first pixel of each input line without checking that the input has any pixels. For an image with width 0 or height 0 the line pointers are NULL, so `Image.filter()` with `MedianFilter`, `MinFilter`, `MaxFilter` or `RankFilter` kills the interpreter with SIGSEGV instead of returning or raising:\n\n```python\nfrom PIL import Image, ImageFilter\nim = Image.new(\"L\", (10, 10)).crop((3, 0, 3, 10))   # size (0, 10); crop() accepts left == right\nim.filter(ImageFilter.MedianFilter(3))               # SIGSEGV\n```\n\nUnder gdb on the stock build the fault is at `ImagingExpand (imIn=…, margin=1) at src/libImaging/Filter.c:105` (the `EXPAND(UINT8, image8)` expansion), called from `_expand_image` at `src/_imaging.c:1151`, with `imIn-\u003exsize = 0` and `imIn-\u003eimage[0] = 0x0`. The same happens for every mode the rank filters accept (`L`, `RGB`, `RGBA`, `I`, `F`), and the core `expand(m)` method with `m \u003e= 1` crashes the same way in any mode, including `P`. The same input through `BoxBlur`, `GaussianBlur`, `Kernel`, `ModeFilter` and `UnsharpMask` returns a `(0, 10)` image; a `(1, 10)` crop through `MedianFilter` also returns normally.\n\nThe unchecked edge reads date from the PIL import (`9a640e315`, 2010, so every Pillow release): the zero-height case has crashed since then, and the zero-width case since `fd907fbdc` (\"Fix 0-width and 0-height images other way\", Pillow 4.3.0), which stopped backing zero-width images with a dummy block and left their row pointers NULL. It is adjacent to the `margin == 0` divide-by-zero fix in #9755 — same `expand()` → `ImagingExpand()` path, but the unhandled degenerate value here is the image size rather than the kernel size.\n\nThe path:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/ImageFilter.py:111-116   RankFilter.filter  (MedianFilter/MinFilter/MaxFilter subclass it)\n        image = image.expand(self.size // 2)\n        return image.rankfilter(self.size, self.rank)\n```\n\n`expand(m)` with `m \u003e 0` is `ImagingExpand(self-\u003eimage, m)` (`src/_imaging.c:1142-1151`). It validates only `margin`, allocates an output of `(xsize+2*margin) × (ysize+2*margin)` — which is non-empty, so the allocation succeeds — and then fills the margin by replicating edge pixels of the input:\n\n```c\n// src/libImaging/Filter.c:70-101\n    imOut =\n        ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n    …\n#define EXPAND_LINE(type, image, yin, yout)                       \\\n    {                                                             \\\n        for (x = 0; x \u003c margin; x++) {                            \\\n            imOut-\u003eimage[yout][x] = imIn-\u003eimage[yin][0];          \\   // line 79\n        }                                                         \\\n        …                                                         \\\n        for (x = 0; x \u003c margin; x++) {                            \\\n            imOut-\u003eimage[yout][margin + imIn-\u003exsize + x] =        \\\n                imIn-\u003eimage[yin][imIn-\u003exsize - 1];                \\   // [-1] when xsize == 0\n        }                                                         \\\n    }\n\n#define EXPAND(type, image)                                                      \\\n    {                                                                            \\\n        for (y = 0; y \u003c margin; y++) {                                           \\\n            EXPAND_LINE(type, image, 0, y);                                      \\   // line 0 of a 0-line image\n        }                                                                        \\\n        …\n        for (y = 0; y \u003c margin; y++) {                                           \\\n            EXPAND_LINE(type, image, imIn-\u003eysize - 1, margin + imIn-\u003eysize + y); \\   // line -1 when ysize == 0\n        }                                                                        \\\n    }\n```\n\nWhy the line pointers are NULL: `ImagingNewPrologueSubtype` callocs the `im-\u003eimage` pointer array (`src/libImaging/Storage.c:216`, at least one entry), and `ImagingAllocateArray` returns before assigning any of them for an empty image:\n\n```c\n// src/libImaging/Storage.c:423-426\n    /* 0-width or 0-height image. No need to do anything */\n    if (!im-\u003elinesize || !im-\u003eysize) {\n        return im;\n    }\n```\n\nSo with `xsize == 0` every `imIn-\u003eimage[y]` is NULL and `imIn-\u003eimage[yin][0]` faults; with `ysize == 0` the top-margin pass reads `imIn-\u003eimage[0]` (the single calloc'd NULL slot) and faults the same way, and the bottom-margin pass would index `imIn-\u003eimage[-1]`. `ImagingRankFilter` is never reached.\n\nZero-size images are ordinary objects in Pillow and arrive at `filter()` through public API only:\n\n- `Image.crop(box)` rejects only inverted boxes (`src/PIL/Image.py:1385-1390`); `left == right` or `upper == lower` yields a 0×N / N×0 image. `ImageOps.crop(im, border)` with `border == width // 2`, and `Image.new(mode, (0, n))`, do the same.\n- From a file: `ImageFile.__init__` refuses a zero first-frame size (`src/PIL/ImageFile.py:167-173`), but `TiffImagePlugin._setup()` takes `ImageWidth`/`ImageLength` of every later IFD as-is on `seek()` (`src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py:1468-1481`). For an uncompressed page of an image opened by path, `ImageFile.load()` takes the mmap branch (`src/PIL/ImageFile.py:307, 325-347`) and `Image.core.map_buffer` builds an `8×0` core image without complaint (`src/map.c:127-141`), so `im.seek(1); im.load()` succeeds and a following `im.filter(ImageFilter.MedianFilter(3))` segfaults in `ImagingExpand` exactly as above (verified under gdb: `imIn-\u003eysize = 0`, `imIn-\u003eimage[0] = 0x0`). A 384-byte two-page TIFF is enough.\n\n## Impact\n\nDenial of service: the whole embedding process (web worker, pipeline process) dies with SIGSEGV rather than the request failing with a Python exception. It is reachable by a remote user in two realistic shapes: (a) an application that crops an upload to user-supplied coordinates and then applies a median/min/max filter (denoise, dilate/erode, thumbnail clean-up) — a box with `x0 == x1` or `y0 == y1` passes `crop()` and crashes the worker; (b) an application that opens an uploaded TIFF from disk by path, iterates its pages and applies a rank filter to each — a crafted second IFD with `ImageLength = 0` crashes it. The faulting access is a read at address 0 (or a fixed small negative offset from the pointer array for the `[-1]` cases, which is not reached because the first read already faults), so there is no memory disclosure or corruption beyond the crash. The same shape, a zero-dimension image crashing C code reached through public API, was fixed in `ImagingOffset` in 39d14a0db (GHSA-f87v-9578-p47w).\n\n## Reproducing\n\n`repro` is a single Python script that uses the installed Pillow as-is (no rebuild). It runs each case in a fresh child interpreter, with core dumps disabled. That way a segfault shows up as the child's exit status and the script itself keeps running. Each child builds `Image.new(\"L\", (10, 10)).crop(box)`, prints its size, calls `im.filter(...)` and then prints the size of the result.\n\n- **Control**: a `(1, 10)` crop (`(3, 0, 4, 10)`) through `MedianFilter(3)`. The rank filter accepts a thin image and must return normally.\n- **PoC**: the rank filters on empty crops.\n  - A `(0, 10)` crop (`left == right`, `(3, 0, 3, 10)`) through `MedianFilter(3)` and `MaxFilter(5)`.\n  - A `(10, 0)` crop (`upper == lower`, `(0, 3, 10, 3)`) through `MinFilter(3)`.\n  - A `(0, 0)` crop (`(3, 3, 3, 3)`) through `RankFilter(3, 4)`.\n\nExpected output on the stock build:\n\n```\n  crop(3, 0, 4, 10)    MedianFilter(3)    (1, 10) -\u003e (1, 10)\n  crop(3, 0, 3, 10)    MedianFilter(3)    (0, 10) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\n  crop(3, 0, 3, 10)    MaxFilter(5)       (0, 10) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\n  crop(0, 3, 10, 3)    MinFilter(3)       (10, 0) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\n  crop(3, 3, 3, 3)     RankFilter(3, 4)   (0, 0) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\nBUG\n```\n\nThe size printed before `-\u003e` shows that the empty image was created without error. The crash happens inside `filter()`, in `ImagingExpand`. The last line is `BUG` if the control returned normally and at least one rank-filter child was killed by SIGSEGV. Otherwise it is `OK`. The script covers the crop route only; the TIFF route described above fails the same way (`im.seek(1); im.load(); im.filter(ImageFilter.MedianFilter(3))` on a by-path two-page TIFF whose second IFD has `ImageLength = 0`). With the attached patch, every PoC line prints the unchanged size (for example `(0, 10) -\u003e (0, 10)`) and the script prints `OK`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nThe attached patch handles the empty input in `ImagingExpand` (`src/libImaging/Filter.c`), right after the `margin` checks and before the `ImagingNewDirty` allocation and the replication loops. With `xsize == 0` or `ysize == 0` there are no edge pixels to replicate, so it returns a zeroed (`ImagingNew`, not `ImagingNewDirty`) image of the expanded size, with the input's palette copied. This keeps `ImagingExpand`'s size contract, so `ImagingRankFilter` then produces an output of `xsize - 2*margin` × `ysize - 2*margin`, i.e. the original empty size, matching every other filter. It mirrors the existing zero-dimension early returns in `src/libImaging/BoxBlur.c` (127004368) and `src/libImaging/Offset.c` (39d14a0db):\n\n```diff\n--- a/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n@@ -67,6 +67,17 @@ ImagingExpand(Imaging imIn, int margin) {\n         return (Imaging)ImagingError_ValueError(\"filter size too large\");\n     }\n \n+    if (imIn-\u003exsize == 0 || imIn-\u003eysize == 0) {\n+        /* No edge pixels to replicate (and no lines to read from), so\n+           return a zeroed image of the expanded size */\n+        imOut = ImagingNew(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n+        if (!imOut) {\n+            return NULL;\n+        }\n+        ImagingCopyPalette(imOut, imIn);\n+        return imOut;\n+    }\n+\n     imOut =\n         ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n     if (!imOut) {\n```\n\nThe patch also adds `test_rankfilter_zero_dimension` next to `test_rankfilter_error` in `Tests/test_image_filter.py`, which filters `Image.new(\"L\", size)` for `size` in `((0, 6), (6, 0), (0, 0))` with `MinFilter`, `MedianFilter`, `MaxFilter` and `RankFilter(3, 2)` and asserts the size is preserved. Applied to a clean checkout and rebuilt, the reproducer prints `OK` and `Tests/test_image_filter.py` passes. A Python-level guard in `RankFilter.filter` (`if 0 in image.size: return image.copy()`) would also stop the crash, but the C-level check protects any other caller of `expand()`.\n\n## The patch\n\nReturn a zeroed image from ImagingExpand when the input has zero width or height instead of reading NULL line pointers\n\nApplies to `src/libImaging/Filter.c` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_image_filter.py b/Tests/test_image_filter.py\nindex 974d4b7a3..8dc8e1a1a 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_image_filter.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_image_filter.py\n@@ -155,6 +155,23 @@ def test_rankfilter_error(filter: ImageFilter.RankFilter) -\u003e None:\n         im.filter(filter).getpixel((1, 1))\n \n \n+@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+    \"filter\",\n+    (\n+        ImageFilter.MinFilter,\n+        ImageFilter.MedianFilter,\n+        ImageFilter.MaxFilter,\n+        ImageFilter.RankFilter(3, 2),\n+    ),\n+)\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"size\", ((0, 6), (6, 0), (0, 0)))\n+def test_rankfilter_zero_dimension(\n+    filter: ImageFilter.RankFilter, size: tuple[int, int]\n+) -\u003e None:\n+    im = Image.new(\"L\", size)\n+    assert im.filter(filter).size == size\n+\n+\n def test_rankfilter_properties() -\u003e None:\n     rankfilter = ImageFilter.RankFilter(3, 2)\n \ndiff --git a/src/libImaging/Filter.c b/src/libImaging/Filter.c\nindex 3492a871f..d702db5f9 100644\n--- a/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n@@ -67,6 +67,17 @@ ImagingExpand(Imaging imIn, int margin) {\n         return (Imaging)ImagingError_ValueError(\"filter size too large\");\n     }\n \n+    if (imIn-\u003exsize == 0 || imIn-\u003eysize == 0) {\n+        /* No edge pixels to replicate (and no lines to read from), so\n+           return a zeroed image of the expanded size */\n+        imOut = ImagingNew(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n+        if (!imOut) {\n+            return NULL;\n+        }\n+        ImagingCopyPalette(imOut, imIn);\n+        return imOut;\n+    }\n+\n     imOut =\n         ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n     if (!imOut) {\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 1,333 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n# Pillow: rank filters (Median/Min/Max/RankFilter) on a 0-wide or 0-high image\n# SIGSEGV in ImagingExpand (src/libImaging/Filter.c) - NULL line pointer read.\n# Each case runs in a child interpreter; BUG if the control returns and any\n# zero-size case dies of SIGSEGV.\nimport signal, subprocess, sys\n\nCHILD = \"\"\"\nimport resource; resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))\nfrom PIL import Image, ImageFilter\nim = Image.new(\"L\", (10, 10)).crop(%s)      # public API; crop accepts x0==x1 / y0==y1\nprint(im.size, \"-\u003e\", end=\" \", flush=True)\nprint(im.filter(ImageFilter.%s).size)\n\"\"\"\n\ndef run(box, flt):\n    p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", CHILD % (box, flt)],\n                       capture_output=True, text=True)\n    out = p.stdout.strip()\n    if p.returncode \u003c 0:\n        out += \" killed by \" + signal.Signals(-p.returncode).name\n    print(f\"  crop{box!s:\u003c16} {flt:\u003c18} {out}\")\n    return p.returncode\n\nctrl = run((3, 0, 4, 10), \"MedianFilter(3)\")          # (1,10): must work\nrcs = [run((3, 0, 3, 10), \"MedianFilter(3)\"),        # (0,10)\n       run((3, 0, 3, 10), \"MaxFilter(5)\"),\n       run((0, 3, 10, 3), \"MinFilter(3)\"),           # (10,0)\n       run((3, 3, 3, 3), \"RankFilter(3, 4)\")]        # (0,0)\nbug = ctrl == 0 and any(rc == -signal.SIGSEGV for rc in rcs)\nprint(\"BUG\" if bug else \"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---",
                "text": "```\n============================================================================\nNULL dereference in ImagingExpand via rank filters on zero-size images\nLocation: src/libImaging/Filter.c:79  in ImagingExpand()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nApplying Pillow's median, minimum, maximum or rank filter to an image with zero width or zero height crashes the whole Python process instead of raising an error. Empty images like this are easy to produce with ordinary calls, for example a crop box whose left and right edges (or top and bottom edges) are equal, or the second page of a crafted multi-page image file. An attacker who can supply crop coordinates or an uploaded file to an application that runs one of these filters can kill its worker process. The effect is a crash only, with no memory disclosure or corruption.\n\nThe fault is in `ImagingExpand()` in `src/libImaging/Filter.c`. This is the edge-padding step that `RankFilter.filter()` always runs through the core `expand()` method, so `MedianFilter`, `MinFilter` and `MaxFilter` go through it too. It fills the margin by copying the input's edge pixels, reading `imIn-\u003eimage[y][0]` and rows `0` / `ysize-1`, and never checks that the input has any pixels. For an empty image, `ImagingAllocateArray` leaves the line pointers NULL, so the first read dereferences NULL and the process dies with SIGSEGV, in every mode.\n\nZero-size images reach this point through public API alone:\n\n- `Image.crop()`, `ImageOps.crop()` and `Image.new()`;\n- a multi-page TIFF opened by path whose second IFD declares `ImageLength = 0`, on which `seek(1)` and `load()` succeed.\n\nEvery other `ImageFilter` returns an empty image for the same inputs. The zero-height case has crashed since the original PIL import, and the zero-width case since Pillow 4.3.0. This is separate from the `margin == 0` fix in #9755: the path is the same, but here the degenerate value is the image size rather than the kernel size. It has the same shape as the zero-dimension `ImagingOffset` crash fixed under GHSA-f87v-9578-p47w.\n\nThe attached reproducer shows a one-pixel-wide control crop filtering normally, while four empty crops passed through the rank filters are each killed by SIGSEGV. A patch that returns early in `ImagingExpand` for empty input is included.\n\n## Details\n\n`ImagingExpand()` (`src/libImaging/Filter.c`), the edge-padding helper that `ImageFilter.RankFilter` runs before the actual rank filter, reads the first pixel of each input line without checking that the input has any pixels. For an image with width 0 or height 0 the line pointers are NULL, so `Image.filter()` with `MedianFilter`, `MinFilter`, `MaxFilter` or `RankFilter` kills the interpreter with SIGSEGV instead of returning or raising:\n\n```python\nfrom PIL import Image, ImageFilter\nim = Image.new(\"L\", (10, 10)).crop((3, 0, 3, 10))   # size (0, 10); crop() accepts left == right\nim.filter(ImageFilter.MedianFilter(3))               # SIGSEGV\n```\n\nUnder gdb on the stock build the fault is at `ImagingExpand (imIn=…, margin=1) at src/libImaging/Filter.c:105` (the `EXPAND(UINT8, image8)` expansion), called from `_expand_image` at `src/_imaging.c:1151`, with `imIn-\u003exsize = 0` and `imIn-\u003eimage[0] = 0x0`. The same happens for every mode the rank filters accept (`L`, `RGB`, `RGBA`, `I`, `F`), and the core `expand(m)` method with `m \u003e= 1` crashes the same way in any mode, including `P`. The same input through `BoxBlur`, `GaussianBlur`, `Kernel`, `ModeFilter` and `UnsharpMask` returns a `(0, 10)` image; a `(1, 10)` crop through `MedianFilter` also returns normally.\n\nThe unchecked edge reads date from the PIL import (`9a640e315`, 2010, so every Pillow release): the zero-height case has crashed since then, and the zero-width case since `fd907fbdc` (\"Fix 0-width and 0-height images other way\", Pillow 4.3.0), which stopped backing zero-width images with a dummy block and left their row pointers NULL. It is adjacent to the `margin == 0` divide-by-zero fix in #9755 — same `expand()` → `ImagingExpand()` path, but the unhandled degenerate value here is the image size rather than the kernel size.\n\nThe path:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/ImageFilter.py:111-116   RankFilter.filter  (MedianFilter/MinFilter/MaxFilter subclass it)\n        image = image.expand(self.size // 2)\n        return image.rankfilter(self.size, self.rank)\n```\n\n`expand(m)` with `m \u003e 0` is `ImagingExpand(self-\u003eimage, m)` (`src/_imaging.c:1142-1151`). It validates only `margin`, allocates an output of `(xsize+2*margin) × (ysize+2*margin)` — which is non-empty, so the allocation succeeds — and then fills the margin by replicating edge pixels of the input:\n\n```c\n// src/libImaging/Filter.c:70-101\n    imOut =\n        ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n    …\n#define EXPAND_LINE(type, image, yin, yout)                       \\\n    {                                                             \\\n        for (x = 0; x \u003c margin; x++) {                            \\\n            imOut-\u003eimage[yout][x] = imIn-\u003eimage[yin][0];          \\   // line 79\n        }                                                         \\\n        …                                                         \\\n        for (x = 0; x \u003c margin; x++) {                            \\\n            imOut-\u003eimage[yout][margin + imIn-\u003exsize + x] =        \\\n                imIn-\u003eimage[yin][imIn-\u003exsize - 1];                \\   // [-1] when xsize == 0\n        }                                                         \\\n    }\n\n#define EXPAND(type, image)                                                      \\\n    {                                                                            \\\n        for (y = 0; y \u003c margin; y++) {                                           \\\n            EXPAND_LINE(type, image, 0, y);                                      \\   // line 0 of a 0-line image\n        }                                                                        \\\n        …\n        for (y = 0; y \u003c margin; y++) {                                           \\\n            EXPAND_LINE(type, image, imIn-\u003eysize - 1, margin + imIn-\u003eysize + y); \\   // line -1 when ysize == 0\n        }                                                                        \\\n    }\n```\n\nWhy the line pointers are NULL: `ImagingNewPrologueSubtype` callocs the `im-\u003eimage` pointer array (`src/libImaging/Storage.c:216`, at least one entry), and `ImagingAllocateArray` returns before assigning any of them for an empty image:\n\n```c\n// src/libImaging/Storage.c:423-426\n    /* 0-width or 0-height image. No need to do anything */\n    if (!im-\u003elinesize || !im-\u003eysize) {\n        return im;\n    }\n```\n\nSo with `xsize == 0` every `imIn-\u003eimage[y]` is NULL and `imIn-\u003eimage[yin][0]` faults; with `ysize == 0` the top-margin pass reads `imIn-\u003eimage[0]` (the single calloc'd NULL slot) and faults the same way, and the bottom-margin pass would index `imIn-\u003eimage[-1]`. `ImagingRankFilter` is never reached.\n\nZero-size images are ordinary objects in Pillow and arrive at `filter()` through public API only:\n\n- `Image.crop(box)` rejects only inverted boxes (`src/PIL/Image.py:1385-1390`); `left == right` or `upper == lower` yields a 0×N / N×0 image. `ImageOps.crop(im, border)` with `border == width // 2`, and `Image.new(mode, (0, n))`, do the same.\n- From a file: `ImageFile.__init__` refuses a zero first-frame size (`src/PIL/ImageFile.py:167-173`), but `TiffImagePlugin._setup()` takes `ImageWidth`/`ImageLength` of every later IFD as-is on `seek()` (`src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py:1468-1481`). For an uncompressed page of an image opened by path, `ImageFile.load()` takes the mmap branch (`src/PIL/ImageFile.py:307, 325-347`) and `Image.core.map_buffer` builds an `8×0` core image without complaint (`src/map.c:127-141`), so `im.seek(1); im.load()` succeeds and a following `im.filter(ImageFilter.MedianFilter(3))` segfaults in `ImagingExpand` exactly as above (verified under gdb: `imIn-\u003eysize = 0`, `imIn-\u003eimage[0] = 0x0`). A 384-byte two-page TIFF is enough.\n\n## Impact\n\nDenial of service: the whole embedding process (web worker, pipeline process) dies with SIGSEGV rather than the request failing with a Python exception. It is reachable by a remote user in two realistic shapes: (a) an application that crops an upload to user-supplied coordinates and then applies a median/min/max filter (denoise, dilate/erode, thumbnail clean-up) — a box with `x0 == x1` or `y0 == y1` passes `crop()` and crashes the worker; (b) an application that opens an uploaded TIFF from disk by path, iterates its pages and applies a rank filter to each — a crafted second IFD with `ImageLength = 0` crashes it. The faulting access is a read at address 0 (or a fixed small negative offset from the pointer array for the `[-1]` cases, which is not reached because the first read already faults), so there is no memory disclosure or corruption beyond the crash. The same shape, a zero-dimension image crashing C code reached through public API, was fixed in `ImagingOffset` in 39d14a0db (GHSA-f87v-9578-p47w).\n\n## Reproducing\n\n`repro` is a single Python script that uses the installed Pillow as-is (no rebuild). It runs each case in a fresh child interpreter, with core dumps disabled. That way a segfault shows up as the child's exit status and the script itself keeps running. Each child builds `Image.new(\"L\", (10, 10)).crop(box)`, prints its size, calls `im.filter(...)` and then prints the size of the result.\n\n- **Control**: a `(1, 10)` crop (`(3, 0, 4, 10)`) through `MedianFilter(3)`. The rank filter accepts a thin image and must return normally.\n- **PoC**: the rank filters on empty crops.\n  - A `(0, 10)` crop (`left == right`, `(3, 0, 3, 10)`) through `MedianFilter(3)` and `MaxFilter(5)`.\n  - A `(10, 0)` crop (`upper == lower`, `(0, 3, 10, 3)`) through `MinFilter(3)`.\n  - A `(0, 0)` crop (`(3, 3, 3, 3)`) through `RankFilter(3, 4)`.\n\nExpected output on the stock build:\n\n```\n  crop(3, 0, 4, 10)    MedianFilter(3)    (1, 10) -\u003e (1, 10)\n  crop(3, 0, 3, 10)    MedianFilter(3)    (0, 10) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\n  crop(3, 0, 3, 10)    MaxFilter(5)       (0, 10) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\n  crop(0, 3, 10, 3)    MinFilter(3)       (10, 0) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\n  crop(3, 3, 3, 3)     RankFilter(3, 4)   (0, 0) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\nBUG\n```\n\nThe size printed before `-\u003e` shows that the empty image was created without error. The crash happens inside `filter()`, in `ImagingExpand`. The last line is `BUG` if the control returned normally and at least one rank-filter child was killed by SIGSEGV. Otherwise it is `OK`. The script covers the crop route only; the TIFF route described above fails the same way (`im.seek(1); im.load(); im.filter(ImageFilter.MedianFilter(3))` on a by-path two-page TIFF whose second IFD has `ImageLength = 0`). With the attached patch, every PoC line prints the unchanged size (for example `(0, 10) -\u003e (0, 10)`) and the script prints `OK`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nThe attached patch handles the empty input in `ImagingExpand` (`src/libImaging/Filter.c`), right after the `margin` checks and before the `ImagingNewDirty` allocation and the replication loops. With `xsize == 0` or `ysize == 0` there are no edge pixels to replicate, so it returns a zeroed (`ImagingNew`, not `ImagingNewDirty`) image of the expanded size, with the input's palette copied. This keeps `ImagingExpand`'s size contract, so `ImagingRankFilter` then produces an output of `xsize - 2*margin` × `ysize - 2*margin`, i.e. the original empty size, matching every other filter. It mirrors the existing zero-dimension early returns in `src/libImaging/BoxBlur.c` (127004368) and `src/libImaging/Offset.c` (39d14a0db):\n\n```diff\n--- a/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n@@ -67,6 +67,17 @@ ImagingExpand(Imaging imIn, int margin) {\n         return (Imaging)ImagingError_ValueError(\"filter size too large\");\n     }\n \n+    if (imIn-\u003exsize == 0 || imIn-\u003eysize == 0) {\n+        /* No edge pixels to replicate (and no lines to read from), so\n+           return a zeroed image of the expanded size */\n+        imOut = ImagingNew(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n+        if (!imOut) {\n+            return NULL;\n+        }\n+        ImagingCopyPalette(imOut, imIn);\n+        return imOut;\n+    }\n+\n     imOut =\n         ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n     if (!imOut) {\n```\n\nThe patch also adds `test_rankfilter_zero_dimension` next to `test_rankfilter_error` in `Tests/test_image_filter.py`, which filters `Image.new(\"L\", size)` for `size` in `((0, 6), (6, 0), (0, 0))` with `MinFilter`, `MedianFilter`, `MaxFilter` and `RankFilter(3, 2)` and asserts the size is preserved. Applied to a clean checkout and rebuilt, the reproducer prints `OK` and `Tests/test_image_filter.py` passes. A Python-level guard in `RankFilter.filter` (`if 0 in image.size: return image.copy()`) would also stop the crash, but the C-level check protects any other caller of `expand()`.\n\n## The patch\n\nReturn a zeroed image from ImagingExpand when the input has zero width or height instead of reading NULL line pointers\n\nApplies to `src/libImaging/Filter.c` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_image_filter.py b/Tests/test_image_filter.py\nindex 974d4b7a3..8dc8e1a1a 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_image_filter.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_image_filter.py\n@@ -155,6 +155,23 @@ def test_rankfilter_error(filter: ImageFilter.RankFilter) -\u003e None:\n         im.filter(filter).getpixel((1, 1))\n \n \n+@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+    \"filter\",\n+    (\n+        ImageFilter.MinFilter,\n+        ImageFilter.MedianFilter,\n+        ImageFilter.MaxFilter,\n+        ImageFilter.RankFilter(3, 2),\n+    ),\n+)\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"size\", ((0, 6), (6, 0), (0, 0)))\n+def test_rankfilter_zero_dimension(\n+    filter: ImageFilter.RankFilter, size: tuple[int, int]\n+) -\u003e None:\n+    im = Image.new(\"L\", size)\n+    assert im.filter(filter).size == size\n+\n+\n def test_rankfilter_properties() -\u003e None:\n     rankfilter = ImageFilter.RankFilter(3, 2)\n \ndiff --git a/src/libImaging/Filter.c b/src/libImaging/Filter.c\nindex 3492a871f..d702db5f9 100644\n--- a/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n@@ -67,6 +67,17 @@ ImagingExpand(Imaging imIn, int margin) {\n         return (Imaging)ImagingError_ValueError(\"filter size too large\");\n     }\n \n+    if (imIn-\u003exsize == 0 || imIn-\u003eysize == 0) {\n+        /* No edge pixels to replicate (and no lines to read from), so\n+           return a zeroed image of the expanded size */\n+        imOut = ImagingNew(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n+        if (!imOut) {\n+            return NULL;\n+        }\n+        ImagingCopyPalette(imOut, imIn);\n+        return imOut;\n+    }\n+\n     imOut =\n         ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n     if (!imOut) {\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 1,333 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n# Pillow: rank filters (Median/Min/Max/RankFilter) on a 0-wide or 0-high image\n# SIGSEGV in ImagingExpand (src/libImaging/Filter.c) - NULL line pointer read.\n# Each case runs in a child interpreter; BUG if the control returns and any\n# zero-size case dies of SIGSEGV.\nimport signal, subprocess, sys\n\nCHILD = \"\"\"\nimport resource; resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))\nfrom PIL import Image, ImageFilter\nim = Image.new(\"L\", (10, 10)).crop(%s)      # public API; crop accepts x0==x1 / y0==y1\nprint(im.size, \"-\u003e\", end=\" \", flush=True)\nprint(im.filter(ImageFilter.%s).size)\n\"\"\"\n\ndef run(box, flt):\n    p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", CHILD % (box, flt)],\n                       capture_output=True, text=True)\n    out = p.stdout.strip()\n    if p.returncode \u003c 0:\n        out += \" killed by \" + signal.Signals(-p.returncode).name\n    print(f\"  crop{box!s:\u003c16} {flt:\u003c18} {out}\")\n    return p.returncode\n\nctrl = run((3, 0, 4, 10), \"MedianFilter(3)\")          # (1,10): must work\nrcs = [run((3, 0, 3, 10), \"MedianFilter(3)\"),        # (0,10)\n       run((3, 0, 3, 10), \"MaxFilter(5)\"),\n       run((0, 3, 10, 3), \"MinFilter(3)\"),           # (10,0)\n       run((3, 3, 3, 3), \"RankFilter(3, 4)\")]        # (0,0)\nbug = ctrl == 0 and any(rc == -signal.SIGSEGV for rc in rcs)\nprint(\"BUG\" if bug else \"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112392](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112392)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4326](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4326), [GHSA-27h8-gf7j-v5hc](https://osv.dev/GHSA-27h8-gf7j-v5hc), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4326](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4326)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e Pillow is a Python imaging library. Prior to 13.0.0, Pillow's rank-based filters can dereference invalid image storage for a zero-size image. ImagingExpand() allocates a non-empty output and then faults while replicating edges by reading the first pixel from empty input storage; ImagingRankFilter is never reached. An attacker can supply or cause creation of a zero-size image to an application that applies a rank, median, minimum, or maximum filter. This causes a native process crash. This issue is fixed in version 13.0.0.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-27h8-gf7j-v5hc](https://osv.dev/GHSA-27h8-gf7j-v5hc)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e NULL dereference in ImagingExpand via rank filters on zero-size images\n\u003e Location: src/libImaging/Filter.c:79  in ImagingExpand()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Applying Pillow's median, minimum, maximum or rank filter to an image with zero width or zero height crashes the whole Python process instead of raising an error. Empty images like this are easy to produce with ordinary calls, for example a crop box whose left and right edges (or top and bottom edges) are equal, or the second page of a crafted multi-page image file. An attacker who can supply crop coordinates or an uploaded file to an application that runs one of these filters can kill its worker process. The effect is a crash only, with no memory disclosure or corruption.\n\u003e \n\u003e The fault is in `ImagingExpand()` in `src/libImaging/Filter.c`. This is the edge-padding step that `RankFilter.filter()` always runs through the core `expand()` method, so `MedianFilter`, `MinFilter` and `MaxFilter` go through it too. It fills the margin by copying the input's edge pixels, reading `imIn-\u003eimage[y][0]` and rows `0` / `ysize-1`, and never checks that the input has any pixels. For an empty image, `ImagingAllocateArray` leaves the line pointers NULL, so the first read dereferences NULL and the process dies with SIGSEGV, in every mode.\n\u003e \n\u003e Zero-size images reach this point through public API alone:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `Image.crop()`, `ImageOps.crop()` and `Image.new()`;\n\u003e - a multi-page TIFF opened by path whose second IFD declares `ImageLength = 0`, on which `seek(1)` and `load()` succeed.\n\u003e \n\u003e Every other `ImageFilter` returns an empty image for the same inputs. The zero-height case has crashed since the original PIL import, and the zero-width case since Pillow 4.3.0. This is separate from the `margin == 0` fix in #9755: the path is the same, but here the degenerate value is the image size rather than the kernel size. It has the same shape as the zero-dimension `ImagingOffset` crash fixed under GHSA-f87v-9578-p47w.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached reproducer shows a one-pixel-wide control crop filtering normally, while four empty crops passed through the rank filters are each killed by SIGSEGV. A patch that returns early in `ImagingExpand` for empty input is included.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImagingExpand()` (`src/libImaging/Filter.c`), the edge-padding helper that `ImageFilter.RankFilter` runs before the actual rank filter, reads the first pixel of each input line without checking that the input has any pixels. For an image with width 0 or height 0 the line pointers are NULL, so `Image.filter()` with `MedianFilter`, `MinFilter`, `MaxFilter` or `RankFilter` kills the interpreter with SIGSEGV instead of returning or raising:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from PIL import Image, ImageFilter\n\u003e im = Image.new(\"L\", (10, 10)).crop((3, 0, 3, 10))   # size (0, 10); crop() accepts left == right\n\u003e im.filter(ImageFilter.MedianFilter(3))               # SIGSEGV\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Under gdb on the stock build the fault is at `ImagingExpand (imIn=…, margin=1) at src/libImaging/Filter.c:105` (the `EXPAND(UINT8, image8)` expansion), called from `_expand_image` at `src/_imaging.c:1151`, with `imIn-\u003exsize = 0` and `imIn-\u003eimage[0] = 0x0`. The same happens for every mode the rank filters accept (`L`, `RGB`, `RGBA`, `I`, `F`), and the core `expand(m)` method with `m \u003e= 1` crashes the same way in any mode, including `P`. The same input through `BoxBlur`, `GaussianBlur`, `Kernel`, `ModeFilter` and `UnsharpMask` returns a `(0, 10)` image; a `(1, 10)` crop through `MedianFilter` also returns normally.\n\u003e \n\u003e The unchecked edge reads date from the PIL import (`9a640e315`, 2010, so every Pillow release): the zero-height case has crashed since then, and the zero-width case since `fd907fbdc` (\"Fix 0-width and 0-height images other way\", Pillow 4.3.0), which stopped backing zero-width images with a dummy block and left their row pointers NULL. It is adjacent to the `margin == 0` divide-by-zero fix in #9755 — same `expand()` → `ImagingExpand()` path, but the unhandled degenerate value here is the image size rather than the kernel size.\n\u003e \n\u003e The path:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/ImageFilter.py:111-116   RankFilter.filter  (MedianFilter/MinFilter/MaxFilter subclass it)\n\u003e         image = image.expand(self.size // 2)\n\u003e         return image.rankfilter(self.size, self.rank)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `expand(m)` with `m \u003e 0` is `ImagingExpand(self-\u003eimage, m)` (`src/_imaging.c:1142-1151`). It validates only `margin`, allocates an output of `(xsize+2*margin) × (ysize+2*margin)` — which is non-empty, so the allocation succeeds — and then fills the margin by replicating edge pixels of the input:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e // src/libImaging/Filter.c:70-101\n\u003e     imOut =\n\u003e         ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n\u003e     …\n\u003e #define EXPAND_LINE(type, image, yin, yout)                       \\\n\u003e     {                                                             \\\n\u003e         for (x = 0; x \u003c margin; x++) {                            \\\n\u003e             imOut-\u003eimage[yout][x] = imIn-\u003eimage[yin][0];          \\   // line 79\n\u003e         }                                                         \\\n\u003e         …                                                         \\\n\u003e         for (x = 0; x \u003c margin; x++) {                            \\\n\u003e             imOut-\u003eimage[yout][margin + imIn-\u003exsize + x] =        \\\n\u003e                 imIn-\u003eimage[yin][imIn-\u003exsize - 1];                \\   // [-1] when xsize == 0\n\u003e         }                                                         \\\n\u003e     }\n\u003e \n\u003e #define EXPAND(type, image)                                                      \\\n\u003e     {                                                                            \\\n\u003e         for (y = 0; y \u003c margin; y++) {                                           \\\n\u003e             EXPAND_LINE(type, image, 0, y);                                      \\   // line 0 of a 0-line image\n\u003e         }                                                                        \\\n\u003e         …\n\u003e         for (y = 0; y \u003c margin; y++) {                                           \\\n\u003e             EXPAND_LINE(type, image, imIn-\u003eysize - 1, margin + imIn-\u003eysize + y); \\   // line -1 when ysize == 0\n\u003e         }                                                                        \\\n\u003e     }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Why the line pointers are NULL: `ImagingNewPrologueSubtype` callocs the `im-\u003eimage` pointer array (`src/libImaging/Storage.c:216`, at least one entry), and `ImagingAllocateArray` returns before assigning any of them for an empty image:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e // src/libImaging/Storage.c:423-426\n\u003e     /* 0-width or 0-height image. No need to do anything */\n\u003e     if (!im-\u003elinesize || !im-\u003eysize) {\n\u003e         return im;\n\u003e     }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e So with `xsize == 0` every `imIn-\u003eimage[y]` is NULL and `imIn-\u003eimage[yin][0]` faults; with `ysize == 0` the top-margin pass reads `imIn-\u003eimage[0]` (the single calloc'd NULL slot) and faults the same way, and the bottom-margin pass would index `imIn-\u003eimage[-1]`. `ImagingRankFilter` is never reached.\n\u003e \n\u003e Zero-size images are ordinary objects in Pillow and arrive at `filter()` through public API only:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `Image.crop(box)` rejects only inverted boxes (`src/PIL/Image.py:1385-1390`); `left == right` or `upper == lower` yields a 0×N / N×0 image. `ImageOps.crop(im, border)` with `border == width // 2`, and `Image.new(mode, (0, n))`, do the same.\n\u003e - From a file: `ImageFile.__init__` refuses a zero first-frame size (`src/PIL/ImageFile.py:167-173`), but `TiffImagePlugin._setup()` takes `ImageWidth`/`ImageLength` of every later IFD as-is on `seek()` (`src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py:1468-1481`). For an uncompressed page of an image opened by path, `ImageFile.load()` takes the mmap branch (`src/PIL/ImageFile.py:307, 325-347`) and `Image.core.map_buffer` builds an `8×0` core image without complaint (`src/map.c:127-141`), so `im.seek(1); im.load()` succeeds and a following `im.filter(ImageFilter.MedianFilter(3))` segfaults in `ImagingExpand` exactly as above (verified under gdb: `imIn-\u003eysize = 0`, `imIn-\u003eimage[0] = 0x0`). A 384-byte two-page TIFF is enough.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Denial of service: the whole embedding process (web worker, pipeline process) dies with SIGSEGV rather than the request failing with a Python exception. It is reachable by a remote user in two realistic shapes: (a) an application that crops an upload to user-supplied coordinates and then applies a median/min/max filter (denoise, dilate/erode, thumbnail clean-up) — a box with `x0 == x1` or `y0 == y1` passes `crop()` and crashes the worker; (b) an application that opens an uploaded TIFF from disk by path, iterates its pages and applies a rank filter to each — a crafted second IFD with `ImageLength = 0` crashes it. The faulting access is a read at address 0 (or a fixed small negative offset from the pointer array for the `[-1]` cases, which is not reached because the first read already faults), so there is no memory disclosure or corruption beyond the crash. The same shape, a zero-dimension image crashing C code reached through public API, was fixed in `ImagingOffset` in 39d14a0db (GHSA-f87v-9578-p47w).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e `repro` is a single Python script that uses the installed Pillow as-is (no rebuild). It runs each case in a fresh child interpreter, with core dumps disabled. That way a segfault shows up as the child's exit status and the script itself keeps running. Each child builds `Image.new(\"L\", (10, 10)).crop(box)`, prints its size, calls `im.filter(...)` and then prints the size of the result.\n\u003e \n\u003e - **Control**: a `(1, 10)` crop (`(3, 0, 4, 10)`) through `MedianFilter(3)`. The rank filter accepts a thin image and must return normally.\n\u003e - **PoC**: the rank filters on empty crops.\n\u003e   - A `(0, 10)` crop (`left == right`, `(3, 0, 3, 10)`) through `MedianFilter(3)` and `MaxFilter(5)`.\n\u003e   - A `(10, 0)` crop (`upper == lower`, `(0, 3, 10, 3)`) through `MinFilter(3)`.\n\u003e   - A `(0, 0)` crop (`(3, 3, 3, 3)`) through `RankFilter(3, 4)`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Expected output on the stock build:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e   crop(3, 0, 4, 10)    MedianFilter(3)    (1, 10) -\u003e (1, 10)\n\u003e   crop(3, 0, 3, 10)    MedianFilter(3)    (0, 10) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\n\u003e   crop(3, 0, 3, 10)    MaxFilter(5)       (0, 10) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\n\u003e   crop(0, 3, 10, 3)    MinFilter(3)       (10, 0) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\n\u003e   crop(3, 3, 3, 3)     RankFilter(3, 4)   (0, 0) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\n\u003e BUG\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The size printed before `-\u003e` shows that the empty image was created without error. The crash happens inside `filter()`, in `ImagingExpand`. The last line is `BUG` if the control returned normally and at least one rank-filter child was killed by SIGSEGV. Otherwise it is `OK`. The script covers the crop route only; the TIFF route described above fails the same way (`im.seek(1); im.load(); im.filter(ImageFilter.MedianFilter(3))` on a by-path two-page TIFF whose second IFD has `ImageLength = 0`). With the attached patch, every PoC line prints the unchanged size (for example `(0, 10) -\u003e (0, 10)`) and the script prints `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached patch handles the empty input in `ImagingExpand` (`src/libImaging/Filter.c`), right after the `margin` checks and before the `ImagingNewDirty` allocation and the replication loops. With `xsize == 0` or `ysize == 0` there are no edge pixels to replicate, so it returns a zeroed (`ImagingNew`, not `ImagingNewDirty`) image of the expanded size, with the input's palette copied. This keeps `ImagingExpand`'s size contract, so `ImagingRankFilter` then produces an output of `xsize - 2*margin` × `ysize - 2*margin`, i.e. the original empty size, matching every other filter. It mirrors the existing zero-dimension early returns in `src/libImaging/BoxBlur.c` (127004368) and `src/libImaging/Offset.c` (39d14a0db):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n\u003e @@ -67,6 +67,17 @@ ImagingExpand(Imaging imIn, int margin) {\n\u003e          return (Imaging)ImagingError_ValueError(\"filter size too large\");\n\u003e      }\n\u003e  \n\u003e +    if (imIn-\u003exsize == 0 || imIn-\u003eysize == 0) {\n\u003e +        /* No edge pixels to replicate (and no lines to read from), so\n\u003e +           return a zeroed image of the expanded size */\n\u003e +        imOut = ImagingNew(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n\u003e +        if (!imOut) {\n\u003e +            return NULL;\n\u003e +        }\n\u003e +        ImagingCopyPalette(imOut, imIn);\n\u003e +        return imOut;\n\u003e +    }\n\u003e +\n\u003e      imOut =\n\u003e          ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n\u003e      if (!imOut) {\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The patch also adds `test_rankfilter_zero_dimension` next to `test_rankfilter_error` in `Tests/test_image_filter.py`, which filters `Image.new(\"L\", size)` for `size` in `((0, 6), (6, 0), (0, 0))` with `MinFilter`, `MedianFilter`, `MaxFilter` and `RankFilter(3, 2)` and asserts the size is preserved. Applied to a clean checkout and rebuilt, the reproducer prints `OK` and `Tests/test_image_filter.py` passes. A Python-level guard in `RankFilter.filter` (`if 0 in image.size: return image.copy()`) would also stop the crash, but the C-level check protects any other caller of `expand()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch\n\u003e \n\u003e Return a zeroed image from ImagingExpand when the input has zero width or height instead of reading NULL line pointers\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/libImaging/Filter.c` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/Tests/test_image_filter.py b/Tests/test_image_filter.py\n\u003e index 974d4b7a3..8dc8e1a1a 100644\n\u003e --- a/Tests/test_image_filter.py\n\u003e +++ b/Tests/test_image_filter.py\n\u003e @@ -155,6 +155,23 @@ def test_rankfilter_error(filter: ImageFilter.RankFilter) -\u003e None:\n\u003e          im.filter(filter).getpixel((1, 1))\n\u003e  \n\u003e  \n\u003e +@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n\u003e +    \"filter\",\n\u003e +    (\n\u003e +        ImageFilter.MinFilter,\n\u003e +        ImageFilter.MedianFilter,\n\u003e +        ImageFilter.MaxFilter,\n\u003e +        ImageFilter.RankFilter(3, 2),\n\u003e +    ),\n\u003e +)\n\u003e +@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"size\", ((0, 6), (6, 0), (0, 0)))\n\u003e +def test_rankfilter_zero_dimension(\n\u003e +    filter: ImageFilter.RankFilter, size: tuple[int, int]\n\u003e +) -\u003e None:\n\u003e +    im = Image.new(\"L\", size)\n\u003e +    assert im.filter(filter).size == size\n\u003e +\n\u003e +\n\u003e  def test_rankfilter_properties() -\u003e None:\n\u003e      rankfilter = ImageFilter.RankFilter(3, 2)\n\u003e  \n\u003e diff --git a/src/libImaging/Filter.c b/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n\u003e index 3492a871f..d702db5f9 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n\u003e @@ -67,6 +67,17 @@ ImagingExpand(Imaging imIn, int margin) {\n\u003e          return (Imaging)ImagingError_ValueError(\"filter size too large\");\n\u003e      }\n\u003e  \n\u003e +    if (imIn-\u003exsize == 0 || imIn-\u003eysize == 0) {\n\u003e +        /* No edge pixels to replicate (and no lines to read from), so\n\u003e +           return a zeroed image of the expanded size */\n\u003e +        imOut = ImagingNew(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n\u003e +        if (!imOut) {\n\u003e +            return NULL;\n\u003e +        }\n\u003e +        ImagingCopyPalette(imOut, imIn);\n\u003e +        return imOut;\n\u003e +    }\n\u003e +\n\u003e      imOut =\n\u003e          ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n\u003e      if (!imOut) {\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 1,333 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e # Pillow: rank filters (Median/Min/Max/RankFilter) on a 0-wide or 0-high image\n\u003e # SIGSEGV in ImagingExpand (src/libImaging/Filter.c) - NULL line pointer read.\n\u003e # Each case runs in a child interpreter; BUG if the control returns and any\n\u003e # zero-size case dies of SIGSEGV.\n\u003e import signal, subprocess, sys\n\u003e \n\u003e CHILD = \"\"\"\n\u003e import resource; resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))\n\u003e from PIL import Image, ImageFilter\n\u003e im = Image.new(\"L\", (10, 10)).crop(%s)      # public API; crop accepts x0==x1 / y0==y1\n\u003e print(im.size, \"-\u003e\", end=\" \", flush=True)\n\u003e print(im.filter(ImageFilter.%s).size)\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e def run(box, flt):\n\u003e     p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", CHILD % (box, flt)],\n\u003e                        capture_output=True, text=True)\n\u003e     out = p.stdout.strip()\n\u003e     if p.returncode \u003c 0:\n\u003e         out += \" killed by \" + signal.Signals(-p.returncode).name\n\u003e     print(f\"  crop{box!s:\u003c16} {flt:\u003c18} {out}\")\n\u003e     return p.returncode\n\u003e \n\u003e ctrl = run((3, 0, 4, 10), \"MedianFilter(3)\")          # (1,10): must work\n\u003e rcs = [run((3, 0, 3, 10), \"MedianFilter(3)\"),        # (0,10)\n\u003e        run((3, 0, 3, 10), \"MaxFilter(5)\"),\n\u003e        run((0, 3, 10, 3), \"MinFilter(3)\"),           # (10,0)\n\u003e        run((3, 3, 3, 3), \"RankFilter(3, 4)\")]        # (0,0)\n\u003e bug = ctrl == 0 and any(rc == -signal.SIGSEGV for rc in rcs)\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if bug else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-27h8-gf7j-v5hc | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4326 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112392\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112392](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112392)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4326](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4326), [GHSA-27h8-gf7j-v5hc](https://osv.dev/GHSA-27h8-gf7j-v5hc), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4326](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4326)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e Pillow is a Python imaging library. Prior to 13.0.0, Pillow's rank-based filters can dereference invalid image storage for a zero-size image. ImagingExpand() allocates a non-empty output and then faults while replicating edges by reading the first pixel from empty input storage; ImagingRankFilter is never reached. An attacker can supply or cause creation of a zero-size image to an application that applies a rank, median, minimum, or maximum filter. This causes a native process crash. This issue is fixed in version 13.0.0.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-27h8-gf7j-v5hc](https://osv.dev/GHSA-27h8-gf7j-v5hc)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e NULL dereference in ImagingExpand via rank filters on zero-size images\n\u003e Location: src/libImaging/Filter.c:79  in ImagingExpand()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Applying Pillow's median, minimum, maximum or rank filter to an image with zero width or zero height crashes the whole Python process instead of raising an error. Empty images like this are easy to produce with ordinary calls, for example a crop box whose left and right edges (or top and bottom edges) are equal, or the second page of a crafted multi-page image file. An attacker who can supply crop coordinates or an uploaded file to an application that runs one of these filters can kill its worker process. The effect is a crash only, with no memory disclosure or corruption.\n\u003e \n\u003e The fault is in `ImagingExpand()` in `src/libImaging/Filter.c`. This is the edge-padding step that `RankFilter.filter()` always runs through the core `expand()` method, so `MedianFilter`, `MinFilter` and `MaxFilter` go through it too. It fills the margin by copying the input's edge pixels, reading `imIn-\u003eimage[y][0]` and rows `0` / `ysize-1`, and never checks that the input has any pixels. For an empty image, `ImagingAllocateArray` leaves the line pointers NULL, so the first read dereferences NULL and the process dies with SIGSEGV, in every mode.\n\u003e \n\u003e Zero-size images reach this point through public API alone:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `Image.crop()`, `ImageOps.crop()` and `Image.new()`;\n\u003e - a multi-page TIFF opened by path whose second IFD declares `ImageLength = 0`, on which `seek(1)` and `load()` succeed.\n\u003e \n\u003e Every other `ImageFilter` returns an empty image for the same inputs. The zero-height case has crashed since the original PIL import, and the zero-width case since Pillow 4.3.0. This is separate from the `margin == 0` fix in #9755: the path is the same, but here the degenerate value is the image size rather than the kernel size. It has the same shape as the zero-dimension `ImagingOffset` crash fixed under GHSA-f87v-9578-p47w.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached reproducer shows a one-pixel-wide control crop filtering normally, while four empty crops passed through the rank filters are each killed by SIGSEGV. A patch that returns early in `ImagingExpand` for empty input is included.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImagingExpand()` (`src/libImaging/Filter.c`), the edge-padding helper that `ImageFilter.RankFilter` runs before the actual rank filter, reads the first pixel of each input line without checking that the input has any pixels. For an image with width 0 or height 0 the line pointers are NULL, so `Image.filter()` with `MedianFilter`, `MinFilter`, `MaxFilter` or `RankFilter` kills the interpreter with SIGSEGV instead of returning or raising:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from PIL import Image, ImageFilter\n\u003e im = Image.new(\"L\", (10, 10)).crop((3, 0, 3, 10))   # size (0, 10); crop() accepts left == right\n\u003e im.filter(ImageFilter.MedianFilter(3))               # SIGSEGV\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Under gdb on the stock build the fault is at `ImagingExpand (imIn=…, margin=1) at src/libImaging/Filter.c:105` (the `EXPAND(UINT8, image8)` expansion), called from `_expand_image` at `src/_imaging.c:1151`, with `imIn-\u003exsize = 0` and `imIn-\u003eimage[0] = 0x0`. The same happens for every mode the rank filters accept (`L`, `RGB`, `RGBA`, `I`, `F`), and the core `expand(m)` method with `m \u003e= 1` crashes the same way in any mode, including `P`. The same input through `BoxBlur`, `GaussianBlur`, `Kernel`, `ModeFilter` and `UnsharpMask` returns a `(0, 10)` image; a `(1, 10)` crop through `MedianFilter` also returns normally.\n\u003e \n\u003e The unchecked edge reads date from the PIL import (`9a640e315`, 2010, so every Pillow release): the zero-height case has crashed since then, and the zero-width case since `fd907fbdc` (\"Fix 0-width and 0-height images other way\", Pillow 4.3.0), which stopped backing zero-width images with a dummy block and left their row pointers NULL. It is adjacent to the `margin == 0` divide-by-zero fix in #9755 — same `expand()` → `ImagingExpand()` path, but the unhandled degenerate value here is the image size rather than the kernel size.\n\u003e \n\u003e The path:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/ImageFilter.py:111-116   RankFilter.filter  (MedianFilter/MinFilter/MaxFilter subclass it)\n\u003e         image = image.expand(self.size // 2)\n\u003e         return image.rankfilter(self.size, self.rank)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `expand(m)` with `m \u003e 0` is `ImagingExpand(self-\u003eimage, m)` (`src/_imaging.c:1142-1151`). It validates only `margin`, allocates an output of `(xsize+2*margin) × (ysize+2*margin)` — which is non-empty, so the allocation succeeds — and then fills the margin by replicating edge pixels of the input:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e // src/libImaging/Filter.c:70-101\n\u003e     imOut =\n\u003e         ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n\u003e     …\n\u003e #define EXPAND_LINE(type, image, yin, yout)                       \\\n\u003e     {                                                             \\\n\u003e         for (x = 0; x \u003c margin; x++) {                            \\\n\u003e             imOut-\u003eimage[yout][x] = imIn-\u003eimage[yin][0];          \\   // line 79\n\u003e         }                                                         \\\n\u003e         …                                                         \\\n\u003e         for (x = 0; x \u003c margin; x++) {                            \\\n\u003e             imOut-\u003eimage[yout][margin + imIn-\u003exsize + x] =        \\\n\u003e                 imIn-\u003eimage[yin][imIn-\u003exsize - 1];                \\   // [-1] when xsize == 0\n\u003e         }                                                         \\\n\u003e     }\n\u003e \n\u003e #define EXPAND(type, image)                                                      \\\n\u003e     {                                                                            \\\n\u003e         for (y = 0; y \u003c margin; y++) {                                           \\\n\u003e             EXPAND_LINE(type, image, 0, y);                                      \\   // line 0 of a 0-line image\n\u003e         }                                                                        \\\n\u003e         …\n\u003e         for (y = 0; y \u003c margin; y++) {                                           \\\n\u003e             EXPAND_LINE(type, image, imIn-\u003eysize - 1, margin + imIn-\u003eysize + y); \\   // line -1 when ysize == 0\n\u003e         }                                                                        \\\n\u003e     }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Why the line pointers are NULL: `ImagingNewPrologueSubtype` callocs the `im-\u003eimage` pointer array (`src/libImaging/Storage.c:216`, at least one entry), and `ImagingAllocateArray` returns before assigning any of them for an empty image:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e // src/libImaging/Storage.c:423-426\n\u003e     /* 0-width or 0-height image. No need to do anything */\n\u003e     if (!im-\u003elinesize || !im-\u003eysize) {\n\u003e         return im;\n\u003e     }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e So with `xsize == 0` every `imIn-\u003eimage[y]` is NULL and `imIn-\u003eimage[yin][0]` faults; with `ysize == 0` the top-margin pass reads `imIn-\u003eimage[0]` (the single calloc'd NULL slot) and faults the same way, and the bottom-margin pass would index `imIn-\u003eimage[-1]`. `ImagingRankFilter` is never reached.\n\u003e \n\u003e Zero-size images are ordinary objects in Pillow and arrive at `filter()` through public API only:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `Image.crop(box)` rejects only inverted boxes (`src/PIL/Image.py:1385-1390`); `left == right` or `upper == lower` yields a 0×N / N×0 image. `ImageOps.crop(im, border)` with `border == width // 2`, and `Image.new(mode, (0, n))`, do the same.\n\u003e - From a file: `ImageFile.__init__` refuses a zero first-frame size (`src/PIL/ImageFile.py:167-173`), but `TiffImagePlugin._setup()` takes `ImageWidth`/`ImageLength` of every later IFD as-is on `seek()` (`src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py:1468-1481`). For an uncompressed page of an image opened by path, `ImageFile.load()` takes the mmap branch (`src/PIL/ImageFile.py:307, 325-347`) and `Image.core.map_buffer` builds an `8×0` core image without complaint (`src/map.c:127-141`), so `im.seek(1); im.load()` succeeds and a following `im.filter(ImageFilter.MedianFilter(3))` segfaults in `ImagingExpand` exactly as above (verified under gdb: `imIn-\u003eysize = 0`, `imIn-\u003eimage[0] = 0x0`). A 384-byte two-page TIFF is enough.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Denial of service: the whole embedding process (web worker, pipeline process) dies with SIGSEGV rather than the request failing with a Python exception. It is reachable by a remote user in two realistic shapes: (a) an application that crops an upload to user-supplied coordinates and then applies a median/min/max filter (denoise, dilate/erode, thumbnail clean-up) — a box with `x0 == x1` or `y0 == y1` passes `crop()` and crashes the worker; (b) an application that opens an uploaded TIFF from disk by path, iterates its pages and applies a rank filter to each — a crafted second IFD with `ImageLength = 0` crashes it. The faulting access is a read at address 0 (or a fixed small negative offset from the pointer array for the `[-1]` cases, which is not reached because the first read already faults), so there is no memory disclosure or corruption beyond the crash. The same shape, a zero-dimension image crashing C code reached through public API, was fixed in `ImagingOffset` in 39d14a0db (GHSA-f87v-9578-p47w).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e `repro` is a single Python script that uses the installed Pillow as-is (no rebuild). It runs each case in a fresh child interpreter, with core dumps disabled. That way a segfault shows up as the child's exit status and the script itself keeps running. Each child builds `Image.new(\"L\", (10, 10)).crop(box)`, prints its size, calls `im.filter(...)` and then prints the size of the result.\n\u003e \n\u003e - **Control**: a `(1, 10)` crop (`(3, 0, 4, 10)`) through `MedianFilter(3)`. The rank filter accepts a thin image and must return normally.\n\u003e - **PoC**: the rank filters on empty crops.\n\u003e   - A `(0, 10)` crop (`left == right`, `(3, 0, 3, 10)`) through `MedianFilter(3)` and `MaxFilter(5)`.\n\u003e   - A `(10, 0)` crop (`upper == lower`, `(0, 3, 10, 3)`) through `MinFilter(3)`.\n\u003e   - A `(0, 0)` crop (`(3, 3, 3, 3)`) through `RankFilter(3, 4)`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Expected output on the stock build:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e   crop(3, 0, 4, 10)    MedianFilter(3)    (1, 10) -\u003e (1, 10)\n\u003e   crop(3, 0, 3, 10)    MedianFilter(3)    (0, 10) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\n\u003e   crop(3, 0, 3, 10)    MaxFilter(5)       (0, 10) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\n\u003e   crop(0, 3, 10, 3)    MinFilter(3)       (10, 0) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\n\u003e   crop(3, 3, 3, 3)     RankFilter(3, 4)   (0, 0) -\u003e killed by SIGSEGV\n\u003e BUG\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The size printed before `-\u003e` shows that the empty image was created without error. The crash happens inside `filter()`, in `ImagingExpand`. The last line is `BUG` if the control returned normally and at least one rank-filter child was killed by SIGSEGV. Otherwise it is `OK`. The script covers the crop route only; the TIFF route described above fails the same way (`im.seek(1); im.load(); im.filter(ImageFilter.MedianFilter(3))` on a by-path two-page TIFF whose second IFD has `ImageLength = 0`). With the attached patch, every PoC line prints the unchanged size (for example `(0, 10) -\u003e (0, 10)`) and the script prints `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached patch handles the empty input in `ImagingExpand` (`src/libImaging/Filter.c`), right after the `margin` checks and before the `ImagingNewDirty` allocation and the replication loops. With `xsize == 0` or `ysize == 0` there are no edge pixels to replicate, so it returns a zeroed (`ImagingNew`, not `ImagingNewDirty`) image of the expanded size, with the input's palette copied. This keeps `ImagingExpand`'s size contract, so `ImagingRankFilter` then produces an output of `xsize - 2*margin` × `ysize - 2*margin`, i.e. the original empty size, matching every other filter. It mirrors the existing zero-dimension early returns in `src/libImaging/BoxBlur.c` (127004368) and `src/libImaging/Offset.c` (39d14a0db):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n\u003e @@ -67,6 +67,17 @@ ImagingExpand(Imaging imIn, int margin) {\n\u003e          return (Imaging)ImagingError_ValueError(\"filter size too large\");\n\u003e      }\n\u003e  \n\u003e +    if (imIn-\u003exsize == 0 || imIn-\u003eysize == 0) {\n\u003e +        /* No edge pixels to replicate (and no lines to read from), so\n\u003e +           return a zeroed image of the expanded size */\n\u003e +        imOut = ImagingNew(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n\u003e +        if (!imOut) {\n\u003e +            return NULL;\n\u003e +        }\n\u003e +        ImagingCopyPalette(imOut, imIn);\n\u003e +        return imOut;\n\u003e +    }\n\u003e +\n\u003e      imOut =\n\u003e          ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n\u003e      if (!imOut) {\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The patch also adds `test_rankfilter_zero_dimension` next to `test_rankfilter_error` in `Tests/test_image_filter.py`, which filters `Image.new(\"L\", size)` for `size` in `((0, 6), (6, 0), (0, 0))` with `MinFilter`, `MedianFilter`, `MaxFilter` and `RankFilter(3, 2)` and asserts the size is preserved. Applied to a clean checkout and rebuilt, the reproducer prints `OK` and `Tests/test_image_filter.py` passes. A Python-level guard in `RankFilter.filter` (`if 0 in image.size: return image.copy()`) would also stop the crash, but the C-level check protects any other caller of `expand()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch\n\u003e \n\u003e Return a zeroed image from ImagingExpand when the input has zero width or height instead of reading NULL line pointers\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/libImaging/Filter.c` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/Tests/test_image_filter.py b/Tests/test_image_filter.py\n\u003e index 974d4b7a3..8dc8e1a1a 100644\n\u003e --- a/Tests/test_image_filter.py\n\u003e +++ b/Tests/test_image_filter.py\n\u003e @@ -155,6 +155,23 @@ def test_rankfilter_error(filter: ImageFilter.RankFilter) -\u003e None:\n\u003e          im.filter(filter).getpixel((1, 1))\n\u003e  \n\u003e  \n\u003e +@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n\u003e +    \"filter\",\n\u003e +    (\n\u003e +        ImageFilter.MinFilter,\n\u003e +        ImageFilter.MedianFilter,\n\u003e +        ImageFilter.MaxFilter,\n\u003e +        ImageFilter.RankFilter(3, 2),\n\u003e +    ),\n\u003e +)\n\u003e +@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"size\", ((0, 6), (6, 0), (0, 0)))\n\u003e +def test_rankfilter_zero_dimension(\n\u003e +    filter: ImageFilter.RankFilter, size: tuple[int, int]\n\u003e +) -\u003e None:\n\u003e +    im = Image.new(\"L\", size)\n\u003e +    assert im.filter(filter).size == size\n\u003e +\n\u003e +\n\u003e  def test_rankfilter_properties() -\u003e None:\n\u003e      rankfilter = ImageFilter.RankFilter(3, 2)\n\u003e  \n\u003e diff --git a/src/libImaging/Filter.c b/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n\u003e index 3492a871f..d702db5f9 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/Filter.c\n\u003e @@ -67,6 +67,17 @@ ImagingExpand(Imaging imIn, int margin) {\n\u003e          return (Imaging)ImagingError_ValueError(\"filter size too large\");\n\u003e      }\n\u003e  \n\u003e +    if (imIn-\u003exsize == 0 || imIn-\u003eysize == 0) {\n\u003e +        /* No edge pixels to replicate (and no lines to read from), so\n\u003e +           return a zeroed image of the expanded size */\n\u003e +        imOut = ImagingNew(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n\u003e +        if (!imOut) {\n\u003e +            return NULL;\n\u003e +        }\n\u003e +        ImagingCopyPalette(imOut, imIn);\n\u003e +        return imOut;\n\u003e +    }\n\u003e +\n\u003e      imOut =\n\u003e          ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, imIn-\u003exsize + 2 * margin, imIn-\u003eysize + 2 * margin);\n\u003e      if (!imOut) {\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 1,333 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e # Pillow: rank filters (Median/Min/Max/RankFilter) on a 0-wide or 0-high image\n\u003e # SIGSEGV in ImagingExpand (src/libImaging/Filter.c) - NULL line pointer read.\n\u003e # Each case runs in a child interpreter; BUG if the control returns and any\n\u003e # zero-size case dies of SIGSEGV.\n\u003e import signal, subprocess, sys\n\u003e \n\u003e CHILD = \"\"\"\n\u003e import resource; resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))\n\u003e from PIL import Image, ImageFilter\n\u003e im = Image.new(\"L\", (10, 10)).crop(%s)      # public API; crop accepts x0==x1 / y0==y1\n\u003e print(im.size, \"-\u003e\", end=\" \", flush=True)\n\u003e print(im.filter(ImageFilter.%s).size)\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e def run(box, flt):\n\u003e     p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", CHILD % (box, flt)],\n\u003e                        capture_output=True, text=True)\n\u003e     out = p.stdout.strip()\n\u003e     if p.returncode \u003c 0:\n\u003e         out += \" killed by \" + signal.Signals(-p.returncode).name\n\u003e     print(f\"  crop{box!s:\u003c16} {flt:\u003c18} {out}\")\n\u003e     return p.returncode\n\u003e \n\u003e ctrl = run((3, 0, 4, 10), \"MedianFilter(3)\")          # (1,10): must work\n\u003e rcs = [run((3, 0, 3, 10), \"MedianFilter(3)\"),        # (0,10)\n\u003e        run((3, 0, 3, 10), \"MaxFilter(5)\"),\n\u003e        run((0, 3, 10, 3), \"MinFilter(3)\"),           # (10,0)\n\u003e        run((3, 3, 3, 3), \"RankFilter(3, 4)\")]        # (0,0)\n\u003e bug = ctrl == 0 and any(rc == -signal.SIGSEGV for rc in rcs)\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if bug else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-27h8-gf7j-v5hc | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4326 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112392\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-112392",
              "name": "CVE-2026-112392",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.2"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-112392: Pillow: NULL dereference in ImagingExpand via rank filters on zero-size images",
                "text": "CVE-2026-112392: Pillow: NULL dereference in ImagingExpand via rank filters on zero-size images"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-111181",
                "GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Impact\n\nA single request can crash any application using `body-parser` with default options. When a request carries a `Content-Encoding` (for example `gzip`), `body-parser` pipes it into a decompression stream and, after reading the body, leaves that stream piped with no error listener. A payload of one valid compressed frame followed by trailing bytes decodes and the application responds normally, but the trailing bytes then reach the already-finished decoder, which emits an error that is re-thrown as an uncaught exception and terminates the process. No authentication or matching route is required, since parsing runs before routing.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in body-parser 2.4.0 and 1.20.9. Upgrade to one of these versions.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nPass `inflate: false` to the body-parser middleware if the application does not need to accept compressed request bodies. This rejects encoded requests before any decompression stream is created.",
                "text": "### Impact\n\nA single request can crash any application using `body-parser` with default options. When a request carries a `Content-Encoding` (for example `gzip`), `body-parser` pipes it into a decompression stream and, after reading the body, leaves that stream piped with no error listener. A payload of one valid compressed frame followed by trailing bytes decodes and the application responds normally, but the trailing bytes then reach the already-finished decoder, which emits an error that is re-thrown as an uncaught exception and terminates the process. No authentication or matching route is required, since parsing runs before routing.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in body-parser 2.4.0 and 1.20.9. Upgrade to one of these versions.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nPass `inflate: false` to the body-parser middleware if the application does not need to accept compressed request bodies. This rejects encoded requests before any decompression stream is created."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111181](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-111181)**.\n\n## [GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828](https://osv.dev/GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e A single request can crash any application using `body-parser` with default options. When a request carries a `Content-Encoding` (for example `gzip`), `body-parser` pipes it into a decompression stream and, after reading the body, leaves that stream piped with no error listener. A payload of one valid compressed frame followed by trailing bytes decodes and the application responds normally, but the trailing bytes then reach the already-finished decoder, which emits an error that is re-thrown as an uncaught exception and terminates the process. No authentication or matching route is required, since parsing runs before routing.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed in body-parser 2.4.0 and 1.20.9. Upgrade to one of these versions.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Pass `inflate: false` to the body-parser middleware if the application does not need to accept compressed request bodies. This rejects encoded requests before any decompression stream is created.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | body-parser | 1.20.6 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | body-parser | 1.20.6 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828 | body-parser | 1.20.9, 2.4.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111181\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111181\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111181](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-111181)**.\n\n## [GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828](https://osv.dev/GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e A single request can crash any application using `body-parser` with default options. When a request carries a `Content-Encoding` (for example `gzip`), `body-parser` pipes it into a decompression stream and, after reading the body, leaves that stream piped with no error listener. A payload of one valid compressed frame followed by trailing bytes decodes and the application responds normally, but the trailing bytes then reach the already-finished decoder, which emits an error that is re-thrown as an uncaught exception and terminates the process. No authentication or matching route is required, since parsing runs before routing.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed in body-parser 2.4.0 and 1.20.9. Upgrade to one of these versions.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Pass `inflate: false` to the body-parser middleware if the application does not need to accept compressed request bodies. This rejects encoded requests before any decompression stream is created.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | body-parser | 1.20.6 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | body-parser | 1.20.6 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828 | body-parser | 1.20.9, 2.4.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111181\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111181\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
