              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112394](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112394)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4327](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4327), [GHSA-4558-3h8r-v8xp](https://osv.dev/GHSA-4558-3h8r-v8xp), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4327](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4327)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e Pillow is a Python imaging library. Prior to 13.0.0, Pillow's P;2L and P;4L raw decoders can read past the end of an input buffer. ImagingRawDecode calculates a packed-pixel row size, while the unpackers read separately padded bit planes. A caller can supply a buffer and image dimensions whose accepted size is smaller than the unpacker's actual footprint. This can disclose up to three adjacent heap bytes in output pixels or crash the process. This issue is fixed in version 13.0.0.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-4558-3h8r-v8xp](https://osv.dev/GHSA-4558-3h8r-v8xp)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e On `main`, even with the guard in #9725, it's possible to have these decoders read 1 to 3 bytes past the end of the passed buffer with a suitable width/height.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImagingRawDecode` and the `P;2L` / `P;4L` unpackers disagree about how many bytes a line of data occupies.\n\u003e \n\u003e The raw decoder derives the line size from the bits per pixel\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e state-\u003ebytes = (state-\u003exsize * state-\u003ebits + 7) / 8;\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e and uses it both for the bounds check and to advance to the next line. For `P;2L` and `P;4L`, `bits` is 2 and 4 respectively.\n\u003e \n\u003e But these two rawmodes are not packed pixels but 2 or 4 *bit planes*, each padded to a whole number of bytes. `unpackP2L` computes `s = (pixels + 7) / 8` and reads plane *k* at offset `k * s`, and `unpackP4L` does the same across four planes. Their real footprint is therefore `planes * ((xsize + 7) / 8)`.\n\u003e \n\u003e For some widths, padding each plane separately makes this footprint larger than `state-\u003ebytes`. For example, at width 1, the decoder accounts for one byte, while the unpackers read two or four bytes. The decoder accepts data that is too short for the last line and on the other hand advances to subsequent lines by less than a line, so every line after the first is also read from the wrong offset.\n\u003e \n\u003e #9725's check is misshapen for this:\n\u003e \n\u003e * It can reject data of the right length: 8x1 `P;2L` image is 2 bytes but is not accepted.\n\u003e * It still accepts buffers shorter than the unpackers require for a given image shape.\n\u003e * It measures the length with `len(data)`, which is an element count rather than a byte count; e.g. `memoryview(array(\"I\", [0,0,0,0,0]))` (20 bytes) is rejected while `mv.tobytes()` is accepted.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC ( `ghsa_p2l_p4l.py`)\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from array import array\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e # 1 byte past\n\u003e Image.frombytes(\"P\", (1, 4), array(\"B\", [0] * 4), \"raw\", \"P;2L\")\n\u003e \n\u003e # 3 bytes past\n\u003e Image.frombytes(\"P\", (1, 8), array(\"B\", [0] * 8), \"raw\", \"P;4L\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e is the almost-simplest reproducer.\n\u003e \n\u003e (`array` is needed to get ASan to reliably scream, but the overread is observable with e.g. bytes literals. Python allocates `bytes`es with an extra trailing null byte, from an arena by default to boot, so ASan doesn't see the overread.)\n\u003e \n\u003e On my Mac, I can get ASan to scream about this with\n\u003e \n\u003e ```bash\n\u003e export asan=$(clang -print-file-name=libclang_rt.asan_osx_dynamic.dylib)\n\u003e env CFLAGS=\"-fsanitize=address -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g -O1\" LDFLAGS=\"-fsanitize=address\" uv pip install -e .\n\u003e env DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES=$asan PYTHONMALLOC=malloc ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 python3 ghsa_p2l_p4l.py\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e like so:\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e python3(29144,0x202fea2c0) malloc: nano zone abandoned due to inability to reserve vm space.\n\u003e unpackP2L: in=0x602000037370 i=0 j=0 pixels=1 reads 0x602000037370 0x602000037371\n\u003e unpackP2L: in=0x602000037371 i=0 j=0 pixels=1 reads 0x602000037371 0x602000037372\n\u003e unpackP2L: in=0x602000037372 i=0 j=0 pixels=1 reads 0x602000037372 0x602000037373\n\u003e unpackP2L: in=0x602000037373 i=0 j=0 pixels=1 reads 0x602000037373 0x602000037374\n\u003e =================================================================\n\u003e ==29144==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow on address 0x602000037374 at pc 0x00010778c684 bp 0x00016d029f40 sp 0x00016d029f38\n\u003e READ of size 1 at 0x602000037374 thread T0\n\u003e     #0 0x00010778c680 in unpackP2L+0x17c (_imaging.cpython-314-darwin.so:arm64+0xb4680)\n\u003e     #1 0x000107765360 in ImagingRawDecode+0x348 (_imaging.cpython-314-darwin.so:arm64+0x8d360)\n\u003e     #2 0x0001076f259c in _decode+0x15c (_imaging.cpython-314-darwin.so:arm64+0x1a59c)\n\u003e     #3 0x0001030d921c in method_vectorcall_VARARGS.llvm.8905850704545875072+0x84 (python3.14:arm64+0x10030521c)\n\u003e     #4 0x000102e585b8 in _TAIL_CALL_CALL.llvm.12785051352303791014+0x13c (python3.14:arm64+0x1000845b8)\n\u003e     #5 0x000102f1c17c in _PyEval_Vector+0x308 (python3.14:arm64+0x10014817c)\n\u003e     #6 0x000102f31864 in PyEval_EvalCode+0x9c (python3.14:arm64+0x10015d864)\n\u003e     #7 0x000102f3fa14 in run_mod.llvm.1224844961852478530+0x120 (python3.14:arm64+0x10016ba14)\n\u003e     #8 0x000103063eb0 in pyrun_file+0xa0 (python3.14:arm64+0x10028feb0)\n\u003e     #9 0x000102dfe834 in _PyRun_SimpleFileObject+0xfc (python3.14:arm64+0x10002a834)\n\u003e     #10 0x00010306cb60 in _PyRun_AnyFileObject+0x4c (python3.14:arm64+0x100298b60)\n\u003e     #11 0x00010306ca54 in pymain_run_file_obj+0xa0 (python3.14:arm64+0x100298a54)\n\u003e     #12 0x000102ffb80c in pymain_run_file+0x44 (python3.14:arm64+0x10022780c)\n\u003e     #13 0x000102ff7d0c in Py_RunMain+0x46c (python3.14:arm64+0x100223d0c)\n\u003e     #14 0x000102fb0820 in pymain_main.llvm.7308572997346072671+0x1cc (python3.14:arm64+0x1001dc820)\n\u003e     #15 0x000102fb0644 in main+0x20 (python3.14:arm64+0x1001dc644)\n\u003e     #16 0x000194c86b94  (\u003cunknown module\u003e)\n\u003e \n\u003e 0x602000037374 is located 0 bytes after 4-byte region [0x602000037370,0x602000037374)\n\u003e allocated by thread T0 here:\n\u003e     #0 0x0001044a130c in malloc+0x78 (libclang_rt.asan_osx_dynamic.dylib:arm64e+0x3d30c)\n\u003e     #1 0x000102f22324 in newarrayobject+0x6c (python3.14:arm64+0x10014e324)\n\u003e     #2 0x000102f21b4c in array_new+0x320 (python3.14:arm64+0x10014db4c)\n\u003e     #3 0x0001030fc390 in type_call+0x5c (python3.14:arm64+0x100328390)\n\u003e     #4 0x000102e58ae4 in _TAIL_CALL_CALL.llvm.12785051352303791014+0x668 (python3.14:arm64+0x100084ae4)\n\u003e     #5 0x000102f1c17c in _PyEval_Vector+0x308 (python3.14:arm64+0x10014817c)\n\u003e     #6 0x000102f31864 in PyEval_EvalCode+0x9c (python3.14:arm64+0x10015d864)\n\u003e     #7 0x000102f3fa14 in run_mod.llvm.1224844961852478530+0x120 (python3.14:arm64+0x10016ba14)\n\u003e     #8 0x000103063eb0 in pyrun_file+0xa0 (python3.14:arm64+0x10028feb0)\n\u003e     #9 0x000102dfe834 in _PyRun_SimpleFileObject+0xfc (python3.14:arm64+0x10002a834)\n\u003e     #10 0x00010306cb60 in _PyRun_AnyFileObject+0x4c (python3.14:arm64+0x100298b60)\n\u003e     #11 0x00010306ca54 in pymain_run_file_obj+0xa0 (python3.14:arm64+0x100298a54)\n\u003e     #12 0x000102ffb80c in pymain_run_file+0x44 (python3.14:arm64+0x10022780c)\n\u003e     #13 0x000102ff7d0c in Py_RunMain+0x46c (python3.14:arm64+0x100223d0c)\n\u003e     #14 0x000102fb0820 in pymain_main.llvm.7308572997346072671+0x1cc (python3.14:arm64+0x1001dc820)\n\u003e     #15 0x000102fb0644 in main+0x20 (python3.14:arm64+0x1001dc644)\n\u003e     #16 0x000194c86b94  (\u003cunknown module\u003e)\n\u003e \n\u003e SUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow (_imaging.cpython-314-darwin.so:arm64+0xb4680) in unpackP2L+0x17c\n\u003e Shadow bytes around the buggy address:\n\u003e   0x602000037080: fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa\n\u003e   0x602000037100: fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa\n\u003e   0x602000037180: fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa\n\u003e   0x602000037200: fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa\n\u003e   0x602000037280: fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa\n\u003e =\u003e0x602000037300: fa fa 00 fa fa fa 00 00 fa fa fd fa fa fa[04]fa\n\u003e   0x602000037380: fa fa 04 fa fa fa fd fd fa fa fd fd fa fa fd fd\n\u003e   0x602000037400: fa fa fd fd fa fa fd fd fa fa fd fa fa fa 00 fa\n\u003e   0x602000037480: fa fa 01 fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa\n\u003e   0x602000037500: fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa\n\u003e   0x602000037580: fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa\n\u003e Shadow byte legend (one shadow byte represents 8 application bytes):\n\u003e   Addressable:           00\n\u003e   Partially addressable: 01 02 03 04 05 06 07\n\u003e   Heap left redzone:       fa\n\u003e   Freed heap region:       fd\n\u003e   Stack left redzone:      f1\n\u003e   Stack mid redzone:       f2\n\u003e   Stack right redzone:     f3\n\u003e   Stack after return:      f5\n\u003e   Stack use after scope:   f8\n\u003e   Global redzone:          f9\n\u003e   Global init order:       f6\n\u003e   Poisoned by user:        f7\n\u003e   Container overflow:      fc\n\u003e   Array cookie:            ac\n\u003e   Intra object redzone:    bb\n\u003e   ASan internal:           fe\n\u003e   Left alloca redzone:     ca\n\u003e   Right alloca redzone:    cb\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e (To get it to scream differently about `unpackP4L`, comment out the P;2L case.)\n\u003e \n\u003e The above output is with `unpackP2L` and `unpackP4L` off 5eee92031a1986dfdaac7c78cdf0b764c0697118 lazily instrumented to print out what it's reading:\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ```patch\n\u003e diff --git a/src/libImaging/Unpack.c b/src/libImaging/Unpack.c\n\u003e index a51568209..4f867737a 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/Unpack.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/Unpack.c\n\u003e @@ -548,6 +548,8 @@ unpackP4(UINT8 *out, const UINT8 *in, int pixels) {\n\u003e      }\n\u003e  }\n\u003e  \n\u003e +#include \u003cstdio.h\u003e\n\u003e +\n\u003e  static void\n\u003e  unpackP2L(UINT8 *out, const UINT8 *in, int pixels) {\n\u003e      int i, j, m, s;\n\u003e @@ -555,6 +557,8 @@ unpackP2L(UINT8 *out, const UINT8 *in, int pixels) {\n\u003e      m = 128;\n\u003e      s = (pixels + 7) / 8;\n\u003e      for (i = j = 0; i \u003c pixels; i++) {\n\u003e +        fprintf(stderr, \"unpackP2L: in=%p i=%d j=%d pixels=%d reads %p %p\\n\",\n\u003e +            in, i, j, pixels, in + j, in + j + s);\n\u003e          out[i] = ((in[j] \u0026 m) ? 1 : 0) + ((in[j + s] \u0026 m) ? 2 : 0);\n\u003e          if ((m \u003e\u003e= 1) == 0) {\n\u003e              m = 128;\n\u003e @@ -570,6 +574,8 @@ unpackP4L(UINT8 *out, const UINT8 *in, int pixels) {\n\u003e      m = 128;\n\u003e      s = (pixels + 7) / 8;\n\u003e      for (i = j = 0; i \u003c pixels; i++) {\n\u003e +        fprintf(stderr, \"unpackP4L: in=%p i=%d j=%d pixels=%d reads %p %p %p %p\\n\",\n\u003e +            in, i, j, pixels, in + j, in + j + s, in + j + 2 * s, in + j + 3 * s);\n\u003e          out[i] = ((in[j] \u0026 m) ? 1 : 0) + ((in[j + s] \u0026 m) ? 2 : 0) +\n\u003e                   ((in[j + 2 * s] \u0026 m) ? 4 : 0) + ((in[j + 3 * s] \u0026 m) ? 8 : 0);\n\u003e          if ((m \u003e\u003e= 1) == 0) {\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Only a very specific use case is affected: `Image.frombytes()` or `Image.frombuffer()` calls of the shape above.\n\u003e The PCX decoder, which is the only Pillow-internal user of the P;2L and P;4L unpackers, already re-packs PCX rows into a format where overreads aren't an issue.\n\u003e \n\u003e The impact is that up to 1 byte (`P;2L`) or 3 bytes (`P;4L`) of heap memory following the caller's buffer are read and mixed into the pixel values of the last decoded row(s), one bit per affected plane per pixel. A crash is also possible if the read reaches unmapped memory.\n\u003e \n\u003e As far as I can tell, all published Pillow versions are affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Notes\n\u003e \n\u003e These findings were momentarily public (though in no detail) in https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/9723#issuecomment-5566006996; Andrew asked to retract the comment and post this instead.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-4558-3h8r-v8xp | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4327 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112394\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112394](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112394)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4327](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4327), [GHSA-4558-3h8r-v8xp](https://osv.dev/GHSA-4558-3h8r-v8xp), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4327](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4327)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e Pillow is a Python imaging library. Prior to 13.0.0, Pillow's P;2L and P;4L raw decoders can read past the end of an input buffer. ImagingRawDecode calculates a packed-pixel row size, while the unpackers read separately padded bit planes. A caller can supply a buffer and image dimensions whose accepted size is smaller than the unpacker's actual footprint. This can disclose up to three adjacent heap bytes in output pixels or crash the process. This issue is fixed in version 13.0.0.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-4558-3h8r-v8xp](https://osv.dev/GHSA-4558-3h8r-v8xp)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e On `main`, even with the guard in #9725, it's possible to have these decoders read 1 to 3 bytes past the end of the passed buffer with a suitable width/height.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImagingRawDecode` and the `P;2L` / `P;4L` unpackers disagree about how many bytes a line of data occupies.\n\u003e \n\u003e The raw decoder derives the line size from the bits per pixel\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e state-\u003ebytes = (state-\u003exsize * state-\u003ebits + 7) / 8;\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e and uses it both for the bounds check and to advance to the next line. For `P;2L` and `P;4L`, `bits` is 2 and 4 respectively.\n\u003e \n\u003e But these two rawmodes are not packed pixels but 2 or 4 *bit planes*, each padded to a whole number of bytes. `unpackP2L` computes `s = (pixels + 7) / 8` and reads plane *k* at offset `k * s`, and `unpackP4L` does the same across four planes. Their real footprint is therefore `planes * ((xsize + 7) / 8)`.\n\u003e \n\u003e For some widths, padding each plane separately makes this footprint larger than `state-\u003ebytes`. For example, at width 1, the decoder accounts for one byte, while the unpackers read two or four bytes. The decoder accepts data that is too short for the last line and on the other hand advances to subsequent lines by less than a line, so every line after the first is also read from the wrong offset.\n\u003e \n\u003e #9725's check is misshapen for this:\n\u003e \n\u003e * It can reject data of the right length: 8x1 `P;2L` image is 2 bytes but is not accepted.\n\u003e * It still accepts buffers shorter than the unpackers require for a given image shape.\n\u003e * It measures the length with `len(data)`, which is an element count rather than a byte count; e.g. `memoryview(array(\"I\", [0,0,0,0,0]))` (20 bytes) is rejected while `mv.tobytes()` is accepted.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC ( `ghsa_p2l_p4l.py`)\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from array import array\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e # 1 byte past\n\u003e Image.frombytes(\"P\", (1, 4), array(\"B\", [0] * 4), \"raw\", \"P;2L\")\n\u003e \n\u003e # 3 bytes past\n\u003e Image.frombytes(\"P\", (1, 8), array(\"B\", [0] * 8), \"raw\", \"P;4L\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e is the almost-simplest reproducer.\n\u003e \n\u003e (`array` is needed to get ASan to reliably scream, but the overread is observable with e.g. bytes literals. Python allocates `bytes`es with an extra trailing null byte, from an arena by default to boot, so ASan doesn't see the overread.)\n\u003e \n\u003e On my Mac, I can get ASan to scream about this with\n\u003e \n\u003e ```bash\n\u003e export asan=$(clang -print-file-name=libclang_rt.asan_osx_dynamic.dylib)\n\u003e env CFLAGS=\"-fsanitize=address -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g -O1\" LDFLAGS=\"-fsanitize=address\" uv pip install -e .\n\u003e env DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES=$asan PYTHONMALLOC=malloc ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 python3 ghsa_p2l_p4l.py\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e like so:\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e python3(29144,0x202fea2c0) malloc: nano zone abandoned due to inability to reserve vm space.\n\u003e unpackP2L: in=0x602000037370 i=0 j=0 pixels=1 reads 0x602000037370 0x602000037371\n\u003e unpackP2L: in=0x602000037371 i=0 j=0 pixels=1 reads 0x602000037371 0x602000037372\n\u003e unpackP2L: in=0x602000037372 i=0 j=0 pixels=1 reads 0x602000037372 0x602000037373\n\u003e unpackP2L: in=0x602000037373 i=0 j=0 pixels=1 reads 0x602000037373 0x602000037374\n\u003e =================================================================\n\u003e ==29144==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow on address 0x602000037374 at pc 0x00010778c684 bp 0x00016d029f40 sp 0x00016d029f38\n\u003e READ of size 1 at 0x602000037374 thread T0\n\u003e     #0 0x00010778c680 in unpackP2L+0x17c (_imaging.cpython-314-darwin.so:arm64+0xb4680)\n\u003e     #1 0x000107765360 in ImagingRawDecode+0x348 (_imaging.cpython-314-darwin.so:arm64+0x8d360)\n\u003e     #2 0x0001076f259c in _decode+0x15c (_imaging.cpython-314-darwin.so:arm64+0x1a59c)\n\u003e     #3 0x0001030d921c in method_vectorcall_VARARGS.llvm.8905850704545875072+0x84 (python3.14:arm64+0x10030521c)\n\u003e     #4 0x000102e585b8 in _TAIL_CALL_CALL.llvm.12785051352303791014+0x13c (python3.14:arm64+0x1000845b8)\n\u003e     #5 0x000102f1c17c in _PyEval_Vector+0x308 (python3.14:arm64+0x10014817c)\n\u003e     #6 0x000102f31864 in PyEval_EvalCode+0x9c (python3.14:arm64+0x10015d864)\n\u003e     #7 0x000102f3fa14 in run_mod.llvm.1224844961852478530+0x120 (python3.14:arm64+0x10016ba14)\n\u003e     #8 0x000103063eb0 in pyrun_file+0xa0 (python3.14:arm64+0x10028feb0)\n\u003e     #9 0x000102dfe834 in _PyRun_SimpleFileObject+0xfc (python3.14:arm64+0x10002a834)\n\u003e     #10 0x00010306cb60 in _PyRun_AnyFileObject+0x4c (python3.14:arm64+0x100298b60)\n\u003e     #11 0x00010306ca54 in pymain_run_file_obj+0xa0 (python3.14:arm64+0x100298a54)\n\u003e     #12 0x000102ffb80c in pymain_run_file+0x44 (python3.14:arm64+0x10022780c)\n\u003e     #13 0x000102ff7d0c in Py_RunMain+0x46c (python3.14:arm64+0x100223d0c)\n\u003e     #14 0x000102fb0820 in pymain_main.llvm.7308572997346072671+0x1cc (python3.14:arm64+0x1001dc820)\n\u003e     #15 0x000102fb0644 in main+0x20 (python3.14:arm64+0x1001dc644)\n\u003e     #16 0x000194c86b94  (\u003cunknown module\u003e)\n\u003e \n\u003e 0x602000037374 is located 0 bytes after 4-byte region [0x602000037370,0x602000037374)\n\u003e allocated by thread T0 here:\n\u003e     #0 0x0001044a130c in malloc+0x78 (libclang_rt.asan_osx_dynamic.dylib:arm64e+0x3d30c)\n\u003e     #1 0x000102f22324 in newarrayobject+0x6c (python3.14:arm64+0x10014e324)\n\u003e     #2 0x000102f21b4c in array_new+0x320 (python3.14:arm64+0x10014db4c)\n\u003e     #3 0x0001030fc390 in type_call+0x5c (python3.14:arm64+0x100328390)\n\u003e     #4 0x000102e58ae4 in _TAIL_CALL_CALL.llvm.12785051352303791014+0x668 (python3.14:arm64+0x100084ae4)\n\u003e     #5 0x000102f1c17c in _PyEval_Vector+0x308 (python3.14:arm64+0x10014817c)\n\u003e     #6 0x000102f31864 in PyEval_EvalCode+0x9c (python3.14:arm64+0x10015d864)\n\u003e     #7 0x000102f3fa14 in run_mod.llvm.1224844961852478530+0x120 (python3.14:arm64+0x10016ba14)\n\u003e     #8 0x000103063eb0 in pyrun_file+0xa0 (python3.14:arm64+0x10028feb0)\n\u003e     #9 0x000102dfe834 in _PyRun_SimpleFileObject+0xfc (python3.14:arm64+0x10002a834)\n\u003e     #10 0x00010306cb60 in _PyRun_AnyFileObject+0x4c (python3.14:arm64+0x100298b60)\n\u003e     #11 0x00010306ca54 in pymain_run_file_obj+0xa0 (python3.14:arm64+0x100298a54)\n\u003e     #12 0x000102ffb80c in pymain_run_file+0x44 (python3.14:arm64+0x10022780c)\n\u003e     #13 0x000102ff7d0c in Py_RunMain+0x46c (python3.14:arm64+0x100223d0c)\n\u003e     #14 0x000102fb0820 in pymain_main.llvm.7308572997346072671+0x1cc (python3.14:arm64+0x1001dc820)\n\u003e     #15 0x000102fb0644 in main+0x20 (python3.14:arm64+0x1001dc644)\n\u003e     #16 0x000194c86b94  (\u003cunknown module\u003e)\n\u003e \n\u003e SUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow (_imaging.cpython-314-darwin.so:arm64+0xb4680) in unpackP2L+0x17c\n\u003e Shadow bytes around the buggy address:\n\u003e   0x602000037080: fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa\n\u003e   0x602000037100: fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa\n\u003e   0x602000037180: fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa\n\u003e   0x602000037200: fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa\n\u003e   0x602000037280: fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa fa fa fd fa\n\u003e =\u003e0x602000037300: fa fa 00 fa fa fa 00 00 fa fa fd fa fa fa[04]fa\n\u003e   0x602000037380: fa fa 04 fa fa fa fd fd fa fa fd fd fa fa fd fd\n\u003e   0x602000037400: fa fa fd fd fa fa fd fd fa fa fd fa fa fa 00 fa\n\u003e   0x602000037480: fa fa 01 fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa\n\u003e   0x602000037500: fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa\n\u003e   0x602000037580: fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa fa\n\u003e Shadow byte legend (one shadow byte represents 8 application bytes):\n\u003e   Addressable:           00\n\u003e   Partially addressable: 01 02 03 04 05 06 07\n\u003e   Heap left redzone:       fa\n\u003e   Freed heap region:       fd\n\u003e   Stack left redzone:      f1\n\u003e   Stack mid redzone:       f2\n\u003e   Stack right redzone:     f3\n\u003e   Stack after return:      f5\n\u003e   Stack use after scope:   f8\n\u003e   Global redzone:          f9\n\u003e   Global init order:       f6\n\u003e   Poisoned by user:        f7\n\u003e   Container overflow:      fc\n\u003e   Array cookie:            ac\n\u003e   Intra object redzone:    bb\n\u003e   ASan internal:           fe\n\u003e   Left alloca redzone:     ca\n\u003e   Right alloca redzone:    cb\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e (To get it to scream differently about `unpackP4L`, comment out the P;2L case.)\n\u003e \n\u003e The above output is with `unpackP2L` and `unpackP4L` off 5eee92031a1986dfdaac7c78cdf0b764c0697118 lazily instrumented to print out what it's reading:\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ```patch\n\u003e diff --git a/src/libImaging/Unpack.c b/src/libImaging/Unpack.c\n\u003e index a51568209..4f867737a 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/Unpack.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/Unpack.c\n\u003e @@ -548,6 +548,8 @@ unpackP4(UINT8 *out, const UINT8 *in, int pixels) {\n\u003e      }\n\u003e  }\n\u003e  \n\u003e +#include \u003cstdio.h\u003e\n\u003e +\n\u003e  static void\n\u003e  unpackP2L(UINT8 *out, const UINT8 *in, int pixels) {\n\u003e      int i, j, m, s;\n\u003e @@ -555,6 +557,8 @@ unpackP2L(UINT8 *out, const UINT8 *in, int pixels) {\n\u003e      m = 128;\n\u003e      s = (pixels + 7) / 8;\n\u003e      for (i = j = 0; i \u003c pixels; i++) {\n\u003e +        fprintf(stderr, \"unpackP2L: in=%p i=%d j=%d pixels=%d reads %p %p\\n\",\n\u003e +            in, i, j, pixels, in + j, in + j + s);\n\u003e          out[i] = ((in[j] \u0026 m) ? 1 : 0) + ((in[j + s] \u0026 m) ? 2 : 0);\n\u003e          if ((m \u003e\u003e= 1) == 0) {\n\u003e              m = 128;\n\u003e @@ -570,6 +574,8 @@ unpackP4L(UINT8 *out, const UINT8 *in, int pixels) {\n\u003e      m = 128;\n\u003e      s = (pixels + 7) / 8;\n\u003e      for (i = j = 0; i \u003c pixels; i++) {\n\u003e +        fprintf(stderr, \"unpackP4L: in=%p i=%d j=%d pixels=%d reads %p %p %p %p\\n\",\n\u003e +            in, i, j, pixels, in + j, in + j + s, in + j + 2 * s, in + j + 3 * s);\n\u003e          out[i] = ((in[j] \u0026 m) ? 1 : 0) + ((in[j + s] \u0026 m) ? 2 : 0) +\n\u003e                   ((in[j + 2 * s] \u0026 m) ? 4 : 0) + ((in[j + 3 * s] \u0026 m) ? 8 : 0);\n\u003e          if ((m \u003e\u003e= 1) == 0) {\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Only a very specific use case is affected: `Image.frombytes()` or `Image.frombuffer()` calls of the shape above.\n\u003e The PCX decoder, which is the only Pillow-internal user of the P;2L and P;4L unpackers, already re-packs PCX rows into a format where overreads aren't an issue.\n\u003e \n\u003e The impact is that up to 1 byte (`P;2L`) or 3 bytes (`P;4L`) of heap memory following the caller's buffer are read and mixed into the pixel values of the last decoded row(s), one bit per affected plane per pixel. A crash is also possible if the read reaches unmapped memory.\n\u003e \n\u003e As far as I can tell, all published Pillow versions are affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Notes\n\u003e \n\u003e These findings were momentarily public (though in no detail) in https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/9723#issuecomment-5566006996; Andrew asked to retract the comment and post this instead.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-4558-3h8r-v8xp | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4327 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112394\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
