              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\nPillow's libImaging `_decodeTile` decoder, used to decode tiled TIFF images, allocates a per-tile buffer using `realloc(state-\u003ebuffer, TIFFTileSize(tiff))`. After each `TIFFReadTile` it then iterates `tile_y` from 0 up to `current_tile_length` and calls the configured row unpacker (here `copy4`) with the source pointer `state-\u003ebuffer + tile_y * row_byte_size`. When libtiff returns a `tile_bytes_size` smaller than the worst-case `row_byte_size * tile_length`, the inner stride-and-copy loop walks past the realloc'd region and `copy4` performs a 1024-byte `memcpy` that reads up to `(tile_length-1) * row_byte_size + row_byte_size` bytes from a buffer sized only to `tile_bytes_size`. The supplied PoC TIFF triggers a 1024-byte over-read exactly 0 bytes past a 196608-byte allocation. An attacker who can convince a Pillow-using application to decode a crafted TIFF can trigger a process crash (DoS) and may cause adjacent heap memory to be copied into the decoded pixel buffer.\n\n### Details\n\nPillow's TIFF decoder, when libtiff reports the file is tiled, takes the `_decodeTile` path. The function:\n\n1. Asks libtiff for `TIFFTileSize(tiff)` (the bytes-per-tile) and `TIFFTileRowSize(tiff)` (the bytes per row *as stored by libtiff*).\n2. Reallocates `state-\u003ebuffer` to exactly `tile_bytes_size`.\n3. For each tile origin `(x, y)` and each plane, calls `TIFFReadTile` into `state-\u003ebuffer`, then walks the tile row by row, calling the configured `ImagingShuffler` (here `copy4`) on each row's worth of source bytes.\n\nThe validation done before the loop is:\n\n```c\nif (tile_bytes_size \u003e ((tile_length * state-\u003ebits / planes + 7) / 8) * tile_width) {\n    state-\u003eerrcode = IMAGING_CODEC_BROKEN;\n    return -1;\n}\n```\n\nThis rejects oversized libtiff tiles, but it does **not** reject *undersized* ones — i.e. the case where libtiff returns a `tile_bytes_size` strictly **less** than `row_byte_size * tile_length`. The loop body, however, indexes into the buffer assuming the buffer is at least `row_byte_size * tile_length` bytes long.\n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\n```c\n// src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:446-490 — _decodeTile\nnew_data = realloc(state-\u003ebuffer, state-\u003ebytes);   // bytes = tile_bytes_size\nif (!new_data) { ... }\nstate-\u003ebuffer = new_data;\n\nfor (y = state-\u003eyoff; y \u003c state-\u003eysize; y += tile_length) {\n    for (plane = 0; plane \u003c planes; plane++) {\n        ImagingShuffler shuffler = unpackers[plane];\n        for (x = state-\u003exoff; x \u003c state-\u003exsize; x += tile_width) {\n            if (TIFFReadTile(tiff, (tdata_t)state-\u003ebuffer, x, y, 0, plane) == -1) { ... }\n\n            current_tile_width  = min((INT32)tile_width,  state-\u003exsize - x);\n            current_tile_length = min((INT32)tile_length, state-\u003eysize - y);\n            for (tile_y = 0; tile_y \u003c current_tile_length; tile_y++) {\n                shuffler(\n                    (UINT8 *)im-\u003eimage[tile_y + y] + x * im-\u003epixelsize,\n                    state-\u003ebuffer + tile_y * row_byte_size,   // \u003c-- OOB stride\n                    current_tile_width\n                );\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```c\n// src/libImaging/Unpack.c:1288-1291 — copy4 (the crash site)\nstatic void\ncopy4(UINT8 *out, const UINT8 *in, int pixels) {\n    /* RGBA, CMYK quadruples */\n    memcpy(out, in, 4 * pixels);   // \u003c-- ASAN-detected READ here\n}\n```\n\nThe PoC's tile metadata yields `tile_width = tile_length = 256`, `row_byte_size = 1024` (4 bytes/pixel × 256 pixels), and `tile_bytes_size = 196608` (which is 256·256·3 = three-channel-byte storage). The realloc'd buffer is 196608 bytes. The unpacker iterates `tile_y` from 0 to 255 and for the highest rows the source pointer `state-\u003ebuffer + tile_y * 1024` already lies at or past offset 196608 — the very last row's `memcpy(in=buffer+255·1024, 1024)` reads 1024 bytes starting `260096 - 196608 = 63488` bytes past the allocation; ASAN flags the very first byte past the heap region (`READ of size 1024 at … 0 bytes after 196608-byte region`).\n\nThe mismatch is real: libtiff's `TIFFTileSize` returns the *packed* per-tile storage (which depends on the file's photometric/separation layout — it can legitimately be smaller than `row_byte_size × tile_length`), while the inner loop strides as if the buffer always equals `row_byte_size × tile_length`.\n\n\n## Attribution\n\nThis issue was found by Anthropic from using Claude to study security of open source projects, and I am from Ada Logics helping validate the found issues and report to maintainers. The data in this report has been reviewed manually.",
                "text": "### Summary\n\nPillow's libImaging `_decodeTile` decoder, used to decode tiled TIFF images, allocates a per-tile buffer using `realloc(state-\u003ebuffer, TIFFTileSize(tiff))`. After each `TIFFReadTile` it then iterates `tile_y` from 0 up to `current_tile_length` and calls the configured row unpacker (here `copy4`) with the source pointer `state-\u003ebuffer + tile_y * row_byte_size`. When libtiff returns a `tile_bytes_size` smaller than the worst-case `row_byte_size * tile_length`, the inner stride-and-copy loop walks past the realloc'd region and `copy4` performs a 1024-byte `memcpy` that reads up to `(tile_length-1) * row_byte_size + row_byte_size` bytes from a buffer sized only to `tile_bytes_size`. The supplied PoC TIFF triggers a 1024-byte over-read exactly 0 bytes past a 196608-byte allocation. An attacker who can convince a Pillow-using application to decode a crafted TIFF can trigger a process crash (DoS) and may cause adjacent heap memory to be copied into the decoded pixel buffer.\n\n### Details\n\nPillow's TIFF decoder, when libtiff reports the file is tiled, takes the `_decodeTile` path. The function:\n\n1. Asks libtiff for `TIFFTileSize(tiff)` (the bytes-per-tile) and `TIFFTileRowSize(tiff)` (the bytes per row *as stored by libtiff*).\n2. Reallocates `state-\u003ebuffer` to exactly `tile_bytes_size`.\n3. For each tile origin `(x, y)` and each plane, calls `TIFFReadTile` into `state-\u003ebuffer`, then walks the tile row by row, calling the configured `ImagingShuffler` (here `copy4`) on each row's worth of source bytes.\n\nThe validation done before the loop is:\n\n```c\nif (tile_bytes_size \u003e ((tile_length * state-\u003ebits / planes + 7) / 8) * tile_width) {\n    state-\u003eerrcode = IMAGING_CODEC_BROKEN;\n    return -1;\n}\n```\n\nThis rejects oversized libtiff tiles, but it does **not** reject *undersized* ones — i.e. the case where libtiff returns a `tile_bytes_size` strictly **less** than `row_byte_size * tile_length`. The loop body, however, indexes into the buffer assuming the buffer is at least `row_byte_size * tile_length` bytes long.\n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\n```c\n// src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:446-490 — _decodeTile\nnew_data = realloc(state-\u003ebuffer, state-\u003ebytes);   // bytes = tile_bytes_size\nif (!new_data) { ... }\nstate-\u003ebuffer = new_data;\n\nfor (y = state-\u003eyoff; y \u003c state-\u003eysize; y += tile_length) {\n    for (plane = 0; plane \u003c planes; plane++) {\n        ImagingShuffler shuffler = unpackers[plane];\n        for (x = state-\u003exoff; x \u003c state-\u003exsize; x += tile_width) {\n            if (TIFFReadTile(tiff, (tdata_t)state-\u003ebuffer, x, y, 0, plane) == -1) { ... }\n\n            current_tile_width  = min((INT32)tile_width,  state-\u003exsize - x);\n            current_tile_length = min((INT32)tile_length, state-\u003eysize - y);\n            for (tile_y = 0; tile_y \u003c current_tile_length; tile_y++) {\n                shuffler(\n                    (UINT8 *)im-\u003eimage[tile_y + y] + x * im-\u003epixelsize,\n                    state-\u003ebuffer + tile_y * row_byte_size,   // \u003c-- OOB stride\n                    current_tile_width\n                );\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```c\n// src/libImaging/Unpack.c:1288-1291 — copy4 (the crash site)\nstatic void\ncopy4(UINT8 *out, const UINT8 *in, int pixels) {\n    /* RGBA, CMYK quadruples */\n    memcpy(out, in, 4 * pixels);   // \u003c-- ASAN-detected READ here\n}\n```\n\nThe PoC's tile metadata yields `tile_width = tile_length = 256`, `row_byte_size = 1024` (4 bytes/pixel × 256 pixels), and `tile_bytes_size = 196608` (which is 256·256·3 = three-channel-byte storage). The realloc'd buffer is 196608 bytes. The unpacker iterates `tile_y` from 0 to 255 and for the highest rows the source pointer `state-\u003ebuffer + tile_y * 1024` already lies at or past offset 196608 — the very last row's `memcpy(in=buffer+255·1024, 1024)` reads 1024 bytes starting `260096 - 196608 = 63488` bytes past the allocation; ASAN flags the very first byte past the heap region (`READ of size 1024 at … 0 bytes after 196608-byte region`).\n\nThe mismatch is real: libtiff's `TIFFTileSize` returns the *packed* per-tile storage (which depends on the file's photometric/separation layout — it can legitimately be smaller than `row_byte_size × tile_length`), while the inner loop strides as if the buffer always equals `row_byte_size × tile_length`.\n\n\n## Attribution\n\nThis issue was found by Anthropic from using Claude to study security of open source projects, and I am from Ada Logics helping validate the found issues and report to maintainers. The data in this report has been reviewed manually."
