            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-96641",
                "GHSA-4rm8-3hmm-39fg"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\nAffected Object and Source\n\nPackage: Pillow 12.3.0\nSource: PyPI sdist pillow-12.3.0.tar.gz\nSHA-256: 3b8182a766685eaa002637e28b4ec8d6b18819a0c71f579bf0dbaa5830297cce\nSink: src/_imaging.c:2809:15, xsize += dx\n\n### Details\nThe code in Pillow 12.3.0 is:\n```c\nfor (xsize = 0; *text; text++) {\n    int dx = self-\u003eglyphs[*text].dx;\n    if (dx \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 xsize \u003e INT_MAX - dx) {\n        PyErr_SetString(PyExc_OverflowError, \"Width too large\");\n        return -1;\n    }\n    xsize += dx;\n}\n```\nThe PILfont glyph advance is parsed from a signed 16-bit field, so the valid range for `dx` is `[-32768, 32767]`. The existing check only proves:\n`dx \u003e 0 =\u003e xsize \u003c= INT_MAX - dx`\nIt does **not** prove the other half of the contract:\n`dx \u003c 0 =\u003e xsize \u003e= INT_MIN - dx`\nTaking `dx = -32768` and repeating it 65,537 times, the mathematical sum is `-2,147,516,416`, which is less than `INT_MIN = -2,147,483,648`. On the 65,537th addition, the operation `-2147483648 + -32768` is performed, resulting in **C signed overflow undefined behavior**.\n\n### PoC\n```python\n\"\"\"Minimal public-API reproducer for the Pillow 12.3.0 textwidth underflow.\n\nRun with an ordinary Pillow 12.3.0 build to observe the wrapped width, or with\nthe PyC-Contract UBSan build to obtain the exact native diagnostic.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport struct\nfrom io import BytesIO\n\nimport PIL\nfrom PIL import Image, ImageFont\n\n\nADVANCE = -(2**15)\nCONTROL_REPEAT = 65_536\nWITNESS_REPEAT = 65_537\n\n\ndef bitmap_font(advance: int) -\u003e ImageFont.ImageFont:\n    metrics = bytearray(256 * 20)\n    glyph_offset = ord(\"A\") * 20\n    metrics[glyph_offset : glyph_offset + 2] = struct.pack(\"\u003eh\", advance)\n    font_data = b\"PILfont\\n;;;;;;1;\\nDATA\\n\" + bytes(metrics)\n    font = ImageFont.ImageFont()\n    font._load_pilfont_data(BytesIO(font_data), Image.new(\"1\", (1, 1)))\n    return font\n\n\ndef main() -\u003e None:\n    font = bitmap_font(ADVANCE)\n    print(f\"Pillow={PIL.__version__}\")\n    print(f\"control_math={ADVANCE * CONTROL_REPEAT}\")\n    print(f\"control_return={font.getlength('A' * CONTROL_REPEAT)}\")\n    print(f\"witness_math={ADVANCE * WITNESS_REPEAT}\")\n    print(f\"witness_return={font.getlength('A' * WITNESS_REPEAT)}\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nObservations from a regular 12.3.0 release build:\n\n```text\nPillow=12.3.0\ncontrol_math=-2147483648\ncontrol_return=0\nwitness_math=-2147516416\nwitness_return=2147450880\n```\n\nThe adjacent control uses 65,536 repetitions, producing a mathematical result of exactly `INT_MIN`, and the function subsequently returns 0 according to existing semantics. Adding just a single character flips the return value to `2,147,450,880`.\n\n\n### Impact\nThe directly confirmed impact is width semantic integrity violation: getlength() and boundary calculations based on getsize() may return a huge positive value, while the true mathematical width is negative. The high-level getmask() typically passes through the decompression-bomb check, which mitigates the risk of direct massive allocation; however, internal font objects and applications that use the width for layout, quotas, serialization, or subsequent allocations may be affected by resource consumption or logic errors.\n\nTherefore, the current recommended severity rating is \"Reachable native integer undefined behavior, with potential DoS/logic integrity impact; specific security severity to be confirmed by the upstream threat model\", rather than a memory corruption vulnerability.\n\n### Recommended Patch and Regression Tests\n\nIt is recommended to extend the overflow check to cover both directions:\n```c\nif ((dx \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 xsize \u003e INT_MAX - dx) ||\n    (dx \u003c 0 \u0026\u0026 xsize \u003c INT_MIN - dx)) {\n    PyErr_SetString(PyExc_OverflowError, \"Width too large\");\n    return -1;\n}\n```\nAt a minimum, four test cases should be added:\n1. `dx = 32767, repeat = 65538` — upper adjacent control (should succeed);\n2. `dx = 32767, repeat = 65539` — should raise `OverflowError`;\n3. `dx = -32768, repeat = 65536` — lower adjacent control (should succeed);\n4. `dx = -32768, repeat = 65537` — should raise `OverflowError`.\n\nAdditionally, it should be verified that exception propagation is consistent across `getlength()`, `getbbox()`, `getmask()`, and the underlying `font.getsize()`.",
                "text": "### Summary\nAffected Object and Source\n\nPackage: Pillow 12.3.0\nSource: PyPI sdist pillow-12.3.0.tar.gz\nSHA-256: 3b8182a766685eaa002637e28b4ec8d6b18819a0c71f579bf0dbaa5830297cce\nSink: src/_imaging.c:2809:15, xsize += dx\n\n### Details\nThe code in Pillow 12.3.0 is:\n```c\nfor (xsize = 0; *text; text++) {\n    int dx = self-\u003eglyphs[*text].dx;\n    if (dx \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 xsize \u003e INT_MAX - dx) {\n        PyErr_SetString(PyExc_OverflowError, \"Width too large\");\n        return -1;\n    }\n    xsize += dx;\n}\n```\nThe PILfont glyph advance is parsed from a signed 16-bit field, so the valid range for `dx` is `[-32768, 32767]`. The existing check only proves:\n`dx \u003e 0 =\u003e xsize \u003c= INT_MAX - dx`\nIt does **not** prove the other half of the contract:\n`dx \u003c 0 =\u003e xsize \u003e= INT_MIN - dx`\nTaking `dx = -32768` and repeating it 65,537 times, the mathematical sum is `-2,147,516,416`, which is less than `INT_MIN = -2,147,483,648`. On the 65,537th addition, the operation `-2147483648 + -32768` is performed, resulting in **C signed overflow undefined behavior**.\n\n### PoC\n```python\n\"\"\"Minimal public-API reproducer for the Pillow 12.3.0 textwidth underflow.\n\nRun with an ordinary Pillow 12.3.0 build to observe the wrapped width, or with\nthe PyC-Contract UBSan build to obtain the exact native diagnostic.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport struct\nfrom io import BytesIO\n\nimport PIL\nfrom PIL import Image, ImageFont\n\n\nADVANCE = -(2**15)\nCONTROL_REPEAT = 65_536\nWITNESS_REPEAT = 65_537\n\n\ndef bitmap_font(advance: int) -\u003e ImageFont.ImageFont:\n    metrics = bytearray(256 * 20)\n    glyph_offset = ord(\"A\") * 20\n    metrics[glyph_offset : glyph_offset + 2] = struct.pack(\"\u003eh\", advance)\n    font_data = b\"PILfont\\n;;;;;;1;\\nDATA\\n\" + bytes(metrics)\n    font = ImageFont.ImageFont()\n    font._load_pilfont_data(BytesIO(font_data), Image.new(\"1\", (1, 1)))\n    return font\n\n\ndef main() -\u003e None:\n    font = bitmap_font(ADVANCE)\n    print(f\"Pillow={PIL.__version__}\")\n    print(f\"control_math={ADVANCE * CONTROL_REPEAT}\")\n    print(f\"control_return={font.getlength('A' * CONTROL_REPEAT)}\")\n    print(f\"witness_math={ADVANCE * WITNESS_REPEAT}\")\n    print(f\"witness_return={font.getlength('A' * WITNESS_REPEAT)}\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nObservations from a regular 12.3.0 release build:\n\n```text\nPillow=12.3.0\ncontrol_math=-2147483648\ncontrol_return=0\nwitness_math=-2147516416\nwitness_return=2147450880\n```\n\nThe adjacent control uses 65,536 repetitions, producing a mathematical result of exactly `INT_MIN`, and the function subsequently returns 0 according to existing semantics. Adding just a single character flips the return value to `2,147,450,880`.\n\n\n### Impact\nThe directly confirmed impact is width semantic integrity violation: getlength() and boundary calculations based on getsize() may return a huge positive value, while the true mathematical width is negative. The high-level getmask() typically passes through the decompression-bomb check, which mitigates the risk of direct massive allocation; however, internal font objects and applications that use the width for layout, quotas, serialization, or subsequent allocations may be affected by resource consumption or logic errors.\n\nTherefore, the current recommended severity rating is \"Reachable native integer undefined behavior, with potential DoS/logic integrity impact; specific security severity to be confirmed by the upstream threat model\", rather than a memory corruption vulnerability.\n\n### Recommended Patch and Regression Tests\n\nIt is recommended to extend the overflow check to cover both directions:\n```c\nif ((dx \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 xsize \u003e INT_MAX - dx) ||\n    (dx \u003c 0 \u0026\u0026 xsize \u003c INT_MIN - dx)) {\n    PyErr_SetString(PyExc_OverflowError, \"Width too large\");\n    return -1;\n}\n```\nAt a minimum, four test cases should be added:\n1. `dx = 32767, repeat = 65538` — upper adjacent control (should succeed);\n2. `dx = 32767, repeat = 65539` — should raise `OverflowError`;\n3. `dx = -32768, repeat = 65536` — lower adjacent control (should succeed);\n4. `dx = -32768, repeat = 65537` — should raise `OverflowError`.\n\nAdditionally, it should be verified that exception propagation is consistent across `getlength()`, `getbbox()`, `getmask()`, and the underlying `font.getsize()`."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-96641](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-96641)**.\n\n## [GHSA-4rm8-3hmm-39fg](https://osv.dev/GHSA-4rm8-3hmm-39fg)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e Affected Object and Source\n\u003e \n\u003e Package: Pillow 12.3.0\n\u003e Source: PyPI sdist pillow-12.3.0.tar.gz\n\u003e SHA-256: 3b8182a766685eaa002637e28b4ec8d6b18819a0c71f579bf0dbaa5830297cce\n\u003e Sink: src/_imaging.c:2809:15, xsize += dx\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e The code in Pillow 12.3.0 is:\n\u003e ```c\n\u003e for (xsize = 0; *text; text++) {\n\u003e     int dx = self-\u003eglyphs[*text].dx;\n\u003e     if (dx \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 xsize \u003e INT_MAX - dx) {\n\u003e         PyErr_SetString(PyExc_OverflowError, \"Width too large\");\n\u003e         return -1;\n\u003e     }\n\u003e     xsize += dx;\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e The PILfont glyph advance is parsed from a signed 16-bit field, so the valid range for `dx` is `[-32768, 32767]`. The existing check only proves:\n\u003e `dx \u003e 0 =\u003e xsize \u003c= INT_MAX - dx`\n\u003e It does **not** prove the other half of the contract:\n\u003e `dx \u003c 0 =\u003e xsize \u003e= INT_MIN - dx`\n\u003e Taking `dx = -32768` and repeating it 65,537 times, the mathematical sum is `-2,147,516,416`, which is less than `INT_MIN = -2,147,483,648`. On the 65,537th addition, the operation `-2147483648 + -32768` is performed, resulting in **C signed overflow undefined behavior**.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e ```python\n\u003e \"\"\"Minimal public-API reproducer for the Pillow 12.3.0 textwidth underflow.\n\u003e \n\u003e Run with an ordinary Pillow 12.3.0 build to observe the wrapped width, or with\n\u003e the PyC-Contract UBSan build to obtain the exact native diagnostic.\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e from __future__ import annotations\n\u003e \n\u003e import struct\n\u003e from io import BytesIO\n\u003e \n\u003e import PIL\n\u003e from PIL import Image, ImageFont\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ADVANCE = -(2**15)\n\u003e CONTROL_REPEAT = 65_536\n\u003e WITNESS_REPEAT = 65_537\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def bitmap_font(advance: int) -\u003e ImageFont.ImageFont:\n\u003e     metrics = bytearray(256 * 20)\n\u003e     glyph_offset = ord(\"A\") * 20\n\u003e     metrics[glyph_offset : glyph_offset + 2] = struct.pack(\"\u003eh\", advance)\n\u003e     font_data = b\"PILfont\\n;;;;;;1;\\nDATA\\n\" + bytes(metrics)\n\u003e     font = ImageFont.ImageFont()\n\u003e     font._load_pilfont_data(BytesIO(font_data), Image.new(\"1\", (1, 1)))\n\u003e     return font\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def main() -\u003e None:\n\u003e     font = bitmap_font(ADVANCE)\n\u003e     print(f\"Pillow={PIL.__version__}\")\n\u003e     print(f\"control_math={ADVANCE * CONTROL_REPEAT}\")\n\u003e     print(f\"control_return={font.getlength('A' * CONTROL_REPEAT)}\")\n\u003e     print(f\"witness_math={ADVANCE * WITNESS_REPEAT}\")\n\u003e     print(f\"witness_return={font.getlength('A' * WITNESS_REPEAT)}\")\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n\u003e     main()\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Observations from a regular 12.3.0 release build:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e Pillow=12.3.0\n\u003e control_math=-2147483648\n\u003e control_return=0\n\u003e witness_math=-2147516416\n\u003e witness_return=2147450880\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The adjacent control uses 65,536 repetitions, producing a mathematical result of exactly `INT_MIN`, and the function subsequently returns 0 according to existing semantics. Adding just a single character flips the return value to `2,147,450,880`.\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e The directly confirmed impact is width semantic integrity violation: getlength() and boundary calculations based on getsize() may return a huge positive value, while the true mathematical width is negative. The high-level getmask() typically passes through the decompression-bomb check, which mitigates the risk of direct massive allocation; however, internal font objects and applications that use the width for layout, quotas, serialization, or subsequent allocations may be affected by resource consumption or logic errors.\n\u003e \n\u003e Therefore, the current recommended severity rating is \"Reachable native integer undefined behavior, with potential DoS/logic integrity impact; specific security severity to be confirmed by the upstream threat model\", rather than a memory corruption vulnerability.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Recommended Patch and Regression Tests\n\u003e \n\u003e It is recommended to extend the overflow check to cover both directions:\n\u003e ```c\n\u003e if ((dx \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 xsize \u003e INT_MAX - dx) ||\n\u003e     (dx \u003c 0 \u0026\u0026 xsize \u003c INT_MIN - dx)) {\n\u003e     PyErr_SetString(PyExc_OverflowError, \"Width too large\");\n\u003e     return -1;\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e At a minimum, four test cases should be added:\n\u003e 1. `dx = 32767, repeat = 65538` — upper adjacent control (should succeed);\n\u003e 2. `dx = 32767, repeat = 65539` — should raise `OverflowError`;\n\u003e 3. `dx = -32768, repeat = 65536` — lower adjacent control (should succeed);\n\u003e 4. `dx = -32768, repeat = 65537` — should raise `OverflowError`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Additionally, it should be verified that exception propagation is consistent across `getlength()`, `getbbox()`, `getmask()`, and the underlying `font.getsize()`.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-4rm8-3hmm-39fg | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-96641\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-96641](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-96641)**.\n\n## [GHSA-4rm8-3hmm-39fg](https://osv.dev/GHSA-4rm8-3hmm-39fg)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e Affected Object and Source\n\u003e \n\u003e Package: Pillow 12.3.0\n\u003e Source: PyPI sdist pillow-12.3.0.tar.gz\n\u003e SHA-256: 3b8182a766685eaa002637e28b4ec8d6b18819a0c71f579bf0dbaa5830297cce\n\u003e Sink: src/_imaging.c:2809:15, xsize += dx\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e The code in Pillow 12.3.0 is:\n\u003e ```c\n\u003e for (xsize = 0; *text; text++) {\n\u003e     int dx = self-\u003eglyphs[*text].dx;\n\u003e     if (dx \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 xsize \u003e INT_MAX - dx) {\n\u003e         PyErr_SetString(PyExc_OverflowError, \"Width too large\");\n\u003e         return -1;\n\u003e     }\n\u003e     xsize += dx;\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e The PILfont glyph advance is parsed from a signed 16-bit field, so the valid range for `dx` is `[-32768, 32767]`. The existing check only proves:\n\u003e `dx \u003e 0 =\u003e xsize \u003c= INT_MAX - dx`\n\u003e It does **not** prove the other half of the contract:\n\u003e `dx \u003c 0 =\u003e xsize \u003e= INT_MIN - dx`\n\u003e Taking `dx = -32768` and repeating it 65,537 times, the mathematical sum is `-2,147,516,416`, which is less than `INT_MIN = -2,147,483,648`. On the 65,537th addition, the operation `-2147483648 + -32768` is performed, resulting in **C signed overflow undefined behavior**.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e ```python\n\u003e \"\"\"Minimal public-API reproducer for the Pillow 12.3.0 textwidth underflow.\n\u003e \n\u003e Run with an ordinary Pillow 12.3.0 build to observe the wrapped width, or with\n\u003e the PyC-Contract UBSan build to obtain the exact native diagnostic.\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e from __future__ import annotations\n\u003e \n\u003e import struct\n\u003e from io import BytesIO\n\u003e \n\u003e import PIL\n\u003e from PIL import Image, ImageFont\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ADVANCE = -(2**15)\n\u003e CONTROL_REPEAT = 65_536\n\u003e WITNESS_REPEAT = 65_537\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def bitmap_font(advance: int) -\u003e ImageFont.ImageFont:\n\u003e     metrics = bytearray(256 * 20)\n\u003e     glyph_offset = ord(\"A\") * 20\n\u003e     metrics[glyph_offset : glyph_offset + 2] = struct.pack(\"\u003eh\", advance)\n\u003e     font_data = b\"PILfont\\n;;;;;;1;\\nDATA\\n\" + bytes(metrics)\n\u003e     font = ImageFont.ImageFont()\n\u003e     font._load_pilfont_data(BytesIO(font_data), Image.new(\"1\", (1, 1)))\n\u003e     return font\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def main() -\u003e None:\n\u003e     font = bitmap_font(ADVANCE)\n\u003e     print(f\"Pillow={PIL.__version__}\")\n\u003e     print(f\"control_math={ADVANCE * CONTROL_REPEAT}\")\n\u003e     print(f\"control_return={font.getlength('A' * CONTROL_REPEAT)}\")\n\u003e     print(f\"witness_math={ADVANCE * WITNESS_REPEAT}\")\n\u003e     print(f\"witness_return={font.getlength('A' * WITNESS_REPEAT)}\")\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n\u003e     main()\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Observations from a regular 12.3.0 release build:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e Pillow=12.3.0\n\u003e control_math=-2147483648\n\u003e control_return=0\n\u003e witness_math=-2147516416\n\u003e witness_return=2147450880\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The adjacent control uses 65,536 repetitions, producing a mathematical result of exactly `INT_MIN`, and the function subsequently returns 0 according to existing semantics. Adding just a single character flips the return value to `2,147,450,880`.\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e The directly confirmed impact is width semantic integrity violation: getlength() and boundary calculations based on getsize() may return a huge positive value, while the true mathematical width is negative. The high-level getmask() typically passes through the decompression-bomb check, which mitigates the risk of direct massive allocation; however, internal font objects and applications that use the width for layout, quotas, serialization, or subsequent allocations may be affected by resource consumption or logic errors.\n\u003e \n\u003e Therefore, the current recommended severity rating is \"Reachable native integer undefined behavior, with potential DoS/logic integrity impact; specific security severity to be confirmed by the upstream threat model\", rather than a memory corruption vulnerability.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Recommended Patch and Regression Tests\n\u003e \n\u003e It is recommended to extend the overflow check to cover both directions:\n\u003e ```c\n\u003e if ((dx \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 xsize \u003e INT_MAX - dx) ||\n\u003e     (dx \u003c 0 \u0026\u0026 xsize \u003c INT_MIN - dx)) {\n\u003e     PyErr_SetString(PyExc_OverflowError, \"Width too large\");\n\u003e     return -1;\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e At a minimum, four test cases should be added:\n\u003e 1. `dx = 32767, repeat = 65538` — upper adjacent control (should succeed);\n\u003e 2. `dx = 32767, repeat = 65539` — should raise `OverflowError`;\n\u003e 3. `dx = -32768, repeat = 65536` — lower adjacent control (should succeed);\n\u003e 4. `dx = -32768, repeat = 65537` — should raise `OverflowError`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Additionally, it should be verified that exception propagation is consistent across `getlength()`, `getbbox()`, `getmask()`, and the underlying `font.getsize()`.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-4rm8-3hmm-39fg | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-96641\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-96641",
              "name": "CVE-2026-96641",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-96641: Pillow: Reachable undefined behavior in native integer operations, potentially leading to denial of service or logic integrity compromise",
                "text": "CVE-2026-96641: Pillow: Reachable undefined behavior in native integer operations, potentially leading to denial of service or logic integrity compromise"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-112396",
                "BIT-pillow-2026-112396",
                "PYSEC-2026-4328",
