              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\nSaving an image as TIFF to an in-memory buffer can trigger a heap use-after-free if libtiff rejects a tag at encode time. When `TIFFWriteScanline` fails, the encoder frees its output buffer but leaves the libtiff handle open. Later, when the encoder object is torn down, `TIFFClose` flushes buffered bytes through the write callback and writes into the freed buffer. An application that re-encodes attacker-influenced images to TIFF in memory can be crashed and is a candidate for remote code execution.\n\n### Details\nThe failure path in `ImagingLibTiffEncode` frees `clientstate-\u003edata` for the in-memory case but does not close the TIFF or null `clientstate-\u003etiff` (the file-descriptor branch just above it does set `tiff = NULL`):\n\n- `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1041-1046`\n\nTeardown then calls `ImagingLibTiffEncodeCleanup`, which runs `TIFFClose(tiff)` because `tiff` is still set:\n\n- `src/encode.c:99-101`\n- `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:955`\n\n`TIFFClose` flushes through `_tiffWriteProc`, which reallocs and memcpys into the freed `state-\u003edata`:\n\n- `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:103-115`\n\nThe Predictor tag is a convenient trigger: the floating-point predictor (value 3) is invalid for integer sample data, so libtiff aborts the write.\n\n### PoC\n```python\nimport io\nfrom PIL import Image\n\n# In-memory TIFF re-encode. libtiff rejects Predictor=3 (floating point) on integer\n# data, so TIFFWriteScanline fails. The encoder frees clientstate-\u003edata but leaves the\n# TIFF open; cleanup's TIFFClose then writes through the callback into the freed buffer.\nim = Image.new(\"L\", (64, 64))\nbuf = io.BytesIO()\nim.save(buf, format=\"TIFF\", compression=\"tiff_lzw\", tiffinfo={317: 3})  # 317 = Predictor\n```\n\nRun it:\n```\n$ python 01_poc.py\nPredictorSetup: Floating point \"Predictor\" not supported with 1 data format.\nOSError: encoder error -2 when writing image file\nSegmentation fault (core dumped)\n```\n\nObserved on a source build of commit `3e2ab96` with libtiff. The process aborts during cleanup after the encoder error. Depending on heap state the same run sometimes reports `corrupted double-linked list` instead of a segfault; both are the same write into freed memory.\n\nUnder AddressSanitizer (build with `-fsanitize=address`), relevant frames:\n```\n==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free on address 0x7ef737b48804\nWRITE of size 4 at 0x7ef737b48804 thread T0\n    #1 _tiffWriteProc                 src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:115\n    #3 TIFFFlush                      libtiff.so.6\n    #5 TIFFClose                      libtiff.so.6\n    #6 ImagingLibTiffEncodeCleanup    src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:955\n    #7 _dealloc                       src/encode.c:100\nfreed by thread T0 here:\n    #1 ImagingLibTiffEncode           src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1045\n    #2 _encode                        src/encode.c:139\npreviously allocated by thread T0 here:\n    #1 ImagingLibTiffEncodeInit       src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:863\n    #2 PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew    src/encode.c:757\nSUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:115 in _tiffWriteProc\n```\nThe buffer allocated in `ImagingLibTiffEncodeInit` is freed in `ImagingLibTiffEncode`, then written by `_tiffWriteProc` during the `TIFFClose` flush that runs from the encoder's `_dealloc`.\n\n### Impact\nUse-after-free in a C extension. Affects any service that converts or thumbnails untrusted images to TIFF in memory (a `BytesIO` or other non-seekable target). Result is a crash and possible memory corruption leading to code execution.",
                "text": "### Summary\nSaving an image as TIFF to an in-memory buffer can trigger a heap use-after-free if libtiff rejects a tag at encode time. When `TIFFWriteScanline` fails, the encoder frees its output buffer but leaves the libtiff handle open. Later, when the encoder object is torn down, `TIFFClose` flushes buffered bytes through the write callback and writes into the freed buffer. An application that re-encodes attacker-influenced images to TIFF in memory can be crashed and is a candidate for remote code execution.\n\n### Details\nThe failure path in `ImagingLibTiffEncode` frees `clientstate-\u003edata` for the in-memory case but does not close the TIFF or null `clientstate-\u003etiff` (the file-descriptor branch just above it does set `tiff = NULL`):\n\n- `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1041-1046`\n\nTeardown then calls `ImagingLibTiffEncodeCleanup`, which runs `TIFFClose(tiff)` because `tiff` is still set:\n\n- `src/encode.c:99-101`\n- `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:955`\n\n`TIFFClose` flushes through `_tiffWriteProc`, which reallocs and memcpys into the freed `state-\u003edata`:\n\n- `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:103-115`\n\nThe Predictor tag is a convenient trigger: the floating-point predictor (value 3) is invalid for integer sample data, so libtiff aborts the write.\n\n### PoC\n```python\nimport io\nfrom PIL import Image\n\n# In-memory TIFF re-encode. libtiff rejects Predictor=3 (floating point) on integer\n# data, so TIFFWriteScanline fails. The encoder frees clientstate-\u003edata but leaves the\n# TIFF open; cleanup's TIFFClose then writes through the callback into the freed buffer.\nim = Image.new(\"L\", (64, 64))\nbuf = io.BytesIO()\nim.save(buf, format=\"TIFF\", compression=\"tiff_lzw\", tiffinfo={317: 3})  # 317 = Predictor\n```\n\nRun it:\n```\n$ python 01_poc.py\nPredictorSetup: Floating point \"Predictor\" not supported with 1 data format.\nOSError: encoder error -2 when writing image file\nSegmentation fault (core dumped)\n```\n\nObserved on a source build of commit `3e2ab96` with libtiff. The process aborts during cleanup after the encoder error. Depending on heap state the same run sometimes reports `corrupted double-linked list` instead of a segfault; both are the same write into freed memory.\n\nUnder AddressSanitizer (build with `-fsanitize=address`), relevant frames:\n```\n==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free on address 0x7ef737b48804\nWRITE of size 4 at 0x7ef737b48804 thread T0\n    #1 _tiffWriteProc                 src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:115\n    #3 TIFFFlush                      libtiff.so.6\n    #5 TIFFClose                      libtiff.so.6\n    #6 ImagingLibTiffEncodeCleanup    src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:955\n    #7 _dealloc                       src/encode.c:100\nfreed by thread T0 here:\n    #1 ImagingLibTiffEncode           src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1045\n    #2 _encode                        src/encode.c:139\npreviously allocated by thread T0 here:\n    #1 ImagingLibTiffEncodeInit       src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:863\n    #2 PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew    src/encode.c:757\nSUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:115 in _tiffWriteProc\n```\nThe buffer allocated in `ImagingLibTiffEncodeInit` is freed in `ImagingLibTiffEncode`, then written by `_tiffWriteProc` during the `TIFFClose` flush that runs from the encoder's `_dealloc`.\n\n### Impact\nUse-after-free in a C extension. Affects any service that converts or thumbnails untrusted images to TIFF in memory (a `BytesIO` or other non-seekable target). Result is a crash and possible memory corruption leading to code execution."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112396](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112396)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4328](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4328), [GHSA-4rr5-89j8-mwwm](https://osv.dev/GHSA-4rr5-89j8-mwwm), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4328](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4328)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e Pillow is a Python imaging library. From 11.3.0 until 13.0.0, Pillow has a heap use-after-free when libtiff rejects a tag while encoding TIFF data to an in-memory buffer. When an application saves attacker-influenced TIFF tags to BytesIO, libtiff can reject a tag during encoding in ImagingLibTiffEncode; the error path then frees the output buffer without closing or clearing the TIFF handle, and later cleanup flushes through the write callback into freed memory, which can crash the process and may enable code execution. This issue is fixed in version 13.0.0.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-4rr5-89j8-mwwm](https://osv.dev/GHSA-4rr5-89j8-mwwm)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e Saving an image as TIFF to an in-memory buffer can trigger a heap use-after-free if libtiff rejects a tag at encode time. When `TIFFWriteScanline` fails, the encoder frees its output buffer but leaves the libtiff handle open. Later, when the encoder object is torn down, `TIFFClose` flushes buffered bytes through the write callback and writes into the freed buffer. An application that re-encodes attacker-influenced images to TIFF in memory can be crashed and is a candidate for remote code execution.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e The failure path in `ImagingLibTiffEncode` frees `clientstate-\u003edata` for the in-memory case but does not close the TIFF or null `clientstate-\u003etiff` (the file-descriptor branch just above it does set `tiff = NULL`):\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1041-1046`\n\u003e \n\u003e Teardown then calls `ImagingLibTiffEncodeCleanup`, which runs `TIFFClose(tiff)` because `tiff` is still set:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/encode.c:99-101`\n\u003e - `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:955`\n\u003e \n\u003e `TIFFClose` flushes through `_tiffWriteProc`, which reallocs and memcpys into the freed `state-\u003edata`:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:103-115`\n\u003e \n\u003e The Predictor tag is a convenient trigger: the floating-point predictor (value 3) is invalid for integer sample data, so libtiff aborts the write.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import io\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e # In-memory TIFF re-encode. libtiff rejects Predictor=3 (floating point) on integer\n\u003e # data, so TIFFWriteScanline fails. The encoder frees clientstate-\u003edata but leaves the\n\u003e # TIFF open; cleanup's TIFFClose then writes through the callback into the freed buffer.\n\u003e im = Image.new(\"L\", (64, 64))\n\u003e buf = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e im.save(buf, format=\"TIFF\", compression=\"tiff_lzw\", tiffinfo={317: 3})  # 317 = Predictor\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Run it:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e $ python 01_poc.py\n\u003e PredictorSetup: Floating point \"Predictor\" not supported with 1 data format.\n\u003e OSError: encoder error -2 when writing image file\n\u003e Segmentation fault (core dumped)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Observed on a source build of commit `3e2ab96` with libtiff. The process aborts during cleanup after the encoder error. Depending on heap state the same run sometimes reports `corrupted double-linked list` instead of a segfault; both are the same write into freed memory.\n\u003e \n\u003e Under AddressSanitizer (build with `-fsanitize=address`), relevant frames:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free on address 0x7ef737b48804\n\u003e WRITE of size 4 at 0x7ef737b48804 thread T0\n\u003e     #1 _tiffWriteProc                 src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:115\n\u003e     #3 TIFFFlush                      libtiff.so.6\n\u003e     #5 TIFFClose                      libtiff.so.6\n\u003e     #6 ImagingLibTiffEncodeCleanup    src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:955\n\u003e     #7 _dealloc                       src/encode.c:100\n\u003e freed by thread T0 here:\n\u003e     #1 ImagingLibTiffEncode           src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1045\n\u003e     #2 _encode                        src/encode.c:139\n\u003e previously allocated by thread T0 here:\n\u003e     #1 ImagingLibTiffEncodeInit       src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:863\n\u003e     #2 PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew    src/encode.c:757\n\u003e SUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:115 in _tiffWriteProc\n\u003e ```\n\u003e The buffer allocated in `ImagingLibTiffEncodeInit` is freed in `ImagingLibTiffEncode`, then written by `_tiffWriteProc` during the `TIFFClose` flush that runs from the encoder's `_dealloc`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e Use-after-free in a C extension. Affects any service that converts or thumbnails untrusted images to TIFF in memory (a `BytesIO` or other non-seekable target). Result is a crash and possible memory corruption leading to code execution.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-4rr5-89j8-mwwm | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4328 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112396\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112396](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112396)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4328](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4328), [GHSA-4rr5-89j8-mwwm](https://osv.dev/GHSA-4rr5-89j8-mwwm), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4328](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4328)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e Pillow is a Python imaging library. From 11.3.0 until 13.0.0, Pillow has a heap use-after-free when libtiff rejects a tag while encoding TIFF data to an in-memory buffer. When an application saves attacker-influenced TIFF tags to BytesIO, libtiff can reject a tag during encoding in ImagingLibTiffEncode; the error path then frees the output buffer without closing or clearing the TIFF handle, and later cleanup flushes through the write callback into freed memory, which can crash the process and may enable code execution. This issue is fixed in version 13.0.0.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-4rr5-89j8-mwwm](https://osv.dev/GHSA-4rr5-89j8-mwwm)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e Saving an image as TIFF to an in-memory buffer can trigger a heap use-after-free if libtiff rejects a tag at encode time. When `TIFFWriteScanline` fails, the encoder frees its output buffer but leaves the libtiff handle open. Later, when the encoder object is torn down, `TIFFClose` flushes buffered bytes through the write callback and writes into the freed buffer. An application that re-encodes attacker-influenced images to TIFF in memory can be crashed and is a candidate for remote code execution.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e The failure path in `ImagingLibTiffEncode` frees `clientstate-\u003edata` for the in-memory case but does not close the TIFF or null `clientstate-\u003etiff` (the file-descriptor branch just above it does set `tiff = NULL`):\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1041-1046`\n\u003e \n\u003e Teardown then calls `ImagingLibTiffEncodeCleanup`, which runs `TIFFClose(tiff)` because `tiff` is still set:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/encode.c:99-101`\n\u003e - `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:955`\n\u003e \n\u003e `TIFFClose` flushes through `_tiffWriteProc`, which reallocs and memcpys into the freed `state-\u003edata`:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:103-115`\n\u003e \n\u003e The Predictor tag is a convenient trigger: the floating-point predictor (value 3) is invalid for integer sample data, so libtiff aborts the write.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import io\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e # In-memory TIFF re-encode. libtiff rejects Predictor=3 (floating point) on integer\n\u003e # data, so TIFFWriteScanline fails. The encoder frees clientstate-\u003edata but leaves the\n\u003e # TIFF open; cleanup's TIFFClose then writes through the callback into the freed buffer.\n\u003e im = Image.new(\"L\", (64, 64))\n\u003e buf = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e im.save(buf, format=\"TIFF\", compression=\"tiff_lzw\", tiffinfo={317: 3})  # 317 = Predictor\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Run it:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e $ python 01_poc.py\n\u003e PredictorSetup: Floating point \"Predictor\" not supported with 1 data format.\n\u003e OSError: encoder error -2 when writing image file\n\u003e Segmentation fault (core dumped)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Observed on a source build of commit `3e2ab96` with libtiff. The process aborts during cleanup after the encoder error. Depending on heap state the same run sometimes reports `corrupted double-linked list` instead of a segfault; both are the same write into freed memory.\n\u003e \n\u003e Under AddressSanitizer (build with `-fsanitize=address`), relevant frames:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free on address 0x7ef737b48804\n\u003e WRITE of size 4 at 0x7ef737b48804 thread T0\n\u003e     #1 _tiffWriteProc                 src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:115\n\u003e     #3 TIFFFlush                      libtiff.so.6\n\u003e     #5 TIFFClose                      libtiff.so.6\n\u003e     #6 ImagingLibTiffEncodeCleanup    src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:955\n\u003e     #7 _dealloc                       src/encode.c:100\n\u003e freed by thread T0 here:\n\u003e     #1 ImagingLibTiffEncode           src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:1045\n\u003e     #2 _encode                        src/encode.c:139\n\u003e previously allocated by thread T0 here:\n\u003e     #1 ImagingLibTiffEncodeInit       src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:863\n\u003e     #2 PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew    src/encode.c:757\n\u003e SUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:115 in _tiffWriteProc\n\u003e ```\n\u003e The buffer allocated in `ImagingLibTiffEncodeInit` is freed in `ImagingLibTiffEncode`, then written by `_tiffWriteProc` during the `TIFFClose` flush that runs from the encoder's `_dealloc`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e Use-after-free in a C extension. Affects any service that converts or thumbnails untrusted images to TIFF in memory (a `BytesIO` or other non-seekable target). Result is a crash and possible memory corruption leading to code execution.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-4rr5-89j8-mwwm | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4328 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112396\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
