              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\nA PPM or PGM file can declare a width of 0, which Pillow accepts, producing an image of size `(0, N)`. Passing that image to `ImageMorph.MorphOp.apply()` makes the native morphology code index a row buffer at `outrow[width - 1]`, which is `outrow[-1]` when the width is 0. The process crashes.\n\n### Details\nThe PPM parser reads width and height with no lower-bound check:\n\n- `src/PIL/PpmImagePlugin.py:119`\n\nThe native `apply()` writes the row edges with no guard that the width is positive:\n\n- `src/_imagingmorph.c:81` sets `outrow[0] = outrow[width - 1] = 0;`\n\n`match()` and `get_on_pixels()` use the same edge-indexing pattern.\n\n### PoC\n```python\nfrom PIL import Image, ImageMorph\n\n# A width-0 image (also producible from a crafted PPM header). apply() indexes\n# outrow[width - 1] == outrow[-1] in native code.\nim = Image.new(\"L\", (0, 5))\nImageMorph.MorphOp(op_name=\"dilation4\").apply(im)\n```\n\nRun it:\n```\n$ python 03_poc.py\nSegmentation fault (core dumped)\n```\n\nObserved on a source build of commit `3e2ab96`.\n\nUnder AddressSanitizer:\n```\n==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: SEGV on unknown address (pc ... in apply)\n    #0 apply   src/_imagingmorph.c:81\nSUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: SEGV src/_imagingmorph.c:81 in apply\n```\nWith width 0 the `outrow[width - 1]` write lands on an unmapped page, so it surfaces as a SEGV pinned to the edge-write line rather than a redzone overflow.\n\n### Impact\nOut-of-bounds write in a C extension. Affects any application that runs a morphology operation on an image opened from untrusted input. Result is a crash and possible memory corruption.",
                "text": "### Summary\nA PPM or PGM file can declare a width of 0, which Pillow accepts, producing an image of size `(0, N)`. Passing that image to `ImageMorph.MorphOp.apply()` makes the native morphology code index a row buffer at `outrow[width - 1]`, which is `outrow[-1]` when the width is 0. The process crashes.\n\n### Details\nThe PPM parser reads width and height with no lower-bound check:\n\n- `src/PIL/PpmImagePlugin.py:119`\n\nThe native `apply()` writes the row edges with no guard that the width is positive:\n\n- `src/_imagingmorph.c:81` sets `outrow[0] = outrow[width - 1] = 0;`\n\n`match()` and `get_on_pixels()` use the same edge-indexing pattern.\n\n### PoC\n```python\nfrom PIL import Image, ImageMorph\n\n# A width-0 image (also producible from a crafted PPM header). apply() indexes\n# outrow[width - 1] == outrow[-1] in native code.\nim = Image.new(\"L\", (0, 5))\nImageMorph.MorphOp(op_name=\"dilation4\").apply(im)\n```\n\nRun it:\n```\n$ python 03_poc.py\nSegmentation fault (core dumped)\n```\n\nObserved on a source build of commit `3e2ab96`.\n\nUnder AddressSanitizer:\n```\n==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: SEGV on unknown address (pc ... in apply)\n    #0 apply   src/_imagingmorph.c:81\nSUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: SEGV src/_imagingmorph.c:81 in apply\n```\nWith width 0 the `outrow[width - 1]` write lands on an unmapped page, so it surfaces as a SEGV pinned to the edge-write line rather than a redzone overflow.\n\n### Impact\nOut-of-bounds write in a C extension. Affects any application that runs a morphology operation on an image opened from untrusted input. Result is a crash and possible memory corruption."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112397](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112397)**.\n\n## [GHSA-58r4-6rv3-gfh8](https://osv.dev/GHSA-58r4-6rv3-gfh8)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e A PPM or PGM file can declare a width of 0, which Pillow accepts, producing an image of size `(0, N)`. Passing that image to `ImageMorph.MorphOp.apply()` makes the native morphology code index a row buffer at `outrow[width - 1]`, which is `outrow[-1]` when the width is 0. The process crashes.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e The PPM parser reads width and height with no lower-bound check:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/PIL/PpmImagePlugin.py:119`\n\u003e \n\u003e The native `apply()` writes the row edges with no guard that the width is positive:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/_imagingmorph.c:81` sets `outrow[0] = outrow[width - 1] = 0;`\n\u003e \n\u003e `match()` and `get_on_pixels()` use the same edge-indexing pattern.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from PIL import Image, ImageMorph\n\u003e \n\u003e # A width-0 image (also producible from a crafted PPM header). apply() indexes\n\u003e # outrow[width - 1] == outrow[-1] in native code.\n\u003e im = Image.new(\"L\", (0, 5))\n\u003e ImageMorph.MorphOp(op_name=\"dilation4\").apply(im)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Run it:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e $ python 03_poc.py\n\u003e Segmentation fault (core dumped)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Observed on a source build of commit `3e2ab96`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Under AddressSanitizer:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: SEGV on unknown address (pc ... in apply)\n\u003e     #0 apply   src/_imagingmorph.c:81\n\u003e SUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: SEGV src/_imagingmorph.c:81 in apply\n\u003e ```\n\u003e With width 0 the `outrow[width - 1]` write lands on an unmapped page, so it surfaces as a SEGV pinned to the edge-write line rather than a redzone overflow.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e Out-of-bounds write in a C extension. Affects any application that runs a morphology operation on an image opened from untrusted input. Result is a crash and possible memory corruption.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-58r4-6rv3-gfh8 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112397\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112397](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112397)**.\n\n## [GHSA-58r4-6rv3-gfh8](https://osv.dev/GHSA-58r4-6rv3-gfh8)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e A PPM or PGM file can declare a width of 0, which Pillow accepts, producing an image of size `(0, N)`. Passing that image to `ImageMorph.MorphOp.apply()` makes the native morphology code index a row buffer at `outrow[width - 1]`, which is `outrow[-1]` when the width is 0. The process crashes.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e The PPM parser reads width and height with no lower-bound check:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/PIL/PpmImagePlugin.py:119`\n\u003e \n\u003e The native `apply()` writes the row edges with no guard that the width is positive:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/_imagingmorph.c:81` sets `outrow[0] = outrow[width - 1] = 0;`\n\u003e \n\u003e `match()` and `get_on_pixels()` use the same edge-indexing pattern.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from PIL import Image, ImageMorph\n\u003e \n\u003e # A width-0 image (also producible from a crafted PPM header). apply() indexes\n\u003e # outrow[width - 1] == outrow[-1] in native code.\n\u003e im = Image.new(\"L\", (0, 5))\n\u003e ImageMorph.MorphOp(op_name=\"dilation4\").apply(im)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Run it:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e $ python 03_poc.py\n\u003e Segmentation fault (core dumped)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Observed on a source build of commit `3e2ab96`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Under AddressSanitizer:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: SEGV on unknown address (pc ... in apply)\n\u003e     #0 apply   src/_imagingmorph.c:81\n\u003e SUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: SEGV src/_imagingmorph.c:81 in apply\n\u003e ```\n\u003e With width 0 the `outrow[width - 1]` write lands on an unmapped page, so it surfaces as a SEGV pinned to the edge-write line rather than a redzone overflow.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e Out-of-bounds write in a C extension. Affects any application that runs a morphology operation on an image opened from untrusted input. Result is a crash and possible memory corruption.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-58r4-6rv3-gfh8 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112397\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
