              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Impact\n\nzsh users activate an environment with the bash `activate` script, which puts the environment name into `PS1`. Since\n21.13.0 the script escapes backslashes, dollar signs and backticks in that name, but only when `BASH_VERSION` is set, so\nzsh gets the raw value. With the `PROMPT_SUBST` option set, zsh runs parameter expansion, command substitution and\narithmetic expansion on `PS1` each time it draws the prompt. A name that contains `$(...)`, backticks or `${...}` then\nruns as commands in the user's shell, once per prompt.\n\nThe name comes from the environment directory, or from `--prompt` or `VIRTUALENV_PROMPT`. After you create an\nenvironment with:\n\n```console\n$ virtualenv 'x$(touch PWNED)y'\n```\n\n`source 'x$(touch PWNED)y/bin/activate'` in a zsh session with `PROMPT_SUBST` set creates `PWNED`, and the prompt shows\n`(xy)`. Passing that value as `--prompt` to an environment with a plain name has the same effect. Without\n`PROMPT_SUBST`, zsh shows the name as text and runs nothing. bash runs nothing either.\n\nA plain zsh starts with `PROMPT_SUBST` off. oh-my-zsh users have it on: oh-my-zsh loads\n[`lib/theme-and-appearance.zsh`](https://github.com/ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh/blob/4d4cfc287e9d887b81242c0e431b5f49f9cec5c1/lib/theme-and-appearance.zsh#L5)\nat startup, and that file sets it. An attacker needs to influence the environment path or prompt: a CI job or wrapper\ntool that passes a branch, project or job name through, or a directory in a cloned repository or unpacked archive.\n`--prompt .` takes the current directory's name.\n\nzsh also reads `%` in `PS1` as a prompt escape, so a name with `%n` showed the user name in place of those characters,\nthough without running commands.\n\nThe bug is in releases up to and including 21.14.3. The payload runs on 20.26.6 and 21.14.3, and the reporter confirmed\n21.9.1.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in 21.14.4. Under zsh, `activate` now escapes the name the way it does under bash and doubles\n`%`, so the example above runs nothing and the prompt shows the name as given. zsh gets the escaping even when\n`PROMPT_SUBST` is off, because a user can set the option after activation; a name with a backslash, dollar sign or\nbacktick then shows the added backslashes.\nFix: https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/commit/5b915367da82bd024b2a0d28d2f98007034b4aa2\n\n### Workarounds\n\nSet `VIRTUAL_ENV_DISABLE_PROMPT=1` before you source `activate`, which leaves `PS1` alone, or run\n`unsetopt prompt_subst` if your prompt does not need it. Check the name of an environment you did not create before you\nsource its `activate`.\n\n### Credits\n\nKaya Emre Arikan (@kemrec) reported this issue.",
                "text": "### Impact\n\nzsh users activate an environment with the bash `activate` script, which puts the environment name into `PS1`. Since\n21.13.0 the script escapes backslashes, dollar signs and backticks in that name, but only when `BASH_VERSION` is set, so\nzsh gets the raw value. With the `PROMPT_SUBST` option set, zsh runs parameter expansion, command substitution and\narithmetic expansion on `PS1` each time it draws the prompt. A name that contains `$(...)`, backticks or `${...}` then\nruns as commands in the user's shell, once per prompt.\n\nThe name comes from the environment directory, or from `--prompt` or `VIRTUALENV_PROMPT`. After you create an\nenvironment with:\n\n```console\n$ virtualenv 'x$(touch PWNED)y'\n```\n\n`source 'x$(touch PWNED)y/bin/activate'` in a zsh session with `PROMPT_SUBST` set creates `PWNED`, and the prompt shows\n`(xy)`. Passing that value as `--prompt` to an environment with a plain name has the same effect. Without\n`PROMPT_SUBST`, zsh shows the name as text and runs nothing. bash runs nothing either.\n\nA plain zsh starts with `PROMPT_SUBST` off. oh-my-zsh users have it on: oh-my-zsh loads\n[`lib/theme-and-appearance.zsh`](https://github.com/ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh/blob/4d4cfc287e9d887b81242c0e431b5f49f9cec5c1/lib/theme-and-appearance.zsh#L5)\nat startup, and that file sets it. An attacker needs to influence the environment path or prompt: a CI job or wrapper\ntool that passes a branch, project or job name through, or a directory in a cloned repository or unpacked archive.\n`--prompt .` takes the current directory's name.\n\nzsh also reads `%` in `PS1` as a prompt escape, so a name with `%n` showed the user name in place of those characters,\nthough without running commands.\n\nThe bug is in releases up to and including 21.14.3. The payload runs on 20.26.6 and 21.14.3, and the reporter confirmed\n21.9.1.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in 21.14.4. Under zsh, `activate` now escapes the name the way it does under bash and doubles\n`%`, so the example above runs nothing and the prompt shows the name as given. zsh gets the escaping even when\n`PROMPT_SUBST` is off, because a user can set the option after activation; a name with a backslash, dollar sign or\nbacktick then shows the added backslashes.\nFix: https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/commit/5b915367da82bd024b2a0d28d2f98007034b4aa2\n\n### Workarounds\n\nSet `VIRTUAL_ENV_DISABLE_PROMPT=1` before you source `activate`, which leaves `PS1` alone, or run\n`unsetopt prompt_subst` if your prompt does not need it. Check the name of an environment you did not create before you\nsource its `activate`.\n\n### Credits\n\nKaya Emre Arikan (@kemrec) reported this issue."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-113726](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-113726)**.\n\n## [GHSA-5vjq-rrrf-7h2q](https://osv.dev/GHSA-5vjq-rrrf-7h2q)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e zsh users activate an environment with the bash `activate` script, which puts the environment name into `PS1`. Since\n\u003e 21.13.0 the script escapes backslashes, dollar signs and backticks in that name, but only when `BASH_VERSION` is set, so\n\u003e zsh gets the raw value. With the `PROMPT_SUBST` option set, zsh runs parameter expansion, command substitution and\n\u003e arithmetic expansion on `PS1` each time it draws the prompt. A name that contains `$(...)`, backticks or `${...}` then\n\u003e runs as commands in the user's shell, once per prompt.\n\u003e \n\u003e The name comes from the environment directory, or from `--prompt` or `VIRTUALENV_PROMPT`. After you create an\n\u003e environment with:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```console\n\u003e $ virtualenv 'x$(touch PWNED)y'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `source 'x$(touch PWNED)y/bin/activate'` in a zsh session with `PROMPT_SUBST` set creates `PWNED`, and the prompt shows\n\u003e `(xy)`. Passing that value as `--prompt` to an environment with a plain name has the same effect. Without\n\u003e `PROMPT_SUBST`, zsh shows the name as text and runs nothing. bash runs nothing either.\n\u003e \n\u003e A plain zsh starts with `PROMPT_SUBST` off. oh-my-zsh users have it on: oh-my-zsh loads\n\u003e [`lib/theme-and-appearance.zsh`](https://github.com/ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh/blob/4d4cfc287e9d887b81242c0e431b5f49f9cec5c1/lib/theme-and-appearance.zsh#L5)\n\u003e at startup, and that file sets it. An attacker needs to influence the environment path or prompt: a CI job or wrapper\n\u003e tool that passes a branch, project or job name through, or a directory in a cloned repository or unpacked archive.\n\u003e `--prompt .` takes the current directory's name.\n\u003e \n\u003e zsh also reads `%` in `PS1` as a prompt escape, so a name with `%n` showed the user name in place of those characters,\n\u003e though without running commands.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug is in releases up to and including 21.14.3. The payload runs on 20.26.6 and 21.14.3, and the reporter confirmed\n\u003e 21.9.1.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed in 21.14.4. Under zsh, `activate` now escapes the name the way it does under bash and doubles\n\u003e `%`, so the example above runs nothing and the prompt shows the name as given. zsh gets the escaping even when\n\u003e `PROMPT_SUBST` is off, because a user can set the option after activation; a name with a backslash, dollar sign or\n\u003e backtick then shows the added backslashes.\n\u003e Fix: https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/commit/5b915367da82bd024b2a0d28d2f98007034b4aa2\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Set `VIRTUAL_ENV_DISABLE_PROMPT=1` before you source `activate`, which leaves `PS1` alone, or run\n\u003e `unsetopt prompt_subst` if your prompt does not need it. Check the name of an environment you did not create before you\n\u003e source its `activate`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e Kaya Emre Arikan (@kemrec) reported this issue.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | virtualenv | 20.31.2 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | virtualenv | 21.7.4 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-5vjq-rrrf-7h2q | virtualenv | 21.14.4 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113726\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113726\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-113726](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-113726)**.\n\n## [GHSA-5vjq-rrrf-7h2q](https://osv.dev/GHSA-5vjq-rrrf-7h2q)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e zsh users activate an environment with the bash `activate` script, which puts the environment name into `PS1`. Since\n\u003e 21.13.0 the script escapes backslashes, dollar signs and backticks in that name, but only when `BASH_VERSION` is set, so\n\u003e zsh gets the raw value. With the `PROMPT_SUBST` option set, zsh runs parameter expansion, command substitution and\n\u003e arithmetic expansion on `PS1` each time it draws the prompt. A name that contains `$(...)`, backticks or `${...}` then\n\u003e runs as commands in the user's shell, once per prompt.\n\u003e \n\u003e The name comes from the environment directory, or from `--prompt` or `VIRTUALENV_PROMPT`. After you create an\n\u003e environment with:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```console\n\u003e $ virtualenv 'x$(touch PWNED)y'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `source 'x$(touch PWNED)y/bin/activate'` in a zsh session with `PROMPT_SUBST` set creates `PWNED`, and the prompt shows\n\u003e `(xy)`. Passing that value as `--prompt` to an environment with a plain name has the same effect. Without\n\u003e `PROMPT_SUBST`, zsh shows the name as text and runs nothing. bash runs nothing either.\n\u003e \n\u003e A plain zsh starts with `PROMPT_SUBST` off. oh-my-zsh users have it on: oh-my-zsh loads\n\u003e [`lib/theme-and-appearance.zsh`](https://github.com/ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh/blob/4d4cfc287e9d887b81242c0e431b5f49f9cec5c1/lib/theme-and-appearance.zsh#L5)\n\u003e at startup, and that file sets it. An attacker needs to influence the environment path or prompt: a CI job or wrapper\n\u003e tool that passes a branch, project or job name through, or a directory in a cloned repository or unpacked archive.\n\u003e `--prompt .` takes the current directory's name.\n\u003e \n\u003e zsh also reads `%` in `PS1` as a prompt escape, so a name with `%n` showed the user name in place of those characters,\n\u003e though without running commands.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug is in releases up to and including 21.14.3. The payload runs on 20.26.6 and 21.14.3, and the reporter confirmed\n\u003e 21.9.1.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed in 21.14.4. Under zsh, `activate` now escapes the name the way it does under bash and doubles\n\u003e `%`, so the example above runs nothing and the prompt shows the name as given. zsh gets the escaping even when\n\u003e `PROMPT_SUBST` is off, because a user can set the option after activation; a name with a backslash, dollar sign or\n\u003e backtick then shows the added backslashes.\n\u003e Fix: https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/commit/5b915367da82bd024b2a0d28d2f98007034b4aa2\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Set `VIRTUAL_ENV_DISABLE_PROMPT=1` before you source `activate`, which leaves `PS1` alone, or run\n\u003e `unsetopt prompt_subst` if your prompt does not need it. Check the name of an environment you did not create before you\n\u003e source its `activate`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e Kaya Emre Arikan (@kemrec) reported this issue.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | virtualenv | 20.31.2 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | virtualenv | 21.7.4 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-5vjq-rrrf-7h2q | virtualenv | 21.14.4 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113726\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113726\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
