            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-114501",
                "GHSA-654x-cwwv-5j9c"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\nWhen [`PILLOW_ALIGNMENT`](https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference/block_allocator.html#memory-pools) is set above 1, exporting an image to Arrow returns uninitialized heap memory mixed in with the pixel data. That memory can include pixels from images the process freed earlier. \n\n### Details\n\nWith an alignment above 1, the default allocator pads each row up to `aligned_linesize` and starts the first row at an aligned offset into the block. It then exports `im-\u003eblocks[0].ptr` as `xsize * ysize` tightly packed pixels, so exported data includes the padding bytes, and can include a few bytes from before the first row.\n\nTested vulnerable on 11.2.1, 12.3.0, 4c1ed27a5.\n\n### PoC\n\nWhether things leak may depend on allocator configuration. This does show a leak on my Mac.\n\n```python\nimport os\n\nos.environ[\"PILLOW_ALIGNMENT\"] = \"64\"\n\nfrom PIL import Image\n\n# Image A: 64 px wide, so its rows fill the whole aligned stride\na = Image.new(\"L\", (64, 40), 0x41).transpose(Image.Transpose.FLIP_LEFT_RIGHT)\ndel a\n\n# Image B: 61 px wide, so each row has 3 bytes of padding; its pixels are all 0\nb = Image.new(\"L\", (61, 40), 0).transpose(Image.Transpose.FLIP_LEFT_RIGHT)\n\ntry:\n    exported = Image.fromarrow(b, b.mode, b.size).tobytes()\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(\"Not vulnerable:\", e)\nelse:\n    if leaked := len(exported) - exported.count(0):\n        raise RuntimeError(f\"{leaked} of {len(exported)} exported bytes are not B's pixels\")\n```\n\n### Impact\n\nInformation disclosure for applications that set `PILLOW_ALIGNMENT` and export images to Arrow. Likely any image whose row length isn't a multiple of the alignment will do. In a service handling several users' images, one user's exported data can contain pixels from another user's image, or other stale heap contents.",
                "text": "### Summary\n\nWhen [`PILLOW_ALIGNMENT`](https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference/block_allocator.html#memory-pools) is set above 1, exporting an image to Arrow returns uninitialized heap memory mixed in with the pixel data. That memory can include pixels from images the process freed earlier. \n\n### Details\n\nWith an alignment above 1, the default allocator pads each row up to `aligned_linesize` and starts the first row at an aligned offset into the block. It then exports `im-\u003eblocks[0].ptr` as `xsize * ysize` tightly packed pixels, so exported data includes the padding bytes, and can include a few bytes from before the first row.\n\nTested vulnerable on 11.2.1, 12.3.0, 4c1ed27a5.\n\n### PoC\n\nWhether things leak may depend on allocator configuration. This does show a leak on my Mac.\n\n```python\nimport os\n\nos.environ[\"PILLOW_ALIGNMENT\"] = \"64\"\n\nfrom PIL import Image\n\n# Image A: 64 px wide, so its rows fill the whole aligned stride\na = Image.new(\"L\", (64, 40), 0x41).transpose(Image.Transpose.FLIP_LEFT_RIGHT)\ndel a\n\n# Image B: 61 px wide, so each row has 3 bytes of padding; its pixels are all 0\nb = Image.new(\"L\", (61, 40), 0).transpose(Image.Transpose.FLIP_LEFT_RIGHT)\n\ntry:\n    exported = Image.fromarrow(b, b.mode, b.size).tobytes()\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(\"Not vulnerable:\", e)\nelse:\n    if leaked := len(exported) - exported.count(0):\n        raise RuntimeError(f\"{leaked} of {len(exported)} exported bytes are not B's pixels\")\n```\n\n### Impact\n\nInformation disclosure for applications that set `PILLOW_ALIGNMENT` and export images to Arrow. Likely any image whose row length isn't a multiple of the alignment will do. In a service handling several users' images, one user's exported data can contain pixels from another user's image, or other stale heap contents."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-114501](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-114501)**.\n\n## [GHSA-654x-cwwv-5j9c](https://osv.dev/GHSA-654x-cwwv-5j9c)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e When [`PILLOW_ALIGNMENT`](https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference/block_allocator.html#memory-pools) is set above 1, exporting an image to Arrow returns uninitialized heap memory mixed in with the pixel data. That memory can include pixels from images the process freed earlier. \n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e With an alignment above 1, the default allocator pads each row up to `aligned_linesize` and starts the first row at an aligned offset into the block. It then exports `im-\u003eblocks[0].ptr` as `xsize * ysize` tightly packed pixels, so exported data includes the padding bytes, and can include a few bytes from before the first row.\n\u003e \n\u003e Tested vulnerable on 11.2.1, 12.3.0, 4c1ed27a5.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e \n\u003e Whether things leak may depend on allocator configuration. This does show a leak on my Mac.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import os\n\u003e \n\u003e os.environ[\"PILLOW_ALIGNMENT\"] = \"64\"\n\u003e \n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e # Image A: 64 px wide, so its rows fill the whole aligned stride\n\u003e a = Image.new(\"L\", (64, 40), 0x41).transpose(Image.Transpose.FLIP_LEFT_RIGHT)\n\u003e del a\n\u003e \n\u003e # Image B: 61 px wide, so each row has 3 bytes of padding; its pixels are all 0\n\u003e b = Image.new(\"L\", (61, 40), 0).transpose(Image.Transpose.FLIP_LEFT_RIGHT)\n\u003e \n\u003e try:\n\u003e     exported = Image.fromarrow(b, b.mode, b.size).tobytes()\n\u003e except ValueError as e:\n\u003e     print(\"Not vulnerable:\", e)\n\u003e else:\n\u003e     if leaked := len(exported) - exported.count(0):\n\u003e         raise RuntimeError(f\"{leaked} of {len(exported)} exported bytes are not B's pixels\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Information disclosure for applications that set `PILLOW_ALIGNMENT` and export images to Arrow. Likely any image whose row length isn't a multiple of the alignment will do. In a service handling several users' images, one user's exported data can contain pixels from another user's image, or other stale heap contents.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-654x-cwwv-5j9c | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-114501\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-114501](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-114501)**.\n\n## [GHSA-654x-cwwv-5j9c](https://osv.dev/GHSA-654x-cwwv-5j9c)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e When [`PILLOW_ALIGNMENT`](https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference/block_allocator.html#memory-pools) is set above 1, exporting an image to Arrow returns uninitialized heap memory mixed in with the pixel data. That memory can include pixels from images the process freed earlier. \n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e With an alignment above 1, the default allocator pads each row up to `aligned_linesize` and starts the first row at an aligned offset into the block. It then exports `im-\u003eblocks[0].ptr` as `xsize * ysize` tightly packed pixels, so exported data includes the padding bytes, and can include a few bytes from before the first row.\n\u003e \n\u003e Tested vulnerable on 11.2.1, 12.3.0, 4c1ed27a5.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e \n\u003e Whether things leak may depend on allocator configuration. This does show a leak on my Mac.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import os\n\u003e \n\u003e os.environ[\"PILLOW_ALIGNMENT\"] = \"64\"\n\u003e \n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e # Image A: 64 px wide, so its rows fill the whole aligned stride\n\u003e a = Image.new(\"L\", (64, 40), 0x41).transpose(Image.Transpose.FLIP_LEFT_RIGHT)\n\u003e del a\n\u003e \n\u003e # Image B: 61 px wide, so each row has 3 bytes of padding; its pixels are all 0\n\u003e b = Image.new(\"L\", (61, 40), 0).transpose(Image.Transpose.FLIP_LEFT_RIGHT)\n\u003e \n\u003e try:\n\u003e     exported = Image.fromarrow(b, b.mode, b.size).tobytes()\n\u003e except ValueError as e:\n\u003e     print(\"Not vulnerable:\", e)\n\u003e else:\n\u003e     if leaked := len(exported) - exported.count(0):\n\u003e         raise RuntimeError(f\"{leaked} of {len(exported)} exported bytes are not B's pixels\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Information disclosure for applications that set `PILLOW_ALIGNMENT` and export images to Arrow. Likely any image whose row length isn't a multiple of the alignment will do. In a service handling several users' images, one user's exported data can contain pixels from another user's image, or other stale heap contents.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-654x-cwwv-5j9c | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-114501\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
