              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nJupyterLab 4.5.0 enabled copying and pasing cells through the system clipboard. The paste path parses clipboard text as cell JSON and inserts the cells without clearing `metadata.trusted`, so a payload can declare its own output as trusted. JupyterLab does not sanitize a trusted output and evaluates the `\u003cscript\u003e` elements it contains, so pasting the cell runs attacker's JavaScript in the JupyterLab origin. The same payload with `\"trusted\": false` is sanitized.\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n    \"execution_count\": null,\n    \"source\": \"\",\n    \"metadata\": { \"trusted\": true },\n    \"outputs\": [\n      {\n        \"output_type\": \"display_data\",\n        \"metadata\": {},\n        \"data\": { \"text/html\": \"\u003cscript\u003e/* runs on paste */\u003c/script\u003e\" }\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nUsers are affected if all of the following are true:\n\n- they run JupyterLab 4.5.0 through 4.6.3, or an application that bundles it such as Notebook 7.5+;\n- if running 4.5.2 or newer, `useSystemClipboardForCells` is enabled - it is off by default in 4.5.2+, and always enabled on 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 with no opt-out\n- they run a Paste Cells command (e.g. from context menu, command palette, shortcut) while attacker-controlled text is in the clipboard; and\n- `pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput` is off, the default, so the pasted cell keeps its outputs\n\n\n### Impact\n\nThe script runs in the JupyterLab page, in the same origin and session as the authenticated user, so it can call the Jupyter Server REST API as that user: read and write any file under the server root, start a kernel and run code in it, and open a terminal where terminals are enabled. It runs on paste, without the user executing anything.\n\nOnly the output of a code cell can run a script this way. Markdown and raw cells are always sanitized, as the [trust documentation](https://jupyterlab.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/notebook.html#trust) states.\n\nThe attacker needs no JupyterLab account and no access to the server, only the user's clipboard at the moment of the paste. A web page can replace the text you copy from it, or write the clipboard directly once you have given it permission. Both need you to click or press a key on that page first.\n\n### Patches\n\nJupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. Cells read from the system clipboard that cannot be traced to the current window no longer carry `metadata.trusted` into the notebook, so their outputs are sanitized like any other content of unknown origin.\n\nUsers of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nOn 4.5.2 and later, confirm that the system clipboard is not in use for cells. The setting is off by default; check Settings, Notebook, or the raw value:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n    \"useSystemClipboardForCells\": false\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWith that setting off, cell copy and paste use JupyterLab's in-application clipboard, which never leaves the page. The cost is that cells can no longer be copied between browser tabs or windows.\n\nOn 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 there is no such setting, and every paste reads the system clipboard and upgrade is highly recommended.\n\nPasting cells without their outputs also stops the script from running, at the cost of discarding outputs on every paste:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n    \"pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput\": true\n  }\n}\n```",
                "text": "## Description\n\nJupyterLab 4.5.0 enabled copying and pasing cells through the system clipboard. The paste path parses clipboard text as cell JSON and inserts the cells without clearing `metadata.trusted`, so a payload can declare its own output as trusted. JupyterLab does not sanitize a trusted output and evaluates the `\u003cscript\u003e` elements it contains, so pasting the cell runs attacker's JavaScript in the JupyterLab origin. The same payload with `\"trusted\": false` is sanitized.\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n    \"execution_count\": null,\n    \"source\": \"\",\n    \"metadata\": { \"trusted\": true },\n    \"outputs\": [\n      {\n        \"output_type\": \"display_data\",\n        \"metadata\": {},\n        \"data\": { \"text/html\": \"\u003cscript\u003e/* runs on paste */\u003c/script\u003e\" }\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nUsers are affected if all of the following are true:\n\n- they run JupyterLab 4.5.0 through 4.6.3, or an application that bundles it such as Notebook 7.5+;\n- if running 4.5.2 or newer, `useSystemClipboardForCells` is enabled - it is off by default in 4.5.2+, and always enabled on 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 with no opt-out\n- they run a Paste Cells command (e.g. from context menu, command palette, shortcut) while attacker-controlled text is in the clipboard; and\n- `pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput` is off, the default, so the pasted cell keeps its outputs\n\n\n### Impact\n\nThe script runs in the JupyterLab page, in the same origin and session as the authenticated user, so it can call the Jupyter Server REST API as that user: read and write any file under the server root, start a kernel and run code in it, and open a terminal where terminals are enabled. It runs on paste, without the user executing anything.\n\nOnly the output of a code cell can run a script this way. Markdown and raw cells are always sanitized, as the [trust documentation](https://jupyterlab.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/notebook.html#trust) states.\n\nThe attacker needs no JupyterLab account and no access to the server, only the user's clipboard at the moment of the paste. A web page can replace the text you copy from it, or write the clipboard directly once you have given it permission. Both need you to click or press a key on that page first.\n\n### Patches\n\nJupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. Cells read from the system clipboard that cannot be traced to the current window no longer carry `metadata.trusted` into the notebook, so their outputs are sanitized like any other content of unknown origin.\n\nUsers of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nOn 4.5.2 and later, confirm that the system clipboard is not in use for cells. The setting is off by default; check Settings, Notebook, or the raw value:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n    \"useSystemClipboardForCells\": false\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWith that setting off, cell copy and paste use JupyterLab's in-application clipboard, which never leaves the page. The cost is that cells can no longer be copied between browser tabs or windows.\n\nOn 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 there is no such setting, and every paste reads the system clipboard and upgrade is highly recommended.\n\nPasting cells without their outputs also stops the script from running, at the cost of discarding outputs on every paste:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n    \"pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput\": true\n  }\n}\n```"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-102831](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-102831)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4057](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4057), [PYSEC-2026-4112](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4112), [GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv](https://osv.dev/GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4057](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4057)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Description\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab 4.5.0 enabled copying and pasing cells through the system clipboard. The paste path parses clipboard text as cell JSON and inserts the cells without clearing `metadata.trusted`, so a payload can declare its own output as trusted. JupyterLab does not sanitize a trusted output and evaluates the `\u003cscript\u003e` elements it contains, so pasting the cell runs attacker's JavaScript in the JupyterLab origin. The same payload with `\"trusted\": false` is sanitized.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e [\n\u003e   {\n\u003e     \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n\u003e     \"execution_count\": null,\n\u003e     \"source\": \"\",\n\u003e     \"metadata\": { \"trusted\": true },\n\u003e     \"outputs\": [\n\u003e       {\n\u003e         \"output_type\": \"display_data\",\n\u003e         \"metadata\": {},\n\u003e         \"data\": { \"text/html\": \"\u003cscript\u003e/* runs on paste */\u003c/script\u003e\" }\n\u003e       }\n\u003e     ]\n\u003e   }\n\u003e ]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Users are affected if all of the following are true:\n\u003e \n\u003e - they run JupyterLab 4.5.0 through 4.6.3, or an application that bundles it such as Notebook 7.5+;\n\u003e - if running 4.5.2 or newer, `useSystemClipboardForCells` is enabled - it is off by default in 4.5.2+, and always enabled on 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 with no opt-out\n\u003e - they run a Paste Cells command (e.g. from context menu, command palette, shortcut) while attacker-controlled text is in the clipboard; and\n\u003e - `pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput` is off, the default, so the pasted cell keeps its outputs\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e The script runs in the JupyterLab page, in the same origin and session as the authenticated user, so it can call the Jupyter Server REST API as that user: read and write any file under the server root, start a kernel and run code in it, and open a terminal where terminals are enabled. It runs on paste, without the user executing anything.\n\u003e \n\u003e Only the output of a code cell can run a script this way. Markdown and raw cells are always sanitized, as the [trust documentation](https://jupyterlab.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/notebook.html#trust) states.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attacker needs no JupyterLab account and no access to the server, only the user's clipboard at the moment of the paste. A web page can replace the text you copy from it, or write the clipboard directly once you have given it permission. Both need you to click or press a key on that page first.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. Cells read from the system clipboard that cannot be traced to the current window no longer carry `metadata.trusted` into the notebook, so their outputs are sanitized like any other content of unknown origin.\n\u003e \n\u003e Users of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e On 4.5.2 and later, confirm that the system clipboard is not in use for cells. The setting is off by default; check Settings, Notebook, or the raw value:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e {\n\u003e   \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n\u003e     \"useSystemClipboardForCells\": false\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e With that setting off, cell copy and paste use JupyterLab's in-application clipboard, which never leaves the page. The cost is that cells can no longer be copied between browser tabs or windows.\n\u003e \n\u003e On 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 there is no such setting, and every paste reads the system clipboard and upgrade is highly recommended.\n\u003e \n\u003e Pasting cells without their outputs also stops the script from running, at the cost of discarding outputs on every paste:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e {\n\u003e   \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n\u003e     \"pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput\": true\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4112](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4112)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Description\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab 4.5.0 enabled copying and pasing cells through the system clipboard. The paste path parses clipboard text as cell JSON and inserts the cells without clearing `metadata.trusted`, so a payload can declare its own output as trusted. JupyterLab does not sanitize a trusted output and evaluates the `\u003cscript\u003e` elements it contains, so pasting the cell runs attacker's JavaScript in the JupyterLab origin. The same payload with `\"trusted\": false` is sanitized.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e [\n\u003e   {\n\u003e     \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n\u003e     \"execution_count\": null,\n\u003e     \"source\": \"\",\n\u003e     \"metadata\": { \"trusted\": true },\n\u003e     \"outputs\": [\n\u003e       {\n\u003e         \"output_type\": \"display_data\",\n\u003e         \"metadata\": {},\n\u003e         \"data\": { \"text/html\": \"\u003cscript\u003e/* runs on paste */\u003c/script\u003e\" }\n\u003e       }\n\u003e     ]\n\u003e   }\n\u003e ]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Users are affected if all of the following are true:\n\u003e \n\u003e - they run JupyterLab 4.5.0 through 4.6.3, or an application that bundles it such as Notebook 7.5+;\n\u003e - if running 4.5.2 or newer, `useSystemClipboardForCells` is enabled - it is off by default in 4.5.2+, and always enabled on 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 with no opt-out\n\u003e - they run a Paste Cells command (e.g. from context menu, command palette, shortcut) while attacker-controlled text is in the clipboard; and\n\u003e - `pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput` is off, the default, so the pasted cell keeps its outputs\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e The script runs in the JupyterLab page, in the same origin and session as the authenticated user, so it can call the Jupyter Server REST API as that user: read and write any file under the server root, start a kernel and run code in it, and open a terminal where terminals are enabled. It runs on paste, without the user executing anything.\n\u003e \n\u003e Only the output of a code cell can run a script this way. Markdown and raw cells are always sanitized, as the [trust documentation](https://jupyterlab.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/notebook.html#trust) states.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attacker needs no JupyterLab account and no access to the server, only the user's clipboard at the moment of the paste. A web page can replace the text you copy from it, or write the clipboard directly once you have given it permission. Both need you to click or press a key on that page first.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. Cells read from the system clipboard that cannot be traced to the current window no longer carry `metadata.trusted` into the notebook, so their outputs are sanitized like any other content of unknown origin.\n\u003e \n\u003e Users of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e On 4.5.2 and later, confirm that the system clipboard is not in use for cells. The setting is off by default; check Settings, Notebook, or the raw value:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e {\n\u003e   \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n\u003e     \"useSystemClipboardForCells\": false\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e With that setting off, cell copy and paste use JupyterLab's in-application clipboard, which never leaves the page. The cost is that cells can no longer be copied between browser tabs or windows.\n\u003e \n\u003e On 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 there is no such setting, and every paste reads the system clipboard and upgrade is highly recommended.\n\u003e \n\u003e Pasting cells without their outputs also stops the script from running, at the cost of discarding outputs on every paste:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e {\n\u003e   \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n\u003e     \"pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput\": true\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv](https://osv.dev/GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Description\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab 4.5.0 enabled copying and pasing cells through the system clipboard. The paste path parses clipboard text as cell JSON and inserts the cells without clearing `metadata.trusted`, so a payload can declare its own output as trusted. JupyterLab does not sanitize a trusted output and evaluates the `\u003cscript\u003e` elements it contains, so pasting the cell runs attacker's JavaScript in the JupyterLab origin. The same payload with `\"trusted\": false` is sanitized.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e [\n\u003e   {\n\u003e     \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n\u003e     \"execution_count\": null,\n\u003e     \"source\": \"\",\n\u003e     \"metadata\": { \"trusted\": true },\n\u003e     \"outputs\": [\n\u003e       {\n\u003e         \"output_type\": \"display_data\",\n\u003e         \"metadata\": {},\n\u003e         \"data\": { \"text/html\": \"\u003cscript\u003e/* runs on paste */\u003c/script\u003e\" }\n\u003e       }\n\u003e     ]\n\u003e   }\n\u003e ]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Users are affected if all of the following are true:\n\u003e \n\u003e - they run JupyterLab 4.5.0 through 4.6.3, or an application that bundles it such as Notebook 7.5+;\n\u003e - if running 4.5.2 or newer, `useSystemClipboardForCells` is enabled - it is off by default in 4.5.2+, and always enabled on 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 with no opt-out\n\u003e - they run a Paste Cells command (e.g. from context menu, command palette, shortcut) while attacker-controlled text is in the clipboard; and\n\u003e - `pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput` is off, the default, so the pasted cell keeps its outputs\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e The script runs in the JupyterLab page, in the same origin and session as the authenticated user, so it can call the Jupyter Server REST API as that user: read and write any file under the server root, start a kernel and run code in it, and open a terminal where terminals are enabled. It runs on paste, without the user executing anything.\n\u003e \n\u003e Only the output of a code cell can run a script this way. Markdown and raw cells are always sanitized, as the [trust documentation](https://jupyterlab.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/notebook.html#trust) states.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attacker needs no JupyterLab account and no access to the server, only the user's clipboard at the moment of the paste. A web page can replace the text you copy from it, or write the clipboard directly once you have given it permission. Both need you to click or press a key on that page first.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. Cells read from the system clipboard that cannot be traced to the current window no longer carry `metadata.trusted` into the notebook, so their outputs are sanitized like any other content of unknown origin.\n\u003e \n\u003e Users of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e On 4.5.2 and later, confirm that the system clipboard is not in use for cells. The setting is off by default; check Settings, Notebook, or the raw value:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e {\n\u003e   \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n\u003e     \"useSystemClipboardForCells\": false\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e With that setting off, cell copy and paste use JupyterLab's in-application clipboard, which never leaves the page. The cost is that cells can no longer be copied between browser tabs or windows.\n\u003e \n\u003e On 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 there is no such setting, and every paste reads the system clipboard and upgrade is highly recommended.\n\u003e \n\u003e Pasting cells without their outputs also stops the script from running, at the cost of discarding outputs on every paste:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e {\n\u003e   \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n\u003e     \"pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput\": true\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | jupyterlab | 4.6.3 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | notebook | 7.6.2 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv | jupyterlab | 4.5.11, 4.6.4 |\n| GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv | notebook | 7.6.3 |\n| GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv | jupyterlite | 0.8.4 |\n| GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv | jupyterlite-core | 0.8.4 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4057 | jupyterlab | 4.5.11, 4.6.4 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4112 | notebook | 7.6.3 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102831\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-102831](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-102831)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4057](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4057), [PYSEC-2026-4112](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4112), [GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv](https://osv.dev/GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4057](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4057)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Description\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab 4.5.0 enabled copying and pasing cells through the system clipboard. The paste path parses clipboard text as cell JSON and inserts the cells without clearing `metadata.trusted`, so a payload can declare its own output as trusted. JupyterLab does not sanitize a trusted output and evaluates the `\u003cscript\u003e` elements it contains, so pasting the cell runs attacker's JavaScript in the JupyterLab origin. The same payload with `\"trusted\": false` is sanitized.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e [\n\u003e   {\n\u003e     \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n\u003e     \"execution_count\": null,\n\u003e     \"source\": \"\",\n\u003e     \"metadata\": { \"trusted\": true },\n\u003e     \"outputs\": [\n\u003e       {\n\u003e         \"output_type\": \"display_data\",\n\u003e         \"metadata\": {},\n\u003e         \"data\": { \"text/html\": \"\u003cscript\u003e/* runs on paste */\u003c/script\u003e\" }\n\u003e       }\n\u003e     ]\n\u003e   }\n\u003e ]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Users are affected if all of the following are true:\n\u003e \n\u003e - they run JupyterLab 4.5.0 through 4.6.3, or an application that bundles it such as Notebook 7.5+;\n\u003e - if running 4.5.2 or newer, `useSystemClipboardForCells` is enabled - it is off by default in 4.5.2+, and always enabled on 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 with no opt-out\n\u003e - they run a Paste Cells command (e.g. from context menu, command palette, shortcut) while attacker-controlled text is in the clipboard; and\n\u003e - `pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput` is off, the default, so the pasted cell keeps its outputs\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e The script runs in the JupyterLab page, in the same origin and session as the authenticated user, so it can call the Jupyter Server REST API as that user: read and write any file under the server root, start a kernel and run code in it, and open a terminal where terminals are enabled. It runs on paste, without the user executing anything.\n\u003e \n\u003e Only the output of a code cell can run a script this way. Markdown and raw cells are always sanitized, as the [trust documentation](https://jupyterlab.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/notebook.html#trust) states.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attacker needs no JupyterLab account and no access to the server, only the user's clipboard at the moment of the paste. A web page can replace the text you copy from it, or write the clipboard directly once you have given it permission. Both need you to click or press a key on that page first.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. Cells read from the system clipboard that cannot be traced to the current window no longer carry `metadata.trusted` into the notebook, so their outputs are sanitized like any other content of unknown origin.\n\u003e \n\u003e Users of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e On 4.5.2 and later, confirm that the system clipboard is not in use for cells. The setting is off by default; check Settings, Notebook, or the raw value:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e {\n\u003e   \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n\u003e     \"useSystemClipboardForCells\": false\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e With that setting off, cell copy and paste use JupyterLab's in-application clipboard, which never leaves the page. The cost is that cells can no longer be copied between browser tabs or windows.\n\u003e \n\u003e On 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 there is no such setting, and every paste reads the system clipboard and upgrade is highly recommended.\n\u003e \n\u003e Pasting cells without their outputs also stops the script from running, at the cost of discarding outputs on every paste:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e {\n\u003e   \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n\u003e     \"pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput\": true\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4112](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4112)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Description\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab 4.5.0 enabled copying and pasing cells through the system clipboard. The paste path parses clipboard text as cell JSON and inserts the cells without clearing `metadata.trusted`, so a payload can declare its own output as trusted. JupyterLab does not sanitize a trusted output and evaluates the `\u003cscript\u003e` elements it contains, so pasting the cell runs attacker's JavaScript in the JupyterLab origin. The same payload with `\"trusted\": false` is sanitized.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e [\n\u003e   {\n\u003e     \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n\u003e     \"execution_count\": null,\n\u003e     \"source\": \"\",\n\u003e     \"metadata\": { \"trusted\": true },\n\u003e     \"outputs\": [\n\u003e       {\n\u003e         \"output_type\": \"display_data\",\n\u003e         \"metadata\": {},\n\u003e         \"data\": { \"text/html\": \"\u003cscript\u003e/* runs on paste */\u003c/script\u003e\" }\n\u003e       }\n\u003e     ]\n\u003e   }\n\u003e ]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Users are affected if all of the following are true:\n\u003e \n\u003e - they run JupyterLab 4.5.0 through 4.6.3, or an application that bundles it such as Notebook 7.5+;\n\u003e - if running 4.5.2 or newer, `useSystemClipboardForCells` is enabled - it is off by default in 4.5.2+, and always enabled on 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 with no opt-out\n\u003e - they run a Paste Cells command (e.g. from context menu, command palette, shortcut) while attacker-controlled text is in the clipboard; and\n\u003e - `pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput` is off, the default, so the pasted cell keeps its outputs\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e The script runs in the JupyterLab page, in the same origin and session as the authenticated user, so it can call the Jupyter Server REST API as that user: read and write any file under the server root, start a kernel and run code in it, and open a terminal where terminals are enabled. It runs on paste, without the user executing anything.\n\u003e \n\u003e Only the output of a code cell can run a script this way. Markdown and raw cells are always sanitized, as the [trust documentation](https://jupyterlab.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/notebook.html#trust) states.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attacker needs no JupyterLab account and no access to the server, only the user's clipboard at the moment of the paste. A web page can replace the text you copy from it, or write the clipboard directly once you have given it permission. Both need you to click or press a key on that page first.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. Cells read from the system clipboard that cannot be traced to the current window no longer carry `metadata.trusted` into the notebook, so their outputs are sanitized like any other content of unknown origin.\n\u003e \n\u003e Users of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e On 4.5.2 and later, confirm that the system clipboard is not in use for cells. The setting is off by default; check Settings, Notebook, or the raw value:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e {\n\u003e   \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n\u003e     \"useSystemClipboardForCells\": false\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e With that setting off, cell copy and paste use JupyterLab's in-application clipboard, which never leaves the page. The cost is that cells can no longer be copied between browser tabs or windows.\n\u003e \n\u003e On 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 there is no such setting, and every paste reads the system clipboard and upgrade is highly recommended.\n\u003e \n\u003e Pasting cells without their outputs also stops the script from running, at the cost of discarding outputs on every paste:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e {\n\u003e   \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n\u003e     \"pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput\": true\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv](https://osv.dev/GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Description\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab 4.5.0 enabled copying and pasing cells through the system clipboard. The paste path parses clipboard text as cell JSON and inserts the cells without clearing `metadata.trusted`, so a payload can declare its own output as trusted. JupyterLab does not sanitize a trusted output and evaluates the `\u003cscript\u003e` elements it contains, so pasting the cell runs attacker's JavaScript in the JupyterLab origin. The same payload with `\"trusted\": false` is sanitized.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e [\n\u003e   {\n\u003e     \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n\u003e     \"execution_count\": null,\n\u003e     \"source\": \"\",\n\u003e     \"metadata\": { \"trusted\": true },\n\u003e     \"outputs\": [\n\u003e       {\n\u003e         \"output_type\": \"display_data\",\n\u003e         \"metadata\": {},\n\u003e         \"data\": { \"text/html\": \"\u003cscript\u003e/* runs on paste */\u003c/script\u003e\" }\n\u003e       }\n\u003e     ]\n\u003e   }\n\u003e ]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Users are affected if all of the following are true:\n\u003e \n\u003e - they run JupyterLab 4.5.0 through 4.6.3, or an application that bundles it such as Notebook 7.5+;\n\u003e - if running 4.5.2 or newer, `useSystemClipboardForCells` is enabled - it is off by default in 4.5.2+, and always enabled on 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 with no opt-out\n\u003e - they run a Paste Cells command (e.g. from context menu, command palette, shortcut) while attacker-controlled text is in the clipboard; and\n\u003e - `pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput` is off, the default, so the pasted cell keeps its outputs\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e The script runs in the JupyterLab page, in the same origin and session as the authenticated user, so it can call the Jupyter Server REST API as that user: read and write any file under the server root, start a kernel and run code in it, and open a terminal where terminals are enabled. It runs on paste, without the user executing anything.\n\u003e \n\u003e Only the output of a code cell can run a script this way. Markdown and raw cells are always sanitized, as the [trust documentation](https://jupyterlab.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/notebook.html#trust) states.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attacker needs no JupyterLab account and no access to the server, only the user's clipboard at the moment of the paste. A web page can replace the text you copy from it, or write the clipboard directly once you have given it permission. Both need you to click or press a key on that page first.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e JupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. Cells read from the system clipboard that cannot be traced to the current window no longer carry `metadata.trusted` into the notebook, so their outputs are sanitized like any other content of unknown origin.\n\u003e \n\u003e Users of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e On 4.5.2 and later, confirm that the system clipboard is not in use for cells. The setting is off by default; check Settings, Notebook, or the raw value:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e {\n\u003e   \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n\u003e     \"useSystemClipboardForCells\": false\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e With that setting off, cell copy and paste use JupyterLab's in-application clipboard, which never leaves the page. The cost is that cells can no longer be copied between browser tabs or windows.\n\u003e \n\u003e On 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 there is no such setting, and every paste reads the system clipboard and upgrade is highly recommended.\n\u003e \n\u003e Pasting cells without their outputs also stops the script from running, at the cost of discarding outputs on every paste:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e {\n\u003e   \"@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker\": {\n\u003e     \"pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput\": true\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | jupyterlab | 4.6.3 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | notebook | 7.6.2 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv | jupyterlab | 4.5.11, 4.6.4 |\n| GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv | notebook | 7.6.3 |\n| GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv | jupyterlite | 0.8.4 |\n| GHSA-6966-vjj6-99xv | jupyterlite-core | 0.8.4 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4057 | jupyterlab | 4.5.11, 4.6.4 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4112 | notebook | 7.6.3 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102831\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
