              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-53538](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-53538)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-3037](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-3037), [GHSA-6jv3-5f52-599m](https://osv.dev/GHSA-6jv3-5f52-599m), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-3037](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-3037)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `QuerystringParser` treated `;` as a field separator in `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` bodies, in addition to `\u0026`. The [WHATWG URL standard](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#urlencoded-parsing), modern browsers, and Python's `urllib.parse` (since the CVE-2021-23336 fix) treat only `\u0026` as a separator. This creates a parser differential: the same bytes are tokenized into different fields than a WHATWG compliant intermediary would produce, allowing an attacker to smuggle extra form fields past an upstream body inspecting component.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e In `python_multipart/multipart.py`, the `FIELD_NAME` and `FIELD_DATA` states located the next separator by scanning for `\u0026` and, failing that, for `;`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e sep_pos = data.find(b\"\u0026\", i)\n\u003e if sep_pos == -1:\n\u003e     sep_pos = data.find(b\";\", i)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e As a result, `;` acted as a field boundary. Because the fallback only triggered when no `\u0026` remained in the current chunk, tokenization also depended on unrelated bytes later in the buffer and on how the body was split across `write()` calls. This is the same class of issue as CVE-2021-23336 in CPython's `urllib.parse`.\n\u003e \n\u003e For example, a body inspecting WAF or gateway that follows the WHATWG rule (only `\u0026` separates fields) receives:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e role=user\u0026x=;role=admin\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The upstream parses two fields, `role=user` and `x=\";role=admin\"`, sees a benign `role=user`, and forwards the request. `QuerystringParser` parsed the same bytes as three fields: `role=\"user\"`, `x=\"\"`, and `role=\"admin\"`. The application (for example via Starlette/FastAPI `request.form()`, where the last value wins) then received `role=admin`, a value the upstream validator never saw.\n\u003e \n\u003e The parser is reachable through the public `QuerystringParser` class, the high level `FormParser`, `create_form_parser`, and `parse_form` APIs, and Starlette/FastAPI `request.form()` for url encoded bodies.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Interpretation conflict / HTTP parameter pollution. An attacker can smuggle extra or overriding form fields past an upstream component that applies the WHATWG separator rule, reaching the backend with parameters the intermediary did not observe.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to `python-multipart` `0.0.30` or later, which treats only `\u0026` as a field separator per the [WHATWG URL standard](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#urlencoded-parsing). `;` is parsed as ordinary field data, matching `urllib.parse`, browsers, and other compliant parsers.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-6jv3-5f52-599m](https://osv.dev/GHSA-6jv3-5f52-599m)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `QuerystringParser` treated `;` as a field separator in `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` bodies, in addition to `\u0026`. The [WHATWG URL standard](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#urlencoded-parsing), modern browsers, and Python's `urllib.parse` (since the CVE-2021-23336 fix) treat only `\u0026` as a separator. This creates a parser differential: the same bytes are tokenized into different fields than a WHATWG compliant intermediary would produce, allowing an attacker to smuggle extra form fields past an upstream body inspecting component.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e In `python_multipart/multipart.py`, the `FIELD_NAME` and `FIELD_DATA` states located the next separator by scanning for `\u0026` and, failing that, for `;`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e sep_pos = data.find(b\"\u0026\", i)\n\u003e if sep_pos == -1:\n\u003e     sep_pos = data.find(b\";\", i)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e As a result, `;` acted as a field boundary. Because the fallback only triggered when no `\u0026` remained in the current chunk, tokenization also depended on unrelated bytes later in the buffer and on how the body was split across `write()` calls. This is the same class of issue as CVE-2021-23336 in CPython's `urllib.parse`.\n\u003e \n\u003e For example, a body inspecting WAF or gateway that follows the WHATWG rule (only `\u0026` separates fields) receives:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e role=user\u0026x=;role=admin\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The upstream parses two fields, `role=user` and `x=\";role=admin\"`, sees a benign `role=user`, and forwards the request. `QuerystringParser` parsed the same bytes as three fields: `role=\"user\"`, `x=\"\"`, and `role=\"admin\"`. The application (for example via Starlette/FastAPI `request.form()`, where the last value wins) then received `role=admin`, a value the upstream validator never saw.\n\u003e \n\u003e The parser is reachable through the public `QuerystringParser` class, the high level `FormParser`, `create_form_parser`, and `parse_form` APIs, and Starlette/FastAPI `request.form()` for url encoded bodies.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Interpretation conflict / HTTP parameter pollution. An attacker can smuggle extra or overriding form fields past an upstream component that applies the WHATWG separator rule, reaching the backend with parameters the intermediary did not observe.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to `python-multipart` `0.0.30` or later, which treats only `\u0026` as a field separator per the [WHATWG URL standard](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#urlencoded-parsing). `;` is parsed as ordinary field data, matching `urllib.parse`, browsers, and other compliant parsers.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | python-multipart | 0.0.20 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-6jv3-5f52-599m | python-multipart | 0.0.30 |\n| PYSEC-2026-3037 | python-multipart | 0.0.30 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-53538\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-53538](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-53538)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-3037](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-3037), [GHSA-6jv3-5f52-599m](https://osv.dev/GHSA-6jv3-5f52-599m), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-3037](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-3037)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `QuerystringParser` treated `;` as a field separator in `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` bodies, in addition to `\u0026`. The [WHATWG URL standard](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#urlencoded-parsing), modern browsers, and Python's `urllib.parse` (since the CVE-2021-23336 fix) treat only `\u0026` as a separator. This creates a parser differential: the same bytes are tokenized into different fields than a WHATWG compliant intermediary would produce, allowing an attacker to smuggle extra form fields past an upstream body inspecting component.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e In `python_multipart/multipart.py`, the `FIELD_NAME` and `FIELD_DATA` states located the next separator by scanning for `\u0026` and, failing that, for `;`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e sep_pos = data.find(b\"\u0026\", i)\n\u003e if sep_pos == -1:\n\u003e     sep_pos = data.find(b\";\", i)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e As a result, `;` acted as a field boundary. Because the fallback only triggered when no `\u0026` remained in the current chunk, tokenization also depended on unrelated bytes later in the buffer and on how the body was split across `write()` calls. This is the same class of issue as CVE-2021-23336 in CPython's `urllib.parse`.\n\u003e \n\u003e For example, a body inspecting WAF or gateway that follows the WHATWG rule (only `\u0026` separates fields) receives:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e role=user\u0026x=;role=admin\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The upstream parses two fields, `role=user` and `x=\";role=admin\"`, sees a benign `role=user`, and forwards the request. `QuerystringParser` parsed the same bytes as three fields: `role=\"user\"`, `x=\"\"`, and `role=\"admin\"`. The application (for example via Starlette/FastAPI `request.form()`, where the last value wins) then received `role=admin`, a value the upstream validator never saw.\n\u003e \n\u003e The parser is reachable through the public `QuerystringParser` class, the high level `FormParser`, `create_form_parser`, and `parse_form` APIs, and Starlette/FastAPI `request.form()` for url encoded bodies.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Interpretation conflict / HTTP parameter pollution. An attacker can smuggle extra or overriding form fields past an upstream component that applies the WHATWG separator rule, reaching the backend with parameters the intermediary did not observe.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to `python-multipart` `0.0.30` or later, which treats only `\u0026` as a field separator per the [WHATWG URL standard](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#urlencoded-parsing). `;` is parsed as ordinary field data, matching `urllib.parse`, browsers, and other compliant parsers.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-6jv3-5f52-599m](https://osv.dev/GHSA-6jv3-5f52-599m)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `QuerystringParser` treated `;` as a field separator in `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` bodies, in addition to `\u0026`. The [WHATWG URL standard](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#urlencoded-parsing), modern browsers, and Python's `urllib.parse` (since the CVE-2021-23336 fix) treat only `\u0026` as a separator. This creates a parser differential: the same bytes are tokenized into different fields than a WHATWG compliant intermediary would produce, allowing an attacker to smuggle extra form fields past an upstream body inspecting component.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e In `python_multipart/multipart.py`, the `FIELD_NAME` and `FIELD_DATA` states located the next separator by scanning for `\u0026` and, failing that, for `;`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e sep_pos = data.find(b\"\u0026\", i)\n\u003e if sep_pos == -1:\n\u003e     sep_pos = data.find(b\";\", i)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e As a result, `;` acted as a field boundary. Because the fallback only triggered when no `\u0026` remained in the current chunk, tokenization also depended on unrelated bytes later in the buffer and on how the body was split across `write()` calls. This is the same class of issue as CVE-2021-23336 in CPython's `urllib.parse`.\n\u003e \n\u003e For example, a body inspecting WAF or gateway that follows the WHATWG rule (only `\u0026` separates fields) receives:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e role=user\u0026x=;role=admin\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The upstream parses two fields, `role=user` and `x=\";role=admin\"`, sees a benign `role=user`, and forwards the request. `QuerystringParser` parsed the same bytes as three fields: `role=\"user\"`, `x=\"\"`, and `role=\"admin\"`. The application (for example via Starlette/FastAPI `request.form()`, where the last value wins) then received `role=admin`, a value the upstream validator never saw.\n\u003e \n\u003e The parser is reachable through the public `QuerystringParser` class, the high level `FormParser`, `create_form_parser`, and `parse_form` APIs, and Starlette/FastAPI `request.form()` for url encoded bodies.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Interpretation conflict / HTTP parameter pollution. An attacker can smuggle extra or overriding form fields past an upstream component that applies the WHATWG separator rule, reaching the backend with parameters the intermediary did not observe.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to `python-multipart` `0.0.30` or later, which treats only `\u0026` as a field separator per the [WHATWG URL standard](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#urlencoded-parsing). `;` is parsed as ordinary field data, matching `urllib.parse`, browsers, and other compliant parsers.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | python-multipart | 0.0.20 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-6jv3-5f52-599m | python-multipart | 0.0.30 |\n| PYSEC-2026-3037 | python-multipart | 0.0.30 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-53538\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-53538",
              "name": "CVE-2026-53538",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.7"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-53538: python-multipart: Semicolon treated as querystring field separator enables parameter smuggling",
                "text": "CVE-2026-53538: python-multipart: Semicolon treated as querystring field separator enables parameter smuggling"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-110816",
                "GHSA-6pq8-jhm2-2vcw"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "## Summary\n\n`qs.stringify(obj, { arrayFormat: 'comma' })` throws an uncaught `TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value` when an array in `obj` contains an element that carries an own, non-callable `toString` property (for example `{ a: [ { toString: 'x' } ] }`). A remote, unauthenticated attacker who can influence an object that the application later re-serializes with the comma array format triggers the throw. In a service that lacks a synchronous error boundary around the serialization call, the uncaught exception crashes the worker process, producing a denial of service.\n\n## Root Cause\n\nWhen `arrayFormat: 'comma'` is selected, the comma branch collapses the whole array into a single value via `Array.prototype.join`, or, under `encodeValuesOnly: true`, maps each element through the value encoder before joining. Both paths perform an implicit string coercion (`ToPrimitive` with hint `\"string\"`) on each element. For an object whose own `toString` is a non-callable value, coercion skips the shadowed non-callable `toString`, falls through to `Object.prototype.valueOf` (which returns the object rather than a primitive), and throws. The comma branch runs this coercion before the per-element null and skip handling in the main loop, so nothing downstream can neutralize the hostile element, and the throw is never caught inside the library.\n\n```text\nlib/stringify.js  (comma-array branch)\n  line 152-154: obj = utils.maybeMap(obj, function (v) { return v == null ? v : encoder(v); });  // encodeValuesOnly path\n  line 156:     objKeys = [{ value: obj.length \u003e 0 ? obj.join(',') || null : void undefined }];   // default comma path\nlib/utils.js\n  line 292:     string = String(str);   // value encoder coercion reached via the encodeValuesOnly path\n```\n\n## Impact\n\nPrerequisites: the application calls `qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` (a documented, supported option) on an object whose shape can be influenced by attacker input. Attacker-controllable sources include `qs.parse(input, { allowPrototypes: true })`, which retains a `toString` segment (Express 4's default query parser, Express 5's `extended` query parser, and body-parser's `extended` urlencoded parser all set it), `qs.parse(input, { plainObjects: true })`, whose null-prototype elements throw even without a `toString` key (eg, `a[][b]=c`), and `JSON.parse` of a request body, which needs no qs parse option at all. No authentication or user interaction is required. Demonstrated capability: a single request forces an uncaught synchronous `TypeError` in the serialization call. In a naive single-process Node service with no synchronous error boundary, this crashes the process and takes the service offline (availability impact). Under a framework such as Express that traps synchronous handler throws, the plausible outcome is a per-request 500 rather than a full outage; that lower-impact case is noted but not the worst credible supported deployment. There is no confidentiality or integrity impact, no memory unsafety, and no prototype pollution.\n\n## Proof of Concept\n\n`Install qs at the affected version (for example 6.16.0) and run the following script with Node.js. No network setup is required; it demonstrates the throw in isolation.`\n\n```js\nvar qs = require('qs');\n\n// Source A: qs.parse with allowPrototypes retains the hostile toString segment\nqs.stringify(qs.parse('a[0][toString]=x', { allowPrototypes: true }), { arrayFormat: 'comma' });\n\n// Source B: a JSON request body, no qs parse option needed\nqs.stringify(JSON.parse('{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}'), { arrayFormat: 'comma' });\n\n// Also affected: the encodeValuesOnly sub-path\nqs.stringify(JSON.parse('{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}'), { arrayFormat: 'comma', encodeValuesOnly: true });\n```\n\n```text\nA default-join (allowPrototypes) =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\nB default-join (JSON.parse) =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\nC encodeValuesOnly path =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\nN2 callable toString =\u003e NO THROW; output=\"a=z\"\nN3 isBuffer vector (CVE-2026-82417) =\u003e NO THROW; output=\"a%5Bconstructor%5D%5BisBuffer%5D=x\"\nqs version: 6.16.0\n```\n\n## Attack Chain\n\n1. **Exposure:** A remote, unauthenticated request reaches an endpoint whose handler eventually serializes attacker-influenced data with `qs.stringify(obj, { arrayFormat: 'comma' })`.\n2. **Control:** The attacker supplies data that becomes `{ a: [ { toString: \u003cnon-callable value\u003e } ] }`, either via `a[0][toString]=x` parsed with `allowPrototypes`, or via a JSON body `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}`.\n3. **Path:** In `stringify`, the primitive/buffer guard at `lib/stringify.js:134` is false for an array, so control reaches the comma branch at `lib/stringify.js:149`.\n4. **Guard:** On the `encodeValuesOnly` path, the closure `v == null ? v : encoder(v)` at `lib/stringify.js:152-154` passes only `null`/`undefined` through; a non-null object still reaches the encoder. The default path skips that closure entirely and reaches `obj.join(',')` at `lib/stringify.js:156` with no guard at all.\n5. **Primitive:** The element is coerced to a string (`Array.prototype.join`, or `String(str)` at `lib/utils.js:292`). `ToPrimitive` skips the non-callable own `toString`, `valueOf` returns the object, and a `TypeError` is thrown.\n6. **Result:** The uncaught synchronous exception propagates out of the serialization call, failing the request and, in a service without a synchronous error boundary, crashing the process (denial of service).\n\n## Bypass Evidence\n\nThis is a different element type on the code path CVE-2026-8723 (GHSA-q8mj-m7cp-5q26) patched, plus the default comma path, which that fix never covered. CVE-2026-8723 addressed a `TypeError` crash on `null`/`undefined` entries in the comma plus `encodeValuesOnly` path. Its fix (commit 21f80b3) wraps the encoder in `function (v) { return v == null ? v : encoder(v); }`, so it only passes `null` and `undefined` through. A non-null element with a hostile own `toString` fails the `v == null` test, so the encoder still runs and still throws; the encodeValuesOnly case (case C above) throws on the exact fixed code. The default comma path (`encodeValuesOnly` unset, the library default) never entered that fix at all: `git log -S \"obj.join(','\" -- lib/stringify.js` shows no security fix has ever touched the `obj.join(',')` line, and cases A and B throw with `encodeValuesOnly` unset. The related isBuffer hardening for CVE-2026-82417 (commit e83d321) is a different sink at the `lib/stringify.js:134` guard, which is never entered for the array element; the negative control `{a:{constructor:{isBuffer:'x'}}}` serializes cleanly. Disproof attempts that failed: a `valueOf`-only object does not throw (it serializes to `[object Object]`), confirming the trigger is an element with no callable `toString` (an own non-callable `toString`, or a null-prototype object with no `toString` at all, as `qs.parse` produces under `plainObjects`); nested arrays (`{\"a\":[[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]]}`) throw the same way; a callable `toString` serializes cleanly; and the default (non-comma) array format serializes cleanly, confirming the comma format is required. All positive and negative cases were reproduced on the exact published 6.16.0 artifact.\n\n## Affected Versions\n\n- `Ecosystem: npm`\n- `Package: qs`\n- `Confirmed affected range: \u003e= 6.7.0, \u003c= 6.16.0`\n- `Latest release checked: 6.16.0 (npm latest; git tag v6.16.0)`\n- `Fix status: fixed in 6.16.1 (a952e75)`\n\nThe `arrayFormat: 'comma'` join sink was introduced in qs 6.7.0, so 6.7.0 is the lower boundary; releases before 6.7.0 lack the sink. No released version fixes this vector, and the latest published release (6.16.0) reproduces the throw, so the closed upper bound `\u003c= 6.16.0` covers every affected release. The `encodeValuesOnly` sub-path was added later, but the default comma-join vector already affects 6.7.0, so 6.7.0 governs the lower boundary.\n\n## Suggested Fix\n\nThe comma array serialization must not allow an implicit string coercion of an array element to throw an uncaught exception. Coercion of each comma element should be performed defensively (for example by wrapping the join and per-element encode so that a coercion failure is caught and handled), and this protection must cover both the default `obj.join(',')` path and the `encodeValuesOnly` encoder path, not only the previously patched `null`/`undefined` case. As an interim mitigation without a code change, applications can avoid `arrayFormat: 'comma'` when serializing data derived from untrusted input, sanitize parsed objects (avoid `allowPrototypes`/`plainObjects` on untrusted input and strip unexpected own properties), or wrap `qs.stringify` calls in a synchronous error boundary so a coercion failure cannot crash the process.\n\nReported by zx (GitHub: @manus-pi).\n\n---\n\n## Independent report by @Lucidsec\n\nThe following was reported separately, after this advisory, and is merged here as a duplicate.\n\n### Summary\n\nIn project [qs](https://github.com/ljharb/qs), in versions `\u003e= 6.7.0, \u003c= 6.16.0` (every version that supports `arrayFormat: 'comma'`), `qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` can throw an uncaught `TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value` when an array element has a non-callable own `toString`. It is fixed in 6.16.1. Related: CWE-248 and CWE-704.\n\n### Technical Details\n\n`qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` joins an array at `lib/stringify.js:156` via `obj.join(',')`. An element whose own `toString` is not callable makes `Array.prototype.join` throw. There is no type check and no catch on that path.\n\nA second path exists with `encodeValuesOnly`: at `lib/stringify.js:151-154`, `maybeMap` only skips `v == null`, then `lib/utils.js:292` does `String(str)` and throws the same `TypeError` (this second site exists since v6.11.1; before that, the same input throws at `join`).\n\nWho can trigger: someone who can shape an array element passed to `stringify`. The host must use non-default `arrayFormat: 'comma'`. Default `arrayFormat` `indices` does not fold arrays and is not affected. A parse-then-stringify path also needs `plainObjects: true` (null-prototype elements from `lib/parse.js:200`) or `allowPrototypes: true` (which Express 4's default query parser, Express 5's `extended` query parser, and body-parser's `extended` urlencoded parser all set). Default `qs.parse('a[0][toString]=x')` returns `{}` because the prototype guard drops `toString`. Direct JSON handed to `stringify` can supply `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}`.\n\nCause: the comma fold at `lib/stringify.js:149-156` collapses the array into a single value before the main loop at `lib/stringify.js:172-212`, so `skipNulls` and `strictNullHandling` only ever see the joined value, never individual elements. `isNonNullishPrimitive` / `isBuffer` at `lib/stringify.js:134` inspect the whole value, not each element. A normal object uses `Object.prototype.toString` and becomes `[object Object]`. An own non-callable `toString` is skipped, `valueOf` returns the object, and `join` throws. (The skipped TODO test at `test/stringify.js:684-689` uses a plain `{ b: 1 }` element, which does not throw; it covers the separate question of what comma format should output for object elements, not this crash.) Earlier comma crash fixes did not cover object elements.\n\n### Impact\n\nOne small input makes that serialization path throw a synchronous, catchable `TypeError`. Behind a framework error boundary (eg, a synchronous Express handler), the request fails with a 500 and the process survives. In a host with no error boundary (eg, a bare `http.createServer` handler), the uncaught exception exits the process (the client sees `ECONNRESET`). Availability only.\n\n### Proof of Concept\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eShow the proof of concept\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nChecked on the latest commit that was checked: `07b1d4d82c8f9301c105ea4b94fa2302cfd6e8b4`. Still present. Demonstrated.\n\n- Payload `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}` throws at `lib/stringify.js:156`.\n- Baseline `{\"a\":[\"x\",\"y\"]}` returns 200 with body `a=x%2Cy`.\n- A plain object element stringifies to `a=%5Bobject%20Object%5D` and does not throw.\n- `encodeValuesOnly: true` throws via `utils.js:292`.\n- `plainObjects` parse plus comma throws.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n### Suggested Fix\n\nBefore `join`, map elements with the existing primitive check; skip or replace non-primitives so `join` cannot throw. On the `encodeValuesOnly` path, do not send non-primitives into `String()`. Optionally guard `lib/utils.js:292`. Add new regression tests.",
                "text": "## Summary\n\n`qs.stringify(obj, { arrayFormat: 'comma' })` throws an uncaught `TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value` when an array in `obj` contains an element that carries an own, non-callable `toString` property (for example `{ a: [ { toString: 'x' } ] }`). A remote, unauthenticated attacker who can influence an object that the application later re-serializes with the comma array format triggers the throw. In a service that lacks a synchronous error boundary around the serialization call, the uncaught exception crashes the worker process, producing a denial of service.\n\n## Root Cause\n\nWhen `arrayFormat: 'comma'` is selected, the comma branch collapses the whole array into a single value via `Array.prototype.join`, or, under `encodeValuesOnly: true`, maps each element through the value encoder before joining. Both paths perform an implicit string coercion (`ToPrimitive` with hint `\"string\"`) on each element. For an object whose own `toString` is a non-callable value, coercion skips the shadowed non-callable `toString`, falls through to `Object.prototype.valueOf` (which returns the object rather than a primitive), and throws. The comma branch runs this coercion before the per-element null and skip handling in the main loop, so nothing downstream can neutralize the hostile element, and the throw is never caught inside the library.\n\n```text\nlib/stringify.js  (comma-array branch)\n  line 152-154: obj = utils.maybeMap(obj, function (v) { return v == null ? v : encoder(v); });  // encodeValuesOnly path\n  line 156:     objKeys = [{ value: obj.length \u003e 0 ? obj.join(',') || null : void undefined }];   // default comma path\nlib/utils.js\n  line 292:     string = String(str);   // value encoder coercion reached via the encodeValuesOnly path\n```\n\n## Impact\n\nPrerequisites: the application calls `qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` (a documented, supported option) on an object whose shape can be influenced by attacker input. Attacker-controllable sources include `qs.parse(input, { allowPrototypes: true })`, which retains a `toString` segment (Express 4's default query parser, Express 5's `extended` query parser, and body-parser's `extended` urlencoded parser all set it), `qs.parse(input, { plainObjects: true })`, whose null-prototype elements throw even without a `toString` key (eg, `a[][b]=c`), and `JSON.parse` of a request body, which needs no qs parse option at all. No authentication or user interaction is required. Demonstrated capability: a single request forces an uncaught synchronous `TypeError` in the serialization call. In a naive single-process Node service with no synchronous error boundary, this crashes the process and takes the service offline (availability impact). Under a framework such as Express that traps synchronous handler throws, the plausible outcome is a per-request 500 rather than a full outage; that lower-impact case is noted but not the worst credible supported deployment. There is no confidentiality or integrity impact, no memory unsafety, and no prototype pollution.\n\n## Proof of Concept\n\n`Install qs at the affected version (for example 6.16.0) and run the following script with Node.js. No network setup is required; it demonstrates the throw in isolation.`\n\n```js\nvar qs = require('qs');\n\n// Source A: qs.parse with allowPrototypes retains the hostile toString segment\nqs.stringify(qs.parse('a[0][toString]=x', { allowPrototypes: true }), { arrayFormat: 'comma' });\n\n// Source B: a JSON request body, no qs parse option needed\nqs.stringify(JSON.parse('{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}'), { arrayFormat: 'comma' });\n\n// Also affected: the encodeValuesOnly sub-path\nqs.stringify(JSON.parse('{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}'), { arrayFormat: 'comma', encodeValuesOnly: true });\n```\n\n```text\nA default-join (allowPrototypes) =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\nB default-join (JSON.parse) =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\nC encodeValuesOnly path =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\nN2 callable toString =\u003e NO THROW; output=\"a=z\"\nN3 isBuffer vector (CVE-2026-82417) =\u003e NO THROW; output=\"a%5Bconstructor%5D%5BisBuffer%5D=x\"\nqs version: 6.16.0\n```\n\n## Attack Chain\n\n1. **Exposure:** A remote, unauthenticated request reaches an endpoint whose handler eventually serializes attacker-influenced data with `qs.stringify(obj, { arrayFormat: 'comma' })`.\n2. **Control:** The attacker supplies data that becomes `{ a: [ { toString: \u003cnon-callable value\u003e } ] }`, either via `a[0][toString]=x` parsed with `allowPrototypes`, or via a JSON body `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}`.\n3. **Path:** In `stringify`, the primitive/buffer guard at `lib/stringify.js:134` is false for an array, so control reaches the comma branch at `lib/stringify.js:149`.\n4. **Guard:** On the `encodeValuesOnly` path, the closure `v == null ? v : encoder(v)` at `lib/stringify.js:152-154` passes only `null`/`undefined` through; a non-null object still reaches the encoder. The default path skips that closure entirely and reaches `obj.join(',')` at `lib/stringify.js:156` with no guard at all.\n5. **Primitive:** The element is coerced to a string (`Array.prototype.join`, or `String(str)` at `lib/utils.js:292`). `ToPrimitive` skips the non-callable own `toString`, `valueOf` returns the object, and a `TypeError` is thrown.\n6. **Result:** The uncaught synchronous exception propagates out of the serialization call, failing the request and, in a service without a synchronous error boundary, crashing the process (denial of service).\n\n## Bypass Evidence\n\nThis is a different element type on the code path CVE-2026-8723 (GHSA-q8mj-m7cp-5q26) patched, plus the default comma path, which that fix never covered. CVE-2026-8723 addressed a `TypeError` crash on `null`/`undefined` entries in the comma plus `encodeValuesOnly` path. Its fix (commit 21f80b3) wraps the encoder in `function (v) { return v == null ? v : encoder(v); }`, so it only passes `null` and `undefined` through. A non-null element with a hostile own `toString` fails the `v == null` test, so the encoder still runs and still throws; the encodeValuesOnly case (case C above) throws on the exact fixed code. The default comma path (`encodeValuesOnly` unset, the library default) never entered that fix at all: `git log -S \"obj.join(','\" -- lib/stringify.js` shows no security fix has ever touched the `obj.join(',')` line, and cases A and B throw with `encodeValuesOnly` unset. The related isBuffer hardening for CVE-2026-82417 (commit e83d321) is a different sink at the `lib/stringify.js:134` guard, which is never entered for the array element; the negative control `{a:{constructor:{isBuffer:'x'}}}` serializes cleanly. Disproof attempts that failed: a `valueOf`-only object does not throw (it serializes to `[object Object]`), confirming the trigger is an element with no callable `toString` (an own non-callable `toString`, or a null-prototype object with no `toString` at all, as `qs.parse` produces under `plainObjects`); nested arrays (`{\"a\":[[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]]}`) throw the same way; a callable `toString` serializes cleanly; and the default (non-comma) array format serializes cleanly, confirming the comma format is required. All positive and negative cases were reproduced on the exact published 6.16.0 artifact.\n\n## Affected Versions\n\n- `Ecosystem: npm`\n- `Package: qs`\n- `Confirmed affected range: \u003e= 6.7.0, \u003c= 6.16.0`\n- `Latest release checked: 6.16.0 (npm latest; git tag v6.16.0)`\n- `Fix status: fixed in 6.16.1 (a952e75)`\n\nThe `arrayFormat: 'comma'` join sink was introduced in qs 6.7.0, so 6.7.0 is the lower boundary; releases before 6.7.0 lack the sink. No released version fixes this vector, and the latest published release (6.16.0) reproduces the throw, so the closed upper bound `\u003c= 6.16.0` covers every affected release. The `encodeValuesOnly` sub-path was added later, but the default comma-join vector already affects 6.7.0, so 6.7.0 governs the lower boundary.\n\n## Suggested Fix\n\nThe comma array serialization must not allow an implicit string coercion of an array element to throw an uncaught exception. Coercion of each comma element should be performed defensively (for example by wrapping the join and per-element encode so that a coercion failure is caught and handled), and this protection must cover both the default `obj.join(',')` path and the `encodeValuesOnly` encoder path, not only the previously patched `null`/`undefined` case. As an interim mitigation without a code change, applications can avoid `arrayFormat: 'comma'` when serializing data derived from untrusted input, sanitize parsed objects (avoid `allowPrototypes`/`plainObjects` on untrusted input and strip unexpected own properties), or wrap `qs.stringify` calls in a synchronous error boundary so a coercion failure cannot crash the process.\n\nReported by zx (GitHub: @manus-pi).\n\n---\n\n## Independent report by @Lucidsec\n\nThe following was reported separately, after this advisory, and is merged here as a duplicate.\n\n### Summary\n\nIn project [qs](https://github.com/ljharb/qs), in versions `\u003e= 6.7.0, \u003c= 6.16.0` (every version that supports `arrayFormat: 'comma'`), `qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` can throw an uncaught `TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value` when an array element has a non-callable own `toString`. It is fixed in 6.16.1. Related: CWE-248 and CWE-704.\n\n### Technical Details\n\n`qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` joins an array at `lib/stringify.js:156` via `obj.join(',')`. An element whose own `toString` is not callable makes `Array.prototype.join` throw. There is no type check and no catch on that path.\n\nA second path exists with `encodeValuesOnly`: at `lib/stringify.js:151-154`, `maybeMap` only skips `v == null`, then `lib/utils.js:292` does `String(str)` and throws the same `TypeError` (this second site exists since v6.11.1; before that, the same input throws at `join`).\n\nWho can trigger: someone who can shape an array element passed to `stringify`. The host must use non-default `arrayFormat: 'comma'`. Default `arrayFormat` `indices` does not fold arrays and is not affected. A parse-then-stringify path also needs `plainObjects: true` (null-prototype elements from `lib/parse.js:200`) or `allowPrototypes: true` (which Express 4's default query parser, Express 5's `extended` query parser, and body-parser's `extended` urlencoded parser all set). Default `qs.parse('a[0][toString]=x')` returns `{}` because the prototype guard drops `toString`. Direct JSON handed to `stringify` can supply `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}`.\n\nCause: the comma fold at `lib/stringify.js:149-156` collapses the array into a single value before the main loop at `lib/stringify.js:172-212`, so `skipNulls` and `strictNullHandling` only ever see the joined value, never individual elements. `isNonNullishPrimitive` / `isBuffer` at `lib/stringify.js:134` inspect the whole value, not each element. A normal object uses `Object.prototype.toString` and becomes `[object Object]`. An own non-callable `toString` is skipped, `valueOf` returns the object, and `join` throws. (The skipped TODO test at `test/stringify.js:684-689` uses a plain `{ b: 1 }` element, which does not throw; it covers the separate question of what comma format should output for object elements, not this crash.) Earlier comma crash fixes did not cover object elements.\n\n### Impact\n\nOne small input makes that serialization path throw a synchronous, catchable `TypeError`. Behind a framework error boundary (eg, a synchronous Express handler), the request fails with a 500 and the process survives. In a host with no error boundary (eg, a bare `http.createServer` handler), the uncaught exception exits the process (the client sees `ECONNRESET`). Availability only.\n\n### Proof of Concept\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eShow the proof of concept\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nChecked on the latest commit that was checked: `07b1d4d82c8f9301c105ea4b94fa2302cfd6e8b4`. Still present. Demonstrated.\n\n- Payload `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}` throws at `lib/stringify.js:156`.\n- Baseline `{\"a\":[\"x\",\"y\"]}` returns 200 with body `a=x%2Cy`.\n- A plain object element stringifies to `a=%5Bobject%20Object%5D` and does not throw.\n- `encodeValuesOnly: true` throws via `utils.js:292`.\n- `plainObjects` parse plus comma throws.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n### Suggested Fix\n\nBefore `join`, map elements with the existing primitive check; skip or replace non-primitives so `join` cannot throw. On the `encodeValuesOnly` path, do not send non-primitives into `String()`. Optionally guard `lib/utils.js:292`. Add new regression tests."
