              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "```\n============================================================================\nQuadratic GIF comment concatenation allows DoS in Image.open()\nLocation: src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py:266  in GifImageFile._seek()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nOpening a crafted GIF file with `Image.open()` takes time that grows with the square of the file's size, so anyone who can submit an image to a service that uses Pillow can keep one worker busy at full processor load for a long time with a small file. A 2 MB file takes about 5 seconds to open, a 4 MB file about 35 seconds, and an 8 MB file about 3 minutes. An ordinary GIF of the same size opens in well under a second. The cost is paid inside `Image.open()` itself, with default settings, before the application has seen the image's size or format.\n\nThe bug is in `GifImageFile._seek()` in `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py`, which builds `info[\"comment\"]` by appending to an immutable `bytes` object with no size limit. It appends once per 255-byte sub-block (`comment += block`, line 261) and once per comment extension (`info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment`, line 266). Each append copies everything collected so far. A file made of many tiny comment extensions forces a full re-copy for each one. Because `_open()` ends with `self._seek(0)`, any code that only opens an uploaded file to identify it, read its dimensions, or make a thumbnail pays the full cost. The decompression-bomb check does not help, since the image is 1×1. Memory use stays linear, so the effect is processor exhaustion and latency, bounded by whatever upload size the service allows (about 1 second at 1 MB). This is the same kind of issue as the EPS open-phase slowdown fixed as CVE-2021-28677, here in the GIF comment handling. The quadratic pattern was introduced in commit b2b737b68b06 (\"Allow arbitrary number of comment extension subblocks\", November 2018) and has shipped since Pillow 5.4.0. The attached script builds 2 MB and 4 MB test files and compares each against a byte-identical control whose extensions are labelled as plain text instead of comment. The 4 MB comment file took about 34 seconds to open, against 0.26 seconds for its control. A patch that collects the pieces in a list and joins them once is also attached.\n\n## Details\n\n`GifImageFile._seek()` collects GIF comment extensions into `info[\"comment\"]` using `bytes` concatenation, once per 255-byte sub-block and once per comment extension, with no limit on the total. `bytes` is immutable, so every `+=` allocates a new object and copies everything accumulated so far; the work done by `Image.open()` is therefore quadratic in the amount of comment data in frame 0.\n\nA 1×1 GIF89a whose body is nothing but the 5-byte comment extension `21 FE 01 41 00` repeated makes this directly observable. `Image.open(BytesIO(data))` on such a file, stock build at this commit, 2-CPU machine:\n\n| file size | comment extensions (`21 FE …`) | same bytes labelled plain-text extension (`21 01 …`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 MB | 1.2 s | 0.09 s |\n| 2 MB | 4.9 s | 0.13 s |\n| 4 MB | 35 s | 0.26 s |\n| 8 MB | 183 s | — |\n\nThe control column is the identical byte stream with the extension label changed from `0xFE` to `0x01`; it goes through the same loop and the same `self.data()` reads but is discarded instead of concatenated, and scales linearly. No `load()` is needed and no warning or exception is raised; `im.info[\"comment\"]` simply ends up holding the multi-megabyte blob.\n\nThe code, `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py:229-281` (trimmed):\n\n```python\n            elif s == b\"!\":\n                s = self.fp.read(1)\n                block = self.data()\n                …\n                elif s[0] == 254:\n                    #\n                    # comment extension\n                    #\n                    comment = b\"\"\n\n                    # Read this comment block\n                    while block:\n                        comment += block                      # :261  one copy per sub-block\n                        block = self.data()\n\n                    if \"comment\" in info:\n                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n                        info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment    # :266  one copy per extension\n                    else:\n                        info[\"comment\"] = comment\n                    s = b\"\"\n                    continue\n                …\n                while self.data():                            # every other extension: read and drop\n                    pass\n```\n\n`self.data()` (`:97-102`) reads one length byte and then that many bytes, so each call is cheap and bounded; the cost is entirely in the two `+=` lines. For a single comment extension of N bytes delivered as N/255 sub-blocks, line 261 copies about N²/510 bytes in total (4 MB → 2.3 s, 8 MB → 9.5 s, 16 MB → 38 s measured). Splitting the same data into many small comment extensions is worse, because line 266 re-copies the whole of `info[\"comment\"]` once per extension: with 1-byte extensions a file of F bytes causes roughly F²/25 bytes of copying, which is the table above (growth ×4–7 per doubling once the buffer outgrows the CPU caches).\n\nThis runs during `Image.open()`: `_open()` ends with `self._seek(0)` (`:135`), so any application that merely opens an uploaded file to identify it, read its size, or thumbnail it pays the full cost before it has made any decision about the image. `Image._decompression_bomb_check` is not involved (the image is 1×1), and unlike PNG text chunks (`PngImagePlugin.MAX_TEXT_CHUNK` / `MAX_TEXT_MEMORY`) there is no cap on GIF comment size. Later frames repeat the same loop for their own comments via `seek()` / `n_frames`.\n\nWith both `+=` sites replaced by list-append and a single `b\"\".join()` (as in the attached patch), the same 16 MB single-extension file opens in 0.03 s, confirming the concatenation is the whole cost.\n\n## Impact\n\nAnyone who can get a GIF to a service that calls `PIL.Image.open()` on it — avatar/media uploads, thumbnailers, format sniffing, anything using `ImageOps`/`thumbnail()` on user files — can pin one worker process at 100 % CPU for about 5 s with a 2 MB file, 35 s with a 4 MB file and 3 minutes with an 8 MB file, under default settings, with the time growing quadratically in the permitted upload size. A benign GIF of the same size opens in well under a second, so request-cost assumptions based on file size do not hold. Memory use is only linear (the comment is retained in `im.info[\"comment\"]`), so the effect is CPU/latency denial of service of the worker pool rather than memory exhaustion; a handful of concurrent uploads is enough to saturate a typical pool. Upload size limits bound it (≈1 s at 1 MB).\n\nThis is the same shape as CVE-2021-28677 (\"EPS DOS on _open\", `docs/releasenotes/8.2.0.rst`), where a quadratic method of accumulating data in the open phase, before the image was accepted, was treated as a denial-of-service vulnerability; here the format is GIF and the accumulator is the comment extension.\n\nThe quadratic accumulation was introduced in `b2b737b68b06` (\"Allow arbitrary number of comment extension subblocks\", 2018-11-27), first released in Pillow 5.4.0.\n\n## Reproducing\n\n`repro` is a single Python script that uses the installed Pillow. Run it with no arguments. It needs no files, no network and no rebuild.\n\nIt builds two GIFs of the same length, at 2 MB and at 4 MB. Each is the 13-byte GIF89a header for a 1×1 screen, followed by `size/5` copies of the 5-byte extension `21 \u003clabel\u003e 01 41 00`, then a minimal 1×1 image and the trailer:\n\n- **comments**: label `0xFE`. Each extension is a 1-byte comment, so `_seek()` appends each one to `info[\"comment\"]` with `+=` (`GifImagePlugin.py:266`).\n- **control**: label `0x01` (plain-text extension). The bytes are the same apart from the label. `_seek()` reads them through the same loop and the same `self.data()` calls, then throws them away.\n\nThe script times `Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))` on each file. It does not call `load()`, because `_open()` already parses frame 0. It checks that the image came out as 1×1. Typical output on a 2-CPU machine:\n\n```\n2 MB GIF   control (0x01):   0.16 s   comments (0xFE):   5.14 s\n4 MB GIF   control (0x01):   0.26 s   comments (0xFE):  33.53 s\ndoubling 2 MB -\u003e 4 MB multiplied open() time by x6.5 (linear: ~x2)\n4 MB comment GIF took x129 as long as the same-size control\nBUG\n```\n\nFor a linear parser, doubling the file would roughly double the time, and the two files would take about the same time. The script prints `BUG` when all three of these hold:\n\n- doubling the comment file from 2 MB to 4 MB multiplies its open time by at least 3×;\n- the 4 MB comment file takes at least 20× as long as its control;\n- the 4 MB comment file takes at least 8 s.\n\nOtherwise it prints `OK`. The run is deterministic, CPU-bound on one core, and takes about 40 s.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nThe attached patch changes `GifImageFile._seek()` in `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py` to accumulate comment data in a local list instead of with `bytes` concatenation, and to join it once when the frame's info is committed:\n\n```diff\n@@ def _seek(self, frame: int, update_image: bool = True) -\u003e None:\n         info: dict[str, Any] = {}\n+        comment_blocks: list[bytes] | None = None\n         frame_transparency = None\n@@\n                 elif s[0] == 254:\n                     #\n                     # comment extension\n                     #\n-                    comment = b\"\"\n+                    if comment_blocks is None:\n+                        comment_blocks = []\n+                    else:\n+                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n+                        comment_blocks.append(b\"\\n\")\n \n                     # Read this comment block\n                     while block:\n-                        comment += block\n+                        comment_blocks.append(block)\n                         block = self.data()\n-\n-                    if \"comment\" in info:\n-                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n-                        info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment\n-                    else:\n-                        info[\"comment\"] = comment\n                     s = b\"\"\n                     continue\n@@\n-        if info.get(\"comment\"):\n-            self.info[\"comment\"] = info[\"comment\"]\n+        if comment_blocks:\n+            comment = b\"\".join(comment_blocks)\n+            if comment:\n+                self.info[\"comment\"] = comment\n         for k in [\"duration\", \"extension\"]:\n```\n\nThis removes the quadratic copying at both sites (`:261` per sub-block and `:266` per extension); the 4 MB reproducer file opens in about 0.3 s, the same as its control, and the reproducer no longer prints `BUG` on a patched build. The resulting `info[\"comment\"]` is unchanged for empty, multi-sub-block, multi-extension and later-frame comments — including `Tests/images/multiple_comments.gif` → `b\"Test comment 1\\nTest comment 2\"` — and `Tests/test_file_gif.py` passes unchanged. The GIF writer (`_write_local_header` / `_save`) is not affected.\n\nThe patch deliberately adds no size cap. Unlike PNG `zTXt`, which `PngImagePlugin.MAX_TEXT_CHUNK` guards against decompression amplification, a GIF comment can be no larger than the file that carries it, so once the copy is linear there is nothing left to bound, and a hard cap would reject valid files with large comments. If a limit is wanted anyway, a `MAX_COMMENT_SIZE` check inside the sub-block loop is a small policy addition on top of this patch.\n\n## The patch\n\nGIF: collect comment extension sub-blocks in a list and join once per frame instead of repeated bytes += in GifImageFile._seek()\n\nApplies to `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py\nindex 11fb180fc..05007d2ac 100644\n--- a/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py\n+++ b/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py\n@@ -217,6 +217,7 @@ class GifImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n         palette: ImagePalette.ImagePalette | Literal[False] | None = None\n \n         info: dict[str, Any] = {}\n+        comment_blocks: list[bytes] | None = None\n         frame_transparency = None\n         interlace = None\n         frame_dispose_extent = None\n@@ -254,18 +255,16 @@ class GifImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n                     #\n                     # comment extension\n                     #\n-                    comment = b\"\"\n+                    if comment_blocks is None:\n+                        comment_blocks = []\n+                    else:\n+                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n+                        comment_blocks.append(b\"\\n\")\n \n                     # Read this comment block\n                     while block:\n-                        comment += block\n+                        comment_blocks.append(block)\n                         block = self.data()\n-\n-                    if \"comment\" in info:\n-                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n-                        info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment\n-                    else:\n-                        info[\"comment\"] = comment\n                     s = b\"\"\n                     continue\n                 elif s[0] == 255 and frame == 0 and block is not None:\n@@ -433,8 +432,10 @@ class GifImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n                 )\n             ]\n \n-        if info.get(\"comment\"):\n-            self.info[\"comment\"] = info[\"comment\"]\n+        if comment_blocks:\n+            comment = b\"\".join(comment_blocks)\n+            if comment:\n+                self.info[\"comment\"] = comment\n         for k in [\"duration\", \"extension\"]:\n             if k in info:\n                 self.info[k] = info[k]\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 2,263 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Quadratic CPU in Image.open() on GIF comment extensions.\n\nGifImageFile._seek() appends every comment extension to info[\"comment\"] with\nbytes `+=` (src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py:266), copying the whole accumulated\ncomment each time.  Image.open() runs _seek(0) eagerly, so merely opening a GIF\nmade of many tiny comment extensions costs O(size^2) CPU.\n\nWe time Image.open() on two files of identical length:\n  ATTACK  - a 1x1 GIF whose body is thousands of 1-byte comment extensions (label 0xFE)\n  CONTROL - the same bytes with label 0x01 (plain-text extension), which _seek()\n            reads through the same loop but discards instead of concatenating.\nA linear parser would take ~2x longer when the file doubles, and the two files\nwould cost about the same.\n\"\"\"\nimport io\nimport time\n\nfrom PIL import Image\n\nHEADER = b\"GIF89a\" + b\"\\x01\\x00\\x01\\x00\" + b\"\\x00\\x00\\x00\"  # 1x1 screen, no palette\nIMAGE = b\",\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x01\\x00\\x01\\x00\\x00\" + b\"\\x02\\x02\\x44\\x01\\x00\"  # 1x1 frame\nTRAILER = b\";\"\n\n\ndef make_gif(size: int, label: int) -\u003e bytes:\n    # each extension is `21 \u003clabel\u003e 01 'A' 00`: one 1-byte sub-block, then terminator\n    extension = b\"!\" + bytes([label]) + b\"\\x01A\\x00\"\n    return HEADER + extension * (size // 5) + IMAGE + TRAILER\n\n\ndef time_open(data: bytes) -\u003e float:\n    start = time.perf_counter()\n    with Image.open(io.BytesIO(data)) as im:  # no load(): the cost is in open()\n        elapsed = time.perf_counter() - start\n        assert im.size == (1, 1)\n    return elapsed\n\n\nMB = 1 \u003c\u003c 20\nattack, control = {}, {}\nfor size in (2 * MB, 4 * MB):\n    control[size] = time_open(make_gif(size, 0x01))\n    attack[size] = time_open(make_gif(size, 0xFE))\n    print(f\"{size // MB} MB GIF   control (0x01): {control[size]:6.2f} s   \"\n          f\"comments (0xFE): {attack[size]:6.2f} s\", flush=True)\n\ngrowth = attack[4 * MB] / attack[2 * MB]\nvs_control = attack[4 * MB] / control[4 * MB]\nprint(f\"doubling 2 MB -\u003e 4 MB multiplied open() time by x{growth:.1f} (linear: ~x2)\")\nprint(f\"4 MB comment GIF took x{vs_control:.0f} as long as the same-size control\")\n\n# BUG: super-linear growth, far slower than the control, and seconds of CPU per open()\nprint(\"BUG\" if growth \u003e= 3 and vs_control \u003e= 20 and attack[4 * MB] \u003e= 8 else \"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---",
                "text": "```\n============================================================================\nQuadratic GIF comment concatenation allows DoS in Image.open()\nLocation: src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py:266  in GifImageFile._seek()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nOpening a crafted GIF file with `Image.open()` takes time that grows with the square of the file's size, so anyone who can submit an image to a service that uses Pillow can keep one worker busy at full processor load for a long time with a small file. A 2 MB file takes about 5 seconds to open, a 4 MB file about 35 seconds, and an 8 MB file about 3 minutes. An ordinary GIF of the same size opens in well under a second. The cost is paid inside `Image.open()` itself, with default settings, before the application has seen the image's size or format.\n\nThe bug is in `GifImageFile._seek()` in `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py`, which builds `info[\"comment\"]` by appending to an immutable `bytes` object with no size limit. It appends once per 255-byte sub-block (`comment += block`, line 261) and once per comment extension (`info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment`, line 266). Each append copies everything collected so far. A file made of many tiny comment extensions forces a full re-copy for each one. Because `_open()` ends with `self._seek(0)`, any code that only opens an uploaded file to identify it, read its dimensions, or make a thumbnail pays the full cost. The decompression-bomb check does not help, since the image is 1×1. Memory use stays linear, so the effect is processor exhaustion and latency, bounded by whatever upload size the service allows (about 1 second at 1 MB). This is the same kind of issue as the EPS open-phase slowdown fixed as CVE-2021-28677, here in the GIF comment handling. The quadratic pattern was introduced in commit b2b737b68b06 (\"Allow arbitrary number of comment extension subblocks\", November 2018) and has shipped since Pillow 5.4.0. The attached script builds 2 MB and 4 MB test files and compares each against a byte-identical control whose extensions are labelled as plain text instead of comment. The 4 MB comment file took about 34 seconds to open, against 0.26 seconds for its control. A patch that collects the pieces in a list and joins them once is also attached.\n\n## Details\n\n`GifImageFile._seek()` collects GIF comment extensions into `info[\"comment\"]` using `bytes` concatenation, once per 255-byte sub-block and once per comment extension, with no limit on the total. `bytes` is immutable, so every `+=` allocates a new object and copies everything accumulated so far; the work done by `Image.open()` is therefore quadratic in the amount of comment data in frame 0.\n\nA 1×1 GIF89a whose body is nothing but the 5-byte comment extension `21 FE 01 41 00` repeated makes this directly observable. `Image.open(BytesIO(data))` on such a file, stock build at this commit, 2-CPU machine:\n\n| file size | comment extensions (`21 FE …`) | same bytes labelled plain-text extension (`21 01 …`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 MB | 1.2 s | 0.09 s |\n| 2 MB | 4.9 s | 0.13 s |\n| 4 MB | 35 s | 0.26 s |\n| 8 MB | 183 s | — |\n\nThe control column is the identical byte stream with the extension label changed from `0xFE` to `0x01`; it goes through the same loop and the same `self.data()` reads but is discarded instead of concatenated, and scales linearly. No `load()` is needed and no warning or exception is raised; `im.info[\"comment\"]` simply ends up holding the multi-megabyte blob.\n\nThe code, `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py:229-281` (trimmed):\n\n```python\n            elif s == b\"!\":\n                s = self.fp.read(1)\n                block = self.data()\n                …\n                elif s[0] == 254:\n                    #\n                    # comment extension\n                    #\n                    comment = b\"\"\n\n                    # Read this comment block\n                    while block:\n                        comment += block                      # :261  one copy per sub-block\n                        block = self.data()\n\n                    if \"comment\" in info:\n                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n                        info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment    # :266  one copy per extension\n                    else:\n                        info[\"comment\"] = comment\n                    s = b\"\"\n                    continue\n                …\n                while self.data():                            # every other extension: read and drop\n                    pass\n```\n\n`self.data()` (`:97-102`) reads one length byte and then that many bytes, so each call is cheap and bounded; the cost is entirely in the two `+=` lines. For a single comment extension of N bytes delivered as N/255 sub-blocks, line 261 copies about N²/510 bytes in total (4 MB → 2.3 s, 8 MB → 9.5 s, 16 MB → 38 s measured). Splitting the same data into many small comment extensions is worse, because line 266 re-copies the whole of `info[\"comment\"]` once per extension: with 1-byte extensions a file of F bytes causes roughly F²/25 bytes of copying, which is the table above (growth ×4–7 per doubling once the buffer outgrows the CPU caches).\n\nThis runs during `Image.open()`: `_open()` ends with `self._seek(0)` (`:135`), so any application that merely opens an uploaded file to identify it, read its size, or thumbnail it pays the full cost before it has made any decision about the image. `Image._decompression_bomb_check` is not involved (the image is 1×1), and unlike PNG text chunks (`PngImagePlugin.MAX_TEXT_CHUNK` / `MAX_TEXT_MEMORY`) there is no cap on GIF comment size. Later frames repeat the same loop for their own comments via `seek()` / `n_frames`.\n\nWith both `+=` sites replaced by list-append and a single `b\"\".join()` (as in the attached patch), the same 16 MB single-extension file opens in 0.03 s, confirming the concatenation is the whole cost.\n\n## Impact\n\nAnyone who can get a GIF to a service that calls `PIL.Image.open()` on it — avatar/media uploads, thumbnailers, format sniffing, anything using `ImageOps`/`thumbnail()` on user files — can pin one worker process at 100 % CPU for about 5 s with a 2 MB file, 35 s with a 4 MB file and 3 minutes with an 8 MB file, under default settings, with the time growing quadratically in the permitted upload size. A benign GIF of the same size opens in well under a second, so request-cost assumptions based on file size do not hold. Memory use is only linear (the comment is retained in `im.info[\"comment\"]`), so the effect is CPU/latency denial of service of the worker pool rather than memory exhaustion; a handful of concurrent uploads is enough to saturate a typical pool. Upload size limits bound it (≈1 s at 1 MB).\n\nThis is the same shape as CVE-2021-28677 (\"EPS DOS on _open\", `docs/releasenotes/8.2.0.rst`), where a quadratic method of accumulating data in the open phase, before the image was accepted, was treated as a denial-of-service vulnerability; here the format is GIF and the accumulator is the comment extension.\n\nThe quadratic accumulation was introduced in `b2b737b68b06` (\"Allow arbitrary number of comment extension subblocks\", 2018-11-27), first released in Pillow 5.4.0.\n\n## Reproducing\n\n`repro` is a single Python script that uses the installed Pillow. Run it with no arguments. It needs no files, no network and no rebuild.\n\nIt builds two GIFs of the same length, at 2 MB and at 4 MB. Each is the 13-byte GIF89a header for a 1×1 screen, followed by `size/5` copies of the 5-byte extension `21 \u003clabel\u003e 01 41 00`, then a minimal 1×1 image and the trailer:\n\n- **comments**: label `0xFE`. Each extension is a 1-byte comment, so `_seek()` appends each one to `info[\"comment\"]` with `+=` (`GifImagePlugin.py:266`).\n- **control**: label `0x01` (plain-text extension). The bytes are the same apart from the label. `_seek()` reads them through the same loop and the same `self.data()` calls, then throws them away.\n\nThe script times `Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))` on each file. It does not call `load()`, because `_open()` already parses frame 0. It checks that the image came out as 1×1. Typical output on a 2-CPU machine:\n\n```\n2 MB GIF   control (0x01):   0.16 s   comments (0xFE):   5.14 s\n4 MB GIF   control (0x01):   0.26 s   comments (0xFE):  33.53 s\ndoubling 2 MB -\u003e 4 MB multiplied open() time by x6.5 (linear: ~x2)\n4 MB comment GIF took x129 as long as the same-size control\nBUG\n```\n\nFor a linear parser, doubling the file would roughly double the time, and the two files would take about the same time. The script prints `BUG` when all three of these hold:\n\n- doubling the comment file from 2 MB to 4 MB multiplies its open time by at least 3×;\n- the 4 MB comment file takes at least 20× as long as its control;\n- the 4 MB comment file takes at least 8 s.\n\nOtherwise it prints `OK`. The run is deterministic, CPU-bound on one core, and takes about 40 s.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nThe attached patch changes `GifImageFile._seek()` in `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py` to accumulate comment data in a local list instead of with `bytes` concatenation, and to join it once when the frame's info is committed:\n\n```diff\n@@ def _seek(self, frame: int, update_image: bool = True) -\u003e None:\n         info: dict[str, Any] = {}\n+        comment_blocks: list[bytes] | None = None\n         frame_transparency = None\n@@\n                 elif s[0] == 254:\n                     #\n                     # comment extension\n                     #\n-                    comment = b\"\"\n+                    if comment_blocks is None:\n+                        comment_blocks = []\n+                    else:\n+                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n+                        comment_blocks.append(b\"\\n\")\n \n                     # Read this comment block\n                     while block:\n-                        comment += block\n+                        comment_blocks.append(block)\n                         block = self.data()\n-\n-                    if \"comment\" in info:\n-                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n-                        info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment\n-                    else:\n-                        info[\"comment\"] = comment\n                     s = b\"\"\n                     continue\n@@\n-        if info.get(\"comment\"):\n-            self.info[\"comment\"] = info[\"comment\"]\n+        if comment_blocks:\n+            comment = b\"\".join(comment_blocks)\n+            if comment:\n+                self.info[\"comment\"] = comment\n         for k in [\"duration\", \"extension\"]:\n```\n\nThis removes the quadratic copying at both sites (`:261` per sub-block and `:266` per extension); the 4 MB reproducer file opens in about 0.3 s, the same as its control, and the reproducer no longer prints `BUG` on a patched build. The resulting `info[\"comment\"]` is unchanged for empty, multi-sub-block, multi-extension and later-frame comments — including `Tests/images/multiple_comments.gif` → `b\"Test comment 1\\nTest comment 2\"` — and `Tests/test_file_gif.py` passes unchanged. The GIF writer (`_write_local_header` / `_save`) is not affected.\n\nThe patch deliberately adds no size cap. Unlike PNG `zTXt`, which `PngImagePlugin.MAX_TEXT_CHUNK` guards against decompression amplification, a GIF comment can be no larger than the file that carries it, so once the copy is linear there is nothing left to bound, and a hard cap would reject valid files with large comments. If a limit is wanted anyway, a `MAX_COMMENT_SIZE` check inside the sub-block loop is a small policy addition on top of this patch.\n\n## The patch\n\nGIF: collect comment extension sub-blocks in a list and join once per frame instead of repeated bytes += in GifImageFile._seek()\n\nApplies to `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py\nindex 11fb180fc..05007d2ac 100644\n--- a/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py\n+++ b/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py\n@@ -217,6 +217,7 @@ class GifImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n         palette: ImagePalette.ImagePalette | Literal[False] | None = None\n \n         info: dict[str, Any] = {}\n+        comment_blocks: list[bytes] | None = None\n         frame_transparency = None\n         interlace = None\n         frame_dispose_extent = None\n@@ -254,18 +255,16 @@ class GifImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n                     #\n                     # comment extension\n                     #\n-                    comment = b\"\"\n+                    if comment_blocks is None:\n+                        comment_blocks = []\n+                    else:\n+                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n+                        comment_blocks.append(b\"\\n\")\n \n                     # Read this comment block\n                     while block:\n-                        comment += block\n+                        comment_blocks.append(block)\n                         block = self.data()\n-\n-                    if \"comment\" in info:\n-                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n-                        info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment\n-                    else:\n-                        info[\"comment\"] = comment\n                     s = b\"\"\n                     continue\n                 elif s[0] == 255 and frame == 0 and block is not None:\n@@ -433,8 +432,10 @@ class GifImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n                 )\n             ]\n \n-        if info.get(\"comment\"):\n-            self.info[\"comment\"] = info[\"comment\"]\n+        if comment_blocks:\n+            comment = b\"\".join(comment_blocks)\n+            if comment:\n+                self.info[\"comment\"] = comment\n         for k in [\"duration\", \"extension\"]:\n             if k in info:\n                 self.info[k] = info[k]\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 2,263 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Quadratic CPU in Image.open() on GIF comment extensions.\n\nGifImageFile._seek() appends every comment extension to info[\"comment\"] with\nbytes `+=` (src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py:266), copying the whole accumulated\ncomment each time.  Image.open() runs _seek(0) eagerly, so merely opening a GIF\nmade of many tiny comment extensions costs O(size^2) CPU.\n\nWe time Image.open() on two files of identical length:\n  ATTACK  - a 1x1 GIF whose body is thousands of 1-byte comment extensions (label 0xFE)\n  CONTROL - the same bytes with label 0x01 (plain-text extension), which _seek()\n            reads through the same loop but discards instead of concatenating.\nA linear parser would take ~2x longer when the file doubles, and the two files\nwould cost about the same.\n\"\"\"\nimport io\nimport time\n\nfrom PIL import Image\n\nHEADER = b\"GIF89a\" + b\"\\x01\\x00\\x01\\x00\" + b\"\\x00\\x00\\x00\"  # 1x1 screen, no palette\nIMAGE = b\",\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x01\\x00\\x01\\x00\\x00\" + b\"\\x02\\x02\\x44\\x01\\x00\"  # 1x1 frame\nTRAILER = b\";\"\n\n\ndef make_gif(size: int, label: int) -\u003e bytes:\n    # each extension is `21 \u003clabel\u003e 01 'A' 00`: one 1-byte sub-block, then terminator\n    extension = b\"!\" + bytes([label]) + b\"\\x01A\\x00\"\n    return HEADER + extension * (size // 5) + IMAGE + TRAILER\n\n\ndef time_open(data: bytes) -\u003e float:\n    start = time.perf_counter()\n    with Image.open(io.BytesIO(data)) as im:  # no load(): the cost is in open()\n        elapsed = time.perf_counter() - start\n        assert im.size == (1, 1)\n    return elapsed\n\n\nMB = 1 \u003c\u003c 20\nattack, control = {}, {}\nfor size in (2 * MB, 4 * MB):\n    control[size] = time_open(make_gif(size, 0x01))\n    attack[size] = time_open(make_gif(size, 0xFE))\n    print(f\"{size // MB} MB GIF   control (0x01): {control[size]:6.2f} s   \"\n          f\"comments (0xFE): {attack[size]:6.2f} s\", flush=True)\n\ngrowth = attack[4 * MB] / attack[2 * MB]\nvs_control = attack[4 * MB] / control[4 * MB]\nprint(f\"doubling 2 MB -\u003e 4 MB multiplied open() time by x{growth:.1f} (linear: ~x2)\")\nprint(f\"4 MB comment GIF took x{vs_control:.0f} as long as the same-size control\")\n\n# BUG: super-linear growth, far slower than the control, and seconds of CPU per open()\nprint(\"BUG\" if growth \u003e= 3 and vs_control \u003e= 20 and attack[4 * MB] \u003e= 8 else \"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112401](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112401)**.\n\n## [GHSA-74r2-7ff9-mjj2](https://osv.dev/GHSA-74r2-7ff9-mjj2)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e Quadratic GIF comment concatenation allows DoS in Image.open()\n\u003e Location: src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py:266  in GifImageFile._seek()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Opening a crafted GIF file with `Image.open()` takes time that grows with the square of the file's size, so anyone who can submit an image to a service that uses Pillow can keep one worker busy at full processor load for a long time with a small file. A 2 MB file takes about 5 seconds to open, a 4 MB file about 35 seconds, and an 8 MB file about 3 minutes. An ordinary GIF of the same size opens in well under a second. The cost is paid inside `Image.open()` itself, with default settings, before the application has seen the image's size or format.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug is in `GifImageFile._seek()` in `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py`, which builds `info[\"comment\"]` by appending to an immutable `bytes` object with no size limit. It appends once per 255-byte sub-block (`comment += block`, line 261) and once per comment extension (`info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment`, line 266). Each append copies everything collected so far. A file made of many tiny comment extensions forces a full re-copy for each one. Because `_open()` ends with `self._seek(0)`, any code that only opens an uploaded file to identify it, read its dimensions, or make a thumbnail pays the full cost. The decompression-bomb check does not help, since the image is 1×1. Memory use stays linear, so the effect is processor exhaustion and latency, bounded by whatever upload size the service allows (about 1 second at 1 MB). This is the same kind of issue as the EPS open-phase slowdown fixed as CVE-2021-28677, here in the GIF comment handling. The quadratic pattern was introduced in commit b2b737b68b06 (\"Allow arbitrary number of comment extension subblocks\", November 2018) and has shipped since Pillow 5.4.0. The attached script builds 2 MB and 4 MB test files and compares each against a byte-identical control whose extensions are labelled as plain text instead of comment. The 4 MB comment file took about 34 seconds to open, against 0.26 seconds for its control. A patch that collects the pieces in a list and joins them once is also attached.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `GifImageFile._seek()` collects GIF comment extensions into `info[\"comment\"]` using `bytes` concatenation, once per 255-byte sub-block and once per comment extension, with no limit on the total. `bytes` is immutable, so every `+=` allocates a new object and copies everything accumulated so far; the work done by `Image.open()` is therefore quadratic in the amount of comment data in frame 0.\n\u003e \n\u003e A 1×1 GIF89a whose body is nothing but the 5-byte comment extension `21 FE 01 41 00` repeated makes this directly observable. `Image.open(BytesIO(data))` on such a file, stock build at this commit, 2-CPU machine:\n\u003e \n\u003e | file size | comment extensions (`21 FE …`) | same bytes labelled plain-text extension (`21 01 …`) |\n\u003e |---|---|---|\n\u003e | 1 MB | 1.2 s | 0.09 s |\n\u003e | 2 MB | 4.9 s | 0.13 s |\n\u003e | 4 MB | 35 s | 0.26 s |\n\u003e | 8 MB | 183 s | — |\n\u003e \n\u003e The control column is the identical byte stream with the extension label changed from `0xFE` to `0x01`; it goes through the same loop and the same `self.data()` reads but is discarded instead of concatenated, and scales linearly. No `load()` is needed and no warning or exception is raised; `im.info[\"comment\"]` simply ends up holding the multi-megabyte blob.\n\u003e \n\u003e The code, `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py:229-281` (trimmed):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e             elif s == b\"!\":\n\u003e                 s = self.fp.read(1)\n\u003e                 block = self.data()\n\u003e                 …\n\u003e                 elif s[0] == 254:\n\u003e                     #\n\u003e                     # comment extension\n\u003e                     #\n\u003e                     comment = b\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e                     # Read this comment block\n\u003e                     while block:\n\u003e                         comment += block                      # :261  one copy per sub-block\n\u003e                         block = self.data()\n\u003e \n\u003e                     if \"comment\" in info:\n\u003e                         # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n\u003e                         info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment    # :266  one copy per extension\n\u003e                     else:\n\u003e                         info[\"comment\"] = comment\n\u003e                     s = b\"\"\n\u003e                     continue\n\u003e                 …\n\u003e                 while self.data():                            # every other extension: read and drop\n\u003e                     pass\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `self.data()` (`:97-102`) reads one length byte and then that many bytes, so each call is cheap and bounded; the cost is entirely in the two `+=` lines. For a single comment extension of N bytes delivered as N/255 sub-blocks, line 261 copies about N²/510 bytes in total (4 MB → 2.3 s, 8 MB → 9.5 s, 16 MB → 38 s measured). Splitting the same data into many small comment extensions is worse, because line 266 re-copies the whole of `info[\"comment\"]` once per extension: with 1-byte extensions a file of F bytes causes roughly F²/25 bytes of copying, which is the table above (growth ×4–7 per doubling once the buffer outgrows the CPU caches).\n\u003e \n\u003e This runs during `Image.open()`: `_open()` ends with `self._seek(0)` (`:135`), so any application that merely opens an uploaded file to identify it, read its size, or thumbnail it pays the full cost before it has made any decision about the image. `Image._decompression_bomb_check` is not involved (the image is 1×1), and unlike PNG text chunks (`PngImagePlugin.MAX_TEXT_CHUNK` / `MAX_TEXT_MEMORY`) there is no cap on GIF comment size. Later frames repeat the same loop for their own comments via `seek()` / `n_frames`.\n\u003e \n\u003e With both `+=` sites replaced by list-append and a single `b\"\".join()` (as in the attached patch), the same 16 MB single-extension file opens in 0.03 s, confirming the concatenation is the whole cost.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Anyone who can get a GIF to a service that calls `PIL.Image.open()` on it — avatar/media uploads, thumbnailers, format sniffing, anything using `ImageOps`/`thumbnail()` on user files — can pin one worker process at 100 % CPU for about 5 s with a 2 MB file, 35 s with a 4 MB file and 3 minutes with an 8 MB file, under default settings, with the time growing quadratically in the permitted upload size. A benign GIF of the same size opens in well under a second, so request-cost assumptions based on file size do not hold. Memory use is only linear (the comment is retained in `im.info[\"comment\"]`), so the effect is CPU/latency denial of service of the worker pool rather than memory exhaustion; a handful of concurrent uploads is enough to saturate a typical pool. Upload size limits bound it (≈1 s at 1 MB).\n\u003e \n\u003e This is the same shape as CVE-2021-28677 (\"EPS DOS on _open\", `docs/releasenotes/8.2.0.rst`), where a quadratic method of accumulating data in the open phase, before the image was accepted, was treated as a denial-of-service vulnerability; here the format is GIF and the accumulator is the comment extension.\n\u003e \n\u003e The quadratic accumulation was introduced in `b2b737b68b06` (\"Allow arbitrary number of comment extension subblocks\", 2018-11-27), first released in Pillow 5.4.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e `repro` is a single Python script that uses the installed Pillow. Run it with no arguments. It needs no files, no network and no rebuild.\n\u003e \n\u003e It builds two GIFs of the same length, at 2 MB and at 4 MB. Each is the 13-byte GIF89a header for a 1×1 screen, followed by `size/5` copies of the 5-byte extension `21 \u003clabel\u003e 01 41 00`, then a minimal 1×1 image and the trailer:\n\u003e \n\u003e - **comments**: label `0xFE`. Each extension is a 1-byte comment, so `_seek()` appends each one to `info[\"comment\"]` with `+=` (`GifImagePlugin.py:266`).\n\u003e - **control**: label `0x01` (plain-text extension). The bytes are the same apart from the label. `_seek()` reads them through the same loop and the same `self.data()` calls, then throws them away.\n\u003e \n\u003e The script times `Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))` on each file. It does not call `load()`, because `_open()` already parses frame 0. It checks that the image came out as 1×1. Typical output on a 2-CPU machine:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e 2 MB GIF   control (0x01):   0.16 s   comments (0xFE):   5.14 s\n\u003e 4 MB GIF   control (0x01):   0.26 s   comments (0xFE):  33.53 s\n\u003e doubling 2 MB -\u003e 4 MB multiplied open() time by x6.5 (linear: ~x2)\n\u003e 4 MB comment GIF took x129 as long as the same-size control\n\u003e BUG\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e For a linear parser, doubling the file would roughly double the time, and the two files would take about the same time. The script prints `BUG` when all three of these hold:\n\u003e \n\u003e - doubling the comment file from 2 MB to 4 MB multiplies its open time by at least 3×;\n\u003e - the 4 MB comment file takes at least 20× as long as its control;\n\u003e - the 4 MB comment file takes at least 8 s.\n\u003e \n\u003e Otherwise it prints `OK`. The run is deterministic, CPU-bound on one core, and takes about 40 s.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached patch changes `GifImageFile._seek()` in `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py` to accumulate comment data in a local list instead of with `bytes` concatenation, and to join it once when the frame's info is committed:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e @@ def _seek(self, frame: int, update_image: bool = True) -\u003e None:\n\u003e          info: dict[str, Any] = {}\n\u003e +        comment_blocks: list[bytes] | None = None\n\u003e          frame_transparency = None\n\u003e @@\n\u003e                  elif s[0] == 254:\n\u003e                      #\n\u003e                      # comment extension\n\u003e                      #\n\u003e -                    comment = b\"\"\n\u003e +                    if comment_blocks is None:\n\u003e +                        comment_blocks = []\n\u003e +                    else:\n\u003e +                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n\u003e +                        comment_blocks.append(b\"\\n\")\n\u003e  \n\u003e                      # Read this comment block\n\u003e                      while block:\n\u003e -                        comment += block\n\u003e +                        comment_blocks.append(block)\n\u003e                          block = self.data()\n\u003e -\n\u003e -                    if \"comment\" in info:\n\u003e -                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n\u003e -                        info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment\n\u003e -                    else:\n\u003e -                        info[\"comment\"] = comment\n\u003e                      s = b\"\"\n\u003e                      continue\n\u003e @@\n\u003e -        if info.get(\"comment\"):\n\u003e -            self.info[\"comment\"] = info[\"comment\"]\n\u003e +        if comment_blocks:\n\u003e +            comment = b\"\".join(comment_blocks)\n\u003e +            if comment:\n\u003e +                self.info[\"comment\"] = comment\n\u003e          for k in [\"duration\", \"extension\"]:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e This removes the quadratic copying at both sites (`:261` per sub-block and `:266` per extension); the 4 MB reproducer file opens in about 0.3 s, the same as its control, and the reproducer no longer prints `BUG` on a patched build. The resulting `info[\"comment\"]` is unchanged for empty, multi-sub-block, multi-extension and later-frame comments — including `Tests/images/multiple_comments.gif` → `b\"Test comment 1\\nTest comment 2\"` — and `Tests/test_file_gif.py` passes unchanged. The GIF writer (`_write_local_header` / `_save`) is not affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e The patch deliberately adds no size cap. Unlike PNG `zTXt`, which `PngImagePlugin.MAX_TEXT_CHUNK` guards against decompression amplification, a GIF comment can be no larger than the file that carries it, so once the copy is linear there is nothing left to bound, and a hard cap would reject valid files with large comments. If a limit is wanted anyway, a `MAX_COMMENT_SIZE` check inside the sub-block loop is a small policy addition on top of this patch.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch\n\u003e \n\u003e GIF: collect comment extension sub-blocks in a list and join once per frame instead of repeated bytes += in GifImageFile._seek()\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py\n\u003e index 11fb180fc..05007d2ac 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py\n\u003e +++ b/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py\n\u003e @@ -217,6 +217,7 @@ class GifImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e          palette: ImagePalette.ImagePalette | Literal[False] | None = None\n\u003e  \n\u003e          info: dict[str, Any] = {}\n\u003e +        comment_blocks: list[bytes] | None = None\n\u003e          frame_transparency = None\n\u003e          interlace = None\n\u003e          frame_dispose_extent = None\n\u003e @@ -254,18 +255,16 @@ class GifImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e                      #\n\u003e                      # comment extension\n\u003e                      #\n\u003e -                    comment = b\"\"\n\u003e +                    if comment_blocks is None:\n\u003e +                        comment_blocks = []\n\u003e +                    else:\n\u003e +                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n\u003e +                        comment_blocks.append(b\"\\n\")\n\u003e  \n\u003e                      # Read this comment block\n\u003e                      while block:\n\u003e -                        comment += block\n\u003e +                        comment_blocks.append(block)\n\u003e                          block = self.data()\n\u003e -\n\u003e -                    if \"comment\" in info:\n\u003e -                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n\u003e -                        info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment\n\u003e -                    else:\n\u003e -                        info[\"comment\"] = comment\n\u003e                      s = b\"\"\n\u003e                      continue\n\u003e                  elif s[0] == 255 and frame == 0 and block is not None:\n\u003e @@ -433,8 +432,10 @@ class GifImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e                  )\n\u003e              ]\n\u003e  \n\u003e -        if info.get(\"comment\"):\n\u003e -            self.info[\"comment\"] = info[\"comment\"]\n\u003e +        if comment_blocks:\n\u003e +            comment = b\"\".join(comment_blocks)\n\u003e +            if comment:\n\u003e +                self.info[\"comment\"] = comment\n\u003e          for k in [\"duration\", \"extension\"]:\n\u003e              if k in info:\n\u003e                  self.info[k] = info[k]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 2,263 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \"\"\"Quadratic CPU in Image.open() on GIF comment extensions.\n\u003e \n\u003e GifImageFile._seek() appends every comment extension to info[\"comment\"] with\n\u003e bytes `+=` (src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py:266), copying the whole accumulated\n\u003e comment each time.  Image.open() runs _seek(0) eagerly, so merely opening a GIF\n\u003e made of many tiny comment extensions costs O(size^2) CPU.\n\u003e \n\u003e We time Image.open() on two files of identical length:\n\u003e   ATTACK  - a 1x1 GIF whose body is thousands of 1-byte comment extensions (label 0xFE)\n\u003e   CONTROL - the same bytes with label 0x01 (plain-text extension), which _seek()\n\u003e             reads through the same loop but discards instead of concatenating.\n\u003e A linear parser would take ~2x longer when the file doubles, and the two files\n\u003e would cost about the same.\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e import io\n\u003e import time\n\u003e \n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e HEADER = b\"GIF89a\" + b\"\\x01\\x00\\x01\\x00\" + b\"\\x00\\x00\\x00\"  # 1x1 screen, no palette\n\u003e IMAGE = b\",\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x01\\x00\\x01\\x00\\x00\" + b\"\\x02\\x02\\x44\\x01\\x00\"  # 1x1 frame\n\u003e TRAILER = b\";\"\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def make_gif(size: int, label: int) -\u003e bytes:\n\u003e     # each extension is `21 \u003clabel\u003e 01 'A' 00`: one 1-byte sub-block, then terminator\n\u003e     extension = b\"!\" + bytes([label]) + b\"\\x01A\\x00\"\n\u003e     return HEADER + extension * (size // 5) + IMAGE + TRAILER\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def time_open(data: bytes) -\u003e float:\n\u003e     start = time.perf_counter()\n\u003e     with Image.open(io.BytesIO(data)) as im:  # no load(): the cost is in open()\n\u003e         elapsed = time.perf_counter() - start\n\u003e         assert im.size == (1, 1)\n\u003e     return elapsed\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e MB = 1 \u003c\u003c 20\n\u003e attack, control = {}, {}\n\u003e for size in (2 * MB, 4 * MB):\n\u003e     control[size] = time_open(make_gif(size, 0x01))\n\u003e     attack[size] = time_open(make_gif(size, 0xFE))\n\u003e     print(f\"{size // MB} MB GIF   control (0x01): {control[size]:6.2f} s   \"\n\u003e           f\"comments (0xFE): {attack[size]:6.2f} s\", flush=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e growth = attack[4 * MB] / attack[2 * MB]\n\u003e vs_control = attack[4 * MB] / control[4 * MB]\n\u003e print(f\"doubling 2 MB -\u003e 4 MB multiplied open() time by x{growth:.1f} (linear: ~x2)\")\n\u003e print(f\"4 MB comment GIF took x{vs_control:.0f} as long as the same-size control\")\n\u003e \n\u003e # BUG: super-linear growth, far slower than the control, and seconds of CPU per open()\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if growth \u003e= 3 and vs_control \u003e= 20 and attack[4 * MB] \u003e= 8 else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-74r2-7ff9-mjj2 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112401\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112401](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112401)**.\n\n## [GHSA-74r2-7ff9-mjj2](https://osv.dev/GHSA-74r2-7ff9-mjj2)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e Quadratic GIF comment concatenation allows DoS in Image.open()\n\u003e Location: src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py:266  in GifImageFile._seek()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Opening a crafted GIF file with `Image.open()` takes time that grows with the square of the file's size, so anyone who can submit an image to a service that uses Pillow can keep one worker busy at full processor load for a long time with a small file. A 2 MB file takes about 5 seconds to open, a 4 MB file about 35 seconds, and an 8 MB file about 3 minutes. An ordinary GIF of the same size opens in well under a second. The cost is paid inside `Image.open()` itself, with default settings, before the application has seen the image's size or format.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug is in `GifImageFile._seek()` in `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py`, which builds `info[\"comment\"]` by appending to an immutable `bytes` object with no size limit. It appends once per 255-byte sub-block (`comment += block`, line 261) and once per comment extension (`info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment`, line 266). Each append copies everything collected so far. A file made of many tiny comment extensions forces a full re-copy for each one. Because `_open()` ends with `self._seek(0)`, any code that only opens an uploaded file to identify it, read its dimensions, or make a thumbnail pays the full cost. The decompression-bomb check does not help, since the image is 1×1. Memory use stays linear, so the effect is processor exhaustion and latency, bounded by whatever upload size the service allows (about 1 second at 1 MB). This is the same kind of issue as the EPS open-phase slowdown fixed as CVE-2021-28677, here in the GIF comment handling. The quadratic pattern was introduced in commit b2b737b68b06 (\"Allow arbitrary number of comment extension subblocks\", November 2018) and has shipped since Pillow 5.4.0. The attached script builds 2 MB and 4 MB test files and compares each against a byte-identical control whose extensions are labelled as plain text instead of comment. The 4 MB comment file took about 34 seconds to open, against 0.26 seconds for its control. A patch that collects the pieces in a list and joins them once is also attached.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `GifImageFile._seek()` collects GIF comment extensions into `info[\"comment\"]` using `bytes` concatenation, once per 255-byte sub-block and once per comment extension, with no limit on the total. `bytes` is immutable, so every `+=` allocates a new object and copies everything accumulated so far; the work done by `Image.open()` is therefore quadratic in the amount of comment data in frame 0.\n\u003e \n\u003e A 1×1 GIF89a whose body is nothing but the 5-byte comment extension `21 FE 01 41 00` repeated makes this directly observable. `Image.open(BytesIO(data))` on such a file, stock build at this commit, 2-CPU machine:\n\u003e \n\u003e | file size | comment extensions (`21 FE …`) | same bytes labelled plain-text extension (`21 01 …`) |\n\u003e |---|---|---|\n\u003e | 1 MB | 1.2 s | 0.09 s |\n\u003e | 2 MB | 4.9 s | 0.13 s |\n\u003e | 4 MB | 35 s | 0.26 s |\n\u003e | 8 MB | 183 s | — |\n\u003e \n\u003e The control column is the identical byte stream with the extension label changed from `0xFE` to `0x01`; it goes through the same loop and the same `self.data()` reads but is discarded instead of concatenated, and scales linearly. No `load()` is needed and no warning or exception is raised; `im.info[\"comment\"]` simply ends up holding the multi-megabyte blob.\n\u003e \n\u003e The code, `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py:229-281` (trimmed):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e             elif s == b\"!\":\n\u003e                 s = self.fp.read(1)\n\u003e                 block = self.data()\n\u003e                 …\n\u003e                 elif s[0] == 254:\n\u003e                     #\n\u003e                     # comment extension\n\u003e                     #\n\u003e                     comment = b\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e                     # Read this comment block\n\u003e                     while block:\n\u003e                         comment += block                      # :261  one copy per sub-block\n\u003e                         block = self.data()\n\u003e \n\u003e                     if \"comment\" in info:\n\u003e                         # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n\u003e                         info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment    # :266  one copy per extension\n\u003e                     else:\n\u003e                         info[\"comment\"] = comment\n\u003e                     s = b\"\"\n\u003e                     continue\n\u003e                 …\n\u003e                 while self.data():                            # every other extension: read and drop\n\u003e                     pass\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `self.data()` (`:97-102`) reads one length byte and then that many bytes, so each call is cheap and bounded; the cost is entirely in the two `+=` lines. For a single comment extension of N bytes delivered as N/255 sub-blocks, line 261 copies about N²/510 bytes in total (4 MB → 2.3 s, 8 MB → 9.5 s, 16 MB → 38 s measured). Splitting the same data into many small comment extensions is worse, because line 266 re-copies the whole of `info[\"comment\"]` once per extension: with 1-byte extensions a file of F bytes causes roughly F²/25 bytes of copying, which is the table above (growth ×4–7 per doubling once the buffer outgrows the CPU caches).\n\u003e \n\u003e This runs during `Image.open()`: `_open()` ends with `self._seek(0)` (`:135`), so any application that merely opens an uploaded file to identify it, read its size, or thumbnail it pays the full cost before it has made any decision about the image. `Image._decompression_bomb_check` is not involved (the image is 1×1), and unlike PNG text chunks (`PngImagePlugin.MAX_TEXT_CHUNK` / `MAX_TEXT_MEMORY`) there is no cap on GIF comment size. Later frames repeat the same loop for their own comments via `seek()` / `n_frames`.\n\u003e \n\u003e With both `+=` sites replaced by list-append and a single `b\"\".join()` (as in the attached patch), the same 16 MB single-extension file opens in 0.03 s, confirming the concatenation is the whole cost.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Anyone who can get a GIF to a service that calls `PIL.Image.open()` on it — avatar/media uploads, thumbnailers, format sniffing, anything using `ImageOps`/`thumbnail()` on user files — can pin one worker process at 100 % CPU for about 5 s with a 2 MB file, 35 s with a 4 MB file and 3 minutes with an 8 MB file, under default settings, with the time growing quadratically in the permitted upload size. A benign GIF of the same size opens in well under a second, so request-cost assumptions based on file size do not hold. Memory use is only linear (the comment is retained in `im.info[\"comment\"]`), so the effect is CPU/latency denial of service of the worker pool rather than memory exhaustion; a handful of concurrent uploads is enough to saturate a typical pool. Upload size limits bound it (≈1 s at 1 MB).\n\u003e \n\u003e This is the same shape as CVE-2021-28677 (\"EPS DOS on _open\", `docs/releasenotes/8.2.0.rst`), where a quadratic method of accumulating data in the open phase, before the image was accepted, was treated as a denial-of-service vulnerability; here the format is GIF and the accumulator is the comment extension.\n\u003e \n\u003e The quadratic accumulation was introduced in `b2b737b68b06` (\"Allow arbitrary number of comment extension subblocks\", 2018-11-27), first released in Pillow 5.4.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e `repro` is a single Python script that uses the installed Pillow. Run it with no arguments. It needs no files, no network and no rebuild.\n\u003e \n\u003e It builds two GIFs of the same length, at 2 MB and at 4 MB. Each is the 13-byte GIF89a header for a 1×1 screen, followed by `size/5` copies of the 5-byte extension `21 \u003clabel\u003e 01 41 00`, then a minimal 1×1 image and the trailer:\n\u003e \n\u003e - **comments**: label `0xFE`. Each extension is a 1-byte comment, so `_seek()` appends each one to `info[\"comment\"]` with `+=` (`GifImagePlugin.py:266`).\n\u003e - **control**: label `0x01` (plain-text extension). The bytes are the same apart from the label. `_seek()` reads them through the same loop and the same `self.data()` calls, then throws them away.\n\u003e \n\u003e The script times `Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))` on each file. It does not call `load()`, because `_open()` already parses frame 0. It checks that the image came out as 1×1. Typical output on a 2-CPU machine:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e 2 MB GIF   control (0x01):   0.16 s   comments (0xFE):   5.14 s\n\u003e 4 MB GIF   control (0x01):   0.26 s   comments (0xFE):  33.53 s\n\u003e doubling 2 MB -\u003e 4 MB multiplied open() time by x6.5 (linear: ~x2)\n\u003e 4 MB comment GIF took x129 as long as the same-size control\n\u003e BUG\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e For a linear parser, doubling the file would roughly double the time, and the two files would take about the same time. The script prints `BUG` when all three of these hold:\n\u003e \n\u003e - doubling the comment file from 2 MB to 4 MB multiplies its open time by at least 3×;\n\u003e - the 4 MB comment file takes at least 20× as long as its control;\n\u003e - the 4 MB comment file takes at least 8 s.\n\u003e \n\u003e Otherwise it prints `OK`. The run is deterministic, CPU-bound on one core, and takes about 40 s.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached patch changes `GifImageFile._seek()` in `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py` to accumulate comment data in a local list instead of with `bytes` concatenation, and to join it once when the frame's info is committed:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e @@ def _seek(self, frame: int, update_image: bool = True) -\u003e None:\n\u003e          info: dict[str, Any] = {}\n\u003e +        comment_blocks: list[bytes] | None = None\n\u003e          frame_transparency = None\n\u003e @@\n\u003e                  elif s[0] == 254:\n\u003e                      #\n\u003e                      # comment extension\n\u003e                      #\n\u003e -                    comment = b\"\"\n\u003e +                    if comment_blocks is None:\n\u003e +                        comment_blocks = []\n\u003e +                    else:\n\u003e +                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n\u003e +                        comment_blocks.append(b\"\\n\")\n\u003e  \n\u003e                      # Read this comment block\n\u003e                      while block:\n\u003e -                        comment += block\n\u003e +                        comment_blocks.append(block)\n\u003e                          block = self.data()\n\u003e -\n\u003e -                    if \"comment\" in info:\n\u003e -                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n\u003e -                        info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment\n\u003e -                    else:\n\u003e -                        info[\"comment\"] = comment\n\u003e                      s = b\"\"\n\u003e                      continue\n\u003e @@\n\u003e -        if info.get(\"comment\"):\n\u003e -            self.info[\"comment\"] = info[\"comment\"]\n\u003e +        if comment_blocks:\n\u003e +            comment = b\"\".join(comment_blocks)\n\u003e +            if comment:\n\u003e +                self.info[\"comment\"] = comment\n\u003e          for k in [\"duration\", \"extension\"]:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e This removes the quadratic copying at both sites (`:261` per sub-block and `:266` per extension); the 4 MB reproducer file opens in about 0.3 s, the same as its control, and the reproducer no longer prints `BUG` on a patched build. The resulting `info[\"comment\"]` is unchanged for empty, multi-sub-block, multi-extension and later-frame comments — including `Tests/images/multiple_comments.gif` → `b\"Test comment 1\\nTest comment 2\"` — and `Tests/test_file_gif.py` passes unchanged. The GIF writer (`_write_local_header` / `_save`) is not affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e The patch deliberately adds no size cap. Unlike PNG `zTXt`, which `PngImagePlugin.MAX_TEXT_CHUNK` guards against decompression amplification, a GIF comment can be no larger than the file that carries it, so once the copy is linear there is nothing left to bound, and a hard cap would reject valid files with large comments. If a limit is wanted anyway, a `MAX_COMMENT_SIZE` check inside the sub-block loop is a small policy addition on top of this patch.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch\n\u003e \n\u003e GIF: collect comment extension sub-blocks in a list and join once per frame instead of repeated bytes += in GifImageFile._seek()\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py\n\u003e index 11fb180fc..05007d2ac 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py\n\u003e +++ b/src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py\n\u003e @@ -217,6 +217,7 @@ class GifImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e          palette: ImagePalette.ImagePalette | Literal[False] | None = None\n\u003e  \n\u003e          info: dict[str, Any] = {}\n\u003e +        comment_blocks: list[bytes] | None = None\n\u003e          frame_transparency = None\n\u003e          interlace = None\n\u003e          frame_dispose_extent = None\n\u003e @@ -254,18 +255,16 @@ class GifImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e                      #\n\u003e                      # comment extension\n\u003e                      #\n\u003e -                    comment = b\"\"\n\u003e +                    if comment_blocks is None:\n\u003e +                        comment_blocks = []\n\u003e +                    else:\n\u003e +                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n\u003e +                        comment_blocks.append(b\"\\n\")\n\u003e  \n\u003e                      # Read this comment block\n\u003e                      while block:\n\u003e -                        comment += block\n\u003e +                        comment_blocks.append(block)\n\u003e                          block = self.data()\n\u003e -\n\u003e -                    if \"comment\" in info:\n\u003e -                        # If multiple comment blocks in frame, separate with \\n\n\u003e -                        info[\"comment\"] += b\"\\n\" + comment\n\u003e -                    else:\n\u003e -                        info[\"comment\"] = comment\n\u003e                      s = b\"\"\n\u003e                      continue\n\u003e                  elif s[0] == 255 and frame == 0 and block is not None:\n\u003e @@ -433,8 +432,10 @@ class GifImageFile(ImageFile.ImageFile):\n\u003e                  )\n\u003e              ]\n\u003e  \n\u003e -        if info.get(\"comment\"):\n\u003e -            self.info[\"comment\"] = info[\"comment\"]\n\u003e +        if comment_blocks:\n\u003e +            comment = b\"\".join(comment_blocks)\n\u003e +            if comment:\n\u003e +                self.info[\"comment\"] = comment\n\u003e          for k in [\"duration\", \"extension\"]:\n\u003e              if k in info:\n\u003e                  self.info[k] = info[k]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 2,263 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \"\"\"Quadratic CPU in Image.open() on GIF comment extensions.\n\u003e \n\u003e GifImageFile._seek() appends every comment extension to info[\"comment\"] with\n\u003e bytes `+=` (src/PIL/GifImagePlugin.py:266), copying the whole accumulated\n\u003e comment each time.  Image.open() runs _seek(0) eagerly, so merely opening a GIF\n\u003e made of many tiny comment extensions costs O(size^2) CPU.\n\u003e \n\u003e We time Image.open() on two files of identical length:\n\u003e   ATTACK  - a 1x1 GIF whose body is thousands of 1-byte comment extensions (label 0xFE)\n\u003e   CONTROL - the same bytes with label 0x01 (plain-text extension), which _seek()\n\u003e             reads through the same loop but discards instead of concatenating.\n\u003e A linear parser would take ~2x longer when the file doubles, and the two files\n\u003e would cost about the same.\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e import io\n\u003e import time\n\u003e \n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e HEADER = b\"GIF89a\" + b\"\\x01\\x00\\x01\\x00\" + b\"\\x00\\x00\\x00\"  # 1x1 screen, no palette\n\u003e IMAGE = b\",\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x01\\x00\\x01\\x00\\x00\" + b\"\\x02\\x02\\x44\\x01\\x00\"  # 1x1 frame\n\u003e TRAILER = b\";\"\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def make_gif(size: int, label: int) -\u003e bytes:\n\u003e     # each extension is `21 \u003clabel\u003e 01 'A' 00`: one 1-byte sub-block, then terminator\n\u003e     extension = b\"!\" + bytes([label]) + b\"\\x01A\\x00\"\n\u003e     return HEADER + extension * (size // 5) + IMAGE + TRAILER\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def time_open(data: bytes) -\u003e float:\n\u003e     start = time.perf_counter()\n\u003e     with Image.open(io.BytesIO(data)) as im:  # no load(): the cost is in open()\n\u003e         elapsed = time.perf_counter() - start\n\u003e         assert im.size == (1, 1)\n\u003e     return elapsed\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e MB = 1 \u003c\u003c 20\n\u003e attack, control = {}, {}\n\u003e for size in (2 * MB, 4 * MB):\n\u003e     control[size] = time_open(make_gif(size, 0x01))\n\u003e     attack[size] = time_open(make_gif(size, 0xFE))\n\u003e     print(f\"{size // MB} MB GIF   control (0x01): {control[size]:6.2f} s   \"\n\u003e           f\"comments (0xFE): {attack[size]:6.2f} s\", flush=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e growth = attack[4 * MB] / attack[2 * MB]\n\u003e vs_control = attack[4 * MB] / control[4 * MB]\n\u003e print(f\"doubling 2 MB -\u003e 4 MB multiplied open() time by x{growth:.1f} (linear: ~x2)\")\n\u003e print(f\"4 MB comment GIF took x{vs_control:.0f} as long as the same-size control\")\n\u003e \n\u003e # BUG: super-linear growth, far slower than the control, and seconds of CPU per open()\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if growth \u003e= 3 and vs_control \u003e= 20 and attack[4 * MB] \u003e= 8 else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-74r2-7ff9-mjj2 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112401\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-112401",
              "name": "CVE-2026-112401",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.9"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-112401: Pillow: Quadratic GIF comment concatenation allows DoS in Image.open()",
                "text": "CVE-2026-112401: Pillow: Quadratic GIF comment concatenation allows DoS in Image.open()"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-32597",
                "PYSEC-2026-120",
                "GHSA-752w-5fwx-jx9f"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "## Summary\n\nPyJWT does not validate the `crit` (Critical) Header Parameter defined in\nRFC 7515 §4.1.11. When a JWS token contains a `crit` array listing\nextensions that PyJWT does not understand, the library accepts the token\ninstead of rejecting it. This violates the **MUST** requirement in the RFC.\n\nThis is the same class of vulnerability as CVE-2025-59420 (Authlib),\nwhich received CVSS 7.5 (HIGH).\n\n---\n\n## RFC Requirement\n\nRFC 7515 §4.1.11:\n\n\u003e The \"crit\" (Critical) Header Parameter indicates that extensions to this\n\u003e specification and/or [JWA] are being used that **MUST** be understood and\n\u003e processed. [...] If any of the listed extension Header Parameters are\n\u003e **not understood and supported** by the recipient, then the **JWS is invalid**.\n\n---\n\n## Proof of Concept\n\n```python\nimport jwt  # PyJWT 2.8.0\nimport hmac, hashlib, base64, json\n\n# Construct token with unknown critical extension\nheader = {\"alg\": \"HS256\", \"crit\": [\"x-custom-policy\"], \"x-custom-policy\": \"require-mfa\"}\npayload = {\"sub\": \"attacker\", \"role\": \"admin\"}\n\ndef b64url(data):\n    return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(data).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\n\nh = b64url(json.dumps(header, separators=(\",\", \":\")).encode())\np = b64url(json.dumps(payload, separators=(\",\", \":\")).encode())\nsig = b64url(hmac.new(b\"secret\", f\"{h}.{p}\".encode(), hashlib.sha256).digest())\ntoken = f\"{h}.{p}.{sig}\"\n\n# Should REJECT — x-custom-policy is not understood by PyJWT\ntry:\n    result = jwt.decode(token, \"secret\", algorithms=[\"HS256\"])\n    print(f\"ACCEPTED: {result}\")\n    # Output: ACCEPTED: {'sub': 'attacker', 'role': 'admin'}\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f\"REJECTED: {e}\")\n```\n\n**Expected:** `jwt.exceptions.InvalidTokenError: Unsupported critical extension: x-custom-policy`\n**Actual:** Token accepted, payload returned.\n\n### Comparison with RFC-compliant library\n\n```python\n# jwcrypto — correctly rejects\nfrom jwcrypto import jwt as jw_jwt, jwk\nkey = jwk.JWK(kty=\"oct\", k=b64url(b\"secret\"))\njw_jwt.JWT(jwt=token, key=key, algs=[\"HS256\"])\n# raises: InvalidJWSObject('Unknown critical header: \"x-custom-policy\"')\n```\n\n---\n\n## Impact\n\n- **Split-brain verification** in mixed-library deployments (e.g., API\n  gateway using jwcrypto rejects, backend using PyJWT accepts)\n- **Security policy bypass** when `crit` carries enforcement semantics\n  (MFA, token binding, scope restrictions)\n- **Token binding bypass** — RFC 7800 `cnf` (Proof-of-Possession) can be\n  silently ignored\n- See CVE-2025-59420 for full impact analysis\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Fix\n\nIn `jwt/api_jwt.py`, add validation in `_validate_headers()` or\n`decode()`:\n\n```python\n_SUPPORTED_CRIT = {\"b64\"}  # Add extensions PyJWT actually supports\n\ndef _validate_crit(self, headers: dict) -\u003e None:\n    crit = headers.get(\"crit\")\n    if crit is None:\n        return\n    if not isinstance(crit, list) or len(crit) == 0:\n        raise InvalidTokenError(\"crit must be a non-empty array\")\n    for ext in crit:\n        if ext not in self._SUPPORTED_CRIT:\n            raise InvalidTokenError(f\"Unsupported critical extension: {ext}\")\n        if ext not in headers:\n            raise InvalidTokenError(f\"Critical extension {ext} not in header\")\n```\n\n---\n\n## CWE\n\n- CWE-345: Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity\n- CWE-863: Incorrect Authorization\n\n## References\n\n- [RFC 7515 §4.1.11](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7515.html#section-4.1.11)\n- [CVE-2025-59420 — Authlib crit bypass (CVSS 7.5)](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-9ggr-2464-2j32)\n- [RFC 7800 — Proof-of-Possession Key Semantics](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7800)",
                "text": "## Summary\n\nPyJWT does not validate the `crit` (Critical) Header Parameter defined in\nRFC 7515 §4.1.11. When a JWS token contains a `crit` array listing\nextensions that PyJWT does not understand, the library accepts the token\ninstead of rejecting it. This violates the **MUST** requirement in the RFC.\n\nThis is the same class of vulnerability as CVE-2025-59420 (Authlib),\nwhich received CVSS 7.5 (HIGH).\n\n---\n\n## RFC Requirement\n\nRFC 7515 §4.1.11:\n\n\u003e The \"crit\" (Critical) Header Parameter indicates that extensions to this\n\u003e specification and/or [JWA] are being used that **MUST** be understood and\n\u003e processed. [...] If any of the listed extension Header Parameters are\n\u003e **not understood and supported** by the recipient, then the **JWS is invalid**.\n\n---\n\n## Proof of Concept\n\n```python\nimport jwt  # PyJWT 2.8.0\nimport hmac, hashlib, base64, json\n\n# Construct token with unknown critical extension\nheader = {\"alg\": \"HS256\", \"crit\": [\"x-custom-policy\"], \"x-custom-policy\": \"require-mfa\"}\npayload = {\"sub\": \"attacker\", \"role\": \"admin\"}\n\ndef b64url(data):\n    return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(data).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\n\nh = b64url(json.dumps(header, separators=(\",\", \":\")).encode())\np = b64url(json.dumps(payload, separators=(\",\", \":\")).encode())\nsig = b64url(hmac.new(b\"secret\", f\"{h}.{p}\".encode(), hashlib.sha256).digest())\ntoken = f\"{h}.{p}.{sig}\"\n\n# Should REJECT — x-custom-policy is not understood by PyJWT\ntry:\n    result = jwt.decode(token, \"secret\", algorithms=[\"HS256\"])\n    print(f\"ACCEPTED: {result}\")\n    # Output: ACCEPTED: {'sub': 'attacker', 'role': 'admin'}\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f\"REJECTED: {e}\")\n```\n\n**Expected:** `jwt.exceptions.InvalidTokenError: Unsupported critical extension: x-custom-policy`\n**Actual:** Token accepted, payload returned.\n\n### Comparison with RFC-compliant library\n\n```python\n# jwcrypto — correctly rejects\nfrom jwcrypto import jwt as jw_jwt, jwk\nkey = jwk.JWK(kty=\"oct\", k=b64url(b\"secret\"))\njw_jwt.JWT(jwt=token, key=key, algs=[\"HS256\"])\n# raises: InvalidJWSObject('Unknown critical header: \"x-custom-policy\"')\n```\n\n---\n\n## Impact\n\n- **Split-brain verification** in mixed-library deployments (e.g., API\n  gateway using jwcrypto rejects, backend using PyJWT accepts)\n- **Security policy bypass** when `crit` carries enforcement semantics\n  (MFA, token binding, scope restrictions)\n- **Token binding bypass** — RFC 7800 `cnf` (Proof-of-Possession) can be\n  silently ignored\n- See CVE-2025-59420 for full impact analysis\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Fix\n\nIn `jwt/api_jwt.py`, add validation in `_validate_headers()` or\n`decode()`:\n\n```python\n_SUPPORTED_CRIT = {\"b64\"}  # Add extensions PyJWT actually supports\n\ndef _validate_crit(self, headers: dict) -\u003e None:\n    crit = headers.get(\"crit\")\n    if crit is None:\n        return\n    if not isinstance(crit, list) or len(crit) == 0:\n        raise InvalidTokenError(\"crit must be a non-empty array\")\n    for ext in crit:\n        if ext not in self._SUPPORTED_CRIT:\n            raise InvalidTokenError(f\"Unsupported critical extension: {ext}\")\n        if ext not in headers:\n            raise InvalidTokenError(f\"Critical extension {ext} not in header\")\n```\n\n---\n\n## CWE\n\n- CWE-345: Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity\n- CWE-863: Incorrect Authorization\n\n## References\n\n- [RFC 7515 §4.1.11](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7515.html#section-4.1.11)\n- [CVE-2025-59420 — Authlib crit bypass (CVSS 7.5)](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-9ggr-2464-2j32)\n- [RFC 7800 — Proof-of-Possession Key Semantics](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7800)"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-32597](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-32597)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-120](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-120), [GHSA-752w-5fwx-jx9f](https://osv.dev/GHSA-752w-5fwx-jx9f), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-120](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-120)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e PyJWT is a JSON Web Token implementation in Python. Prior to 2.12.0, PyJWT does not validate the crit (Critical) Header Parameter defined in RFC 7515 §4.1.11. When a JWS token contains a crit array listing extensions that PyJWT does not understand, the library accepts the token instead of rejecting it. This violates the MUST requirement in the RFC. This vulnerability is fixed in 2.12.0.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-752w-5fwx-jx9f](https://osv.dev/GHSA-752w-5fwx-jx9f)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e PyJWT does not validate the `crit` (Critical) Header Parameter defined in\n\u003e RFC 7515 §4.1.11. When a JWS token contains a `crit` array listing\n\u003e extensions that PyJWT does not understand, the library accepts the token\n\u003e instead of rejecting it. This violates the **MUST** requirement in the RFC.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is the same class of vulnerability as CVE-2025-59420 (Authlib),\n\u003e which received CVSS 7.5 (HIGH).\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ## RFC Requirement\n\u003e \n\u003e RFC 7515 §4.1.11:\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e The \"crit\" (Critical) Header Parameter indicates that extensions to this\n\u003e \u003e specification and/or [JWA] are being used that **MUST** be understood and\n\u003e \u003e processed. [...] If any of the listed extension Header Parameters are\n\u003e \u003e **not understood and supported** by the recipient, then the **JWS is invalid**.\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Proof of Concept\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import jwt  # PyJWT 2.8.0\n\u003e import hmac, hashlib, base64, json\n\u003e \n\u003e # Construct token with unknown critical extension\n\u003e header = {\"alg\": \"HS256\", \"crit\": [\"x-custom-policy\"], \"x-custom-policy\": \"require-mfa\"}\n\u003e payload = {\"sub\": \"attacker\", \"role\": \"admin\"}\n\u003e \n\u003e def b64url(data):\n\u003e     return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(data).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\n\u003e \n\u003e h = b64url(json.dumps(header, separators=(\",\", \":\")).encode())\n\u003e p = b64url(json.dumps(payload, separators=(\",\", \":\")).encode())\n\u003e sig = b64url(hmac.new(b\"secret\", f\"{h}.{p}\".encode(), hashlib.sha256).digest())\n\u003e token = f\"{h}.{p}.{sig}\"\n\u003e \n\u003e # Should REJECT — x-custom-policy is not understood by PyJWT\n\u003e try:\n\u003e     result = jwt.decode(token, \"secret\", algorithms=[\"HS256\"])\n\u003e     print(f\"ACCEPTED: {result}\")\n\u003e     # Output: ACCEPTED: {'sub': 'attacker', 'role': 'admin'}\n\u003e except Exception as e:\n\u003e     print(f\"REJECTED: {e}\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **Expected:** `jwt.exceptions.InvalidTokenError: Unsupported critical extension: x-custom-policy`\n\u003e **Actual:** Token accepted, payload returned.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Comparison with RFC-compliant library\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # jwcrypto — correctly rejects\n\u003e from jwcrypto import jwt as jw_jwt, jwk\n\u003e key = jwk.JWK(kty=\"oct\", k=b64url(b\"secret\"))\n\u003e jw_jwt.JWT(jwt=token, key=key, algs=[\"HS256\"])\n\u003e # raises: InvalidJWSObject('Unknown critical header: \"x-custom-policy\"')\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e - **Split-brain verification** in mixed-library deployments (e.g., API\n\u003e   gateway using jwcrypto rejects, backend using PyJWT accepts)\n\u003e - **Security policy bypass** when `crit` carries enforcement semantics\n\u003e   (MFA, token binding, scope restrictions)\n\u003e - **Token binding bypass** — RFC 7800 `cnf` (Proof-of-Possession) can be\n\u003e   silently ignored\n\u003e - See CVE-2025-59420 for full impact analysis\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested Fix\n\u003e \n\u003e In `jwt/api_jwt.py`, add validation in `_validate_headers()` or\n\u003e `decode()`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e _SUPPORTED_CRIT = {\"b64\"}  # Add extensions PyJWT actually supports\n\u003e \n\u003e def _validate_crit(self, headers: dict) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     crit = headers.get(\"crit\")\n\u003e     if crit is None:\n\u003e         return\n\u003e     if not isinstance(crit, list) or len(crit) == 0:\n\u003e         raise InvalidTokenError(\"crit must be a non-empty array\")\n\u003e     for ext in crit:\n\u003e         if ext not in self._SUPPORTED_CRIT:\n\u003e             raise InvalidTokenError(f\"Unsupported critical extension: {ext}\")\n\u003e         if ext not in headers:\n\u003e             raise InvalidTokenError(f\"Critical extension {ext} not in header\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ## CWE\n\u003e \n\u003e - CWE-345: Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity\n\u003e - CWE-863: Incorrect Authorization\n\u003e \n\u003e ## References\n\u003e \n\u003e - [RFC 7515 §4.1.11](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7515.html#section-4.1.11)\n\u003e - [CVE-2025-59420 — Authlib crit bypass (CVSS 7.5)](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-9ggr-2464-2j32)\n\u003e - [RFC 7800 — Proof-of-Possession Key Semantics](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7800)\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | pyjwt | 2.10.1 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-752w-5fwx-jx9f | pyjwt | 2.12.0 |\n| PYSEC-2026-120 | pyjwt | 2.12.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-32597\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-32597](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-32597)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-120](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-120), [GHSA-752w-5fwx-jx9f](https://osv.dev/GHSA-752w-5fwx-jx9f), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-120](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-120)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e PyJWT is a JSON Web Token implementation in Python. Prior to 2.12.0, PyJWT does not validate the crit (Critical) Header Parameter defined in RFC 7515 §4.1.11. When a JWS token contains a crit array listing extensions that PyJWT does not understand, the library accepts the token instead of rejecting it. This violates the MUST requirement in the RFC. This vulnerability is fixed in 2.12.0.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-752w-5fwx-jx9f](https://osv.dev/GHSA-752w-5fwx-jx9f)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e PyJWT does not validate the `crit` (Critical) Header Parameter defined in\n\u003e RFC 7515 §4.1.11. When a JWS token contains a `crit` array listing\n\u003e extensions that PyJWT does not understand, the library accepts the token\n\u003e instead of rejecting it. This violates the **MUST** requirement in the RFC.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is the same class of vulnerability as CVE-2025-59420 (Authlib),\n\u003e which received CVSS 7.5 (HIGH).\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ## RFC Requirement\n\u003e \n\u003e RFC 7515 §4.1.11:\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e The \"crit\" (Critical) Header Parameter indicates that extensions to this\n\u003e \u003e specification and/or [JWA] are being used that **MUST** be understood and\n\u003e \u003e processed. [...] If any of the listed extension Header Parameters are\n\u003e \u003e **not understood and supported** by the recipient, then the **JWS is invalid**.\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Proof of Concept\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import jwt  # PyJWT 2.8.0\n\u003e impo