              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "`download_wheel()` runs `pip download` and hands the result straight to the\nseeder, with nothing checking the bytes it gets back. The embedded pip and\nsetuptools wheels carry a `BUNDLE_SHA256` that virtualenv checks on every load,\nbut a wheel fetched over the network for the periodic-update feature or the\n`--download` flag had no equivalent verification.\n\nA compromised index, a stale mirror, or a MITM'd download (when TLS is\nintercepted, e.g. via a misconfigured or malicious CA) could substitute a\ndifferent wheel under the same distribution/version/filename, and virtualenv\nwould cache and seed it into every environment created afterward with no\nwarning.\n\nFixed in 21.7.12 by computing the downloaded wheel's sha256 and comparing it\nagainst the digest PyPI's public JSON API reports for that exact release,\nindependent of which index actually served the file. The check is skipped, not\ntreated as failure, when a custom index (`PIP_INDEX_URL`,\n`PIP_EXTRA_INDEX_URL`, `PIP_INDEX`) is configured, since a private index can\nlegitimately serve a different, intentionally rebuilt wheel.\n",
                "text": "`download_wheel()` runs `pip download` and hands the result straight to the\nseeder, with nothing checking the bytes it gets back. The embedded pip and\nsetuptools wheels carry a `BUNDLE_SHA256` that virtualenv checks on every load,\nbut a wheel fetched over the network for the periodic-update feature or the\n`--download` flag had no equivalent verification.\n\nA compromised index, a stale mirror, or a MITM'd download (when TLS is\nintercepted, e.g. via a misconfigured or malicious CA) could substitute a\ndifferent wheel under the same distribution/version/filename, and virtualenv\nwould cache and seed it into every environment created afterward with no\nwarning.\n\nFixed in 21.7.12 by computing the downloaded wheel's sha256 and comparing it\nagainst the digest PyPI's public JSON API reports for that exact release,\nindependent of which index actually served the file. The check is skipped, not\ntreated as failure, when a custom index (`PIP_INDEX_URL`,\n`PIP_EXTRA_INDEX_URL`, `PIP_INDEX`) is configured, since a private index can\nlegitimately serve a different, intentionally rebuilt wheel.\n"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-102930](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-102930)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4011](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4011), [GHSA-94p9-xgh2-xp45](https://osv.dev/GHSA-94p9-xgh2-xp45), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4011](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4011)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e `download_wheel()` runs `pip download` and hands the result straight to the\n\u003e seeder, with nothing checking the bytes it gets back. The embedded pip and\n\u003e setuptools wheels carry a `BUNDLE_SHA256` that virtualenv checks on every load,\n\u003e but a wheel fetched over the network for the periodic-update feature or the\n\u003e `--download` flag had no equivalent verification.\n\u003e \n\u003e A compromised index, a stale mirror, or a MITM'd download (when TLS is\n\u003e intercepted, e.g. via a misconfigured or malicious CA) could substitute a\n\u003e different wheel under the same distribution/version/filename, and virtualenv\n\u003e would cache and seed it into every environment created afterward with no\n\u003e warning.\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed in 21.7.12 by computing the downloaded wheel's sha256 and comparing it\n\u003e against the digest PyPI's public JSON API reports for that exact release,\n\u003e independent of which index actually served the file. The check is skipped, not\n\u003e treated as failure, when a custom index (`PIP_INDEX_URL`,\n\u003e `PIP_EXTRA_INDEX_URL`, `PIP_INDEX`) is configured, since a private index can\n\u003e legitimately serve a different, intentionally rebuilt wheel.\n\u003e \n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-94p9-xgh2-xp45](https://osv.dev/GHSA-94p9-xgh2-xp45)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e `download_wheel()` runs `pip download` and hands the result straight to the seeder, with nothing checking the bytes it gets back. The embedded pip and setuptools wheels carry a `BUNDLE_SHA256` that virtualenv checks on every load, but a wheel fetched over the network for the periodic-update feature or the `--download` flag had no equivalent verification.\n\u003e \n\u003e A compromised index, a stale mirror, or a MITM'd download (when TLS is intercepted, e.g. via a misconfigured or malicious CA) could substitute a different wheel under the same distribution/version/filename, and virtualenv would cache and seed it into every environment created afterward with no warning.\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed by computing the downloaded wheel's sha256 and comparing it against the digest PyPI's public JSON API reports for that exact release, independent of which index actually served the file. The check is skipped, not treated as failure, when a custom index (`PIP_INDEX_URL`, `PIP_EXTRA_INDEX_URL`, `PIP_INDEX`) is configured, since a private index can legitimately serve a different, intentionally rebuilt wheel. Fix: https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/pull/3251\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | virtualenv | 20.31.2 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | virtualenv | 21.7.4 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-94p9-xgh2-xp45 | virtualenv | 21.7.12 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4011 | virtualenv | 21.7.12 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102930\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102930\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-102930](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-102930)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4011](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4011), [GHSA-94p9-xgh2-xp45](https://osv.dev/GHSA-94p9-xgh2-xp45), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4011](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4011)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e `download_wheel()` runs `pip download` and hands the result straight to the\n\u003e seeder, with nothing checking the bytes it gets back. The embedded pip and\n\u003e setuptools wheels carry a `BUNDLE_SHA256` that virtualenv checks on every load,\n\u003e but a wheel fetched over the network for the periodic-update feature or the\n\u003e `--download` flag had no equivalent verification.\n\u003e \n\u003e A compromised index, a stale mirror, or a MITM'd download (when TLS is\n\u003e intercepted, e.g. via a misconfigured or malicious CA) could substitute a\n\u003e different wheel under the same distribution/version/filename, and virtualenv\n\u003e would cache and seed it into every environment created afterward with no\n\u003e warning.\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed in 21.7.12 by computing the downloaded wheel's sha256 and comparing it\n\u003e against the digest PyPI's public JSON API reports for that exact release,\n\u003e independent of which index actually served the file. The check is skipped, not\n\u003e treated as failure, when a custom index (`PIP_INDEX_URL`,\n\u003e `PIP_EXTRA_INDEX_URL`, `PIP_INDEX`) is configured, since a private index can\n\u003e legitimately serve a different, intentionally rebuilt wheel.\n\u003e \n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-94p9-xgh2-xp45](https://osv.dev/GHSA-94p9-xgh2-xp45)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e `download_wheel()` runs `pip download` and hands the result straight to the seeder, with nothing checking the bytes it gets back. The embedded pip and setuptools wheels carry a `BUNDLE_SHA256` that virtualenv checks on every load, but a wheel fetched over the network for the periodic-update feature or the `--download` flag had no equivalent verification.\n\u003e \n\u003e A compromised index, a stale mirror, or a MITM'd download (when TLS is intercepted, e.g. via a misconfigured or malicious CA) could substitute a different wheel under the same distribution/version/filename, and virtualenv would cache and seed it into every environment created afterward with no warning.\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed by computing the downloaded wheel's sha256 and comparing it against the digest PyPI's public JSON API reports for that exact release, independent of which index actually served the file. The check is skipped, not treated as failure, when a custom index (`PIP_INDEX_URL`, `PIP_EXTRA_INDEX_URL`, `PIP_INDEX`) is configured, since a private index can legitimately serve a different, intentionally rebuilt wheel. Fix: https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/pull/3251\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | virtualenv | 20.31.2 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | virtualenv | 21.7.4 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-94p9-xgh2-xp45 | virtualenv | 21.7.12 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4011 | virtualenv | 21.7.12 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config fi