              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-110858](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-110858)**.\n\n## [GHSA-f9j4-qggq-h239](https://osv.dev/GHSA-f9j4-qggq-h239)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `Remote.pull()` in GitPython forwards its positional `refspec` argument directly to the `git pull` child process while its unsafe-option guard inspects only keyword arguments. An application that passes attacker-influenced data as the `refspec` (for example a user-supplied branch or refspec parameter in a web or CI service) can supply an option-shaped value such as `--upload-pack=\u003ccommand\u003e`. The value bypasses the `unsafe_git_pull_options` check, is placed after the `--` separator on the `git pull` command line, and is executed as the upload-pack program for local-path or SSH transports. This yields arbitrary command execution with the privileges of the calling process using the documented default arguments (`allow_unsafe_options=False`).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Root Cause\n\u003e \n\u003e `Remote.pull()` is documented to reject dangerous options unless `allow_unsafe_options=True`, and `--upload-pack` is explicitly listed as such an option. The guard, however, only screens keyword arguments and never the positional `refspec`. The check builds its candidate list with `Git._option_candidates([], kwargs)`, passing an empty list in place of the positionals, so the `refspec` value is never compared against `unsafe_git_pull_options = [\"--upload-pack\"]`. The normalized `refspec` is then forwarded verbatim to the `git pull` invocation. The leading `--` separator that neutralizes an option-shaped refspec for `git fetch` and `git push` does not protect `git pull`, because `git pull` re-forwards its trailing positionals to an internal `git fetch` without re-inserting a `--` separator, so the positional becomes an option to that internal fetch.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e Affected function: git.remote.Remote.pull\n\u003e Source: git/remote.py (guard at the check_unsafe_options call using _option_candidates([], kwargs); sink at the self.repo.git.pull(\"--\", self, refspec, ...) invocation)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e An attacker who controls the `refspec` value passed to `Remote.pull()` achieves arbitrary operating-system command execution as the process invoking GitPython, with no additional privileges and no user interaction, on the default code path. Exploitation requires that the pulled remote uses a transport that runs upload-pack on the local side, namely a local filesystem path or SSH; over HTTPS the `--upload-pack` value is inert because the server ignores a client-supplied upload-pack program. This transport requirement narrows the population of vulnerable deployments but does not add work for the attacker once a vulnerable target is reached. Working checkouts commonly use local-path or SSH origins, so the exposed population is substantial.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Proof of Concept\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e Environment: GitPython from the affected source tree; system git 2.43.0.\n\u003e The pulled origin uses a local filesystem transport (protocol.file.allow=always for git \u003e= 2.38).\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Reproduction steps:\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. Create an origin repository reachable over a local-path transport.\n\u003e 2. In a consumer repository, register that origin and call `Remote.pull()` with an attacker-controlled refspec.\n\u003e 3. Observe that the injected command executes.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import git\n\u003e repo = git.Repo(\"consumer\")                 # has an origin using a local-path or ssh transport\n\u003e origin = repo.remote(\"origin\")\n\u003e # Attacker-controlled refspec value; default arguments (allow_unsafe_options=False):\n\u003e origin.pull(refspec=\"--upload-pack=touch /tmp/pwned; git-upload-pack\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e cmdline: git pull -v -- origin --upload-pack=touch /tmp/pwned; git-upload-pack\n\u003e [attack]  marker created (RCE)      : True\n\u003e [isolate] upload_pack passed as KWARG blocked : True   (only the positional refspec escapes)\n\u003e [sibling] same value via git fetch  : safe            (pull-specific behavior)\n\u003e [control] benign refspec            : safe\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Attack Chain\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. **Exposure:** An application calls `Remote.pull(refspec=\u003cvalue\u003e)` on the default path, where `\u003cvalue\u003e` is derived from attacker-influenced input such as a branch or refspec request parameter.\n\u003e 2. **Control:** The attacker supplies the string `--upload-pack=\u003ccommand\u003e; git-upload-pack`. The value passes through `Git._unpack_args`, which only flattens and stringifies it, preserving the option shape.\n\u003e 3. **Path:** The protocol loop calls `Git.check_unsafe_protocols` on the value, which only matches remote-helper transport URLs (a `\u003ctransport\u003e::` form) and does not flag an `--upload-pack=` token.\n\u003e 4. **Guard:** The unsafe-option guard calls `check_unsafe_options(options=Git._option_candidates([], kwargs), ...)`. Because positionals are passed as an empty list, the positional `refspec` is never inspected against `unsafe_git_pull_options`.\n\u003e 5. **Primitive:** The value is forwarded to `git pull -v -- \u003cremote\u003e --upload-pack=\u003ccommand\u003e; git-upload-pack`; `git pull` re-forwards the positional to an internal `git fetch` without a `--` separator, so it is honored as the upload-pack program.\n\u003e 6. **Result:** For a local-path or SSH transport, git executes `\u003ccommand\u003e` as the upload-pack program, giving arbitrary command execution as the calling process user.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Bypass Evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e The relevant guard is `Remote.pull()`'s own `check_unsafe_options` call, present in the current released artifact. It is incomplete: it inspects `Git._option_candidates([], kwargs)`, which only considers keyword arguments, so the positional `refspec` is never checked. This was confirmed empirically: passing the identical value as the `upload_pack=` keyword argument raises `UnsafeOptionError`, while passing it as the positional `refspec` does not, proving that only the positional escapes the guard.\n\u003e \n\u003e The `--` separator on the `git pull` command line was tested as a possible mitigation and refuted: with system git 2.43.0, `git pull -- \u003cremote\u003e '--upload-pack=\u003ccmd\u003e'` executed the injected command, while the identical value supplied to `git fetch -- ...` and `git push -- ...` did not execute, because those subcommands honor the `--` terminator whereas `git pull` re-forwards positionals to an internal fetch without one. The `check_unsafe_protocols` transport check was also examined and does not flag the `--upload-pack=` token because it has no `\u003ctransport\u003e::` form. No prior published advisory for this package addresses `Remote.pull`, the fetch or pull refspec positional, or `--upload-pack` injection via a refspec; the closest accepted issue covers the same class (a positional argument escaping a keyword-only guard) in a different function and sink.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected Versions\n\u003e \n\u003e - `Ecosystem: pip (PyPI)`\n\u003e - `Package: GitPython`\n\u003e - `Confirmed affected range: \u003e= 3.1.30`\n\u003e - `Latest release checked: 3.1.62 (PyPI); source verified against the 3.1.62 release tag`\n\u003e - `Fix status: not fixed in any released version or on the development branch`\n\u003e \n\u003e The keyword-only pull guard together with the positional `refspec` sink is present in every released tag from 3.1.30 through the latest release 3.1.62, verified by inspecting the shipped `git/remote.py` in each tagged artifact. Version 3.1.30 introduced the `unsafe_git_pull_options` guard; the affected range is stated from that boundary under the incomplete-guard framing. Releases before 3.1.30 forward the `refspec` positionally with no unsafe-option guard and no `--` separator and are also exploitable, but the range is stated conservatively from the guard-introduction boundary. No fixed release exists.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested Fix\n\u003e \n\u003e Enforce the unsafe-option invariant on the positional `refspec`, not only on keyword arguments. The guard should build its candidate list from the actual forwarded positionals (for example by passing the normalized refspec list into `Git._option_candidates` alongside `kwargs`) so that an option-shaped `refspec` such as `--upload-pack=...` is rejected with `UnsafeOptionError` unless `allow_unsafe_options=True`. The same correction should be verified for any other reference or refspec forwarding path that relies on the keyword-only guard. As an interim mitigation without a code change, callers should validate or reject refspec values that begin with `-`, avoid passing untrusted data as the `refspec`, and prefer HTTPS remotes where a client-supplied upload-pack program is inert.\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by zx (GitHub: @manus-pi).\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | gitpython | 3.1.59 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-f9j4-qggq-h239 | gitpython | 3.2.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110858\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-110858](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-110858)**.\n\n## [GHSA-f9j4-qggq-h239](https://osv.dev/GHSA-f9j4-qggq-h239)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `Remote.pull()` in GitPython forwards its positional `refspec` argument directly to the `git pull` child process while its unsafe-option guard inspects only keyword arguments. An application that passes attacker-influenced data as the `refspec` (for example a user-supplied branch or refspec parameter in a web or CI service) can supply an option-shaped value such as `--upload-pack=\u003ccommand\u003e`. The value bypasses the `unsafe_git_pull_options` check, is placed after the `--` separator on the `git pull` command line, and is executed as the upload-pack program for local-path or SSH transports. This yields arbitrary command execution with the privileges of the calling process using the documented default arguments (`allow_unsafe_options=False`).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Root Cause\n\u003e \n\u003e `Remote.pull()` is documented to reject dangerous options unless `allow_unsafe_options=True`, and `--upload-pack` is explicitly listed as such an option. The guard, however, only screens keyword arguments and never the positional `refspec`. The check builds its candidate list with `Git._option_candidates([], kwargs)`, passing an empty list in place of the positionals, so the `refspec` value is never compared against `unsafe_git_pull_options = [\"--upload-pack\"]`. The normalized `refspec` is then forwarded verbatim to the `git pull` invocation. The leading `--` separator that neutralizes an option-shaped refspec for `git fetch` and `git push` does not protect `git pull`, because `git pull` re-forwards its trailing positionals to an internal `git fetch` without re-inserting a `--` separator, so the positional becomes an option to that internal fetch.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e Affected f