              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\nA link reference definition whose label or title is never closed is O(n²) in markdown-it 15.0.2 with default options. Continuation lines are pulled lazily with str += lineContent in src/rules_block/reference.ts, and the subsequent charCodeAt and slice calls flatten the V8 cons-string, copying everything collected so far; this runs before the rule decides the definition is invalid. It is a regression introduced by PR #1004 in 14.0.0: 13.0.2 processed a 313 KB input in 125 ms, while every version since takes about 11 seconds on the same input. At the sizes below, 200 KB takes 3.6 seconds, 400 KB takes 18.1 seconds, and 800 KB takes 74.0 seconds, which is roughly 4–5× per doubling. No options are required to trigger it.\n\n### Details\n\nsrc/rules_block/reference.ts appends continuation lines with str += lineContent at lines 74, 83, 100, 133, and 150, then keeps indexing the accumulated string via str.charCodeAt(pos) in the label and whitespace loops and str.slice(...) in parseLinkTitle. V8 represents += as a cons-string, and the next charCodeAt or slice flattens it, copying everything collected so far, so one definition spanning n lines costs O(n²). Isolation confirms the rule is responsible: block-only parsing with inline disabled is still quadratic, md.disable('reference') makes the same input linear, and the same text without the leading [ is linear. The fix is to track positions in state.src instead of building str, or to collect line slices in an array and parse across them. At minimum, stop scanning the label after 999 characters (CommonMark §6.3) before pulling more lines, and cap title continuation. Add a pathological test with a 100k-line unclosed title and label. Open PR #1215 edits the same file but its label check runs after the label loop, so it does not bound either variant and the fix can land together with that PR.\n\nThe observed output from PoC was:\nunclosed title n=100000   200009 B    3585.5 ms\nunclosed title n=200000   400009 B   18107.1 ms   (5.05x)\nunclosed title n=400000   800009 B   73954.4 ms   (4.08x)\n\nRegression comparison at 313 KB: 13.0.2 → 125 ms, 14.1.0 → 11.4 s, 15.0.0 → 10.6 s, 15.0.2 → 10.7 s.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nHow to execute the PoC:\n1. mkdir -p markdown-it-poc \u0026\u0026 cd markdown-it-poc\n2. npm init -y\n3. npm i markdown-it@15.0.2\n4. node poc_markdown_it.js\n\nPoC below is:\n```javascript\n// poc_markdown_it.js\n// Requires: npm install markdown-it@15.0.2\nconst MarkdownIt = require('markdown-it');\nconst md = new MarkdownIt();\n\nfunction bench(label, src) {\n  const t0 = process.hrtime.bigint();\n  md.render(src);\n  const t1 = process.hrtime.bigint();\n  console.log(\n    `${label.padEnd(30)} ${String(src.length).padStart(9)} B ${(Number(t1 - t0) / 1e6).toFixed(1).padStart(9)} ms`\n  );\n}\n\n// Unclosed title: one reference definition spread over many lines\nfor (const n of [100000, 200000, 400000]) {\n  bench(`unclosed title n=${n}`, '[a]: /u \"' + 'x\\n'.repeat(n));\n}\n\n// Unclosed label\nfor (const n of [5000, 10000, 20000]) {\n  bench(`unclosed label n=${n}`, '[' + 'a\\n'.repeat(n));\n}\n```\n\n### Impact\n\nThis is an algorithmic-complexity denial of service (CWE-407). Any server that renders user Markdown with markdown-it is affected with default options, including comments, issues, chat, wikis, docs previews, and LLM chat UIs. A ~400 KB request body blocks the Node.js event loop for about 18 seconds and an ~800 KB body for about 74 seconds. This is the same class and attacker model as GHSA-253c, but it is reachable without any opt-in option, since the affected path is a core block rule.",
                "text": "### Summary\n\nA link reference definition whose label or title is never closed is O(n²) in markdown-it 15.0.2 with default options. Continuation lines are pulled lazily with str += lineContent in src/rules_block/reference.ts, and the subsequent charCodeAt and slice calls flatten the V8 cons-string, copying everything collected so far; this runs before the rule decides the definition is invalid. It is a regression introduced by PR #1004 in 14.0.0: 13.0.2 processed a 313 KB input in 125 ms, while every version since takes about 11 seconds on the same input. At the sizes below, 200 KB takes 3.6 seconds, 400 KB takes 18.1 seconds, and 800 KB takes 74.0 seconds, which is roughly 4–5× per doubling. No options are required to trigger it.\n\n### Details\n\nsrc/rules_block/reference.ts appends continuation lines with str += lineContent at lines 74, 83, 100, 133, and 150, then keeps indexing the accumulated string via str.charCodeAt(pos) in the label and whitespace loops and str.slice(...) in parseLinkTitle. V8 represents += as a cons-string, and the next charCodeAt or slice flattens it, copying everything collected so far, so one definition spanning n lines costs O(n²). Isolation confirms the rule is responsible: block-only parsing with inline disabled is still quadratic, md.disable('reference') makes the same input linear, and the same text without the leading [ is linear. The fix is to track positions in state.src instead of building str, or to collect line slices in an array and parse across them. At minimum, stop scanning the label after 999 characters (CommonMark §6.3) before pulling more lines, and cap title continuation. Add a pathological test with a 100k-line unclosed title and label. Open PR #1215 edits the same file but its label check runs after the label loop, so it does not bound either variant and the fix can land together with that PR.\n\nThe observed output from PoC was:\nunclosed title n=100000   200009 B    3585.5 ms\nunclosed title n=200000   400009 B   18107.1 ms   (5.05x)\nunclosed title n=400000   800009 B   73954.4 ms   (4.08x)\n\nRegression comparison at 313 KB: 13.0.2 → 125 ms, 14.1.0 → 11.4 s, 15.0.0 → 10.6 s, 15.0.2 → 10.7 s.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nHow to execute the PoC:\n1. mkdir -p markdown-it-poc \u0026\u0026 cd markdown-it-poc\n2. npm init -y\n3. npm i markdown-it@15.0.2\n4. node poc_markdown_it.js\n\nPoC below is:\n```javascript\n// poc_markdown_it.js\n// Requires: npm install markdown-it@15.0.2\nconst MarkdownIt = require('markdown-it');\nconst md = new MarkdownIt();\n\nfunction bench(label, src) {\n  const t0 = process.hrtime.bigint();\n  md.render(src);\n  const t1 = process.hrtime.bigint();\n  console.log(\n    `${label.padEnd(30)} ${String(src.length).padStart(9)} B ${(Number(t1 - t0) / 1e6).toFixed(1).padStart(9)} ms`\n  );\n}\n\n// Unclosed title: one reference definition spread over many lines\nfor (const n of [100000, 200000, 400000]) {\n  bench(`unclosed title n=${n}`, '[a]: /u \"' + 'x\\n'.repeat(n));\n}\n\n// Unclosed label\nfor (const n of [5000, 10000, 20000]) {\n  bench(`unclosed label n=${n}`, '[' + 'a\\n'.repeat(n));\n}\n```\n\n### Impact\n\nThis is an algorithmic-complexity denial of service (CWE-407). Any server that renders user Markdown with markdown-it is affected with default options, including comments, issues, chat, wikis, docs previews, and LLM chat UIs. A ~400 KB request body blocks the Node.js event loop for about 18 seconds and an ~800 KB body for about 74 seconds. This is the same class and attacker model as GHSA-253c, but it is reachable without any opt-in option, since the affected path is a core block rule."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111758](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-111758)**.\n\n## [GHSA-gfr2-rv44-r23c](https://osv.dev/GHSA-gfr2-rv44-r23c)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e A link reference definition whose label or title is never closed is O(n²) in markdown-it 15.0.2 with default options. Continuation lines are pulled lazily with str += lineContent in src/rules_block/reference.ts, and the subsequent charCodeAt and slice calls flatten the V8 cons-string, copying everything collected so far; this runs before the rule decides the definition is invalid. It is a regression introduced by PR #1004 in 14.0.0: 13.0.2 processed a 313 KB input in 125 ms, while every version since takes about 11 seconds on the same input. At the sizes below, 200 KB takes 3.6 seconds, 400 KB takes 18.1 seconds, and 800 KB takes 74.0 seconds, which is roughly 4–5× per doubling. No options are required to trigger it.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e src/rules_block/reference.ts appends continuation lines with str += lineContent at lines 74, 83, 100, 133, and 150, then keeps indexing the accumulated string via str.charCodeAt(pos) in the label and whitespace loops and str.slice(...) in parseLinkTitle. V8 represents += as a cons-string, and the next charCodeAt or slice flattens it, copying everything collected so far, so one definition spanning n lines costs O(n²). Isolation confirms the rule is responsible: block-only parsing with inline disabled is still quadratic, md.disable('reference') makes the same input linear, and the same text without the leading [ is linear. The fix is to track positions in state.src instead of building str, or to collect line slices in an array and parse across them. At minimum, stop scanning the label after 999 characters (CommonMark §6.3) before pulling more lines, and cap title continuation. Add a pathological test with a 100k-line unclosed title and label. Open PR #1215 edits the same file but its label check runs after the label loop, so it does not bound either variant and the fix can land together with that PR.\n\u003e \n\u003e The observed output from PoC was:\n\u003e unclosed title n=100000   200009 B    3585.5 ms\n\u003e unclosed title n=200000   400009 B   18107.1 ms   (5.05x)\n\u003e unclosed title n=400000   800009 B   73954.4 ms   (4.08x)\n\u003e \n\u003e Regression comparison at 313 KB: 13.0.2 → 125 ms, 14.1.0 → 11.4 s, 15.0.0 → 10.6 s, 15.0.2 → 10.7 s.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Proof of concept\n\u003e \n\u003e How to execute the PoC:\n\u003e 1. mkdir -p markdown-it-poc \u0026\u0026 cd markdown-it-poc\n\u003e 2. npm init -y\n\u003e 3. npm i markdown-it@15.0.2\n\u003e 4. node poc_markdown_it.js\n\u003e \n\u003e PoC below is:\n\u003e ```javascript\n\u003e // poc_markdown_it.js\n\u003e // Requires: npm install markdown-it@15.0.2\n\u003e const MarkdownIt = require('markdown-it');\n\u003e const md = new MarkdownIt();\n\u003e \n\u003e function bench(label, src) {\n\u003e   const t0 = process.hrtime.bigint();\n\u003e   md.render(src);\n\u003e   const t1 = process.hrtime.bigint();\n\u003e   console.log(\n\u003e     `${label.padEnd(30)} ${String(src.length).padStart(9)} B ${(Number(t1 - t0) / 1e6).toFixed(1).padStart(9)} ms`\n\u003e   );\n\u003e }\n\u003e \n\u003e // Unclosed title: one reference definition spread over many lines\n\u003e for (const n of [100000, 200000, 400000]) {\n\u003e   bench(`unclosed title n=${n}`, '[a]: /u \"' + 'x\\n'.repeat(n));\n\u003e }\n\u003e \n\u003e // Unclosed label\n\u003e for (const n of [5000, 10000, 20000]) {\n\u003e   bench(`unclosed label n=${n}`, '[' + 'a\\n'.repeat(n));\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e This is an algorithmic-complexity denial of service (CWE-407). Any server that renders user Markdown with markdown-it is affected with default options, including comments, issues, chat, wikis, docs previews, and LLM chat UIs. A ~400 KB request body blocks the Node.js event loop for about 18 seconds and an ~800 KB body for about 74 seconds. This is the same class and attacker model as GHSA-253c, but it is reachable without any opt-in option, since the affected path is a core block rule.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | markdown-it | 14.3.0 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | markdown-it | 14.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-gfr2-rv44-r23c | markdown-it | 14.3.3, 15.0.3 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111758\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111758\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
