              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-113715](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-113715)**.\n\n## [GHSA-qmx5-4p3x-wvqf](https://osv.dev/GHSA-qmx5-4p3x-wvqf)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e The venv creator reads the standard library's generated pyvenv.cfg with splitlines() and merges all resulting keys over\n\u003e virtualenv's configuration. Line-boundary characters from destination data can therefore become configuration structure\n\u003e before output sanitization.\n\u003e \n\u003e Anthropic's OSS Scanner reported this as `ANT-2026-6ZQHJ6VI` in `virtualenv-20261015-005`, received on 15 October 2026.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected area\n\u003e \n\u003e Configuration readback by the stdlib venv creator.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Attacker-controlled input\n\u003e \n\u003e An untrusted caller must influence the destination pathname passed to the venv creator. That pathname must contain a\n\u003e character that Python treats as a line boundary. The builtin creator follows a different configuration-writing path.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected code\n\u003e \n\u003e The relevant 21.14.8 source is in\n\u003e [venv readback](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/blob/a795ab116356afde11039cf2763d77e8a06da738/src/virtualenv/create/via_global_ref/venv.py),\n\u003e [configuration parser](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/blob/a795ab116356afde11039cf2763d77e8a06da738/src/virtualenv/create/pyenv_cfg.py).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Mechanism\n\u003e \n\u003e With `--creator venv`, virtualenv asks the standard library to create an environment, reads its `pyvenv.cfg` with\n\u003e `str.splitlines()`, and merges the parsed keys over virtualenv's chosen settings. CPython writes the destination into a\n\u003e `command =` value without escaping every character that `splitlines()` recognizes as a line boundary. A destination\n\u003e containing U+2028, for example, can therefore supply new `home` and `include-system-site-packages` keys during readback.\n\u003e Virtualenv writes those keys as real configuration lines. The builtin creator does not use this readback path.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproduction and evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e The scanner used one destination value with U+2028 between `home = /attacker/chosen`,\n\u003e `include-system-site-packages = true`, and an absorbing `x =` key. A `python -m venv --without-pip` control kept the\n\u003e value inside its `command` line and reported the base interpreter's `sys._home`. `virtualenv --creator venv --no-seed`\n\u003e on the same value wrote separate injected configuration lines and its interpreter reported\n\u003e `sys._home = /attacker/chosen`. The path did not require a literal newline byte. The control establishes that the extra\n\u003e keys came from virtualenv's readback, not from the standard library's initial file alone.\n\u003e \n\u003e The altered `home` can change which standard-library files the interpreter loads if an attacker also controls that\n\u003e location; `include-system-site-packages = true` weakens environment isolation. Choosing the venv creator is required,\n\u003e either explicitly or through a rare fallback. The report traces the readback to the 20.x rewrite, first released in\n\u003e 20.0.0b1. [PR #3426](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/pull/3426) rejects line-boundary characters before creation and\n\u003e rebuilds the final configuration from selected settings.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Attack sequence\n\u003e \n\u003e ```mermaid\n\u003e sequenceDiagram\n\u003e     participant A as Destination supplier\n\u003e     participant S as stdlib venv\n\u003e     participant V as virtualenv readback\n\u003e     participant C as Final pyvenv.cfg\n\u003e     participant P as Environment Python\n\u003e     A-\u003e\u003eS: path contains U+2028 and home = /attacker/chosen\n\u003e     S-\u003e\u003eC: write path inside command value\n\u003e     V-\u003e\u003eC: read with splitlines()\n\u003e     V-\u003e\u003eV: parse suffix as separate keys\n\u003e     V-\u003e\u003eC: rewrite home and include-system-site-packages\n\u003e     P-\u003e\u003eC: read injected keys on startup\n\u003e     C--\u003e\u003eP: sys._home changes and isolation is disabled\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducer files\n\u003e \n\u003e The following files are from the scanner report for `ANT-2026-6ZQHJ6VI`. They were tested against commit `a857a7823c73`\n\u003e in the scanner image. The `repro` entrypoint expects companion files under `/tmp/repro.d`; cases that change user IDs\n\u003e need a disposable root-capable container. The last output line is `BUG` when the reported behavior occurs. These files\n\u003e are included as evidence and were not rerun for this advisory update.\n\u003e \n\u003e **`repro`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e import os\n\u003e import shutil\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e \n\u003e work = \"/tmp/repro-venv-creator-cfg\"\n\u003e shutil.rmtree(work, ignore_errors=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e # the attacker's input: the text of a destination path.\n\u003e # U+2028 ends a line for str.splitlines() but not for CPython's own pyvenv.cfg reader.\n\u003e # The slashes only make nested directories; the last key, x, absorbs the rest of the path.\n\u003e LS = \"\\u2028\"\n\u003e dest = f\"{work}/proj{LS}home = /attacker/chosen{LS}include-system-site-packages = true{LS}x = /env\"\n\u003e \n\u003e base_python = os.path.realpath(shutil.which(\"python\"))\n\u003e real_home = os.path.dirname(base_python)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def pyvenv_cfg_lines(env):\n\u003e     # split on \"\\n\" only, as the interpreter does\n\u003e     return open(f\"{env}/pyvenv.cfg\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read().split(\"\\n\")\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def sys_home(env):\n\u003e     # site.py sets sys._home from the `home` key of the environment's pyvenv.cfg\n\u003e     probe = [f\"{env}/bin/python\", \"-c\", \"import sys; print(sys._home)\"]\n\u003e     return subprocess.run(probe, capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout.strip()\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e # control: the same path through the standard library alone\n\u003e stdlib_env = dest + \"-stdlib\"\n\u003e subprocess.run([base_python, \"-m\", \"venv\", \"--without-pip\", stdlib_env], check=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e # attack: the same path through virtualenv; --creator venv is the one non-default option\n\u003e virtualenv_env = dest + \"-virtualenv\"\n\u003e created = subprocess.run([\"virtualenv\", \"--quiet\", \"--no-periodic-update\", \"--no-seed\", \"--creator\", \"venv\", virtualenv_env])\n\u003e if created.returncode != 0:  # refusing such a destination is a valid fix\n\u003e     print(\"virtualenv refused the destination\")\n\u003e     print(\"OK\")\n\u003e     raise SystemExit\n\u003e \n\u003e # check\n\u003e stdlib_cfg, stdlib_home = pyvenv_cfg_lines(stdlib_env), sys_home(stdlib_env)\n\u003e virtualenv_cfg, virtualenv_home = pyvenv_cfg_lines(virtualenv_env), sys_home(virtualenv_env)\n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"CONTROL  python -m venv wrote pyvenv.cfg:\")\n\u003e for line in stdlib_cfg:\n\u003e     print(\"    \" + ascii(line))\n\u003e print(f\"CONTROL  python -m venv: sys._home = {stdlib_home}\")\n\u003e print(\"OBSERVED virtualenv --creator venv wrote pyvenv.cfg:\")\n\u003e for line in virtualenv_cfg:\n\u003e     print(\"    \" + ascii(line))\n\u003e print(f\"OBSERVED virtualenv --creator venv: sys._home = {virtualenv_home}\")\n\u003e \n\u003e keys_injected = \"home = /attacker/chosen\" in virtualenv_cfg and \"include-system-site-packages = true\" in virtualenv_cfg\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if stdlib_home == real_home and keys_injected and virtualenv_home == \"/attacker/chosen\" else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Destination path data can change configuration boundaries and therefore environment settings. The effect depends on\n\u003e which settings change and how the environment is subsequently used.\n\u003e \n\u003e Severity: low.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Versions tested\n\u003e \n\u003e The confirmed release is 21.14.8. The affected range records that release only; it does not establish the first affected\n\u003e version or exclude earlier releases. Fixed in 21.14.9.\n\u003e \n\u003e The fix rejects line boundaries in the supplied destination before filesystem access and checks the resolved\n\u003e path again before invoking stdlib venv. The final configuration is rebuilt from the selected interpreter and virtualenv\n\u003e options, without merging the intermediate stdlib file or its vendor-specific keys. Ordinary destination paths remain\n\u003e supported.\n\u003e \n\u003e Fix: [PR #3426](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/pull/3426).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Known mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e Reject line-boundary characters in externally supplied destination paths, or use the builtin creator where supported.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected release\n\u003e \n\u003e - [x] The problem reproduces on virtualenv 21.14.8.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by Anthropic's OSS Scanner as ANT-2026-6ZQHJ6VI.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | virtualenv | 20.31.2 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | virtualenv | 21.7.4 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-qmx5-4p3x-wvqf | virtualenv | 21.14.9 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113715\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113715\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-113715](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-113715)**.\n\n## [GHSA-qmx5-4p3x-wvqf](https://osv.dev/GHSA-qmx5-4p3x-wvqf)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e The venv creator reads the standard library's generated pyvenv.cfg with splitlines() and merges all resulting keys over\n\u003e virtualenv's configuration. Line-boundary characters from destination data can therefore become configuration structure\n\u003e before output sanitization.\n\u003e \n\u003e Anthropic's OSS Scanner reported this as `ANT-2026-6ZQHJ6VI` in `virtualenv-20261015-005`, received on 15 October 2026.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected area\n\u003e \n\u003e Configuration readback by the stdlib venv creator.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Attacker-controlled input\n\u003e \n\u003e An untrusted caller must influence the destination pathname passed to the venv creator. That pathname must contain a\n\u003e character that Python treats as a line boundary. The builtin creator follows a different configuration-writing path.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected code\n\u003e \n\u003e The relevant 21.14.8 source is in\n\u003e [venv readback](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/blob/a795ab116356afde11039cf2763d77e8a06da738/src/virtualenv/create/via_global_ref/venv.py),\n\u003e [configuration parser](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/blob/a795ab116356afde11039cf2763d77e8a06da738/src/virtualenv/create/pyenv_cfg.py).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Mechanism\n\u003e \n\u003e With `--creator venv`, virtualenv asks the standard library to create an environment, reads its `pyvenv.cfg` with\n\u003e `str.splitlines()`, and merges the parsed keys over virtualenv's chosen settings. CPython writes the destination into a\n\u003e `command =` value without escaping every character that `splitlines()` recognizes as a line boundary. A destination\n\u003e containing U+2028, for example, can therefore supply new `home` and `include-system-site-packages` keys during readback.\n\u003e Virtualenv writes those keys as real configuration lines. The builtin creator does not use this readback path.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproduction and evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e The scanner used one destination value with U+2028 between `home = /attacker/chosen`,\n\u003e `include-system-site-packages = true`, and an absorbing `x =` key. A `python -m venv --without-pip` control kept the\n\u003e value inside its `command` line and reported the base interpreter's `sys._home`. `virtualenv --creator venv --no-seed`\n\u003e on the same value wrote separate injected configuration lines and its interpreter reported\n\u003e `sys._home = /attacker/chosen`. The path did not require a literal newline byte. The control establishes that the extra\n\u003e keys came from virtualenv's readback, not from the standard library's initial file alone.\n\u003e \n\u003e The altered `home` can change which standard-library files the interpreter loads if an attacker also controls that\n\u003e location; `include-system-site-packages = true` weakens environment isolation. Choosing the venv creator is required,\n\u003e either explicitly or through a rare fallback. The report traces the readback to the 20.x rewrite, first released in\n\u003e 20.0.0b1. [PR #3426](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/pull/3426) rejects line-boundary characters before creation and\n\u003e rebuilds the final configuration from selected settings.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Attack sequence\n\u003e \n\u003e ```mermaid\n\u003e sequenceDiagram\n\u003e     participant A as Destination supplier\n\u003e     participant S as stdlib venv\n\u003e     participant V as virtualenv readback\n\u003e     participant C as Final pyvenv.cfg\n\u003e     participant P as Environment Python\n\u003e     A-\u003e\u003eS: path contains U+2028 and home = /attacker/chosen\n\u003e     S-\u003e\u003eC: write path inside command value\n\u003e     V-\u003e\u003eC: read with splitlines()\n\u003e     V-\u003e\u003eV: parse suffix as separate keys\n\u003e     V-\u003e\u003eC: rewrite home and include-system-site-packages\n\u003e     P-\u003e\u003eC: read injected keys on startup\n\u003e     C--\u003e\u003eP: sys._home changes and isolation is disabled\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducer files\n\u003e \n\u003e The following files are from the scanner report for `ANT-2026-6ZQHJ6VI`. They were tested against commit `a857a7823c73`\n\u003e in the scanner image. The `repro` entrypoint expects companion files under `/tmp/repro.d`; cases that change user IDs\n\u003e need a disposable root-capable container. The last output line is `BUG` when the reported behavior occurs. These files\n\u003e are included as evidence and were not rerun for this advisory update.\n\u003e \n\u003e **`repro`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e import os\n\u003e import shutil\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e \n\u003e work = \"/tmp/repro-venv-creator-cfg\"\n\u003e shutil.rmtree(work, ignore_errors=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e # the attacker's input: the text of a destination path.\n\u003e # U+2028 ends a line for str.splitlines() but not for CPython's own pyvenv.cfg reader.\n\u003e # The slashes only make nested directories; the last key, x, absorbs the rest of the path.\n\u003e LS = \"\\u2028\"\n\u003e dest = f\"{work}/proj{LS}home = /attacker/chosen{LS}include-system-site-packages = true{LS}x = /env\"\n\u003e \n\u003e base_python = os.path.realpath(shutil.which(\"python\"))\n\u003e real_home = os.path.dirname(base_python)\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def pyvenv_cfg_lines(env):\n\u003e     # split on \"\\n\" only, as the interpreter does\n\u003e     return open(f\"{env}/pyvenv.cfg\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read().split(\"\\n\")\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e def sys_home(env):\n\u003e     # site.py sets sys._home from the `home` key of the environment's pyvenv.cfg\n\u003e     probe = [f\"{env}/bin/python\", \"-c\", \"import sys; print(sys._home)\"]\n\u003e     return subprocess.run(probe, capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout.strip()\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e # control: the same path through the standard library alone\n\u003e stdlib_env = dest + \"-stdlib\"\n\u003e subprocess.run([base_python, \"-m\", \"venv\", \"--without-pip\", stdlib_env], check=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e # attack: the same path through virtualenv; --creator venv is the one non-default option\n\u003e virtualenv_env = dest + \"-virtualenv\"\n\u003e created = subprocess.run([\"virtualenv\", \"--quiet\", \"--no-periodic-update\", \"--no-seed\", \"--creator\", \"venv\", virtualenv_env])\n\u003e if created.returncode != 0:  # refusing such a destination is a valid fix\n\u003e     print(\"virtualenv refused the destination\")\n\u003e     print(\"OK\")\n\u003e     raise SystemExit\n\u003e \n\u003e # check\n\u003e stdlib_cfg, stdlib_home = pyvenv_cfg_lines(stdlib_env), sys_home(stdlib_env)\n\u003e virtualenv_cfg, virtualenv_home = pyvenv_cfg_lines(virtualenv_env), sys_home(virtualenv_env)\n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"CONTROL  python -m venv wrote pyvenv.cfg:\")\n\u003e for line in stdlib_cfg:\n\u003e     print(\"    \" + ascii(line))\n\u003e print(f\"CONTROL  python -m venv: sys._home = {stdlib_home}\")\n\u003e print(\"OBSERVED virtualenv --creator venv wrote pyvenv.cfg:\")\n\u003e for line in virtualenv_cfg:\n\u003e     print(\"    \" + ascii(line))\n\u003e print(f\"OBSERVED virtualenv --creator venv: sys._home = {virtualenv_home}\")\n\u003e \n\u003e keys_injected = \"home = /attacker/chosen\" in virtualenv_cfg and \"include-system-site-packages = true\" in virtualenv_cfg\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if stdlib_home == real_home and keys_injected and virtualenv_home == \"/attacker/chosen\" else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Destination path data can change configuration boundaries and therefore environment settings. The effect depends on\n\u003e which settings change and how the environment is subsequently used.\n\u003e \n\u003e Severity: low.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Versions tested\n\u003e \n\u003e The confirmed release is 21.14.8. The affected range records that release only; it does not establish the first affected\n\u003e version or exclude earlier releases. Fixed in 21.14.9.\n\u003e \n\u003e The fix rejects line boundaries in the supplied destination before filesystem access and checks the resolved\n\u003e path again before invoking stdlib venv. The final configuration is rebuilt from the selected interpreter and virtualenv\n\u003e options, without merging the intermediate stdlib file or its vendor-specific keys. Ordinary destination paths remain\n\u003e supported.\n\u003e \n\u003e Fix: [PR #3426](https://github.com/pypa/virtualenv/pull/3426).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Known mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e Reject line-boundary characters in externally supplied destination paths, or use the builtin creator where supported.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected release\n\u003e \n\u003e - [x] The problem reproduces on virtualenv 21.14.8.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by Anthropic's OSS Scanner as ANT-2026-6ZQHJ6VI.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | virtualenv | 20.31.2 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | virtualenv | 21.7.4 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-qmx5-4p3x-wvqf | virtualenv | 21.14.9 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113715\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113715\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
