              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-96639](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-96639)**.\n\n## [GHSA-v9pv-5gc8-w2m6](https://osv.dev/GHSA-v9pv-5gc8-w2m6)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e Pillow multi-frame image loaders can retain native `ImagingCore` storage allocated for one frame while changing the Python-side image `mode` and decoder tiles for a later frame.\n\u003e \n\u003e If frame 0 allocates storage for a narrow mode such as `L`, and a later frame of the same dimensions switches to a wider mode such as `RGB` or `CMYK`, Pillow may decode wider pixels into the stale narrow native buffer. This causes native\n\u003e out-of-bounds writes and process crashes. In the MPO/JPEG path, I also demonstrated command execution in matching Python/Pillow runtime profiles through Pillow's documented `ImageSequence.Iterator` + `frame.load()` path.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e The root cause appears to be that `ImageFile.load_prepare()` only reallocates native storage when `_im is None`. It does not verify that the retained native `ImagingCore` storage still matches `self.mode` and `self.size`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Several multi-frame `seek()` / `_open()` paths can update `self._mode` and decoder tiles for a later frame without invalidating `_im`. The decoder then writes or reads according to the new frame mode while the native storage still belongs to the old frame mode.\n\u003e \n\u003e Verified affected surfaces:\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. **PSD**\n\u003e \n\u003e    `PsdImageFile.seek()` can switch from grayscale/native `L` storage to RGB layer decoding while retaining `_im`. This produces attacker-controlled native out-of-bounds writes and reliable crashes.\n\u003e \n\u003e 2. **MPO/JPEG**\n\u003e \n\u003e    A crafted two-frame MPO can make frame 0 allocate `L` storage, then make frame 1 decode `CMYK` / `RGB` data into the retained `L` storage. The documented `ImageSequence.Iterator` path reaches this with `frame.load()`.\n\u003e    Verified native memory corruption, crash, over-read behavior, and scoped harmless-marker command execution on Pillow 12.3.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e 3. **DCX**\n\u003e \n\u003e    `DcxImageFile.seek()` calls `PcxImageFile._open()`, which changes mode/tile  state without a native-storage mode guard. A crafted DCX with `L` then `RGB` PCX frames produces retained-storage mismatch and crash.\n\u003e \n\u003e 4. **MIC**\n\u003e \n\u003e    MIC can call `TiffImageFile._open()` directly and bypass TIFF's normal guarded `seek()` behavior. With a `NextIFD`-based reachable frame transition, this produces retained-storage mismatch and crash.\n\u003e \n\u003e TIFF appears to be a useful positive control because its `seek()` path checks\n\u003e both size and mode before reusing storage.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e - process crash / denial of service,\n\u003e - native out-of-bounds write,\n\u003e - native over-read in the MPO path.\n\u003e \n\u003e Stronger proven impact:\n\u003e \n\u003e - command execution in matching Python/Pillow runtime profiles through the MPO/JPEG path.\n\u003e \n\u003e Server-side deployment impact:\n\u003e \n\u003e - Create a local vulnerable web upload service that accepts an unauthenticated MPO upload and automatically processes it with Pillow.\n\u003e - The attack is one HTTP request and requires no user interaction after upload.\n\u003e - The fixed harmless command-output marker succeeded 10/10 times.\n\u003e - The `_im=None` control was clean 10/10 times.\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproduction Evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e Tested version:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e Pillow 12.3.0\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e MPO terminal proof environments:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e Python 3.12.3 + Pillow 12.3.0, clean PyPI environment:\n\u003e marker 10/10, control 0/10\n\u003e \n\u003e Python 3.13.14 + Pillow 12.3.0, prepared profile:\n\u003e marker 10/10, control 0/10\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Server-side web-upload proof:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e control 1..10: MARKER_MISSING\n\u003e control_summary ok 0 missing 10 /10\n\u003e \n\u003e vulnerable 1..10: MARKER_OK marker_output \u003cserver-user\u003e\n\u003e vulnerable_summary ok 10 missing 0 /10\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The terminal proof uses a fixed harmless marker command only. It does not use a reverse shell, persistence, data exfiltration, or arbitrary attacker-selected commands.\n\u003e \n\u003e I can provide the following private artifacts to maintainers:\n\u003e \n\u003e - minimal crash fixtures for PSD, MPO, DCX, and MIC,\n\u003e - non-weaponized MPO marker PoC,\n\u003e - local vulnerable web upload demo,\n\u003e - gdb/GEF geometry receipts,\n\u003e - control-run logs,\n\u003e - proposed regression-test shape.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Minimal Reproduction Shape\n\u003e \n\u003e For the MPO path, the vulnerable consumer shape is:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e from io import BytesIO\n\u003e from PIL import Image, ImageSequence\n\u003e \n\u003e source = BytesIO(open(path, \"rb\").read())\n\u003e image = Image.open(source)\n\u003e iterator = ImageSequence.Iterator(image)\n\u003e next(iterator).load()\n\u003e next(iterator).load()\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The crafted image shape is:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e frame 0: mode L, dimensions W x H\n\u003e frame 1: same W x H, wider mode such as CMYK\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Frame 0 allocates native `L` storage. Frame 1 changes the logical mode and decoder state but can reuse the old native storage. The frame 1 decoder writes using the wider mode into the narrow storage.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested Fix Direction\n\u003e \n\u003e Before reusing native `ImagingCore` storage, Pillow should verify that the retained storage is compatible with the current logical image mode and size.\n\u003e \n\u003e A central guard in `ImageFile.load_prepare()` could compare native storage mode/size against `self.mode` / `self.size` and allocate fresh storage when they differ. Alternatively, every mode-changing `seek()` / `_open()` path should explicitly invalidate `_im` before decoding.\n\u003e \n\u003e Caution: TIFF has specialized tile-size behavior and already has a mode/size guard, so a central fix should avoid regressing TIFF tile-buffer handling.\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ## Credit\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e Rotem Kama\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Methodology Note\n\u003e \n\u003e AI-assisted analysis was used for discovery and verification support. Claims in this report are limited to the local artifacts, controls, and command outputs described above.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-v9pv-5gc8-w2m6 | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-96639\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-96639](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-96639)**.\n\n## [GHSA-v9pv-5gc8-w2m6](https://osv.dev/GHSA-v9pv-5gc8-w2m6)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e Pillow multi-frame image loaders can retain native `ImagingCore` storage allocated for one frame while changing the Python-side image `mode` and decoder tiles for a later frame.\n\u003e \n\u003e If frame 0 allocates storage for a narrow mode such as `L`, and a later frame of the same dimensions switches to a wider mode such as `RGB` or `CMYK`, Pillow may decode wider pixels into the stale narrow native buffer. This causes native\n\u003e out-of-bounds writes and process crashes. In the MPO/JPEG path, I also demonstrated command execution in matching Python/Pillow runtime profiles through Pillow's documented `ImageSequence.Iterator` + `frame.load()` path.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e The root cause appears to be that `ImageFile.load_prepare()` only reallocates native storage when `_im is None`. It does not verify that the retained native `ImagingCore` storage still matches `self.mode` and `self.size`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Several multi-frame `seek()` / `_open()` paths can update `self._mode` and decoder tiles for a later frame without invalidating `_im`. The decoder then writes or reads according to the new frame mode while the native storage still belongs to the old frame mode.\n\u003e \n\u003e Verified affected surfaces:\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. **PSD**\n\u003e \n\u003e    `PsdImageFile.seek()` can switch from grayscale/native `L` storage to RGB layer decoding while retaining `_im`. This produces attacker-controlled native out-of-bounds writes and reliable crashes.\n\u003e \n\u003e 2. **MPO/JPEG**\n\u003e \n\u003e    A crafted two-frame MPO can make frame 0 allocate `L` storage, then make frame 1 decode `CMYK` / `RGB` data into the retained `L` storage. The documented `ImageSequence.Iterator` path reaches this with `frame.load()`.\n\u003e    Verified native memory corruption, crash, over-read behavior, and scoped harmless-marker command execution on Pillow 12.3.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e 3. **DCX**\n\u003e \n\u003e    `DcxImageFile.seek()` calls `PcxImageFile._open()`, which changes mode/tile  state without a native-storage mode guard. A crafted DCX with `L` then `RGB` PCX frames produces retained-storage mismatch and crash.\n\u003e \n\u003e 4. **MIC**\n\u003e \n\u003e    MIC can call `TiffImageFile._open()` directly and bypass TIFF's normal guarded `seek()` behavior. With a `NextIFD`-based reachable frame transition, this produces retained-storage mismatch and crash.\n\u003e \n\u003e TIFF appears to be a useful positive control because its `seek()` path checks\n\u003e both size and mode before reusing storage.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e - process crash / denial of service,\n\u003e - native out-of-bounds write,\n\u003e - native over-read in the MPO path.\n\u003e \n\u003e Stronger proven impact:\n\u003e \n\u003e - command execution in matching Python/Pillow runtime profiles through the MPO/JPEG path.\n\u003e \n\u003e Server-side deployment impact:\n\u003e \n\u003e - Create a local vulnerable web upload service that accepts an unauthenticated MPO upload and automatically processes it with Pillow.\n\u003e - The attack is one HTTP request and requires no user interaction after upload.\n\u003e - The fixed harmless command-output marker succeeded 10/10 times.\n\u003e - The `_im=None` control was clean 10/10 times.\n\u003e \n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproduction Evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e Tested version:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e Pillow 12.3.0\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e MPO terminal proof environments:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e Python 3.12.3 + Pillow 12.3.0, clean PyPI environment:\n\u003e marker 10/10, control 0/10\n\u003e \n\u003e Python 3.13.14 + Pillow 12.3.0, prepared profile:\n\u003e marker 10/10, control 0/10\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Server