              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe undici WebSocket client is vulnerable to a denial-of-service attack via unbounded memory consumption during permessage-deflate decompression. When a WebSocket connection negotiates the permessage-deflate extension, the client decompresses incoming compressed frames without enforcing any limit on the decompressed data size. A malicious WebSocket server can send a small compressed frame (a \"decompression bomb\") that expands to an extremely large size in memory, causing the Node.js process to exhaust available memory and crash or become unresponsive.\n\nThe vulnerability exists in the `PerMessageDeflate.decompress()` method, which accumulates all decompressed chunks in memory and concatenates them into a single Buffer without checking whether the total size exceeds a safe threshold.\n\n## Impact\n\n- Remote denial of service against any Node.js application using undici's WebSocket client\n- A single compressed WebSocket frame of ~6 MB can decompress to ~1 GB or more\n- Memory exhaustion occurs in native/external memory, bypassing V8 heap limits\n- No application-level mitigation is possible as decompression occurs before message delivery\n\n### Patches\n\nUsers should upgrade to fixed versions.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nNo workaround are possible.",
                "text": "## Description\n\nThe undici WebSocket client is vulnerable to a denial-of-service attack via unbounded memory consumption during permessage-deflate decompression. When a WebSocket connection negotiates the permessage-deflate extension, the client decompresses incoming compressed frames without enforcing any limit on the decompressed data size. A malicious WebSocket server can send a small compressed frame (a \"decompression bomb\") that expands to an extremely large size in memory, causing the Node.js process to exhaust available memory and crash or become unresponsive.\n\nThe vulnerability exists in the `PerMessageDeflate.decompress()` method, which accumulates all decompressed chunks in memory and concatenates them into a single Buffer without checking whether the total size exceeds a safe threshold.\n\n## Impact\n\n- Remote denial of service against any Node.js application using undici's WebSocket client\n- A single compressed WebSocket frame of ~6 MB can decompress to ~1 GB or more\n- Memory exhaustion occurs in native/external memory, bypassing V8 heap limits\n- No application-level mitigation is possible as decompression occurs before message delivery\n\n### Patches\n\nUsers should upgrade to fixed versions.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nNo workaround are possible."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-1526](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-1526)**.\n\n## [GHSA-vrm6-8vpv-qv8q](https://osv.dev/GHSA-vrm6-8vpv-qv8q)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Description\n\u003e \n\u003e The undici WebSocket client is vulnerable to a denial-of-service attack via unbounded memory consumption during permessage-deflate decompression. When a WebSocket connection negotiates the permessage-deflate extension, the client decompresses incoming compressed frames without enforcing any limit on the decompressed data size. A malicious WebSocket server can send a small compressed frame (a \"decompression bomb\") that expands to an extremely large size in memory, causing the Node.js process to exhaust available memory and crash or become unresponsive.\n\u003e \n\u003e The vulnerability exists in the `PerMessageDeflate.decompress()` method, which accumulates all decompressed chunks in memory and concatenates them into a single Buffer without checking whether the total size exceeds a safe threshold.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e - Remote denial of service against any Node.js application using undici's WebSocket client\n\u003e - A single compressed WebSocket frame of ~6 MB can decompress to ~1 GB or more\n\u003e - Memory exhaustion occurs in native/external memory, bypassing V8 heap limits\n\u003e - No application-level mitigation is possible as decompression occurs before message delivery\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Users should upgrade to fixed versions.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e No workaround are possible.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | undici | 5.28.4 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-vrm6-8vpv-qv8q | undici | 6.24.0, 7.24.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-1526\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-1526](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-1526)**.\n\n## [GHSA-vrm6-8vpv-qv8q](https://osv.dev/GHSA-vrm6-8vpv-qv8q)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Description\n\u003e \n\u003e The undici WebSocket client is vulnerable to a denial-of-service attack via unbounded memory consumption during permessage-deflate decompression. When a WebSocket connection negotiates the permessage-deflate extension, the client decompresses incoming compressed frames without enforcing any limit on the decompressed data size. A malicious WebSocket server can send a small compressed frame (a \"decompression bomb\") that expands to an extremely large size in memory, causing the Node.js process to exhaust available memory and crash or become unresponsive.\n\u003e \n\u003e The vulnerability exists in the `PerMessageDeflate.decompress()` method, which accumulates all decompressed chunks in memory and concatenates them into a single Buffer without checking whether the total size exceeds a safe threshold.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e - Remote denial of service against any Node.js application using undici's WebSocket client\n\u003e - A single compressed WebSocket frame of ~6 MB can decompress to ~1 GB or more\n\u003e - Memory exhaustion occurs in native/external memory, bypassing V8 heap limits\n\u003e - No application-level mitigation is possible as decompression occurs before message delivery\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Users should upgrade to fixed versions.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e No workaround are possible.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | undici | 5.28.4 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-vrm6-8vpv-qv8q | undici | 6.24.0, 7.24.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-1526\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-1526",
              "name": "CVE-2026-1526",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-1526: Undici has Unbounded Memory Consumption in WebSocket permessage-deflate Decompression",
                "text": "CVE-2026-1526: Undici has Unbounded Memory Consumption in WebSocket permessage-deflate Decompression"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-112899",
                "GHSA-vx6w-vv9f-6gvg"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "```\n============================================================================\nEPS load(scale) lets Ghostscript render past the decompression bomb limit\nLocation: src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:92  in Ghostscript()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nWhen an application opens an untrusted Encapsulated PostScript file and asks Pillow to render it at a larger scale, Pillow has Ghostscript produce an image several times bigger than Pillow's configured size limit allows. The size check runs only after Ghostscript has finished rendering and written the full raw image to a temporary file. An attacker who can upload a file of about 100 bytes makes each such request spend scale² times the Ghostscript rendering work and temporary disk space that the limit is meant to permit. For example, at scale 2 that is a 972 MB temporary file.\n\nThe cause is in `Ghostscript()` in `src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py`. It multiplies the file-declared size by the caller's `scale` (lines 93-94) and passes the product to `gs` as the `-g` output geometry. It never calls `Image._decompression_bomb_check()` on the scaled size. The only check is the one `Image.open()` ran on the unscaled size, so a file declaring 9000×9000 passes, and `load(scale=2)` then asks Ghostscript for 18000×18000 (3.6× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`). A file declaring 18000×18000 directly would be refused before Ghostscript ever ran. `DecompressionBombError` fires only when Pillow reopens the output file, so Pillow's own in-process allocation stays bounded and there is no memory corruption.\n\nThe effect is a constant-factor overshoot rather than an unbounded one, for three reasons:\n\n* The multiplier is an integer chosen by the application, not by the file.\n* With the default warning filter, `Image.open()` already lets files up to 2× the limit reach Ghostscript at `scale=1`.\n* Any application feeding untrusted files to Ghostscript is already exposed to a tiny file that keeps Ghostscript busy indefinitely, since `gs` runs with no timeout.\n\nThis is the EPS counterpart of the WMF `load(dpi)` gap closed in af70b075a. It has been present since the decompression-bomb limit was introduced in ce2955ec7 (2.5.0). The attached reproducer stubs out Ghostscript and shows a 76-byte file passing `Image.open()` and reaching `gs` with `-g18000x18000`. A one-line patch mirroring the WMF check is included.\n\n## Details\n\n`EpsImageFile.load(scale=N)` (the documented way to rasterise an EPS at higher resolution) renders `width*N × height*N` pixels through Ghostscript, but the only decompression-bomb check on the way is the one `Image.open()` ran on the *unscaled*, file-declared `width × height`. The scaled product is never passed to `Image._decompression_bomb_check()` before `gs` is started.\n\nThis 111-byte EPS declares 9000×9000 = 81,000,000 pixels, below the default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` (89,478,485), so `Image.open()` accepts it without even a warning:\n\n```\n%!PS-Adobe-3.0 EPSF-3.0\n%%BoundingBox: 0 0 9000 9000\n%%EndComments\n0 0 moveto 9000 9000 lineto stroke\nshowpage\n```\n\n`im.load(scale=2)` on it then executes\n\n```\ngs -q -g18000x18000 -r144.000000x144.000000 -dBATCH -dNOPAUSE -dSAFER -sDEVICE=pnmraw -sOutputFile=/tmp/tmpXXXX -c 0 0 translate -f attack.eps -c showpage\n```\n\ni.e. asks Ghostscript for an 18000×18000 = 324,000,000-pixel raster (3.6× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`), whose `pnmraw` output is a 972,000,019-byte file in the temp directory. An EPS that declares 18000×18000 directly is rejected by `Image.open()` with `DecompressionBombError` before Ghostscript is ever run; via `scale=2` the same raster is rendered first and the `DecompressionBombError` only appears afterwards, when Pillow opens the file Ghostscript produced. (Between 1× and 2× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`, `Image.open()` only emits `DecompressionBombWarning`, so with the default warning filter an EPS declaring e.g. 13377×13377 is already rendered at `scale=1` — a 537 MB PNM. Against that baseline, `scale=2` is 4× an already-permitted render.)\n\nThe arithmetic is in `Ghostscript()`:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:91-97\n    # Hack to support hi-res rendering\n    scale = int(scale) or 1\n    width = size[0] * scale\n    height = size[1] * scale\n    # resolution is dependent on bbox and size\n    res_x = 72.0 * width / (bbox[2] - bbox[0])\n    …\n# src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:139-142\n    command = [\n        gs_binary,\n        \"-q\",  # quiet mode\n        f\"-g{width:d}x{height:d}\",  # set output geometry (pixels)\n    …\n# src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:165-168\n        subprocess.check_call(command, startupinfo=startupinfo)\n        with Image.open(outfile) as out_im:\n            out_im.load()\n            return out_im.im.copy()\n```\n\nand its caller does nothing between taking `scale` from the application and invoking it:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:411-417\n    def load(self, scale: int = 1, transparency: bool = False) -\u003e …:\n        # Load EPS via Ghostscript\n        if self.tile:\n            assert self.fp is not None\n            self.im = Ghostscript(self.tile, self.size, self.fp, scale, transparency)\n```\n\n`size` here is `self._size`, computed in `_open()` from the file's `%%BoundingBox` (or `%ImageData`) and checked once by `Image.open()` at `src/PIL/Image.py:3700`. For the benign path (`scale=1`) that check covers exactly what Ghostscript is asked to render. For `scale=N` the rendered pixel count is N² times what was checked, so a file can sit just under the limit at open time and have Ghostscript render 4× (scale 2), 9× (scale 3), 16× (scale 4) the configured budget. The first point at which Pillow looks at the real size again is `Image.open(outfile)` at line 166 — after `subprocess.check_call` has returned, i.e. after Ghostscript has spent the CPU and written `width*height*3` bytes to `tempfile.mkstemp()`'s directory.\n\n`WmfStubImageFile.load(dpi)` has the same shape (file-derived extent × caller-supplied factor → new raster) and, since af70b075a, calls `Image._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)` on the recomputed size before rendering (`src/PIL/WmfImagePlugin.py:127-131`). The EPS path lacks the equivalent line. The gap dates from ce2955ec7128 (\"Throw an exception when an opened image is larger than an arbitrary limit\", 2014-05-14, first released in 2.5.0), which added the check to `Image.open()` without covering the `scale` path.\n\n## Impact\n\nAffected are applications that open untrusted EPS/PS with Ghostscript installed and call `load(scale=N)` with a fixed N \u003e 1, as the handbook suggests for non-blurry output. Handing untrusted EPS to Ghostscript at all already lets the same attacker tie up a worker with an equally tiny file (e.g. a PostScript `{} loop`; Pillow runs `gs` with no timeout), so this bug adds a constant factor rather than a new denial-of-service capability. Within that setting, a ~110-byte upload makes each request run Ghostscript over N² × `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` pixels (N² × 2·`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` if the application does not turn `DecompressionBombWarning` into an error) and write a `W·H·3`-byte PNM into the temp directory — 972 MB for the example above at scale 2, 2.2 GB at scale 3, 8.6 GB for a 178-Mpixel declaration at scale 4 — before any limit is applied. The operator's `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` setting is exceeded by a factor of N²; N is an integer chosen by the application, not by the file, so the work stays bounded at N² × 2·`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`. The consequence is N² more Ghostscript CPU and temp-disk use per request than the configured limit intends; Pillow's own in-process pixel allocation remains bounded because `Image.open(outfile)` re-checks the PNM's size, and there is no memory corruption.\n\nThis is a sibling of the WMF `load(dpi)` issue fixed in af70b075a (\"Add decompression bomb check when loading at a different DPI\"); the EPS `scale` path was not covered by that change. Unlike the WMF case, where the file-controlled `_inch` makes the ratio unbounded, the EPS multiplier is fixed by the application.\n\n## Reproducing\n\nRun `repro` against the installed Pillow. Ghostscript does not need to be installed: the script sets `EpsImagePlugin.gs_binary = \"gs\"` so that `has_ghostscript()` skips its `gs --version` probe, and replaces `subprocess.check_call` with a function that records the command line Pillow builds and then raises `OSError`, so nothing is actually rendered.\n\nIt writes a 76-byte EPS declaring `%%BoundingBox: 0 0 9000 9000` (the example above without the drawing line; 81 Mpixel, below the default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` of 89,478,485) and turns `DecompressionBombWarning` into an error, so any bomb check — warning or hard error — counts. It then calls `Image.open()` and `im.load(scale=2)`. `Image.open()` accepts the file and `load(scale=2)` reaches the intercepted Ghostscript call with `-g18000x18000` (324,000,000 pixels, 3.6× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`, a 972 MB `pnmraw` output with real Ghostscript). The script prints the geometry it captured.\n\nIt prints `BUG` when Ghostscript was invoked with a `-g` geometry above `2*MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` (178,956,970) and no decompression-bomb check fired before that call; otherwise it prints `OK`. With the attached patch applied, `load(scale=2)` raises `DecompressionBombError` before Ghostscript is called and the script prints `OK`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nThe attached patch checks the scaled size in `Ghostscript()` right after the scaled `width`/`height` are computed (`src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:93-94`) and before the `gs` command is built, mirroring `WmfStubImageFile.load`:\n\n```diff\n--- a/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n+++ b/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n@@ -92,6 +92,7 @@ def Ghostscript(\n     scale = int(scale) or 1\n     width = size[0] * scale\n     height = size[1] * scale\n+    Image._decompression_bomb_check((width, height))\n     # resolution is dependent on bbox and size\n     res_x = 72.0 * width / (bbox[2] - bbox[0])\n     res_y = 72.0 * height / (bbox[3] - bbox[1])\n```\n\n`load(scale=N)` now raises `DecompressionBombError` / emits `DecompressionBombWarning` under the same `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` thresholds `Image.open()` applies, before Ghostscript runs instead of after. Placing the check in `Ghostscript()` rather than in `EpsImageFile.load()` is the single point where the rendered extent is decided, so it also covers direct callers of the module-level function. With the patch applied to a clean checkout the reproducer prints `OK` and `Tests/test_file_eps.py` passes. One cosmetic side effect: at `scale=1` the check re-emits the `DecompressionBombWarning` that `Image.open()` already issued for sizes between 1× and 2× the limit; guarding the call with `if scale \u003e 1:` would avoid the duplicate.\n\nThe patch also adds `test_load_scale_decompression_bomb` to `Tests/test_file_eps.py`, in the style of the WMF test: it sets `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` to `im.width * im.height` and expects `im.load(scale=2)` to raise `DecompressionBombError`. Like the rest of that file it is skipped without Ghostscript, because `has_ghostscript()` is consulted at the top of `Ghostscript()`; moving the check above that call would let the test run without it.\n\n## The patch\n\nAdd decompression bomb check on the scaled size in EpsImagePlugin.Ghostscript() before Ghostscript is invoked\n\nApplies to `src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_file_eps.py b/Tests/test_file_eps.py\nindex 70eced62a..de331d97f 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_file_eps.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_file_eps.py\n@@ -98,6 +98,14 @@ def test_sanity(filename: str, size: tuple[int, int], scale: int) -\u003e None:\n         assert image.format == \"EPS\"\n \n \n+@pytest.mark.skipif(not HAS_GHOSTSCRIPT, reason=\"Ghostscript not available\")\n+def test_load_scale_decompression_bomb(monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch) -\u003e None:\n+    with Image.open(FILE1) as im:\n+        monkeypatch.setattr(Image, \"MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS\", im.width * im.height)\n+        with pytest.raises(Image.DecompressionBombError):\n+            im.load(scale=2)\n+\n+\n @pytest.mark.skipif(not HAS_GHOSTSCRIPT, reason=\"Ghostscript not available\")\n def test_load() -\u003e None:\n     with Image.open(FILE1) as im:\ndiff --git a/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\nindex 5eaefe339..ccc6f1d85 100644\n--- a/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n+++ b/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n@@ -92,6 +92,7 @@ def Ghostscript(\n     scale = int(scale) or 1\n     width = size[0] * scale\n     height = size[1] * scale\n+    Image._decompression_bomb_check((width, height))\n     # resolution is dependent on bbox and size\n     res_x = 72.0 * width / (bbox[2] - bbox[0])\n     res_y = 72.0 * height / (bbox[3] - bbox[1])\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 1,845 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n# EpsImagePlugin.Ghostscript(): load(scale=N) hands gs a -g geometry of\n# (W*N)x(H*N) with no _decompression_bomb_check on the scaled size; only the\n# unscaled WxH was checked by Image.open(). gs is not installed here, so we\n# set gs_binary and intercept subprocess.check_call to read the argv Pillow builds.\nimport os, subprocess, tempfile, warnings\nfrom PIL import Image, EpsImagePlugin\n\nEpsImagePlugin.gs_binary = \"gs\"  # skip the `gs --version` probe\npath = os.path.join(tempfile.mkdtemp(), \"a.eps\")\nwith open(path, \"w\") as f:  # 9000x9000 = 81 Mpx \u003c MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS (89.5 Mpx)\n    f.write(\"%!PS-Adobe-3.0 EPSF-3.0\\n%%BoundingBox: 0 0 9000 9000\\n%%EndComments\\nshowpage\\n\")\n\ncalls = []\ndef fake_check_call(cmd, **kw):\n    calls.append(cmd)\n    raise OSError(\"gs intercepted\")  # stop here; we only need the argv\nsubprocess.check_call = fake_check_call\n\nhard = 2 * Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS\nbomb_before_gs = False\nwith warnings.catch_warnings():\n    warnings.simplefilter(\"error\", Image.DecompressionBombWarning)\n    try:\n        with Image.open(path) as im:\n            print(\"Image.open accepted\", os.path.getsize(path), \"byte EPS, size\", im.size)\n            im.load(scale=2)\n    except (Image.DecompressionBombError, Image.DecompressionBombWarning) as e:\n        bomb_before_gs = not calls\n        print(\"bomb check fired\", \"before\" if bomb_before_gs else \"after\", \"gs:\", e)\n    except OSError as e:\n        print(\"load() reached gs:\", e)\n\nover = False\nfor c in calls:\n    g = next(a for a in c if a.startswith(\"-g\"))[2:]\n    w, h = map(int, g.split(\"x\"))\n    over |= w * h \u003e hard\n    print(f\"gs argv geometry -g{g} = {w*h} px ({w*h/Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS:.1f}x MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS, \"\n          f\"hard limit {hard}); pnmraw output would be {w*h*3} bytes\")\nprint(\"BUG\" if calls and over and not bomb_before_gs else \"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---",
                "text": "```\n============================================================================\nEPS load(scale) lets Ghostscript render past the decompression bomb limit\nLocation: src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:92  in Ghostscript()\nCommit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n============================================================================\n```\n\nWhen an application opens an untrusted Encapsulated PostScript file and asks Pillow to render it at a larger scale, Pillow has Ghostscript produce an image several times bigger than Pillow's configured size limit allows. The size check runs only after Ghostscript has finished rendering and written the full raw image to a temporary file. An attacker who can upload a file of about 100 bytes makes each such request spend scale² times the Ghostscript rendering work and temporary disk space that the limit is meant to permit. For example, at scale 2 that is a 972 MB temporary file.\n\nThe cause is in `Ghostscript()` in `src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py`. It multiplies the file-declared size by the caller's `scale` (lines 93-94) and passes the product to `gs` as the `-g` output geometry. It never calls `Image._decompression_bomb_check()` on the scaled size. The only check is the one `Image.open()` ran on the unscaled size, so a file declaring 9000×9000 passes, and `load(scale=2)` then asks Ghostscript for 18000×18000 (3.6× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`). A file declaring 18000×18000 directly would be refused before Ghostscript ever ran. `DecompressionBombError` fires only when Pillow reopens the output file, so Pillow's own in-process allocation stays bounded and there is no memory corruption.\n\nThe effect is a constant-factor overshoot rather than an unbounded one, for three reasons:\n\n* The multiplier is an integer chosen by the application, not by the file.\n* With the default warning filter, `Image.open()` already lets files up to 2× the limit reach Ghostscript at `scale=1`.\n* Any application feeding untrusted files to Ghostscript is already exposed to a tiny file that keeps Ghostscript busy indefinitely, since `gs` runs with no timeout.\n\nThis is the EPS counterpart of the WMF `load(dpi)` gap closed in af70b075a. It has been present since the decompression-bomb limit was introduced in ce2955ec7 (2.5.0). The attached reproducer stubs out Ghostscript and shows a 76-byte file passing `Image.open()` and reaching `gs` with `-g18000x18000`. A one-line patch mirroring the WMF check is included.\n\n## Details\n\n`EpsImageFile.load(scale=N)` (the documented way to rasterise an EPS at higher resolution) renders `width*N × height*N` pixels through Ghostscript, but the only decompression-bomb check on the way is the one `Image.open()` ran on the *unscaled*, file-declared `width × height`. The scaled product is never passed to `Image._decompression_bomb_check()` before `gs` is started.\n\nThis 111-byte EPS declares 9000×9000 = 81,000,000 pixels, below the default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` (89,478,485), so `Image.open()` accepts it without even a warning:\n\n```\n%!PS-Adobe-3.0 EPSF-3.0\n%%BoundingBox: 0 0 9000 9000\n%%EndComments\n0 0 moveto 9000 9000 lineto stroke\nshowpage\n```\n\n`im.load(scale=2)` on it then executes\n\n```\ngs -q -g18000x18000 -r144.000000x144.000000 -dBATCH -dNOPAUSE -dSAFER -sDEVICE=pnmraw -sOutputFile=/tmp/tmpXXXX -c 0 0 translate -f attack.eps -c showpage\n```\n\ni.e. asks Ghostscript for an 18000×18000 = 324,000,000-pixel raster (3.6× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`), whose `pnmraw` output is a 972,000,019-byte file in the temp directory. An EPS that declares 18000×18000 directly is rejected by `Image.open()` with `DecompressionBombError` before Ghostscript is ever run; via `scale=2` the same raster is rendered first and the `DecompressionBombError` only appears afterwards, when Pillow opens the file Ghostscript produced. (Between 1× and 2× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`, `Image.open()` only emits `DecompressionBombWarning`, so with the default warning filter an EPS declaring e.g. 13377×13377 is already rendered at `scale=1` — a 537 MB PNM. Against that baseline, `scale=2` is 4× an already-permitted render.)\n\nThe arithmetic is in `Ghostscript()`:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:91-97\n    # Hack to support hi-res rendering\n    scale = int(scale) or 1\n    width = size[0] * scale\n    height = size[1] * scale\n    # resolution is dependent on bbox and size\n    res_x = 72.0 * width / (bbox[2] - bbox[0])\n    …\n# src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:139-142\n    command = [\n        gs_binary,\n        \"-q\",  # quiet mode\n        f\"-g{width:d}x{height:d}\",  # set output geometry (pixels)\n    …\n# src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:165-168\n        subprocess.check_call(command, startupinfo=startupinfo)\n        with Image.open(outfile) as out_im:\n            out_im.load()\n            return out_im.im.copy()\n```\n\nand its caller does nothing between taking `scale` from the application and invoking it:\n\n```python\n# src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:411-417\n    def load(self, scale: int = 1, transparency: bool = False) -\u003e …:\n        # Load EPS via Ghostscript\n        if self.tile:\n            assert self.fp is not None\n            self.im = Ghostscript(self.tile, self.size, self.fp, scale, transparency)\n```\n\n`size` here is `self._size`, computed in `_open()` from the file's `%%BoundingBox` (or `%ImageData`) and checked once by `Image.open()` at `src/PIL/Image.py:3700`. For the benign path (`scale=1`) that check covers exactly what Ghostscript is asked to render. For `scale=N` the rendered pixel count is N² times what was checked, so a file can sit just under the limit at open time and have Ghostscript render 4× (scale 2), 9× (scale 3), 16× (scale 4) the configured budget. The first point at which Pillow looks at the real size again is `Image.open(outfile)` at line 166 — after `subprocess.check_call` has returned, i.e. after Ghostscript has spent the CPU and written `width*height*3` bytes to `tempfile.mkstemp()`'s directory.\n\n`WmfStubImageFile.load(dpi)` has the same shape (file-derived extent × caller-supplied factor → new raster) and, since af70b075a, calls `Image._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)` on the recomputed size before rendering (`src/PIL/WmfImagePlugin.py:127-131`). The EPS path lacks the equivalent line. The gap dates from ce2955ec7128 (\"Throw an exception when an opened image is larger than an arbitrary limit\", 2014-05-14, first released in 2.5.0), which added the check to `Image.open()` without covering the `scale` path.\n\n## Impact\n\nAffected are applications that open untrusted EPS/PS with Ghostscript installed and call `load(scale=N)` with a fixed N \u003e 1, as the handbook suggests for non-blurry output. Handing untrusted EPS to Ghostscript at all already lets the same attacker tie up a worker with an equally tiny file (e.g. a PostScript `{} loop`; Pillow runs `gs` with no timeout), so this bug adds a constant factor rather than a new denial-of-service capability. Within that setting, a ~110-byte upload makes each request run Ghostscript over N² × `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` pixels (N² × 2·`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` if the application does not turn `DecompressionBombWarning` into an error) and write a `W·H·3`-byte PNM into the temp directory — 972 MB for the example above at scale 2, 2.2 GB at scale 3, 8.6 GB for a 178-Mpixel declaration at scale 4 — before any limit is applied. The operator's `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` setting is exceeded by a factor of N²; N is an integer chosen by the application, not by the file, so the work stays bounded at N² × 2·`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`. The consequence is N² more Ghostscript CPU and temp-disk use per request than the configured limit intends; Pillow's own in-process pixel allocation remains bounded because `Image.open(outfile)` re-checks the PNM's size, and there is no memory corruption.\n\nThis is a sibling of the WMF `load(dpi)` issue fixed in af70b075a (\"Add decompression bomb check when loading at a different DPI\"); the EPS `scale` path was not covered by that change. Unlike the WMF case, where the file-controlled `_inch` makes the ratio unbounded, the EPS multiplier is fixed by the application.\n\n## Reproducing\n\nRun `repro` against the installed Pillow. Ghostscript does not need to be installed: the script sets `EpsImagePlugin.gs_binary = \"gs\"` so that `has_ghostscript()` skips its `gs --version` probe, and replaces `subprocess.check_call` with a function that records the command line Pillow builds and then raises `OSError`, so nothing is actually rendered.\n\nIt writes a 76-byte EPS declaring `%%BoundingBox: 0 0 9000 9000` (the example above without the drawing line; 81 Mpixel, below the default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` of 89,478,485) and turns `DecompressionBombWarning` into an error, so any bomb check — warning or hard error — counts. It then calls `Image.open()` and `im.load(scale=2)`. `Image.open()` accepts the file and `load(scale=2)` reaches the intercepted Ghostscript call with `-g18000x18000` (324,000,000 pixels, 3.6× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`, a 972 MB `pnmraw` output with real Ghostscript). The script prints the geometry it captured.\n\nIt prints `BUG` when Ghostscript was invoked with a `-g` geometry above `2*MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` (178,956,970) and no decompression-bomb check fired before that call; otherwise it prints `OK`. With the attached patch applied, `load(scale=2)` raises `DecompressionBombError` before Ghostscript is called and the script prints `OK`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nThe attached patch checks the scaled size in `Ghostscript()` right after the scaled `width`/`height` are computed (`src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:93-94`) and before the `gs` command is built, mirroring `WmfStubImageFile.load`:\n\n```diff\n--- a/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n+++ b/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n@@ -92,6 +92,7 @@ def Ghostscript(\n     scale = int(scale) or 1\n     width = size[0] * scale\n     height = size[1] * scale\n+    Image._decompression_bomb_check((width, height))\n     # resolution is dependent on bbox and size\n     res_x = 72.0 * width / (bbox[2] - bbox[0])\n     res_y = 72.0 * height / (bbox[3] - bbox[1])\n```\n\n`load(scale=N)` now raises `DecompressionBombError` / emits `DecompressionBombWarning` under the same `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` thresholds `Image.open()` applies, before Ghostscript runs instead of after. Placing the check in `Ghostscript()` rather than in `EpsImageFile.load()` is the single point where the rendered extent is decided, so it also covers direct callers of the module-level function. With the patch applied to a clean checkout the reproducer prints `OK` and `Tests/test_file_eps.py` passes. One cosmetic side effect: at `scale=1` the check re-emits the `DecompressionBombWarning` that `Image.open()` already issued for sizes between 1× and 2× the limit; guarding the call with `if scale \u003e 1:` would avoid the duplicate.\n\nThe patch also adds `test_load_scale_decompression_bomb` to `Tests/test_file_eps.py`, in the style of the WMF test: it sets `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` to `im.width * im.height` and expects `im.load(scale=2)` to raise `DecompressionBombError`. Like the rest of that file it is skipped without Ghostscript, because `has_ghostscript()` is consulted at the top of `Ghostscript()`; moving the check above that call would let the test run without it.\n\n## The patch\n\nAdd decompression bomb check on the scaled size in EpsImagePlugin.Ghostscript() before Ghostscript is invoked\n\nApplies to `src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/Tests/test_file_eps.py b/Tests/test_file_eps.py\nindex 70eced62a..de331d97f 100644\n--- a/Tests/test_file_eps.py\n+++ b/Tests/test_file_eps.py\n@@ -98,6 +98,14 @@ def test_sanity(filename: str, size: tuple[int, int], scale: int) -\u003e None:\n         assert image.format == \"EPS\"\n \n \n+@pytest.mark.skipif(not HAS_GHOSTSCRIPT, reason=\"Ghostscript not available\")\n+def test_load_scale_decompression_bomb(monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch) -\u003e None:\n+    with Image.open(FILE1) as im:\n+        monkeypatch.setattr(Image, \"MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS\", im.width * im.height)\n+        with pytest.raises(Image.DecompressionBombError):\n+            im.load(scale=2)\n+\n+\n @pytest.mark.skipif(not HAS_GHOSTSCRIPT, reason=\"Ghostscript not available\")\n def test_load() -\u003e None:\n     with Image.open(FILE1) as im:\ndiff --git a/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\nindex 5eaefe339..ccc6f1d85 100644\n--- a/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n+++ b/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n@@ -92,6 +92,7 @@ def Ghostscript(\n     scale = int(scale) or 1\n     width = size[0] * scale\n     height = size[1] * scale\n+    Image._decompression_bomb_check((width, height))\n     # resolution is dependent on bbox and size\n     res_x = 72.0 * width / (bbox[2] - bbox[0])\n     res_y = 72.0 * height / (bbox[3] - bbox[1])\n```\n\n## The reproducer\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 1,845 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nIts last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n# EpsImagePlugin.Ghostscript(): load(scale=N) hands gs a -g geometry of\n# (W*N)x(H*N) with no _decompression_bomb_check on the scaled size; only the\n# unscaled WxH was checked by Image.open(). gs is not installed here, so we\n# set gs_binary and intercept subprocess.check_call to read the argv Pillow builds.\nimport os, subprocess, tempfile, warnings\nfrom PIL import Image, EpsImagePlugin\n\nEpsImagePlugin.gs_binary = \"gs\"  # skip the `gs --version` probe\npath = os.path.join(tempfile.mkdtemp(), \"a.eps\")\nwith open(path, \"w\") as f:  # 9000x9000 = 81 Mpx \u003c MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS (89.5 Mpx)\n    f.write(\"%!PS-Adobe-3.0 EPSF-3.0\\n%%BoundingBox: 0 0 9000 9000\\n%%EndComments\\nshowpage\\n\")\n\ncalls = []\ndef fake_check_call(cmd, **kw):\n    calls.append(cmd)\n    raise OSError(\"gs intercepted\")  # stop here; we only need the argv\nsubprocess.check_call = fake_check_call\n\nhard = 2 * Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS\nbomb_before_gs = False\nwith warnings.catch_warnings():\n    warnings.simplefilter(\"error\", Image.DecompressionBombWarning)\n    try:\n        with Image.open(path) as im:\n            print(\"Image.open accepted\", os.path.getsize(path), \"byte EPS, size\", im.size)\n            im.load(scale=2)\n    except (Image.DecompressionBombError, Image.DecompressionBombWarning) as e:\n        bomb_before_gs = not calls\n        print(\"bomb check fired\", \"before\" if bomb_before_gs else \"after\", \"gs:\", e)\n    except OSError as e:\n        print(\"load() reached gs:\", e)\n\nover = False\nfor c in calls:\n    g = next(a for a in c if a.startswith(\"-g\"))[2:]\n    w, h = map(int, g.split(\"x\"))\n    over |= w * h \u003e hard\n    print(f\"gs argv geometry -g{g} = {w*h} px ({w*h/Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS:.1f}x MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS, \"\n          f\"hard limit {hard}); pnmraw output would be {w*h*3} bytes\")\nprint(\"BUG\" if calls and over and not bomb_before_gs else \"OK\")\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112899](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112899)**.\n\n## [GHSA-vx6w-vv9f-6gvg](https://osv.dev/GHSA-vx6w-vv9f-6gvg)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e EPS load(scale) lets Ghostscript render past the decompression bomb limit\n\u003e Location: src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:92  in Ghostscript()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e When an application opens an untrusted Encapsulated PostScript file and asks Pillow to render it at a larger scale, Pillow has Ghostscript produce an image several times bigger than Pillow's configured size limit allows. The size check runs only after Ghostscript has finished rendering and written the full raw image to a temporary file. An attacker who can upload a file of about 100 bytes makes each such request spend scale² times the Ghostscript rendering work and temporary disk space that the limit is meant to permit. For example, at scale 2 that is a 972 MB temporary file.\n\u003e \n\u003e The cause is in `Ghostscript()` in `src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py`. It multiplies the file-declared size by the caller's `scale` (lines 93-94) and passes the product to `gs` as the `-g` output geometry. It never calls `Image._decompression_bomb_check()` on the scaled size. The only check is the one `Image.open()` ran on the unscaled size, so a file declaring 9000×9000 passes, and `load(scale=2)` then asks Ghostscript for 18000×18000 (3.6× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`). A file declaring 18000×18000 directly would be refused before Ghostscript ever ran. `DecompressionBombError` fires only when Pillow reopens the output file, so Pillow's own in-process allocation stays bounded and there is no memory corruption.\n\u003e \n\u003e The effect is a constant-factor overshoot rather than an unbounded one, for three reasons:\n\u003e \n\u003e * The multiplier is an integer chosen by the application, not by the file.\n\u003e * With the default warning filter, `Image.open()` already lets files up to 2× the limit reach Ghostscript at `scale=1`.\n\u003e * Any application feeding untrusted files to Ghostscript is already exposed to a tiny file that keeps Ghostscript busy indefinitely, since `gs` runs with no timeout.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is the EPS counterpart of the WMF `load(dpi)` gap closed in af70b075a. It has been present since the decompression-bomb limit was introduced in ce2955ec7 (2.5.0). The attached reproducer stubs out Ghostscript and shows a 76-byte file passing `Image.open()` and reaching `gs` with `-g18000x18000`. A one-line patch mirroring the WMF check is included.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `EpsImageFile.load(scale=N)` (the documented way to rasterise an EPS at higher resolution) renders `width*N × height*N` pixels through Ghostscript, but the only decompression-bomb check on the way is the one `Image.open()` ran on the *unscaled*, file-declared `width × height`. The scaled product is never passed to `Image._decompression_bomb_check()` before `gs` is started.\n\u003e \n\u003e This 111-byte EPS declares 9000×9000 = 81,000,000 pixels, below the default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` (89,478,485), so `Image.open()` accepts it without even a warning:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e %!PS-Adobe-3.0 EPSF-3.0\n\u003e %%BoundingBox: 0 0 9000 9000\n\u003e %%EndComments\n\u003e 0 0 moveto 9000 9000 lineto stroke\n\u003e showpage\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `im.load(scale=2)` on it then executes\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e gs -q -g18000x18000 -r144.000000x144.000000 -dBATCH -dNOPAUSE -dSAFER -sDEVICE=pnmraw -sOutputFile=/tmp/tmpXXXX -c 0 0 translate -f attack.eps -c showpage\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e i.e. asks Ghostscript for an 18000×18000 = 324,000,000-pixel raster (3.6× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`), whose `pnmraw` output is a 972,000,019-byte file in the temp directory. An EPS that declares 18000×18000 directly is rejected by `Image.open()` with `DecompressionBombError` before Ghostscript is ever run; via `scale=2` the same raster is rendered first and the `DecompressionBombError` only appears afterwards, when Pillow opens the file Ghostscript produced. (Between 1× and 2× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`, `Image.open()` only emits `DecompressionBombWarning`, so with the default warning filter an EPS declaring e.g. 13377×13377 is already rendered at `scale=1` — a 537 MB PNM. Against that baseline, `scale=2` is 4× an already-permitted render.)\n\u003e \n\u003e The arithmetic is in `Ghostscript()`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:91-97\n\u003e     # Hack to support hi-res rendering\n\u003e     scale = int(scale) or 1\n\u003e     width = size[0] * scale\n\u003e     height = size[1] * scale\n\u003e     # resolution is dependent on bbox and size\n\u003e     res_x = 72.0 * width / (bbox[2] - bbox[0])\n\u003e     …\n\u003e # src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:139-142\n\u003e     command = [\n\u003e         gs_binary,\n\u003e         \"-q\",  # quiet mode\n\u003e         f\"-g{width:d}x{height:d}\",  # set output geometry (pixels)\n\u003e     …\n\u003e # src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:165-168\n\u003e         subprocess.check_call(command, startupinfo=startupinfo)\n\u003e         with Image.open(outfile) as out_im:\n\u003e             out_im.load()\n\u003e             return out_im.im.copy()\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e and its caller does nothing between taking `scale` from the application and invoking it:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:411-417\n\u003e     def load(self, scale: int = 1, transparency: bool = False) -\u003e …:\n\u003e         # Load EPS via Ghostscript\n\u003e         if self.tile:\n\u003e             assert self.fp is not None\n\u003e             self.im = Ghostscript(self.tile, self.size, self.fp, scale, transparency)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `size` here is `self._size`, computed in `_open()` from the file's `%%BoundingBox` (or `%ImageData`) and checked once by `Image.open()` at `src/PIL/Image.py:3700`. For the benign path (`scale=1`) that check covers exactly what Ghostscript is asked to render. For `scale=N` the rendered pixel count is N² times what was checked, so a file can sit just under the limit at open time and have Ghostscript render 4× (scale 2), 9× (scale 3), 16× (scale 4) the configured budget. The first point at which Pillow looks at the real size again is `Image.open(outfile)` at line 166 — after `subprocess.check_call` has returned, i.e. after Ghostscript has spent the CPU and written `width*height*3` bytes to `tempfile.mkstemp()`'s directory.\n\u003e \n\u003e `WmfStubImageFile.load(dpi)` has the same shape (file-derived extent × caller-supplied factor → new raster) and, since af70b075a, calls `Image._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)` on the recomputed size before rendering (`src/PIL/WmfImagePlugin.py:127-131`). The EPS path lacks the equivalent line. The gap dates from ce2955ec7128 (\"Throw an exception when an opened image is larger than an arbitrary limit\", 2014-05-14, first released in 2.5.0), which added the check to `Image.open()` without covering the `scale` path.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Affected are applications that open untrusted EPS/PS with Ghostscript installed and call `load(scale=N)` with a fixed N \u003e 1, as the handbook suggests for non-blurry output. Handing untrusted EPS to Ghostscript at all already lets the same attacker tie up a worker with an equally tiny file (e.g. a PostScript `{} loop`; Pillow runs `gs` with no timeout), so this bug adds a constant factor rather than a new denial-of-service capability. Within that setting, a ~110-byte upload makes each request run Ghostscript over N² × `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` pixels (N² × 2·`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` if the application does not turn `DecompressionBombWarning` into an error) and write a `W·H·3`-byte PNM into the temp directory — 972 MB for the example above at scale 2, 2.2 GB at scale 3, 8.6 GB for a 178-Mpixel declaration at scale 4 — before any limit is applied. The operator's `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` setting is exceeded by a factor of N²; N is an integer chosen by the application, not by the file, so the work stays bounded at N² × 2·`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`. The consequence is N² more Ghostscript CPU and temp-disk use per request than the configured limit intends; Pillow's own in-process pixel allocation remains bounded because `Image.open(outfile)` re-checks the PNM's size, and there is no memory corruption.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a sibling of the WMF `load(dpi)` issue fixed in af70b075a (\"Add decompression bomb check when loading at a different DPI\"); the EPS `scale` path was not covered by that change. Unlike the WMF case, where the file-controlled `_inch` makes the ratio unbounded, the EPS multiplier is fixed by the application.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e Run `repro` against the installed Pillow. Ghostscript does not need to be installed: the script sets `EpsImagePlugin.gs_binary = \"gs\"` so that `has_ghostscript()` skips its `gs --version` probe, and replaces `subprocess.check_call` with a function that records the command line Pillow builds and then raises `OSError`, so nothing is actually rendered.\n\u003e \n\u003e It writes a 76-byte EPS declaring `%%BoundingBox: 0 0 9000 9000` (the example above without the drawing line; 81 Mpixel, below the default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` of 89,478,485) and turns `DecompressionBombWarning` into an error, so any bomb check — warning or hard error — counts. It then calls `Image.open()` and `im.load(scale=2)`. `Image.open()` accepts the file and `load(scale=2)` reaches the intercepted Ghostscript call with `-g18000x18000` (324,000,000 pixels, 3.6× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`, a 972 MB `pnmraw` output with real Ghostscript). The script prints the geometry it captured.\n\u003e \n\u003e It prints `BUG` when Ghostscript was invoked with a `-g` geometry above `2*MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` (178,956,970) and no decompression-bomb check fired before that call; otherwise it prints `OK`. With the attached patch applied, `load(scale=2)` raises `DecompressionBombError` before Ghostscript is called and the script prints `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached patch checks the scaled size in `Ghostscript()` right after the scaled `width`/`height` are computed (`src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:93-94`) and before the `gs` command is built, mirroring `WmfStubImageFile.load`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e --- a/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n\u003e +++ b/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n\u003e @@ -92,6 +92,7 @@ def Ghostscript(\n\u003e      scale = int(scale) or 1\n\u003e      width = size[0] * scale\n\u003e      height = size[1] * scale\n\u003e +    Image._decompression_bomb_check((width, height))\n\u003e      # resolution is dependent on bbox and size\n\u003e      res_x = 72.0 * width / (bbox[2] - bbox[0])\n\u003e      res_y = 72.0 * height / (bbox[3] - bbox[1])\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `load(scale=N)` now raises `DecompressionBombError` / emits `DecompressionBombWarning` under the same `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` thresholds `Image.open()` applies, before Ghostscript runs instead of after. Placing the check in `Ghostscript()` rather than in `EpsImageFile.load()` is the single point where the rendered extent is decided, so it also covers direct callers of the module-level function. With the patch applied to a clean checkout the reproducer prints `OK` and `Tests/test_file_eps.py` passes. One cosmetic side effect: at `scale=1` the check re-emits the `DecompressionBombWarning` that `Image.open()` already issued for sizes between 1× and 2× the limit; guarding the call with `if scale \u003e 1:` would avoid the duplicate.\n\u003e \n\u003e The patch also adds `test_load_scale_decompression_bomb` to `Tests/test_file_eps.py`, in the style of the WMF test: it sets `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` to `im.width * im.height` and expects `im.load(scale=2)` to raise `DecompressionBombError`. Like the rest of that file it is skipped without Ghostscript, because `has_ghostscript()` is consulted at the top of `Ghostscript()`; moving the check above that call would let the test run without it.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch\n\u003e \n\u003e Add decompression bomb check on the scaled size in EpsImagePlugin.Ghostscript() before Ghostscript is invoked\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/Tests/test_file_eps.py b/Tests/test_file_eps.py\n\u003e index 70eced62a..de331d97f 100644\n\u003e --- a/Tests/test_file_eps.py\n\u003e +++ b/Tests/test_file_eps.py\n\u003e @@ -98,6 +98,14 @@ def test_sanity(filename: str, size: tuple[int, int], scale: int) -\u003e None:\n\u003e          assert image.format == \"EPS\"\n\u003e  \n\u003e  \n\u003e +@pytest.mark.skipif(not HAS_GHOSTSCRIPT, reason=\"Ghostscript not available\")\n\u003e +def test_load_scale_decompression_bomb(monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch) -\u003e None:\n\u003e +    with Image.open(FILE1) as im:\n\u003e +        monkeypatch.setattr(Image, \"MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS\", im.width * im.height)\n\u003e +        with pytest.raises(Image.DecompressionBombError):\n\u003e +            im.load(scale=2)\n\u003e +\n\u003e +\n\u003e  @pytest.mark.skipif(not HAS_GHOSTSCRIPT, reason=\"Ghostscript not available\")\n\u003e  def test_load() -\u003e None:\n\u003e      with Image.open(FILE1) as im:\n\u003e diff --git a/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n\u003e index 5eaefe339..ccc6f1d85 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n\u003e +++ b/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n\u003e @@ -92,6 +92,7 @@ def Ghostscript(\n\u003e      scale = int(scale) or 1\n\u003e      width = size[0] * scale\n\u003e      height = size[1] * scale\n\u003e +    Image._decompression_bomb_check((width, height))\n\u003e      # resolution is dependent on bbox and size\n\u003e      res_x = 72.0 * width / (bbox[2] - bbox[0])\n\u003e      res_y = 72.0 * height / (bbox[3] - bbox[1])\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 1,845 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e # EpsImagePlugin.Ghostscript(): load(scale=N) hands gs a -g geometry of\n\u003e # (W*N)x(H*N) with no _decompression_bomb_check on the scaled size; only the\n\u003e # unscaled WxH was checked by Image.open(). gs is not installed here, so we\n\u003e # set gs_binary and intercept subprocess.check_call to read the argv Pillow builds.\n\u003e import os, subprocess, tempfile, warnings\n\u003e from PIL import Image, EpsImagePlugin\n\u003e \n\u003e EpsImagePlugin.gs_binary = \"gs\"  # skip the `gs --version` probe\n\u003e path = os.path.join(tempfile.mkdtemp(), \"a.eps\")\n\u003e with open(path, \"w\") as f:  # 9000x9000 = 81 Mpx \u003c MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS (89.5 Mpx)\n\u003e     f.write(\"%!PS-Adobe-3.0 EPSF-3.0\\n%%BoundingBox: 0 0 9000 9000\\n%%EndComments\\nshowpage\\n\")\n\u003e \n\u003e calls = []\n\u003e def fake_check_call(cmd, **kw):\n\u003e     calls.append(cmd)\n\u003e     raise OSError(\"gs intercepted\")  # stop here; we only need the argv\n\u003e subprocess.check_call = fake_check_call\n\u003e \n\u003e hard = 2 * Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS\n\u003e bomb_before_gs = False\n\u003e with warnings.catch_warnings():\n\u003e     warnings.simplefilter(\"error\", Image.DecompressionBombWarning)\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         with Image.open(path) as im:\n\u003e             print(\"Image.open accepted\", os.path.getsize(path), \"byte EPS, size\", im.size)\n\u003e             im.load(scale=2)\n\u003e     except (Image.DecompressionBombError, Image.DecompressionBombWarning) as e:\n\u003e         bomb_before_gs = not calls\n\u003e         print(\"bomb check fired\", \"before\" if bomb_before_gs else \"after\", \"gs:\", e)\n\u003e     except OSError as e:\n\u003e         print(\"load() reached gs:\", e)\n\u003e \n\u003e over = False\n\u003e for c in calls:\n\u003e     g = next(a for a in c if a.startswith(\"-g\"))[2:]\n\u003e     w, h = map(int, g.split(\"x\"))\n\u003e     over |= w * h \u003e hard\n\u003e     print(f\"gs argv geometry -g{g} = {w*h} px ({w*h/Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS:.1f}x MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS, \"\n\u003e           f\"hard limit {hard}); pnmraw output would be {w*h*3} bytes\")\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if calls and over and not bomb_before_gs else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-vx6w-vv9f-6gvg | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112899\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112899](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112899)**.\n\n## [GHSA-vx6w-vv9f-6gvg](https://osv.dev/GHSA-vx6w-vv9f-6gvg)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e EPS load(scale) lets Ghostscript render past the decompression bomb limit\n\u003e Location: src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:92  in Ghostscript()\n\u003e Commit:   a707c6b21c313880d0771e54965d142dd42358b6\n\u003e ============================================================================\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e When an application opens an untrusted Encapsulated PostScript file and asks Pillow to render it at a larger scale, Pillow has Ghostscript produce an image several times bigger than Pillow's configured size limit allows. The size check runs only after Ghostscript has finished rendering and written the full raw image to a temporary file. An attacker who can upload a file of about 100 bytes makes each such request spend scale² times the Ghostscript rendering work and temporary disk space that the limit is meant to permit. For example, at scale 2 that is a 972 MB temporary file.\n\u003e \n\u003e The cause is in `Ghostscript()` in `src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py`. It multiplies the file-declared size by the caller's `scale` (lines 93-94) and passes the product to `gs` as the `-g` output geometry. It never calls `Image._decompression_bomb_check()` on the scaled size. The only check is the one `Image.open()` ran on the unscaled size, so a file declaring 9000×9000 passes, and `load(scale=2)` then asks Ghostscript for 18000×18000 (3.6× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`). A file declaring 18000×18000 directly would be refused before Ghostscript ever ran. `DecompressionBombError` fires only when Pillow reopens the output file, so Pillow's own in-process allocation stays bounded and there is no memory corruption.\n\u003e \n\u003e The effect is a constant-factor overshoot rather than an unbounded one, for three reasons:\n\u003e \n\u003e * The multiplier is an integer chosen by the application, not by the file.\n\u003e * With the default warning filter, `Image.open()` already lets files up to 2× the limit reach Ghostscript at `scale=1`.\n\u003e * Any application feeding untrusted files to Ghostscript is already exposed to a tiny file that keeps Ghostscript busy indefinitely, since `gs` runs with no timeout.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is the EPS counterpart of the WMF `load(dpi)` gap closed in af70b075a. It has been present since the decompression-bomb limit was introduced in ce2955ec7 (2.5.0). The attached reproducer stubs out Ghostscript and shows a 76-byte file passing `Image.open()` and reaching `gs` with `-g18000x18000`. A one-line patch mirroring the WMF check is included.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `EpsImageFile.load(scale=N)` (the documented way to rasterise an EPS at higher resolution) renders `width*N × height*N` pixels through Ghostscript, but the only decompression-bomb check on the way is the one `Image.open()` ran on the *unscaled*, file-declared `width × height`. The scaled product is never passed to `Image._decompression_bomb_check()` before `gs` is started.\n\u003e \n\u003e This 111-byte EPS declares 9000×9000 = 81,000,000 pixels, below the default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` (89,478,485), so `Image.open()` accepts it without even a warning:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e %!PS-Adobe-3.0 EPSF-3.0\n\u003e %%BoundingBox: 0 0 9000 9000\n\u003e %%EndComments\n\u003e 0 0 moveto 9000 9000 lineto stroke\n\u003e showpage\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `im.load(scale=2)` on it then executes\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e gs -q -g18000x18000 -r144.000000x144.000000 -dBATCH -dNOPAUSE -dSAFER -sDEVICE=pnmraw -sOutputFile=/tmp/tmpXXXX -c 0 0 translate -f attack.eps -c showpage\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e i.e. asks Ghostscript for an 18000×18000 = 324,000,000-pixel raster (3.6× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`), whose `pnmraw` output is a 972,000,019-byte file in the temp directory. An EPS that declares 18000×18000 directly is rejected by `Image.open()` with `DecompressionBombError` before Ghostscript is ever run; via `scale=2` the same raster is rendered first and the `DecompressionBombError` only appears afterwards, when Pillow opens the file Ghostscript produced. (Between 1× and 2× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`, `Image.open()` only emits `DecompressionBombWarning`, so with the default warning filter an EPS declaring e.g. 13377×13377 is already rendered at `scale=1` — a 537 MB PNM. Against that baseline, `scale=2` is 4× an already-permitted render.)\n\u003e \n\u003e The arithmetic is in `Ghostscript()`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:91-97\n\u003e     # Hack to support hi-res rendering\n\u003e     scale = int(scale) or 1\n\u003e     width = size[0] * scale\n\u003e     height = size[1] * scale\n\u003e     # resolution is dependent on bbox and size\n\u003e     res_x = 72.0 * width / (bbox[2] - bbox[0])\n\u003e     …\n\u003e # src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:139-142\n\u003e     command = [\n\u003e         gs_binary,\n\u003e         \"-q\",  # quiet mode\n\u003e         f\"-g{width:d}x{height:d}\",  # set output geometry (pixels)\n\u003e     …\n\u003e # src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:165-168\n\u003e         subprocess.check_call(command, startupinfo=startupinfo)\n\u003e         with Image.open(outfile) as out_im:\n\u003e             out_im.load()\n\u003e             return out_im.im.copy()\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e and its caller does nothing between taking `scale` from the application and invoking it:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e # src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:411-417\n\u003e     def load(self, scale: int = 1, transparency: bool = False) -\u003e …:\n\u003e         # Load EPS via Ghostscript\n\u003e         if self.tile:\n\u003e             assert self.fp is not None\n\u003e             self.im = Ghostscript(self.tile, self.size, self.fp, scale, transparency)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `size` here is `self._size`, computed in `_open()` from the file's `%%BoundingBox` (or `%ImageData`) and checked once by `Image.open()` at `src/PIL/Image.py:3700`. For the benign path (`scale=1`) that check covers exactly what Ghostscript is asked to render. For `scale=N` the rendered pixel count is N² times what was checked, so a file can sit just under the limit at open time and have Ghostscript render 4× (scale 2), 9× (scale 3), 16× (scale 4) the configured budget. The first point at which Pillow looks at the real size again is `Image.open(outfile)` at line 166 — after `subprocess.check_call` has returned, i.e. after Ghostscript has spent the CPU and written `width*height*3` bytes to `tempfile.mkstemp()`'s directory.\n\u003e \n\u003e `WmfStubImageFile.load(dpi)` has the same shape (file-derived extent × caller-supplied factor → new raster) and, since af70b075a, calls `Image._decompression_bomb_check(self.size)` on the recomputed size before rendering (`src/PIL/WmfImagePlugin.py:127-131`). The EPS path lacks the equivalent line. The gap dates from ce2955ec7128 (\"Throw an exception when an opened image is larger than an arbitrary limit\", 2014-05-14, first released in 2.5.0), which added the check to `Image.open()` without covering the `scale` path.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Affected are applications that open untrusted EPS/PS with Ghostscript installed and call `load(scale=N)` with a fixed N \u003e 1, as the handbook suggests for non-blurry output. Handing untrusted EPS to Ghostscript at all already lets the same attacker tie up a worker with an equally tiny file (e.g. a PostScript `{} loop`; Pillow runs `gs` with no timeout), so this bug adds a constant factor rather than a new denial-of-service capability. Within that setting, a ~110-byte upload makes each request run Ghostscript over N² × `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` pixels (N² × 2·`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` if the application does not turn `DecompressionBombWarning` into an error) and write a `W·H·3`-byte PNM into the temp directory — 972 MB for the example above at scale 2, 2.2 GB at scale 3, 8.6 GB for a 178-Mpixel declaration at scale 4 — before any limit is applied. The operator's `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` setting is exceeded by a factor of N²; N is an integer chosen by the application, not by the file, so the work stays bounded at N² × 2·`MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`. The consequence is N² more Ghostscript CPU and temp-disk use per request than the configured limit intends; Pillow's own in-process pixel allocation remains bounded because `Image.open(outfile)` re-checks the PNM's size, and there is no memory corruption.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a sibling of the WMF `load(dpi)` issue fixed in af70b075a (\"Add decompression bomb check when loading at a different DPI\"); the EPS `scale` path was not covered by that change. Unlike the WMF case, where the file-controlled `_inch` makes the ratio unbounded, the EPS multiplier is fixed by the application.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproducing\n\u003e \n\u003e Run `repro` against the installed Pillow. Ghostscript does not need to be installed: the script sets `EpsImagePlugin.gs_binary = \"gs\"` so that `has_ghostscript()` skips its `gs --version` probe, and replaces `subprocess.check_call` with a function that records the command line Pillow builds and then raises `OSError`, so nothing is actually rendered.\n\u003e \n\u003e It writes a 76-byte EPS declaring `%%BoundingBox: 0 0 9000 9000` (the example above without the drawing line; 81 Mpixel, below the default `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` of 89,478,485) and turns `DecompressionBombWarning` into an error, so any bomb check — warning or hard error — counts. It then calls `Image.open()` and `im.load(scale=2)`. `Image.open()` accepts the file and `load(scale=2)` reaches the intercepted Ghostscript call with `-g18000x18000` (324,000,000 pixels, 3.6× `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`, a 972 MB `pnmraw` output with real Ghostscript). The script prints the geometry it captured.\n\u003e \n\u003e It prints `BUG` when Ghostscript was invoked with a `-g` geometry above `2*MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` (178,956,970) and no decompression-bomb check fired before that call; otherwise it prints `OK`. With the attached patch applied, `load(scale=2)` raises `DecompressionBombError` before Ghostscript is called and the script prints `OK`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e The attached patch checks the scaled size in `Ghostscript()` right after the scaled `width`/`height` are computed (`src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py:93-94`) and before the `gs` command is built, mirroring `WmfStubImageFile.load`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e --- a/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n\u003e +++ b/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n\u003e @@ -92,6 +92,7 @@ def Ghostscript(\n\u003e      scale = int(scale) or 1\n\u003e      width = size[0] * scale\n\u003e      height = size[1] * scale\n\u003e +    Image._decompression_bomb_check((width, height))\n\u003e      # resolution is dependent on bbox and size\n\u003e      res_x = 72.0 * width / (bbox[2] - bbox[0])\n\u003e      res_y = 72.0 * height / (bbox[3] - bbox[1])\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e `load(scale=N)` now raises `DecompressionBombError` / emits `DecompressionBombWarning` under the same `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` thresholds `Image.open()` applies, before Ghostscript runs instead of after. Placing the check in `Ghostscript()` rather than in `EpsImageFile.load()` is the single point where the rendered extent is decided, so it also covers direct callers of the module-level function. With the patch applied to a clean checkout the reproducer prints `OK` and `Tests/test_file_eps.py` passes. One cosmetic side effect: at `scale=1` the check re-emits the `DecompressionBombWarning` that `Image.open()` already issued for sizes between 1× and 2× the limit; guarding the call with `if scale \u003e 1:` would avoid the duplicate.\n\u003e \n\u003e The patch also adds `test_load_scale_decompression_bomb` to `Tests/test_file_eps.py`, in the style of the WMF test: it sets `MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS` to `im.width * im.height` and expects `im.load(scale=2)` to raise `DecompressionBombError`. Like the rest of that file it is skipped without Ghostscript, because `has_ghostscript()` is consulted at the top of `Ghostscript()`; moving the check above that call would let the test run without it.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The patch\n\u003e \n\u003e Add decompression bomb check on the scaled size in EpsImagePlugin.Ghostscript() before Ghostscript is invoked\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies to `src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py` at the commit above.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```diff\n\u003e diff --git a/Tests/test_file_eps.py b/Tests/test_file_eps.py\n\u003e index 70eced62a..de331d97f 100644\n\u003e --- a/Tests/test_file_eps.py\n\u003e +++ b/Tests/test_file_eps.py\n\u003e @@ -98,6 +98,14 @@ def test_sanity(filename: str, size: tuple[int, int], scale: int) -\u003e None:\n\u003e          assert image.format == \"EPS\"\n\u003e  \n\u003e  \n\u003e +@pytest.mark.skipif(not HAS_GHOSTSCRIPT, reason=\"Ghostscript not available\")\n\u003e +def test_load_scale_decompression_bomb(monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch) -\u003e None:\n\u003e +    with Image.open(FILE1) as im:\n\u003e +        monkeypatch.setattr(Image, \"MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS\", im.width * im.height)\n\u003e +        with pytest.raises(Image.DecompressionBombError):\n\u003e +            im.load(scale=2)\n\u003e +\n\u003e +\n\u003e  @pytest.mark.skipif(not HAS_GHOSTSCRIPT, reason=\"Ghostscript not available\")\n\u003e  def test_load() -\u003e None:\n\u003e      with Image.open(FILE1) as im:\n\u003e diff --git a/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py b/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n\u003e index 5eaefe339..ccc6f1d85 100644\n\u003e --- a/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n\u003e +++ b/src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py\n\u003e @@ -92,6 +92,7 @@ def Ghostscript(\n\u003e      scale = int(scale) or 1\n\u003e      width = size[0] * scale\n\u003e      height = size[1] * scale\n\u003e +    Image._decompression_bomb_check((width, height))\n\u003e      # resolution is dependent on bbox and size\n\u003e      res_x = 72.0 * width / (bbox[2] - bbox[0])\n\u003e      res_y = 72.0 * height / (bbox[3] - bbox[1])\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## The reproducer\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eReproducer — \u003ccode\u003erepro\u003c/code\u003e, 1,845 bytes (click to open)\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Its last line is `BUG` when the issue triggers and `OK` when it does not. Re-run on a clean build of that commit it printed `BUG`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e #!/usr/bin/env python3\n\u003e # EpsImagePlugin.Ghostscript(): load(scale=N) hands gs a -g geometry of\n\u003e # (W*N)x(H*N) with no _decompression_bomb_check on the scaled size; only the\n\u003e # unscaled WxH was checked by Image.open(). gs is not installed here, so we\n\u003e # set gs_binary and intercept subprocess.check_call to read the argv Pillow builds.\n\u003e import os, subprocess, tempfile, warnings\n\u003e from PIL import Image, EpsImagePlugin\n\u003e \n\u003e EpsImagePlugin.gs_binary = \"gs\"  # skip the `gs --version` probe\n\u003e path = os.path.join(tempfile.mkdtemp(), \"a.eps\")\n\u003e with open(path, \"w\") as f:  # 9000x9000 = 81 Mpx \u003c MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS (89.5 Mpx)\n\u003e     f.write(\"%!PS-Adobe-3.0 EPSF-3.0\\n%%BoundingBox: 0 0 9000 9000\\n%%EndComments\\nshowpage\\n\")\n\u003e \n\u003e calls = []\n\u003e def fake_check_call(cmd, **kw):\n\u003e     calls.append(cmd)\n\u003e     raise OSError(\"gs intercepted\")  # stop here; we only need the argv\n\u003e subprocess.check_call = fake_check_call\n\u003e \n\u003e hard = 2 * Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS\n\u003e bomb_before_gs = False\n\u003e with warnings.catch_warnings():\n\u003e     warnings.simplefilter(\"error\", Image.DecompressionBombWarning)\n\u003e     try:\n\u003e         with Image.open(path) as im:\n\u003e             print(\"Image.open accepted\", os.path.getsize(path), \"byte EPS, size\", im.size)\n\u003e             im.load(scale=2)\n\u003e     except (Image.DecompressionBombError, Image.DecompressionBombWarning) as e:\n\u003e         bomb_before_gs = not calls\n\u003e         print(\"bomb check fired\", \"before\" if bomb_before_gs else \"after\", \"gs:\", e)\n\u003e     except OSError as e:\n\u003e         print(\"load() reached gs:\", e)\n\u003e \n\u003e over = False\n\u003e for c in calls:\n\u003e     g = next(a for a in c if a.startswith(\"-g\"))[2:]\n\u003e     w, h = map(int, g.split(\"x\"))\n\u003e     over |= w * h \u003e hard\n\u003e     print(f\"gs argv geometry -g{g} = {w*h} px ({w*h/Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS:.1f}x MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS, \"\n\u003e           f\"hard limit {hard}); pnmraw output would be {w*h*3} bytes\")\n\u003e print(\"BUG\" if calls and over and not bomb_before_gs else \"OK\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-vx6w-vv9f-6gvg | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112899\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-112899",
