            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-110180",
                "GHSA-wjr9-j795-jxcr"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Impact\n\n`fast-uri` versions `\u003e= 2.4.2, \u003c= 2.4.7`, `\u003e= 3.1.3, \u003c= 3.1.8`, and `\u003e= 4.0.1, \u003c= 4.2.2` can silently rewrite the host of an HTTP-family URL (`http`, `https`, `ws`, `wss`, or any scheme with `domainHost: true`). For those schemes `canonicalizeHost()` passes the parsed host to the WHATWG `URL` parser and adopts its `.hostname` without checking that the parser read the whole input as a host. RFC 3986 permits `@` inside the host group and the WHATWG parser strips TAB, LF, and CR, so the authority `fast-uri` reports is rewritten by that second parser with no `error` set: data before an `@` is dropped and whitespace is removed.\n\nFor example, `fast-uri` parses `http://a@b@evil.example/` to host `evil.example` (dropping the `b` segment), and a host containing a raw TAB, CR, or LF has those characters stripped. `equal()` then treats a whitespace-smuggled host as the same as the clean one, and `resolve()` rewrites the authority the same way.\n\nApplications that use `fast-uri` to enforce host-based policy (denylists, loopback filtering, redirect validation, outbound proxy routing) on one representation, such as the raw input, see a policy/use desync: the canonical host `fast-uri` returns differs from what was checked, and traffic can be steered to an unintended destination.\n\n### Patches\n\nUpgrade to `fast-uri` `2.4.8`, `3.1.9`, or `4.2.3`.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nReject URIs whose authority contains more than one `@`, or contains TAB, CR, or LF, before calling `fast-uri`. Alternatively, treat any non-empty `parse(x).error` as fatal, and compare hosts only from the same `parse()` result.",
                "text": "### Impact\n\n`fast-uri` versions `\u003e= 2.4.2, \u003c= 2.4.7`, `\u003e= 3.1.3, \u003c= 3.1.8`, and `\u003e= 4.0.1, \u003c= 4.2.2` can silently rewrite the host of an HTTP-family URL (`http`, `https`, `ws`, `wss`, or any scheme with `domainHost: true`). For those schemes `canonicalizeHost()` passes the parsed host to the WHATWG `URL` parser and adopts its `.hostname` without checking that the parser read the whole input as a host. RFC 3986 permits `@` inside the host group and the WHATWG parser strips TAB, LF, and CR, so the authority `fast-uri` reports is rewritten by that second parser with no `error` set: data before an `@` is dropped and whitespace is removed.\n\nFor example, `fast-uri` parses `http://a@b@evil.example/` to host `evil.example` (dropping the `b` segment), and a host containing a raw TAB, CR, or LF has those characters stripped. `equal()` then treats a whitespace-smuggled host as the same as the clean one, and `resolve()` rewrites the authority the same way.\n\nApplications that use `fast-uri` to enforce host-based policy (denylists, loopback filtering, redirect validation, outbound proxy routing) on one representation, such as the raw input, see a policy/use desync: the canonical host `fast-uri` returns differs from what was checked, and traffic can be steered to an unintended destination.\n\n### Patches\n\nUpgrade to `fast-uri` `2.4.8`, `3.1.9`, or `4.2.3`.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nReject URIs whose authority contains more than one `@`, or contains TAB, CR, or LF, before calling `fast-uri`. Alternatively, treat any non-empty `parse(x).error` as fatal, and compare hosts only from the same `parse()` result."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-110180](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-110180)**.\n\n## [GHSA-wjr9-j795-jxcr](https://osv.dev/GHSA-wjr9-j795-jxcr)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e `fast-uri` versions `\u003e= 2.4.2, \u003c= 2.4.7`, `\u003e= 3.1.3, \u003c= 3.1.8`, and `\u003e= 4.0.1, \u003c= 4.2.2` can silently rewrite the host of an HTTP-family URL (`http`, `https`, `ws`, `wss`, or any scheme with `domainHost: true`). For those schemes `canonicalizeHost()` passes the parsed host to the WHATWG `URL` parser and adopts its `.hostname` without checking that the parser read the whole input as a host. RFC 3986 permits `@` inside the host group and the WHATWG parser strips TAB, LF, and CR, so the authority `fast-uri` reports is rewritten by that second parser with no `error` set: data before an `@` is dropped and whitespace is removed.\n\u003e \n\u003e For example, `fast-uri` parses `http://a@b@evil.example/` to host `evil.example` (dropping the `b` segment), and a host containing a raw TAB, CR, or LF has those characters stripped. `equal()` then treats a whitespace-smuggled host as the same as the clean one, and `resolve()` rewrites the authority the same way.\n\u003e \n\u003e Applications that use `fast-uri` to enforce host-based policy (denylists, loopback filtering, redirect validation, outbound proxy routing) on one representation, such as the raw input, see a policy/use desync: the canonical host `fast-uri` returns differs from what was checked, and traffic can be steered to an unintended destination.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to `fast-uri` `2.4.8`, `3.1.9`, or `4.2.3`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Reject URIs whose authority contains more than one `@`, or contains TAB, CR, or LF, before calling `fast-uri`. Alternatively, treat any non-empty `parse(x).error` as fatal, and compare hosts only from the same `parse()` result.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | fast-uri | 3.1.5 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | fast-uri | 3.1.5 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-wjr9-j795-jxcr | fast-uri | 2.4.8, 3.1.9, 4.2.3 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110180\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110180\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-110180](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-110180)**.\n\n## [GHSA-wjr9-j795-jxcr](https://osv.dev/GHSA-wjr9-j795-jxcr)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e `fast-uri` versions `\u003e= 2.4.2, \u003c= 2.4.7`, `\u003e= 3.1.3, \u003c= 3.1.8`, and `\u003e= 4.0.1, \u003c= 4.2.2` can silently rewrite the host of an HTTP-family URL (`http`, `https`, `ws`, `wss`, or any scheme with `domainHost: true`). For those schemes `canonicalizeHost()` passes the parsed host to the WHATWG `URL` parser and adopts its `.hostname` without checking that the parser read the whole input as a host. RFC 3986 permits `@` inside the host group and the WHATWG parser strips TAB, LF, and CR, so the authority `fast-uri` reports is rewritten by that second parser with no `error` set: data before an `@` is dropped and whitespace is removed.\n\u003e \n\u003e For example, `fast-uri` parses `http://a@b@evil.example/` to host `evil.example` (dropping the `b` segment), and a host containing a raw TAB, CR, or LF has those characters stripped. `equal()` then treats a whitespace-smuggled host as the same as the clean one, and `resolve()` rewrites the authority the same way.\n\u003e \n\u003e Applications that use `fast-uri` to enforce host-based policy (denylists, loopback filtering, redirect validation, outbound proxy routing) on one representation, such as the raw input, see a policy/use desync: the canonical host `fast-uri` returns differs from what was checked, and traffic can be steered to an unintended destination.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to `fast-uri` `2.4.8`, `3.1.9`, or `4.2.3`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Reject URIs whose authority contains more than one `@`, or contains TAB, CR, or LF, before calling `fast-uri`. Alternatively, treat any non-empty `parse(x).error` as fatal, and compare hosts only from the same `parse()` result.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/pnpm-lock.yaml | fast-uri | 3.1.5 |\n| lockfile:/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/pnpm-lock.yaml | fast-uri | 3.1.5 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-wjr9-j795-jxcr | fast-uri | 2.4.8, 3.1.9, 4.2.3 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/egolint/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110180\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`/home/runner/work/empathy/empathy/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110180\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
